<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.110/en/sujato"><b>bad friendship</b></a> as the most detrimental external factor that leads to great harm for a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.110/en/sujato">AN 1.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.111/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> as the most significant external factor that leads to a person's great benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.111/en/sujato">AN 1.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.112/en/sujato"><b>pursuit of unwholesome states</b></a> combined with the neglect of wholesome states leads to great harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.112/en/sujato">AN 1.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.113/en/sujato"><b>pursuit of wholesome states</b></a> while avoiding unwholesome states is an internal factor that leads to great benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.113/en/sujato">AN 1.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that monks who correctly identify and explain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.140/en/sujato"><b>non-Dhamma</b></a> as non-Dhamma are acting for the welfare and happiness of many? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.140/en/sujato">AN 1.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that monks who properly distinguish between what is Dhamma and what is not generate much <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.140/en/sujato"><b>merit</b></a> for themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.140/en/sujato">AN 1.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that those who clarify the difference between true teaching and false teaching help to maintain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.140/en/sujato"><b>true Dhamma</b></a> in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.140/en/sujato">AN 1.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.170/en/sujato"><b>one person</b></a> whose arising in the world is for the benefit of many and the compassion for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.170/en/sujato">AN 1.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the appearance of a Tathagata, an Arahant, a Perfectly Enlightened One, is for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.170/en/sujato"><b>welfare of many</b></a>, including both devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.170/en/sujato">AN 1.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the manifestation of a Tathagata in the world is an event that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.171/en/sujato"><b>rare to appear</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.171/en/sujato">AN 1.171</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the arising of a Tathagata as the appearance of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.172/en/sujato"><b>extraordinary man</b></a> in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.172/en/sujato">AN 1.172</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.173/en/sujato"><b>passing away</b></a> of a Tathagata is a cause for regret for many people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.173/en/sujato">AN 1.173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared himself to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.174/en/sujato"><b>without a counterpart</b></a>, secondless, and incomparable among all beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.174/en/sujato">AN 1.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.174/en/sujato"><b>foremost of bipeds</b></a>, unequaled and peerless in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.174/en/sujato">AN 1.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a Tathagata is a unique individual who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.174/en/sujato"><b>peerless</b></a> and without equal among humans and devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.174/en/sujato">AN 1.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is impossible for a person possessed of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.268/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> to ever regard any conditioned thing as permanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.268/en/sujato">AN 1.268</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted a person of right view with a worldling, noting that only a worldling might view a conditioned thing as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.268/en/sujato"><b>permanent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.268/en/sujato">AN 1.268</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.112/en/sujato"><b>neglecting</b></a> the practice of wholesome qualities while engaging in unwholesome ones is a path to great misfortune? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.112/en/sujato">AN 1.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged constant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.113/en/sujato"><b>devotion</b></a> to wholesome states as a primary means to achieve great benefit in one's life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.113/en/sujato">AN 1.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that his own appearance as the Tathagata is specifically for the happiness of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.170/en/sujato"><b>devas and humans</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.170/en/sujato">AN 1.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how for a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.314/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a>, whatever bodily, verbal, or mental actions they perform in accordance with that view lead only to what is unpleasant, disagreeable, and painful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.314/en/sujato">AN 1.314</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the negative outcomes of a person's intentions and volitional formations under the influence of wrong view to the bitter fruit produced by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.314/en/sujato"><b>neem seed</b></a>, a kosātaki seed, or a bitter gourd seed planted in moist soil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.314/en/sujato">AN 1.314</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, just as a bitter seed converts the nutrients it absorbs from the earth and water into pungency, a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.314/en/sujato"><b>bad view</b></a> causes all their aspirations and deeds to result in suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.314/en/sujato">AN 1.314</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.315/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>, their bodily, verbal, and mental conduct—along with their intentions and wishes—all lead to what is palatable, agreeable, and conducive to happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.315/en/sujato">AN 1.315</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.315/en/sujato"><b>sugarcane seed</b></a> to illustrate how an auspicious view ensures that all of a person’s efforts and volitional formations result in sweetness and deliciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.315/en/sujato">AN 1.315</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that just as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.315/en/sujato"><b>grape seed</b></a> or a rice seed produces a sweet and delightful essence from the moisture of the earth, a person with right view experiences pleasant results from all their actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.315/en/sujato">AN 1.315</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.315/en/sujato"><b>auspicious view</b></a> as the specific reason why a person’s mental volitions, aspirations, and choices lead to outcomes that are desirable and beneficial? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.315/en/sujato">AN 1.315</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a "seed" is bad—referring to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.314/en/sujato"><b>micchādiṭṭhi</b></a> (wrong view)—even the neutral resources of the earth and water are processed in a way that leads to a bitter and unpleasant result? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.314/en/sujato">AN 1.314</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.315/en/sujato"><b>nature of the view</b></a> is the determining factor for whether one's life experiences and deeds result in "sweetness" (happiness) or "bitterness" (suffering)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.315/en/sujato">AN 1.315</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one with right view, every <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.315/en/sujato"><b>volitional formation</b></a> (<i>saṅkhārā</i>) and prayer is directed toward what is pleasant and beneficial because the underlying "seed" of their perspective is wholesome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.315/en/sujato">AN 1.315</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is impossible for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.284/en/sujato"><b>bodily misconduct</b></a> to result in a fruit that is desirable, pleasant, and agreeable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.284/en/sujato">AN 1.284</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, it is entirely possible for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.285/en/sujato"><b>verbal misconduct</b></a> to result in a fruit that is undesirable, unpleasant, and disagreeable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.285/en/sujato">AN 1.285</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught it is an impossibility for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.286/en/sujato"><b>mental misconduct</b></a> to yield a desirable or pleasant result, as the moral law of causality dictates it can only lead to unpleasant fruits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.286/en/sujato">AN 1.286</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized it is impossible for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.287/en/sujato"><b>bodily good conduct</b></a> to produce an undesirable, unpleasant, or disagreeable fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.287/en/sujato">AN 1.287</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that it is possible for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.288/en/sujato"><b>verbal good conduct</b></a> to result in a fruit that is desirable, pleasant, and agreeable to the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.288/en/sujato">AN 1.288</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.289/en/sujato"><b>mental good conduct</b></a> has the inherent capacity to produce agreeable and pleasant results, whereas the opposite is impossible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.289/en/sujato">AN 1.289</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that someone who engages in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.290/en/sujato"><b>bodily misconduct</b></a> cannot, upon the breakup of the body after death, be reborn in a good destination or a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.290/en/sujato">AN 1.290</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is a natural possibility that a person committed to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.291/en/sujato"><b>verbal misconduct</b></a> will be reborn in a state of misery, a bad destination, or hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.291/en/sujato">AN 1.291</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that it is impossible for rebirth in a heavenly world to be the result of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.292/en/sujato"><b>mental misconduct</b></a>, as such conduct leads instead to the lower realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.292/en/sujato">AN 1.292</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught it is impossible for a person who has practiced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.293/en/sujato"><b>bodily good conduct</b></a> to be reborn in hell or a state of misery after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.293/en/sujato">AN 1.293</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.294/en/sujato"><b>verbal good conduct</b></a> as a cause that makes it possible for an individual to be reborn in a good destination or a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.294/en/sujato">AN 1.294</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that someone characterized by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.295/en/sujato"><b>mental good conduct</b></a> will find it possible to be reborn in a heavenly world after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.295/en/sujato">AN 1.295</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.311/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> (yoniso manasikāra) is the single most important factor for causing unarisen right view to arise in a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.311/en/sujato">AN 1.311</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for those who already possess right view, it is through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.311/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> that this right view increases and reaches fulfillment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.311/en/sujato">AN 1.311</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the moral law of causality is so fixed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.284/en/sujato"><b>misconduct</b></a> of body, speech, or mind can never result in a pleasant experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.284/en/sujato">AN 1.284</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that in a poorly expounded teaching and discipline, both the person who encourages others to practice it and the one who follows those instructions produce <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.320/en/sujato"><b>much demerit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.320/en/sujato">AN 1.320</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, in a well-expounded teaching and discipline, those who encourage others to practice and those who practice it themselves all generate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.321/en/sujato"><b>much merit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.321/en/sujato">AN 1.321</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an individual who is energetic and exerts great effort within a badly expounded teaching and discipline still lives in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.324/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> because the teaching itself is fundamentally flawed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.324/en/sujato">AN 1.324</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that in a well-expounded teaching and discipline, a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.325/en/sujato"><b>lazy</b></a> lives in suffering because they fail to take advantage of such an excellent and effective teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.325/en/sujato">AN 1.325</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the foul smell of even a tiny bit of excrement to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.328/en/sujato"><b>continued existence</b></a>, stating that he does not praise rebirth for even the brief duration of a finger-snap? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.328/en/sujato">AN 1.328</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the comparison of scarce parklands versus vast rough terrain to illustrate that beings born on land are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.333/en/sujato"><b>few in number</b></a> compared to the vast number of beings born in water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.333/en/sujato">AN 1.333</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that beings who are reborn as humans are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.334/en/sujato"><b>few</b></a>, while the number of beings reborn in other, non-human realms is much more numerous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.334/en/sujato">AN 1.334</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that those reborn in the central provinces are few, while far more beings are reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.335/en/sujato"><b>outlying districts</b></a> among those who do not understand the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.335/en/sujato">AN 1.335</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who possess the wisdom to distinguish the meaning of what is well-spoken from what is ill-spoken are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.336/en/sujato"><b>few in number</b></a>, while those who are dull are many? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.336/en/sujato">AN 1.336</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who possess the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.337/en/sujato"><b>noble eye of wisdom</b></a> are rare, whereas the vast majority of beings are immersed in ignorance and confusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.337/en/sujato">AN 1.337</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that those beings who actually get the opportunity to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.338/en/sujato"><b>see a Tathagata</b></a> are very few compared to the multitude who do not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.338/en/sujato">AN 1.338</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that the number of beings who have the rare chance to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.339/en/sujato"><b>hear the Dhamma</b></a> and Discipline proclaimed by a Tathagata is extremely small? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.339/en/sujato">AN 1.339</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that among those who hear the Dhamma, only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.340/en/sujato"><b>few retain</b></a> the teachings in their memory, while many more do not hold onto them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.340/en/sujato">AN 1.340</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even among those who have retained the teachings, those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.341/en/sujato"><b>examine the meaning</b></a> of those teachings are very few? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.341/en/sujato">AN 1.341</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who understand both the meaning and the text of the Dhamma and then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.342/en/sujato"><b>practice in accordance</b></a> with it are rare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.342/en/sujato">AN 1.342</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that those who actually experience a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.343/en/sujato"><b>sense of urgency</b></a> when faced with things that should cause urgency are few compared to those who remain unperturbed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.343/en/sujato">AN 1.343</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that of those who do feel a sense of urgency, only a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.344/en/sujato"><b>few strive wisely</b></a> to overcome their spiritual situation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.344/en/sujato">AN 1.344</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those who can gain concentration and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.345/en/sujato"><b>one-pointedness of mind</b></a> by making relinquishment their object are very few? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.345/en/sujato">AN 1.345</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the rarity of those who obtain choice, delicious food to the small number of beings who obtain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.346/en/sujato"><b>taste of liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.346/en/sujato">AN 1.346</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his disciples to specifically train themselves so that they might become those who obtain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.347/en/sujato"><b>taste of the Dhamma</b></a> and the taste of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.347/en/sujato">AN 1.347</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy comparing the small amount of pleasant parks and groves in Jambudīpa to the very few beings who, after passing away as humans, are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.348/en/sujato"><b>reborn as humans</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.348/en/sujato">AN 1.348</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the number of beings who pass away as humans and are reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.349/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a> is far greater than the number of those who are reborn again in the human realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.349/en/sujato">AN 1.349</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who pass away as humans and find rebirth in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.350/en/sujato"><b>sphere of afflicted spirits</b></a> are much more numerous than those who manage to attain a human rebirth once more? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.350/en/sujato">AN 1.350</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is a rare occurrence for beings passing away as humans to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.351/en/sujato"><b>reborn among devas</b></a>, whereas those reborn in hell are far more common? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.351/en/sujato">AN 1.351</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that beings who pass away as devas and are successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.354/en/sujato"><b>reborn as devas</b></a> are few, while a much larger number are reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.354/en/sujato">AN 1.354</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a being passing away from a celestial realm, it is uncommon to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.357/en/sujato"><b>reborn as a human</b></a>, as the majority are reborn in the animal realm or other lower destinations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.357/en/sujato">AN 1.357</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that among beings passing away from hell, only a small minority are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.360/en/sujato"><b>reborn as humans</b></a>, while the vast majority are reborn back into hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.360/en/sujato">AN 1.360</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that beings who pass away from hell and are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.363/en/sujato"><b>reborn among devas</b></a> are rare compared to the many who are reborn in the sphere of afflicted spirits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.363/en/sujato">AN 1.363</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that of the beings who pass away from the animal realm, very few are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.366/en/sujato"><b>reborn as humans</b></a>, with far more being reborn in hell or the animal realm again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.366/en/sujato">AN 1.366</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that beings passing away from the animal realm who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.369/en/sujato"><b>reborn among devas</b></a> are quite few, while those who end up in the sphere of afflicted spirits are many? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.369/en/sujato">AN 1.369</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings who pass away from the sphere of afflicted spirits and are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.372/en/sujato"><b>reborn as humans</b></a> are few, whereas those reborn in hell are far more numerous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.372/en/sujato">AN 1.372</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that only a small number of beings passing away from the sphere of afflicted spirits are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.375/en/sujato"><b>reborn among devas</b></a>, while many more are reborn back into the sphere of afflicted spirits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.375/en/sujato">AN 1.375</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the vast amount of rugged terrain, stakes, and thorns on earth to the majority of beings who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.348/en/sujato"><b>reborn in the animal realm</b></a> after passing away as humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.348/en/sujato">AN 1.348</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings who pass away from the animal realm and are reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.367/en/sujato"><b>unfortunate destinations</b></a> like hell are much more common than those who find a higher rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.367/en/sujato">AN 1.367</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that whether a being passes away from hell, the animal realm, or the sphere of afflicted spirits, attaining a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.360-377/en/sujato"><b>human or celestial birth</b></a> is always the rare exception rather than the rule? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.360-377/en/sujato">AN 1.360-377</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.378/en/sujato"><b>forest-dwelling</b></a> as the foremost acquisition among the various gains a monk can attain, highlighting its importance for seclusion and spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.378/en/sujato">AN 1.378</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha designated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.379/en/sujato"><b>alms-gathering</b></a> as a supreme gain, emphasizing the value of this traditional monastic practice for sustaining the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.379/en/sujato">AN 1.379</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.380/en/sujato"><b>wearing rag-robes</b></a> as a pinnacle of acquisition for those dedicated to a life of simplicity and renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.380/en/sujato">AN 1.380</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.381/en/sujato"><b>possessing only three robes</b></a> to be a foremost acquisition, illustrating the virtue of minimizing personal belongings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.381/en/sujato">AN 1.381</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha regarded <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.382/en/sujato"><b>being a speaker of Dhamma</b></a> as a supreme acquisition, recognizing the vital role of those who can clearly articulate the teachings to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.382/en/sujato">AN 1.382</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.383/en/sujato"><b>being a master of the Discipline</b></a> as a foremost gain for a monk, as it ensures the preservation of monastic rules and communal harmony? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.383/en/sujato">AN 1.383</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.384/en/sujato"><b>great learning</b></a> as a supreme acquisition, suggesting that the deep internalizing of the Dhamma is a significant gain for those on the path to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.384/en/sujato">AN 1.384</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.385/en/sujato"><b>venerable seniority</b></a> as a foremost acquisition, acknowledging the stability and respect that comes with long-term commitment to the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.385/en/sujato">AN 1.385</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.386/en/sujato"><b>perfection of deportment</b></a> as a supreme gain, noting that a practitioner's composed and graceful behavior is a significant spiritual asset? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.386/en/sujato">AN 1.386</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recognized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.387/en/sujato"><b>having a following</b></a> as a foremost acquisition, where the support of dedicated disciples can help further the spread of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.387/en/sujato">AN 1.387</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.388/en/sujato"><b>having a great retinue</b></a> as a major gain, highlighting the impact of a large, well-organized community of practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.388/en/sujato">AN 1.388</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.389/en/sujato"><b>noble birth</b></a> as a foremost acquisition, noting how a favorable family background can be used as a foundation for spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.389/en/sujato">AN 1.389</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.390/en/sujato"><b>beauty of complexion</b></a> to be a supreme gain, which can inspire confidence and joy in others when it is paired with spiritual virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.390/en/sujato">AN 1.390</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.391/en/sujato"><b>goodness of speech</b></a> as a pinnacle of acquisition, referring to the ability to communicate the Dhamma clearly and pleasantly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.391/en/sujato">AN 1.391</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.392/en/sujato"><b>fewness of wishes</b></a> is one of the most valuable gains an individual can possess, as it fosters contentment and reduces the burden of worldly attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.392/en/sujato">AN 1.392</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha regarded <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.393/en/sujato"><b>freedom from illness</b></a> as a paramount acquisition, recognizing that physical health provides the necessary foundation for sustained meditative practice and mental development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.393/en/sujato">AN 1.393</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a simile of the great ocean to explain that when mindfulness of the body is developed, all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.575/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a> partaking of true knowledge are included within it, just as all rivulets that flow into the sea are included within the great ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.575/en/sujato">AN 1.575</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that mindfulness of the body is the one thing that, when developed and cultivated, leads to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.576/en/sujato"><b>great sense of urgency</b></a> regarding spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.576/en/sujato">AN 1.576</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified mindfulness of the body as a single practice that, through consistent cultivation, results in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.577/en/sujato"><b>great benefit</b></a> for the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.577/en/sujato">AN 1.577</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, mindfulness of the body is the primary quality that leads a person to find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.578/en/sujato"><b>great security from bondage</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.578/en/sujato">AN 1.578</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the development of mindfulness of the body is the essential foundation for achieving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.579/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a> in one's daily life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.579/en/sujato">AN 1.579</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by practicing mindfulness of the body, a practitioner can attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.580/en/sujato"><b>attainment of knowledge and vision</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.580/en/sujato">AN 1.580</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that mindfulness of the body is a direct path to experiencing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.581/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwelling</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.581/en/sujato">AN 1.581</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that mindfulness of the body is the one thing that leads to the realization of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.582/en/sujato"><b>fruit of true knowledge and liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.582/en/sujato">AN 1.582</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when mindfulness of the body is cultivated, both the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.583/en/sujato"><b>body and mind</b></a> become tranquil and thoughts and examinations are stilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.583/en/sujato">AN 1.583</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the practice of mindfulness of the body ensures that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.584/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome states</b></a> that have not yet arisen do not arise, and those that have already arisen are abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.584/en/sujato">AN 1.584</a></p></div><hr>
