<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.607/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> leads to the development of extraordinary wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.607/en/sujato">AN 1.607</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.608/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> as the one thing that leads to wide-ranging wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.608/en/sujato">AN 1.608</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.609/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> leads to plentiful wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.609/en/sujato">AN 1.609</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.610/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> is the single thing that leads to quick wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.610/en/sujato">AN 1.610</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.611/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> leads to nimble wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.611/en/sujato">AN 1.611</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.612/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> leads to the realization of joyous wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.612/en/sujato">AN 1.612</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.613/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a>, when developed, leads to fleet wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.613/en/sujato">AN 1.613</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.614/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> leads to the development of sharp wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.614/en/sujato">AN 1.614</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the cultivation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.615/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> leads to penetrative wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.615/en/sujato">AN 1.615</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who do not partake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.616/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> do not partake of the Deathless, while those who partake of this specific mindfulness practice are truly partaking of the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.616/en/sujato">AN 1.616</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the vital importance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.616/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> by asserting that to engage with this practice is equivalent to engaging with the Deathless state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.616/en/sujato">AN 1.616</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for those whom <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.627/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> is not realized, the Deathless is also not realized, whereas the realization of this mindfulness leads directly to the realization of the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.627/en/sujato">AN 1.627</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the realization of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.627/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> as a definitive spiritual milestone, teaching that once this mindfulness is fully realized, the Deathless is realized as well? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an1.627/en/sujato">AN 1.627</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two distinct types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato"><b>faults</b></a> that one should be aware of: those that are visible in this very life and those that pertain to the future life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato">AN 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato"><b>fault visible in this life</b></a> can be understood by observing how a criminal is seized by authorities and subjected to various severe punishments for their wrongdoings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato">AN 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed many graphic historical punishments, such as being flogged, mutilated, or even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato"><b>sprayed with boiling oil</b></a>, as examples of the immediate dangers one faces when committing crimes in society? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato">AN 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a person who is truly afraid of worldly consequences will refrain from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato"><b>plundering</b></a> the property of others because they fear the harsh penalties imposed by the state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato">AN 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato"><b>fault pertaining to the future life</b></a> as the realization that misconduct of body, speech, and mind leads to painful results in the next life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato">AN 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one should reflect that engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato"><b>bodily misconduct</b></a>, verbal misconduct, or mental misconduct will result in being reborn in a state of misery or hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato">AN 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha encouraged practitioners to abandon bad habits and instead develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato"><b>good conduct</b></a> of body, speech, and mind to avoid the spiritual dangers that follow after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato">AN 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by fearing the consequences of future rebirths, an individual learns to maintain a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato"><b>pure self</b></a> and protects their spiritual integrity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato">AN 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha specifically instructed his followers to train themselves to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato"><b>afraid of faults</b></a>, urging them to see the inherent danger in both immediate legal punishments and future karmic repercussions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato">AN 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that if a person consistently sees the danger in wrong actions and remains afraid of committing faults, they can expect to eventually be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato"><b>released from all faults</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.1/en/sujato">AN 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two types of striving that are difficult to achieve in the world: the effort of laypeople to provide requisites and the effort of monastics for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.2/en/sujato"><b>relinquishment of all acquisitions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.2/en/sujato">AN 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, between the effort to provide physical requisites and the effort to abandon all attachments, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.2/en/sujato"><b>striving for the relinquishment</b></a> of all acquisitions is the foremost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.2/en/sujato">AN 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers to specifically train themselves with the resolve to engage in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.2/en/sujato"><b>striving for the abandonment</b></a> of all acquisitions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.2/en/sujato">AN 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reached supreme enlightenment and the unsurpassed security from bondage by realizing two qualities: discontent with wholesome states and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.5/en/sujato"><b>unflinching striving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.5/en/sujato">AN 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha practiced a form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.5/en/sujato"><b>unflinching effort</b></a> characterized by the determination that his flesh and blood might dry up as long as his skin, sinews, and bones remained, until he attained what is reachable by manly strength? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.5/en/sujato">AN 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that his own realization of enlightenment was achieved through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.5/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> and persistent effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.5/en/sujato">AN 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to strive without turning back so that they might soon realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.5/en/sujato"><b>supreme goal of the holy life</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.5/en/sujato">AN 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's attainment of the unsurpassed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.5/en/sujato"><b>security from bondage</b></a> was the result of never slacking in his energy or settling for his current wholesome achievements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.5/en/sujato">AN 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that dwelling on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.6/en/sujato"><b>gratification in things</b></a> that fetter prevents a person from successfully abandoning passion, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.6/en/sujato">AN 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who remains focused on the gratification of fettering things is not released from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.6/en/sujato"><b>birth, aging, and death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.6/en/sujato">AN 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by contemplating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.6/en/sujato"><b>revulsion toward things</b></a> that fetter, a person can successfully abandon the defilements of passion, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.6/en/sujato">AN 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that one who contemplates revulsion in things that fetter is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.6/en/sujato"><b>released from suffering</b></a>, including sorrow, lamentation, and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.6/en/sujato">AN 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified moral shamelessness and lack of moral dread as the two <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.7/en/sujato"><b>dark qualities</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.7/en/sujato">AN 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized moral shame and moral dread as the two <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.8/en/sujato"><b>bright qualities</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.8/en/sujato">AN 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the world is sustained and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.9/en/sujato"><b>protected by two qualities</b></a>: moral shame and moral dread? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.9/en/sujato">AN 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that without moral shame and dread, human society would fall into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.9/en/sujato"><b>promiscuous confusion</b></a> similar to that of goats, fowl, pigs, dogs, and jackals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.9/en/sujato">AN 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that without the protection of moral shame and dread, there would be no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.9/en/sujato"><b>recognition of family</b></a>, such as mothers, maternal aunts, or the wives of teachers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.9/en/sujato">AN 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed it is specifically because of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.9/en/sujato"><b>moral shame and moral dread</b></a> that humanity is able to maintain social order and show respect to elders and parents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.9/en/sujato">AN 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are two specific strengths available to practitioners, namely the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.11/en/sujato"><b>power of reflection</b></a> and the power of development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.11/en/sujato">AN 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.11/en/sujato"><b>power of reflection</b></a> as the mental process of considering how bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct leads to painful results in both this life and the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.11/en/sujato">AN 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that when a person uses reflection to abandon misconduct and cultivate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.11/en/sujato"><b>good conduct</b></a>, they are able to maintain themselves in a state of purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.11/en/sujato">AN 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato"><b>power of development</b></a> as a specific strength that belongs to those known as "trainees" (<i>sekha</i>) who are still training on the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato">AN 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that by relying on the power of development, the Buddha stated that a practitioner is able to successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato"><b>abandon lust</b></a>, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato">AN 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that once a practitioner has used the power of development to abandon lust, hatred, and delusion, they will not perform any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome act</b></a> or engage in evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato">AN 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as part of the power of development, the Buddha described entering and dwelling in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato"><b>first jhāna</b></a>, a state characterized by rapture and pleasure born of seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato">AN 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato"><b>second jhāna</b></a> as a state of internal confidence and unification of mind that is free from thought and examination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato">AN 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato"><b>third jhāna</b></a>, a person dwells equanimous and mindful, experiencing a physical pleasure that the noble ones call "dwelling happily"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato">AN 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a> as a state of pure mindfulness and equanimity, achieved by the abandoning of both pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.13/en/sujato">AN 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that if both the offender and the accuser in a dispute fail to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato"><b>properly reflect</b></a> on their own actions, the conflict is expected to become long-lasting and harsh? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato">AN 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha observed that when monks fail to reflect on their own roles during a legal issue, they will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato"><b>not live comfortably</b></a> within the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato">AN 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha promised that if both the offender and the accuser engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato"><b>proper reflection</b></a>, the dispute will not lead to prolonged harshness or wildness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato">AN 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato"><b>offending monk</b></a> should reflect that their own unwholesome bodily behavior was the very reason they were seen and criticized by another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato">AN 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that an offender should realize their own actions caused another monk to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato"><b>displeased</b></a>, which led that monk to speak words of dissatisfaction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato">AN 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who has committed an offense should acknowledge that their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato"><b>displeasure</b></a> at being corrected led them to tell others about the situation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato">AN 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha highlighted that an offending monk should eventually admit that their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato"><b>transgression</b></a> has overcome them, comparing it to the way a tax falls on a merchant’s goods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato">AN 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a monk who acts as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato"><b>accuser</b></a> in a dispute should reflect that they would not have witnessed a fault if the other monk had not first engaged in unwholesome bodily conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato">AN 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that an accuser must reflect on how they became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato"><b>displeased</b></a> upon seeing an offense and subsequently spoke words that expressed that dissatisfaction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato">AN 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that when an accuser acknowledges that their words caused another to become upset, they should admit, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato"><b>the fault is mine</b></a>," taking responsibility for their part in the escalation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato">AN 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that when both parties in a dispute practice thorough self-reflection, the monks involved will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato"><b>live comfortably</b></a> and the issue will be settled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.15/en/sujato">AN 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the brahmin Jāṇussoṇi that beings are reborn in the plane of misery, a bad destination, or even in hell because of what they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.17/en/sujato"><b>done and not done</b></a>, which specifically refers to them having performed bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct while simultaneously failing to perform bodily, verbal, and mental good conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.17/en/sujato">AN 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the reason some beings are reborn in a happy destination or the heavenly world after the breakup of the body is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.17/en/sujato"><b>what they have done and not done</b></a>, which he defined as the person having practiced bodily, verbal, and mental good conduct while having successfully abstained from all forms of misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.17/en/sujato">AN 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the venerable Ānanda that there are five distinct dangers to be expected when a person engages in what is categorically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.18/en/sujato"><b>not to be done</b></a>—namely bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct—which include self-reproach, being censured by the wise, the spread of a bad reputation, dying in a state of confusion, and rebirth in a state of deprivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.18/en/sujato">AN 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when a person performs bodily, verbal, and mental <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.18/en/sujato"><b>good conduct</b></a>, which he calls "what is to be done," they can expect five specific benefits: they will not reproach themselves, the wise will praise them after investigation, a good reputation will spread, they will die unconfused, and they will be reborn in a happy destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.18/en/sujato">AN 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one of the severe worldly consequences for a person who persists in bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct is that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.18/en/sujato"><b>bad reputation</b></a> will spread about them, and further warned that such an individual is likely to meet their end in a state of mental confusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.18/en/sujato">AN 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the venerable Ānanda that an individual who practices what is "to be done" will find that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.18/en/sujato"><b>the wise praise</b></a> them after a thorough investigation of their character, and such a person will ultimately pass away with a clear mind and be reborn in the heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.18/en/sujato">AN 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught it is possible to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.19/en/sujato"><b>abandon the unwholesome</b></a>, noting that he would never ask his followers to give up these qualities if it were an impossible task? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.19/en/sujato">AN 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the reason one should strive toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.19/en/sujato"><b>abandoning the unwholesome</b></a> is specifically because those qualities, when left unabandoned, lead to one's long-term harm and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.19/en/sujato">AN 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that it is within a person's capability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.19/en/sujato"><b>develop the wholesome</b></a>, and that he only encourages this development because it is a goal that can actually be achieved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.19/en/sujato">AN 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person is successful in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.19/en/sujato"><b>developing the wholesome</b></a>, it leads directly to their long-term welfare and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.19/en/sujato">AN 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified poorly phrased words and wrongly interpreted meanings as the two primary factors that lead to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.20/en/sujato"><b>disappearance of the True Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.20/en/sujato">AN 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.20/en/sujato"><b>phrasing of the teachings</b></a> is poorly laid down, the underlying meaning of those teachings will also be wrongly interpreted and difficult to follow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.20/en/sujato">AN 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.20/en/sujato"><b>longevity of the True Dhamma</b></a> is ensured when followers maintain well-laid phrasing and a correct interpretation of the teachings' meaning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.20/en/sujato">AN 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when the phrasing of a teaching is well-placed, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.20/en/sujato"><b>meaning of the Dhamma</b></a> becomes easy to understand and correctly apply? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.20/en/sujato">AN 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a fool as a person who fails to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.21/en/sujato"><b>recognize a transgression</b></a> as a transgression, lacking the self-awareness to see their own faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.21/en/sujato">AN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the two marks of a fool is the refusal to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.21/en/sujato"><b>accept a confession</b></a> of a fault when it is offered by another in accordance with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.21/en/sujato">AN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a wise person as one who is able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.21/en/sujato"><b>see a transgression</b></a> as a transgression, acknowledging their own mistakes clearly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.21/en/sujato">AN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person is characterized by the righteous <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.21/en/sujato"><b>acceptance of an apology</b></a> when someone else confesses a fault to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.21/en/sujato">AN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a person with a hostile and malicious mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.22/en/sujato"><b>misrepresents the Tathagata</b></a> by speaking untruths about him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.22/en/sujato">AN 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that even a person who has faith can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.22/en/sujato"><b>misrepresent the Tathagata</b></a> if they have wrongly grasped or misunderstood the core of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.22/en/sujato">AN 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an individual whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.26/en/sujato"><b>conduct is concealed</b></a> and secretive in their daily life can expect a future rebirth in hell or the animal realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.26/en/sujato">AN 2.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that maintaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.26/en/sujato"><b>unconcealed conduct</b></a> and acting with openness and honesty leads a person toward a favorable rebirth as a human or among the devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.26/en/sujato">AN 2.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.26/en/sujato"><b>secretive behavior</b></a> as a dangerous quality that results in one's destination being one of the two "states of misery" after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.26/en/sujato">AN 2.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.26/en/sujato"><b>openness in action</b></a> is a defining trait of those whose future path leads upward toward the heavenly worlds or a human existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.26/en/sujato">AN 2.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as both a proponent of action and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.34/en/sujato"><b>proponent of non-action</b></a> depending on whether one is referring to wholesome or unwholesome deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.34/en/sujato">AN 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advocated for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.34/en/sujato"><b>non-action</b></a> specifically in regard to bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct and the performance of various evil unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.34/en/sujato">AN 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered himself a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.34/en/sujato"><b>proponent of action</b></a> when it comes to the performance of bodily, verbal, and mental good conduct and various wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.34/en/sujato">AN 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a brahmin questioned his teachings, the Buddha clarified that he teaches the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.34/en/sujato"><b>non-performance of evil</b></a> rather than a total rejection of all activity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.34/en/sujato">AN 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the householder Anāthapiṇḍika that there are only two types of individuals in the world who are truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.35/en/sujato"><b>worthy of gifts</b></a>: the trainee (<i>sekha</i>) and the one beyond training (<i>asekha</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.35/en/sujato">AN 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that gifts should be given to those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.35/en/sujato"><b>straight in body</b></a>, speech, and mind, as such offerings yield great fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.35/en/sujato">AN 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.35/en/sujato"><b>trainee and the one beyond training</b></a> as the "field" for those making offerings, where what is given results in a great harvest of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.35/en/sujato">AN 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described those worthy of offerings as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.35/en/sujato"><b>upright in conduct</b></a> across all three doors of action: body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.35/en/sujato">AN 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the brahmin Ujjaya that he does not praise <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato"><b>violent sacrifices</b></a> where living beings such as cattle, goats, rams, or poultry are slaughtered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato">AN 4.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, Arahants and those who have entered the path to Arahantship will never attend a sacrifice that involves the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato"><b>slaughter of animals</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato">AN 4.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly praised <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato"><b>non-violent sacrifices</b></a>, such as regular giving or traditional family offerings, where no living creatures are harmed or killed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato">AN 4.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato"><b>Great Sages</b></a> who have fared well and followed the right path will only approach sacrifices that are free from violence and animal cruelty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato">AN 4.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged wise people to perform <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato"><b>non-violent offerings</b></a> because such sacrifices lead to great fruit and are beneficial for the donor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato">AN 4.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that for a person making an offering, a non-violent sacrifice is always <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato"><b>better and never worse</b></a> than one involving harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato">AN 4.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that when a sacrifice is performed without violence, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato"><b>deities are pleased</b></a> with the donor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato">AN 4.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified major ritual sacrifices of his time that involved significant animal slaughter as being of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato"><b>little fruit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato">AN 4.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato"><b>proper sacrifice</b></a> as one that is "non-violent" (<i>nirārambha</i>), where the donor gives regularly and follows family traditions without causing death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato">AN 4.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified he does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato"><b>reject all sacrifices</b></a>, but only those that are "violent" (<i>sārambha</i>) and involve the destruction of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato">AN 4.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a sacrifice becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato"><b>vast and expansive</b></a> when it is conducted in a way that avoids the slaughter of goats, cattle, and other various creatures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.39/en/sujato">AN 4.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.35/en/sujato"><b>straight in speech</b></a> and mind are the ones to whom offerings should be made to ensure the most merit for the giver? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.35/en/sujato">AN 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five types of persons found in the world, including one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato"><b>gives and then despises</b></a> the recipient because they harbor the thought, "I am the giver, this person is the receiver"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato">AN 5.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person might develop a habit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato"><b>despising through association</b></a> after living with another person for a period of two or three years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato">AN 5.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato"><b>easily led</b></a> (or gullible) as one who quickly believes whatever praise or blame they hear being spoken about others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato">AN 5.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a "wavering" or "fickle" person as someone who possesses only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato"><b>limited faith</b></a>, unstable devotion, and temporary affection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato">AN 5.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato"><b>dull and stupid</b></a> if they are unable to distinguish between wholesome and unwholesome states of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato">AN 5.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person lacking discernment fails to recognize the difference between <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato"><b>blameworthy and blameless</b></a> qualities in themselves and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato">AN 5.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of individuals who are so confused that they cannot tell the difference between <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato"><b>inferior and superior</b></a> states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato">AN 5.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person as "dull" if they fail to see the contrast between <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato"><b>dark and bright</b></a> states that have opposite counterparts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato">AN 5.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the vivid term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato"><b>dung-speaker</b></a> to describe someone who deliberately tells a lie when summoned to testify in an assembly or a court of law? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato">AN 3.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a "dung-speaker" is a person who utters a conscious lie either for their own sake, for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato"><b>sake of others</b></a>, or for some material gain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato">AN 3.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a "dung-speaker" as someone who says "I know" when they do not know, or "I saw" when they did not see, for the sake of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato"><b>trifling material gain</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato">AN 3.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato"><b>flower-speaker</b></a> as a person who, when summoned as a witness, honestly reports what they know and what they have seen without lying? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato">AN 3.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato"><b>flower-speaker</b></a> as one who refuses to tell a conscious lie, even when brought before a royal family, a guild, or an assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato">AN 3.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized a person as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato"><b>honey-speaker</b></a> if they abandon harsh speech and instead speak words that are gentle and affectionate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato">AN 3.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described speech that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato"><b>goes to the heart</b></a>, is polite, and is pleasing to many people as the defining quality of a "honey-speaker"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato">AN 3.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered speech that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato"><b>soothing to the ear</b></a> and delightful to the mind to be the hallmark of a person known as a "honey-speaker"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato">AN 3.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato"><b>abandoning harsh speech</b></a>, an individual becomes a "honey-speaker" whose words are affectionate and heart-warming to those who hear them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato">AN 3.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a "wavering" person as one who displays <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato"><b>short-lived devotion</b></a> and fickle confidence toward the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato">AN 5.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a person might provide <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato"><b>medicinal requisites</b></a> for the sick, yet still look down upon the person who receives their gift? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.141/en/sujato">AN 5.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three specific types of speakers existing in the world: the dung-speaker, the flower-speaker, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato"><b>honey-speaker</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.28/en/sujato">AN 3.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a monk as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato"><b>superior person</b></a> when they have abandoned five factors and have become endowed with five other specific factors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato">AN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a monk who has abandoned five specific factors as one who has successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato"><b>abandoned sensual desire</b></a>, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato">AN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is considered "fully accomplished" when they possess the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of virtue</b></a> belonging to one beyond training (an <i>asekha</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato">AN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is endowed with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of concentration</b></a> of one beyond training is one of the five ways a practitioner becomes "fully accomplished" in the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato">AN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who possesses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of wisdom</b></a> of one beyond training is regarded as a superior person who has lived the spiritual life to its fulfillment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato">AN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as "fully accomplished" when they are endowed with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of liberation</b></a> that belongs to one who has completed their training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato">AN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the final factor of a fully accomplished monk is being endowed with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of knowledge and vision</b></a> of liberation belonging to one beyond training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato">AN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk who has abandoned the five hindrances and attained the five aggregates of one beyond training is called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato"><b>consummate</b></a> in this Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato">AN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a monk to be truly accomplished, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> must be entirely abandoned along with other mental obstructions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato">AN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma-factors</b></a> that are foremost, long-standing, traditional, and ancient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato">AN 4.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the four ancient Dhamma-factors have never been confused and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato"><b>will not be confused</b></a> in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato">AN 4.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the four ancient Dhamma-factors are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato"><b>not rejected</b></a> by wise ascetics, brahmins, or any discerning people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato">AN 4.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato"><b>non-covetousness</b></a> as a foremost and ancient Dhamma-factor that should be maintained by practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato">AN 4.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato"><b>non-ill will</b></a> is one of the four traditional Dhamma-factors that are unconfused and beyond reproach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato">AN 4.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato"><b>right mindfulness</b></a> as an essential Dhamma-factor that is considered ancient and foremost in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato">AN 4.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> as the fourth ancient and traditional factor of Dhamma that wise people do not disparage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato">AN 4.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato"><b>without covetousness</b></a> and with a mind free of ill will to align with the ancient Dhamma-factors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato">AN 4.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should remain mindful and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato"><b>internally well-composed</b></a> with a one-pointed mind to fulfill the four traditional principles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.29/en/sujato">AN 4.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as having "lived the life" when they have fully abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato"><b>dullness and drowsiness</b></a> along with the other four hindrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato">AN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who has abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> and the other four hindrances is considered to have abandoned five factors necessary for becoming a superior person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.12/en/sujato">AN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are two types of longings that are extremely difficult for a person to abandon, one of which is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.118/en/sujato"><b>longing for gain</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.118/en/sujato">AN 2.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.118/en/sujato"><b>longing for life</b></a> itself as one of the two specific desires that are exceptionally hard for a human being to give up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.118/en/sujato">AN 2.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.119/en/sujato"><b>first to do a favor</b></a> for others, acting without being prompted or repaying a previous act, is one of the two types of individuals who are considered rare to find in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.119/en/sujato">AN 2.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.119/en/sujato"><b>grateful and thankful</b></a> for the kindness and favors shown to them by others is considered one of the rarest types of people to find within the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.119/en/sujato">AN 2.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an individual who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.120/en/sujato"><b>satisfied</b></a> with what they have received is one of the two types of people who are remarkably rare and difficult to find in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.120/en/sujato">AN 2.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a person who is capable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.120/en/sujato"><b>satisfying others</b></a> is a specific type of rare person who is difficult to encounter in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.120/en/sujato">AN 2.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized someone who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.121/en/sujato"><b>hoards everything</b></a> they receive instead of using or sharing it as one of two types of persons who are extremely difficult to satisfy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.121/en/sujato">AN 2.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.121/en/sujato"><b>squanders everything</b></a> they get immediately is considered one of the two kinds of individuals who are perpetually hard to satisfy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.121/en/sujato">AN 2.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.122/en/sujato"><b>not hoard</b></a> what they have gained is considered one of the two types of people who are easy to satisfy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.122/en/sujato">AN 2.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said an individual who does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.122/en/sujato"><b>not squander</b></a> what they receive but uses it appropriately is one of the two types of persons who are considered easy to satisfy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.122/en/sujato">AN 2.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that focusing on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.123/en/sujato"><b>sign of the beautiful</b></a> is one of the two primary conditions that lead to the arising of lust (rāga) in a person's mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.123/en/sujato">AN 2.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.123/en/sujato"><b>improper attention</b></a> (ayoniso manasikāra) as a key mental condition that facilitates the arising of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.123/en/sujato">AN 2.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that focusing on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.124/en/sujato"><b>sign of the repulsive</b></a> is one of the two conditions that lead to the arising of hatred (dosa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.124/en/sujato">AN 2.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.125/en/sujato"><b>voice of another</b></a> person, when not investigated with wisdom, is one of the two external conditions that lead to the development of wrong view (micchādiṭṭhi)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.125/en/sujato">AN 2.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.125/en/sujato"><b>improper attention</b></a> as one of the two essential internal conditions required for the arising of wrong view in an individual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.125/en/sujato">AN 2.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that hearing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.126/en/sujato"><b>voice of another</b></a>, specifically the teaching of the true Dhamma, is one of the two fundamental conditions for the arising of right view (sammādiṭṭhi)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.126/en/sujato">AN 2.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.126/en/sujato"><b>proper attention</b></a> (yoniso manasikāra) is the essential internal condition required for the arising of right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.126/en/sujato">AN 2.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified monastic offenses into two main categories based on their relative severity: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.127/en/sujato"><b>light and grave</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.127/en/sujato">AN 2.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between monastic offenses based on whether they are considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.128/en/sujato"><b>gross</b></a> (duṭṭhullā, sometimes translated as "wicked") or not gross? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.128/en/sujato">AN 2.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized monastic offenses by whether they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.129/en/sujato"><b>with a remainder</b></a> (sāvasesā), meaning they can be rehabilitated, or without a remainder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.129/en/sujato">AN 2.129</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three specific types of persons existing in the world: the one without expectation, the one with expectation, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>one who has rid himself of expectation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>person without expectation</b></a> (nirāso) as someone born into a low-caste family—such as scavengers, bamboo-workers, hunters, or carriage-makers—who has no hope of ever being consecrated as a king? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>born in a low family</b></a> might suffer from being "ugly, unsightly, and deformed," while also lacking access to basic necessities like food, clothing, and shelter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned physical ailments such as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>one-eyed or lame</b></a> when illustrating the difficult life of a person who lacks the expectation of ever achieving royal status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the "person with expectation" (āsaṃso) in a worldly sense is exemplified by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>eldest son</b></a> of a consecrated khattiya king, who rightfully expects to be consecrated himself one day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>consecrated king</b></a> as a "person who has rid himself of expectation" (vigatāso) because his former desire to be crowned has already been stilled and fulfilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as there are three types of people in the general world, there are also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>three types of monks</b></a> regarding their spiritual progress and expectations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>immoral monk</b></a>—one who is "rotten within" and merely a monk in name—as a person without expectation because he does not even hope to achieve liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>fake recluse</b></a> as someone who, upon hearing that another monk has attained liberation, has no expectation that he himself will ever reach that state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>virtuous monk</b></a> of good character is considered a "person with expectation" because he aspires to one day realize the destruction of the taints for himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom to describe the specific spiritual goal that a virtuous monk expects to realize through his own direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> as a "person who has rid himself of expectation" because they have already realized the destruction of the taints and have no further need to hope for it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that for an Arahant, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>expectation of liberation</b></a> they held before they were freed has been completely "stilled"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavānaṃ khayā) as the ultimate spiritual milestone that a monk with expectation hopes to achieve in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who lacks moral discipline to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>poor and deformed person</b></a> in the worldly analogy, as both lack the necessary conditions to expect a higher status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that a person of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>low family</b></a> might be "short of food and drink" and lead a life of "straitened circumstances" where even basic clothing is hard to obtain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>muddhāvasitto</b></a> to refer to a "head-anointed" or consecrated khattiya king when explaining the analogy of expectation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to a person who is "impure, of suspicious behavior, and of covert deeds" as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>rotten within</b></a>, which prevents them from having spiritual expectations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that hearing about another's success—whether a king's coronation or a monk's liberation—is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>catalyst</b></a> that reveals whether a person possesses expectation or has already stilled it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a monk who has already attained the goal, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato"><b>former expectation</b></a> of being liberated is no longer present because they have already achieved the "untainted" state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.13/en/sujato">AN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.87/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> (<i>cetovimutti</i>) and liberation by wisdom (<i>paññāvimutti</i>) as two essential qualities that should be developed by a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.87/en/sujato">AN 2.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha paired <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.88/en/sujato"><b>exertion and composure</b></a> (<i>paggāha</i> and <i>avikkhepa</i>) as two complementary qualities to be cultivated together in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.88/en/sujato">AN 2.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized all experience into the two primary domains of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.89/en/sujato"><b>mentality and materiality</b></a> (<i>nāma</i> and <i>rūpa</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.89/en/sujato">AN 2.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.90/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge and liberation</b></a> (<i>vijjā</i> and <i>vimutti</i>) are two qualities that result from a well-developed path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.90/en/sujato">AN 2.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.91/en/sujato"><b>view of existence</b></a> and the view of non-existence as two problematic perspectives that can keep a person bound to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.91/en/sujato">AN 2.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.92/en/sujato"><b>shamelessness and fearlessness</b></a> of wrongdoing (<i>ahirika</i> and <i>anottappa</i>) as two unwholesome qualities that lead to an ethical decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.92/en/sujato">AN 2.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.93/en/sujato"><b>moral shame and moral dread</b></a> (<i>hirī</i> and <i>ottappa</i>) as two vital protective qualities that guard a person against unwholesome actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.93/en/sujato">AN 2.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.94/en/sujato"><b>recalcitrance and bad friendship</b></a> (<i>dovacassatā</i> and <i>pāpamittatā</i>) are two major obstacles that prevent a person from receiving beneficial guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.94/en/sujato">AN 2.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.95/en/sujato"><b>amenability and good friendship</b></a> (<i>sovacassatā</i> and <i>kalyāṇamittatā</i>) as two key qualities that foster growth in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.95/en/sujato">AN 2.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.96/en/sujato"><b>skill in elements</b></a> and skill in attention (<i>dhātukusalatā</i> and <i>manasikārakusalatā</i>) are two important analytical capacities for a practitioner to develop? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.96/en/sujato">AN 2.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for those in the monastic life, the Buddha emphasized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.97/en/sujato"><b>skill in offenses</b></a> and skill in rehabilitation from offenses as two essential areas of expertise for maintaining discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.97/en/sujato">AN 2.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the word <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato"><b>fear</b></a> as a designation for sensual pleasures because those who are obsessed by desire for them are never free from danger in this world or the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato">AN 8.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described sensual pleasures as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> because those bound by the lust for them are not liberated from birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato">AN 8.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called sensual pleasures <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato"><b>a disease</b></a> and a boil because they cause internal affliction and agitation to those who are attached to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato">AN 8.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha designated sensual pleasures as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato"><b>a dart</b></a> because they pierce and wound the hearts of ordinary people who are infatuated by delightful forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato">AN 8.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that sensual pleasures are like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato"><b>a bog</b></a> in which the uninstructed worldling gets stuck, making it extremely difficult to escape the cycle of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato">AN 8.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato"><b>womb</b></a> as a designation for sensual pleasures, explaining that being bound by lust for them ensures a person will keep returning to birth again and again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato">AN 8.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato"><b>ardent bhikkhu</b></a> who maintains clear comprehension can cross the difficult mire of sensual pleasures and observe the world beyond the reach of birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.56/en/sujato">AN 8.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the five ways to remove resentment toward a person is by developing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.161/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> toward that individual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.161/en/sujato">AN 5.161</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that if resentment arises toward someone, one should practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.161/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> for that person as a method to remove the ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.161/en/sujato">AN 5.161</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, cultivating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.161/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> toward an individual is an effective method for dispelling resentment that has arisen toward them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.161/en/sujato">AN 5.161</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that resentment toward a person can be removed by practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.161/en/sujato"><b>non-attention</b></a> and not reflecting on that individual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.161/en/sujato">AN 5.161</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that reflecting on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.161/en/sujato"><b>ownership of deeds</b></a>—recognizing that a person is the owner and heir to their own actions—is a way to remove resentment toward them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.161/en/sujato">AN 5.161</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified ten specific qualities that allow a senior monk to live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato"><b>comfortably</b></a> in any direction or region he might dwell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato">AN 10.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a senior monk should be of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato"><b>long standing</b></a> and long-ordained to be considered one who dwells comfortably wherever he goes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato">AN 10.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a senior monk must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and restrained by the Patimokkha to maintain a pleasant life while wandering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato">AN 10.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a senior monk who dwells comfortably should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a> and have memorized teachings that are complete and pure in their meaning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato">AN 10.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a senior monk should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato"><b>skilled in settling issues</b></a>, understanding how legal questions arise and how they are effectively resolved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato">AN 10.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a senior monk as one who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato"><b>lover of the Dhamma</b></a> and who experiences intense joy in the higher teachings and discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato">AN 10.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a senior monk should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato"><b>content</b></a> with any kind of robes, almsfood, and other basic necessities to live comfortably in any direction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato">AN 10.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised a senior monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato"><b>graceful</b></a> and composed in his movements, especially when entering and sitting in inhabited areas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato">AN 10.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a senior monk who dwells comfortably is an attainer of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a>, which provide a pleasant dwelling in the present life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato">AN 10.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the highest quality of a senior monk as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>, having realized the liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.98/en/sujato">AN 10.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Venerable Upāli that one primary cause of disputes and quarrels within the community is when monks mistakenly explain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato"><b>non-Dhamma as Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato">AN 10.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a major reason why monks may not live in comfort is because some present the actual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma as non-Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato">AN 10.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the misrepresentation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato"><b>non-Vinaya as Vinaya</b></a> as a key condition for the arising of contention and strife in the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato">AN 10.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when practitioners declare what is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato"><b>Vinaya as non-Vinaya</b></a>, it leads to social friction and a lack of peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato">AN 10.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a source of conflict arises when people claim that what was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato"><b>not spoken by the Tathāgata</b></a> was actually spoken or uttered by him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato">AN 10.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that disputes arise when monks assert that what was truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato"><b>spoken by the Tathāgata</b></a> was never actually said or spoken by him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato">AN 10.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that social disharmony in the monastic order can be caused by falsely presenting what was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato"><b>not practiced by the Tathāgata</b></a> as something he did practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato">AN 10.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that mischaracterizing what was actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato"><b>practiced by the Tathāgata</b></a> as something he did not practice is a reason monks live in discomfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato">AN 10.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that declaring what was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato"><b>not prescribed by the Tathāgata</b></a> as being prescribed is a condition for quarrels and division? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato">AN 10.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a cause for the arising of disputes is the act of presenting what was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato"><b>prescribed by the Tathāgata</b></a> as something that was never prescribed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.41/en/sujato">AN 10.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of the "near shore" to describe <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.118/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a>, while describing "right view" as the "far shore"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.118/en/sujato">AN 10.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.118/en/sujato"><b>wrong liberation</b></a> as the "near shore" and "right liberation" as the "far shore" that a practitioner should strive to reach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.118/en/sujato">AN 10.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that among human beings, only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.118/en/sujato"><b>few go to the far shore</b></a>, while the rest of the population simply runs up and down the near shore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.118/en/sujato">AN 10.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged practitioners to abandon "dark states" and instead <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.118/en/sujato"><b>develop bright states</b></a> by moving from home to homelessness and seeking delight in solitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.118/en/sujato">AN 10.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described those who have fully developed the factors of enlightenment and have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.118/en/sujato"><b>destroyed the taints</b></a> as being fully extinguished in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.118/en/sujato">AN 10.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is faithless, immoral, and lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.219/en/sujato"><b>moral shame</b></a> and moral dread is "deposited in hell" as if brought there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.219/en/sujato">AN 4.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that being endowed with faith, virtue, moral shame, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.219/en/sujato"><b>moral dread</b></a> leads a person to be "deposited in heaven" as if brought there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.219/en/sujato">AN 4.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.219/en/sujato"><b>immorality</b></a> and the lack of faith as two of the four qualities that lead a person to an unfortunate rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.219/en/sujato">AN 4.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed there are four types of kamma, including a specific kind that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.232/en/sujato"><b>neither-dark-nor-bright</b></a> with neither-dark-nor-bright results which leads to the destruction of kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.232/en/sujato">AN 4.232</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that some actions are considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.232/en/sujato"><b>dark kamma with dark results</b></a>, while others are bright with bright results, both of which he realized through his own direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.232/en/sujato">AN 4.232</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained there are two kinds of gifts—a material gift and a gift of the Dhamma—and that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.141/en/sujato"><b>gift of the Dhamma</b></a> is the foremost of the two? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.141/en/sujato">AN 2.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two types of sacrifices, a material sacrifice and a sacrifice of the Dhamma, and declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.142/en/sujato"><b>sacrifice of the Dhamma</b></a> is the supreme choice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.142/en/sujato">AN 2.142</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between material generosity and Dhamma generosity, asserting that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.143/en/sujato"><b>generosity in the Dhamma</b></a> is the most excellent form of giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.143/en/sujato">AN 2.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are two kinds of relinquishment—relinquishing material items and relinquishing through the Dhamma—and that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.144/en/sujato"><b>relinquishment of the Dhamma</b></a> is the foremost of these? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.144/en/sujato">AN 2.144</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke about two kinds of wealth, material wealth and the wealth of the Dhamma, and emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.145/en/sujato"><b>wealth of the Dhamma</b></a> is the most significant form of possession? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.145/en/sujato">AN 2.145</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized mutual enjoyment into material enjoyment and enjoyment of the Dhamma, stating that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.146/en/sujato"><b>partaking in the Dhamma</b></a> is the superior way to share experiences with others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.146/en/sujato">AN 2.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that although there is both material distribution and distribution of the Dhamma, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.147/en/sujato"><b>distribution of the Dhamma</b></a> is the foremost of the two? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.147/en/sujato">AN 2.147</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described two ways to show favor to others—either through material goods or through the Dhamma—and noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.148/en/sujato"><b>favor through the Dhamma</b></a> is the more beneficial path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.148/en/sujato">AN 2.148</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that of the two types of assistance—material and spiritual—the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.149/en/sujato"><b>assistance of the Dhamma</b></a> is the most helpful for another person's growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.149/en/sujato">AN 2.149</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between two kinds of compassion, namely material compassion and compassion through the Dhamma, and proclaimed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.150/en/sujato"><b>compassion through the Dhamma</b></a> to be the highest form of sympathy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.150/en/sujato">AN 2.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while material gifts have their place in the world, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.141/en/sujato"><b>supreme gift</b></a> is always the gift of the Dhamma because of its lasting spiritual impact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.141/en/sujato">AN 2.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his followers to recognize that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.142/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma sacrifice</b></a> is much more valuable than any material sacrifice or offering one might provide? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.142/en/sujato">AN 2.142</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the most profound act of letting go as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.144/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma relinquishment</b></a>, placing it above the simple giving up of material possessions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.144/en/sujato">AN 2.144</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that one who possesses the teachings holds a type of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.145/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma wealth</b></a> that far outweighs the value of any material riches or worldly prosperity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.145/en/sujato">AN 2.145</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that sharing a meal or material goods is considered inferior to the act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.146/en/sujato"><b>sharing the Dhamma</b></a> with another person for their well-being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.146/en/sujato">AN 2.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that providing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.149/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma assistance</b></a> to someone is the most effective way to offer support and help them on their path toward liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.149/en/sujato">AN 2.149</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that showing compassion through the instruction of the teachings, known as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.150/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma compassion</b></a>, is the foremost way to truly empathize with the suffering of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.150/en/sujato">AN 2.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>wheel-turning monarch</b></a>, despite being a supreme righteous ruler, does not roll the wheel of sovereignty without acknowledging a "king" above himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked who the king of a righteous wheel-turning monarch is, the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a> (righteousness or truth) as that monarch's own sovereign? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a righteous king as one who relies upon the Dhamma, honors it, and makes the Dhamma his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>standard and banner</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a wheel-turning monarch provides <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>righteous protection</b></a> and guardianship for his own household and those within his private circle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a righteous king ensures that defense and protection are provided for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>khattiyas</b></a> (the noble warrior class) and his loyal followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a righteous wheel-turning monarch arranges for the protection and warding of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>army</b></a> to maintain order and peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted how a righteous ruler provides safety and guardianship for both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>brahmins and householders</b></a> within his realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wheel-turning monarch extends righteous protection to all people regardless of location, including those in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>town and country</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted a righteous king provides protection and defense for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>ascetics and brahmins</b></a> who lead a spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a truly righteous wheel-turning monarch even provides protection and warding for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>beasts and birds</b></a> within his kingdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the righteous wheel-turning monarch to himself, stating that the Tathāgata is also a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>righteous king</b></a> of Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the Tathāgata, as a king of Dhamma, relies solely on the Dhamma and holds it as his supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>sovereign</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how he provides righteous protection by identifying which types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>bodily action</b></a> should be cultivated and which should be avoided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha provides guardianship for his followers by clearly distinguishing between <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>verbal actions</b></a> that are wholesome and those that are unwholesome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, as a righteous king of Dhamma, provides protection by teaching which forms of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>mental action</b></a> are to be pursued and which are to be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by providing protection for actions of body, speech, and mind, he sets in motion the supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>Wheel of Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the Wheel of Dhamma he set in motion is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>irresistible</b></a> and cannot be turned back by any ascetic or brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that neither a deva, Māra, nor <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>Brahmā</b></a> has the power to turn back the Wheel of Dhamma once it has been set in motion righteously? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's role as a teacher is likened to a king because he establishes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>guardianship</b></a> over the moral and spiritual conduct of his followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that just as a worldly monarch rules by Dhamma, the Tathāgata <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato"><b>honors and respects</b></a> the Dhamma as his own ultimate authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.14/en/sujato">AN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who is frequently angry and whose anger persists for a long time to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.132/en/sujato"><b>inscription in rock</b></a>, which is not easily erased by wind or water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.132/en/sujato">AN 3.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who gets angry often but whose anger does not last long as being like an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.132/en/sujato"><b>inscription in the soil</b></a>, which is quickly wiped away by the elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.132/en/sujato">AN 3.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who remains friendly and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.132/en/sujato"><b>harmonious</b></a>, even when spoken to harshly or disagreeably, as being like an inscription in water that disappears immediately? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.132/en/sujato">AN 3.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are three types of persons found in the world: one like an inscription in rock, one like an inscription in the ground, and one like an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.132/en/sujato"><b>inscription in water</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.132/en/sujato">AN 3.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.132/en/sujato"><b>line drawn in the earth</b></a> to illustrate a person whose anger, though frequent, does not linger for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.132/en/sujato">AN 3.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed five specific types of people who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.137/en/sujato"><b>sleep little</b></a> at night and spend a great deal of time awake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.137/en/sujato">AN 5.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a woman who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.137/en/sujato"><b>longing for a man</b></a> is among those who will sleep very little and remain awake for much of the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.137/en/sujato">AN 5.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that a man who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.137/en/sujato"><b>longing for a woman</b></a> is one of the five types of people who experience great wakefulness during the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.137/en/sujato">AN 5.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a thief who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.137/en/sujato"><b>intent on stealing</b></a> will typically sleep very little at night because of their objective? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.137/en/sujato">AN 5.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a king who is deeply involved in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.137/en/sujato"><b>royal duties</b></a> as a person who stays awake for much of the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.137/en/sujato">AN 5.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk (bhikkhu) who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.137/en/sujato"><b>intent on disjunction</b></a> from suffering and fetters will sleep very little and remain largely awake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.137/en/sujato">AN 5.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who lacks sensory restraint to a royal elephant that is merely a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato"><b>consumer of food</b></a> and only serves to occupy space? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato">AN 5.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an elephant is considered just a "consumer of food" if it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato"><b>unable to endure</b></a> sights, sounds, smells, tastes, and touches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato">AN 5.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato"><b>consumer of food</b></a> to describe a monk who is unable to endure visual forms (sights)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato">AN 5.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato"><b>unable to endure sounds</b></a> as one who is merely known by the name "bhikkhu" but is actually just a consumer of food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato">AN 5.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk who cannot handle various <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato"><b>smells</b></a> is like a royal elephant that eats the king's food without performing its proper function? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato">AN 5.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato"><b>unable to endure tastes</b></a> is simply a "consumer of food" who occupies a place in the community without true merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato">AN 5.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato"><b>unable to endure tactile sensations</b></a> as being no different from a royal elephant that just eats and drops dung? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato">AN 5.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who fails to endure the five types of sensory objects is known as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato"><b>trampler of beds and chairs</b></a> while merely consuming the community's food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato">AN 5.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who cannot restrain his senses is merely a "consumer of food" who takes up a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato"><b>food-allocation ticket</b></a> without truly fulfilling the duties of a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.138/en/sujato">AN 5.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who insults and reviles his fellow practitioners in the holy life may face the danger of committing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.211/en/sujato"><b>defeat</b></a> (a Pārājika offense) or another grave, defiling offense? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.211/en/sujato">AN 5.211</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the five specific dangers for a monk who reviles his companions is that he may be afflicted by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.211/en/sujato"><b>severe illness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.211/en/sujato">AN 5.211</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a monk who is a reviler of his fellow practitioners risks meeting his end while he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.211/en/sujato"><b>confused</b></a> and muddled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.211/en/sujato">AN 5.211</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who reviles noble fellow practitioners is likely, after the breakup of the body and death, to be reborn in a state of misery or even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.211/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.211/en/sujato">AN 5.211</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is a maker of quarrels and disputes in the Sangha faces the danger that he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.212/en/sujato"><b>does not attain</b></a> the spiritual states he has not yet reached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.212/en/sujato">AN 5.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who habitually causes contention and legal issues within the community will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.212/en/sujato"><b>fall away</b></a> from the spiritual attainments he has already achieved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.212/en/sujato">AN 5.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.212/en/sujato"><b>bad reputation</b></a> spreads about a monk who is known for causing quarrels, disputes, and litigation within the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.212/en/sujato">AN 5.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified dying in a state of confusion as one of the major dangers for a monk who persists in being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.212/en/sujato"><b>maker of disputes</b></a> among his fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.212/en/sujato">AN 5.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk who causes quarrels and disputes in the community is destined for a bad destination and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.212/en/sujato"><b>rebirth in hell</b></a> after he passes away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.212/en/sujato">AN 5.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk possessing psychic power and mastery of the mind could resolve a large log of wood into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato"><b>earth element</b></a> because that element exists within the log? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato">AN 6.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while pointing to a massive log on Vulture’s Peak, the Venerable Sāriputta explained that a monk with psychic power could resolve it into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato"><b>water element</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato">AN 6.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Sāriputta, a monk who has attained mastery over his mind could resolve a simple log of wood into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato"><b>fire element</b></a> by focusing on the heat element present within it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato">AN 6.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta stated that a monk endowed with psychic powers could perceive and resolve a physical object like a log into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato"><b>air element</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato">AN 6.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta explained that because the element of beauty exists even in a log, a monk with psychic power could resolve that log into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato"><b>the beautiful</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato">AN 6.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk with mastery of the mind could resolve a large log into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato"><b>the ugly</b></a> (the unattractive) because the unattractive element is present within it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato">AN 6.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta used the sight of a large log to demonstrate how a monk who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato"><b>master of the mind</b></a> can use psychic powers to transform their perception of physical matter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.41/en/sujato">AN 6.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has gone forth should frequently reflect on the fact that they have entered upon a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato"><b>classless condition</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato">AN 10.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that monks and nuns should reflect often on how their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato"><b>livelihood is dependent</b></a> on the support of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato">AN 10.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised those in the monastic life to regularly contemplate that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato"><b>different manner</b></a> of behavior and deportment is required of them compared to laypeople? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato">AN 10.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to ask themselves if their own conscience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato"><b>reproaches them</b></a> regarding their personal virtue and moral conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato">AN 10.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that monastics should consider whether their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato"><b>wise companions</b></a> in the holy life might find fault with their virtue after observing them closely? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato">AN 10.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who have gone forth should frequently reflect on the inevitable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato"><b>separation</b></a> from all that is dear and appealing to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato">AN 10.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that one should often reflect on being the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato"><b>owner of kamma</b></a> and that they will be the heir to whatever actions they perform, whether good or bad? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato">AN 10.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should reflect on how their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato"><b>nights and days</b></a> are passing to ensure their time is spent meaningfully in the practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato">AN 10.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised monastics to regularly check whether they truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato"><b>delight in empty huts</b></a> and secluded places for meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato">AN 10.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should reflect on whether they have attained a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato"><b>superhuman state</b></a> or noble knowledge to avoid being embarrassed when questioned by fellow practitioners in their final hours? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.48/en/sujato">AN 10.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when two 120-year-old brahmins visited the Buddha, he confirmed their regret that they had reached the end of their lives without having done what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato"><b>beautiful and good</b></a> to provide protection from fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato">AN 3.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the world as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato"><b>on fire</b></a> with the inevitable fires of old age, sickness, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato">AN 3.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that restraint in body, speech, and mind serves as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato"><b>shelter and refuge</b></a> for a person even after they have passed away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato">AN 3.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato"><b>burning house</b></a> to explain that only the items salvaged from the flames are of any lasting use to the owner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato">AN 3.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged people to "salvage" their lives from the world through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato"><b>giving</b></a>, as what is given is considered well-salvaged and leads to future benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato">AN 3.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato"><b>merit</b></a> a person makes while they are still alive is what leads to their happiness in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato">AN 3.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato"><b>restraint in body</b></a>, speech, and mind is what provides an island and a resort for a person in a world scorched by mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.52/en/sujato">AN 3.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are two specific skills required for meditative success, namely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.163/en/sujato"><b>skill in attainment</b></a> and skill in emergence from attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.163/en/sujato">AN 2.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.164/en/sujato"><b>rectitude</b></a> (uprightness) and gentleness as two fundamental qualities that a practitioner should cultivate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.164/en/sujato">AN 2.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the two qualities of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.165/en/sujato"><b>patience</b></a> and mildness are essential for a peaceful and harmonious life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.165/en/sujato">AN 2.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.166/en/sujato"><b>amity</b></a> (kindly speech) and hospitality as two key social virtues? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.166/en/sujato">AN 2.166</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha grouped <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.167/en/sujato"><b>non-harming</b></a> and purity together as two vital qualities for one's spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.167/en/sujato">AN 2.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.168/en/sujato"><b>unguarded sense doors</b></a> and practicing immoderation in eating are two behaviors that lead to spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.168/en/sujato">AN 2.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the practice of keeping <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.169/en/sujato"><b>guarded sense doors</b></a> combined with moderation in eating as two pillars of discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.169/en/sujato">AN 2.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of two types of mental strength, which are the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.170/en/sujato"><b>power of reflection</b></a> and the power of development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.170/en/sujato">AN 2.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.171/en/sujato"><b>power of mindfulness</b></a> and the power of concentration as two distinct but complementary forces in the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.171/en/sujato">AN 2.171</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the two essential meditative qualities of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.172/en/sujato"><b>serenity</b></a> (samatha) and insight (vipassanā) should be developed in tandem? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.172/en/sujato">AN 2.172</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.173/en/sujato"><b>failure in virtue</b></a> and failure in view as two major obstacles that prevent a person from reaching enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.173/en/sujato">AN 2.173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.174/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in virtue</b></a> and accomplishment in view are two of the most important achievements for a Buddhist follower? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.174/en/sujato">AN 2.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.175/en/sujato"><b>purification of virtue</b></a> and the purification of view are two necessary stages of cleansing the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.175/en/sujato">AN 2.175</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that both the purification of view and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.176/en/sujato"><b>striving in accordance with view</b></a> are required to make actual progress on the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.176/en/sujato">AN 2.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha surprisingly taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.177/en/sujato"><b>dissatisfaction with wholesome states</b></a> (never being content with one's current level of goodness) and indefatigability in striving are two keys to final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.177/en/sujato">AN 2.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.178/en/sujato"><b>forgetfulness</b></a> and a lack of clear comprehension are two negative states that hinder one's awareness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.178/en/sujato">AN 2.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.179/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> and clear comprehension are the two essential tools for maintaining constant presence of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.179/en/sujato">AN 2.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for the direct knowledge and full understanding of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.310/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a>, one must cultivate the two qualities of serenity and insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.310/en/sujato">AN 2.310</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended the development of serenity and insight specifically for the abandonment and destruction of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.320/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.320/en/sujato">AN 2.320</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the only way to achieve the total relinquishment and cessation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.330/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> is through the cultivation of serenity and insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.330/en/sujato">AN 2.330</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once recounted a past life where he served as a professional wheelwright for a king named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>Sacetana</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a wheel finished in only six days eventually toppled to the ground because of its inherent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>defects</b></a> in the wood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who has not abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>crookedness of body</b></a>, speech, and mind will inevitably fall away from the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a high-quality wheel that stood as if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>fixed to an axle</b></a> after its momentum was spent because it was crafted to be perfectly balanced and without flaws? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a master wheelwright knows the defects in timber, he as a Perfectly Enlightened One is skilled in identifying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>crookedness of speech</b></a> in his followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a story about King Sacetana, who commissioned a new <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>pair of wheels</b></a> for a war chariot in preparation for a battle occurring six months later? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified himself as a former craftsman who spent nearly six months working on a single wheel to ensure it was free of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>crookedness and flaws</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a wheel with defects in its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>rim, spokes, and hub</b></a> would fail to remain upright once its initial momentum ceased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his disciples to train themselves by vowing to abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>crookedness of mind</b></a>, which includes mental faults and internal flaws? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a practitioner who stands firm in the Dhamma and Discipline to a wheel that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>meticulously crafted</b></a> over many months to be perfect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha related how a wheelwright was able to finish a second wheel in just <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>six days</b></a>, but it lacked the durability and balance of the one that took six months? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered the teachings of the Sacetana Sutta while staying in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>Deer Park</b></a> at Isipatana near Varanasi? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that internal faults and flaws are the primary reasons a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>falls away</b></a> from the path of spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, in a past life as a wheelwright, explained to a king that while two wheels may look the same, there is a significant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>difference</b></a> in their quality based on how they were made? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha describes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>Perfectly Enlightened One</b></a> as someone who is an expert in identifying and helping others remove the "crookedness" of their character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>crookedness of wood</b></a> as a metaphor for the unwholesome tendencies that cause a person to stumble in their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a wheel with a rim, spokes, and hub that were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>without crookedness</b></a> as a model for how a person should cultivate their own conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even a monk or nun can be like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>defective wheel</b></a> if they do not work to abandon their personal faults and flaws? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the goal of a practitioner is to remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>established</b></a> in the Dhamma and Discipline, just as a well-made wheel remains standing after it stops rolling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed to his monks that he was the very <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>skilled wheelwright</b></a> featured in the ancient story he was telling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a wheel's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>momentum</b></a> to illustrate that even if someone appears to be progressing, their internal defects will eventually cause them to fail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that King Sacetana could not perceive any difference between two wheels until they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>set in motion</b></a> to test their quality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified "faults" and "flaws" as specific categories of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome conduct</b></a> that must be completely abandoned through training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a wheel made in haste had a hub that was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>crooked and flawed</b></a>, mirroring the state of an untrained mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considers a monk or nun to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato"><b>standing firm</b></a> in the Dhamma only when they have abandoned all crookedness of body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.15/en/sujato">AN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for those beings who engage in good conduct of body, speech, and mind during the early hours, it is truly a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.155/en/sujato"><b>good morning</b></a> regardless of external signs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.155/en/sujato">AN 3.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person experiences a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.155/en/sujato"><b>good mid-day</b></a> only when they practice righteous conduct through their body, speech, and mind during that period of the day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.155/en/sujato">AN 3.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who maintain good conduct of body, speech, and mind in the later hours of the day are the ones who truly possess a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.155/en/sujato"><b>good evening</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.155/en/sujato">AN 3.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the practice of the holy life among fellow practitioners as the source of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.155/en/sujato"><b>good omen</b></a>, a good blessing, and a truly auspicious moment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.155/en/sujato">AN 3.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person performs a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.155/en/sujato"><b>right action</b></a> (circumambulation) of body, speech, and mind, their aspirations and goals become auspiciously successful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.155/en/sujato">AN 3.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged followers to flourish in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.155/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's teaching</b></a>, attaining happiness and health along with all their relatives through the achievement of their wholesome goals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.155/en/sujato">AN 3.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a lay follower who lacks faith and is immoral is technically known as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.175/en/sujato"><b>outcaste lay follower</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.175/en/sujato">AN 5.175</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.175/en/sujato"><b>stained lay follower</b></a> as one who is superstitious and believes in omens rather than trusting in the results of their own kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.175/en/sujato">AN 5.175</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized a lay follower as "rejected" if they seek those <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.175/en/sujato"><b>worthy of gifts</b></a> outside of the Buddhist community and perform their primary service there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.175/en/sujato">AN 5.175</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.175/en/sujato"><b>jewel lay follower</b></a> as an individual who possesses firm faith, maintains high virtue, and is not superstitious? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.175/en/sujato">AN 5.175</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a lay follower who believes in the results of kamma rather than <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.175/en/sujato"><b>omens</b></a> is comparable to a beautiful white lotus among people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.175/en/sujato">AN 5.175</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the lay follower who does not look for those worthy of offerings outside the Dhamma but instead provides <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.175/en/sujato"><b>first service</b></a> to the internal community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.175/en/sujato">AN 5.175</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a fool is characterized as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.9/en/sujato"><b>broken and ruined</b></a> because they choose to engage in bad conduct of body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.9/en/sujato">AN 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an unwise and incompetent person remains blameworthy and generates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.9/en/sujato"><b>much demerit</b></a> through their physical, verbal, and mental misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.9/en/sujato">AN 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a wise and competent person as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.9/en/sujato"><b>unbroken and unruined</b></a> because they strictly maintain good conduct in body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.9/en/sujato">AN 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a wise person is blameless in the eyes of the observant and generates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.9/en/sujato"><b>much merit</b></a> by consistently practicing good conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.9/en/sujato">AN 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.9/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a> is one who preserves themselves in an unblemished and unhurt state through their commitment to ethical behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.9/en/sujato">AN 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.9/en/sujato"><b>misconduct</b></a> in body, speech, and mind is the specific set of three qualities that marks a person as a fool and a bad man? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.9/en/sujato">AN 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.22/en/sujato"><b>respect and deference</b></a> toward his fellow practitioners is incapable of fulfilling the training of proper conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.22/en/sujato">AN 5.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained it is impossible for a monk to fulfill the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.22/en/sujato"><b>training of a trainee</b></a> (sekha) without first successfully completing the duties of proper conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.22/en/sujato">AN 5.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner cannot fulfill the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.22/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of virtue</b></a> (sīlakkhandha) if they have not first fulfilled the training of a trainee? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.22/en/sujato">AN 5.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who fails to fulfill the aggregate of virtue will find it impossible to fulfill the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.22/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of concentration</b></a> (samādhikkhandha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.22/en/sujato">AN 5.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that without first fulfilling the aggregate of concentration, a monk cannot hope to fulfill the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.22/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of wisdom</b></a> (paññākkhandha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.22/en/sujato">AN 5.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk is respectful and deferential to his fellow monks, it is entirely possible for him to fulfill the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.22/en/sujato"><b>duties of proper conduct</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.22/en/sujato">AN 5.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato"><b>great sal trees</b></a> growing near the Himalayas to illustrate how a household grows in five ways when they depend on a person of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato">AN 5.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as trees grow in branches, leaves, and foliage, a family grows in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato"><b>spiritual faith</b></a> when they have a faithful person of good family to rely upon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato">AN 5.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that members of a household, including relatives and employees, grow in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> through their association with a faithful person of good family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato">AN 5.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a family grows in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato"><b>learning</b></a> (suta) when they live in dependence on a person who is established in faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato">AN 5.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a person of faith in a household encourages their family and friends to grow in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato"><b>generosity</b></a> (cāga)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato">AN 5.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a tree grows in heartwood, a family grows in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> (paññā) when they depend on a faithful person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato">AN 5.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a faithful person of virtue to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato"><b>rocky mountain</b></a> in a great forest, upon which all the surrounding trees depend for their growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato">AN 5.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said wise family members and subordinates will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato"><b>emulate the conduct</b></a> and generosity of a virtuous person after observing their good deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato">AN 5.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who practice the Dhamma and follow the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato"><b>path leading to a good destination</b></a> will eventually rejoice in the deva world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.40/en/sujato">AN 5.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should train his mind so that it becomes familiar with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato"><b>going forth</b></a>, which ensures that arisen bad unwholesome states do not obsess or overwhelm the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato">AN 10.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a practitioner should cultivate a mind familiar with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato"><b>perception of impermanence</b></a> as a core part of their mental training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato">AN 10.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to maintain a mind familiar with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-self</b></a> to prevent unwholesome states from taking root? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato">AN 10.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that those who have gone forth should train their minds to be familiar with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato"><b>perception of unattractiveness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato">AN 10.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that developing a mind familiar with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato"><b>perception of danger</b></a> in the world is a necessary discipline for a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato">AN 10.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk should train his mind to recognize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato"><b>even and the uneven</b></a> in the world so that he is not overwhelmed by unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato">AN 10.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed practitioners to train their minds to know the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato"><b>existence and non-existence</b></a> of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato">AN 10.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk must train his mind to be familiar with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato"><b>origin and passing away</b></a> of the world to ensure spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato">AN 10.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato"><b>perception of abandoning</b></a> as one of the key mental states a monk must cultivate in his training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato">AN 10.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind familiar with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato"><b>perception of dispassion</b></a> is essential for a monk to prevent unwholesome qualities from dwelling in the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato">AN 10.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to cultivate a mind familiar with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato"><b>perception of cessation</b></a> as part of their path toward liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato">AN 10.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that a monk who masters twelve specific mental perceptions can expect one of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato"><b>two fruits</b></a>: final knowledge in this life or non-returning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.59/en/sujato">AN 10.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the Sangha may carry out an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.89/en/sujato"><b>act of reconciliation</b></a> against a monk if he actively works to prevent householders from receiving gains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.89/en/sujato">AN 8.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.89/en/sujato"><b>scolds and abuses</b></a> householders may be subjected to a formal act of reconciliation by the monastic community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.89/en/sujato">AN 8.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the Sangha can perform an act of reconciliation against a monk who speaks in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.89/en/sujato"><b>dispraise of the Buddha</b></a>, the Dhamma, or the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.89/en/sujato">AN 8.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the failure to fulfill a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.89/en/sujato"><b>righteous promise</b></a> made to a householder as a reason for the Sangha to carry out an act of reconciliation against a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.89/en/sujato">AN 8.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that the Sangha should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.89/en/sujato"><b>rescind the act of reconciliation</b></a> if the monk stops harming householders and instead speaks in praise of the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.89/en/sujato">AN 8.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a monk who has been subjected to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.90/en/sujato"><b>verdict of further misconduct</b></a> is prohibited from ordaining others or giving dependence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.90/en/sujato">AN 8.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk undergoing a sentence for further misconduct must not accept an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.90/en/sujato"><b>appointment to exhort nuns</b></a>, even if he had been previously authorized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.90/en/sujato">AN 8.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha established that a monk who has received a verdict of further misconduct must not be placed in any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.90/en/sujato"><b>individual position</b></a> or accept any formal Sangha appointment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.90/en/sujato">AN 8.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna explained that according to the Buddha, true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato"><b>elderly status</b></a> is not determined by biological age, but by whether one has transcended the pursuit of sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato">AN 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna taught that even if a man is eighty, ninety, or a hundred years old, he is still considered a "child" or a "fool" if he continues to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato"><b>consume sensual pleasures</b></a> and remains obsessed with seeking them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato">AN 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna stated that a young person with black hair and the bloom of youth is considered a "wise elder" if they do <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato"><b>not dwell in sensual pleasures</b></a> and are not consumed by sensual thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato">AN 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a brahmin named Kandarāyana accused Venerable Mahākaccāna of being disrespectful to seniors, Mahākaccāna clarified the Buddha's distinction between <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato"><b>youthful status</b></a> and elderly status based on spiritual conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato">AN 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahākaccāna, a person who is eighty years old but is still burned by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato"><b>fever of sensual desire</b></a> does not truly count as an elder in the Buddhist tradition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato">AN 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Kandarāyana was so moved by Venerable Mahākaccāna’s teaching that he paid homage to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato"><b>feet of young monks</b></a>, recognizing that they were the ones who truly stood in "elderly status"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato">AN 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna resided in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato"><b>Gundāvana in Madhurā</b></a> when he delivered the discourse on how wisdom and restraint define a true senior monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato">AN 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna defined a person who has abandoned the search for sensual pleasures as "wise," noting that such a person reaches <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato"><b>seniority</b></a> regardless of their actual age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato">AN 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Kandarāyana declared himself a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a> of the Buddha after hearing Venerable Mahākaccāna explain that biological age does not equate to spiritual maturity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato">AN 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna highlighted that a person is considered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato"><b>fool</b></a> if they are consumed by sensual thoughts, even if they have reached a century of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.38/en/sujato">AN 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the influence of monks within the Sangha to a kingdom, explaining that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato"><b>bad monks</b></a> are powerful, amiable monks become weak and marginalized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato">AN 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that when "amiable monks" (pesalā bhikkhū) are weak, they often <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato"><b>stay silent</b></a> or move to bordering districts because of the dominance of undisciplined monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato">AN 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the rise of powerful bad monks leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato"><b>harm and suffering</b></a> of many people, including both devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato">AN 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy of a king and robbers, stating that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato"><b>kings are strong</b></a>, robbers are weak, allowing people to travel safely and conduct their business? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato">AN 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when amiable, well-behaved monks are strong, it results in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato"><b>bad monks becoming weak</b></a> and forced to withdraw from the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato">AN 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the strength and influence of amiable monks is for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato"><b>welfare and happiness</b></a> of the many, including the benefit of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato">AN 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that when robbers are powerful, it is difficult for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato"><b>kings to inspect</b></a> their remote districts or travel through the countryside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato">AN 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when lawlessness prevails, it is not easy for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato"><b>brahmins and householders</b></a> to go out or look after their external work? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato">AN 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when bad monks lose their power to disciplined, amiable monks, they often <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato"><b>slink away</b></a> or remain quiet in the midst of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato">AN 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the prosperity of the world is linked to the strength of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato"><b>amiable monks</b></a>, whose presence brings about the welfare of both the human and divine realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.39/en/sujato">AN 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses three specific qualities—guarding the sense doors, moderation in eating, and devotion to wakefulness—is practicing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>unmistakable way</b></a> to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that guarding the sense doors involves not grasping at the signs or features of a visual object so that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>evil unwholesome states</b></a> like greed and sorrow do not flow into the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that one should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>moderate in eating</b></a> by reflecting wisely that food is taken not for amusement or beauty, but simply for the support and maintenance of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>wakefulness</b></a> as purifying the mind of obstructive states through walking and sitting during the day and the first and last watches of the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when resting during the middle watch of the night, the Buddha advised lying down on the right side in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>lion's posture</b></a>, remaining mindful and clearly comprehending while keeping the perception of rising in mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is restrained in their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>mental faculty</b></a> prevents unwholesome states like covetousness and displeasure from overwhelming them when they cognize mental phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that practicing with the three qualities of sense restraint, moderation in eating, and wakefulness provides a firm basis for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should eat food only for the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>ending old feelings</b></a> of hunger and not creating new feelings of overindulgence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that guarding the sense doors requires one to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>not one who grasps at signs</b></a> or features when experiencing sights, sounds, smells, tastes, or touches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the proper purpose of nutrition as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>support of the holy life</b></a>, rather than for intoxication, decoration, or beautification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three things—bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct—as being conduct that leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.17/en/sujato"><b>self-affliction</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.17/en/sujato">AN 3.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct not only harm oneself but also lead to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.17/en/sujato"><b>affliction of others</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.17/en/sujato">AN 3.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.17/en/sujato"><b>bad conduct</b></a> through body, speech, and mind results in the affliction of both oneself and others simultaneously? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.17/en/sujato">AN 3.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.17/en/sujato"><b>good conduct</b></a> in body, speech, and mind is the way to avoid self-affliction and the affliction of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.17/en/sujato">AN 3.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that three qualities—good bodily conduct, good verbal conduct, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.17/en/sujato"><b>good mental conduct</b></a>—do not lead to the affliction of oneself or anyone else? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.17/en/sujato">AN 3.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should use the first watch of the night to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>purify the mind</b></a> of obstructive states while walking back and forth or sitting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should guard the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>tongue faculty</b></a> so that after tasting a flavor, unwholesome states like greed and sorrow do not flow in? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended keeping the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>perception of rising</b></a> in mind while resting in the middle watch of the night to remain devoted to wakefulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that when one guards the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>sense of touch</b></a>, one does not grasp at the signs or features of tactile objects, thereby protecting the faculty from unwholesome influences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato"><b>moderate in eating</b></a> as one who consumes food only so that he may live a blameless life and dwell in comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.16/en/sujato">AN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a Vajjiputtan monk told the Buddha he was unable to train in the more than one hundred and fifty rules that come up for recitation every fortnight, the Buddha suggested he focus instead on just <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato"><b>three trainings</b></a>: the training in higher virtue, the training in higher mind, and the training in higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato">AN 3.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that through persistent training in higher virtue, higher mind, and higher wisdom, a practitioner will eventually ensure that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato"><b>lust, hatred, and delusion</b></a> are abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato">AN 3.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying at the Gabled Hall in the Great Woods of Vesālī, he explained that a monk who has abandoned lust, hatred, and delusion will no longer engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome and evil</b></a> deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato">AN 3.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized individuals into four types who prioritize their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.43/en/sujato"><b>anger</b></a>, contempt, gain, or honor over the Good Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.43/en/sujato">AN 4.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a group of people who are defined by their commitment to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.43/en/sujato"><b>Good Dhamma</b></a>, placing it above personal anger, contempt, gain, and honor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.43/en/sujato">AN 4.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that monks who value anger and contempt above all else will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.43/en/sujato"><b>not flourish</b></a> in the Dhamma taught by the Perfectly Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.43/en/sujato">AN 4.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined prioritizing anger over the Good Dhamma as one of four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.44/en/sujato"><b>bad qualities</b></a> (asaddhammā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.44/en/sujato">AN 4.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified the act of valuing the Good Dhamma over gain and honor as one of the four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.44/en/sujato"><b>good qualities</b></a> (saddhammā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.44/en/sujato">AN 4.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a vivid metaphor, comparing a monk who values gain and honor over the Dhamma to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.44/en/sujato"><b>rotten seed</b></a> that cannot grow even when planted in a good field? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.44/en/sujato">AN 4.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared those who hold the Good Dhamma in high esteem to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.44/en/sujato"><b>medicinal herbs</b></a> that flourish when they are supported by moisture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.44/en/sujato">AN 4.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato"><b>Vajjiputtan monk</b></a> successfully abandoned lust, hatred, and delusion after he committed himself to the three simplified trainings of higher virtue, mind, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato">AN 3.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that individuals who value <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.43/en/sujato"><b>contempt</b></a> (makkha) more than the Good Dhamma are unable to grow in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.43/en/sujato">AN 4.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato"><b>training in higher mind</b></a> (adhicittasikkhā) is one of the three essential components for a monk wishing to simplify his path toward liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato">AN 3.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that those who value the Good Dhamma will truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.43/en/sujato"><b>flourish</b></a> in the teachings that have been well-expounded by the Perfectly Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.43/en/sujato">AN 4.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha designated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.44/en/sujato"><b>valuing honor</b></a> (sakkāragarutā) over the Good Dhamma as an "untrue" or "bad" quality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.44/en/sujato">AN 4.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a practitioner successfully trains in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato"><b>higher wisdom</b></a> (adhipaññāsikkhā) alongside virtue and mind, they will no longer do what is unwholesome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato">AN 3.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that the simple <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato"><b>abandonment of lust</b></a>, hatred, and delusion is the direct result of perfecting the three higher trainings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato">AN 3.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically mentions that one who values <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.43/en/sujato"><b>gain</b></a> (lābhagaru) instead of the Good Dhamma is one of the four types of persons who will fail to grow in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.43/en/sujato">AN 4.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.44/en/sujato"><b>valuing the Good Dhamma</b></a> are like plants that grow robustly when they have an "ancestry of moisture"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.44/en/sujato">AN 4.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha addressed the monks to explain that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato"><b>higher virtue</b></a> (adhisīlasikkhā) is the first of the three trainings that lead a monk to stop practicing what is evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.84/en/sujato">AN 3.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato"><b>good person</b></a> is born into a family, it is for the good, welfare, and happiness of many people, including their parents, spouse, and children? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato">AN 5.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a good person who is born into a family to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato"><b>great rain cloud</b></a> that provides for the growth of all crops, thereby benefiting many people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato">AN 5.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is established in the Dhamma and uses their wealth to benefit many is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato"><b>guarded by Dhamma</b></a> and protected by the deities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato">AN 5.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that fame never leaves a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato"><b>established in the Dhamma</b></a>, possesses virtuous behavior, and is a person of great learning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato">AN 5.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is firm in the Dhamma, perfect in virtue, and a speaker of truth as being like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato"><b>gold from the Jambu river</b></a>, whom no one is worthy of blaming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato">AN 5.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato"><b>devas and Brahma</b></a> praise a person who is established in the Dhamma, possesses a sense of shame, and is a speaker of truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato">AN 5.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a good person provides for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato"><b>welfare and happiness</b></a> of their bondservants, workers, and staff, in addition to their own family and friends? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato">AN 5.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that a good person born into a family acts for the benefit and happiness of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato"><b>ascetics and brahmins</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.42/en/sujato">AN 5.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a practitioner who enters the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato"><b>first jhāna</b></a> and contemplates the five aggregates as impermanent and non-self can be reborn among the devas of the Pure Abodes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato">AN 4.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should view the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato"><b>disease and a boil</b></a> while in a state of meditative absorption? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato">AN 4.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified rebirth among the devas of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato"><b>Pure Abodes</b></a> as an attainment that is unique to those who practice insight and is not shared with ordinary worldlings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato">AN 4.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the contemplation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> as a dart, a misery, and an affliction as a path to higher rebirth for those who have attained the meditative jhānas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato">AN 4.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner who attains the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato"><b>second jhāna</b></a> and views its mental factors as empty and non-self will be reborn in the Pure Abodes after the breaking up of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato">AN 4.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a practitioner in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato"><b>third jhāna</b></a> who sees the aggregates as alien and disintegrating attains a celestial rebirth that ordinary worldlings cannot reach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato">AN 4.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a> who contemplates the factors of that state as empty and non-self will, after death, pass away to the company of the devas of the Pure Abodes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato">AN 4.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato"><b>four types of persons</b></a> in the world who, through attaining the jhānas and practicing insight into the nature of the aggregates, secure rebirth in the Pure Abodes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.124/en/sujato">AN 4.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.79/en/sujato"><b>delighting in talk</b></a> is one of eight specific qualities that lead to the spiritual decline of a monk who is still a trainee? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.79/en/sujato">AN 8.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a trainee monk faces decline if they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.79/en/sujato"><b>unguarded sense doors</b></a>, whereas guarding the doors of the senses leads to their non-decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.79/en/sujato">AN 8.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.79/en/sujato"><b>immoderation in eating</b></a> as a primary factor that contributes to the falling away and decline of a monk who has not yet reached the final goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.79/en/sujato">AN 8.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.79/en/sujato"><b>mental proliferation</b></a>—the tendency to delight in conceptual elaboration—is one of the obstacles that leads to the decline of a trainee? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.79/en/sujato">AN 8.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.79/en/sujato"><b>delighting in sleep</b></a> is a quality that leads to the falling away of a monk who is still a trainee in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.79/en/sujato">AN 8.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.15/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind by loving-kindness</b></a> is well-practiced and developed, a practitioner can expect to sleep happily and wake up happily? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.15/en/sujato">AN 11.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who successfully cultivates loving-kindness will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.15/en/sujato"><b>not see evil dreams</b></a> while they sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.15/en/sujato">AN 11.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that a person who develops the liberation of mind by loving-kindness becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.15/en/sujato"><b>dear to humans</b></a> and non-human beings alike? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.15/en/sujato">AN 11.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that neither fire, nor poison, nor weapons can harm a person who has practiced and developed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.15/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind by loving-kindness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.15/en/sujato">AN 11.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that for one practicing loving-kindness, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.15/en/sujato"><b>mind is quickly concentrated</b></a> and their facial complexion becomes clear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.15/en/sujato">AN 11.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a practitioner of loving-kindness does not attain a higher realization in this life, they will be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.15/en/sujato"><b>reborn in the Brahma world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.15/en/sujato">AN 11.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who reviles or insults his fellow practitioners of the holy life may <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.88/en/sujato"><b>lose what they have attained</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.88/en/sujato">AN 10.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that insulting noble ones and fellow practitioners can lead a person to suffer from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.88/en/sujato"><b>serious illness</b></a> or ailments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.88/en/sujato">AN 10.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the ten dangers of reviling fellow monks is reaching <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.88/en/sujato"><b>madness or confusion of mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.88/en/sujato">AN 10.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who reviles and insults his companions in the holy life may face a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.88/en/sujato"><b>confused death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.88/en/sujato">AN 10.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that reviling fellow practitioners can lead to rebirth in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.88/en/sujato"><b>hell or an unhappy destination</b></a> after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.88/en/sujato">AN 10.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.120/en/sujato"><b>purification of body</b></a> as consisting of abstaining from killing living beings, taking what is not given, and sexual misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.120/en/sujato">AN 3.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.120/en/sujato"><b>purification of speech</b></a> involves abstaining from false speech, divisive speech, harsh speech, and idle chatter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.120/en/sujato">AN 3.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.120/en/sujato"><b>purification of mind</b></a> as being non-covetous, having a mind without ill will, and possessing right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.120/en/sujato">AN 3.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.79/en/sujato"><b>delighting in company</b></a> and social groups is a specific quality that leads to the spiritual decline of a trainee monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.79/en/sujato">AN 8.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that monks who rely on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.41/en/sujato"><b>misapprehended suttas</b></a> with incorrect phrasing and interpretation act for the harm and suffering of many people, including both devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.41/en/sujato">AN 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that when monks obstruct the true meaning and the Dhamma through poorly grasped teachings, they cause the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.41/en/sujato"><b>Good Dhamma to disappear</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.41/en/sujato">AN 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that monks who correctly grasp the teachings with proper phrasing and interpretation generate much <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.41/en/sujato"><b>merit</b></a> for themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.41/en/sujato">AN 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, monks who teach <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.41/en/sujato"><b>correctly apprehended suttas</b></a> are practicing for the welfare and happiness of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.41/en/sujato">AN 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the proper preservation of the phrasing and meaning of the teachings as the way monks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.41/en/sujato"><b>maintain the Good Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.41/en/sujato">AN 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between two types of groups, describing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.42/en/sujato"><b>shallow assembly</b></a> as one where the members are restless, puffed up, talkative, and lack mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.42/en/sujato">AN 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.42/en/sujato"><b>deep assembly</b></a> as a group of monks who are not restless or fickle, but are instead concentrated and possessed of clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.42/en/sujato">AN 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that monks in a shallow assembly often have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.42/en/sujato"><b>wandering minds</b></a> and uncontrolled senses, which leads to their lack of composure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.42/en/sujato">AN 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.42/en/sujato"><b>deep assembly</b></a>—composed of monks who are one-pointed in mind and have restrained senses—to be the foremost of the two types of assemblies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.42/en/sujato">AN 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized monks who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.42/en/sujato"><b>loose-tongued</b></a> and lacking in mental concentration as belonging to an "uttānā" or shallow assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.42/en/sujato">AN 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.43/en/sujato"><b>divided assembly</b></a> as one where the monks are argumentative, quarrelsome, and constantly engaged in disputes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.43/en/sujato">AN 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the vivid metaphor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.43/en/sujato"><b>weapons of the tongue</b></a> to describe how monks in a divided and quarrelsome assembly wound one another with their words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.43/en/sujato">AN 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.43/en/sujato"><b>harmonious assembly</b></a>, where monks live in concord, cordially, and without dispute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.43/en/sujato">AN 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described monks who live in total harmony as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.43/en/sujato"><b>blending like milk and water</b></a> while viewing each other with eyes of affection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.43/en/sujato">AN 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.43/en/sujato"><b>harmonious assembly</b></a> to be the foremost and best type among the different ways a group of monks can live together? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.43/en/sujato">AN 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked his monks if they would feel humiliated and repelled if wanderers of other sects suggested they lived the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato"><b>spiritual life</b></a> solely for the sake of rebirth in a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato">AN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a practitioner feels repelled by the prospect of practicing for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato"><b>divine life-span</b></a>, they should feel even more repelled and disgusted by the practice of bodily misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato">AN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one should maintain a sense of loathing toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato"><b>bodily misconduct</b></a> even more than they might feel regarding the temporary pleasures of divine beauty and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato">AN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that practitioners should feel a sense of humiliation regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato"><b>verbal misconduct</b></a>, just as they would if they were accused of practicing only for the sake of divine rewards? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato">AN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should feel repelled by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato"><b>mental misconduct</b></a> even more than they would be by the idea of practicing for divine fame or authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato">AN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato"><b>divine beauty</b></a>, happiness, and fame as things that his disciples should not find to be the primary motivation for their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato">AN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as one might be disgusted by being told they practice for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato"><b>divine happiness</b></a>, they should prioritize being disgusted by unwholesome actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato">AN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato"><b>divine authority</b></a> and sovereign power in the deva realms is not the goal for which a monk lives the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato">AN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a person should feel a sense of shame toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome conduct</b></a> involving the body, speech, and mind, rather than seeking status in a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.18/en/sujato">AN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato"><b>shopkeeper</b></a> who is incapable of acquiring wealth or increasing what he has because he fails to properly attend to his business in the morning, midday, and evening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato">AN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato"><b>properly apply</b></a> himself to the object of concentration during the three periods of the day is unable to attain unreached wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato">AN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk becomes incapable of making wholesome states flourish if he fails to consistently focus on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato"><b>object of concentration</b></a> in the morning, midday, and evening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato">AN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a successful tradesman to show that a monk can attain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a> that have not yet been reached by being diligent throughout the entire day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato">AN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three specific factors or "limbs" that make a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato"><b>tradesman</b></a> capable of acquiring wealth and causing it to prosper? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato">AN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk is "capable" of attaining unreached wholesome states if he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato"><b>properly attends</b></a> to the object of concentration in the morning, midday, and evening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato">AN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can increase and develop the wholesome states he has already acquired by consistently maintaining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato"><b>basis of concentration</b></a> throughout the day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato">AN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the acquisition of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato"><b>unacquired wealth</b></a> by a shopkeeper to the acquisition of unreached wholesome qualities by a dedicated practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato">AN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that spiritual success requires the same level of consistent effort as a merchant who manages his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato"><b>business affairs</b></a> diligently from morning until evening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.19/en/sujato">AN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the arising of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.94/en/sujato"><b>stainless Dhamma-eye</b></a> in a noble disciple to the autumn sun rising into the sky and dispelling all darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.94/en/sujato">AN 3.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.94/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma-eye</b></a> arises, three fetters—personal identity view, doubt, and the wrong grasp of rules and observances—are immediately abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.94/en/sujato">AN 3.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, after a noble disciple abandons the first three fetters, they further move beyond the qualities of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.94/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire and ill will</b></a> to enter the first jhana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.94/en/sujato">AN 3.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a noble disciple passes away while in a state where they have abandoned certain fetters and entered the first jhana, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.94/en/sujato"><b>no fetter</b></a> remaining that would bring them back to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.94/en/sujato">AN 3.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.189/en/sujato"><b>eight liberations</b></a> as specific qualities that are to be realized by the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.189/en/sujato">AN 4.189</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the recollection of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.189/en/sujato"><b>past lives</b></a> (pubbenivāso) is a quality that must be realized through the faculty of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.189/en/sujato">AN 4.189</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the passing away and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.189/en/sujato"><b>rebirth of beings</b></a> are qualities to be realized through the eye (the divine eye)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.189/en/sujato">AN 4.189</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.189/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavānaṃ khayo) is a specific realization that is achieved through wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.189/en/sujato">AN 4.189</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified things to be realized into four distinct categories: those to be realized by the body, by mindfulness, by the eye, and by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.189/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.189/en/sujato">AN 4.189</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.115/en/sujato"><b>easily measured</b></a> person as one who is restless, puffed up, fickle, talkative, and of muddled mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.115/en/sujato">AN 3.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.115/en/sujato"><b>difficult to measure</b></a> person as one who is not restless, not puffed up, and possesses a concentrated, one-pointed mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.115/en/sujato">AN 3.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to an Arahant, one whose taints are destroyed, as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.115/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable person</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.115/en/sujato">AN 3.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three types of persons existing in the world: the easily measured, the difficult to measure, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.115/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.115/en/sujato">AN 3.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized an easily measured person by traits such as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.115/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained faculties</b></a>, being unconcentrated, and possessing a wandering mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.115/en/sujato">AN 3.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasts the easily measured person with the difficult to measure person, who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.115/en/sujato"><b>not talkative</b></a>, has established mindfulness, and is clearly comprehending? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.115/en/sujato">AN 3.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.94/en/sujato"><b>first jhana</b></a> is a state reached by being secluded from sensual pleasures and unwholesome states, characterized by rapture and pleasure born of seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.94/en/sujato">AN 3.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the arising of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.94/en/sujato"><b>dustless Dhamma-eye</b></a> occurs when a noble disciple abandons the fetter of doubt (vicikicchā) along with personal identity view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.94/en/sujato">AN 3.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when King Pasenadi of Kosala was overcome with grief after the death of Queen Mallika, the Buddha taught him that there are five things that cannot be obtained by any recluse, brahmin, deva, Mara, or Brahma, including the wish that what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.49/en/sujato"><b>subject to aging</b></a> should not grow old? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.49/en/sujato">AN 5.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person who is suffering and grieving over the death of a loved one, it is better to understand that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.49/en/sujato"><b>sorrowing and lamenting</b></a> does not recover what is lost and only causes one's own body to become lean and pale? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.49/en/sujato">AN 5.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> as the forerunner in the entry into unwholesome states, which is immediately followed by a lack of shame and a lack of moral dread? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato">AN 10.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, for one who is immersed in ignorance, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> arises, which then serves as the foundation for the development of wrong intention and all other unwholesome factors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato">AN 10.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato"><b>wrong action</b></a> arises as a direct result of wrong speech within the sequence of factors that lead to unwholesome outcomes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato">AN 10.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato"><b>wrong livelihood</b></a> naturally follows from wrong action, eventually leading to wrong effort and wrong mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato">AN 10.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the final stages of the unwholesome path involve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato"><b>wrong concentration</b></a>, which leads an individual toward wrong knowledge and wrong liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato">AN 10.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge</b></a> is the forerunner in the attainment of wholesome states, and it is followed by the development of a sense of shame and moral dread? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato">AN 10.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one who possesses true knowledge, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> arises, which then leads to the perfection of right intention and right speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato">AN 10.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> is a necessary precursor to the development of right knowledge and ultimately right liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.105/en/sujato">AN 10.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described one form of giving as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.31/en/sujato"><b>giving on impulse</b></a>, where a person offers a gift spontaneously or perhaps out of a sense of being bothered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.31/en/sujato">AN 8.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.31/en/sujato"><b>giving from fear</b></a> as one of the eight ways that a person might choose to give a gift? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.31/en/sujato">AN 8.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that some people give a gift because they think, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.31/en/sujato"><b>Giving is good</b></a>," acknowledging the inherent value of the act of generosity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.31/en/sujato">AN 8.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a person might give because they believe that by doing so, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.31/en/sujato"><b>good reputation</b></a> will be spread about them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.31/en/sujato">AN 8.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted a very high motivation for giving as being for the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.31/en/sujato"><b>adorning the mind</b></a> and as an equipment for the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.31/en/sujato">AN 8.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that giving can sometimes be motivated by negative emotions, such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.33/en/sujato"><b>giving out of hatred</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.33/en/sujato">AN 8.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person might give a gift because they feel bound by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.33/en/sujato"><b>family tradition</b></a>, believing they should not let the ancient lineage of giving decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.33/en/sujato">AN 8.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that some people practice generosity with the hope that after the breakup of the body they will be reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.33/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.33/en/sujato">AN 8.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that a person might give a gift because when they do so, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.33/en/sujato"><b>mind becomes clear</b></a> and joy and happiness arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.33/en/sujato">AN 8.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.33/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> as one of the specific grounds or motives from which a person might choose to give a gift? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.33/en/sujato">AN 8.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are nine specific perceptions which, when developed and cultivated, lead to great fruit and benefit, eventually culminating in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.16/en/sujato"><b>Deathless</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.16/en/sujato">AN 9.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.16/en/sujato"><b>perception of unattractiveness</b></a> and the perception of death as two of the nine essential mental qualities that lead to spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.16/en/sujato">AN 9.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended cultivating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.16/en/sujato"><b>perception of repulsiveness in food</b></a> as a way to achieve a state that merges with and ends in the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.16/en/sujato">AN 9.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.16/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-delight</b></a> in the entire world as a fundamental practice for reaching the final goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.16/en/sujato">AN 9.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should develop the perception of impermanence and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.16/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-self</b></a> in what is suffering to attain great spiritual results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.16/en/sujato">AN 9.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a monk should not visit or sit down with a family that does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.17/en/sujato"><b>rise politely</b></a> to welcome him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.17/en/sujato">AN 9.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a family is not fit to be visited if they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.17/en/sujato"><b>conceal possessions</b></a> that they actually have when a monk arrives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.17/en/sujato">AN 9.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a family that gives only a little when they have much, or gives <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.17/en/sujato"><b>coarse food</b></a> even when they possess choice food, is not a family a monk should frequent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.17/en/sujato">AN 9.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk should visit and sit with a family that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.17/en/sujato"><b>gives with respect</b></a> rather than without respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.17/en/sujato">AN 9.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the willingness of a family to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.17/en/sujato"><b>sit down to hear the Dhamma</b></a> as one of the nine reasons why a monk is justified in visiting them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.17/en/sujato">AN 9.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato"><b>five types of wealth</b></a>, which include faith, virtuous behavior, learning, generosity, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato">AN 5.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato"><b>wealth of faith</b></a> as a noble disciple placing their trust in the Enlightenment of the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato">AN 5.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato"><b>wealth of virtuous behavior</b></a> as the practice of abstaining from killing, stealing, sexual misconduct, false speech, and intoxicants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato">AN 5.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato"><b>wealth of learning</b></a> consists of being well-versed in teachings that are good in the beginning, middle, and end, and that lead to the complete holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato">AN 5.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person possesses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato"><b>wealth of generosity</b></a> when they dwell at home with a mind free from the stain of stinginess and delight in giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato">AN 5.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato"><b>wealth of wisdom</b></a> involves possessing the noble, penetrative understanding of the arising and passing away of phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato">AN 5.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person who has firm faith in the Tathāgata and possesses noble virtue is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato"><b>not poor</b></a> and their life is not in vain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato">AN 5.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the wise should pursue faith, virtue, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato"><b>confidence in the Sangha</b></a> while remembering the teachings of the Buddhas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.46/en/sujato">AN 5.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.16/en/sujato"><b>perception of abandonment</b></a> and the perception of dispassion as essential practices that lead to a great spiritual result? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.16/en/sujato">AN 9.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a family is worthy of being visited if they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.17/en/sujato"><b>listen to what is said</b></a> when a monk speaks the Dhamma to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.17/en/sujato">AN 9.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato"><b>shallow assembly</b></a> as one where the elder monks are indulgent and lax, leading the way in backsliding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato">AN 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato"><b>deep assembly</b></a> as a group in which the elder monks are not indulgent or lax, but instead are leaders in seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato">AN 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that in a shallow assembly, the later generation of monks will follow the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato"><b>example of elders</b></a> who have set aside the task of seclusion and neglect spiritual effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato">AN 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a deep assembly is distinguished by monks who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato"><b>arouse energy</b></a> for the sake of attaining what has not yet been attained and realizing what has not yet been realized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato">AN 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato"><b>deep assembly</b></a> as the foremost among the two types of assemblies he discussed regarding spiritual depth and diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato">AN 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when monks in an assembly are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato"><b>leaders in backsliding</b></a>, they fail to strive for the realization of the Dhamma, thus making that assembly "shallow"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato">AN 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that in a deep assembly, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato"><b>later generation</b></a> of monks follows the positive example of their elders by prioritizing seclusion and effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.44/en/sujato">AN 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.45/en/sujato"><b>ignoble assembly</b></a> as one where the monks do not understand suffering as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.45/en/sujato">AN 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.45/en/sujato"><b>noble assembly</b></a> as a group where the monks truly understand the origin of suffering as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.45/en/sujato">AN 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an ignoble assembly is one in which members lack the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.45/en/sujato"><b>knowledge of cessation</b></a>, failing to understand the cessation of suffering as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.45/en/sujato">AN 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble assembly is superior because its monks understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.45/en/sujato"><b>way leading to cessation</b></a> of suffering as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.45/en/sujato">AN 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.45/en/sujato"><b>noble assembly</b></a> is the foremost type of assembly when compared to an ignoble assembly that lacks understanding of the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.45/en/sujato">AN 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha designated an assembly as ignoble if the monks within it do not possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.45/en/sujato"><b>correct understanding</b></a> regarding the origin and nature of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.45/en/sujato">AN 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato"><b>dregs of an assembly</b></a> to describe a group where monks follow the wrong path of desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato">AN 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato"><b>cream of an assembly</b></a> as a group where monks do not follow the wrong path of hatred or anger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato">AN 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that monks who act out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> constitute what he called the "dregs" of an assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato">AN 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a group that does not follow the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato"><b>wrong path of fear</b></a> is considered to be the "cream" of an assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato">AN 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an assembly where monks act out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> or fear is merely the "dregs" compared to a more virtuous group? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato">AN 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato"><b>cream of an assembly</b></a> as the foremost type of assembly because its members avoid the four wrong paths of desire, hatred, delusion, and fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato">AN 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that any assembly where members move by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato"><b>desire or favoritism</b></a> is categorized as the "dregs" of an assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.46/en/sujato">AN 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the spiritual development of a monk to the success of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>shrewd shopkeeper</b></a> who, by possessing three specific qualities, quickly attains greatness and wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a shopkeeper is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>shrewd</b></a> when they know the purchase price and sale price of their merchandise, as well as the exact profit they will make? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a shopkeeper as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>capable</b></a> if they are skilled in the actual practice of buying and selling goods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a shopkeeper possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>support</b></a> (or "good credit") when wealthy householders trust them enough to provide the capital necessary for their business and family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can reach greatness in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a> before long if he possesses three spiritual qualities equivalent to those of a successful businessman? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defines a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>shrewd monk</b></a> as one who understands the Four Noble Truths—suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path—as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identifies the monk's spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>capability</b></a> as living with aroused energy to abandon unwholesome states and to acquire wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha describes a monk as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>attained support</b></a> when he frequently approaches learned elders to ask questions and clarify the meaning of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is considered firm in his effort and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>sturdy in his endeavor</b></a> when he does not shirk the responsibility of cultivating wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a monk to be well-supported, he should seek out those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a> and have memorized the Dhamma, the Vinaya, and the Matika? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that senior monks provide spiritual support by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>dispelling doubts</b></a> on various points of doctrine for those who come to them with questions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>cakkhumā</b></a> (shrewd or sharp-sighted) to describe both a merchant who knows his profit margins and a monk who clearly perceives the reality of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a shopkeeper who is shrewd, capable, and well-supported will quickly attain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>vastness in wealth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is shrewd and energetic can clarify <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>obscure points</b></a> of the teaching by interrogating learned elders about their specific meanings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considers the knowledge of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> to be a primary requirement for a monk to be spiritually "shrewd"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that wealthy householders will entrust their funds to a shopkeeper they perceive as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>able to support</b></a> his family while providing them with periodic returns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described senior monks as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>masters of the disciplines</b></a> who reveal what was previously unrevealed to their students? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that the same three factors leading to material success also lead to a monk's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato"><b>spiritual greatness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.20/en/sujato">AN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that he and his monks wandered through the long round of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato"><b>rebirths</b></a> for a great length of time because they had not understood and penetrated four specific noble qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato">AN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato"><b>noble virtue</b></a> as the first of four essential qualities that must be fully realized to end the cycle of wandering in samsara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato">AN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, failing to understand and penetrate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato"><b>noble concentration</b></a> is one of the reasons that people continue to roam and wander through the long round of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato">AN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the long journey of wandering and roaming in the cycle of life is perpetuated by the lack of penetration into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato"><b>noble wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato">AN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato"><b>noble liberation</b></a> is understood and penetrated, the craving for existence is cut off and the conduit to future existence is destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato">AN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato"><b>no more rebirth</b></a> for one who has understood and penetrated the four noble qualities of virtue, concentration, wisdom, and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato">AN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato"><b>Vajjis at Bhandagama</b></a> when he delivered the teaching on the four qualities that lead to the end of wandering in samsara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato">AN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that once noble virtue, concentration, wisdom, and liberation are realized, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato"><b>craving for existence</b></a> is completely uprooted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato">AN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described in verse as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato"><b>ender of suffering</b></a> who, after becoming fully enlightened, taught the Dhamma to his monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato">AN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to himself as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato"><b>Gotama the famous</b></a> when speaking of his own penetration into the supreme qualities of virtue, concentration, wisdom, and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato">AN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is characterized as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato"><b>all-seeing</b></a> and as one who has attained final Nibbana after proclaiming the Dhamma to end suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato">AN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four distinct ways in which an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.78/en/sujato"><b>offering is purified</b></a> based on the virtue of the giver and the recipient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.78/en/sujato">AN 4.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an offering is purified by the giver but not the recipient when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.78/en/sujato"><b>giver is virtuous</b></a> and of good character, while the recipients are immoral and of bad character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.78/en/sujato">AN 4.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an offering can be purified by the recipient but not the giver if the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.78/en/sujato"><b>recipient is virtuous</b></a> while the giver is immoral and of bad character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.78/en/sujato">AN 4.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an offering is purified by neither the giver nor the recipient when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.78/en/sujato"><b>both are immoral</b></a> and of bad character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.78/en/sujato">AN 4.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that an offering is purified by both parties when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.78/en/sujato"><b>both are virtuous</b></a> and possess a good character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.78/en/sujato">AN 4.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato"><b>conduit to existence</b></a> to describe what is exhausted once a person has fully understood and penetrated noble virtue, concentration, wisdom, and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.1/en/sujato">AN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.78/en/sujato"><b>good character</b></a> (<i>kalyāṇadhammo</i>) as one whose presence helps to purify an offering made or received? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.78/en/sujato">AN 4.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato"><b>restraint of the faculties</b></a> is lacking, virtue is destroyed for one who lacks that restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato">AN 6.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato"><b>restraint of the faculties</b></a>, virtue possesses its proximate cause and can reach fulfillment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato">AN 6.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> cannot be achieved if a person lacks virtue, as virtue is the necessary condition for it to arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato">AN 6.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when right concentration is present, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of things as they really are</b></a> possesses its proximate cause and can reach fulfillment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato">AN 6.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for someone lacking right concentration, the proximate cause for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of things as they really are</b></a> is destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato">AN 6.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato"><b>revulsion and dispassion</b></a> cannot arise for someone who is deficient in knowledge and vision of things as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato">AN 6.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the final goal of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a> is unattainable for someone who lacks revulsion and dispassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato">AN 6.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a tree with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato"><b>damaged branches and foliage</b></a> to illustrate how a lack of sense restraint prevents the development of virtue and higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato">AN 6.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk with restraint of the faculties to a tree with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato"><b>complete branches and foliage</b></a>, which allows the bark, softwood, and heartwood to reach full maturity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.50/en/sujato">AN 6.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that monks who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.26/en/sujato"><b>deceitful and stubborn</b></a> are not considered his disciples and have fallen away from his Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.26/en/sujato">AN 4.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described monks who are talkative, fraudulent, and haughty as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.26/en/sujato"><b>unconcentrated</b></a> and unable to reach growth or fulfillment in the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.26/en/sujato">AN 4.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified monks who are not deceitful, are wise, and are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.26/en/sujato"><b>well-concentrated</b></a> as his true disciples who flourish in the Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.26/en/sujato">AN 4.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that those who are free from talkativeness and are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.26/en/sujato"><b>not stubborn</b></a> are the ones who truly grow in the teaching he has well-expounded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.26/en/sujato">AN 4.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato"><b>rag-robes</b></a> as one of the four things that are trifles, easy to obtain, and blameless for a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato">AN 4.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato"><b>alms-food</b></a> is a blameless and easily obtained requisite among all types of meals for a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato">AN 4.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato"><b>foot of a tree</b></a> to be an easy and blameless lodging for those practicing the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato">AN 4.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato"><b>putrid urine</b></a> as a medicinal requisite that is a trifle, easy to get, and entirely blameless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato">AN 4.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a monk is content with trifles and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato"><b>blameless things</b></a>, this contentment is considered a specific attainment of monkhood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato">AN 4.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is content with simple, easy-to-find requisites experiences no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato"><b>mental distress</b></a> regarding his lodging, robes, or food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato">AN 4.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a monk who is satisfied with what is easy to obtain is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato"><b>obstructed in any direction</b></a> he may go to practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.27/en/sujato">AN 4.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how resentment is frequently born from a person dwelling on the past, specifically holding onto the thought that "that person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato"><b>has done me harm</b></a>," which is the first of nine grounds for ill-will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato">AN 9.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the second ground for resentment as the immediate perception of a current injury, where one binds oneself to the thought that "this person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato"><b>is doing me harm</b></a>" in the present moment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato">AN 9.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that resentment can arise from future-oriented anxiety, such as when an individual becomes angry while anticipating that "this person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato"><b>will do me harm</b></a>" at a later time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato">AN 9.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, our social attachments often lead to resentment when we believe someone <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato"><b>has harmed a person</b></a> who is dear and agreeable to us? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato">AN 9.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a common ground for resentment is witnessing a situation in which someone <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato"><b>is currently harming</b></a> a friend or loved one whom we find agreeable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato">AN 9.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the sixth ground for resentment as the mental preoccupation with the idea that another person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato"><b>will harm someone</b></a> dear and agreeable to us in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato">AN 9.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that resentment can be directed toward those who benefit people we dislike, such as when we think that someone <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato"><b>has done a favor</b></a> to a person who is disagreeable to us? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato">AN 9.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that seeing someone <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato"><b>doing a favor</b></a> for an enemy or someone we find unpleasant can trigger a strong sense of resentment within us? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato">AN 9.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that anticipating that a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato"><b>will do a favor</b></a> to one's enemy is considered the ninth ground for resentment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato">AN 9.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha offered a specific technique for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.30/en/sujato"><b>removal of resentment</b></a>, which involves recognizing the inevitability of harm or benefit and asking oneself, "Where could it be possible to get anything different?" to help let go of anger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.30/en/sujato">AN 9.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of four "wheels" or blessings that lead to the prosperity of gods and humans, the first being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.31/en/sujato"><b>living in a suitable locality</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.31/en/sujato">AN 4.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.31/en/sujato"><b>associating with superior people</b></a> (sappurisāvassayo) as one of the four essential "wheels" that allow a person to quickly attain greatness and abundance in wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.31/en/sujato">AN 4.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.31/en/sujato"><b>right self-direction</b></a>—the conscious choice to steer oneself toward wholesome behavior—is a vital factor for achieving long-term happiness and fame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.31/en/sujato">AN 4.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.31/en/sujato"><b>merit performed in the past</b></a> as a primary condition that enables humans and gods to experience the expansion of their wealth, fame, and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.31/en/sujato">AN 4.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described four "bases of social unity" that act as the glue of society, the first of which is the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>giving</b></a> (dāna)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>endearing speech</b></a> (peyyavajja) and beneficial conduct as essential practices for maintaining social harmony and ensuring parents receive the respect of their children? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the four bases of social unity—giving, endearing speech, beneficial conduct, and impartiality—to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>linchpin of a moving chariot</b></a>, because without them, society would fall apart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two types of assemblies, distinguishing between one that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato"><b>trained in bombast</b></a> and one that is trained in inquiry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato">AN 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned of an assembly where monks do not listen to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato"><b>deep discourses</b></a> spoken by the Tathāgata, such as those dealing with emptiness, but instead favor poetic works by outsiders? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato">AN 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an assembly trained in inquiry consists of monks who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato"><b>lend an ear</b></a> and apply their minds to understand the profound, supramundane teachings of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato">AN 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, members of a healthy spiritual community should engage in cross-examination by asking one another, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato"><b>“How is this? What is the meaning of this?”</b></a> when studying the suttas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato">AN 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha criticized assemblies that focus on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato"><b>literary works</b></a> composed by poets—characterized by fancy words and phrases—rather than the actual teachings of the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato">AN 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised an assembly that seeks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato"><b>dispel doubts</b></a> regarding various points of the Dhamma through active discussion and investigation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato">AN 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the superior assembly as the one that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato"><b>trained in inquiry</b></a> and not in the mere bombast of fancy rhetoric? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato">AN 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a key trait of a wise assembly is that they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato"><b>make clear</b></a> what was previously obscure and uncover what was previously hidden in the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato">AN 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically encouraged the study of discourses that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato"><b>connected with emptiness</b></a> as a primary focus for those seeking true training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato">AN 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that in an assembly trained only in bombast, the monks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato"><b>do not clarify</b></a> what is unclarified, leading to persistent confusion regarding the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato">AN 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the study of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato"><b>supramundane</b></a> suttas as a distinguishing factor of an assembly that is truly trained in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato">AN 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the assembly trained in inquiry to be the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato"><b>foremost</b></a> of the two types of assemblies he described? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.47/en/sujato">AN 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.52/en/sujato"><b>two persons</b></a> who arise in the world specifically for the welfare and happiness of many people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.52/en/sujato">AN 2.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.52/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a>, the Arahant, the Perfectly Enlightened One, as one of the rare individuals whose appearance benefits both devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.52/en/sujato">AN 2.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.52/en/sujato"><b>wheel-turning monarch</b></a> alongside the Tathāgata as the only two persons who arise for the benefit and happiness of the manyfolk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.52/en/sujato">AN 2.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the appearance of a Perfectly Enlightened One and a Wheel-Turning Monarch is intended for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.52/en/sujato"><b>welfare of devas</b></a> and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.52/en/sujato">AN 2.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when these two specific individuals arise in the world, they do so for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.52/en/sujato"><b>good of the many</b></a> and the happiness of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.52/en/sujato">AN 2.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>three types of sick people</b></a> to be found existing in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a type of sick person who does not recover from their illness regardless of whether they receive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>proper food</b></a>, medicine, or a suitable attendant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, there is a type of sick person who will recover from their illness whether or not they receive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>suitable food</b></a>, medicine, or medical assistance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a specific type of sick person who only recovers from their affliction if they receive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>proper medicine</b></a>, food, and care, but will not get well otherwise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>sick-food</b></a> and medicine are specifically permitted because of the type of person who can only recover when these items are available? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even patients who will recover regardless of treatment, or those who will not recover at all, should still be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>properly attended to</b></a> because of the existence of the patient who specifically needs care to get well? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the three types of sick people in the world to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>three types of people</b></a> in terms of their spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned a person who does not enter the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>fixed course of rightness</b></a> in wholesome states regardless of whether they see a Tathāgata or hear his teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who enters the fixed course of rightness in wholesome states whether or not they have the opportunity of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>seeing the Tathāgata</b></a> or hearing the Dhamma-Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who enters the fixed course of rightness in wholesome states only if they get to hear the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma-Vinaya</b></a> proclaimed by a Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>teaching of the Dhamma</b></a> is authorized because of the person who requires hearing the teachings to enter the fixed course of rightness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma should be shared with everyone because of the existence of the individual who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>progresses only</b></a> upon hearing the Dhamma-Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>three types of people</b></a> compared to sick people who are found existing in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>fixed course of rightness</b></a> to describe the inevitable attainment of wholesome states for certain individuals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who, much like a patient who will recover anyway, enters the fixed course of rightness even without the opportunity of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>seeing the Tathāgata</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as medical care is permitted for the sake of one specific type of patient, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>teaching of the Dhamma</b></a> is authorized for the sake of the person who can only benefit from hearing it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even those who might not seem to benefit from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato"><b>hearing the Dhamma</b></a> should still be taught because of the existence of those who will truly succeed through the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.22/en/sujato">AN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that if a monk or nun perceives an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato"><b>abundance of lust</b></a> within themselves, they should conclude that they are declining in wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato">AN 4.158</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, a practitioner is considered to be declining in wholesome qualities if they notice an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato"><b>abundance of hatred</b></a> present within their own mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato">AN 4.158</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato"><b>abundance of delusion</b></a> identified within oneself is one of the four specific indicators that a person is falling away from wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato">AN 4.158</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that a sign of decline in wholesome qualities occurs when a practitioner’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato"><b>eye of wisdom</b></a> fails to function when contemplating deep matters of what is and is not the case? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato">AN 4.158</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta encouraged practitioners by saying that if they observe a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato"><b>diminution of lust</b></a> in themselves, they can confidently conclude they are not declining in wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato">AN 4.158</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato"><b>diminution of hatred</b></a> within a monk or nun is a clear indication that they are maintaining their progress and not declining in wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato">AN 4.158</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato"><b>diminution of delusion</b></a> as a primary factor for a practitioner to realize they are successfully avoiding decline in their spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato">AN 4.158</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta described non-decline in wholesome qualities as a state where a practitioner's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato"><b>eye of wisdom functions</b></a> effectively regarding deep points of what is and is not the case? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.158/en/sujato">AN 4.158</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person develops immeasurable concentration, they gain the personal knowledge that this state brings <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.27/en/sujato"><b>present happiness</b></a> and results in future happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.27/en/sujato">AN 5.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a practitioner who develops immeasurable concentration personally realizes that such concentration is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.27/en/sujato"><b>noble and unworldly</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.27/en/sujato">AN 5.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who develops immeasurable concentration gains the specific knowledge that this state is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.27/en/sujato"><b>not practiced by unworthy persons</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.27/en/sujato">AN 5.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that immeasurable concentration is peaceful, sublime, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.27/en/sujato"><b>attained through stilling</b></a>, rather than being a state maintained by forceful suppression? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.27/en/sujato">AN 5.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five distinct benefits of walking meditation, one of which is that it makes a person capable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.29/en/sujato"><b>enduring long journeys</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.29/en/sujato">AN 5.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that practitioners who engage in walking meditation develop a physical and mental constitution that allows them to better <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.29/en/sujato"><b>endure striving</b></a> in their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.29/en/sujato">AN 5.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a physical benefit of walking meditation is that what has been eaten, drunk, and consumed is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.29/en/sujato"><b>properly digested</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.29/en/sujato">AN 5.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that concentration gained during walking meditation is particularly valuable because it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.29/en/sujato"><b>long-lasting</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.29/en/sujato">AN 5.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should not visit a family that fails to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato"><b>rise pleasantly</b></a> to greet him when he arrives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato">AN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk should not sit down in the home of a family that does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato"><b>greet him pleasantly</b></a> upon his arrival? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato">AN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised monks against visiting families who fail to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato"><b>offer a seat</b></a> in a pleasant and welcoming manner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato">AN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a family is not worthy of a visit if they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato"><b>hide what they have</b></a> when a monk comes to their home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato">AN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a monk should avoid families who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato"><b>give little</b></a> to others even when they possess a great deal themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato">AN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that a family is unworthy of being visited if they provide <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato"><b>coarse offerings</b></a> even when they have choice or exquisite items available? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato">AN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a family is not worthy of being visited by a monk if they give their gifts <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato"><b>without respect</b></a> rather than with careful attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.13/en/sujato">AN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a monk as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world when he possesses six specific qualities of sense restraint and equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato">AN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk worthy of hospitality as one who, upon seeing a form with the eye, remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato"><b>equanimous, mindful, and clearly comprehending</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato">AN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk is worthy of gifts if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato"><b>neither glad nor sad</b></a> after hearing a sound with his ears, but instead maintains mental balance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato">AN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a monk deserves reverential salutation if he remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato"><b>unmoved</b></a> and mindful even after smelling an odor with his nose? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato">AN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is a worthy recipient of offerings when he experiences a taste with the tongue but stays <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato"><b>composed and mindful</b></a>, avoiding both delight and aversion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato">AN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a monk is worthy of being called a guest-gift recipient if he experiences a tactile sensation but continues to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato"><b>dwell with equanimity</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato">AN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a monk as being worthy of hospitality if he cognizes a mental phenomenon but remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato"><b>unperturbed and aware</b></a>, neither pleased nor displeased by the thought? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.1/en/sujato">AN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.85/en/sujato"><b>Ascetic (Samaṇo)</b></a> is a specific designation for the Tathāgata, the Arahant, the Perfectly Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.85/en/sujato">AN 8.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.85/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin (Brāhmaṇo)</b></a> is a title that rightfully describes the Tathāgata because he is the ultimate spiritual authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.85/en/sujato">AN 8.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the Tathāgata as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.85/en/sujato"><b>Healer (Bhisakko)</b></a>, emphasizing his role in curing the spiritual ailments of beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.85/en/sujato">AN 8.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the expression <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.85/en/sujato"><b>One who has reached the end of knowledge (Vedagū)</b></a> as a synonym for himself and his complete enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.85/en/sujato">AN 8.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Tathāgata as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.85/en/sujato"><b>Released one (Vimutto)</b></a> because he has attained the highest liberation and won the battle over suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.85/en/sujato">AN 8.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha metaphorically called himself a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.85/en/sujato"><b>great elephant (nāga)</b></a> who is perfectly tamed, has crossed over the battle, and has attained final extinguishment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.85/en/sujato">AN 8.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.27/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a>, one of two destinations is to be expected: hell or the animal realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.27/en/sujato">AN 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.28/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>, one of two destinations is to be expected: the world of the devas or the human world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.28/en/sujato">AN 2.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he frequented <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.30/en/sujato"><b>remote lodgings</b></a> and forest dwellings for two specific reasons: for his own pleasant dwelling in this life and out of compassion for future generations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.30/en/sujato">AN 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato"><b>serenity</b></a> (samatha) and insight (vipassanā) as the two primary qualities that partake of true knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato">AN 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when serenity is developed, the mind is developed, and through the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a>, lust is abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato">AN 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato"><b>insight</b></a> is developed, wisdom is developed, and through the development of wisdom, ignorance is abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato">AN 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a mind defiled by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> is not liberated, and wisdom defiled by ignorance is not developed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato">AN 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> (cetovimutti) as the result of the fading away of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato">AN 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato"><b>liberation by wisdom</b></a> (paññāvimutti) as the result of the fading away of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato">AN 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the "level of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.32/en/sujato"><b>unworthy person</b></a>" (asappurisa) as being characterized by ingratitude and a lack of appreciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.32/en/sujato">AN 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.32/en/sujato"><b>ingratitude</b></a> is a quality recognized and practiced by those who are unworthy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.32/en/sujato">AN 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the "level of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.32/en/sujato"><b>worthy person</b></a>" (sappurisa) as being characterized by gratitude and appreciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.32/en/sujato">AN 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught it is not easy to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato"><b>repay</b></a> one's mother and father for all they have done? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato">AN 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said even if one were to carry their mother on one shoulder and their father on the other for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato"><b>a hundred years</b></a> while attending to all their physical needs, one would still not have repaid them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato">AN 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that even establishing one's parents in supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato"><b>sovereignty</b></a> over the earth with all its wealth would not be enough to repay the debt of gratitude owed to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato">AN 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato"><b>parents</b></a> are of great help to their children because they are their providers, their nourishers, and the ones who show them this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato">AN 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one truly repays their parents by establishing them in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> if they are faithless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato">AN 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person fulfills their duty to their parents by establishing them in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato"><b>virtuous behavior</b></a> if they are immoral? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato">AN 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught one repays their parents by establishing them in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato"><b>generosity</b></a> if they are miserly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato">AN 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a child truly compensates their parents by establishing them in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> if they are unwise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato">AN 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>unworthy living</b></a> as a state where monks of all levels of seniority—senior, middle, and new—do not wish to speak to one another or be spoken to by their peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a senior monk in a state of unworthy living specifically desires that neither middle-level nor <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>newly ordained monks</b></a> should offer him any advice or correction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that in a community characterized by unworthy living, a monk who is corrected by a fellow monk will assume the speaker is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>acting without sympathy</b></a> and without concern for his well-being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a sign of unworthy living is when a monk responds to a fellow monk's advice by harassing the speaker and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>refusing to make amends</b></a> even when he recognizes he has committed an offense? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>worthy living</b></a> as a harmonious state where monks at all stages of their training are both willing to speak to others and willing to be addressed by them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that in a community of worthy living, even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>senior monk</b></a> is willing to be corrected by others and will take steps to make amends for any offense he sees he has committed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that in a community practicing worthy living, a monk receiving correction will think the speaker is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>motivated by sympathy</b></a> and is seeking his benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk in a worthy living environment as saying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>"It is good"</b></a> when a fellow monk addresses him, rather than reacting with hostility or harassment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>newly ordained monk</b></a> in a worthy community should be open to speaking with and being addressed by both senior and middle-level monks for the sake of communal harmony? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that in unworthy living, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>middle-level monk</b></a> might resolve not to speak to senior or junior monks and also wish that they would refrain from speaking to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted two ways for a spiritual community to reside together, identifying those who live as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>the unworthy</b></a> and those who live as the worthy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that monks in a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>worthy living</b></a> will not harass those who point out their faults but will instead cooperate to rectify their behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.64/en/sujato"><b>monastic happiness</b></a>, or the happiness of one who has gone forth into homelessness, as being superior to the happiness enjoyed by a layperson? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.64/en/sujato">AN 2.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two distinct types of happiness available to people, namely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.64/en/sujato"><b>household happiness</b></a> and the happiness of the monkhood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.64/en/sujato">AN 2.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that of the two kinds of happiness—household and monastic—the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.64/en/sujato"><b>happiness of homelessness</b></a> is the foremost and most excellent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.64/en/sujato">AN 2.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared two different kinds of pleasant experiences and declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.65/en/sujato"><b>happiness of renunciation</b></a> to be superior to sensual happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.65/en/sujato">AN 2.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly differentiated between <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.65/en/sujato"><b>sensual happiness</b></a> and the happiness that comes from renouncing worldly desires and attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.65/en/sujato">AN 2.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that between the happiness of the senses and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.65/en/sujato"><b>happiness of renunciation</b></a>, the latter is the supreme form of happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.65/en/sujato">AN 2.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a monk in a community of worthy living will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>not harass</b></a> even a junior monk who tries to correct him, seeing the correction as a service? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a hallmark of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato"><b>unworthy living</b></a> is when a monk intentionally avoids being corrected by his fellow monks, regardless of their level of seniority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.62/en/sujato">AN 2.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato"><b>three types of persons</b></a> existing in the world, categorized by the specific nature of the bodily, verbal, and mental volitional formations they generate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato">AN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who generates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato"><b>afflicted volitional formations</b></a> through their body, speech, and mind is reborn in an afflicted world where they experience painful consequences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato">AN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that beings who are reborn in an afflicted world are struck by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato"><b>afflicted contacts</b></a>, which in turn lead them to experience exclusively painful feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato">AN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the experience of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato"><b>exclusively painful feelings</b></a> to the suffering endured by beings in hell, who have generated solely afflicted volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato">AN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who generates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato"><b>unafflicted volitional formations</b></a> of body, speech, and mind as one who is reborn in an unafflicted world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato">AN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that being reborn in an unafflicted world results in being struck by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato"><b>unafflicted contacts</b></a>, which facilitate the experience of pleasant feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato">AN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato"><b>Subhakiṇha gods</b></a> as examples of beings who experience exclusively pleasant feelings because they have generated only unafflicted volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato">AN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a person who generates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato"><b>mixed volitional formations</b></a>—meaning those that are both afflicted and unafflicted—is reborn in a world that reflects that same mixture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato">AN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those reborn in a mixed world experience both afflicted and unafflicted contacts, leading to feelings of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato"><b>mixed pleasure and pain</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato">AN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato"><b>human beings</b></a> as a class of beings who experience mixed pleasure and pain as a result of their past afflicted and unafflicted volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato">AN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that in addition to humans, some gods and some <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato"><b>fallen beings</b></a> (vinipātikā) also experience a mixture of pleasure and pain based on their past actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato">AN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that our future experience of happiness or suffering is rooted in the quality of our current <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato"><b>mental volitional formations</b></a>, alongside our bodily and verbal actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato">AN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the entire spectrum of rebirth, from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato"><b>beings in hell</b></a> to the highest gods, is determined by the degree to which one's volitional formations are afflicted or unafflicted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.23/en/sujato">AN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato"><b>perversions of perception</b></a>, mind, and view that cause sentient beings to misinterpret reality and remain bound to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato">AN 4.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that seeing permanence in what is actually impermanent is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato"><b>distortion of the mind</b></a> that prevents a person from seeing things as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato">AN 4.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the primary perversions of view is the mistaken belief that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato"><b>pleasure</b></a> to be found in what is inherently suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato">AN 4.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described the common tendency to perceive a "self" in things that are actually non-self as a fundamental <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato"><b>perversion of view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato">AN 4.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings who perceive beauty in what is actually foul are victims of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> and are described as "deranged" in their mental processing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato">AN 4.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how those who have regained their sanity by listening to the Dhamma see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato"><b>impermanent as impermanent</b></a> and thereby transcend all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato">AN 4.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that when "bringers of light" arise in the world, they proclaim the Dhamma that leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato"><b>subsidence of suffering</b></a> by correcting distorted perceptions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato">AN 4.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that by adopting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>—specifically seeing the foul as foul and suffering as suffering—a practitioner can truly transcend the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato">AN 4.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized distortions of reality into three distinct levels: perversions of perception, perversions of mind, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato"><b>perversions of view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato">AN 4.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that beings "slain by wrong view" are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato"><b>deranged</b></a> and bound to the yoke of Mara because they cannot see things as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.49/en/sujato">AN 4.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk’s way of living determines whether their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato"><b>death</b></a> will be full of regret or free from remorse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato">AN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta warned monks that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato"><b>delighting in work</b></a> or being intent on various activities is a lifestyle choice that leads to a regretful passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato">AN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Sāriputta, a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato"><b>delighting in talk</b></a> and intent on chatter is one who has not properly abandoned identity for the sake of ending suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato">AN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> Sāriputta pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato"><b>delighting in sleep</b></a> is one of the six factors that prevents a practitioner from making a complete end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato">AN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> Sāriputta taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato"><b>delighting in company</b></a> and fond of society is said to be "delighting in identity" and fails to reach the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato">AN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta defined a person who avoids delighting in social groups and mental proliferation as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato"><b>delighting in Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato">AN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> Sāriputta warned that anyone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato"><b>delighting in proliferation</b></a>—the mental tendency to elaborate on perceptions—will miss out on Nibbāna, the unsurpassed security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato">AN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> Sāriputta explained that a monk who successfully abandons the delight in work, talk, and sleep is one who has truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato"><b>abandoned identity</b></a> for the sake of final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato">AN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Sāriputta, the path to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato"><b>death without regret</b></a> involves not being fond of society or socializing with other people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato">AN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> Sāriputta taught that those who abandon mental proliferation and instead delight in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato"><b>state of non-proliferation</b></a> are the ones who attain Nibbāna, the ultimate peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.15/en/sujato">AN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.45/en/sujato"><b>noble right concentration</b></a> as one-pointedness of mind that is equipped and supported by seven other factors of the path, starting from right view and ending with right mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.45/en/sujato">AN 7.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.45/en/sujato"><b>seven requisites for concentration</b></a>—specifically right view, right intention, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, and right mindfulness—which must be present for concentration to be considered noble? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.45/en/sujato">AN 7.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk has entered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.72/en/sujato"><b>unmistakable way</b></a> (or undeviating practice) for the destruction of the taints when he is possessed of four specific qualities: thoughts of renunciation, non-ill will, harmlessness, and right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.72/en/sujato">AN 4.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.72/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of renunciation</b></a> as one of the four essential factors that ensure a practitioner is correctly applying themselves to the path that leads to the end of all mental fermentations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.72/en/sujato">AN 4.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned a "trainee" monk (a <i>sekha</i>) that they will face spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.89/en/sujato"><b>decline and falling away</b></a> if they find delight in work, delight in talk, delight in sleep, delight in social company, or fail to review the mind as it is liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.89/en/sujato">AN 5.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.89/en/sujato"><b>delighting in social groups</b></a> and company is a specific behavior that leads to the spiritual decline of a monk who is still in training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.89/en/sujato">AN 5.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a trainee monk ensures their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.89/en/sujato"><b>non-decline</b></a> and progress toward liberation by not delighting in work, talk, sleep, or company, and by consistently reviewing the state of their liberated mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.89/en/sujato">AN 5.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that three specific "ascetic perceptions," when cultivated, will completely fulfill seven noble qualities, including being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.101/en/sujato"><b>scrupulous in virtue</b></a> and acting consistently in regard to the moral precepts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.101/en/sujato">AN 10.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to reflect on the fact that they have "entered upon a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.101/en/sujato"><b>classless condition</b></a>" as a means to foster the humility and focus necessary for spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.101/en/sujato">AN 10.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an ascetic should constantly remind themselves that their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.101/en/sujato"><b>life is dependent on others</b></a>, a perception that helps them fulfill the quality of being non-covetous and without ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.101/en/sujato">AN 10.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the perception "I must behave in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.101/en/sujato"><b>different way</b></a>" from laypeople is a core thought for a monk that leads to becoming eager for training and free from conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.101/en/sujato">AN 10.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a monk who develops the proper ascetic perceptions will always be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.101/en/sujato"><b>desirous of training</b></a> and will live with aroused energy in his spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.101/en/sujato">AN 10.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that cultivating the three perceptions of a renunciant leads a monk to correctly understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.101/en/sujato"><b>purpose of life's requisites</b></a>, such as food, clothing, and shelter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.101/en/sujato">AN 10.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.67/en/sujato"><b>eight unnoble modes of expression</b></a>, which include claiming to have seen something when one has not, or claiming to have heard something when one has not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.67/en/sujato">AN 8.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized it as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.67/en/sujato"><b>unnoble speech</b></a> to say that one has sensed or cognized something when, in reality, those things were not actually sensed or cognized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.67/en/sujato">AN 8.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.68/en/sujato"><b>noble modes of expression</b></a> as accurately stating that one has not seen what was not seen and has seen what was actually seen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.68/en/sujato">AN 8.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.68/en/sujato"><b>noble mode of speech</b></a> to honestly declare that one has not heard, sensed, or cognized something if one has truly not experienced those things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.68/en/sujato">AN 8.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.68/en/sujato"><b>noble speech</b></a> will truthfully say they have heard what they have heard and cognized what they have cognized, without any deception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.68/en/sujato">AN 8.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta described a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>fettered internally</b></a> as a practitioner who is virtuous and follows the Pātimokkha, but upon rebirth in a heavenly realm, eventually returns to "this state" (this world)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Sāriputta, a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>fettered externally</b></a> is one who maintains virtue and attains a peaceful liberation of mind, becoming a non-returner after rebirth in a heavenly realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once used a psychic power to disappear from the Jetavana monastery and instantaneously reappear at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>Eastern Park</b></a>, comparing the speed of his travel to a strong man extending his arm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that up to sixty "even-minded" deities can stand simultaneously on a space as tiny as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>tip of a needle</b></a> without ever crowding or obstructing one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk should be one who "sees <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>danger in slight faults</b></a>" while training himself in the rules of the Pātimokkha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that the reason deities can occupy a miniscule space without conflict is that they have specifically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>developed their minds</b></a> in a way that allows for such harmony? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted his followers to train themselves to possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>peaceful faculties</b></a> and a peaceful mind, modeling themselves after the harmony of even-minded deities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that having a peaceful mind naturally leads to a practitioner performing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>peaceful bodily actions</b></a> in their daily life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta identified a specific type of person who is practicing for the fading away and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>cessation of existence</b></a>, leading them to become a non-returner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that wanderers of other sects have suffered a great <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>loss</b></a> because they have not had the opportunity to hear this specific Dhamma exposition on even-mindedness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk practicing for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a> and the destruction of greed is classified as one who is "fettered externally"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha heard from deities that the Venerable Sāriputta was teaching at the Mansion of Migāra’s Mother, he visited him specifically out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> for the assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a monk's mind is peaceful, his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>verbal actions</b></a> will also be peaceful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner with a peaceful mind will offer a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>peaceful offering</b></a> to their fellow companions in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta described a practitioner who is practicing for the fading away and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>cessation of sensual lust</b></a> as one who will not return to this world after passing away from a heavenly rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly told the Venerable Sāriputta that the monks should train themselves by saying, "We will have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>peaceful minds</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the "even-minded" deities personally informed the Buddha that the Venerable Sāriputta's audience was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>delighted</b></a> by his teaching on internal and external fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>mental actions</b></a> become peaceful only when they have first successfully trained themselves to have peaceful faculties and a peaceful mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta held his teaching at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>Mansion of Migāra’s Mother</b></a>, a location also known as the Pubbārāma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the peacefulness of a practitioner should extend to how they treat their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>fellow practitioners</b></a> (sabrahmacārīs)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta defined a person as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> based on their possession of proper conduct and resort while remaining restrained by the Pātimokkha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even when many deities occupy a space as small as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>needle’s point</b></a>, they do not bother or harm one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta taught that those who are "fettered internally" still possess the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato"><b>bounden duty</b></a> to return to human existence after their time in the celestial realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.36/en/sujato">AN 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are two kinds of happiness—worldly and unworldly—and that the foremost of these is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.68/en/sujato"><b>unworldly happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.68/en/sujato">AN 2.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, while both worldly happiness and unworldly happiness exist, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.68/en/sujato"><b>unworldly kind</b></a> is superior to the worldly kind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.68/en/sujato">AN 2.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two distinct types of well-being, bodily happiness and mental happiness, but concluded that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.70/en/sujato"><b>mental happiness</b></a> is the foremost of the two? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.70/en/sujato">AN 2.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that although one may experience bodily happiness, the highest form of well-being is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.70/en/sujato"><b>mental happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.70/en/sujato">AN 2.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that bad unwholesome qualities arise only when there is a sign, and that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.77/en/sujato"><b>abandoning that sign</b></a>, those bad unwholesome qualities do not come to be? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.77/en/sujato">AN 2.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that bad unwholesome qualities arise from a source, and that it is through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.78/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of that source</b></a> that these unwholesome qualities are prevented from occurring? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.78/en/sujato">AN 2.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that bad unwholesome qualities arise only with a reason, and that through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.79/en/sujato"><b>abandoning that reason</b></a>, those unwholesome states do not arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.79/en/sujato">AN 2.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that bad unwholesome qualities arise in conjunction with volitional formations, and that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.80/en/sujato"><b>abandoning those volitional formations</b></a>, the unwholesome qualities do not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.80/en/sujato">AN 2.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, bad unwholesome qualities arise only with a condition, and that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.81/en/sujato"><b>abandoning that condition</b></a>, those bad unwholesome qualities are ceased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.81/en/sujato">AN 2.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that bad unwholesome qualities arise only when there is form, and that through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.82/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of that form</b></a>, such unwholesome qualities do not come into existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.82/en/sujato">AN 2.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that bad unwholesome qualities arise with feeling, and that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.83/en/sujato"><b>abandoning that feeling</b></a>, those bad unwholesome qualities no longer occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.83/en/sujato">AN 2.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that bad unwholesome qualities arise only with perception, and that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.84/en/sujato"><b>abandoning that perception</b></a>, these unwholesome qualities are stopped from arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.84/en/sujato">AN 2.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that bad unwholesome qualities arise only with consciousness, and that it is through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.85/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of that consciousness</b></a> that those unwholesome qualities do not come to be? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.85/en/sujato">AN 2.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that bad unwholesome qualities arise with a conditioned thing as an object, and that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.86/en/sujato"><b>abandoning that conditioned thing</b></a>, those unwholesome qualities do not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.86/en/sujato">AN 2.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who helps another go for refuge to the Triple Gem—the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha—as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato"><b>highly helpful</b></a> to that individual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato">AN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who enables another to understand the Four Noble Truths—suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path—as they really are is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato"><b>extremely beneficial</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato">AN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the most helpful people in the world is someone through whom another person attains the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> and enters upon liberation of mind and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato">AN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is not easy to repay a person who has helped one find the path to liberation, even by performing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato"><b>acts of service</b></a> like bowing, rising for them, or greeting them with joined palms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato">AN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that providing robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines is insufficient to fully repay those who help one attain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato"><b>spiritual liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato">AN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato"><b>three types of persons</b></a> who are more helpful to another than anyone else could possibly be in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato">AN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is easily provoked to anger and resentment by even a small remark as having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato"><b>mind like a sore</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato">AN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who reacts with hostility and irritability to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato"><b>festering wound</b></a> that discharges matter when touched by a mere stick or shard? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato">AN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened a person who clearly perceives the Four Noble Truths as they really are to an individual having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato"><b>mind like lightning</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato">AN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the sudden realization of suffering and its cessation to a person with good sight seeing objects in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato"><b>darkness of night</b></a> during a momentary flash of lightning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato">AN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who has attained liberation through the destruction of the taints in this very life as having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato"><b>mind like a diamond</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato">AN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that just as a diamond can cut through any gem or stone, the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato"><b>liberated mind</b></a> is capable of cutting through all defilements and taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato">AN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato"><b>three kinds of persons</b></a> found in the world: those with minds like sores, those with minds like lightning, and those with minds like diamonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato">AN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the <i>Vajirūpamasutta</i>, the Buddha explains that correctly understanding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> is the defining characteristic of a person with a mind like lightning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato">AN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the person with a diamond-like mind as one who has realized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom after the taints have been destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato">AN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed there is no other person more helpful to a human being than those who guide them toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato"><b>taking refuge</b></a> in the Triple Gem? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato">AN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even providing for all the physical needs of a spiritual benefactor is not a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato"><b>proper repayment</b></a> for being shown the path to the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato">AN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato"><b>mind like a sore</b></a> to describe someone who displays anger, hatred, and resentment even when spoken to only slightly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato">AN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified someone who helps another realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> and the way leading to its cessation as one of the three most helpful people in existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.24/en/sujato">AN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has attained the destruction of taints possesses a mind as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato"><b>unbreakable as a diamond</b></a>, capable of cutting through everything? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.25/en/sujato">AN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how gains, honor, and renown arose for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.68/en/sujato"><b>Devadatta</b></a> only for his own destruction and downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.68/en/sujato">AN 4.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the way gains and honor destroy a person of bad character to how a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.68/en/sujato"><b>banana tree</b></a> is destroyed by its own fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.68/en/sujato">AN 4.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a bamboo or a reed is destroyed by its own fruit, so too are a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.68/en/sujato"><b>gains, honor, and renown</b></a> potentially the cause of their own ruin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.68/en/sujato">AN 4.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.68/en/sujato"><b>mule</b></a> being destroyed by its own pregnancy to illustrate how honor and renown can lead to the downfall of a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.68/en/sujato">AN 4.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while staying on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.68/en/sujato"><b>Vulture's Peak</b></a> in Rājagaha, the Buddha addressed the monks specifically about how the pursuit of fame led to the ruin of the monk Devadatta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.68/en/sujato">AN 4.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as the dawn is the precursor to the sun's rising, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.121/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> is the precursor and forerunner to the arising of all wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.121/en/sujato">AN 10.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, for one who possesses right view, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.121/en/sujato"><b>right intention</b></a> naturally comes into being, eventually leading to the fulfillment of the entire spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.121/en/sujato">AN 10.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a sequence of spiritual development where right concentration leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.121/en/sujato"><b>right knowledge</b></a>, which in turn leads to right liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.121/en/sujato">AN 10.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.121/en/sujato"><b>right liberation</b></a> as the final quality that arises for one who has successfully developed the preceding nine factors of the path starting from right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.121/en/sujato">AN 10.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the natural phenomenon of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.121/en/sujato"><b>dawn</b></a> as a simile to describe how right view serves as the first sign of the coming "sunlight" of wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.121/en/sujato">AN 10.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated there are ten specific qualities that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.123/en/sujato"><b>pure and bright</b></a>, and that these qualities cannot be found outside of the Sugata's discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.123/en/sujato">AN 10.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the spiritual path consisting of ten factors, from right view to right liberation, is found <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.123/en/sujato"><b>nowhere else</b></a> except within the discipline of the Sugata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.123/en/sujato">AN 10.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that among the ten qualities the Buddha described as exceptionally pure and bright is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.123/en/sujato"><b>right action</b></a>, which is a core component of the discipline he taught for spiritual purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.123/en/sujato">AN 10.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.123/en/sujato"><b>right livelihood</b></a> and right effort among the ten pure and bright qualities that lead a practitioner to spiritual perfection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.123/en/sujato">AN 10.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the "teaching that results in suffering" as the combination of ten unwholesome states beginning with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.143/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> and ending with wrong liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.143/en/sujato">AN 10.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a "teaching that results in happiness" is characterized by the development of ten wholesome qualities, culminating in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.143/en/sujato"><b>right liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.143/en/sujato">AN 10.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.143/en/sujato"><b>wrong intention</b></a> and wrong speech as part of a set of factors that inevitably lead to an outcome of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.143/en/sujato">AN 10.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted the path to suffering with the path to happiness, highlighting that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.143/en/sujato"><b>right mindfulness</b></a> and right concentration are essential for a joyful outcome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.143/en/sujato">AN 10.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are eight rewards to be expected from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind by loving-kindness</b></a> when it is cultivated, frequently practiced, and used as a firm foundation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who has diligently practiced and developed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>deliverance of mind through loving-kindness</b></a> is able to sleep in comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one of the specific benefits of well-developed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> is that the practitioner wakes up in comfort and with a peaceful state of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner who consistently develops the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> through loving-kindness is protected from seeing any evil or distressing dreams while they sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who has made the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>mind of loving-kindness</b></a> their primary vehicle and constant practice becomes naturally dear and pleasing to human beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the practice of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind by loving-kindness</b></a> makes a person dear not only to humans but also to non-human beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>deities protect</b></a> those who have fully developed, practiced, and consolidated the liberation of mind through loving-kindness in their daily lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that neither fire, poison, nor weapons can harm or take effect upon a person who has successfully cultivated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind through loving-kindness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a practitioner of loving-kindness does not attain a higher spiritual penetration in this life, their practice still leads them to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>reborn in the Brahma world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said for one who develops <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>boundless loving-kindness</b></a> and contemplates the destruction of the grounds for rebirth, their spiritual fetters become thin and weakened? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if a person feels loving-kindness toward just a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>single living being</b></a> with an uncorrupted mind, they become wholesome and virtuous through that act? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>ariyan practitioner</b></a> who feels compassionate for all living beings creates a vast and mountain-like amount of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the light of all the stars to the moon to show that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness of the mind</b></a> far outshines the merit of vast royal sacrifices? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that one who does not kill, cause others to kill, or conquer, and who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness for all beings</b></a>, has no enmity with anyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.1/en/sujato">AN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Anāthapiṇḍika that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.110/en/sujato"><b>mind is corrupted</b></a> by ill will, one's bodily, verbal, and mental actions likewise become corrupted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.110/en/sujato">AN 3.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when a person’s mind is overwhelmed by ill will, leading to corrupted actions, they do not have a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.110/en/sujato"><b>good death</b></a> or a fortunate passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.110/en/sujato">AN 3.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.110/en/sujato"><b>poorly roofed house</b></a> where the peak, rafters, and walls all get wet and rot to illustrate how a corrupted mind ruins one's conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.110/en/sujato">AN 3.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.110/en/sujato"><b>mind is uncorrupted</b></a> and free from ill will, one's bodily, verbal, and mental actions also remain uncorrupted and pure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.110/en/sujato">AN 3.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha compared an uncorrupted mind to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.110/en/sujato"><b>well-roofed peaked house</b></a>, where the peak, rafters, and walls are all protected from the rain and do not rot? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.110/en/sujato">AN 3.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified nine <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato"><b>grounds for resentment</b></a>, which include the recurring thought that someone has harmed you in the past, is harming you in the present, or will harm you in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato">AN 9.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, resentment can arise when one perceives that another person has harmed, is harming, or will harm a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato"><b>person dear</b></a> and agreeable to oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato">AN 9.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the causes of resentment is the thought that someone has benefited, is benefiting, or will benefit a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato"><b>person disliked</b></a> and disagreeable to oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.29/en/sujato">AN 9.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained nine ways of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.30/en/sujato"><b>removing resentment</b></a> by reflecting on the futility of anger, specifically by contemplating that while someone may have harmed you or your loved ones, one should think: "What can be done about it?" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.30/en/sujato">AN 9.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described four "wheels" or conditions—living in a suitable locality, relying on good people, aright self-resolve, and having merit from the past—that allow humans and gods to attain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.31/en/sujato"><b>growth in wealth</b></a> and abundance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.31/en/sujato">AN 4.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.31/en/sujato"><b>aright resolve</b></a> regarding one's own mind to be one of the four essential factors for achieving greatness and prosperity in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.31/en/sujato">AN 4.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>bases of social unity</b></a>—giving, endearing speech, beneficent conduct, and impartiality—which he compared to the linchpin that keeps a moving chariot on its course? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if the four bases of social unity were not practiced, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>mother or father</b></a> would not receive the honor or respect they deserve from their children? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.27/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> can expect to be reborn in one of two destinations: hell or the animal realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.27/en/sujato">AN 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.28/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>, one of two happy destinations is to be expected: heaven or the human realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.28/en/sujato">AN 2.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he resorted to secluded <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.30/en/sujato"><b>forest dwellings</b></a> for two reasons: his own pleasant dwelling in this life and out of compassion for future generations who might follow his example? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.30/en/sujato">AN 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato"><b>serenity</b></a> (samatha) is developed, the mind is developed, and through a developed mind, one is able to abandon lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato">AN 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato"><b>insight</b></a> (vipassanā) leads to the development of wisdom, which results in the total abandoning of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.31/en/sujato">AN 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.32/en/sujato"><b>ungrateful and unthankful</b></a> is the characteristic mark of an unworthy person, whereas gratitude and thankfulness are the hallmarks of a worthy person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.32/en/sujato">AN 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said it is not easy to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato"><b>repay one's parents</b></a>, and even if one were to carry them on one's shoulders for a hundred years and provide for all their physical needs, it would still not constitute full repayment for their gift of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato">AN 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one truly repays their parents by encouraging them to establish themselves in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato"><b>faith, virtue, generosity, and wisdom</b></a> if they lack these qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.33/en/sujato">AN 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākaccāna explained that nobles, brahmins, and householders <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.37/en/sujato"><b>quarrel with each other</b></a> because they are obsessed with and shackled by their attachment to sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.37/en/sujato">AN 2.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākaccāna stated that the reason ascetics quarrel with other ascetics is their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.37/en/sujato"><b>shackling to views</b></a> and their obsession with dogmatic positions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.37/en/sujato">AN 2.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Mahākaccāna, the Buddha is the one in the world who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.37/en/sujato"><b>transcended obsession</b></a> with both sensual pleasures and dogmatic views, thereby ending all such quarrels? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an2.37/en/sujato">AN 2.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.1/en/sujato"><b>fears</b></a> that arise in the world do so because of the fool, and not because of the wise person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.1/en/sujato">AN 3.1</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that whatever <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.1/en/sujato"><b>perils</b></a> arise, they are all produced by the fool rather than by the wise individual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.1/en/sujato">AN 3.1</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.1/en/sujato"><b>misfortunes</b></a> originate from the actions of fools, and never from those who are wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.1/en/sujato">AN 3.1</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.1/en/sujato"><b>fire</b></a> starting in a simple reed or grass hut that spreads to destroy even well-built, protected mansions to show how fear and danger arise from the fool? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.1/en/sujato">AN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.1/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a>, peril, or misfortune that ever arises from a wise person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.1/en/sujato">AN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to train themselves to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.1/en/sujato"><b>abandon</b></a> the three qualities of a fool and instead undertake the three qualities of a wise person to be free from peril? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.1/en/sujato">AN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that both the fool and the wise person are defined and recognized by their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato"><b>deeds</b></a> or actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato">AN 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> is made brilliant and manifest through their actual conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato">AN 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato"><b>bodily misconduct</b></a> as one of the three primary markers by which a fool is known? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato">AN 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato"><b>verbal misconduct</b></a> is a key indicator that a person possesses the qualities of a fool? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato">AN 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato"><b>mental misconduct</b></a> is the third quality, along with bodily and verbal misconduct, that reveals a person to be a fool? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato">AN 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato"><b>good bodily conduct</b></a> as one of the three traits by which a wise person is to be recognized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato">AN 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a wise person is known for their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato"><b>good verbal conduct</b></a>, which distinguishes them from the fool? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato">AN 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato"><b>good mental conduct</b></a> is a definitive characteristic of a person who is truly wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.2/en/sujato">AN 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a fool is recognized because they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.5/en/sujato"><b>ask questions unwisely</b></a>, without proper attention or reflection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.5/en/sujato">AN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.5/en/sujato"><b>answering questions unwisely</b></a> as a sign that a person lacks the qualities of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.5/en/sujato">AN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a fool is someone who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.5/en/sujato"><b>fails to approve</b></a> when another person answers a question wisely and with clear, smooth language? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.5/en/sujato">AN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a wise person is known for their ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.5/en/sujato"><b>ask questions wisely</b></a>, using proper reflection and attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.5/en/sujato">AN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a wise person will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.5/en/sujato"><b>approve and appreciate</b></a> when another person provides a wise answer that is well-phrased and smooth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.5/en/sujato">AN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>three types of persons</b></a> existing in the world who are categorized by whether or not they should be associated with, served, and honored? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>inferior in virtuous conduct</b></a>, concentration, and wisdom is generally one whom others should not associate with, serve, or honor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite advising against associating with inferior people, the Buddha clarified that one may still interact with such a person out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>compassion and pity</b></a> for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended associating with a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>one’s equal</b></a> in the spiritual qualities of virtuous conduct, concentration, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the benefit of associating with equals is that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>conversation on virtue</b></a>, concentration, and wisdom will take place between them, which will be both steady and comfortable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>superior in wisdom</b></a>, virtuous conduct, and concentration is one who should be associated with, served, and honored with respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested associating with those superior to ourselves in order to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>fulfill the aggregate of virtue</b></a> that we have not yet completed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that honoring a superior person allows one to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>support with wisdom</b></a> in various respects the aggregate of virtuous conduct that has already been fulfilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised serving those more advanced than oneself so that one may <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>fulfill the aggregate of concentration</b></a> which remains unfulfilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by associating with a superior person, one can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>support the aggregate of concentration</b></a> through wisdom in many different ways? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the primary reason for honoring a superior person is the opportunity to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>fulfill the aggregate of wisdom</b></a> if it is currently incomplete? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even if one's aggregate of wisdom is already fulfilled, associating with a superior helps to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>support that wisdom</b></a> further in various respects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned in verse that a person who associates with those who are inferior to them will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>decline in qualities</b></a> over time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a person who associates with their equals <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>never suffers decline</b></a> in their spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by following those who are excellent, a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato"><b>quickly rises</b></a> and improves, and therefore one should always seek to associate with those superior to oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.26/en/sujato">AN 3.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if a person were to experience the results of their actions exactly as they were performed, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>spiritual life</b></a> would be impossible, and there would be no opportunity for the ending of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for some individuals, even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>trifling bad deed</b></a> can lead them to rebirth in hell, while for others, the same deed might only be experienced as a result in the present life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>grain of salt</b></a> thrown into a small cup of water to show how a minor bad action can make a person’s experience completely "salty" or painful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a grain of salt is thrown into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>Ganges River</b></a>, the vast amount of water prevents the river from becoming salty or undrinkable, just as a developed person is not overwhelmed by a minor bad deed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an undeveloped person as someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>undeveloped in body</b></a>, virtue, mind, and wisdom, making them susceptible to severe consequences from even small mistakes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>magnanimous person</b></a> (mahatto) as one who has developed their body, virtue, mind, and wisdom, allowing them to dwell in an immeasurable state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that for a person who is "limited" and of "small character," a small evil deed is enough to bring about <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>rebirth in hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person of great character to a large mass of water, explaining that for such a person, the result of a small bad deed is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>felt in the present</b></a> and hardly appears to be anything at all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>poor man</b></a> being imprisoned for stealing even half a kahāpaṇa coin to illustrate how those with little merit suffer greatly for small offenses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>rich and wealthy</b></a> person might not be imprisoned for stealing half a coin, just as a spiritually wealthy person is not dragged down by a minor ethical lapse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>sheep-butcher</b></a> who has the power to kill or bind a poor man for stealing a sheep, but lacks the power to do so if the thief is a king or a royal minister? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>undeveloped in virtue</b></a> (abhāvitasīlo) makes a person's heart small and limited, leading to greater suffering from their kammic results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>undeveloped in mind</b></a> (abhāvitacitto) lacks the internal space to dilute the impact of their past unwholesome actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized an undeveloped person as someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>small-hearted</b></a> (paritto) and dwells in misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a developed practitioner as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>dwelling immeasurably</b></a> (appamāṇavihārī), which allows them to transcend the heavy results of minor bad deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if results were strictly one-to-one with actions, there would be no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>opportunity</b></a> for anyone to rightly make an end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that for a person with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>undeveloped wisdom</b></a> (abhāvitapañño), even a tiny bad deed can be the cause of their downfall into a state of woe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>result felt in the present</b></a> (diṭṭhadhamma-vedanīyaṃ) to describe how a great person experiences the ripening of minor past unwholesome kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a person of great character, the result of a small bad deed is so minor that it <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>hardly appears</b></a> at all in their experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the spiritual development of a person to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>great mass of water</b></a>, which cannot be made undrinkable by a single lump of salt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that "as a person experiences the result" of a deed is the correct way to view <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>kamma</b></a>, rather than a rigid view that ignores the person's mental development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>undeveloped in body</b></a> is "limited" and "small-hearted," making them easily overwhelmed by the consequences of their actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>king or royal minister</b></a> to show that social or spiritual "wealth" changes how a person is treated for the same offense compared to someone who is poor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for some, a debt of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>hundred kahāpaṇas</b></a> does not lead to imprisonment, while for the poor, even a fraction of that amount does? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> is lived for the purpose of making an end of suffering, which is only possible if kamma results can be modified by spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person of limited character as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>dwelling in misery</b></a> (appadukkhavihārī), implying that they are constantly vulnerable to even small negative occurrences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>developed body</b></a>, mind, virtue, and wisdom are the four pillars that allow a person to handle the ripening of past unwholesome actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a rigid view of kamma—believing results are exactly equal to deeds—leaves no room for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>rightly making an end of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's analogy of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>cup of water</b></a> shows that the environment (one's mental and spiritual state) is what determines how "salty" or painful a deed's result will be? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato"><b>magnanimous</b></a> (mahatto) has a vast internal capacity that prevents small unwholesome results from leading them to a bad destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.100/en/sujato">AN 3.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three distinct types of failure that lead to a person's decline, specifically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>failure in virtue</b></a>, failure in mind, and failure in view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are three great accomplishments a practitioner should strive for, which are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in virtue</b></a>, accomplishment in mind, and accomplishment in view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>failure in virtue</b></a> is characterized by bodily actions such as destroying life, taking what is not given, and engaging in sexual misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>failure in virtue</b></a> also includes four types of harmful speech: false speech, divisive speech, harsh speech, and idle chatter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>failure in mind</b></a> as the state of being covetous and possessing a mind of ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>failure in view</b></a> as holding the wrong belief that there is no fruit or result of good and bad deeds and that giving has no efficacy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>failure in view</b></a> mistakenly believes there is no "this world," no "other world," and that one's mother and father have no special moral significance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> includes denying the existence of beings who are reborn spontaneously and denying that there are ascetics who have directly known and realized this world and the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that by reason of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>failure in virtue</b></a>, beings are reborn after death in a state of misery, a bad destination, the nether world, or hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>failure in mind</b></a>, characterized by covetousness and ill will, is a direct cause for rebirth in a state of misery or hell upon the dissolution of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>failure in view</b></a> as a primary reason why beings, after death, find themselves reborn in a bad destination or the nether world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in virtue</b></a> as the practice of abstaining from destroying life, taking what is not given, and engaging in sexual misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in virtue</b></a> involves the discipline of abstaining from false speech, divisive speech, harsh speech, and idle chatter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in mind</b></a> as the state of being uncovetous and possessing a mind free from ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in view</b></a> is the right view that there is fruit and result of good and bad deeds and that acts of giving are meaningful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> as the understanding that there is this world and another world, that parents have moral significance, and that beings are reborn spontaneously? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in view</b></a> as recognizing that there are ascetics and brahmins who have practiced rightly and have directly realized this world and the next through their own knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that those who possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in virtue</b></a>, mind, or view are reborn after death in a good destination, even in a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.117/en/sujato">AN 3.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato"><b>clouds</b></a> and used them as a direct analogy to describe four distinct types of persons found in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato">AN 4.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is a speaker but not a doer is comparable to a cloud that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato"><b>thunders but does not rain</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato">AN 4.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is a doer but not a speaker as being similar to a cloud that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato"><b>rains but does not thunder</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato">AN 4.101</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a type of person who is neither a speaker nor a doer, likening them to a cloud that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato"><b>neither thunders nor rains</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato">AN 4.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is both a speaker and a doer is equivalent to a cloud that both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato"><b>thunders and rains</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato">AN 4.101</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically defined the person who thunders but does not rain as someone who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato"><b>speaker but not a doer</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato">AN 4.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato"><b>doer but not a speaker</b></a> is categorized as one who "rains but does not thunder"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato">AN 4.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato"><b>neither a speaker nor a doer</b></a> exists in the world, mirroring the behavior of a cloud that neither thunders nor rains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato">AN 4.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the most balanced type of person as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato"><b>both a speaker and a doer</b></a>, just as some clouds both thunder and rain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato">AN 4.101</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha addressed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato"><b>monks</b></a> at Sāvatthī to explain how individuals in the world can be understood through the imagery of four types of clouds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato">AN 4.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the Pāli term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato"><b>valāhaka</b></a> to refer to the clouds in his analogy for the four different types of persons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.101/en/sujato">AN 4.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda reported to the Buddha that a monk named Bāhiya, a co-resident of Venerable Anuruddha, was completely committed to causing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>schism in the Sangha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Ānanda, despite Bāhiya’s attempts to divide the community, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Anuruddha</b></a> did not consider it worth his while to speak even a single word to intervene in the matter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that it is actually Venerable Ānanda, along with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>Sāriputta and Moggallāna</b></a>, who are typically responsible for settling any legal issues and disputes that arise within the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>four reasons</b></a> or motives why a "bad monk" (pāpabhikkhu) actually finds delight and satisfaction in causing a schism within the monastic community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>immoral monk</b></a> of bad character delights in schism because he knows a united Sangha would expel him, whereas a divided one might not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a corrupt monk who seeks to divide the order as being "inner-festering" and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>rotten within</b></a>, despite his outward claim to be a true contemplative? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>concealed actions</b></a> and suspicious behavior favors a schism because he believes his misconduct will go unpunished if the community remains in conflict? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk who holds a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> delights in a schism because he realizes that a harmonious and united Sangha would correctly identify his errors and expel him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who possesses views held with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>dogmatic adherence</b></a> (antaggāhikāya diṭṭhiyā) actively seeks the division of the Sangha to protect his own position? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a monk who makes his living through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>wrong livelihood</b></a> prefers a divided community, as he knows a united Sangha would never tolerate such behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a bad monk may be motivated by being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>desirous of gain</b></a>, honor, and renown, leading him to believe that a schism will better serve his personal ambitions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a corrupt monk understands a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>united Sangha</b></a> will not honor, respect, or provide offerings to him, which is why he delights in the order's division? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a monk seeking personal fame believes that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>divided groups</b></a> within the Sangha will be more likely to provide him with the honor and worship he craves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a monk who strives for schism as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>not a contemplative</b></a> (assamaṇo) even though he claims to be one, hiding his true impure nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato"><b>suspicious conduct</b></a> (saṅkassarasamācāro) is specifically drawn to creating factions in the monastic community to avoid being held accountable by the collective whole? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.243/en/sujato">AN 4.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>delicate ascetic</b></a> among ascetics as a monk who possesses five specific qualities related to his requisites, his relationships, his health, and his meditative attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one quality of a "delicate ascetic" is that he mostly receives and uses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>robes</b></a> when he is specifically invited to accept them, and only rarely when he is not invited? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is considered a delicate ascetic if he primarily consumes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> that has been offered upon invitation, rather than food obtained without a specific request? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a delicate ascetic as a monk who mostly uses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>lodgings</b></a> (resting places) when he has been invited to do so, and only rarely uses them without such an invitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who mostly accepts <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>medicinal requisites</b></a> for use in sickness upon invitation, rather than uninvited, fulfills one of the conditions for being called a delicate ascetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a delicate ascetic is one whose companions in the holy life treat him mostly with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>agreeable bodily actions</b></a> and very rarely with disagreeable ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk is a delicate ascetic when his fellow monks interact with him predominantly through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>agreeable verbal actions</b></a>, showing him respect and kindness in their speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a delicate ascetic is one towards whom his companions mostly maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>agreeable mental actions</b></a>, harboring thoughts of goodwill and kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that a delicate ascetic is a monk to whom people mostly present <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>agreeable offerings</b></a> and rarely present those that are disagreeable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a delicate ascetic as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>seldom ill</b></a>, rarely experiencing physical afflictions arising from bile, phlegm, wind, or environmental changes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a delicate ascetic is a monk who does not often suffer from painful feelings produced by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>kamma results</b></a> or improper care? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a delicate ascetic as someone who can obtain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a>—which are high meditative states—whenever he wants, without any difficulty or trouble? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the four jhanas as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwellings</b></a> in this very life that a delicate ascetic is able to attain with ease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a delicate ascetic, through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>, realizes and dwells in the taintless liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that if anyone could be truly and rightly called a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>delicate ascetic among ascetics</b></a>, it would be he himself, because he embodies all the requisite qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that he personally experiences the ease of a delicate ascetic, as his fellow monks treat him mostly with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato"><b>agreeable actions</b></a> of body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.104/en/sujato">AN 5.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the removal of gross mental impurities to how a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>gold washer</b></a> first washes away coarse dust, sand, and gravel from gold ore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>gross impurities</b></a> that a monk intent on the higher mind must first abandon and dispel? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once gross misconduct is abandoned, a monk must deal with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>moderate impurities</b></a> of the mind, which include unwholesome thoughts of sensuality, ill-will, and harmfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, even after dispelling sensual thoughts, a monk still faces <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>subtle impurities</b></a> such as thoughts regarding his relatives, his country, and his reputation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a monk's mind is cleared of social and personal distractions, only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of Dhamma</b></a> remain, leading toward a unified state of concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>goldsmith</b></a> smelting gold in a crucible to describe how a monk refines their mind until it becomes radiant, malleable, and free of dross? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once the mind is fully purged of its defilements, it becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>malleable and wieldy</b></a>, allowing a person to direct it toward any state that can be realized by direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a refined mind to gold that a goldsmith can use to create any kind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>ornament</b></a>, such as a gold plate, earrings, a necklace, or a gold garland? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a monk with a concentrated mind can experience various <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>supernormal powers</b></a>, such as appearing as many persons and then returning to being one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who has mastered concentration can pass through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>walls and mountains</b></a> without obstruction, as if he were moving through empty space? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that a monk who has developed his mind can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>walk on water</b></a> without sinking, just as if he were walking on solid ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a level of psychic power where a monk can reach out and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>touch the sun</b></a> and moon with his hand, despite their great power and might? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that with a purified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>divine ear</b></a>, a monk can hear both divine and human sounds, whether they are far or near? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a monk can use his mind to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>know the minds</b></a> of others, discerning whether another person's mind is affected by lust, hatred, or delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ability of a practitioner to discern with their own mind whether another person's mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>concentrated or scattered</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>recollect past lives</b></a> in great detail, including his names, families, and experiences of pleasure and pain across many eons of world-contraction and expansion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> as a power that allows one to see beings passing away and being reborn according to their kamma, whether they are beautiful or ugly, happy or miserable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> as the ultimate spiritual realization where one enters and dwells in the taintless liberation of mind and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between a concentration maintained by forceful restraint and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>serene concentration</b></a> that is unified and based on the inner stillness of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who has cleared his mind of impurities becomes capable of realizing any state that can be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato"><b>known by direct knowledge</b></a>, provided the necessary conditions are met? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.101/en/sujato">AN 3.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three distinct types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>purification</b></a>, which consist of purity of body, purity of speech, and purity of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>purity of body</b></a> as a practitioner abstaining from the destruction of life, from taking what is not given, and from incelibacy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an essential part of bodily purification is the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from killing</b></a> living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from stealing</b></a>, or taking what is not given, is a fundamental component of achieving purity of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>purity of speech</b></a> is attained when a practitioner abstains from false speech, malicious speech, harsh speech, and idle chatter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the avoidance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>malicious speech</b></a> as one of the core requirements for a person to be considered pure in their verbal conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>purity of mind</b></a> involves the internal recognition of whether sensual desire is present or absent within oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>mental purification</b></a>, one must understand how sensual desire arises, how it is abandoned, and how to prevent it from arising again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>purity of mind</b></a> by clearly knowing when ill will is present in the heart and knowing the method for its abandonment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the clear comprehension of the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a>—and the knowledge of how to remove this hindrance—as a vital part of mental purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that part of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>mental purification</b></a> is being aware of restlessness and remorse when they occur and understanding how they can be prevented from arising in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that achieving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>purity of mind</b></a> requires a person to know if doubt is present within them and to understand how that doubt is eventually abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>taintless</b></a> to describe the ultimate state of a mind that has been fully purified and is free from defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to a person who is pure in body, speech, and mind as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>washed away evil</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's instructions on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>mental purification</b></a> focus on the systematic observation of the five hindrances: sensual desire, ill will, sloth and torpor, restlessness and remorse, and doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from harsh speech</b></a> is a necessary component for the complete purification of one's verbal actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is accomplished in all three types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato"><b>purification</b></a> as someone who is truly pure and free from mental influxes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.121/en/sujato">AN 3.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared four types of people found in the world to four distinct types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>pools of water</b></a>, categorized by their actual depth and their outward appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is "shallow but appears deep" as someone who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>inspiring behavior</b></a> in their movements and dress, yet lacks a true understanding of the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person may have a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>graceful deportment</b></a> when looking ahead, looking aside, or wearing their robes, but still be considered spiritually "shallow" if they do not understand the nature of suffering as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a type of person who is "deep but appears shallow" because they do not exhibit <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>inspiring conduct</b></a> in their physical actions, yet they have realized the reality of the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person can be spiritually "deep" if they understand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>as it really is</b></a> the origin of suffering, its cessation, and the path leading to its end, even if their external appearance is not traditionally impressive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the "shallow and appearing shallow" person as one who both lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>graceful behavior</b></a> in their daily activities and fails to understand the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the most spiritually excellent person as being "deep and appearing deep," as they both maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>inspiring deportment</b></a> and possess a profound understanding of the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the specific example of how a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>wearing robes and carrying a bowl</b></a> as a measure of whether they appear spiritually "deep" to those observing them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who lacks insight into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> is like a shallow pool of water, regardless of how composed or impressive they may look on the outside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s analogy of a pool that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>deep and appearing deep</b></a> refers to an individual who is both internally wise and externally refined in their conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>outward appearance</b></a> in going forward, returning, bending, or stretching can be "inspiring" even if they have no genuine spiritual realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>shallow appearance</b></a> applies to those who do not carry themselves with grace or confidence-inspiring behavior in their physical movements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that understanding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>way leading to the cessation of suffering</b></a> is what constitutes the "depth" of a person's character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>deep appearance</b></a> to describe the external visual quality of someone who is graceful and composed in their physical posture and attire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>four types of persons</b></a> currently existing in the world, categorized by the interplay between their external deportment and their internal wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato"><b>internal wisdom</b></a> regarding the reality of suffering is the essential factor that makes a person truly "deep," even if they appear "shallow" to the casual observer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.104/en/sujato">AN 4.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>four fears of offenses</b></a> that a monk or nun should maintain to ensure they either avoid committing them or make proper amends according to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the danger of committing a grave Pārājika offense to a criminal who is bound, paraded through the streets, and taken out the south gate to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>beheaded</b></a> by the king's men? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk or nun has a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>strong perception of fear</b></a> regarding Pārājika offenses, they will refrain from such acts or, if already committed, will make amends according to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the image of a man wearing black clothes and carrying a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>club on his shoulder</b></a> while confessing his bad deeds to a crowd to represent the fear one should have toward Saṅghādisesa offenses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the fear of Pācittiya offenses by describing a man with disheveled hair carrying a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>bag of ashes</b></a> on his shoulder as he confesses his wrongdoings to a large group of people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having a strong sense of fear regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>Pāṭidesanīya offenses</b></a>—offenses that must be acknowledged—ensures that a practitioner will properly rectify any such mistakes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a criminal being bound with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>strong rope</b></a> and having his head shaved before being paraded through the streets to demonstrate the severe social and legal consequences of unwholesome actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a practitioner has already fallen into a Saṅghādisesa offense, a strong sense of fear allows them to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>make amends</b></a> according to the Dhamma rather than concealing the mistake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that in ancient times, a criminal sentenced to death would be paraded from square to square and eventually taken out through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>south gate</b></a> of the city for execution? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a bystander observing a criminal's execution and resolving never to commit such <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>evil deeds</b></a> as an analogy for how a monk or nun should view the danger of monastic offenses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of someone wearing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>black clothes</b></a> and having disheveled hair to represent the state of a person who has committed a blameworthy act and is seeking to appease those they have offended? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk or nun who maintains a strong fear of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>Saṅghādisesa offenses</b></a> will act in a way that leads to the restoration of their purity if they happen to breach these rules? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for those with a strong fear of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>Pācittiya offenses</b></a>, the expectation is that they will either not commit them or will otherwise confess and rectify them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a king who, after being shown a criminal, orders that the man be bound with his arms behind his back in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>tight binding</b></a> before being taken to the place of execution? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a criminal being paraded from street to street and square to square to the sound of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>harsh-sounding drum</b></a> to illustrate the public shame associated with grave misdeeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person confessing a deed by saying they would do whatever is necessary so that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>venerable ones</b></a> might be satisfied with their restitution? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>mosalla</b></a> to describe a punishment involving a club, which served as a metaphor for the gravity and required penance for Saṅghādisesa offenses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the most severe category of monastic rules, known as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>Pārājika</b></a>, should be viewed with the same level of fear as one would view a crime deserving of capital punishment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha equated the fear of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>blameworthy deeds</b></a> (upavajja) to the specific category of monastic rules called Pāṭidesanīya, emphasizing the importance of admitting even minor faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the different levels of monastic discipline into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato"><b>four fears</b></a> to help practitioners maintain their conduct through a constant awareness of the internal and external consequences of their actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.244/en/sujato">AN 4.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five "dwellings in comfort," the first of which involves a monk maintaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness in bodily action</b></a> toward his fellow practitioners both in public and in private? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato">AN 5.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner dwells in comfort when they maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness in speech</b></a> toward their companions in the spiritual life, whether they are seen by others or are alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato">AN 5.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the ways a monk lives at ease is by establishing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness in mind</b></a> toward his fellow monks, manifesting this internal attitude both openly and in private? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato">AN 5.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the importance of possessing virtues that are unbroken, untarnished, and liberating, and that a monk dwells in comfort when he shares such <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato"><b>commonality of virtue</b></a> with his fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato">AN 5.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a "dwelling in comfort" includes possessing the noble view that leads to the complete destruction of suffering, and that a practitioner should live in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato"><b>commonality of view</b></a> with their companions in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato">AN 5.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying at Kosambi in Ghosita’s Park, the Venerable Ānanda approached him to ask specifically about how a monk might <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato"><b>dwell in comfort</b></a> while living within the community of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato">AN 5.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Ānanda that a monk can live comfortably in the Sangha if he is personally accomplished in virtue but does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato"><b>harass others</b></a> regarding their own level of higher virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato">AN 5.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk finds comfort in the community when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato"><b>observant of himself</b></a> and his own conduct, rather than being observant of the faults or actions of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato">AN 5.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk can dwell at ease even if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato"><b>unknown and unrecognized</b></a> by others, provided he does not become anxious or distressed by his lack of fame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato">AN 5.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the attainment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a>—which he described as "pleasant dwellings in this very life"—as a key requirement for a monk to live with complete comfort in the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato">AN 5.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Ānanda that a monk who dwells in comfort is one who can obtain the four meditative absorptions (jhanas) <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato"><b>whenever he wishes</b></a>, without difficulty or trouble? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato">AN 5.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the ultimate dwelling in comfort as the state where a monk, through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>, enters and dwells in the stainless liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato">AN 5.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato"><b>no higher dwelling</b></a> or more sublime state of comfort than the one achieved by a monk who has realized the end of all mental taints through direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato">AN 5.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that sharing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato"><b>noble view</b></a>—one that is truly liberating and leads to the end of suffering—is essential for the communal harmony and comfort of those living the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato">AN 5.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha differentiated between practitioners by noting that one dwells in comfort when they possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato"><b>unbroken virtues</b></a> that are praised by the wise and are conducive to concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato">AN 5.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Ānanda asked if there was "another way" to dwell in comfort, the Buddha progressively added more advanced qualities, eventually culminating in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato"><b>full liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato">AN 5.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwellings</b></a> (diṭṭhadhammasukhavihāra) to describe the four jhanas, which provide immediate mental ease for a monk in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato">AN 5.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered it a specific form of progress when a monk remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato"><b>unfazed by obscurity</b></a>, not trembling or worrying because he is not famous among his peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato">AN 5.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s criteria for dwelling in comfort in the Sangha includes a monk being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato"><b>accomplished in virtue</b></a> himself, emphasizing personal integrity as the foundational layer of communal ease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.106/en/sujato">AN 5.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching on "dwellings in comfort" highlights that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato"><b>mutual respect</b></a>, expressed through kindness in body, speech, and mind, is what allows a community of practitioners to live together harmoniously? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.105/en/sujato">AN 5.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk devoted to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>higher mind</b></a> must periodically attend to three specific mental themes: the sign of concentration, the sign of exertion, and the sign of equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a practitioner focuses exclusively on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>sign of concentration</b></a>, their mind is likely to lapse into sloth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, focusing solely on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>sign of exertion</b></a> without balance will cause the mind to be overcome by restlessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if a monk attends only to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>sign of equanimity</b></a>, his mind will not be rightly concentrated for the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the development of the higher mind to a skilled <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>goldsmith</b></a> who must carefully balance heat, water, and observation to refine gold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a goldsmith blowing on a furnace to represent the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>mental exertion</b></a> required to refine the mind from its impurities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the danger of over-exertion by noting that just as gold will burn if it is only blown upon, the mind will become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>restless</b></a> if it is only pushed with effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened the sign of concentration to a goldsmith sprinkling gold with water, which serves to cool and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>steady</b></a> the process? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a goldsmith only sprinkles water on the gold, it will be extinguished, just as the mind becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>slothful</b></a> if one focuses only on concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk balances concentration, exertion, and equanimity, his mind becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>malleable</b></a> and wieldy for spiritual work? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a mind that has been properly balanced among the three signs becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>luminous</b></a> and is no longer brittle or easily broken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once the mind is refined and steady, it can be successfully directed toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk with a refined mind to a goldsmith who can create any kind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>ornament</b></a>, such as a ring or a necklace, from purified gold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind made "wieldy" through balanced meditation can achieve various forms of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a>, including the recollection of past lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the higher mind attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> only when the signs of concentration, exertion, and equanimity are attended to "from time to time"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>pabhassara</b></a> to describe the radiant or luminous quality of a mind that has reached a state of balanced concentration and purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a goldsmith only looks on at the gold with equanimity, it will not reach <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>right perfection</b></a>, just as a mind without exertion or concentration will not ripen spiritually? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who masters the balance of the three mental signs becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>capable</b></a> of witnessing and realizing any state that is attainable through higher knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>restlessness</b></a> (uddhacca) as the specific consequence of over-emphasizing mental exertion in one's practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ultimate goal of refining the mind through these three signs is to gain the ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato"><b>realize</b></a> for oneself the liberation of the mind through wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.102/en/sujato">AN 3.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>Baliharaṇa Woods</b></a> at Kusināra when he taught his followers about the conditions that make an offering to a monk result in great fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a monk is served fine food by a householder and then hopes to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>served such food</b></a> again in the future, his state of mind is considered negligent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a gift given to a monk who consumes his almsfood with greed, infatuation, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>obsession</b></a> does not result in great fruit for the donor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a negligent monk as one who eats his meal without seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> of attachment or possessing the wisdom regarding the escape from such bondages? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a monk is living negligently if he entertains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of sensual desire</b></a> while receiving and consuming a meal provided by a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that when a monk entertains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of ill will</b></a> or cruelty during his meal, the offering made to him by a layperson does not have great fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>negligent</b></a> in his mental discipline causes a donor's gift to lack great fruitfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a gift given to a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>diligent</b></a> and lives heedfully results in great fruit for the giver? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a diligent monk as one who, when served <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>fine food</b></a>, does not wish or hope for such luxurious meals to be offered to him in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a monk who consumes almsfood without being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>greedy</b></a> or infatuated is considered to be practicing with diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted a monk is living heedfully when he eats his food while clearly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>seeing the danger</b></a> of attachment and possessing wisdom regarding the escape from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a diligent monk entertains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of renunciation</b></a> even while he is being served a delicious meal by a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an offering becomes highly fruitful when the recipient monk maintains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of non-ill will</b></a> and non-cruelty during the meal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the primary reason a gift to a specific monk has great fruit is because that monk dwells as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>heedful</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ideal way for a monk to receive a meal is to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>not obsessed</b></a> with the food, thereby ensuring the donor's generosity is most effective? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even when a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato"><b>householder's son</b></a> personally serves a monk fine food, the ultimate benefit of that act depends on the monk's internal state of diligence and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.123/en/sujato">AN 3.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are four types of individuals in the world who can be compared to four specific types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>mangoes</b></a> based on their outward appearance and their internal spiritual state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is spiritually "unripe" but appears "ripe" as someone who has an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>inspiring outward appearance</b></a> in their movements and how they wear their robes, yet does not truly understand the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who understands the truth of suffering and the path to its cessation, even if they lack an inspiring outward manner in their physical deportment, is like a mango that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>ripe but looks unripe</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized an individual who neither possesses an inspiring physical presence nor understands the nature of suffering as being comparable to a mango that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>unripe and looks unripe</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the ideal individual as one who is like a mango that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>ripe and looks ripe</b></a>, meaning they possess both a graceful, inspiring outward demeanor and a profound internal understanding of the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defines "inspiring" behavior (<i>pāsādika</i>) in this context as being graceful and composed when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>going forward and returning</b></a>, looking ahead and aside, and bending or stretching one's limbs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person’s internal "ripeness" is determined by whether they understand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>as it really is</b></a> the truth of suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the way leading to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that some practitioners may be perfectly disciplined in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>carrying their outer robe</b></a> and bowl, yet still be considered "unripe" if they lack the realization of the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if a person is not particularly inspiring in their physical actions—such as how they look around or move—they are considered "ripe" if they have gained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>true insight</b></a> into the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a mango's color versus its actual fruit quality to warn that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>outward appearance</b></a> can often be a deceptive indicator of a person's actual spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Buddha's comparison, the "color" of the mango represents the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>external deportment</b></a> of a person, while the actual "ripeness" of the fruit represents their internal realization of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described four specific combinations of traits in people, just as there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>four types of mangoes</b></a>: those that look ripe but are unripe, those that look unripe but are ripe, those that are unripe both ways, and those that are ripe both ways? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a person who does not understand "This is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a>" is spiritually "unripe," regardless of how much poise they display in their public behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the spiritual path involves more than just aesthetics, as a person who looks "unripe" due to uninspiring movements can still be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato"><b>spiritually mature</b></a> if they have realized the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.105/en/sujato">AN 4.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the spiritual life (<i>brahmacariya</i>) as having training as its benefit, wisdom as its supreme goal, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato"><b>liberation as its essence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato">AN 4.245</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that training in higher conduct was prescribed specifically for the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato"><b>inspiring the uninspired</b></a> and increasing the faith of those who already have it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato">AN 4.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a practitioner should undertake the rules of training in a way that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato"><b>unbroken and untattered</b></a>, being free from spots or blotches in their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato">AN 4.245</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the training in the fundamental spiritual life is prescribed for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato"><b>total destruction of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato">AN 4.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the spiritual life is "wisdom-surpassing" when the practitioner has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato"><b>thoroughly examined the teachings</b></a> with wisdom for the sake of ending suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato">AN 4.245</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined liberation as the essence of the spiritual life, which is achieved when the teachings for the destruction of suffering are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato"><b>touched by release</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato">AN 4.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the spiritual life is "governed by mindfulness" when a person maintains well-established <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato"><b>internal mindfulness</b></a> regarding their progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato">AN 4.245</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that mindfulness should be used to recognize when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato"><b>training in conduct</b></a> is incomplete, so that one may strive to fulfill it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato">AN 4.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to use mindfulness to ensure that even when a training is fulfilled, they continue to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato"><b>support that training</b></a> with wisdom in various circumstances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato">AN 4.245</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.248/en/sujato"><b>associating with superior persons</b></a> (<i>sappurisasaṃsevo</i>) as the first of four essential factors that lead to the growth of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.248/en/sujato">AN 4.248</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.248/en/sujato"><b>hearing the true Dhamma</b></a> as a critical requirement for a practitioner seeking the development of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.248/en/sujato">AN 4.248</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.248/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> (<i>yoniso manasikāra</i>) is one of the four primary qualities that conduce to the increase of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.248/en/sujato">AN 4.248</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.248/en/sujato"><b>practice in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a> as the final factor in a fourfold path leading to the growth of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.248/en/sujato">AN 4.248</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that mindfulness acts as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato"><b>governing factor</b></a> when it helps a practitioner decide to examine a teaching they have not yet investigated with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato">AN 4.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the spiritual life is lived with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato"><b>training as its benefit</b></a> when one consistently trains in the rules prescribed for the destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato">AN 4.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should maintain internal mindfulness to determine if they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato"><b>attained liberation</b></a> in regard to specific teachings, or if more effort is needed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.245/en/sujato">AN 4.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the four factors of associating with superior persons, hearing the true Dhamma, careful attention, and right practice are the specific qualities that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.248/en/sujato"><b>lead to wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.248/en/sujato">AN 4.248</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.109/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a>, dwelling restrained by the Pātimokkha and seeing danger in the slightest faults, is one who can successfully dwell in all four directions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.109/en/sujato">AN 5.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as "of the four quarters" if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.109/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a> and has accumulated teachings that are good in the beginning, middle, and end, and has penetrated them with right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.109/en/sujato">AN 5.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.109/en/sujato"><b>contentment</b></a> with any kind of robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicinal requisites as a fundamental quality for a monk to be at home wherever he goes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.109/en/sujato">AN 5.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is fit to travel in any direction if he can obtain the four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.109/en/sujato"><b>meditative absorptions</b></a> (jhanas) at will, without any difficulty or trouble? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.109/en/sujato">AN 5.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk truly becomes a "dweller of the four quarters" when he realizes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.109/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> and dwells in the stainless liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.109/en/sujato">AN 5.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who visits lay families becomes disliked and unappealing if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.111/en/sujato"><b>intimate without familiarity</b></a>, acting too close to people he does not actually know well? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.111/en/sujato">AN 5.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who visits families is considered uninspiring if he tries to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.111/en/sujato"><b>exercise authority</b></a> over matters or items that are not within his rightful control? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.111/en/sujato">AN 5.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.111/en/sujato"><b>whispering in the ear</b></a> is one of the specific behaviors that causes a monk to be viewed with disfavor and lack of respect by the families he visits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.111/en/sujato">AN 5.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted a monk who visits lay families becomes an object of dislike if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.111/en/sujato"><b>excessively asking</b></a> for things, rather than being modest in his needs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.111/en/sujato">AN 5.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who visits families is loved and respected if he avoids <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.111/en/sujato"><b>improper associations</b></a> and maintains a dignified distance from laypeople? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.111/en/sujato">AN 5.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who visits families becomes dear and appealing to them when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.111/en/sujato"><b>not whispering</b></a> and avoids trying to control things that are not his? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.111/en/sujato">AN 5.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an attendant monk (pacchāsamaṇa) should not be selected if he walks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.112/en/sujato"><b>too far away</b></a> or too close behind his senior monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.112/en/sujato">AN 5.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is considered unsuitable as an attendant if he fails to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.112/en/sujato"><b>take the bowl</b></a> and robes belonging to his senior when the situation requires it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.112/en/sujato">AN 5.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a proper attendant monk should have the presence of mind to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.112/en/sujato"><b>stop his senior</b></a> if the senior begins to speak in a way that borders on a monastic offense? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.112/en/sujato">AN 5.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk should not be taken as an attendant if he habitually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.112/en/sujato"><b>interrupts the speech</b></a> of his senior instead of waiting for a proper gap in the conversation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.112/en/sujato">AN 5.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a monk is fit to serve as an attendant if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.112/en/sujato"><b>wise and intelligent</b></a> and does not walk at an inappropriate distance from his senior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.112/en/sujato">AN 5.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his enlightenment, while he was still an unawakened bodhisatta, the Buddha sought to understand what constitutes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato"><b>gratification</b></a>, the danger, and the escape in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato">AN 3.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato"><b>gratification in the world</b></a> as the pleasure and joy that arise dependent on the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato">AN 3.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato"><b>danger in the world</b></a> is found in the fact that the world is impermanent, suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato">AN 3.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato"><b>escape from the world</b></a> as the removal and abandonment of desire and lust for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato">AN 3.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared he did not claim to have attained unsurpassed perfect enlightenment until he had directly known the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato"><b>gratification, danger, and escape</b></a> regarding the world as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato">AN 3.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once he understood the nature of the world, the knowledge and vision arose in him that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato"><b>liberation is unshakable</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato">AN 3.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon reaching full awakening, the Buddha realized that he had reached his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato"><b>final birth</b></a> and that there would be no more future wandering in rebirth for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.103/en/sujato">AN 3.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly mentioned that he went in search of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato"><b>gratification of the world</b></a> and successfully discovered exactly what it was? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato">AN 3.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that whatever gratification there is to be found in the world has been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato"><b>clearly seen by him</b></a> with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato">AN 3.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha conducted a specific search for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato"><b>danger of the world</b></a> and found the true extent of that danger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato">AN 3.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that all the danger present in the world has been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato"><b>thoroughly investigated</b></a> and seen clearly by him through his own wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato">AN 3.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he went on a search for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato"><b>escape from the world</b></a> and attained the discovery of that escape? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato">AN 3.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato"><b>clearly seen with wisdom</b></a> the full extent of the escape that exists for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato">AN 3.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha only made his claim of enlightenment to the world—including its devas, Maras, and Brahmas—after he had attained a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge</b></a> of the world's gratification, danger, and escape? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.104/en/sujato">AN 3.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he finds it uncomfortable even to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato"><b>think about a direction</b></a> where monks are living in a state of quarreling, strife, and dispute, let alone travel there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato">AN 3.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, monks who are engaged in bitter arguments and wounding each other with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato"><b>weapons of the mouth</b></a> are considered to have abandoned the three wholesome qualities of renunciation, non-ill will, and non-harming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato">AN 3.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the habitual cultivation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato"><b>sensual thoughts</b></a>, thoughts of ill will, and thoughts of harming as the primary reasons why a community of monks falls into discord? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato">AN 3.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is eager to visit and even just think about a region where he knows the monks are living in concord and looking at each other with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato"><b>eyes of affection</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato">AN 3.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the beautiful metaphor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato"><b>milk and water</b></a> blending together to describe the ideal relationship between monks who live together in harmony and without dispute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato">AN 3.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that monks who live together peacefully have successfully cultivated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato"><b>thought of renunciation</b></a>, as well as thoughts of non-ill will and non-harming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato">AN 3.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha expressed that when monks abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of harming</b></a> and instead live in concord, it makes that entire location pleasant for him to consider? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato">AN 3.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha observes a harmonious community, he takes it as a certain sign that those practitioners have abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of ill will</b></a> and have instead made the thought of non-ill will abundant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.124/en/sujato">AN 3.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared he teaches the Dhamma only based on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> (abhiññā), and never without such direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato">AN 3.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that his teaching of the Dhamma is always provided with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato"><b>causal basis</b></a> (sanidāna), rather than being delivered without a foundation or source? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato">AN 3.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his method of teaching the Dhamma as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato"><b>exhibiting marvels</b></a> (sappāṭihāriya), meaning it is convincing and effective, rather than being unconvincing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato">AN 3.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because the Buddha teaches with direct knowledge and a causal basis, he asserts that his advice and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato"><b>instruction should be followed</b></a> by his disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato">AN 3.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to feel great joy and satisfaction in the fact that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma is well expounded</b></a> by the Blessed One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato">AN 3.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner should be satisfied and happy because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato"><b>Sangha is practicing well</b></a>, following the path laid out by the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato">AN 3.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that at the conclusion of the Buddha's powerful discourse at the Gotamaka Shrine, the entire <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato"><b>thousandfold world system</b></a> shook in response to his words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato">AN 3.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once delivered a teaching specifically at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato"><b>Gotamaka Shrine</b></a> in Vesālī to explain why his instructions are worthy of being followed with complete confidence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.125/en/sujato">AN 3.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato"><b>four types of persons</b></a> existing in the world by comparing their behavior to four specific kinds of oxen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato">AN 4.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato"><b>harasses their own assembly</b></a> but not others as being like an ox that is fierce toward its own herd but peaceful toward another's? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato">AN 4.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, there is a type of person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato"><b>harasses another's assembly</b></a> but not their own, which he compared to an ox that is fierce only toward a different herd? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato">AN 4.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato"><b>harasses both</b></a> their own assembly and the assemblies of others, likening them to an ox that is fierce toward both its own herd and foreign ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato">AN 4.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a peaceful individual who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato"><b>harasses neither</b></a> their own assembly nor the assembly of others, paralleling an ox that is not fierce toward any herd? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato">AN 4.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the Pali term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato"><b>balībadda</b></a> to refer to the oxen in his similes, which serve as metaphors for how different individuals interact with their own social circles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato">AN 4.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified individuals based on whether they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato"><b>fierce</b></a> toward their own group, toward external groups, toward both, or toward neither? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato">AN 4.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's category for an individual who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato"><b>harasser of their own circle</b></a> refers to someone who causes terror or distress specifically to those within their own assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato">AN 4.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato"><b>fierce toward others</b></a> but not their own group behaves like an ox that only shows aggression to those outside its own herd? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato">AN 4.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the word <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato"><b>sakaparisa</b></a> to denote one's "own assembly," which is the specific group that the first and third types of persons are said to harass? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato">AN 4.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato"><b>source of terror</b></a> to every group they encounter to an ox that attacks both its own and foreign herds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato">AN 4.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that these four distinct types of individuals are "found <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato"><b>existing in the world</b></a>," suggesting these behavioral patterns are recognizable human traits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato">AN 4.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the most desirable type of person among the four as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato"><b>not a harasser</b></a> of their own circle or of anyone else's? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.108/en/sujato">AN 4.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato"><b>ignoble search</b></a> as occurring when someone who is themselves subject to aging seeks out that which is also subject to aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato">AN 4.255</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato"><b>ignoble quest</b></a> as the act of searching for things subject to illness when one is personally subject to illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato">AN 4.255</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that searching for things subject to death, while being subject to death oneself, constitutes an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato"><b>ignoble search</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato">AN 4.255</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato"><b>ignoble quest</b></a> occurs when a person subject to defilement seeks out that which is also subject to defilement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato">AN 4.255</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato"><b>noble search</b></a> as when someone subject to aging recognizes the danger in it and seeks the unaging, supreme security from bondage, Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato">AN 4.255</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato"><b>noble quest</b></a> involves seeing the peril in illness and seeking the unailing, supreme security from bondage, Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato">AN 4.255</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato"><b>noble search</b></a> is undertaken by one who, being subject to death, perceives the danger in it and seeks the deathless, supreme security from bondage, Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato">AN 4.255</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato"><b>noble quest</b></a> as seeking the undefiled, supreme security from bondage, Nibbāna, after realizing the danger of being subject to defilement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato">AN 4.255</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Elder Mālukyaputta approached the Buddha to request a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato"><b>brief teaching</b></a> so that he could live alone, diligent and resolute in his meditation practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato">AN 4.257</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the elderly Mālukyaputta asked for a short teaching, the Buddha remarked on how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato"><b>old and aged</b></a> he was to be asking for a brief exhortation that is usually given to younger monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato">AN 4.257</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that craving arises in a monk because of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato"><b>robes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato">AN 4.257</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> as one of the four specific things that cause craving to arise in a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato">AN 4.257</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato"><b>lodgings</b></a> serve as a condition for the arising of craving in a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato">AN 4.257</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that craving can arise in a monk regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato"><b>future existence</b></a> or the states of success and failure (itibhavābhava)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato">AN 4.257</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a monk has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato"><b>abandoned craving</b></a> and cut it off at the root, he has made an end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato">AN 4.257</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who has destroyed craving as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato"><b>shattered the fetters</b></a> and brought suffering to an end through the right breakthrough of conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato">AN 4.257</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after receiving a brief exhortation from the Buddha, the Elder Mālukyaputta lived alone and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato"><b>diligent</b></a>, soon reaching the final goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato">AN 4.257</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Elder Mālukyaputta, through his own direct knowledge, became one of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato"><b>arahants</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato">AN 4.257</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching helped Mālukyaputta realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato"><b>supreme goal</b></a> of the spiritual life, for which people leave the home life for homelessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.257/en/sujato">AN 4.257</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined Nibbāna as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato"><b>supreme security</b></a> from bondage, which is uniquely characterized as being unaging, unailing, and deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.255/en/sujato">AN 4.255</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is considered incapable of entering and dwelling in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.113/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> if he is intolerant of forms, sounds, odors, tastes, and tactile objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.113/en/sujato">AN 5.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.113/en/sujato"><b>tolerant of forms</b></a> and other sensory experiences possesses one of the essential qualities to successfully enter and dwell in right concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.113/en/sujato">AN 5.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.113/en/sujato"><b>intolerance of sounds</b></a> as a specific hindrance that makes a practitioner unfit to attain the meditative state of right concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.113/en/sujato">AN 5.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.113/en/sujato"><b>tolerant of tactile objects</b></a>, along with smells and tastes, is a necessary factor for a monk to be capable of entering right concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.113/en/sujato">AN 5.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while staying at Andhakavinda, the Buddha instructed Ven. Ānanda that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato"><b>new monks</b></a> who have recently joined the Dhamma-Vinaya should be encouraged and established in five specific qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato">AN 5.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that those new to the monastic life should be established in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato"><b>restraint of the Pātimokkha</b></a>, which includes being restrained by the monastic code and possessing proper behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato">AN 5.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that newcomers to the monkhood should be trained to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato"><b>seeing fear in slight faults</b></a> while they undertake and practice the training rules? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato">AN 5.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that senior monks should encourage new practitioners to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato"><b>guarded sense doors</b></a>, remaining mindful and alert to protect their sensory intake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato">AN 5.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who have recently gone forth should maintain a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato"><b>guarded mind</b></a> through the continuous application of protective mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato">AN 5.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that new monks practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato"><b>limited speech</b></a> by placing boundaries on their talk to avoid excessive or unbeneficial conversation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato">AN 5.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Ven. Ānanda that new monks should be encouraged to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato"><b>forest dwellers</b></a>, utilizing the seclusion of the wilderness for their spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato">AN 5.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that seeking out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato"><b>remote lodgings</b></a> and jungle thickets is a form of bodily seclusion that newcomers should be settled into? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato">AN 5.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is vital to establish new monks in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> to ensure they are properly aligned with the fundamental principles of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato">AN 5.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that monks who have recently arrived in the Dhamma-Vinaya must be settled in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato"><b>right vision</b></a> as one of the five essential foundations for their training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.114/en/sujato">AN 5.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings only become enamored with the world because there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.105/en/sujato"><b>gratification</b></a> to be found in it, and if such satisfaction did not exist, they would not be attached to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.105/en/sujato">AN 3.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, beings would never become disillusioned with the world or seek detachment if there were no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.105/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> inherent within it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.105/en/sujato">AN 3.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the only reason beings are actually able to transcend this world is because there is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.105/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.105/en/sujato">AN 3.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that as long as beings do not understand gratification, danger, and escape as they really are, they cannot live with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.105/en/sujato"><b>mind that has transcended boundaries</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.105/en/sujato">AN 3.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha does not consider certain ascetics or brahmins to be true "ascetics among ascetics" if they fail to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.106/en/sujato"><b>gratification, danger, and escape</b></a> of the world as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.106/en/sujato">AN 3.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined true practitioners as those who, through their own direct knowledge, realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.106/en/sujato"><b>goal of asceticism</b></a> and the goal of brahminhood in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.106/en/sujato">AN 3.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the discipline of the Noble One, the Buddha described the act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.107/en/sujato"><b>singing</b></a> as a form of wailing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.107/en/sujato">AN 3.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.107/en/sujato"><b>dancing</b></a> is regarded as a form of madness within the training and discipline of a Noble One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.107/en/sujato">AN 3.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered excessive laughter that shows the teeth to be a sign of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.107/en/sujato"><b>childishness</b></a> in the discipline of the Noble One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.107/en/sujato">AN 3.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that when one feels delight in the Dhamma, it is sufficient to simply show a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.107/en/sujato"><b>smile</b></a> rather than laughing out loud? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.107/en/sujato">AN 3.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested one should essentially "tear down the bridge" regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.107/en/sujato"><b>singing and dancing</b></a> and instead be satisfied with the joy found in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.107/en/sujato">AN 3.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three specific activities for which there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.108/en/sujato"><b>satiety</b></a>, meaning one can never have enough of them through mere indulgence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.108/en/sujato">AN 3.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one can never reach a point of complete fulfillment or satisfaction through the constant indulgence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.108/en/sujato"><b>sleep</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.108/en/sujato">AN 3.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the drinking of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.108/en/sujato"><b>liquor and wine</b></a> as one of the three things that never brings a sense of being satisfied through indulgence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.108/en/sujato">AN 3.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that there is no end to the desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.108/en/sujato"><b>sexual intercourse</b></a> through its continued practice, as it is one of the three things that provides no lasting satiety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.108/en/sujato">AN 3.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings are only released and detached from the world when they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.105/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> of the world's gratification, danger, and escape as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.105/en/sujato">AN 3.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once warned a monk in the Deer Park at Isipatana not to make himself into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato"><b>carrion</b></a>, a state that attracts the metaphorical "flies" of unwholesome thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato">AN 3.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the term "carrion" in a spiritual sense as the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato"><b>covetousness</b></a> within a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato">AN 3.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> to the "stench of raw flesh" that emanates from a person who has made themselves into spiritual carrion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato">AN 3.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato"><b>evil unwholesome thoughts</b></a> are like flies that swarm and pursue a person who is spiritually foul and leaking with the stench of raw flesh? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato">AN 3.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed a monk with muddled mindfulness, loose sense faculties, and an agitated mind, and warned him that his lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato"><b>sense restraint</b></a> would inevitably attract unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato">AN 3.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk is unguarded in his eye and ear faculties, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato"><b>thoughts based on lust</b></a> will swarm him like flies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato">AN 3.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk who has made himself into carrion and is leaking with the stench of raw flesh remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato"><b>far from Nibbāna</b></a> and will only partake of distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato">AN 3.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "foolish and unwise" person as one who wanders through villages or forests without having attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato"><b>serenity of mind</b></a>, being constantly pursued by the "flies" of unwholesome thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato">AN 3.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that those who are accomplished in virtue and delight in the stillness of wisdom dwell happily once they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato"><b>destroyed the flies</b></a> of unwholesome thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato">AN 3.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s direct advice to a distracted monk was so powerful that it caused the monk to be immediately <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato"><b>deeply stirred</b></a> with a sense of spiritual urgency? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.128/en/sujato">AN 3.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato"><b>women</b></a> as one of three things that traditionally function in secrecy rather than openly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato">AN 3.131</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato"><b>mantras of brahmins</b></a> are among the things that function in secrecy and not openly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato">AN 3.131</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> as something that functions and thrives in secrecy rather than out in the open? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato">AN 3.131</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the way his teachings shine to the way the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato"><b>disk of the moon</b></a> shines when it is open and not concealed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato">AN 3.131</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the openness of the Dhamma to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato"><b>disk of the sun</b></a>, which shines most brilliantly when it is not hidden? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato">AN 3.131</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma and Discipline</b></a> proclaimed by the Tathāgata shine when they are open and revealed, not when they are kept secret? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato">AN 3.131</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly listed three specific things—the moon, the sun, and the Dhamma—as entities that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato"><b>shine openly</b></a> and never in secrecy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato">AN 3.131</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato"><b>three things</b></a> that function in secrecy (women, brahmin mantras, and wrong view) and three things that shine openly (the moon, the sun, and the Dhamma-Vinaya)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.131/en/sujato">AN 3.131</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a botanical analogy to describe human character, comparing four types of people to four types of trees based on the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato"><b>heartwood or softwood</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato">AN 4.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is both personally immoral and surrounded by an immoral assembly as being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato"><b>softwood tree</b></a> surrounded by other softwood trees? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato">AN 4.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is of bad character but is surrounded by an assembly of virtuous people is comparable to a softwood tree surrounded by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato"><b>heartwood trees</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato">AN 4.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a person who is personally virtuous but resides within an assembly of immoral people is like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato"><b>heartwood tree</b></a> surrounded by softwood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato">AN 4.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the most excellent type of person as one who is both virtuous and surrounded by a virtuous assembly, comparing them to a heartwood tree surrounded by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato"><b>heartwood</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato">AN 4.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha utilized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato"><b>Rukkhasutta</b></a> to demonstrate that an individual's personal virtue and the quality of their social circle are distinct factors that determine their "type" in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato">AN 4.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined an "immoral" person (<i>dussīlo</i>) as someone of bad character (<i>pāpadhammo</i>), a state he likened to the weak and less valuable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato"><b>inner core</b></a> of a softwood tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato">AN 4.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the people one associates with as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato"><b>assembly</b></a> (<i>parisā</i>), which can be either virtuous and of good character or immoral and of bad character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato">AN 4.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described a virtuous person (<i>sīlavā</i>) as having a good character (<i>kalyāṇadhammo</i>), which provides a spiritual strength analogous to the durable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato"><b>heartwood</b></a> found in certain trees? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.109/en/sujato">AN 4.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the four specific reasons why families who have attained great wealth do not last long is because they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato"><b>do not search for what is lost</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato">AN 4.258</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a family's great wealth may be short-lived if they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato"><b>do not repair what is old</b></a> or worn out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato">AN 4.258</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato"><b>immoderate in eating and drinking</b></a> as a primary factor that prevents wealthy families from maintaining their prosperity for a long duration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato">AN 4.258</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that placing an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato"><b>immoral woman or man</b></a> in a position of authority is a key reason why families with great wealth eventually fail to remain stable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato">AN 4.258</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that families who have attained great wealth will successfully endure for a long time if they consistently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato"><b>search for what is lost</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato">AN 4.258</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that wealthy families maintain their longevity and status by making it a point to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato"><b>repair what is old</b></a> and decaying? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato">AN 4.258</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a family that remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato"><b>moderate in eating and drinking</b></a> is far more likely to preserve its great wealth over many generations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato">AN 4.258</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wealthy family ensures its continued prosperity when they choose to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato"><b>place a virtuous woman or man</b></a> in a position of authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.258/en/sujato">AN 4.258</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who is worthy of gifts and is an unsurpassed field of merit for the world to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.259/en/sujato"><b>king's thoroughbred horse</b></a> that possesses four specific qualities: beauty, strength, speed, and proportion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.259/en/sujato">AN 4.259</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a monk as being "possessed of beauty" when that monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.259/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a>, remains restrained by the rules of the Patimokkha, and trains himself in the various rules of training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.259/en/sujato">AN 4.259</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk possesses spiritual "strength" when he lives with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.259/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> for the sake of abandoning unwholesome states and acquiring wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.259/en/sujato">AN 4.259</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk's spiritual "speed" to his ability to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.259/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> as they really are, including suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path leading to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.259/en/sujato">AN 4.259</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as being "possessed of proportion" when he is a frequent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.259/en/sujato"><b>recipient of robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.259/en/sujato">AN 4.259</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a king's horse is worthy of the king if it has beauty, strength, speed, and proportion, a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.259/en/sujato"><b>worthy of gifts</b></a> and hospitality if he possesses the spiritual equivalents of these four traits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.259/en/sujato">AN 4.259</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even a weak and ill monk can expect to soon realize the destruction of the taints if he possesses five qualities, including dwelling as one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.121/en/sujato"><b>contemplating foulness</b></a> in the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.121/en/sujato">AN 5.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.121/en/sujato"><b>perception of repulsiveness</b></a> in food as one of the five essential factors that lead a sick person to achieve mind-liberation and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.121/en/sujato">AN 5.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a sick monk could attain enlightenment in this very life if he maintains the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.121/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-delight</b></a> in the entire world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.121/en/sujato">AN 5.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged those who are weak and ill to dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.121/en/sujato"><b>contemplating impermanence</b></a> in all conditioned things as a path toward reaching the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.121/en/sujato">AN 5.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.121/en/sujato"><b>perception of death</b></a> is well established within a sick monk, it serves as a powerful quality for the realization of the supreme goal of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.121/en/sujato">AN 5.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while staying in the Great Wood at Vesālī, the Buddha visited a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.121/en/sujato"><b>sick ward</b></a> specifically to instruct the monks on how illness can be a time for deep spiritual breakthrough? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.121/en/sujato">AN 5.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a sick person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.123/en/sujato"><b>difficult to attend to</b></a> if they habitually do what is unsuitable for their recovery or do not know moderation in what is suitable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.123/en/sujato">AN 5.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one reason a patient becomes hard to care for is if they are a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.123/en/sujato"><b>non-doer of medicine</b></a>, refusing to take the treatments prescribed for their ailment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.123/en/sujato">AN 5.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a patient is difficult to support if they do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.123/en/sujato"><b>truthfully disclose</b></a> the status of their illness to a caregiver, such as failing to report when a pain is increasing or remains the same? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.123/en/sujato">AN 5.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a lack of endurance as a major obstacle to care, noting that a patient is difficult to attend to if they cannot bear <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.123/en/sujato"><b>painful bodily feelings</b></a> that are sharp, racking, and life-threatening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.123/en/sujato">AN 5.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a sick person as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.123/en/sujato"><b>easy to attend to</b></a> if they act suitably, know moderation, take their medicine, and report their condition honestly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.123/en/sujato">AN 5.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that being an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.123/en/sujato"><b>endurer of feelings</b></a> is a key quality that makes a sick person easy for others to care for during their illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.123/en/sujato">AN 5.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the householder Anāthapiṇḍika that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>mind is unprotected</b></a>, one’s bodily, verbal, and mental actions also become unprotected? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, if a person's bodily, verbal, and mental actions are unprotected, those <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>actions become soaked</b></a> with defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that when a person's bodily, verbal, and mental actions are "soaked," they eventually become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>rotten actions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person whose bodily, verbal, and mental actions have become "rotten" will not have a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>good death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>peaked house</b></a> that is poorly thatched to illustrate how an unprotected mind leads to the ruin of one's actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that in a poorly thatched peaked house, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>roof peak</b></a>, the rafters, and the walls all remain unprotected from the elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that just as an unprotected house becomes "soaked" and "rotten" from rain, the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>unprotected mind</b></a> leads to the corruption of all a person's deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly linked the lack of mental protection to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>inauspicious passing</b></a> at the time of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>mind is protected</b></a>, one's bodily, verbal, and mental actions are also simultaneously protected? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person with a protected mind performs bodily, verbal, and mental actions that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>not soaked</b></a> by lust or corruption? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when a person's actions are not "soaked" by defilements, they are considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>actions not rotten</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that a person whose bodily, verbal, and mental actions are "not rotten" will experience an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>auspicious death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a protected mind to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>well-thatched house</b></a> where the roof peak, rafters, and walls are all safe from being soaked and rotting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that in a well-thatched peaked house, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>rafters</b></a> and walls are protected from the rain, just as a guarded mind protects one's conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>condition of the mind</b></a> as the primary factor determining whether one's physical and verbal conduct remains pure or becomes corrupt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the <i>Arakkhitasutta</i>, the Buddha explains that the protection of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>verbal actions</b></a> is directly dependent on the protection of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that protecting the mind prevents the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>seeping in</b></a> of corrupting influences into one's daily behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>apūtika</b></a> (not rotten) to describe the wholesome state of actions belonging to someone who guards their mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>guarded mind</b></a> emphasizes that internal mental discipline is the foundation for an ethical and successful end of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the danger of neglect by showing how a single point of failure—the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato"><b>unprotected mind</b></a>—leads to the decay of every other aspect of a person's character and deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.109/en/sujato">AN 3.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who is worthy of honor and gifts to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>warrior</b></a> who is worthy of a king, provided that both possess the three specific skills of shooting from afar, shooting with lightning-like precision, and piercing through a large mass? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is said to "shoot from afar" when he uses correct wisdom to see that any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>—whether it is past, future, or present, internal or external—is not his, is not what he is, and is not his self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk possesses the skill of shooting from a distance when he perceives any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>, whether it be gross or subtle, far or near, as "this is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a spiritual practitioner mimics a warrior's range when they view any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> as not-self, regardless of whether that perception is inferior or superior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the practice of seeing all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> through the lens of "this is not my self" as the spiritual equivalent of a soldier's ability to strike a target from a great distance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as "shooting from afar" when he regards any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a>—whether it is internal or external—as "netaṃ mama" (this is not mine) and "nesohamasmi" (this I am not)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the skill of shooting like lightning (being a master marksman) as the ability of a monk to understand the truth of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk acts with the precision of a master marksman when he truly understands the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened a monk to a warrior who never misses when that monk clearly understands the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that understanding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>way leading to the cessation</b></a> of suffering is the spiritual counterpart to an elite warrior's lightning-like accuracy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk "pierces through large bodies" in a spiritual sense when he pierces through the great <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>mass of ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a monk to be an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world when he has mastered the abilities to see things as not-self, understand the Four Noble Truths, and destroy ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>worthy of gifts</b></a> and hospitality when he possesses three specific qualities modeled after the excellence of a king's warrior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk should view all five aggregates as "not-self" with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato"><b>correct wisdom</b></a>, whether those aggregates are past, future, present, far, or near? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.133/en/sujato">AN 3.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended associating with a friend who possesses three specific qualities, one of which is that they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.135/en/sujato"><b>give what is hard to give</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.135/en/sujato">AN 3.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a true friend worth seeking out as someone who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.135/en/sujato"><b>does what is hard to do</b></a> for the benefit of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.135/en/sujato">AN 3.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is a good friend if they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.135/en/sujato"><b>endure what is hard to endure</b></a> on behalf of their companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.135/en/sujato">AN 3.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the three essential criteria for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.135/en/sujato"><b>friend</b></a> to be worth associating with are generosity, helpful action, and patient endurance in difficult times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.135/en/sujato">AN 3.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared four types of people found in the world to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato"><b>four poisonous snakes</b></a>, categorized by how quickly their venom acts and how lethal it is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato">AN 4.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who gets angry often but whose anger does not persist for a long time as being like a snake with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato"><b>quick poison</b></a> that is not lethal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato">AN 4.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who does not get angry often but allows their anger to linger for a long duration is similar to a snake possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato"><b>lethal poison</b></a> that is not quick-acting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato">AN 4.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a particularly dangerous type of individual who both gets angry frequently and harbors that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato"><b>anger for a long time</b></a>, comparing them to a snake with poison that is both quick and lethal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato">AN 4.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there is a type of person who is neither frequently angry nor prone to long-lasting resentment, likening them to a snake that has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato"><b>neither quick poison</b></a> nor lethal poison? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato">AN 4.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato"><b>quick poison</b></a> (<i>āgataviso</i>) to specifically illustrate the temperament of someone who is easily provoked but quick to forgive or forget? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato">AN 4.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato"><b>lethal poison</b></a> (<i>ghoraviso</i>) to describe the internal state of a person whose anger, once triggered, stays with them for a very long period? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato">AN 4.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching in the Āsīvisasutta distinguishes between the frequency of a person's emotional outbursts and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato"><b>duration of their resentment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato">AN 4.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as there are four distinct types of snakes in nature, there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato"><b>four types of persons</b></a> existing in the world who can be understood through the lens of their anger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato">AN 4.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's classification of people in the Āsīvisasutta hinges on whether their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato"><b>anger persists</b></a> (<i>anuseti</i>) for a long time or whether it is merely a frequent but fleeting occurrence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.110/en/sujato">AN 4.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a monk is considered unfit to resort to remote lodgings in the forest if he is plagued by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.262/en/sujato"><b>sensual thoughts</b></a>, thoughts of ill will, and thoughts of harming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.262/en/sujato">AN 4.262</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who is witless, dull, and intellectually deficient is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.262/en/sujato"><b>not ready</b></a> to live in the solitude of forest dwellings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.262/en/sujato">AN 4.262</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is fit for remote forest lodgings only when he is characterized by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.262/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of renunciation</b></a>, thoughts of non-ill will, and thoughts of harmlessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.262/en/sujato">AN 4.262</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.262/en/sujato"><b>wise and intelligent</b></a> is a necessary requirement for a monk to properly utilize secluded and solitary forest settings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.262/en/sujato">AN 4.262</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that one who personally takes what is not given and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.265/en/sujato"><b>encourages others</b></a> to steal is acting in a way that leads to being cast into hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.265/en/sujato">AN 4.265</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that simply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.265/en/sujato"><b>approving of stealing</b></a> or speaking in praise of taking what is not given are among the four qualities that lead a person to rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.265/en/sujato">AN 4.265</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is "deposited in heaven" if they personally abstain from stealing and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.265/en/sujato"><b>instigate others</b></a> to also abstain from taking what is not given? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.265/en/sujato">AN 4.265</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.265/en/sujato"><b>praising the abstinence</b></a> from taking what is not given as a specific quality that, along with personal virtue, leads a person to a heavenly rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.265/en/sujato">AN 4.265</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.1/en/sujato"><b>power of faith</b></a> as the first of five essential spiritual strengths that a trainee on the path should possess? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.1/en/sujato">AN 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted his followers to train themselves to become possessed of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.1/en/sujato"><b>power of moral shame</b></a> and the power of moral dread as part of their spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.1/en/sujato">AN 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha counted the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.1/en/sujato"><b>power of energy</b></a> as one of the five specific "powers of a trainee" that practitioners must develop through diligent training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.1/en/sujato">AN 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an attendant is unfit to care for the sick if they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.124/en/sujato"><b>not competent</b></a> to prepare the necessary medicine for the patient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.124/en/sujato">AN 5.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that an unqualified attendant does not know what is suitable or unsuitable for a patient, and might actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.124/en/sujato"><b>remove what is suitable</b></a> while providing what is harmful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.124/en/sujato">AN 5.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that an attendant who looks after the sick with a desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.124/en/sujato"><b>material gain</b></a>, rather than out of a heart of loving-kindness, is considered unfit for the task? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.124/en/sujato">AN 5.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a person is fit to attend to the sick if they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.124/en/sujato"><b>not repelled</b></a> by the task of removing excrement, urine, vomit, or spittle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.124/en/sujato">AN 5.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified a key quality of a competent attendant as the ability to instruct, rouse, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.124/en/sujato"><b>gladden the sick</b></a> person from time to time with a Dhamma talk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.124/en/sujato">AN 5.124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha listed five qualities that lead to a short life, the first of which is the habit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.125/en/sujato"><b>doing what is unsuitable</b></a> for one's own health and well-being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.125/en/sujato">AN 5.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that even when something is suitable for one's health, failing to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.125/en/sujato"><b>know moderation</b></a> in that suitable thing can lead to a shortened lifespan? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.125/en/sujato">AN 5.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified the habit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.125/en/sujato"><b>eating undigested food</b></a> (or food that is difficult to digest) as one of the primary factors that results in a short life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.125/en/sujato">AN 5.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.125/en/sujato"><b>roaming at unseemly times</b></a>—engaging in activities at inappropriate hours—is practicing a habit that leads to a short life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.125/en/sujato">AN 5.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that failing to lead a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.125/en/sujato"><b>celibate life</b></a> (for those in the monastic community) is specifically listed as a factor that leads to a short life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.125/en/sujato">AN 5.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.126/en/sujato"><b>eats food that is easy to digest</b></a> is practicing one of the five behaviors that are conducive to a long life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.126/en/sujato">AN 5.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha mentioned that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.126/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> (sīlavā) is not only a spiritual quality but also a factor that supports the attainment of a long life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.126/en/sujato">AN 5.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.126/en/sujato"><b>good friends</b></a> (kalyāṇamitto) is a vital external factor that contributes to a person's longevity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.126/en/sujato">AN 5.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.126/en/sujato"><b>immoral</b></a> (dussīlo) is a detrimental quality that serves as one of the factors leading to a short life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.126/en/sujato">AN 5.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.126/en/sujato"><b>associating with bad friends</b></a> (pāpamitto) is a dangerous habit that can result in a person living a shorter life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.126/en/sujato">AN 5.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk who remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.127/en/sujato"><b>discontented with any kind of robe</b></a> provided to him is considered unfit to live in seclusion away from the monastic community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.127/en/sujato">AN 5.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.127/en/sujato"><b>discontented with any almsfood</b></a> he receives is unable to properly withdraw from the Sangha to live in solitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.127/en/sujato">AN 5.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that for a monk to be fit for seclusion, he must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.127/en/sujato"><b>contented with any kind of lodging</b></a>, whether it is humble or simple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.127/en/sujato">AN 5.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk who frequently dwells with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.127/en/sujato"><b>many sensual thoughts</b></a> is not yet ready to leave the community and live a life of solitary withdrawal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.127/en/sujato">AN 5.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified that a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.127/en/sujato"><b>dwells with many thoughts of renunciation</b></a> (nekkhammasaṅkappabahulo) is truly fit to live in seclusion away from the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.127/en/sujato">AN 5.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that any action performed through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.111/en/sujato"><b>greed</b></a> is considered unwholesome and blameworthy, inevitably resulting in suffering for the person who performs it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.111/en/sujato">AN 3.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, actions motivated by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.111/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> do not lead to the ending of one's spiritual burdens, but rather lead to the further origination of kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.111/en/sujato">AN 3.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.111/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> as one of the three primary roots that cause actions to be unwholesome and lead to painful consequences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.111/en/sujato">AN 3.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that actions based on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.113/en/sujato"><b>non-greed</b></a> are wholesome and blameless, serving as a foundation for the experience of future happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.113/en/sujato">AN 3.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person acts out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.113/en/sujato"><b>non-hatred</b></a>, that kamma is considered blameless and leads toward the ultimate cessation of kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.113/en/sujato">AN 3.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that kamma born of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.113/en/sujato"><b>non-delusion</b></a> is a source of wholesome action that results in happiness rather than the continued accumulation of kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.113/en/sujato">AN 3.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who is not actually celibate but makes a false <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato"><b>claim of celibacy</b></a> is destined for a state of deprivation and hell if they do not abandon such behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato">AN 3.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato"><b>falsely accusing</b></a> a person who is living a pure celibate life of being unchaste is a grave action that leads one toward rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato">AN 3.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the specific wrong view that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato"><b>no fault</b></a> in indulging in sensual pleasures as one of the three things that lead a person to be reborn in a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato">AN 3.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the appearance of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a>—an Arahant and a Perfectly Enlightened One—to be one of the rarest events to occur in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato">AN 3.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that finding a person who is capable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato"><b>teaching the Dhamma</b></a> and the discipline as proclaimed by the Tathāgata is an extremely rare occurrence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato">AN 3.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato"><b>grateful and thankful</b></a> is one of the three types of people who are very difficult to find in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato">AN 3.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between kamma that leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.111/en/sujato"><b>origination of kamma</b></a> and kamma that leads to its cessation based on whether the underlying motive is greed, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.111/en/sujato">AN 3.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified actions as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.111/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome</b></a> if they are produced by greed, hatred, or delusion, as these actions are fundamentally blameworthy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.111/en/sujato">AN 3.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined actions as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.113/en/sujato"><b>wholesome</b></a> when they are produced by non-greed, non-hatred, or non-delusion, leading to blameless results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.113/en/sujato">AN 3.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who hold the view that there is no harm in sensual pleasures and consequently fall into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato"><b>indulgence</b></a> are among those destined for hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.114/en/sujato">AN 3.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically linked the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.113/en/sujato"><b>cessation of kamma</b></a> to actions that are performed without greed, hatred, or delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.113/en/sujato">AN 3.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the fact that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.136/en/sujato"><b>conditioned things are impermanent</b></a> is a stable law and a fixed principle of phenomena, whether or not a Tathāgata arises in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.136/en/sujato">AN 3.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, he first awakens to and realizes the fact that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.136/en/sujato"><b>conditioned things are impermanent</b></a> before he explains, sets it forth, and clarifies it for others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.136/en/sujato">AN 3.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that even if a Tathāgata does not appear in the world, it remains a fixed principle of phenomena that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.136/en/sujato"><b>conditioned things are suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.136/en/sujato">AN 3.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized his role in teaching the Dhamma by stating that he reveals, analyzes, and makes plain the truth that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.136/en/sujato"><b>conditioned things are suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.136/en/sujato">AN 3.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the principle that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato"><b>phenomena are non-self</b></a> is a fixed law that exists independently of the presence of a Fully Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato">AN 3.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, after awakening to the truth that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato"><b>phenomena are non-self</b></a>, uses his teaching to analyze and clarify this specific nature of reality for all beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato">AN 3.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the various doctrines of reclusive brahmins to woven garments, and identified the teaching of Makkhali as the worst, similar to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato"><b>hair blanket</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato">AN 3.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato"><b>hair blanket</b></a> as being cold in cold weather, hot in hot weather, ugly in color, foul-smelling, and unpleasant to the touch? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato">AN 3.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to Makkhali as a "misguided man" because he promoted the harmful view that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato"><b>no action</b></a>, no efficacy of action, and no energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato">AN 3.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that all Fully Enlightened Buddhas of the past were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato"><b>proponents of action</b></a>, proponents of the efficacy of action, and proponents of energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato">AN 3.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha affirmed that all Fully Enlightened Buddhas of the future will also be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato"><b>proponents of energy</b></a> and the efficacy of action, contrary to the views of Makkhali? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato">AN 3.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly identified himself as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato"><b>proponent of the efficacy of action</b></a> and energy, opposing the views of those who deny the power of human effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato">AN 3.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy of a trap placed at a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato"><b>river mouth</b></a> to catch fish to explain how Makkhali’s teachings bring harm and suffering to many? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato">AN 3.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called the teacher Makkhali a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato"><b>human net</b></a> who has appeared in the world for the misfortune and ruin of many beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.137/en/sujato">AN 3.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is deposited in hell if they are endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.149/en/sujato"><b>impure bodily conduct</b></a>, impure verbal conduct, and impure mental conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.149/en/sujato">AN 3.149</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.149/en/sujato"><b>impure verbal conduct</b></a> as one of the three factors that can lead a person to a destination in hell, as if they were carried there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.149/en/sujato">AN 3.149</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.149/en/sujato"><b>impure mental conduct</b></a> is destined for a state of misery and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.149/en/sujato">AN 3.149</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is deposited in heaven if they are endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.149/en/sujato"><b>pure bodily conduct</b></a>, pure verbal conduct, and pure mental conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.149/en/sujato">AN 3.149</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that anyone endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.151/en/sujato"><b>blameworthy conduct</b></a> of body, speech, and mind is reborn in hell as if they were carried and placed there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.151/en/sujato">AN 3.151</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that being endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.151/en/sujato"><b>blameless conduct</b></a> in bodily, verbal, and mental actions leads a person to a good destination and a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.151/en/sujato">AN 3.151</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a famous horse trainer named Kesi once visited the Buddha to discuss the different methods used to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>train horses</b></a> and people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the horse trainer Kesi explained to the Buddha that he subdues a horse to be trained using <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>gentleness</b></a>, harshness, or a combination of both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Kesi the horse trainer encounters a horse that does not respond to gentle or harsh training, he chooses to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>kill</b></a> the animal so it does not bring discredit to his teacher's lineage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the horse trainer Kesi recognized the Buddha as the unexcelled <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>trainer of persons</b></a> to be tamed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained he trains people using a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>gentle method</b></a> by teaching about the nature and results of good conduct of body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's gentle training involves explaining how virtuous actions lead to rebirth among <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>devas</b></a> or human beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>harsh method</b></a> of training involves teaching about the nature and consequences of bad conduct of body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during harsh training, the Buddha warns that immoral actions can lead to rebirth in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a>, the animal realm, or the realm of hungry ghosts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uses a combined method of training where he teaches both the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>rewards of virtue</b></a> and the dire consequences of misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha surprised Kesi by claiming that he also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>kills</b></a> a person who cannot be tamed by gentle, harsh, or combined methods of instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Kesi asked why a Buddha would kill, the Buddha clarified that taking the life of a living being is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>not proper</b></a> for a Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Buddha's discipline, to "kill" a person means the Tathāgata no longer considers that person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>worth admonishing</b></a> or instructing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being ignored by the Teacher and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>wise fellow practitioners</b></a> is considered "slaughter" in the Noble Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person is "slain" when they are so unteachable that the Tathāgata and other practitioners <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>stop speaking</b></a> to them for their own benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Kesi the horse trainer realized that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>spiritually abandoned</b></a> by the Buddha and the wise is a much more severe form of "death" than physical killing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's "killing" of an untameable person serves as a metaphor for the total <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>loss of opportunity</b></a> for spiritual growth within the community of practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s training regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>bad conduct</b></a> specifically addresses the negative outcomes for the mind and the person's future destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uses the analogy of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato"><b>animal husbandry</b></a> to help a professional trainer understand the deep psychological and ethical nuances of spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.111/en/sujato">AN 4.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a bad monk to a "great thief" who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>five specific factors</b></a> that allow them to commit crimes such as housebreaking and highway robbery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a great thief relies on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>uneven terrain</b></a>, specifically river gorges or mountain peaks, to evade capture while committing their crimes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a great thief relies on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>thicket of trees</b></a>, grass, or dense jungles to hide and conduct their illicit activities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha noted that a great thief often seeks the protection of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>kings or royal ministers</b></a> so that these powerful figures will speak in their defense if they are ever accused of a crime? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a great thief is "generous with wealth" because they use their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>ill-gotten gains</b></a> to pay off and appease anyone who might attempt to speak against them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described a great thief as a "lone walker" who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>acts alone</b></a> during their crimes to ensure that their secret plans are never leaked to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that a bad monk who possesses the same five qualities as a thief carries himself along <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>broken and impaired</b></a>, becoming blameworthy in the eyes of the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a bad monk as "reliant on the uneven" when they engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome bodily conduct</b></a>, as well as unwholesome verbal and mental conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha said a bad monk is "reliant on a thicket" if they hold <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>wrong views</b></a> or extremist viewpoints that obscure the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha observed that a bad monk might rely on the influence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>powerful officials</b></a>, like kings or ministers, to protect themselves from criticism or discipline from the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a bad monk is "generous with acquisitions" when they use robes, almsfood, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>medicinal requisites</b></a> to bribe or appease those who would otherwise speak out against their bad behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described a bad monk as a "lone walker" when they settle alone in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>border districts</b></a> specifically to exploit local families for material gain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a bad monk who mimics the strategies of a great thief <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>generates much demerit</b></a> rather than spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>extremist views</b></a> to describe the spiritual "thicket" that a bad monk hides within to avoid the corrective light of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when a bad monk is "reliant on the uneven," they are defined by their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome mental conduct</b></a>, mirroring how a thief relies on rough, impassable terrain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a great thief might use a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>riverbank or jungle</b></a> as a refuge, just as a bad monk uses wrong view as a refuge for their misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that a bad monk who relies on powerful people is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>censured by the wise</b></a>, even if they are protected from worldly punishment by kings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha highlighted that a great thief maintains secrecy to prevent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>confidential plans</b></a> from spreading, similar to how a bad monk operates in remote areas to hide their true nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha characterized a "great thief" as someone who not only steals but also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>plunders wealth</b></a> and lies in ambush on highways? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s comparison shows that a bad monk is like a thief because they use <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato"><b>material gains</b></a> like alms and robes to silence those who would correct their lack of virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.103/en/sujato">AN 5.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an ascetic experiences suffering when they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.128/en/sujato"><b>discontent with whatever robes</b></a>, almsfood, or lodgings they receive, coupled with a lack of delight in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.128/en/sujato">AN 5.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the "happiness of an ascetic" is found in being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.128/en/sujato"><b>content with any kind of robes</b></a>, almsfood, lodgings, and medicinal requisites, while also finding joy in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.128/en/sujato">AN 5.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five specific factors that lead to an ascetic's happiness, the final and perhaps most crucial being that the monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.128/en/sujato"><b>delights in the holy life</b></a> (brahmacariya)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.128/en/sujato">AN 5.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed five "incurable" actions—including the murder of one's mother or father—that inevitably result in being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.129/en/sujato"><b>reborn in the plane of misery</b></a> and hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.129/en/sujato">AN 5.129</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that anyone who, with an evil intent, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.129/en/sujato"><b>sheds the blood of a Tathāgata</b></a> has committed one of the five grave deeds that lead to inescapable suffering in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.129/en/sujato">AN 5.129</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.129/en/sujato"><b>causing a schism in the Sangha</b></a> as an "incurable" deed that dooms a person to rebirth in the lower realms after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.129/en/sujato">AN 5.129</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.130/en/sujato"><b>loss of relatives or wealth</b></a>, while difficult, are not the reasons why beings are reborn in states of deprivation or hell after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.130/en/sujato">AN 5.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is specifically the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.130/en/sujato"><b>loss of virtue and right view</b></a> that causes beings to descend into the plane of misery and the hell world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.130/en/sujato">AN 5.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.130/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in virtue</b></a> is one of the primary reasons why beings are reborn in a happy destination or the heavenly world after they pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.130/en/sujato">AN 5.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that beings do not reach the heavenly world because of their success in wealth or health, but rather through their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.130/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in right view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.130/en/sujato">AN 5.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five types of misfortune, explaining that while the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.130/en/sujato"><b>misfortune of illness</b></a> is a type of loss, it is not the catalyst for a bad rebirth in the same way as spiritual failure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.130/en/sujato">AN 5.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described five types of "accomplishments" or successes, noting that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.130/en/sujato"><b>success in relatives or wealth</b></a> is insufficient on its own to lead a person to a happy rebirth after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.130/en/sujato">AN 5.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three specific types of people found in the world: one who should be loathed, one who should be looked upon with equanimity, and one who should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>associated with</b></a> and served? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a loathsome person as someone who is immoral and of bad character, comparing them to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>snake covered in dung</b></a> who smirches others with a bad reputation even if they do not "bite"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised looking upon an irascible and easily offended person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> rather than associating with them, to avoid being abused or reviled by them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared an angry person who reacts poorly to even slight criticism to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>festering sore</b></a> that discharges even more when it is struck by a stick or a shard? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that associating with a person of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>virtuous character</b></a> is beneficial because, even if one does not follow their example, one still gains a good reputation by being known as their companion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>cesspool</b></a> to describe an individual who becomes increasingly offensive and full of hatred when they are disturbed or addressed by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an impostor recluse—one who is immoral but claims to be a monk—as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>rotten within</b></a> and a "heap of corruption"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that associating with a person of bad character will cause a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>bad reputation</b></a> to spread about you, even if you do not adopt their specific immoral behaviors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a highly irritable person to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>tinder brand</b></a> of tinduka wood that crackles and sizzles even more when it is struck? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who associates with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>inferior person</b></a> will eventually decline in their own character and standing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who associates with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>equals</b></a> will not fail or decline, maintaining their current level of virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged followers to associate with those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>superior</b></a> to themselves, as this allows a person to rise and improve quickly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person to be loathed as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>covert in deeds</b></a> and engages in suspicious behavior while pretending to be a practitioner of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized an individual who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>full of despair</b></a> and displays surliness when spoken to as someone who should be looked upon with equanimity but not served or associated with? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to an immoral person as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>heap of corruption</b></a> (kasambujāto), indicating they are impure and leaky in their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>virtuous person</b></a> of good character is someone who should be served, honored, and stayed with as a "good friend" (kalyāṇamitto)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if you associate with an irascible person, they might <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>revile</b></a> you or do you harm, which is why he recommended keeping a distance through equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>impure</b></a> and "suspicious in behavior" if they claim to be celibate while they are actually not practicing celibacy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>kalyāṇadhammo</b></a> to describe a person of good character who is worthy of being served and honored by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that to better oneself, one should always seek out and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato"><b>honor</b></a> those who are more advanced in virtue and character than oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.27/en/sujato">AN 3.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who personally destroys life, encourages others to destroy life, and approves of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.163/en/sujato"><b>destruction of life</b></a> is deposited in hell as if brought there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.163/en/sujato">AN 3.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who abstains from the destruction of life, encourages others to abstain, and approves of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.164/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from killing</b></a> is deposited in heaven as if brought there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.164/en/sujato">AN 3.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that personally taking what is not given, inciting others to do the same, and showing approval for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.165/en/sujato"><b>taking what is not given</b></a> are three qualities that lead a person to be reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.165/en/sujato">AN 3.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one is reborn in a heavenly world if they personally refrain from taking what is not given, encourage others to refrain, and are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.166/en/sujato"><b>delighted by honesty</b></a> and the act of abstaining from theft? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.166/en/sujato">AN 3.166</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that engaging in sexual misconduct, urging others to engage in it, and giving approval to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.167/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> are actions that result in a person being cast into hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.167/en/sujato">AN 3.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who refrains from sexual misconduct, encourages others toward such restraint, and approves of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.168/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from sexual misconduct</b></a> will experience a heavenly rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.168/en/sujato">AN 3.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified speaking falsehoods, inciting others to lie, and approving of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.169/en/sujato"><b>false speech</b></a> as three factors that lead a person to be deposited in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.169/en/sujato">AN 3.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his followers to personally refrain from lying, to urge others to refrain, and to approve of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.170/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from false speech</b></a> as a certain path leading to heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.170/en/sujato">AN 3.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that using divisive speech to create rifts, encouraging others to do so, and approving of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.171/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a> are unwholesome behaviors that lead one to hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.171/en/sujato">AN 3.171</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who personally refrains from divisive speech, encourages others toward speech that promotes concord, and approves of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.172/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from divisive speech</b></a> is destined for heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.172/en/sujato">AN 3.172</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that using harsh language, inciting others to use it, and approving of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.173/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a> are three unwholesome qualities that result in a person being deposited in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.173/en/sujato">AN 3.173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who avoid harsh language, encourage others to avoid it, and approve of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.174/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from harsh speech</b></a> are deposited in heaven as if brought there by their own merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.174/en/sujato">AN 3.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that engaging in idle chatter, encouraging others to do the same, and showing approval for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.175/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a> results in a person being reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.175/en/sujato">AN 3.175</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that being covetous of others' possessions, inciting others to be covetous, and approving of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.177/en/sujato"><b>covetousness</b></a> are actions that lead directly to hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.177/en/sujato">AN 3.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being free from covetousness, encouraging others to be non-covetous, and approving of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.178/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from covetousness</b></a> leads a person to a heavenly destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.178/en/sujato">AN 3.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that possessing a mind of ill will, inciting others to ill will, and approving of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.179/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> are the three unwholesome conditions for being deposited in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.179/en/sujato">AN 3.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified holding wrong views, encouraging others to adopt them, and approving of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.181/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> as the three factors that cause one to be reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.181/en/sujato">AN 3.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that holding right view, encouraging others toward it, and approving of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.182/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> are the three qualities that result in being deposited in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.182/en/sujato">AN 3.182</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared four types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>excellent thoroughbred horses</b></a> found in the world to four types of excellent thoroughbred persons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the most sensitive type of thoroughbred horse as one that is stirred and feels a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>sense of urgency</b></a> just by seeing the shadow of the goad-stick? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an "excellent thoroughbred person" comparable to a horse stirred by a goad's shadow is one who feels urgency just by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>hearing that someone</b></a> in another village is suffering or has died? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned a type of thoroughbred horse that is not moved by the shadow of a goad but is stirred and feels urgency when its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>hair is touched</b></a> by the goad? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a thoroughbred person who is moved to spiritual urgency not by news, but by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>personally seeing</b></a> a man or woman who is suffering or has passed away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a third kind of thoroughbred horse that only feels a sense of urgency when its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>skin is pierced</b></a> by the goad? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, some thoroughbred persons only experience a sense of urgency when one of their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>relatives or kinsmen</b></a> falls into suffering or dies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the least sensitive thoroughbred horse as one that only reacts and performs its duty when the goad <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>penetrates to the bone</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who only feels spiritual urgency when they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>personally afflicted</b></a> with intense, racking, and life-threatening bodily feelings to a horse that must be struck to the bone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when an excellent person is stirred by a sense of urgency, they strive carefully and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>realize the supreme truth</b></a> with their own body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is stirred and strives resolutely eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>penetrates and sees</b></a> the ultimate truth through the depth of their wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a thoroughbred horse, upon seeing the shadow of the goad, reflects on what task the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>horse trainer</b></a> will require of it and how it should respond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>purisājānīya</b></a> to refer to an "excellent thoroughbred person" who responds to the reality of suffering and death with spiritual exertion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that hearing about the death of a stranger can be like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>shadow of a goad</b></a> for a person with a keen sense of spiritual urgency? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the most extreme trigger for urgency as being "touched" by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>painful bodily feelings</b></a> that are sharp, severe, and life-threatening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a thoroughbred person, upon being stirred by the reality of death, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>strives judiciously</b></a> (yoniso padahati) to reach the goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized an excellent thoroughbred horse based on how it responds to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>goad-stick</b></a>, ranging from its shadow to its penetration of the bone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the sense of urgency (saṃvega) can be triggered by news of someone <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>passing away</b></a> (kālaṅkato) in a village or town other than one's own? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "thoroughbred person" as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>resolute and self-controlled</b></a> (pahitatto), leading them to realize the supreme truth with the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for some people, the sight of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>suffering woman or man</b></a> is enough to spark the sense of urgency needed to strive for liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who only develops urgency when they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>personally touched</b></a> by severe bodily pain is still considered a "thoroughbred person" if they use that experience to strive for the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato"><b>four horses</b></a> to illustrate how different people respond with varying degrees of sensitivity to the presence of suffering and death in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.113/en/sujato">AN 4.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that there are five spiritual powers a noble disciple should possess: faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato">AN 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato"><b>power of faith</b></a> as the conviction a noble disciple has in the enlightenment of the Tathāgata, recognizing him as the Arahat, the Perfectly Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato">AN 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato"><b>power of energy</b></a> as living with energy aroused for the abandoning of unwholesome states and the acquisition of wholesome states, being firm in exertion and not shirking the responsibility of cultivating goodness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato">AN 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato"><b>power of mindfulness</b></a> involves a noble disciple possessing great discretion and the ability to remember and recall what was done and said long ago? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato">AN 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato"><b>power of concentration</b></a> as the attainment of the four jhānas, starting with the first jhāna, which is accompanied by thought and examination and born of seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato">AN 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato"><b>power of wisdom</b></a> as a noble disciple being endowed with wisdom regarding the arising and passing away of phenomena, a wisdom that is noble and penetrative and leads to the complete destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato">AN 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that within the power of concentration, the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato"><b>second jhāna</b></a> as a state of internal confidence and unification of mind that is free from thought and examination and born of concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato">AN 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato"><b>third jhāna</b></a> as a state where a noble disciple experiences a physical happiness while remaining equanimous and mindful, a state praised by noble ones as "equanimous, mindful, and dwelling happily"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato">AN 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a> is characterized by neither-pain-nor-pleasure and the purity of mindfulness due to equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.14/en/sujato">AN 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha instructed that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato"><b>power of faith</b></a> is to be seen or found by observing the four factors of stream-entry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato">AN 5.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato"><b>power of energy</b></a> is to be clearly seen within the practice of the four right strivings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato">AN 5.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato"><b>power of mindfulness</b></a> is to be seen specifically in the four establishments of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato">AN 5.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato"><b>power of concentration</b></a> is to be seen and recognized through the practice of the four jhānas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato">AN 5.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato"><b>power of wisdom</b></a> is to be seen in the deep understanding of the four noble truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato">AN 5.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha categorized faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom specifically as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato"><b>five powers</b></a> (balāni) that lead to spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato">AN 5.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the five powers are not just abstract concepts but are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato"><b>to be seen</b></a> through specific practical applications such as the factors of stream-entry and the noble truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.15/en/sujato">AN 5.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>wheel-turning monarch</b></a>, a righteous king of Dhamma, does not rule without a king above him, as there is always a higher principle of authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the "king" whom a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>righteous wheel-turning monarch</b></a> relies upon, honors, respects, and reveres is the Dhamma itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a righteous king maintains the Dhamma as his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>banner and flag</b></a>, meaning he acknowledges the Dhamma as his supreme and guiding authority in all matters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a righteous king provides <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>righteous protection</b></a>, shelter, and guard for the people within his own household to ensure their well-being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a righteous wheel-turning monarch extends his protection and care to his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>warriors and vassals</b></a>, ensuring their safety through the principles of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a righteous king provides guard and shelter for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>army</b></a>, ensuring that even military forces are governed by righteous standards? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a righteous ruler provides safety and protection for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>brahmins and householders</b></a> throughout his realm, treating all classes of citizens with Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that a righteous wheel-turning monarch provides protection for both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>town and country folk</b></a>, ensuring that justice and safety reach every corner of the kingdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a righteous king provides guard and shelter even for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>ascetics and brahmins</b></a>, honoring those dedicated to spiritual life through his righteous governance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that a righteous king's protection is so comprehensive it even includes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>beasts and birds</b></a> within his kingdom, reflecting a universal compassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once a righteous monarch provides total protection by the Dhamma, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>wheel he turns</b></a> cannot be turned back by any human enemy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified himself as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>righteous king of Dhamma</b></a>, who, like a worldly monarch, relies upon and honors the Dhamma as his supreme authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he provides righteous protection for monks by specifying which kinds of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>bodily conduct</b></a> should be cultivated and which should be avoided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha provides shelter for the community of nuns by instructing them on which types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>verbal conduct</b></a> are beneficial to cultivate or harmful and should be avoided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha provides guard for male lay followers by clarifying the types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>mental conduct</b></a> they should pursue to protect their spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha provides righteous protection for female lay followers by advising them on which types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>livelihood</b></a> are appropriate and which should be shunned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha provides a safety net for his followers by teaching them which <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>villages and towns</b></a> are suitable to visit and which are inappropriate to frequent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that having provided protection to the fourfold assembly, he turns the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>supreme Wheel of Dhamma</b></a> by means of the Dhamma alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the Wheel of Dhamma he sets in motion is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>irreversible</b></a>, meaning it cannot be turned back by any ascetic, brahmin, deva, Mara, or Brahma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the Wheel of Dhamma cannot be turned back by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato"><b>anyone in the world</b></a> once it has been established by a Tathāgata who honors the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.133/en/sujato">AN 5.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>three types of persons</b></a> existing in the world, classified according to the specific nature of their wisdom and their ability to retain the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>upside-down wisdom</b></a> as someone who frequently visits monasteries to hear the Dhamma from monks but fails to pay attention to the beginning, middle, or end of the talk while they are sitting there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>inverted pot</b></a> to explain that just as water poured onto such a pot runs off and does not stay, a person with "upside-down wisdom" hears the Dhamma but does not retain any of it in their mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person with "upside-down wisdom" is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>stupid and undiscerning</b></a> because even if they constantly visit the presence of monks, they lack the wisdom to grasp the meaning of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>lap-like wisdom</b></a> as one who, while sitting in the assembly, actually pays attention to the beginning, middle, and end of the Dhamma teaching, yet forgets everything immediately upon standing up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the concept of "lap-like wisdom" by comparing it to a man with various <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>edibles</b></a>—such as sesame seeds, rice, cakes, and jujubes—piled in his lap, who then scatters them all because of a lapse in mindfulness when he rises from his seat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>lap-like wisdom</b></a> is considered "better" than one with "upside-down wisdom," because the former at least understands the phrasing of the teaching while they are still sitting and listening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>broad wisdom</b></a> person as one who not only pays attention to the Dhamma while sitting in the assembly but also continues to mindfully contemplate the beginning, middle, and end of the teaching after they have left their seat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>upright pot</b></a> to describe the person with "broad wisdom," noting that just as water poured into an upright vessel stays and does not run off, the Dhamma stays with such a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>good in the beginning</b></a>, good in the middle, and good in the end, possessing both the correct meaning and phrasing for those who listen attentively? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the person with "broad wisdom" as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>best of the three</b></a> types because of their superior ability to retain and internalize the spiritual teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>undistracted in mind</b></a> is better able to grasp the phrasing of the Dhamma and hold onto it after hearing it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the "holy life" (brahmacariya) as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>completely perfect and pure</b></a>, a message that is proclaimed through the Dhamma to those who visit the monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that for a person with "upside-down wisdom," it makes no difference if they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>constantly visit</b></a> the monks, as they still cannot master the beginning, middle, or end of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>excellent intentions</b></a> to describe the mental state of the person with broad wisdom who successfully retains the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the ultimate result for a person with broad wisdom who retains the Dhamma is that they can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>make an end of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who remembers the Dhamma after rising from their seat is someone who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>practices in accordance</b></a> with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically mentioned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>sesame and rice</b></a> as part of a simile to describe how a forgetful person loses the Dhamma teachings they just heard? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the person with "broad wisdom" as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>grasps the phrasing</b></a> of the teaching while sitting and maintains that grasp even after they have finished listening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that for the "lap-like wisdom" person, whatever they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>grasped is forgotten</b></a> the moment they rise, similar to spilling food from a cloth in one's lap? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that hearing the Dhamma is a process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>frequent visitation</b></a> to the presence of monks, but the benefit gained depends entirely on the hearer's wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for the upside-down person, no matter how much Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato"><b>poured into them</b></a>, it simply doesn't stay because of the "orientation" of their wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.30/en/sujato">AN 3.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four specific types of bonds that keep beings trapped in suffering, which he called the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>four yokes</b></a>: the yoke of sensual desire, the yoke of existence, the yoke of views, and the yoke of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>yoke of sensual desire</b></a> occurs when a person fails to understand the origin, the passing away, the gratification, the danger, and the escape regarding sensual pleasures as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>yoke of existence</b></a> persists when one does not truly understand how various forms of being or existence originate, pass away, or how to escape them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>yoke of views</b></a> as the state where a person remains bound because they do not understand the gratification, the danger, and the escape in relation to various philosophical or speculative views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>yoke of ignorance</b></a> is characterized by not understanding the origin, passing away, gratification, danger, and escape regarding the six bases of contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being bound by the four yokes means one is joined to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome states</b></a> that are defiling, lead to renewed existence, and cause future birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>not secure from bondage</b></a> (Pali: <i>ayogakkhemī</i>) to describe anyone who remains tethered to sensual desire, existence, views, and ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>disconnection from the yoke</b></a> of sensual desire is achieved only when one understands the origin, passing away, gratification, danger, and escape of sensual pleasures as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person understands existence in all its five aspects—origin, passing away, gratification, danger, and escape—they achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>disconnection from the yoke of existence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>disconnection from the yoke of views</b></a> as the freedom that comes from truly knowing the origin, passing away, gratification, danger, and escape regarding all views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that one attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>disconnection from the yoke of ignorance</b></a> when they no longer harbor ignorance or unknowing regarding the six bases of contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>secure from bondage</b></a> (Pali: <i>yogakkhemī</i>) to describe a person who has successfully disconnected from the yokes of sensual desire, existence, views, and ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are "secure from bondage" are no longer joined to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>evil states</b></a> that lead to renewed existence and the distress of birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>craving for sensual pleasures</b></a>, along with delight, affection, infatuation, and passion for them, as the underlying tendencies that reinforce the yoke of sensual desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>craving for existence</b></a> and thirst for being are the factors that keep the yoke of existence underlying one's mental continuum? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>craving for views</b></a>, including passion and obsession with specific viewpoints, is what maintains the yoke of views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings continue to wander through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>saṃsāra</b></a>, the cycle of birth and death, because they are bound by the yokes of sensual desire, existence, views, and ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person who has fully understood sensual pleasures and completely abandoned the yoke of existence is considered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>sage</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ultimate spiritual goal as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>disconnected from all yokes</b></a>, having discarded the yoke of views and dispelled ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called those who have overcome all four bonds <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>crossers of the yokes</b></a>, indicating they have transcended the forces that tie beings to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>undiscovered ignorance</b></a> regarding the six bases of contact—the internal and external spheres of experience—is a primary bond that must be broken for liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching on the yokes highlights that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge</b></a> of the gratification, danger, and escape of our experiences is the key to breaking the bonds of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.10/en/sujato">AN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a king’s elephant becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>worthy of a king</b></a> and a royal possession when it possesses four specific qualities: being a listener, a destroyer, an endurer, and a goer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>listener</b></a> when he pays full attention and applies his whole mind to the Dhamma and Discipline as it is being taught? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a king's elephant as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>destroyer</b></a> in battle to illustrate how a monk should actively eliminate unwholesome mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is a "destroyer" when he does not tolerate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>sensual thoughts</b></a> that have arisen, but instead abandons, dispels, and wipes them out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who endures harsh speech and painful physical feelings is like a royal elephant who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>endures</b></a> the strikes of swords, arrows, and axes in combat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk’s rapid progress toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> to a king's elephant that quickly travels to a destination it has never visited before? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a monk as an "endurer" if he is able to bear cold, heat, hunger, thirst, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>stings of insects</b></a> and gadflies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who eliminates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of ill will</b></a> is acting with the same discipline as a royal elephant that destroys enemy elephants and riders on the battlefield? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a king's elephant as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>goer</b></a> when it quickly heads in whatever direction its trainer sends it, regardless of whether it has been there before? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk becomes an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>unexcelled field of merit</b></a> for the world when he possesses the four qualities of being a listener, destroyer, endurer, and goer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a royal elephant is considered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>listener</b></a> when it focuses its whole mind on the training and instructions provided by its elephant trainer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of harmfulness</b></a> as one of the specific unwholesome states a monk must "slay" or destroy to be considered a true spiritual warrior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the destination of Nibbāna as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>unvisited quarter</b></a> because it represents the stilling of all formations and the total destruction of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is an "endurer" when he remains patient and composed even when experiencing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>bodily feelings</b></a> that are painful, racking, and life-threatening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the monk’s abandonment of evil <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome states</b></a> to a king’s elephant killing enemy soldiers, horses, and chariots during a battle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered a monk to be a "goer" when he moves quickly toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>fading away of lust</b></a> and the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a royal elephant must endure the loud <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>sounds of drums</b></a>, kettledrums, and conchs in battle to be worthy of serving a king? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk must not only abandon but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>abolish</b></a> and lead to nonexistence any evil unwholesome qualities whenever they arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>worthy of gifts</b></a> and hospitality by mastering the same four disciplined qualities required of a royal elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as a "listener" when he listens to the Dhamma with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato"><b>lending an ear</b></a>, treating the teachings as a matter of vital importance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.114/en/sujato">AN 4.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who practices for the benefit of others but not for their own benefit as someone who is not personally accomplished in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.18/en/sujato"><b>virtuous behavior</b></a> themselves, yet encourages others to establish it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.18/en/sujato">AN 5.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person may be acting for the welfare of others without acting for their own welfare if they lack <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.18/en/sujato"><b>personal concentration</b></a> while urging others to achieve it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.18/en/sujato">AN 5.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner who is not personally accomplished in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.18/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> but encourages others to become accomplished in it is considered to be practicing for the benefit of others but not for themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.18/en/sujato">AN 5.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identifies a specific type of practitioner who has not personally achieved <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.18/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a> but nevertheless encourages and inspires others to achieve that same state of freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.18/en/sujato">AN 5.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of practitioners who lack the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.18/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision</b></a> of liberation themselves, yet still act for the benefit of others by encouraging them toward that specific attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.18/en/sujato">AN 5.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the most complete practitioner as one who is personally accomplished in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.20/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> while simultaneously encouraging others to establish themselves in that same virtuous behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.20/en/sujato">AN 5.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that practicing for both one's own benefit and the benefit of others involves being accomplished in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.20/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> oneself and also leading others to establish themselves in it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.20/en/sujato">AN 5.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a monk works for the welfare of both himself and others when he is personally endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.20/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> and actively encourages others to develop wisdom as well? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.20/en/sujato">AN 5.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha regarded a practitioner as practicing for both their own benefit and the benefit of others if they have attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.20/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a> and encourage others to reach that same liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.20/en/sujato">AN 5.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk who is accomplished in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.20/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a> and encourages others to attain it is practicing for the benefit of all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.20/en/sujato">AN 5.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught it is impossible for a monk who lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.21/en/sujato"><b>respect and deference</b></a> toward his fellow practitioners to ever fulfill the rules of proper conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.21/en/sujato">AN 5.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that without fulfilling the rules of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.21/en/sujato"><b>proper conduct</b></a> (<i>abhisamācārika</i>), it is impossible for a practitioner to complete the training (<i>sekha</i>) required for the higher life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.21/en/sujato">AN 5.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that one cannot fulfill the training of a trainee without first having perfected the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.21/en/sujato"><b>virtues</b></a>, as each stage of spiritual development is built upon the previous one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.21/en/sujato">AN 5.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that perfecting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.21/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> is a necessary prerequisite before one can successfully fulfill the practice of right concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.21/en/sujato">AN 5.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a crown-born prince aspires to kingship if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>well-born on both sides</b></a>, meaning he has a pure descent for seven generations back through both his mother and father? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a prince worthy of the throne as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>handsome and attractive</b></a>, possessing a supreme beauty of complexion that makes him pleasing to look upon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a factor for a prince's successful aspiration to the throne is being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>dear and agreeable</b></a> to his own mother and father? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being beloved by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>town and country people</b></a> as one of the five essential qualities for a crown-born prince to seek kingship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed proficiency in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>martial arts</b></a>, specifically the use of elephants, horses, chariots, bows, and swords, as a requirement for a prince to aspire to royal power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the royal aspirations of a prince to a monk who, possessing five specific qualities, can rightfully aspire to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the first quality of an aspiring monk as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> in the Tathāgata’s enlightenment, acknowledging him as the teacher of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should have a digestive system that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>neither too cold nor too hot</b></a>, providing a physical balance that is suitable for spiritual striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>honest and sincere</b></a>, rather than a deceiver or a hypocrite, when revealing his true nature to his Teacher or wise companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a successful monk as one who lives with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> for the sake of abandoning unwholesome states and fully acquiring wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the fifth quality of an aspiring monk is possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>penetrative wisdom</b></a> regarding the arising and passing away of phenomena, which leads to the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a prince reflects on his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>martial skills</b></a> with elephants and horses as a reason to conclude, "Why shouldn't I aspire to kingship?" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a monk should reflect on being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>free from illness</b></a> as a vital internal condition for seeking the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>not a deceiver</b></a> can more easily progress in the Dhamma by being transparent with his spiritual teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>firm in effort</b></a> and does not shirk the responsibility of cultivating wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk’s wisdom should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>noble</b></a> and directed toward the total destruction of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>pure descent</b></a> to describe a prince who has not been reproached or disparaged regarding his birth for seven generations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk can gain the confidence to seek the highest goal by reflecting, "I am <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>wise</b></a>, possessing the wisdom that understands arising and passing away"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>faith in enlightenment</b></a> has a solid foundation to ask himself why he should not achieve the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato"><b>unwavering</b></a> in the pursuit of wholesome states is a necessary characteristic for any monk who seeks to end the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.135/en/sujato">AN 5.135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that parents are called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato"><b>Brahma</b></a> in those families where they are properly honored by their children in the home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato">AN 3.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to parents as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato"><b>early teachers</b></a> because they are the first to show this world to their children and are of great benefit to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato">AN 3.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that parents are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato"><b>worthy of offerings</b></a> from their children because they act as providers and nurturers throughout their children's lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato">AN 3.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a wise person should respect their parents by providing them with food, drink, clothing, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato"><b>bedding</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato">AN 3.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified personal service, such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato"><b>massaging and bathing</b></a> one's parents, as a proper way for a wise person to show them honor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato">AN 3.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that those who serve their parents with devotion are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato"><b>praised in this life</b></a> and will rejoice in the heavenly world after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato">AN 3.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described parents as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato"><b>compassionate</b></a> toward their offspring, which is one of the primary reasons they are considered worthy of gifts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato">AN 3.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the designation <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato"><b>Brahma</b></a> is applied to parents because they are responsible for showing the world to their children? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato">AN 3.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda once asked the Buddha if a monk could attain a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> where there is no "I-making" regarding the conscious body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato">AN 3.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that it is possible for a monk to reach a state where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato"><b>mine-making</b></a> and the underlying tendency to conceit no longer exist for all external signs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato">AN 3.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the highest state of concentration as focusing on Nibbana, which he characterized as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato"><b>stilling of all formations</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato">AN 3.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified Nibbana as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato"><b>relinquishment of all acquisitions</b></a> and the total destruction of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato">AN 3.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk can dwell in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom where "I-making" and conceit are completely absent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato">AN 3.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha quoted a verse from the "Questions of Puṇṇaka" in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato"><b>Pārāyana Vagga</b></a> to illustrate the state of a person who has crossed over birth and old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato">AN 3.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato"><b>undisturbed</b></a> anywhere in the world and is without hope has truly crossed over the cycle of birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato">AN 3.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha referred to Nibbana as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> and cessation, describing it as the most peaceful and sublime state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato">AN 3.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato"><b>understood the world</b></a>—both its high and low aspects—is someone for whom no agitation exists anywhere? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato">AN 3.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that attaining a state without the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to conceit</b></a> is the specific goal of the meditative concentration he recommended to Ānanda? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato">AN 3.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described a liberated person who has understood the world as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato"><b>without smoke</b></a>, peaceful, and without trouble? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.32/en/sujato">AN 3.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that families where parents are honored are considered to be with those <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato"><b>worthy of gifts</b></a>, placing parents on the same level as the highest recipients of offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.31/en/sujato">AN 3.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a monk is considered lazy and lacking ardor if he tolerates a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>thought of sensuality</b></a> that arises while he is walking, rather than abandoning it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who fails to dispel a thought of ill will while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>standing</b></a> is described as lacking energy and the dread of wrongdoing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>sitting</b></a> but allows a thought of harming to persist without trying to eliminate it is called "constantly and continually lazy"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>lying down</b></a> awake, a monk is considered by the Buddha to have low energy if he tolerates unwholesome thoughts instead of making them reach non-existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as "ardent" and "resolute" if he immediately dispels and abolishes a thought of sensuality as soon as it arises while he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>walking</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered a monk to have "aroused energy" if he does not tolerate a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>thought of ill will</b></a> that arises while he is standing still? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is "resolute" if he abandons a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>thought of harming</b></a> while sitting and does not let it persist in his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who remains awake while lying down is called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>ardent</b></a> if he promptly removes any unwholesome thoughts that arise in his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>dread of wrongdoing</b></a> (ottāpī) to describe a monk who is diligent enough to clear his mind of unwholesome thoughts across all four physical postures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who cultivates evil thoughts based on the "home life" is following a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>wrong path</b></a> and is infatuated with things that cause delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who indulges in unwholesome thoughts while walking, standing, sitting, or lying down is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>incapable</b></a> of reaching supreme enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk is capable of attaining supreme enlightenment if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>delighting in the stilling of thoughts</b></a> regardless of his physical posture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uses the Pali term <i>pahitatto</i>, meaning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>resolute</b></a>, to specifically describe a monk who consistently abandons unwholesome thoughts of sensuality, ill-will, and harming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk who tolerates evil thoughts is described by the Buddha as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>low energy</b></a> (<i>hīnavīriyo</i>), even if those thoughts arise while he is simply sitting or lying down awake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that a monk who masters his mind by quieting his thoughts is capable of touching <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>supreme enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>awake</b></a> while lying down is just as critical a time as walking or sitting for a monk to monitor and abandon unwholesome mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the effort to dispel unwholesome thoughts must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato"><b>constant and continuous</b></a> for a monk to be truly considered one with "aroused energy"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.11/en/sujato">AN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should exercise <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.116/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> by actively abandoning bodily misconduct and instead developing good bodily conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.116/en/sujato">AN 4.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised practitioners to never be negligent regarding their speech, but to purposefully abandon verbal misconduct and cultivate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.116/en/sujato"><b>good verbal conduct</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.116/en/sujato">AN 4.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the importance of heedfulness in one's internal life, instructing that a person should abandon mental misconduct and cultivate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.116/en/sujato"><b>good mental conduct</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.116/en/sujato">AN 4.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the abandonment of wrong view and the development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.116/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> as one of the four essential areas where a practitioner must exercise constant heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.116/en/sujato">AN 4.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who has successfully abandoned misconduct in body, speech, and mind, and has further abandoned wrong view, no longer has any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.116/en/sujato"><b>fear of death</b></a> in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.116/en/sujato">AN 4.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should exercise mindfulness and protection of the mind to ensure it does not become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.117/en/sujato"><b>lustful</b></a> toward things that provoke lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.117/en/sujato">AN 4.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended a "protection of the mind" through heedfulness, specifically so that the mind does not become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.117/en/sujato"><b>full of hatred</b></a> toward things that provoke hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.117/en/sujato">AN 4.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that heedfulness involves a constant guarding of the mind so that it does not become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.117/en/sujato"><b>deluded</b></a> by things that are provocative of delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.117/en/sujato">AN 4.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that one should guard the mind against becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.117/en/sujato"><b>intoxicated</b></a> or infatuated by things that provoke such a state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.117/en/sujato">AN 4.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who has freed his mind from lust, hatred, delusion, and intoxication is someone who does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.117/en/sujato"><b>shake or tremble</b></a> and remains unshaken even by the words of other recluses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.117/en/sujato">AN 4.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.119/en/sujato"><b>fear of birth</b></a> as the first of four fundamental fears that sentient beings face? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.119/en/sujato">AN 4.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.119/en/sujato"><b>fear of old age</b></a> and the fear of illness as two of the major types of fear inherent in existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.119/en/sujato">AN 4.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are four primary fears, culminating in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.119/en/sujato"><b>fear of death</b></a>, which along with the fears of birth, old age, and illness, define the scope of worldly dread? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.119/en/sujato">AN 4.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the mind to gold, explaining that just as gold has five impurities that make it brittle, the mind has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>five corruptions</b></a> that prevent it from being rightly concentrated for the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified iron, copper, tin, lead, and silver as the five specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>impurities of gold</b></a> which, when present, prevent the metal from being malleable, wieldy, or luminous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when gold is freed from its five impurities, it becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>malleable and luminous</b></a>, allowing a goldsmith to fashion it into any desired ornament? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind corrupted by sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>brittle</b></a> and unfit for spiritual work? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a> (kāmacchanda) as the first of five mental corruptions that must be removed for the mind to become wieldy and capable of higher knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a mind freed from corruptions is like refined gold that can be used to create a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>gold garland</b></a>, a signet ring, or a necklace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once the mind is freed from the five corruptions, it can be directed toward any state realizable by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>higher knowledge</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a psychic power where a monk with a concentrated mind can become many and then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>become one again</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk with a purified mind could pass through walls, ramparts, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>mountains</b></a> without obstruction, as if he were moving through empty space? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned the ability to perform the feat of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>diving into the earth</b></a> and emerging from it as if it were water, as a result of mental development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner with a wieldy mind can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>walk on water</b></a> without sinking, just as if they were walking on solid ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one could travel through the air <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>cross-legged</b></a> like a winged bird once the mind is properly concentrated and freed from corruptions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the incredible ability to touch and stroke the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>sun and moon</b></a> with one's hand as a potential power of a developed mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a monk can use the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>divine ear</b></a> element to hear both divine and human sounds, regardless of whether they are far or near? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>minds of others</b></a>, knowing if another person's mind is lustful, hating, or deluded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a concentrated mind allows one to discern if another's mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>contracted or scattered</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>recollection of past lives</b></a> as remembering many eons of world-expansion and world-contraction, including one's specific names and clans in those lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a>, one can see beings passing away and being reborn in happy or miserable states according to their kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that beings who engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>misconduct of body</b></a>, speech, and mind, and who revile the noble ones, are reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that those who possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> and perform good deeds are reborn after death in a happy destination or the heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavānaṃ khaya) as the ultimate realization achieved through the liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> (vicikicchā) as one of the five mental corruptions that prevent the mind from being properly concentrated for spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.23/en/sujato">AN 5.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a king's eldest son as being qualified to aspire to viceroyship if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>well-born on both sides</b></a>, from both the mother’s and father’s side, with pure descent for seven generations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the five qualities that makes a prince aspire to deputy kingship is being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>handsome and attractive</b></a>, possessing a supreme beauty of complexion that is pleasing to behold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a crown prince feels justified in seeking power when he knows he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>dear and agreeable</b></a> to his parents, which is considered a necessary attribute for his high position? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted a prince's aspiration for the throne is strengthened when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>dear to the army</b></a>, ensuring he has the support and affection of the military forces? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a wise prince as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>competent and intelligent</b></a> enough to reflect upon and understand the welfare of the past, future, and present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a monk can rightfully aspire to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> by possessing five specific spiritual qualities, just as a prince aspires to viceroyship through five royal qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk should aspire to spiritual liberation if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>virtuous and restrained</b></a> by the Pātimokkha, maintaining proper conduct and resort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>seeing danger</b></a> in even the slightest faults is one who possesses the moral foundation to aspire to the ultimate goal of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>learned and a retainer</b></a> of what he has heard, accumulating teachings that are good in the beginning, middle, and end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ideal monk's knowledge as including teachings that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>pure and complete</b></a>, which have been well-retained by him, practiced in speech, and examined with the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to ensure the Dhamma they learn is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>well-penetrated by view</b></a>, which serves as a key factor in their confidence to seek the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk's mind must be well-established in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a> to be properly qualified for the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>energetic monk</b></a> as one who lives with energy aroused for the sake of abandoning unwholesome states and undertaking wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>steadfast in effort</b></a> and does not shirk the responsibility of cultivating wholesome qualities, regardless of the difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk should possess the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>wisdom of arising and passing away</b></a>, which is noble and penetrative, leading to the complete destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the internal confidence of a royal prince to the internal confidence of a monk who reflects, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>Why should I not aspire</b></a> to the destruction of the taints?" when he recognizes his own spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a prince's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>pure lineage</b></a> for seven generations on both sides is analogous to a monk's firm foundation in moral virtue and the Pātimokkha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a prince has the right to deputy kingship through specific personal traits, a monk endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato"><b>five specific dhammas</b></a> possesses the necessary spiritual qualifications to attain Enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.136/en/sujato">AN 5.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Ven. Sāriputta that while he could teach the Dhamma in brief, in detail, or both, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato"><b>those who understand</b></a> it are rare to find? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato">AN 3.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should train in such a way that "I-making," "mine-making," and the underlying tendency to conceit do not occur in relation to this <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato"><b>conscious body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato">AN 3.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Ven. Sāriputta that one should train to ensure that "I-making," "mine-making," and the underlying tendency to conceit do not arise regarding any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato"><b>external signs</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato">AN 3.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a state where a person dwells in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom because they no longer possess the underlying tendencies of "I-making" and conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato">AN 3.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a monk has removed conceit and "I-making" from both the internal body and the external world, he is said to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato"><b>cut off craving</b></a> and revolved the fetter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato">AN 3.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk effectively makes an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a> through the right breakthrough and understanding of conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato">AN 3.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha quoted a verse from the "Questions of Udaya" in the Pārāyana to illustrate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato"><b>abandonment of sensual perceptions</b></a> and dejection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato">AN 3.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato"><b>removal of remorse</b></a> as a necessary step for attaining the "liberation through final knowledge" that he described to Ven. Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato">AN 3.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato"><b>purity of mindfulness</b></a> and equanimity, preceded by the thinking of the Dhamma, as key elements of the liberation he taught? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato">AN 3.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the ultimate liberation of which he spoke as involving the total <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato"><b>breaking of ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.33/en/sujato">AN 3.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato"><b>perfect in virtue</b></a> and restrained by the Pātimokkha, while remaining scrupulous in seeing danger in even the slightest faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato">AN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a practitioner as "ardent and scrupulous" if they have abandoned covetousness, ill will, and the five hindrances even while they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato"><b>walking</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato">AN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is considered to have "resolute intent" if his energy is aroused and his mind is concentrated even while he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato"><b>standing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato">AN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk has discarded sloth, torpor, and doubt while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato"><b>sitting</b></a>, he is said to be "constantly energetic" and "resolute"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato">AN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that even while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato"><b>lying down</b></a>, if a monk is awake and has rid his mind of restlessness and remorse, he is still regarded as being "ardent" and "scrupulous"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato">AN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to maintain a state where their body is tranquil and their mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato"><b>one-pointed</b></a> across all four physical postures of walking, standing, sitting, and lying down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato">AN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed practitioners to carefully observe the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato"><b>rise and fall</b></a> of the five aggregates of clinging as part of their constant spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato">AN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a monk of "resolute intent" as one who is always mindful and training for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato"><b>peace of mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato">AN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> is characterized by a mind that is not "shrunken" and a body that is not "disturbed," regardless of one's physical activity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato">AN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that a practitioner must see "danger in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato"><b>slightest faults</b></a>" to properly undertake and train in the training rules? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.12/en/sujato">AN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato"><b>four right strivings</b></a> as the essential types of effort a practitioner should exert to overcome unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato">AN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato"><b>generate desire</b></a> and exert effort specifically to prevent the arising of evil, unwholesome states that have not yet occurred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato">AN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the second right striving as the effort to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato"><b>abandon</b></a> evil, unwholesome states that have already arisen in the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato">AN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that practitioners should strive and apply the mind for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato"><b>arising</b></a> of wholesome states that have not yet arisen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato">AN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's fourth right striving involves making an effort for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato"><b>maintenance</b></a>, increase, and fulfillment of wholesome states that have already arisen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato">AN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that those who master the right strivings have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato"><b>overcome Mara’s realm</b></a> and defeated the "Army of Namuchi"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato">AN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that through right striving, practitioners become "unattached" and reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato"><b>far shore</b></a>, transcending the fear of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato">AN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato"><b>unperturbed</b></a> (aneja) to describe those who have successfully conquered all of Mara's forces through diligent spiritual effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato">AN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the process of right striving requires one to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato"><b>uphold the mind</b></a> and push forward with energy to ensure unwholesome states do not persist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato">AN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described those who have mastered right striving as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato"><b>happy</b></a> because they have surpassed the entire power of the "Evil One"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.13/en/sujato">AN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>four fears</b></a> that serve as a motivation for people to behave ethically: the fear of self-reproach, the fear of others’ reproach, the fear of punishment, and the fear of a bad destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>fear of self-reproach</b></a> occurs when an individual reflects that if they were to engage in misconduct of body, speech, or mind, they could not avoid blaming themselves regarding their own virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who is afraid of self-reproach will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>abandon bodily misconduct</b></a> and instead develop good conduct in their actions, speech, and thoughts to maintain themselves in purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>fear of others’ reproach</b></a> as the concern that if one engages in misconduct, other people will rightfully criticize one's behavior and lack of virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who fear the reproach of others will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>cultivate good conduct</b></a> in speech and mind, ensuring they live a life that cannot be justly criticized by their peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>fear of punishment</b></a> as the dread one feels when witnessing a criminal being seized by authorities and subjected to various harsh tortures for their crimes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed several severe physical penalties used by kings that instill fear in observers, such as having one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>hands and feet cut off</b></a> as a consequence of committing evil deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that the fear of punishment is driven by seeing offenders have their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>heads cut off</b></a> with a sword or their ears and noses severed as a result of their criminal actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person fearing punishment as one who reflects: "If I were to commit such an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>evil deed</b></a>, the kings would seize me and subject me to these same terrible tortures"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that out of fear of punishment, a person chooses not to go about <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>plundering others’ goods</b></a>, but instead abandons misconduct and maintains their own purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>fear of a bad destination</b></a>, which is the realization that misconduct in body, speech, and mind leads to painful results in the future beyond this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the fear of a bad destination involves reflecting on the possibility of being reborn in a state of misery or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>hell after death</b></a> as a direct result of unwholesome actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how the fear of a bad destination motivates an individual to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>pursue good conduct</b></a> in body, speech, and mind to ensure they do not face a downfall after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha consistently grouped <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>verbal and mental misconduct</b></a> alongside physical misconduct as the behaviors one must abandon when motivated by any of the four types of fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by recognizing these four fears and practicing good conduct, a person successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>maintains themselves in purity</b></a> throughout their life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned vivid historical punishments such as the "fire-wreath" or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>burning turban</b></a> (jotimālika) as examples of the types of torture that instill a fear of legal punishment in people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>evil result</b></a> of misconduct is specifically something experienced "in the future" (abhisamparāyaṃ), which forms the basis for the fear of a bad destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that the fear of punishment is specifically related to the actions of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>kings who inflict torture</b></a> on those who have committed crimes like theft? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the fear of self-reproach is uniquely based on an internal assessment of one's own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>virtuous behavior</b></a> and the desire to remain blameless in one's own eyes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato"><b>four types of fears</b></a>—self-reproach, others' reproach, punishment, and bad destinations—are the fundamental reasons why a person chooses to live an ethical and pure life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.121/en/sujato">AN 4.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for an immoral person who is deficient in virtue, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato"><b>proximate cause</b></a> for the development of right concentration is destroyed, which prevents them from establishing a firm foundation for higher meditative states,? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato">AN 5.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when right concentration is absent in a practitioner, the necessary conditions for attaining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision</b></a> of things as they really are cannot be fulfilled, leaving the person unable to see the true nature of existence,? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato">AN 5.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who lacks the knowledge and vision of things as they really are will also fail to develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato"><b>revulsion and dispassion</b></a> toward worldly phenomena, which are essential steps for letting go of attachments,? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato">AN 5.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if revulsion and dispassion are not present in a person’s mind, the final goal of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a> remains out of reach, as the causal chain for spiritual freedom has been broken,? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato">AN 5.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person lacking moral discipline to a tree with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato"><b>defective branches and foliage</b></a>, illustrating how a failure at the most basic level of virtue prevents the deeper "layers" of the spiritual path from ever maturing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato">AN 5.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a botanical analogy to show that just as a tree with unhealthy branches cannot produce proper bark, sapwood, or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato"><b>heartwood</b></a>, an immoral person cannot attain the "heartwood" of final liberation,? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato">AN 5.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a virtuous person who is complete in moral discipline, the requirement for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> is naturally provided, as virtue serves as the indispensable support for a focused and steady mind,? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato">AN 5.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when right concentration is fully present and stable, it acts as the immediate prerequisite for a practitioner to experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato"><b>knowing and seeing</b></a> things as they actually are, rather than through the lens of delusion,? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato">AN 5.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the successful attainment of knowledge and vision of things as they really are is what directly enables a person to fulfill the quality of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato"><b>revulsion and dispassion</b></a>, leading to a turning away from suffering,? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato">AN 5.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a sequence of spiritual growth where a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato"><b>complete virtue</b></a> is likened to a healthy tree whose branches, foliage, bark, and sapwood all reach full maturation, eventually resulting in the fulfillment of the knowledge and vision of liberation,? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.24/en/sujato">AN 5.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who is worthy of gifts and veneration to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>royal elephant</b></a> that possesses five specific qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a royal elephant is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>listener</b></a> when it pays full attention and listens carefully to whatever task its trainer directs it to perform? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a royal elephant as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>slayer</b></a> because it destroys enemy elephants, riders, horses, and chariots when it enters into battle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a royal elephant acts as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>protector</b></a> by guarding its own body parts, such as its trunk, feet, and head, as well as its rider during combat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a royal elephant is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>endurer</b></a> when it can withstand the blows of spears, swords, and arrows, as well as the deafening noise of drums and conchs in battle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a royal elephant is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>goer</b></a> if it quickly travels to any direction its trainer sends it, regardless of whether it has been there before? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk becomes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>listener</b></a> when he listens to the Dhamma and Vinaya with full attention and an eager ear, intending to understand the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a monk as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>slayer</b></a> when he refuses to tolerate arisen sensual thoughts and instead abandons, dispels, and eliminates them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a monk who is a "slayer" of unwholesome states must also abandon and eliminate thoughts of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> and harmfulness as soon as they arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>protector</b></a> when he guards his sense faculties, such as the eye, so that unwholesome states like greed and dejection do not flow in? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk protects his mind by not seizing upon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>signs or features</b></a> of things he perceives through his senses to prevent the arising of unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified a monk as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>endurer</b></a> if he can bear cold, heat, hunger, thirst, and the stings of insects or contact with creeping things without complaint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk must be an "endurer" who tolerates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>harsh words</b></a> and ill-spoken, unwelcome utterances directed toward him by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised a monk who can endure <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>painful physical feelings</b></a> that are sharp, severe, and even life-threatening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>goer</b></a> when he quickly moves toward the "untraveled direction," which refers to the realization of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the ultimate goal of Nibbāna as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>stilling of all formations</b></a> and the relinquishment of all acquisitions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that Nibbāna is characterized by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a>, fading away, and the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk possessed of these five qualities—being a listener, slayer, protector, endurer, and goer—becomes an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a royal elephant protects its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>trunk and tail</b></a> in battle, just as a monk must protect his sense doors from unwholesome influences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a monk to be a "listener," he must focus his whole mind and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>listen to the Dhamma</b></a> when the teaching and discipline are being proclaimed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the "slayer" quality of a monk as the ability to bring <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>bad unwholesome states</b></a> to an end whenever they arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is worthy of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato"><b>venerated with joined palms</b></a> when he embodies the five internal strengths modeled by a royal elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.140/en/sujato">AN 5.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>greed, hatred, and delusion</b></a> as the three primary sources for the origination of kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, kamma born of greed ripens wherever a person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>selfhood is reborn</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the results of kamma produced by hatred can be experienced in three timeframes: in the present life, the next life, or in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>subsequent lives</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>intact seeds</b></a>, which are not rotten or wind-damaged, to illustrate how kamma born of delusion grows when it is sown in fertile soil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that just as seeds provided with rain reach growth and expansion, so too does kamma born of greed lead to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>experience of results</b></a> wherever a person is reborn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified non-greed, non-hatred, and non-delusion as three alternative <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>sources for the origination</b></a> of kamma that lead to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when greed is removed, any kamma produced by non-greed is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>cut off at the root</b></a> and rendered incapable of future arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared kamma that has been obliterated to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>palm stump</b></a>, which is removed from its site and can never grow again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described kamma born of non-hatred as being effectively <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>abandoned</b></a> once the underlying hatred has been dispelled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the destruction of kamma through the simile of a man burning seeds until they become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>ashes</b></a> and then winnowing them in a strong wind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that once delusion is gone, kamma born of non-delusion becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>obliterated</b></a> and is no longer subject to future arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that whether kamma performed through greed, hate, or delusion is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>small or great</b></a>, its result must be experienced in some form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that for a wise person, giving rise to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>knowledge</b></a> (vijjā) is the key to dispelling the origins of kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk who understands the origins of kamma and develops wisdom can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>abandon all miserable destinations</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha compared the total elimination of kamma to throwing the ashes of burned seeds into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>fast-flowing river</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described kamma born of greed as reaching <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>growth, increase, and expansion</b></a> when the conditions for its ripening are met? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that kamma born of hatred is like a seed sown in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>well-prepared soil</b></a>, which eventually yields its fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that one experiences the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>result of kamma</b></a> specifically in the place where one's selfhood is produced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to someone who understands the nature of greed, hate, and delusion as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a> (viddasu)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when the source of kamma is removed, it is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato"><b>not subject to future arising</b></a>, just like a burned seed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.34/en/sujato">AN 3.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while sleeping on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>bed of leaves</b></a> on a cattle-path during a frosty winter night, claimed to be one of those who sleep happily in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Hatthaka of Āḷavī that even a wealthy householder living in a draft-free <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>gabled house</b></a> with soft couches and beautiful wives might still sleep in misery due to internal suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>lust, hatred, and delusion</b></a> as the true "fevers" of the body and mind that prevent a person from experiencing peaceful sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that because he has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>abandoned lust</b></a> and cut it off at the root like a palm stump, he sleeps happily regardless of harsh external conditions like cold winds or hard ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>brahmin who has attained Nibbāna</b></a> always sleeps happily because they are no longer "smeared" by sensual pleasures and have become cooled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>palm stump</b></a> to describe how a Tathāgata has rendered the defilements of hatred and delusion incapable of any future arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha maintained a state of happiness while wearing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>thin saffron robes</b></a> and sleeping outdoors during the "eight days of frost" when the winter winds are most biting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the enlightened mind as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>cut all attachments</b></a> and removed the heart's distress, which leads to the ultimate peace of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a wealthy person with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>couch covered in fleecy covers</b></a> and woolens might still burn with the fevers of hatred, causing them to sleep in pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to himself as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>attained to peace</b></a>, a state where one is "without acquisitions" and thus sleeps happily every day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the "fevers" born of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> are more disruptive to a person's rest than the physical discomfort of sleeping on the rough, cattle-trampled earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by Hatthaka if he slept well, the Buddha confirmed his happiness by stating he is among those in the world who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>sleep happily</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>cooled</b></a> (sītibhūto) as a primary characteristic of one who is no longer stuck in the heat of sensual cravings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the detailed luxury of a householder's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>pillows and oil lamps</b></a> to illustrate that external comfort cannot replace the spiritual freedom that leads to true rest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's peaceful demeanor while sitting in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato"><b>Siṃsapā Grove</b></a> prompted Hatthaka of Āḷavī to approach and ask him about the quality of his sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.35/en/sujato">AN 3.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four distinct types of effort known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato"><b>four strivings</b></a>: the striving by restraint, the striving by abandonment, the striving by development, and the striving by protection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato">AN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the striving by restraint involves guarding the eye faculty to prevent evil unwholesome states such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato"><b>greed and sorrow</b></a> from flowing into the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato">AN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the striving by abandonment requires a monk to not tolerate an arisen <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato"><b>thought of sensuality</b></a>, but instead to dispel it, eliminate it, and make it cease to exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato">AN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the striving by development as the cultivation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a>, which include mindfulness, investigation of phenomena, energy, rapture, tranquility, concentration, and equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato">AN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the striving by protection consists of guarding a favorable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato"><b>sign of concentration</b></a>, such as the perception of a skeleton or a corpse that is discolored or swollen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato">AN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk who is ardent in practicing the four strivings can reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato"><b>destruction of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato">AN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four ways of "going wrong," which include acting out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.17/en/sujato"><b>desire, hatred, delusion, or fear</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.17/en/sujato">AN 4.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a person transgresses the Dhamma through desire, hatred, fear, or delusion, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.17/en/sujato"><b>reputation</b></a> will dwindle like the moon in the dark fortnight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.17/en/sujato">AN 4.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the striving by restraint includes not seizing upon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato"><b>signs or secondary details</b></a> of things seen with the eye or heard with the ear to ensure the sense faculties remain guarded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato">AN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the striving by development as practicing enlightenment factors that are based upon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato"><b>seclusion, dispassion, and cessation</b></a>, ultimately maturing in relinquishment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato">AN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the striving by abandonment involves taking unwholesome states that have arisen, such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of ill-will</b></a> or cruelty, and making them cease to exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato">AN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended protecting the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato"><b>perception of a skeleton</b></a> or a worm-infested corpse as specific meditative signs to be maintained in the striving by protection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato">AN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any individual who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.17/en/sujato"><b>transgresses the Dhamma</b></a> because of desire, hatred, fear, or delusion will see their fame decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.17/en/sujato">AN 4.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to these four strivings as being taught by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato"><b>Kinsman of the Sun</b></a> to help practitioners reach the end of all pain and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.14/en/sujato">AN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the spiritual journey of a monk to someone descending into the water, noting that there are four specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>perils</b></a> to be expected in both situations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the "fear of waves" as a metaphor for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>anger and exasperation</b></a> a new monk might feel when receiving correction or advice from fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>fear of crocodiles</b></a> as a designation for gluttony, specifically regarding the resentment a monk feels when being told what and when they are permitted to eat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the "fear of whirlpools" as a metaphor for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a> that can pull a practitioner back into the lay life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the "fear of sharks" to represent the danger of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> that can arise in a monk's mind when seeing a woman while on almsround with unguarded senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a monk becomes angry and gives up the training because they resent being instructed on how to dress or behave, they are said to have succumbed to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>fear of waves</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, some people go forth into homelessness because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>overwhelmed by suffering</b></a> and seek to find the end of the entire mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a monk who resents being told not to eat at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>wrong time</b></a>—after noon—is essentially being defeated by the metaphoric peril of a crocodile? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>unguarded body</b></a>, speech, and mind who enters a village for alms is particularly vulnerable to the "fear of whirlpools"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the desire to return to a household life to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>enjoy wealth</b></a> and make merit can be a spiritual peril comparable to being caught in a whirlpool? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>women</b></a> as the spiritual peril designated by the term "fear of sharks" for a monk who has gone forth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the training as involving instructions on how to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>advance and retreat</b></a>, how to look ahead and aside, and how to carry one's robes and bowl? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that seeing someone enjoying the five cords of sensual pleasure can lead a monk to give up the training and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>return to the lower life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a monk might feel that rules regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>allowable food</b></a> and eating times are an attempt by others to muzzle or restrict them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the "fear of sharks" occurs when a monk sees a woman who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>ill-clad</b></a> or poorly covered, causing lust to invade his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that some monks struggle with discipline because they remember being laypeople who gave <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>advice and instructions</b></a> to others and now resent receiving them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that the peril of whirlpools arises when a practitioner lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>established mindfulness</b></a> while interacting with the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the four dangers of water—waves, crocodiles, whirlpools, and sharks—as direct analogies for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>challenges</b></a> faced by those in the Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk's mind can be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>invaded by lust</b></a> if they encounter tempting sights without having restrained their senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who succumbs to any of the four perils and leaves the monkhood is said to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>disavowed the training</b></a> and returned to the lay life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the "fear of crocodiles" specifically as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>devotion to the belly</b></a>, which makes a practitioner sensitive to food-related restrictions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for one who has gone forth out of faith, maintaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato"><b>restrained senses</b></a> is crucial to avoid being overwhelmed by the perils of sensual pleasure and lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.122/en/sujato">AN 4.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four types of persons existing in the world based on their attainment of different <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>meditative absorptions</b></a> and the specific heavenly destinations they reach after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner who attains the first jhāna—a state of rapture and happiness born of seclusion—is reborn among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>Brahmakāyika devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the life span of the celestial beings known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>Brahmakāyika devas</b></a> is one aeon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an ordinary person who lives out a full life span in the Brahmakāyika heaven might still later be reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a>, the animal realm, or the sphere of afflicted spirits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that his disciple, after living a full life span in the Brahmakāyika realm, attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>final Nibbāna</b></a> in that very state and does not return to lower realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the second jhāna as a meditative state of internal confidence and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>unification of mind</b></a> that is free from thought and examination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who master the second jhāna and remain committed to that state are reborn after death among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>Ābhassara devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the life span of the celestial beings known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>Ābhassara devas</b></a> is two aeons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted a significant difference between an ordinary person and a noble disciple regarding their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>future destination</b></a> once their life span in the Ābhassara heavenly realm comes to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the third jhāna as a state where the meditator is equanimous and mindful, experiencing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>happiness with the body</b></a> that noble ones describe as 'dwelling happily'? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that practitioners of the third jhāna who do not fall away from their practice are reborn among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>Subhakiṇha devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the life span of the celestial beings known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>Subhakiṇha devas</b></a> is four aeons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that while an ordinary person might fall into the animal realm after their time in the Subhakiṇha heaven, a disciple of the Buddha attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>final Nibbāna</b></a> right there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the fourth jhāna as a state of neither-pain-nor-pleasure and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>purity of mindfulness</b></a> due to equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the destination for one who masters the fourth jhāna and remains dedicated to it is rebirth among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>Vehapphala devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the life span of the celestial beings known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>Vehapphala devas</b></a> is five hundred aeons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even an ordinary person who attains the highest meditative states can still end up in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>sphere of afflicted spirits</b></a> once their celestial life span is exhausted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the first jhāna as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>secluded from sensual pleasures</b></a> and unwholesome states, accompanied by thought and examination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>aeon</b></a> to describe the vast units of time that different classes of celestial beings live in their respective realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>noble disciple</b></a> differs from an ordinary person because the disciple attains parinibbāna within the heavenly realm rather than returning to a lower existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the second jhāna as a meditative state born of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> and characterized by rapture and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.123/en/sujato">AN 4.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after his enlightenment, while staying at the foot of the Goatherd’s Banyan Tree, the Buddha reflected that living without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>respect and deference</b></a> is a painful experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha searched for an ascetic or brahmin to live under as a teacher, but he could not find anyone in the world of devas, Māras, or Brahmās who was more <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>perfect in virtue</b></a> than himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha investigated whether there was any being superior to him in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of concentration</b></a> to whom he could show honor, but he found no such person in the entire universe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared he saw no one in the world of humans or deities who possessed a more <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>complete aggregate of wisdom</b></a> than his own? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha looked for someone with a more <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>perfect aggregate of liberation</b></a> than himself to live under, but he concluded that no such person existed in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because he found no superior teacher, the Buddha decided he would live honoring, respecting, and depending on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>very Dhamma</b></a> to which he had awakened? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati, knowing the Buddha's internal reflection, vanished from the Brahma world and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>appeared before the Buddha</b></a> as quickly as a strong man might extend his arm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati encouraged the Buddha's decision by stating that all the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>Perfectly Enlightened Ones</b></a> of the past also lived honoring and respecting the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Brahmā Sahampati, all the Buddhas of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>future</b></a> will also live honoring and depending on the Dhamma as their teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati described it as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>nature of the Buddhas</b></a> to dwell honoring the True Dhamma, whether they are from the past, future, or present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was described by Brahmā Sahampati as the one who is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>destroyer of sorrow</b></a> for many people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati advised that anyone who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>desires greatness</b></a> and loves themselves should hold the True Dhamma in high esteem? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, after considering Brahmā Sahampati's request, chose to live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>depending on the Dhamma</b></a> he had realized, honoring it as his superior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that although the Buddha honors the Dhamma above all, he also mentioned that he has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>respect for the Sangha</b></a> now that it has attained greatness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Brahmā Sahampati appeared to the Buddha, he showed his respect by arranging his upper robe over one shoulder and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>kneeling on the ground</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted these events about his early days after enlightenment while he was staying at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>Anāthapiṇḍika’s Monastery</b></a> in Jeta’s Grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly searched for someone with a more <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>complete aggregate of virtue</b></a> to fulfill his own training, only to find no one superior to himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati used a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>verse</b></a> to remind the Buddha that honoring the Dhamma is a sacred tradition among all Buddhas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s reflection on the pain of living without a teacher occurred while he was sitting in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> near the Nerañjarā River? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that honoring the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato"><b>True Dhamma</b></a> is a way for a practitioner to remember the teachings of the Buddhas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.21/en/sujato">AN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>bases of liberation</b></a> where a diligent, ardent, and resolute monk’s unliberated mind can reach freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the first base of liberation occurs when a teacher or a fellow monk in the position of a teacher <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>teaches the Dhamma</b></a> to a practitioner, leading to an experience of its meaning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk experiences the meaning and the Dhamma while listening to a teacher, a sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>gladness is born</b></a> within him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk can reach the destruction of the taints by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>teaching the Dhamma</b></a> in detail to others exactly as he has heard and learned it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>reciting the Dhamma</b></a> in detail, as it has been heard and learned, is a distinct base of liberation that can lead to concentration of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a practitioner who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>thinks about and ponders</b></a> the Dhamma in their mind can experience a deep sense of gladness and rapture, leading toward liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one can attain liberation by properly grasping, attending to, and penetrating a certain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>object of concentration</b></a> with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the mind is filled with rapture through the Dhamma, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>body becomes tranquil</b></a>, which then leads to the experience of happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the attainment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>supreme security from bondage</b></a> as a primary goal for those who dwell diligently in the five bases of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that when a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>experiences the meaning</b></a> of the Dhamma while teaching it to others, his mind eventually becomes concentrated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the sequential process where a practitioner with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>tranquil body</b></a> feels happiness, which then causes the mind to become concentrated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for liberation to occur, a monk must live as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>diligent and ardent</b></a> while engaging with the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that examining the Dhamma with the mind allows a monk to become an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>experiencer of the Dhamma</b></a>, which triggers gladness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>rapture is born</b></a> in a person who is filled with gladness after experiencing the depth of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the destruction of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>undestroyed taints</b></a> (āsavā) as a benefit for those who practice the five bases of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>properly grasped</b></a> an object of concentration through wisdom, it serves as the fifth base of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who feels <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a> derived from the Dhamma finds that their mind becomes concentrated as a result? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically mentioned that listening to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>fellow monk</b></a> in the position of a teacher can be the catalyst for the mind to be liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the act of pondering and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>examining the Dhamma</b></a> with the mind is just as effective for liberation as listening to a teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a practitioner with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>rapture in mind</b></a>, the body becomes tranquil, which is a necessary step toward concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that one can reach <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed security</b></a> from bondage by reciting the Dhamma in detail as they have previously learned it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.26/en/sujato">AN 5.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who lacks faith, moral shame, moral dread, is lazy, and is unwise, dwells in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato"><b>suffering in this very life</b></a>, experiencing vexation, despair, and fever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato">AN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who is possessed of five negative qualities—being faithless, shameless, unafraid of wrongdoing, lazy, and unwise—can expect a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato"><b>bad destination</b></a> after the breakup of the body and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato">AN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five specific qualities—faith, moral shame, moral dread, aroused energy, and wisdom—that allow a monk to dwell in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato"><b>happiness in this very life</b></a> without vexation or despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato">AN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses the five wholesome qualities of faith, moral shame, moral dread, energy, and wisdom can expect a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato"><b>good destination</b></a> in the afterlife? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato">AN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk or nun abandons the training to return to the lower life, they face five <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.5/en/sujato"><b>grounds for criticism</b></a> from their peers, specifically that they lacked faith, moral shame, moral dread, energy, and wisdom regarding wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.5/en/sujato">AN 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that a monk or nun who perseveres in the perfectly complete and pure holy life, even while weeping and in pain, receives five <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.5/en/sujato"><b>grounds for praise</b></a> because they maintain faith and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.5/en/sujato">AN 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato"><b>moral shame and moral dread</b></a> regarding unwholesome actions is a primary factor that leads a practitioner to live a life filled with suffering and distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato">AN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato"><b>energetic and wise</b></a> regarding wholesome qualities is able to live without the internal "fever" of mind that afflicts those who are lazy and unwise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato">AN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even if a person is in deep emotional distress, they are still considered to be living a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.5/en/sujato"><b>pure holy life</b></a> if they possess qualities like faith, energy, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.5/en/sujato">AN 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a person who gives up the spiritual training will be specifically criticized for having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.5/en/sujato"><b>no energy</b></a> in wholesome states, which is one of the five grounds for blame in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.5/en/sujato">AN 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the absence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.5/en/sujato"><b>faith in wholesome states</b></a> is a justifiable reason for one's fellow practitioners to find fault with a monk or nun who abandons their vows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.5/en/sujato">AN 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for those who possess faith, moral shame, and moral dread, they can live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato"><b>without vexation</b></a> and without despair in their daily lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.3/en/sujato">AN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that approximately five hundred Licchavis gathered at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato"><b>Sarandada Shrine</b></a> to hold a discussion regarding five specific types of treasures that they considered rare in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato">AN 5.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Licchavis believed the appearance of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato"><b>elephant treasure</b></a> is one of the five rare things that is difficult to find in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato">AN 5.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the Licchavis were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato"><b>intent on sensual pleasures</b></a> because their conversation focused solely on worldly treasures such as jewels and wives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato">AN 5.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the appearance of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a>, an Arahant, a Perfectly Enlightened One, is a truly rare and difficult event to occur in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato">AN 5.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who teaches the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma and discipline</b></a> proclaimed by the Tathāgata as one of the five spiritual treasures that are rare in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato">AN 5.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato"><b>understands the Dhamma</b></a> and discipline proclaimed by the Tathāgata when it is being taught is a rare and difficult find? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato">AN 5.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato"><b>practices in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a> is considered a rare treasure among beings in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato">AN 5.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato"><b>grateful and thankful</b></a> person is one of the five rare individuals whose appearance is difficult to find in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato">AN 5.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Licchavis assigned a man to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato"><b>station himself</b></a> on the road to watch for the Buddha and report back to them as soon as he saw the Buddha approaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato">AN 5.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Licchavis considered the appearance of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato"><b>horse treasure</b></a> to be one of the five worldly objects that are exceptionally rare to find in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato">AN 5.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the appearance of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato"><b>jewel treasure</b></a> was listed by the Licchavis as one of the five things they believed to be exceptionally rare, which the Buddha later contrasted with spiritual rarities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato">AN 5.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Licchavis identified the appearance of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato"><b>householder treasure</b></a> as one of the world's rare treasures, highlighting their group's focus on worldly prosperity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato">AN 5.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was invited by the Licchavis to visit the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato"><b>Sarandada Shrine</b></a> specifically out of their desire for him to show them compassion by his presence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.143/en/sujato">AN 5.143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once stayed at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato"><b>Tikaṇḍakī Grove</b></a> in Sāketa, where he delivered a detailed teaching to the monks on how to consciously train their perceptions of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato">AN 5.144</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is beneficial for a monk to, from time to time, dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato"><b>perceiving the repulsive</b></a> in what is otherwise unrepulsive as a method of mental discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato">AN 5.144</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk should practice perceiving the repulsive in the unrepulsive specifically so that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> does not arise toward things that normally provoke lustful attraction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato">AN 5.144</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a practitioner should occasionally dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato"><b>perceiving the unrepulsive</b></a> in things that are generally considered repulsive to help balance the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato">AN 5.144</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that perceiving the unrepulsive in the repulsive is a training exercise designed to ensure that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> does not arise toward things that typically provoke anger or aversion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato">AN 5.144</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that a monk should sometimes practice perceiving the repulsive in both the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato"><b>unrepulsive and the repulsive</b></a> to simultaneously prevent the arising of both lust and hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato">AN 5.144</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner may choose to dwell perceiving the unrepulsive in both the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato"><b>repulsive and the unrepulsive</b></a> as a way to keep both hatred and lust from taking root in the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato">AN 5.144</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an advanced state of practice where a monk avoids perceptions of both the repulsive and the unrepulsive and instead dwells <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato"><b>equanimous</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato">AN 5.144</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato"><b>mindful and clearly comprehending</b></a> is essential when a monk chooses to discard dualistic perceptions to maintain a state of perfect equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato">AN 5.144</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the ultimate goal of dwelling in equanimity—avoiding both repulsive and unrepulsive perceptions—is to prevent lust, hatred, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> from arising in any way or in any place? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.144/en/sujato">AN 5.144</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>divine messengers</b></a> that appear among human beings as reminders to practice virtue before it is too late? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who engages in misconduct by body, speech, and mind is handed over to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>King Yama</b></a> by the hell wardens after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the first divine messenger as an old person, broken-toothed and grey-haired, appearing as a warning that one is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>exempt from old age</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how King Yama interrogates those in hell, asking if they saw the second divine messenger in the form of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>sick person</b></a> struggling in their own excrement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the third divine messenger is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>corpse</b></a>, bloated and blue, which serves as a final warning that all humans are subject to death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the bad kamma leading to hell is not created by one's parents, siblings, or friends, but is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>personally created</b></a> by the individual who must experience its results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a specific torture in hell called the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>fivefold pinning</b></a>, where red-hot iron stakes are driven through a person's hands, feet, and chest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person in hell does not die until their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>evil kamma</b></a> is completely exhausted, despite enduring excruciating and sharp pains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha detailed how hell wardens use adzes and axes to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>carve the limbs</b></a> of those who neglected to do good deeds while they were humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned a torture where beings are harnessed to a chariot and forced to move back and forth across <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>burning ground</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described hell wardens forcing beings to climb up and down a massive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>mountain of embers</b></a> that is ablaze and glowing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>boiling copper cauldron</b></a> where beings are cooked, rising to the surface and sinking to the bottom amidst swirling foam? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>Great Hell</b></a> as a place with four corners and four gates, enclosed by an iron wall and covered with an iron roof? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the floor of the Great Hell is made of incandescent iron, and its flames spread for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>one hundred yojanas</b></a> in all directions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how King Yama once wished to be reborn as a human so he could meet a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a> and learn the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that his descriptions of hell and King Yama are based on his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> and vision, rather than something he heard from another ascetic or brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>negligent</b></a> despite being urged by the divine messengers will sorrow for a long time in inferior states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that "good people" (sappurisa) are those who are spurred to action by the divine messengers and never neglect the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>noble Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by seeing peril in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a>, one can be liberated through the destruction of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described King Yama’s interrogation as a process of checking if a person of sound mind and mature age failed to realize their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>mortal nature</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said those who are heedful and at peace are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>extinguished in this very life</b></a>, having transcended all enmity and fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that those who have overcome all suffering are those who are no longer <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato"><b>heedless</b></a> after being warned by the messengers of old age, sickness, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.36/en/sujato">AN 3.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying on the bank of the river Nerañjarā under the Ajapāla banyan tree when he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>first enlightened</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that some elderly brahmins once criticized the Buddha, claiming he did not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>show respect</b></a> to them because he did not bow down, rise for them, or offer them a seat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an eighty, ninety, or one hundred-year-old person is merely a "foolish senior" if they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained in speech</b></a> and speak what is unbeneficial, false, and contrary to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a young person in the prime of youth can be considered a "wise senior" if they speak the truth and are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>restrained by the Dhamma</b></a> and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>virtuous and restrained</b></a> by the Pātimokkha as the first of four specific qualities that make one a true senior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk should see <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>danger in slight faults</b></a> and train himself in the training rules to possess the qualities of a senior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a learned person as someone who has memorized and mastered the Dhamma which is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>good in the beginning</b></a>, middle, and end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a quality of a senior is being a "bearer of what has been learned" whose knowledge of the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>well penetrated by view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a true senior is someone who can obtain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>four jhānas</b></a>, which provide a pleasant dwelling in this very life, at will and without difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the highest quality of a senior is the realization of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>taintless liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom through the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person with an agitated mind who talks nonsense and has unsettled thoughts is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>far from seniority</b></a>, regardless of their chronological age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>abandoned birth and death</b></a> and completed the holy life is a person he truly calls a "senior"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that "seniority" is defined by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> rather than just by the number of years a person has lived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a "wise senior" is one whose speech is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato"><b>worth recording</b></a>, reasonable, and always connected with the goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.22/en/sujato">AN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who dwells pervading the entire world with a mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> and maintains that state until death will be reborn among the gods of Brahma’s company? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato">AN 4.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the lifespan of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato"><b>Brahmakāyika gods</b></a>, the celestial destination for those who master the meditation on loving-kindness, is exactly one eon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato">AN 4.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by pervading the world with a mind of compassion, a person can be reborn among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato"><b>Ābhassara gods</b></a>, who possess a lifespan of two eons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato">AN 4.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato"><b>Subhakiṇha gods</b></a> as the rebirth destination for those who dwell pervading the world with a mind of altruistic joy, and that these deities live for four eons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato">AN 4.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the longest lifespan among the four heavenly rebirths mentioned in this sutta belongs to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato"><b>Vehapphala gods</b></a>, who live for five hundred eons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato">AN 4.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person can be reborn among the Vehapphala gods by dwelling with a mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> that is pervaded through the whole world as abundant, exalted, and immeasurable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato">AN 4.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted a significant difference in future destiny, noting that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato"><b>ordinary person</b></a> may still go to hell, the animal realm, or the sphere of afflicted spirits after their life in the Brahma heavens is completely exhausted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato">AN 4.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that unlike an uninstructed worldling, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato"><b>noble disciple</b></a> who is reborn in these high heavens through meditative development will attain final Nibbāna in that very state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato">AN 4.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the practice of the divine abodes as pervading the entire world—above, below, and across—with a mind that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato"><b>without hostility</b></a> and without ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato">AN 4.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the distinction between a worldling and a noble disciple as the difference in their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato"><b>future destination</b></a> following the exhaustion of their lifespan in these various Brahma realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato">AN 4.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that to achieve these specific rebirths, a practitioner must find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato"><b>gratification</b></a> in the meditative state and remain focused on it as they pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato">AN 4.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a mind developed through states like loving-kindness or equanimity becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable</b></a> and exalted, reaching out to all beings everywhere as if they were oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.125/en/sujato">AN 4.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the loud noise made by a large crowd of Brahmin householders outside his gate to the sound of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>fishermen</b></a> hauling in a large catch of fish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha expressed a desire to avoid being associated with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>fame and renown</b></a>, stating that he wanted nothing to do with it and did not want it to come to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who cannot easily obtain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>bliss of renunciation</b></a> or the bliss of enlightenment might instead indulge in the "dung-like" pleasure of gain, honor, and renown? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the natural result for one who eats, drinks, and consumes food is the production of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>excrement and urine</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that sorrow, lamentation, and pain are the inevitable outcomes that arise from things that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>held dear</b></a> when they undergo change and alteration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person is devoted to meditation on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>unattractive</b></a>, they will naturally find the "beautiful" to be repulsive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by dwelling as a contemplator of impermanence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>six contact bases</b></a>, a person develops a sense of repulsion toward contact itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that contemplating the rise and fall of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> leads to a person finding clinging to be repulsive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Nagita served as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>attendant</b></a> to the Buddha and was the one who explained that the loud noise outside was caused by Brahmin householders bringing food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Nagita used the metaphor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>rainwater</b></a> flowing down to low ground to describe how people from all over would naturally follow the Buddha wherever he went? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Nagita argued that people were drawn to the Buddha because of his exceptional <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>virtue and wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha made a sharp distinction between the pleasure of gain and renown and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>bliss of seclusion</b></a>, which he could obtain whenever he wanted without difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically referred to the pleasure derived from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>drowsiness</b></a> as a type of pleasure that those who fail to find the bliss of enlightenment might settle for? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>bliss of peace</b></a> as one of the superior types of happiness that he was able to gain easily and without toil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>dung-like pleasure</b></a> to describe the satisfaction found in gain, honor, and praise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if one can effortlessly attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>bliss of enlightenment</b></a>, they would not indulge in the inferior pleasures of fame or material gain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha stayed at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato"><b>Icchanangala</b></a>, he insisted on his preference for solitude despite Nagita's request for him to accept the large offerings from the public? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.30/en/sujato">AN 5.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised monks not to associate with a fellow monk as a friend if he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.146/en/sujato"><b>makes one work</b></a> or engages in secular chores? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.146/en/sujato">AN 5.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.146/en/sujato"><b>engages in litigation</b></a> or legal disputes is one whom a person should not seek out as a friend? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.146/en/sujato">AN 5.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.146/en/sujato"><b>antagonistic toward senior monks</b></a> should not be associated with as a friend? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.146/en/sujato">AN 5.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned against befriending a monk who is addicted to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.146/en/sujato"><b>aimless wandering</b></a> and does not maintain a settled residence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.146/en/sujato">AN 5.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that a worthy friend is a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.146/en/sujato"><b>capable of instructing</b></a> others with a Dhamma talk from time to time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.146/en/sujato">AN 5.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.147/en/sujato"><b>careless giving</b></a>, or giving without proper respect, as one of the five types of gifts given by an unworthy person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.147/en/sujato">AN 5.147</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the gift of a worthy person as one where the donor is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.147/en/sujato"><b>giving with one's own hand</b></a> rather than through an intermediary? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.147/en/sujato">AN 5.147</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a worthy person gives with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.147/en/sujato"><b>view of the future</b></a>, understanding that the gift will bear fruit, whereas an unworthy person gives without such consideration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.147/en/sujato">AN 5.147</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.148/en/sujato"><b>giving with faith</b></a> results in one becoming wealthy and having an extremely beautiful and attractive appearance in future lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.148/en/sujato">AN 5.148</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.148/en/sujato"><b>giving carefully</b></a> results in wealth and also ensures that one's children, spouse, and employees are obedient and attentive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.148/en/sujato">AN 5.148</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.148/en/sujato"><b>giving at the right time</b></a> leads to great wealth and ensures that one's needs are met abundantly exactly when they arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.148/en/sujato">AN 5.148</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that giving with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.148/en/sujato"><b>unattached heart</b></a> leads to wealth and a mind that is naturally inclined to enjoy excellent sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.148/en/sujato">AN 5.148</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.148/en/sujato"><b>giving without harming</b></a> oneself or others ensures that one's future wealth is safe from destruction by fire, water, kings, or thieves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.148/en/sujato">AN 5.148</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.147/en/sujato"><b>giving something discarded</b></a>—as if throwing it away—as a characteristic of a gift from an unworthy person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.147/en/sujato">AN 5.147</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.146/en/sujato"><b>lacks the power</b></a> to gladden and encourage others with Dhamma talk is a friend who should not be associated with? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.146/en/sujato">AN 5.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.147/en/sujato"><b>giving thoughtfully</b></a> is a hallmark of a worthy person, distinguishing them from those who give without consideration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.147/en/sujato">AN 5.147</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that on the eighth day of the fortnight, the ministers and councilors of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>Four Great Kings</b></a> wander through the world to see if people are living righteously and honoring their parents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>sons</b></a> of the Four Great Kings personally patrol the world on the fourteenth day of the fortnight to observe if humans are practicing merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that on the fifteenth day of the fortnight, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>Four Great Kings</b></a> themselves wander the world to check if many people are observing the Uposatha and honoring recluses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how the Tāvatiṃsa gods gather in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>Sudhamma assembly hall</b></a> to receive reports from the Four Great Kings about the conduct of human beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Tāvatiṃsa gods become displeased and worry that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>divine hosts</b></a> will decline if they hear that only a few humans are living virtuously? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the Tāvatiṃsa gods rejoice when they hear that many humans are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>honoring the elders</b></a> in their families and keeping the Uposatha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Tāvatiṃsa gods' fear that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>Asura hosts</b></a> will fill up if human beings fail to practice merit and honor their duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>Sakka, Lord of the Gods</b></a>, once recited a verse encouraging his fellow gods to observe the eight-factored Uposatha on the specific days of the lunar month? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha critiqued a verse by Sakka, stating it was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>poorly stated</b></a> because Sakka himself is not yet free from greed, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that only an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a>, who has destroyed the taints and is free from greed, hatred, and delusion, is truly qualified to recommend the Uposatha by saying "one like me"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the eighth, fourteenth, and fifteenth days as the primary times for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>Uposatha observance</b></a>, during which celestial beings monitor human behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that celestial beings look for humans who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>filial toward their mothers</b></a> and fathers when they travel through the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned the Tāvatiṃsa gods are elated when they believe the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>divine hosts</b></a> will increase due to the high number of humans living righteously? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>respectful to recluses</b></a> and brahmins is one of the key virtues reported by the Four Great Kings to the heavenly assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an Arahant is someone who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>laid down the burden</b></a> and attained the true goal, making their endorsement of the Uposatha more appropriate than that of Sakka? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato"><b>eight-factored Uposatha</b></a> should be observed on the eighth, fourteenth, and fifteenth days, as well as during the "extraordinary fortnight"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.37/en/sujato">AN 3.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he has fully awakened to the world, the origin of the world, the cessation of the world, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato"><b>way leading to the cessation</b></a> of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato">AN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained he is called the "Tathagata" because everything he speaks, says, or explains from the night of his enlightenment until his final Nibbana is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato"><b>exactly so</b></a> and not otherwise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato">AN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to have fully awakened to everything in the world that is seen, heard, sensed, or cognized, including everything <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato"><b>examined by the mind</b></a> of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato">AN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared he is known as the "Tathagata" because he is one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato"><b>acts as he speaks</b></a> and speaks as he acts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato">AN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described as the conqueror who is unconquered by anyone, an all-seeing wielder of power who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato"><b>reached supreme peace</b></a> and Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato">AN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is characterized as a wise sage who, having understood the whole world as it really is, remains completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato"><b>detached from the world</b></a> and its bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato">AN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is referred to as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato"><b>unexcelled lion</b></a> who set the Wheel of Brahma in motion for the benefit of both devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato">AN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is recognized as the one who has reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato"><b>destruction of all kamma</b></a> and is fully liberated through the destruction of the acquisitions of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato">AN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is praised as the best of those who tame others, the sage among those who bring peace, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato"><b>foremost of those who release</b></a> others from the suffering of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato">AN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato"><b>no person equal</b></a> to him in the entire world of devas, Maras, and Brahmas, or among the population of deities and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.23/en/sujato">AN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner who pervades the world with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> must also contemplate the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—as impermanent and suffering to attain a higher rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato">AN 4.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who dwells in a state of compassion but views the five aggregates as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato"><b>disease and a tumor</b></a> can be reborn in the Pure Abodes, a destination not shared with ordinary worldlings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato">AN 4.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a specific meditation where one pervades the entire world, above, below, and across, with a mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato"><b>altruistic joy</b></a> that is vast, boundless, and free from enmity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato">AN 4.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified eleven distinct ways to view the five aggregates during meditation, including seeing them as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato"><b>dart and an affliction</b></a>, to ensure one progresses toward a divine rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato">AN 4.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> while perceiving the five aggregates as alien, empty, and non-self leads to a companionship with the Suddhāvāsa (Pure Abodes) deities after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato">AN 4.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a>, which include consciousness and volitional formations, should be viewed as "disintegrating" and "void" by those seeking to transcend common worldly existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato">AN 4.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that while many can practice the four immeasurable states, the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato"><b>insight into non-self</b></a> applied to the aggregates makes the resulting rebirth in the Pure Abodes unique to advanced practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato">AN 4.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the practice of pervading all directions with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable mind</b></a> of loving-kindness as a path that, when combined with wisdom, bypasses the standard destinations of the "puthujjana" or worldling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato">AN 4.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even the most sublime mental states, like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a>, must be paired with the realization that all form and feeling are "other" and "misery" to achieve liberation from the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.126/en/sujato">AN 4.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato"><b>wonderful and marvelous</b></a> qualities appear in the world only when a Tathāgata, an Arahant, a Perfectly Enlightened One, manifests? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato">AN 4.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that although people generally delight in and are attached to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a>, they will actually listen and pay attention when a Tathāgata teaches the Dhamma of non-attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato">AN 4.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted as a marvel that people who normally revel in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a> and pride will apply their minds to understand when he teaches the Dhamma specifically for the removal of conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato">AN 4.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted it is extraordinary that people who are typically fond of excitement and lack of peace will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato"><b>lend an ear</b></a> to teachings regarding peace and tranquility when they are delivered by a Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato">AN 4.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared people living in ignorance to being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato"><b>enclosed in an egg</b></a>, yet they still show a surprising willingness to listen when a Tathāgata teaches how to remove that ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato">AN 4.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the manifestation of a Tathāgata is the rare occasion when a generation of people immersed in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> will finally set their hearts on gaining knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato">AN 4.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the people's willingness to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato"><b>fix their minds</b></a> on the teaching of non-attachment as one of the four most amazing phenomena associated with the appearance of an Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato">AN 4.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized humanity as generally being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato"><b>rejoicing in worldliness</b></a>, which makes their sudden interest in the Dhamma of peace a truly marvelous occurrence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato">AN 4.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the appearance of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a> is the sole cause for the manifestation of these four specific marvelous qualities that transform how people respond to spiritual truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.128/en/sujato">AN 4.128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Princess Sumanā once approached the Buddha while accompanied by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>five hundred chariots</b></a> and five hundred royal princesses?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was asked by Princess Sumanā whether there would be a difference between two disciples of equal faith, virtue, and wisdom if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>one was a giver</b></a> and the other was not?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a giver, when reborn as a deva, surpasses a non-giver in five ways: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>divine life span</b></a>, beauty, happiness, fame, and authority?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even when reborn as a human being, a person who was a giver continues to surpass a non-giver in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>human life span</b></a> and human beauty?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person who practiced giving will, upon being reborn as a human, enjoy more <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>human happiness</b></a>, fame, and authority than one who did not give?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that if two such individuals leave the household life for homelessness, the one who was a giver will mostly receive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>robes and almsfood</b></a> when invited rather than uninvited?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monastic who was previously a giver will receive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>lodgings and medicinal requisites</b></a> mostly through invitations, whereas a non-giver receives them mostly uninvited?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that fellow monastics will treat a former giver with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>pleasing bodily actions</b></a> and pleasing verbal actions more often than they would a non-giver?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monastic who was a giver in the past is more likely to be the recipient of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>pleasing mental actions</b></a> and pleasing offerings from their companions in the holy life?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that if both a giver and a non-giver attain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a>, there is absolutely no difference or distinction between them in terms of their liberation?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that "liberation is the same as liberation," meaning a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>non-giver who becomes an Arahant</b></a> is not inferior in their release from suffering to an Arahant who was a giver?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Princess Sumanā concluded it is truly worthwhile to give gifts and perform merit because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>meritorious deeds</b></a> are helpful to devas, humans, and those who have gone forth?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha fully agreed with Princess Sumanā, affirming that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>merit is helpful</b></a> for beings in all states of existence, including those in the monastic life?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person endowed with virtue and faith who practices giving to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>spotless moon</b></a> which outshines all the stars in the world?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>thundering rain cloud</b></a> with a hundred peaks and wreaths of lightning to describe how a generous person benefits the earth, both high and low?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise disciple of the Fully Enlightened One surpasses a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>stingy person</b></a> in five specific ways: life span, fame, beauty, happiness, and authority?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a generous person as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>abounding in wealth</b></a> and destined to rejoice in the heavenly world after death?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that a giver reborn as a deva will specifically surpass others in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.31/en/sujato"><b>divine authority</b></a> and fame?</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who has attained a temporary state of liberation will suffer a decline in his spiritual progress if he indulges in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.150/en/sujato"><b>delight in work</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.150/en/sujato">AN 5.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk’s attainment of temporary liberation can be lost if he becomes characterized by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.150/en/sujato"><b>delight in talk</b></a> rather than maintaining his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.150/en/sujato">AN 5.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.150/en/sujato"><b>delight in sleep</b></a> as one of the five specific factors that contribute to the falling away or decline of a monk who has reached temporary liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.150/en/sujato">AN 5.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.150/en/sujato"><b>unguarded sense doors</b></a> are a primary cause for a monk to lose the benefits of his temporary liberation and return to a state of decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.150/en/sujato">AN 5.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.150/en/sujato"><b>immoderation in eating</b></a> is a quality that leads directly to the spiritual decline of a monk who has achieved a temporary state of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.150/en/sujato">AN 5.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk can ensure he does not decline from his temporary liberation by practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.150/en/sujato"><b>moderation in eating</b></a> and avoiding a delight in worldly activities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.150/en/sujato">AN 5.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an individual is incapable of entering the fixed course of rightness regarding wholesome states if they listen to the Dhamma while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.151/en/sujato"><b>despising the talk</b></a> itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.151/en/sujato">AN 5.151</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one cannot enter the fixed course of rightness if they listen to the Dhamma while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.151/en/sujato"><b>despising the speaker</b></a> who is delivering the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.151/en/sujato">AN 5.151</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.151/en/sujato"><b>despising oneself</b></a> is a significant mental hindrance that prevents a person from successfully entering the fixed course of rightness while hearing the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.151/en/sujato">AN 5.151</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that listening to the True Dhamma with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.151/en/sujato"><b>distracted mind</b></a> makes it impossible for an individual to enter the fixed course of rightness in wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.151/en/sujato">AN 5.151</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who lacks a focused mind and practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.151/en/sujato"><b>unwise attention</b></a> is incapable of entering the fixed course of rightness, even while hearing the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.151/en/sujato">AN 5.151</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who listens to the Dhamma with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.153/en/sujato"><b>disparaging mind</b></a>, being overwhelmed by denigration, is unable to enter the fixed course of rightness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.153/en/sujato">AN 5.153</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that listening to a teaching with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.153/en/sujato"><b>fault-finding mind</b></a>, looking for mistakes in the presentation, prevents one from entering the fixed course of rightness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.153/en/sujato">AN 5.153</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.153/en/sujato"><b>hostile toward the teacher</b></a> during a Dhamma talk creates a mental obstruction that keeps one from the fixed course of rightness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.153/en/sujato">AN 5.153</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.153/en/sujato"><b>dull and stupid</b></a> is naturally incapable of entering the fixed course of rightness when hearing the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.153/en/sujato">AN 5.153</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the habit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.153/en/sujato"><b>presuming to understand</b></a> what one has not yet actually understood as a barrier to entering the fixed course of rightness in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.153/en/sujato">AN 5.153</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recalled how his father had several <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>lotus ponds</b></a>—some with blue lotuses, some with red, and others with white—created solely for his enjoyment during his youth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his early life as so delicate that he wore only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>Kāsian cloth</b></a> for his turbans, jackets, and robes, as this was the finest material available? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned he had a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>white sunshade</b></a> held over him day and night as a youth to protect him from cold, heat, dust, and dew? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha lived in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>three palaces</b></a> specially built for him by his father: one for the winter, one for the summer, and one for the rainy season? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>four months</b></a> of the rainy season inside a palace being entertained by female musicians, without once descending to the lower floors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted how his father's household provided <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>servants</b></a> and workers with fine rice and meat, while other households typically fed their workers broken rice and sour gruel? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha realized that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>untaught worldling</b></a>, though subject to aging themselves, is often repelled and disgusted when they see another person who is old? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha overcame his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>intoxication with youth</b></a> after reflecting that he, too, was subject to aging and could not escape it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reflected that it would be inappropriate for someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>subject to illness</b></a> to be repelled by the sight of a sick person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>intoxication with health</b></a> was completely abandoned when he realized that he could not avoid the nature of sickness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pondered how people who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>subject to death</b></a> often feel disgusted when seeing a dead person, forgetting their own mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha abandoned his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>intoxication with life</b></a> after contemplating that he himself was subject to death and had not transcended it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being intoxicated with youth, health, or life causes people to engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>misconduct</b></a> through their body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that individuals who live with misconduct due to worldly intoxications face rebirth in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a> or other painful realms after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that even a monk might <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>give up the training</b></a> and return to a lower life if they become intoxicated with their own youth, health, or life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha found <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>security in renunciation</b></a> by seeing the freedom that comes from letting go of the intoxications of youth, health, and life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he became incapable of indulging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> once he had clearly seen the state of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha became fully committed to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> after recognizing that the common human reaction of disgust toward the old and sick was not fitting for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that seeing the truth of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> provides an ussāho (enthusiasm or zeal) that overcomes all worldly intoxications? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato"><b>intoxications</b></a>—youth, health, and life—that prevent people from seeing the reality of their own existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.39/en/sujato">AN 3.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to have fully understood everything that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato"><b>seen, heard, sensed, and cognized</b></a> by all beings in the world, including devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato">AN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if he were to say he does not know what is seen, heard, or sensed by the world, it would be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato"><b>falsehood</b></a> on his part? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato">AN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even though the Buddha has direct knowledge of the world, he does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato"><b>conceive</b></a> of the seen, the unseen, what is to be seen, or the one who sees? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato">AN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato"><b>conceive</b></a> of himself as a "hearer" or a "cognizer," nor does he identify with what is heard or cognized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato">AN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato"><b>poised</b></a> (tādī) among all things seen, heard, sensed, and cognized, and that no one is considered more excellent than such a one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato">AN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that while other people are stuck in their obsessions with views, he has seen the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato"><b>dart</b></a> of clinging and remains without any such obsession? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato">AN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the spiritual life is not lived for the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato"><b>deceiving</b></a> or cajoling people, nor for the sake of gains, honor, and fame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato">AN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that the spiritual life is not lived so that one may be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato"><b>known by people</b></a> in a certain way or to win at debates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato">AN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the spiritual life is lived specifically for the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato"><b>restraint</b></a> and abandoning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato">AN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the spiritual life is lived for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> and cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato">AN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the spiritual life as a path that leads to and merges with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato"><b>Nibbana</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato">AN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the spiritual life is a path that has been followed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato"><b>great sages</b></a> (mahesibhi) throughout time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato">AN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that those who practice the spiritual life according to his teaching will ultimately make an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato">AN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to those who follow his instructions as those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato"><b>doers of the Teacher’s message</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.25/en/sujato">AN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said he has direct knowledge of everything <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato"><b>searched for</b></a> and examined by the minds of all beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato">AN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that regarding things seen, heard, or sensed, he does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato"><b>climb</b></a> onto them or rely on them as absolute truths in the way others do? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.24/en/sujato">AN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a type of person who is "withdrawn in body but not in mind," referring to someone who lives in remote forest dwellings yet continues to entertain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.138/en/sujato"><b>sensual thoughts</b></a>, thoughts of ill will, and thoughts of harming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.138/en/sujato">AN 4.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person can be "not withdrawn in body but withdrawn in mind" if they do not live in secluded forests but consistently cultivate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.138/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of renunciation</b></a>, non-ill will, and non-harming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.138/en/sujato">AN 4.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an individual who neither lives in remote dwellings nor maintains a pure mind of renunciation is considered to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.138/en/sujato"><b>neither withdrawn in body nor mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.138/en/sujato">AN 4.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the ideal practitioner as one who is "withdrawn in both body and mind," meaning they reside in remote forest lodgings and maintain a mind focused on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.138/en/sujato"><b>non-ill will</b></a> and renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.138/en/sujato">AN 4.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described four types of persons based on whether they hold <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.137/en/sujato"><b>virtue, concentration, and wisdom</b></a> as their priority and as supreme qualities in their lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.137/en/sujato">AN 4.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a category of person who does not hold <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.137/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a>, concentration, or wisdom as a priority and does not view these qualities as supreme? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.137/en/sujato">AN 4.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that some people prioritize and hold <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.137/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> as supreme, yet they do not yet place the same importance on developing concentration or wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.137/en/sujato">AN 4.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that certain individuals may hold both virtue and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.137/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> as their priority and as supreme, while they still lack this same regard for the development of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.137/en/sujato">AN 4.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the person who prioritizes and holds all three qualities—<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.137/en/sujato"><b>virtue, concentration, and wisdom</b></a>—as supreme in their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.137/en/sujato">AN 4.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are four types of radiance in the world, and identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.141/en/sujato"><b>radiance of wisdom</b></a> as being the foremost among them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.141/en/sujato">AN 4.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.141/en/sujato"><b>radiance of wisdom</b></a> surpasses even the brilliance of the sun and the moon in terms of its excellence and spiritual value? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.141/en/sujato">AN 4.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the radiance of the moon, the radiance of the sun, and the radiance of fire as worldly radiances, but placed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.141/en/sujato"><b>radiance of wisdom</b></a> above them all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.141/en/sujato">AN 4.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four kinds of light—moonlight, sunlight, firelight, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.142/en/sujato"><b>light of wisdom</b></a>—as the primary sources of light in existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.142/en/sujato">AN 4.142</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that of all the forms of illumination, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.142/en/sujato"><b>light of wisdom</b></a> is the supreme type of light? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.142/en/sujato">AN 4.142</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that while the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.142/en/sujato"><b>light of the sun</b></a> is great, it does not reach the same level of supremacy as the light provided by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.142/en/sujato">AN 4.142</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even a person living in a remote forest is not truly "withdrawn in body" if they occupy their time with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.138/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of ill will</b></a> and harming others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.138/en/sujato">AN 4.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that "withdrawal in mind" is defined by the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.138/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of non-harming</b></a> and renunciation rather than by one's physical environment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.138/en/sujato">AN 4.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.142/en/sujato"><b>light of fire</b></a> as one of the four major types of light, though it is ranked below the light of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.142/en/sujato">AN 4.142</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that some people fail to progress because they are not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.137/en/sujato"><b>regardful of concentration</b></a> and do not view it as a supreme quality to be mastered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.137/en/sujato">AN 4.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.141/en/sujato"><b>radiance of the moon</b></a> as a significant type of radiance, yet insisted that the radiance of wisdom is the most excellent of all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.141/en/sujato">AN 4.141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Princess Cundī once approached the Buddha while accompanied by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>five hundred chariots</b></a> and five hundred royal princesses to seek his guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Princess Cundī’s brother, Prince Cunda, believed that any man or woman who takes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>refuge in the Triple Gem</b></a>—the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha—is destined for a good rebirth after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Princess Cundī relayed her brother's view that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from intoxicants</b></a> and other unwholesome actions through the five precepts leads a person to a good destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Princess Cundī asked the Buddha to define what kind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>Teacher</b></a> one should have confidence in to ensure a rebirth in a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Tathāgata, the Arahant, and the Perfectly Enlightened One is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>foremost among all beings</b></a>, whether they are footless, two-footed, four-footed, or many-footed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for those who have confidence in the Buddha, the result of that confidence is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>foremost result</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> to be the foremost among all conditioned things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> (virāga) is the foremost of all things, whether those things are conditioned or unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of dispassion as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>crushing of pride</b></a> and the uprooting of attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the destruction of craving, cessation, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> as the ultimate expressions of dispassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Sangha of the Tathāgata’s disciples, consisting of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>four pairs of persons</b></a>, is the foremost among all communities and groups? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the Sangha of his disciples as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world, worthy of hospitality and offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>virtues dear to the noble ones</b></a> as the foremost among all virtues because they are unbroken, untorn, and lead to concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>merit of giving</b></a> increases to the highest degree when a person offers gifts to the foremost recipients? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that giving to the foremost recipients leads to the attainment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>foremost life span</b></a>, beauty, and fame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person who gives to the foremost and is concentrated on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>foremost Dhamma</b></a> will experience joy in their next life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who has confidence in the foremost things will rejoice whether they are reborn as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>deva or a human</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for those who understand the foremost Dhamma and have confidence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato"><b>foremost Sangha</b></a>, happiness and strength will follow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.32/en/sujato">AN 5.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>True Dhamma</b></a> will fall into confusion and eventually disappear if monks fail to learn the nine-fold teachings, which include discourses, verses, and marvels? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>disappearance of the Dhamma</b></a> is accelerated when monks do not teach the teachings to others in detail as they have heard and learned them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>stability of the Dhamma</b></a> is ensured when monks diligently learn all nine divisions of the teachings, such as suttas, songs, and expositions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when monks do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>make others recite</b></a> the Dhamma in detail as they have mastered it, it leads to the confusion and disappearance of the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>pondering the Dhamma</b></a> with the mind as one of the five essential practices that lead to the non-disappearance of the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>nine-fold division</b></a> of the teachings—ranging from Jatakas to analytical talk—must be mastered by monks to prevent the teachings from being lost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for the Dhamma to endure, monks must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>teach in detail</b></a> to others exactly what they have heard and learned from the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>reciting the Dhamma</b></a> in detail is a critical factor for monks who wish to prevent the confusion and eventual disappearance of the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if monks do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>mentally inspect</b></a> the teachings they have learned, it contributes significantly to the decline and disappearance of the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>non-confusion</b></a> of the True Dhamma is maintained when monks actively make others recite the teachings in detail as they have been learned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>True Dhamma disappears</b></a> when monks neglect to recite the teachings they have heard and mastered for themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>examining the teachings</b></a> with the mind ensures the long-term stability and non-disappearance of the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically mentioned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>nine divisions</b></a> of the Dhamma, including Udānas and Itivuttakas, must be studied to ensure the spiritual teaching's survival? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that monks should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>recite in detail</b></a> what they have learned to keep the True Dhamma from falling into a state of confusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>confusion of the Dhamma</b></a> occurs when monks do not ponder, examine, or mentally inspect the meaning of the teachings they have acquired? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the True Dhamma's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>non-disappearance</b></a> is supported by monks who teach the Dhamma in detail to others as they have heard and mastered it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>five factors</b></a> that lead to the stability of the True Dhamma, starting with the thorough learning of various types of spiritual teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when monks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>do not learn</b></a> the Dhamma—including verses, expositions, and marvels—it is the first factor leading to the teaching's disappearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>mental inspection</b></a> of the Dhamma involves pondering and examining the teachings with the mind to prevent their confusion and loss? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato"><b>stability of the True Dhamma</b></a>, monks must make others recite the teachings in detail exactly as they were originally heard? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.155/en/sujato">AN 5.155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are three distinct <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>authorities</b></a> that can govern a person's behavior: the authority of oneself, the authority of the world, and the authority of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person should not go forth from the household life for the sake of robes, almsfood, or lodgings, but because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>overcome by suffering</b></a> such as birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>authority of oneself</b></a> involves a practitioner reflecting that it is improper to seek out the same or worse sensual pleasures than those they originally abandoned when leaving the household life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person makes themselves their own authority, they should maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> and unconfused mindfulness to abandon unwholesome states and develop wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>authority of the world</b></a> as reflecting on the fact that there are ascetics and brahmins with psychic powers who can see others from a distance and read their minds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that practitioners should consider the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>authority of the world</b></a> because there are deities with the divine eye and the ability to know the minds of others who can perceive if a person is living with bad, unwholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>authority of the Dhamma</b></a> involves reflecting on the fact that the Dhamma is well-expounded, visible here and now, and to be personally realized by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that under the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>authority of the Dhamma</b></a>, the Buddha suggests a practitioner should consider that their fellow practitioners are watching and that it would be improper to remain lazy or negligent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when considering the authority of the world, one should avoid <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome thoughts</b></a>—such as sensual thoughts, thoughts of ill will, or thoughts of harming—because others with psychic powers may know one's true state of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>secrecy</b></a> in the world for one who does an evil deed, because a person's own self always knows whether they are telling the truth or a lie? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>deities and Tathagatas</b></a> see a fool who behaves crookedly in the world, even if that person tries to hide their evil actions from others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a sage who is governed by himself, the world, and the Dhamma, and who is a persistent meditator, will not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>fall away</b></a> from the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who exerts effort and is governed by the three authorities can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>vanquish Mara</b></a> and overcome the end-maker (death)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha describes a sage who has reached the end of birth as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>knower of the world</b></a> who is wise and remains unattached to all things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a practitioner makes the Dhamma their authority, the Buddha says their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>body becomes tranquil</b></a> and their mind becomes concentrated and one-pointed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk uses himself as the authority, he preserves himself in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>purity</b></a> by abandoning what is blameworthy and developing what is blameless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the context of Dhamma-authority, the Buddha describes his teaching as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>timeless</b></a> and inviting all to "come and see" for themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that certain practitioners possess the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a>, which allows them to see others from a distance and know their internal thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the ultimate goal of the spiritual life as making an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>end to suffering</b></a>, a state he called the destruction of the entire mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encourages practitioners to check their own behavior by asking if it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>fitting</b></a> to act unwholesomely after having left the household life out of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>self is the witness</b></a> to one's own truth or lies, and that one should not underestimate the importance of this internal testimony? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to a person who has attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato"><b>destruction of birth</b></a> as a sage who has reached the goal through persistent exertion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.40/en/sujato">AN 3.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a person of a good family generates much merit when there is the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.41/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a>, an object to be given, and those worthy of gifts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.41/en/sujato">AN 3.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for merit to be produced in abundance by a clan member, there must be the presence of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.41/en/sujato"><b>gift</b></a> or an object to be given? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.41/en/sujato">AN 3.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a faithful person of a good family produces great merit specifically when there is the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.41/en/sujato"><b>those worthy of offerings</b></a> to receive their gifts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.41/en/sujato">AN 3.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person of faith and confidence by their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.42/en/sujato"><b>desire to see</b></a> those who are virtuous and lead a moral life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.42/en/sujato">AN 3.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the three ways to recognize a faithful person is by their eagerness to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.42/en/sujato"><b>hear the true Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.42/en/sujato">AN 3.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a faithful person as one who dwells at home with a mind free from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.42/en/sujato"><b>stain of stinginess</b></a>, being devoted to charity and distributing gifts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.42/en/sujato">AN 3.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.45/en/sujato"><b>giving</b></a> as one of the three primary things prescribed by wise and good people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.45/en/sujato">AN 3.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.45/en/sujato"><b>going forth</b></a> from the household life into homelessness is a practice specifically prescribed and praised by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.45/en/sujato">AN 3.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.45/en/sujato"><b>service to parents</b></a> as a core duty prescribed by wise and virtuous people for a good person to follow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.45/en/sujato">AN 3.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the wise prescribe practices such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.45/en/sujato"><b>non-violence</b></a>, restraint, and self-control for those seeking a beneficial life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.45/en/sujato">AN 3.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that people in a village or town generate much merit when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.46/en/sujato"><b>virtuous monastics</b></a> live in their vicinity and depend on them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.46/en/sujato">AN 3.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, people generate merit through the three channels of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.46/en/sujato"><b>body, speech, and mind</b></a> when they live near and support virtuous renunciants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.46/en/sujato">AN 3.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a faithful head of a household to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.48/en/sujato"><b>Himalayas</b></a>, the king of mountains, because of the way they provide a foundation for their family to grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.48/en/sujato">AN 3.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as trees grow in the mountains, a family grows in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.48/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> when they live in dependence on a faithful head of the household? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.48/en/sujato">AN 3.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that family members, including wives, children, and relatives, grow in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.48/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> when they are supported by a faithful and moral head of the family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.48/en/sujato">AN 3.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that dependents of a faithful head of household grow in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.48/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a>, similar to how trees in the forest grow their heartwood when supported by a great mountain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.48/en/sujato">AN 3.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how employees and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.48/en/sujato"><b>dependents</b></a> grow in good qualities by observing the virtue and generosity of a faithful householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.48/en/sujato">AN 3.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who emulate the virtuous conduct of a faithful leader will rejoice in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.48/en/sujato"><b>deva world</b></a> after they pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.48/en/sujato">AN 3.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to one who follows the prescriptions of the wise as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.45/en/sujato"><b>noble person</b></a> who is endowed with vision and partakes of a world of bliss? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.45/en/sujato">AN 3.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.42/en/sujato"><b>bountiful</b></a> in giving to describe a faithful person who is always ready to be asked for help and shares their resources with others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.42/en/sujato">AN 3.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.49/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> should be applied to prevent the arising of evil, unwholesome states that have not yet arisen in one’s mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.49/en/sujato">AN 3.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a practitioner must use <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.49/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> to bring about the arising of wholesome states that have not yet been developed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.49/en/sujato">AN 3.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.49/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> is required to endure arisen bodily feelings that are painful, racking, sharp, and life-threatening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.49/en/sujato">AN 3.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a monk who strives in preventing unwholesome states, developing wholesome states, and enduring painful feelings as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.49/en/sujato"><b>ardent</b></a>, alert, and mindful for the making of an end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.49/en/sujato">AN 3.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that two brahmins, who were 120 years old and in the final stage of life, approached the Buddha to confess they had failed to do <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato"><b>meritorious deeds</b></a> that provide safety from fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato">AN 3.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the world is constantly being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato"><b>swept away</b></a> by the forces of aging, sickness, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato">AN 3.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato"><b>restraint</b></a> of body, speech, and mind serves as a person's only true shelter, island, and refuge after they have passed away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato">AN 3.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that life is of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato"><b>short duration</b></a> and is being carried off, and that for one being led away by old age, there are no external protections? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato">AN 3.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged those who see the inherent fear in death to perform <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato"><b>meritorious deeds</b></a> that bring about future happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato">AN 3.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato"><b>self-control</b></a> through the body, speech, and mind is the specific factor that leads to the happiness of a person in the afterlife? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato">AN 3.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a world is being led on by aging and death, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato"><b>merit</b></a> created while a person is still living is the only thing that provides long-term benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato">AN 3.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed two elderly brahmins that since they had reached the end of their lives without doing good, they should prioritize <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato"><b>meritorious actions</b></a> that would lead to their long-term welfare and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.51/en/sujato">AN 3.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato"><b>visible here and now</b></a> because a person who abandons lust no longer intends for their own harm, the harm of others, or the harm of both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato">AN 3.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato"><b>infatuated with lust</b></a> and overcome by it, they experience mental suffering and dejection as a direct consequence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato">AN 3.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato"><b>overcome by hatred</b></a> and with a mind obsessed by it will conduct themselves wrongly through body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato">AN 3.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato"><b>delusion is abandoned</b></a>, a person correctly understands their own good, the good of others, and the good of both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato">AN 3.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that someone <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato"><b>overcome by delusion</b></a> fails to understand things as they really are, including what is beneficial for themselves and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato">AN 3.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the Dhamma as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato"><b>timeless</b></a> because the relief from suffering that comes from abandoning lust, hatred, and delusion can be realized immediately? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato">AN 3.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his teaching as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato"><b>inviting one to come and see</b></a> because the results of practicing the Dhamma are evident in how one's intentions and actions change for the better? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato">AN 3.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato"><b>overcome by lust</b></a> will intend for their own harm as well as the harm of others, losing sight of what is truly beneficial? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato">AN 3.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato"><b>onward-leading</b></a> because it guides the practitioner away from the mental dejection caused by being obsessed with hatred and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato">AN 3.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the Dhamma is to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato"><b>personally experienced by the wise</b></a> through the direct observation of how abandoning unwholesome states leads to the cessation of mental pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato">AN 3.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's explanation of the Dhamma, a brahmin compared the teaching to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato"><b>revealing what was hidden</b></a> or showing the way to someone who was lost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato">AN 3.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a brahmin was so moved by the Buddha's description of how the Dhamma addresses lust, hatred, and delusion that he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato"><b>went for refuge</b></a> to the Buddha, Dhamma, and Saṅgha for the rest of his life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.53/en/sujato">AN 3.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>four noble lineages</b></a> that are considered ancient, original, traditional, and have never been rejected by wise practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk belongs to the ancient noble lineage when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>content with any robe</b></a> he receives, rather than seeking high-quality ones through improper means? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner should not only be content with simple requisites but also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>speak in praise</b></a> of such contentment to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that if a monk does not obtain a robe, he should not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>fret or worry</b></a>, maintaining his composure and commitment to the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to use their robes without being greedy or infatuated, but rather with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>awareness of the danger</b></a> of attachment and the wisdom of spiritual escape? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>contentment with almsfood</b></a> as a core component of the noble lineage, requiring one to accept whatever food is offered without complaint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that even when a monk is content with simple food, he must be careful not to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>exalt himself</b></a> or look down on others who might have different circumstances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>contentment with lodging</b></a>—being satisfied with any type of dwelling—as one of the four essential traditions of the noble ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk who is content with his lodging uses it without being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>tied to it</b></a>, always seeing the higher purpose of his practice rather than physical comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the fourth noble lineage described by the Buddha involves a practitioner who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>delights in meditation</b></a> and finds joy in the development of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha praised the practitioner who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>delights in abandoning</b></a> unwholesome qualities, making the act of letting go a source of spiritual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who stays within these ancient lineages as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>skillful and energetic</b></a>, possessing clear comprehension and mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses these four noble lineages can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>conquer discontent</b></a> in any direction he chooses to live, rather than being overcome by it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the "wise one" or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>sage</b></a> (dhīra) is someone who remains steady and is not shaken by either delight or discontent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a person who has abandoned all karma and lives purely is like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>coin of gold</b></a> that no one can rightfully find fault with? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha noted that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>devas and Brahmā</b></a> offer their praise to a practitioner who firmly establishes themselves in the four noble lineages? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that these four lineages have been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>unadulterated</b></a> since ancient times and will continue to be respected by wise people in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically defined the noble lineage as a path where one avoids <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato"><b>wrong ways of seeking</b></a> requisites, maintaining integrity regardless of gain or loss? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.28/en/sujato">AN 4.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>factors of the Dhamma</b></a> (dhammapadāni) that are considered primordial, ancient, and traditional? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described these four factors of the Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>unadulterated</b></a>, meaning they have never been mixed with unwholesome states and will never be rejected by wise spiritual practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>non-covetousness</b></a> (anabhijjhā) as the first of the four primordial factors of the Dhamma that are not disparaged by wise ascetics and brahmins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>non-ill will</b></a> (abyāpāda) is an ancient and original factor of the Dhamma that has remained valid through the ages? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>right mindfulness</b></a> (sammāsati) is a traditional pillar of the spiritual life that no wise person should ever criticize? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> (sammāsamādhi) as one of the four essential factors of the Dhamma that are considered unadulterated and ancient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued it is impossible to reject the factor of non-covetousness while still trying to present a greedy, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>lustful person</b></a> as a legitimate ascetic or brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that if one rejects the factor of non-ill will, they cannot logically point to someone with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>depraved mind</b></a> and claim they are a true practitioner of the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha maintained that it is impossible to discard the factor of right mindfulness and then successfully promote someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>lacking clear comprehension</b></a> as a spiritual authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that if a person rejects right concentration, they have no grounds to present an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>unconcentrated person</b></a> with a scattered mind as a worthy holy person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that criticizing the factor of non-covetousness implies that one believes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>covetous people</b></a> who are full of desire are actually the ones who should be honored? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who disparage the factor of non-ill will effectively place themselves in the position of praising individuals with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>hateful minds</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that anyone who mocks right mindfulness is indirectly suggesting that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>muddled and confused</b></a> people are the ones worthy of praise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that if one finds fault with right concentration, they are essentially saying that practitioners with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>scattered minds</b></a> are the ones to be admired? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned even the people of Ukkala, who held extreme views like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>nihilism</b></a> (natthikavāda) and the denial of the efficacy of action, did not think these four factors of Dhamma should be criticized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, even groups like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>deniers of a cause</b></a> (ahetukavādā) respected the factors of non-covetousness and non-ill will to avoid being blamed and resented by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described a person as "heedful" (appamatto) if they are free from ill-will, always mindful, and have a mind that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>well-concentrated within</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the path of heedfulness as specifically involving a training in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>removal of covetousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha concluded that the four factors of Dhamma are so fundamental that they remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato"><b>not disparaged</b></a> by any wise ascetics or brahmins, regardless of their specific traditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.30/en/sujato">AN 4.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared his teaching of the Dhamma to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>roar of a lion</b></a>, the king of beasts, who comes out of his lair in the evening to stretch, survey the four quarters, and roar three times before departing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that when animals hear the lion’s roar, they are struck with fear and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>sense of urgency</b></a>, causing them to seek refuge in burrows, water, forests, or the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, even powerful <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>royal elephants</b></a> who are bound with strong leather thongs will break their bonds and flee in terror upon hearing the lion's roar? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when a Tathāgata arises in the world as a fully enlightened being, he roars a spiritual roar by teaching the Four Noble Truths regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>identity</b></a> (sakkāya), its origin, its cessation, and the path to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>long-lived devas</b></a>, who are beautiful and live in high celestial mansions, experience fear and terror when they hear the Dhamma and realize their own state is not permanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that celestial beings often mistakenly believe themselves to be eternal, but hearing the Dhamma makes them realize, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>We are impermanent</b></a>," and subject to the laws of identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>incomparable teacher</b></a> for the world and its devas, having set the Wheel of Dhamma in motion through his own direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> is the specific practice that leads to the subsiding of suffering and the final cessation of identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, devas realize they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>included in identity</b></a> (sakkāyapariyāpannā) only after hearing the words of an Arahant who has fully liberated himself from worldly bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that just as the lion is the most powerful among animals, the Tathāgata possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>great psychic power</b></a> and majesty over the entire world, including the celestial realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a practitioner becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato"><b>fully liberated</b></a> (tādino) by understanding the cessation of identity, a realization that causes even the highest gods to tremble with the truth of transience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.32/en/sujato">AN 4.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the Tathāgata, the Arahant, the Perfectly Enlightened One, as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato"><b>foremost of all beings</b></a>, whether they are footless, two-footed, four-footed, many-footed, with form or formless, percipient, non-percipient, or neither-percipient-nor-non-percipient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato">AN 4.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that individuals who place their confidence in the Buddha are placing their confidence in the foremost, and for those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato"><b>confidence in the foremost</b></a>, the resulting fruit of that confidence is also foremost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato">AN 4.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> is considered the foremost of all conditioned states (dhammā saṅkhatā), or those things brought about by causes and conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato">AN 4.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> (virāga)—which includes the crushing of pride, the removal of thirst, the uprooting of attachment, and the destruction of craving—to be the foremost of all states, whether they are conditioned or unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato">AN 4.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> as the ultimate cessation of suffering and the "cutting off of the round" of rebirth, identifying it as the highest possible spiritual goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato">AN 4.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Sangha of the Tathāgata’s disciples, specifically the four pairs of persons or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato"><b>eight individuals</b></a> who have reached various stages of enlightenment, is the foremost of all groups or communities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato">AN 4.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the community of noble disciples as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world, meaning that it is the most fruitful place for people to plant the "seeds" of their good deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato">AN 4.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person gives gifts to those who are foremost, such as the Sangha, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato"><b>merit increases</b></a> to the highest degree, resulting in the growth of their longevity, beauty, fame, reputation, happiness, and strength? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato">AN 4.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for those who understand the foremost Dhamma and have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato"><b>confidence in the Sangha</b></a>, the spiritual rewards are superior to any other kind of confidence one might hold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato">AN 4.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise donor who is concentrated on the foremost Dhamma will eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato"><b>attain the foremost</b></a> state of peace and rejoice in that achievement, whether they find themselves reborn as a deva in the celestial realms or as a human being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.34/en/sujato">AN 4.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.146/en/sujato"><b>occasions</b></a>, or "times," that should be cultivated: hearing the Dhamma, discussing the Dhamma, investigation, and insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.146/en/sujato">AN 4.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.146/en/sujato"><b>hearing the Dhamma</b></a> as one of the four essential times that contribute to a practitioner's spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.146/en/sujato">AN 4.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged regular <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.146/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma discussion</b></a> as one of the four key times to be pursued for progress on the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.146/en/sujato">AN 4.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when the four times—hearing the Dhamma, discussing the Dhamma, investigation, and insight—are properly developed and pursued, they lead to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.147/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.147/en/sujato">AN 4.147</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the sequential and proper development of specific spiritual times is the process that brings one gradually to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.147/en/sujato"><b>exhaustion of mental fermentations</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.147/en/sujato">AN 4.147</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a simile of heavy rain on a mountaintop filling up <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato"><b>mountain clefts</b></a> and ravines to describe how the four times of practice eventually lead to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato">AN 4.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the culmination of spiritual practice to how small streams eventually fill the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato"><b>great ocean</b></a>, illustrating how the destruction of the taints is reached through gradual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato">AN 4.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained there are four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato"><b>incalculable periods</b></a> in the life cycle of an eon, the duration of which cannot be reckoned in years or even hundreds of thousands of years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato">AN 4.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught it is impossible to calculate the specific number of years that pass during the period when a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato"><b>cosmic eon</b></a> is in the process of contracting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato">AN 4.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described one of the four incalculable periods of time as the stage when an eon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato"><b>remains contracted</b></a> after the world system has dissolved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato">AN 4.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the period during which an eon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato"><b>is expanding</b></a> is so vast that its duration cannot be counted in terms of millennia? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato">AN 4.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the state where an eon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato"><b>remains expanded</b></a> as the final incalculable period within the cyclical life of the universe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.156/en/sujato">AN 4.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two distinct types of disease that afflict beings, classifying them as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato"><b>bodily disease</b></a> and mental disease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato">AN 4.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that while some people can claim to be free from physical illness for up to a hundred years or more, those who are free from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato"><b>mental disease</b></a> for even a single moment are rare in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato">AN 4.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the only individuals who can truly claim to be free from mental disease are the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato"><b>Arahants</b></a>, those whose mental taints have been entirely destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato">AN 4.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a specific "disease" for a monk as being full of strong desire and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato"><b>dissatisfied</b></a> with whatever robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicinal requisites they receive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato">AN 4.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that harboring <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato"><b>evil desires</b></a> for the sake of gaining honor, gain, and fame is considered a serious spiritual disease for one who has gone forth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato">AN 4.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned against a practitioner <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato"><b>striving and exerting</b></a> effort specifically for the purpose of obtaining status, honor, and fame rather than for spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato">AN 4.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified it as a "disease" when a monk visits families or speaks the Dhamma with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato"><b>calculated motive</b></a> to acquire personal gain and praise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato">AN 4.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted his followers to train themselves to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato"><b>patient</b></a> and to endure physical hardships such as cold, heat, hunger, and thirst? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato">AN 4.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should become one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato"><b>endures</b></a> ill-spoken and unwelcome words, as well as sharp, piercing, and life-threatening bodily pains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato">AN 4.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to actively train themselves to not pursue <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato"><b>honor and renown</b></a>, emphasizing that they should not exert themselves for the sake of being known by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.157/en/sujato">AN 4.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that while this body is produced by food, a practitioner must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>rely on food</b></a> in order to eventually abandon food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that even though the body is produced by craving, a monk uses a specific form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a>—the desire to realize liberation—to eventually abandon all craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda stated that conceit can be used to abandon conceit, such as when a monk hears of another's enlightenment and thinks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>"If he can do it, why not I?"</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while food, craving, and conceit can be used strategically to transcend them, Venerable Ānanda noted that the Buddha declared an absolute <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>breaking of the bridge</b></a> regarding sexual intercourse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that a monk should consume food reflectively, not for amusement or vanity, but simply for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>support of the spiritual life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that by eating food with proper reflection, a practitioner aims to stop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>old feelings</b></a> of hunger without creating new feelings of overindulgence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing Venerable Ānanda's teaching, a nun confessed her transgression, acknowledging she had acted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>foolishly</b></a> and out of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that it is a sign of growth in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>Noble One's Discipline</b></a> when someone sees their transgression as such and makes amends for the purpose of future restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the discourse on how to abandon craving and conceit was delivered by Venerable Ānanda while he was residing at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>Ghositārāma</b></a> in Kosambī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explicitly taught that this body is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>produced by food</b></a>, yet the very food it depends on must ultimately be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Ānanda, the physical body is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>produced by craving</b></a>, and that very craving is something that must be eventually abandoned by the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda described the body as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>produced by conceit</b></a>, and emphasized that this conceit must be abandoned through the path of practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that hearing about another monk's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> can spark a healthy craving in a practitioner to reach that same goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that comparing oneself to an enlightened monk by asking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>"Why can't I?"</b></a> is a way of using conceit to eventually abandon it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda clarified that while food and craving are used as means to an end, the Buddha specifically spoke of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>elimination of sexual intercourse</b></a> as a "breaking of the bridge"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that a monk should eat only for the purpose of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>body's endurance</b></a> and to keep it alive for spiritual practice, rather than for intoxication or beauty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a nun realized she had acted out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome intentions</b></a>, she bowed at Venerable Ānanda's feet and asked him to accept her confession? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda accepted a nun's confession because she <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>recognized her mistake</b></a> and sought to make amends in accordance with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda highlighted that seeing one's transgression and undertaking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>future restraint</b></a> is the proper way to progress in the spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that food should be taken specifically for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>ending of discomfort</b></a> and the prevention of harm, ensuring one lives blamelessly and in comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that by relying on the desire to achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom, a practitioner can eventually abandon the very craving that drove them to practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.159/en/sujato">AN 4.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the existence of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>Fortunate One</b></a> in the world, or the Discipline of a Fortunate One, is for the welfare and happiness of many beings, including devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>Fortunate One</b></a> (Sugata) as a Tathāgata who is an Arahant, a Perfectly Enlightened One, and a supreme guide for those to be tamed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>Discipline of the Fortunate One</b></a> as the proclamation of a holy life that is perfectly complete, pure, and good in its beginning, middle, and end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the true Dhamma can fall into confusion and eventually disappear if monks learn suttas incorrectly with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>misplaced words</b></a> and phrases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when the phrasing of a sutta is misplaced, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>meaning</b></a> of the teaching also becomes difficult to interpret and is easily misconstrued? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>difficult to speak to</b></a> and lacking patience as specific qualities in practitioners that lead to the disappearance of the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the true Dhamma is threatened when monks do not take <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>instruction</b></a> respectfully or with the proper attitude to learn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even learned monks, such as those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>bearers of the Dhamma</b></a> and the Vinaya, contribute to the decline of the teachings if they do not carefully teach the suttas to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that if expert monks fail to pass on their knowledge, the suttas become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>cut at the root</b></a> and lose their foundation once those monks pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that elder monks who become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>luxurious and lax</b></a>, neglecting solitude and energy, lead the way toward the confusion and disappearance of the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>younger generation</b></a> of monks may follow the bad example of elders who have given up the quest for spiritual attainment, further endangering the longevity of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the stability and non-disappearance of the true Dhamma depends on monks learning suttas correctly with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>well-placed words</b></a> and phrases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when the phrasing of the teachings is well-placed, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>interpretation of the meaning</b></a> becomes clear and easy to follow for practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>easy to speak to</b></a> and amenable as essential qualities for monks to ensure the long-term stability of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that monks who are patient and take <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>instruction</b></a> skillfully are practicing one of the four essential factors that keep the true Dhamma from vanishing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised learned monks who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>carefully teach</b></a> the suttas to others, ensuring that the teachings maintain a foundation even after the current teachers are gone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the suttas remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>supported</b></a> and not "cut at the root" when specialized knowledge is diligently shared among the community of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the Dhamma is preserved when elder monks are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>not luxurious</b></a> and refuse to fall into laxity regarding their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that elder monks should be leaders in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> and exert energy to attain what has not yet been attained, thereby preserving the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato"><b>younger generation</b></a> follows the positive, energetic example of their elders, the true Dhamma is maintained for the benefit of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.160/en/sujato">AN 4.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave a specific discourse to Uggaha’s daughters because they were soon to depart for their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>husbands' families</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wife should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>the first to rise</b></a> and the last to lie down in her husband's home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised wives to be "always ready to help" (<i>kiṅkārapaṭissāviniyo</i>) and to maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>pleasant conduct</b></a> toward their husbands? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a wife should address her husband using <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>sweet speech</b></a> (<i>piyavādiniyo</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed wives to honor, respect, and revere their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>husband’s parents</b></a> as they would their own mother and father? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wife should show respect and offer hospitality to any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>ascetics and brahmins</b></a> that her husband holds in high regard? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that when visitors arrive at the home, a wife should properly welcome them with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>a seat and water</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a wife should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>skilled and diligent</b></a> in her husband's home industries, such as working with wool or cotton? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wife should possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>resourceful investigative skill</b></a> (<i>tatrupāyāya vīmaṃsāya</i>) to ensure household tasks are performed correctly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised wives to remain aware of the work done by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>domestic help</b></a>, including slaves, messengers, and workers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a wife should monitor the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>health and strength</b></a> of the household staff and care for them when they are ill? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wife should fairly distribute <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>choice food</b></a> and portions of meals among the servants and employees of the house? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed wives to guard and protect the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>wealth and grain</b></a> that the husband brings home, including silver and gold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a wife should avoid being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>thief or a spendthrift</b></a> with the resources her husband provides? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a woman who possesses five specific qualities of conduct will be reborn among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>devas of the agreeable group</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wife should never <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>despise her husband</b></a>, who works hard and provides for her needs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged wives to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>industrious and energetic</b></a>, ensuring they do not become lazy in managing their household duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a wise woman should not provoke her husband through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>jealous conduct</b></a> (<i>issācārena</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that a woman who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato"><b>follows her husband’s will</b></a> and protects the wealth he has gathered is destined for a pleasant heavenly rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.33/en/sujato">AN 5.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha was staying at Vesālī in the Gabled Hall, he was asked by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>General Sīha</b></a> whether it is possible to describe a visible fruit of giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a primary visible fruit of being a donor and a master of giving is that the person becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>dear and agreeable</b></a> to many people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that good and superior persons naturally choose to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>frequent and associate</b></a> with someone who is a generous donor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a donor who is a master of giving earns a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>good reputation</b></a> that spreads far and wide among the people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a donor can approach any assembly—whether of nobles, brahmins, householders, or monastics—<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>without embarrassment</b></a> and with full confidence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the reward of being reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> after death as the fruit of giving that pertains specifically to the future life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>General Sīha</b></a> informed the Buddha that he did not need to rely on faith regarding the visible benefits of giving, as he had personally experienced four of them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that General Sīha testified to the Buddha that, as a donor himself, he is personally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>dear and agreeable</b></a> to a great number of people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that General Sīha shared with the Buddha that good and superior persons <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>regularly visit</b></a> him because of his practice as a master of giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that General Sīha mentioned to the Buddha that he has a far-reaching reputation for being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>supporter of the Saṅgha</b></a> and a person of charitable action? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that General Sīha told the Buddha that he enters various social assemblies, such as those of the khattiyas or brahmins, feeling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>confident and composed</b></a> due to his character as a donor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while General Sīha had personal experience of the worldly fruits of giving, he told the Buddha he accepted the teaching on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>heavenly rebirth</b></a> based on his faith in the Buddha's word? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed to General Sīha that it is indeed true that a donor and master of giving is reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>good destination</b></a> after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught in verse that those who practice giving and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>discard selfishness</b></a> will eventually dwell among the devas for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described wise individuals as those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>seek happiness</b></a> by removing the stain of stinginess through the act of giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that generous people who have performed merit wander through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>Nandana Grove</b></a> in heaven, glowing with their own radiance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that in the heavenly realms, givers find delight while being fully provided with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.34/en/sujato">AN 5.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who practices giving receives five distinct benefits, which include becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.35/en/sujato"><b>dear and agreeable</b></a> to many people and gaining a good reputation that spreads far and wide? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.35/en/sujato">AN 5.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that good and superior persons naturally associate with a generous donor, and these <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.35/en/sujato"><b>peaceful individuals</b></a> will then teach the donor the Dhamma that removes all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.35/en/sujato">AN 5.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified one of the five primary benefits of giving as the reward of being reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.35/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> after the breakup of the body and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.35/en/sujato">AN 5.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described five specific types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.36/en/sujato"><b>timely giving</b></a>, which include offering gifts to a guest, to someone about to depart on a journey, and to those who are sick? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.36/en/sujato">AN 5.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to practice timely giving by offering gifts during a famine and by providing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.36/en/sujato"><b>new crops and fruits</b></a> first to those who are virtuous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.36/en/sujato">AN 5.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a gift is given at the proper time to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.36/en/sujato"><b>noble ones</b></a> who are upright, the resulting offering becomes vast and brings great fruit to the giver? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.36/en/sujato">AN 5.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.36/en/sujato"><b>rejoice or assist</b></a> in the act of timely giving do not lose out, but rather become sharers in the merit produced by that gift? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.36/en/sujato">AN 5.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by giving food, a donor is essentially giving five specific things to the recipients: life, beauty, happiness, strength, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.37/en/sujato"><b>perspicacity</b></a> (intelligence or ready wit)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.37/en/sujato">AN 5.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who gives life through the gift of food becomes a partaker of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.37/en/sujato"><b>divine or human life</b></a> themselves in their future existences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.37/en/sujato">AN 5.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a wise donor who provides strength and beauty to others through food will find that they also gain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.37/en/sujato"><b>strength and beauty</b></a> in return, whether in heaven or as a human? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.37/en/sujato">AN 5.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said the wise giver who bestows happiness on others through the gift of food will eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.37/en/sujato"><b>attain happiness</b></a> for themselves as a result of that merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.37/en/sujato">AN 5.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the giver of food who provides <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.37/en/sujato"><b>perspicacity</b></a> to others will become long-lived and famous in whatever state they are reborn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.37/en/sujato">AN 5.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when good and virtuous people in the world show compassion, they prioritize showing it to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.38/en/sujato"><b>person of faith</b></a> rather than to one who lacks faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.38/en/sujato">AN 5.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that virtuous persons will first approach, first receive gifts from, and first teach the Dhamma to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.38/en/sujato"><b>layperson of faith</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.38/en/sujato">AN 5.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one of the five specific benefits for a person of faith is that, after the breakup of the body, they are reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.38/en/sujato"><b>good destination</b></a>, even in a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.38/en/sujato">AN 5.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a faithful layperson to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.38/en/sujato"><b>great banyan tree</b></a> at a crossroads that provides a refuge for many birds, just as the person of faith is a refuge for monks, nuns, and lay followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.38/en/sujato">AN 5.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person of faith who is a "field of merit" as being endowed with moral virtue, humble in conduct, gentle, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.38/en/sujato"><b>mild in speech</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.38/en/sujato">AN 5.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are free from lust, hatred, and delusion—the supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.38/en/sujato"><b>fields of merit</b></a> in the world—naturally associate with a person who is established in faith and virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.38/en/sujato">AN 5.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five specific reasons why parents desire a son to be born into their family, including the hope that he will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.39/en/sujato"><b>support them</b></a> in return for the support they provided him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.39/en/sujato">AN 5.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that parents wish for a son with the expectation that he will perform the necessary <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.39/en/sujato"><b>duties and work</b></a> for the family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.39/en/sujato">AN 5.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that parents desire a son to ensure that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.39/en/sujato"><b>family lineage</b></a> will endure for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.39/en/sujato">AN 5.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that one reason parents want a son is so that he will be the one to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.39/en/sujato"><b>take charge</b></a> of the family inheritance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.39/en/sujato">AN 5.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that parents hope a son will offer <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.39/en/sujato"><b>offerings to the departed</b></a> on their behalf after they have passed away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.39/en/sujato">AN 5.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised a son who is grateful, helpful, obedient, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.39/en/sujato"><b>endowed with virtue</b></a>, stating that such a person is highly commendable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.39/en/sujato">AN 5.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the true Dhamma is led to confusion and disappearance when monks learn <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>poorly learned discourses</b></a> that contain wrongly placed words and syllables? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when the words and syllables of a discourse are wrongly placed, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>meaning</b></a> of that teaching also becomes difficult to follow and understand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one factor leading to the decline of the true Dhamma is when monks are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>difficult to speak to</b></a>, impatient, and do not accept instruction with respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if learned elders—those who know the discourses, the discipline, and the outlines—do not carefully teach others, the discourses become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>rootless</b></a> and without support after those elders pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified that the true Dhamma disappears when elder monks become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>indulgent and slack</b></a>, taking the lead in backsliding and neglecting the practice of seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that when elder monks lack energy to attain the unattained, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>later generations</b></a> will follow their example, also becoming indulgent and slack in their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>divided Sangha</b></a> leads to mutual insults and abandonment, which contributes to the disappearance of the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that when there is conflict within the Sangha, those <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>without faith</b></a> do not gain it, and some who already have faith may lose it or change their minds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the true Dhamma will endure when monks learn <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>well-learned discourses</b></a> with correctly placed words and syllables? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that when the phrasing of a teaching is well-placed, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>meaning</b></a> of the Dhamma is easily understood and followed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma is preserved when monks are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>easy to speak to</b></a>, patient, and capable of receiving instructions and advice properly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that learned elders who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>carefully teach</b></a> the discourses to others ensure that the teachings do not lose their foundation when those elders pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the true Dhamma lasts longer when elder monks are not indulgent or slack, but instead prioritize <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> and spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when elders exert <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>energy</b></a> to realize what has not yet been realized, the generations that follow will imitate their diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>harmonious Sangha</b></a>, where monks live in agreement without disputes, is a key factor for the long-lasting endurance of the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when the Sangha is unified and shares a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>single recitation</b></a>, they live in comfort and avoid the harmful practices of mutual insults and rejection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that in a harmonious community, those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>not yet confident</b></a> gain confidence in the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a unified Sangha causes those who are already <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>confident</b></a> to experience even greater growth in their faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>disappearance of the true Dhamma</b></a> is caused by exactly five specific behaviors related to learning, conduct, teaching, personal effort, and communal harmony? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha designated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>five factors</b></a> that directly contribute to the stability and non-disappearance of the Dhamma for future generations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato"><b>rootless</b></a> to describe a situation where the Dhamma has no one left to pass it on because the learned elders failed to teach their students? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.156/en/sujato">AN 5.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>ill-directed talk</b></a> as a conversation that provokes anger and resentment in a listener because it highlights a spiritual quality they currently lack? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person who lacks faith, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>talk on faith</b></a> is ill-directed because it causes them to manifest displeasure, ill will, and irritation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>immoral person</b></a> becomes irritated and offensive when others discuss the importance of moral virtue in their presence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person with little learning will feel <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>resentful</b></a> and angry when hearing a discourse regarding the benefits of being well-learned in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that talk on generosity is ill-directed for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>miserly person</b></a> because they do not see the quality of giving within themselves and thus cannot find joy in the topic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an unwise person becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>annoyed and angry</b></a> when a conversation focuses on the development and value of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that talk is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>well-directed talk</b></a> when it is spoken to a person who already possesses the specific virtue or quality being discussed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a faithful person finds <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>joy and rapture</b></a> when hearing others speak about faith, because they recognize that same quality in their own heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>virtuous person</b></a> does not manifest ill will when moral discipline is discussed, as they possess the accomplishment of virtue themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is well-learned in the teachings will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>not become irritated</b></a> or defensive when others praise the value of having much learning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a generous person, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>talk on generosity</b></a> is pleasant and well-directed because they derive happiness from their own accomplishment in giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a wise person remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>calm and patient</b></a> when hearing talk on wisdom, rather than becoming angry or displaying displeasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person's reaction to a spiritual topic depends on whether they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>possess that quality</b></a> within themselves, which determines if they feel joy or anger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in virtue</b></a> to describe the internal state that allows a person to listen to talk on morality with a happy and peaceful mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said an unlearned person lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in learning</b></a>, which is why hearing about the importance of spiritual knowledge causes them to experience irritation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the reaction of a miserly person to talk of giving as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>displaying anger</b></a> and resentment because they cannot find any joy in that particular subject? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in wisdom</b></a> allows them to listen to profound talks on discernment without feeling threatened or manifesting ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when a person sees their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in faith</b></a> during a discussion on the topic, it leads directly to the experience of spiritual rapture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined talk as "ill-directed" or "well-directed" specifically by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>comparing one person to another</b></a> and their respective spiritual attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a generous person, the perception of their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in giving</b></a> is the primary source of the joy they feel when hearing the topic of generosity praised? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.157/en/sujato">AN 5.157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that there are exactly five ways for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>removal of resentment</b></a> that should be used whenever resentment arises toward any person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, if a person has impure bodily conduct but possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>pure verbal conduct</b></a>, one should focus only on their good speech to remove resentment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta compared focusing on a person’s good speech despite their bad actions to a monk wearing rag-robes who finds a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>useful rag</b></a> in a dung heap, picks it up, and leaves the rest behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that if a person has impure verbal conduct but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>pure bodily conduct</b></a>, one should disregard their speech and focus only on their good actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>scum-covered pond</b></a> to describe how a thirsty person should push aside the algae with their hands to drink the clear water, just as we should look past a person's bad words to see their good deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that even if someone has both impure bodily and verbal conduct, but still gains occasional <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>mental clarity</b></a> and serenity, that spark of clarity is what one should focus on? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta compared finding a person's rare moments of mental goodness to a thirsty traveler who finds a tiny bit of water in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>cow’s footprint</b></a> and carefully drinks it so as not to disturb the sediment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that for a person who lacks any goodness in their body, speech, or mind, one should instead develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a>, pity, and sympathy for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta likened a person without any good qualities to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>sick traveler</b></a> who is exhausted and alone on a long road, needing someone to care for them so they do not perish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that we should feel compassion for a person of bad character because we should fear they will fall into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>states of misery</b></a> or hell after death due to their misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta encouraged monks to wish that a person of poor character would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>give up misconduct</b></a> and develop good conduct in body, speech, and mind for their own long-term welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta described a person with pure bodily conduct, pure verbal conduct, and frequent mental clarity as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>all-around inspiring person</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that when encountering a truly virtuous and clear-minded person, we should focus on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>all their qualities</b></a>—their good actions, their good speech, and their serene mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta compared a virtuous and clear-minded person to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>pristine lake</b></a> with clear, sweet, and cool water where a traveler can bathe, drink, and rest comfortably in the shade? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that by focusing on the virtues of an inspiring person, one's own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>mind becomes serene</b></a> and resentment is completely removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta warned that one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>not attend</b></a> to the impure bodily conduct of someone who at least speaks well, so that resentment does not take root? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta advised that for a person with good deeds but bad speech, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>impure verbal conduct</b></a> should not be thought about at that time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta emphasized that even a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>impure conduct</b></a> in both body and speech deserves our attention if they have even a small amount of mental peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a person who is helpful and virtuous is like a lake shaded by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>various trees</b></a>, offering a place of total refreshment for the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>sympathy</b></a> (<i>anukampā</i>) to describe the specific attitude one must adopt toward a person who lacks any redeeming qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that resentment should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>subdued in its entirety</b></a> by applying the appropriate mental strategy for the specific type of person encountered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that focusing on a virtuous person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato"><b>accomplishments</b></a> is the most effective way to produce confidence and joy in the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.162/en/sujato">AN 5.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk falls into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.158/en/sujato"><b>timidity</b></a> and diffidence when he lacks faith, is immoral, has little learning, is lazy, and lacks wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.158/en/sujato">AN 5.158</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five specific qualities—being faithful, virtuous, well-learned, energetic, and wise—that allow a monk to dwell with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.158/en/sujato"><b>self-confidence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.158/en/sujato">AN 5.158</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.158/en/sujato"><b>lacking faith</b></a> and possesses poor moral conduct is predisposed to experiencing timidity in his spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.158/en/sujato">AN 5.158</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Venerable Ānanda that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.159/en/sujato"><b>teaching the Dhamma</b></a> to others is not an easy task and requires the speaker to establish five specific qualities within themselves first? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.159/en/sujato">AN 5.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a person should only teach the Dhamma to others with the intention to deliver a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.159/en/sujato"><b>graduated discourse</b></a>, moving step-by-step through the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.159/en/sujato">AN 5.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a Dhamma speaker should speak <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.159/en/sujato"><b>out of compassion</b></a> and kindness for their audience rather than for selfish reasons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.159/en/sujato">AN 5.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that one should teach the Dhamma without an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.159/en/sujato"><b>ulterior motive</b></a> involving material gain, such as the hope for robes, food, or other offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.159/en/sujato">AN 5.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an effective teacher must speak the Dhamma <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.159/en/sujato"><b>without hurting</b></a> themselves or others, meaning they should not praise themselves or disparage others while speaking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.159/en/sujato">AN 5.159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.160/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> as one of the five things that, once it has arisen in the mind, is extremely difficult to dispel? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.160/en/sujato">AN 5.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.160/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> and delusion are mental states that are particularly difficult to remove once they have taken hold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.160/en/sujato">AN 5.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a sudden flash of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.160/en/sujato"><b>inspired wit</b></a> or a quick-witted thought (paṭibhāna) is one of the things that is difficult to dispel once it has arisen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.160/en/sujato">AN 5.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.160/en/sujato"><b>thought of going</b></a> or the urge to travel (gamikacitta) is among the five things that are difficult to dispel once they have occurred to a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.160/en/sujato">AN 5.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>visible here and now</b></a> because a person who abandons lust no longer intends for their own harm, the harm of others, or the harm of both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>overcome by lust</b></a> and their mind is obsessed by it, they experience mental suffering and dejection as a direct consequence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>infatuated with greed</b></a> and overcome by it will conduct themselves wrongly through body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>lust is abandoned</b></a>, a person correctly understands their own good, the good of others, and the good of both as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that someone <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>overcome by hatred</b></a> fails to understand things as they really are, including what is beneficial for themselves and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the Dhamma as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>timeless</b></a> because the relief from suffering and the ending of harmful intentions through abandoning hatred can be realized immediately? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his teaching as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>inviting one to come and see</b></a> because the shift from a mind obsessed by delusion to one that no longer intends for harm is personally verifiable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>overcome by delusion</b></a> will intend for their own harm as well as the harm of others, losing sight of what is truly beneficial? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>onward-leading</b></a> because it guides the practitioner away from the mental dejection caused by being obsessed with greed and hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the Dhamma is to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>personally experienced by the wise</b></a> through the direct observation of how abandoning delusion leads to the cessation of mental pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>obsessed by hatred</b></a>, a person will engage in misconduct through body, speech, and mind, but they cease such actions once hatred is removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person whose mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>overcome by delusion</b></a> fails to understand their own good, while one who has abandoned delusion understands it correctly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the ending of the obsessive states of lust, hatred, and delusion is the reason the Dhamma is called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>personally realized</b></a> by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's explanation of how the Dhamma is visible here and now, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato"><b>brahmin wanderer</b></a> declared the teaching excellent and went for refuge for life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.54/en/sujato">AN 3.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>Magadhan minister</b></a> named Vassakāra once visited the Buddha at the Squirrels’ Sanctuary in the Bamboo Grove at Rājagaha to discuss the qualities of a great man? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Vassakāra defined a person of great wisdom as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>highly learned</b></a> in whatever subjects they have heard and studied? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was told by Vassakāra that a "great man" must be able to understand and explain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>meaning of statements</b></a> clearly, identifying the specific purpose behind what is said? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the brahmin Vassakāra, a person with great wisdom must possess an excellent and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>powerful memory</b></a>, being able to recall things done and said from a long time ago? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vassakāra the brahmin believed a "great man" should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>skillful and diligent</b></a> in managing the various duties and practical affairs required of a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha redefined the concept of a "great man" as one who actively practices for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>welfare of many</b></a> and for the happiness of the entire world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person of great wisdom is one who establishes a great number of people in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>noble method</b></a>, which consists of wholesome and virtuous qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a truly great man as being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>master of his thoughts</b></a>, possessing the ability to think only the thoughts he wishes to think and dismiss those he does not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified mastery over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>pathways of thought</b></a> and intention as a fundamental quality that distinguishes a person with great wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a "great man" is one who can obtain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>four jhānas</b></a>—the higher mental states that provide pleasant dwellings in this life—whenever he desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that these four jhānas are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>meditative absorptions</b></a> that a person of great wisdom should be able to achieve without any difficulty or hardship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a truly great man has achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>, attaining a state of mind and wisdom that is completely liberated in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the highest wisdom as the ability to dwell in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>taintless liberation</b></a> of mind through one's own direct knowledge and personal realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Vassakāra marveled at the Buddha’s explanation and acknowledged that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>Buddha himself</b></a> perfectly possesses all four of these superior qualities of a great man? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly confirmed to Vassakāra that he indeed practices for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>happiness of many</b></a> and has established a multitude of people in the noble method? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha affirmed his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>mastery over thought</b></a> and his ability to enter any of the four jhānas at will without any lack or trouble? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared he has personally realized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>liberation of wisdom</b></a> through the total destruction of all mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s act of setting the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>Wheel of Dhamma</b></a> in motion involves teaching the reality of identity (sakkāya), its origin, and its ultimate cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> as the specific spiritual practice that leads directly to the subsiding of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha teaches the Dhamma, even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>long-lived devas</b></a> with great beauty and fame become frightened, realizing that their own existence is actually impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that celestial beings, upon hearing the words of the Buddha, come to the profound realization that they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato"><b>not eternal</b></a> and have not yet transcended the cycle of identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.35/en/sujato">AN 4.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.161/en/sujato"><b>four modes of practice</b></a> for spiritual development, ranging from painful practice with sluggish direct knowledge to pleasant practice with quick direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.161/en/sujato">AN 4.161</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner experiences <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>painful practice</b></a> when they are by nature strongly subject to passion, hatred, and delusion, frequently feeling the pain and displeasure born from these states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>sluggish direct knowledge</b></a> as occurring when the five spiritual faculties—faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom—appear only weakly in a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>quick direct knowledge</b></a> as the result of the five spiritual faculties—faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom—appearing intensely and sharply within a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>pleasant practice</b></a> as being undertaken by someone who is not by nature strongly subject to passion, hatred, or delusion, and thus does not frequently feel the pain born from those defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who possesses weak spiritual faculties will only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>sluggishly attain</b></a> the immediate destruction of the taints, regardless of whether their practice is otherwise pleasant or painful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>intense passion</b></a> as one of the natural tendencies that can make a practitioner’s path to enlightenment emotionally painful and full of displeasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>faculty of wisdom</b></a> (paññindriyaṃ) is one of the five essential qualities that determines the speed at which a person can reach the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>painful practice with quick direct knowledge</b></a> occurs when a person with strong natural defilements nevertheless possesses sharp and intense spiritual faculties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the ultimate goal of all four modes of practice as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>, which refers to the final liberation from mental fermentations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>pleasant practice with quick direct knowledge</b></a> is achieved when a practitioner is not naturally subject to strong defilements and simultaneously possesses sharp spiritual faculties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>strong hatred</b></a> is a natural disposition that causes a person to frequently experience suffering and displeasure throughout their spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>faculty of faith</b></a> (saddhindriyaṃ) as the first of the five internal faculties that directly influence the speed of a monk’s spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>strong delusion</b></a> as a primary factor that contributes to a "painful" mode of practice during the search for liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>weak faculties</b></a> to describe the state of faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom when they result in slow or sluggish spiritual attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>faculty of energy</b></a> (vīriyindriyaṃ) as one of the key indicators of whether a person’s path to the destruction of the taints will be slow or fast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>faculty of mindfulness</b></a> (satindriyaṃ) must be intense for a practitioner to experience the specific mode of practice known as quick direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato"><b>faculty of concentration</b></a> (samādhindriyaṃ) as an essential component that, when weak, leads to sluggish direct knowledge even if the practitioner's natural disposition is pleasant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.162/en/sujato">AN 4.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the householder Anāthapiṇḍika that there are five <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>deeds to be done with wealth</b></a> that a noble disciple should perform using riches gained through righteous means? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that wealth is only truly praiseworthy when it is acquired through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>energetic exertion</b></a>, the strength of one's arms, and the sweat of one's brow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a noble disciple uses their wealth to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>make themselves happy</b></a> and pleased, ensuring they maintain themselves properly in comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a primary use of a person's wealth is to provide happiness and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>pleasure to one's parents</b></a>, children, wife, and household workers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that righteously earned wealth should be used to make one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>friends and colleagues</b></a> happy and pleased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that wealth provides a necessary defense against <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>various calamities</b></a>, including those caused by fire, water, kings, thieves, or even hateful heirs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a "fivefold offering" (<i>pañcabali</i>) as a proper use of wealth, which includes the duty of making <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>offerings to kin</b></a> (<i>ñātibali</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged householders to use their wealth to provide <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>offerings to guests</b></a> (<i>atithibali</i>) as one of the five specific deeds for the benefit of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wealth should be used to perform <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>offerings to the departed</b></a> (<i>pubbapetabali</i>) to honor and support deceased relatives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>offerings to the king</b></a> (<i>rājabali</i>), such as taxes and state duties, as a legitimate and necessary use of a noble disciple's wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a noble disciple should use their resources to make <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>offerings to the devas</b></a> (<i>devatābali</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised using wealth to provide an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>uplifting offering</b></a> to virtuous ascetics and brahmins who are patient, gentle, and self-restrained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a person uses their wealth for these five righteous purposes, they will have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>no regret</b></a> regardless of whether their wealth later increases or decreases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who utilizes their wealth for such merit is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>praised here</b></a> in this life and rejoices in the heavenly world after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ultimate purpose of wealth for a wise householder is to support those <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato"><b>living the spiritual life</b></a> who have quelled their intoxication and negligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.41/en/sujato">AN 5.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.164/en/sujato"><b>fit to live with</b></a> his fellow practitioners if he is personally accomplished in virtue and is also capable of answering questions regarding the accomplishment of virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.164/en/sujato">AN 5.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, a monk is considered worthy of his companions' company when he is personally accomplished in concentration and can explain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.164/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment of concentration</b></a> to others who ask? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.164/en/sujato">AN 5.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified being personally accomplished in wisdom and having the ability to address questions on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.164/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment of wisdom</b></a> as essential factors for a monk to be fit to live with his peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.164/en/sujato">AN 5.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated a monk should be personally accomplished in liberation and also be a person who can provide answers when questioned about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.164/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment of liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.164/en/sujato">AN 5.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta emphasized that a monk is fit to live with his fellow practitioners if he is accomplished in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.164/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a> and can clearly answer questions regarding that specific accomplishment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.164/en/sujato">AN 5.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that one of the five reasons people ask questions of others is simply out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.165/en/sujato"><b>dullness and stupidity</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.165/en/sujato">AN 5.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta noted that some individuals ask questions because they are of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.165/en/sujato"><b>evil wishes</b></a> and are dominated by their own selfish desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.165/en/sujato">AN 5.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, a person might ask a question of another specifically out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.165/en/sujato"><b>contempt</b></a>, intending to disparage or belittle the person they are questioning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.165/en/sujato">AN 5.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a genuine <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.165/en/sujato"><b>desire to know</b></a> is one of the five distinct motivations for a person to approach another with a question? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.165/en/sujato">AN 5.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta shared that his personal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.165/en/sujato"><b>motive for asking questions</b></a> was to see if the other person could answer correctly, with the intention that if they could not, he would provide the correct answer himself for their benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.165/en/sujato">AN 5.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how ancient settlements were once so densely populated that villages and towns were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato"><b>within a cock's flight</b></a> of each other? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato">AN 3.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one reason for the depletion of the human population is that people are currently inflamed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato"><b>unrighteous lust</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato">AN 3.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato"><b>excessive greed</b></a> and obsession with wrong Dhamma as key factors that cause once-thriving districts to become uninhabited? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato">AN 3.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when people are overcome by unrighteous lust and greed, they take up sharp weapons and engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato"><b>mutual slaughter</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato">AN 3.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the sky does not rain properly when people are obsessed with wrong Dhamma, leading to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato"><b>scarcity of crops</b></a> and famine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato">AN 3.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that moral corruption can lead to crops being destroyed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato"><b>blight and mildew</b></a>, causing many human deaths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato">AN 3.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how non-human spirits let loose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato"><b>fierce spirits</b></a> upon human beings who are overcome by unrighteous lust and excessive greed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato">AN 3.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was asked by a brahmin why the world, which was once as crowded as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato"><b>Avici hell</b></a>, had seen such a decline in the number of human beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato">AN 3.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that villages, towns, and cities become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato"><b>non-cities</b></a> and abandoned because of the internal moral decay of the population? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.56/en/sujato">AN 3.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was traveling along the highway between the towns of Ukkaṭṭha and Setabya, he was followed by a brahmin named Dona who was amazed to see <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato"><b>thousand-spoked wheels</b></a> complete with rims and hubs imprinted in the Buddha's footprints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato">AN 4.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the brahmin Dona first saw the Buddha's footprints, he concluded that they could not possibly belong to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato"><b>human being</b></a> because of their extraordinary and perfect characteristics? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato">AN 4.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Dona described the Buddha as appearing serene and inspiring confidence, possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato"><b>peaceful faculties</b></a> and a peaceful mind, like a perfectly tamed and guarded tusker elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato">AN 4.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by the brahmin Dona if he would become a deva (a god), the Buddha explicitly replied that he would not become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato"><b>deva</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato">AN 4.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed the brahmin Dona that he would not become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato"><b>gandhabba</b></a> (a heavenly musician), because the specific taints that would lead to such a rebirth had been destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato">AN 4.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the brahmin Dona questioned if he was a yakkha (a spirit), the Buddha clarified that he was not a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato"><b>yakkha</b></a>, as he had uprooted the causes for such an existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato">AN 4.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even though he was walking on the road as a person, the Buddha told the brahmin Dona that he was not a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato"><b>human being</b></a> in the conventional sense of being bound by the taints of human existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato">AN 4.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the brahmin Dona that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a> (āsavas) which would otherwise lead him to be reborn as a deva, gandhabba, yakkha, or human have been cut off at the root like a palm stump? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato">AN 4.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared himself to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato"><b>white lotus</b></a> which, despite being born and growing in the water, rises above the surface and remains completely unsullied by the water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato">AN 4.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that although he was born in the world and grew up in the world, he lives having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato"><b>transcended the world</b></a> and remains unsullied by it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato">AN 4.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the brahmin Dona asked what kind of being he was, the Buddha instructed the brahmin to remember him simply as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato"><b>Buddha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato">AN 4.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the brahmin Dona that those taints by which he might have gone to a human birth have been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato"><b>destroyed and demolished</b></a>, making any future rebirth impossible for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.36/en/sujato">AN 4.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>four modes of practice</b></a> leading to the destruction of the taints, which differ based on whether the experience is pleasant or painful and whether knowledge is attained quickly or sluggishly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>painful practice</b></a> involves specific contemplations such as the foulness of the body and the repulsiveness of food to achieve spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk follows a difficult path when he dwells <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>contemplating foulness</b></a> in the body while relying on the five powers of a trainee to reach liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a mode of practice where a person maintains the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>perception of repulsiveness</b></a> in food as a means to weaken the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-delight</b></a> in the entire world as a component of the painful mode of practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that contemplating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>impermanence in all formations</b></a> is a foundational exercise for those whose path to direct knowledge is physically or mentally painful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of death</b></a> must be well established internally for a practitioner to successfully navigate the painful mode of practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>pleasant practice</b></a> as the ability to enter and dwell in the four meditative jhanas, which are characterized by seclusion from sensual pleasures and unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated all four modes of practice require the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>five powers of a trainee</b></a>: the power of faith, moral shame, moral dread, energy, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>sluggish direct knowledge</b></a> occurs when a practitioner's five spiritual faculties—faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom—are dull? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>quick direct knowledge</b></a> is achieved when a person possesses keen and predominant spiritual faculties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>dullness of faculties</b></a> like faith and concentration is the specific reason why some practitioners are slow to reach the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>keen spiritual faculties</b></a> allows a monk to quickly attain the "immediate" state that leads to the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a> as the basis for a practice that is pleasant, involving the movement from the joy and happiness of seclusion to the purity of equanimity and mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>moral shame and moral dread</b></a> as essential "powers" (sekhabalāni) that support a trainee regardless of whether their meditative path is easy or difficult? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>the power of energy</b></a> (vīriyabala) as one of the five trainee powers necessary for any monk seeking the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner relies on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato"><b>concentration and wisdom</b></a> as both faculties and powers to ensure that their progress toward enlightenment is either quick or steady? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.163/en/sujato">AN 4.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five things in the world that are welcome, agreeable, and pleasant, but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>difficult to obtain</b></a>: long life, beauty, happiness, fame, and heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught the householder Anāthapiṇḍika that five desired worldly states, such as long life and happiness, cannot be acquired through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>prayer or wishing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that if desirable qualities like beauty or fame could be gained simply through prayer, then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>no one would lack</b></a> anything in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha advised that a noble disciple who desires long life should not merely pray for it or delight in the idea of it, but should instead follow the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>way leading to long life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that for those who desire beauty, the correct approach is not to entreat for it but to practice the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>way leading to beauty</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that if a person desires happiness, they must actively engage in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>way leading to happiness</b></a> rather than relying on prayers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that obtaining fame requires a person to practice the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>way leading to fame</b></a> rather than just wishing for its arrival? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the attainment of heaven is not achieved through prayer or entreaty, but by a disciple practicing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>way leading to heaven</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that by following the appropriate practice, one can obtain benefits like long life, beauty, and happiness in both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>divine or human</b></a> forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that wise people specifically praise <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> (appamāda) when it is applied to the performance of meritorious deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a heedful person is called wise because they secure <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>both benefits</b></a>: the welfare of the present life and the welfare of the life to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha encouraged those who desire high birth and various pleasures to be diligent in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>performing merit</b></a> rather than just wishing for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on why prayer is ineffective for gaining worldly benefits was specifically prompted by a visit from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>householder Anāthapiṇḍika</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a> (paṇḍito) as one who, through the breakthrough to their own welfare, achieves the goals of both this world and the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato"><b>noble disciple</b></a> should not pray for long life, but should instead undertake the practice that actually leads to the acquisition of long life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.43/en/sujato">AN 5.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk who desires to reprove another should first establish <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>five qualities</b></a> within himself before speaking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, one should only speak to correct another <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>in due time</b></a> and never out of time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta emphasized that any rebuke must be based on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>what is factual</b></a> and not on what is false? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that when one monk corrects another, he should speak with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>gentle speech</b></a> rather than harsh language? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, the primary motivation for reproving another person must be to speak only what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>beneficial</b></a> and not what is harmful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that correction should be offered with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>loving mind</b></a> instead of an inner state of hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that a person who has been reproved improperly (such as at the wrong time or falsely) should be encouraged to feel <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>no remorse</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta noted that an improper rebuker—one who speaks falsely or harshly—should experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>remorse</b></a> so that other monks do not think it is acceptable to reprove others based on falsehoods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that if a person is reproved at the right time and with truth, they should actually be encouraged to feel <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>remorse</b></a> for their mistake to help them improve? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, a person who reproves another truthfully and with a loving mind should have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>no remorse</b></a> regarding their actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta believed that a person being reproved should strive to establish themselves in two specific qualities: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>truth and non-anger</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta declared that even if others reproved him—whether at the right time or wrong, or with a loving or hateful mind—he would personally remain established in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>truth and non-anger</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta practiced complete honesty when criticized, stating that if a fault was present in him, he would acknowledge it by saying, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>it exists in me</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that despite such clear instructions, some <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>empty men</b></a> still refuse to take advice or correction gracefully? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta characterized those who refuse correction as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>faithless</b></a>, deceitful, and having entered the monkhood merely for a livelihood rather than out of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta described people who reject advice as often being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained</b></a> in their senses, immoderate in eating, and not devoted to wakefulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified those who do not value training as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>lazy</b></a>, lacking energy, and possessing poor mindfulness and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta defined "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>sons of good family</b></a>" as those who have gone forth out of faith, are honest, and are willing to accept advice carefully? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that those who accept correction are often characterized by being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>restrained</b></a> in their senses, moderate in eating, and diligent in their spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Venerable Sāriputta to specifically focus on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato"><b>exhorting</b></a> and instructing fellow monks who are faithful and sincere? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.167/en/sujato">AN 5.167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the wanderer Vacchagotta asked the Buddha if he taught that gifts should only be given to him and his disciples, the Buddha clarified that such a claim was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>misrepresentation</b></a> of his actual teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any person who prevents someone else from giving a gift creates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>three obstacles</b></a>: an obstacle to the donor's merit, an obstacle to the recipients' gain, and harm to their own spiritual state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a person stops another from giving, they have already <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>gashed and uprooted</b></a> themselves spiritually before they even affect the donor or the recipient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the value of even the smallest act of charity, stating that merit is acquired even when someone throws bowl-rinsing water into a cesspool with the thought, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>May the living beings</b></a> there feed on this"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha encouraged giving to all, he specified that a gift given to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>virtuous person</b></a> yields far greater fruit than a gift given to an immoral person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five specific factors a recipient should have abandoned for a gift to be of great fruit, the first of which is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a donation to yield the highest results, the recipient must have abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a>, which is one of the five hindrances to spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a worthy recipient of gifts as one who has completely abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a>, ensuring their mind is active and clear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> as factors that must be abandoned by a practitioner for them to become a "field of merit" that brings great fruit to donors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a gift has great fruit when the recipient has abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a>, having achieved certainty in the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ideal recipient of a gift as someone "beyond training" (asekkha) who possesses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of virtuous behavior</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a donation is most effective when given to one who possesses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of concentration</b></a>, representing a mind that is steady and focused? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a worthy recipient must possess the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of wisdom</b></a>, signifying a deep understanding of the nature of reality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the highest level of a gift-recipient as one who possesses the aggregate of liberation and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of knowledge and vision</b></a> of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy of oxen to explain merit, noting that just as people value a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>tamed bull</b></a> for its strength and ability to pull a load regardless of its color, so too is a virtuous person valued regardless of their social background? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person's birth—whether they are a Khattiya, Brahmin, Vessa, Sudda, or an outcast—is irrelevant to their worthiness as a recipient of gifts compared to their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>virtue and self-taming</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who has finished their spiritual task and is free from taints (āsavas) as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>dust-free field</b></a> where offerings become immensely fruitful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that "fools" who lack wisdom give gifts to the unworthy ("outside"), whereas the wise support the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>peaceful ones</b></a> who live according to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that those whose faith in the "One Well Gone" (the Sugata) is firmly rooted will eventually go to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> or be reborn in a good family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded his teaching to Vacchagotta by stating that wise people who practice charity toward the virtuous will, in due course, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato"><b>attain Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.57/en/sujato">AN 3.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses four specific qualities—virtue, sense restraint, moderation in eating, and devotion to wakefulness—is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>incapable of falling away</b></a> from his progress and is in the very presence of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>accomplished in virtue</b></a> when he dwells restrained by the Pātimokkha, is perfect in conduct and resort, and sees danger in even the slightest faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>guarded in the sense doors</b></a> when he does not grasp at the signs or features of objects perceived by the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, or mind, thereby preventing unwholesome states from flowing in? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that sense restraint involves actively practicing for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>restraining the eye faculty</b></a> so that evil, unwholesome states like covetousness and displeasure do not overwhelm the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>moderation in eating</b></a> by reflecting wisely that food is not for amusement, intoxication, or beautification, but solely for the support and maintenance of the physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that food should be consumed for the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>ending discomfort</b></a> and assisting the spiritual life, rather than for the sake of vanity or indulgence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>devotion to wakefulness</b></a> as a practice where a monk purifies his mind of obstructive qualities through walking and sitting during the day and the first watch of the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to spend the middle watch of the night lying down on their right side in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>lion’s posture</b></a>, placing one foot on the other while remaining mindful and clearly comprehending? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to maintain a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>perception of rising</b></a> while sleeping, so that they might promptly wake up during the last watch of the night to continue purifying their minds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by reflecting wisely on the purpose of food, a monk ensures that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>old feelings are terminated</b></a> and new feelings of discomfort do not arise, leading to a blameless and comfortable life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>restrained in the mind faculty</b></a> avoids the influx of evil, unwholesome states of covetousness and displeasure that occur when the mind is left unguarded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk who lives <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>ardently and untiringly</b></a> day and night, cultivating wholesome qualities, is effectively working toward the attainment of security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>delighting in heedfulness</b></a> (appamāda) as a critical mindset that prevents a monk from falling away from his spiritual attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk should be one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>sees danger in heedlessness</b></a> (pamāda) to ensure he remains steadfast on the path toward the realization of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the continuous practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>walking and sitting</b></a> throughout the day and parts of the night as a primary method for a monk to cleanse his heart of obstructive states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato"><b>restrained by the Pātimokkha</b></a> is one who trains himself thoroughly in the rules of training, which is the foundation for avoiding spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.37/en/sujato">AN 4.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato"><b>four modes of practice</b></a>: the intolerant practice, the tolerant practice, the practice of taming, and the practice of calming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato">AN 4.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato"><b>intolerant practice</b></a> as the behavior of one who reviles back when reviled, acts angrily when others are angry with them, and quarrels back when someone quarrels with them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato">AN 4.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato"><b>tolerant practice</b></a> as when a person does not revile back at one who reviles them, does not return anger, and does not respond to quarreling with more quarreling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato">AN 4.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato"><b>practice of taming</b></a> involves guarding the sense faculties so that when a person sees a form with the eye, they do not grasp at its signs or features? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato">AN 4.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that in the practice of taming, a monk practices for the restraint of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato"><b>eye faculty</b></a> to prevent evil unwholesome states like covetousness and displeasure from flowing in? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato">AN 4.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the restraint of hearing, smelling, tasting, and touching within the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato"><b>practice of taming</b></a>, ensuring that a practitioner guards all physical sense doors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato">AN 4.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the practice of taming also applies to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato"><b>mind faculty</b></a>, where a practitioner does not grasp at the signs of mental objects but instead practices for their restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato">AN 4.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato"><b>practice of calming</b></a> as the discipline of not tolerating arisen thoughts of sensual desire, but instead abandoning, dispelling, and terminating them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato">AN 4.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk following the mode of calming will not harbor an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato"><b>arisen thought of ill-will</b></a> or harmfulness, but will instead work to bring such thoughts to nonexistence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato">AN 4.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the practice of calming requires a practitioner to eliminate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato"><b>evil unwholesome states</b></a> as soon as they arise, rather than allowing them to persist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.164/en/sujato">AN 4.164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the householder Ugga of Vesālī told the Buddha he had heard face-to-face that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>one who gives</b></a> what is agreeable (manāpa) will receive what is agreeable in return? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha accepted an offering of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>sal-flower snacks</b></a> from the householder Ugga, which Ugga gave because they were personally agreeable to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the householder Ugga offered the Buddha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>pork with jujube</b></a> prepared with many sauces, requesting the Buddha to accept it out of compassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha accepted a dish of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>fried vegetables</b></a> prepared in oil from the householder Ugga as part of a meal consisting of Ugga's favorite foods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder Ugga provided the Buddha with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>choice hill rice</b></a> served with many soups and curries, illustrating his practice of giving what is agreeable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha received a gift of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>Kāsi cloths</b></a> from the householder Ugga, which were considered highly agreeable and valuable garments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the householder Ugga realized a luxurious couch was not suitable for a monk, he instead offered the Buddha a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>sandalwood plank</b></a> worth over a hundred thousand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught in his verses of appreciation that a "good person" (sappuriso) is one who gives what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>difficult to give</b></a> to those who are upright? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that one who gives with a clear heart to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>upright ones</b></a> (Arahants) is like one sowing seeds in a supreme field of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the giver of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>various requisites</b></a>—such as clothing, bedding, and food—to those who are liberated will receive agreeable results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the householder Ugga passed away, the Buddha's teachings on giving led to Ugga being reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>mind-made body</b></a> as a deity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha was staying at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>Jetavana</b></a> in Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery when the newly reborn deity Ugga visited him, illuminating the entire woods with his radiance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha asked the deity Ugga whether his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>intentions were fulfilled</b></a> regarding the agreeable rewards he sought through his past generosity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the deity Ugga confirmed to the Buddha that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>agreeable purpose</b></a> had been entirely fulfilled following his death and rebirth in the celestial realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a person who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>giver of the foremost</b></a> (aggassa dātā) will eventually receive what is foremost for themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that one who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>giver of the best</b></a> (varassa dātā) will consistently become a recipient of the best things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>giver of the excellent</b></a> (seṭṭhadāyī) is the one who attains an excellent state in their future lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that whoever gives the foremost, the best, and the excellent will be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato"><b>long-lived and famous</b></a> wherever they are reborn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.44/en/sujato">AN 5.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that for a person who is unvirtuous and fails in virtue, the proximal cause for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> is destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato">AN 5.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, when right concentration is lacking, a person cannot fulfill the proximal cause for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of things as they really are</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato">AN 5.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that without the knowledge and vision of things as they really are, a practitioner lacks the proximal cause for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato"><b>revulsion and dispassion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato">AN 5.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that if a person fails to develop revulsion and dispassion, they will also fail to fulfill the proximal cause for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato">AN 5.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato"><b>tree deficient in branches and foliage</b></a> to describe how a lack of virtue prevents the development of further spiritual qualities like bark, softwood, and heartwood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato">AN 5.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that for a virtuous person who is possessed of virtue, the proximal cause for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> is naturally provided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato">AN 5.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, the presence of right concentration is the essential proximal cause for achieving the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of things as they really are</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato">AN 5.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that for someone who possesses the knowledge and vision of things as they really are, the proximal cause for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato"><b>revulsion and dispassion</b></a> is successfully established? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato">AN 5.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that when a tree is complete in its branches and foliage, its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato"><b>bark, softwood, and heartwood</b></a> also go to fulfillment, just as a virtuous person's spiritual path leads to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato">AN 5.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified the fulfillment of revulsion and dispassion as the direct proximal cause for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.168/en/sujato">AN 5.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Tikaṇṇa the brahmin approached the Buddha and spoke in high praise of the traditional <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>three-knowledge brahmins</b></a>, asserting their superiority based on their mastery of Vedic lore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the traditional view shared with the Buddha, a brahmin is considered "three-knowledge" only if they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>well-born</b></a> for seven generations on both the maternal and paternal sides? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Tikaṇṇa the brahmin defined the traditional three knowledges as mastery of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>three Vedas</b></a>, including the glosses, rituals, phonology, and history? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the traditional brahminical definition of a three-knowledge person is entirely different from how one becomes a person of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a> in the Noble One’s Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a bhikkhu seeking the three knowledges must first enter and dwell in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>first jhana</b></a>, a state born of seclusion and accompanied by rapture and pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the attainment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhana</b></a>, characterized by purified mindfulness and equanimity, as a prerequisite for directing the mind to the three true knowledges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the first of the three true knowledges is the ability to recollect manifold <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>past lives</b></a>, spanning through many eons of world-contraction and world-expansion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that with the first true knowledge, a practitioner remembers specific details of previous existences, such as their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>name, clan, and appearance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the first true knowledge is realized, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>ignorance is banished</b></a> and light arises for the meditator who dwells diligent and ardent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the second true knowledge as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a>, which allows one to see beings passing away and being reborn according to their kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings who engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>bad conduct</b></a> of body, speech, and mind are reborn in states of misery or hell after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>good conduct</b></a> who do not revile the Noble Ones are reborn in a happy destination or the heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the third true knowledge as the knowledge of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavas)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the third knowledge involves understanding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>suffering, its origin, its cessation</b></a>, and the path to its cessation exactly as they are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that through the third true knowledge, the mind is liberated from the taints of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire, existence, and ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who has attained the third knowledge knows for certain that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a> and the holy life has been lived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true sage as one who knows their past lives, sees heaven and hell, and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>overcome death</b></a> through the destruction of birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that he calls only someone with these three spiritual realizations a true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>three-knowledge brahmin</b></a>, dismissing mere verbal recitation as insufficient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Tikaṇṇa the brahmin admitted to the Buddha that the traditional brahmin definition of a "three-knowledge" person is not worth a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>sixteenth part</b></a> of the Buddha's definition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is praised in this sutta as the one who has abandoned all and is endowed with these three knowledges, being the one with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>final body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the three knowledges are attained by a meditator whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato"><b>mind is mastered</b></a> and one-pointed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.58/en/sujato">AN 3.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato"><b>withdrawn</b></a> when they have discarded individual truths, discarded searches, and stilled bodily activities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato">AN 4.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a monk has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato"><b>discarded individual truths</b></a> when they have relinquished speculative views held by various recluses and brahmins, such as the idea that the world is eternal or non-eternal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato">AN 4.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, part of discarding individual truths involves letting go of the speculative view regarding whether the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato"><b>soul and the body</b></a> are identical or separate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato">AN 4.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a monk who has discarded individual truths no longer holds dogmatic positions on whether a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata exists after death</b></a>, does not exist, both, or neither? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato">AN 4.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a monk who has discarded searches as one for whom the search for sensual pleasures and the search for existence are abandoned, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato"><b>search for a holy life</b></a> has been stilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato">AN 4.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the stilling of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato"><b>bodily activities</b></a> as the state where a monk, through the abandoning of pleasure and pain, enters and dwells in the fourth jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato">AN 4.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the fourth jhāna—the state where bodily activities are stilled—as having neither pain nor pleasure and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato"><b>purification of mindfulness</b></a> by equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato">AN 4.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for a monk to be truly considered withdrawn, the Buddha taught they must have abandoned the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato"><b>conceit ‘I am’</b></a>, cutting it off at the root so it is no longer subject to future arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato">AN 4.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato"><b>palmyra stump</b></a> to describe how a withdrawn monk has completely uprooted the conceit "I am," making it unable to sprout again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato">AN 4.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is liberated through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a> as one who has relinquished all searches and uprooted the grounds for speculative views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato">AN 4.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha referred to a monk who is peaceful, mindful, and has made a breakthrough of conceit as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato"><b>unconquered</b></a> and truly withdrawn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.38/en/sujato">AN 4.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are five <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato"><b>streams of merit</b></a> and streams of the wholesome that bring about happiness, are heavenly, and result in bliss for the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato">AN 5.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a donor gains an immeasurable stream of merit when a monk makes use of a donated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato"><b>robe</b></a> while entering and dwelling in an immeasurable concentration of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato">AN 5.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person receives an incalculable stream of the wholesome when a monk consumes their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> and subsequently enters into an immeasurable concentration of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato">AN 5.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that providing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato"><b>dwelling</b></a> for a monk who uses it to attain an immeasurable concentration of mind results in a vast stream of merit that is conducive to heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato">AN 5.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the donation of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato"><b>bed or chair</b></a> to a monk as a source of immeasurable merit, provided the recipient enters a deep state of mental concentration while using it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato">AN 5.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that giving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato"><b>medicinal requisites</b></a> for the sick to a monk who dwells in immeasurable concentration of mind creates a stream of merit that leads to what is wished for and agreeable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato">AN 5.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the difficulty of measuring the merit of a noble disciple to the difficulty of measuring the amount of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato"><b>water in the ocean</b></a> in terms of specific quantities like gallons or liters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato">AN 5.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that for a disciple endowed with these five streams of merit, their goodness is simply reckoned as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato"><b>incalculable, immeasurable mass</b></a> of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato">AN 5.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato"><b>noble disciple</b></a> who is possessed of these five streams of merit is like the great ocean, which is far too vast for its contents to be easily measured? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato">AN 5.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato"><b>rivers flowing</b></a> into the great sea to illustrate how streams of merit flow toward a wise and generous person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato">AN 5.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the merit of a gift is particularly powerful when the monk who receives it enters and dwells in an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable concentration of mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato">AN 5.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a> as a giver of food, drink, and clothing who is reached by merit just as the many rivers reach the vast and terrifying ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.45/en/sujato">AN 5.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddaji stated that the supreme form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato"><b>seeing</b></a> is to behold the Great Brahmā, the vanquisher, the unvanquished, the all-seeing, and the wielder of power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato">AN 5.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddaji identified the sounds of the Ābhassara devas shouting "Oh, what bliss!" as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato"><b>foremost hearing</b></a> among all things that can be heard? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato">AN 5.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Bhaddaji, the serene and tranquil happiness experienced by the Subhakiṇha devas represents the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato"><b>supreme happiness</b></a> available in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato">AN 5.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddaji considered the mental state of the devas who have reached the sphere of nothingness to be the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato"><b>foremost perception</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato">AN 5.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddaji taught that the state of the devas who have reached the sphere of neither-perception-nor-non-perception is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato"><b>foremost existence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato">AN 5.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda clarified that the true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato"><b>foremost seeing</b></a> is that vision which leads immediately to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato">AN 5.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato"><b>foremost hearing</b></a> is not listening to the shouts of deities, but is that hearing which results in the immediate destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato">AN 5.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Ānanda, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato"><b>foremost happiness</b></a> is the joy of one whose experience of pleasure leads directly to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato">AN 5.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato"><b>foremost perception</b></a> as the perception of a person whose state of mind leads immediately to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato">AN 5.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato"><b>foremost existence</b></a> as the state of one who has become such that it leads immediately to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.170/en/sujato">AN 5.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a lay follower who destroys life, takes what is not given, and commits sexual misconduct is characterized by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.171/en/sujato"><b>diffidence</b></a> when entering an assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.171/en/sujato">AN 5.171</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a lay follower who abstains from taking life, stealing, and sexual misconduct is endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.171/en/sujato"><b>self-confidence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.171/en/sujato">AN 5.171</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the drinking of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.171/en/sujato"><b>intoxicants</b></a> as one of the five specific behaviors that cause a lay follower to fall into timidity and lack of self-assurance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.171/en/sujato">AN 5.171</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a lay follower who refrains from false speech is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.171/en/sujato"><b>self-confident</b></a> and assured in their conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.171/en/sujato">AN 5.171</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato"><b>impatient way</b></a> as a practice where a person cannot endure physical hardships such as cold, heat, hunger, and thirst, nor the stings and bites of insects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato">AN 4.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato"><b>patient way</b></a> involves the capacity to endure the touch of gadflies and mosquitoes, as well as the harsh contact of the wind and sun? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato">AN 4.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato"><b>way of taming</b></a> as a practice where a practitioner does not grasp at the signs or features of objects seen with the eye, heard with the ear, or perceived through other senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato">AN 4.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato"><b>way of calming</b></a> requires that one does not tolerate arisen sensual thoughts, but instead abandons, dispels, and eliminates them entirely? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato">AN 4.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person following the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato"><b>patient way</b></a> is able to endure ill-spoken and unwelcome words, as well as bodily feelings that are painful, piercing, and even life-threatening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato">AN 4.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato"><b>way of taming</b></a> involves practicing restraint of the sense faculties to prevent evil unwholesome states like covetousness and displeasure from flowing in while one cognizes mental phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato">AN 4.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the removal and termination of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of ill will</b></a> and thoughts of harmfulness as essential components of the way of calming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato">AN 4.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato"><b>impatient way</b></a> by an individual's inability to endure bodily feelings that are racking, sharp, severe, and disagreeable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato">AN 4.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato"><b>way of taming</b></a>, a practitioner specifically guards and attains restraint over the mind faculty to ensure that no unwholesome states arise from mental cognition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato">AN 4.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato"><b>way of calming</b></a> must not only settle arisen unwholesome states but also bring them to "non-existence"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.165/en/sujato">AN 4.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Brahmin Jāṇussoṇi asked the Buddha who should receive offerings, he suggested that gifts should be given to those Brahmins who possess the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>triple knowledge</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned the traditional definition of the "triple knowledge," noting that the way a Brahmin is defined as such by other Brahmins is entirely different from how one is defined in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>Noble One’s discipline</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Jāṇussoṇi defined a "triple-knowledge" Brahmin as someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>well-born on both sides</b></a> for seven generations and is a master of the three Vedas, rituals, and grammar? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person to attain the true triple knowledge, they must first enter and dwell in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhana</b></a>, a state of deep concentration that is purified and bright? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the mind necessary for the triple knowledge as one that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>malleable and wieldy</b></a>, steady, and has attained imperturbability through meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the "first knowledge" in his discipline as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>recollection of past lives</b></a>, where a practitioner remembers numerous past births in all their details and features? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a practitioner attains the first true knowledge, ignorance is dispelled and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>light arises</b></a>, just as it does for one who dwells diligent, ardent, and resolute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the "second knowledge" as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a>, which allows a practitioner to see beings passing away and being reborn according to their kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by using the divine eye, a practitioner understands how beings who engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>misconduct of body</b></a>, speech, and mind are reborn in states of misery or even hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a practitioner with the divine eye sees that beings with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>, who act well in body, speech, and mind, are reborn in a happy destination or even the heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the "third knowledge" as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>, where one understands the Four Noble Truths as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the third knowledge involves direct realization of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>way leading to the cessation</b></a> of suffering and the cessation of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described liberation as the mind being freed from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>taint of sensual desire</b></a>, the taint of existence, and the taint of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a practitioner's mind is liberated, the knowledge arises that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a> and the holy life has been lived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a true "triple-knowledge Brahmin" in the Noble discipline as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>muni</b></a> (silent sage) who has attained the end of birth and is accomplished in direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a verse to explain that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>virtuous and concentrated</b></a>, and whose mind is mastered and one-pointed, is the one who truly knows past lives and sees heaven and states of deprivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha insisted that he only calls a person a "triple-knowledge Brahmin" if they possess the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>three true knowledges</b></a> he described, rather than just reciting Vedic hymns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's explanation, Jāṇussoṇi remarked that the traditional Brahmin definition of triple knowledge was not worth a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>sixteenth part</b></a> of the Buddha's definition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for the triple knowledge to arise, darkness must be dispelled by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato"><b>arising of true knowledge</b></a> through diligent and resolute practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.59/en/sujato">AN 3.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato"><b>four developments of concentration</b></a> which, when cultivated and frequently practiced, lead to distinct spiritual results such as a pleasant dwelling or the destruction of taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato">AN 4.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the practice of the four jhanas as the specific development of concentration that leads to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwelling</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato">AN 4.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner can attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato"><b>attainment of knowledge and vision</b></a> by attending to the perception of light and resolving on the perception of day, regardless of whether it is day or night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato">AN 4.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that mindfulness and clear comprehension are developed when a practitioner ensures that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato"><b>feelings are known</b></a> as they arise, as they remain present, and as they pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato">AN 4.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the development of concentration leading to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> involves dwelling in contemplation of the rise and fall of the five aggregates of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato">AN 4.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to cultivate a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato"><b>bright mind</b></a> that is open and unhindered by maintaining a constant perception of light that does not change between day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato">AN 4.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato"><b>perceptions and thoughts</b></a> must be clearly known as they arise, stay, and vanish in order to develop the concentration that leads to mindfulness and clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato">AN 4.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that to reach the end of suffering, the Buddha instructed that one must understand the origin and passing away of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato"><b>material form</b></a> as the first of the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato">AN 4.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized contemplating the nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a>, including their arising and disappearance, as a necessary step for the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato">AN 4.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato"><b>crossed over birth and old age</b></a> as someone who is peaceful, free from the "smoke" of defilements, and without distress or longing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato">AN 4.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that understanding the origin and passing away of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is part of the specific concentration practice required to eliminate the taints of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato">AN 4.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha quoted the "Questions of Puṇṇaka" from the Pārāyana to describe a sage who has evaluated the world and remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato"><b>unperturbed</b></a> anywhere in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.41/en/sujato">AN 4.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ānanda</b></a> taught that any monk or nun who declares the attainment of arahantship has done so by following one of four specific paths involving serenity and insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato">AN 4.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda described the first path to liberation as a process where a practitioner develops <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato"><b>insight preceded by serenity</b></a>, which eventually causes the noble path to be born within them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato">AN 4.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that a second way to reach awakening is by developing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato"><b>serenity preceded by insight</b></a>, a method where the path arises after the practitioner has already established analytical vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato">AN 4.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Ānanda, a third path to enlightenment involves a practitioner developing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato"><b>serenity and insight joined in tandem</b></a>, meaning both qualities are cultivated together until the noble path is realized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato">AN 4.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda identified a fourth path where a practitioner’s mind is initially <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato"><b>seized by agitation</b></a> regarding the Dhamma, but eventually settles and becomes unified in concentration, leading to the birth of the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato">AN 4.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that once the noble path is born through any of these four methods, the practitioner must continue to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato"><b>pursue and cultivate</b></a> it to ensure the final destruction of mental obstructions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato">AN 4.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda stated that by frequently practicing the noble path, a practitioner's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato"><b>fetters are abandoned</b></a> and their underlying unwholesome tendencies are completely uprooted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato">AN 4.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda specifically mentioned that in the fourth path of practice, there comes a time when the mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato"><b>becomes steadied internally</b></a> and unified, which is the necessary condition for the path to awaken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato">AN 4.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda emphasized that regardless of which of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato"><b>four paths</b></a> a monk or nun takes, they all lead to the same result of declaring the final realization of arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.170/en/sujato">AN 4.170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, no being in the universe can successfully wish that what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>subject to aging</b></a> should not age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained it is impossible for anyone to obtain a condition where that which is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>subject to illness</b></a> does not fall ill? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that no power in the world can prevent that which is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>subject to death</b></a> from eventually dying? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that no one can prevent the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>destruction</b></a> of things that are naturally subject to being destroyed or wearing out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified that which is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>subject to vanishing</b></a> as a state that cannot be stopped from vanishing by any being, including a Brahma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an uninstructed worldling who suffers over aging as being pierced by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>poisonous dart of sorrow</b></a> that causes them to torment themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an instructed noble disciple reflects that aging, death, and destruction happen to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>all beings</b></a> who undergo birth and passing away, and not just to themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when an uninstructed person sorrows over the inevitable, they become confused, and even their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>food no longer tastes good</b></a> to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that excessive lamentation over illness or death can lead to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>deterioration of the body</b></a> and a loss of physical well-being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that when a person falls into grief and sorrow, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>work and duties</b></a> are neglected and do not proceed as they should? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that when a person is overwhelmed by sorrow, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>enemies are delighted</b></a> to see their distress and lack of composure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that when a person laments over what is subject to vanishing, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>friends become dejected</b></a> and unhappy due to their suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple who pulls out the poisonous dart of sorrow becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>sorrowless and dartless</b></a>, attaining peace within themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a wise person as one who knows how to determine what is beneficial and does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>tremble in misfortune</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a wise person faces misfortune with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>unchanged countenance</b></a>, it is their enemies who become distressed instead? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>sorrowing and lamenting</b></a> provides not even the slightest bit of benefit when dealing with the unavoidable facts of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that if a purpose can be achieved, one should strive for it using <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>skillful speech</b></a>, mantras, gifts, or tradition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a goal is truly unattainable, one should endure it without sorrow, reflecting that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>kamma is strong</b></a> and asking, "What can I do now?" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mark of an instructed noble disciple is the ability to face aging and death <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato"><b>without sorrow</b></a>, having understood the universal nature of all beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.48/en/sujato">AN 5.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato"><b>immoral person</b></a> as one who has failed to abandon five specific fears and animosities—such as the destruction of life and taking what is not given—and warned that such an individual is destined for rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato">AN 5.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato"><b>virtuous person</b></a> is one who has successfully abandoned five types of harmful conduct, thereby ensuring that upon the breakup of the body, they are reborn in a happy destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato">AN 5.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when someone engages in the destruction of life, they generate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato"><b>fears and animosities</b></a> that affect them both in this life and in future lives, while also causing themselves significant mental pain and grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato">AN 5.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person who abstains from the destruction of life, the fear and animosity usually caused by such actions are completely stilled in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato"><b>present life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato">AN 5.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> results in immediate mental distress and animosity, and that these negative states only subside when a person firmly abstains from such behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato">AN 5.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified the consumption of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato"><b>intoxicants</b></a>—specifically wine and liquor that are a basis for heedlessness—as one of the five primary sources of fear and animosity that an individual must abandon to be considered virtuous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato">AN 5.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that an individual who tells lies will experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato"><b>mental pain</b></a> and grief as a direct consequence, but by abstaining from false speech, these negative mental states can be entirely stilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato">AN 5.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that after the breakup of the body, a person of poor wisdom who has not abandoned the five animosities is destined for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato">AN 5.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person who abandons the five fears is one who reaches a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato"><b>happy destination</b></a> because they have eliminated the internal and external conflicts arising from immoral conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato">AN 5.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the quality of one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato"><b>future life</b></a> is directly determined by whether they have abandoned the five animosities, as these actions produce ongoing fear and mental suffering that transcend the present life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.174/en/sujato">AN 5.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>sectarian tenets</b></a> which, when scrutinized by the wise, are found to lead to a doctrine of inaction and the denial of personal responsibility for one's deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued against the view that every pleasure, pain, or neutral experience a person feels is entirely due to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>past deeds</b></a>, noting that such a fatalistic belief leaves no room for the will to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refuted the belief that all human experiences are created by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>will of a supreme deity</b></a> because such a perspective makes moral effort and spiritual progress meaningless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha criticized the theory that human experiences occur <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>without cause or condition</b></a>, as this view fails to provide a logical basis for choosing wholesome actions over unwholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that if all actions were determined by past karma or divine creation, then people would become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>murderers and thieves</b></a> simply because of those predetermined factors, effectively absolving them of any personal blame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for those who rely on fatalistic sectarian views, there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>desire or effort</b></a> to perform what should be done or to avoid what should not be done? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his own teaching as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>irrefutable Dhamma</b></a> that is untainted and unblamable, specifically because it does not fall into the trap of promoting inaction or moral nihilism? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the human being is fundamentally composed of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>six elements</b></a>: earth, water, fire, air, space, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s irrefutable teaching includes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>six bases of contact</b></a>, which are the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind, through which we experience the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha analyzed the complexities of the human mind through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>eighteen mental explorations</b></a>, which involve the different ways we react to sense objects with joy, grief, or equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>descent into the womb</b></a> occurs in dependence on the six elements, marking the beginning of an individual's conditioned existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that once descent into the womb has occurred, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> emerges as a necessary condition for the development of the sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>six sense bases</b></a> arise in dependence on name-and-form, which then allows for the occurrence of contact with external objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> arises in dependence on contact, and it is for the person who experiences these feelings that he proclaims the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>noble truth of suffering</b></a> as encompassing birth, aging, death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, dejection, and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> by tracing the chain of dependent origination back to ignorance as the underlying condition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> is achieved through the complete and remainderless fading away of ignorance, which causes the entire mass of suffering to cease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>noble eightfold path</b></a>—comprising right view, right intention, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right concentration—as the way leading to the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that if the theory of past-action determinism were true, then the very concept of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>spiritual life</b></a> would be impossible because no one would have the freedom to change their behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>earth element</b></a> is one of the six fundamental components of the Dhamma that cannot be refuted by wise recluses or brahmins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>desire to act</b></a> (chanda) is lost due to beliefs in determinism, one lives in a state of muddled mindfulness and lacks any real protection from unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the entire <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>mass of suffering</b></a> originates from a sequence of conditions starting with ignorance and leading through volition, consciousness, and birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s second refutation focused on those who believe experiences are due to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>creation by a Lord</b></a>, explaining that such a view provides no path for self-transformation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato"><b>joy and grief</b></a> as mental explorations that occur specifically after the mind perceives a mental phenomenon, as part of the eighteen ways the mind explores its objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.61/en/sujato">AN 3.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that an uninstructed worldling often identifies a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>great fire</b></a> as a danger that separates a mother and son, though in reality, they may sometimes find each other again after such a disaster? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that while a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>great flood</b></a> can wash away entire villages and towns, it is considered a "separable" danger because parents and children often reunite once the waters recede? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>uprising in the countryside</b></a>, where people flee in panic, as a situation that the common person wrongly believes creates an absolute separation between a mother and her child? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that fire, flood, and war are actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>separable dangers</b></a> because there are occasions when a mother still finds her son and a son still finds his mother despite the chaos? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>old age</b></a> as one of the three true "mother-and-son-separating" fears, because a parent and child cannot protect one another from the process of aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>illness</b></a> is a danger where a mother cannot intervene to say, "Let me be sick instead so that my son does not suffer"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>death</b></a> is an ultimate fear where a son is powerless to take his mother's place to prevent her from dying? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a child is undergoing the process of aging, a mother <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>cannot aging for another</b></a> to spare them, emphasizing the individual nature of physical decay? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even the deepest bond between a mother and son cannot facilitate a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>transfer of sickness</b></a>, as each person must experience their own bodily ailments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>uninstructed worldling</b></a> focuses on external disasters like fires and floods as the primary fears, while overlooking the deeper, unavoidable separations of aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed there is a specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>path and way</b></a> that leads to the abandoning and transcending of both separable and inseparable fears? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> as the method for overcoming the fundamental fears of old age, illness, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> as the first factor of the practice designed to help a practitioner transcend the three true mother-and-son-separating dangers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> as the final factor of the path that leads to the total transcendence of suffering and fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>villages and towns</b></a> burning to illustrate the type of worldly disaster that causes great panic but is ultimately less separating than the process of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of people <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>mounted on wheels</b></a> fleeing the countryside to escape danger, highlighting the desperate attempts humans make to avoid external threats? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the fear of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>great deluge</b></a> as a "mother-and-son fear" because the potential for reunion still exists, unlike the absolute separation caused by mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the practice of the Dhamma is aimed at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>transcending</b></a> of all fears, including those that are misunderstood by common people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato"><b>limitations of parental love</b></a> by pointing out that a mother cannot shield her son from the inherent suffering of birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.62/en/sujato">AN 3.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deva named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>Rohitassa</b></a> once approached the Buddha at Jetavana late at night to ask if it is possible to reach the end of the world—a place where one is not born, does not age, and does not die—by traveling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained to the deva Rohitassa that the end of the world, where there is no rebirth or death, cannot be known, seen, or reached by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>traveling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Rohitassa revealed he was once a seer with great psychic power who was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>sky-walker</b></a>, capable of moving with immense speed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the seer Rohitassa claimed his speed in a past life was so great that he could cross the distance between the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>eastern and western oceans</b></a> in a single stride? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Rohitassa compared his past physical speed to that of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>skilled archer</b></a>, trained and practiced, who could easily shoot a light arrow across the shadow of a palm tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his psychic speed and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>hundred-year lifespan</b></a> spent traveling without pause (except for basic needs), Rohitassa died on his journey without ever reaching the physical end of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that although the world's end is not reachable by travel, there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>making an end of suffering</b></a> without reaching that end of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the world, its origin, and its cessation within this very <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>fathom-long body</b></a> which is endowed with perception and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha proclaimed that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>origin of the world</b></a> is not something to be found externally, but is made known within the conscious framework of an individual's own being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refers to an enlightened person who has understood the world's end as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>knower of the world</b></a> (lokavidū), who has fulfilled the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>cessation of the world</b></a> and the path leading to it are truths contained within our own physical and mental experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a person who has reached the world's end and is at peace <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>does not long</b></a> for this world or for any world beyond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Rohitassa expressed amazement at the Buddha's wisdom, noting how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken</b></a> the Buddha was regarding the impossibility of reaching the world's end through physical movement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that the way to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato"><b>deliverance from suffering</b></a> is found by knowing the end of the world through internal peace rather than through external travel? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.45/en/sujato">AN 4.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was visited by a deva named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>Rohitassa</b></a> who came to ask if it was possible to reach the "end of the world"—a place where there is no birth, aging, or death—through physical travel? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the deva Rohitassa that the end of the world, where one does not pass away or take rebirth, is not a place that can be known, seen, or reached by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>traveling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Rohitassa revealed to the Buddha that in a past life he was a seer named Rohitassa who possessed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>psychic power</b></a> and could walk through the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Rohitassa described his past physical speed to the Buddha as being so immense that he could cross from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>eastern ocean to the western ocean</b></a> in just one stride? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Rohitassa compared his extraordinary past speed to the ease with which a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>skilled archer</b></a>, well-trained and practiced, could shoot a light arrow across the shadow of a palm tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite having a hundred-year lifespan and traveling constantly at incredible speed, the deva Rohitassa confessed to the Buddha that he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>died along the way</b></a> without ever finding the physical end of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that although the end of the world cannot be reached by travel, it is impossible to make an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a> without first reaching the world's end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that the world, its origin, its cessation, and the path to its cessation are all found within this <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>fathom-long carcass</b></a> endowed with perception and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the internal experience of a human being, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>endowed with perception</b></a> and mind, as the location where the entire cosmos and its ending must be understood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refers to an enlightened person who has understood the end of the world through wisdom as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>knower of the world</b></a> (lokavidū)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who has reached the end of the world has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>fulfilled the holy life</b></a> and is truly at peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person who has reached the world's end and is "stilled" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>does not long</b></a> for this world or for any world beyond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Rohitassa was so impressed by the Buddha's insight that he called the Buddha's teaching <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken</b></a>, marveling at how physical travel could never lead to the end of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato"><b>deliverance</b></a> from suffering is only possible by knowing the end of the world within oneself, rather than searching for it in the external universe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.46/en/sujato">AN 4.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> explained to Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika that to claim "something else" exists after the remainderless fading away and cessation of the six bases of contact is to conceptually proliferate that which is unproliferated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato">AN 4.173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato"><b>Sāriputta and Moggallāna</b></a> as the standard and measure by which all his monk disciples should be compared? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato">AN 4.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika</b></a> initiated a deep philosophical discussion with Sāriputta by asking four distinct logical questions about what remains after the total cessation of the six bases of contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato">AN 4.173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a faithful nun should aspire to be like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato"><b>Khemā and Uppalavaṇṇā</b></a>, who serve as the ideal model for all his nun disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato">AN 4.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato"><b>range of proliferation</b></a> extends exactly as far as the range of the six bases of contact, and no further? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato">AN 4.173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha held up <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato"><b>Citta the householder</b></a> and Hatthaka of Āḷavī as the perfect standard and measure for his male lay followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato">AN 4.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato"><b>Sāriputta</b></a> cautioned that claiming "nothing else" exists after the remainderless fading away of the six bases of contact is actually a form of conceptual proliferation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato">AN 4.173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato"><b>Khujjuttarā</b></a> and Veḷukaṇḍakiyā, Nanda’s mother, as the exemplary models for all his female lay disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato">AN 4.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato"><b>stilling of proliferation</b></a> (papañcavūpasamo) is achieved through the remainderless fading away and cessation of the six bases of contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato">AN 4.173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato"><b>faithful monk</b></a> to rightly aspire to become like the two chief disciples, Sāriputta and Moggallāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato">AN 4.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta rejected the notion that "both something and nothing else" exists after the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato"><b>cessation of contact</b></a>, noting that such a statement merely proliferates the unproliferated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato">AN 4.173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the phrase <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato"><b>"the standard and the measure"</b></a> (tulā etaṃ pamāṇaṃ) to define the ideal role models for each of the four groups of his disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato">AN 4.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that as far as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato"><b>six bases of contact</b></a> go, so far goes the range of conceptual proliferation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato">AN 4.173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato"><b>faithful layman</b></a> making a proper aspiration should desire to become like Citta the householder and Hatthaka of Āḷavī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato">AN 4.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that saying "neither something nor nothing else" exists after the fading away of the six bases of contact is an attempt to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato"><b>proliferate the unproliferated</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato">AN 4.173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha designated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato"><b>Nanda’s mother</b></a>, also known as Veḷukaṇḍakiyā, as one of the two primary models for female lay followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato">AN 4.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika sought to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato"><b>intended meaning</b></a> of Sāriputta's refusal to answer whether anything remains after the total cessation of the six bases of contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato">AN 4.173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato"><b>faithful laywoman</b></a> should rightly pray to become like the lay disciples Khujjuttarā and Veḷukaṇḍakiyā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato">AN 4.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato"><b>cessation of proliferation</b></a> (papañcanirodho) as being synonymous with the total cessation of the six bases of contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.173/en/sujato">AN 4.173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato"><b>Hatthaka of Āḷavī</b></a> and Citta the householder represent the highest standard for his male lay disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.176/en/sujato">AN 4.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Rāhula that both the internal and external <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato"><b>earth element</b></a> should be seen with correct wisdom as "this is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato">AN 4.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, once a person sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato"><b>water element</b></a> as it really is with correct wisdom, they become disenchanted with it and their mind becomes dispassionate toward it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato">AN 4.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Rāhula that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato"><b>fire element</b></a>, whether internal or external, is simply the fire element and should be viewed with the understanding that "this is not mine"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato">AN 4.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a monk sees neither self nor anything belonging to a self in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato"><b>four elements</b></a>, he has cut off craving and made an end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato">AN 4.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato"><b>air element</b></a> as "this is not my self" with correct wisdom leads to the mind's dispassion toward it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato">AN 4.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato"><b>breakthrough to conceit</b></a>, a monk who no longer perceives a self or what belongs to a self in the four elements has discarded the fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato">AN 4.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that both internal and external forms of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato"><b>elements</b></a> are just the element itself and should be regarded with the understanding "this I am not"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato">AN 4.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with the four elements—earth, water, fire, and air—is a direct result of seeing them as they really are with correct wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.177/en/sujato">AN 4.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained to Ānanda that the reason some beings do not attain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna in this very life</b></a> is that they do not understand certain types of perceptions as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato">AN 4.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato"><b>perceptions leading to decline</b></a> as one of the four categories of perception that beings must understand correctly to reach final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato">AN 4.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, understanding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato"><b>perceptions leading to stability</b></a> as they really are is a necessary condition for a person to attain Nibbāna in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato">AN 4.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a lack of knowledge regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato"><b>perceptions leading to distinction</b></a> prevents some beings from reaching the final goal in their current lifetime? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato">AN 4.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that those who attain Nibbāna in this life are those who truly understand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato"><b>perceptions leading to penetration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato">AN 4.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta told Ānanda that the cause and condition for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato"><b>attaining Nibbāna</b></a> in this life is the correct understanding of four specific types of perceptions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato">AN 4.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta used the phrase <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato"><b>"knowing as it really is"</b></a> (yathābhūtaṃ pajānanti) to describe the essential insight into four classes of perception required for final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato">AN 4.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that failing to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato"><b>four types of perceptions</b></a>—those leading to decline, stability, distinction, and penetration—is why some beings fail to attain Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.179/en/sujato">AN 4.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada taught King Muṇḍa that there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>five things</b></a> that cannot be obtained by any ascetic, brahmin, deva, Mara, or Brahma in the world, including the wish that what is subject to aging should not age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when King Muṇḍa's beloved Queen Bhaddā died, he was so consumed by grief that he refused to bathe, eat, or conduct his royal duties, spending his days and nights <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>fascinated</b></a> by her deceased body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his extreme grief, King Muṇḍa ordered his treasurer to preserve Queen Bhaddā’s body in an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>iron oil vat</b></a> covered by another iron vat, simply so he could continue to look at her for as long as possible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Nārada, it is impossible for anyone in the world to successfully demand that something <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>subject to illness</b></a> should not fall ill? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada pointed out that no being in the universe, including the highest gods, can prevent what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>subject to death</b></a> from eventually dying? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada explained that one of the five facts of life is that anything <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>subject to destruction</b></a> cannot be willed to remain undestroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada described the "uninstructed worldling" as someone who, when faced with the aging of what is subject to aging, fails to reflect that this is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>universal nature</b></a> of all beings that come and go? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada taught that when a person grieves and laments over the inevitable, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>enemies</b></a> become elated and full of joy at their suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada warned that excessive sorrow and weeping cause one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>friends</b></a> to become distressed and dejected? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada explained that an uninstructed person who sorrows over the passing of things becomes pierced by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>poisoned dart of grief</b></a>, which causes them to only torment themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada contrasted the common person with the "well-taught noble disciple," who reflects that they are not the only one facing death, as death is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>shared destiny</b></a> of all sentient beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada taught that a noble disciple does not sorrow or lament when things are destroyed, and is thus said to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>pulled out</b></a> the poisoned dart of grief that pierces the hearts of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada described the noble disciple who overcomes sorrow as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>sorrowless and dartless</b></a>, having successfully quenched the fires of grief within themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada observed that when a person is overwhelmed by grief, they might lose their appetite for food and their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>physical appearance</b></a> may deteriorate into an ugly or sickly state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada taught that weeping and sorrowing does not provide even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>small amount</b></a> of benefit or help when someone has passed away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada suggested that a wise person should remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>unwavering</b></a> in the face of misfortune, so that their enemies are distressed to see their calm and unchanged facial expression? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada advised that if a goal can be achieved through speech, mantras, gifts, or tradition, one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>strive</b></a> in that place to obtain it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada taught that if a desired outcome is truly unobtainable, one should accept it without sorrow, reflecting that it is a result of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>strong kamma</b></a> and asking themselves, "What can I do now?" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the discourse Venerable Nārada gave to King Muṇḍa is specifically titled <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>"The Removal of the Dart of Grief"</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing Venerable Nārada’s teaching, King Muṇḍa declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>dart of grief</b></a> he had been carrying since the death of his queen was finally abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that immediately after being consoled by Venerable Nārada, King Muṇḍa decided to finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>cremate</b></a> the body of Queen Bhaddā and build a stupa for her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Muṇḍa’s treasurer, Piyaka, was the one who initially recognized the King's dangerous state of mind and recommended that he visit <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nārada</b></a> to find relief from his sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada was widely known and praised as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>wise and learned</b></a> monk who was an eloquent speaker and an arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Muṇḍa showed great respect to Venerable Nārada by traveling as far as his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>royal chariot</b></a> could go and then proceeding on foot into the monastery to hear the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nārada taught that anything <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>subject to passing away</b></a> will inevitably pass away, and this is one of the fundamental truths that no one can change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Muṇḍa credited Venerable Nārada’s teaching as the reason he was able to resume bathing, eating, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato"><b>managing his affairs</b></a> after a long period of paralyzing grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.50/en/sujato">AN 5.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>obstructions and hindrances</b></a> that overspread the mind and weaken a person's wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a mind filled with hindrances to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>mountain river</b></a> whose current has been scattered and diverted by opening side channels? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who has not abandoned the five hindrances cannot realize any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>superior human state</b></a> or the distinction in knowledge and vision worthy of the noble ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a> is one of the five primary obstructions that overspread the mind and lead to the weakening of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> acts as a mental hindrance that suppresses one's clarity and prevents the development of powerful wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>dullness and drowsiness</b></a> as a specific mental obstruction that makes a practitioner's wisdom weak and ineffective? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> are among the five factors that overspread the mind and hinder a person's spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> is a hindrance that overspreads the mind, resulting in a wisdom that is unable to see the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a river whose current is "scattered, spread out, and diverted" to describe a mind that has not yet <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>abandoned</b></a> the five hindrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who successfully abandons the five hindrances is like a mountain river with its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>side channels</b></a> closed, allowing the current to flow fast and far? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>powerful wisdom</b></a> gained by abandoning the hindrances, a person can truly understand their own good and the good of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the entirety of what is called the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.52/en/sujato"><b>mass of unwholesome</b></a> states specifically as the five hindrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.52/en/sujato">AN 5.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said it is correct to speak of the five hindrances as being a complete <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.52/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome heap</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.52/en/sujato">AN 5.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a mountain river's side channels are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>closed off</b></a>, the water remains concentrated and powerful, much like a mind free from the five hindrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a mind overspread by hindrances is like a river that is neither <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>swift-flowing</b></a> nor able to travel a long distance toward the ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>impossible</b></a> for someone with weak wisdom, caused by the five hindrances, to attain superior knowledge and vision worthy of the noble ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>wisdom becomes weak</b></a> precisely because the five hindrances overspread and suppress the mind's potential? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the five hindrances are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>obstructions</b></a> (āvaraṇā) that prevent a person from seeing the truth of their own benefit or the benefit of both themselves and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that only by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato"><b>removing</b></a> the five hindrances can a practitioner's wisdom become powerful enough to realize liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.51/en/sujato">AN 5.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered the five hindrances to be the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.52/en/sujato"><b>sole constituents</b></a> when one correctly refers to the "entirety of the mass of unwholesome"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.52/en/sujato">AN 5.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the householder Anāthapiṇḍika that a lay follower should not be satisfied merely by providing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato"><b>requisites</b></a> such as robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines to the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato">AN 5.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed lay followers to train themselves to periodically enter and dwell in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato"><b>rapture of seclusion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato">AN 5.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta exclaimed that it was "wonderful and amazing" when the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato"><b>material support</b></a> alone is not enough for a lay follower's satisfaction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato">AN 5.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that when a noble disciple dwells in the rapture of seclusion, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato"><b>five states</b></a> of experience do not occur for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato">AN 5.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, a noble disciple in the rapture of seclusion does not experience the pain and displeasure connected with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato">AN 5.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta noted that the pleasure and joy connected with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> are absent when a noble disciple enters the rapture of seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato">AN 5.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that any pain and displeasure connected with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome states</b></a> ceases to exist for a disciple dwelling in the rapture of seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato">AN 5.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that the pleasure and joy connected with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome states</b></a> do not occur while one is experiencing the rapture of seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato">AN 5.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even the pain and displeasure connected with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a> are absent when a noble disciple dwells in the rapture of seclusion, according to Venerable Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato">AN 5.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave his full approval to Venerable Sāriputta's explanation of the five states that cease during seclusion by saying, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato"><b>"Good, good, Sāriputta!"</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato">AN 5.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly prohibited lay followers from engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato"><b>five trades</b></a> that cause harm to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato">AN 5.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a lay follower should not engage in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato"><b>trade in weapons</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato">AN 5.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is unallowable for a lay follower to engage in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato"><b>trade in human beings</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato">AN 5.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato"><b>trade in meat</b></a> is one of the five specific businesses the Buddha said a lay follower should avoid? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato">AN 5.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a lay follower should not make a living through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato"><b>trade in intoxicants</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato">AN 5.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato"><b>trade in poison</b></a> was specifically forbidden by the Buddha for those who identify as lay followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato">AN 5.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato"><b>upāsaka</b></a> (lay follower) when defining who should refrain from harmful trades like selling weapons and poison? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato">AN 5.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's list of prohibited trades applies to any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a>, emphasizing ethical livelihood within the Buddhist community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.177/en/sujato">AN 5.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a lay follower's spiritual growth involves both ethical livelihood and the development of internal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato"><b>spiritual joy</b></a> through seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.176/en/sujato">AN 5.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once asked his monks if they had ever seen or heard of a king seizing and punishing a person for the reason that they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from killing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he had never seen nor heard of a person being executed, imprisoned, or banished by a king for the virtue of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from stealing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that kings only punish individuals when it is reported that they have committed an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>evil deed</b></a>, such as taking the life of a man or a woman? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, there is no record of a king punishing a person specifically for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from sexual misconduct</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that kings seize and punish those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>take what is not given</b></a> from a village or a forest with the intent to steal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked his monks if they had ever seen a king punish someone because they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from false speech</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that kings punish people for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> when they are found to have had relations with women or girls protected by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that kings do not punish a person for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from intoxicants</b></a> such as liquor and wine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how kings punish individuals who use <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>false speech</b></a> to ruin the wealth or interests of a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated kings punish those who, while being addicted to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>liquor and wine</b></a>, commit crimes like taking the life of another person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that kings punish individuals who, under the influence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>intoxicants</b></a>, take what is not given from a village or a forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that kings punish people who, due to their addiction to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>liquor and wine</b></a>, commit sexual misconduct with the wives or daughters of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that kings punish those who, while indulging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>intoxicants</b></a>, ruin the wealth of others through the use of lies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the concept of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>abstinence</b></a> (veramaṇi) to describe the moral restraint that protects a person from being executed or banished by the state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha asked about kings punishing the wicked, the monks replied that they had indeed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>seen and heard</b></a> of such punishments many times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that he had never seen or heard of someone being banished from their land for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from liquor</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a king might <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>execute</b></a>, imprison, or banish a person if they are reported to have taken the life of another, regardless of their gender? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that state punishments are strictly the result of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>bad actions</b></a> (pāpakamma) and never the result of moral restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the act of taking what is not given as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>theft</b></a> that leads to being seized by kings and treated "according to the case"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that kings punish those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>ruin a householder</b></a> or his son through the use of false speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from the five precepts</b></a> is a blameless course of action that avoids all royal penalties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that reports of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato"><b>evil deeds</b></a> are the specific trigger for kings to seize a person and inflict various forms of punishment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.178/en/sujato">AN 5.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Vacchagotta praised the Buddha’s physical appearance, noting that his faculties were exceptionally serene and his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>complexion</b></a> was pure and radiant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vacchagotta compared the Buddha’s radiant appearance to a yellow <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>autumn jujube</b></a> fruit that is remarkably pure and bright? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vacchagotta likened the Buddha's serene appearance to a ripe <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>palm fruit</b></a> just loosened from its stalk, which is pure and bright? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s radiant complexion was compared by Vacchagotta to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>gold ornament</b></a>, well-burnished by a skilled smith and placed on a red blanket, which shines and glows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that various luxurious <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>high and large beds</b></a>, such as those covered with wool or silk, are difficult for renunciants to obtain and are not permitted for them even if they are acquired? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three specific types of "high and luxurious beds" that he can obtain at will: the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>divine, Brahma, and noble</b></a> high and luxurious beds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>divine high bed</b></a> as the state of dwelling in the four meditative absorptions (jhanas) after collecting grass or leaves to sit on in a forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the first jhana as a state of rapture and pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>thought and examination</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the second jhana as a state of internal confidence and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>unification of mind</b></a>, born of concentration and free from thought and examination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the third jhana, the Buddha explained that one dwells in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a>, mindful and clearly comprehending, experiencing pleasure with the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the fourth jhana is a state of neither-pain-nor-pleasure, characterized by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>purity of mindfulness</b></a> due to equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if he walks, stands, sits, or lies down while in the state of the four jhanas, his posture or bed is considered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>divine posture</b></a> or bed at that time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>Brahma high bed</b></a> as the practice of pervading the entire world with a mind imbued with loving-kindness, compassion, altruistic joy, and equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> (metta) as pervading all quarters of the world with a mind that is vast, exalted, and measureless, free from hostility and ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind imbued with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> (karuna) should be directed above, below, and all around, encompassing the entire world with kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha practiced pervading the world with a mind imbued with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>altruistic joy</b></a> (mudita), which is described as being vast and without limit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>noble high bed</b></a> as the direct knowledge and experience that lust, hatred, and delusion have been completely abandoned and destroyed at the root? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> (rāga) has been cut off like a palm stump, made non-existent, and is not subject to future arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> (dosa) has been uprooted and destroyed, such that it will never arise again in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha affirmed that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> (moha) is gone, having been eliminated at the root and rendered incapable of further growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, sitting or lying down while knowing one's defilements are utterly destroyed constitutes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>noble bed</b></a> at that time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Vacchagotta was so impressed by the Buddha's teaching that he declared the Dhamma had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>made clear</b></a> in many ways, like setting upright what had been overturned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vacchagotta compared the Buddha’s clear explanation of the Dhamma to carrying a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato"><b>lamp into the darkness</b></a> so that those with eyes might see forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.63/en/sujato">AN 3.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four specific things that are considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.47/en/sujato"><b>very far apart</b></a> from one another, which include the vast distance between the sky and the earth as well as the distance between the near and far shores of the ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.47/en/sujato">AN 4.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the distance between the place where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.47/en/sujato"><b>sun rises</b></a> and the place where it sets as one of the primary examples of things that are extremely distant from each other? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.47/en/sujato">AN 4.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the distance between the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.47/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma of good people</b></a> and the Dhamma of bad people is even greater than the physical distance between the sky and the earth or the two shores of the sea? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.47/en/sujato">AN 4.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the association of good people is stable and remains the same for as long as it lasts, while the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.47/en/sujato"><b>association of bad people</b></a> is fleeting and vanishes very quickly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.47/en/sujato">AN 4.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the physical distance of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.47/en/sujato"><b>near shore</b></a> and the far shore of the ocean to illustrate how the path of the wicked is fundamentally distant from the path of the virtuous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.47/en/sujato">AN 4.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the contrast between the way of the good and the way of the bad as being "farther than far," emphasizing that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.47/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a> practiced by the wise is worlds apart from the conduct of the ignoble? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.47/en/sujato">AN 4.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highly commended Venerable Visākha Pañcālaputta for his ability to instruct, surge, and gladden his fellow monks using <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.48/en/sujato"><b>polished speech</b></a> that was clear, distinct, and easy to understand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.48/en/sujato">AN 4.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that a wise person mixed among fools cannot be distinguished if they remain silent, but they become known to others when they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.48/en/sujato"><b>speaking</b></a> and teaching the path to the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.48/en/sujato">AN 4.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted monks to "speak and illuminate the Dhamma" because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.48/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken words</b></a> are considered the banner of the seers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.48/en/sujato">AN 4.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his followers to "hold up the banner of the seers," explaining that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.48/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a> itself is the true banner of those who have attained spiritual vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.48/en/sujato">AN 4.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while staying at Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, the Buddha emerged from his evening meditation specifically to praise the way Venerable Visākha was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.48/en/sujato"><b>inspiring the monks</b></a> with a comprehensive and unattached Dhamma talk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.48/en/sujato">AN 4.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mark of a wise teacher is their ability to reveal the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.48/en/sujato"><b>Deathless state</b></a> to others through their speech and instructions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.48/en/sujato">AN 4.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>four great references</b></a> as a systematic method for monks to verify whether a teaching is truly the Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers neither to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>approve or reject</b></a> a person's statement immediately, even if that person claims to have heard a teaching directly from the Teacher himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, once a monk presents a teaching as the Teacher's instruction, his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>words and phrasing</b></a> must be carefully learned and then checked against the Suttas and the Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a teaching does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>fit into the Suttas</b></a> or show up in the Vinaya, it must be concluded that it is not the word of the Blessed One and should be discarded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a monk’s reported teaching <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>aligns with the Suttas</b></a> and Vinaya, one can reach the definite conclusion that it is indeed the word of the Blessed One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's first great reference involves a scenario where a monk claims to have heard a teaching <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>face to face</b></a> from the Blessed One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's second great reference describes a monk who claims to have heard and received the Dhamma and Vinaya from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>Saṅgha with elders</b></a> and a leader in a specific dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's third great reference pertains to a monk claiming he received a teaching from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>several elder monks</b></a> who are learned, heirs to the traditions, and experts in the Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's fourth great reference involves a monk claiming to have heard a teaching from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>single elder monk</b></a> who is highly learned and an expert in the Mātikā (outlines)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered this specific discourse on the four great references while staying at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>Ananda Shrine</b></a> in the town of Bhoganagara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that an elder monk serving as a source for the Dhamma should be an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>heir to the traditions</b></a> (āgatāgamo) to be considered a learned authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if a teaching is claimed to be "the Teacher's instruction," it must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>compared with the Vinaya</b></a> to ensure it was not misapprehended by the person reporting it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that if a teaching fails the test of verification, it should be recognized as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>misapprehension</b></a> by the monk or the group of monks who provided it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>experts in the Dhamma</b></a> (dhammadharā) as one of the essential qualifications for elder monks who are cited as authorities in the great references? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>experts in the Vinaya</b></a> (vinayadharā) as one of the essential qualifications for elder monks who are cited as authorities in the great references? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>experts in the Mātikā</b></a> (mātikādharā) as one of the essential qualifications for elder monks who are cited as authorities in the great references? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a> (bahussutā) to describe the type of monks whose claims to the Dhamma are substantial enough to warrant the formal verification process? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that after a teaching fits the Suttas and Vinaya, one must conclude that it was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>learned well</b></a> by the monk who reported it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha insisted that monks must reach a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>definite conclusion</b></a> regarding a teaching's authenticity only after it has been properly compared with the established scriptures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's criteria for the four great references apply whether a teaching is heard from him, a whole Saṅgha, a group of elders, or just a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>single elder</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a teaching is definitely not the word of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>Sammāsambuddha</b></a> if it cannot be found to fit into the Suttas or show up in the Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's guidance in the Mahāpadesasutta serves as a safeguard to ensure the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato"><b>Teacher’s instruction</b></a> remains pure and uncorrupted by individual misunderstandings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.180/en/sujato">AN 4.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> in the enlightenment of the Tathāgata as the first of five factors essential for a monk’s spiritual striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato">AN 5.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk fit for exertion as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato"><b>rarely ill</b></a>, possessing a digestive system that is balanced and capable of enduring the rigors of practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato">AN 5.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato"><b>honest and open</b></a>, revealing their true nature to their teacher or wise companions in the holy life without deceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato">AN 5.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk engaged in striving should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato"><b>without fraud</b></a>, ensuring they do not present themselves hypocritically to their fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato">AN 5.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the fourth factor of striving as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> for the purpose of abandoning unwholesome states and acquiring wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato">AN 5.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered a monk to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato"><b>steadfast in effort</b></a> when they remain firm and do not shirk the responsibility of cultivating wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato">AN 5.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato"><b>penetrative wisdom</b></a> that discerns the arising and passing away of phenomena as the final factor necessary for the complete destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato">AN 5.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato"><b>old age</b></a> as the first of five unsuitable times for striving, because one who is overcome by decay finds it difficult to focus on intensive practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato">AN 5.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato"><b>illness</b></a> is an improper time for spiritual exertion, as the body is plagued by disease and pain, making concentration difficult? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato">AN 5.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato"><b>times of famine</b></a> as an unsuitable occasion for striving because it becomes difficult to survive on alms-food and sustain one's physical strength? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato">AN 5.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato"><b>social insecurity</b></a>, such as when there are threats from wild tribes and people must flee, is an unsuitable time for a monk to dedicate himself to striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato">AN 5.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato"><b>divided Sangha</b></a>, where monks are busy insulting and rebuking one another, is a major obstacle to spiritual progress for everyone involved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato">AN 5.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a schism in the community leads to those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato"><b>not yet confident</b></a> failing to gain faith, while some who are already confident may lose it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato">AN 5.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato"><b>youth</b></a>, characterized by black hair and the vigor of the first stage of life, as the ideal first occasion for making a determined spiritual effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato">AN 5.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended the time of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato"><b>rarely afflicted</b></a> by illness as a suitable occasion for striving, as it allows for a digestion that can support the physical demands of practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato">AN 5.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato"><b>abundance of crops</b></a> and the ease of obtaining alms create a favorable environment for monks to focus entirely on their spiritual goals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato">AN 5.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to strive when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato"><b>people live in harmony</b></a>, viewing one another with eyes of affection and blending together like milk and water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato">AN 5.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato"><b>harmonious Sangha</b></a>, where monks live in peace and agreement without disputes, to be the fifth and final suitable occasion for successful striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato">AN 5.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the community is in accord, those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato"><b>without confidence</b></a> are inspired to gain it, and those who already have faith see it increase? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.54/en/sujato">AN 5.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato"><b>moderate digestion</b></a>—one that is neither too cold nor too hot—as a specific physical requirement for a monk to effectively endure the exertion of striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.53/en/sujato">AN 5.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a householder can declare themselves a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>stream-enterer</b></a> if they are restrained in the five training rules and possess four mental states known as "pleasant dwellings in this very life"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the first of the five training rules for a noble disciple as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>restrained from killing</b></a> living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a noble householder must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>restrained from stealing</b></a>, or taking what is not given, to be considered restrained in action? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple's restraint in action includes total <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>abstinence from sexual misconduct</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from false speech</b></a> is one of the five training rules necessary for a layperson to attain the status of a stream-enterer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>abstinence from intoxicants</b></a>, such as liquor and wine that cause heedlessness, as a vital training rule for mental purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described four specific mental states as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwellings</b></a> in this very life, which are used to purify a mind that is not yet fully pure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the first pleasant dwelling involves possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence</b></a> in the Buddha as the "teacher of devas and humans"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a noble disciple gains a pleasant dwelling by having confirmed confidence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>well-expounded Dhamma</b></a>, which is to be personally experienced by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the third pleasant dwelling as confirmed confidence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>Sangha of disciples</b></a>, who are described as an "unsurpassed field of merit for the world"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the fourth pleasant dwelling as possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>virtues dear to the noble ones</b></a>, which are unblemished and lead to concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a householder with these qualities can realize they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>finished with hell</b></a> and the animal realm, and are certain of attaining enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>danger in hells</b></a> should lead a person to avoid evil actions and take up the noble Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>simile of bulls</b></a> of various colors to show that a person’s caste or social class is irrelevant to their spiritual worth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a strong bull is useful regardless of its color, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>virtuous person</b></a> is the best recipient of offerings regardless of whether they were born a Khattiya, Brahmin, or Outcaste? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that offerings given to those who are peaceful, tamed, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>finished with birth and death</b></a> result in a "vast sacrifice" or great merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>ignorant give gifts</b></a> to those outside the Dhamma, whereas the wise attend upon the peaceful ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who attend upon the wise develop a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>deeply rooted confidence</b></a> in the Sugata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that virtuous people who practice giving and follow the Dhamma will go to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> or be reborn into a distinguished family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the wise who attend on the peaceful and follow the path will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato"><b>attain Nibbāna</b></a> through a gradual process of practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.179/en/sujato">AN 5.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once visited the town of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>Kesaputta</b></a>, which was inhabited by a people known as the Kālāmas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the Kālāmas it was appropriate for them to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>doubt and perplexity</b></a> when different teachers provided contradictory and confusing doctrines? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised the Kālāmas that they should not accept a teaching merely because it has been passed down by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>oral tradition</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a teaching should not be followed simply because it belongs to a long-standing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>lineage of teaching</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned against accepting ideas based solely on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>hearsay</b></a> or reports from others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned the Kālāmas not to adopt views just because they are found in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>collection of scriptures</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that truth should not be determined by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>logical reasoning</b></a> alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should not accept a proposition based on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>inferential reasoning</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised the Kālāmas not to rely on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>reasoned cogitation</b></a> when trying to discern the truth of a teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a teaching should not be accepted just because it seems to agree with one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>reflective acceptance of a view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>seeming competence</b></a> of a speaker is not a sufficient reason to accept their words as true? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the Kālāmas they should not follow a teacher simply because they think, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>The ascetic is our guru</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the Kālāmas to rely on what they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>know for themselves</b></a> rather than relying on external authority or tradition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined unwholesome qualities as those that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>censured by the wise</b></a> and lead to harm and suffering when undertaken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>greed</b></a> arises in a person, it overcomes them and leads them to kill, steal, and lie? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a person overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> is driven to commit harmful actions that lead to their own long-term suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> causes a person to engage in misconduct that results in great harm for themselves and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that wholesome qualities like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>non-greed</b></a> lead to a person's welfare and happiness over a long period? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a noble disciple as one who dwells pervading the entire world with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>mind of loving-kindness</b></a> that is vast and measureless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a disciple should dwell pervading the world with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>mind of equanimity</b></a>, free from enmity and ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha offered "four assurances" to those with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>purified mind</b></a>, providing comfort regardless of their specific beliefs about the afterlife? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's first assurance is that if there is a next world and karma, a virtuous person will be reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's second assurance states that even if there is no next world, a person of good conduct remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>happy in this life</b></a>, free from suffering and ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's third assurance posits that if suffering follows evil deeds, a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>intends no evil</b></a> toward anyone will be safe from such suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's fourth assurance suggests that if evil deeds do not result in suffering, a person who avoids them still sees themselves as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato"><b>pure in both ways</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.65/en/sujato">AN 3.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who conducts themselves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato"><b>wrongly toward their mother</b></a> is considered a fool and an incompetent person who brings much demerit upon themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato">AN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who behaves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato"><b>wrongly toward their father</b></a> is viewed as a bad person who keeps themselves in a damaged and uprooted state, earning the censure of the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato">AN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an individual who practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato"><b>wrongly toward the Tathāgata</b></a> is a foolish person who generates much demerit and remains blameworthy in the eyes of the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato">AN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a person conducts themselves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato"><b>wrongly toward a disciple</b></a> of the Tathāgata, they are considered a bad person who is blameworthy and subject to reproach by those with understanding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato">AN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person as being in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato"><b>damaged and uprooted state</b></a> if they behave incorrectly toward four specific types of people: their mother, their father, the Tathāgata, and the Tathāgata's disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato">AN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a person who conducts themselves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato"><b>rightly toward their mother</b></a> is considered wise and competent, maintaining themselves in an undamaged and non-uprooted condition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato">AN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described one who behaves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato"><b>rightly toward their father</b></a> as a good person who generates much merit and is beyond the censure of the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato">AN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person who conducts themselves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato"><b>rightly toward the Tathāgata</b></a> lives in a blameless state and accumulates a great deal of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato">AN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an individual who acts <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato"><b>rightly toward a disciple</b></a> of the Tathāgata is considered a good person who is praised by those with understanding for their conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato">AN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a wise person as one who keeps themselves in an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato"><b>undamaged and non-uprooted state</b></a> by practicing right conduct toward their parents, the Buddha, and the Buddha's disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato">AN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that because of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato"><b>righteous conduct</b></a> toward their mother and father, a wise person is praised by others in this life and rejoices in heaven after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.4/en/sujato">AN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk worthy of offerings to a warrior who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato"><b>skillful in his stance</b></a>, shoots from afar, is a marksman, and pierces a great mass? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato">AN 4.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato"><b>skillful in his stance</b></a> when he is virtuous and trains himself in the rules of training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato">AN 4.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as "shooting from afar" when he sees that any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>—whether past, future, or present—is not mine, not I, and not my self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato">AN 4.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a monk "shoots from afar" by seeing any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>, whether internal or external, as not being his self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato">AN 4.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identifies a monk as one who shoots from afar when he views any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> with right wisdom as "not mine, not I, and not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato">AN 4.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk to a long-distance archer if he regards all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a>, whether gross or subtle, as not being his self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato">AN 4.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk "shoots from afar" when he recognizes any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a>—whether near or far—as "not mine, not I, and not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato">AN 4.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called a monk a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato"><b>marksman</b></a> if he understands as it really is the truth of suffering and the path leading to the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato">AN 4.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato"><b>pierces a great mass</b></a> when he successfully shatters the great mass of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato">AN 4.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses virtue, right wisdom regarding the five aggregates, and knowledge of the Four Noble Truths is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato">AN 4.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared there is no one in the world—including devas, Mara, or Brahma—who can provide a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato"><b>guarantee</b></a> against four specific inevitable things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato">AN 4.182</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated no one, not even a powerful deity, can guarantee that what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato"><b>subject to aging</b></a> should not age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato">AN 4.182</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that neither an ascetic nor a brahmin can provide a guarantee that what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato"><b>subject to illness</b></a> should not fall ill? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato">AN 4.182</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, no being in the universe, not even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato"><b>Mara</b></a>, can guarantee that what is subject to death should not die? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato">AN 4.182</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that no one can guarantee that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato"><b>results of past evil deeds</b></a>, which lead to future birth and suffering, will not be experienced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato">AN 4.182</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described certain past evil deeds as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato"><b>leading to future birth</b></a> and noted that no one can prevent their ripening by a mere wish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato">AN 4.182</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that shattering the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato"><b>great mass of ignorance</b></a> is the spiritual equivalent of a warrior piercing a great enemy force in battle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato">AN 4.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on the four "non-guarantees" applies to everyone in the cosmos, including the high deity <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato"><b>Brahma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato">AN 4.182</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considers a monk to be a spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato"><b>marksman</b></a> when he understands the Four Noble Truths as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.181/en/sujato">AN 4.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato"><b>results of kamma</b></a> that are defiling and painful cannot be stopped from ripening by any person or god in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.182/en/sujato">AN 4.182</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted a story where a monk and a nun, who were mother and son, fell into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>sexual intercourse</b></a> because they desired to see each other frequently during the rains retreat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly corrected the notion that a mother would not feel lust for her son, or a son for his mother, by explaining how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>frequent seeing</b></a> leads to intimacy and eventually to infatuation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there is no other single form that is so enticing, intoxicating, and such an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>obstacle</b></a> to the unsurpassed security from bondage as the form of a woman is to a man? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that those who are infatuated and obsessed with the form of a woman will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>sorrow for a long time</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that just as with form, he did not perceive any other sound, smell, taste, or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>touch</b></a> that is as intoxicating and obstructive to spiritual progress as those of a woman? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a woman's presence—whether she is walking, standing, sitting, sleeping, or even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>laughing</b></a>—can completely take possession of a man's mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that even the sight of a woman who is unconscious or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>deceased</b></a> can still occupy and overwhelm the mind of a man? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if anything could be rightly described as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>Mara's total snare</b></a>, it is a woman? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that it is better to converse with a person wielding a sword or even a demon than to sit alone and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>converse with a woman</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that one would be safer sitting next to a deadly venomous snake than sitting in private with a woman, due to the potential for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>spiritual downfall</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that women can bind those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>muddled mindfulness</b></a> through their glances, smiles, dress, and charming speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a>—forms, sounds, smells, tastes, and touches—are found within the form of a woman? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are carried away by the flood of sensual desires and do not fully understand them will continue to wander through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>cycle of birth and death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that those who have fully understood sensual pleasures and walk "fearless from any quarter" have reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the process of spiritual failure starting from frequent seeing, which leads to intimacy, then to trust, and finally to a state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>infatuated</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the goal of the monk's path as achieving the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed security from bondage</b></a>, which is directly threatened by indulgence in sensual desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who have "gone to the far shore" are those who have transcended the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>flood of sensuality</b></a> in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a woman's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato"><b>charming speech</b></a> and delightful smiles are specific ways they bind and attract the minds of men? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.55/en/sujato">AN 5.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once broke into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>smile</b></a> while walking in the country of the Kosalans because he recalled a prosperous and populous city that had once existed at that very spot? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's attendant Ananda observed that a Tathagata does not manifest a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>smile</b></a> without a specific cause or reason? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that during the time of the past Buddha Kassapa, a lay follower named Gavesi was a leader to five hundred people, yet none of them were initially <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>fulfillers of the virtues</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the location of a current <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>sal grove</b></a> as the former site of a city where the Buddha Kassapa once lived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how the lay follower Gavesi decided to distinguish himself from his peers by resolving to become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>fulfiller of the virtues</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when Gavesi's five hundred followers saw him improve his behavior, they also resolved to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>fulfillers of the virtues</b></a> so as not to be left behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted Gavesi sought to excel further by becoming a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>celibate</b></a>, living apart and abstaining from sexual intercourse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told how Gavesi’s followers, recognizing him as their benefactor and leader, also chose to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>celibate</b></a> to match his level of practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared that Gavesi pushed his discipline even further by becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>one who eats a single meal a day</b></a>, abstaining from eating at night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the five hundred followers of Gavesi followed his lead once again and became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>those who eat a single meal a day</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha narrated how Gavesi, not satisfied with lay practice, eventually approached the Buddha Kassapa to request the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>going forth</b></a> and higher ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted that Gavesi became an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>arahant</b></a> shortly after his ordination by living alone, diligent, ardent, and resolute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that Gavesi's five hundred followers eventually decided to shave their heads and put on saffron robes to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>go forth</b></a> into homelessness as well? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that Gavesi's five hundred followers all eventually attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>supreme liberation</b></a> of the holy life after following their leader's example? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the story of Gavesi to encourage Ananda and the monks to always strive for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>higher and higher excellence</b></a> in their spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Gavesi as a "helpful leader and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>instigator</b></a>" who motivated five hundred others toward enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ultimate end of the holy life is to realize for oneself that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a> and the religious life has been lived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that Gavesi's success came from being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>diligent, ardent, and resolute</b></a> while dwelling in solitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that Gavesi and his followers achieved their goal because they were always striving for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>more and more lofty states</b></a> without becoming complacent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha Kassapa was the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>Arahant and Perfectly Enlightened One</b></a> who served as the spiritual teacher for Gavesi in the distant past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that through continuous effort, one can experience the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato"><b>bliss of liberation</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.180/en/sujato">AN 5.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while staying in Sāvatthī at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>Migāramātā’s Mansion</b></a>, Venerable Nandaka taught Sāḷha and Rohana that spiritual practice should be based on personal realization rather than mere tradition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka cautioned against accepting a teaching simply because it has been passed down through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>oral tradition</b></a>, emphasizing that one must personally understand the nature of wholesome and unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka explained that one should not follow a path merely because of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>lineage</b></a> or historical continuity, but should instead evaluate if the practice leads to suffering or happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka advised spiritual seekers not to rely on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>hearsay</b></a> or rumors when determining the truth of a doctrine, urging them to investigate the qualities of the mind for themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Nandaka, a teaching should not be accepted just because it is found in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>collection of scriptures</b></a>, as the ultimate test is whether the qualities it promotes are blameless and praised by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka taught that one should not adopt a view based solely on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>logical reasoning</b></a> or intellectual speculation, as these methods alone do not guarantee the removal of greed and hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>inferential reasoning</b></a> is insufficient on its own to confirm a spiritual truth, requiring instead a direct personal knowledge of what leads to one's own harm or benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka warned against relying on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>reflection on reasons</b></a> or superficial appearances to validate a teaching, suggesting that one should look at the practical results of the teaching in one's life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka taught that truth is not established merely through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>acceptance of a view after pondering it</b></a>, but through knowing for oneself that certain qualities are unwholesome and lead to pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka advised that a student should not follow a teacher based only on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>speaker’s seeming competence</b></a>, but should focus on whether the teaching actually helps them abandon greed, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka remarked that one should not accept a teaching based on the simple respect or thought: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>“The ascetic is our guru,”</b></a> but should instead verify the teachings through personal experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>covetousness</b></a> (greed) as a root of evil that can drive a person to kill, steal, commit adultery, and lie, resulting in long-term suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka explained how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> (hatred) corrupts the mind and leads individuals to engage in harmful actions and even encourage others to do the same, leading to their own detriment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> (delusion) as a condition where a confused person destroys life and takes what is not given, unaware that such actions lead to long-term harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka taught that unwholesome qualities are those that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>censured by the wise</b></a> and, when fully undertaken, result in harm and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>non-covetousness</b></a> as a wholesome quality that leads a person to refrain from killing and stealing, ultimately bringing long-term welfare and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Nandaka, when a person is free from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a>, they do not engage in sexual misconduct or lying, nor do they incite others to do so, which is praised by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge</b></a> (the opposite of delusion) allows a person to live without harming others, leading to a state that is blameless and conducive to happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka described a noble disciple as one who dwells pervading the entire world with a mind imbued with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a>, compassion, altruistic joy, and equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka taught that through deep understanding, a disciple's mind is liberated from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>taint of sensual desire</b></a>, the taint of being, and the taint of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka explained that once a person is liberated, they possess the knowledge that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a>, the holy life has been lived, and there is no further return to any state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka described a person who has removed greed, hatred, and delusion as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato"><b>cooled</b></a> and extinguished in this very life, experiencing bliss through a self that has become divine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.66/en/sujato">AN 3.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato"><b>going with the stream</b></a> as someone who indulges in sensual pleasures and performs evil deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato">AN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato"><b>going against the stream</b></a> as one who does not indulge in sensual pleasures or perform evil deeds, even if they must live the holy life weeping and in pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato">AN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato"><b>steady self</b></a> is one who has destroyed the five lower fetters and will attain final Nibbāna in a higher realm without returning from that world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato">AN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato"><b>brahmin who has crossed over</b></a> as an individual who, through the destruction of the taints, has achieved liberation of mind and wisdom in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato">AN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained in sensual pleasures</b></a> and overcome by craving as people who are destined to repeatedly undergo birth and old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato">AN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a> who, established in mindfulness, abandons both sensual pleasures and evil deeds despite the suffering it may cause in the process? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato">AN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to a person who is of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato"><b>spontaneous birth</b></a> as one who is no longer subject to returning to the human world after destroying the five lower fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato">AN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a practitioner who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato"><b>mastered his mind</b></a> and has his faculties composed after abandoning the five lower fetters is referred to as one of "steady self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato">AN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato"><b>gone to the far shore</b></a> to describe a sage who has fulfilled the holy life and whose mental taints are completely extinguished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato">AN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato"><b>four types of persons</b></a> existing in the world, categorized by how they navigate the "stream" of worldly existence and craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.5/en/sujato">AN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk, a nun, a layman, and a laywoman who are competent and disciplined are the specific individuals who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato"><b>beautify the Sangha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato">AN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato"><b>learned and an upholder of the Dhamma</b></a> in order to truly bring beauty and distinction to the spiritual community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato">AN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato"><b>self-confidence</b></a> (visārada) as one of the essential qualities for both monastics and lay followers to effectively represent and beautify the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato">AN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato"><b>in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a> is a necessary requirement for any person—whether monastic or lay—to be considered a "beautifier of the Sangha"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato">AN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically included the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato"><b>faithful laywoman</b></a> and layman, alongside monks and nuns, as those who illuminate the community through their virtue and knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato">AN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato"><b>beautifier of the Sangha</b></a> for any person who is competent, learned, and follows the principles of the Dhamma in their daily life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato">AN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato"><b>virtuous monk</b></a> and a learned nun are both equally capable of bringing honor and beauty to the community of practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato">AN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato"><b>faithful layman</b></a> who possesses the quality of self-confidence and knowledge of the Dhamma is a pillar that beautifies the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato">AN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato"><b>disciplined</b></a> (vinīta) is a core attribute for any of the four types of practitioners who wish to contribute positively to the reputation of the Buddhist community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato">AN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato"><b>competent person</b></a> as one who is not only learned but also actively practices the Dhamma, thereby shining brightly within the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.7/en/sujato">AN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Magadhan chief minister, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato"><b>Vassakāra the brahmin</b></a>, once approached the Buddha at the Bamboo Grove in Rājagaha to express his view that there is no fault in a person simply reporting exactly what they have seen, heard, sensed, or cognized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato">AN 4.183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha rejected a simplistic approach to truth-telling, explaining that he does not claim that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato"><b>everything seen</b></a> should be spoken, nor does he claim that everything seen should not be spoken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato">AN 4.183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly taught that a person should not speak about what they have seen if reporting such <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato"><b>seen information</b></a> causes unwholesome states to increase and wholesome states to decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato">AN 4.183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato"><b>not speaking</b></a> about something one has seen would cause wholesome states to decline and unwholesome states to increase, then that particular sight should indeed be spoken about? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato">AN 4.183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that what one has heard should not be spoken if the act of speaking about that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato"><b>heard information</b></a> leads to the growth of unwholesome qualities and the depletion of wholesome qualities in oneself and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato">AN 4.183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that certain things one has heard must be spoken if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato"><b>remaining silent</b></a> about them would result in the decline of wholesome states and the increase of unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato">AN 4.183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that what one has sensed (muta) should not be expressed if such <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato"><b>speech</b></a> results in the flourishing of unwholesome mental states and the fading of wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato">AN 4.183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that what one has sensed should be spoken if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato"><b>failing to speak</b></a> about it would lead to an increase in unwholesome states and a decline in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato">AN 4.183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that even what is cognized (viññāta) should be withheld from speech if expressing that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato"><b>cognized knowledge</b></a> causes unwholesome states to increase and wholesome states to decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato">AN 4.183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha maintained that what is cognized should be spoken if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato"><b>withholding the information</b></a> would cause wholesome states to decline and unwholesome states to increase? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.183/en/sujato">AN 4.183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a practitioner is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>guarded in the sense faculties</b></a>, their body may feel heavy and the teachings may no longer occur to them clearly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>moderation in eating</b></a> as a vital factor in preventing a person's mind from being overwhelmed by sloth and torpor during their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a practitioner who is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>devoted to wakefulness</b></a> will often find themselves dissatisfied with the holy life and lacking a sense of direction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>insight into wholesome qualities</b></a> can lead a person to experience deep-seated doubt and perplexity regarding the nature of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended developing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>qualities that lead to enlightenment</b></a> specifically during the first and last watches of the night to maintain mental clarity and spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a state of mental disorientation where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>quarters are not clear</b></a> as a symptom of failing to discipline one's senses and eating habits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha helped a struggling monk overcome a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>sluggish and heavy body</b></a> by instructing him to guard his sense doors and practice moderation in food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> will take possession of the mind and overwhelm it when a practitioner neglects the effort to stay watchful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>dissatisfaction with the holy life</b></a> can be remedied by becoming an observer of wholesome qualities and practicing diligence during the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a monk that by becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>guarded in the sense doors</b></a>, he would eventually find that the teachings would occur to him with perfect clarity once again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>moderate in eating</b></a> and devoted to wakefulness will no longer suffer from doubt or uncertainty regarding the dhammas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk who attained Arahantship reported to the Buddha that after his liberation, his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>body no longer felt heavy</b></a> and his mind was finally free from the fog of confusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that for a practitioner who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>insight into wholesome qualities</b></a>, the quarters are clear and their mind is no longer besieged by sloth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his followers to live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>secluded and diligent</b></a> lives so they could realize the supreme goal of the holy life for themselves in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>perplexity in the teachings</b></a> is entirely absent in those who are devoted to the development of the bodhipakkhiya dhammas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted all of his monks to train themselves to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>watchful in the night</b></a> to ensure they remained satisfied and clear-headed in their spiritual journey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s specific advice on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>guarding the sense faculties</b></a> served as the primary remedy for a monk who felt like his body and mind were failing him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the successful completion of the holy life as a state where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a> and the practitioner understands that there is no further state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that for a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>not moderate in eating</b></a>, the body becomes sluggish and the mind becomes susceptible to lingering doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato"><b>devoted to the practice</b></a> of cultivating wholesome qualities, one can transform dissatisfaction into a state of joyous and unwavering resolution? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.56/en/sujato">AN 5.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five distinct reasons why a person might choose to become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato"><b>forest-dweller</b></a>, indicating that the outward practice alone does not reveal one's true motivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato">AN 5.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, some individuals take up the life of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato"><b>forest-dweller</b></a> simply out of dullness and confusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato">AN 5.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a person might live as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato"><b>forest-dweller</b></a> because they are driven by evil wishes and dominated by desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato">AN 5.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that some practitioners might adopt the lifestyle of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato"><b>forest-dweller</b></a> due to madness and an unsettled mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato">AN 5.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted some people become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato"><b>forest-dweller</b></a> specifically because they know such a practice is praised by the Buddhas and their disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato">AN 5.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the highest type of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato"><b>forest-dweller</b></a> is the one who lives in the forest for the sake of fewness of wishes, contentment, effacement, seclusion, and the specific goal of the practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato">AN 5.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of the "cream of ghee"—the finest essence of milk—to describe the excellence of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato"><b>forest-dweller</b></a> who practices out of a desire for contentment and seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato">AN 5.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato"><b>forest-dweller</b></a> who practices for the purpose of fewness of wishes and seclusion to be the chief, best, foremost, and most distinguished among all types of forest-dwellers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.181/en/sujato">AN 5.181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized five different types of individuals who practice as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato"><b>bowl-food eater</b></a>, which refers to the ascetic practice of eating all food from a single alms-bowl? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato">AN 5.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person may practice as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato"><b>bowl-food eater</b></a> merely out of dullness and confusion rather than for genuine spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato">AN 5.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned a practitioner might become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato"><b>bowl-food eater</b></a> while being inwardly motivated by evil wishes and being dominated by desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato">AN 5.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha observed that the ascetic discipline of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato"><b>bowl-food eater</b></a> can sometimes be undertaken by someone suffering from madness and an unsettled mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato">AN 5.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha mentioned that some individuals take up the practice of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato"><b>bowl-food eater</b></a> primarily because the practice has been praised by the Buddhas and their disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato">AN 5.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the most superior <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato"><b>bowl-food eater</b></a> as the one whose practice is rooted in fewness of wishes, contentment, effacement, and seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato">AN 5.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato"><b>bowl-food eater</b></a> who practices for the sake of seclusion and "this-being-the-purpose" to the cream of ghee, representing the pinnacle of that spiritual discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato">AN 5.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that among all five kinds of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato"><b>bowl-food eaters</b></a>, the one who practices out of fewness of wishes and contentment is the foremost, best, and most excellent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.190/en/sujato">AN 5.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>three topics</b></a> of conversation, which consist of talk about the past, talk about the future, and talk about the present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a person is unfit to talk with if, when asked a question, they do not provide a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>categorical answer</b></a> to a question that should be answered categorically? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person is fit to talk with if they provide an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>analytical answer</b></a> when a question requires an analytical response rather than a simple one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person as unfit to talk with if they fail to answer a question with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>counter-question</b></a> when such a response is the appropriate way to clarify the inquiry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one should be able to identify a question that should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>set aside</b></a>, and that a person who fails to do so is considered unfit for discussion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is fit to talk with if they stand by what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>possible and impossible</b></a>, remaining consistent with logic and recognized facts during a discussion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>evades the question</b></a>, changes the subject, or leads the talk outward when asked a specific question is unfit to be a partner in discussion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a person is unfit to talk with if they manifest <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>anger and ill will</b></a> or resentment when questioned by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>overwhelms and mocks</b></a> their opponent during a conversation is considered unfit to talk with? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person fit to talk with is one who does not engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>seizing on slips</b></a> or mistakes made by the other person in a discussion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a person as having "supporting conditions" for progress if they listen with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>attentive ear</b></a>, whereas one who does not listen lacks such conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the ultimate goal of discussion, consultation, and listening is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind through non-clinging</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an attentive person who hears the Dhamma can directly know, fully understand, and successfully engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>abandoning</b></a> unwholesome states to touch right liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the talk of non-nobles as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>contradictory talk</b></a> where individuals look for each other's faults and rejoice in their opponent's defeat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person who wishes to talk should first know the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>right time</b></a> for the conversation and focus on topics related to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged conversation that is consistent with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>noble conduct</b></a>, which should be conducted by one who is not aggressive or arrogant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that in a noble discussion, one should speak with a mind that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>without conceit</b></a> and stay focused on clear understanding rather than personal victory? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a wise person does not find fault in others' speech but instead expresses appreciation for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken words</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the noble ones engage in consultation for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>confidence</b></a> and knowledge, rather than to attack or crush others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that one should not practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato"><b>fault-finding</b></a> or use speech that is aimed at putting others down, as this is the way the noble ones discuss things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.67/en/sujato">AN 3.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared certain spiritual obstacles to the way <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato"><b>clouds</b></a> act as a corruption of the sun and moon, preventing them from glowing, beaming, and shining? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato">AN 4.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato"><b>fog</b></a> as one of the four physical corruptions that can obscure the sun and moon, similar to how certain behaviors obscure the spiritual radiance of a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato">AN 4.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato"><b>smoke and dust</b></a> are natural corruptions of the sun and moon that prevent them from radiating their full light in the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato">AN 4.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato"><b>Rāhu, lord of the asuras</b></a>, as a corruption of the sun and moon because he prevents them from beaming or shining clearly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato">AN 4.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that some ascetics and brahmins do not shine or beam spiritually because they drink <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato"><b>wine and liquor</b></a>, which he classified as a primary corruption? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato">AN 4.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the indulgence in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato"><b>sexual intercourse</b></a> as a corruption for ascetics and brahmins that prevents them from attaining spiritual brilliance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato">AN 4.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that religious practitioners who accept <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato"><b>gold and silver</b></a> are affected by a corruption that stops them from glowing with the purity of their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato">AN 4.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that living by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato"><b>wrong livelihood</b></a> is the fourth corruption that prevents certain ascetics and brahmins from beaming or shining with spiritual success? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato">AN 4.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato"><b>shrouded in ignorance</b></a> and defiled by passion and hatred will continue to increase the "terrible charnel ground" through rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato">AN 4.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is referred to in this sutta as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato"><b>Kinsman of the Sun</b></a>, the one who pointed out the corruptions that keep practitioners from spiritual purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato">AN 4.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that people who are slaves to craving and delight in pleasing forms will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato"><b>take up future existence</b></a> rather than finding liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato">AN 4.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when ascetics and brahmins are defiled by corruptions such as greed and wrong livelihood, they are considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato"><b>unclean</b></a> and fail to radiate the light of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.50/en/sujato">AN 4.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Jāṇussoṇi once presented a view to the Buddha claiming that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>no one subject to death</b></a> who does not fear it or fall into terror when death approaches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha corrected Jāṇussoṇi’s generalized view by explaining that while some people subject to death do fear it, there are others who do <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>not fear death</b></a> or fall into terror? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has not yet abandoned lust, desire, and craving for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> will experience intense sorrow and lamentation when a life-threatening illness strikes them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one reason a person fears death is the agonizing realization that their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>dear sensual pleasures</b></a> will leave them, or that they must leave those pleasures behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified attachment to the physical form as a source of terror, stating that someone not free from craving for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>body</b></a> will weep and become confused when facing death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the fear of death as being tied to the thought that "my <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>dear body</b></a> will leave me," leading an attached person to beat their breast and pine when a grave disease afflicts them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>not done what is good</b></a> or wholesome, but has instead done what is evil and cruel, will fear death because of the destination they expect to reach hereafter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that failing to provide a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>shelter for the fearful</b></a> during one's life contributes to a person's terror and confusion when they realize their own life is coming to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>perplexed and in doubt</b></a> regarding the True Dhamma will experience anxiety and fall into terror when confronted by a serious illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>not reached certainty</b></a> in the True Dhamma as the fourth type of person who is subject to death and remains afraid of it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an individual who has become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>free from lust</b></a> and craving for sensual pleasures is the first type of person who does not fear death or fall into terror? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, someone who has abandoned thirst and passion for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> will not sorrow or pine even when they are struck by a terminal disease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who has removed all craving and feverish longing for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>physical body</b></a> will remain calm and unconfused when facing the end of their life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>done what is wholesome</b></a> and virtuous does not fear death because they know they have built a "shelter for the fearful" through their past actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a virtuous person as someone who reflects that they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>not done evil</b></a> or cruel deeds, and thus they do not lament or weep when a deadly illness occurs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>without doubt</b></a> and has attained certainty in the True Dhamma will not experience terror when death approaches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>four specific types</b></a> of individuals who, though subject to death, do not sorrow, pine, or become confused when faced with their own mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato"><b>True Dhamma</b></a> (Saddhamma) to refer to the teaching that, when understood with certainty, removes the fear and terror associated with dying? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.184/en/sujato">AN 4.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught five themes that should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>frequently reflected upon</b></a> by every woman, man, layperson, and monastic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised people to reflect, "I am subject to aging, I am not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>exempt from aging</b></a>," as a way to abandon or weaken the pride of youth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the contemplation of being subject to illness to help individuals overcome the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>intoxication with health</b></a> that often leads to misconduct in body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, regularly contemplating that one is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>subject to death</b></a> can eliminate or diminish the intoxication with life that causes beings to act unskillfully? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that reflecting on the inevitable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>separation and division</b></a> from all that is dear and pleasing serves to reduce lustful desire for loved ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized reflecting on being the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>owner of one's kamma</b></a> and the heir to one's actions as a means to encourage the abandoning of all bad conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>intoxication with youth</b></a> as a state of mind that leads beings to engage in misconduct through their body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by frequently reflecting on being the owner of one's actions, a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>bad conduct</b></a> is either entirely abandoned or at least significantly weakened? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person frequently reflects on these five themes of aging, illness, death, separation, and kamma, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>Path is born</b></a> within them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by pursuing and developing the Path through these reflections, a practitioner's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>fetters are abandoned</b></a> and their underlying tendencies are destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised expanding one's reflection to realize that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>all beings</b></a> who undergo birth and death are equally subject to aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should reflect that they are not the only one subject to illness, but that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>every being</b></a> across the entire range of existence is subject to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested reflecting that all beings, throughout their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>coming and going</b></a> and rebirth, are subject to death just as oneself is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for all beings who undergo the process of birth and death, there will eventually be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>separation and division</b></a> from everything they hold dear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that all beings are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>owners of their actions</b></a> and heirs to their actions, and will be the recipients of whatever good or bad deeds they perform? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>ordinary people</b></a> often feel repelled or disgusted when they see others who are aging, ill, or dying, even though they themselves are subject to those same conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reflected that it would not be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>proper or fitting</b></a> for him to feel repelled by other beings who are aging, ill, or dying, since he is also subject to those same laws of nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how he overcame all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>forms of intoxication</b></a> regarding health, youth, and life by knowing the state without acquisitions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of finding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>safety in renunciation</b></a> (<i>nekkhamma</i>) as a key perspective that helped him overcome the intoxications of worldly life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared that his zeal and effort were directed toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>attaining Nibbāna</b></a> once he clearly saw the dangers inherent in the intoxications of youth and health? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that once he had fully realized the truth of aging, illness, and death, he was no longer <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>capable of indulging</b></a> in sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha affirmed that he would never turn back from his spiritual path and would remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>intent on the celibate life</b></a> (<i>brahmacariya</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should reflect on being the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato"><b>heir of one's kamma</b></a>, meaning that whatever action one performs—whether beautiful or evil—one will be the one to inherit its results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.57/en/sujato">AN 5.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was questioned by the brahmin Dona for allegedly failing to show respect or offer a seat to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>elderly brahmins</b></a> who were advanced in years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "brahmin like Brahmā" as one who is pure in birth for seven generations and lives a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>celibate student life</b></a> for forty-eight years while mastering the Vedic hymns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a "brahmin like Brahmā" seeks his teacher’s fee only through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>practice of alms-gathering</b></a>, refusing to earn it through trade, agriculture, or service to kings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a brahmin who attains the four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>divine abodes</b></a>—loving-kindness, compassion, altruistic joy, and equanimity—is considered one who is "like Brahmā"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a "brahmin like a deva" as one who, after forty-eight years of student life, seeks a brahmin wife only for the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>progeny</b></a> and not for mere sensual enjoyment or play? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned ancient sages such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>Atthaka and Vamaka</b></a> as the original authors and reciters of the hymns that modern brahmins continue to chant and repeat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a "brahmin who stays within boundaries" is one who follows the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>ancient traditions</b></a> of his ancestors regarding marriage and conduct without ever overstepping them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized a brahmin who takes a wife from any caste—including nobles, merchants, or even scavengers—as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>brahmin who breaks boundaries</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "brahmin outcaste" as one who makes a living through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>all kinds of work</b></a>, such as farming and trade, while still claiming the status and birthright of a brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Dona claimed to the Buddha that he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>pure in birth</b></a> on both his mother’s and father’s side back through seven generations of ancestors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a "brahmin like a deva" will practice the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a> after going forth from the household life and is subsequently reborn in a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted how certain brahmins justified their varied occupations by claiming they were like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>fire</b></a>, which burns both clean and unclean things without being defiled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a "brahmin like Brahmā" renounces the world and pervades all directions with a mind imbued with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> that is vast, exalted, and measureless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a brahmin "stays within boundaries" when he remains a householder following the birth of a child because of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>delight in his children</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>brahmin outcaste</b></a> as the fifth type of brahmin recognized by the ancient sages of the brahmin tradition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a proper brahmin student should seek a teacher's fee only by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>begging for alms</b></a> with an alms bowl, without looking down on the practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "brahmin like a deva" as one who only approaches his wife for the sake of offspring and never while she is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>pregnant or nursing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a brahmin who fulfills the requirements of a "brahmin like Brahmā" is reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>Brahma world</b></a> after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed ten <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>ancient sages</b></a>—including Vasettha, Kassapa, and Bhagu—who originally designated the five different types of brahmins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a "brahmin like a deva" is reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> after death because of his practice of the four jhanas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Dona, after hearing the Buddha's detailed explanation, admitted that he did not even fulfill the standards of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>brahmin outcaste</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that a "brahmin who breaks boundaries" is one who seeks his teacher's fee through both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>righteous and unrighteous</b></a> means, including buying and selling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "brahmin who stays within boundaries" as one who never buys a wife, but instead receives a brahmin woman through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>pouring of water</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a "brahmin outcaste" seeks his teacher's fee through various worldly professions such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato"><b>royal service</b></a>, archery, or trade? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.192/en/sujato">AN 5.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> is considered to be "slightly fault-ridden" but is "slow to fade away"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> as being "greatly fault-ridden" but "quick to fade away"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> is uniquely problematic because it is both "greatly fault-ridden" and "slow to fade away"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the primary cause for the arising and growth of lust is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>attractive feature</b></a> of an object when combined with improper attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>repulsive feature</b></a> as the condition that causes unarisen hatred to arise or existing hatred to increase when one gives it improper attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>improper attention</b></a> is the specific reason why unarisen delusion arises and existing delusion increases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that unarisen lust does not arise, and arisen lust is abandoned, through proper attention to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>unattractive feature</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind by loving-kindness</b></a>, when accompanied by proper attention, is the method to prevent hatred from arising and to abandon it once it has arisen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>proper attention</b></a> is the essential factor for ensuring that unarisen delusion does not arise and that arisen delusion is successfully abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted lust and hatred by noting that while lust is only "slightly fault-ridden," <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> is considered "greatly fault-ridden"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha differentiated the three unwholesome roots by explaining that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>lust and delusion</b></a> are both "slow to fade away," whereas hatred is notably quick to fade away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his monks on how to answer wanderers of other sects who might ask about the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>differentiation</b></a> and distinction between the qualities of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that giving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>improper attention</b></a> to the attractive feature of things is the specific fuel that leads to the further expansion and abundance of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that unarisen hatred will inevitably arise if a person continues to give <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>improper attention</b></a> to a repulsive feature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha indicated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> shares the negative characteristic of being "greatly fault-ridden" with hatred, but shares the difficult characteristic of being "slow to fade away" with lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that contemplating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>unattractive feature</b></a> is the specific meditative corrective required to abandon lust through proper attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> serves as the direct antidote for hatred, ensuring it either does not arise or is abandoned if it has already taken root in the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the fundamental <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato"><b>difference</b></a> between the three taints of lust, hatred, and delusion based on their relative degree of fault and their varying speeds of dissipation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.68/en/sujato">AN 3.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>streams of merit</b></a> and streams of the wholesome that are bringers of happiness, lead to heaven, and result in what is pleasant, liked, and charming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a monk uses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>robes</b></a> provided by a donor while entering and dwelling in an immeasurable concentration of mind, it generates an immeasurable stream of merit for that donor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how providing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> to a monk who maintains an immeasurable concentration of mind creates a stream of the wholesome that is conducive to heaven and long-term welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the gift of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>lodgings</b></a> to a monk who has attained an immeasurable concentration of mind is one of the four primary ways to generate a vast flow of merit for the giver? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that providing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>medicines</b></a> and provisions for the sick to a monk in deep concentration results in an immeasurable stream of merit that leads to happiness and a favorable rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the merit from supporting virtuous practitioners as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>conducive to heaven</b></a> and bringing about a "great mass of merit" that is impossible to quantify? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught it is not easy to grasp the exact measure of merit for a noble disciple who provides these four requisites, just as it is difficult to measure the amount of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>water in the ocean</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>great ocean</b></a> to illustrate that the merit gained by a generous donor is incalculable, immeasurable, and vast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the cumulative results of these four types of giving as an incalculable and immeasurable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>great mass of merit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the way many rivers flow into the sea to how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>streams of merit</b></a> constantly approach a wise person who provides food, drink, and clothing to practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the great ocean as a "mighty lake" and an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>abode of treasures</b></a> when emphasizing the enormous scale of merit that flows to a generous and wise person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>giver of food</b></a>, drink, and clothing—and who provides bedding and places to sit—is reached by streams of merit just as the ocean is reached by its flowing rivers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the merit from these four streams is so vast that it cannot be counted in terms of hundreds or thousands of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>gallons of water</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified specific acts of generosity—providing robes, food, shelter, and medicine—as the four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>bringers of happiness</b></a> that lead a person toward a heavenly destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a noble disciple who facilitates a monk’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable concentration</b></a> through the gift of requisites, the resulting merit is reckoned simply as a vast and immeasurable heap of goodness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.51/en/sujato">AN 4.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato"><b>four truths</b></a> of the brahmins which he himself realized through his own direct knowledge and subsequently made known to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato">AN 4.185</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the first truth of the brahmins as the ethical principle that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato"><b>all living beings</b></a> are not to be killed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato">AN 4.185</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who speaks the truth regarding non-violence should not use that fact to consider themselves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato"><b>superior, equal, or inferior</b></a> to any other person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato">AN 4.185</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who understands the truth of non-killing should specifically practice with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato"><b>kindness and compassion</b></a> for all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato">AN 4.185</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the second truth of the brahmins as the realization that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> are impermanent, suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato">AN 4.185</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that truly understanding the nature of sensual pleasures should lead a person to practice for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato"><b>revulsion, dispassion, and cessation</b></a> regarding them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato">AN 4.185</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the third truth of the brahmins as the understanding that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato"><b>all states of existence</b></a> are impermanent, suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato">AN 4.185</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a person who recognizes the truth about the nature of existence should practice specifically for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato"><b>cessation of states</b></a> of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato">AN 4.185</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared the fourth truth of the brahmins as the profound realization: “<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato"><b>I am nothing anywhere</b></a> for anyone, and there is nothing of mine anywhere in anything”? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato">AN 4.185</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person recognizes they possess nothing and are nothing to anyone, they are practicing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato"><b>way to nothingness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.185/en/sujato">AN 4.185</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once stayed at the Gabled Hall in the Great Wood near Vesālī, where he was approached by a group of rowdy young Licchavis who set aside their bows and dogs to sit in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>silent worship</b></a> before him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahānāma the Licchavi was so surprised to see the normally "wild and rough" Licchavi youths sitting quietly before the Buddha that he exclaimed that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>Vajjis will prosper</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was told by Mahānāma that the young Licchavis were known for looting families of sugar cane, berries, and sweets, and even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>harassing women</b></a> of the clans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any clansman who possesses five specific qualities will experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>only growth</b></a> and never decline, whether he is a king, a general, or a village headman? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that wealth should be acquired through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>energetic striving</b></a>, the strength of one’s arms, and the sweat of one's brow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that wealth must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>righteously gained</b></a> in order to be used effectively for one's own prosperity and the benefit of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified honoring and supporting one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>parents</b></a> with righteously earned wealth as the first of five ways to ensure a person's growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when parents are honored by their child, they show compassion by wishing them a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>long life</b></a>, which leads to that child's continued growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a righteous person should use their wealth to honor and support their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>wife and children</b></a> to maintain a prosperous household? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>slaves and workers</b></a> as a group that a clansman should honor and provide for to ensure his own success and avoid decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person's prosperity is bolstered when they use their resources to honor their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>field workers</b></a> and business associates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that honoring <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>deities</b></a> who receive offerings is one of the five essential practices for a leader to ensure they do not decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that deities who are honored by a righteous person will respond with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a>, helping that person to prosper? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that supporting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>ascetics and brahmins</b></a> with righteously gained wealth is a key factor in a clansman's growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person is supported by the compassion of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>spiritual teachers</b></a>, they can expect only growth and no decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the same five principles of wealth distribution apply to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>village headman</b></a> just as much as they apply to an anointed king? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who performs their duties toward their family and the departed is considered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>virtuous person</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a wise householder who generates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>joy</b></a> for ascetics, brahmins, and deities will be praised in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who lead a righteous life and support their inner circle will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato"><b>rejoice in heaven</b></a> after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.58/en/sujato">AN 5.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>Saṅgārava</b></a> once asked the Buddha why some hymns that have been recited for a long time do not occur to the mind, while others that have not been recited for a long time do? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the mind is obsessed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a>, even hymns that have been studied for a long time will not occur to one's memory? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a mind obsessed with sensual desire to a bowl of water mixed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>dyes</b></a> such as lac, turmeric, indigo, or madder, where a person cannot see their own reflection clearly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>ill-will</b></a> acts as a mental hindrance that prevents one from understanding their own good, the good of others, or the good of both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a bowl of water <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>heated by fire</b></a>, boiling and bubbling, to describe the state of a mind overcome by ill-will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person's mind is obsessed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a>, they fail to understand the escape from that state as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a mind possessed by sloth and torpor to a bowl of water covered with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>moss</b></a> and water plants, which obscures one's vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> in the mind can cause even long-studied hymns to escape one's memory when they are needed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a mind overwhelmed by restlessness and remorse to a bowl of water whipped by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>wind</b></a>, stirred up and rippling so that a reflection cannot be seen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a mind overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> is like a bowl of water that is turbid, muddy, and placed in the dark, preventing any clear understanding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the mind is free from the five hindrances, even hymns that have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>not been recited</b></a> for a long time will occur to the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a mind free from sensual desire to a bowl of water not mixed with any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>dyes</b></a>, allowing a person to see their reflection as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that when the mind is not obsessed by ill-will, it is like a bowl of water <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>not heated</b></a> by fire, remaining still and clear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind free from sloth and torpor is like water <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>not covered</b></a> by moss or water plants, providing clarity of vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a mind free from restlessness and remorse to water that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>not whipped</b></a> by the wind, thus remaining calm and reflective? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a mind free from doubt is like a bowl of water that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>clear and bright</b></a> and placed in the light, allowing one to see clearly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who understands the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> from arisen hindrances like ill-will or doubt can truly understand their own good and the good of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Saṅgārava that being obsessed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>mental hindrances</b></a> is the specific reason why one might fail to recall things they have long studied? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used various <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>similes of water</b></a> to illustrate how different mental states affect the clarity of one's consciousness and memory? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's explanation of the five hindrances, the brahmin <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>Saṅgārava</b></a> declared the teaching excellent and took refuge in the Buddha as a lay follower? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the mind is clear and unhindered, a person can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>understand and see</b></a> the truth as it really is regarding their own welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that memory recall is directly tied to whether the mind is currently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato"><b>obsessed</b></a> by hindrances like desire, ill-will, or sloth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.193/en/sujato">AN 5.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified greed, hatred, and delusion as the three <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome roots</b></a> from which many evil states arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, any action a greedy person initiates through body, speech, or mind is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person overcome by hatred who causes suffering to others through killing or imprisonment is performing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome deeds</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person acting under the influence of delusion may cause others suffering through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>confiscation or banishment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that someone who harms others while their mind is obsessed by greed is called one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>speaks at the wrong time</b></a> and one who speaks falsely? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an unwholesome person who is told a fact will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>deny it</b></a> and will not admit the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person overcome by unwholesome states based on hatred dwells in suffering in this very life, full of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>distress and despair</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person dominated by delusion, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>bad destination</b></a> is to be expected after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person overcome by greed, hatred, and delusion to a tree that is strangled and brought to ruin by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>three creepers</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified non-greed, non-hatred, and non-delusion as the three <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>wholesome roots</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any action performed by a person who is not greedy, whether through body, speech, or mind, is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>wholesome</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person not overcome by hatred who refrains from causing others suffering through violence or theft is performing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>wholesome actions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person free from delusion who does not harm others is called one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>speaks at the right time</b></a> and one who speaks truthfully? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a wholesome person who is told a fact will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>admit it</b></a> and not deny the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person acting in accordance with the wholesome roots as someone who speaks in accordance with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma and Discipline</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person who has abandoned greed, hatred, and delusion, these unwholesome states are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>cut off at the root</b></a> like a palm tree stump? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who has eradicated the unwholesome roots dwells happily in this very life, free from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>anguish and fever</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person who has made unwholesome states incapable of future arising attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna in this very life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a simile of a man who cuts a creeper at the root, chops it into pieces, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>burns it to ashes</b></a> to describe the total destruction of unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that after burning the unwholesome "creepers" to ashes, they should be winnowed in a strong wind or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>washed away in a river</b></a> so they never grow again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>non-hatred</b></a> as a wholesome root that prevents a person from being overwhelmed by ill will and harming others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when unwholesome states are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato"><b>rendered non-existent</b></a>, a person experiences peace and happiness in the present moment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.69/en/sujato">AN 3.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato"><b>streams of merit</b></a> and streams of the wholesome that bring happiness, lead to heaven, and result in what is wished for, pleasing, and charming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato">AN 4.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the first stream of merit arises when a noble disciple possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence in the Buddha</b></a>, recognizing him as an Arahant, perfectly enlightened, and a teacher of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato">AN 4.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a noble disciple gains a stream of merit by having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence in the Dhamma</b></a>, which is well-expounded by the Blessed One, visible here and now, and timeless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato">AN 4.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the third stream of merit as possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence in the Saṅgha</b></a> of the Blessed One's disciples, who are practicing the good, upright, and proper way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato">AN 4.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the fourth stream of merit consists of a disciple possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato"><b>virtues liked by the noble ones</b></a>, which are unbroken, untorn, unblemished, and conducive to concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato">AN 4.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the Sangha of disciples as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world, consisting of four pairs of persons or eight individual types who are worthy of gifts and hospitality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato">AN 4.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the four streams of merit are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato"><b>conducive to heaven</b></a> and lead to a pleasant ripening of one's actions, benefiting a person with happiness and welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato">AN 4.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato"><b>not poor</b></a> and their life is "not in vain" if they have firm faith in the Tathāgata, good virtue, and confidence in the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato">AN 4.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the virtues that lead to merit as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato"><b>praised by the wise</b></a>, untarnished, and not misapprehended by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato">AN 4.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the wise to devote themselves to faith, virtue, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato"><b>straight vision</b></a> while remembering the teachings of the Buddhas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato">AN 4.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato"><b>inviting inspection</b></a> (<i>ehipassiko</i>), serving as one of the primary objects of confirmed confidence for a noble disciple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato">AN 4.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the Dhamma, as a source of merit, is something that must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato"><b>personally experienced by the wise</b></a> (<i>paccattaṃ veditabbo viññūhī</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.52/en/sujato">AN 4.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the entire world is led by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a>, is swept away by it, and falls under the power of the mind that has arisen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the world is constantly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>swept away</b></a> by the mind, which exerts complete control over it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for someone to be considered a "bearer of the Dhamma," they do not necessarily need to memorize all nine branches of the teachings, but must instead be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>practicing in accordance</b></a> with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person can be called "one who has learned much" even if they only understand the meaning and the Dhamma of a single <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>four-line verse</b></a>, provided they practice according to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined someone who is "learned and of penetrating wisdom" as a person who has heard the teaching on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> and has clearly seen its meaning through their own wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>penetrating wisdom</b></a> when they have heard about the origin of suffering and have personally penetrated its deeper meaning with their wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person of deep insight as one who has heard the teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> and has used wisdom to see its truth clearly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a person is "learned and of penetrating wisdom" when they hear about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>way leading to the cessation</b></a> of suffering and successfully penetrate that meaning with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a wise person of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>great wisdom</b></a> as someone who does not intend for their own affliction or harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a truly wise person never intends for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>affliction of others</b></a>, illustrating the compassionate nature of great wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person of great wisdom avoids intentions that lead to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>affliction of both</b></a> themselves and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person of great wisdom as someone who thinks only of their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>own welfare</b></a> in a way that is also conducive to the welfare of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a wise person with great wisdom focuses their thoughts on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>welfare of the whole world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly mentioned that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>many teachings</b></a> include various formats such as suttas, songs, expositions, verses, and inspired utterances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised a monk for having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>fine inquiry</b></a> when the monk asked about what leads the world and what defines a person of great wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that to be a bearer of the Dhamma, one must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>understand the meaning</b></a> of the teaching and not just the words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a person is "learned" when they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>penetrate and see</b></a> the truth of the Four Noble Truths with their own wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined "great wisdom" not by intellectual capacity alone, but by a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato"><b>intentions</b></a> toward the welfare and benefit of all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.186/en/sujato">AN 4.186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato"><b>attainment of the unwholesome</b></a> does not occur as long as a person maintains faith in wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato">AN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, it is only when faith disappears and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato"><b>faithlessness</b></a> takes its place that a person attains unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato">AN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that unwholesome attainments are kept at bay as long as a practitioner possesses the quality of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato"><b>shame</b></a> (hirī) regarding wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato">AN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that once shame vanishes and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato"><b>shamelessness</b></a> (ahirika) prevails, the attainment of unwholesome states follows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato">AN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato"><b>moral dread</b></a> (ottappa) prevents the occurrence of unwholesome attainments in a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato">AN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when moral dread disappears and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato"><b>lack of moral dread</b></a> (anottappa) takes over, unwholesome states will attain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato">AN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that unwholesome states cannot be attained as long as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato"><b>energy</b></a> (vīriya) is actively established in wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato">AN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato"><b>laziness</b></a> (kosajja) as the state that dominates and allows for unwholesome attainment once energy has been lost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato">AN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> (paññā) regarding wholesome qualities acts as a barrier against the attainment of the unwholesome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato">AN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when wisdom is lost and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato"><b>foolishness</b></a> (duppaññā) takes its place, a person then falls into unwholesome attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.6/en/sujato">AN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that for the most part, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato"><b>beings are fond</b></a> of and play with sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato">AN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a clansman who abandons a life of wealth and luxury to go forth into homelessness can be truly called a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato"><b>clansman gone forth out of faith</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato">AN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> as being inferior, middling, or superior, yet noted that they are all collectively known simply as "sensual pleasures"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato">AN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a simile of a nurse who must forcefully remove a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato"><b>stick or stone</b></a> from an infant's mouth to illustrate how he must strictly supervise certain monks to protect them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato">AN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a nurse acts out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> and a desire for the child's welfare even when she must use force that causes the child pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato">AN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that as long as a monk has not yet established faith, shame, moral dread, energy, and wisdom, the Buddha must continue to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato"><b>protect</b></a> that monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato">AN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, once a monk has fully developed faith and wisdom, he becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato"><b>self-guarded</b></a> and is no longer capable of being negligent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato">AN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato"><b>unconcerned</b></a> about a monk's potential for negligence only after that monk has successfully established the five spiritual qualities of faith, shame, moral dread, energy, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.7/en/sujato">AN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked about the Buddha’s lucidity of wisdom, the Brahmin Piṅgiyānī replied that only someone equal to the Buddha could truly know the full extent of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Piṅgiyānī described the Buddha as being "praised by the praised," calling him the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>best of devas and humans</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Piṅgiyānī used a simile of a man satisfied by the best of tastes to explain that once a person hears the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma of the Buddha</b></a>, they no longer long for the inferior theories of other teachers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Brahmin Piṅgiyānī, the Buddha's teaching—whether delivered in prose, verse, or exposition—is like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>honey ball</b></a> because no matter where one tastes it, it provides a delicious and sweet flavor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Piṅgiyānī compared the Buddha's Dhamma to a piece of yellow or red <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>sandalwood</b></a>, noting that whether one smells it at the root, the middle, or the top, it always provides an exquisite fragrance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Piṅgiyānī stated that whenever someone hears the Buddha’s teaching, they immediately gain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>mental clarity</b></a> and satisfaction of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Piṅgiyānī likened the Buddha's ability to teach to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>skilled physician</b></a> who is able to instantly remove the suffering and illness of a patient who is severely afflicted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Brahmin Piṅgiyānī, hearing the Dhamma from the Buddha causes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>sorrow and lamentation</b></a>, as well as pain, dejection, and anguish, to completely vanish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Piṅgiyānī compared the Buddha's teaching to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>cool pond</b></a> with clear and pleasant water, which provides total relief to a traveler exhausted and parched by the heat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Piṅgiyānī explained that just as bathing in a refreshing pond allays all fatigue, hearing the Dhamma from the Buddha allays all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>distress and fever</b></a> of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Kāraṇapālī, while supervising the work projects of the Licchavis, was so impressed by Piṅgiyānī's description of the Buddha that he paid homage by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>bowing</b></a> toward the Great Wood where the Buddha was staying? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Kāraṇapālī declared that Piṅgiyānī had made the Dhamma clear as if he were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>bringing a lamp</b></a> into the darkness so that those with eyes could see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Piṅgiyānī pointed out that the Buddha's teachings include various forms such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>marvelous accounts</b></a> (abbhutadhamma) and systematic expositions, all of which lead to joy and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Kāraṇapālī expressed his devotion by reciting the three-fold <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>homage to the Buddha</b></a>—"Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sammāsambuddhassa"—after hearing about the Dhamma's qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Piṅgiyānī described hearing the Buddha's Dhamma as an experience that brings about immediate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>joy and happiness</b></a> (pāmojjaṃ and somanassaṃ)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Kāraṇapālī asked if Piṅgiyānī considered the Buddha to be wise, Piṅgiyānī responded that the Buddha is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>venerated by the best</b></a> of men? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Piṅgiyānī emphasized that the Dhamma is consistently beneficial whether it is encountered in the form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato"><b>verses or songs</b></a> (geyya)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.194/en/sujato">AN 5.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the lady Visākhā that there are three distinct ways to keep the Uposatha, which are the cowherd’s observance, the Nigaṇṭhas’ observance, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>noble observance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who spends the Observance day preoccupied with thoughts of what they ate today and what they will eat tomorrow to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>cowherd</b></a> who only thinks about where the cattle grazed and where they will graze the next day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the "cowherd’s observance" is not of great fruit or benefit because the practitioner passes the day with a mind accompanied by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>covetousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha criticized the Nigaṇṭha sect’s version of the Observance day because they only teach their disciples to show compassion to living beings within a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>limited distance</b></a> of one hundred yojanas, rather than to all beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out the hypocrisy of the Nigaṇṭha observance, where a disciple is told to claim they have no possessions while their parents and family still recognize them and they still <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>recognize their family</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the Nigaṇṭhas’ practice of claiming to have no belongings while still being supported by their family is actually a form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>lying</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the "noble observance" (<i>ariyuposatha</i>) as the process of cleansing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>defiled mind</b></a> through the application of a proper method? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a man cleaning a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>dirty head</b></a> using paste, clay, and water to explain how a noble disciple cleanses their mind by recollecting the qualities of the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a noble disciple cleanses their mind by recollecting the Buddha, it is called the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>Brahmuposatha</b></a>, during which they are said to dwell with the Supreme Being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the cleansing of the mind through the recollection of the Dhamma to cleaning a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>dirty body</b></a> using a scourer, bath powder, and water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a noble disciple who dwells with a mind purified by the Dhamma is said to be observing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>Dhammuposatha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a laundryman cleaning a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>soiled cloth</b></a> with heat, salt, and cow dung to describe how a disciple cleanses their mind by recollecting the qualities of the Saṅgha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a disciple's mind becomes placid and joyful by thinking of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>community of disciples</b></a>, they are practicing the Saṅghuposatha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the purification of the mind through recollecting one's own virtuous behavior to the polishing of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>dirty mirror</b></a> with oil, ashes, and a brush? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>Sīluposatha</b></a> as an observance where a disciple focuses on their own virtues that are "unbroken, untorn, unspotted, and unsplattered"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mind can be cleansed by recollecting the faith, virtue, and wisdom of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>deities</b></a> in various heavens, such as the Tāvatiṃsa and Tusita gods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the process of cleansing the mind by recollecting the gods to the refining of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>tarnished gold</b></a> in a furnace with salt and red chalk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the noble observance, a disciple reflects that they possess the same <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>qualities of faith</b></a> and wisdom that led the deities to be reborn in their heavenly states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a person keeping the noble observance should resolve to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>imitate the Arahants</b></a> by abstaining from killing, stealing, and sexual activity for that day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by abstaining from false speech and being a speaker of truth during the Observance, a disciple mimics the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>truthfulness of Arahants</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that those observing the Uposatha should abstain from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>intoxicating drinks</b></a> such as liquor and wine, which are the basis for heedlessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners of the Uposatha to eat only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>one meal</b></a> and abstain from food at the "wrong time" (after noon), just as the Arahants do? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the abstinence from dancing, singing, music, and the use of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>perfumes and jewelry</b></a> as a core part of the noble eight-factored observance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the Observance day, the Buddha advised sleeping on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>low bed</b></a> or a grass mat rather than high and luxurious beds to follow the example of the Arahants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the noble eight-factored observance to be of such great fruit that it is worth more than ruling the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>sixteen great nations</b></a>, such as Aṅga and Magadha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that fifty human years are equivalent to just a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>single day and night</b></a> for the gods of the Four Great Kings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one hundred human years are equal to only one day and night for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>Tāvatiṃsa gods</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the lifespan of a deity in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>Tusita heaven</b></a> is four thousand celestial years, where four hundred human years constitute a single celestial day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that sixteen hundred human years make up just one day and night for the gods who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato"><b>exercise control</b></a> over the creations of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.70/en/sujato">AN 3.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a husband and wife to see each other in both this life and the hereafter, they must be matched in their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato">AN 4.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder Nakulapitā told the Buddha he had never transgressed against his wife, Nakulamātā, even in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato"><b>thought</b></a> since they were married as youths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato">AN 4.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato"><b>moral discipline</b></a> as one of the four essential qualities a couple must share to be reunited in future lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato">AN 4.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when both husband and wife are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato"><b>equal in generosity</b></a>, they can expect to see each other in the life to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato">AN 4.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a couple who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato"><b>equal in wisdom</b></a> will experience the benefit of seeing each other in both this world and the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato">AN 4.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a harmonious couple who lives by the Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato"><b>kindly in speech</b></a> toward one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato">AN 4.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a couple shares the same level of virtue, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato"><b>enemies</b></a> become dejected because of the couple's mutual success and well-being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato">AN 4.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that couples who are matched in their virtuous behavior and practice the Dhamma together will rejoice in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato"><b>deva world</b></a> after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato">AN 4.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder's wife Nakulamātā expressed to the Buddha her deep desire to remain with her husband not only in this life but also in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato"><b>future lives</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato">AN 4.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a couple who practices the Dhamma together as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato"><b>many goals</b></a> met and living in great comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.55/en/sujato">AN 4.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the Koliyan daughter Suppavāsā that by giving food, a noble disciple provides <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato"><b>long life</b></a> to the recipients? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato">AN 4.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a donor who gives food to others also gives them the gift of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato"><b>beauty</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato">AN 4.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a noble disciple who offers food to practitioners is actually giving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a> to those who receive it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato">AN 4.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that providing food to others is a significant way of giving them <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato"><b>strength</b></a> to continue their lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato">AN 4.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by giving the gift of strength through food, the giver will personally partake in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato"><b>divine or human strength</b></a> in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato">AN 4.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highly praised the offering of food that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato"><b>well-prepared</b></a>, clean, and delicious? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato">AN 4.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that alms given to those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato"><b>upright</b></a> and have reached greatness yield immense fruit for the giver? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato">AN 4.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged givers to remove the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato"><b>stain of stinginess</b></a> completely, right down to its roots? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato">AN 4.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who give food with a joyful heart and overcome stinginess will reach a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato"><b>heavenly place</b></a> after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato">AN 4.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by giving long life to others through food, the giver becomes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato"><b>partaker of long life</b></a> in either a divine or human state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.57/en/sujato">AN 4.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once described an assembly of monks at the Eastern Park as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>free of chaff</b></a> and pure, having been established in the core? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>pure assembly</b></a> of practitioners is so rare that it is difficult to find or even see in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the community of noble monks as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world, worthy of gifts, hospitality, and offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>small gift</b></a> is given to a pure assembly of monks, it becomes great, and a great gift given to them becomes even greater? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated an assembly of accomplished monks is so significant that it is worth traveling many leagues to see, even if one must carry a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>travel bag</b></a> for the journey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined certain monks in the assembly as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>attained to the divine</b></a> because they dwell having entered and reached the four jhānas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as "attained to the divine" if he dwells in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>first jhāna</b></a>, which is accompanied by thought and examination, with rapture and happiness born of seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized those who dwell pervading all directions with a mind of loving-kindness, compassion, altruistic joy, and equanimity as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>attained to brahma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk dwells <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>attaining to brahma</b></a> when he pervades the entire world with a mind of loving-kindness that is vast, exalted, and measureless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk is considered "attained to brahma" when he pervades the world with a mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> that is free from enmity and ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the practice of pervading all directions with a mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>altruistic joy</b></a> as a way for a practitioner to be described as "attained to brahma"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as "attained to brahma" when he pervades the world with a mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a>, specifically one that is abundant, exalted, and measureless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>attained to the imperturbable</b></a> to describe monks who have transcended perceptions of forms and dwell in the formless meditative attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is "attained to the imperturbable" when he transcends perceptions of sensory impact and dwells in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>base of the infinity of space</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that reaching the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>base of the infinity of consciousness</b></a> by transcending the base of the infinity of space is a mark of one who is "attained to the imperturbable"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk dwells "attained to the imperturbable" when he completely transcends the base of the infinity of consciousness and enters the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>base of nothingness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>attained to the noble</b></a> when he understands suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the way leading to its cessation as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized monks who possess the experiential realization of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> as those who have "attained to the noble"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.190/en/sujato">AN 4.190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that developing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.61/en/sujato"><b>perception of unattractiveness</b></a> is one of five practices that, when frequently cultivated, lead to great fruit, great benefit, and culminate in the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.61/en/sujato">AN 5.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.61/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-delight</b></a> in the entire world as a specific mental training that brings about great fruit and is grounded in the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.61/en/sujato">AN 5.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.61/en/sujato"><b>perception of repulsiveness in food</b></a> as one of five perceptions that, when developed and practiced, leads to the highest spiritual goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.61/en/sujato">AN 5.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.62/en/sujato"><b>perception of impermanence</b></a> is a vital practice for noble growth, leading to great benefit and the ultimate realization of the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.62/en/sujato">AN 5.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that cultivating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.62/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-self</b></a>, along with four other specific perceptions, is highly beneficial and ends in the state of the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.62/en/sujato">AN 5.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a noble disciple as one who grows in five specific qualities, the first being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.63/en/sujato"><b>growth in faith</b></a>, which allows them to take hold of the essence of their person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.63/en/sujato">AN 5.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an excellent person is one who increases in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.63/en/sujato"><b>wisdom and generosity</b></a>, thereby grasping what is truly essential in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.63/en/sujato">AN 5.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the noble disciple who experiences <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.63/en/sujato"><b>growth in virtue</b></a> and learning, as they are considered to be growing with a noble growth that provides what is best for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.63/en/sujato">AN 5.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk is fit to live with his fellow practitioners if he is not only personally accomplished in virtue but can also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.66/en/sujato"><b>answer questions</b></a> about the accomplishment of virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.66/en/sujato">AN 5.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha deemed a monk fit for the celibate life if he possesses personal accomplishment in concentration and the ability to give an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.66/en/sujato"><b>explanation of concentration</b></a> when questioned by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.66/en/sujato">AN 5.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a bhikkhu is truly companionable when he is personally accomplished in liberation and can provide <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.66/en/sujato"><b>answers on liberation</b></a> to his fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.66/en/sujato">AN 5.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the destruction of the taints is achieved through five specific practices, including dwelling as one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.70/en/sujato"><b>contemplating unattractiveness</b></a> in the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.70/en/sujato">AN 5.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for the taints to be destroyed, a monk must ensure that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.70/en/sujato"><b>perception of death</b></a> is well established internally? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.70/en/sujato">AN 5.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.70/en/sujato"><b>contemplating impermanence</b></a> in all conditioned things as a key factor that leads directly to the total destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.70/en/sujato">AN 5.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying in the Gabled Hall in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato"><b>Great Wood</b></a> at Vesālī, he was visited by five hundred Licchavis who were remarkably color-coordinated, with groups dressed entirely in blue, yellow, red, and white? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato">AN 5.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was described as outshining five hundred brightly dressed Licchavi nobles in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato"><b>appearance and glory</b></a>, much like the sun outshines other objects in the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato">AN 5.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Piṅgiyānī, inspired by the Buddha's presence, recited a verse comparing the Buddha to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato"><b>fragrant red lotus</b></a> that blooms in the morning, spreading its scent without it ever fading? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato">AN 5.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the brahmin Piṅgiyānī praised the Buddha, the five hundred Licchavis were so impressed they gave him <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato"><b>five hundred upper robes</b></a>, all of which the brahmin then immediately offered to the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato">AN 5.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the appearance of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a>, an Arahant, a Perfectly Enlightened One, is the first of five treasures that are extremely rare and difficult to find in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato">AN 5.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato"><b>expound the Dhamma</b></a> and Vinaya proclaimed by the Tathāgata is considered a rare and precious treasure in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato">AN 5.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato"><b>understands the Dhamma</b></a> and Vinaya after it has been proclaimed by a Tathāgata as one of the five rare treasures of humanity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato">AN 5.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato"><b>practices in accordance</b></a> with the Dhamma, having already understood the teachings proclaimed by a Tathāgata, is a rare find in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato">AN 5.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato"><b>grateful and appreciative</b></a> person is the fifth rare treasure whose appearance in the world is difficult to encounter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato">AN 5.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Piṅgiyānī addressed the Buddha as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato"><b>Angīrasa</b></a> in his verses, describing him as a radiant being who shines like the sun in the firmament? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.195/en/sujato">AN 5.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda explained to the wanderer Channa that when a person is overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> and their mind is obsessed by it, they frequently intend for their own affliction, the affliction of others, and the affliction of both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Ānanda, a person who is overcome and obsessed by hatred experiences <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>mental suffering</b></a> and grief, but these negative states cease once hatred is successfully abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda taught that being blinded by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> leads a person to engage in misconduct through body, speech, and mind, causing harm to themselves and those around them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda described lust as something that causes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>blindness</b></a>, a lack of vision, and a lack of knowledge, ultimately leading to the destruction of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>—consisting of right view through right concentration—as the specific way and practice for abandoning lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda stated that once <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> is abandoned, a person no longer intends for their own affliction or the affliction of others, and they no longer experience associated mental grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Ānanda, a person obsessed with delusion fails to understand their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>own good</b></a> as it really is, but upon abandoning delusion, they can clearly perceive the good of themselves and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda categorized lust, hatred, and delusion as states that side with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>vexation</b></a> and do not lead toward the attainment of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda taught that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> is abandoned, a person naturally refrains from bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct that they would have otherwise committed while under its influence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda explained that hatred is a "maker of non-knowledge" and a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>destroyer of wisdom</b></a>" because it prevents the mind from seeing the truth of a situation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda pointed out that a person overcome by delusion experiences an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>affliction of both</b></a> themselves and others, demonstrating the social and personal danger of an unliberated mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda described the state of being lustful as having a mind that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>obsessed</b></a>, which hinders one's ability to understand the welfare of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda emphasized that the reason the Dhamma prescribes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>abandoning</b></a> of lust, hatred, and delusion is because of the visible and immediate dangers these states produce in one's life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda taught that delusion results in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>lack of vision</b></a>, acting as an internal darkness that obstructs the path to spiritual liberation and Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda noted that once hatred is removed, a person no longer engages in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>verbal misconduct</b></a>, showing how ethical conduct is rooted in the purification of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wanderer Channa, after hearing Ven. Ānanda's explanation, praised the teaching as a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>splendid path</b></a>" that is sufficient for the practice of heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda explained that the danger in lust is seen when a person, driven by desire, performs <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>bodily misconduct</b></a> that leads to long-term suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Ānanda, hatred is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>unproductive of Nibbāna</b></a> because it creates internal turmoil and prevents the peace necessary for enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda defined the abandonment of delusion as the key to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>understanding as it really is</b></a> the benefit of both oneself and others simultaneously? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda taught that the removal of lust, hatred, and delusion is not just a theoretical goal but a practical necessity because these three states are the primary causes of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato"><b>mental grief</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.71/en/sujato">AN 3.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>well-expounded Dhamma</b></a> as one that is taught specifically for the purpose of abandoning lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Ānanda, those in the world who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>practicing well</b></a> are specifically those who are engaged in the practice of abandoning lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda explained that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>well-faring</b></a> (sukatā) in the world are those who have abandoned lust, hatred, and delusion, ensuring they are no longer subject to future arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda taught that a teaching is truly well-expounded when its primary focus is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of lust</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda suggested that a Dhamma is validly described as well-taught if its core aim is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of hatred</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of delusion</b></a> as one of the three primary criteria for a teaching to be considered well-expounded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>palm stump</b></a> to describe the state where lust, hatred, and delusion have been so thoroughly uprooted that they can never grow again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda described a person who has attained the spiritual goal as someone whose defilements are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>no longer subject to future arising</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda engaged in a profound dialogue with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>householder disciple of the Ājīvakas</b></a> while staying at Ghosita’s Park in Kosambi? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that rather than directly praising his own teacher, Ven. Ānanda used <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>counter-questions</b></a> to lead a householder to realize for himself the qualities of a true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a householder praised Ven. Ānanda’s method of teaching because it avoided <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>extolling one’s own Dhamma</b></a> while staying focused on the objective truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a householder was impressed by Ven. Ānanda’s teaching style, which managed to state the truth without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>disparaging the Dhamma of others</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a householder noted that Ven. Ānanda’s teaching was effective because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>meaning was expressed</b></a> clearly without the speaker being brought into the discussion in an egoistic way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda described a person as "well-gone" (sukatā) when their lust, hatred, and delusion are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>uprooted</b></a> and completely obliterated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a householder compared Ven. Ānanda’s explanation of the Dhamma to someone <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>holding up a lamp</b></a> in the darkness so that those with eyes could see forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a householder praised Ven. Ānanda for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>revealing what was hidden</b></a> through his skillful use of questions and clear criteria for spiritual success? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a householder who was previously a disciple of the Ājīvakas ended up <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>taking refuge</b></a> in the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha after a conversation with Ven. Ānanda? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda defined a "well-gone" person as one whose greed, hatred, and delusion have been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato"><b>cut off at the root</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.72/en/sujato">AN 3.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda chose to answer a question on behalf of the Buddha because the Blessed One had only recently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato"><b>recovered from an illness</b></a> and the question posed was very deep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato">AN 3.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahānāma the Sakyan asked the Buddha whether <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> comes before knowledge, or if knowledge precedes concentration in the development of the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato">AN 3.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda explained that the Buddha taught both the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato"><b>virtue of a trainee</b></a> and the virtue of one beyond training (an adept) as part of the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato">AN 3.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato"><b>virtue of a trainee</b></a> as being virtuous, staying restrained by the Pātimokkha, and training in the rules of conduct while seeing danger in the slightest faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato">AN 3.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Ānanda, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato"><b>concentration of a trainee</b></a> is characterized by the attainment of the four jhānas, which are states of deep meditative focus and seclusion from sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato">AN 3.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato"><b>wisdom of a trainee</b></a> as the realistic understanding of the Four Noble Truths, including the nature of suffering and the path leading to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato">AN 3.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda taught that a noble disciple who is fully endowed with trainee-level virtue, concentration, and wisdom eventually achieves the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato">AN 3.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahānāma the Sakyan noted he had long understood the Buddha's teaching that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato"><b>knowledge</b></a> belongs to one who is concentrated, rather than to one who is unconcentrated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato">AN 3.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda explicitly mentioned that the Buddha spoke about both the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato"><b>concentration of a trainee</b></a> and the concentration of one beyond training (an adept)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato">AN 3.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda explained that by successfully training in virtue, concentration, and wisdom, a practitioner attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato">AN 3.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda took Mahānāma the Sakyan <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato"><b>by the arm</b></a> to lead him aside so they could discuss the Dhamma without disturbing the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato">AN 3.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Ānanda, the Buddha's teaching on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> encompasses both the developmental stage of a trainee and the finalized stage of one who has completed the training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.73/en/sujato">AN 3.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Ven. Ānanda that those for whom one has compassion—such as friends, colleagues, relatives, and kinsmen—should be encouraged and established in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence</b></a> in the Triple Gem? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that while the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>four great elements</b></a>—earth, water, fire, and air—might undergo change or alteration, a noble disciple possessing confirmed confidence in the Buddha will never undergo such a change in their spiritual security? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is an impossible occurrence for a noble disciple who possesses confirmed confidence in the Buddha to ever be reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one should settle their loved ones in confirmed confidence in the Dhamma, describing it as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>visible here and now</b></a>, immediate, and inviting one to come and see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the Sangha of the Blessed One’s disciples as consisting of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>four pairs of persons</b></a>, or eight types of individuals, who are worthy of gifts, hospitality, and offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Sangha of disciples is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world, and that establishing others in this confidence is a way to show them true compassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a noble disciple with confirmed confidence in the Sangha is protected from ever falling into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>animal realm</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the Blessed One as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>teacher of gods and men</b></a>, a quality that forms a basis for the confirmed confidence a noble disciple should have? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that it is impossible for a noble disciple endowed with confirmed confidence in the Dhamma to be reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>sphere of afflicted spirits</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>applicable</b></a> (opaneyyiko) and something to be personally experienced by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Sangha of the Blessed One's disciples is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>upright</b></a>, methodical, and dutiful in its practice, making it worthy of reverential salutation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even if the physical world experienced an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>alteration</b></a> in its fundamental elements, a noble disciple's unwavering faith in the Triple Gem remains steadfast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged Ven. Ānanda to establish his friends and colleagues in the belief that the Buddha is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>fully enlightened</b></a> and endowed with clear vision and virtuous conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Blessed One as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>knower of worlds</b></a> and an unsurpassed guide for those to be tamed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that confirmed confidence in the Dhamma means recognizing it as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>well-expounded</b></a> by the Blessed One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the Sangha as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>methodical</b></a> (ñāyappaṭipanno) in its practice, which is one of the four ways the disciplehood of the Blessed One is defined? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that confirmed confidence in the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>safe haven</b></a> that prevents a disciple from ever facing rebirth in the lower realms of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly used the phrase "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>no possibility</b></a>" to describe the chance of a noble disciple with confirmed confidence in the Triple Gem falling into hell or other bad destinations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the Blessed One as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>sublime</b></a> (sugato), a title representing the perfection of the path he walked and taught? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that compassion for one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato"><b>kinsmen</b></a> is best expressed by establishing them in the three bases of confirmed confidence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.75/en/sujato">AN 3.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an agricultural metaphor to explain the renewal of existence, stating that for beings hindered by ignorance and fettered by craving, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato"><b>kamma is the field</b></a>, consciousness is the seed, and craving is the moisture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato">AN 3.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Ven. Ānanda that if there were no kamma ripening in the sense sphere, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato"><b>sense-sphere existence</b></a> would never be discerned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato">AN 3.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, consciousness becomes established in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato"><b>low realm</b></a> (the sense realm) when craving acts as the moisture for the seed of consciousness in beings hindered by ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato">AN 3.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that without kamma ripening in the form sphere, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato"><b>form-sphere existence</b></a> could not possibly occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato">AN 3.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the process where consciousness is established in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato"><b>middling realm</b></a> (the form realm) for beings fettered by craving, which results in the production of future existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato">AN 3.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if no kamma ripened in the formless sphere, then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato"><b>formless-sphere existence</b></a> would not be manifested? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato">AN 3.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when consciousness is established in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato"><b>sublime realm</b></a> (the formless realm) for beings hindered by ignorance, it leads to the production of renewed existence in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.76/en/sujato">AN 3.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda explained to the Buddha that one cannot say <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.78/en/sujato"><b>categorically</b></a> whether every precept, observance, or holy life is fruitful, as its value depends on the specific results it produces in the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.78/en/sujato">AN 3.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Ānanda, any religious observance or holy life is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.78/en/sujato"><b>unfruitful</b></a> if its practice causes unwholesome states to increase and wholesome states to decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.78/en/sujato">AN 3.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda taught that a precept, observance, or holy life is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.78/en/sujato"><b>fruitful</b></a> only if its practice leads to the decline of unwholesome states and the growth of wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.78/en/sujato">AN 3.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Ven. Ānanda explained his analytical view on the fruitfulness of spiritual practices, the Buddha expressed his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.78/en/sujato"><b>approval</b></a> of Ānanda’s answer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.78/en/sujato">AN 3.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Ven. Ānanda to the other monks, stating that even though Ānanda is still a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.78/en/sujato"><b>trainee</b></a> (sekha), it is difficult to find anyone who is his equal in wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.78/en/sujato">AN 3.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized Ven. Ānanda as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.78/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> that is rarely matched among those who have not yet completed their spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.78/en/sujato">AN 3.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the householder Anāthapiṇḍika that a noble disciple who provides food to others is essentially <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.58/en/sujato"><b>giving life</b></a> to those recipients? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.58/en/sujato">AN 4.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha's instructions to Anāthapiṇḍika, a person who gives food to others is also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.58/en/sujato"><b>bestowing beauty</b></a> upon them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.58/en/sujato">AN 4.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a layperson gives food, they are actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.58/en/sujato"><b>giving happiness</b></a> to the recipients, and as a result, the giver will partake in happiness themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.58/en/sujato">AN 4.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by giving food, a noble disciple is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.58/en/sujato"><b>giving strength</b></a> to the recipients, which leads the giver to eventually possess strength whether in a divine or human state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.58/en/sujato">AN 4.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a giver of food becomes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.58/en/sujato"><b>partaker in life</b></a>, beauty, happiness, and strength, regardless of whether they are reborn in a divine or human realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.58/en/sujato">AN 4.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed the bhikkhus that a donor who gives food bestows <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.59/en/sujato"><b>four things</b></a>—life, beauty, happiness, and strength—upon the recipients? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.59/en/sujato">AN 4.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said in verse that a person who gives food at the proper time to those who are self-controlled and rely on the gifts of others becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.59/en/sujato"><b>long-lived and famous</b></a> wherever they are reborn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.59/en/sujato">AN 4.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four specific qualities that constitute the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato"><b>proper practice for a householder</b></a>, which leads to the acquisition of fame and rebirth in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato">AN 4.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Anāthapiṇḍika that a noble disciple follows the proper path for a householder when they attend to the Saṅgha with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato"><b>robes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato">AN 4.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed providing the Saṅgha with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> as one of the four essential ways a householder practices the path that leads to heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato">AN 4.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a layperson is practicing correctly when they support the community of monks by providing them with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato"><b>lodgings</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato">AN 4.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a householder fulfills their proper practice by attending to the Saṅgha with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato"><b>medicines</b></a> and provisions for the sick? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato">AN 4.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that for wise householders who provide for virtuous practitioners, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato"><b>merit grows</b></a> constantly, both day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato">AN 4.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that after a householder performs the "auspicious deed" of supporting the Saṅgha with the four requisites, they proceed to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato"><b>heavenly place</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.60/en/sujato">AN 4.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wealth acquired by righteous means is the first of four things that are welcome, agreeable, and pleasant, yet <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>hard to obtain</b></a> in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, attaining fame and a good reputation along with one's relatives and teachers is a desirable goal that is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>difficult to acquire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a long life and the maintenance of longevity as the third of four things that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>pleasant and rare</b></a> to find in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the fourth thing hard to obtain in the world is rebirth in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> after the breaking up of the body and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the householder Anāthapiṇḍika that four specific accomplishments—faith, virtue, generosity, and wisdom—lead to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>attainment</b></a> of things that are otherwise hard to obtain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment of faith</b></a> as a noble disciple having confidence in the enlightenment of the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment of virtue</b></a> involves a noble disciple abstaining from killing, stealing, sexual misconduct, false speech, and the use of intoxicants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment of generosity</b></a> as dwelling at home with a mind free from the stain of stinginess, being open-handed and delighting in giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a noble disciple with the accomplishment of generosity is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>devoted to charity</b></a> and finds joy in distributing alms and being a person to whom others can go for help? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the mind is overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>unrighteous greed</b></a>, a person does what should not be done and neglects their own benefit and duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person whose mind is overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> will fail to do what is right and will consequently lose their reputation and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> as a defilement of the mind that causes a person to perform improper actions and fail in their responsibilities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> are mental defilements that a noble disciple of great wisdom must recognize and abandon to achieve mental clarity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> as a defilement of the mind that, when recognized and let go, allows a noble disciple to possess wide and great wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a noble disciple who has abandoned mental defilements as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>possessing wide wisdom</b></a> and who sees the truth clearly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>four rightful actions</b></a> a noble disciple performs with wealth earned through persistent effort and righteous means? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the first rightful use of wealth is to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>make oneself happy</b></a> and to also bring happiness to one's parents, wife, children, and friends? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended using wealth to provide <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>security against misfortunes</b></a> arising from fire, floods, kings, thieves, or unloved heirs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a householder should make a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>fivefold offering</b></a> using their wealth, which includes offerings to relatives, guests, ancestors, the king, and deities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the fourth rightful action for a noble disciple is to make offerings to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>recluses and brahmins</b></a> who are patient, self-controlled, and striving for peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wealth spent on anything other than the four rightful actions is considered wealth that has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>failed to reach</b></a> its proper destination or been used inappropriately? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said in verse that a wise person living at home who uses wealth for the four rightful actions is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato"><b>praised here</b></a> in this life and will rejoice in heaven after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.61/en/sujato">AN 4.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the householder Anāthapiṇḍika that there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato"><b>four kinds of happiness</b></a> that can be acquired by a layperson who enjoys sensual pleasures from time to time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato">AN 4.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato"><b>happiness of possession</b></a> as the joy a person feels when reflecting on wealth that has been earned through persistent effort, the strength of one’s own arms, and the sweat of one's brow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato">AN 4.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a householder experiences happiness when their wealth is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato"><b>righteously gained</b></a> and acquired through legitimate means? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato">AN 4.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato"><b>happiness of enjoyment</b></a> arises when a householder makes use of their righteously earned wealth to both sustain themselves and perform meritorious deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato">AN 4.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato"><b>happiness of debtlessness</b></a> as the mental satisfaction a person feels when they realize they do not owe any debt, whether small or great, to anyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato">AN 4.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato"><b>happiness of blamelessness</b></a> is experienced by a noble disciple who is endowed with faultless actions of body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato">AN 4.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically mentioned that a noble disciple finds joy in reflecting on being endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato"><b>blameless mental action</b></a>, which is considered the highest form of domestic happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato">AN 4.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the combined happiness of possession, enjoyment, and debtlessness is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato"><b>not worth one-sixteenth</b></a> of the happiness that comes from living a blameless life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato">AN 4.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered this teaching on the four types of happiness specifically to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato"><b>Anāthapiṇḍika</b></a>, one of his most prominent lay disciples and a wealthy provider for the needy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato">AN 4.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person who understands the joy of debtlessness and possession can eventually see with insight and realize that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato"><b>blamelessness</b></a> is far superior to any material wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.62/en/sujato">AN 4.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that families where the children honor their parents within the home are considered to be living <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.63/en/sujato"><b>with Brahma</b></a>, as "Brahma" is a designation for one's parents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.63/en/sujato">AN 4.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to parents as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.63/en/sujato"><b>early teachers</b></a> (<i>pubbācariya</i>) because they are the first to instruct and guide their children in life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.63/en/sujato">AN 4.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, parents are known as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.63/en/sujato"><b>early deities</b></a> (<i>pubbadevatā</i>) within the household because of the immense care and protection they provide to their children? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.63/en/sujato">AN 4.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated parents are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.63/en/sujato"><b>worthy of offerings</b></a> (<i>āhuneyya</i>) from their children because they have nourished them, brought them up, and introduced them to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.63/en/sujato">AN 4.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged children to show reverence to their parents through practical acts of service, such as providing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.63/en/sujato"><b>food and drink</b></a>, clothing, and even bathing or washing their feet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.63/en/sujato">AN 4.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person who serves their parents is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.63/en/sujato"><b>praised in this life</b></a> and experiences joy in a heavenly rebirth after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.63/en/sujato">AN 4.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.65/en/sujato"><b>four types of persons</b></a> in the world based on how they evaluate or "measure" others, including those who judge by outward form or by the sound of a person's reputation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.65/en/sujato">AN 4.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.65/en/sujato"><b>measured by form</b></a> as someone who places their trust and confidence in others based solely on their physical appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.65/en/sujato">AN 4.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that those who are governed by desire and lust and who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.65/en/sujato"><b>carried away by sound</b></a> or reputation fail to understand the true internal nature of a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.65/en/sujato">AN 4.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who truly understands both the internal and external reality, and who has removed mental hindrances, is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.65/en/sujato"><b>not misled</b></a> by mere sound or fame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.65/en/sujato">AN 4.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized individuals into four groups: those possessing lust, those possessing hatred, those possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.66/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a>, and those possessing conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.66/en/sujato">AN 4.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that people who are bound by desire and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.66/en/sujato"><b>shrouded in ignorance</b></a> are effectively blind and fail to see things as they really are, which only increases their bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.66/en/sujato">AN 4.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that performing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.66/en/sujato"><b>unskillful karma</b></a> born of lust, hatred, and delusion inevitably leads to a future of distress and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.66/en/sujato">AN 4.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha attributed the death of a monk from a snakebite to the fact that the monk had not suffused the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato"><b>four royal snake families</b></a> with a mind of loving-kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato">AN 4.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato"><b>Virūpakkhas</b></a>, the Erāpathas, the Chabyāputtas, and the Kaṇhāgotamakas as the four royal lineages of snakes that should be met with loving-kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato">AN 4.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha authorized the use of a specific protective chant (paritta) for the purposes of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato"><b>self-protection</b></a> and self-guarding against dangerous creatures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato">AN 4.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a verse of loving-kindness that encompasses all beings, including those that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato"><b>footless</b></a>, two-footed, four-footed, and even many-footed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato">AN 4.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers to maintain a universal aspiration that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato"><b>all beings</b></a>, all breathing things, and all creatures should see what is good and that no evil should come to anyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato">AN 4.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the qualities of the Buddha, Dhamma, and Saṅgha as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato"><b>measureless</b></a>, in stark contrast to the finite and "measurable" nature of creeping creatures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato">AN 4.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed several types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato"><b>creeping things</b></a>, such as scorpions, centipedes, spiders, lizards, and rats, as being limited in comparison to the infinite qualities of the Triple Gem? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato">AN 4.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once a practitioner has made a protection and expressed loving-kindness, they should request that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato"><b>beings depart</b></a> and not cause harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato">AN 4.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s protective instruction concludes with an expression of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato"><b>homage</b></a> to the Blessed One and to the seven Perfectly Enlightened Ones (Sammāsambuddhas)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato">AN 4.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that if a bhikkhu suffuses the four royal snake families with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a>, he would not die even if he were to be bitten by a snake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.67/en/sujato">AN 4.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are four specific qualities of a person—virtue, purity, strength, and wisdom—that can only be truly known through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>four specific circumstances</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> can only be truly discerned by living together with them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one cannot judge someone's character in a short time, as their qualities must be observed over a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>long period</b></a> and not cursorily? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that to correctly assess another person's virtue or wisdom, the observer must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>attentive</b></a> rather than inattentive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that only a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a>, and not one who is dull-witted, is capable of accurately knowing the true nature of another's qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person lacking virtue as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>inconsistent</b></a>, breaking or spotting their conduct over a long duration rather than practicing continuously? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>purity</b></a> of character is to be known through dealing with them, particularly in social or business interactions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified an impure person as someone who speaks one way in private but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>communicates differently</b></a> when they are with two, three, or many people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a person of pure communication as one who remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>consistent in speech</b></a> regardless of whether they are alone with one person or in a large group? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person’s true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>strength</b></a> of character is only truly revealed when they are faced with adversities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>adversity</b></a> as including the loss of one's relatives, the loss of wealth, or the loss of health through illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a person lacking internal strength will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>sorrow and lament</b></a>, beating their breast and becoming confused when struck by misfortune? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person of strength as one who reflects on how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>eight worldly conditions</b></a> revolve around the world, thus remaining untroubled by loss? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>eight worldly conditions</b></a> that affect all beings as gain and loss, fame and disrepute, praise and blame, and pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> can be clearly discerned through the process of discussion and conversation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared judging a person of little wisdom to a man watching a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>small fish</b></a> surfacing in a pond, noting the minor disturbance it makes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a wise person can be recognized by their ability to discuss <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>deep subjects</b></a> that are peaceful, sublime, and beyond the sphere of mere reasoning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person with wisdom is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>capable of explaining</b></a> the Dhamma in detail, making the meaning clear and easy to understand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>large fish</b></a> making a great furrow and splash in a pond to illustrate how a wise person's depth is revealed through their questions and conversation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a person lacking wisdom is unable to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>articulate the meaning</b></a> of the teachings, whether in brief or in detail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>manner of approach</b></a> to a problem and the way one formulates a question are indicators of whether one is wise or dull-witted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that to truly understand another's wisdom, one must observe their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato"><b>intellectual behavior</b></a> over a long time and with careful attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.192/en/sujato">AN 4.192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was once questioned by Bhaddiya the Licchavi about a rumor claiming the Buddha was a sorcerer who used <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>converting magic</b></a> to win over the disciples of other sects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised Bhaddiya not to accept a teaching merely because of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>oral tradition</b></a>, lineage, hearsay, or because it is found in a collection of scriptures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a person should not rely on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>logical reasoning</b></a>, inferred hints, or a partiality for a view they have pondered when deciding which spiritual path to follow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should only abandon certain qualities when they know for themselves that those qualities are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome</b></a>, blameworthy, and censured by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>greed</b></a> arises within a person, it leads to their own harm and long-term suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a person overcome by greed is driven to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>kill living beings</b></a>, take what is not given, commit adultery, and tell lies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> as a state that, when it overwhelms a person's mind, leads them to act in ways that cause harm to themselves and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> as an unwholesome quality that causes a person to engage in misconduct through body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>aggressive obstinacy</b></a> is a dangerous mental state that leads a person to encourage others to engage in harmful behaviors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged Bhaddiya to accept and practice qualities that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>praised by the wise</b></a> and lead to welfare and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>non-greed</b></a> arises for a person's benefit, ensuring they do not steal or commit sexual misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person free from hatred does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>harm living beings</b></a> or speak falsehoods, resulting in their long-term happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>non-delusion</b></a> as a wholesome state that leads a person to avoid blameworthy actions and live for the welfare of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>non-aggressiveness</b></a> as a quality that leads to welfare and happiness by preventing a person from being driven to harm others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>good people</b></a> (sappurisā) in the world actively encourage others to dwell having removed greed, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified to Bhaddiya that he never actually asked people to be his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>disciples</b></a>, but rather taught them how to abandon unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the teaching, Bhaddiya the Licchavi declared the Buddha’s "converting magic" to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>excellent and beautiful</b></a> because it leads to the removal of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhaddiya the Licchavi wished that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>beloved kinsmen</b></a> and relatives could also be "converted" by the Buddha’s teaching for their long-term welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>khattiyas</b></a> (the warrior-noble class) practiced to abandon unwholesome qualities, it would lead to their lasting happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained his teaching would benefit people of all social classes—including <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>brahmins, vessas, and suddas</b></a>—if they used it to discard unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>entire world</b></a>, including its devas, Maras, and Brahmas, would experience long-term welfare if they were "converted" to abandon greed and hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato"><b>great sal trees</b></a> would benefit if they could be "converted" to understand the difference between unwholesome and wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.193/en/sujato">AN 4.193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that the Buddha, the Perfectly Enlightened One, declared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>four factors of striving for purification</b></a> for the purpose of purifying beings and overcoming sorrow and lamentation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Ānanda, the ultimate goal of practicing the four factors of striving is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>realization of Nibbāna</b></a> and the destruction of pain and grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>purification of virtue</b></a> as the first factor of striving, which involves being virtuous, restrained by the Pātimokkha, and training in the rules of discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>purification of mind</b></a> as the second factor of striving, specifically referring to the attainment and dwelling in the four jhānas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>purification of view</b></a> consists of understanding the Four Noble Truths—suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path leading to its cessation—exactly as they are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the fourth factor of striving described by Venerable Ānanda is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>purification of liberation</b></a>, which involves detaching and freeing the mind to touch right liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Ānanda spoke to the Koliyans at the town of Sāmuga, he addressed them by the clan name <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>Byagghapajjas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that striving for purification requires a specific set of mental qualities, including <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>desire and effort</b></a>, to complete what is unfulfilled in one's virtue or meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda emphasized that for each factor of purification, a practitioner must apply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a> to support and protect the progress they have made? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the four factors of striving are essential for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>attainment of the true way</b></a> (ñāya), leading a practitioner directly toward spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>purification of liberation</b></a>, Venerable Ānanda explained that a noble disciple must first be endowed with purified virtue, mind, and view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one must possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>persistent effort</b></a> (appaṭivānī) and exertion to ensure that their purification of view is maintained and perfected? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda described the noble disciple as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>detaches his mind</b></a> from things that incite lust (rajanīyesu dhammesu) as part of the striving for liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is striving for purification must use <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> to assist in maintaining the levels of virtue and mental clarity they have already achieved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>Perfectly Enlightened One</b></a> as the source of the teaching on the four factors of striving, describing him as "the one who knows and sees"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>purification of virtue</b></a> should see danger even in the slightest faults and train scrupulously in the rules of discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>right liberation</b></a> is "touched" or experienced once the mind has been successfully freed from things that should be liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on the factors of striving includes the need for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato"><b>unflagging persistence</b></a> and endeavor to reach the final goal of ending all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.194/en/sujato">AN 4.194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vappa the Sakyan, a prominent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>disciple of the Nigaṇṭhas</b></a> (Jains), was initially questioned by Venerable Mahāmoggallāna about whether a fully restrained person could still be affected by taints in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna asked Vappa if a person who is restrained in body, speech, and mind—and has attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge</b></a>—would still have taints flow in that cause future suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vappa the Sakyan told Venerable Mahāmoggallāna that he believed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>past bad kamma</b></a> that has not yet ripened would cause taints and suffering to flow into a person in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proposed a method of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>civilized discussion</b></a> to Vappa, where they would agree on what is agreeable, reject what is rejectable, and ask for further clarification on the meaning of statements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person who abstains from harmful <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>bodily activities</b></a>, the taints, distress, and fever that normally arise from such activities do not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner of restraint creates no new kamma and wears away <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>old kamma</b></a> through repeated contact, leading to its total exhaustion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the process of wearing away old kamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>visible here and now</b></a>, timeless, and inviting inspection by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>ignorance fades</b></a> and true knowledge arises, the taints, distress, and fever born of ignorance no longer affect the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by abstaining from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>mental activities</b></a> that lead to taints, one prevents the arising of future suffering and distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk whose mind is rightly liberated has attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>six constant abidings</b></a>, allowing him to remain equanimous toward all sensory experiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a liberated person as being neither glad nor sad when seeing a form with the eye, but instead staying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>equanimous</b></a>, mindful, and clearly comprehending? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk with a rightly liberated mind maintains the same <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>state of equanimity</b></a> whether he hears a sound, smells an odor, tastes a flavor, or feels a tactile object? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a liberated person remains mindful and unperturbed even when cognizing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> with the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who has achieved liberation understands when he is feeling a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>feeling limited to the body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a liberated monk clearly perceives when he is experiencing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>feeling limited to life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that at the breakup of the body and the end of life, all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>unrelished feelings</b></a> will become cool right then and there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a shadow appearing because of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>pillar</b></a> to illustrate how taints depend on specific causes for their existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the total destruction of taints to a man who chops down a pillar, burns it to ashes, and scatters them in a strong wind so the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>shadow never reappears</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vappa the Sakyan compared his time spent serving the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>foolish Nigaṇṭhas</b></a> to a merchant who seeks profit but only ends up with weariness and distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vappa the Sakyan declared that he would "winnow" his old <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato"><b>confidence in the Nigaṇṭhas</b></a> like chaff in a strong wind because of the clarity of the Buddha's teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.195/en/sujato">AN 4.195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are intent on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>self-mortification</b></a> (asceticism) as their primary spiritual practice are incapable of "crossing the flood" of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>purification of virtue</b></a> as only one factor of the ascetic life, rather than the complete path to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>impure conduct</b></a> of body, speech, mind, and livelihood is incapable of attaining supreme enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a man trying to cross a river with a tree trunk that was polished on the outside but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>uncleaned on the inside</b></a> to illustrate why superficial religious practices lead to disaster? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a practitioner can only successfully cross the flood of suffering by ensuring their practice is like a boat that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>well-cleaned inside</b></a> and equipped with a rudder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a noble disciple to a royal warrior who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>long-distance shooter</b></a> because they see all five aggregates as "not mine, not I, and not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened a noble disciple with right view to a warrior who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>marksman</b></a>, because they understand the Four Noble Truths exactly as they are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a noble disciple with right liberation as a warrior who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>pierces a great mass</b></a>, specifically referring to the destruction of the mass of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>right wisdom</b></a> involves seeing any form—whether past, future, present, internal, or external—as "not mine, not I, and not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one must view all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>feelings</b></a>, whether they are gross or subtle, inferior or superior, as not being one's self to reach liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a>, regardless of whether it is near or far, must be viewed through right wisdom as "this is not mine, this I am not"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a noble disciple should regard all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> as not being their self to achieve spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> in all its forms—past, present, or future—is not to be identified as one's self if one wishes to be liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a person as being capable of knowledge and vision when they possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>purified conduct</b></a> of body, speech, mind, and livelihood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as a boat with a rudder allows a man to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>reach the far shore</b></a> safely, purified spiritual conduct leads to the safe attainment of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> is the specific quality that allows a disciple to understand the path leading to the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>supreme enlightenment</b></a> is impossible for those whose mental, verbal, and physical behaviors remain unpurified? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that identifying with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> is a major barrier that must be overcome through the application of right wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> as a "great mass" that a noble disciple must pierce through the attainment of right liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.196/en/sujato">AN 4.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato"><b>contemplating foulness</b></a> in the body is one of five specific qualities which, when frequently cultivated, lead to the fruits and benefits of liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato">AN 5.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato"><b>perception of repulsiveness</b></a> in food as a necessary mental training for a monk to achieve a state of spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato">AN 5.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that developing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-delight</b></a> in the entire world is a vital practice for reaching the goal of final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato">AN 5.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who dwells <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato"><b>contemplating impermanence</b></a> in all conditioned things is practicing one of the five essential factors for attaining the fruit of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato">AN 5.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of death</b></a> well established internally is a fundamental requirement for a practitioner to gain the fruit of liberation of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato">AN 5.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of "one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato"><b>lifted the crossbar</b></a>" to describe a monk who has successfully abandoned all ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato">AN 5.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who has completely abandoned the wandering in births that leads to renewed existence as "one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato"><b>filled the moat</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato">AN 5.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to a monk who has eradicated craving as "one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato"><b>pulled up the pillar</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato">AN 5.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato"><b>unbolted</b></a> to characterize a practitioner who has abandoned the five lower fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato">AN 5.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk becomes a "noble one whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato"><b>banner is lowered</b></a>" and whose burden is dropped once they have abandoned the conceit "I am"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.71/en/sujato">AN 5.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between a person who merely learns the teachings and a true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato"><b>dweller in the Dhamma</b></a>, noting that the latter is someone who does not neglect internal serenity of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato">AN 5.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who spends his entire day teaching the Dhamma in detail to others but neglects seclusion and internal serenity is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato"><b>intent on teaching</b></a> rather than a true dweller in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato">AN 5.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that a practitioner who focuses excessively on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato"><b>reciting the Dhamma</b></a> at the expense of mental composure and solitude is not yet considered a "dweller in the Dhamma"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato">AN 5.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if a monk spends his time thinking about and examining the Dhamma in his mind, he is merely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato"><b>intent on thinking</b></a> if he fails to cultivate internal serenity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato">AN 5.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk becomes a "dweller in the Dhamma" only when he learns the teachings but still devotes himself to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato"><b>internal serenity of mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato">AN 5.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that one can master all branches of the teachings—including suttas, verses, and marvels—but still fail to be a "dweller in the Dhamma" if they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato"><b>neglect seclusion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato">AN 5.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to seek out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato"><b>tree roots</b></a> or empty huts for meditation, reminding them that this is the essential practice a compassionate teacher provides for his disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato">AN 5.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned his monks to meditate diligently and not be negligent so that they would not have to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato"><b>filled with regret</b></a> later in life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato">AN 5.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed a nine-fold division of the teachings, including <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato"><b>marvelous teachings</b></a> and expositions, to show that while study is important, it should not replace the practice of meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato">AN 5.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a "dweller in the Dhamma" as a monk who learns the teachings but does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato"><b>not spend the day</b></a> exclusively in study, ensuring he prioritizes internal mental calm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato">AN 5.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who masters the Dhamma but fails to practice meditation as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato"><b>intent on study</b></a> rather than actually living in accordance with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.73/en/sujato">AN 5.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a practitioner becomes a true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato"><b>dweller in the Dhamma</b></a> only when they not only learn the various teachings like suttas and verses but also understand their meaning through wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato">AN 5.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who masters the nine-fold division of the teachings—including suttas, songs, and expositions—but fails to penetrate their meaning with wisdom as being merely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato"><b>intent on study</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato">AN 5.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that a monk who teaches the Dhamma in detail to others as he has heard and learned it, yet lacks a wisdom-based understanding of the meaning, is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato"><b>intent on teaching</b></a> rather than a dweller in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato">AN 5.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized a monk who spends his time reciting the teachings in detail as he has learned them, without understanding the meaning with wisdom, as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato"><b>intent on recitation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato">AN 5.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even a practitioner who spends their time pondering, examining, and mentally inspecting the Dhamma is only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato"><b>intent on thinking</b></a> if they still do not understand the meaning with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato">AN 5.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the crucial difference between someone merely preoccupied with academic study and a true "dweller in the Dhamma" is whether the person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato"><b>understands the meaning</b></a> of the teachings with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato">AN 5.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to find seclusion at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato"><b>tree roots</b></a> or in empty huts to practice meditation, describing this as his compassionate instruction for their welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato">AN 5.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned his disciples to be diligent and practice meditation so that they would not have to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato"><b>filled with regret</b></a> later in their lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato">AN 5.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato"><b>marvelous teachings</b></a> and questions and answers as part of the nine-fold Dhamma that a monk might learn while still failing to be a true "dweller in the Dhamma" if he lacks wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato">AN 5.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that his exhortation to avoid negligence and to meditate in quiet places was an act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> for his disciples, representing the duty of a teacher who seeks his students' welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.74/en/sujato">AN 5.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>five types of warriors</b></a> who exist in the world as a metaphor for the different types of people found among the community of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a warrior who loses heart and is unable to enter battle simply by seeing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>cloud of dust</b></a> raised by the approaching enemy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who gives up the training and returns to the lay life after merely hearing about a beautiful woman to a warrior who falters at the sight of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>approaching dust</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a warrior who can withstand the sight of dust but collapses in fear upon seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>enemy's banner</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened a monk who maintains his resolve while hearing about women, but abandons his vows upon actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>seeing a woman</b></a>, to a warrior who is defeated by the sight of a battle flag? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a third type of warrior who can endure the sight of dust and banners but becomes paralyzed upon hearing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>shouting</b></a> of the enemy forces? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who is approached by a woman in the forest who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>mocks and flirts</b></a> with him, causing him to abandon the spiritual life, to a warrior who cannot endure the sounds of battle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is physically overcome by a woman in seclusion and engages in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>sexual intercourse</b></a> is like a warrior who enters the clash of battle but is struck and wounded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>struck and wounded</b></a> in battle to describe a monk who fails to give up the training before violating his celibacy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the warrior who triumphs in battle and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>occupies the summit</b></a> of the field as the highest of the five types of combatants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "victorious" monk as one who, when physically approached by a woman in seclusion, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>extricates himself</b></a> and departs to continue his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk seeking final victory should resort to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>solitary lodging</b></a>, such as a forest, a mountain cave, or a cemetery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner must purify the mind by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>abandoning the five hindrances</b></a>, which include sensual desire, ill will, and sloth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the process of cleansing the heart of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> as dwelling with a mind of compassion for the welfare of all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who overcomes the five hindrances can enter and dwell in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a>, which are states of deep meditative concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk with a concentrated and purified mind directs it toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> to achieve final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk truly understands reality when he knows the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a>: suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three specific types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a>—the taint of sensual desire, the taint of existence, and the taint of ignorance—that must be removed for spiritual freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that when a monk's mind is liberated, the monk possesses the knowledge that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a> and the spiritual life has been lived to its completion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a warrior who has "won the battle" to describe a monk who has attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who conquers his internal defilements is like a warrior who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>vanquished the enemy</b></a> and occupies the seat of victory? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's comparison of monks to warriors was intended to highlight the courage and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato"><b>perseverance</b></a> required to sustain the spiritual life in the face of worldly temptations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.75/en/sujato">AN 5.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>five types of warriors</b></a> in the world to serve as a comparison for the different types of individuals found among the community of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who violates his vow of celibacy to a warrior who enters battle only to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>killed and finished off</b></a> by the enemy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that entering a village for alms with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>unguarded senses</b></a> and a lack of mindfulness can lead a practitioner to be overcome by lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened a monk who gives up his training on the road back to the monastery to a wounded warrior who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>dies on the way</b></a> to see his relatives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the intense psychological struggle of a monk overcome by sensual desire as a state where he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>burning in body</b></a> and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who succumbs to lust and leaves the order despite the care and advice of his fellow monks is like a warrior who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>dies from his injury</b></a> even after being brought home for treatment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the monk who listens to the guidance of his companions and resolves to stay in the holy life, comparing him to a warrior who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>recovers from his illness</b></a> after being wounded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>skeleton</b></a> to describe sensual pleasures, emphasizing that they offer very little gratification and are full of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared sensual pleasures to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>piece of meat</b></a> that leads to conflict and despair among those who fight over it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened the danger of sensual pleasures to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>torch of grass</b></a> carried against the wind, which threatens to burn the person holding it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described seeking sensual gratification as being as dangerous and painful as falling into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>pit of glowing coals</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the temporary and illusory nature of sensual pleasures to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>dream</b></a> that vanishes upon waking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that sensual pleasures are like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>borrowed goods</b></a> which must eventually be returned, leaving the possessor with nothing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the risks of pursuing sensual pleasures to a person climbing a tree for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>fruit</b></a> only to have the tree cut down beneath them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the graphic image of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>butcher's knife and block</b></a> to represent the inherent violence and pain found in sensual desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the piercing nature of sensual pleasures to being impaled by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>sword stake</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened sensual pleasures to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>snake’s head</b></a> to illustrate the lethal danger they pose to one's spiritual well-being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the most successful monk as the one who wins the battle against defilements and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>occupies the summit</b></a> of the battlefield? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who guards his senses is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>not a grasper of signs</b></a>, meaning he does not let his mind be led away by the outward appearance of things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>restraint of the mind faculty</b></a> is crucial to prevent unwholesome states like covetousness and dejection from arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to seek out a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>secluded lodging</b></a>, such as a forest or an empty hut, to deepen their meditation practice after returning from alms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by sitting in meditation with an upright body, a monk can work to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>abandon the five hindrances</b></a> that obstruct wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who attains the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a> develops a mind that is pure, bright, and ready for higher knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the goal of the spiritual life as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>knowledge of the destruction of the taints</b></a>, where one fully understands the nature of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a liberated monk knows for certain that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a> and there is no further rebirth for him in any state of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened a monk who has destroyed all defilements to a warrior who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>won the battle</b></a> and remains as the victor on the field? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk truly fulfills his purpose when he can say that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>holy life has been lived</b></a> and what had to be done has been done? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who achieves final liberation as one who understands reality <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>as it really is</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato"><b>swords and shields</b></a> to describe how warriors prepare for battle, just as monks must prepare themselves with virtue and mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.76/en/sujato">AN 5.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while he was still a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>Bodhisatta</b></a> intent on awakening, experienced five great dreams that heralded his future enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha dreamt the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>great earth</b></a> was his bed and the Himalayas, the king of mountains, served as his pillow before he attained Buddhahood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that his dream of lying across the earth with his hands and feet in the three oceans signified his attainment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>supreme enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his enlightenment, the Buddha dreamt of a woody creeper called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>tiriyā grass</b></a> growing from his navel and reaching up to the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that his dream of a plant growing from his navel to the sky symbolized that he would fully awaken to and proclaim the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> to gods and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once dreamt of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>white worms</b></a> with black heads crawling up from his feet and covering his legs as far as the knees? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha interpreted the dream of white worms crawling up his legs as representing many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>white-clothed laypeople</b></a> who would take refuge in the Tathāgata for life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described a dream where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>four birds</b></a> of various colors came from four directions, fell at his feet, and then became entirely white? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the four colored birds turning white at his feet represented members of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>four castes</b></a> going forth into homelessness and realizing supreme liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his enlightenment, the Buddha dreamt he was walking back and forth on top of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>mountain of dung</b></a> without being soiled by it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha interpreted his dream of walking unsoiled on dung as his ability to receive and use <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>requisites</b></a> like robes and almsfood without being attached or infatuated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>wisdom of escape</b></a> to describe the mindful way he consumes offerings like robes and medicine while seeing the danger in them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Buddha's first great dream, his left hand rested in the eastern ocean and his right hand in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>western ocean</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Buddha's first great dream, both of his feet rested in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>southern ocean</b></a> while he lay upon the earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that those who go forth in the Dhamma and Vinaya, regardless of being khattiyas, brahmins, vessas, or suddas, can achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>supreme liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's dream of walking upon a mountain of dung symbolized his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>uninfatuated</b></a> use of almsfood, lodgings, and other medicinal requisites? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified the specific plant in his second dream as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>tiriyā grass</b></a>, which grew from his navel to touch the clouds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned the five dreams occurred specifically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>before his enlightenment</b></a> while he was still an unawakened Bodhisatta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha's dream involving white worms signified a future with many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>lay disciples</b></a> who would go to him for refuge until the end of their lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato"><b>Mahāsupinasutta</b></a> to explain the profound symbolic meanings behind the five great visions he saw before his awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.196/en/sujato">AN 5.196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for speech to be well-spoken and blameless, it must be spoken at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.198/en/sujato"><b>right time</b></a> rather than the wrong time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.198/en/sujato">AN 5.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.198/en/sujato"><b>true</b></a>, rather than false, as one of the five essential factors of well-spoken speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.198/en/sujato">AN 5.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that speech which is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.198/en/sujato"><b>gentle</b></a>, as opposed to harsh, is considered beyond reproach by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.198/en/sujato">AN 5.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that well-spoken speech must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.198/en/sujato"><b>connected with the goal</b></a> and beneficial to the listener? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.198/en/sujato">AN 5.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that speech is only truly well-spoken when it is motivated by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.198/en/sujato"><b>mind of loving-kindness</b></a> rather than by hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.198/en/sujato">AN 5.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when laypeople experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.199/en/sujato"><b>clarity of mind</b></a> upon seeing virtuous monastics, they are practicing a way that leads to heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.199/en/sujato">AN 5.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when a family shows respect by rising and greeting virtuous monastics, they are engaging in a practice that leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.199/en/sujato"><b>birth in a high family</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.199/en/sujato">AN 5.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a family that removes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.199/en/sujato"><b>stain of stinginess</b></a> when monastics visit is following a path that leads to great influence and preeminence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.199/en/sujato">AN 5.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that when laypeople share their resources with monastics according to their ability, they are on a path that leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.199/en/sujato"><b>great wealth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.199/en/sujato">AN 5.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that when a family asks questions and listens to the Dhamma from visiting monastics, they are practicing a path that leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.199/en/sujato"><b>great wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.199/en/sujato">AN 5.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Kimila asked why the teaching does not last long after the Buddha's passing, the Buddha said it is because followers live without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato"><b>respect for the Teacher</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato">AN 5.201</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that the True Dhamma will not endure if the community of monks, nuns, and lay followers lives without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato"><b>respect for the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato">AN 5.201</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato"><b>respect for the Saṅgha</b></a> as a cause for the disappearance of his teachings after his final Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato">AN 5.201</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the teaching will decline if monastics and lay followers live without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato"><b>respect for the training</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato">AN 5.201</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the True Dhamma fails to last when members of the fourfold assembly live without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato"><b>mutual respect</b></a> and deference for one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato">AN 5.201</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha told Venerable Kimila that the True Dhamma will remain for a long time if monks, nuns, and laypeople live with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato"><b>respect for the Teacher</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato">AN 5.201</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the longevity of the True Dhamma is ensured when the community lives with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato"><b>respect for the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato">AN 5.201</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the True Dhamma will be long-lasting if his followers maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato"><b>respect for the Saṅgha</b></a> after his passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato">AN 5.201</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the teaching is preserved when monastics and lay followers live with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato"><b>respect for the training</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato">AN 5.201</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that for the True Dhamma to endure, it is necessary for his disciples to live with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato"><b>mutual respect</b></a> and deference toward each other? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.201/en/sujato">AN 5.201</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the five benefits of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.202/en/sujato"><b>listening to the Dhamma</b></a> is that a person hears what they have not heard before? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.202/en/sujato">AN 5.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.202/en/sujato"><b>clarifying what has been heard</b></a> is a specific spiritual benefit gained when a person actively listens to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.202/en/sujato">AN 5.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.202/en/sujato"><b>dispelling of doubt</b></a> as a key advantage for those who dedicate themselves to listening to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.202/en/sujato">AN 5.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.202/en/sujato"><b>straightening one's view</b></a> is a significant benefit attained through the practice of listening to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.202/en/sujato">AN 5.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person listens to the Dhamma, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.202/en/sujato"><b>mind becomes serene</b></a> and confident? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.202/en/sujato">AN 5.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.206/en/sujato"><b>not free from lust</b></a> for sensual pleasures as a "mental shackle" that prevents the mind from inclining toward spiritual striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.206/en/sujato">AN 5.206</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is not free from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.206/en/sujato"><b>lust for the body</b></a> possesses a mental shackle that hinders his devotion to ardor and perseverance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.206/en/sujato">AN 5.206</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.206/en/sujato"><b>lust for forms</b></a> as one of the five mental shackles that keep a person from applying themselves to the spiritual goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.206/en/sujato">AN 5.206</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who eats until their stomach is bulging and then indulges in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.206/en/sujato"><b>pleasure of sleep</b></a> is bound by a mental shackle that blocks spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.206/en/sujato">AN 5.206</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that leading the spiritual life with the specific aspiration to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.206/en/sujato"><b>reborn as a deva</b></a> is actually a mental shackle that prevents true spiritual ardor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.206/en/sujato">AN 5.206</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that reciting the Dhamma with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.209/en/sujato"><b>singing intonation</b></a> causes the reciter to become attached to the sound of their own voice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.209/en/sujato">AN 5.209</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that householders may criticize monks who chant with a long musical tone, complaining that they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.209/en/sujato"><b>sing like laypeople</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.209/en/sujato">AN 5.209</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person who enjoys the musical sound of their own voice while chanting, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.209/en/sujato"><b>concentration is broken</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.209/en/sujato">AN 5.209</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a teacher chants the Dhamma with a singing intonation, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.209/en/sujato"><b>future generations</b></a> will follow that example as a bad precedent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.209/en/sujato">AN 5.209</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that falling asleep with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.210/en/sujato"><b>muddled mindfulness</b></a> and no clear comprehension leads to sleeping poorly and waking up in distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.210/en/sujato">AN 5.210</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.210/en/sujato"><b>deities do not protect</b></a> a person who falls asleep while being unmindful and unaware of their state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.210/en/sujato">AN 5.210</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.210/en/sujato"><b>sleeping soundly</b></a> is one of the five benefits for a person who falls asleep with mindfulness established and clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.210/en/sujato">AN 5.210</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.210/en/sujato"><b>waking up happily</b></a> and without bad dreams are rewards for those who maintain mindfulness when they go to sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.210/en/sujato">AN 5.210</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an immoral person who has failed in virtue suffers a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato"><b>great loss of wealth</b></a> due to their negligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato">AN 5.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato"><b>bad reputation</b></a> is one of the five primary dangers for a person who lacks moral virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato">AN 5.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that an immoral person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato"><b>lacks confidence</b></a> and feels timid whenever they approach an assembly of nobles, brahmins, householders, or monastics? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato">AN 5.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the grave dangers of failing in virtue is that a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato"><b>dies confused</b></a> rather than with a clear mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato">AN 5.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a person who is immoral and has failed in virtue is reborn in a state of misery or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a> after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato">AN 5.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that a virtuous person, through diligence, acquires a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato"><b>great mass of wealth</b></a> as a primary benefit of their moral conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato">AN 5.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato"><b>good reputation</b></a> naturally spreads about a person who is accomplished in and committed to moral virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato">AN 5.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a virtuous person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato"><b>approaches assemblies</b></a>—whether of nobles, brahmins, householders, or monastics—with full confidence and without any sense of confusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato">AN 5.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato"><b>dying unconfused</b></a> as one of the five specific spiritual benefits for a person who is well-established in moral virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato">AN 5.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha promised that a person who is accomplished in virtue is reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> after the death of the body and the breakup of its form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.213/en/sujato">AN 5.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato"><b>talkative person</b></a> is highly prone to telling lies, which is one of the five dangers of excessive speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato">AN 5.214</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the tendency to use <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a> as a common danger for those who speak excessively and without restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato">AN 5.214</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that people who are overly talkative often resort to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a>, which is a negative trait that leads to spiritual harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato">AN 5.214</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a> as one of the inevitable negative outcomes for a person who talks too much? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato">AN 5.214</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a talkative person who indulges in wrong speech is reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato"><b>state of misery</b></a> or hell after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato">AN 5.214</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a person who speaks in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato"><b>measured way</b></a> avoids telling lies, which is a significant benefit of practicing controlled speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato">AN 5.214</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that avoiding both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato"><b>divisive and harsh speech</b></a> are key benefits gained by those who speak only what is necessary and in a measured manner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato">AN 5.214</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a person who practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato"><b>measured speech</b></a> and avoids idle chatter is reborn in a happy destination or a heavenly world after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.214/en/sujato">AN 5.214</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.215/en/sujato"><b>impatient person</b></a> is disliked and disagreeable to many people and is prone to having many enemies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.215/en/sujato">AN 5.215</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.215/en/sujato"><b>liked and agreeable</b></a> to many people as a primary benefit for those who practice patience and restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.215/en/sujato">AN 5.215</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the grave spiritual dangers of impatience is that it leads a person to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.215/en/sujato"><b>die confused</b></a> rather than with a clear and mindful state of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.215/en/sujato">AN 5.215</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that an impatient person is often <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.216/en/sujato"><b>cruel and full of remorse</b></a>, which are mental states that hinder spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.216/en/sujato">AN 5.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.216/en/sujato"><b>patience</b></a> leads to a life where one is not cruel and remains free from the burden of remorse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.216/en/sujato">AN 5.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that a person who is established in patience is reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.216/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> after the death of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.216/en/sujato">AN 5.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.217/en/sujato"><b>uninspiring person</b></a> suffers the danger of self-reproach and being censured by the wise who have investigated their conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.217/en/sujato">AN 5.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.217/en/sujato"><b>inspiring person</b></a> earns a good reputation that spreads far and wide, in addition to being praised by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.217/en/sujato">AN 5.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.217/en/sujato"><b>dying unconfused</b></a> and attaining a happy destination as the final benefits for a person who lives in an inspiring way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.217/en/sujato">AN 5.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that when a monastic is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.218/en/sujato"><b>uninspiring</b></a>, those who lack confidence do not gain it, and the confidence of some who already have it may change for the worse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.218/en/sujato">AN 5.218</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.218/en/sujato"><b>Teacher’s instruction</b></a> is considered "not carried out" when a follower fails to behave in a way that inspires confidence in others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.218/en/sujato">AN 5.218</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.218/en/sujato"><b>inspiring</b></a> person effectively carries out the Teacher's instruction by helping those who lack confidence to gain it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.218/en/sujato">AN 5.218</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that being an inspiring person ensures that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.218/en/sujato"><b>future generations</b></a> will follow a good example rather than a bad one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.218/en/sujato">AN 5.218</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mind of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.218/en/sujato"><b>inspiring person</b></a> becomes serene and clear as a result of their virtuous and confident behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.218/en/sujato">AN 5.218</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda once asked the Buddha if there is any type of fragrance that travels both with the wind and against the wind, unlike <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato"><b>common fragrances</b></a> derived from roots, heartwood, or flowers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato">AN 3.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the fragrances of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato"><b>root, heartwood, and jasmine</b></a> only travel with the wind and are incapable of spreading against it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato">AN 3.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the fragrance of a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato"><b>taken refuge</b></a> in the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Saṅgha is a quality that spreads through all directions, even against the wind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato">AN 3.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, observing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato"><b>five precepts</b></a>—abstaining from killing, stealing, sexual misconduct, false speech, and intoxicants—creates a "fragrance" of virtue that travels in every direction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato">AN 3.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who dwells at home with a mind free from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato"><b>stain of stinginess</b></a> as possessing a spiritual fragrance that wafts both with and against the wind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato">AN 3.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato"><b>open-handed</b></a> to describe a virtuous person whose reputation and good character spread through the world like a fragrance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato">AN 3.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person is virtuous and generous, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato"><b>ascetics and brahmins</b></a> in all directions will speak their praise, effectively spreading their "fragrance" everywhere? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato">AN 3.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato"><b>deities</b></a> speak the praise of a virtuous man or woman, contributing to a fragrance of character that travels to every quarter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato">AN 3.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the quality of one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato"><b>delights in relinquishment</b></a> as a component of the unique fragrance that can travel against the direction of the wind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato">AN 3.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted natural scents like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato"><b>sandalwood and tagara</b></a> with the "fragrance of the good," noting that only the latter can pervade all directions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato">AN 3.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato"><b>virtuous person</b></a> as someone who is devoted to charity and finds joy in sharing, which creates a reputation that spreads regardless of the wind's direction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato">AN 3.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that while the scent of flowers does not go against the wind, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato"><b>fragrance of the good</b></a> travels to every direction because a good person "pervades all quarters"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.79/en/sujato">AN 3.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when kings are unrighteous, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato"><b>royal officials</b></a> also become unrighteous, creating a cascade effect that eventually leads to the unrighteousness of the entire population? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato">AN 4.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the widespread unrighteousness of town and country people causes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato"><b>moon and sun</b></a> to course unevenly in the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato">AN 4.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when the sun and moon course unevenly, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato"><b>constellations and stars</b></a> also begin to move through the heavens in an uneven and irregular manner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato">AN 4.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the irregular movement of celestial bodies leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato"><b>days and nights</b></a> coursing unevenly, which in turn disrupts the regular cycles of months and fortnights? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato">AN 4.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that when months and fortnights course unevenly, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato"><b>seasons and years</b></a> also become irregular, causing the winds to blow unevenly and out of their proper course? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato">AN 4.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when the winds blow unevenly and out of course, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato"><b>deities</b></a> become upset, which results in the sky-deity failing to provide rain properly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato">AN 4.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when rain does not fall properly at the right times, agricultural <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato"><b>crops</b></a> ripen unevenly, losing their nutritional value? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato">AN 4.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that humans who consume crops that have ripened unevenly due to the unrighteousness of their leaders become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato"><b>short-lived</b></a>, ugly, weak, and sickly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato">AN 4.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a herd of cattle crossing a river to explain that if the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato"><b>leading bull</b></a> goes crooked, the entire herd will follow a crooked path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato">AN 4.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that among human beings, the person considered the leader is the guide for others, and if that leader <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato"><b>practices unrighteousness</b></a>, the entire realm will suffer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato">AN 4.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when kings are righteous, the celestial bodies like the sun and moon course <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato"><b>evenly</b></a>, leading to regular days, nights, and seasons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato">AN 4.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a leader practices the Dhamma (righteousness), the rest of the people will follow their example, and the whole <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato"><b>realm</b></a> will dwell in happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato">AN 4.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that when rain falls properly due to righteous leadership, humans who eat the evenly ripened crops become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato"><b>long-lived</b></a>, beautiful, strong, and healthy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.70/en/sujato">AN 4.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk has entered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.71/en/sujato"><b>irrefutable path</b></a> (apaṇṇakappaṭipadaṃ) when he possesses four specific qualities that ensure his basis for destroying the taints is correctly established? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.71/en/sujato">AN 4.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.71/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> (sīlavā) as the first of four essential qualities for a monk to be practicing the path that leads to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.71/en/sujato">AN 4.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.71/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a> (bahussuto) as one of the four key qualities required for a monk to have his persistence properly aroused for spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.71/en/sujato">AN 4.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk must have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.71/en/sujato"><b>aroused persistence</b></a> (āraddhavīriyo) as part of the fourfold practice that ensures he is on the irrefutable path to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.71/en/sujato">AN 4.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.71/en/sujato"><b>wise</b></a> (paññavā) is the fourth quality necessary for a monk to be considered fully engaged in the practice for the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.71/en/sujato">AN 4.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a woman who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>irritable</b></a> and easily offended, manifesting anger even when slightly criticized, will be reborn as someone who is unattractive and ill-favored? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a woman who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>not irritable</b></a> and remains calm even when criticized at length will be reborn as someone who is beautiful, charming, and extremely good-looking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a woman who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>not generous</b></a> and fails to give food, drink, or clothing to ascetics or brahmins will be reborn into a state of poverty and lack of wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>generosity</b></a>—the giving of items like food, vehicles, garlands, and dwellings—as the specific cause for a woman to be reborn wealthy and affluent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>envious person</b></a> who resents the success, honors, and gains of others will be reborn as someone with little influence and low social status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a woman who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>not envious</b></a> of the honors and gains received by others will be reborn as one of great influence and high status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Queen Mallikā asked the Buddha why some women are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>unattractive and poor</b></a> with little influence, while others are beautiful and wealthy with great influence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained it is possible for a woman to be reborn <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>unattractive but wealthy</b></a> if she was irritable and angry in her past life but also practiced extensive generosity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>beautiful but poor</b></a> results from having been patient and not irritable in a past life while neglecting to give gifts to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Queen Mallikā realized that her own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>ill-favored appearance</b></a> was the result of having been an irritable and offensive person in a previous existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Queen Mallikā attributed her current state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>rich and affluent</b></a> to her past actions of providing food, drink, and clothing to ascetics and brahmins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Queen Mallikā believed she possessed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>great influence</b></a> over other maidens because she had not been envious or resentful of others' gains in a past life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Queen Mallikā vowed to the Buddha that from that day forward she would remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>not irritable</b></a>, even if she were criticized or spoken to harshly at great length? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Queen Mallikā explicitly resolved to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>not envious</b></a> of others' honors or respect, promising not to harbor resentment when others receive gains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>lighting and dwellings</b></a> in the list of gifts that, when given to holy men, lead to a person's future wealth and prosperity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a woman who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>manifests anger</b></a> and resentment after even a small critique as being "offensive and malicious"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the combination of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>patience and generosity</b></a> leads to being reborn both beautiful and wealthy with high status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Queen Mallikā formally committed to the Buddha that she would continue giving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato"><b>offerings</b></a> such as food, garlands, and scents to ensure she maintained her practice of generosity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.197/en/sujato">AN 4.197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk living in the forest should contemplate five <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>future dangers</b></a> to arouse sufficient energy for the attainment of the as-yet-unattained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised forest-dwelling monks to reflect that a bite from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>snake, scorpion, or centipede</b></a> could be a fatal obstruction to their spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to realize that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>tripping or falling</b></a> while alone in the wilderness could lead to a sudden death that halts their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the very <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>food one has eaten</b></a> could disagree with the body or cause an affliction, serving as a life-ending future danger for a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the internal disturbance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>bile</b></a> as a potential physiological danger that a monk should consider to motivate his meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the sudden provocation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>phlegm</b></a> within the body is a future danger that can lead to death for one living alone in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a forest-dweller should be aware that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>sharp internal winds</b></a> could arise and end his life before he achieves his spiritual goals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that encountering <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>wild beasts</b></a> like lions or tigers is a realistic danger for a monk that should spur him toward more diligent effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically mentioned that being killed by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>leopard, bear, or hyena</b></a> in the wild constitutes a serious obstruction to one's path that must be contemplated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised monks to consider that meeting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>violent youths</b></a> or outlaws who might take their lives is a danger inherent in forest living? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>non-human beings</b></a> (spirits) in the forest who might take a monk's life is a "future danger" to be contemplated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that contemplating these dangers is sufficient reason for a monk to live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>heedful and ardent</b></a> in his pursuit of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>obstruction</b></a> (<i>antarāyo</i>) to describe how death from forest hazards prevents a monk from reaching his spiritual destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on the five future dangers was intended to help a monk strive for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>attainment of the unattained</b></a> while he still has the chance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to reflect on these dangers specifically when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>dwelling alone</b></a> in the forest to maintain their focus on practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by seeing these five future dangers, a monk should start <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>arousing energy</b></a> to achieve what has not yet been achieved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the goal of contemplating forest hazards as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>realization of the unrealized</b></a> truths of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who dwells with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato"><b>resolute intent</b></a> is one who has deeply considered the fragility of his life in the face of nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.77/en/sujato">AN 5.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the five dangers for a monk who engages in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.222/en/sujato"><b>long wandering</b></a> without settling down is that he fails to attain the spiritual goals he has not yet attained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.222/en/sujato">AN 5.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who wanders indefinitely without a fixed residence risks the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.222/en/sujato"><b>loss of spiritual attainments</b></a> he has already achieved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.222/en/sujato">AN 5.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a monk who stays in a residence for an appropriate length of time, rather than wandering constantly, becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.222/en/sujato"><b>confident</b></a> in the spiritual qualities he has already realized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.222/en/sujato">AN 5.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that constant, unsettled wandering can cause a monk to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.222/en/sujato"><b>afflicted by severe illness</b></a> and health problems? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.222/en/sujato">AN 5.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a monk who does not settle down for a proper duration of time often finds himself <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.222/en/sujato"><b>without friends</b></a> and companions in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.222/en/sujato">AN 5.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five dangers of staying too long in a single residence, one of which is that a monk ends up accumulating a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.223/en/sujato"><b>large store of goods</b></a> and possessions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.223/en/sujato">AN 5.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, staying too long in one place can lead a monk to accumulate an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.223/en/sujato"><b>excess of medicine</b></a> and medicinal supplies beyond what is necessary? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.223/en/sujato">AN 5.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that staying too long in a residence can involve a monk in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.223/en/sujato"><b>many duties</b></a> and business, making him too busy for his spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.223/en/sujato">AN 5.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that a monk who dwells too long in one location may begin <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.223/en/sujato"><b>socializing improperly</b></a> with both laypeople and fellow monastics? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.223/en/sujato">AN 5.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who stays too long in a residence often <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.223/en/sujato"><b>departs with longing</b></a> and attachment to that place, rather than leaving with a detached mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.223/en/sujato">AN 5.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.224/en/sujato"><b>stinginess regarding a residence</b></a> as a primary danger for a monk who remains in one place for too long? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.224/en/sujato">AN 5.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who dwells too long in a single area may develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.224/en/sujato"><b>stinginess regarding families</b></a>, becoming possessive of his lay supporters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.224/en/sujato">AN 5.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that staying too long in a residence can lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.224/en/sujato"><b>stinginess regarding praise</b></a>, where a person becomes jealous of the reputation or beauty of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.224/en/sujato">AN 5.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.224/en/sujato"><b>stinginess regarding Dhamma</b></a> as a danger of staying too long, where a person becomes reluctant to share their knowledge of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.224/en/sujato">AN 5.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned monks that visiting families can lead to the danger of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.225/en/sujato"><b>sitting in private</b></a> with a woman, which is inappropriate for a renunciant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.225/en/sujato">AN 5.225</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who frequents lay families may find himself <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.225/en/sujato"><b>full of sensual thoughts</b></a>, which hinders his progress on the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.225/en/sujato">AN 5.225</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that excessive association with families leads to the frequent sight of women, which can eventually lead to a dangerous <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.226/en/sujato"><b>intimacy</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.226/en/sujato">AN 5.226</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that once a monk's mind is overcome by the results of too much social intimacy, he may end up <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.226/en/sujato"><b>giving up the training</b></a> and returning to the lower life of a layperson? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.226/en/sujato">AN 5.226</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a monk who stays in a residence for a proper duration, he is able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.223/en/sujato"><b>depart without attachment</b></a> when it is time to move on? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.223/en/sujato">AN 5.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who associates too closely with families risks committing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.226/en/sujato"><b>defiled offense</b></a> that could ruin his spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.226/en/sujato">AN 5.226</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the three essential duties of an ascetic (samaṇa) as the undertaking of the training in higher moral discipline, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.81/en/sujato"><b>training in higher mind</b></a>, and the training in higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.81/en/sujato">AN 3.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to cultivate an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.81/en/sujato"><b>intense desire</b></a> (tibbo chando) specifically for the three higher trainings of discipline, mind, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.81/en/sujato">AN 3.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an ascetic's primary responsibility is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.81/en/sujato"><b>training in higher moral discipline</b></a>, which serves as the foundational pillar for the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.81/en/sujato">AN 3.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one's spiritual efforts to be successful, they must resolve that their desire will be intense for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.81/en/sujato"><b>training in higher wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.81/en/sujato">AN 3.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a donkey following a herd of cattle to describe a person who claims to be a monk but lacks the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.82/en/sujato"><b>monastic qualities</b></a> and commitment required of the vocation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.82/en/sujato">AN 3.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as a donkey does not have the same color, voice, or hoof-prints as cattle, a superficial practitioner lacks the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.82/en/sujato"><b>intense desire</b></a> for spiritual training found in dedicated monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.82/en/sujato">AN 3.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who physically follows the Sangha and says, "I am a monk, too," but lacks the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.82/en/sujato"><b>higher moral discipline</b></a> of others is like a donkey tag-along in a cattle herd? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.82/en/sujato">AN 3.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a practitioner who lacks the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.82/en/sujato"><b>training in higher mind</b></a> is only mimicking the monastic life, much like a donkey claiming to be a cow while lacking a cow's voice and appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.82/en/sujato">AN 3.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who does not share the same desire for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.82/en/sujato"><b>training in higher wisdom</b></a> as other practitioners is only following the community of monks "from behind" without truly belonging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.82/en/sujato">AN 3.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded his teaching on the "donkey-monk" by urging practitioners to ensure their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.82/en/sujato"><b>desire will be intense</b></a> for all three levels of higher spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.82/en/sujato">AN 3.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the "preliminary duties" (pubbe karaṇīyāni) of a monk to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato"><b>urgent tasks of a farmer</b></a> who must prepare his field and crops according to the seasons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato">AN 3.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a farmer's first duty as ensuring his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato"><b>field is well-plowed</b></a> and well-harrowed, which serves as a metaphor for the necessary preparation in a monk's training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato">AN 3.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the timely planting of seeds in a field to the monk's essential preliminary duty of undertaking the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato"><b>training in higher moral discipline</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato">AN 3.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened the agricultural task of letting water into a field and draining it out to the monk's internal effort in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato"><b>training in higher mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato">AN 3.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato"><b>training in higher wisdom</b></a> as one of the three "preliminary duties" that must be performed by a monk, just as a farmer has specific tasks to do before his harvest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato">AN 3.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a farmer performs his agricultural tasks with precision, a monk must have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato"><b>intense desire</b></a> for higher moral discipline, higher mind, and higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato">AN 3.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the "duties of a farmer" metaphor to show that spiritual progress requires <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato"><b>specific actions</b></a> taken at the proper time, rather than just wishful thinking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.83/en/sujato">AN 3.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha consistently emphasized that the "training in higher mind" (adhicittasikkhā) is one of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.81/en/sujato"><b>three pillars of practice</b></a> that defines the path of an ascetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.81/en/sujato">AN 3.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>bad person</b></a> can be recognized by their tendency to reveal the dispraise of others even when they have not been asked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a bad person is asked to speak of another person’s faults, they will speak of them in full detail without omitting anything, which reveals their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome nature</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a bad person will not reveal the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>praise of others</b></a> even when they are asked, let alone when they are not asked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that if a bad person is pressured by a question to speak of someone else’s good qualities, they will do so omitingly and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>incompletely</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a bad person as someone who will not reveal their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>own dispraise</b></a> even when they are asked to do so? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said when a bad person is forced by questioning to admit their own faults, they will speak of them with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>omissions</b></a> and without detail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a bad person as one who eagerly reveals their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>own praise</b></a> even when they have not been asked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a bad person is asked about their own good qualities, they will speak of them in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>full detail</b></a> and without omitting anything? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>good person</b></a> as someone who does not reveal the dispraise of others even when they are asked to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a good person is pressured to speak of another’s faults, they will speak of them with omissions and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>without detail</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a good person as one who reveals the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>praise of others</b></a> even when they are not asked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a good person is asked to speak of another’s good qualities, they will do so in full detail and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>without omitting</b></a> anything? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a good person is recognizable because they reveal their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>own faults</b></a> even when they have not been asked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a good person is questioned about their own shortcomings, they will speak of them in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>full detail</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a good person as someone who does not reveal their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>own praise</b></a> even when they are asked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a good person is forced to speak of their own good qualities, they will speak of them omitingly and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>incompletely</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>newlywed bride</b></a> who initially possesses a strong sense of shame and fear toward her in-laws and husband, but can lose it over time through familiarity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted his followers to train themselves to dwell with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato"><b>mind like a newlywed bride</b></a>, maintaining a persistent sense of moral shame and moral dread? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.73/en/sujato">AN 4.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>four types of persons</b></a> existing in the world, categorized by whether they torment themselves, torment others, torment both, or torment neither? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>torments himself</b></a> as someone who pursues the practice of torturing and mortifying his own body through various extreme ascetic penances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted some ascetics practice self-torment by being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>one who licks his hands</b></a> after eating, refusing to use a bowl or accept food that is brought specifically to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how some self-tormenting ascetics wear <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>garments of hemp</b></a>, bark, or even owls' wings, and might practice "bathed-in-thorns" or constant standing as a form of religious penance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>torments others</b></a> as one who engages in cruel occupations such as a butcher of sheep, a hunter, a fisherman, a thief, or an executioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>torments both himself and others</b></a> as a powerful figure, like a king or a wealthy brahmin, who undergoes personal rituals of penance while ordering a massive animal sacrifice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted the suffering of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>slaves and messengers</b></a> who, driven by fear and the threat of punishment, are forced to carry out the preparations for large-scale animal sacrifices with tears in their eyes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>torments neither</b></a> himself nor others lives a life that is hungerless, extinguished, and cooled, experiencing bliss with a self that has become divine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the path to becoming one who torments neither begins when a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>Tathagata appears</b></a> in the world, an Arahant who is perfectly enlightened and teaches the Dhamma that is good in the beginning, middle, and end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the household life as a "crowded path of dust" and taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> in its full perfection is best lived by shaving one's hair and beard and going forth into homelessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who torments neither himself nor others <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>abandons the destruction of life</b></a>, dwelling compassionate and kind to all living beings while abstaining from ever using a club or a sword? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the speech of a practitioner as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>reconciles those who are divided</b></a>, speaking only words that promote friendship, concord, and peace among people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk should practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>restraint of the sense faculties</b></a>, ensuring that unwholesome states like greed and dejection do not flow in through the eye, ear, or mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>contentment</b></a> with his simple robes and almsfood to a bird that flies away carrying only its wings, wherever it goes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that after abandoning the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>five hindrances</b></a>—sensual desire, ill will, sloth and torpor, restlessness and remorse, and doubt—a practitioner purifies the mind of defilements that weaken wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the path to becoming "one who torments neither" as involving the attainment and dwelling in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a>, which are states of deep meditative concentration and bliss? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a concentrated and purified mind can be directed to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>recollection of manifold past dwellings</b></a>, remembering thousands of previous births and their specific details? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a practitioner develops the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> to see beings passing away and being reborn according to their kamma, whether they are inferior or superior, fair or ugly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that true liberation involves understanding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>suffering, its origin, its cessation</b></a>, and the way leading to its cessation as they really are in the light of the three knowledges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the ultimate state of a person who torments neither as one whose mind is liberated from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato"><b>taints of sensual desire</b></a>, the taint of existence, and the taint of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.198/en/sujato">AN 4.198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are five <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>future perils</b></a> which, when foreseen, should motivate a monk to live diligently, ardently, and resolutely? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised monks to reflect that while they are currently young and black-haired, the time will come when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>old age</b></a> touches the body, making it difficult to practice the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when a person is overcome by aging, it is no longer easy to frequent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>remote jungle lodgings</b></a> or isolated dwellings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to stir up energy for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>attainment</b></a> of what has not yet been attained before the undesirable condition of old age arrives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of being currently free from illness as having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>digestion</b></a> that is neither too cold nor too hot, which is suitable for spiritual striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that when one is overwhelmed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>illness</b></a>, it is difficult to give proper attention to the teachings of the Buddhas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged monks to initiate energy in advance so they may <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>dwell in comfort</b></a> through the Dhamma even when they eventually become sick? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that while food may currently be plentiful and easy to obtain through alms, a time of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>famine</b></a> and poor crops could eventually occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that during times of scarcity, people migrate to where food is available, leading to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>crowded dwellings</b></a> that are not conducive to meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is difficult to attend to the teachings when living in an environment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>social congestion</b></a> caused by people fleeing from famine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted a future peril where social harmony ends and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>danger from insurgents</b></a> arises, causing the population to scatter to safer areas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that in times of public insecurity and fear, it becomes nearly impossible to dwell in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>secluded lodgings</b></a> in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that a practitioner should strive for spiritual realization now, so that they can remain at ease even during times of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>public danger</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a currently ideal state where the community of monks is harmonious, in agreement, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>without dispute</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned of a future peril where a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>schism in the Sangha</b></a> occurs, making the spiritual life much harder to maintain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when the monastic community is split, it is difficult to give heart to the teachings or seek <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>solitude</b></a> for meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to realize the truth before a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>divided community</b></a> makes the practice of the Dhamma more difficult? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>stir up energy</b></a> (<i>vīriyaṃ ārabhāmi</i>) to describe the effort one should make today to reach the goal before future obstacles arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that achieving the Dhamma allows a person to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>live at ease</b></a> even if they are in the midst of a divided Sangha or external strife? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that seeing these five future perils is sufficient reason for a monk to live with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato"><b>diligent effort</b></a> for the sake of realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.78/en/sujato">AN 5.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wealth is never entirely secure because it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato"><b>shared with fire</b></a>, which is one of the five primary dangers that can destroy one’s possessions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato">AN 5.227</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that wealth is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato"><b>shared with water</b></a>, meaning it is constantly at risk of being swept away or damaged by floods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato">AN 5.227</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato"><b>shared with kings</b></a> as a danger of wealth, referring to the possibility of one's property being seized or taxed by the state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato">AN 5.227</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato"><b>shared with thieves</b></a> as a major disadvantage of wealth, as riches often attract those who wish to steal them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato">AN 5.227</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that wealth is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato"><b>shared with unloved heirs</b></a>, who may eventually inherit and consume the property against the original owner's wishes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato">AN 5.227</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person can use their wealth for good by ensuring they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato"><b>make themselves happy</b></a> and maintain their own well-being properly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato">AN 5.227</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the use of wealth to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato"><b>make one’s parents happy</b></a> and provide them with comfort and pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato">AN 5.227</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the benefits of wealth is the ability to provide for the happiness of one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato"><b>wife and children</b></a>, as well as one's workers and servants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato">AN 5.227</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wealth can be used to please and assist one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato"><b>friends and colleagues</b></a>, fostering strong social bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato">AN 5.227</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the highest use of wealth is providing offerings to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato"><b>recluses and brahmins</b></a>, which leads to a happy rebirth in the heavenly worlds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.227/en/sujato">AN 5.227</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that in a family that serves meals too late, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.228/en/sujato"><b>guests</b></a> are not honored at the proper time, which is considered a failing in hospitality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.228/en/sujato">AN 5.228</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that families who eat at irregular or late hours fail to timely honor the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.228/en/sujato"><b>deities</b></a> who are traditionally entitled to receive offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.228/en/sujato">AN 5.228</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that late meals prevent a family from supporting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.228/en/sujato"><b>recluses and brahmins</b></a> who abstain from eating after noon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.228/en/sujato">AN 5.228</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that when meals are served at improper times, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.228/en/sujato"><b>servants and workers</b></a> of the household often perform their tasks with resentment or averted faces? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.228/en/sujato">AN 5.228</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that food consumed at the wrong time or out of season is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.228/en/sujato"><b>not nutritious</b></a> and lacks the vitality found in timely meals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.228/en/sujato">AN 5.228</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha drew a comparison between a black snake and certain women, noting that both can be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.229/en/sujato"><b>impure</b></a> and foul-smelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.229/en/sujato">AN 5.229</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized both a black snake and certain types of women as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.229/en/sujato"><b>treacherous</b></a>, meaning they are prone to betraying those who trust them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.229/en/sujato">AN 5.229</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the deadly venom of a black snake to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.230/en/sujato"><b>intense lust</b></a> that can be found in some individuals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.230/en/sujato">AN 5.230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened the double-tongued nature of a black snake to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.230/en/sujato"><b>slanderous speech</b></a> or divisive words used by some women? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.230/en/sujato">AN 5.230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the treachery of a snake is mirrored in a woman who is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.230/en/sujato"><b>adulteress</b></a> and betrays her partner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.230/en/sujato">AN 5.230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that more than <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>one hundred and fifty training rules</b></a> are recited every fortnight, providing a framework for those who desire their own welfare to train themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the entirety of the Buddhist path into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>three trainings</b></a>: the training in higher virtue, the training in higher mind, and the training in higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who fulfills the training in virtue but only has limited concentration and wisdom can still become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>stream-enterer</b></a>, ensuring they are no longer bound for the lower worlds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a practitioner becomes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>once-returner</b></a> by fulfilling the training in virtue and weakening lust, hatred, and delusion, meaning they will return to this world only one more time to end suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk fulfills the trainings in both virtue and concentration but has limited wisdom, they can become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>non-returner</b></a> who is reborn in a higher realm to attain final Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an individual who fully completes all three trainings—virtue, concentration, and wisdom—attains the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> and reaches liberation in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that falling into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>minor training rules</b></a> and then rehabilitating oneself does not make a practitioner "incapable" of reaching enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that while minor rules may be transgressed and restored, a practitioner must remain unwavering and steady regarding rules that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>fundamental to the holy life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a stream-enterer as one who has achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>destruction of three fetters</b></a>, making them certain of attaining full enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a non-returner as one who is "of spontaneous birth" because they have achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the five lower fetters</b></a> and will never return from that higher state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that those who practice the training rules partially will achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>partial success</b></a>, while those who practice them fully will achieve full success? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>training rules</b></a> he laid down are "not barren," meaning they always lead to spiritual results when practiced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>attainment of Arahantship</b></a> as the direct realization of the "taintless liberation of mind" and "liberation by wisdom" through one's own higher knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the training rules are specifically for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>clansmen</b></a> (sons of good family) who are genuinely interested in their own spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>higher wisdom</b></a> (adhipaññāsikkhā) to describe the third and final stage of the essential training for monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if a monk is only a "limited" practitioner of concentration and wisdom, as long as they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>fulfilling in virtue</b></a>, they can still escape the possibility of falling into the planes of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>training in higher mind</b></a> (adhicittasikkhā) refers to the development of deep concentration and mental discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that the reason a monk restores minor offenses is that he is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato"><b>incapable of progress</b></a>, despite occasional lapses in the lesser training rules? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.86/en/sujato">AN 3.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.74/en/sujato"><b>foremost virtue</b></a> (sīlaggaṃ) as the first of four supreme qualities that a practitioner should cultivate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.74/en/sujato">AN 4.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.74/en/sujato"><b>foremost concentration</b></a> (samādhiggaṃ) is one of the four essential "foremost" things alongside virtue, wisdom, and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.74/en/sujato">AN 4.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.74/en/sujato"><b>foremost wisdom</b></a> (paññāggaṃ) stands as a primary excellence that marks the progression of the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.74/en/sujato">AN 4.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.74/en/sujato"><b>foremost liberation</b></a> (vimuttaggaṃ) as the final of the four supreme qualities that summarize the highest goals of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.74/en/sujato">AN 4.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while staying in the Sal Grove of the Mallas at Kusināra during his final passing, the Buddha specifically urged the monks to ask if they had any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato"><b>doubt or uncertainty</b></a> regarding the Buddha, Dhamma, or Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato">AN 4.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha invited his disciples to question him about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato"><b>path or the practice</b></a> so they would not later regret having been face-to-face with the Teacher and failing to clarify their uncertainty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato">AN 4.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was so concerned for his monks' clarity that he repeated his invitation for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato"><b>questions</b></a> three times during his final moments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato">AN 4.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that if a monk felt too much respect or embarrassment to ask him a question directly, that monk should have a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato"><b>friend</b></a> inform another on their behalf? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato">AN 4.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda expressed his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato"><b>amazement</b></a> to the Buddha because he felt confident that not a single monk in the assembly at Kusināra harbored any doubts about the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato">AN 4.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Ānanda that while Ānanda spoke out of faith, the Buddha had direct <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato"><b>knowledge</b></a> that no monk in that final assembly of five hundred had any uncertainty regarding the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato">AN 4.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that even the very <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato"><b>last monk</b></a> among the five hundred present at his passing was a stream-enterer, no longer subject to downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato">AN 4.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the monks in his final assembly as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato"><b>bound for enlightenment</b></a> (sambodhiparāyaṇo), meaning their eventual full awakening was certain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato">AN 4.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato"><b>stream-enterer</b></a> is one who is no longer subject to rebirth in lower realms and is fixed in their destiny toward awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.76/en/sujato">AN 4.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato"><b>unthinkable things</b></a> (acinteyyāni) that should not be conjectured because trying to think them through would lead to madness and vexation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato">AN 4.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato"><b>range of a Buddha</b></a> (buddhavisayo) is an unthinkable subject that cannot be comprehended through ordinary intellectual speculation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato">AN 4.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that anyone who tries to conjecture the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato"><b>range of one in jhana</b></a> (jhānavisayo) would only experience mental distress and frustration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato">AN 4.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato"><b>result of kamma</b></a> (kammavipāko) to be one of the four unconjecturable matters that are beyond the scope of conventional thought? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato">AN 4.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised against <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato"><b>speculation about the world</b></a> (lokacintā), such as its origins, as it is one of the four topics that can lead to madness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato">AN 4.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who obsessively ponder the four unthinkable subjects will suffer from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato"><b>portion of madness</b></a> and vexation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato">AN 4.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s list of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato"><b>four unthinkables</b></a> highlights specific areas—the powers of a Buddha, the depths of meditation, the workings of kamma, and the nature of the cosmos—that are unsuitable for mere theoretical speculation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.77/en/sujato">AN 4.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described craving as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato"><b>the weaver</b></a> and a net that ensnares the world, causing it to become as tangled as a ball of string? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato">AN 4.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, craving causes the world to become like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato"><b>tangled skein</b></a> and a knotted ball of thread, which prevents beings from escaping the states of misery and the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato">AN 4.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified thirty-six <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato"><b>mental explorations of craving</b></a>, which are divided into eighteen based on the internal and eighteen based on the external? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato">AN 4.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the thirty-six mental explorations of craving are multiplied by the past, the future, and the present, they total <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato"><b>one hundred and eight</b></a> distinct varieties of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato">AN 4.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that internal craving begins with the fundamental notion <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato"><b>"I am,"</b></a> which then branches out into seventeen further variations such as "I am such" and "I am otherwise"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato">AN 4.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that external craving is rooted in the thought <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato"><b>"By this I am,"</b></a> which leads to numerous ways of identifying oneself through external things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato">AN 4.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized craving as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato"><b>the spreader</b></a> because it extends itself throughout the world, keeping beings enmeshed and unable to transcend suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato">AN 4.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that craving is the specific reason the world is overwhelmed and cannot pass beyond the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato"><b>cycle of rebirth</b></a> (<i>saṃsāra</i>) or states of downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato">AN 4.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the world's condition under the influence of craving to being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato"><b>matted like grass</b></a>, illustrating how craving creates a confused and tangled state of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato">AN 4.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to craving as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato"><b>the clinger</b></a> because it enmeshes the world, preventing beings from escaping a bad destination or the round of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato">AN 4.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the internal mental explorations of craving includes the speculation <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato"><b>"I shall be,"</b></a> reflecting the mind's attachment to various forms of future existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato">AN 4.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized craving as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato"><b>the mover</b></a> because it flows along and spreads, causing the entire world to be overwhelmed and bound to saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.199/en/sujato">AN 4.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned of five <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>future dangers</b></a> that have not yet arisen but will eventually emerge to cause the corruption of both the Dhamma and the Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted a time when monks would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>undeveloped in wisdom</b></a>, virtue, mind, and body, yet would continue to give higher ordination to others, creating a cycle of poorly trained practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when teachers are themselves undeveloped, they are unable to successfully train their students in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>higher virtue</b></a>, higher mind, and higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>corruption of the Dhamma</b></a> inevitably leads to the corruption of the Vinaya (Discipline), and vice versa? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that future monks would provide <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>dependence</b></a> (mentorship) to others despite being undeveloped in body, virtue, mind, and wisdom themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that future monks who are undeveloped in mind and wisdom might discuss <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>Abhidhamma and Vedalla</b></a> but will actually be falling into darkness without realizing it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha foresaw a period where monks would no longer have the desire to listen to or learn <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>deep suttas</b></a> that were spoken directly by the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the most essential and profound teachings as those that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>connected with emptiness</b></a> and are supramundane in nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that future monks would prefer to listen to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>literary works</b></a>, poetry, and the words of disciples rather than the Buddha’s own profound discourses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted that future elder monks would become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>indulgent and lax</b></a>, effectively taking the lead in backsliding rather than spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when monks become lax and indulgent, they will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>neglect seclusion</b></a> and fail to exert the energy required to realize what has not yet been realized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that future generations of practitioners would follow the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>bad example</b></a> set by indulgent elders, leading to a widespread decline in spiritual effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>proactive and vigilant</b></a> by recognizing these future dangers now so they can strive to abandon them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the lack of training in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>higher mind</b></a> (adhicitta) as a critical danger that arises when unqualified monks take on the responsibility of teaching others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>corrupt Discipline</b></a> (Vinaya) acts as a catalyst for the further corruption of the Dhamma itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the true teachings of the Tathāgata as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>supramundane</b></a> and having meanings that are deep and difficult to see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that monks who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>undeveloped in body</b></a> and virtue lack the foundation necessary to guide others in the higher spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that future monks would fail to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>arouse energy</b></a> for the sake of reaching the unreached or witnessing the truth for themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that future monks would fall into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>dark states</b></a> (kaṇhadhamma) while mistakenly believing they are making progress through intellectual discussions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that monks must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato"><b>strive for abandonment</b></a> of these five future dangers to ensure the integrity of the spiritual path for the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.79/en/sujato">AN 5.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a resident bhikkhu is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.231/en/sujato"><b>unworthy of respect</b></a> if he is not accomplished in proper conduct and duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.231/en/sujato">AN 5.231</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a resident bhikkhu who is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.231/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a> and has not retained the teachings he has heard is deemed unworthy of respect by his peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.231/en/sujato">AN 5.231</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a resident bhikkhu as unworthy of respect if he does not practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.231/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> or find delight in solitude and quietude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.231/en/sujato">AN 5.231</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a resident bhikkhu becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.231/en/sujato"><b>worthy of respect</b></a> when he is wise, alert, and possesses the wisdom to understand the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.231/en/sujato">AN 5.231</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a resident bhikkhu is dear and agreeable to his fellow monks when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.232/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and lives restrained by the Pātimokkha, seeing fear in even the slightest faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.232/en/sujato">AN 5.232</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a resident bhikkhu is highly esteemed by his companions when he has deeply penetrated the Dhamma through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.232/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>, understanding teachings that are good in the beginning, middle, and end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.232/en/sujato">AN 5.232</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha noted that a resident bhikkhu is respected when he possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.232/en/sujato"><b>polished speech</b></a> that is clear, distinct, and helpful for communicating the meaning of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.232/en/sujato">AN 5.232</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a resident bhikkhu is held in high regard when he can obtain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.232/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a>—which provide a pleasant dwelling in this very life—at will and without difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.232/en/sujato">AN 5.232</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that a resident bhikkhu becomes truly dear and respected when he realizes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.232/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> and dwells in the stainless liberation of mind and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.232/en/sujato">AN 5.232</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a resident bhikkhu <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.233/en/sujato"><b>beautifies the residence</b></a> where he lives when he is accomplished in virtue and strictly follows the training rules? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.233/en/sujato">AN 5.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a resident bhikkhu who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.233/en/sujato"><b>highly learned</b></a> and has mastered the teachings through recitation and reflection is a source of beauty for his dwelling place? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.233/en/sujato">AN 5.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a resident bhikkhu adorns his residence when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.233/en/sujato"><b>capable of instructing</b></a> those who approach him by showing, urging, and gladdening them with a Dhamma talk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.233/en/sujato">AN 5.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a residence is graced when its resident monk can easily gain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.233/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a>, which are considered higher mental states that lead to a peaceful life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.233/en/sujato">AN 5.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that a resident bhikkhu who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.231/en/sujato"><b>foolish and dull</b></a>, and lacks the capacity for speech that is clear and understandable, is deemed unworthy of respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.231/en/sujato">AN 5.231</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three essential disciplines for spiritual development known as the training in higher virtuous behavior, the training in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.89/en/sujato"><b>higher mind</b></a>, and the training in higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.89/en/sujato">AN 3.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the training in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.89/en/sujato"><b>higher virtuous behavior</b></a> as a state where a monk dwells restrained by the Pātimokkha, is perfect in conduct and resort, and trains himself in the rules of discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.89/en/sujato">AN 3.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner engaged in the training of higher virtuous behavior is someone who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.89/en/sujato"><b>sees danger</b></a> even in the slightest of faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.89/en/sujato">AN 3.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the training in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.89/en/sujato"><b>higher mind</b></a> specifically as the attainment and dwelling in the four meditative states known as jhānas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.89/en/sujato">AN 3.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the training in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.89/en/sujato"><b>higher wisdom</b></a> involves realistically understanding the Four Noble Truths: suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path leading to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.89/en/sujato">AN 3.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the training in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato"><b>higher wisdom</b></a> is fulfilled when a person, through the destruction of the taints, enters and dwells in the taintless liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato">AN 3.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to be energetic, steadfast, and mindful, while maintaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato"><b>guarded senses</b></a> as they move through the three higher trainings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato">AN 3.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of overcoming all directions—above, below, and across—by developing an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable concentration</b></a> in one's daily life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato">AN 3.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the deliberate practice of the three higher trainings and pure conduct as the path of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato"><b>trainee</b></a> (sekha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato">AN 3.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who has reached the end of the spiritual journey and the completion of the three trainings as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato"><b>wise one</b></a> who has gone to the end of the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato">AN 3.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the final liberation of the mind to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato"><b>quenching of a lamp</b></a>, which occurs once consciousness ceases and craving is destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato">AN 3.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the liberation of the mind is achieved through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a>, which follows the cessation of consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato">AN 3.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ideal practitioner as one who remains consistent in their practice regardless of the time or situation—whether it is day or night, or whether they are looking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato"><b>upward or downward</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato">AN 3.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato"><b>undivided mindfulness</b></a> and remain meditative are able to overcome all directions through their spiritual power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.90/en/sujato">AN 3.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta once asked the Buddha about the specific cause and condition why some people's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato"><b>business undertakings</b></a> result in failure while others exceed their expectations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato">AN 4.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person’s business results in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato"><b>loss</b></a> because in a past life they invited an ascetic or brahmin to ask for requisites but then failed to give them what they had offered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato">AN 4.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a business will not turn out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato"><b>as intended</b></a> if a person previously offered to provide for an ascetic or brahmin but then gave less than what they had originally intended to give? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato">AN 4.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person gives an ascetic or brahmin exactly what they intended after inviting them to ask for help, their future business ventures will turn out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato"><b>according to their intentions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato">AN 4.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that giving more than what was originally intended to an ascetic or brahmin leads to a future where one's business results actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato"><b>exceed one's intentions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato">AN 4.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha linked a person's commercial success or failure in "this state of existence" to how they fulfilled an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato"><b>invitation</b></a> to provide support to religious practitioners in a previous life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato">AN 4.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato"><b>cause and condition</b></a> for why identical business ventures can lead to vastly different financial outcomes for different individuals based on their past integrity in giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato">AN 4.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Ven. Sāriputta that the way one treats a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato"><b>samaṇa or brahmin</b></a> when offering them requisites directly influences whether one's future trade will be successful or ruinous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato">AN 4.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato"><b>four outcomes</b></a> of business—ranging from failing to meet expectations to exceeding them—all of which are rooted in the honesty of one's past generosity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato">AN 4.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that failing to follow through on a promise to provide a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato"><b>requirement</b></a> to a contemplative leads to a karmic result where one's own livelihood and business affairs later fall into decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.79/en/sujato">AN 4.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are four distinct ways that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>affection and hatred</b></a> are born: affection born of affection, hatred born of affection, affection born of hatred, and hatred born of hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>affection born of affection</b></a> arises when a person feels love toward those who treat their own loved ones with kindness and care? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>hatred born of affection</b></a> occurs when a person develops resentment toward others because those others have treated their loved ones in an unpleasant or disagreeable way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>affection born of hatred</b></a> as the feeling of love that arises toward those who treat one's enemies or disliked persons in an unpleasant and disagreeable manner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>hatred born of hatred</b></a> arises when a person feels resentment toward those who treat their enemies or disliked persons with kindness and appreciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk enters and dwells in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>first jhana</b></a>, the four types of affection and hatred are temporarily absent and do not exist in him at that time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that through the attainment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhana</b></a>, a monk experiences a state of pure equanimity where neither affection nor hatred born of past associations are present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that only through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (Arahantship) are the four kinds of affection and hatred permanently abandoned and cut off like a palm stump, never to arise again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>towering</b></a> (usseneti) as the act of a monk regarding any of the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, or consciousness—as self or belonging to self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a monk "towers" (usseneti) when he perceives his self as possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>, form as being in his self, or his self as being in form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk "towers" when he regards the aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> as self, or views self as having feeling, or feeling in self, or self in feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that "towering" (usseneti) occurs when a monk views the aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> as self, or views the self as possessing perception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is engaged in "towering" when he regards <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> as self, or views himself as possessing these mental formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as "towering" when he regards <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> as self, or perceives himself as having consciousness or consciousness residing in himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>does not retaliate</b></a> (na patiseneti) when he remains patient and does not scold or show anger toward those who abuse or provoke him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>smolders</b></a> (dhūpāyati) when he is preoccupied with the thought "I am," or various projections like "I shall be" or "I shall not be"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>does not smolder</b></a> (na dhūpāyati) once the underlying notion of "I am" and all related proliferations about future existence have been completely extinguished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>blazes</b></a> (pajjalati) when he identifies his existence through specific conditions, thinking "by this I am," "by this I have been," or "by this I shall be"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>does not blaze</b></a> (na pajjalati) when the identification "by this I am" is no longer present, preventing the fire of conditioned views from arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>worries</b></a> (sampajjhāyati) if the conceit "I am" (asmimāno) has not yet been abandoned, uprooted, or made nonexistent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato"><b>does not worry</b></a> (na sampajjhāyati) as one who has successfully uprooted the conceit "I am" so that it is no longer subject to future arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.200/en/sujato">AN 4.200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.8/en/sujato"><b>faithless</b></a> falls away from his progress and fails to stand firm in the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.8/en/sujato">AN 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.8/en/sujato"><b>lack of shame</b></a>—the absence of a conscientious hesitation to do evil—as one of the five qualities that cause a practitioner to fall away from the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.8/en/sujato">AN 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.8/en/sujato"><b>lacks moral dread</b></a>, meaning he does not fear the consequences of wrongdoing, is prone to falling away from the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.8/en/sujato">AN 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.8/en/sujato"><b>lazy</b></a> is a specific condition that prevents a monk from standing firm and leads him to fall away from the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.8/en/sujato">AN 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.8/en/sujato"><b>unwise</b></a> monk, who lacks spiritual discernment, is among those who fall away and fail to remain established in the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.8/en/sujato">AN 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> in the teaching is one who does not fall away, but instead stands firm in the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato">AN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato"><b>sense of shame</b></a> regarding unwholesome actions is characterized as one who stands firm in the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato">AN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato"><b>moral dread</b></a>—the inner fear of the results of bad conduct—is capable of remaining established in the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato">AN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato"><b>energetic</b></a> monk, who has aroused his persistence in practice, as someone who stands firm and does not fall away from the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato">AN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato"><b>wise</b></a> as a fundamental quality that ensures a monk remains established in the true Dhamma without falling away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato">AN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato"><b>irreverent and lacks deference</b></a> is incapable of achieving growth, increase, or expansion in the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato">AN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato"><b>faithless</b></a> and lacks respect is incapable of experiencing progress or maturation within the Buddha's teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato">AN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato"><b>shameless</b></a> and disrespectful is incapable of experiencing the growth and expansion intended in the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato">AN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato"><b>lacks moral dread</b></a> and shows no reverence is barred from achieving increase and fulfillment in his spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato">AN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato"><b>lazy</b></a> monk who is also irreverent lacks the capacity for growth, increase, and expansion in the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato">AN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato"><b>unwise</b></a> monk who lacks reverence and deference is incapable of reaching the expansion and increase offered by the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato">AN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato"><b>respectful</b></a> and possesses deference is capable of achieving great growth, increase, and expansion in the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato">AN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato"><b>wise and energetic</b></a>, while also maintaining reverence and deference, is fully capable of reaching the full expansion of the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.10/en/sujato">AN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a resident monk is of great help to his monastery when he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.234/en/sujato"><b>repairs what is broken</b></a> and dilapidated within the residence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.234/en/sujato">AN 5.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a resident monk as being highly beneficial to his monastery when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.234/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a>, restrained by the Patimokkha, and trains himself in the rules of training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.234/en/sujato">AN 5.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a resident monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.234/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a>, bearing in mind what he has learned and penetrating the teachings well by view, is one of five types who help their residence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.234/en/sujato">AN 5.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a resident monk is of great help to his dwelling place if he is an obtainer at will of the four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.234/en/sujato"><b>jhanas</b></a>, which provide a pleasant dwelling in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.234/en/sujato">AN 5.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised resident monks who show compassion by encouraging laypeople to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.234/en/sujato"><b>make merit</b></a> when a large Sangha of monks from various regions arrives at their monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.234/en/sujato">AN 5.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a resident monk shows true compassion to laypeople when he encourages them to establish themselves in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.235/en/sujato"><b>higher virtue</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.235/en/sujato">AN 5.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a resident monk shows compassion for the laity when he visits those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.235/en/sujato"><b>sick</b></a> and arouses their mindfulness toward the goal of arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.235/en/sujato">AN 5.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned a compassionate resident monk is one who settles laypeople in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.235/en/sujato"><b>vision of the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.235/en/sujato">AN 5.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a compassionate resident monk consumes whatever <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.235/en/sujato"><b>food</b></a> he is given, whether coarse or fine, and does not misuse the gifts of faith provided by laypeople? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.235/en/sujato">AN 5.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a resident monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.239/en/sujato"><b>stingy regarding lodgings</b></a> and stingy regarding families is deposited in hell as if carried there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.239/en/sujato">AN 5.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.239/en/sujato"><b>stingy regarding praise</b></a> as one of five qualities that cause a resident monk to be reborn in a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.239/en/sujato">AN 5.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a resident monk who does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.239/en/sujato"><b>not misuse gifts of faith</b></a> and is not stingy regarding gains is reborn in a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.239/en/sujato">AN 5.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.239/en/sujato"><b>stinginess regarding gains</b></a> is a quality that leads a resident monk directly to hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.239/en/sujato">AN 5.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was at Saṅkavā, a resident monk named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>Kassapagotta</b></a> became resentful and dissatisfied when the Buddha gave a talk concerning the rules of training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Kassapagotta initially criticized the Buddha, thinking that the Teacher was being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>exceedingly fastidious</b></a> in his discourse on the training rules? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the Buddha departed for Rajagaha, the monk Kassapagotta was overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>remorse</b></a> and regret for his earlier resentment toward the Buddha's teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Kassapagotta packed his robe and bowl and traveled to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>Vulture’s Peak</b></a> in Rajagaha specifically to confess his transgression directly to the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it constitutes growth in the Discipline of the Noble One for a person to see their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>fault</b></a> as a fault and make amends according to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he would not speak in praise of a senior monk if that monk is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>fond of the training</b></a> and fails to encourage others to undertake it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained he does not praise monks who neglect the training because other monks might follow their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>bad example</b></a>, leading to their long-term harm and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha will speak in praise of a senior monk if that monk is devoted to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>rules of training</b></a> and speaks in praise of undertaking them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when the Teacher praises a monk, others are likely to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>associate with</b></a> that monk and eventually emulate his conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when a monk is fond of the training, he should also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>praise others</b></a> who share that same commitment, speaking what is true and factual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s standard for praising a monk remains the same whether the monk is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>senior elder</b></a>, a middle-aged monk, or a newly ordained one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that following the example of a monk who is not fond of the training leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> for those who follow him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that following the example of a monk who loves the training leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>long-term welfare</b></a> and happiness for his companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared he would not praise even a newly ordained monk if that monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>disregards</b></a> the importance of the training rules? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Kassapagotta confessed to the Buddha that he had acted like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>fool</b></a> and was confused when he initially resented the Buddha's teaching on discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha accepted Kassapagotta's confession and noted that it is part of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>discipline</b></a> to achieve future restraint after recognizing a mistake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considers a monk's willingness to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>encourage others</b></a> to train as a key reason to speak in praise of that monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that his praise of a monk is intended to help other monks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>emulate</b></a> that monk's good qualities for their own benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching in the Saṅkavāsutta highlights that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato"><b>spiritual seniority</b></a> is not based on years alone, but on one's genuine attitude toward the training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.91/en/sujato">AN 3.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha possesses four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato"><b>intrepidities</b></a> (vesārajjāni) which allow him to claim the place of the bull and roar his lion's roar in any assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato">AN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that because he possesses specific intrepidities, he can justly claim the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato"><b>place of the bull</b></a> (āsabhaṃ ṭhānaṃ) and set the supreme wheel in motion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato">AN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that his possession of four intrepidities enables him to confidently roar his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato"><b>lion's roar</b></a> in various assemblies without any fear of being justly challenged? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato">AN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is able to set the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato"><b>supreme wheel</b></a> (brahmacakkaṃ) in motion because he sees no reason why anyone in the world—whether a deva, Mara, or Brahma—could justly charge him with lack of knowledge or realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato">AN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he lives in security and fearlessness because no ascetic or brahmin can justly charge him with being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato"><b>unawakened</b></a> to certain things while he claims to be a Perfectly Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato">AN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha dwells in intrepidity because he sees no ground for anyone to justly claim that he has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato"><b>undestroyed taints</b></a> while he claims to be one who has completely destroyed all taints (khīṇāsavassa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato">AN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that his fearlessness arises from the fact that no one in the world can justly argue that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato"><b>obstructive things</b></a> he has identified do not actually pose an obstacle to those who engage in them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato">AN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted his intrepidity by stating that no one can justly claim that the Dhamma he teaches does not lead to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato"><b>complete destruction of suffering</b></a> for those who practice it correctly for that purpose? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato">AN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that by not seeing any grounds for being justly challenged on his enlightenment or teachings, he has attained a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato"><b>security</b></a> and fearlessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato">AN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the Tathagata has transcended all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato"><b>paths of speech</b></a> (vādapathā) and is unsurpassed by any ascetic or brahmin who relies on speculative views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato">AN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as the supreme being among <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato"><b>gods and humans</b></a>, one who has reached the further shore of existence and set the Dhamma wheel in motion out of compassion for all beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.8/en/sujato">AN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato"><b>four places</b></a> where craving typically arises in a monk, specifically relating to their basic requisites and their state of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato">AN 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that one of the primary reasons craving (taṇhā) arises in a monk is for the sake of obtaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato"><b>robes</b></a> (cīvara)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato">AN 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that craving arises in a monk because of the need for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> (piṇḍapāta), which is one of the four specific ways craving manifests? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato">AN 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato"><b>lodgings</b></a> (senāsana) are one of the specific factors that cause craving to arise within a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato">AN 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how craving arises in a monk for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato"><b>being this or that</b></a> (itibhavābhava), referring to the cycle of existence and non-existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato">AN 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha famously described craving as a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato"><b>companion</b></a> (taṇhā dutiyo puriso), which causes them to wander for a long time through the cycle of saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato">AN 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to wander mindfully and without clinging after they have understood the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> (ādīnava) of craving as the very origin of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.9/en/sujato">AN 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato"><b>bad person</b></a> as someone who lacks faith, lacks a sense of shame, lacks fear of wrongdoing, has little learning, is lazy, is muddled in mindfulness, and is unwise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato">AN 4.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato"><b>worse than a bad person</b></a> if they not only lack faith, shame, and wisdom themselves, but also actively encourage others to be faithless, shameless, and unwise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato">AN 4.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato"><b>good person</b></a> is characterized by seven specific qualities: having faith, a sense of shame, fear of wrongdoing, being learned, being energetic, being mindful, and being wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato">AN 4.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato"><b>better than a good person</b></a> as one who possesses wholesome qualities such as faith and energy, and also encourages others to develop those same wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato">AN 4.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato"><b>lazy</b></a> and encouraging others to be lazy is one of the traits that classifies someone as being "worse than a bad person"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato">AN 4.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person becomes "better than a good person" when they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a> in the teachings and additionally encourage others to also become learned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato">AN 4.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato"><b>fear of wrongdoing</b></a> as a key characteristic of a good person, whereas lacking this fear contributes to being classified as a bad person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato">AN 4.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato"><b>mindful</b></a> and encourages others to establish mindfulness is considered "better than a good person"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato">AN 4.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized individuals who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato"><b>unwise</b></a> and lead others toward being unwise as "worse than a bad person"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato">AN 4.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> and encouraging others to acquire faith is a fundamental requirement for being recognized as "better than a good person"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.202/en/sujato">AN 4.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>future perils</b></a> that have not yet arisen but will eventually emerge, and he urged monks to be vigilant and strive to abandon them before they manifest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted a time when monks would become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>desirous of fine robes</b></a>, causing them to abandon the practice of wearing rag-robes and living in remote forest dwellings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that monks seeking high-quality clothing would eventually move to villages and royal capitals and engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>improper searches</b></a> that are unsuitable for their vocation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>fine almsfood</b></a> as a future danger that would lead monks to give up the practice of alms-gathering and solitary living? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha foresaw monks settling in populated areas specifically to search for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>delicious tastes</b></a>, which he classified as the second peril that would threaten the spiritual community in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk’s desire for exquisite food would lead him to commit <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>unfitting acts</b></a> simply to secure better meals, pulling him away from the path of Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a future peril where monks, being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>desirous of fine dwellings</b></a>, would abandon the ascetic tradition of living at the foot of a tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that monks seeking comfortable lodging would desert <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>remote lodgings</b></a> in the jungle and move to cities, engaging in improper ways to obtain better housing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that monks living in close <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>association with nuns</b></a> and female probationers would eventually find themselves dissatisfied with the celibate holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the risk of a monk committing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>defiled offense</b></a> or returning to a lower life as a direct consequence of improper association with female practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted monks would live in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>close association</b></a> with monastery attendants and male novices, leading to a focus on material accumulation rather than spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that monks associating too closely with monastery staff would become devoted to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>storing up and consuming</b></a> various articles, contrary to their monastic training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha foresaw a time when monks would even make <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>gross hints</b></a> or indications regarding land and crops, demonstrating a preoccupation with worldly resources? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that monks should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>be vigilant</b></a> toward these five perils while they are still unarisen so they can prevent them from taking hold in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the transition from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>forest-dwelling</b></a> to living in villages for the sake of material comfort as a significant future peril for the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the abandonment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>rag-robe wearing</b></a> in favor of high-quality clothing was the first sign of a monk falling away from his training in this specific discourse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that monks who prioritize <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>material gains</b></a> like robes, food, and lodging would eventually abandon their dedication to the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged monks to recognize these dangers and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato"><b>strive for their abandonment</b></a> as a means of protecting the future of the teaching and discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.80/en/sujato">AN 5.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who engages in impure bodily, verbal, and mental conduct to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato"><b>charnel ground</b></a> because they share five specific undesirable characteristics? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato">AN 5.249</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is just as impure as a charnel ground when they are possessed of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato"><b>impure bodily conduct</b></a>, impure verbal conduct, and impure mental conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato">AN 5.249</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened the foul smell of a charnel ground to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato"><b>bad reputation</b></a> that spreads about a person who acts, speaks, and thinks in unwholesome ways? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato">AN 5.249</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as a charnel ground is perilous and scary, an unvirtuous person is one whom <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato"><b>virtuous fellow monks</b></a> will keep far away from and avoid? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato">AN 5.249</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a charnel ground being an abode for wild beasts and non-humans to an unethical person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato"><b>lives together</b></a> with other people of similar bad character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato">AN 5.249</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha equated a charnel ground being a place of much weeping to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato"><b>distress</b></a> felt by virtuous practitioners when they are forced to live in the company of a person with impure conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato">AN 5.249</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato"><b>five drawbacks</b></a> of a charnel ground—being impure, foul-smelling, perilous, an abode for wild beasts, and a place of weeping—to illustrate the nature of a person of bad conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato">AN 5.249</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person as being like a charnel ground if their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato"><b>impure verbal conduct</b></a> mirrors the foulness and impurity found in a place where the dead are cast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato">AN 5.249</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if a person's outward actions are hidden, possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato"><b>impure mental conduct</b></a> makes them comparable to a charnel ground in its impurity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato">AN 5.249</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when virtuous monks see a person of bad character, they feel a sense of suffering and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato"><b>lamentation</b></a> similar to the weeping of people at a charnel ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.249/en/sujato">AN 5.249</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the spiritual life to a farmer who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato"><b>three urgent duties</b></a>, which include quickly plowing and harrowing the field, planting the seeds, and managing the irrigation of the water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato">AN 3.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a farmer lacks the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato"><b>psychic power</b></a> or authority to command his crops to sprout today, bear fruit tomorrow, and ripen the day after, as the process must happen naturally over time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato">AN 3.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato"><b>three urgent duties</b></a> of a monk as the undertaking of the training in higher virtuous behavior, the training in higher mind, and the training in higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato">AN 3.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk cannot simply wish for his mind to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato"><b>liberated from the taints</b></a> through non-clinging on a specific schedule, just as a farmer cannot force his grain to ripen instantly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato">AN 3.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the training in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato"><b>higher virtuous behavior</b></a> is a fundamental and urgent task that a practitioner must undertake with diligence to progress toward enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato">AN 3.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified the training in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato"><b>higher mind</b></a>, which refers to the development of deep concentration and mental discipline, as one of the three essential and urgent labors of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato">AN 3.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that consistent training in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato"><b>higher wisdom</b></a> is the final urgent duty that eventually leads to the mind's liberation from taints through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato">AN 3.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his followers to develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato"><b>keen desire</b></a> for undertaking the three trainings, ensuring they have the necessary motivation to perform their spiritual duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato">AN 3.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of crops ripening due to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato"><b>seasonal changes</b></a> to illustrate that spiritual liberation occurs at its own proper time when the necessary conditions of training have been met? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato">AN 3.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the necessity of a monk's practice to a farmer who must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato"><b>plant the seeds</b></a> in a well-prepared field, illustrating that effort must be applied correctly before a harvest of enlightenment can be expected? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.92/en/sujato">AN 3.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how wanderers of other sects prescribe <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>three types of seclusion</b></a>, which consist specifically of seclusion in robes, seclusion in alms-food, and seclusion in lodging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that wanderers of other sects practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>seclusion in robes</b></a> by wearing unusual materials such as hemp, shrouds, rags from a dust heap, or garments made of bark and grass? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>seclusion in alms-food</b></a> among other sects as including diets of greens, millet, water plants, or even cow dung and forest roots? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that wanderers of other sects find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>seclusion in lodging</b></a> by staying in remote or harsh locations like the foot of a tree, a cemetery, the open air, or a heap of straw? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that in his own teaching and discipline, a monk’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>three types of seclusion</b></a> are being virtuous and secluded from immorality, having right view and being secluded from wrong view, and having destroyed the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is considered truly secluded when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and has abandoned immoral behavior, thereby becoming spiritually secluded from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk attains a vital form of seclusion when he possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> and has abandoned and become secluded from wrong view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>one whose taints are destroyed</b></a> as being truly secluded, because he has abandoned and become secluded from all mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk who is virtuous, has right view, and has destroyed the taints is one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>reached the peak</b></a>, reached the essence, and is pure and established in the essence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a farmer who must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>reap the grain</b></a> quickly and then collect it to illustrate the systematic process of reaching spiritual purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the monk’s spiritual progress to a farmer who must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>thresh the grain</b></a> and remove the straw after hauling and heaping the harvest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened the refinement of a monk's mind to a farmer who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>removes the chaff</b></a> and winnows the grain to ensure it becomes clean and usable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as a farmer must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>remove the husks</b></a> by pounding the grain, a monk must undergo specific mental trainings to reach the essence of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that some wanderers of other sects seek seclusion by wearing unusual garments, such as blankets made of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>human hair</b></a> or horse hair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that some ascetics of other sects wear garments made of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>owl feathers</b></a> or animal skins as a form of robe seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that wanderers of other sects might limit their diet to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>rice scum</b></a> or the flour from oil-seeds as a rigorous form of religious seclusion in alms-food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who has attained the destruction of taints to a farmer's grain that has become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>pure and established in the essence</b></a> after being threshed, winnowed, and pounded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that true seclusion in his teaching involves more than just physical isolation, requiring one to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato"><b>secluded from wrong view</b></a> by adopting right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.93/en/sujato">AN 3.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three distinct types of assemblies among his followers, which he categorized as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato"><b>foremost assembly</b></a>, the divided assembly, and the harmonious assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato">AN 3.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato"><b>foremost assembly</b></a> as a group where the senior monks are not luxurious or lax, do not shirk their duties, lead the way in seclusion, and arouse energy for attaining the unattained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato">AN 3.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that in a healthy spiritual community, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato"><b>later generations</b></a> follow the example of senior monks who are diligent and dedicated to reaching what has not yet been reached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato">AN 3.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato"><b>divided assembly</b></a> as one where monks live in quarrels, brawls, and disputes, stabbing one another with verbal daggers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato">AN 3.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato"><b>harmonious assembly</b></a> as one where monks live cordially and without disputes, viewing each other with eyes of affection and blending together like milk and water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato">AN 3.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when monks live in harmony and mutual appreciation, they generate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato"><b>much merit</b></a> through their peaceful conduct and lack of contention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato">AN 3.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that monks living in a harmonious assembly dwell in a "divine dwelling," specifically the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind by altruistic joy</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato">AN 3.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a monk filled with joy, rapture arises, and for one with a rapturous mind, the body becomes tranquil, which leads to the experience of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato">AN 3.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one who is happy, the mind becomes concentrated, and this <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> is a natural result of a tranquil and joyful state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato">AN 3.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato"><b>analogy of rain</b></a> on a mountain top—which fills the crevices, then the ponds, then the rivers, and finally the ocean—to illustrate how the spiritual joy of a harmonious community eventually fulfills the goal of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato">AN 3.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the phrase <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato"><b>verbal daggers</b></a> to describe the sharp and harmful speech used by monks in a divided assembly who are caught up in disputes and brawls? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.95/en/sujato">AN 3.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>four types of persons</b></a> existing in the world based on their current circumstances and their future destinations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is "darkness bound for darkness" as someone born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>low family</b></a> who also engages in misconduct of body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed families such as the Chandalas, bamboo-workers, hunters, chariot-makers, and sweepers as examples of birth into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>destitute family</b></a> where food and clothing are hard to find? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is physically deformed, ill, or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>lacking basic necessities</b></a> like food and shelter is considered to be in a state of "darkness"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if a person born into hardship also practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>misconduct of body</b></a>, speech, and mind, they will be reborn in a plane of misery or hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the person who is "darkness bound for light" as one born in a low family with physical afflictions who nevertheless practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>good conduct</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even a person born with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>physical deformities</b></a> like being blind, lame, or paralyzed can be bound for a good destination through virtuous actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a person who is "light bound for darkness" as someone born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>high family</b></a> with great wealth who subsequently chooses to engage in evil deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified birth into a wealthy Khattiya, Brahmin, or householder family as a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>light</b></a> in terms of one's current existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who is handsome, attractive, and possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>abundant wealth</b></a> can still be bound for hell if they practice misconduct in body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the person who is "light bound for light" as one born into a high-status family with great wealth who also practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>good conduct of body</b></a>, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having access to luxuries like garlands, scents, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>vehicles</b></a> is a sign of a person currently living in a state of "light"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>mental misconduct</b></a>, when combined with bodily and verbal misconduct, leads a person toward a bad destination or the lower world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>bound for light</b></a> to describe someone who, upon the breakup of the body after death, is reborn in a good destination or a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the person "light bound for light" as someone who moves from a life of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>beauty and prosperity</b></a> to an even better state after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that regardless of one's birth, it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>misconduct of speech</b></a> and other actions that determine whether one is bound for darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person born with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato"><b>extreme physical beauty</b></a> and grace is considered to be in a state of "light" in their current life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.85/en/sujato">AN 4.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>four types of persons</b></a> who are ascetics existing in the world, classified as the unshakeable ascetic, the white lotus ascetic, the pink lotus ascetic, and the delicate ascetic among ascetics? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>unshakeable ascetic</b></a> as a trainee who is still on the path, practicing and dwelling in the aspiration for the unexcelled security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the unshakeable ascetic to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>eldest son</b></a> of an anointed Khattiya king, who, though not yet consecrated, is considered unshakeable because he is certain to succeed the throne? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>white lotus ascetic</b></a> as one who has attained the destruction of the taints and dwells in the taintless liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom, but does not personally experience the eight liberations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>pink lotus ascetic</b></a> is a person who has attained the destruction of the taints and also dwells having personally experienced the eight liberations with the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>delicate ascetic among ascetics</b></a> to describe a person who is highly supported by others and receives the majority of their requisites, such as robes and almsfood, through being invited or requested? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a delicate ascetic is someone whose fellow practitioners treat them with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>agreeable bodily actions</b></a>, verbal actions, and mental actions, and who rarely experience disagreeable treatment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a delicate ascetic is one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>seldom ill</b></a>, suffering very little from afflictions caused by bile, phlegm, wind, or the change of seasons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified himself as the person who could most rightly be called the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>delicate ascetic among ascetics</b></a> because of the abundance of requisites he received and his mastery over meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the delicate ascetic as one who gains the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a>—which are higher mental states providing a pleasant dwelling in this very life—whenever they want, without any difficulty or trouble? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a delicate ascetic has realized for themselves the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>, reaching a state of liberation through direct knowledge in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that for a delicate ascetic, even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>medicines and provisions</b></a> for the sick are mostly received by request rather than being unrequested? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for the delicate ascetic, fellow monks present <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>agreeable gifts</b></a> and offerings much more frequently than disagreeable ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that illnesses arising from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>internal imbalances</b></a>, such as a combination of humors or improper care, do not often arise for the person categorized as a delicate ascetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified the unshakeable ascetic as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>trainee</b></a> (sekha), distinguishing them from the other three types who have reached the end of the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> (cetovimutti) to describe the attainment shared by the white lotus, pink lotus, and delicate ascetics? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>eight liberations</b></a> (vimokkha) are a specific meditative attainment that distinguishes the "pink lotus" ascetic from the "white lotus" ascetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that the delicate ascetic among ascetics receives <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>lodgings</b></a> (senāsana) mostly by request, illustrating a life of relative ease and support? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that for the delicate ascetic, fellow practitioners act with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato"><b>agreeable mental actions</b></a>, signifying a harmonious and supportive communal environment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.87/en/sujato">AN 4.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four types of ascetics in the world, categorized as the unshakeable ascetic, the white lotus ascetic, the pink lotus ascetic, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.88/en/sujato"><b>delicate ascetic among ascetics</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.88/en/sujato">AN 4.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.88/en/sujato"><b>unshakeable ascetic</b></a> as a stream-enterer who has destroyed the three fetters and is no longer subject to rebirth in the lower worlds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.88/en/sujato">AN 4.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.88/en/sujato"><b>white lotus ascetic</b></a> is a once-returner who, through the destruction of three fetters and the weakening of greed, hatred, and delusion, will return to this world only once more to make an end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.88/en/sujato">AN 4.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.88/en/sujato"><b>pink lotus ascetic</b></a> as a non-returner who has destroyed the five lower fetters and will attain final Nibbāna in a higher realm without ever returning to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.88/en/sujato">AN 4.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.88/en/sujato"><b>delicate ascetic</b></a> among ascetics is one who has attained the destruction of the taints and dwells in the taintless liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.88/en/sujato">AN 4.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified people into four groups based on their own character and the character of their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.91/en/sujato"><b>followers</b></a>, using the terms "asura" for the immoral and "deva" for the virtuous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.91/en/sujato">AN 4.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.91/en/sujato"><b>asura with an asura retinue</b></a>" as an immoral person of bad character whose circle of followers is also immoral and of bad character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.91/en/sujato">AN 4.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who is an "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.91/en/sujato"><b>asura with a deva retinue</b></a>" as someone who is personally immoral but whose followers are virtuous and of good character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.91/en/sujato">AN 4.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person of good character and virtue whose followers are unfortunately immoral and of bad character is known as a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.91/en/sujato"><b>deva with an asura retinue</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.91/en/sujato">AN 4.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the ideal combination as a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.91/en/sujato"><b>deva with a deva retinue</b></a>," which refers to a virtuous person of good character whose followers are also virtuous and well-behaved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.91/en/sujato">AN 4.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.88/en/sujato"><b>spontaneously reborn</b></a> to describe the pink lotus ascetic (non-returner), who attains final Nibbāna in the pure abodes after passing away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.88/en/sujato">AN 4.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha realized through his own direct knowledge and then proclaimed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>four kinds of kamma</b></a>, categorized as dark with dark results, bright with bright results, dark-and-bright with dark-and-bright results, and neither-dark-nor-bright? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>dark kamma with dark results</b></a> as the performance of afflicted bodily, verbal, and mental volitional formations that lead to a purely painful existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who generates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>afflicted volitional formations</b></a> through body, speech, and mind will be reborn in an afflicted world where they encounter afflicted contacts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the experience of beings who perform dark kamma to that of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>hell beings</b></a>, because they experience feelings that are exclusively painful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>bright kamma with bright results</b></a> as the performance of unafflicted bodily, verbal, and mental volitional formations that lead to rebirth in an unafflicted world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who generate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>unafflicted volitional formations</b></a> encounter unafflicted contacts and experience purely pleasant feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the experience of purely pleasant feelings resulting from bright kamma is similar to that of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>Subhakiṇha devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>dark-and-bright kamma</b></a> as a type of action where a person performs volitional formations that are a mixture of both afflicted and unafflicted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, performing mixed kamma leads to rebirth in a world that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>both afflicted and unafflicted</b></a>, where one experiences mixed pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>human beings</b></a> experience a mixture of pleasure and pain because they are among the beings reborn through dark-and-bright kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>some devas</b></a> and some beings in the lower world experience mixed results of pleasure and pain because of their previous dark-and-bright kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there is a specific kind of kamma that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>neither dark nor bright</b></a> with neither-dark-nor-bright results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described neither-dark-nor-bright kamma as the type of action that leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>destruction of kamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined neither-dark-nor-bright kamma as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>volition to abandon</b></a> dark kamma, bright kamma, and dark-and-bright kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>mixed pleasure and pain</b></a> as the specific result for beings like humans who perform both afflicted and unafflicted actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, being touched by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>unafflicted contacts</b></a> is the mechanism by which beings who perform bright kamma experience exclusively pleasant feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that generating afflicted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>mental formations</b></a>, alongside afflicted bodily and verbal ones, is what leads to rebirth in a purely painful world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the four types of kamma were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato"><b>personally realized</b></a> by him through his own direct knowledge before he made them known to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.233/en/sujato">AN 4.233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato"><b>bad person</b></a> as an individual who engages in the ten unwholesome courses of action, including the destruction of life, stealing, sexual misconduct, and holding wrong view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato">AN 4.204</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato"><b>one more bad than the bad person</b></a> is someone who not only personally commits unwholesome acts like killing or lying but also encourages others to engage in those same behaviors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato">AN 4.204</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato"><b>good person</b></a> as one who abstains from harming others, from taking what is not given, and from false, divisive, harsh, or idle speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato">AN 4.204</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato"><b>one better than the good person</b></a> as an individual who personally practices wholesome conduct and also actively urges others to practice that same restraint and virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato">AN 4.204</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that harboring <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato"><b>covetousness</b></a> and encouraging others to be covetous are specific traits that categorize someone as being "more bad than the bad person"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato">AN 4.204</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person is considered "more bad than the bad person" if they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> and additionally incite others to also harbor ill will toward others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato">AN 4.204</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a "better than the good person" as one who is personally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato"><b>without ill will</b></a> and also encourages others to develop a mind free from ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato">AN 4.204</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> as a characteristic of a bad person, especially when they also influence others to adopt those same incorrect views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato">AN 4.204</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> who also encourages others to establish themselves in right view is considered "one better than the good person"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.204/en/sujato">AN 4.204</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.211/en/sujato"><b>immoral monk</b></a> or a nun of bad character is considered a polluter of the assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.211/en/sujato">AN 4.211</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.211/en/sujato"><b>virtuous layman</b></a> or laywoman who possesses good character is someone who beautifies the assembly of followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.211/en/sujato">AN 4.211</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four types of people as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.211/en/sujato"><b>polluters of an assembly</b></a>: a monk, nun, layman, or laywoman who is immoral and of bad character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.211/en/sujato">AN 4.211</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.213/en/sujato"><b>ingratitude</b></a> and unthankfulness, when combined with bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct, lead a person to be reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.213/en/sujato">AN 4.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.213/en/sujato"><b>gratitude</b></a> and thankfulness, and who practices good conduct in body, speech, and mind, is reborn in a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.213/en/sujato">AN 4.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.213/en/sujato"><b>bodily misconduct</b></a> is one of the four qualities that can cause a person to be "deposited in hell"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.213/en/sujato">AN 4.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.213/en/sujato"><b>mental misconduct</b></a> as a key factor that, alongside ingratitude, leads to a miserable destination after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.213/en/sujato">AN 4.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.213/en/sujato"><b>good verbal conduct</b></a> and gratitude as two of the four qualities that lead an individual to a happy rebirth in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.213/en/sujato">AN 4.213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an ignoble way of speaking as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.217/en/sujato"><b>claiming to have seen</b></a> something that one has not actually seen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.217/en/sujato">AN 4.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person speaks falsely if they claim to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.217/en/sujato"><b>heard</b></a>, sensed, or cognized something when they have not actually done so? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.217/en/sujato">AN 4.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble way of speaking involves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.217/en/sujato"><b>saying one has not seen</b></a> something when one truly has not seen it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.217/en/sujato">AN 4.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato"><b>bad person</b></a> as an individual who destroys life, takes what is not given, engages in sexual misconduct, and speaks falsely, divisively, harshly, and with idle chatter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato">AN 4.203</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato"><b>one more bad than the bad person</b></a> is someone who not only engages in unwholesome actions like killing and stealing themselves but also encourages others to do the same? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato">AN 4.203</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato"><b>good person</b></a> is characterized by their personal abstinence from the destruction of life, from taking what is not given, from sexual misconduct, and from false, divisive, harsh, and idle speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato">AN 4.203</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato"><b>one better than the good person</b></a> as an individual who both abstains from unwholesome conduct personally and actively encourages others to also practice those same abstinences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato">AN 4.203</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified someone as being "more bad than the bad person" if they engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a> while simultaneously inciting others to speak in a way that causes division? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato">AN 4.203</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who personally uses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a> and additionally urges others to speak harshly is considered "more bad" than one who only speaks harshly for themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato">AN 4.203</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a>, when combined with the act of encouraging others to also engage in meaningless talk, makes a person "one more bad than the bad person"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato">AN 4.203</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person becomes "better than the good person" by refraining from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato"><b>destruction of life</b></a> and also encouraging others to refrain from killing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato">AN 4.203</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized someone as "one better than the good person" if they abstain from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato"><b>taking what is not given</b></a> and also encourage others to abstain from stealing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato">AN 4.203</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a person who abstains from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> and also encourages others to practice the same restraint is considered "one better than the good person"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato">AN 4.203</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that excellence in speech involves not only personally abstaining from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato"><b>false speech</b></a> but also actively encouraging others to abstain from lying? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.203/en/sujato">AN 4.203</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught an elder monk is disliked and disagreeable to his companions in the holy life if he becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.81/en/sujato"><b>excited by things</b></a> that excite? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.81/en/sujato">AN 5.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an elder monk loses the respect and esteem of his fellow practitioners when he becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.81/en/sujato"><b>irritated by things</b></a> that irritate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.81/en/sujato">AN 5.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated an elder monk becomes dear and agreeable to his companions when he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.81/en/sujato"><b>does not become confused</b></a> by things that are confusing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.81/en/sujato">AN 5.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that an elder monk is held in high regard and respected when he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.81/en/sujato"><b>does not become intoxicated</b></a> by things that intoxicate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.81/en/sujato">AN 5.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.82/en/sujato"><b>not free from lust</b></a>, hatred, and delusion are qualities that make an elder monk disagreeable and not esteemed by his companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.82/en/sujato">AN 5.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an elder monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.82/en/sujato"><b>insulting and domineering</b></a> will not be liked or respected by his fellow monks in the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.82/en/sujato">AN 5.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an elder monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.82/en/sujato"><b>free from hatred</b></a> and delusion is considered dear, agreeable, and worthy of respect by his companions in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.82/en/sujato">AN 5.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an elder monk as being highly esteemed when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.82/en/sujato"><b>not insulting</b></a> and not domineering toward others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.82/en/sujato">AN 5.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an elder monk is disliked and not respected if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.83/en/sujato"><b>deceitful</b></a> and a talker who flatters others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.83/en/sujato">AN 5.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an elder monk who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.83/en/sujato"><b>belittler</b></a> or one who uses signs and omens to manipulate others for requisites is not esteemed by his companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.83/en/sujato">AN 5.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that an elder monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.83/en/sujato"><b>pursues gain with gain</b></a> is viewed as disagreeable and uninspiring by those living the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.83/en/sujato">AN 5.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised an elder monk as being dear and respected when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.83/en/sujato"><b>not a sign-reader</b></a> and does not resort to deceptive ways to obtain requisites? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.83/en/sujato">AN 5.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught an elder monk is dear and agreeable to his companions in the holy life when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.84/en/sujato"><b>possessing faith</b></a> in the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.84/en/sujato">AN 5.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an elder monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.84/en/sujato"><b>lacks a sense of shame</b></a> regarding misconduct is considered disagreeable and is not respected by his fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.84/en/sujato">AN 5.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated an elder monk becomes inspiring and worthy of respect to his fellow monks when he possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.84/en/sujato"><b>fear of wrongdoing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.84/en/sujato">AN 5.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an elder monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.84/en/sujato"><b>lazy</b></a> and lacks energy becomes disliked and is not held in high esteem by his companions in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.84/en/sujato">AN 5.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an elder monk as being dear and agreeable to those he lives with when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.84/en/sujato"><b>wise</b></a> and possesses understanding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.84/en/sujato">AN 5.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught an elder monk is disliked and considered disagreeable by his companions if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.85/en/sujato"><b>unable to endure forms</b></a> that are perceived through the eye? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.85/en/sujato">AN 5.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an elder monk becomes dear and agreeable to his fellow practitioners when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.85/en/sujato"><b>able to endure sounds</b></a> that he hears? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.85/en/sujato">AN 5.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated an elder monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.85/en/sujato"><b>unable to endure smells</b></a> is not respected or held in high regard by his companions in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.85/en/sujato">AN 5.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that an elder monk is considered inspiring and worthy of respect when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.85/en/sujato"><b>able to endure tastes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.85/en/sujato">AN 5.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained an elder monk is disagreeable to his companions if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.85/en/sujato"><b>unable to endure tactile objects</b></a> that come into contact with the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.85/en/sujato">AN 5.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught an elder monk is dear and respected when he is virtuous and dwells <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.87/en/sujato"><b>restrained by the Pātimokkha</b></a>, seeing danger in even the slightest faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.87/en/sujato">AN 5.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an elder monk is highly esteemed when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.87/en/sujato"><b>learned in the Dhamma</b></a> and has accumulated teachings that are good in the beginning, middle, and end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.87/en/sujato">AN 5.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated an elder monk is dear and agreeable to his companions when he is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.87/en/sujato"><b>good speaker</b></a> with a delivery that is polite, clear, and easy to understand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.87/en/sujato">AN 5.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an elder monk as inspiring and worthy of respect when he can obtain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.87/en/sujato"><b>four jhānas</b></a>, which provide a pleasant dwelling in this very life, at will and without difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.87/en/sujato">AN 5.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that an elder monk is held in high regard when he has realized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.87/en/sujato"><b>taintless liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom through the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.87/en/sujato">AN 5.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an elder monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.84/en/sujato"><b>lacking in fear of wrongdoing</b></a> is disliked and not held in high regard by his companions in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.84/en/sujato">AN 5.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised an elder monk as being dear and respected when he possesses a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.87/en/sujato"><b>polite delivery</b></a> of speech that is free from flaws and makes the meaning of the Dhamma clear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.87/en/sujato">AN 5.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a senior monk of long standing and long ordination can act for the harm and suffering of many people if he possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> and perverted vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato">AN 5.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato"><b>famous elder monk</b></a> who is well-known and followed by many laypeople and monastics can lead a great number of people away from the true Dhamma if he holds wrong views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato">AN 5.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an elder monk who is a frequent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato"><b>recipient of requisites</b></a>—such as robes, alms-food, and medicine—can cause great misfortune for devas and humans if he lacks right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato">AN 5.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even a highly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato"><b>learned elder monk</b></a> who has memorized many teachings can lead many people into false Dhamma if he has not personally penetrated those teachings with right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato">AN 5.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a prominent elder monk has wrong view, many people follow his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato"><b>example</b></a>, which leads them to be established in what is not the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato">AN 5.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a senior monk of long ordination acts for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato"><b>benefit of many</b></a> when he possesses right view and helps pull people away from false teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato">AN 5.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a famous elder monk with a large following as a source of happiness for devas and humans, provided that he is established in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> and correct vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato">AN 5.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato"><b>senior monk</b></a> who is respected and well-provided with requisites serves the welfare of the world when he uses his influence to establish people in the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato">AN 5.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that an elder monk who is highly learned and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato"><b>well penetrated</b></a> the teachings with right view is a person who practices for the happiness of many? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato">AN 5.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the influence of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato"><b>renowned elder monk</b></a> is so significant that his possession of right view or wrong view determines whether his many followers are led toward or away from the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato">AN 5.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that an elder monk who holds <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato"><b>correct vision</b></a> acts for the purpose and happiness of a great multitude, including both devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato">AN 5.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato"><b>true Dhamma</b></a> as being preserved and promoted when senior, famous, and learned monks are established in right view and guide their followers accordingly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.88/en/sujato">AN 5.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are five distinct dangers in personal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato"><b>devotion to a person</b></a>, as excessive attachment to a single individual can lead a follower to eventually fall away from the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato">AN 5.250</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that if a monk you are devoted to is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato"><b>suspended by the Sangha</b></a> for an offense, you might react with resentment toward other monks and stop visiting them to hear the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato">AN 5.250</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a person one is devoted to is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato"><b>made to sit at the end</b></a> of the assembly as a disciplinary measure, the devotee may become full of lack of confidence in the rest of the monkhood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato">AN 5.250</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha cautioned that if a teacher or person of devotion <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato"><b>departs for another region</b></a>, a follower who is too personally attached may stop associating with other monks and consequently lose the opportunity to hear the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato">AN 5.250</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that if a monk someone is devoted to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato"><b>disrobes and wanders away</b></a>, the devotee might become so discouraged that they stop seeking out other spiritual friends, leading to their own spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato">AN 5.250</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato"><b>death of a beloved person</b></a> as a significant danger for those with personal devotion, as the grief and loss can cause them to withdraw from the community and fall away from the Good Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato">AN 5.250</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that a person who stops associating with the community of monks because their favorite teacher is gone will eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato"><b>fail to hear the Dhamma</b></a>, which is essential for maintaining one's path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato">AN 5.250</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that having an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato"><b>excess of lack of confidence</b></a> in other monks—triggered by the disciplinary fate of a single admired person—is a primary reason why some practitioners fall away from the Good Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.250/en/sujato">AN 5.250</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha specified that a monk should only grant higher ordination to others if he himself is fully endowed with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.251/en/sujato"><b>asekha aggregate of virtue</b></a>, representing the moral conduct of one beyond training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.251/en/sujato">AN 5.251</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk must possess the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.251/en/sujato"><b>asekha aggregate of concentration</b></a>—the mental stillness of one who has completed the training—before he is considered qualified to ordain new monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.251/en/sujato">AN 5.251</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that being endowed with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.251/en/sujato"><b>asekha aggregate of wisdom</b></a> is a mandatory requirement for a monk to take on the responsibility of giving higher ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.251/en/sujato">AN 5.251</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha included the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.251/en/sujato"><b>asekha aggregate of liberation</b></a> as a necessary spiritual quality for any monk who wishes to act as a preceptor and guide others into the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.251/en/sujato">AN 5.251</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a monk is truly fit to give higher ordination only when he possesses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.251/en/sujato"><b>asekha aggregate of knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.251/en/sujato">AN 5.251</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a practitioner is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.264/en/sujato"><b>incapable of entering the first jhāna</b></a> if they have not yet abandoned five specific types of stinginess and a lack of gratitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.264/en/sujato">AN 5.264</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person must specifically abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.264/en/sujato"><b>stinginess regarding dwellings</b></a> in order to be capable of entering and dwelling in the meditative state of the first jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.264/en/sujato">AN 5.264</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.264/en/sujato"><b>stinginess regarding gains</b></a> as a major mental obstruction that prevents a monk from attaining higher states of concentration like the first jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.264/en/sujato">AN 5.264</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that abandoning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.264/en/sujato"><b>stinginess regarding praise</b></a> and status is an essential prerequisite for a monk to successfully enter and dwell in the first jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.264/en/sujato">AN 5.264</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.264/en/sujato"><b>ingratitude and unthankfulness</b></a> as key unwholesome qualities that must be abandoned before one can become capable of achieving the first jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.264/en/sujato">AN 5.264</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Mahānāma the Sakyan that a noble disciple who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>attained the fruit</b></a> and understood the teaching should frequently dwell in six specific recollections? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that when a noble disciple recollects the qualities of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a>, their mind is not obsessed at that time by lust, hatred, or delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner who focuses on the enlightened qualities of the Teacher gains a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>straight mind</b></a>, which leads to inspiration in the meaning and the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the joy arising from a straight mind leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>gladness</b></a>, which in turn generates rapture and physical tranquility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a noble disciple practicing recollection as one who dwells <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>poised among a crooked generation</b></a>, remaining unafflicted even while others are afflicted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a noble disciple who has entered the "stream of the Dhamma" often develops <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Dhamma</b></a>, reflecting that the teaching is well-expounded, visible here and now, and timeless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>ehipassiko</b></a>, meaning it invites one to "come and see" for oneself, rather than requiring blind faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when a disciple recollects the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>Saṅgha</b></a>, they are reflecting on the community of noble ones who practice the good way, the straight way, and the true way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the noble Saṅgha as consisting of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>four pairs of persons</b></a>, or eight individual types of noble beings, who are worthy of gifts and hospitality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha referred to the community of noble disciples as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world, making them the most worthy recipients of offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged disciples to recollect their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>virtues</b></a>, specifically those that are unbroken, spotless, and leading to concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described the virtues of a noble disciple as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>free</b></a>—meaning they are not bound by craving or views—and praised by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>recollection of generosity</b></a> where one reflects on being dwelling at home with a mind devoid of the stain of stinginess? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha highlighted that a noble disciple should feel fortunate and say, "It is a gain for me," because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>devoted to charity</b></a> and delight in sharing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>recollection of deities</b></a>, where a practitioner reflects on the faith and virtue that led various devas to be reborn in heavenly realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha suggested that a disciple should compare themselves to the deities, noting that they too possess the same qualities of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>faith, virtue, and learning</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, reflecting on one's own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> in comparison to the deities helps to straighten the mind and remove the hindrances of lust and hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that for one who has a straight mind through recollection, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>rapture arises</b></a>, which leads to the calming of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>physical tranquility</b></a> as a prerequisite for experiencing the happiness that allows the mind to become concentrated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a noble disciple who practices these six recollections is said to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>entered the stream of the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>Tāvatiṃsā and Yāma devas</b></a> among the various classes of deities that a disciple should keep in mind during meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a disciple who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>open-handed</b></a> and "ready to be asked" for help develops a mind that is straight and focused on the Good Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato"><b>inspiration in the Dhamma</b></a> (dhammaveda) is gained directly through the process of straightening one's mind toward a wholesome object? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.10/en/sujato">AN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are six <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>qualities to be remembered</b></a> which are essential for fostering harmony and mutual respect within a spiritual community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha advised that a practitioner should maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness in bodily action</b></a> toward their fellow practitioners, practicing this both openly in public and privately in secret? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, maintaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness in verbal action</b></a> toward one's companions is a fundamental principle that leads to a cooperative and peaceful communal life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized the importance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness in mental action</b></a>, teaching that one's private thoughts toward fellow monks should be rooted in goodwill to ensure a healthy community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk should be an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>unreserved sharer</b></a> of whatever righteous gains they receive, ensuring that even the small amount of food in their bowl is shared with virtuous companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>righteous gains</b></a>—those obtained in accordance with the Dhamma—as something that should be enjoyed in common with others rather than kept for oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described certain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>unbroken virtues</b></a>, which are spotless and praised by the wise, as a necessary quality for practitioners to hold in common with their peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>virtues leading to concentration</b></a> is a key social glue that allows a spiritual community to dwell together in peace and mutual appreciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>noble view</b></a>—one that is "leading out" toward the complete destruction of suffering—is a vital principle that practitioners must share to maintain unity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a view which is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>emancipating</b></a> and leads to the end of all suffering is a core requirement for a monk to be in harmony with his fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that these six principles are not just for personal growth but are specifically for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.12/en/sujato"><b>creating love</b></a> and affection between members of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.12/en/sujato">AN 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that practicing these six qualities is the primary way of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.12/en/sujato"><b>creating respect</b></a> among companions, ensuring they value one another's presence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.12/en/sujato">AN 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified these six qualities as being conducive to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.12/en/sujato"><b>solidarity</b></a>, helping members of a group support and look out for one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.12/en/sujato">AN 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the consistent practice of loving-kindness and shared virtue leads directly to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.12/en/sujato"><b>non-dispute</b></a>, preventing internal conflicts within a community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.12/en/sujato">AN 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described these six "qualities to be remembered" as being essential for the attainment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.12/en/sujato"><b>concord</b></a> and harmony among people living together? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.12/en/sujato">AN 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the ultimate communal result of following these six principles is the achievement of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.12/en/sujato"><b>unity</b></a>, where the group functions as a single, harmonious body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.12/en/sujato">AN 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that sharing food—even just the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>contents of a bowl</b></a>—with virtuous companions is a powerful act that builds communal bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the virtues a practitioner shares with others should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>productive of freedom</b></a> and unclouded by unwholesome desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who dwells in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>commonality of view</b></a> with his peers regarding the path to ending suffering will avoid major disagreements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha characterized the six principles of cordiality as things that must be maintained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato"><b>both openly and privately</b></a> to truly transform a group into a unified Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.11/en/sujato">AN 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who is worthy of gifts and offerings to a king’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato"><b>thoroughbred horse</b></a>, noting that both must possess three specific qualities—beauty, strength, and speed—to be truly exceptional? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato">AN 3.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the spiritual "beauty" of a monk as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato"><b>accomplished in virtue</b></a>, which involves living restrained by the rules of the Patimokkha and maintaining proper conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato">AN 3.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk possesses the quality of "beauty" when he trains himself in the rules of conduct and remains habitually vigilant by seeing danger in even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato"><b>slight faults</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato">AN 3.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk’s spiritual "strength" as the constant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato"><b>arousal of energy</b></a> dedicated to abandoning unwholesome qualities and acquiring wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato">AN 3.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is considered "strong" when he is firm and persistent in his practice, never shunning the duty of cultivating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato">AN 3.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk’s spiritual "speed" to his ability to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> exactly as they are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato">AN 3.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk possesses the quality of "speed" when he understands, in accordance with reality, the nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> and its origin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato">AN 3.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk has spiritual "speed" when he clearly recognizes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> and the practical path that leads to that cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato">AN 3.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses the three qualities of virtue, energy, and wisdom is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato"><b>worthy of hospitality</b></a>, worthy of offerings, and worthy of reverential salutation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato">AN 3.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk who has perfected the qualities of beauty (virtue), strength (effort), and speed (wisdom) becomes an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the entire world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.96/en/sujato">AN 3.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato"><b>four types of persons</b></a> existing in the world today based on their specific progress in developing internal serenity and higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato">AN 4.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described one type of individual as a person who has gained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato"><b>internal serenity of mind</b></a> but has not yet attained insight into phenomena through higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato">AN 4.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a practitioner who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato"><b>insight into phenomena</b></a> through higher wisdom but has not yet succeeded in gaining internal serenity of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato">AN 4.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out there are some individuals who have gained neither <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato"><b>internal serenity</b></a> of mind nor insight into phenomena through higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato">AN 4.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered the most accomplished type of person to be the one who has successfully gained both internal serenity of mind and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato"><b>higher wisdom</b></a> regarding phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato">AN 4.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a person who has attained internal serenity of mind should use that stability to apply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato"><b>spiritual exertion</b></a> specifically toward gaining insight into phenomena through higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato">AN 4.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who possess insight but lack serenity should establish themselves in their wisdom and work toward gaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato"><b>internal serenity of mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato">AN 4.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the dramatic imagery of a person with their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato"><b>head on fire</b></a> to illustrate the extreme urgency required for someone who lacks both internal serenity and higher wisdom to acquire these qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato">AN 4.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed those who lack both serenity and insight to apply extraordinary desire, effort, zeal, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato"><b>clear comprehension</b></a> to obtain these wholesome spiritual states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato">AN 4.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a practitioner has successfully attained both serenity and insight, they should then focus their efforts on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato">AN 4.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even those who already possess both internal serenity and higher wisdom must continue their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato"><b>spiritual practice</b></a> to reach the ultimate goal of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato">AN 4.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened the intense effort needed to gain wholesome qualities to the way a person would frantically try to extinguish <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato"><b>burning clothes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato">AN 4.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that through the correct application of effort, a person who initially lacks both serenity and insight can eventually transform into someone who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato"><b>both qualities</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato">AN 4.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato"><b>wholesome qualities</b></a> to refer specifically to the dual attainments of internal serenity of mind and insight into phenomena through higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato">AN 4.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato"><b>extraordinary zeal</b></a> and unwavering perseverance as the primary requirements for a person to overcome a complete lack of meditative progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato">AN 4.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on these four types of persons highlights that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato"><b>meditative development</b></a> involves a deliberate balance between internal calmness and analytical higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato">AN 4.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that "establishing oneself" in one's existing spiritual strength is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato"><b>necessary foundation</b></a> before one can successfully develop a missing quality like serenity or insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.93/en/sujato">AN 4.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized individuals into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato"><b>distinct groups</b></a> to help his followers recognize their specific spiritual needs and apply the appropriate remedies for their progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.92/en/sujato">AN 4.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified he had never even met the young man Soṇakāyana, who had been spreading false reports that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>non-doing of all deeds</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the brahmin Sikhāmoggallāna that he had discovered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>four types of action</b></a> (kamma) through his own direct knowledge and realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>dark action with dark result</b></a> as the generation of afflictive volitional formations of the body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who perform dark actions are reborn in an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>afflictive world</b></a> where they are touched by afflictive contacts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the experience of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>exclusive suffering</b></a> felt by those in an afflictive world to the state of beings in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>bright action with bright result</b></a> involves the performance of non-afflictive volitional formations of the body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those who perform bright actions are reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>non-afflictive world</b></a> where they experience non-afflictive contacts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cited the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>Subhakiṇha devas</b></a> as examples of beings who experience exclusive pleasure as a result of their non-afflictive actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>dark-and-bright action</b></a> as the generation of volitional formations that are a mixture of both afflictive and non-afflictive qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that human beings experience a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>mixture of pleasure and pain</b></a> because they are reborn in a world corresponding to actions that are both dark and bright? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified certain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>devas and fallen spirits</b></a> as beings who, like humans, experience mixed feelings of pleasure and pain based on their prior actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there is a fourth kind of action called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>neither-dark-nor-bright action</b></a>, which does not lead to a worldly rebirth but to a specific spiritual goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the fourth type of action is intended specifically for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>destruction of kamma</b></a>, rather than for producing further results in the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the action that leads to the end of kamma as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>volition to abandon</b></a> the dark, bright, and mixed types of action? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Sikhāmoggallāna reported the claim that the Buddha taught <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>annihilationism</b></a>, the Buddha responded by detailing his realization of how actions lead to different states of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> (saṅkhāra) to describe the underlying mental, verbal, and bodily activities that constitute the first three types of kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the world is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>based on action</b></a> and stands through the undertaking of actions, a point he addressed when discussing the misconceptions of the young man Soṇakāyana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>afflictive feelings</b></a> are the direct result of being touched by afflictive contacts in an afflictive world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted his teaching on the four types of action was not based on hearsay but was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>personally realized</b></a> through his own higher knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's explanation of kamma in this sutta highlights how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato"><b>quality of one's intention</b></a>—whether afflictive, non-afflictive, or aimed at abandonment—determines the nature of one's future experience and rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.234/en/sujato">AN 4.234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a trainee monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>busy with many tasks</b></a> and activities risks falling away from his spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a trainee monk leads himself to decline when he neglects seclusion and fails to devote himself to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>internal tranquility</b></a> of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a trainee monk spending his entire day engaged in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>trivial work</b></a> is a factor that leads to his spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>inappropriate socializing</b></a> with both householders and other renunciants as a behavior that causes a trainee monk to fall away from his training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a trainee monk who enters a village at an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>improper time</b></a> and returns very late in the day is practicing a habit that leads to spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a trainee monk to avoid decline, he must be able to easily engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>talk on effacement</b></a>, which refers to ten specific types of speech that help open the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk's progress is supported by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>talk on wanting little</b></a>, and failing to obtain such conversation contributes to his decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>talk on contentment</b></a> as a vital topic for monks to ensure they do not fall away from the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>talk on seclusion</b></a> as a specific type of beneficial conversation that helps a trainee monk maintain internal tranquility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that trainee monks should prioritize <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>talk on aloofness</b></a> from society to prevent themselves from losing focus on their meditative development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>talk on energy</b></a> as one of the ten beneficial topics of conversation that lead to the non-decline of a trainee monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a trainee monk who can easily engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>talk on virtue</b></a> is following a practice that ensures his continued progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>talk on concentration</b></a> is an essential form of speech that supports a monk in not neglecting his meditation and internal peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>talk on wisdom</b></a> as a key factor that prevents a trainee monk from falling away from the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>talk on liberation</b></a>, as this type of speech is specifically suited for opening the mind and ensuring spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included talk on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a> as the final of ten beneficial topics that safeguard a trainee monk from decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>trainee monk</b></a> ensures his non-decline when he is not busy with many tasks and instead devotes himself to internal tranquility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a trainee monk who does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato"><b>not neglect seclusion</b></a> and avoids wasting time on trivial work is following the path to non-decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.90/en/sujato">AN 5.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>six elements of deliverance</b></a> that serve as specific escapes from unwholesome states of mind when they are fully developed and cultivated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained it is impossible for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> to obsess the mind of a person who has correctly developed the liberation of mind through loving-kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the liberation of mind through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> as the definitive escape from the state of harmfulness and cruelty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>altruistic joy</b></a>, when made into a vehicle and a basis for the mind, serves as the escape from discontent and boredom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the liberation of mind through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> is the specific mental quality that allows one to escape the pull of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>signless liberation of mind</b></a> is the escape from all signs that the consciousness might otherwise follow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>uprooting of the conceit 'I am'</b></a> as the element of deliverance that acts as the escape from the dart of doubt and perplexity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned practitioners not to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>misrepresent the Tathagata</b></a> by claiming to have achieved a liberation of mind while still being overcome by the very unwholesome state it is meant to remove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>dart</b></a> to describe the nature of doubt and perplexity that can only be removed when the notion of "I am" is completely eradicated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught it is "impossible and inconceivable" for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>harmfulness</b></a> to remain in the mind of someone who has fully cultivated compassion and made it a basis for their life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a liberation of mind to be effective, it must be "developed, cultivated, made a vehicle, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>made a basis</b></a>, established, consolidated, and properly undertaken"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that if one has properly practiced the liberation of mind through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a>, it is impossible for lust to continue to obsess and overwhelm their consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> (mettā) specifically as the "escape" (nissaraṇa) from the state of ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a practitioner no longer regards anything as "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>this I am</b></a>," they effectively remove the underlying cause of doubt and hesitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>discontent</b></a> (arati) cannot persist in the mind of one who has frequently practiced and consolidated the liberation of mind through altruistic joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mind's tendency to follow signs and appearances is countered by the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>signless deliverance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that claiming a spiritual attainment while the mind is still possessed by its opposite is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>falsehood</b></a> because such co-existence is logically impossible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the process of spiritual training as turning a quality like compassion into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>vehicle</b></a> (yānīkatā) to be used for mental deliverance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>uprooting of conceit</b></a> regarding the self is the final of the six elements of deliverance he presented for the removal of doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> (rāga) is naturally abandoned through the sustained practice of the equanimity liberation of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.13/en/sujato">AN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who is worthy of gifts and offerings to a king’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato"><b>thoroughbred horse</b></a> that is considered an asset to the king when it possesses the three specific qualities of beauty, strength, and speed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato">AN 3.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a bhikkhu becomes an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world when he is endowed with three metaphorical qualities: beauty, strength, and speed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato">AN 3.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk's "beauty" is defined by him being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato"><b>perfect in virtue</b></a> and dwelling restrained by the rules of the monastic code? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato">AN 3.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a bhikkhu possesses the quality of "beauty" when he remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato"><b>restrained by the Pātimokkha</b></a>, maintains proper conduct, and trains himself strictly in the training rules? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato">AN 3.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a virtuous monk demonstrates "beauty" by being one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato"><b>sees danger in slight faults</b></a> and undertakes the training rules with full commitment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato">AN 3.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a bhikkhu's "strength" as the state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato"><b>aroused in energy</b></a> for the purpose of abandoning unwholesome qualities and acquiring wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato">AN 3.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as possessing "strength" when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato"><b>steadfast and firm</b></a> in his effort, never shirking the duty of cultivating wholesome states of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato">AN 3.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a monk's "speed" as the spiritual attainment reached through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the five lower fetters</b></a>, which leads to becoming a non-returner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato">AN 3.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a bhikkhu possesses "speed" when he is a being of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato"><b>spontaneous rebirth</b></a> who attains Nibbāna in a higher realm without ever returning to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato">AN 3.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who possesses beauty (virtue), strength (energy), and speed (the path to non-returning) is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato"><b>worthy of the elbow-extended salutation</b></a> and is a person to whom offerings should be made? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.97/en/sujato">AN 3.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who is immoral and of bad character to coarse <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>hempen cloth</b></a>, which is ugly in color, rough to the touch, and of very little value? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk’s lack of virtue is equivalent to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>bad color</b></a> of low-quality cloth, making him unappealing regardless of whether he is a new, middle-aged, or senior monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, associating with and following an immoral monk leads to long-term harm and suffering, a quality he likened to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>rough touch</b></a> of coarse bark cloth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that offerings of robes, almsfood, and medicine given to an immoral monk result in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>little fruit</b></a> and little benefit for the donor, just as cheap cloth has little market value? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened a senior monk who is immoral to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>worn-out hempen cloth</b></a> that people use only for wiping out cooking pots or simply throw away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how an immoral senior monk might be rebuked in the assembly as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>foolish and incompetent</b></a> person who has no business speaking among the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared an angry senior monk who speaks inappropriately to cloth being cast onto a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>rubbish heap</b></a>, signifying his loss of respect and standing within the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of fine <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>Kāsian cloth</b></a>—which is beautiful, soft, and expensive—to describe a monk who is virtuous and of good character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk possesses "beautiful color" when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and maintains a high standard of conduct and character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that associating with and serving a virtuous monk leads to long-term welfare and happiness, much like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>pleasant touch</b></a> of fine, high-quality fabric? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that support given to a virtuous monk in the form of requisites yields <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>great fruit</b></a> and immense benefit for the giver? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared even an old, worn piece of Kāsian cloth to a virtuous monk, noting that such cloth is still used to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>wrap jewels</b></a> or is kept in a perfume box because of its enduring quality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a senior virtuous monk as one whom others respect by remaining silent and saying, "the senior monk is speaking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma and Vinaya</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his disciples to train themselves with the specific goal of being like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>Kāsian cloth</b></a> and avoiding the qualities of coarse hempen cloth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that following the example of an immoral monk results in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>long-term suffering</b></a> for those who imitate his behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term "high value" as a metaphor for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>merit generated</b></a> when someone provides for the needs of a monk who is truly virtuous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk’s worth is based on his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>wholesome character</b></a> rather than just his age or the number of years he has been ordained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a senior monk who remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>restrained and calm</b></a> while speaking in the assembly as being comparable to the most precious and well-kept fabrics? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the way an old, virtuous monk is cherished to how people store fine garments in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>perfume box</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a monk should strive to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato"><b>pleasant to associate with</b></a>, just as fine cloth is pleasant to the touch, by cultivating deep virtue and kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an3.99/en/sujato">AN 3.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four distinct types of persons existing in the world based on their attainment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>internal serenity of mind</b></a> and insight into phenomena through higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a specific type of practitioner who has successfully gained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>internal serenity of mind</b></a> but has not yet gained insight into phenomena through higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of an individual who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>insight into phenomena</b></a> through higher wisdom but has not yet attained internal serenity of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized some people as having gained neither <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>internal serenity of mind</b></a> nor insight into phenomena through higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who is a "gainer" of both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>internal serenity of mind</b></a> and insight into phenomena through higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised a person who has serenity but lacks insight to approach a practitioner of insight and ask how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>formations should be seen</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that those seeking to develop insight should inquire from others about how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>formations should be explored</b></a> and discerned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by questioning an experienced practitioner on how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>formations are discerned via insight</b></a>, a person with serenity can eventually gain insight as well? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged a person who has insight but lacks serenity to approach a practitioner of serenity and ask how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>mind should be steadied</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that a practitioner lacking internal serenity should specifically ask, "How should the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>mind be settled</b></a> and made unified?" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that through the process of asking how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>mind should be concentrated</b></a>, an insight practitioner can eventually gain internal serenity of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed those who lack both serenity and insight to approach a person who possesses both and ask for guidance on how to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>steady the mind</b></a> and see formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even a person starting with neither serenity nor insight can eventually gain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>both qualities</b></a> by proactively seeking instructions from those who are accomplished in them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a teacher should answer questions about serenity or insight according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>what they have seen</b></a> and known in their own practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that once a person has gained both serenity and insight, they should dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>established in those wholesome states</b></a> while making further effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the final spiritual duty for one who possesses both serenity and insight is to strive for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s guidance on how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato"><b>formations should be discerned via insight</b></a> involves asking others to explain the method based on their own realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.94/en/sujato">AN 4.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato"><b>four types of persons</b></a> existing in the world based on whether they practice for their own benefit, the benefit of others, both, or neither? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato">AN 4.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a person as practicing for their own benefit but not the benefit of others when they personally practice for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato"><b>removal of lust</b></a> but do not encourage others to do the same? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato">AN 4.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that practicing for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato"><b>removal of hatred</b></a> personally, while failing to encourage others to remove their own hatred, characterizes an individual who practices only for their own benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato">AN 4.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an individual who strives for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato"><b>removal of delusion</b></a> for themselves, but does not prompt others to remove delusion, is categorized as one who practices for their own benefit but not for others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato">AN 4.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who practices for the benefit of others but not for their own benefit as one who encourages others in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato"><b>removal of lust</b></a>, yet does not practice to remove lust within themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato">AN 4.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a type of person who practices for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato"><b>neither their own benefit</b></a> nor the benefit of others because they do not personally strive to remove lust, hatred, and delusion, nor do they encourage others to do so? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato">AN 4.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the most commendable practitioner as the one who practices for both their own benefit and the benefit of others by personally striving for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato"><b>removal of lust</b></a>, hatred, and delusion while also encouraging others to do the same? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato">AN 4.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who encourages others in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato"><b>removal of hatred</b></a> but does not practice for the removal of hatred themselves is considered to be practicing for the benefit of others but not for their own benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato">AN 4.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a person does not practice for the removal of delusion themselves but encourages others in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato"><b>removal of delusion</b></a>, they are categorized as practicing for the benefit of others and not for themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.96/en/sujato">AN 4.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four types of kamma that he realized through his own direct knowledge, including a specific kind of action that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.235/en/sujato"><b>neither-dark-nor-bright</b></a> with neither-dark-nor-bright results, which leads to the total destruction of kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.235/en/sujato">AN 4.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, engaging in actions such as killing, stealing, and sexual misconduct is classified as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.235/en/sujato"><b>dark kamma</b></a> which inevitably leads to dark results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.235/en/sujato">AN 4.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who abstains from taking what is not given and avoids false speech is performing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.235/en/sujato"><b>bright kamma</b></a> that leads to bright results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.235/en/sujato">AN 4.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that some actions involve a mixture of harmful and harmless intentions, resulting in kamma that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.235/en/sujato"><b>both dark and bright</b></a> and which produces mixed results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.235/en/sujato">AN 4.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the path to the ending of all action as the intention to abandon dark kamma, bright kamma, and mixed kamma, calling this the kamma that leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.235/en/sujato"><b>destruction of kamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.235/en/sujato">AN 4.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his followers to boldly declare a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato"><b>lion’s roar</b></a>, stating that the four types of true ascetics are only found within his teaching and that other doctrines are empty of them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato">AN 4.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the "first ascetic" as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato"><b>stream-enterer</b></a> (sotāpanna), a person who has destroyed the first three fetters and is no longer subject to rebirth in the lower worlds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato">AN 4.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the "second ascetic" is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato"><b>once-returner</b></a> (sakadāgāmī), who has weakened lust, hatred, and delusion and will return to this world only one more time to finish the work of ending suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato">AN 4.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the "third ascetic" as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato"><b>non-returner</b></a> (anāgāmī), who has destroyed the five lower fetters and will attain final Nibbāna in a higher realm without ever returning to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato">AN 4.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the "fourth ascetic" as an Arahant who has attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavas), reaching a state of liberation that is realized through direct knowledge in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato">AN 4.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a stream-enterer is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato"><b>fixed in destiny</b></a> (niyata), meaning they are certain to attain full enlightenment and can never again fall into lower states of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato">AN 4.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the "fourth ascetic" attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> (cetovimutti) and liberation by wisdom (paññāvimutti), having personally verified these states through higher knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato">AN 4.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a non-returner is of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato"><b>spontaneous birth</b></a> (opapātiko), meaning they are reborn in the Pure Abodes to attain final Nibbāna there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato">AN 4.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by relying on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.242/en/sujato"><b>good person</b></a> (sappurisa), a practitioner can expect to grow in four specific noble qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.242/en/sujato">AN 4.242</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.242/en/sujato"><b>noble virtue</b></a> (ariya sīla) as the first of four major benefits a person gains when they associate with and rely on a good person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.242/en/sujato">AN 4.242</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that growth in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.242/en/sujato"><b>noble concentration</b></a> (ariya samādhi) is a direct result of placing one's trust in and learning from a good person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.242/en/sujato">AN 4.242</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the vital benefits of relying on a good person is the development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.242/en/sujato"><b>noble wisdom</b></a> (ariya paññā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.242/en/sujato">AN 4.242</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.242/en/sujato"><b>noble liberation</b></a> (ariya vimutti) as the ultimate benefit one can expect to cultivate by relying on the guidance of a good person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.242/en/sujato">AN 4.242</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the consumption of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.235/en/sujato"><b>intoxicants</b></a> like wine and liquor as a form of dark kamma that leads to negligence and dark results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.235/en/sujato">AN 4.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the four types of ascetics are defined by their progressive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato"><b>destruction of fetters</b></a> (saṃyojana), leading from stream-entry to full liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.241/en/sujato">AN 4.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person is practicing for their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato"><b>own benefit</b></a>, but not the benefit of others, if they personally abstain from killing and stealing but do not encourage others to do the same? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato">AN 4.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person practicing for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato"><b>benefit of others</b></a>, but not their own, as one who encourages others to observe ethical training rules while they themselves continue to engage in misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato">AN 4.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the most deficient of the four types of people as the one who practices for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato"><b>neither benefit</b></a>, meaning they personally fail to follow the training rules and also fail to encourage others to follow them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato">AN 4.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ideal practitioner is one practicing for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato"><b>both benefits</b></a>, characterized by personally observing the training rules while simultaneously encouraging others to undertake them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato">AN 4.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that personally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from killing</b></a> living beings is a primary way an individual practices for their own benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato">AN 4.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person acts for the benefit of others when they actively <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato"><b>encourage others</b></a> to abstain from taking what is not given and other forms of misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato">AN 4.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined practicing for one's own benefit as including personal restraint from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato">AN 4.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who personally refrains from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato"><b>false speech</b></a> but remains silent rather than urging others to be truthful is practicing only for their own benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato">AN 4.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized personally abstaining from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato"><b>intoxicants</b></a>—such as wine, liquor, and other drinks that cause negligence—as a fundamental component of practicing for one's own benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato">AN 4.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that to be a person of the highest caliber, one must both personally follow the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato"><b>five training rules</b></a> and also exhort others to do the same? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato">AN 4.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the criteria of the five training rules to distinguish who is truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato"><b>practicing for the welfare</b></a> of the world versus those who only look after their own spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an4.99/en/sujato">AN 4.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five specific states as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.94/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwellings</b></a> in this very life, which include the four meditative absorptions (jhanas) and the final destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.94/en/sujato">AN 5.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk can attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.94/en/sujato"><b>first jhana</b></a> and dwell in ease by being secluded from sensual pleasures and unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.94/en/sujato">AN 5.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ultimate "pleasant dwelling" is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.94/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>, through which one realizes the taintless liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.94/en/sujato">AN 5.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk practicing mindfulness of breathing will soon penetrate the unshakable if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.96/en/sujato"><b>easy to support</b></a> and content with the basic requisites of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.96/en/sujato">AN 5.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.96/en/sujato"><b>learned in the Dhamma</b></a>—retaining and investigating teachings that are pure and complete—as a vital factor for a monk to quickly penetrate the unshakable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.96/en/sujato">AN 5.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a monk practicing mindfulness of breathing should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.96/en/sujato"><b>devoted to wakefulness</b></a> and have little sleep to successfully realize the unshakable state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.96/en/sujato">AN 5.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.96/en/sujato"><b>eats little</b></a> and is not devoted to filling his stomach is more likely to quickly penetrate the unshakable through mindfulness of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.96/en/sujato">AN 5.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.96/en/sujato"><b>reviewing the mind</b></a> as it is liberated as a necessary final factor for a monk to penetrate the unshakable while practicing mindfulness of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.96/en/sujato">AN 5.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that a monk should be of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.97/en/sujato"><b>few duties</b></a> and activities to better develop mindfulness of breathing and attain the unshakable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.97/en/sujato">AN 5.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.97/en/sujato"><b>talk on effacement</b></a>—such as talk on fewness of wishes, seclusion, and wisdom—helps a monk quickly penetrate the unshakable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.97/en/sujato">AN 5.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that talk regarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.97/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a> is a type of conversation that is conducive to "opening the heart" for a meditating monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.97/en/sujato">AN 5.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.98/en/sujato"><b>forest dwellers</b></a> who utilize remote lodgings as a factor to help them penetrate the unshakable through mindfulness of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.98/en/sujato">AN 5.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.94/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhana</b></a>—a state of pure mindfulness and equanimity—as one of the five ways a monk can dwell pleasantly in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.94/en/sujato">AN 5.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a learned monk as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.96/en/sujato"><b>mentally investigated</b></a> and penetrated by view the teachings that describe the entirely pure holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.96/en/sujato">AN 5.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.98/en/sujato"><b>content with requisites</b></a> and stays in remote dwellings possesses the necessary qualities to quickly penetrate the unshakable through meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.98/en/sujato">AN 5.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared his teaching method to a lion's hunt, explaining that just as a lion attacks all prey with meticulous care, the Tathagata teaches the Dhamma with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> to every audience without exception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato">AN 5.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a lion, the king of beasts, emerges from its lair in the evening to stretch, survey the four quarters, and roar a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato"><b>lion's roar</b></a> three times before setting out for food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato">AN 5.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that whether a lion strikes a large animal like an elephant or a small creature like a hare or a cat, it always does so with care and never <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato"><b>carelessly</b></a>, in order to maintain its mastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato">AN 5.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when he teaches the Dhamma to any assembly, this act of teaching is considered his own version of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato"><b>lion's roar</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato">AN 5.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he teaches with the same degree of thoroughness and care to all people, including <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato"><b>ordinary people</b></a> such as scavengers and hunters, as he does to his own monks and nuns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato">AN 5.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the reason he teaches with such earnest care to every person is because he is deeply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato"><b>respectful of the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato">AN 5.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a lion to demonstrate that the Tathagata never treats a teaching opportunity with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato"><b>negligence</b></a>, regardless of the status of the listeners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.99/en/sujato">AN 5.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five specific qualities that serve as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato"><b>confidence-generating factors</b></a> for a trainee, effectively removing their timidity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato">AN 5.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a trainee who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> is free from the timidity and lack of confidence that typically plagues those who lack faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato">AN 5.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and restrained by the monastic code is a primary quality that removes the timidity found in those of immoral character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato">AN 5.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato"><b>much learned</b></a>—meaning one has retained and investigated the teachings—as a quality that provides a trainee with great confidence in any assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato">AN 5.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> to abandon unwholesome states and acquire wholesome ones is a quality that eliminates the timidity often felt by the lazy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato">AN 5.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> regarding the rising and falling of phenomena is a crucial factor for a trainee to overcome timidity and lack of confidence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato">AN 5.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk can be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.102/en/sujato"><b>suspected</b></a> of being an "evil monk" by the community if he frequents certain improper locations, even if his character is actually beyond reproach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.102/en/sujato">AN 5.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.102/en/sujato"><b>prostitutes</b></a> as his resort or regular field of activity will inevitably be viewed with suspicion and distrust by his peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.102/en/sujato">AN 5.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that monks who frequently visit <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.102/en/sujato"><b>widows</b></a> as their resort are likely to be suspected of being unprincipled, regardless of their actual behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.102/en/sujato">AN 5.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed frequenting the company of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.102/en/sujato"><b>old maids</b></a> as one of the five behaviors that cause a monk to be suspected of being an "evil monk"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.102/en/sujato">AN 5.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who makes a resort of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.102/en/sujato"><b>eunuchs</b></a> will find himself suspected and mistrusted by the monastic community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.102/en/sujato">AN 5.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that even a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.102/en/sujato"><b>unshakeable</b></a> in his realization can still be suspected of being an evil monk if he is seen frequently visiting nuns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.102/en/sujato">AN 5.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a trainee's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato"><b>confidence</b></a> as being rooted in five specific dhammas: faith, virtue, learning, energy, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an5.101/en/sujato">AN 5.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that a monk who lives a life devoted to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato"><b>delight in work</b></a> and activities will fail to have a fortunate death or a fortunate passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato">AN 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, a monk who is preoccupied with and devoted to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato"><b>delight in talk</b></a> is living in a way that leads to an unfortunate death rather than a peaceful passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato">AN 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta warned the monks that being addicted to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato"><b>delight in sleep</b></a> is one of the key factors that prevents a practitioner from experiencing an auspicious death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato">AN 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk who is devoted to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato"><b>delight in company</b></a> and group association is considered to be delighting in personal identity and has not yet abandoned the cause of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato">AN 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato"><b>delight in socializing</b></a> as a hindrance that leads a monk to an unfortunate passing, whereas avoiding such delight leads to a fortunate death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato">AN 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that anyone who is devoted to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato"><b>mental proliferation</b></a> (papañca) misses out on Nibbāna, which is described as the supreme security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato">AN 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta described a practitioner who abandons worldly delights and instead <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato"><b>delights in Nibbāna</b></a> as one who has successfully abandoned personal identity for the complete ending of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato">AN 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk who does not delight in work, talk, sleep, or company is the one who truly prepares for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato"><b>fortunate death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato">AN 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta compared a person who is obsessed with mental proliferation to a "beast" who wanders away from attaining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato"><b>supreme security from bondage</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato">AN 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta concluded that by letting go of proliferation and finding joy in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato"><b>state of non-proliferation</b></a>, a monk successfully attains Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.14/en/sujato">AN 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā warned her husband that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>dying with concern</b></a> for his family or possessions is a painful experience and is criticized by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā reassured her ailing husband she could support their children after his death because she was highly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>skilled at spinning cotton</b></a> and carding wool? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā told her husband that he should not worry about her remarrying after his passing because they had both lived a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>celibate life</b></a> as householders for sixteen years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā asserted to her husband that, even after he was gone, she would be even more <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>eager to see</b></a> the Buddha and the community of monks than she was while he was alive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā encouraged her husband to let go of his anxieties by declaring herself to be one of the laywomen who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>accomplished in virtue</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā claimed she was among the Buddha's lay female disciples who had gained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>internal serenity of mind</b></a> through meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā stated she had reached a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>self-confidence</b></a> and had become independent of others in the Teacher’s instruction within the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Nakulapitā he was very fortunate to have a wife who was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>wise counselor</b></a> and advisor who truly desired his welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that Nakulamātā was one of his female lay followers who had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>crossed over doubt</b></a> and gained a firm footing in the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>Deer Park</b></a> at the Bhesakaḷā Grove when he was visited by the householder Nakulapitā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā’s exhortation was so powerful that Nakulapitā’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>severe illness</b></a> subsided immediately upon hearing her words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed Nakulapitā was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>leaning on a staff</b></a> when he finally recovered enough to go and pay homage to the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā told her husband that if he had any doubt about her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>attainment of serenity</b></a>, he should go and ask the Buddha for confirmation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Nakulamātā for being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>compassionate</b></a> and for providing her husband with the spiritual guidance he needed during his life-threatening affliction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā explained that she was fully capable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>managing the household</b></a> and caring for their children, proving that her husband had no reason to worry about their survival? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā claimed she was among those laywomen who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>gained a footing</b></a> in the Buddha's Dhamma and Discipline, becoming free from all perplexity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā suggested that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>death with longing</b></a> for family or unfinished business is a state of suffering that the Buddha does not approve of? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā reminded her husband of their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>shared commitment</b></a> to spiritual life as a way to help him let go of the fear that she might seek another partner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that Nakulamātā was one of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>white-clad laywomen</b></a> disciples who was fully accomplished in the precepts of virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulamātā’s speech was so effective that it acted as the primary cause for her husband’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato"><b>painful affliction</b></a> to be completely cured on the spot? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.16/en/sujato">AN 6.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that three types of existence—sensual existence, form existence, and formless existence—must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.105/en/sujato"><b>abandoned</b></a> by a monk seeking liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.105/en/sujato">AN 6.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a monk should train himself in three specific areas: the training in higher virtue, the training in higher mind, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.105/en/sujato"><b>training in higher wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.105/en/sujato">AN 6.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk who has both abandoned the three types of existence and completed the three trainings is one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.105/en/sujato"><b>cut off craving</b></a> and discarded the fetter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.105/en/sujato">AN 6.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified craving for sensual pleasures, craving for existence, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.106/en/sujato"><b>craving for extermination</b></a> as the three types of craving that must be abandoned to end suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.106/en/sujato">AN 6.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are three distinct types of conceit a practitioner must abandon: conceit, inferiority conceit, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.106/en/sujato"><b>arrogance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.106/en/sujato">AN 6.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who has successfully abandoned three types of craving and three types of conceit has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.106/en/sujato"><b>made an end of suffering</b></a> through the right breakthrough by conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.106/en/sujato">AN 6.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha prescribed the specific development of the perception of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.107/en/sujato"><b>foulness</b></a> as the mental training required to abandon lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.107/en/sujato">AN 6.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.107/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> as the specific remedy for abandoning hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.107/en/sujato">AN 6.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.107/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> is the essential quality that must be developed in order to abandon delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.107/en/sujato">AN 6.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.109/en/sujato"><b>thought of renunciation</b></a> should be intentionally developed as the means to abandon sensual thought? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.109/en/sujato">AN 6.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.109/en/sujato"><b>thought of non-ill-will</b></a> is the specific meditation to be developed to abandon the thought of ill-will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.109/en/sujato">AN 6.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one should develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.109/en/sujato"><b>thought of non-harming</b></a> in order to successfully abandon the thought of harming others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.109/en/sujato">AN 6.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.110/en/sujato"><b>perception of renunciation</b></a> as the mental state one must develop to abandon sensual perception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.110/en/sujato">AN 6.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches that a monk should develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.110/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-ill-will</b></a> to abandon the perception of ill-will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.110/en/sujato">AN 6.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha states the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.110/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-harming</b></a> is the quality to be developed to abandon the perception of harming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.110/en/sujato">AN 6.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identifies <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.114/en/sujato"><b>contentment</b></a> as the wholesome quality that must be developed to abandon its opposite, discontent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.114/en/sujato">AN 6.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches that one must develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.114/en/sujato"><b>clear comprehension</b></a> as the specific remedy to abandon a lack of clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.114/en/sujato">AN 6.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommends developing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.114/en/sujato"><b>fewness of wishes</b></a> as the practice required to abandon the state of great desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.114/en/sujato">AN 6.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that training in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.105/en/sujato"><b>higher mind</b></a> is one of the three essential disciplines for a monk who has abandoned the three types of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.105/en/sujato">AN 6.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who has abandoned three types of existence and completed the three trainings is said to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.105/en/sujato"><b>discarded the fetter</b></a> and thoroughly understood conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.105/en/sujato">AN 6.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a simile comparing seven types of people to individuals in various states of being in water, ranging from those who stay submerged to those who cross over to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>far shore</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is "once submerged, stays submerged" as someone who is completely possessed of exclusively black <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome states</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who "emerges and then sinks back down" is one who initially gains faith, moral shame, moral dread, energy, and wisdom, but those <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>wholesome qualities</b></a> eventually decrease and disappear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the person who "emerges and stands still" as one whose faith, moral shame, moral dread, energy, and wisdom <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>neither increase</b></a> nor decrease, remaining static? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who "emerges, sees, and looks around" is a stream-enterer who, with the destruction of three fetters, is no longer bound for the lower world and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>fixed in destiny</b></a> toward enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who "emerges and swims across" as a once-returner who has destroyed three fetters and weakened lust, hatred, and delusion, returning to this world only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>one more time</b></a> to end suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the person who "emerges and gains a footing" as a non-returner who, with the destruction of the five lower fetters, is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>spontaneously reborn</b></a> in a higher realm and attains Nibbana there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the person who has "crossed over, gone to the far shore, and stands on dry land" as a "Brahmin" who has attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>destruction of taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the quality of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> (<i>saddhā</i>) in wholesome states is a primary factor that allows a person to "emerge" from the depths of unwholesome suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>moral shame</b></a> (<i>hirī</i>) as one of the essential wholesome qualities that helps a person emerge from unwholesome states, even if that person might later sink back down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>moral dread</b></a> (<i>ottappaṃ</i>) as a necessary component for spiritual progress, alongside faith and energy, in his water-based analogy of human types? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>energy</b></a> (<i>vīriyaṃ</i>) in wholesome states is crucial for ensuring a person does not merely stay submerged in unwholesome behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> (<i>paññā</i>) is the quality that, when fully developed through the destruction of taints, allows a person to stand on dry land as an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term "Brahmin" to describe an Arahant who has reached the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>taintless liberation</b></a> of mind and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that someone who has reached the state of "seeing and looking around" has specifically achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>destruction of three fetters</b></a> (identity view, doubt, and ritual attachment)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the "Once-returner" as someone who has not only destroyed the first three fetters but has also achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>diminishing</b></a> of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the person who "gains a footing" (the non-returner) is one who will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>never return</b></a> from that higher world to this one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized individuals based on how their wholesome qualities—faith, shame, dread, energy, and wisdom—<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato"><b>waver or grow</b></a> while they navigate the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.15/en/sujato">AN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>eight inopportune times</b></a> when it is impossible to live the holy life, even if a Tathāgata has appeared in the world and is teaching the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even when the Dhamma leading to Nibbana is being proclaimed, being born in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a> constitutes a major inopportune time that prevents one from practicing the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, being born in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>animal realm</b></a> is considered an inopportune moment for spiritual progress, regardless of whether a fully enlightened Buddha is currently present in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that being born in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>sphere of afflicted spirits</b></a> (petas) is one of the eight states where a person is unable to take advantage of the Buddha's presence to lead the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even being born among a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>long-lived deva host</b></a> can be an inopportune time that hinders the practice of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described birth in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>remote border regions</b></a> among "barbarous" people as a significant obstacle, as these areas lack the presence of monks, nuns, and lay followers necessary for the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that holding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>wrong views</b></a>—such as the belief that there are no fruits or results of good and bad kamma—creates an inopportune time that prevents a person from understanding the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>unintelligent</b></a>, dull, or unable to distinguish the meaning of what is well spoken or poorly spoken, they are experiencing an inopportune time for spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even if a person is wise and born in a central land, it is still an inopportune time if a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a> has not appeared and the Dhamma is not being taught? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated there is only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>one opportune moment</b></a> for the holy life, which occurs when a Tathāgata appears, the Dhamma is taught, and a person is born in a central land with the intelligence to understand it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the combination of gaining a human birth and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>teaching of the Dhamma</b></a> by a Tathāgata is an extremely rare occurrence in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who misses the rare opportune moment for spiritual practice to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>merchant</b></a> who fails to make a profit and later regrets his loss for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who miss the brief and rare opportune moment for practice will eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>sorrow in hell</b></a> once they have passed away and are reborn there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person enveloped in ignorance as one who will long experience the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>round of birth and death</b></a> because they failed to grasp the true Dhamma when the opportunity was available? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, referred to in the text as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>Sun’s Kinsman</b></a>, taught that one should live always mindful and guarded in the restraints of the senses to protect the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by following the path he proclaimed, a person can reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavakkhaya) and successfully go beyond the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged people to make an effort in the holy life now because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>opportune moment</b></a> is so rare and should not be allowed to pass by without fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>conducive to peace</b></a>, leading to Nibbana, and tending toward enlightenment for those who practice it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the cutting off of all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendencies</b></a> (anusaya) as a key achievement for those who have seized the opportune moment to practice the path he taught? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that while uninstructed worldlings often speak of the "right moment," they do not actually understand what truly constitutes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato"><b>favorable or unfavorable time</b></a> for the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.29/en/sujato">AN 8.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can attain the destruction of the taints and spiritual liberation in this very life if he possesses five specific personal qualities and utilizes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato"><b>lodging</b></a> that also possesses five specific supportive factors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato">AN 10.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the first essential factor for a monk's success as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> in the enlightenment of the Tathāgata, believing him to be the Perfectly Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato">AN 10.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized physical health as a prerequisite for spiritual exertion, stating that a monk should have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato"><b>good digestion</b></a> that is neither too cold nor too hot but balanced and suitable for striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato">AN 10.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner must be honest and sincere, acting as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato"><b>reveals himself</b></a> as he really is to his teacher or to wise companions in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato">AN 10.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk should live with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a>, being firm in his effort to abandon unwholesome states and to fully undertake wholesome states without shirking his duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato">AN 10.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the necessary wisdom for a monk as the noble and penetrative understanding of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato"><b>rising and falling</b></a> of phenomena, which leads directly to the complete destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato">AN 10.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a suitable lodging should be neither too far from nor too near a village, and should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato"><b>convenient for commuting</b></a> so that a monk can easily obtain his needs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato">AN 10.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an ideal dwelling for meditation is one that is little crowded by day and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato"><b>quiet and still</b></a> at night, free from the noise and uproar of people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato">AN 10.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended lodgings where a practitioner has little contact with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato"><b>disturbing insects</b></a> like flies and mosquitoes, or harsh environmental factors like wind, burning sun, and reptiles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato">AN 10.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a supportive lodging is one where learned elder monks reside who can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato"><b>dispel doubts</b></a> by clarifying the Dhamma and Vinaya when a monk approaches them with questions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.11/en/sujato">AN 10.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person possessing wrong view, all ten factors including wrong knowledge and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato"><b>wrong liberation</b></a> lead only to what is undesirable, unlovely, and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato">AN 10.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how any bodily, verbal, or mental action performed by someone with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> is ultimately destined to lead to harmful and disagreeable results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato">AN 10.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person with wrong view to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato"><b>bitter seed</b></a>—such as a neem, bitter gourd, or bitter cucumber seed—which turns any moisture or soil nutrients it absorbs into a bitter and acrid taste? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato">AN 10.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato"><b>bad view</b></a> as the underlying reason why a person's intentions, aspirations, and volitional formations result in suffering instead of benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato">AN 10.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a person with right view, all ten path factors including right mindfulness and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> lead to results that are desirable, lovely, and agreeable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato">AN 10.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato"><b>sweet seed</b></a>—such as sugarcane, hill rice, or grape—to illustrate how right view transforms every experience and nutrient absorbed into something sweet and pleasant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato">AN 10.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato"><b>intentions</b></a> and desires of a person established in right view are naturally directed toward happiness and beneficial outcomes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato">AN 10.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato"><b>good view</b></a> is the fundamental cause for a person's bodily, verbal, and mental deeds to culminate in pleasure and long-term welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato">AN 10.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> of a person with wrong view are conditioned by that view to lead to what is unpalatable and painful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato">AN 10.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato"><b>right knowledge</b></a> as one of the ten qualities that, when grounded in right view, lead to outcomes that are agreeable and beneficial? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato">AN 10.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato"><b>mental action</b></a> undertaken by someone with right view is part of a complete set of factors that result in happiness and agreeableness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato">AN 10.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person with right view, the quality of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato"><b>right liberation</b></a> is the pinnacle of a path where all deeds and aspirations lead to what is lovely and pleasant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.104/en/sujato">AN 10.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the quality of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.115/en/sujato"><b>gentleness/amenability</b></a> should be developed specifically to overcome the problem of unruliness in a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.115/en/sujato">AN 6.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person should cultivate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.115/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> as the direct remedy to abandon the influence of bad friendship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.115/en/sujato">AN 6.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.115/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of breathing</b></a> as the specific mental training required to abandon mental distraction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.115/en/sujato">AN 6.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a practitioner is incapable of dwelling in the contemplation of the body if they still find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.117/en/sujato"><b>delight in work</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.117/en/sujato">AN 6.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.117/en/sujato"><b>delight in talk</b></a> as one of the six obstacles that make it impossible for a person to successfully contemplate the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.117/en/sujato">AN 6.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one must abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.117/en/sujato"><b>delight in sleep</b></a> in order to become capable of dwelling in the contemplation of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.117/en/sujato">AN 6.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.117/en/sujato"><b>delight in company</b></a> prevents a practitioner from being able to maintain the contemplation of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.117/en/sujato">AN 6.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.117/en/sujato"><b>guard the sense doors</b></a> is unable to dwell in the contemplation of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.117/en/sujato">AN 6.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.117/en/sujato"><b>immoderation in eating</b></a> is a quality that must be abandoned before one can successfully practice contemplation of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.117/en/sujato">AN 6.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recognized the householder <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.120/en/sujato"><b>Anāthapiṇḍika</b></a> as a lay follower who had realized the deathless and was firmly established in the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.120/en/sujato">AN 6.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha named the householder <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.121/en/sujato"><b>Citta Macchikāsaṇḍika</b></a> as an exemplary lay follower who possessed confirmed confidence in the Dhamma and had realized the deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.121/en/sujato">AN 6.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the lay follower <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.122/en/sujato"><b>Hatthaka of Āḷavī</b></a> was established in the Tathāgata and had seen the deathless through confirmed confidence in the Saṅgha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.122/en/sujato">AN 6.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.123/en/sujato"><b>Mahānāma the Sakyan</b></a> as a lay follower who had attained the deathless by possessing noble virtue and noble knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.123/en/sujato">AN 6.123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the physician <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.127/en/sujato"><b>Jīvaka Komārabhacca</b></a> in a list of lay followers who were established in the Tathāgata and had realized the deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.127/en/sujato">AN 6.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.142/en/sujato"><b>perception of impermanence</b></a> is one of the six specific things that should be developed for the direct knowledge of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.142/en/sujato">AN 6.142</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended cultivating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.142/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-self</b></a> in what is suffering as a necessary practice for the direct knowledge of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.142/en/sujato">AN 6.142</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.142/en/sujato"><b>perception of dispassion</b></a> as a crucial mental quality to develop in order to achieve the direct knowledge of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.142/en/sujato">AN 6.142</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that developing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.142/en/sujato"><b>perception of cessation</b></a> is part of the six-fold practice required for the direct knowledge of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.142/en/sujato">AN 6.142</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a monk's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato"><b>keen desire</b></a> to undertake the training, coupled with an undiminished love for it into the future, as the first of seven bases for spiritual excellence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato">AN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should possess a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato"><b>keen desire for scrutiny</b></a> of the Dhamma to be established in the bases of excellence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato">AN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato"><b>keen desire to remove desire</b></a> as a vital quality for a monk to achieve excellence and ensure continued spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato">AN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk's excellence is supported by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato"><b>keen desire for seclusion</b></a> and an undiminished love for solitude into the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato">AN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato"><b>arousal of energy</b></a> is a base of excellence when a monk maintains a keen desire and an undiminished love for such effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato">AN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and alertness</b></a> as one of the seven bases of excellence that a monk should cultivate with keen interest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato">AN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk attains a state of excellence through a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato"><b>keen desire for the penetration of views</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato">AN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized these seven specific qualities—including desire for training, energy, and seclusion—as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato"><b>seven bases of excellence</b></a> for those in the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato">AN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha visited the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato"><b>Sarandada Shrine</b></a> in Vesali, where he delivered a significant teaching to the Licchavis on the principles of social stability? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato">AN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while speaking to the Licchavis, the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato"><b>frequent assemblies</b></a> are a primary condition that leads to a nation's prosperity rather than its decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato">AN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the Vajjis would avoid decline as long as they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato"><b>assembled in harmony</b></a>, adjourned in harmony, and conducted their collective business in harmony? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato">AN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised the Vajjis that not authorizing what has not been authorized and following their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato"><b>ancient Vajjian traditions</b></a> is a key condition for their continued growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato">AN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a society thrives when its members <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato"><b>honor and respect the elders</b></a> and consider the advice of those elders worth listening to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato">AN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a nation will prosper only as long as they do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato"><b>forcibly detain</b></a> or abduct women or girls from their families? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato">AN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato"><b>honoring shrines</b></a> and maintaining traditional religious offerings is one of the seven conditions that prevent a people from falling into decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato">AN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that providing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato"><b>proper protection and shelter</b></a> for Arahants, so that they may live comfortably within the realm, is a condition for a nation's prosperity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato">AN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha presented a specific set of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato"><b>seven conditions for non-decline</b></a> to the Licchavis to help ensure the long-term success of their community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.21/en/sujato">AN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk's spiritual success is tied to having an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato"><b>undiminished love for removing desire</b></a>, which serves as one of the fundamental bases of excellence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.20/en/sujato">AN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma is for those with few wishes, and not for those with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>many wishes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while meditating in solitude, Venerable Anuruddha reflected on seven <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>Great Man’s thoughts</b></a> regarding who the Dhamma truly belongs to, which the Buddha later confirmed and expanded upon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha added an eighth thought to Venerable Anuruddha's list, stating that the Dhamma is for one who delights in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>non-proliferation</b></a> rather than one who delights in mental proliferation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>fewness of wishes</b></a> as someone who, despite being accomplished in virtue, does not wish for others to know that they possess such qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>contentment</b></a> as being satisfied with whatever basic requisites one happens to receive, such as robes, alms food, lodgings, and medicines? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>solitude</b></a> should maintain a mind sloping toward seclusion even when they are visited by other monks, laypeople, or even royalty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the Dhamma belongs to one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> for the abandoning of unwholesome states and the acquisition of wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>extraordinary mindfulness</b></a> as one who is able to remember and recall what was done and said even a long time ago? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma is for one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>concentrated</b></a>, specifically referring to the successful attainment of the four jhanas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> as being endowed with the noble and penetrative understanding of the rising and passing away of phenomena that leads to the complete destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk who masters the eight thoughts and the four jhanas will view a simple <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>rag-robe</b></a> with as much contentment and joy as a wealthy householder views a chest full of multicolored clothes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the simple <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>alms food</b></a> of a contented monk to the finest rice and various choice curries enjoyed by a wealthy householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is satisfied with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>lodging at the foot of a tree</b></a> lives as comfortably as a person in a luxurious, draft-free gabled mansion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk's simple <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>grass-spread bed</b></a> to a luxurious couch covered with fleecy rugs and wool covers for one who has mastered the Great Man's thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>fermented urine medicine</b></a> will seem as pleasant and useful as ghee, honey, or molasses to a monk who dwells in contentment and has attained the jhanas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his psychic power to appear instantly before <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Anuruddha</b></a> in the Eastern Bamboo Park after reading his mind from the Bhesakaḷā Grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>delighting in non-proliferation</b></a> as a mind that launches into and is released in the cessation of mental proliferation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> after contemplating the eight thoughts taught by the Buddha and living diligently in the Eastern Bamboo Park? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon attaining enlightenment, Venerable Anuruddha declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a> and the purpose of the holy life has been fulfilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha traveled from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>Bhesakaḷā Grove</b></a> to see Anuruddha just as quickly as a strong man might extend his flexed arm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s visit to Anuruddha was prompted specifically by his awareness of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>mental reflections</b></a> that had arisen in Anuruddha’s mind during his meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha reflected that the Dhamma is for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>contented</b></a> and not for the discontented, a thought which the Buddha highly praised? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to these eight specific reflections as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of a Great Man</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha recited verses stating that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>Teacher</b></a>, the unsurpassed in the world, approached him with a mind-made body through his psychic power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma is for those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>secluded</b></a> and not for those who delight in worldly company and socializing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the Dhamma is for one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>not lazy</b></a> but has instead initiated persistent energy to overcome unwholesome mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's instruction on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>eight thoughts</b></a> was intended to guide a monk toward the complete peace and unbinding of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after instructing Anuruddha personally, the Buddha returned to his location and repeated the teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>eight thoughts</b></a> to a larger assembly of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>three true knowledges</b></a> and fulfilled the Buddha's teaching after specifically contemplating the message about non-proliferation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma is for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato"><b>unmuddled in mind</b></a> and not for those who are habitually confused or forgetful of their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.30/en/sujato">AN 8.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a person doubts the Teacher, their mind does not incline toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>ardor</b></a>, devotion, or exertion, which is the first form of mental barrenness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, being "offended and displeased" with one’s fellow practitioners creates a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>mental barrenness</b></a> that prevents spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person with unabandoned mental shackles to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>waning moon</b></a>, which decreases in beauty, brightness, and diameter day by day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified doubt regarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a> (the Teaching) as a specific type of mental barrenness that stops a practitioner from applying themselves to the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a monastic is perplexed or lacks confidence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>training</b></a>, their mind will fail to incline toward the necessary perseverance for enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who is not free from lust and desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> possesses a "mental shackle" that binds their mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified an unexhausted desire for the physical <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>body</b></a> as one of the five mental shackles that prevents a person's mind from moving toward liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being obsessed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>forms</b></a> (rūpa) is a form of mental bondage that makes the mind stiff and unworkable for meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that eating as much as one's stomach can hold and then indulging in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>pleasure of sleep</b></a> is a shackle that stops the mind from engaging in spiritual exertion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered practicing the holy life with the goal of being reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>heavenly host</b></a> to be a mental shackle rather than a path to true freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>waxing moon</b></a> to describe how wholesome qualities grow in a person who has successfully abandoned the five mental barrennesses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said when a practitioner is "resolved and has confidence" in the Saṅgha, their mind finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>inclines</b></a> toward ardor and perseverance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a mind hindered by doubt as being unable to achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>devotion</b></a> or steady application to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a person with "unsevered" mental shackles, only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>decline</b></a> in wholesome states can be expected, regardless of the time of day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that discarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>perplexity</b></a> toward the training is essential for a practitioner’s mind to bend toward right exertion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when one is free from anger and resentment toward their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>fellow practitioners</b></a>, they have abandoned the fifth form of mental barrenness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mind becomes capable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> (padhāna) only after the shackles of sensual lust and physical obsession are severed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>mental shackles</b></a> (cetasovinibandha) to specifically describe the internal bonds that keep a person tied to sensual desire and the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that a practitioner who abandons these obstacles will see their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a> increase in color, luminosity, and breadth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that even the pursuit of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>divine status</b></a> through the holy life is a form of mental bondage that prevents ultimate spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>perseverance</b></a> (sātacca) as a key quality that is only possible when the mind is no longer "perplexed" by the teacher or the training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized "eating until the stomach is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>stuffed</b></a>" as a direct cause for a mind to remain bound and unable to strive for enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato"><b>clear mind</b></a>, free from doubt in the Teacher, Dhamma, and Saṅgha, is the necessary foundation for any spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.14/en/sujato">AN 10.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned a custom in the southern land known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>bone-washing</b></a> ceremony, which was a festive occasion involving food, drink, dancing, and music, yet he considered it to be low, vulgar, and unbeneficial for spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted a common worldly ritual with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>noble washing</b></a>, which—unlike secular traditions—leads to complete disenchantment, dispassion, cessation, and the ultimate realization of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>noble washing</b></a> is the specific practice through which beings who are subject to birth, aging, and death can finally gain liberation from these inescapable worldly conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by practicing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>noble washing</b></a>, practitioners can fully release themselves from the burdens of sorrow, lamentation, pain, dejection, and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> as the first factor of the noble washing, which functions to wash away wrong view and the numerous evil, unwholesome states that arise because of it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when one develops <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>, many wholesome states of mind reach fulfillment and completion through that development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>right intention</b></a> acts as a purifying agent within the noble washing, effectively cleansing the mind of wrong intention and its associated unwholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>right speech</b></a> is a critical component of the noble washing, as it serves to wash away all forms of wrong speech and the negative mental states that would otherwise stem from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that through the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>right action</b></a>, a person washes away wrong action, ensuring that unwholesome states are eliminated and wholesome states are brought to fulfillment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>right livelihood</b></a> is used in the noble washing to purify one's life by washing away wrong livelihood and the evil states that accompany it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>right effort</b></a> as a factor that washes away wrong effort, helping the practitioner to abandon harmful mental states while fostering those that are beneficial? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the cultivation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>right mindfulness</b></a> is essential to the noble washing because it washes away wrong mindfulness and the various unwholesome qualities that depend on it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> serves to wash away wrong concentration, allowing for the fulfillment of wholesome mental states and the purification of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>right knowledge</b></a> as a transcendent factor of the noble washing that cleanses the practitioner of wrong knowledge and its resultant evil, unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>right liberation</b></a> is the culmination of the noble washing, which finally washes away wrong liberation and brings the development of wholesome states to total perfection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>wrong liberation</b></a> is washed away by right liberation, the many evil unwholesome states that were dependent on that wrong liberation are also successfully removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato"><b>noble washing</b></a>, consisting of the purification of ten specific factors (from right view to right liberation), is the only washing that leads to freedom from birth, aging, death, and all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.107/en/sujato">AN 10.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, after emerging from his evening meditation, observed through his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> that several newly ordained monks were still asleep and snoring until the sun rose? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a consecrated khattiya king who indulges in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>pleasure of sleeping</b></a> and drowsiness cannot successfully rule his kingdom for his entire life or remain dear to his people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>much of the night</b></a> sitting in the assembly hall with his senior disciples before retiring to his own dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an ascetic with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>unguarded sense doors</b></a> and who is immoderate in eating will be unable to attain the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a village headman who is addicted to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>pleasure of drowsiness</b></a> will not be pleasing or dear to his village community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed ten eminent elder monks—including Sāriputta, Mahāmoggallāna, and Mahākassapa—who joined him in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>assembly hall</b></a> after rising from their own evening meditations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a general who indulges in the pleasure of sleeping and laziness will fail to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>dear and pleasing</b></a> to his troops or lead them effectively? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his monks that they should specifically train to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>devoted to wakefulness</b></a> rather than sleeping until sunrise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that an ascetic cannot achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> if they lack discernment of wholesome qualities and are not persistent in their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>guild headman</b></a> to show that prioritizing sleep over duty prevents a leader from being respected by their guild? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>moderate in eating</b></a> as a prerequisite for a monk to successfully realize the fruit of asceticism in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged monks to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>discerning wholesome qualities</b></a> and to practice meditation during both the first and last watches of the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that he had never seen or heard of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>royal agent</b></a> who could maintain his position while being obsessed with the pleasure of rest and drowsiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>aids to enlightenment</b></a> (bodhipakkhiya dhamma) must be actively developed through a consistent application to bhāvanā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>liberation by wisdom</b></a> as a state that remains out of reach for those who neglect wakefulness and indulge in excessive sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>hereditary official</b></a> would fail in his career if he chose the pleasure of sleeping over the fulfillment of his administrative duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s use of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> allowed him to perceive the unwholesome laziness of monks who were sleeping while the elders were practicing diligently? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>unguarded at the sense doors</b></a> is a major obstacle that prevents an ascetic from achieving the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically questioned why the younger monks were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato"><b>snoring</b></a> until sunrise instead of following the example of the elder disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.17/en/sujato">AN 6.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while traveling through the Kosalan country with a large community of monks, once stepped off the road to sit under a tree and teach about the spiritual impact of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>fisherman’s trade</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a fisherman who kills and sells fish does not acquire a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>great mass of wealth</b></a> or enjoy luxuries like riding on elephants, horses, or in chariots through his occupation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a fisherman fails to prosper materially because he looks upon the fish <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>intended for slaughter</b></a> with an evil mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned his monks whether they had ever seen or heard of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>butcher of cattle</b></a> who became wealthy and lived in luxury as a result of his profession? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a butcher of cattle does not attain high status or possessions because he views the animals intended for slaughter with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>evil mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>butcher of sheep</b></a> is unable to dwell in wealth or enjoy fine vehicles because of the unwholesome intention required for his trade? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>butcher of pigs</b></a> as one of those who does not achieve material success or luxury because of the unwholesome state of mind toward the creatures he kills? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>fowler</b></a>, or bird-catcher, does not find long-lasting prosperity or the ability to ride in fine vehicles through the act of killing and selling birds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>deer hunter</b></a> who kills and sells game does not dwell in great wealth or enjoy the comforts of high-status transportation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that if looking upon animals with an evil mind prevents material prosperity, then looking upon a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>human being</b></a> intended for slaughter with an evil mind is far more damaging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that looking upon a human being intended for slaughter with an evil mind leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>long-term harm</b></a> and suffering for the perpetrator? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that with the breakup of the body, after death, a person who views others with an evil mind intended for slaughter is reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>state of misery</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even those who kill <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>creatures of the animal realm</b></a> with an evil mind do not achieve the material benefits of high-status living? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the karmic consequence of an evil mind directed toward those intended for slaughter includes rebirth in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>evil mind</b></a> (pāpakena manasā) toward living beings as the primary reason why violent trades do not lead to the accumulation of wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>wandering through Kosala</b></a>, used the sight of a fisherman at work as a teaching moment to explain the relationship between intention and prosperity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that looking upon anyone intended for slaughter with an evil mind results in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>bad destination</b></a> and perdition after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the reason violent occupations like being a fisherman or a butcher do not result in being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato"><b>consumer of wealth</b></a> is the unwholesome mental state involved in the slaughter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.18/en/sujato">AN 6.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of death</b></a>, when developed and cultivated, is of great fruit and benefit, eventually merging in the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught his monks while staying at Nātika in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>Brick Hall</b></a> about the proper way to practice the recollection of mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered a monk to be living <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>negligently</b></a> if they only reflected on whether they might live for a single day and night to attend to the Buddha's teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> as the quality of a monk who contemplates death with every single breath he takes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha criticized the practice of mindfulness of death as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>sluggish</b></a> if the practitioner only thinks about surviving for the duration of a single meal of almsfood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ultimate aim of practicing mindfulness of death is to achieve the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavānaṃ khaya)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>keen mindfulness</b></a> of death as reflecting that one might die in the time it takes to chew and swallow just one morsel of food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that mindfulness of death must be practiced for the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>attending to the teaching</b></a> (sāsanaṃ manasi kareyyaṃ) within the limited time one has left to live? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that mindfulness of death is of such great importance because it <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>ends in the Deathless</b></a> (amatapariyosāna)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even thinking "I might live for the time it takes to swallow <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>four or five morsels</b></a>" is still considered a negligent way to practice meditation on death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his disciples to train themselves specifically to dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>heedfully</b></a> so they could develop a sharp and effective meditation on their own mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised a monk as practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>keenly</b></a> when they meditate on the possibility of death occurring between a single in-breath and out-breath? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha began his discourse in Nātika by directly asking his monks if they were actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>developing mindfulness of death</b></a> in their daily practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the recollection of death should lead to a sense of urgency to ensure much is done for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's dispensation</b></a> (bhagavato sāsanaṃ) while one still has life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between two types of practitioners: those who dwell negligently and those who dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>heedfully</b></a> in their daily reflection on death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>breathing in and out</b></a> as the shortest interval of time a monk should contemplate his survival to be considered practicing with urgency? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that thinking "May I live for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>one day</b></a>" to practice the teaching is an insufficient and sluggish way to contemplate one's own death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the results of properly developed mindfulness of death as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>of great fruit</b></a> (mahapphalā) and of immense benefit to the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded his teaching by instructing the monks on the specific way they should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>train themselves</b></a> to ensure they do not practice mindfulness of death sluggishly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha indicated that the most effective way to practice mindfulness of death is to reflect on it with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato"><b>extreme urgency</b></a>, as if one's life could end within the time of a single mouthful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.19/en/sujato">AN 6.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>Gulture's Peak Mountain</b></a> in Rājagaha when King Ajātasattu of Magadha sought his counsel regarding a planned attack on the Vajjis? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Ajātasattu of Magadha expressed a desire to completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>destroy the Vajjis</b></a>, despite their great power and influence, prompting him to send a messenger to the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that as long as the Vajjis hold <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>frequent assemblies</b></a>, they can expect only growth and not decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the Vajjis would prosper as long as they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>meet in harmony</b></a>, adjourn in harmony, and conduct their communal business in harmony? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the Vajjis would flourish if they do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>authorize what has not been authorized</b></a> and do not abolish what has already been authorized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the Vajjis' welfare is secured as long as they proceed according to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>ancient Vajjian traditions</b></a> as they were authorized in the past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that honoring and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>respecting elders</b></a> among the Vajjis, and deeming them worthy of being listened to, is a vital condition for their nation's growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the protection of women and girls, specifically that they are not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>forcibly detained</b></a> or abducted, as a key condition for the Vajjis' continued prosperity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that the Vajjis would not decline as long as they honor their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>shrines</b></a>, both internal and external, and maintain their traditional righteous offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a nation thrives when it provides <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>righteous protection</b></a>, shelter, and guard for arahants, ensuring that future arahants may come and those already present may live in comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha first taught these <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>seven conditions for welfare</b></a> (aparihāniya dhamma) to the Vajjis themselves while he was staying at the Sārandada Shrine in Vesālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Vassakāra, a high official of Magadha, concluded after hearing the Buddha's discourse that the Vajjis could not be defeated by King Ajātasattu in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>battle</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Vassakāra suggested the only way for a King to overcome the Vajjis would be through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>diplomacy</b></a> or by breaking their alliance and sowing dissension among them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the Buddha's conversation with the brahmin messenger regarding the Vajjis, the venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>Ānanda</b></a> was standing behind the Buddha, fanning him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically questioned Ānanda to confirm that the Vajjis were still practicing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>seven principles</b></a> that prevent decline before answering the king's messenger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that as long as the Vajjis maintain their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>traditional offerings</b></a> to their shrines, their growth is to be expected rather than their decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Ajātasattu's intent, as conveyed to the Buddha, was to bring the Vajjis to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>ruin and destruction</b></a> through military force? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Vassakāra acknowledged that even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>single condition</b></a> for welfare would lead to the Vajjis' growth, let alone all seven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the Vajjis would not decline if they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>adjourn in harmony</b></a> after their collective meetings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha regarded the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>honoring elders</b></a> as a vital component for the stability and success of the Vajjian people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Vajjis must ensure <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>future arahants</b></a> feel welcome to enter their territory to maintain their communal welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's discourse on the conditions for welfare was prompted by a high official seeking to find a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>strategic advantage</b></a> for King Ajātasattu's war plans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the Vajjis would prosper as long as they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato"><b>conduct their business</b></a> in harmony with one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.22/en/sujato">AN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that as long as monks hold <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato"><b>frequent assemblies</b></a> and assemble often, they can expect only growth and not decline in their spiritual community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato">AN 7.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that monks who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato"><b>meet in harmony</b></a>, adjourn in harmony, and conduct the business of the Sangha in harmony are assured of growth rather than decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato">AN 7.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that monks should not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato"><b>abolish what has been authorized</b></a> or authorize what has not been authorized, but should instead proceed according to the training rules? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato">AN 7.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that growth for the Sangha is expected as long as monks honor, respect, and listen to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato"><b>elder monks</b></a> who have been ordained for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato">AN 7.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that monks must not fall under the influence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> that leads to renewed existence if they wish to avoid spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato">AN 7.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that monks should remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato"><b>intent on forest dwellings</b></a> as a vital condition for their welfare and the prevention of decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato">AN 7.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised monks to maintain personal mindfulness so that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato"><b>well-behaved fellow practitioners</b></a> who have not yet arrived might come, and those already present might live in comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato">AN 7.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato"><b>Vulture's Peak Mountain</b></a> in Rājagaha when he first addressed the monks regarding these seven specific principles for non-decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato">AN 7.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato"><b>aparihāniya dhamma</b></a> to describe the conditions that ensure the growth of the community of monks rather than its deterioration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato">AN 7.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that monks who are not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato"><b>fond of work</b></a> or delighted in activity can expect to grow in the Dhamma and avoid decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato">AN 7.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato"><b>fond of talk</b></a> and delighted in chatter is a condition that leads to the decline of a practitioner's spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato">AN 7.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato"><b>fond of sleep</b></a> and delighted in it as one of the seven behaviors that prevent a monk from growing in his training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato">AN 7.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that monks will not decline as long as they are not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato"><b>fond of company</b></a> or delighted in socializing with groups? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato">AN 7.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that monks must not have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato"><b>evil desires</b></a> or fall under the influence of unwholesome intentions if they are to prosper in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato">AN 7.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that avoiding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato"><b>evil friends</b></a>, evil companions, and evil associates is essential for a monk's spiritual growth and non-decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato">AN 7.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned monks against <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato"><b>stopping halfway</b></a> in their spiritual journey after achieving only a low level of distinction, rather than reaching the final goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato">AN 7.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described specific behaviors as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato"><b>principles of non-decline</b></a> which, when strictly maintained, ensure the continued development of a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato">AN 7.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that as long as monks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato"><b>adjourn in harmony</b></a> from their meetings, they can expect their community to flourish and prosper? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato">AN 7.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to regard the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato"><b>elderly members of the Sangha</b></a> as fathers and leaders, and to deem them worthy of being listened to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.23/en/sujato">AN 7.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that practitioners who do not indulge in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato"><b>delight in socializing</b></a> are more likely to experience growth in their Dhamma practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.24/en/sujato">AN 7.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that as long as monks possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a>, they can expect only growth and not decline in their spiritual lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato">AN 7.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato"><b>moral shame</b></a>, or the inner sense of conscience regarding unwholesome actions, as one of the seven principles that prevent the decline of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato">AN 7.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, monks will prosper as long as they maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato"><b>moral dread</b></a>, which is the fear of the consequences of wrongdoing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato">AN 7.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that being a person of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato"><b>great learning</b></a> and having well-retained knowledge of the teachings is a vital condition for the welfare of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato">AN 7.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that growth for the community is assured as long as monks remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato"><b>energetic</b></a> in abandoning unwholesome states and undertaking wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato">AN 7.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the possession of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a>, characterized by the ability to remember and recall what was done and said long ago, as a key quality for non-decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato">AN 7.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that monks will flourish so long as they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato"><b>wise</b></a> and possess the understanding of the rise and fall of phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.25/en/sujato">AN 7.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.26/en/sujato"><b>investigation of phenomena</b></a> enlightenment factor is one of the seven specific conditions that ensure the growth of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.26/en/sujato">AN 7.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted the cultivation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.26/en/sujato"><b>rapture</b></a> (pīti) as an enlightenment factor that serves as a principle of non-decline for practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.26/en/sujato">AN 7.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that growth, rather than decline, is to be expected for monks who cultivate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.26/en/sujato"><b>tranquility</b></a> enlightenment factor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.26/en/sujato">AN 7.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.26/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> enlightenment factor as a critical practice for maintaining the stability and progress of the monkhood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.26/en/sujato">AN 7.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that monks will not decline as long as they cultivate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.26/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> enlightenment factor in their spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.26/en/sujato">AN 7.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk in training (sekha) will face spiritual decline if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.28/en/sujato"><b>fond of work</b></a> and delights in worldly activities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.28/en/sujato">AN 7.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.28/en/sujato"><b>fond of talk</b></a> and delighted in chatter as one of the seven behaviors that lead to the regression of a monk in training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.28/en/sujato">AN 7.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk in training avoids decline by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.28/en/sujato"><b>guarding the sense doors</b></a> and not leaving them unprotected? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.28/en/sujato">AN 7.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.28/en/sujato"><b>moderation in eating</b></a> is a crucial quality for a monk in training to maintain their progress in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.28/en/sujato">AN 7.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a monk in training should not become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.28/en/sujato"><b>overly involved</b></a> in communal duties, but should instead trust that the experienced elder monks will handle those responsibilities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.28/en/sujato">AN 7.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the act of giving to sowing seeds in a field, explaining that just as seeds fail to thrive in poor soil, a gift given to recipients who lack spiritual discipline does not yield <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato"><b>great fruit</b></a> or great benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato">AN 8.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a field which is uneven, rocky, and salt-soiled is not conducive to a rich harvest, just as gifts given to recluses and brahmins who hold <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> result in little splendor or reward? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato">AN 8.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a field that has not been plowed deep or lacks irrigation channels is unproductive, much like the spiritual results of giving to those who live with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato"><b>wrong livelihood</b></a> and wrong effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato">AN 8.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a productive field as one that is level, free of stones, and not salt-soiled, using this as an analogy for the great merit gained when giving to those who possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato"><b>Right Resolve</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato">AN 8.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a gift to be highly fruitful, the recipients should be recluses or brahmins who practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato"><b>Right Speech</b></a>, Right Action, and the other factors of the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato">AN 8.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a gift is offered to those who have attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato"><b>Right Concentration</b></a>, it results in a vast reward, similar to how a well-prepared field with water inlets and outlets produces an abundant crop? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato">AN 8.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a successful harvest—where seeds are good, the field is fertile, and the rain is timely—to illustrate the success that comes from offering food to those of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato"><b>consummate virtue</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato">AN 8.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised those who desire personal success and benefit to associate with and serve those who possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato"><b>consummate wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato">AN 8.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by understanding the world as it really is, a practitioner attains a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato"><b>consummate view</b></a>, which serves as a foundation for further spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato">AN 8.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who is consummate in knowledge and conduct, and who has achieved a consummate deed, is one who truly travels with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato"><b>consummate mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato">AN 8.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the act of "shaking off all stains" and reaching the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato"><b>consummate Nibbāna</b></a> as the ultimate state of being released from all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato">AN 8.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the attainment of Nibbāna as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato"><b>total success</b></a>, representing the final liberation from the cycle of rebirth and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.34/en/sujato">AN 8.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>eight rebirths through giving</b></a>, describing how a person can be reborn in specific human or heavenly realms by making offerings and directing their mind toward that destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed various items that can be offered to recluses or brahmins to facilitate these rebirths, including food, drink, clothing, vehicles, garlands, scents, ointments, bedding, dwellings, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>lighting</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a donor can be reborn among <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>wealthy kshatriyas</b></a> (nobles) if they observe them enjoying the five cords of sensual pleasure and subsequently fix their mind on joining them after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who practices giving and aspires to the company of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>wealthy brahmins</b></a> can achieve that rebirth, provided they focus and develop their mind toward that goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a virtuous donor who offers necessities to recluses can be reborn among <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>wealthy householders</b></a> by setting their heart on that specific status and state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the mental aspiration for a better rebirth only succeeds for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>virtuous person</b></a>, not for an immoral one, because the heart's desire of a virtuous person is effective due to its purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a donor who hears that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>Four Great Kings</b></a> (devas) are long-lived, beautiful, and full of happiness can be reborn among them by fixing their mind on that realm after giving a gift? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that giving gifts while mentally aspiring to the company of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>Tāvatiṃsa devas</b></a> can lead to rebirth in that heavenly realm, provided the donor is virtuous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified rebirth among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>Yāma devas</b></a> as a potential outcome for a donor who hears of their happiness and focuses their mind on being reborn there after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person can achieve rebirth among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>Tusita devas</b></a> by combining acts of generosity with a strong, purified mental resolve toward that specific celestial existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a virtuous person who practices giving and develops their mind can be reborn among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>Nimmānarati devas</b></a>, those who delight in their own creations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the eight rebirths through giving is to the company of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>Paranimmitavasavatti devas</b></a>, who exercise control over the creations of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a donor to be reborn among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>Brahmakāyika devas</b></a> (the Brahma-group), it is not enough to be virtuous; they must also be dispassionate and free from lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically noted that the mental aspiration of a donor for rebirth in the Brahma realm succeeds only for one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>free from lust</b></a>, unlike the other seven types of rebirth which do not strictly require the absence of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person gives a gift and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>fixes the mind</b></a> on a lower state of existence without further spiritual development, that focused mind leads them to that specific rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the goal-oriented focus of a donor as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>mental aspiration</b></a> (cetopaṇidhi), which becomes effective and prospers when the person's character is purified through virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a donor must not only have an initial wish but must also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>develop that thought</b></a> and resolve upon it for it to lead to a specific rebirth after the breaking up of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the act of giving food and drink to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato"><b>recluses and brahmins</b></a> serves as the necessary foundation for making the mental aspirations that lead to these eight types of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.35/en/sujato">AN 8.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>bases of meritorious activity</b></a>, which consist of giving, moral discipline, and mental development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who performs only a meager amount of merit through giving and moral discipline, while failing to engage in meditation, is reborn into a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>human misfortune</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an individual who practices giving and moral discipline to a moderate degree but does not succeed in mental development will experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>human prosperity</b></a> in their next life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that extraordinary practice of giving and moral discipline, even without the achievement of mental development, can lead to rebirth among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>Cātumahārājika devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that the Four Great Kings surpass the other devas in their realm in ten respects, including divine life span and beauty, because they performed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>extraordinary merit</b></a> in their past lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who performs supreme merit through giving and moral discipline, but lacks mental development, may be reborn in the company of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>Tāvatiṃsa devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how Sakka, the lord of the devas, surpasses other deities in the Tāvatiṃsa realm in ten ways, such as possessing superior <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>divine happiness</b></a> and fame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified rebirth among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>Yāma devas</b></a> as a potential destination for those who have cultivated an extraordinary amount of merit through giving and virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that the young deva Suyāma surpasses the other Yāma devas in ten respects, including having superior <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>divine authority</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a donor who is consummate in moral discipline but has not mastered meditation can be reborn among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>Tusita devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the young deva Santusita excels over other Tusita devas because he surpassed them in the practice of giving and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>moral discipline</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>Nimmānarati devas</b></a>—those who delight in their own creations—as a realm where those with extraordinary merit are reborn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the young deva Sunimmito surpasses his peers in ten respects, such as having superior <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>divine sights</b></a> and sounds, due to his superior merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>Paranimmitavasavatti devas</b></a> as a destination for those who have performed supreme giving and virtue but have not achieved mental development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the young deva Vasavattī surpasses other deities in his realm in ten ways, including having superior <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>divine odors</b></a> and tastes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>divine touches</b></a> as one of the ten sensory and existential respects in which celestial rulers excel over their subjects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a person to avoid human misfortune and attain a heavenly rebirth, they must cultivate merit through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>giving</b></a> and moral discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>mental development</b></a> (bhāvanāmayaṃ) to describe the third base of merit, noting that many who succeed in giving and virtue still fail to achieve this practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the degree of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>divine splendor</b></a> one receives in heaven is directly proportional to how much giving and moral discipline they practiced in their past life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato"><b>divine fame</b></a> and divine authority as specific rewards gained by those who surpass others in their cultivation of meritorious activities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.36/en/sujato">AN 8.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a> is considered the foremost of all beings—whether they are footless, two-footed, four-footed, or many-footed—heedfulness is likewise the foremost of all wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato">AN 10.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared wholesome states to the footprints of land animals, explaining that just as all footprints fit within the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato"><b>elephant’s footprint</b></a> because of its great size, all wholesome states are rooted in and converge upon heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato">AN 10.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the architectural simile of a peaked house to illustrate that just as all the rafters lead to, incline toward, and converge at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato"><b>roof peak</b></a>, so too is heedfulness the point of convergence for all wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato">AN 10.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato"><b>black orris</b></a> as the best of all root fragrances, using this as a comparison to show that heedfulness is the best and foremost of all wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato">AN 10.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that among all heartwood fragrances, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato"><b>red sandalwood</b></a> is considered the best, just as heedfulness is considered the most excellent among all wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato">AN 10.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato"><b>jasmine</b></a> to be the foremost of all flower fragrances, serving as a simile for how heedfulness stands as the foremost of all wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato">AN 10.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as all petty kings are followers of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato"><b>wheel-turning monarch</b></a>, who is considered the best among them, all wholesome states are subordinate to and led by the quality of heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato">AN 10.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the radiance of all the stars in the sky is not worth a sixteenth part of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato"><b>moonlight</b></a>, which is considered the best of them, just as heedfulness outshines all other wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato">AN 10.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the power of heedfulness to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato"><b>autumn sun</b></a> which, when it rises into a cloudless sky, dispels all darkness from space and shines with brilliant splendor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato">AN 10.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that just as the great rivers—the Ganges, Yamuna, Aciravati, Sarabhu, and Mahi—all flow, slope, and incline toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato"><b>ocean</b></a>, all wholesome states similarly lead toward heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato">AN 10.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that every single <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato"><b>wholesome state</b></a> in existence is rooted in heedfulness and has heedfulness as its point of convergence and its summit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.15/en/sujato">AN 10.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to live with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>protector</b></a> rather than living without one, explaining that ten specific qualities serve as internal safeguards? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who lives <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>without a protector</b></a>—meaning they lack essential spiritual qualities—dwells in a state of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and remaining restrained by the monastic code (Pātimokkha) as a primary quality that makes for a protector? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>seeing danger</b></a> even in slight faults, and who trains strictly in the rules of conduct, is developing a quality that provides internal protection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a> person as one who has heard much, retained what they heard, and accumulated teachings that are good in the beginning, middle, and end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered it a protective quality for a person to have teachings that describe the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>spiritual life</b></a> as perfectly complete and pure, having mastered them by speech and investigated them with the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>good friend</b></a>, companion, and comrade is not just a social benefit but an essential quality that helps protect a person’s spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the quality of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>easy to speak to</b></a> (amenable), noting that one who is patient and good at taking instruction possesses a quality that makes for a protector? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>skillful and diligent</b></a> in various duties for one’s fellow practitioners as a quality that contributes to internal protection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>sound judgment</b></a> and the ability to organize and execute tasks effectively for the community as a specific quality that serves as a protector? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>lover of the Dhamma</b></a> and is delightful in conversation about the teachings is developing an important quality of protection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that experiencing great joy in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>higher Dhamma</b></a> and higher Discipline (Abhidhamma and Abhivinaya) is one of the ten qualities that makes for a protector? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> to abandon unwholesome states and undertake wholesome states as an essential protective quality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>firm and persistent</b></a> in one's efforts, never shirking the responsibility of cultivating wholesome qualities, as a quality that makes for a protector? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>contentment</b></a> with any kind of robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines is a quality that provides protection to a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>supreme mindfulness</b></a> and alertness as a key protector-making quality, enabling a person to keep the path in mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a mindful person who can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>remember and recall</b></a> what was done and said long ago possesses a quality that makes for an internal protector? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato"><b>penetrative wisdom</b></a> regarding the arising and passing away of things—which leads to the complete destruction of suffering—as the tenth quality that makes for a protector? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.17/en/sujato">AN 10.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted his followers to live with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>protector</b></a> rather than living without one, warning that dwelling without internal safeguards leads to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and remains restrained by the monastic code (Pātimokkha), senior monks consider him worth speaking to and advising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who is virtuous and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>sees danger</b></a> even in slight faults will be viewed as worth instructing by monks of middle rank? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a monk is well-trained in the rules of conduct, even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>newly ordained monks</b></a> will think he is worth advising, ensuring he receives the compassion of his peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a monk who receives the compassion and guidance of his seniors, peers, and juniors, only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>growth in wholesome states</b></a> is to be expected, not decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a> and has accumulated teachings that are "good in the beginning, middle, and end" is viewed by the community as worth instructing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a protector as one whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>teachings are mastered</b></a> by speech and investigated with the mind, making that person a candidate for the helpful advice of fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>good friend</b></a>, companion, and comrade as a protective quality that makes a monk appear worth advising to the rest of the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>easy to speak to</b></a> (amenable) and patient when receiving instruction is a key quality that makes a person worth speaking to by their peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the quality of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>good at taking instruction</b></a>, noting that it invites senior monks to offer the guidance necessary for spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>skillful and diligent</b></a> in the various duties for one's fellow practitioners as a quality that makes a monk worth advising by his community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>sound judgment</b></a> and the ability to organize tasks for fellow monks as a protector-making quality that fosters growth in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>lover of the Dhamma</b></a> who is delightful in conversation as someone whom senior, middle, and new monks alike will deem worth instructing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that experiencing great joy in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>higher Dhamma</b></a> and higher Discipline (Abhidhamma and Abhivinaya) is a quality that makes a practitioner worth advising by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> to abandon unwholesome states and undertake wholesome ones makes a monk a "protector" who is respected and advised by his peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>firm and persistent</b></a> in one's efforts, never shirking the responsibility of wholesome qualities, as a trait that makes one worth instructing by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>content</b></a> with any kind of robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines is seen by his fellow monks as someone worth advising and speaking to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>supreme mindfulness</b></a> and alertness as a protective quality that ensures a monk is viewed by others as worth instructing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mindful person who can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>remember and recall</b></a> what was done and said long ago possesses a quality that leads to the compassion and guidance of fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>penetrative wisdom</b></a> regarding the arising and passing away of things as a protector-making quality that makes a monk worth speaking to by all ranks of the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wisdom which is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>noble and penetrative</b></a> and leads to the destruction of suffering ensures that one will be supported and advised by senior, middle, and new monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for one who has the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>ten qualities</b></a> of a protector, the community's support ensures they will never face a decline in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his monks to live with safeguards, because one who lives <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato"><b>without a protector</b></a> dwells in a state of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.18/en/sujato">AN 10.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>, wrong view is ejected, along with the many bad unwholesome states that arise with wrong view as a condition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato">AN 10.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato"><b>right knowledge</b></a>, wrong knowledge is ejected and many wholesome states reach fulfillment through development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato">AN 10.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are ten "qualities to be ejected," and for one with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato"><b>right liberation</b></a>, wrong liberation and the various unwholesome states conditioned by it are cast off? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato">AN 10.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Brahmin Saṅgārava asked about the "near shore" and the "far shore," the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> as the near shore and right view as the far shore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato">AN 10.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the spiritual "far shore" as consisting of ten specific qualities, including <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato"><b>right intention</b></a>, right speech, and right action? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato">AN 10.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato"><b>wrong livelihood</b></a> is considered part of the "near shore," which refers to the state of existence that most people continue to run along instead of crossing over? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato">AN 10.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato"><b>few people</b></a> among humanity actually reach the far shore of liberation, while the rest of the population just runs along the near bank? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato">AN 10.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who follow the well-expounded Dhamma will cross over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato"><b>realm of Death</b></a>, a domain that is otherwise extremely difficult to traverse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato">AN 10.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the wise to leave behind the "dark way" and instead develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato"><b>bright way</b></a> by moving from the state of attachment to the state of non-attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato">AN 10.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a wise person should seek delight in solitude and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato"><b>abandon sensual pleasures</b></a>, choosing to have nothing of one's own to find true joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato">AN 10.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a wise person should strive to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato"><b>purify oneself</b></a> from all mental defilements that cloud the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato">AN 10.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those whose minds are well-developed in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato"><b>factors of enlightenment</b></a> and who have destroyed their mental taints are fully extinguished and liberated in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato">AN 10.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato"><b>right speech</b></a>, wrong speech is ejected along with the many unwholesome states that arise from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato">AN 10.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato"><b>right action</b></a> acts as a purifying force that ejects wrong action and allows wholesome qualities to reach the fulfillment of their development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato">AN 10.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato"><b>right effort</b></a> as one of the ten qualities that cause the ejection of their wrong counterparts and promote the growth of wholesome mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato">AN 10.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, establishing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato"><b>right mindfulness</b></a> results in the ejection of wrong mindfulness and the cessation of the unwholesome states conditioned by it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.110/en/sujato">AN 10.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> as part of the "far shore" that leads a practitioner away from the dangerous near bank of wrong practices and toward liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato">AN 10.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who delight in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato"><b>relinquishing grasping</b></a> and have well-developed minds are those who have destroyed their taints and reached the goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.117/en/sujato">AN 10.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once asked the Brahmin Jāṇussoṇi why he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>freshly bathed</b></a>, dressed in new linen, and holding a handful of wet kusa grass on the Uposatha day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Jāṇussoṇi explained to the Buddha that his community practiced a ritual called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>the descent</b></a> (paccorohaṇī), which involved specific purification ceremonies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Brahmin ritual described to the Buddha, practitioners would plaster the ground with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>fresh cow dung</b></a> and spread green kusa grass before sleeping as part of their "descent"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Jāṇussoṇi told the Buddha that during their ritual, Brahmins would rise three times during the night to worship the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>sacred fire</b></a> with their hands joined in reverence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Jāṇussoṇi informed the Buddha that his people would feed the ritual fire with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>clarified butter</b></a>, oil, and fresh butter during the descent ceremony? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was told that Brahmins performing their ancestral ritual would address the fire, saying, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>We descend to your honor</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Jāṇussoṇi described to the Buddha how the descent ceremony concluded with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>satisfying other Brahmins</b></a> with choice food and drinks after the night of fire worship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Jāṇussoṇi that the "descent" in the Noble One’s discipline focuses on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>abandoning unwholesome states</b></a> rather than external physical rituals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a noble disciple "descends" by reflecting that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> has a bad result both in this present life and in the life to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a noble disciple should "descend" from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>wrong intention</b></a> by abandoning it after contemplating its negative consequences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the "descent" in the Noble One's discipline as the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>abandoning wrong speech</b></a> because it leads to suffering and harm in both this life and the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one should recognize that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>wrong action</b></a> has a miserable result and thus "descend" from it by giving it up entirely? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>wrong livelihood</b></a> must be abandoned by a practitioner who realizes its harmful results in this life and the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a disciple "descends" when they abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>wrong effort</b></a> after reflecting on the bad result it brings in both the present and future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>wrong mindfulness</b></a> is something to be discarded by those who see its negative impact on one's current and future states of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a practitioner "descends" from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>wrong concentration</b></a> because they recognize its unwholesome fruits in both this life and the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>wrong knowledge</b></a> as a quality from which a noble disciple must "descend" to avoid bad results in this life and the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the final step in the Noble One's "descent" involves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>abandoning wrong liberation</b></a> after contemplating its harmful consequences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Jāṇussoṇi admitted to the Buddha that the traditional Brahmin ritual was not worth even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato"><b>sixteenth part</b></a> of the Buddha’s Noble Descent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.119/en/sujato">AN 10.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of death</b></a>, when developed and cultivated, is of great fruit and benefit, eventually plunging into and culminating in the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised practitioners to reflect at nightfall that death could come from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>snake bite</b></a>, a scorpion sting, or a centipede bite, and that such a possibility should motivate one to check for unabandoned unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, if a person realizes they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome states</b></a> that have not yet been abandoned, they should exercise extraordinary desire and effort to remove them, just as one would if their clothes were on fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the urgency needed to abandon evil mental qualities to the way a person would act with great mindfulness and clear comprehension if their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>head was on fire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a practitioner reflects at daybreak and finds they are free of unwholesome states, they should dwell in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>joy and gladness</b></a> while continuing to train in wholesome states day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>stumbling and falling</b></a> as one of the many common causes of death that a person should consider when reflecting on the urgency of their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>ill-digested food</b></a> could become a cause of death, serving as a reminder that one’s life can end unexpectedly at any time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified internal physical disturbances, such as a provocation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>bile</b></a>, as potential internal causes of death that one must be spiritually prepared for? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that an excess of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>phlegm</b></a> could lead to death, illustrating the fragility of life and the importance of not delaying spiritual work? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>sharp internal winds</b></a> could suddenly bring about the end of one's life, which is why a monk should reflect on whether he is prepared for such an obstacle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while staying at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>Brick Hall</b></a> in Nātika, the Buddha gave a detailed teaching on how to practice mindfulness of death during both the day and the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that if one were to die with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>unabandoned evil qualities</b></a>, those qualities would act as an obstacle to their spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to reflect at nightfall by asking themselves if there are any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome mental states</b></a> that would be an obstacle to them if they were to die during the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the goal of mindfulness of death is for the practice to have the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>Deathless as its consummation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a specific way to reflect at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>daybreak</b></a>, considering the many causes of death that could occur during the day to spur on immediate spiritual effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha required <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>extraordinary effort</b></a> and zeal from his followers to abandon unwholesome states, using the vivid simile of a person trying to extinguish a fire on their own body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>scorpion</b></a> bite as a potential cause of death that a practitioner should keep in mind to maintain their sense of spiritual urgency? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner who is free of unwholesome states should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>training in wholesome states</b></a> throughout the day and night to maximize the benefits of their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the use of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>clear comprehension</b></a> (sampajañña) and mindfulness (sati) as tools to be used when striving to abandon the mental states that hinder spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on mindfulness of death at Nātika highlights that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato"><b>centipedes</b></a> are among the external dangers that should remind us of our mortality and the need for mental purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.20/en/sujato">AN 6.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a lay follower avoids decline in the true Dhamma when they do not neglect <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato"><b>seeing monks</b></a> to receive spiritual guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato">AN 7.29</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the regular practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato"><b>hearing the good Dhamma</b></a> as one of the seven essential factors that prevents a lay follower from falling away from their spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato">AN 7.29</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a lay follower ensures their continuous spiritual progress when they diligently train themselves in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato"><b>higher virtue</b></a> (<i>adhisīla</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato">AN 7.29</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a lay follower thrives when they are full of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato"><b>confidence</b></a> (<i>pasāda</i>) toward the community of monks, whether they are senior, middle-ranking, or newly ordained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato">AN 7.29</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that a lay follower listen to the Dhamma with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato"><b>non-critical mind</b></a>, rather than looking for flaws or faults in the teacher, to ensure they do not decline in the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato">AN 7.29</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a lay follower remains on the right path when they do not seek those <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato"><b>worthy of offerings</b></a> (<i>dakkhiṇeyya</i>) outside of the Buddha's dispensation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato">AN 7.29</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a lay follower's spiritual life faces decline if they prioritize giving their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato"><b>prior service</b></a> and primary support to religious groups outside of the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.29/en/sujato">AN 7.29</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged associating with a friend who is generous enough to give what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato"><b>hard to give</b></a>, such as their own precious resources, to help another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato">AN 7.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true friend worth associating with as someone who performs what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato"><b>hard to do</b></a> for the sake of their companion's benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato">AN 7.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a friend to be sought out is one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato"><b>endures hard words</b></a> and difficult behavior from their companion without retaliating or ending the friendship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato">AN 7.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person as a noble friend when they trust you enough to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato"><b>reveal their secrets</b></a>, showing deep honesty and vulnerability? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato">AN 7.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one quality of a reliable friend is that they carefully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato"><b>keep your secrets</b></a> hidden from others, protecting your privacy and reputation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato">AN 7.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a friend should be associated with if they do <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato"><b>not abandon you</b></a> when you fall into times of trouble or great misfortune? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato">AN 7.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person is a true friend if they do <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato"><b>not despise you</b></a> even when you have become "ruined" or have lost your wealth and social status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.36/en/sujato">AN 7.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are eight distinct qualities of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.37/en/sujato"><b>gift of a good person</b></a>, which include giving items that are clean, choice, and allowable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.37/en/sujato">AN 8.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a truly good person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.37/en/sujato"><b>gives after careful consideration</b></a> rather than acting impulsively when practicing generosity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.37/en/sujato">AN 8.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the specific hallmarks of a good person's generosity is that they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.37/en/sujato"><b>settle the mind in confidence</b></a> while they are in the act of giving to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.37/en/sujato">AN 8.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a good person as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.37/en/sujato"><b>is elated after giving</b></a>, experiencing no regret or remorse even after parting with many of their material possessions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.37/en/sujato">AN 8.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise and faithful person who practices generosity with a mind of liberation will be reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.37/en/sujato"><b>harmless, happy world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.37/en/sujato">AN 8.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a good person born into a family to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.38/en/sujato"><b>great rain cloud</b></a> that nourishes all crops, because their presence brings benefit, welfare, and happiness to a vast number of people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.38/en/sujato">AN 8.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a good person is born for the welfare of many different groups, including their parents, their spouse and children, and even their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.38/en/sujato"><b>slaves, workers, and servants</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.38/en/sujato">AN 8.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.38/en/sujato"><b>departed ancestors and deities</b></a> among the various beings who find happiness and benefit when a good person is born into a family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.38/en/sujato">AN 8.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a wise person living at home as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.38/en/sujato"><b>honors their parents</b></a> both day and night, specifically remembering the help and support they received from them in the past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.38/en/sujato">AN 8.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a good person, established in firm faith and a love for the Dhamma, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.38/en/sujato"><b>honors virtuous ascetics</b></a> and those living a holy life after they have gone forth from the home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.38/en/sujato">AN 8.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.38/en/sujato"><b>removing the stain of stinginess</b></a>, a person becomes helpful to everyone and eventually reaches a blissful and secure world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.38/en/sujato">AN 8.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the practices of giving at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.37/en/sujato"><b>proper time and giving regularly</b></a> as essential characteristics that define a good person's approach to charity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.37/en/sujato">AN 8.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the primary purpose and benefit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato"><b>wholesome virtuous behavior</b></a> is to bring about the absence of regret? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato">AN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato"><b>non-regret</b></a> (or the lack of remorse) is not just a moral state but serves the specific purpose and benefit of giving rise to joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato">AN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the purpose and benefit of experiencing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato"><b>joy</b></a> is to lead the practitioner directly toward the arising of rapture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato">AN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato"><b>rapture</b></a> as a necessary stage in spiritual development that has the purpose and benefit of bringing about physical and mental tranquility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato">AN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the purpose and benefit of achieving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato"><b>tranquility</b></a> is the subsequent experience of happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato">AN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the feeling of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a> serves the essential purpose and benefit of providing the foundation for concentration to arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato">AN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the purpose and benefit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> is the development of the knowledge and vision of things as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato">AN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that gaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of things as they really are</b></a> has the specific purpose and benefit of leading one to disenchantment and dispassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato">AN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ultimate purpose and benefit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato"><b>disenchantment and dispassion</b></a> is the attainment of the knowledge and vision of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato">AN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato"><b>wholesome virtuous behavior</b></a> serves as the starting point that leads a person step-by-step to the highest peak of spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.1/en/sujato">AN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the brahmin Saṅgārava asked about the "near shore" and the "far shore," the Buddha explained that the majority of people merely run along the near bank, while only a few are able to reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>far shore</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>taking life</b></a> as representing the "near shore," whereas the "far shore" is defined by the abstinence from taking life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>taking what is not given</b></a> is considered the "near shore," while the "far shore" is reached by those who abstain from such theft? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> constitutes the "near shore," but those who cross to the "far shore" are those who practice abstinence from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>false speech</b></a> as the "near shore," while the "far shore" is attained through the abstinence from lying? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a> as the "near shore," whereas the "far shore" is defined by refraining from speech that creates discord? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a> as a quality of the "near shore," while the "far shore" is marked by the abstinence from such speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a> is the "near shore," while the "far shore" is reached by those who abstain from useless talk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>covetousness</b></a> as the "near shore," while the "far shore" is characterized by uncovetousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> represents the "near shore," whereas non-ill will is the "far shore" to be attained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> is the "near shore," while right view is the "far shore"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who follow the Dhamma when it is well-expounded will cross over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>realm of death</b></a>, even though it is extremely difficult to cross? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a wise person should abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>dark qualities</b></a> and instead develop bright ones, moving from a home life to homelessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described those who have attained Nibbāna in this world as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato"><b>taint-destroyed</b></a> and radiant, having well-developed their minds in the factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.169/en/sujato">AN 10.169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that unwholesome actions, such as taking life or stealing, are of three kinds: rooted in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.174/en/sujato"><b>greed, hatred, or delusion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.174/en/sujato">AN 10.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified greed, hatred, and delusion as the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.174/en/sujato"><b>sources for the origin of kamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.174/en/sujato">AN 10.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that even mental actions like covetousness, ill will, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.174/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> are each of three kinds, being rooted in either greed, hatred, or delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.174/en/sujato">AN 10.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.174/en/sujato"><b>destruction of greed</b></a>, hatred, and delusion leads to the destruction of the source for the origin of kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.174/en/sujato">AN 10.174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity informed the Buddha that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato"><b>delight in work</b></a> (kammārāmatā) is one of three primary qualities that lead to a monk's spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato">AN 6.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to a deity who visited the Buddha, a practitioner’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato"><b>delight in talk</b></a> (bhassārāmatā) serves as a condition for falling away from wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato">AN 6.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity explained to the Buddha that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato"><b>delight in sleep</b></a> (niddārāmatā) is a specific factor that contributes to a monk's decline in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato">AN 6.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told his monks that it is a misfortune and a poor gain for them if even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato"><b>deities know</b></a> when they are declining in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato">AN 6.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato"><b>delight in company</b></a> (saṅgaṇikārāmatā) as a dangerous quality that leads to a practitioner's spiritual deterioration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato">AN 6.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato"><b>difficult to admonish</b></a> (dovacassatā) is a key trait that causes a person to decline in wholesome mental qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato">AN 6.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned his followers that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato"><b>bad friendship</b></a> (pāpamittatā) is one of the six total factors responsible for a monk's decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato">AN 6.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that anyone in the past who declined in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a> did so specifically because of six factors: delight in work, talk, sleep, and company, combined with being difficult to admonish and having bad friends? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato">AN 6.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that anyone who will decline in the future, or is declining in the present, is doing so because of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato"><b>six factors of decline</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.21/en/sujato">AN 6.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught six specific qualities of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato"><b>non-decline</b></a> (aparihāniyā dhammā) that ensure a practitioner remains established in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato">AN 6.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato"><b>not delighting in work</b></a> (na kammārāmatā) is an essential practice for preventing a monk's spiritual fall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato">AN 6.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato"><b>not delighting in talk</b></a> (na bhassārāmatā) to ensure they do not decline in their spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato">AN 6.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato"><b>not delighting in sleep</b></a> (na niddārāmatā) is a crucial quality for maintaining one's level of wholesome attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato">AN 6.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato"><b>not delighting in company</b></a> (na saṅgaṇikārāmatā) as a preventative measure against spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato">AN 6.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato"><b>easy to admonish</b></a> (sovacassatā) is a positive quality that prevents a monk from falling away from the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato">AN 6.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> (kalyāṇamittatā) as the final of six factors that safeguard a practitioner from spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato">AN 6.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that anyone in the past who did not decline in wholesome states remained successful because they practiced these <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato"><b>six qualities of non-decline</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato">AN 6.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that future practitioners will avoid decline only by adhering to the six factors of non-decline, which include avoiding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato"><b>delight in company</b></a> and talk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.22/en/sujato">AN 6.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can quickly realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato"><b>four analytical knowledges</b></a> (<i>paṭisambhidā</i>) by himself when he possesses seven specific qualities, including understanding when his mind is sluggish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato">AN 7.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the essential factors for attaining the analytical knowledges is for a monk to recognize exactly when his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato"><b>mind is constricted</b></a> internally? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato">AN 7.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk who knows precisely when his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato"><b>mind is scattered</b></a> externally is practicing one of the seven qualities that lead to the realization of the four analytical knowledges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato">AN 7.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk reaches higher spiritual attainments when he is fully aware of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato"><b>feelings</b></a> (<i>vedanā</i>) as they arise, as they persist, and as they disappear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato">AN 7.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the mindful observation of how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato"><b>perceptions</b></a> (<i>saññā</i>) arise and vanish as a key quality for a monk to realize the four analytical knowledges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato">AN 7.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who understands exactly how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato"><b>thoughts</b></a> (<i>vitakkā</i>) arise, stay, and pass away is endowed with one of the seven qualities necessary for spiritual breakthrough? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato">AN 7.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk must have the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato"><b>sign of suitable</b></a> and unsuitable things well-grasped and well-penetrated by wisdom to attain the four analytical knowledges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.38/en/sujato">AN 7.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Venerable Sāriputta for having realized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.39/en/sujato"><b>four analytical knowledges</b></a> by himself because he perfectly understood when his mind was sluggish, constricted, or scattered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.39/en/sujato">AN 7.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.39/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> possessed the seven qualities for spiritual insight, including being fully aware of feelings and thoughts as they arose and passed away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.39/en/sujato">AN 7.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that Venerable Sāriputta had the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.39/en/sujato"><b>sign of inferior</b></a> and superior things well-considered and well-penetrated by wisdom, which contributed to his realization of the analytical knowledges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.39/en/sujato">AN 7.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can exercise <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato"><b>mastery over the mind</b></a>, rather than being controlled by it, if he is skilled in seven specific aspects of concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato">AN 7.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the first quality of mental mastery as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato"><b>skilled in concentration</b></a> (<i>samādhikusalo</i>) itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato">AN 7.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato"><b>skilled in attainment</b></a> (<i>samāpattikusalo</i>) of concentration is a necessary skill for a monk who wishes to direct his mind as he wants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato">AN 7.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato"><b>skilled in maintenance</b></a> (<i>ṭhitikusalo</i>) of concentration to ensure he has full control over his mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato">AN 7.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato"><b>skilled in emergence</b></a> (<i>vuṭṭhānakusalo</i>) from concentration is one of the seven essential skills for mastering the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato">AN 7.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato"><b>skilled in refinement</b></a> (<i>kalyāṇakusalo</i>) of concentration as possessing one of the key qualities for mental mastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato">AN 7.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato"><b>skilled in range</b></a> (<i>gocarakusalo</i>) regarding his concentration can successfully wield power over his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato">AN 7.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato"><b>skilled in resolution</b></a> (<i>abhinīhārakusalo</i>) of concentration as the final of seven qualities required for a monk to master his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.40/en/sujato">AN 7.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> wielded mastery over his mind and was never under the control of his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.41/en/sujato">AN 7.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha attributed Sāriputta’s total <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.41/en/sujato"><b>control of mind</b></a> to his being skilled in the attainment, maintenance, emergence, refinement, range, and resolution of concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.41/en/sujato">AN 7.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>eight streams of merit</b></a> and wholesome streams that bring about happiness, lead to heaven, and result in what is desirable and pleasant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>going for refuge to the Buddha</b></a> as the first of eight streams of merit that flows with happiness for a disciple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>going for refuge to the Dhamma</b></a> as a significant wholesome stream that provides the "nutriment of happiness" for a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>going for refuge to the Sangha</b></a> is a stream of merit that is conducive to a heavenly rebirth and long-term welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha designated five specific practices as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>great gifts</b></a> that are primordial, ancient, and have never been criticized by wise ascetics or brahmins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a noble disciple who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from killing</b></a> living beings is actually giving the gift of freedom from fear to an immeasurable number of beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from taking what is not given</b></a>, a person provides limitless beings with freedom from hostility and freedom from ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from sexual misconduct</b></a> as a "great gift" that has been recognized as traditional and unadulterated since ancient times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from false speech</b></a> allows a practitioner to share in the immeasurable freedom from fear and ill will that they grant to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from intoxicating drinks</b></a> and drugs is a wholesome stream of merit that leads to a blissful destination after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person practices the five precepts, they are giving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>freedom from fear</b></a> to limitless beings and, in turn, they come to share in that same immeasurable freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the five precepts as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>unadulterated practices</b></a> that have not been doubted in the past and will not be doubted by the wise in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that the gifts of moral restraint are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>not criticized by the wise</b></a>, whether they be wise ascetics, brahmins, or other intelligent observers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>wholesome streams</b></a> to describe the specific actions of taking refuge and keeping precepts because they continuously "flow" with happiness for the individual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that giving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>freedom from hostility</b></a> to others through virtuous conduct is considered a superior form of giving that belongs to the ancient lineage of the noble ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the fourth through eighth streams of merit consist of five <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>primordial gifts</b></a> that involve abstaining from harmful behaviors toward other living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that the eight streams of merit—the three refuges and five moral abstentions—will always lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato"><b>welfare and happiness</b></a> for those who develop them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.39/en/sujato">AN 8.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described ten <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>noble dwellings</b></a> in which the noble ones of the past, present, and future abide? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a monk has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>abandoned five factors</b></a> when he has successfully given up sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>possessed of six factors</b></a> as remaining equanimous, mindful, and clearly comprehending regardless of what one sees, hears, smells, tastes, touches, or cognizes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>single guard</b></a> of a noble practitioner as having a mind that is constantly protected by mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk uses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>four supports</b></a> when he uses, endures, avoids, or removes things only after careful reflection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>dispelled individual truths</b></a> as the total relinquishment and abandonment of dogmatic views held by various recluses and brahmins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>completely given up searching</b></a> has abandoned the search for sensual pleasures, the search for existence, and has stilled the search for a holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>unclouded intentions</b></a> as the state where a practitioner has entirely abandoned thoughts of sensuality, ill will, and cruelty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>tranquilized bodily activity</b></a> is achieved when a monk enters the fourth jhāna, a state beyond pleasure and pain with pure mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>well-liberated mind</b></a> when his mind is freed from the taints of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>well-liberated by wisdom</b></a> as the experiential knowledge that lust, hatred, and delusion have been uprooted and will never arise again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that any noble one who will exist in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>future</b></a> will also dwell in these same ten noble dwellings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>abandoned ill will</b></a> has fulfilled one of the five essential factors of a noble dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that letting go of the view <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>"the world is eternal"</b></a> or "the world is not eternal" is a requirement for a monk to have dispelled individual truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the view <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>"the soul is the same as the body"</b></a> is one of the many speculative views that a noble practitioner must vomit up and let go? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the abandonment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>search for existence</b></a> as a key achievement of those who have reached the end of spiritual quests? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>palm stump</b></a> to describe how a wise monk's lust, hatred, and delusion are destroyed so they can never grow again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> toward sensory experiences like taste or touch is a hallmark of a noble dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that his teachings on noble dwellings apply to all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>noble ones</b></a> regardless of the era in which they live? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato"><b>unclouded intentions</b></a> include the total absence of any thoughts related to harming others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.20/en/sujato">AN 10.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha asked Cunda the smith about whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>purification rites</b></a> he approved of, Cunda replied that he followed the traditions of the Brahmins of the western lands who carry water pots and wear garlands of water-plants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Brahminical rites described to the Buddha, a person seeks purification by rising early and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>touching the earth</b></a> or wet cow dung? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was told by Cunda that some practitioners believe they can be purified by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>worshipping the sun</b></a> with joined palms or by descending into water three times in the evening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Cunda that purification in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>Noble One’s discipline</b></a> is entirely different from external rituals like touching green grass or tending a sacred fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three types of bodily actions that make a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>unclean</b></a>: taking life, taking what is not given, and engaging in sexual misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>takes life</b></a> as being blood-handed, given to blows and violence, and completely merciless toward all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>taking what is not given</b></a>—whether it is the property of others in a village or a forest—is a primary source of bodily impurity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> as having relations with those protected by parents, siblings, or other guardians, or those who are already married? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized four types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>verbal actions</b></a> as unclean, which include false speech, divisive speech, harsh speech, and idle chatter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>false speech</b></a> occurs when a person deliberately lies in a formal assembly or for the sake of some small material gain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a> as the act of repeating what one has heard elsewhere in order to cause a rift between people or to break their unity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a> as language that is rough, bitter, offensive to others, and not conducive to peace of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a> as speaking at the wrong time, saying what is worthless, and speaking what is not in accordance with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that three types of mental actions lead to impurity, with the first being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>covetousness</b></a>, where one desires the property of others for themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> as a mind of hate that wishes for living beings to be killed, bound, or destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> involves denying the efficacy of giving, the results of kamma, and the existence of an afterlife or enlightened teachers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that if a person follows the ten unwholesome paths of action, they remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>impure</b></a> even if they perform external rituals like touching the earth or tending a fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ten unwholesome paths of action are the reason why beings are reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a>, the animal realm, or the sphere of afflicted spirits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a person is truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>clean</b></a> if they possess the ten wholesome paths of action, such as abstaining from theft and practicing right view, regardless of whether they perform water ablutions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a purified speaker as one who uses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>polite speech</b></a> that is affectionate, heart-warming, and pleasing to the many? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the ten wholesome paths of action are the specific cause for the existence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato"><b>devas</b></a>, humans, or any other good destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.176/en/sujato">AN 10.176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato"><b>fear</b></a> is actually a designation for sensual pleasures, because someone who is infatuated with and bound by sensual desire is not freed from fears in either this life or the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato">AN 6.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> as a designation for sensual pleasures, explaining that those who are tethered to lust for them cannot escape the miseries of the present or the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato">AN 6.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described sensual pleasures using the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato"><b>disease</b></a>, highlighting how attachment to these pleasures keeps a person from being liberated from afflictions in this world and beyond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato">AN 6.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato"><b>boil</b></a> to refer to sensual pleasures, signifying the danger and irritation they cause to one who is bound by the desire for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato">AN 6.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, sensual pleasures are a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato"><b>bond</b></a>, and those who are obsessed with them remain stuck in the dangers of the current life and future existences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato">AN 6.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to sensual pleasures as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato"><b>bog</b></a>, because one who is mired in lust for them is unable to free themselves from the perils of either the present or the next world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato">AN 6.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who see danger in clinging and attain the destruction of birth and death are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato"><b>quenched</b></a> in this very life, having transcended all enmity and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.23/en/sujato">AN 6.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a monk possessed of six specific qualities regarding concentration could even split the Himalayas, the king of mountains, let alone shatter <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.24/en/sujato"><b>vile ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.24/en/sujato">AN 6.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.24/en/sujato"><b>skill in attainment</b></a> and skill in the maintenance of concentration among the six essential factors that empower a monk to overcome even the greatest obstacles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.24/en/sujato">AN 6.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.24/en/sujato"><b>skilled in emergence</b></a> and skilled in the resolution of concentration is capable of destroying the most profound ignorance, much like splitting a great mountain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.24/en/sujato">AN 6.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified six <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>themes for recollection</b></a> which, when practiced by a noble disciple, prevent the mind from being obsessed by lust, hatred, or delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a noble disciple practices the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Tathāgata</b></a>, their mind becomes straight and is released and risen from greed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Dhamma</b></a>, a noble disciple’s mind becomes straight and emerges from the grip of the five cords of sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Saṅgha</b></a> allows a disciple to maintain a mind that is not obsessed by hatred or delusion, but is instead focused and upright? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that reflecting on one's own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>virtuous behavior</b></a>—virtues that are unbroken, flawless, and conducive to concentration—can lead to a mind that is released from the five cords of sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>recollection of generosity</b></a>, where one rejoices in being open-handed and delighting in sharing, is a specific object by which some beings are purified? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the deities</b></a> as a practice where a disciple reflects on the faith, virtue, learning, generosity, and wisdom they share with beings in heavenly realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the word <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>greed</b></a> (gedha) specifically as a technical designation for the five cords of sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>straight mind</b></a> is a mental state attained during recollection that is emerged, released, and risen from the influence of sensual desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that by taking any of these six recollections as an object, some <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>beings are purified</b></a> in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple should reflect on their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> (saddhā) as being equal to the faith that allowed deities to be reborn in higher realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> (paññā) as one of the five essential qualities to be recollected in oneself while contemplating the success of the deities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>Tusita</b></a> heaven as one of the many realms where deities exist who possess qualities like faith and virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that at the moment a noble disciple recollects the Teaching, their mind is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>obsessed by delusion</b></a> (mohapariyuṭṭhita)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>learning</b></a> (suta) as an important spiritual asset to be recollected when a disciple compares their own progress to that of the deities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>released from greed</b></a> is achieved when a disciple reflects on their own good fortune in being free from the stain of stinginess? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a> as the primary target for release when practicing any of the six recollections? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato"><b>Brahmakāyikā devas</b></a> are a valid object of recollection for a disciple wishing to straighten their mind through the reflection on spiritual virtues? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.25/en/sujato">AN 6.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>seven powers</b></a> of a noble disciple: faith, energy, moral shame, moral dread, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of faith</b></a> as a noble disciple placing their trust in the enlightenment of the Tathāgata, believing him to be the Perfectly Enlightened One and the teacher of gods and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of energy</b></a> as a noble disciple living with energy aroused for the abandoning of unwholesome states and the acquisition of wholesome states, being firm and steady in their effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of moral shame</b></a> is when a noble disciple feels a sense of shame regarding bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct, as well as the acquisition of evil, unwholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of moral dread</b></a> as the fear or dread a noble disciple feels regarding the commission of bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct and the acquisition of unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of mindfulness</b></a> as the ability of a noble disciple to be possessed of supreme mindfulness and discretion, enabling them to remember and recall what was done and said long ago? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of concentration</b></a> is developed when a noble disciple, secluded from sensual pleasures and unwholesome states, enters and dwells in the four jhānas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of wisdom</b></a> consists of possessing the noble and penetrative discernment regarding the arising and passing away of phenomena, which leads to the complete destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a wise monk who possesses the seven powers—faith, energy, shame, dread, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom—is one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>lives happily</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the liberation of the mind to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>extinguishing of a lamp</b></a>, noting that it is achieved by one who investigates the Dhamma with thorough attention and sees its meaning with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>seven powers</b></a> of a noble disciple: faith, energy, moral shame, moral dread, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of faith</b></a> as a noble disciple placing their trust in the enlightenment of the Tathāgata, believing him to be the Perfectly Enlightened One and the teacher of gods and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of energy</b></a> as a noble disciple living with energy aroused for the abandoning of unwholesome states and the acquisition of wholesome states, being firm and steady in their effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of moral shame</b></a> is when a noble disciple feels a sense of shame regarding bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct, as well as the acquisition of evil, unwholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of moral dread</b></a> as the fear or dread a noble disciple feels regarding the commission of bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct and the acquisition of unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of mindfulness</b></a> as the ability of a noble disciple to be possessed of supreme mindfulness and discretion, enabling them to remember and recall what was done and said long ago? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of concentration</b></a> is developed when a noble disciple, secluded from sensual pleasures and unwholesome states, enters and dwells in the four jhānas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of wisdom</b></a> consists of possessing the noble and penetrative discernment regarding the arising and passing away of phenomena, which leads to the complete destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a wise monk who possesses the seven powers—faith, energy, shame, dread, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom—is one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>lives happily</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the liberation of the mind to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>extinguishing of a lamp</b></a>, noting that it is achieved by one who investigates the Dhamma with thorough attention and sees its meaning with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that seniority alone, even after twelve years of practicing the holy life, is not enough for a monk to be designated as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>consummate monk</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>seven grounds</b></a> for being consummate (niddasavatthu) which he personally realized and then taught to his followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the qualities of a consummate monk is a keen desire to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>undertake the training</b></a> and an undiminished love for that undertaking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is considered consummate when he possesses a keen desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>scrutinizing the Dhamma</b></a> and maintains a constant love for such scrutiny? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who has a keen desire for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>removal of desire</b></a> and remains devoted to this goal with lasting love is truly consummate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a consummate monk as one who has a keen desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> and whose love for it never fades away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a consummate monk must maintain a keen desire for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>arousal of energy</b></a> and have an enduring love for such effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a consummate monk as one with a keen desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and alertness</b></a> and an undiminished love for these mental qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>penetration of view</b></a>, coupled with a keen desire and love for it, is one of the seven essential qualities of a monk who is beyond reproach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta once visited the park of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>wanderers of other sects</b></a> in Sāvatthī to engage in friendly conversation while waiting for the appropriate time to seek alms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta overheard wandering ascetics claiming that any person who completes twelve years of pure spiritual life is worthy of being called a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>consummate monk</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Sāriputta heard the views of other ascetics regarding spiritual seniority, he neither <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>accepted nor rejected</b></a> their words but decided to clarify the matter with the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly informed Sāriputta that it is impossible to designate a monk as consummate in this Dhamma and Discipline merely through a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>count of years</b></a> spent in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk who possesses the seven grounds for being consummate is fit to be called beyond reproach, whether he has practiced for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>twelve or twenty-four</b></a> years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even after <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>thirty-six years</b></a> of practicing the holy life, a monk is only truly "consummate" (niddasa) if he possesses the specific seven spiritual qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed a monk can be considered consummate even after <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>forty-eight years</b></a> of holy life, provided he maintains a keen desire for training, energy, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for each of the seven grounds of a consummate monk, there must be both a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato"><b>keen desire</b></a> (tibbacchando) and a love for that quality that does not fade? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.42/en/sujato">AN 7.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato"><b>destruction of life</b></a>, when cultivated and practiced frequently, leads to rebirth in hell, the animal realm, or the sphere of afflicted spirits, and its minimum result for a human being is a short life span? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato">AN 8.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the habit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato"><b>taking what is not given</b></a> can result in rebirth in the realm of afflicted spirits, and its lightest consequence for one reborn as a human is the loss of wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato">AN 8.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> leads to rebirth in hell or the animal realm, while its minimum result for a person is experiencing enmity and rivalry with others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato">AN 8.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato"><b>false speech</b></a> leads to rebirth in miserable states, and its most minor result for a human being is being the target of false accusations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato">AN 8.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a>, if cultivated, leads to rebirth in hell or the animal realm, and even its lightest karmic result for a human is the breaking up of friendships? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato">AN 8.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that the frequent cultivation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a> results in rebirth in lower realms, while its minimum consequence for a human being is having to hear disagreeable sounds from others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato">AN 8.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a> leads to rebirth in the sphere of afflicted spirits, and its lightest result for a person is having speech that is not worth taking to heart by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato">AN 8.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato"><b>drinking wine and liquor</b></a> leads to rebirth in hell or the animal realm, and its minimum result for a human being is mental derangement or madness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato">AN 8.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that performing unwholesome actions like killing or stealing leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato"><b>rebirth in hell</b></a>, the animal realm, or the sphere of afflicted spirits when such deeds are practiced frequently? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato">AN 8.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato"><b>minimum result</b></a> of various types of misconduct as specific misfortunes that follow a person even if they manage to be reborn in the human world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.40/en/sujato">AN 8.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the term "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>lion</b></a>" is actually a designation for the Tathāgata, the Arahant, the Perfectly Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a lion comes out of its lair in the evening, it stretches, surveys the four quarters, and roars three times specifically so it does not cause <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>slaughter to small creatures</b></a> who might be in its path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when the Tathāgata teaches the Dhamma to an assembly, this act of teaching is considered his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>lion’s roar</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to possess ten powers of a Tathāgata, which allow him to occupy the "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>bull’s place</b></a>" and lead with supreme authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by being endowed with ten specific powers, he is able to set in motion the supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>Brahma-wheel</b></a> in various assemblies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the first of the ten powers described by the Buddha is the ability to understand, as it really is, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>possible as possible</b></a> and the impossible as impossible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha possesses a unique power to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>results of kamma</b></a> undertaken in the past, future, and present, in terms of their possibilities and causes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha has the power to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>way leading to all destinations</b></a>, meaning he knows exactly where any path of practice will lead a being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that one of his ten powers is the understanding of the world with its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>many and various elements</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha understands the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>diverse inclinations</b></a> of various beings, which is considered a fundamental power of an Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha has the power to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>state of the faculties</b></a> of other beings and persons, perceiving their level of spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha possesses the power to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>defilement, cleansing, and emergence</b></a> regarding the jhānas, liberations, concentrations, and attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha can recollect his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>manifold past dwellings</b></a>, ranging from a single birth up to many eons of world-contraction and expansion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha recollects past lives, he can recall specific details such as his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>name, clan, and food</b></a> in each previous existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uses his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> to see beings passing away and being reborn according to their kamma, whether they are inferior or superior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha understands how beings who engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>misconduct of body, speech, and mind</b></a> are reborn in states of misery or even in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observes through his psychic power that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>revilers of noble ones</b></a> and holding wrong views leads beings to be reborn in the plane of misery after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha sees that beings with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> and good conduct are reborn in a happy destination, even in the heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the final power of the Tathāgata, as described by the Buddha, is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>, which brings about complete liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.21/en/sujato">AN 10.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the Brahmin Jāṇussoṇi that gifts given in the name of deceased relatives only reach them if they have been reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>realm of departed spirits</b></a>, as this is the only "possible case" for receiving such offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>impossible case</b></a> for a deceased relative reborn in hell to receive ritual offerings, because beings in hell are sustained only by the specific food available in that realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>reborn as an animal</b></a> cannot benefit from gifts offered by their living relatives, as their survival depends entirely on the food appropriate for the animal kingdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that humans who pass away and are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>reborn as humans</b></a> do not receive the food or drink offered by their families in ritual ceremonies, as they are sustained by human food instead? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that deities reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> have no need for offerings from the human realm, as they are sustained by divine food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a departed spirit is sustained both by the food common to their realm and by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>what relatives provide</b></a> from the human world through dedicated offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reassured the Brahmin Jāṇussoṇi that if a specific intended relative is not in the spirit realm, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>other departed relatives</b></a> who have been reborn there will receive and enjoy the gift? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is virtually impossible for a place to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>devoid of relatives</b></a> over a long period of time, meaning there is almost always a departed spirit in one's lineage ready to receive an offering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even if no relatives were in the spirit realm to receive an offering, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>donor is not fruitless</b></a>, as the merit of the act itself returns to the giver? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a person who practiced the "ten courses of unwholesome kamma" but was also generous could be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>reborn as an elephant</b></a> that lives in luxury with fine food and ornaments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who lived an immoral life but frequently gave gifts to recluses might be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>reborn as a horse</b></a> that still receives excellent food and care? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a donor who practiced misconduct but was generous with food and drink might be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>reborn as cattle</b></a> and yet still enjoy the benefit of being well-fed in that state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>reborn as a dog</b></a> because of their past unwholesome actions will still receive food and drink if they were a generous person in their previous human life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who avoids unwholesome actions and practices generosity will be reborn in the human state, where they will enjoy the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a virtuous donor who avoids misconduct will be reborn among the devas and experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>divine sensual pleasures</b></a> as a result of their past giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>destruction of life</b></a> and taking what is not given as primary unwholesome actions that lead to rebirth in lower realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a>, harsh speech, and idle chatter as verbal misconduct that determines a person's destination after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> and false speech are factors that lead a person toward the animal realm or hell if they are not abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included mental states such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>being covetous</b></a> and having a mind of ill will as part of the ten unwholesome courses of kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> as a significant unwholesome factor that leads a person to a state of misery after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the specific items suitable for giving to recluses and brahmins, which include <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>food, drink, and clothing</b></a>, as well as vehicles, scents, and lodging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato"><b>free from ill will</b></a> and holds right view is on the path to being reborn in a good destination like the human or heavenly worlds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.177/en/sujato">AN 10.177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna proclaimed that the Buddha, the "One who Knows and Sees," discovered an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>opening in the midst of confinement</b></a> specifically for the purification of beings and the realization of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahākaccāna, the Buddha identified six bases for recollection as a means to facilitate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>overcoming of sorrow</b></a> and lamentation for all beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna taught that when a noble disciple recollects the qualities of the Tathāgata, their mind is not obsessed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>lust, hatred, or delusion</b></a>, but becomes upright and focused? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna defined the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>greed</b></a> (<i>gedha</i>) specifically as a designation for the "five cords of sensual pleasure" from which a practitioner's mind must emerge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna described the mind of a disciple engaged in these recollections as dwelling in a state <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>like the sky</b></a>—vast, exalted, and measureless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna explained that by taking the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Dhamma</b></a> as an object, a practitioner's mind becomes liberated and emerges from the "confinement" of sensual desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Saṅgha</b></a> as a primary meditation object that leads to mental uprightness and freedom from the obsession of defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna taught that a disciple can achieve a mind released from greed by practicing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>recollection of one's own virtues</b></a>, provided those virtues are unbroken and conducive to concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna encouraged disciples to practice the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>recollection of generosity</b></a> by reflecting on their own good fortune in being free from the stain of stinginess? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>recollection of deities</b></a> involves a disciple reflecting on their own possession of faith, virtue, learning, generosity, and wisdom, comparing themselves to the gods in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahākaccāna, when a disciple's mind is upright through recollection, they dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>without enmity</b></a> (<i>avera</i>) toward others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna stated that the practice of recollection leads to a mind that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>without ill will</b></a> (<i>abyāpajja</i>), which is a key component for spiritual purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna emphasized that these six recollections were realized by the Buddha for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>disappearance of pain</b></a> and displeasure (<i>dukkhadomanassānaṃ atthaṅgamāya</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna characterized the state of mind achieved during these meditation practices as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>measureless</b></a> (<i>appamāṇa</i>), indicating its transcendence over ordinary mental limitations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna highlighted that the mind, when released from greed through recollection, is described as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>exalted</b></a> (<i>mahaggata</i>), reflecting its growth beyond mundane concerns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna taught that some beings achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>purification</b></a> and become "beings of purified nature" solely by taking these six recollections as their meditative object? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna described the practitioner's mind as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>released from greed</b></a> whenever they are mindfully engaged with any of the six bases for recollection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna pointed out that the Buddha's discovery of these methods is for the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>attaining the right method</b></a> (<i>ñāyassa adhigama</i>), which is essential for liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna noted that when a disciple recollects their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> in comparison to the deities, their mind becomes upright and free from the obsession of lust or hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.26/en/sujato">AN 6.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified six specific occasions when a practitioner should visit and approach an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>esteemed bhikkhu</b></a> to seek spiritual guidance and instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should seek out a venerable teacher when their mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>obsessed by sensual desire</b></a> and they do not know the way to escape from it as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner overwhelmed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> should approach an esteemed bhikkhu to receive a teaching specifically for the purpose of abandoning that state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised visiting an esteemed colleague when one’s mind is dominated by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> and the path to its removal is not personally understood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> as a mental condition where a monk should approach a teacher to learn the Dhamma for the sake of letting go of that agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended approaching a more experienced practitioner when one's mind is paralyzed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> and lacks the clarity to find the escape from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a critical occasion for seeking guidance as being when a practitioner does not know the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>object</b></a> of meditation that leads to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> to refer to the ultimate goal of liberation that a teacher can help a student achieve through timely instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an esteemed bhikkhu’s role is to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>teach the Dhamma</b></a> for the explicit purpose of helping another practitioner abandon specific mental hindrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when one is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>overwhelmed</b></a> by unwholesome states, they should humbly request a teacher to explain the method for their abandonment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> (<i>nissaraṇa</i>) as the essential understanding a practitioner needs when their mind is clouded by any of the five hindrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that approaching an esteemed monk is necessary when a practitioner does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>understand as it really is</b></a> how to resolve mental obstacles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching in this sutta focuses on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>proper times</b></a> for a monk to seek out personal spiritual consultation from a venerable peer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha responded to a specific question from an unnamed bhikkhu regarding the frequency and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>occasions</b></a> for visiting a teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the inability to perceive the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>mark</b></a> (<i>nimitta</i>) of meditation which leads to the end of suffering is a primary reason to seek external guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a disciple should state: “I am dwelling with a mind obsessed; it would be good if the venerable one would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>teach me the Dhamma</b></a> for its abandonment”? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha indicated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>immediate</b></a> destruction of the taints is possible when one learns to correctly attend to the right meditative object through a teacher's help? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>obsessed</b></a> by hindrances as a condition where one's internal capacity to find a solution is temporarily compromised? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's advice implies that seeking a teacher is vital for any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato"><b>bhikkhu</b></a> who finds their progress stalled by mental dullness or agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.27/en/sujato">AN 6.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that several elder monks gathered at Bārāṇasī in the Deer Park to discuss the proper time to approach a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>mind-inspiring bhikkhu</b></a> for guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that one elder monk suggested visiting a teacher when they are sitting in meditation after their meal, but another argued this was an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>unsuitable time</b></a> because the teacher might still be tired from walking for alms and eating? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to an elder monk, the evening after a teacher emerges from seclusion is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>not the right time</b></a> to visit them, as their mind may still be occupied with the meditation object they focused on during the day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that an elder monk argued against visiting a teacher in the pre-dawn hours because at that time the teacher's body is refreshed and they are personally occupied with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>attending to the Buddha’s teaching</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna resolved a debate among elder monks by sharing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>six specific occasions</b></a> for visiting a mind-inspiring teacher that he heard directly from the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should visit an esteemed teacher when their mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>obsessed by sensual lust</b></a> and they do not know the way to escape it as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> as a state of mental obsession that makes it the right time to approach a mind-inspiring teacher for a Dhamma talk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a practitioner is overwhelmed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> and lacks the understanding to overcome it, they should seek out an inspiring teacher for help? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised practitioners to approach a teacher when they are struggling with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> and do not know the path to mental peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind paralyzed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> provides a proper occasion to visit an inspiring teacher to learn how to resolve that uncertainty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a practitioner should seek guidance when they do not know the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>mark</b></a> or meditation object that leads to the immediate destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna emphasized that a student should explicitly tell a teacher, "I am obsessed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>sensual lust</b></a>," when requesting a teaching to help them abandon that state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching suggests that a teacher's role during these six occasions is to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>expound the Dhamma</b></a> specifically tailored to help the student abandon their current mental hindrance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (<i>āsavānaṃ khayo</i>) to describe the final liberation that a practitioner can achieve by attending to the right meditation object with a teacher's help? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna stated he heard the list of proper times to visit a teacher <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>face-to-face</b></a> from the Buddha himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>mind-inspiring bhikkhu</b></a> (<i>manobhāvanīya bhikkhu</i>) is the specific designation used by the elder monks for an esteemed teacher who is worthy of being visited for spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>sixth occasion</b></a> for visiting a teacher is when a practitioner lacks the vision to see the specific "mark" required for the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that an elder monk pointed out that a teacher might be suffering from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>fatigue of travel</b></a> after an alms round, suggesting that their physical comfort matters when choosing a time to approach them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that seeking a teacher when one does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>know as it really is</b></a> the escape from mental obsessions is a vital step for spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching emphasizes that a student should ask for a teaching specifically for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato"><b>abandoning</b></a> of their current mental obsession or to understand the cause for the end of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.28/en/sujato">AN 6.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Venerable Udāyī three times how many bases of recollection there are, but Udāyī <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>remained silent</b></a> each time until Venerable Ānanda prompted him to speak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when eventually answering the Buddha, Venerable Udāyī suggested that the recollection of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>past lives</b></a>—remembering one's many births with their aspects and details—was a base of recollection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha dismissed Venerable Udāyī’s focus on past lives as a primary base of recollection, stating that Udāyī was not a "pointless man" devoted to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>higher mind</b></a> (<i>adhicitta</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>five bases of recollection</b></a> that, when developed and cultivated, lead to specific spiritual benefits such as happiness and knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Ānanda, the development of the third jhāna is a base of recollection that leads to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwelling in this life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that a practitioner should focus on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>perception of light</b></a> and the perception of day, both night and day, to obtain knowledge and vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda described a meditative state where the practitioner develops a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>bright mind</b></a> that is open and uncurtained by resolving that the night is just like the day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda recommended reflecting on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>thirty-one parts of the body</b></a>, including hair, skin, organs, and various fluids, to help abandon sensual lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that reflecting on internal organs like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>liver, lungs, and kidneys</b></a> is part of a base of recollection meant to overcome the impurity of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that contemplating a corpse in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>charnel ground</b></a>—whether it is bloated, blue, or being devoured by crows and vultures—is a vital base for recollection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda described the process of seeing bones in a charnel ground that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>white like conch shells</b></a> as a way to realize that one's own body has the same inevitable nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Ānanda, the specific purpose of contemplating bodily decay and the stages of a skeleton is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>uprooting of the conceit 'I am'</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda identified the fourth jhāna, which has neither pain nor pleasure, as a base of recollection that leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>penetration of many elements</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Ānanda’s list of five bases but then supplemented it with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>sixth base of recollection</b></a> for his disciples to remember? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should remain mindful while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>walking and standing</b></a> as a method for developing a specialized base of recollection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that being mindful while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>sitting, lying down, and working</b></a> constitutes a powerful practice when developed and cultivated frequently? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the practice of maintaining constant mindfulness in all physical postures leads specifically to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda defined the base of recollection for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>abandoning sensual lust</b></a> as the systematic contemplation of the body from the soles of the feet up to the hair on the head? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda suggested that by reflecting on a skeleton and scattered bones, a practitioner understands that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato"><b>this body</b></a> has such a nature and has not escaped it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.29/en/sujato">AN 6.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that before a person even begins a ritual sacrifice involving animal slaughter, they have already set up three <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome weapons</b></a>—the weapons of body, speech, and mind—that result in suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>fire of lust</b></a> (rāgaggi) must be abandoned and avoided because a person overcome by lust performs misconduct that leads to rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified one’s own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>parents</b></a> as the "fire of those worthy of offerings" (āhuneyyaggi), meaning they should be honored, respected, and cared for properly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>one’s family and workers</b></a>—including children, wife, and employees—as the "householder’s fire" (gahapataggi) that should be maintained with honor and care? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the "fire of those worthy of a gift" (dakkhiṇeyyaggi) as those <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>ascetics and brahmins</b></a> who are self-tamed, peaceful, and have attained Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that by intending to slaughter animals for a sacrifice, a person who thinks they are making merit is actually making <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>demerit</b></a> and seeking a path to a bad destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Uggatasarīra originally planned a massive sacrifice involving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>five hundred bulls</b></a> and two thousand other animals before being corrected by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>fire of hatred</b></a> (dosaggi) is a fire that should not be socialized with, as it overwhelms the mind and leads to bodily and verbal misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>fire of delusion</b></a> (mohaggi) causes a person to act in ways that lead to rebirth in the plane of misery or the nether world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda intervened when a Brahmin asked a superficial question, advising him instead to ask the Buddha for an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>exhortation</b></a> that would lead to his long-term welfare and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>verbal weapon</b></a> is set up the moment a person gives the order for animals to be slaughtered for a ritual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the act of personally beginning the slaughter of animals constitutes setting up a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>bodily weapon</b></a> that produces painful results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that a common <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>wood fire</b></a> (kaṭṭhaggi) is simply something to be kindled, looked after, or extinguished from time to time as needed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha’s teaching, the Brahmin Uggatasarīra granted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>life and freedom</b></a> to two thousand five hundred animals he had intended to sacrifice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized different types of "fires" to show that while some must be extinguished (like lust), others—like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>filial piety</b></a>—must be carefully tended? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Uggatasarīra wished for his released animals to eat <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>green grass</b></a> and drink cool water, showing a complete change of heart from his earlier intent to kill them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>misconduct in mind</b></a>, such as the thought "let these animals be killed," to be the first "unwholesome weapon" raised during a violent sacrifice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>overcome by hatred</b></a> and acts upon it is effectively stoking a fire that will lead them to the "downfall" (vinipāta)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that honoring parents as a "fire" is appropriate because it is from them that one has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>been produced</b></a> and brought into the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who kindles a sacrificial fire involving animal death is actually seeking the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>path to a bad destination</b></a> while mistakenly believing they are on the path to a good one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described virtuous ascetics as a fire because they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>tame themselves</b></a> and bring themselves to peace and final nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Uggatasarīra the Brahmin released his animals, he specifically mentioned he wanted a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>cool breeze</b></a> to blow upon them, indicating his new-found compassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>properly honoring</b></a> the fires of parents, family, and the virtuous leads to "right happiness" (sammā sukhaṃ)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato"><b>āhuneyyaggi</b></a> to redefine a traditional sacrificial term into a lesson on showing gratitude to one's parents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.47/en/sujato">AN 7.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>seven perceptions</b></a> which, when developed and cultivated, have the Deathless as their ground, destination, and final goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk’s mind recoiling from sexual intercourse to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>chicken feather</b></a> or a piece of hide that shrinks, curls, and rolls up when thrown into a fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, if a monk develops the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>perception of foulness</b></a> (asubhasaññā), his mind will withdraw and recoil from the thought of sexual intercourse rather than being drawn toward it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>perception of death</b></a> (maraṇasaññā) is successfully developed only when a monk's mind recoils from the "love of life" (jīvitanikanti) like a feather shrinking from a flame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by practicing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>perception of repulsiveness in food</b></a> (āhāre paṭikūlasaññā), a practitioner’s mind will shrink away from the craving for tastes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-delight</b></a> in the entire world (sabbaloke anabhiratasaññā) causes the mind to recoil from the various "diversions" or "beauties" of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>perception of impermanence</b></a> (aniccasaññā) is fully developed, the mind recoils from gain, honor, and praise (lābhasakkārasiloka)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>perception of suffering in the impermanent</b></a> (anicce dukkhasaññā) should cause a monk to feel a sharp sense of fear toward laziness, indolence, and negligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the sharp sense of fear a practitioner should feel toward negligence to the fear of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>murderer with an upraised sword</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-self</b></a> in what is suffering (dukkhe anattasaññā) leads to a mind that is free from "I-making," "mine-making," and conceit regarding the conscious body and external signs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a monk's mind is not repelled by sexual intercourse despite practicing the perception of foulness, he should realize he has not yet attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>power of development</b></a> (bhāvanābala)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a practitioner should remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>clearly comprehending</b></a> (sampajāno) when assessing whether their meditation on the seven perceptions has been successful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a mind that has successfully developed the perception of non-self as being "peaceful and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>well-liberated</b></a>" (santaṃ suvimuttaṃ)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>seven perceptions</b></a> as: foulness, death, repulsiveness in food, non-delight in the world, impermanence, suffering in the impermanent, and non-self in suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>love of life</b></a> (jīvitanikanti) is an attachment that the mind must learn to recoil from through the perception of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk's mind is still drawn to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>worldly beauties</b></a> (lokacitresu), it is a sign that his perception of non-delight in the entire world has not been properly developed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the perception of impermanence is a specific antidote to the attachment to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>gain and honor</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a perception to be "well-developed," there must be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>distinction</b></a> (viseso) between the practitioner's state of mind before and after the practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>amatogadha</b></a> to describe how these seven perceptions merge into the "Deathless," which refers to Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the perception of non-self is mastered, the mind transcends all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>modes of conceit</b></a> (vidhā-samatikkantaṃ)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that laziness and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>negligence</b></a> (pamāda) are states that a serious practitioner should view with "sharp fear" once they understand the suffering inherent in impermanence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the goal of these seven perceptions is to achieve a state where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> or "repulsion" (upekkhā vā pāṭikulyatā) is firmly established toward unwholesome attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who has successfully developed these perceptions as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>attained the fruit</b></a> of his meditation training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the perception of suffering in the impermanent leads directly to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-self</b></a> in what is suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a monk’s mind still "follows after" (anusandahati) unwholesome desires like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato"><b>craving for tastes</b></a>, he must acknowledge that his meditation practice is still incomplete? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.49/en/sujato">AN 7.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>pure holy life</b></a> (brahmacariya) as being unbroken, flawless, unblemished, and untarnished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if a person avoids physical intercourse, consenting to have a woman <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>massage and rub</b></a> their body still makes their holy life "broken and spotted"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>joking and playing</b></a> with women is a subtle "sexual fetter" that prevents a practitioner from being fully liberated from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>gazes into the eyes</b></a> of a woman is engaging in a form of sexual attachment, even if no physical contact occurs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that listening to a woman’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>laughter or singing</b></a> through a wall or fence constitutes a bond of sexual union for one who claims to live the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>remembering past laughter</b></a> and conversations shared with women in the past can stain and compromise a person's current practice of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that watching a householder enjoy the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a> and finding satisfaction in that sight is a subtle bond that keeps a practitioner tied to rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the aspiration to be reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>heavenly host</b></a> as a god through one’s virtue and vows as a subtle sexual fetter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he only claimed to have attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>supreme enlightenment</b></a> (sammāsambodhi) after he saw that these seven sexual fetters were completely abandoned in himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that anyone bound by these seven <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>sexual fetters</b></a> (methunasaṃyoga) remains unliberated from the cycle of birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha experienced the realization that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>liberation is unshakeable</b></a> only after overcoming every subtle sexual bond and tendency toward delight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even if a monk abstains from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>sexual intercourse</b></a> (dvayaṃdvayasamāpatti), they are not truly free if they still relish subtle mental or sensory associations with women? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>Jāṇussoṇi</b></a> was the one who approached the Buddha to ask if he truly claimed to be a practitioner of a pure and unbroken holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a practitioner who finds <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>satisfaction and delight</b></a> in subtle sexual bonds as living an "impure holy life" (aparisuddhaṃ brahmacariyaṃ)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being bound by sexual fetters means one is not freed from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>sorrow and lamentation</b></a> or from suffering itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that once he reached enlightenment, he knew this was his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>final birth</b></a> and that there would be no further rebirth for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the holy life should not be practiced with the goal of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>becoming a god</b></a> through the merit of one's virtue or austerities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>relishing and desiring</b></a> subtle sensory pleasures as the reason a person remains fettered to the cycle of pain and dejection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching on the seven sexual fetters was so persuasive that Jāṇussoṇi the Brahmin asked to be remembered as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a> from that day forward? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato"><b>subtle mental associations</b></a> with the opposite sex as "sexual fetters" that prevent the attainment of the highest goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.50/en/sujato">AN 7.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the "discourse on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>bondage and lack of bondage</b></a>," which explains the psychological process by which individuals become mentally tied to or freed from their gender identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a woman becomes bound when she internally attends to her own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>feminine faculty</b></a>, which includes her feminine gestures, appearance, ways, desires, voice, and ornaments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a man becomes bound when he attends to his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>masculine faculty</b></a>, focusing on masculine gestures, appearance, desires, voice, and behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that mental bondage begins when a person becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>excited and delighted</b></a> by their own gender-specific traits and attributes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that once a woman is infatuated with her own femininity, she then turns her attention toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>external masculinity</b></a> in others, attending to masculine gestures and desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a man, after being captivated by his own masculinity, begins to focus on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>external femininity</b></a> in others, including feminine appearance and ornaments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>longing for union</b></a> (saṃyoga) with the opposite sex as a direct consequence of being mentally attached to one's own gender identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the pleasure and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>joy derived from union</b></a> is what people ultimately seek when they preoccupy themselves with gender-specific attributes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a woman fails to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>transcend femininity</b></a> because she remains delighted in her own feminine nature and seeks a connection with men? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a man fails to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>transcend masculinity</b></a> as long as he remains enamored with his own masculine traits and desires union with women? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined "lack of bondage" as a state where a woman no longer attends to her own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>feminine gestures</b></a>, appearance, or voice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a man as being "unbound" when he stops focusing on his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>masculine appearance</b></a>, ways, and desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by not being excited or delighted by one's own gender, a person naturally stops attending to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>faculties of the opposite sex</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a person is no longer infatuated with their own gender identity, they no longer <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>long for connection</b></a> or the pleasure born from such unions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a woman truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>transcends her feminine state</b></a> when she becomes detached from both her own feminine traits and her attraction toward masculinity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a man truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>transcends his masculine state</b></a> when he no longer finds delight in his own masculinity or seeks pleasure through femininity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>feminine ornaments</b></a> (itthālaṅkāra) as one of the internal preoccupations a woman must let go of to achieve mental freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the path to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato"><b>freedom from bondage</b></a> involves actively choosing not to focus on or delight in the various components of gender identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.51/en/sujato">AN 7.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Uposatha observance, when it is possessed of eight factors, is of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.41/en/sujato"><b>great fruit</b></a> and great benefit, possessing great splendor and pervasiveness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.41/en/sujato">AN 8.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a noble disciple observes the Uposatha by reflecting that they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.41/en/sujato"><b>imitating the arahants</b></a> for one day and night in their conduct and discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.41/en/sujato">AN 8.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that on the Uposatha day, a practitioner dwells compassionate and sympathetic toward the welfare of all living beings, having laid aside <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.41/en/sujato"><b>the rod and the sword</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.41/en/sujato">AN 8.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that during the Uposatha, a disciple abstains from taking what is not given and lives with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.41/en/sujato"><b>pure self</b></a> through honesty, just as the arahants do for their entire lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.41/en/sujato">AN 8.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that observing the Uposatha involves abandoning unchastity to live a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.41/en/sujato"><b>celibate life</b></a>, staying apart from sexual intercourse and the "common practice" of the village? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.41/en/sujato">AN 8.41</a></p></div><hr>
