<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that abstaining from wines, liquors, and intoxicants, which are the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>basis for negligence</b></a>, is a critical factor for successfully observing the Uposatha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those observing the Uposatha follow the example of the arahants by becoming a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>one-meal-a-day eater</b></a>, refraining from food at night and at the wrong time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a practitioner on the Uposatha day abstains from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>unseemly shows</b></a>, such as dancing, singing, and music, as well as from wearing garlands and perfumes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that instead of using high and large beds, one observing the Uposatha should use a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>low bed</b></a>, such as a small couch or a straw mat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that exercising sovereign power over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>sixteen great nations</b></a> is not worth even a sixteenth part of the merit gained from an eight-factored Uposatha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described human kingship as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>miserable</b></a> when compared to the vast and superior happiness found in the celestial realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that fifty human years are equivalent to just a single day and night for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>Four Great Kings</b></a> devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that one hundred human years are equal to only one day and night for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>Tāvatiṃsa devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that four hundred human years constitute a single day and night for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>Tusita devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that sixteen hundred human years are equivalent to just one day and night for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>Paranimmitavasavattī devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even the greatest <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>earthly wealth</b></a>—such as pearls, beryl, and various types of gold—does not equal a sixteenth part of the Uposatha's value? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a virtuous man or woman who observes the eight-factored Uposatha will, upon the breakup of the body, attain a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>blissful state</b></a> in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered this discourse while he was staying at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>Migāra’s Mother’s Mansion</b></a> in the Eastern Park near Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Uposatha was originally proclaimed by him as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a>, specifically when practiced with its full eight-fold components? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.43/en/sujato">AN 8.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are three distinct ways to abandon unwholesome states: some by the body, some by speech, and some by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>seeing with wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that certain unwholesome states are abandoned when a person heeds the advice of wise companions and replaces bodily misconduct with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>bodily good conduct</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that some faults must be abandoned through speech by listening to the feedback of companions and cultivating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>verbal good conduct</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>greed</b></a> (lobha) is a mental state that cannot be abandoned by body or speech, but only by seeing it clearly with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> (dosa) belongs to a category of mental defilements that must be abandoned through wisdom rather than physical or verbal actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> (moha) as a quality that is only truly abandoned when one sees and understands its nature with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>anger</b></a> (kodha) as a deep-seated mental state that can only be properly abandoned through the development of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>hostility</b></a> (upanāha) among the qualities that are not removed by external behavior, but only by the internal process of seeing with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the quality of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>denigration</b></a> (makkha)—belittling the good qualities of others—is a defilement that must be abandoned through wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>insolence</b></a> (paḷāsa)—dominating others through arrogance—as a state that persists until it is abandoned by seeing with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>stinginess</b></a> (macchariya) cannot be overcome by physical or verbal effort alone, but must be abandoned through the power of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>evil envy</b></a> (pāpikā issā) as wishing that a householder would lose their wealth, grain, silver, or gold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a specific form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>evil envy</b></a> where one person wishes that another monk or ascetic would not receive robes, almsfood, lodgings, or medical requisites? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>evil desire</b></a> (pāpikā icchā) as wanting others to perceive you as having faith (saddhā) when you actually lack it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered it <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>evil desire</b></a> for a person who is immoral to want others to mistakenly recognize them as being virtuous (sīlavā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is a form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>evil desire</b></a> for a lazy person to want to be known by others as one who has aroused energy (āraddhavīriya)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>evil desire</b></a> as the wish to be known as someone who is mindful (upaṭṭhitassati) when one is actually muddle-minded and forgetful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>evil desire</b></a> as the wish of an unwise person to be known by others as someone possessing wisdom (paññavā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wanting others to think you have destroyed the taints (khīṇāsava) when you have not is a type of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>evil desire</b></a> that must be abandoned through wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a person is not overcome by greed and evil desires, it is because they possess a specific level of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato"><b>understanding</b></a> about how to live so these states do not prevail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.23/en/sujato">AN 10.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was approached by a group of monks who had visited <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>wanderers of other sects</b></a> who claimed that their teaching was identical to the Buddha's because they also taught their disciples to "understand all things"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when monks visited other teachers and heard them claim to teach the same Dhamma as the Buddha, the monks did not approve or disapprove but instead sought the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's clarification</b></a> on the true distinction between the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised his monks to challenge teachers of other sects with a specific numerical series of ten questions, ten objectives, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>ten expositions</b></a> to reveal the depth of his unique realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted that any wanderer of another sect who was asked about the ten great questions would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>unable to answer</b></a> and would fall into vexation because these truths are beyond their range of understanding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that only a Tathagata, a disciple of the Tathagata, or someone who has heard it from them can provide an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>answer that satisfies the mind</b></a> regarding these ten profound questions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the "one thing" as the fundamental truth that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>all beings subsist on food</b></a> (<i>sabbe sattā āhāraṭṭhitikā</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is rightly disenchanted, dispassionate, and liberated regarding the fact that all beings subsist on food can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>make an end of suffering</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified "two things" that a practitioner must see with wisdom as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>name and form</b></a> (nāmarūpa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the "three things" one must understand to reach liberation are the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>three feelings</b></a> (pleasant, painful, and neither-pleasant-nor-painful)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified "four things" as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>four foods</b></a>—nutriment, contact, mental volition, and consciousness—which must be fully understood to end suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who becomes a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>complete seer of the end</b></a>" regarding the four foods can achieve final liberation in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified "five things" in his series of questions as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates of clinging</b></a> (form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>fully understood the meaning</b></a> of the five aggregates of clinging, a monk makes an end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified "six things" as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>six internal sense bases</b></a> (eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>rightly disenchanted</b></a> and dispassionate toward the six internal sense bases is a direct path to the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified "seven things" as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>seven stations of consciousness</b></a> where beings can be reborn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>rightly liberated</b></a> from the seven stations of consciousness attains the end of all suffering in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified "eight things" as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>eight worldly conditions</b></a> (gain and loss, fame and disrepute, praise and blame, and pleasure and pain)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that liberation is found by becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>rightly dispassionate</b></a> toward the eight worldly conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified "nine things" as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>nine abodes of beings</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified "ten things" as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>ten unwholesome courses of action</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's monks initially visited the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>wanderers of other sects</b></a> because they felt it was still "too early" to go into the city of Savatthi for alms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato"><b>fully understood</b></a> the ten unwholesome courses of action and is liberated from them is one who has made an end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.27/en/sujato">AN 10.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is virtuous and possesses virtuous behavior does not need to make a conscious act of will to experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato"><b>lack of remorse</b></a>, as it arises naturally as a consequence of their virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato">AN 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person who feels a lack of remorse, it is a law of nature that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato"><b>joy</b></a> will arise without them needing to specifically intend for it to happen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato">AN 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person experiences joy, they do not need to make an act of will for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato"><b>rapture</b></a> to arise, as it is the natural outcome of a joyful mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato">AN 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that for one whose mind is filled with rapture, the body naturally becomes tranquil, meaning an act of will is not required for the experience of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato"><b>tranquility</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato">AN 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person's body is tranquil, they naturally experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a>, and this happens as a law of nature rather than through a deliberate act of will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato">AN 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one who is happy, the mind naturally becomes concentrated, so the practitioner does not need to consciously strive or make an act of will for the mind to enter <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato">AN 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for one whose mind is concentrated, it is a law of nature that they will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato"><b>know and see things as they really are</b></a> without needing to make a specific act of will to do so? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato">AN 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person knows and sees things as they really are, they naturally become disenchanted and dispassionate, meaning no act of will is required to achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato"><b>disenchantment and dispassion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato">AN 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for someone who is disenchanted and dispassionate, the realization of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a> occurs naturally as the fulfillment of the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato">AN 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the progression of spiritual qualities as a process where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato"><b>states flow into states</b></a> and states fill up other states, ultimately leading a person from the near shore of suffering to the far shore of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.2/en/sujato">AN 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala once visited the Buddha immediately after returning from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>winning a battle</b></a> and securing his objectives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when King Pasenadi Kosala arrived at the monastery, he traveled by chariot as far as the ground allowed and then proceeded to the Buddha’s dwelling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>on foot</b></a> out of respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the resident monks advised King Pasenadi Kosala to approach the Buddha’s dwelling with little noise, enter the porch, cough, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>knock on the crossbar</b></a> to announce his arrival? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala demonstrated extreme humility by falling at the Buddha’s feet, kissing them, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>stroking them with his hands</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala explicitly stated that his extreme displays of veneration were motivated by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>gratitude and appreciation</b></a> toward the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala honored the Buddha because the Buddha worked for the welfare and happiness of the many and established them in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>noble method</b></a> of wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala revered the Buddha as one who was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a>, possessing ancient, noble, and wholesome virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala praised the Buddha for being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>long-time forest dweller</b></a> who frequented remote and secluded jungle lodgings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala admired the Buddha’s complete <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>contentment</b></a> with any kind of robes, almsfood, lodgings, or medical requisites he received? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala regarded the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world, being worthy of gifts, hospitality, and offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala valued the Buddha for his ability to provide <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>effacing talk</b></a>—such as talk on fewness of wishes, solitude, and the arousal of energy—whenever he desired? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala noted that the Buddha’s speech included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>talk on wisdom</b></a>, liberation, and the knowledge and vision of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala honored the Buddha for his mastery over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a>, which are states of high consciousness that provide happiness in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala revered the Buddha’s ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>recollect past lives</b></a>, including details of his name, clan, and experiences across many eons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala praised the Buddha’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a>, through which the Buddha could see beings passing away and being reborn according to their kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala acknowledged the Buddha’s teaching that beings who engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>misconduct of body</b></a>, speech, and mind are reborn in states of misery or hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala recounted the Buddha’s insight that beings who practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>good conduct</b></a> and hold right view are reborn in happy destinations or the heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala honored the Buddha for achieving the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> and living in the stainless liberation of mind and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala told the Buddha that he saw these various spiritual qualities as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>reason</b></a> to offer the highest humility and gestures of love? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his high royal status, King Pasenadi Kosala entered the Buddha’s presence with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>little noise</b></a> and without haste to show his deep respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi Kosala concluded his visit by stating that he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>busy with many duties</b></a> and responsibilities that required his attention as a ruler? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked King Pasenadi Kosala what <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato"><b>purpose</b></a> he saw in performing such extreme acts of humility toward his physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.30/en/sujato">AN 10.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Uposatha observance, when practiced with its eight factors, is of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.44/en/sujato"><b>great fruit</b></a> and great benefit, leading those who practice it to a blameless heavenly state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.44/en/sujato">AN 8.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed to the lay follower Vāseṭṭha that if one's dear relatives and kinsmen were to observe the eight-factored Uposatha, it would lead to their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.44/en/sujato"><b>long-term welfare</b></a> and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.44/en/sujato">AN 8.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.44/en/sujato"><b>khattiyas</b></a> (the warrior-noble class) were to observe the eight-factored Uposatha, it would result in their long-term welfare and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.44/en/sujato">AN 8.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the spiritual benefits of the eight-factored Uposatha are so universal that even if all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.44/en/sujato"><b>brahmins</b></a>, vessas (merchants), or suddas (workers) observed it, they would experience long-term happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.44/en/sujato">AN 8.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if the entire world—including its devas, Māras, and Brahmas—were to observe the eight-factored Uposatha, it would be for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.44/en/sujato"><b>benefit of the world</b></a> for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.44/en/sujato">AN 8.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarkably suggested that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.44/en/sujato"><b>great sāl trees</b></a> would benefit from the eight-factored Uposatha if they were capable of conscious thought, emphasizing the immense power of this practice for any human being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.44/en/sujato">AN 8.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the laywoman Visākhā that a woman who possesses eight specific qualities will be reborn among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>Manāpakāyika devas</b></a> (devas of the delightful corps)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a virtuous wife as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>rising early</b></a> and retiring late, always eager to help and acting in a way that is pleasing to her husband? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a woman who speaks in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>piyavādinī</b></a> (pleasing and endearing) manner is practicing one of the eight qualities that lead to an auspicious rebirth in the celestial realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a woman should honor and respect those whom her husband respects, such as his parents and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>spiritual teachers</b></a>, offering them a seat and water when they arrive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a woman's skill and industry in domestic duties, such as working with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>wool or cotton</b></a>, is considered a virtuous quality that contributes to her future happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a woman should know the work and needs of her husband’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>servants and workers</b></a>, managing the household effectively and providing for them properly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a woman should carefully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>guard and protect</b></a> whatever wealth, grain, silver, or gold her husband brings home, ensuring she is not a spendthrift or a thief of his earnings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that going for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>refuge</b></a> to the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha is an essential quality for a woman seeking to be reborn in a delightful heavenly state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the observation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>five precepts</b></a>—abstaining from killing, stealing, sexual misconduct, false speech, and intoxicants—as a fundamental requirement for a woman's spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised a woman who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>generous</b></a> (cāgavatī), living at home with a mind free from stinginess and delighting in giving and sharing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a woman should not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>despise her husband</b></a>, who works energetically and constantly to support her and provide her with all she desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a woman who follows her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>husband's will</b></a> and remains dedicated to her duties will be reborn among the Manāpakāyika devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a "wise woman" (paṇḍitā) as one who respects her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>husband’s elders</b></a> and maintains a harmonious and industrious household? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a successful laywoman as one who stays <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato"><b>industrious and diligent</b></a> (uṭṭhāhikā analasā), looking after the people in her household and protecting the family's resources? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.47/en/sujato">AN 8.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.178/en/sujato"><b>what is bad</b></a> (<i>asādhu</i>) as the ten courses of unwholesome action, which include the destruction of life, stealing, and sexual misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.178/en/sujato">AN 10.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.178/en/sujato"><b>what is good</b></a> (<i>sādhu</i>) consists of ten specific virtues, such as abstinence from false speech, abstinence from divisive speech, and abstinence from harsh speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.178/en/sujato">AN 10.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.179/en/sujato"><b>ignoble Dhamma</b></a> (<i>anariya dhamma</i>) as a set of behaviors that include idle chatter, covetousness, and ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.179/en/sujato">AN 10.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.179/en/sujato"><b>noble Dhamma</b></a> (<i>ariya dhamma</i>) is practiced when one maintains uncovetousness, non-ill will, and right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.179/en/sujato">AN 10.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.180/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome action</b></a> (<i>akusala</i>) as ten paths of conduct, starting with the destruction of life and ending with wrong view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.180/en/sujato">AN 10.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.180/en/sujato"><b>wholesome action</b></a> (<i>kusala</i>) involves the ten paths of virtue, which include abstinence from taking what is not given and abstinence from sexual misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.180/en/sujato">AN 10.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.183/en/sujato"><b>tainted dhamma</b></a> (<i>sāsava dhamma</i>) as one that involves mental impurities like divisive speech and harsh speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.183/en/sujato">AN 10.183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.183/en/sujato"><b>untainted dhamma</b></a> (<i>anāsava dhamma</i>) is realized through the ten wholesome courses of action, such as abstinence from idle chatter and having right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.183/en/sujato">AN 10.183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.184/en/sujato"><b>blameworthy dhamma</b></a> (<i>sāvajja dhamma</i>) as engaging in the destruction of life, stealing, and harborous mental states like ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.184/en/sujato">AN 10.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.184/en/sujato"><b>blameless dhamma</b></a> (<i>anavajja dhamma</i>) as the ten practices that include being uncovetous and abstaining from false speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.184/en/sujato">AN 10.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.187/en/sujato"><b>dhamma resulting in suffering</b></a> (<i>dukkhudraya dhamma</i>) is defined by the ten unwholesome courses of action like sexual misconduct and wrong view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.187/en/sujato">AN 10.187</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.187/en/sujato"><b>dhamma resulting in happiness</b></a> (<i>sukhudraya dhamma</i>) is found in the ten virtuous practices, such as abstinence from taking what is not given? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.187/en/sujato">AN 10.187</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.188/en/sujato"><b>dhamma with a ripening in suffering</b></a> (<i>dukkhavipāka dhamma</i>) is essentially the path of harsh speech and the nine other unwholesome actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.188/en/sujato">AN 10.188</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.188/en/sujato"><b>dhamma with a ripening in happiness</b></a> (<i>sukhavipāka dhamma</i>) involves cultivating the ten wholesome paths, such as non-ill will and right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.188/en/sujato">AN 10.188</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.178/en/sujato"><b>bad conduct</b></a> as consisting of ten specific actions, including divisive speech and idle chatter, which lead away from peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.178/en/sujato">AN 10.178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.179/en/sujato"><b>noble conduct</b></a> as including abstinence from the destruction of life and abstinence from false speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.179/en/sujato">AN 10.179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.180/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome behavior</b></a> includes covetousness, which the sources describe as part of the ten unwholesome paths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.180/en/sujato">AN 10.180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.183/en/sujato"><b>tainted action</b></a> is what one practices when they engage in harsh speech and harbor wrong view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.183/en/sujato">AN 10.183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.184/en/sujato"><b>blameless action</b></a> involves the cultivation of right view as the foundation for ethical living? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.184/en/sujato">AN 10.184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.188/en/sujato"><b>suffering is the ripening</b></a> of actions such as stealing, sexual misconduct, and the destruction of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.188/en/sujato">AN 10.188</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is possessed of ten unwholesome qualities—including the destruction of life, stealing, and wrong view—is someone who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.199/en/sujato"><b>should not be associated with</b></a> (<i>na sevitabbo</i>), whereas one who abstains from these actions should be associated with? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.199/en/sujato">AN 10.199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who practices the ten courses of wholesome action, such as right view and abstinence from harsh speech, is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.202/en/sujato"><b>worthy of honor</b></a> (<i>pujjo</i>), while one who engages in unwholesome conduct like divisive speech and idle chatter is not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.202/en/sujato">AN 10.202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.203/en/sujato"><b>praiseworthy</b></a> (<i>pāsaṃso</i>) if they fulfill the ten wholesome paths, which include being uncovetous, having non-ill will, and abstaining from false speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.203/en/sujato">AN 10.203</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who engages in the ten unwholesome courses of action, such as sexual misconduct and ill will, as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.204/en/sujato"><b>lacking in reverence</b></a> (<i>agāravo</i>), whereas one who abstains from these behaviors is considered respectful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.204/en/sujato">AN 10.204</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who avoids the ten unwholesome actions, such as divisive speech and the destruction of life, is one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.201/en/sujato"><b>should be attended upon</b></a> (<i>payirupāsitabbo</i>), while one who engages in them should not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.201/en/sujato">AN 10.201</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who follows the ten unwholesome courses of action <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.209/en/sujato"><b>does not grow in wisdom</b></a> (<i>paññāya na vaḍḍhati</i>), but one who maintains right view and abstains from unwholesome speech and conduct does grow in wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.209/en/sujato">AN 10.209</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.207/en/sujato"><b>is purified</b></a> (<i>visujjhati</i>) through the ten courses of wholesome action, which involve abstaining from taking what is not given, sexual misconduct, and the destruction of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.207/en/sujato">AN 10.207</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the ten courses of wholesome action—including non-ill will and right view—as the specific means by which a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.208/en/sujato"><b>overcomes conceit</b></a> (<i>mānaṃ adhibhoti</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.208/en/sujato">AN 10.208</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who engages in unwholesome conduct like idle chatter and stealing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.206/en/sujato"><b>does not succeed</b></a> (<i>na ārādhako</i>), whereas one who is virtuous in the ten ways, such as having right view, is one who succeeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.206/en/sujato">AN 10.206</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person established in the ten courses of wholesome action, such as being uncovetous and abstaining from lying, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.210/en/sujato"><b>generates much merit</b></a> (<i>bahuṃ puññaṃ pasavati</i>), while one who engages in unwholesome actions generates much demerit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.210/en/sujato">AN 10.210</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>destroyer of life</b></a>, characterized as being pitiless and blood-handed, possesses a quality that leads to rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>taking what is not given</b></a>—stealing the property of others from a village or a forest—as one of the ten unwholesome actions that results in a downward rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> with those protected by parents, relatives, or religious commitments is a behavior that leads to being cast into hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>false speech</b></a>, where a person deliberately lies for their own gain or the gain of others, is a specific dhamma that leads to rebirth in a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a>, which aims to break up friendships and create factions, is a quality that causes a person to be reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a>—words that are rough, bitter, and conducive to anger—as one of the ten unwholesome paths leading to a lower realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a>, which is speaking at the wrong time about things that are not beneficial or consistent with the Dhamma, as a quality that leads to rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>covetous person</b></a>, who constantly longs for the wealth and property of others, is practicing a quality that results in being cast into a state of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having a mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a>, characterized by wishing harm or destruction upon other beings, is a mental quality that leads to rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> as the distorted perspective that actions have no consequences and that there is no efficacy in virtue, leading one toward hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>abandoned the destruction of life</b></a> and dwells with compassion for all living beings is reborn in a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>abstinence from stealing</b></a>, where one does not take what belongs to others by way of theft, as a wholesome quality that leads to rebirth in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from sexual misconduct</b></a> by respecting the boundaries of others' relationships is one of the ten virtues that results in a heavenly destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>truthful speech</b></a>, where one speaks only what they know to be true when called as a witness, as a path leading to a heavenly rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>abandoning divisive speech</b></a> and speaking words that promote harmony and concord among people leads to rebirth in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>gentle speech</b></a>—words that are polite, pleasing to the ear, and heart-touching—as a virtue that leads a person to a heavenly realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>timely speech</b></a>, which is speech that is meaningful, reasoned, and connected with the Dhamma, as a quality that results in a heavenly rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>uncovetous</b></a>, or not longing for the belongings of others, is a mental state that leads to being reborn in a happy destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>non-ill will</b></a>, characterized by wishing that all beings live happily and free from enmity, is a quality that leads to heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> as the correct perspective that virtue has fruits and that there is a next world, a realization that leads to rebirth in a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.212/en/sujato">AN 10.212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings are the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>owners of their kamma</b></a> (<i>kammassakā</i>), meaning they are the heirs of their own actions and will experience the results of whatever deeds they perform, whether good or bad? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who destroys life, characterized as being pitiless and blood-handed, is said to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>creep by body</b></a>, speech, and mind, resulting in a crooked destiny? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>crooked bodily kamma</b></a> (<i>jimhaṃ kāyakammaṃ</i>) as a direct consequence of unwholesome actions like stealing and sexual misconduct, which leads to rebirth in miserable states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who engage in crooked behavior through unwholesome speech and conduct may be reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>animal realm</b></a> among creatures that physically "creep" away when they see humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>straight bodily kamma</b></a> (<i>uju kāyakammaṃ</i>) is developed by a person who abandons the destruction of life, lays aside the rod and weapon, and dwells with compassion for all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that beings are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>heirs to their kamma</b></a> (<i>kammadāyādā</i>), and that the specific quality of their future existence is determined by whether their prior actions were beautiful or evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>crooked destination</b></a> (<i>jimhā gati</i>) as one of two possibilities—either hell or the animal realm—for those whose bodily, verbal, and mental actions are twisted by unwholesome intentions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who avoids "creeping" by body, speech, and mind through virtuous conduct can be reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>heavenly worlds</b></a> that are exclusively pleasant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for those with straight conduct, rebirth can occur in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>high-class families</b></a>, such as wealthy Khattiyas, Brahmins, or householders, who possess great wealth and resources? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>creeping exposition</b></a> (<i>saṃsappanīyapariyāyo</i>) to describe the spiritual law that crooked actions lead to rebirth among "creeping" animals like snakes, scorpions, and centipedes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>contacts touch the person</b></a> who has been reborn (<i>upapannamenaṃ phassā phusanti</i>), meaning that one's sensory experiences are the direct result of the kamma they previously performed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, kamma is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>womb from which beings are born</b></a> (<i>kammayonī</i>), serving as the origin and the primary relation for every living being's existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized animals like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>mongooses, cats, and mice</b></a> as "creeping-born" (<i>saṃsappajātikā</i>) creatures whose state of birth is a result of having performed crooked bodily, verbal, and mental actions in a past life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>—the understanding that there is meaning in giving and that deeds have fruits—is essential for ensuring one's kamma remains "straight" rather than "creeping"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>pitiless toward all living beings</b></a> creates a crooked destiny that leads to rebirth in either a state of misery or the animal realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>crooked verbal kamma</b></a> as engaging in the four types of wrong speech—false, divisive, harsh, and idle chatter—which causes a person to "creep" into a lower rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>straight mental kamma</b></a> is cultivated by being uncovetous, maintaining a mind free of ill will, and holding a correct perspective on the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>beings are reborn</b></a> according to the specific nature of their deeds, such that the action itself determines the type of rebirth they will obtain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>straight destination</b></a> (<i>uju gati</i>) is the reward for those whose bodily, verbal, and mental conduct is honest and virtuous, leading to prosperity or heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that kamma is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato"><b>resort of beings</b></a> (<i>kammapaṭisaraṇā</i>), indicating that their own past and present actions are their ultimate foundation and refuge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.216/en/sujato">AN 10.216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are six <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>supreme things</b></a>—supreme seeing, hearing, gain, training, service, and recollection—which lead to the purification of beings and the realization of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>supreme seeing</b></a> (dassanānuttariya) specifically as going to see the Tathāgata or a disciple of the Tathāgata with settled faith and settled love? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered going to see an elephant treasure, a horse treasure, or a gem treasure to be an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>inferior form of seeing</b></a> because it is vulgar, common, and does not lead to disenchantment or peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>supreme hearing</b></a> (savanānuttariya) involves going to hear the Dhamma from the Tathāgata or one of his disciples with established faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified listening to the sounds of drums, lutes, and songs, or the teachings of those with wrong view, as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>vulgar form of hearing</b></a> that fails to lead to enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>supreme gain</b></a> (lābhānuttariya) is the acquisition of faith in the Tathāgata or his disciples, rather than the gain of material wealth or family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the gain of children, a spouse, or wealth as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>inferior gain</b></a> because such gains do not lead to the destruction of suffering or the attainment of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>supreme training</b></a> (sikkhānuttariya) consists of training in higher virtue, higher mind, and higher wisdom within the teaching and discipline of the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified training in elephant riding, horsemanship, or archery as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>ignoble training</b></a> that is unbeneficial for those seeking the end of sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>supreme service</b></a> (pāricariyānuttariya) as the act of serving the Tathāgata or his disciples with settled faith and devotion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that serving members of the ruling class, brahmins, or householders is considered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>lowly service</b></a> when compared to serving those on the path to enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>supreme recollection</b></a> (anussatānuttariya) as the practice of recollecting the qualities of the Tathāgata or his disciples with settled faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that recollecting the acquisition of children or wealth is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>unbeneficial recollection</b></a> that does not contribute to spiritual awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>settled faith</b></a> (niviṭṭhasaddho) and settled love is a prerequisite for seeing or hearing the Tathāgata to be considered truly supreme? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the six supreme things are for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>purification of beings</b></a> and for the overcoming of sorrow and lamentation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that training in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>higher virtue</b></a> (adhisīla) is part of the supreme training that leads to the realization of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described following or serving an ascetic with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> as a common and unbeneficial practice that leads away from the goal of peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who have attained the supreme things develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>recollection connected with seclusion</b></a>, which leads toward the security of the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>higher wisdom</b></a> (adhipaññā) is a key element of the supreme training necessary for the destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>delighting in heedfulness</b></a> and restrained by virtue will eventually reach the place where all suffering ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>supreme gain</b></a> leads specifically to the disappearance of misery and the attainment of the right way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned training in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato"><b>higher mind</b></a> (adhicitta) as a core part of the education that is considered supreme among all types of training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.30/en/sujato">AN 6.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato"><b>delighting in work</b></a> as one of the six factors that lead to the spiritual decline of a monk who is still in training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato">AN 6.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato"><b>delighting in talk</b></a> is a quality that causes a trainee monk to fall away from their spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato">AN 6.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato"><b>delighting in sleep</b></a> is one of the six behaviors that contribute to the spiritual decline of a monk in training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato">AN 6.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato"><b>delighting in company</b></a> is a trait that leads a trainee monk to decline rather than progress toward Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato">AN 6.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato"><b>not guarding the doors of the sense faculties</b></a> is a key factor that leads to the spiritual downfall of a trainee monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato">AN 6.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato"><b>immoderation in eating</b></a> as one of the six specific things that lead to the decline of a monk in training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato">AN 6.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a trainee monk avoids decline by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato"><b>not delighting in work</b></a>, which helps maintain focus on the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato">AN 6.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato"><b>not delighting in talk</b></a> is a quality that leads to the non-decline of a trainee monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato">AN 6.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a trainee monk ensures their spiritual progress by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato"><b>not delighting in sleep</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato">AN 6.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato"><b>not delighting in company</b></a> is one of the six factors that prevent a monk in training from falling away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato">AN 6.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato"><b>guarding the doors of the sense faculties</b></a> is a crucial practice for a trainee monk to remain on the path to enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato">AN 6.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato"><b>moderation in eating</b></a> as a vital quality that prevents the spiritual decline of a monk still in training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.31/en/sujato">AN 6.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity told the Buddha that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.32/en/sujato"><b>reverence for the Teacher</b></a> is one of the six things that lead to a monk's non-decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.32/en/sujato">AN 6.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha approved of a deity’s teaching which stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.32/en/sujato"><b>reverence for the Dhamma</b></a> ensures a monk will not fall away from their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.32/en/sujato">AN 6.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity informed the Buddha that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.32/en/sujato"><b>reverence for the Saṅgha</b></a> is a quality that leads a monk toward Nibbāna and prevents spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.32/en/sujato">AN 6.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.32/en/sujato"><b>reverence for the training</b></a> is one of the six essential factors for a monk’s continued spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.32/en/sujato">AN 6.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.32/en/sujato"><b>reverence for heedfulness</b></a> as a primary quality that prevents a monk from falling away, a statement the Buddha later repeated to his monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.32/en/sujato">AN 6.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted a deity's teaching that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.32/en/sujato"><b>reverence for hospitality</b></a> is a factor that protects a monk from declining in their spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.32/en/sujato">AN 6.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.33/en/sujato"><b>moral shame</b></a> (hiri) is considered incapable of falling away and is in the very presence of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.33/en/sujato">AN 6.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity told the Buddha that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.33/en/sujato"><b>moral dread</b></a> (ottappa) is one of the six qualities that ensure a monk's spiritual non-decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.33/en/sujato">AN 6.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.35/en/sujato"><b>perception of impermanence</b></a> is the first of six specific qualities that partake of true knowledge (vijjābhāgiya)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.35/en/sujato">AN 6.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.35/en/sujato"><b>perception of suffering</b></a> in what is impermanent is a crucial mental practice for those seeking to develop spiritual insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.35/en/sujato">AN 6.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.35/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-self</b></a> in what is suffering as one of the six perceptions that lead to the attainment of true knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.35/en/sujato">AN 6.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.35/en/sujato"><b>perception of abandonment</b></a> as a core component of the training for those striving to understand the Dhamma deeply? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.35/en/sujato">AN 6.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.35/en/sujato"><b>perception of dispassion</b></a> is one of the six things that help a practitioner progress toward enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.35/en/sujato">AN 6.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.35/en/sujato"><b>perception of cessation</b></a> completes the list of six perceptions that are essential for the development of true knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.35/en/sujato">AN 6.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato"><b>angry and resentful</b></a> is a root of dispute that causes a monk to live without respect or reverence for the Teacher, the Dhamma, and the Saṅgha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato">AN 6.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato"><b>contemptuous and domineering</b></a> can create disputes in the Saṅgha that result in the harm and suffering of many people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato">AN 6.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato"><b>envious and miserly</b></a> as a harmful root of dispute that prevents a monk from properly fulfilling his spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato">AN 6.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato"><b>deceitful and fraudulent</b></a> initiates conflict within the community and acts against the welfare of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato">AN 6.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato"><b>evil wishes and wrong view</b></a> is a major root of dispute that leads to a lack of reverence for the Buddhist training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato">AN 6.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned against <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato"><b>adhering to one's own views</b></a> tenaciously, as this stubbornness is a root of dispute that leads to discord within the spiritual community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato">AN 6.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers to strive to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato"><b>abandon bad roots</b></a> of dispute if they notice these unwholesome qualities within themselves or in others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato">AN 6.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato"><b>root of dispute</b></a> is not present, a monk should practice in such a way that these negative qualities do not arise in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato">AN 6.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that disputes caused by a lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato"><b>reverence for the Teacher</b></a>, Dhamma, and Saṅgha lead to the misery of many beings, including both gods and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.36/en/sujato">AN 6.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once stayed in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>Campā</b></a> on the banks of the Gaggarā Lake when he taught about the varying results of giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta asked the Buddha why the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>same gift</b></a> given by different people could result in either little fruit or great fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when someone gives a gift with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>expectation</b></a> or with a mind bound to the result, the gift does not lead to the greatest possible spiritual benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, giving a gift while seeking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>storage</b></a> or thinking "I will enjoy this after death" leads to rebirth among the Four Great Kings but eventually results in returning to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the highest form of giving is when a person gives a gift as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>ornament for the mind</b></a> and a support for the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who gives a gift as a support for the mind can be reborn among the Brahmākāyika devas and become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>non-returner</b></a> who never returns to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned some people give gifts because they feel they should not let their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>family lineage</b></a> and the long-standing traditions of their ancestors fail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who gives because they think, "I <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>cook</b></a>, but these others do not cook; it is not right for me who cooks not to give to those who do not cook"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that giving a gift simply because it makes the giver's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>mind clear</b></a> and brings them joy and happiness still does not lead to the ultimate spiritual goal of non-returning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared some people's motivation for giving to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>great sacrifices</b></a> performed by ancient rishis like Atthaka, Vāmaka, and Vessāmitta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed items such as food, drink, clothing, vehicles, garlands, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>lighting</b></a> as typical gifts given to recluses or brahmins that produce different results based on intention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who give with an eye toward future enjoyment are reborn in the company of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>Four Great Kings</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even if a giver is reborn as a deity, once that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>karma is exhausted</b></a>, they may still return to this human existence unless they gave with the highest motivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identifies the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>cause and reason</b></a> why a gift of the same outward appearance can result in either great fruit or little fruit depending on the giver's intent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that the gifts discussed in this teaching are those offered to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>recluse or brahmin</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the lay followers of Campā approached Venerable Sāriputta because they had not heard a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma talk</b></a> directly from the Buddha's presence for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta suggested the lay followers visit the Buddha on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>Uposatha day</b></a> to have the opportunity to hear his teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the most refined motivation for giving—giving to adorn the mind—leads to rebirth among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>Brahmākāyika devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described one level of motivation where a person gives simply thinking that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>giving is good</b></a>, which leads to a heavenly rebirth but not to the status of a non-returner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato"><b>bound mind</b></a> to describe a state where a giver is overly attached to the result or the act of giving itself, which limits the spiritual outcome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.52/en/sujato">AN 7.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared there are four things a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a> does not need to guard because his conduct in all areas is already perfectly pure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato">AN 7.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha has such purified bodily conduct that he never has to be on guard thinking, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato"><b>may another not know this</b></a>," regarding any of his physical actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato">AN 7.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato"><b>verbal conduct</b></a> is so pure that there is no misconduct of speech he would ever need to hide or protect from the knowledge of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato">AN 7.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a Tathāgata possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato"><b>purified mental conduct</b></a>, meaning he has no mental faults that he would feel the need to conceal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato">AN 7.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that because he possesses a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato"><b>purified livelihood</b></a>, he is free from any wrong way of living that would require him to be secretive or guarded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato">AN 7.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato"><b>irreproachable</b></a> in three specific ways, such that no ascetic, brahmin, or deity in the world could justly find fault with him in those matters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato">AN 7.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stays in a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato"><b>fearlessness</b></a> and self-confidence because he sees no reason why anyone could successfully challenge the truth of his well-expounded Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato">AN 7.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated his Dhamma is so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato"><b>well-expounded</b></a> that no one, whether a Mara or a Brahma, can rightfully claim he has not taught the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato">AN 7.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato"><b>path to Nibbāna</b></a> has been so clearly proclaimed to his disciples that they can attain liberation through their own direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato">AN 7.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reaches a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato"><b>self-confidence</b></a> (vesārajjappatto) because no one can justly accuse him of failing to properly explain the path to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato">AN 7.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed to the fact that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato"><b>hundreds of disciples</b></a> have already attained the destruction of the taints as evidence that his teaching is effective and beyond reproach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato">AN 7.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha describes the goal for his disciples as reaching <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom, having realized it for themselves in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato">AN 7.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared he dwells in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato"><b>security</b></a> (khemappatto) because there is no basis for any being in the world to justly criticize his realization or his assembly of liberated disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.58/en/sujato">AN 7.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a bhikkhu who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato"><b>seven qualities</b></a> will, before long, attain the destruction of the taints and dwell in liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato">AN 7.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the first essential quality for a bhikkhu seeking the end of suffering is being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato"><b>possessor of faith</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato">AN 7.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> (sīlavā) as a core requirement for a monk to achieve the highest spiritual goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato">AN 7.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a> (bahussuto)—having heard and retained much of the Dhamma—is one of the seven factors leading to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato">AN 7.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the importance of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato"><b>secluded</b></a> (paṭisallīno) as a necessary practice for a bhikkhu to realize the liberation of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato">AN 7.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a bhikkhu must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato"><b>energetic</b></a> (āraddhavīriyo) in his practice to reach the state where all mental fermentations are fully destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato">AN 7.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato"><b>mindful and wise</b></a> as the final critical qualities that enable a practitioner to attain enlightenment in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.60/en/sujato">AN 7.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> to see Venerable Mahāmoggallāna nodding off while sitting in the village of Kallavāḷaputta and instantly appeared before him to offer guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised Mahāmoggallāna that if drowsiness arises during meditation, he should not give attention to or dwell upon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> that initially caused the drowsiness to overcome him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Mahāmoggallāna to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>reflect on the Dhamma</b></a> as he had heard and learned it as a practical method to dispel sloth and torpor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that if drowsiness persists, a practitioner should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>recite the Dhamma</b></a> in detail as it has been mastered to help wake up the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended physically pulling both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>earlobes</b></a> and rubbing one's limbs with the hands as a physical technique to overcome sleepiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised Mahāmoggallāna to get up from his seat, wash his eyes with water, and look around at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>stars and constellations</b></a> if he continued to feel drowsy while meditating at night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should develop a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>perception of light</b></a>, staying focused on the perception of day regardless of whether it is day or night, to cultivate a mind that is bright and unclouded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Mahāmoggallāna to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>walking meditation</b></a> with his senses turned inward and his mind not straying outward as a way to abandon persistent drowsiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>lion’s posture</b></a>—lying on the right side with one foot on top of the other—as the proper way to rest while remaining mindful and aware of the time to wake up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Mahāmoggallāna that when he wakes up from rest, he should quickly get up and resolve not to indulge in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>pleasure of sleep</b></a> or the pleasure of reclining? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk should not visit families with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>raised trunk</b></a> (arrogance), because if people are busy and do not pay him attention, he might become dejected and lose his concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that dejection leads to restlessness and a lack of restraint, and a restless person’s mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>far from concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised against engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>contentious speech</b></a>, because such talk leads to excessive talking, which causes restlessness and hinders the stillness of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha did not praise all types of social association, he specifically praised associating with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>secluded lodgings</b></a> that are quiet and suitable for retreat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the core insight for liberation is the realization that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>all things</b></a> are not fit to be adhered to"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who understands that all things are not worth adhering to should contemplate any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>—pleasant, painful, or neutral—as impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by contemplating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>fading away</b></a> and cessation of feelings, a monk does not cling to anything in the world and personally attains Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person does not cling to anything, they do not become anxious, and being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>unagitated</b></a>, they realize for themselves that birth is destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the monk who is liberated by the destruction of craving as one who has reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>ultimate goal</b></a> and is the best among devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha appeared before Mahāmoggallāna as quickly as a strong man might <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato"><b>extend his arm</b></a>, traveling instantly from the Bhesakaḷā Wood to Magadha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.61/en/sujato">AN 7.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Visākhā that a woman who possesses four specific qualities is considered one who has practiced for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>victory in this world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>well-organized in work</b></a> as being skillful and diligent in domestic tasks such as wool and cotton work, and being able to manage them effectively? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a woman manages her workforce well when she knows what work has been done or left undone by servants and employees, and understands their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>strength and health</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a woman who manages her household should properly divide and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>share food</b></a> and portions with the servants and employees? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a woman acts in a way pleasing to her husband when she refuses to do anything he finds displeasing, even for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>sake of her life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a woman protects what is earned by guarding the wealth, grain, silver, or gold her husband brings home, and by being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>not a spendthrift</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four qualities—faith, virtue, generosity, and wisdom—as the means by which a woman practices for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>victory in the next world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described accomplishment in faith as having confidence and believing in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>enlightenment of the Tathāgata</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that accomplishment in virtue for a laywoman involves abstaining from taking life, stealing, sexual misconduct, false speech, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>intoxicating liquors</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized accomplishment in generosity as living at home with a mind free from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>stain of stinginess</b></a> and being devoted to charity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the woman who is "generous and open-handed," noting that she should delight in giving and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>sharing gifts</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a woman who fulfills eight specific qualities—four for this world and four for the next—is called one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>virtuous and truthful</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a virtuous laywoman who possesses these eight qualities clears the path to future welfare and is reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>delightful deva world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on the eight qualities of a successful woman was delivered to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>Visākhā, Migāra’s mother</b></a>, while he was staying at Sāvatthī in the Eastern Monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a woman to be well-organized in her work, she must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>skillful and not lazy</b></a> in managing the various duties within her husband's home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined accomplishment in wisdom as being possessed of wisdom, which is further detailed as a quality necessary for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>victory in the next world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a woman protects her husband's earnings by ensuring she is not a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>drinker of liquor</b></a>, a thief, or a gambler, which would otherwise destroy his wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a woman who is "accomplished in faith" as one who understands the qualities of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato"><b>Blessed One</b></a>, including being an Arahant and a perfectly enlightened one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.49/en/sujato">AN 8.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī first requested the Buddha to allow women to go forth from the household life into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>homelessness</b></a> while he was staying at Kapilavatthu in the Banyan Tree Park? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha initially refused Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī's request for women to enter the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma and Vinaya</b></a> three separate times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī demonstrated her determination by cutting off her hair, donning saffron robes, and walking a long distance to Vesālī with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>swollen feet</b></a> and a dusty body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed to Ānanda that women are capable of attaining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>fruits of the path</b></a>, including the stages of stream-entry, once-returning, non-returning, and arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ānanda successfully appealed to the Buddha by reminding him that Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī was his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>foster mother</b></a> and had nursed him when his own mother died? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stipulated that women's ordination would only be granted if they accepted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>eight capital principles</b></a> that were to be respected and honored for life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's first capital principle for nuns requires that even a nun ordained for a century must respect a monk ordained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>that very day</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha prohibited nuns from spending a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>rains retreat</b></a> in a residence where there were no monks present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, nuns are required to seek from the Sangha of monks the date of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>Uposatha observance</b></a> and the coming of a monk for advice every half-month? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mandated that after the rains retreat, a nun must perform the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>invitation ceremony</b></a> (Pavāraṇā) before both the Sangha of monks and the Sangha of nuns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha required a nun who has committed a serious offense to undergo a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>fortnight of penance</b></a> (mānatta) before both the community of monks and nuns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha established that a female trainee must practice for two years in six rules before seeking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>higher ordination</b></a> from both Sanghas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha strictly forbade nuns from ever <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>reviling or scolding</b></a> a monk in any way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that while monks have permission to admonish nuns, the path of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>admonishment</b></a> is officially closed to nuns regarding monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī accepted the eight capital principles as her ordination, comparing her acceptance to a youth placing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>garland of flowers</b></a> upon their head with both hands? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted that if women had not gone forth into homelessness, the "Good Dhamma" would have remained for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>one thousand years</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that because women were admitted to the Order, the spiritual life would not last long and the "Good Dhamma" would only remain for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>five hundred years</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy comparing the admission of women to a family with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>many women and few men</b></a>, which is easily targeted and destroyed by burglars? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the arrival of women in the Dhamma and Vinaya to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>rice field</b></a> being attacked by the disease known as "white-rust," which causes it to not last long? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened the entry of women into the monastic life to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>sugarcane field</b></a> being ruined by the disease called "red-rust"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he prescribed the eight capital principles for nuns in advance, much like a man might build an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>embankment</b></a> to prevent a great reservoir of water from overflowing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī was accompanied by a large group of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan women</b></a> when she made her arduous journey from Kapilavatthu to see the Buddha in Vesālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ānanda found Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī standing outside the entrance of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>Great Wood</b></a> in Vesālī, weeping and distressed because the Buddha had refused her request? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the eight capital principles as rules that must be respected, honored, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>never transgressed</b></a> for as long as one lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha only agreed to the ordination of women after Ānanda used a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato"><b>different approach</b></a> to question him regarding women's potential for spiritual attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.51/en/sujato">AN 8.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Ānanda that a monk must possess eight specific qualities to be properly appointed as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato"><b>exhorter of nuns</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato">AN 8.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk authorized to advise nuns must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a>, living restrained by the rules of the Pātimokkha and seeing danger in the slightest faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato">AN 8.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha required a monk who instructs nuns to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a> and well-versed in teachings that are good in the beginning, middle, and end, and which proclaim the perfectly complete spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato">AN 8.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk must have both the monks’ and the nuns’ <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato"><b>codes of discipline</b></a> thoroughly learned and well-analyzed in detail to be fit to instruct the community of nuns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato">AN 8.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk assigned to teach nuns should be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato"><b>good speaker</b></a> with a polite, clear, and distinct voice that communicates the meaning effectively? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato">AN 8.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk who exhorts nuns must be capable of inspiring and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato"><b>gladdening</b></a> the community of nuns with a talk on the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato">AN 8.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato"><b>liked and agreeable</b></a> to the majority of nuns as a necessary quality for a monk to be appointed as their instructor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato">AN 8.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified a monk cannot be an exhorter of nuns if he has ever committed a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato"><b>serious offense</b></a> against a woman who has gone forth into the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato">AN 8.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk must have been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato"><b>ordained for twenty years</b></a> or more to be considered for the role of instructing the nuns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.52/en/sujato">AN 8.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī requested a brief teaching, the Buddha provided criteria for distinguishing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.53/en/sujato"><b>true Dhamma and Vinaya</b></a> from what is not the Teacher’s instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.53/en/sujato">AN 8.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī that any qualities leading to passion rather than <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.53/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> should be recognized as not being the true Dhamma or the Teacher’s instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.53/en/sujato">AN 8.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the true Teacher's instruction as those qualities that lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.53/en/sujato"><b>liberation from bondage</b></a> rather than further entanglement in worldly ties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.53/en/sujato">AN 8.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the Dhamma is that which leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.53/en/sujato"><b>shedding of defilements</b></a> rather than their accumulation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.53/en/sujato">AN 8.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī that true Dhamma promotes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.53/en/sujato"><b>fewness of wishes</b></a> and contentment, whereas great desire and lack of contentment are not the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.53/en/sujato">AN 8.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī that qualities leading to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.53/en/sujato"><b>solitude</b></a> and the arousal of energy are the marks of the true Dhamma, while socializing and laziness are not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.53/en/sujato">AN 8.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the Venerable Upāli that one of the primary reasons he prescribed training rules for his disciples was for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.31/en/sujato"><b>excellence of the Saṅgha</b></a> and the comfort of its members? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.31/en/sujato">AN 10.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the Pātimokkha is recited for the specific purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.31/en/sujato"><b>curbing bad people</b></a> while ensuring the well-behaved monks can live in comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.31/en/sujato">AN 10.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that training rules were established for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.31/en/sujato"><b>restraint of taints</b></a> belonging to the present life and the protection against taints in future lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.31/en/sujato">AN 10.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.31/en/sujato"><b>inspiring of those without faith</b></a> as one of the ten key reasons for prescribing training rules to his followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.31/en/sujato">AN 10.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the recitation of the Pātimokkha is essential for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.31/en/sujato"><b>prolongation of the true Dhamma</b></a> and the ongoing support of the monastic discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.31/en/sujato">AN 10.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha prescribed training rules to ensure the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.31/en/sujato"><b>increase of faith</b></a> in those who already possess it, while also winning over those who do not yet have faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.31/en/sujato">AN 10.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should only be appointed to a committee for settling disputes if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.33/en/sujato"><b>virtuous and restrained</b></a> by the Pātimokkha and sees danger even in the smallest faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.33/en/sujato">AN 10.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that a monk chosen for a committee must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.33/en/sujato"><b>learned in the Dhamma</b></a>, having fully mastered and analyzed teachings that are good in the beginning, middle, and end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.33/en/sujato">AN 10.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha required that a monk appointed to settle monastic legal issues have both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.33/en/sujato"><b>Pātimokkhas well-transmitted</b></a> to him in detail, including the specific rules and their thorough analysis? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.33/en/sujato">AN 10.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is fit to serve on a committee for settling disputes only if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.33/en/sujato"><b>unshakeable in the Vinaya</b></a> and cannot be led astray from its principles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.33/en/sujato">AN 10.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.33/en/sujato"><b>skilled in settling legal issues</b></a>, which involves knowing the issue itself, its origin, its cessation, and the specific way leading to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.33/en/sujato">AN 10.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk appointed to a committee must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.33/en/sujato"><b>able to convince</b></a> and reconcile both the proponents and opponents involved in a monastic dispute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.33/en/sujato">AN 10.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk must possess ten specific qualities, including being virtuous and restrained by the Pātimokkha, before he is qualified to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato"><b>give higher ordination</b></a> to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato">AN 10.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who ordains others must be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato"><b>learned person</b></a> who has mastered and analyzed the Dhamma that is good in the beginning, middle, and end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato">AN 10.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is only fit to give higher ordination if the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato"><b>Pātimokkha is well-transmitted</b></a> to him in detail, including its specific rules and thorough analysis? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato">AN 10.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a preceptor must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato"><b>able to care for the sick</b></a>, or ensure they are cared for, as a requirement for giving higher ordination to a disciple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato">AN 10.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha required a monk who gives higher ordination to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato"><b>capable of dispelling dissatisfaction</b></a> or helping someone else to dispel it when it arises in a student? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato">AN 10.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk must be able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato"><b>dispel remorse</b></a> in accordance with the Dhamma before he is considered qualified to ordain new monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato">AN 10.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a preceptor must have the ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato"><b>dispel wrong views</b></a> in accordance with the Dhamma whenever they arise in his disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato">AN 10.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who ordains others must be able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato"><b>encourage higher virtue</b></a> (adhisīla) in his students to ensure their progress in the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato">AN 10.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that a preceptor must be capable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato"><b>encouraging higher mind</b></a> (adhicitta) in his disciples as one of the ten essential qualities of a teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato">AN 10.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is fit to give higher ordination only if he is able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato"><b>encourage higher wisdom</b></a> (adhipaññā) in those he trains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.34/en/sujato">AN 10.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the ten qualities required to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.35/en/sujato"><b>give dependence</b></a> (nissaya) to a junior monk are the same as those required to give higher ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.35/en/sujato">AN 10.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Upāli that a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.35/en/sujato"><b>gives dependence</b></a> must be one who sees danger in even the smallest faults and trains himself in the training rules? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.35/en/sujato">AN 10.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato"><b>schism in the Saṅgha</b></a> as occurring when monks misrepresent non-Dhamma as Dhamma and Dhamma as non-Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato">AN 10.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a schism is created when monks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato"><b>misrepresent the Vinaya</b></a> by claiming what is not Vinaya to be Vinaya and what is Vinaya to be non-Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato">AN 10.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a division in the monastic community occurs if monks claim that what was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato"><b>not spoken by the Tathāgata</b></a> was actually spoken or uttered by him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato">AN 10.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a schism arises when monks claim that what was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato"><b>practiced by the Tathāgata</b></a> was never actually practiced by him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato">AN 10.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that asserting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato"><b>what was not prescribed</b></a> by the Tathāgata was in fact prescribed by him is one of the ten grounds for a formal split in the Saṅgha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato">AN 10.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a schism is formally established when monks, based on ten grounds of misrepresentation, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato"><b>perform separate legal acts</b></a> apart from the rest of the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato">AN 10.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a split in the Saṅgha involves a group of monks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato"><b>reciting a separate Pātimokkha</b></a> based on their misinterpretations of the Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato">AN 10.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that claiming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato"><b>what was prescribed</b></a> by the Tathāgata was not prescribed by him is a serious misrepresentation that leads to a broken community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.37/en/sujato">AN 10.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the Venerable Upāli that the Saṅgha is in a state of concord when monks correctly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.38/en/sujato"><b>explain non-Dhamma as non-Dhamma</b></a> and Dhamma as Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.38/en/sujato">AN 10.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, monastic concord is maintained when monks do not withdraw from the community to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.38/en/sujato"><b>perform separate legal acts</b></a> or recite a separate Pātimokkha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.38/en/sujato">AN 10.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Saṅgha remains united when its members correctly identify <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.38/en/sujato"><b>what was not spoken</b></a> or uttered by the Tathāgata as indeed being unspoken by him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.38/en/sujato">AN 10.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that concord exists when monks accurately represent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.38/en/sujato"><b>what was practiced</b></a> by the Tathāgata as actually having been practiced by him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.38/en/sujato">AN 10.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined concord (saṅghasāmaggī) as the state where monks agree on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.38/en/sujato"><b>what was prescribed</b></a> by the Tathāgata, ensuring the unity of their disciplinary procedures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.38/en/sujato">AN 10.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned the Venerable Ānanda that a person who splits a concordant Saṅgha earns a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.39/en/sujato"><b>misdeed lasting an aeon</b></a>, resulting in suffering in hell for that entire period? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.39/en/sujato">AN 10.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a schism in the Saṅgha occurs when monks misrepresent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.39/en/sujato"><b>non-Dhamma as Dhamma</b></a> and Dhamma as non-Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.39/en/sujato">AN 10.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a schism is formally established when monks, based on ten points of misrepresentation, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.39/en/sujato"><b>recite a separate Pātimokkha</b></a> apart from the rest of the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.39/en/sujato">AN 10.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who causes a schism, because he delights in factions and stands on non-Dhamma, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.39/en/sujato"><b>falls away from security from bondage</b></a> (yogakkhemā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.39/en/sujato">AN 10.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified claiming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.39/en/sujato"><b>what was not prescribed</b></a> by the Tathāgata was actually prescribed by him as one of the ten grounds for a formal split in the Saṅgha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.39/en/sujato">AN 10.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a schismatic who breaks the unity of the monastic order as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.39/en/sujato"><b>doomed to the states of deprivation</b></a> and the hell-realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.39/en/sujato">AN 10.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the Venerable Ānanda that someone who reunites a split Saṅgha earns <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato"><b>brahma merit</b></a> (sublime merit), allowing them to rejoice in heaven for an aeon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato">AN 10.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Saṅgha is restored to concord when monks return to correctly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato"><b>explaining Dhamma as Dhamma</b></a> and non-Dhamma as non-Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato">AN 10.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that concord in the Saṅgha is blissful, and that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato"><b>rejoices in concord</b></a> and stands on the Dhamma does not fall away from security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato">AN 10.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when the Saṅgha is concordant, monks do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato"><b>withdraw and perform</b></a> separate monastic duties, but act as a unified body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato">AN 10.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, providing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato"><b>support for those in concord</b></a> within the monastic community is a highly meritorious act that leads to heavenly rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato">AN 10.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a unified Saṅgha is one where monks correctly identify <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato"><b>what was not practiced</b></a> by the Tathāgata as indeed having been unpracticed by him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato">AN 10.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that concord is achieved when monks correctly represent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato"><b>what was spoken</b></a> by the Tathāgata as having been spoken by him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato">AN 10.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that concord is maintained when monks correctly declare <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato"><b>what was not prescribed</b></a> by the Tathāgata as being unprescribed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato">AN 10.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a person who successfully reunites a divided Saṅgha will experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato"><b>heavenly rejoicing</b></a> for an entire aeon as a result of their wholesome action? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.40/en/sujato">AN 10.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one cannot make an end of suffering without first experiencing the results of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>volitional kamma</b></a> that has been performed and accumulated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>three kinds</b></a> of bodily misconduct involving unwholesome volition that lead to suffering and have painful results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who destroys life as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>bloody-handed</b></a>, devoted to striking and killing, and merciless to all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized stealing as taking by way of theft the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>property of others</b></a>, whether in a village or a forest, which has not been given? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>four kinds</b></a> of verbal misconduct involving unwholesome volition that lead to painful consequences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>deliberate lie</b></a> as a person saying they know when they do not know, or saying they saw when they did not see, for their own sake or the sake of another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a> as repeating what one has heard elsewhere to create a rift between people, or to further incite those who are already divided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a> as language that is rough, bitter to others, offensive, and bordering on anger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned against <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a>, which he defined as talk that is untimely, factless, unbeneficial, and contrary to the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>mental misconduct</b></a> consists of three types: covetousness, ill will, and wrong view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> as having a mind full of hate, wishing for beings to be killed, slaughtered, or destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> includes the belief that there is no fruit or result of good and bad deeds, and no world beyond this one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that because of ten kinds of unwholesome volitional kamma, beings are reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>state of misery</b></a>, a bad destination, or even hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>square die</b></a> thrown into the air to illustrate how a person with unwholesome kamma is inevitably reborn in a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in bodily kamma</b></a> is three-fold, involving wholesome volition that leads to happiness and pleasant results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described one who abstains from the destruction of life as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>compassionate</b></a> and sympathetic toward the welfare of all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that one who abstains from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>stealing</b></a> does not take by way of theft what belongs to others in the village or the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>four kinds</b></a> of wholesome verbal kamma involving wholesome volition that result in happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who speaks the truth as one who, when asked to testify as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>witness</b></a>, does not tell a deliberate lie for any reason? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who avoids divisive speech as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>reconciles those who are divided</b></a> and encourages those who are already united? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised speech that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>gentle and pleasing</b></a> to the ear, affectionate, and which goes to the heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined wholesome speech as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>timely and factual</b></a>, beneficial, and in accordance with the Dhamma and the Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>mental accomplishment</b></a> consists of three types: being non-covetous, having a mind of goodwill, and possessing right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> as believing that there are results of good and bad actions, and that there is this world and a world beyond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that because of ten kinds of wholesome volitional kamma, beings are reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.217/en/sujato">AN 10.217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught it is impossible to make an end of suffering without first experiencing the results of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>volitional kamma</b></a> that has been performed and accumulated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the results of intentional deeds may be experienced in the present life, the next life, or in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>subsequent lives</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a noble disciple as one who dwells pervading the entire world with a mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> that is vast, immeasurable, and free from enmity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a mind which was previously limited can become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable</b></a> and well-developed through systematic meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any "limited kamma" (actions with restricted results) will not remain or persist in a person who has developed an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that if a person were to develop the liberation of mind by loving-kindness starting from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>young age</b></a>, they would be unlikely to perform evil deeds or experience their painful results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the liberation of mind through loving-kindness is a practice that should be developed by both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>women and men</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that this physical body is not something a person can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>take with them</b></a> when they pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a mortal being as one whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>interior is the mind</b></a>, indicating that the mind is the core that continues beyond the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that evil deeds done in the past with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>body born of action</b></a> (karajakāya) will be felt "here" (in the present) and will not follow a person who has developed the mind properly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the liberation of mind by loving-kindness, when well-developed, leads to the state of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>non-returner</b></a> (anāgāmī)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the practice of pervading all directions—above, below, and across—with a mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> that is boundless and free from ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a disciple as one who dwells pervading the whole world with a mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>altruistic joy</b></a> that is expansive and free from enmity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended the development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> to pervade the entire world with a mind that is immeasurable and free from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that for a wise bhikkhu who has not yet reached full enlightenment, the development of the immeasurable states leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>further liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>well-developed</b></a> (subhāvita) to describe a mind that has been transformed from a limited state to a boundless one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by dwelling with a mind of equanimity, any evil kamma done in the past will be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>experienced here</b></a> and will not follow the individual forward? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable mind</b></a> is achieved by systematically radiating the four divine abidings to all beings without exception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.219/en/sujato">AN 10.219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato"><b>twenty unwholesome qualities</b></a>, consisting of ten unwholesome actions performed by oneself combined with encouraging others to do the same, is reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato">AN 10.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person is reborn in heaven if they possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato"><b>twenty wholesome qualities</b></a>, which include personally abstaining from ten unwholesome deeds and encouraging others to also refrain from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato">AN 10.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato"><b>destroying life</b></a> and inciting others to destroy life as two of the twenty unwholesome factors that lead to rebirth in a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato">AN 10.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who refrains from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato"><b>taking what is not given</b></a> and encourages others to also refrain from theft possesses qualities that lead to rebirth in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato">AN 10.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> and leading others to engage in it are specific behaviors that contribute to a person being cast into hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato">AN 10.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that both telling a lie oneself and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato"><b>encouraging others to lie</b></a> are unwholesome verbal actions that result in rebirth in a bad destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato">AN 10.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that avoiding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a> and urging others to also avoid it are part of the twenty virtues that lead to a heavenly rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato">AN 10.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that using <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a> and inciting others to use harsh language are among the unwholesome factors that cause a person to be reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato">AN 10.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a> and encouraging others to engage in such talk as unwholesome verbal conduct that leads to suffering and rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato">AN 10.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having a mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> and encouraging others to dwell in ill will are mental qualities that result in a person being reborn in a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato">AN 10.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> and encouraging others to adopt right view are two essential qualities for being reborn in a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.222/en/sujato">AN 10.222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once used his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> to witness the laywoman Nandamātā of Veḷukaṇḍaka offering a gift to the community of monks led by Sāriputta and Moggallāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato">AN 6.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a gift is considered "endowed with six factors" when both the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato"><b>donor and the recipients</b></a> each possess three specific spiritual qualities during the act of giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato">AN 6.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the internal factors for a donor is to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato"><b>glad before giving</b></a>, which helps ensure the gift is spiritually complete? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato">AN 6.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that while in the actual process of offering a gift, the donor should maintain a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato"><b>mind clear and confident</b></a> to maximize the merit of the deed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato">AN 6.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a donor who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato"><b>gratified after giving</b></a> fulfills the third factor of a donor, completing their part in a "sixfold-factor gift"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato">AN 6.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified that the spiritual quality of the recipients matters, and a gift is more effective when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato"><b>recipients are free from lust</b></a> or are practicing for the removal of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato">AN 6.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the factors that makes a gift exceptionally fruitful is when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato"><b>recipients are free from hatred</b></a> or are working toward its total elimination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato">AN 6.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a gift to be endowed with its full six factors, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato"><b>recipients must be free from delusion</b></a> or at least be practicing for the removal of delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato">AN 6.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a gift possesses all six factors, the resulting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato"><b>stream of merit</b></a> is so vast that it is impossible to calculate its measure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato">AN 6.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the immeasurable merit of a sixfold-factor gift to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato"><b>water in the great ocean</b></a>, which cannot be measured in gallons or buckets? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato">AN 6.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a gift is particularly powerful when the donor has personally rinsed the items and offers them <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato"><b>with their own hands</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato">AN 6.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described wise and faithful donors who practice giving with a heart of letting go as those who will be reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato"><b>happy, unafflicted world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato">AN 6.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to recipients who are restrained and lead the holy life as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato"><b>consummate field</b></a> for a sacrifice, ensuring the gift brings great results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.37/en/sujato">AN 6.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified greed, hatred, and delusion as the three primary <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato"><b>sources for the origination of kamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato">AN 6.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, it is impossible for non-greed to arise from greed, but instead <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato"><b>greed gives rise to greed</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato">AN 6.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that actions born of greed, hatred, and delusion are the cause for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato"><b>appearance of hell</b></a>, the animal realm, and the sphere of afflicted spirits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato">AN 6.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that kamma rooted in greed, hatred, or delusion can never lead to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato"><b>happy destination</b></a>, such as birth as a human or a deva? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato">AN 6.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato"><b>non-greed, non-hatred, and non-delusion</b></a> as the three positive sources for the origination of kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato">AN 6.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that non-greed only gives rise to further <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato"><b>non-greed</b></a>, and never to greed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato">AN 6.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that actions performed without greed, hatred, or delusion lead to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato"><b>appearance of devas and humans</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato">AN 6.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that actions born of non-greed, non-hatred, and non-delusion will never result in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato"><b>birth in hell</b></a> or other miserable destinations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato">AN 6.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that hatred does not produce non-hatred, but rather <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato"><b>hatred gives rise to hatred</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato">AN 6.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as non-hatred produces more non-hatred, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato"><b>non-delusion gives rise to non-delusion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.39/en/sujato">AN 6.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by the Venerable Kimila why the Good Dhamma does not last long after his passing, the Buddha explained it is because followers live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato"><b>without respect for the Teacher</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato">AN 6.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato"><b>disrespect toward the Dhamma</b></a> by monks, nuns, and lay followers as a key reason why the teaching fails to endure for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato">AN 6.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the Good Dhamma would decline if the community lived with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato"><b>disrespect toward the Sangha</b></a> after his final Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato">AN 6.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato"><b>without respect for the training</b></a> is one of the specific causes for the Good Dhamma not remaining for a long period? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato">AN 6.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato"><b>disrespect toward heedfulness</b></a> (appamāda) among followers causes the Good Dhamma to fade away quickly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato">AN 6.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the Good Dhamma will not last long if the community lives with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato"><b>disrespect toward hospitality</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato">AN 6.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha assured Kimila that the Good Dhamma will remain for a long time if followers live with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato"><b>respect toward the Teacher</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato">AN 6.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato"><b>respect toward the training</b></a> is a vital factor for the longevity of his teachings after he is gone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato">AN 6.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Good Dhamma is preserved for a long time when the four assemblies live with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato"><b>respect toward heedfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato">AN 6.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato"><b>respect toward hospitality</b></a> (paṭisanthāra) is one of the six conditions that ensure the Good Dhamma's long duration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.40/en/sujato">AN 6.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>Icchānaṅgala jungle thicket</b></a>, a large group of brahmin householders arrived with abundant food and created a great deal of noise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the loud noise of a crowd of householders waiting to offer him food to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>fishermen hauling in a catch</b></a> of fish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha expressed a strong distaste for public recognition, stating, "May I not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>come together with fame</b></a>, and may fame not come to me"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four types of superior happiness that he could attain at will: the bliss of renunciation, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>bliss of seclusion</b></a>, the bliss of peace, and the bliss of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>bliss of dung</b></a> (mīḷhasukha) to describe the pleasure one derives from gains, honor, and praise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he was not pleased with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>village-dwelling monk</b></a> even if that monk was concentrated, because he foresaw that monastery workers or novices would eventually disturb that monk's meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was pleased with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>forest-dwelling monk who is nodding</b></a> in drowsiness, because he believed the monk would eventually dispel that fatigue and focus on the "perception of the forest"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha approved of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>unsettled forest-dwelling monk</b></a> who is not yet concentrated, because the monk has the potential to either attain concentration or protect the concentration they have already gained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha expressed great satisfaction with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>concentrated forest-dwelling monk</b></a>, as such a person is in a position to liberate their unliberated mind or protect a mind already liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha criticized monks who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>neglect seclusion</b></a> and remote forest lodgings in order to live near towns and villages for the sake of acquiring robes, almsfood, and lodgings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha felt pleased with a forest-dwelling monk who receives many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>requisites like robes and food</b></a> but chooses to brush those gains aside to remain in the solitude of the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha found a deep <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>sense of ease</b></a> when walking alone on a highway when there was no one traveling in front of him or behind him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that one of the benefits of traveling alone is the freedom to answer <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>calls of nature</b></a> without any disturbance or embarrassment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Nāgita, acting as the Buddha’s attendant, urged the Buddha to accept the food from the householders because people from all over would follow him like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>water flowing downhill</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Nāgita attributed the massive public following of the Buddha to the Buddha’s own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>virtue and wisdom</b></a> (sīlapaññāṇa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that only someone who cannot easily obtain the bliss of renunciation and peace would indulge in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>bliss of lethargy</b></a> (middhasukha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha preferred to stay away from monks who lived in the vicinity of villages because of the high likelihood that their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>concentration would be disturbed</b></a> by visitors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a monk, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>seclusion in the forest</b></a> is a superior environment for spiritual progress compared to dwelling near a village? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the pleasure derived from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>gains and honor</b></a> is inferior and should be discarded in favor of the higher blisses associated with the path to enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato"><b>unafflicted</b></a> as a result of staying in remote forest lodgings and maintaining a heart of letting go? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.42/en/sujato">AN 6.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was drying himself after washing at the East Gatehouse, King Pasenadi’s famous <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>white elephant named Seto</b></a> passed by accompanied by the sound of music? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the public saw King Pasenadi's elephant, the Buddha heard them praising the animal as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>handsome and majestic</b></a> "nāga"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Venerable Udāyī that people commonly use the term "nāga" to describe not just large elephants, but also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>large horses, bulls, and snakes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>large tree or a human</b></a> can be referred to as a "nāga" if they possess a great and imposing physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha provided a spiritual definition of a "nāga" as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>does no wrong</b></a> by body, speech, or mind in the entire world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Udāyī described the Buddha as a "Nāga" who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>self-tamed and concentrated</b></a>, delighting in the internal peace of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Udāyī, both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>gods and humans worship</b></a> the Buddha because he is a sage who has reached the far shore of all things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Udāyī compared the Buddha to a "Nāga" who has left the "forest" of defilements behind and has arrived at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's spiritual qualities were described by Udāyī using the metaphor of an elephant, where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>gentleness and non-violence</b></a> serve as the "Nāga’s" feet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the verses recited by Venerable Udāyī, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>austerity and the holy life</b></a> are described as the other two legs of the great spiritual "Nāga"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Udāyī depicted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>faith as the trunk</b></a> and equanimity as the white tusks of the enlightened "Nāga"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and wisdom</b></a> were likened by the Venerable Udāyī to the neck and the head of the spiritual "Nāga"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Udāyī described the spiritual "Nāga" as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>concentrated in all postures</b></a>, whether walking, standing, sitting, or lying down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was said by Venerable Udāyī to consume only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>blameless things</b></a> and to strictly avoid the accumulation of food or clothing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Udāyī taught that the spiritual "Nāga" travels <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>without longing or looking back</b></a>, having cut all fetters and bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is compared by the Venerable Udāyī to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>lotus in the water</b></a>, because although he lives in the world, he remains completely undefiled by it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>activities of the mind are stilled</b></a>, one is said to be "extinguished," similar to a fire that goes out when its fuel is exhausted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Udāyī described the final state of the spiritual "Nāga" as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>devoid of lust, hatred, and delusion</b></a>, eventually reaching Parinibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the metaphor of the "Nāga" is a teaching provided by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato"><b>Wise for the wise</b></a>, so they may truly understand the nature of an enlightened being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.43/en/sujato">AN 6.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly told his monks <b>not to fear merit</b>, explaining that the word "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato"><b>merit</b></a>" (puñña) is actually a synonym for happiness and results in long-lasting, pleasing, and charming experiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato">AN 7.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how he personally developed a mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> (metta) for seven years, and as a result of that practice, he did not return to this human world for seven aeons of world-contraction and world-expansion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato">AN 7.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when the physical world contracts, a person who has highly cultivated loving-kindness can be reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato"><b>Ābhassara realm</b></a> (the Realm of Streaming Radiance), a plane of existence they inhabit while the world below is undergoing destruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato">AN 7.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared that due to his past practice of loving-kindness, he was reborn in an empty Brahma-mansion and held the position of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato"><b>Mahā Brahmā</b></a> seven times, acting as the supreme vanquisher who is all-seeing and wields total power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato">AN 7.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed he had held the title and office of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato"><b>Sakka</b></a>, the lord of the devas, thirty-six different times as a direct consequence of the merit he had accumulated in previous lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato">AN 7.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described having been a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato"><b>Wheel-Turning Monarch</b></a> (cakkavattī) hundreds of times—a righteous king of Dhamma who conquered the four quarters of the earth and brought stability to the countryside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato">AN 7.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a Wheel-Turning Monarch, the Buddha possessed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato"><b>seven treasures</b></a>: the wheel, the elephant, the horse, the jewel, the woman, the householder, and the counselor treasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato">AN 7.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a righteous Wheel-Turning Monarch is able to conquer and govern the entire earth <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato"><b>without stick or sword</b></a>, using only the principles of Dhamma rather than violence or coercion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato">AN 7.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the fruit of wholesome deeds and merit can lead to rebirth in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato"><b>affluent families</b></a> characterized by great wealth, immense possessions, and abundant resources? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato">AN 7.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that anyone who loves themselves and desires to achieve greatness should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato"><b>revere the Good Dhamma</b></a>, keeping the teachings and instructions of the Buddhas firmly in mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.62/en/sujato">AN 7.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the loud noise coming from the house of the householder Anāthapiṇḍika to the sound of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>fishermen</b></a> hauling in a catch of fish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Anāthapiṇḍika explained to the Buddha that the disturbance in his home was caused by his daughter-in-law Sujātā, who showed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>no respect</b></a> to her husband, her in-laws, or even the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>seven types</b></a> of wives that a man may have: those who are like slayers, thieves, mistresses, mothers, sisters, friends, and handmaids? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>slayer-like wife</b></a> as one who is ill-willed, lusts after other men, and is intent on her husband's death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>thief-like wife</b></a> is one who seeks to misappropriate even a small portion of the wealth her husband earns through his trade, craft, or farming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>mistress-like wife</b></a> as one who is lazy, gluttonous, harsh, and one who dominates her energetic, hardworking husband? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>mother-like wife</b></a> as one who is always compassionate and protects her husband and his wealth just as a mother would protect her only son? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>sister-like wife</b></a> is one who is respectful toward her husband, modest, and stays under his influence just like a younger sister toward her elder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>friend-like wife</b></a> as a virtuous woman who rejoices at seeing her husband just as a friend would when meeting a companion not seen for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>handmaid-like wife</b></a> as one who is calm, without anger, and who patiently endures her husband's treatment without a trace of ill-will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that wives who act like slayers, thieves, or mistresses are immoral and harsh, and upon death, they are reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that wives who act like mothers, sisters, friends, or handmaids will be reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>good destination</b></a> in the heavenly world because they are virtuous and self-controlled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Sujātā initially heard the list of seven types of wives, she asked the Buddha to explain them in detail because she did not understand his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>brief teaching</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted a slayer-like wife "despises her husband" and is "bent on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>murder</b></a>" because she is attached to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>mistress-like wife</b></a> is a "fierce woman of rough words" who overcomes her husband's efforts with her aggressive attitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>sister-like wife</b></a> is characterized by "moral dread" (hiri) and is compliant with her husband's wishes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>friend-like wife</b></a> is "high-born" (meaning of good character) and is devoted to her husband? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>handmaid-like wife</b></a> as being "pure-hearted," compliant, and one who obeys her husband's will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after listening to the Buddha's explanation, Sujātā declared that from that moment forward, she would be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato"><b>handmaid-like wife</b></a> to her husband? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.63/en/sujato">AN 7.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if an angry person is well-bathed, perfumed, and dressed in white clothes, they still possess an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>ugly appearance</b></a> because they are overwhelmed by their own rage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an angry person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>sleeps poorly</b></a> and in misery, even if they lie on a luxurious couch covered with fleecy covers, rugs, and soft cushions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an angry person often <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>misinterprets benefit</b></a>, mistaking what is actually harmful for what is beneficial, and grasping what is beneficial as if it were harmful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that an angry person may lose their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>rightfully earned wealth</b></a>, which was acquired through energetic effort and the sweat of their brow, because of their loss of self-control? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that whatever <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>reputation</b></a> or fame a person has gained through diligence and heedfulness will fall away if they are frequently overcome by anger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that an angry person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>friends and relatives</b></a> will eventually avoid and stay far away from them because they find the person's hostile temperament unbearable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person overcome by anger conducts themselves poorly in body, speech, and mind, which eventually leads to rebirth in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a> after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described anger as a form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>inner fear</b></a> born from within the mind, a danger that most people fail to recognize until it is too late? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that when a person is blinded by anger, they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>do not see the Dhamma</b></a> and lose the ability to understand what is truly for their own good? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that whenever anger triumphs over a person, it creates a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>blind darkness</b></a> in the mind, obscuring all moral clarity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the aftermath of a fit of rage to being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>burned by fire</b></a>, as the person suffers and feels intense regret once their anger has finally subsided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person overwhelmed by anger loses both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>moral shame</b></a> (hiri) and moral dread (ottappa), showing no respect in their speech or behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that in a state of extreme anger, a person might even go so far as to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>kill their father</b></a> or mother, destroying the very people who gave them life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that some people, driven to temporary insanity by anger, may commit suicide by using a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>sword or poison</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized anger as a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>snare of death</b></a>" that hides in the "cave" of the heart, and advised that it must be cut off through the power of self-restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person should cut off the unwholesome root of anger by applying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>wisdom and energy</b></a> coupled with a clear understanding of the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that those who have completely abandoned anger, greed, and longing, and who have tamed themselves, finally reach <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed seven specific things that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>enemy wishes</b></a> to happen to their enemy—such as bad looks, pain, and loss—all of which naturally happen to a person who is habitually angry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the manifestation of anger in a human to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>smoke and fire</b></a>, indicating that anger cannot be hidden and will always show its destructive nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that although all beings hold themselves as most dear, an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>angry person</b></a> may actually destroy their own life and well-being while blinded by a grudge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said for one who is overcome by anger, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>no island</b></a> or refuge to protect them from the painful consequences of their unwholesome state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an angry person suffers for a long time because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome mental states</b></a> they grasp while angry lead to a long-term decline in their welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.64/en/sujato">AN 7.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato"><b>moral shame and moral dread</b></a> (hirī and ottappa) are absent, the supporting condition for the restraint of the sense faculties is destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato">AN 7.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is deficient in the restraint of the sense faculties lacks the vital supporting condition for the fulfillment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato">AN 7.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one who is deficient in virtue, the supporting condition for achieving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> (sammāsamādhi) is effectively destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato">AN 7.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that without the attainment of right concentration, a person lacks the essential foundation for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of things as they really are</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato">AN 7.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when knowledge and vision of things as they really are is absent, the supporting condition for the development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato"><b>disenchantment and dispassion</b></a> is lost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato">AN 7.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if disenchantment and dispassion are missing, a practitioner lacks the proximate cause for the final attainment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato">AN 7.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person lacking moral shame and moral dread to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato"><b>tree with deficient branches and foliage</b></a>, noting that just as such a tree’s bark, softwood, and heartwood will not reach fullness, so too will that person's spiritual progress fail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato">AN 7.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato"><b>flourishing tree</b></a> with abundant branches and foliage to illustrate how the presence of moral shame and moral dread allows for the complete development of the bark, softwood, and heartwood of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato">AN 7.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a>, the supporting condition for achieving right concentration is fully present and fulfilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato">AN 7.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato"><b>moral shame and moral dread</b></a> are fully present, they act as the primary supporting condition that naturally leads to sense restraint and, eventually, the knowledge and vision of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.65/en/sujato">AN 7.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the layman Dīghajāṇu asked for teachings that lead to well-being for those who live with children and enjoy luxuries, the Buddha explained four specific qualities that lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>welfare and happiness</b></a> in the present life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment of persistent effort</b></a> as being skillful and diligent in whatever profession a person uses to make a living, whether it be farming, trade, or government service? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who possesses persistent effort is someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>not lazy</b></a> and is endowed with the ability to manage, organize, and carry out their work effectively? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment of protection</b></a> involves taking care to safeguard the wealth that one has earned through righteous means and hard work? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described legitimate wealth as that which is gained through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>strength of one's arms</b></a> and the sweat of one’s brow, emphasizing the importance of honest and vigorous labor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised householders to protect their earnings so that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>kings, thieves, fire</b></a>, water, or hateful heirs would not take it away or destroy it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> as associating with people in one's community who are established in faith, virtue, generosity, and wisdom, regardless of whether they are young or old? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged people to emulate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment of faith</b></a> and other positive qualities they see in their good friends through conversation and shared activities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the importance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>balanced living</b></a>, which means knowing one's income and expenses so that one lives neither too extravagantly nor too stingily? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>goldsmith or his apprentice</b></a> holding a scale to illustrate how a person should understand when their wealth is increasing or decreasing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a person with a small income lives extravagantly, people will mock them as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>"wood-apple eater"</b></a> who consumes their resources wastefully? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that if a person with a large income lives like a pauper, people will criticize them, saying they will die a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>"starving death"</b></a> despite their riches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>four drains</b></a> on accumulated wealth: being a womanizer, being a drunkard, being a gambler, and having evil friends? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who indulges in drinking and gambling to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>great reservoir</b></a> where the inlets are blocked and the drains are opened, causing the water to disappear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for wealth to grow, one must avoid the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>four outlets</b></a> of dissipation, such as drinking and gambling, while maintaining good friendships? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that besides present welfare, the Buddha also taught four qualities that lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>future welfare</b></a> and happiness in the lives to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the accomplishment of faith as placing trust in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata’s enlightenment</b></a> and acknowledging his role as the teacher of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment of virtue</b></a> for a layperson consists of abstaining from killing, stealing, sexual misconduct, false speech, and intoxicants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment of generosity</b></a> as living at home with a mind free from stinginess, being open-handed and delighting in giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment of wisdom</b></a> involves possessing the insight that discerns the arising and falling away of things, which leads to the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.54/en/sujato">AN 8.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>eight types of persons</b></a> existing in the world who are categorized by how they handle the desire for gain and their reactions to success or failure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who lives in seclusion but still develops a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>desire for gain</b></a>, which serves as the starting point for various spiritual states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who exerts effort to obtain gain but fails to get it and subsequently falls into sorrow and lamentation is considered to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>fallen away</b></a> from the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if a monk successfully obtains gain through his efforts, he has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>fallen away from the True Dhamma</b></a> if that gain causes him to become intoxicated, negligent, and careless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a monk who does not even exert effort to get gain, yet still sorrows and becomes confused when no gain comes his way, is one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>fallen away from the True Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who obtains gain without effort but then becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>intoxicated and negligent</b></a> because of that gain is spiritually declining? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the monk who exerts effort for gain but, upon failing to receive it, does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>not sorrow or lament</b></a>, thereby remaining firmly established in the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who strives for and receives gain is spiritually secure if he does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>not become intoxicated</b></a> or negligent because of his success? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a type of person who does not strive for gain and receives none, yet remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>unshaken by sorrow</b></a> or confusion, thus staying true to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the highest state for a monk receiving unexpected gain as being one where he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>does not fall into negligence</b></a> or vanity, regardless of how the gain was acquired? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>True Dhamma</b></a> (Saddhamma) to describe the spiritual path from which a monk can either fall away or remain steadfast depending on his reaction to worldly gains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically addressed monks who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>living in seclusion</b></a> (pavivittassa), highlighting that physical isolation does not automatically remove the internal desire for material support? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of a monk who "beats his breast" and becomes "muddled" as a sign of having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>fallen away</b></a> from spiritual progress due to an obsession with lack of gain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>persistent effort</b></a> in the context of this sutta as "striving, exerting, and struggling" (uṭṭhahati ghaṭati vāyamati) for the sake of obtaining requisites? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted the state of being "intoxicated" (<i>madī</i>) with the state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>not intoxicated</b></a> (<i>na ca madī</i>) to show how a monk should ideally relate to the things he receives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>negligence</b></a> (pamāda) is a primary reason a person falls away from the True Dhamma when they are successful in obtaining what they want? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the lack of sorrow (<i>na ca socī</i>) and the lack of lamentation (<i>na ca paridevī</i>) are indicators that a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>not falling away</b></a> from their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized monks based on whether they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>independent in their livelihood</b></a> (nirāyattavuttino) while still facing the internal challenge of desiring gains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the phrase <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato"><b>not muddled</b></a> (na sammohaṃ āpajjati) to describe the mental clarity of a monk who remains composed even when his efforts to gain resources fail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.61/en/sujato">AN 8.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when an unnamed monk asked the Buddha for a brief teaching so he could practice in solitude, the Buddha initially remarked that some people merely ask for teachings and then continue to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>follow the teacher around</b></a> without actually putting the Dhamma into practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the foundation of spiritual practice is to ensure one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>internal mind</b></a> is steady and well-composed, so that unwholesome states do not overwhelm it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that once the mind is steady, a practitioner should develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> deliverance of mind until it is "frequently practiced, made one’s vehicle, and well-undertaken"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that meditation on loving-kindness should be practiced in various stages, including levels with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>initial and sustained thought</b></a> and levels without them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a specific state of concentration that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>without initial thought</b></a> but contains "only a measure of sustained thought" as part of the training for a monk in seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that concentration can be refined to a point where it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>without rapture</b></a>, yet remains accompanied by pleasure or equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that after mastering loving-kindness, a practitioner should similarly develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> deliverance of mind through all the various levels of concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>altruistic joy</b></a> deliverance of mind should be "made one’s foundation" and "consistently practiced" to ensure the mind is fully liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> as the final of the four "social emotions" (divine abodes) that must be developed into a "deliverance of mind" for spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that after mastering the four divine abodes, a monk should then practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>contemplating the body</b></a> as a body, remaining "ardent, clearly comprehending, and mindful"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the practice of mindfulness requires one to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>remove greed and distress</b></a> with reference to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that the establishment of mindfulness regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>feelings as feelings</b></a> should also be practiced alongside seven different variations of concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who contemplates the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>mind as mind</b></a> while being "ardent, clearly comprehending, and mindful" is following the path to enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should dwell contemplating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>phenomena as phenomena</b></a> (dhammānupassī) as the final stage of the four establishments of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that when a monk’s concentration is "well-developed," he will find that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>wherever he goes</b></a>, he will dwell in ease and comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a well-trained monk will find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>ease in all postures</b></a>, whether standing, sitting, or lying down, due to the power of their meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk who received this "brief teaching" from the Buddha subsequently lived <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>solitary and diligent</b></a> until he achieved the goal of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s instruction was intended to lead a "son of a clan" to realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>supreme goal</b></a> of the holy life in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk who followed the Buddha’s brief teaching eventually declared, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>Birth is destroyed</b></a>, the holy life has been lived, what had to be done has been done"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s advice on concentration includes a state described as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>accompanied by equanimity</b></a> (<i>upekkhāsahagataṃ</i>), which can be applied to both the divine abodes and mindfulness practices? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato"><b>well-undertaken</b></a> (<i>susamāraddhā</i>) to describe the level of proficiency a monk should have with the deliverances of mind like compassion and equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.63/en/sujato">AN 8.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the town of Vesālī and its various local landmarks, describing the Udena, Gotamaka, Sattamba, Bahuputtaka, Sārandada, and Cāpāla shrines as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>delightful</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Ānanda that any person who has fully developed and mastered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>four bases of psychic power</b></a> could, if they so desired, remain alive for an entire aeon or the remainder of one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly told Ānanda that he himself had practiced and perfected the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>four bases of psychic power</b></a>, making it possible for him to live for an aeon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the Buddha giving a "broad hint" about his ability to extend his life for an aeon, Ānanda failed to ask him to stay because his mind was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>possessed by Mara</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha repeated his statement about being able to live for an aeon to Ānanda <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>three times</b></a>, but Ānanda did not perceive the hint or make the request for the Buddha to remain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after Ānanda left to sit under a nearby tree, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>Mara the Evil One</b></a> approached the Buddha and urged him to attain final Nibbāna immediately? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha had previously declared to Mara that he would not pass away until his monks and nuns were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>competent and well-trained</b></a> disciples of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha required his followers to be capable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>rightfully refuting</b></a> opposing doctrines with the help of the Dhamma before he would consider his mission complete? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha vowed he would only attain final Nibbāna once the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> was successful, prosperous, widespread, and well-proclaimed among both devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed Mara at the Cāpāla Shrine that he would officially attain final Nibbāna in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>three months</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha, while mindful and clearly comprehending, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>relinquished the life force</b></a>, a massive and terrifying earthquake occurred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recited a verse about letting go of the "life-process," comparing a sage's renunciation to a warrior breaking his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>own coat of mail</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Ānanda witnessed a great earthquake and asked for its cause, the Buddha explained that there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>eight causes</b></a> and conditions for such an event? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the first cause of an earthquake is when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>mighty winds</b></a> shake the water that supports the earth, causing the earth itself to tremble? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that an ascetic or a deity with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>psychic power</b></a> can cause the earth to shake by mentally focusing on a boundless perception of the water element? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the earth shakes when a Bodhisatta, mindful and clearly comprehending, descends from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>Tusita heaven</b></a> into his mother’s womb? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>birth of a Bodhisatta</b></a>, as he leaves his mother’s womb mindful and clearly comprehending, as the fourth reason for a great earthquake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the earth trembles when a Tathāgata attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>unexcelled supreme enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>setting in motion</b></a> of the wheel of Dhamma by a Tathāgata as the sixth major cause for a massive earthquake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the seventh cause of a great earthquake is when a Tathāgata, mindful and clearly comprehending, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>relinquishes his life force</b></a> (āyusaṅkhāra)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>final Nibbāna</b></a> of a Tathāgata in the Nibbāna element without residue as the eighth and final cause of a great earthquake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the earthquake following his relinquishment of the life force as being accompanied by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>thundering of the heavens</b></a> (devadundubhiyo)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna element without residue</b></a> (anupādisesāya nibbānadhātuyā) to describe the state a Tathāgata enters upon his final passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a person with psychic power can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato"><b>shiver and shake</b></a> the entire earth through mental mastery and a developed perception of the elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.70/en/sujato">AN 8.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an accuser must first reflect on whether they possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>purified bodily conduct</b></a>—conduct that is flawless and unblemished—before they attempt to reprove another person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, if a person tries to accuse another while their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>verbal conduct</b></a> is not purified, their peers might rightfully tell them to go train themselves in speech first? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated an accuser should establish a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>mind of goodwill</b></a> toward their fellow practitioners, ensuring they are free from resentment before pointing out faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one who wishes to reprove another should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>learned in the Dhamma</b></a>, having thoroughly memorized and understood the teachings that are good in the beginning, middle, and end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted the importance of an accuser having both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>Patimokkha codes</b></a> thoroughly learned and well-analyzed so they can properly answer questions about monastic discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when reproving another, one must ensure they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>speak at the right time</b></a> rather than at an improper or inopportune time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that speech used for reproof must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>speech that is true</b></a> and based on facts, rather than being false or inaccurate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that even when correcting or accusing someone, a person should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>speak gently</b></a> rather than using harsh or abrasive language? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a reproof should always be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>beneficial speech</b></a>, intended to help the other person rather than being pointless or harmful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an accuser who lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>inner reflection</b></a> on their own conduct may lose credibility and face criticism from the very people they are trying to correct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>without resentment</b></a> toward his fellow practitioners if he intends to point out their faults effectively? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma teachings an accuser knows should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>well-penetrated by view</b></a>, meaning they have a deep and correct theoretical understanding of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that an accuser who hasn't mastered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>discipline of Vinaya</b></a> will fail to explain where a specific rule was taught when asked by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha required an accuser to check if they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>unblemished conduct</b></a> so that their peer cannot say, "Please, venerable, train yourself in bodily discipline first"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identifies <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>five internal qualities</b></a>—such as pure conduct and being learned—that a person must reflect upon within themselves before they are fit to accuse another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identifies <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>five qualities to establish</b></a> within one's mind—such as speaking truthfully and gently—at the actual moment of reproving someone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that reproof should be done with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>loving heart</b></a> rather than with an inner state of hatred or anger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ideal accuser as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>collector of teachings</b></a>, someone who has accumulated and retained the instructions that lead to a pure spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said an accuser's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato"><b>personal behavior</b></a> must be beyond reproach, meaning it is "without a hole" or "without a crack," to maintain authority when correcting others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.44/en/sujato">AN 10.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato"><b>ten dangers</b></a> that a monk faces when he enters the inner apartments of a king’s palace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato">AN 10.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a monk and a queen <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato"><b>exchange a smile</b></a> while the king is present, the king may become suspicious that they have already committed or will commit a transgression? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato">AN 10.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a woman in the royal palace becomes pregnant and the busy king forgets his own intimacy with her, he might suspect the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato"><b>monk is responsible</b></a> simply because the monk is the only other visitor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato">AN 10.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, if a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato"><b>valuable jewel</b></a> or treasure disappears within the king’s inner apartments, the monk may be unjustly suspected of the theft since he is the only outsider allowed to enter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato">AN 10.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato"><b>secret plans</b></a> or confidential state matters from within the palace are leaked to the public, the monk is often the first person suspected of disclosing them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato">AN 10.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out the danger of a monk being blamed if scandalous <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato"><b>sexual desires</b></a> arise between a father and son within the king's royal household? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato">AN 10.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that when a king promotes someone of low status to a high position, those who are displeased with the choice may blame the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato"><b>monk’s influence</b></a> for the king's decision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato">AN 10.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a king demotes an official of high standing to a lower status, the monk may be accused of being the one who convinced the king to make that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato"><b>unfavorable change</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato">AN 10.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a king dispatches his army at an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato"><b>improper time</b></a>, those who are unhappy with the military action may claim the king was influenced by the monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato">AN 10.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a king dispatches his army at the right time but then suddenly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato"><b>aborts the mission</b></a> mid-way, the monk is often suspected of having caused the king's sudden change of heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato">AN 10.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the presence of sensory distractions, such as the noise of royal elephants and horses or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato"><b>alluring sights and sounds</b></a>, as a major danger for a monk because they are unsuitable for his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato">AN 10.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted how a monk’s status as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato"><b>frequent visitor</b></a> to a king's inner apartments makes him a convenient scapegoat whenever something goes wrong in the royal court? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato">AN 10.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered the environment of a king’s harem to be full of objects and experiences that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato"><b>unsuitable for a monk</b></a> trying to maintain his spiritual discipline and focus? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.45/en/sujato">AN 10.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned the Sakyan lay followers about whether they regularly observed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>eight-factor Uposatha</b></a>, the traditional Buddhist day of intense practice and ethical observance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha admonished his followers for only observing the Uposatha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>sometimes</b></a>, telling them that such inconsistency was a significant loss for their spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reminded the Sakyans that consistent spiritual practice is vital because human life is precarious and inherently filled with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>sorrow and the fear of death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy of a skillful and industrious man who might earn <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>half a kahāpaṇa</b></a> or more in a single day through his hard work to compare worldly effort with spiritual effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even if a man earned a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>hundred kahāpaṇas</b></a> every single day and lived for a hundred years, his accumulated wealth would still be insufficient for true peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that no matter how much material wealth a person earns—even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>thousand kahāpaṇas</b></a> daily—that wealth cannot buy them even half a day of exclusive happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by the Buddha about the nature of worldly desires, the Sakyans admitted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> are impermanent, hollow, and deceptive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a disciple who practices diligently for ten years as instructed can experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>exclusive happiness</b></a> (ekantasukhappaṭisaṃvedī) for a hundred thousand years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a practitioner who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>heedful and ardent</b></a> in their training can reach high spiritual attainments, such as becoming a non-returner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even just <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>one year</b></a> of resolute practice following his instructions can lead a person to become a once-returner or a non-returner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that a disciple who practices with dedication for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>ten months</b></a> could attain spiritual fruits that provide happiness for many lifetimes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>half a month</b></a> of diligent practice can be enough for a person to reach the state of a stream-enterer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarkably taught that practicing exactly as instructed for just <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>one day and night</b></a> can result in exclusive happiness for a hundred years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified becoming a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>stream-enterer</b></a> (sotāpanno) as a definite spiritual fruit for those who follow his training, even for a short period? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>once-returner</b></a> (sakadāgāmī) as a disciple who has made such progress that they will return to this world only one more time before final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>non-returner</b></a> (anāgāmī) as a high level of spiritual realization where the disciple is no longer subject to rebirth in the human realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>badly gained</b></a> (dulladdhaṃ) to describe the missed opportunity of those who fail to observe the Uposatha despite having access to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that worldly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> are "natured to be false" and "hollow," contrasting them with the genuine happiness found in the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the goal of his practice as reaching a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato"><b>exclusive happiness</b></a>, which is vastly superior to any joy derived from the accumulation of wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.46/en/sujato">AN 10.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato"><b>greed</b></a> (lobha) as a primary cause and condition that leads to the performance and persistence of evil actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato">AN 10.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> (dosa) serves as a fundamental cause and condition for the commission of evil deeds and the continuation of unwholesome behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato">AN 10.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> (moha) is a root cause and condition responsible for the arising and persistence of evil actions in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato">AN 10.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato"><b>improper attention</b></a> (ayoniso manasikāra) is a significant factor that causes a person to perform and continue evil kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato">AN 10.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato"><b>wrongly directed mind</b></a> (micchāpaṇihitaṃ cittaṃ) is a primary cause and condition for the performance and persistence of evil actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato">AN 10.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato"><b>non-greed</b></a> (alobha) as a root cause and condition that leads to the performance and persistence of wholesome actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato">AN 10.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato"><b>non-hatred</b></a> (adosa) is a fundamental cause and condition for a person to engage in and develop wholesome kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato">AN 10.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato"><b>non-delusion</b></a> (amoha) acts as an essential cause and condition for the performance and continuation of wholesome deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato">AN 10.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> (yoniso manasikāra) as a vital cause and condition that supports the performance and persistence of wholesome actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.47/en/sujato">AN 10.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is deposited in hell not only for destroying life themselves, but also for inciting others to do so and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato"><b>approving of killing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato">AN 10.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who refrains from taking what is not given, encourages others to refrain, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato"><b>approves of abstaining from theft</b></a> is among those destined for rebirth in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato">AN 10.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that engaging in sexual misconduct, encouraging others to do the same, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato"><b>approving of sexual misconduct</b></a> are three of the thirty qualities that lead to being cast into hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato">AN 10.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who speaks falsehood, urges others to lie, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato"><b>approves of false speech</b></a> possesses qualities that lead to a destination in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato">AN 10.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that refraining from divisive speech, encouraging others to abstain from it, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato"><b>approving of abstaining from divisive speech</b></a> are behaviors that lead to a heavenly rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato">AN 10.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that using harsh speech, inciting others to speak harshly, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato"><b>approving of harsh speech</b></a> are specific factors that contribute to one being reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato">AN 10.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified that engaging in idle chatter, encouraging others in such talk, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato"><b>approving of idle chatter</b></a> are part of a set of thirty unwholesome qualities that lead to a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato">AN 10.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being free from covetousness, encouraging others to be non-covetous, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato"><b>approving of non-covetousness</b></a> are qualities that help lead a person toward heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato">AN 10.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, maintaining a mind of ill will, inciting ill will in others, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato"><b>approving of ill will</b></a> are among the thirty things that result in one being deposited in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato">AN 10.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that holding wrong view, encouraging others to adopt wrong views, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato"><b>approving of wrong view</b></a> are serious obstacles that lead to being reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato">AN 10.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed exactly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato"><b>thirty qualities</b></a>—consisting of personal action, encouragement of others, and approval—that determine whether a person is reborn in hell or heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato">AN 10.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that possessing right view, encouraging others to establish right view, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato"><b>approving of right view</b></a> are essential components of the thirty wholesome qualities leading to heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato">AN 10.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that your destination after death depends not just on your own deeds, but also on whether you <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato"><b>approve of wholesome behavior</b></a> or its opposite in others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.223/en/sujato">AN 10.223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is reborn in hell if they possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>forty qualities</b></a> related to ten types of unwholesome behavior, including their own actions, encouraging others, approving of the acts, and praising them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person is cast into hell for killing living beings, inciting others to do so, approving of the act, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of killing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who takes what is not given, encourages others to steal, approves of theft, and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of stealing</b></a> is destined for a miserable rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that engaging in sexual misconduct, urging others to do so, approving of it, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of sexual misconduct</b></a> are qualities that lead to hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who speaks falsehood, incites others to lie, approves of lying, and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of false speech</b></a> possesses unwholesome qualities that lead to hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of divisive speech</b></a>—along side using such speech, encouraging others to use it, and approving of it—as a factor that leads to being deposited in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that using harsh speech, inciting others to use it, approving of it, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of harsh speech</b></a> are behaviors that result in a person being reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that indulging in idle chatter, encouraging others to do the same, approving of it, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of idle chatter</b></a> are four of the forty qualities that lead to a destination in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being covetous, inciting covetousness in others, approving of it, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of covetousness</b></a> are specific unwholesome factors that lead to hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that having a mind of ill will, encouraging ill will in others, approving of it, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of ill will</b></a> lead to a person being cast into hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that holding wrong view, inciting others to adopt it, approving of it, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of wrong view</b></a> are the final set of unwholesome qualities leading to hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that refraining from killing, encouraging others to abstain from it, approving of such abstinence, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of abstaining from killing</b></a> are qualities that lead to heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that abstaining from theft, encouraging others to refrain from it, approving of the abstinence, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of abstaining from stealing</b></a> contribute to a heavenly rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that refraining from sexual misconduct, encouraging others to refrain, approving of the abstinence, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of abstaining from sexual misconduct</b></a> are wholesome qualities leading to heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who refrains from false speech, encourages others to speak truth, approves of the abstinence, and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of abstaining from false speech</b></a> is deposited in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that refraining from divisive speech, encouraging others to abstain from it, approving of the abstinence, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of abstaining from divisive speech</b></a> are behaviors that lead to rebirth in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of abstaining from harsh speech</b></a>, along with personal abstinence and the encouragement of others, as a wholesome quality that leads to heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that refraining from idle chatter, inciting others to refrain, approving of the abstinence, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of abstaining from idle chatter</b></a> lead to a heavenly destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being non-covetous, encouraging others to be non-covetous, approving of non-covetousness, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of non-covetousness</b></a> are qualities that help a person reach heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that holding right view, encouraging others to do so, approving of it, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise of right view</b></a> are the final components of the forty wholesome qualities that lead to rebirth in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.224/en/sujato">AN 10.224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.237/en/sujato"><b>ten things to be developed</b></a> for the direct knowledge of lust, which include various mental perceptions ranging from unattractiveness to cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.237/en/sujato">AN 10.237</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.237/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-delight in the whole world</b></a> as one of the ten specific qualities a practitioner must cultivate to gain direct knowledge of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.237/en/sujato">AN 10.237</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for the direct knowledge of lust, one should cultivate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.237/en/sujato"><b>perception of suffering in what is impermanent</b></a> as well as the perception of non-self in what is suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.237/en/sujato">AN 10.237</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed the Venerable Ānanda that the primary purpose and benefit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato"><b>wholesome virtuous behavior</b></a> is the attainment of the lack of remorse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato">AN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato"><b>lack of remorse</b></a> serves the specific purpose of leading a practitioner toward the experience of joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato">AN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the experience of joy naturally leads to rapture, and the ultimate purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato"><b>rapture</b></a> is to bring about tranquility of both the body and the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato">AN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that tranquility leads to happiness, and the purpose and benefit of that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a> is the development of concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato">AN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the purpose of concentration is to enable a practitioner to achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of things as they really are</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato">AN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person gains knowledge and vision of things as they really are, it leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato"><b>revulsion</b></a>, which in turn leads to the fading away of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato">AN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the fading away of lust (dispassion) has the final purpose of leading a person to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato">AN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized to Ānanda that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato"><b>wholesome virtuous behavior</b></a> acts as a foundation that leads step-by-step toward the highest summit of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.1/en/sujato">AN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.237/en/sujato"><b>perception of repulsiveness in food</b></a> alongside the perception of death as essential practices for gaining direct knowledge of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.237/en/sujato">AN 10.237</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that cultivating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.237/en/sujato"><b>perception of cessation</b></a> is one of the ten critical mental developments required for the direct knowledge of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.237/en/sujato">AN 10.237</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.237/en/sujato"><b>perception of abandonment</b></a> as a vital mental quality to be developed for overcoming and fully understanding lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.237/en/sujato">AN 10.237</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once encountered a group of monks engaged in "aimless talk" about kings, thieves, and ministers, and responded by defining ten <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato"><b>praiseworthy grounds</b></a> for a monk’s conduct and conversation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato">AN 10.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is on praiseworthy ground when he is personally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato"><b>few of wishes</b></a> and also engages in talk with his fellow monks that encourages having few wishes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato">AN 10.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, it is a commendable quality for a practitioner to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato"><b>content</b></a> with what they have while also actively promoting the topic of contentment in their discussions with others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato">AN 10.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that being physically and mentally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato"><b>secluded</b></a> is considered a praiseworthy state, especially when a monk also speaks to his peers about the benefits of seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato">AN 10.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk reaches a praiseworthy ground when he remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato"><b>aloof from society</b></a> and makes the benefits of such aloofness a theme of his conversation with other monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato">AN 10.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> as a key praiseworthy quality, provided the practitioner is personally energetic and also speaks to others about the importance of arousing energy in their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato">AN 10.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being personally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato"><b>possessed of virtue</b></a> is a praiseworthy ground, particularly when one also engages in talk with others regarding the possession of virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato">AN 10.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk is praiseworthy when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato"><b>possessed of concentration</b></a> himself and consistently talks to his fellow monks about how to achieve and maintain concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato">AN 10.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato"><b>possessed of wisdom</b></a> as one of the ten praiseworthy grounds, especially when the practitioner also speaks to others about the development of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato">AN 10.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is on praiseworthy ground when he has personally achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato"><b>possession of liberation</b></a> and shares talk about liberation with his companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato">AN 10.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a> is a praiseworthy quality for a monk to possess, provided he also discusses the path to this knowledge with others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato">AN 10.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a quality to be truly praiseworthy, a practitioner must not only embody that quality themselves—such as virtue or concentration—but also be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato"><b>speaker on that topic</b></a> to their community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.70/en/sujato">AN 10.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>poverty</b></a> is a significant source of suffering in the world for those who enjoy sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person who enjoys sensual pleasures, the act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>borrowing</b></a> or falling into debt is considered a form of suffering in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the payment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>interest</b></a> is a source of suffering for a person in the world who is already destitute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>pressed for payment</b></a> by creditors as a painful experience for those who live in the world of sensual enjoyments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>hounded</b></a> by creditors when one cannot pay their debts is a form of worldly suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>imprisoned</b></a> due to an inability to settle debts is a terrible suffering for a consumer of sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered a person who lacks faith, moral shame, moral dread, energy, and wisdom to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>poor and destitute</b></a> in the context of the Noble One’s discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who engages in misconduct by body, speech, and mind to a debtor who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>taking a loan</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person tries to conceal their bad deeds with the wish "May they not know me," they are equivalent to a debtor paying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>accrued interest</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described being reproached by fellow practitioners for immoral behavior as the spiritual equivalent of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>pressed for debt</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that when unwholesome, remorseful thoughts haunt a person in seclusion, it is like being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>hounded by creditors</b></a> who follow a debtor everywhere? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified rebirth in hell or the animal realm as the ultimate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>terrible bondage</b></a> that results from the accumulation of spiritual debt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared there is no bond as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>dreadful or obstructive</b></a> to the attainment of supreme security as the bond of hell or the animal realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person with established faith and moral shame lives a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>happy life</b></a> in the Noble One’s discipline, even while still a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the realization of the destruction of all fetters of existence is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>supreme knowledge</b></a> and the highest form of peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the ultimate goal of the spiritual path as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>supreme freedom from debt</b></a>, which is the state of total liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who has abandoned the five hindrances and attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>meditative absorptions</b></a> (jhanas) is on the path to unshakeable liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of a liberated person as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>sorrowless and dustless</b></a>, having completely cut off the ties to further existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that a person who uses righteously earned wealth to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>giving</b></a> with a confident heart is successful in both the present life and the life to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato"><b>moral dread</b></a> and moral shame are essential qualities that lead a person to be considered wealthy in the Noble One's discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.45/en/sujato">AN 6.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahācunda taught that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato"><b>bhikkhus devoted to Dhamma</b></a> disparage meditators, they fail to practice for the welfare and happiness of many people, including devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato">AN 6.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahācunda cautioned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato"><b>meditators</b></a> who look down on those devoted to Dhamma as being restless or talkative are not acting for the benefit and spiritual progress of the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato">AN 6.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahācunda observed that practitioners often fall into the trap of only speaking in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato"><b>praise</b></a> of those who share their own spiritual focus, while neglecting to appreciate different but equally valid approaches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato">AN 6.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahācunda instructed monks who are devoted to Dhamma to train themselves to speak in praise of meditators because meditators are rare people who touch the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato"><b>deathless element</b></a> with their own bodies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato">AN 6.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahācunda taught that meditators should train themselves to speak in praise of Dhamma-devotees because such individuals <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato"><b>penetrate with wisdom</b></a> and see the deep meaning of the Buddha’s teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato">AN 6.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Mahācunda, a lack of mutual respect between scholarly monks and meditative monks prevents them from being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato"><b>practicing for the welfare</b></a> of the many-folk and the world at large? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato">AN 6.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahācunda described both meditators and those devoted to Dhamma as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato"><b>rare and wonderful</b></a> individuals who are difficult to find in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato">AN 6.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahācunda delivered this teaching while staying at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato"><b>Sahajati</b></a> among the Cetis to address the internal harmony of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato">AN 6.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahācunda warned that when Dhamma-devotees and meditators are not pleased with one another, they are not acting for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato"><b>attainment of the goal</b></a> that leads to the happiness of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato">AN 6.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahācunda identified those who dwell having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato"><b>personally experienced</b></a> the deathless element as being worthy of high regard from those who study the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato">AN 6.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahācunda emphasized that meditators should highly value those who can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato"><b>see the deep meaning</b></a> of a phrase through their intellectual and spiritual penetration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato">AN 6.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahācunda taught that to be a truly successful community, practitioners must move beyond <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato"><b>disparaging</b></a> those with different spiritual strengths and instead learn to see the unique value in each path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.46/en/sujato">AN 6.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Khema declared that a monk who is an arahant, with taints destroyed and the goal reached, does not entertain the thought "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato"><b>there is for me a superior</b></a>," an equal, or an inferior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato">AN 6.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Khema described an arahant as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato"><b>liberated by final knowledge</b></a> and has completely destroyed the fetters of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato">AN 6.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sumana stated that an arahant who has fulfilled the holy life does not think "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato"><b>there is no 'I am superior'</b></a>," "there is no 'I am equal'," or "there is no 'I am inferior'," indicating a total absence of comparison? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato">AN 6.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha showed his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato"><b>consent</b></a> to the declarations of Venerable Khema and Venerable Sumana regarding the psychological state of an arahant who has laid down the burden? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato">AN 6.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when noble clansmen declare final knowledge, they express the goal but do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato"><b>bring in the self</b></a> (attā) in their explanation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato">AN 6.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted genuine declarations of enlightenment with those of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato"><b>foolish men</b></a> who declare final knowledge as if they were laughing, without having truly attained it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato">AN 6.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who have finished the holy life and are freed from fetters do not consider themselves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato"><b>among the superiors</b></a>, the inferiors, or even the equals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato">AN 6.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described those who are liberated by final knowledge as individuals for whom <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a> and the holy life has been lived to completion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato">AN 6.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized an arahant as one whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato"><b>fetters of existence</b></a> are utterly destroyed, allowing them to wander detached from worldly ties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.49/en/sujato">AN 6.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda approached the Venerable Sāriputta to ask about the conditions under which a monk may hear <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma not heard before</b></a> and ensure that the teachings he has already heard are not forgotten? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda taught that a monk can ensure that teachings previously contacted by the mind recur to him by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>teaching the Dhamma</b></a> in detail to others exactly as he has heard and learned it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Ānanda, a monk gains an understanding of what was previously not understood by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>making others recite</b></a> the Dhamma in detail based on his own learned knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>nine-fold division</b></a> of the Buddha's teachings—consisting of discourses, prose-and-verse, explanations, verses, inspired utterances, itivuttakas, birth stories, marvelous stories, and catechisms—as essential material for a monk to master? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda explained that a monk maintains his spiritual progress by engaging in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>recitation of the Dhamma</b></a> in detail, ensuring the teachings remain fresh and are not lost to memory? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda emphasized that a monk should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>ponder the Dhamma</b></a> and examine it with his mind to ensure that the teachings he has learned are deeply understood and recur to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda advised practitioners to spend the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>rains residence</b></a> in monasteries where elder monks reside who are heirs to the tradition and experts in the Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda taught that a monk should approach learned elders to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>ask for the meaning</b></a> of difficult passages, specifically asking, "How is this, Bhante? What is the purpose of this?" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Ānanda, when a monk consults expert elders, they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>reveal what was hidden</b></a> and clarify those teachings that were previously obscure or difficult to grasp? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda stated that questioning expert monks is the primary method to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>dispel doubts</b></a> regarding the various points of the Dhamma that may arise during one's study? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta expressed that it was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>wonderful and marvelous</b></a> how clearly Ānanda had explained the ways in which a monk internalizes and retains the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta confirmed that the Venerable Ānanda himself was possessed of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>six qualities</b></a> that made him a master of hearing and retaining the teachings of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta described Ānanda as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>learned monk</b></a> (bahussuto) who had mastered the entire nine-fold collection of the Dhamma through diligent study and practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda taught that a monk ensures his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>previous mental contacts</b></a> with the Dhamma are not lost by mentally pondering and investigating the teachings in his own time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta observed that Ānanda would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>seek out elders</b></a> who were experts in the Vinaya and the Outlines (mātikās) to deepen his own understanding of the Buddha’s discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta acknowledged that Ānanda was one who would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>clarify the obscure</b></a> aspects of the Dhamma for others because he had spent time questioning those more senior than himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda explained that by reciting the Dhamma <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>in detail</b></a>, a monk prevents the confusion or fading of the teachings he has already mastered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Sāriputta, the Venerable Ānanda was an example of a monk who could <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato"><b>understand the unknown</b></a> because he utilized all the proper methods of learning, recitation, and inquiry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.51/en/sujato">AN 6.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>conditioned things</b></a> are impermanent, unstable, and provide no real consolation, urging his followers to become disenchanted with them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>Mount Sineru</b></a>, the king of mountains, is eighty-four thousand leagues high and extends eighty-four thousand leagues deep into the great ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted a time in the distant future when a great drought occurs, and for many hundreds of thousands of years <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>no rain</b></a> will fall upon the earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how, during a period of extreme drought, all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>seedlings and vegetation</b></a>, including all herbs and forest trees, will eventually wither and dry up until they are no more? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that after a long period, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>second sun</b></a> will appear in the sky, causing all the small rivers and ponds to dry up and vanish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>third sun</b></a> appears, the great rivers such as the Ganges, Yamunā, Aciravatī, Sarabhū, and Mahī will completely dry up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the appearance of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>fourth sun</b></a> will cause the seven great lakes, including Lake Anotatta, which are the sources of the great rivers, to evaporate entirely? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha detailed how a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>fifth sun</b></a> will cause the waters of the great ocean to recede until they are only knee-deep, then ankle-deep, and finally disappear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the final remnants of the ocean after the appearance of the fifth sun to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>puddles in a cow’s footprint</b></a> during the autumn rains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>sixth sun</b></a> appears, the great earth and Mount Sineru will smoke and fume like a potter's kiln? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a terrifying future where a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>seventh sun</b></a> appears, causing the great earth and Mount Sineru to burst into a single sheet of flame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said the fire consuming the world will be so intense that its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>flames</b></a>, driven by the wind, will reach as far as the Brahma-world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of how the massive peaks of Mount Sineru, some hundreds of leagues high, will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>crumble away</b></a> while being consumed by the fire of the seven suns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the world is destroyed by fire, just as with burning ghee or oil, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>no ash or soot</b></a> will remain behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked who, except for those who have seen the truth, would believe that the earth and Mount Sineru will eventually be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>burned and destroyed</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted the story of a past teacher named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>Sunetta</b></a>, a spiritual guide who was free from sensual lust and had hundreds of disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Sunetta the teacher taught his disciples the way to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>rebirth in the Brahma-world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that Sunetta’s followers who fully understood his teachings were reborn in the Brahma-world, while those who did not were reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>lower heavenly realms</b></a> or as wealthy humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how Sunetta practiced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> for seven years and as a result did not return to this world for seven eons of world-expansion and world-contraction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that during the periods of world-contraction, Sunetta was reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>Ābhassara realm</b></a> of streaming radiance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that Sunetta, through his merit, was reborn as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>Mahā Brahmā</b></a>, the unconquered Overlord who sees all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared that Sunetta was reborn as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>Sakka</b></a>, the ruler of the devas, thirty-six times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that Sunetta was reborn as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>Wheel-turning Monarch</b></a> many hundreds of times, ruling the earth with righteousness without the need for weapons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that despite his incredible longevity and power, the teacher Sunetta was still not free from suffering because he had not penetrated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato"><b>noble virtue, concentration, wisdom, and liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.66/en/sujato">AN 7.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>royal frontier fortress</b></a> to explain how a noble disciple becomes invincible to Mara and his evil forces? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a noble disciple's faith to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>deeply embedded pillar</b></a> in a fortress that remains unshakable and immovable against external threats? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a noble disciple who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> in the Tathāgata’s enlightenment abandons what is unwholesome and develops what is wholesome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened a noble disciple's sense of shame to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>deep and wide moat</b></a> surrounding a fortress for its protection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a noble disciple is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>conscientious</b></a>, feeling shame towards bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct, just as a fortress is protected by a moat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a noble disciple's fear of wrongdoing to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>high and wide encircling road</b></a> that serves as a safeguard for a frontier fortress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>fear of wrongdoing</b></a> remains pure by abandoning blameworthy states, similar to the protection provided by a fortress road? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a noble disciple's vast learning to a fortress having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>large stock of weapons</b></a> like spears and arrows for defense? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>learned disciple</b></a> as someone who has heard much, retained it, and penetrated the teachings through right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened a noble disciple's aroused energy to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>large army</b></a> consisting of elephant riders, cavalry, and archers stationed in a fortress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> is firm and exerts effort to abandon unwholesome qualities and acquire wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a noble disciple's mindfulness to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>wise gatekeeper</b></a> who admits friends and keeps out strangers to protect a fortress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>mindful disciple</b></a> as one who possesses supreme mindfulness and can remember things done or said long ago? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened a noble disciple's wisdom to a fortress having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>high and wide wall</b></a> finished with plaster to prevent enemies from entering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>wise disciple</b></a> possesses the noble, penetrative wisdom that sees the rising and passing away of things, leading to the destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a fortress must be well-stocked with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>grass, wood, and water</b></a> for the joy and comfort of those within, representing the first jhana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the attainment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>first jhana</b></a> to a fortress being stocked with basic necessities for the comfort and well-being of its inhabitants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened the attainment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>second jhana</b></a> to a fortress being well-provisioned with large stocks of rice and barley? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a fortress stocked with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>rice and barley</b></a> is like a disciple who attains the second jhana for their own internal delight and ease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the attainment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>third jhana</b></a> to a fortress being well-stocked with various beans and cereals like sesame and mung beans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a disciple who attains the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>third jhana</b></a> finds internal joy and comfort, just like residents of a fortress stocked with various cereals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened the attainment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhana</b></a> to a fortress having an abundant supply of various medicines like ghee, butter, and honey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>ghee, butter, oil, honey, molasses, and salt</b></a> as essential medicinal supplies that a frontier fortress must have for the well-being of its people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a noble disciple who masters the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a> can obtain these pleasant dwellings in the present life whenever they want without difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a disciple endowed with seven good qualities and four jhanas is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>invincible to Mara</b></a>, the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>frontier fortress</b></a> as being "invincible to external enemies and foes" when it has seven safeguards and four types of provisions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a fortress has a gatekeeper to ward off strangers, a noble disciple uses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> to guard against unwholesome mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.67/en/sujato">AN 7.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk possesses seven qualities to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>worthy of gifts</b></a>, including knowing the Dhamma, knowing the meaning, knowing himself, knowing moderation, knowing the time, knowing the assembly, and knowing the superiority of persons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>knows the Dhamma</b></a> when he is familiar with the various branches of the teachings, such as discourses, songs, and inspired utterances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the nine branches of the Dhamma that a monk should master as: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>sutta, geyya, veyyākaraṇa</b></a>, gāthā, udāna, itivuttaka, jātaka, abbhutadhamma, and vedalla? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>knows the meaning</b></a> (atthaññū) when he understands the specific significance and purpose of each statement within the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>knows himself</b></a> (attaññū) when he can accurately assess the current extent of his own faith, virtue, learning, generosity, wisdom, and eloquence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>faith, virtue, and learning</b></a> as key internal metrics a monk uses to evaluate his own spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>knows moderation</b></a> (mattaññū) when he understands the proper limit in accepting requisites such as robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicinal supplies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>moderation in receiving</b></a> requisites is an essential quality for a monk to be considered an unsurpassed field of merit for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>knows the time</b></a> (kālaññū) when he correctly identifies the right time for recitation, for inquiry, for exertion, and for seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that knowing the right time for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>inquiry and exertion</b></a> is a critical skill for a monk to be worthy of hospitality and reverential salutation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>knows the assembly</b></a> (parisaññū) when he knows the correct way to approach, stand, sit, and speak when visiting different social groups? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed four types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>social assemblies</b></a>—Khattiyas, Brahmins, householders, and ascetics—that a monk must learn to interact with appropriately? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>knows how to behave</b></a> in an assembly by knowing when it is appropriate to speak and when it is appropriate to maintain noble silence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that knowing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>superiority of persons</b></a> (puggalaparoparaññū) involves judging individuals based on their spiritual inclinations and their commitment to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>desires to see noble ones</b></a> is considered praiseworthy, while a person who has no such desire is considered blameworthy in that regard? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, between two people, the one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>desires to hear</b></a> the good Dhamma is superior to the one who has no interest in hearing the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that listening to the Dhamma with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>attentive ear</b></a> is a quality that makes one person superior to another who listens inattentively? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>retaining the Dhamma</b></a> in memory after hearing it is a mark of superiority over those who forget the teachings they have heard? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>examines the meaning</b></a> of the teachings they have memorized is more praiseworthy than someone who retains the words without investigating their significance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the highest form of individual progress is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>practicing in accordance</b></a> with the Dhamma after having understood both the phrasing and the meaning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between those who practice only for their own benefit and those who practice for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato"><b>both self and others</b></a>, calling the latter the most excellent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.68/en/sujato">AN 7.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato"><b>seven types of wealth</b></a>—faith, virtue, moral shame, moral dread, learning, generosity, and wisdom—as the true riches of a noble disciple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato">AN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato"><b>wealth of faith</b></a> as a noble disciple placing their trust in the Tathāgata’s enlightenment and recognizing him as the perfectly enlightened one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato">AN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato"><b>wealth of virtue</b></a> consists of a noble disciple abstaining from taking life, stealing, sexual misconduct, false speech, and intoxicating drinks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato">AN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato"><b>wealth of moral shame</b></a> as a noble disciple feeling conscientious and ashamed of bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato">AN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato"><b>wealth of moral dread</b></a> involves a noble disciple being afraid of committing bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct because they fear the consequences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato">AN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a noble disciple possesses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato"><b>wealth of learning</b></a> when they have heard much of the Dhamma, retained it in memory, and penetrated it through right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato">AN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Dhamma that constitutes the wealth of learning as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato"><b>good in the beginning</b></a>, middle, and end, and as proclaiming a perfectly pure holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato">AN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato"><b>wealth of generosity</b></a> is found in a noble disciple who dwells with a mind free from the stain of stinginess, being open-handed and delighting in giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato">AN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person with the wealth of generosity as someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato"><b>devoted to charity</b></a> and is always happy to be asked for assistance or resources? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato">AN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato"><b>wealth of wisdom</b></a> as a noble disciple possessing the penetrative wisdom that sees the rising and passing away of things, leading to the destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato">AN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that any man or woman who possesses these seven types of spiritual wealth is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato"><b>not poor</b></a> and that their life is not lived in vain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato">AN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged wise people to devote themselves to faith, virtue, and vision of the Dhamma, while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato"><b>remembering the teachings</b></a> of the Buddhas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato">AN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the seven spiritual riches of faith, virtue, shame, dread, learning, generosity, and wisdom are available to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato"><b>any woman or man</b></a>, ensuring their life is meaningful and fruitful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.6/en/sujato">AN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once encountered a group of monks who, after their meal, were sitting in the assembly hall quarreling and stabbing each other with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>verbal daggers</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught ten specific qualities that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>memorable</b></a> and lead to affection, respect, and communal unity among practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and restrained by the rules of the Patimokkha as the first quality that leads to non-dispute and concord? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk should be one who sees <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>danger in slight faults</b></a> while training in the rules of conduct to foster a harmonious community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a learned monk as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>retained and stored</b></a> teachings that are good in the beginning, middle, and end, and that fully express the pure holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having teachings that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>well-penetrated by view</b></a> and stored in the mind is a quality that leads to the unity of the spiritual community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>good friends</b></a> and good companions is a vital quality for creating respect and helpfulness among fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>easy to admonish</b></a>, patient, and accepting of instruction is a quality that leads to the state of oneness in a group? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha valued those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>skillful and diligent</b></a> in handling the various duties and chores required by their fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>lover of the Dhamma</b></a> and being fond of discussing the teachings is a quality that creates affection within the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that experiencing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>extraordinary joy</b></a> in the study of the Abhidhamma and the Vinaya is a quality that promotes respect and non-dispute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>aroused in energy</b></a> for the sake of abandoning unwholesome qualities and acquiring wholesome ones to benefit the group? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>firm in endeavor</b></a> and not shirking the responsibility of cultivating wholesome states leads to communal concord? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>content with any kind</b></a> of robes, food, lodging, or medicines is a quality that fosters a sense of helpfulness and unity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a mindful person as one possessing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>highest mindfulness</b></a> and prudence, capable of remembering what was done and said long ago? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>recall past actions</b></a> and words as a "memorable quality" that contributes to the harmony of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>noble wisdom</b></a> into the arising and passing away of phenomena is essential for the destruction of suffering and communal unity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the tenth memorable quality as wisdom that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>penetrative</b></a> and leads to the complete end of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that these ten qualities are not only memorable but also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>create respect</b></a> and lead to a state of oneness among practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told his monks it was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato"><b>not suitable</b></a> for people of good birth who left the home life out of faith to live together in conflict and dispute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.50/en/sujato">AN 10.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that if a monk is not skilled in knowing the minds of others, he should at least train himself to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>skilled in the ways of his own mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared mental self-reflection to a young person looking at their own reflection in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>clean mirror</b></a> or a bowl of clear water to see if there is any dust or blemish on their face? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a person works to remove a physical blemish seen in a reflection, a practitioner must exert effort to abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome qualities</b></a> once they are discovered through self-examination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers to investigate whether they are living mostly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>full of covetousness</b></a> or free from covetousness as a fundamental part of their daily mental training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that if a practitioner finds themselves living with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>mind of ill will</b></a>, they should apply extraordinary desire and effort to overcome it, just as if their own clothes were on fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>overcome by dullness and drowsiness</b></a> as a negative mental state that requires immediate and intensive effort to abandon for the sake of spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should actively check if his mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>restless</b></a> or calm, using this self-knowledge to guide the intensity of his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>doubtful</b></a> is a hindrance that must be crossed over through diligent self-review to achieve success in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of someone with their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>head on fire</b></a> to illustrate the extreme urgency and "unremitting mindfulness" needed to abandon unwholesome qualities in the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a practitioner should examine if they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>angry</b></a> or without anger, recognizing that removing anger is essential for maintaining a clear and purified mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a monk discovers his mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>defiled</b></a>, he must use specific tools like zeal, mindfulness, and clear comprehension to abandon those states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>agitated body</b></a> is a condition to be monitored during self-reflection and abandoned through persistent effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged those who find themselves being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>lazy</b></a> to arouse their energy and apply themselves with unremitting zeal to transform their spiritual state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk realizes he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>unconcentrated</b></a>, he should strive for mental stillness with the same intensity as one putting out a fire on their own head? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a practitioner finds through reflection that they are already <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>free from unwholesome states</b></a>, they should feel glad and joyful while continuing their training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed those who have attained wholesome qualities to remain established in them while making a further effort for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>self-examination</b></a> as being "very helpful for wholesome states" when a practitioner regularly and systematically reviews their own mental condition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato"><b>ten pairs of qualities</b></a>, such as being lazy versus energetic or doubtful versus free of doubt, for a monk to use as a checklist for internal review? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.51/en/sujato">AN 10.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that if a monk is not skilled in knowing the minds of others, he should at least train himself to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato"><b>skilled in the ways of his own mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato">AN 10.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta compared the practice of mental self-reflection to a young person checking for dust or blemishes on their face by looking into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato"><b>clean mirror</b></a> or a bowl of clear water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato">AN 10.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato"><b>self-reflection</b></a> is highly helpful for the development of wholesome states when a practitioner systematically reviews their own mental qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato">AN 10.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta encouraged monks to investigate whether they live their daily lives <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato"><b>full of covetousness</b></a> or free from it as a way to monitor their spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato">AN 10.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that a monk should reflect on whether he frequently lives with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato"><b>mind of ill will</b></a> or with a mind that is free from ill will toward others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato">AN 10.52</a></p></div><hr>
