<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that if a monk discovers he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato"><b>angry</b></a>, he should apply the same urgency to abandon that anger as someone would use to extinguish a fire on their own head? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato">AN 10.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta used the simile of a person whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato"><b>head is on fire</b></a> to illustrate the extreme desire and effort a practitioner should use to abandon unwholesome mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato">AN 10.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta advised those who find themselves being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato"><b>lazy</b></a> to immediately arouse their energy and apply unremitting mindfulness to transform their state of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato">AN 10.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that once a monk finds he is free from unwholesome states, he should remain established in those wholesome qualities and strive further for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.52/en/sujato">AN 10.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he does not praise even standing still in wholesome states, let alone decline, but instead praises only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato"><b>growth in wholesome states</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato">AN 10.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined "decline" in wholesome states as a situation where a practitioner’s faith, virtue, learning, generosity, wisdom, and eloquence <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato"><b>neither stand still nor grow</b></a> but instead decrease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato">AN 10.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a monk is not skilled in knowing the minds of others, he should at least train himself to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato"><b>skilled in the ways of his own mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato">AN 10.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the process of self-reflection to a young person examining their own reflection in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato"><b>clean mirror</b></a> or a bowl of clear water to see if there is any dust or blemish on their face? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato">AN 10.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a person works to remove a physical blemish seen in a mirror, a practitioner must exert effort to abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome qualities</b></a> once they are discovered through internal review? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato">AN 10.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed practitioners to investigate whether they live their daily lives <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato"><b>full of covetousness</b></a> or free from it as a fundamental part of their mental development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato">AN 10.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged monks to reflect on whether they are frequently living with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato"><b>mind of ill will</b></a> or with a mind free from ill will toward others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato">AN 10.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato"><b>overcome by dullness and drowsiness</b></a> as a negative state that requires immediate, intensive effort to abandon once it is recognized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.53/en/sujato">AN 10.53</a></p></div><hr>
