<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk who enjoys the meditative bliss of the four jhanas but has not yet reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.71/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (the final end of defilements) is still considered incomplete in his spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.71/en/sujato">AN 8.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a monk who is "inspiring in all respects" as one who has successfully integrated eight specific qualities, ranging from basic faith and virtue to the final <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.71/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.71/en/sujato">AN 8.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to reflect on their own progress and specifically resolve to fulfill any missing factor, such as becoming a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.71/en/sujato"><b>speaker of the Dhamma</b></a> if they find they are learned but cannot yet communicate the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.71/en/sujato">AN 8.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is only "complete in every way" when he possesses a combination of faith, virtue, learning, eloquence, social engagement, confidence, meditative mastery, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.71/en/sujato"><b>liberation by wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.71/en/sujato">AN 8.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described the four jhanas as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.71/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwellings</b></a> in this very life that an accomplished monk should be able to gain at will, without trouble or difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.71/en/sujato">AN 8.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of death</b></a>, when developed and cultivated, is of great fruit and great benefit, merging in and ending in the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once stayed at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>Brick House</b></a> in Nātika when he instructed his monks on the proper way to cultivate mindfulness of death for the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered a monk to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>dwelling heedlessly</b></a> if his practice of mindfulness of death only involved the hope to live for a single night and a day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that practicing mindfulness of death with the expectation to live for just one day is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>sluggish</b></a> form of development that does not lead effectively to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, even contemplating death within the span of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>half a day</b></a> is insufficient for the keen development of mindfulness needed for liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that reflecting on death only as often as one eats <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>one alms meal</b></a> is categorized as a sluggish way of developing the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that monks who think they will live long enough to eat <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>half an alms meal</b></a> are still considered to be practicing mindfulness of death heedlessly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified monks as heedless if they contemplate death only in terms of the time it takes to chew and swallow <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>four or five morsels</b></a> of food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha specifically praised the monk who reflects that he might live only as long as it takes to chew and swallow <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>one morsel of food</b></a> as one who truly dwells heedfully? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the most advanced and heedful form of mindfulness of death involves knowing one might die in the time it takes to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>breathe in and out</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the practice of contemplating death within the duration of a single breath as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>keenly developing</b></a> mindfulness for the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that mindfulness of death should be cultivated specifically for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavānaṃ khayāya), which refers to the total ending of all mental defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized monks who practice mindfulness of death with extreme urgency—such as the span of a single breath—as those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>dwell heedfully</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha urged his followers to adopt a specific training resolution: "We will dwell heedfully; we will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>keenly develop</b></a> mindfulness of death for the destruction of the taints"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that mindfulness of death is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>amatogadhā</b></a>, a term meaning it "merges in the Deathless," leading the practitioner to the state of Nirvana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the ultimate purpose of mindfulness of death as the ability to immediately <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>attend to the teaching</b></a> (sāsana) of the Blessed One without any delay? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that when mindfulness of death is developed and cultivated, it reaches its final consummation in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>Deathless</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>sluggish</b></a> and keen development of mindfulness, based solely on how short a time a practitioner expects to remain alive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha considered the awareness of death during the chewing of a single <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>morsel</b></a> of food to be a practice that leads to great benefit and the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s discourse on mindfulness of death was given to prompt monks to examine their own practice and ensure they were not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato"><b>dwelling heedlessly</b></a> in their spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.73/en/sujato">AN 8.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while staying at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>Brick House</b></a> in Nātika, taught that mindfulness of death, when developed and cultivated, leads to great fruit and great benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the practice of mindfulness of death is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>amatogadhā</b></a>, meaning it "merges in the Deathless" and has the Deathless as its final consummation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to reflect at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>nightfall</b></a> on the many causes of death that could occur before the next morning, such as being bitten by a snake, a scorpion, or a centipede? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that a practitioner should consider how death could come from simple accidents, such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>tripping</b></a> and falling, or even from the food they have already eaten disagreeing with them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that internal physical disturbances, such as a provocation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>bile, phlegm, or sharp winds</b></a>, are potential internal causes that could lead to one's death at any time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the possibility of being attacked by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>humans or non-humans</b></a> as one of the external dangers that could cause a person's death and serve as an obstacle to their spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the importance of a monk checking whether he has any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>unabandoned evil unwholesome states</b></a> that would cause him spiritual harm if he were to die during the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that if a monk discovers unabandoned evil states within himself, he should apply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>extraordinary desire</b></a> and effort to abandon them immediately? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the vivid simile of someone whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>clothes are on fire</b></a> to illustrate the extreme urgency with which one should strive to abandon unwholesome mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk should act with the same intensity as someone whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>head is on fire</b></a> when working to rid the mind of evil unwholesome states that hinder progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, if a monk reflects and finds no unabandoned unwholesome states, he should dwell in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>joy and rapture</b></a> while continuing his training in wholesome qualities day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended repeating the same deep reflection on mortality and internal purity at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>dawn</b></a> as the day begins, to ensure the mind remains focused on liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a practitioner must use both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a> (sati and sampajañña) in the specific effort to abandon the unwholesome states discovered through reflection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described unabandoned evil states as a significant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>obstacle</b></a> (antarāyo) for someone who might pass away during the night or day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha referred to the cultivation of these reflections as being a student who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>training day and night</b></a> in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>snake bites</b></a> as a primary example of the "many causes of death" that practitioners should keep in mind to maintain their urgency? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that mindfulness of death, when practiced through this method of self-examination, is of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>great fruit</b></a> and leads to the highest spiritual goals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the presence of "evil unwholesome states" requires not just effort, but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>unremitting zeal</b></a> (ussoḷhī) and an "unfaltering" (appaṭivānī) attitude to overcome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching on mindfulness of death focuses on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>many causes</b></a> of mortality, ranging from animals and accidents to internal diseases and external attacks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the ultimate goal of successfully abandoning evil states through mindfulness of death is to dwell in a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato"><b>wholesome training</b></a> (kusalesu dhammesu) that is constant and joyous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.74/en/sujato">AN 8.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>eight accomplishments</b></a> that lead a householder to welfare and happiness in both this present life and the life to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in initiative</b></a> as being skillful, diligent, and resourceful in whatever profession one uses to earn a living, such as farming, trade, or cattle-rearing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in protection</b></a> involves a householder carefully safeguarding wealth that was earned through persistent effort and righteous means? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> means associating with people who are mature in virtue, faith, generosity, and wisdom, and actively emulating those qualities in one's own life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>balanced living</b></a> to a person holding a scale who knows exactly how much it tips, meaning a householder should ensure their expenditure does not exceed their income? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that a person with a small income who lives lavishly is criticized by others as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>Udumbarakhādī</b></a>, similar to a "fig-tree glutton" who wastes fruit while eating? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in faith</b></a> means a layperson places their trust in the enlightenment of the Tathāgata, the teacher of gods and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in moral virtue</b></a> requires a householder to abstain from killing, stealing, sexual misconduct, false speech, and the use of intoxicants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in generosity</b></a> involves living at home with a mind free from the stain of stinginess, being open-handed and delighting in sharing with others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in wisdom</b></a> as possessing the noble, penetrative understanding of the rise and fall of things, which leads to the complete destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha cautioned that a person with a large income who lives poorly is criticized as one who will die like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>starveling</b></a>, failing to enjoy their own wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that for true accomplishment in initiative, one must be endowed with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>capacity for inquiry</b></a> and the ability to properly arrange and carry out their work? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>hateful heirs</b></a>, along with kings, thieves, fire, and water, as one of the specific threats a person should protect their wealth from? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged laypeople to specifically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>emulate the virtue</b></a> and generosity of their friends as a central part of the practice of good friendship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, these eight qualities are taught to bring <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>happiness in both worlds</b></a>—both the present world and the one to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha referred to these practices as the path for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>faithful householder</b></a> to clear the way for their long-term well-being and safety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when a householder lives a balanced and virtuous life, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>merit increases</b></a>, providing benefit for both this life and the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>kalyāṇamittatā</b></a> (good friendship) as dwelling with and conversing with those who have achieved accomplishments in faith, virtue, generosity, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically mentioned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>royal service</b></a> and various arts as examples of honorable professions where a person should strive for excellence and initiative? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato"><b>balanced living</b></a> involves knowing one's income and losses so that one's livelihood is neither too lavish nor too meager? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.76/en/sujato">AN 8.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a monk to be dear, agreeable, respected, and esteemed by his fellow practitioners, he must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>perfectly virtuous</b></a> and devoted to internal serenity of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>accomplished in virtue</b></a> and restrained by the Pātimokkha, seeing danger in even the slightest faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk wishes to be a recipient of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>four requisites</b></a>—robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines—he should not neglect meditation or insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk's commitment to virtue and meditation causes the offerings of his donors to yield <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>great fruit and benefit</b></a> for those who provide him with necessities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>deceased relatives</b></a> and kinsmen can gain great fruit and benefit if they remember a virtuous and meditative monk with a confident mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>content with any kind</b></a> of robes, almsfood, lodgings, or medicines, a practitioner must remain devoted to internal serenity and the frequenting of empty huts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk who wishes to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>endure cold and heat</b></a>, hunger, and thirst must be fully possessed of insight and an uninterrupted practice of dhyana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that the ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>endure the sting of insects</b></a>, mosquitoes, wind, sun, and reptiles is bolstered by being accomplished in virtue and internal serenity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>possessed of insight</b></a> and devoted to meditation can endure ill-spoken, unwelcome words and even life-threatening bodily pains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one can become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>conqueror of discontent</b></a> and delight, rather than being conquered by them, by fulfilling the virtues and not neglecting meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk can become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>conqueror of fear</b></a> and dread by training in the rules of discipline and staying committed to internal serenity of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a monk wishes to obtain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>four jhānas</b></a>—which provide a pleasant dwelling in this very life—at will and without difficulty, he must be a frequenter of empty huts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> and the realization of liberation of mind are achieved by those who are perfectly virtuous and devoted to meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>restrained by the Pātimokkha</b></a> as being accomplished in conduct and resort, while seeing danger in the slightest of faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>uninterrupted practitioner of meditation</b></a> (dhyana) is better equipped to handle the hardships of the forest and the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>internal serenity of mind</b></a> and insight are the twin qualities that must accompany virtue to fulfill a monk's spiritual aspirations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that to realize <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato"><b>liberation by wisdom</b></a> through one's own direct knowledge, one must be a frequenter of empty huts and a master of virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.71/en/sujato">AN 10.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato"><b>sound is a thorn</b></a> to the first jhāna, which led several of his senior disciples to seek the silence of the Gosiṅga Sal-tree Wood when loud visitors arrived at their monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato">AN 10.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, for a person who delights in solitude and seclusion, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato"><b>delight in company</b></a> is considered a "thorn" or an obstacle to their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato">AN 10.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a practitioner devoted to meditating on the sign of the unattractive, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato"><b>sign of the beautiful</b></a> (subhanimitta) acts as a thorn that disrupts their focus? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato">AN 10.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato"><b>looking at shows</b></a> or theatrical performances is a thorn for one who is attempting to keep the doors of their senses guarded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato">AN 10.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato"><b>proximity of women</b></a> as a specific thorn to the successful practice of the celibate life (brahmacariya)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato">AN 10.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the progression of meditative states, the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato"><b>thought and examination</b></a> (vitakka and vicāra) are thorns to the attainment of the second jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato">AN 10.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even a positive meditative quality like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato"><b>rapture</b></a> (pīti) becomes a thorn when one is attempting to reach the more subtle third jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato">AN 10.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the physical process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato"><b>in-and-out breathing</b></a> is considered a thorn to the attainment of the fourth jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato">AN 10.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for the highest meditative attainment, the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato"><b>perception and feeling</b></a> are themselves thorns to the attainment of the cessation of perception and feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato">AN 10.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified the unwholesome roots of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato"><b>lust, hatred, and delusion</b></a> as universal thorns that obstruct spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato">AN 10.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised several of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato"><b>senior disciples</b></a>, including Cāla, Upacāla, and Kukkuṭa, for correctly recognizing that loud noises were "thorns" and choosing to move to a quieter forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato">AN 10.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato"><b>arahants</b></a> are those who dwell completely "without thorns," having removed all the internal and external obstacles to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.72/en/sujato">AN 10.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified ten things in the world that are desirable, liked, and agreeable but difficult to obtain, including wealth, beauty, health, virtue, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato"><b>spiritual life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato">AN 10.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that laziness and a lack of initiative are the primary obstacles to obtaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato"><b>wealth</b></a>, while diligence and initiative serve as its necessary "nutriments" or supports? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato">AN 10.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, for those who desire <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato"><b>beauty</b></a> and a pleasant appearance, the lack of grooming and decoration acts as an obstacle, whereas attention to grooming is the support for it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato">AN 10.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that performing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato"><b>suitable actions</b></a> is the essential support for maintaining health, while engaging in unsuitable activities serves as its major obstacle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato">AN 10.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> is the fundamental support for the development of virtue, whereas bad friendship is its primary adversary? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato">AN 10.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato"><b>sense restraint</b></a> is the necessary support for successfully living the celibate spiritual life, while a lack of restraint acts as a barrier to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato">AN 10.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one who desires to keep and gain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato"><b>friends</b></a>, the practice of non-deception is the essential support, while being deceptive or breaking one's word is a significant obstacle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato">AN 10.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato"><b>great learning</b></a> is supported and nourished by regular recitation, and that failing to recite what one has learned is a major obstacle to retaining knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato">AN 10.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato"><b>desire to listen</b></a> and the habit of inquiry (asking questions) as the specific nutriments for the development of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.73/en/sujato">AN 10.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>birth, aging, and death</b></a> did not exist in the world, a Tathāgata (an enlightened one) would not arise and the Dhamma would not shine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person is incapable of abandoning birth, aging, and death without first abandoning the three unwholesome roots of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>lust, hatred, and delusion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one cannot abandon lust, hatred, and delusion without first giving up <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>personal-existence view</b></a>, doubt, and the distorted grasp of rules and vows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that overcoming the "fetter" of doubt is impossible unless one first abandons <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>careless attention</b></a> and the habit of following a wrong path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>mental sluggishness</b></a> as one of the three qualities that must be abandoned before a practitioner is capable of overcoming personal-existence view and doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who does not abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>forgetfulness</b></a> and a lack of clear comprehension is incapable of abandoning careless attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>mental distraction</b></a> must be overcome in order to abandon careless attention, mental sluggishness, and the following of a wrong path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a lack of desire to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>see the Noble Ones</b></a> makes it impossible for a person to abandon forgetfulness and mental distraction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that failing to develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>desire to hear</b></a> the Dhamma (teachings) of the Noble Ones prevents one from overcoming forgetfulness and a lack of clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>upbraiding mind</b></a>—one that is focused on finding faults—is an obstacle that prevents the abandonment of forgetfulness and mental distraction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>restlessness</b></a> and a lack of restraint must be abandoned before a practitioner can develop the desire to see Noble Ones and hear their teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who does not abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>immorality</b></a> is incapable of overcoming a fault-finding mind and the lack of desire to see the Noble Ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>lack of faith</b></a> and ungenerousness as qualities that must be abandoned to properly overcome restlessness, lack of restraint, and immorality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>laziness</b></a> (kosajja) makes it impossible for a person to abandon restlessness and a lack of sense restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>disrespect</b></a> and being difficult to speak to are qualities that prevent the abandonment of faithlessness and ungenerousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>bad friendship</b></a> as a primary condition that prevents a person from overcoming laziness and a lack of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>moral shame</b></a> and moral dread leads to negligence, making it impossible to abandon bad friendship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>negligent person</b></a> (pamatto) is incapable of abandoning disrespect, bad friendship, and being difficult to speak to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>moral dread</b></a> and moral shame is diligent and thus capable of abandoning bad friendship and disrespect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> (kalyāṇamittatā) is a necessary condition for being capable of abandoning faithlessness, ungenerousness, and laziness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> is capable of abandoning restlessness, lack of restraint, and immorality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that becoming a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>virtuous person</b></a> (sīlavā) makes one capable of seeking out the Noble Ones and listening to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>mind free from fault-finding</b></a> (anupārambhacitto) is necessary for abandoning forgetfulness and mental distraction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that once lust, hatred, and delusion are abandoned, a person is finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato"><b>capable of abandoning</b></a> the cycle of birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.76/en/sujato">AN 10.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato"><b>nobles</b></a> (khattiyas), their primary aim in life is the pursuit of wealth and their final goal is sovereignty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato">AN 6.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato"><b>brahmins</b></a> find their fundamental support in mantras and have the attainment of the Brahma-world as their ultimate goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato">AN 6.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato"><b>householders</b></a> (gahapatikas) are supported by their mastery of arts and crafts while they aim for the acquisition of wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato">AN 6.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato"><b>women</b></a>, their primary aim is men, they are preoccupied with ornaments, and they find their support in their sons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato">AN 6.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato"><b>thieves</b></a> have the act of taking as their primary aim and rely on weapons as their fundamental support? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato">AN 6.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato"><b>ascetics</b></a> (samaṇas) as those whose primary aim is patience and gentleness, and whose final goal is the attainment of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato">AN 6.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that while nobles, brahmins, and householders are all aimed at wealth, only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato"><b>ascetics</b></a> are specifically aimed at patience and gentleness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato">AN 6.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato"><b>nobles</b></a> are preoccupied with wisdom, supported by power, and have a deep-seated commitment to the earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato">AN 6.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato"><b>women</b></a> share the same final goal as nobles, which is the attainment of sovereignty or authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato">AN 6.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato"><b>thieves</b></a> are preoccupied with thickets or hiding places and that their ultimate goal is to remain unseen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato">AN 6.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato"><b>householders</b></a>, the final culmination and goal of their life's efforts is simply the completion of their work? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato">AN 6.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato"><b>ascetics</b></a> find their essential support in virtue (sīla) and maintain a commitment to the state of nothingness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.52/en/sujato">AN 6.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta reflected on how a monk who lives with respect and deference toward specific spiritual qualities can successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>abandon the unwholesome</b></a> and develop the wholesome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta identified living with respect and deference toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>Teacher</b></a> as a primary requirement for a monk to abandon unwholesome states and cultivate wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta stated that a monk who lives with respect and deference toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a> is capable of abandoning unwholesome qualities and developing those that are wholesome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that a monk who lives with respect and deference toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>Sangha</b></a> would be able to successfully abandon unwholesome states and foster the growth of wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained that living with respect and deference toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>training</b></a> is essential for any monk who wishes to abandon unwholesome qualities and develop the wholesome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta highlighted that a monk who lives with respect and deference toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> can effectively abandon unwholesome states and develop those that are wholesome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta noted that living with respect and deference toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> allows a monk to abandon unwholesome qualities and cultivate the wholesome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta concluded that a monk who lives with respect and deference toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>amity</b></a> (hospitality) is in a proper position to abandon the unwholesome and develop the wholesome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta compared his realization about the seven things to be respected to a person who finds a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>pure gold nugget</b></a> and wishes to have its purity confirmed by an expert? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta described his desire to present his thoughts on respect to the Buddha as being like a man who brings a gold nugget to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>smiths</b></a> so that its purity might be further verified and refined? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha expressed his full approval of Sāriputta's reflection, confirming that a monk indeed abandons the unwholesome by living with respect toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>Teacher, Dhamma, and Sangha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta argued it is impossible for a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>disrespectful to the Teacher</b></a> to simultaneously be respectful and deferential toward the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta claimed that if a monk lacks respect for the Teacher, he will inevitably also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>lack respect for the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta maintained that a monk who is disrespectful to both the Teacher and the Dhamma cannot possibly be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>respectful to the Sangha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta reasoned that a lack of respect for the Teacher, Dhamma, and Sangha naturally leads to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>lack of respect for the training</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta stated it is impossible for a monk who is disrespectful toward the training to be truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>respectful toward concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained that a monk who is disrespectful to the Teacher, Dhamma, Sangha, training, and concentration will also be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>disrespectful toward heedfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that a monk who lacks respect for heedfulness will inevitably also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>lack respect for amity</b></a> (hospitality)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta asserted it is entirely possible for a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>respectful to the Teacher</b></a> to also be respectful and deferential toward the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta noted that respect for the Dhamma is a prerequisite for a monk to be truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>respectful to the Sangha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that respect for the training provides the necessary foundation for a monk to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>respectful toward concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta argued that a monk who is respectful toward heedfulness will naturally also be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>respectful toward amity</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highly praised Sāriputta for his detailed explanation of how respect for the Teacher, Dhamma, Sangha, training, concentration, heedfulness, and amity are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato"><b>interconnected</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.70/en/sujato">AN 7.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that if a monk is not skilled in reading the minds of others, he should at least train himself to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>skilled in the ways of his own mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the simile of a young person looking at their own reflection in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>clean mirror or a bowl of clear water</b></a> to illustrate how a practitioner should examine their own mental state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that just as a person looks for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>dust or a blemish</b></a> on their face in a mirror, the Buddha taught that a practitioner must look for unwholesome qualities within their own mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, if a practitioner finds a mental blemish during self-examination, they must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>exert effort to remove it</b></a>, just as one would try to clean a spot off their face? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that if a monk examines himself and finds no blemishes, he should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>joyful and satisfied</b></a>, similar to a person with a clean face who is glad to see they are pure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>frequent reviewing</b></a> of one's own mental state is highly helpful for the further development of wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha instructed practitioners to specifically ask themselves whether they have gained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>internal serenity of mind</b></a> as part of their regular self-assessment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that besides serenity, a practitioner must also check if they have gained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>insight into teachings through higher wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha advised a monk who has achieved internal serenity of mind but lacks insight to specifically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>strive for insight</b></a> through higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha advised a monk who has gained insight into the teachings but lacks serenity to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>strive for serenity</b></a> of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha said that if a monk lacks both internal serenity and insight, he should apply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>extraordinary desire and effort</b></a> to acquire these two vital wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha compared the urgency of seeking missing spiritual qualities to someone whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>turban or head is on fire</b></a> and who must act immediately to extinguish the flames? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that even if a monk possesses both serenity and insight, he must continue to practice diligently for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>robes</b></a> into two types: those that should be used and those that should not, based on their spiritual effect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one should not use a robe if wearing it causes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome states to increase</b></a> and wholesome states to decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> should only be accepted and consumed if it leads to the growth of wholesome states in the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha recommended that a practitioner should avoid a particular <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>dwelling or lodging</b></a> if staying there results in a decrease of their wholesome mental qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha instructed that even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>village or town</b></a> should not be frequented if living there causes unwholesome states to flourish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>region or countryside</b></a> is only worth residing in if it supports the decline of unwholesome states and the flourishing of wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha advised that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>person</b></a> should only be associated with if that association causes unwholesome states to decline and wholesome states to increase? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that for any requisite, such as a robe or food, the deciding factor for its use is whether <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states flourish</b></a> because of it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by properly selecting one's environment and requisites, a practitioner becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato"><b>established in wholesome qualities</b></a> and can effectively work toward liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.54/en/sujato">AN 10.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>eight types of persons</b></a> existing in the world based on their internal reactions to the desire for material gains and the effort they make to obtain them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, a monk who strives and makes an effort for gains but fails to receive them, and subsequently sorrows and laments, is considered one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>falls away from the true Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk who exerts himself and successfully acquires gains, but then becomes intoxicated and negligent because of those acquisitions, is a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>falls away from the true Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta described how a monk living in seclusion may experience a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>desire for gains</b></a>, but his spiritual integrity is determined by whether he remains free from sorrow or intoxication regardless of the outcome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta warned that even if a monk does not actively strive for gains, if he fails to receive them and then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>sorrows and laments</b></a>, he is still regarded as one who has fallen away from the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, if a monk makes no effort to acquire gains but receives them anyway, yet becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>intoxicated and negligent</b></a> with what he has received, he falls away from the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta highlighted the virtue of a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>strives and exerts</b></a> himself for gains but does not sorrow, pine, or fall into confusion if those gains do not materialize? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that a monk who makes a dedicated effort and receives gains, but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>does not become intoxicated</b></a> or negligent because of them, is one who does not fall away from the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk who makes no effort for gains and receives none, yet remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>free from sorrow</b></a> and lamentation, is characterized as someone who does not fall away from the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified a monk who does not strive for gains but receives them nonetheless, and yet <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>remains mindful</b></a> and avoids negligence, as one who does not fall away from the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta noted that the disappointment of not receiving gains can lead an untrained monk to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>strike his breast</b></a> and fall into deep confusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta categorized practitioners based on how they manage their minds while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>living in seclusion</b></a> and leading a life of independence from others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta emphasized that the defining factor for not falling away from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>true Dhamma</b></a> is not the presence or absence of material wealth, but one's internal equanimity toward those conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>does not lament</b></a> over a lack of material support—even when he has specifically made an effort to obtain it—is considered spiritually secure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta warned that becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>intoxicated with gains</b></a> is just as spiritually hazardous as falling into despair over the lack of them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta described the state of a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>falls into confusion</b></a> as a direct consequence of an inability to handle the emotional toll of unfulfilled desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta used the specific term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>not falling away</b></a> (accuto) to describe those practitioners who maintain their Dhamma practice regardless of their material success or failure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta’s teaching implies that a monk might <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>strive and exert</b></a> himself for legitimate needs yet still fail to obtain them, serving as a test of his spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta pointed out that even a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato"><b>not making an effort</b></a> for gains can still fall away from the true Dhamma if he reacts to his lack of gains with sorrow and pining? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.77/en/sujato">AN 8.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk of bad character to a crow because both share ten unwholesome qualities, including being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.77/en/sujato"><b>offensive and impudent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.77/en/sujato">AN 10.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the ten traits a bad monk has in common with a crow is being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.77/en/sujato"><b>gluttonous and cruel</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.77/en/sujato">AN 10.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.77/en/sujato"><b>being a hoarder</b></a> and being merciless as specific negative characteristics found in both a common crow and a bad monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.77/en/sujato">AN 10.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a bad monk as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.77/en/sujato"><b>muddled in mindfulness</b></a> and a shouter, which are two of the ten qualities he used to liken such a person to a crow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.77/en/sujato">AN 10.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.77/en/sujato"><b>being squeamish</b></a> (or greedy) and weak as part of the ten unwholesome qualities that define the nature of both a crow and a monk of poor character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.77/en/sujato">AN 10.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified ten <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.79/en/sujato"><b>grounds for resentment</b></a>, which include the recurring thought that "this person has done me harm" in the past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.79/en/sujato">AN 10.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that resentment often arises from the anticipation that another person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.79/en/sujato"><b>will do harm</b></a> to us in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.79/en/sujato">AN 10.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that we often harbor resentment because we believe someone is currently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.79/en/sujato"><b>doing harm</b></a> to someone we love and cherish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.79/en/sujato">AN 10.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that one of the grounds for resentment is the anger felt when an enemy or someone we dislike <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.79/en/sujato"><b>receives a favor</b></a> from another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.79/en/sujato">AN 10.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one of the ten grounds for resentment is simply getting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.79/en/sujato"><b>angry groundlessly</b></a> or at an inappropriate occasion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.79/en/sujato">AN 10.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught ten methods for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.80/en/sujato"><b>removing resentment</b></a>, such as reflecting on a past harm and asking yourself, "How could it be otherwise?" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.80/en/sujato">AN 10.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that when we fear someone will harm us in the future, we should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.80/en/sujato"><b>dispel anger</b></a> by acknowledging that such outcomes are beyond our absolute control? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.80/en/sujato">AN 10.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that one can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.80/en/sujato"><b>remove resentment</b></a> toward those who harm our loved ones by reflecting that such occurrences are a part of life that cannot be simply wished away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.80/en/sujato">AN 10.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.80/en/sujato"><b>not getting angry</b></a> at inappropriate or groundless occasions as one of the ten key ways to practice the removal of resentment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.80/en/sujato">AN 10.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s strategy for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.80/en/sujato"><b>overcoming resentment</b></a> includes letting go of anger when an enemy is helped, by recognizing the futility of holding onto that ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.80/en/sujato">AN 10.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Tathāgata as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.81/en/sujato"><b>detached and released</b></a> from ten specific things, including the five aggregates of form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.81/en/sujato">AN 10.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.81/en/sujato"><b>lotus unsoiled by water</b></a> to describe how he dwells in the world completely detached from it, despite being born and raised within it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.81/en/sujato">AN 10.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the monk Vāhana that a Tathāgata dwells with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.81/en/sujato"><b>mind free of boundaries</b></a> because he has become unfettered from birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.81/en/sujato">AN 10.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.81/en/sujato"><b>released from suffering</b></a> and defilements, much like a beautiful blue, red, or white lotus rises above the water in which it grew? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.81/en/sujato">AN 10.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the Tathāgata dwells <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.81/en/sujato"><b>freed from the aggregates</b></a>, having transcended the limitations of physical form and mental processes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.81/en/sujato">AN 10.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is not devoted to mental development will find that his mind is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>liberated from the taints</b></a> through non-clinging, even if he should give rise to a strong wish for such liberation to occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk is devoted to mental development, his mind will be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>liberated from the taints</b></a> through non-clinging, even if he does not specifically give rise to a wish for that liberation to happen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the lack of development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>four foundations of mindfulness</b></a> as a reason why a practitioner remains unliberated from mental defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the cultivation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>four right strivings</b></a> is essential for the mind to reach a state of being "well-developed," which leads to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who neglects mental development to a hen who fails to properly sit on, warm, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>incubate her eggs</b></a>, making the chicks unable to break through their shells? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as chicks will safely emerge when a hen has properly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>incubated her eggs</b></a>, a monk devoted to practice will naturally find liberation regardless of whether he consciously wishes for it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a successful hen's chicks as being able to break through their eggshells using their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>claws and beaks</b></a> to emerge safely, which serves as a metaphor for the practitioner's liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>five faculties</b></a> and the five powers as two of the key sets of qualities that must be developed to ensure the mind is freed from clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a carpenter or a carpenter's apprentice looking at his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>adze handle</b></a> to show that daily progress in destroying the taints may not be immediately perceptible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a carpenter knows his tool's handle is wearing away because he sees the marks of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>fingers and thumb</b></a>, even if he cannot say exactly how much wood was worn away on any specific day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk devoted to development will eventually have the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>knowledge of exhaustion</b></a>—knowing his taints are destroyed—even if he cannot measure the incremental daily decrease of those taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> as a necessary component for a monk to reach the state where the mind is freed from the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>noble eightfold path</b></a> as the final group of factors in the list of things a monk must cultivate to ensure his mind is liberated from the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>ocean-going ship</b></a> that has been out at sea for six months to illustrate how the bindings of the fetters can be weakened over time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when an ocean ship is hauled onto dry land during the winter, its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>weathered bindings</b></a>—previously exposed to wind and sun—will rot and fall apart easily when hit by rain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a monk devoted to mental development, his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>fetters</b></a> will easily rot and subside, just like the rotted ropes of a weathered ship in the rainy season? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the failure to attain liberation as being due to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>undeveloped self</b></a> (abhāvitattā) regarding the various sets of qualities that lead to enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha attributed the successful liberation of a monk's mind to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>developed self</b></a> (bhāvitattā), specifically through the mastery of the thirty-seven aids to enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>four bases for psychic power</b></a> (iddhipāda) as one of the critical sets of mental qualities that must be cultivated for a monk to be liberated from clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching in the Bhāvanā Sutta highlights that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato"><b>mental development</b></a> is an active process that produces results through consistent application, rather than through mere wishing or passive desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.71/en/sujato">AN 7.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that a person is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>subject to decline</b></a> if they do not hear the Dhamma, forget what they have previously heard, and fail to understand mental states they were once unfamiliar with? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>not subject to decline</b></a> is defined as someone who consistently hears the Dhamma, ensures that heard teachings do not slip from memory, and gains understanding of previously unknown states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta urged practitioners to train themselves with the goal of becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>skilled in the ways of one’s own mind</b></a>, prioritizing this internal mastery even if they lack the skill to read the minds of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta used the simile of a young person examining their reflection in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>polished mirror</b></a> or a bowl of clear water to illustrate how a monk should look within to identify mental impurities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in Venerable Sāriputta’s teaching, just as someone would immediately strive to remove a blemish found on their face, a monk must exert effort to abandon any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome states</b></a> discovered during self-examination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that when a monk reflects and finds no unwholesome states, they should feel the same joy and satisfaction as someone seeing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>clean reflection</b></a> in a mirror? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta recommended that a monk regularly ask themselves whether they live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>free from covetousness</b></a> as a primary step in maintaining spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that one of the vital self-checks Venerable Sāriputta encouraged was determining whether one possesses a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>non-ill-willed mind</b></a>, which is essential for a person not subject to decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta highlighted that a monk should investigate whether they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>abandoned dullness and drowsiness</b></a>, ensuring the mind remains alert and active in practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that self-reflection must include checking if one is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>free from restlessness</b></a>, as a calm and steady mind is a hallmark of a wholesome state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>crossed over doubt</b></a> as a key indicator of spiritual health that every practitioner should look for during their internal reviews? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta emphasized that a monk should evaluate whether they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>free from anger</b></a>, and if they find this quality present, they should remain established in it to reach further goals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta described an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>unsoiled mind</b></a> as a crucial wholesome quality that a monk must verify within themselves through frequent introspection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, a practitioner should check if they have gained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>joy in the Dhamma</b></a> internally, as this joy supports the endurance required for the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that monks should assess whether they have attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>internal serenity of mind</b></a>, a state necessary for deep meditation and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>insight into phenomena</b></a> through higher wisdom as one of the ten specific qualities a monk should look for to ensure they are not declining in their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta used the dramatic simile of someone with their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>clothes or head on fire</b></a> to describe the intense urgency a monk should feel when they realize they lack wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta instructed that if a monk finds they lack all the necessary wholesome states, they must apply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>extraordinary desire and effort</b></a> to acquire them immediately? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even if a monk possesses some good qualities but lacks others, Venerable Sāriputta taught they should stay grounded in their existing virtues while striving for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato"><b>attainment of missing states</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.55/en/sujato">AN 10.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, one of the essential traits for a monk’s progress is being someone who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>quick understanding</b></a> regarding wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta emphasized that a monk should be one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>retains the teachings</b></a> he has heard, as this retention is a foundation for benefiting both himself and his community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated a monk must be an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>examiner of the meaning</b></a> of the teachings he has learned if he wishes to be truly fit for his own welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, understanding both the meaning and the text of the teachings is what allows a monk to be one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>practices in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta described a monk who is fit to benefit others as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>excellent speech</b></a> that is polite, distinct, and clear in communicating the meaning of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk who is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>inspirer of companions</b></a>—one who instructs, encourages, and gladdens his fellow practitioners—is capable of greatly benefiting others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that a monk who possesses good speech and inspires others, but does not examine the meaning of the Dhamma or practice accordingly, is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>fit for others</b></a> but not for himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified a monk who examines the meaning and practices the Dhamma but lacks refined speech as someone <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>fit for himself</b></a> but not for others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even if a monk lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>quick understanding</b></a> of wholesome states, Venerable Sāriputta says he can still benefit both himself and others if he retains the teachings, examines their meaning, practices accordingly, and has good communication skills? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta highlighted that without being an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>examiner of the meaning</b></a> of the Dhamma, a monk is generally incapable of benefiting himself, even if he is a talented speaker? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that personal spiritual welfare is fundamentally tied to being one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>practices the Dhamma</b></a> in accordance with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta regarded <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>clear communication</b></a> of the Dhamma as a mandatory requirement for any monk to be considered capable of benefiting his fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta specified that a monk should be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>gladdener of companions</b></a>, using his speech to uplift and inspire those living the spiritual life alongside him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta described the ideal monk's speech as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>polite and distinct</b></a>, which ensures that the profound meaning of the teachings is successfully conveyed to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, a monk can be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>fit for himself</b></a> even with just two qualities—examining the meaning of the Dhamma and practicing accordingly—even if he lacks social skills like eloquent speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that a monk who only possesses excellent speech and the ability to inspire others is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>fit for others</b></a> but lacks the internal qualities necessary to benefit his own spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>quick to learn</b></a> wholesome states as a characteristic that contributes to a monk's overall fitness to help both himself and his community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato"><b>memorizer of the Dhamma</b></a> he has heard is better equipped to serve both his own growth and the needs of his fellow monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.78/en/sujato">AN 8.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> as the single quality which, when developed and cultivated, secures welfare in both this present life and the future life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the supremacy of heedfulness to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>elephant’s footprint</b></a>, noting that just as the footprints of all land-dwelling animals fit within an elephant’s footprint because of its great size, all beneficial qualities are rooted in heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by a Brahmin if one thing could provide both immediate and long-term benefit, the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> is that unique quality because it encompasses both types of welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>gabled house</b></a> to describe heedfulness, stating that just as all the rafters lead to, slope toward, and meet at the roof peak, all wholesome states are led by and converge in heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>peak</b></a> of a gabled house is considered the foremost part of the structure, just as heedfulness is the foremost of all states that lead to welfare in both this life and the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the practice of heedfulness to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>reaper of grass</b></a> who, after cutting the grass, shakes it and beats it to clean it, illustrating how heedfulness serves to secure one’s welfare through diligent effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the interconnectedness of beneficial results by using the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>bunch of mangoes</b></a>, explaining that when the main stalk of the bunch is cut, all the mangoes attached to it follow along, much like how all benefits follow from the cultivation of heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as all petty kings are followers of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>Wheel-turning Monarch</b></a>, who is considered the foremost among them, so too are all wholesome qualities secondary to the supreme quality of heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>moonlight</b></a> is the foremost of all lights from the stars, just as heedfulness is the foremost quality for achieving success in both the present world and the world to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the radiance of the stars does not equal even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>one-sixteenth</b></a> of the radiance of the moon, using this to emphasize that heedfulness is the preeminent dharma for securing dual welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha explained how heedfulness secures both types of welfare, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin</b></a> who asked the question was so impressed that he immediately went for refuge to the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Saṅgha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> in this sutta specifically addresses how to achieve the "visible" or present-life welfare (diṭṭhadhammika attha) alongside welfare in the life to come (samparāyika attho)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>rafters</b></a> of a gabled house as being "peak-directed" and "peak-sloping," serving as a metaphor for how all virtuous actions should be directed toward and supported by heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>size</b></a> of the elephant's footprint as the reason it is considered the "best" or "foremost" of footprints, comparing this encompassing nature to how heedfulness contains all other beneficial dhammas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by cultivating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a>, one "takes hold of both benefits," referring to the successful attainment of welfare in both the current and future lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in response to a visitor's inquiry about a single practice, the Buddha used the image of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>stalk of mangoes</b></a> to show that if one holds onto the primary quality of heedfulness, all other specific benefits will naturally be included? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato"><b>petty kings</b></a> as being "subject to" or "following" the Wheel-turning Monarch, much like how all other virtues are subservient to the power of heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.53/en/sujato">AN 6.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Venerable Ānanda that it is impossible for a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>lacking faith</b></a> to achieve growth, increase, or fulfillment in the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Ānanda that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>immoral</b></a> has no possibility of reaching growth, expansion, or fulfillment in this Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a monk of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>little learning</b></a> cannot expect to attain growth or increase within the training of the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that growth and fulfillment in the Dhamma and Discipline are impossible for a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>difficult to admonish</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>having bad friends</b></a>, he cannot achieve growth, increase, or fulfillment in the spiritual path of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned Ānanda that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>lazy</b></a> monk will find it impossible to reach fulfillment or growth in this Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>muddle-minded</b></a> and lacks clear awareness cannot reach growth, increase, or fulfillment in the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Venerable Ānanda that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>discontented</b></a> makes it impossible for a monk to achieve any significant growth or expansion in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified that a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>having evil wishes</b></a> cannot find fulfillment, growth, or increase within the Buddha’s Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated to Ānanda that a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> cannot possibly achieve growth or fulfillment in the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>faithful</b></a> has the potential to achieve growth, increase, and fulfillment in the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha assured Ānanda that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> is one who can successfully achieve growth and fulfillment in the spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>much learning</b></a> who retains what he has heard is capable of achieving great growth and increase in the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>easy to admonish</b></a>, he is on the path toward achieving growth, increase, and fulfillment in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Ānanda that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>having good friends</b></a> is a quality that allows a monk to reach expansion and fulfillment in the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>energetic</b></a> is a necessary condition for a monk to achieve growth and increase in this Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato"><b>mindful</b></a> and maintains present awareness can expect to achieve growth and fulfillment in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.82/en/sujato">AN 10.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encountered a monk named Dhammika who was expelled from seven successive monasteries because he would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>insult and revile</b></a> visiting monks until they all left the residences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the lay followers in Venerable Dhammika's birthplace eventually decided to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>expel him</b></a> from all seven of their monasteries because his verbal abuse was driving away the rest of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Dhammika was sent away by the laypeople, the Buddha compared him to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>shore-sighting bird</b></a> used by seafaring merchants, which flies in all directions to find land but returns to the ship if none is found? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the story of a majestic banyan tree named Suppatiṭṭha, which provided a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>cool shade</b></a> and had a massive canopy extending twelve leagues wide? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the Suppatiṭṭha banyan tree had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>enormous fruits</b></a> as large as a measuring pot and as sweet as pure wildflower honey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how the Suppatiṭṭha tree's fruit was shared harmoniously among five groups: the king and his harem, the army, town and country folk, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>ascetics and brahmins</b></a>, and even beasts and birds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how a man, after eating his fill of the Suppatiṭṭha tree’s fruit, ungratefully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>broke a branch</b></a> and went away, prompting the tree deity to stop the tree from ever bearing fruit again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how Sakka, the lord of the devas, used his psychic power to create a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>fierce storm</b></a> that uprooted the Suppatiṭṭha banyan tree as a lesson to its deity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka told the weeping tree deity that the tree was uprooted because the deity had failed to stand in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>duty of a tree</b></a>, which requires allowing people to take its roots, bark, leaves, and fruit without resentment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the tree deity promised to observe the proper duty of a tree, Sakka used his powers to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>restore the tree</b></a> to its original state with its roots firmly planted and its bark healed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>duty of an ascetic</b></a> as not scolding in return when scolded, not acting out of anger when others are angry, and not quarreling in return when someone quarrels? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhammika admitted to the Buddha that he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>not stood</b></a> in the duty of an ascetic when the laypeople expelled him from their monasteries? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned a past teacher named Sunetta who was a non-Buddhist founder of a sect and was completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>free from lust</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who gained confidence in the teacher Sunetta were reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> after death, while those with depraved minds toward him were reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed six famous past teachers—Sunetta, Mūgapakkha, Aranemi, Kuddālaka, Hatthipāla, and Jotipāla—who all taught the path to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>Brahma world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who insults even one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>person possessed of view</b></a> generates far more demerit than one who insults all six famous past teachers who were free from lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to train themselves so that their minds would never be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>depraved</b></a> toward their fellow practitioners in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>seventh person</b></a> in the noble Sangha (the one practicing for the realization of the fruit of stream-entry) as one who has abandoned the basis for wrong views and should never be attacked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a monk has undeveloped or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>weak faculties</b></a> of faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom, he is still a disciple of the Buddha and should not be reviled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that a person who attacks a fellow monk actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>destroys himself</b></a> first before he can harm the other person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by protecting oneself through virtuous behavior, one simultaneously <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>protects others</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a wise person should always keep themselves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>unhurt</b></a> by avoiding the verbal abuse of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha addressed Venerable Dhammika as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>"brahmin Dhammika"</b></a> throughout their conversation at Vulture's Peak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the past teachers as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>compassionate</b></a> and having transcended the fetters of sensual desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that he does not advocate for the same kind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>patience</b></a> toward those outside the Dhamma as he does toward one’s own fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the roots of the legendary Suppatiṭṭha banyan tree extended <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>five leagues</b></a> deep into the earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said the six past teachers like Sunetta and Jotipāla had hundreds of disciples who also achieved <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>rebirth</b></a> in the Brahma world because of their teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a man insults a monk with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>depraved thoughts</b></a>, he produces a massive amount of demerit that leads to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the past teachers as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>fragrance-free</b></a> (nirāmagandhā), a term Bhikkhu Bodhi notes refers to being free from the "stink" of defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded his advice to Dhammika by stating that when one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato"><b>protects oneself</b></a>, the external world is also protected? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.54/en/sujato">AN 6.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once used a massive, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>blazing fire</b></a> that he saw while traveling with a large group of monks to illustrate the dangers of being an immoral person in the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person of bad character and immoral conduct, it would be better to embrace a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>mass of fire</b></a> than to embrace a beautiful woman of the noble, brahmin, or householder class? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>immoral person</b></a> who pretends to be a monk while being "rotten within" faces long-term suffering and rebirth in hell for accepting the services and respect of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is better for an immoral person to have their legs <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>crushed and torn</b></a> by a strong man using a rough hair-rope than to accept the respectful salutations of wealthy nobles or brahmins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that physical torture in the present life only leads to death or pain, but the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>spiritual corruption</b></a> of an immoral monk receiving unearned honor leads to rebirth in a state of misery or hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared receiving a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>reverent salutation</b></a> (añjali) while being immoral to having one's chest pierced by a sharp, oil-polished spear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught it would be better for a corrupt person to have their body wrapped in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>glowing iron sheet</b></a> than to use robes given in faith by wealthy laypeople? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the danger of unearned alms by saying it is better to have one's mouth forced open with an iron spike and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>red-hot iron ball</b></a> tossed inside than to consume almsfood given in faith while being immoral? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a horrific scene where a red-hot iron ball would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>burn the mouth</b></a>, tongue, throat, chest, and intestines before passing through the body, yet claimed this was preferable to an immoral person eating the food of the faithful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said it is better for an immoral person to be forced to sit or lie on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>red-hot iron bed</b></a> or chair than to enjoy the beds and chairs provided by faithful donors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed it is better for a corrupt practitioner to be thrown headfirst into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>boiling copper cauldron</b></a> than to live in a dwelling or monastery provided through the faith of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of someone <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>swirling in foam</b></a> while being cooked in a boiling cauldron to emphasize the severity of the consequences for monks who lack virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to train so that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>offerings they consume</b></a>, such as robes and almsfood, would result in great fruit and benefit for the donors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted his followers to practice in a way that ensures their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>going forth</b></a> into the monkhood (pabbajjā) is not in vain, but becomes fruitful and productive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should strive with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a> (appamāda) whether they are considering their own benefit, the benefit of others, or the benefit of both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the physical death caused by embracing a fire is temporary, whereas the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>rebirth in hell</b></a> caused by immorality leads to suffering for a very long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to an immoral monk as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>internal rot</b></a> (antopūtikassa) to describe their spiritual state despite their outward appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>swept-out like rubbish</b></a> (kasambujātassa) to characterize those who pretend to be practitioners of the holy life but are actually corrupt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was giving this intense teaching, sixty monks were so affected that they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>vomited hot blood</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that sixty monks decided to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>give up the training</b></a> and return to the lower life of a layperson after hearing the Buddha's discourse, feeling the holy life was too difficult to maintain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the severity of the teaching, sixty monks were able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>liberate their minds</b></a> from the taints through non-clinging while listening to the Buddha speak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified an immoral person as one who carries out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>hidden deeds</b></a> while publicly claiming to be a true ascetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk of bad character as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>full of lust</b></a> (avassutassa), which contributes to their spiritual downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even the most painful physical sensations, like having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>sharp spear</b></a> driven into the chest, are minor compared to the agony of hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically mentioned that an immoral monk's acceptance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>robes</b></a> (cīvara) given in faith leads to long-term harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>faith of the donor</b></a> (saddhādeyyaṃ) is a sacred thing that should not be exploited by those who do not keep their vows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on the fire-simile took place while he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>wandering among the Kosalans</b></a> with a large community of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that for the immoral, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>breaking of the body</b></a> after death results in rebirth in a state of deprivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato"><b>soft and tender</b></a> hands and feet of noble women to the searing heat of a fire to show how deceptive worldly pleasures can be for a corrupt monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.72/en/sujato">AN 7.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.56/en/sujato"><b>perception of unattractiveness</b></a> (asubhasaññā) as one of the primary meditative reflections that lead to great fruit and benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.56/en/sujato">AN 10.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.56/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-delight in the entire world</b></a> as a key mental training for reaching the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.56/en/sujato">AN 10.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that developing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.56/en/sujato"><b>perception of suffering in what is impermanent</b></a> is a specific technique used to deepen a practitioner's insight into the nature of reality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.56/en/sujato">AN 10.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.56/en/sujato"><b>perception of cessation</b></a> is among the ten perceptions that culminate in the realization of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.56/en/sujato">AN 10.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.57/en/sujato"><b>perception of a skeleton</b></a> in a list of ten meditative contemplations designed to lead a person toward spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.57/en/sujato">AN 10.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that contemplating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.57/en/sujato"><b>perception of a worm-infested corpse</b></a> is a valid spiritual exercise for developing detachment from the physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.57/en/sujato">AN 10.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.57/en/sujato"><b>perception of a livid corpse</b></a> (vinīlakasaññā) as one of several cemetery contemplations that bring about great spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.57/en/sujato">AN 10.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to cultivate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.57/en/sujato"><b>perception of a bloated corpse</b></a> as a means to overcome attachment and realize the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.57/en/sujato">AN 10.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to his followers that all things (dhammas) are fundamentally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato"><b>rooted in desire</b></a> (chandamūlakā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato">AN 10.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all mental and physical phenomena <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato"><b>originate from attention</b></a> (manasikārasambhavā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato">AN 10.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, all things have their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato"><b>arising from contact</b></a> (phassasamudayā) between the senses and their objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato">AN 10.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that all things <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato"><b>converge on feeling</b></a> (vedanāsamosaraṇā), highlighting the importance of how we experience sensations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato">AN 10.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all things are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato"><b>governed by mindfulness</b></a> (satādhipateyyā), which acts as the presiding factor in one's practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato">AN 10.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that all things are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato"><b>surmounted by wisdom</b></a> (paññuttarā), meaning wisdom is the highest point of understanding them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato">AN 10.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified that all things have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato"><b>liberation as their core</b></a> (vimuttisārā), suggesting that freedom is the essential value of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato">AN 10.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the entire path of all things ultimately <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato"><b>ends in Nibbāna</b></a> (nibbānapariyosānā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.58/en/sujato">AN 10.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified eight <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>grounds for laziness</b></a> that prevent a person from arousing energy to reach the unattained and realize the unrealized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how the prospect of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>upcoming work</b></a> can be used as an excuse to lie down and avoid spiritual exertion, rather than practicing while one still has the chance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even after <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>completing work</b></a>, a lazy person will often choose to lie down, claiming their body is exhausted, instead of arousing energy for the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person facing an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>impending journey</b></a> may use the anticipation of travel fatigue as a reason to be lazy and neglect their spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>finished a journey</b></a> will often justify lying down by claiming they are tired, thereby failing to arouse energy for spiritual attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>sufficient food</b></a> during an alms-round can lead a person to think their body is weak and unfit for work, prompting them to lie down instead of practicing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that receiving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>abundant food</b></a> can also be a ground for laziness, as a person may feel heavy and unfit for exertion after eating their fill? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>slight illness</b></a> can become a ground for laziness if a practitioner uses it as a reason to lie down rather than striving for the unattained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>recovering from illness</b></a> might still avoid spiritual effort by claiming their body is too weak for exertion, despite having recently recovered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted laziness with eight <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>grounds for arousal of energy</b></a>, which are specific situations that should inspire a person to strive for spiritual breakthroughs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>work to do</b></a> to arouse energy beforehand, recognizing that they will not easily be able to attend to the teachings while working? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once a person has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>finished their work</b></a>, they should arouse energy because they are now free from the distraction of labor and can focus on the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised those about to set out on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>journey</b></a> to arouse energy immediately, knowing that travel will hinder their ability to contemplate the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that after a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>journey is completed</b></a>, a person should arouse energy because they are no longer traveling and can now focus on their spiritual goals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when a practitioner receives <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>scant food</b></a>, they should view their body as light and fit for practice, using that lightness as a reason to arouse energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that receiving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>plenty of food</b></a> should be seen as making the body strong and fit, providing the necessary physical support to arouse energy for practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>slight illness</b></a> is actually a reason to arouse energy right away, as there is a possibility that the illness might worsen and prevent future practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that someone <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>newly recovered</b></a> from illness should arouse energy immediately, fearing that the illness might return and block their spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the vivid analogy of a body feeling like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>load of soaked beans</b></a> to describe the heaviness and unfitness for work that follows overeating? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that arousing energy is for the sake of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>attainment of the unattained</b></a> and the realization of what has not yet been realized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the primary difference between a lazy person and an energetic one is whether they choose to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>lie down</b></a> or to strive when faced with the same physical conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically identified the inability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato"><b>attend to the teaching</b></a> of the Buddhas during busy times as a powerful motivator for arousing energy in the present moment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.80/en/sujato">AN 8.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Venerable Puṇṇiya that one of the reasons a Dhamma talk might occur is when a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato"><b>faithful</b></a>, noting that if faith is absent, the inspiration for the Tathāgata to teach may not arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato">AN 10.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even if a monk has faith, the Tathāgata is often inspired to give a Dhamma talk only if that monk also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato"><b>approaches</b></a> the teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato">AN 10.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, he is prompted to give a Dhamma talk when a monk who has approached him then continues to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato"><b>attend upon</b></a> the teacher with respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato">AN 10.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that an essential factor for him to be inspired to teach is when a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato"><b>inquires</b></a> about the teachings, rather than remaining silent after visiting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato">AN 10.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that he is inspired to give a Dhamma talk when a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato"><b>lends an ear</b></a>, listening to the Dhamma with a focused and attentive mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato">AN 10.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Venerable Puṇṇiya that a condition for the Tathāgata’s inspiration to teach is when a monk, having heard the Dhamma, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato"><b>retains the teachings</b></a> in his memory for further reflection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato">AN 10.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that his inspiration to give a Dhamma talk is strengthened when a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato"><b>examines the meaning</b></a> of the teachings he has memorized, seeking a deeper understanding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato">AN 10.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that he is inspired to teach when a monk, after understanding the meaning of the teachings, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato"><b>practices in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato">AN 10.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that he is inspired to give a Dhamma talk when a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato"><b>possessing good speech</b></a>, characterized by a delivery that is polite, clear, and helpful for expressing the meaning of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.83/en/sujato">AN 10.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Soṇa was struggling with his practice, he considered returning to the lay life because he felt that despite being very energetic, his mind was not being liberated from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa once contemplated leaving the monkhood to enjoy his family's wealth and make merit as a layman, prompting the Buddha to use a famous <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>musical simile</b></a> to guide him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his psychic power to know Venerable Soṇa’s private thoughts and instantly traveled from Vulture’s Peak Mountain to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>Cool Wood</b></a> to instruct him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Venerable Soṇa about his past skill as a layperson playing the lute to illustrate how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>strings of practice</b></a> must be properly tuned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if the strings of a lute are stretched too tight, the instrument is neither <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>tuneful nor playable</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that if the strings of a lute are too loose, the instrument will not be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>ready for use</b></a> or produce a proper sound? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a lute is only tuneful and playable when the strings are neither too tight nor too loose, but established in an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>even pitch</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that energy that is too forcefully exerted leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>restlessness</b></a>, which can hinder spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that energy that is too lax inevitably leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>laziness</b></a>, preventing one from reaching enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Venerable Soṇa to determine the right measure of energy and to strike an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>equilibrium of the faculties</b></a> to achieve success in meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after following the Buddha's advice on balancing his energy, Venerable Soṇa dwelt alone and diligent until he became an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after attaining enlightenment, Venerable Soṇa visited the Buddha to declare his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>final knowledge</b></a> by describing the six things an enlightened monk is intent upon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa clarified that an Arahant is intent on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>renunciation</b></a> not out of mere faith, but because they have completely destroyed lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Soṇa, an enlightened monk is intent on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> because they have finished their task and no longer crave gain, honor, or renown? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa stated that an Arahant is intent on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>non-affliction</b></a> because they no longer see rules and vows as the essence of the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a> as one of the six fundamental qualities that a fully liberated monk is intent upon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa explained that a monk whose mind is rightly liberated is intent on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>destruction of grasping</b></a> through the fading away of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa described an enlightened person as being intent on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>non-delusion</b></a>, having reached the end of all mental confusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa taught that even if extremely beautiful or ugly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>forms</b></a> come into the range of an Arahant's vision, they do not overwhelm their mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa emphasized that for a person with a liberated mind, any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>sounds</b></a> that strike the ear fail to seize the mind, which remains steady and imperturbable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa explained that an Arahant's mind remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>unmixed</b></a> and steady, even when encountering powerful smells, tastes, or tactile sensations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>solid rock mountain</b></a> to describe the mind of an Arahant, which cannot be shaken by any sensory experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa compared sensory objects to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>storm of wind and rain</b></a> that, despite coming from any of the four directions, cannot shake a massive, solid mountain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa taught that a monk with a peaceful mind has no need to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>accumulate</b></a> what has already been done, as there is no further task for them to complete? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa described the liberated mind as being established in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>imperturbability</b></a> while simultaneously observing the passing away of all conditioned things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Soṇa declared that a mind is rightly liberated only after one has clearly seen the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato"><b>arising of the sense bases</b></a> and their cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.55/en/sujato">AN 6.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of an ancient teacher named Sunetta who was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato"><b>founder of a sect</b></a> and was entirely free from lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato">AN 7.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how the ancient teacher Sunetta taught his many hundreds of disciples the path for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato"><b>rebirth in the company of Brahmā</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato">AN 7.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those disciples who developed a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato"><b>confident mind</b></a> toward the teachings of the lust-free teacher Sunetta were reborn in a good destination, a heavenly world, after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato">AN 7.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that disciples who did not settle their minds with confidence in the teachings of the lust-free founder Sunetta were reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato"><b>state of misery</b></a> and even in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato">AN 7.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed seven specific ancient teachers—Sunetta, Mūgapakkha, Aranemi, Kuddālaka, Hatthipāla, Jotipāla, and Araka—who were all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato"><b>free from lust</b></a> and had many hundreds of disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato">AN 7.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically identified the ancient teacher <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato"><b>Araka</b></a> as a founder of a sect who, like Sunetta, was free from lust and taught his disciples the way to the Brahma-world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato">AN 7.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person generates a great deal of demerit if they revile or insult seven ancient, lust-free teachers and their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato"><b>assemblies of disciples</b></a> with a malicious mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato">AN 7.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that reviling just one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato"><b>person possessed of right view</b></a> with a malicious mind generates even more demerit than insulting seven ancient teachers who were free from lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato">AN 7.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the negative results of being malicious toward a single fellow practitioner in his dispensation are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato"><b>much greater</b></a> than those resulting from insulting multiple famous non-Buddhist teachers who were free from sensual desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato">AN 7.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that he does not advocate for the same kind of tolerance for those outside the community as he does for how one should treat one's own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato"><b>fellow monks</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato">AN 7.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers to train themselves specifically so that their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato"><b>minds will not be malicious</b></a> toward their fellow monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.73/en/sujato">AN 7.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if the ten perceptions are recited to a sick monk, it is possible that their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>affliction will subside</b></a> immediately upon hearing them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>perception of impermanence</b></a> as the practice of a monk contemplating the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—as being transient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-self</b></a> involves reflecting on the six internal and external sense bases, such as the eye and forms, as being devoid of a permanent self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to practice the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>perception of unattractiveness</b></a> by mentally reviewing the body from the soles of the feet to the top of the head as full of various impurities like bile, phlegm, and pus? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>perception of danger</b></a> as the contemplation of the physical body as a source of numerous pains and diverse afflictions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the body is subject to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>numerous diseases</b></a> including leprosy, boils, eczema, asthma, and diabetes, which are considered inherent dangers of having a physical form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>perception of abandoning</b></a> as the active practice of not tolerating arisen sensual thoughts or thoughts of ill will, but instead eliminating and doing away with them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>perception of dispassion</b></a> as the realization that the stilling of all formations and the destruction of craving is a peaceful and sublime state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>perception of cessation</b></a> as the specific reflection that the relinquishing of all acquisitions and the attainment of Nibbāna is the ultimate peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-delight</b></a> in the entire world as the act of abandoning the mental standpoints, adherences, and underlying tendencies that lead to clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>perception of repulsiveness</b></a> of all formations as a state where a practitioner feels repelled, humiliated, and disgusted by all conditioned things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's method for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of breathing</b></a> begins with the practitioner going to a secluded place like a forest and sitting down cross-legged with an erect body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when practicing breath meditation, one must be fully aware of whether they are breathing in or out a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>long breath</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a trainee to explicitly train in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>experiencing the whole body</b></a> while simultaneously maintaining mindfulness of the inhalation and exhalation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a practitioner should train to breathe in and out while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>tranquilizing the bodily formation</b></a> to deepen their concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as part of the mental development in breathing, the Buddha instructed that one should train to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>experience rapture</b></a> and happiness during the meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that meditators should train to breathe while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>experiencing the mental formation</b></a> and subsequently train to tranquilize that mental formation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave instructions to train in breathing while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>gladdening the mind</b></a> and concentrating the mind to achieve mental liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>contemplating impermanence</b></a> as a vital stage of mindfulness of breathing, requiring the practitioner to observe transience with every breath? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the final stage of breath meditation involves breathing in and out while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>contemplating relinquishment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Venerable Ānanda recited the ten perceptions to the sick Venerable Girimānanda, the latter’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>illness was cured</b></a> and he rose from his bed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified that bodily afflictions can arise from several <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato"><b>internal causes</b></a>, such as imbalances in bile, phlegm, or wind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.60/en/sujato">AN 10.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a> are absent in a person, the necessary foundation for the development of moral shame and moral dread is destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato">AN 8.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who lacks moral shame and moral dread also lacks the vital support required for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato"><b>restraint of the sense faculties</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato">AN 8.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a lack of restraint over one's sense faculties directly leads to the failure and destruction of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato"><b>virtuous behavior</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato">AN 8.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for someone whose virtuous behavior has failed, the essential requirement for achieving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> is missing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato">AN 8.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that without the achievement of right concentration, it is impossible to attain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of things as they really are</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato">AN 8.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a practitioner lacks knowledge and vision of things as they really are, they also lack the proximate cause for developing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato"><b>revulsion and dispassion</b></a> toward worldly phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato">AN 8.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the ultimate goal of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a> cannot be fulfilled if the preceding quality of revulsion and dispassion is absent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato">AN 8.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a tree with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato"><b>deficient branches and foliage</b></a> to illustrate how a person lacking mindfulness cannot fully develop the subsequent stages of the spiritual path, such as the bark, sapwood, and heartwood of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato">AN 8.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person possessing mindfulness and clear comprehension to a healthy tree with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato"><b>complete branches and foliage</b></a>, which allows the bark, sapwood, and heartwood to reach full growth and perfection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato">AN 8.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato"><b>conditional sequence</b></a> of mental states where the presence of one quality, such as right concentration, naturally serves as the vital support for the next quality, leading all the way to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.81/en/sujato">AN 8.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna explained that when a monk makes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>claim of final knowledge</b></a>, asserting that birth is destroyed and the spiritual life has been lived, he may be subject to intense scrutiny by the Tathāgata or a fellow disciple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna stated that a monk who falsely claims enlightenment will fall into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>distress and failure</b></a> when he is closely interrogated and examined by a teacher or a peer skilled in reading the minds of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>angry</b></a> and dwelling with a mind overwhelmed by anger is considered a form of decline in the Dhamma and Discipline proclaimed by the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahāmoggallāna, a monk who dwells with a mind overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>hostility</b></a> is experiencing a state of spiritual decline that prevents progress in the Buddha's teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>contempt</b></a> as one of the ten qualities that constitute a decline in the Dhamma and Discipline for a monk who has not yet reached the end of the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna pointed out that a mind overwhelmed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>insolence</b></a> is a significant obstacle that leads to failure and disaster when a practitioner's spiritual claims are tested? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna explained that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>envious</b></a> and dwells with a mind full of envy is unable to achieve growth or fulfillment in the spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>avarice</b></a>, or a mind possessed by stinginess, is a quality that marks a practitioner's decline within the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna taught that if a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>deceitful</b></a> and dwells with a mind full of deceit, he cannot reach growth, increase, or fulfillment in this path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna warned that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>fraudulent</b></a> and dwells with a mind possessed by fraud is considered to be in a state of spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>evil wishes</b></a> as a primary reason why a monk might experience failure and distress when his spiritual state is examined by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna taught that it is a form of decline for a monk to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>stopping halfway</b></a> in his spiritual progress because he has achieved some mere trifling distinction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna asserted that there is no possibility for a monk to achieve growth, increase, or fulfillment in the Dhamma if he does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>abandon</b></a> the ten unwholesome qualities like anger and deceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna explained that a monk who has successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>given up</b></a> the ten detrimental qualities, such as envy and hostility, can truly expect growth and expansion in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna described how a teacher <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>reading another's mind</b></a> can discern if a monk making a claim of enlightenment is actually still overwhelmed by unwholesome states like greed or anger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna pointed out that achieving a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>trifling distinction</b></a> in meditation or knowledge should not lead a monk to stop his efforts while there is still more to be done in the spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna stated that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>scrutinized</b></a> by those skilled in the ways of others' minds will be found wanting if his mind is still possessed by qualities like insolence or evil wishes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato"><b>ten qualities</b></a> ranging from anger to stopping halfway in progress are precisely what define a practitioner's decline in the Buddha’s Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.84/en/sujato">AN 10.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha critiqued the teacher Purana Kassapa for arbitrarily assigning people to classes of birth, comparing it to forcing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>piece of meat</b></a> on a poor man who did not want it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the reports of Ananda, the teacher Purana Kassapa designated butchers, hunters, and thieves as belonging to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>black class of birth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Purana Kassapa classified monks who follow a "doctrine of action" or "doctrine of deed" as belonging to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>blue class of birth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the teacher Purana Kassapa categorized the Niganthas (Jains) who wear only a single garment as belonging to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>red class of birth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Purana Kassapa designated white-clad lay disciples of naked ascetics as belonging to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>yellow class of birth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that male and female Ajivikas were classified by Purana Kassapa as belonging to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>white class of birth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Purana Kassapa assigned figures such as Makkhali Gosala and Nanda Vaccha to what he called the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>highest white class of birth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person born in a "dark class" as one born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>low family</b></a>—such as a family of Chandalas, hunters, or bamboo-workers—where food and clothing are difficult to obtain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person born into a dark, impoverished class who continues to engage in misconduct of body, speech, and mind is "one born dark who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>generates dark states</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that regardless of a person’s low birth or physical appearance, if they practice good conduct in body, speech, and mind, they are "one born dark who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>generates bright states</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that even a person born in a low, dark class can go forth into homelessness and, by abandoning the five hindrances, attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>neither-dark-nor-bright Nibbana</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person born in a "bright class" as someone born into an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>affluent family</b></a>, such as a high-class Khattiya or Brahmin family with great wealth and resources? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person born into a wealthy and prestigious family who engages in misconduct of body, speech, and mind is "one born bright who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>generates dark states</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person born into a wealthy, "bright" class who continues to practice good conduct is "one born bright who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>generates bright states</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person of high birth and great wealth can still choose to go forth into homelessness and practice the path to reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>neither-dark-nor-bright Nibbana</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that engaging in misconduct of body, speech, and mind leads a person, after death, to a state of misery, a bad destination, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>rebirth in hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that practicing good conduct of body, speech, and mind leads a person, after the breakup of the body, to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>happy destination</b></a> in the heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "dark class" of birth as being characterized by poverty and physical challenges, such as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>deformed or sickly</b></a>, with little access to basic necessities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a "bright class" of birth as being born into a family with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>abundant gold and silver</b></a>, where the person is handsome, attractive, and well-provided for? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for anyone to reach Nibbana, they must first <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>abandon the five hindrances</b></a>, which are corruptions of the mind that weaken wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s path to Nibbana involves having the mind well-established in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that developing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> as they really are is a necessary step for attaining the neither-dark-nor-bright Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha criticized Purana Kassapa's teaching on birth classes because it was proclaimed without the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato"><b>consent of the ascetics</b></a> and brahmins he was categorizing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.57/en/sujato">AN 6.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified six specific methods—restraint, use, endurance, avoidance, dispelling, and development—through which a monk abandons <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a> to become worthy of gifts and hospitality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world when he successfully abandons taints through six different approaches of practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that taints causing vexation and fever can be prevented if a monk dwells with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>restraint of the eye faculty</b></a> through wise consideration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk should practice the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>restraint of the mind faculty</b></a> to ensure that taints which would otherwise arise from lack of control do not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk abandons certain taints through the wise use of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>robes</b></a>, utilizing them only for protection against cold, heat, insects, and to conceal the private parts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> should be used not for amusement or beauty, but solely for the support of the body and to assist the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk uses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>lodgings</b></a> specifically to dispel the perils of the seasons and to enjoy the benefits of seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>medicinal requisites</b></a> should be used only to ward off painful feelings that have arisen and for the sake of maintaining health? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk must be one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>patiently endures</b></a> cold, heat, hunger, thirst, and the stings of insects to abandon the taints associated with non-endurance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to abandon taints by being patient with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>harsh and disagreeable words</b></a> and even severe, life-threatening physical pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a monk should use wise consideration to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>avoid dangerous animals</b></a> such as wild elephants, horses, bulls, dogs, and snakes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should avoid physical hazards like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>deep pits</b></a>, precipices, cesspools, and open sewers to prevent the arising of taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk must avoid <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>bad friends</b></a> and unsuitable resorts that would cause his wise companions to suspect him of unwholesome behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that certain taints are abandoned when a monk does not tolerate an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>arisen sensual thought</b></a> but instead dispels and eliminates it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>dispel thoughts of cruelty</b></a> and any other unwholesome states as soon as they arise, preventing them from persisting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that developing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness enlightenment factor</b></a>, which is based on seclusion and dispassion, is a method for abandoning taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>investigation of phenomena</b></a> as one of the seven enlightenment factors a monk should cultivate to abandon taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>energy enlightenment factor</b></a> helps a monk abandon the taints that arise from a lack of spiritual cultivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that taints are abandoned through the cultivation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>rapture and tranquility</b></a> enlightenment factors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk should develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>equanimity enlightenment factor</b></a>, leading to relinquishment, to eliminate the taints that are abandoned by development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who possesses these six qualities—covering restraint, use, endurance, avoidance, dispelling, and development—is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato"><b>worthy of the world’s homage</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.58/en/sujato">AN 6.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the householder Dārukammika that it is difficult for a layperson who lives a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato"><b>worldly life</b></a>, enjoying sensual pleasures and handling gold and silver, to judge who is truly an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato">AN 6.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder Dārukammika told the Buddha he preferred to give alms specifically to monks who were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato"><b>forest-dwellers</b></a> and rag-robe wearers, believing them to be Arahants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato">AN 6.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even a forest-dwelling monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato"><b>blameworthy</b></a> if he is haughty, unsteady, talkative, and lacks mental concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato">AN 6.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a forest-dwelling monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato"><b>praiseworthy</b></a> only when he is composed, mindful, possessed of clear comprehension, and has restrained faculties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato">AN 6.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who lives in a village can be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato"><b>praiseworthy</b></a> if he maintains a concentrated mind and well-restrained faculties, despite not living in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato">AN 6.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that a monk who accepts <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato"><b>meal invitations</b></a> from laypeople is just as capable of being spiritually advanced and praiseworthy as one who only eats alms-food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato">AN 6.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who wears <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato"><b>robes given by householders</b></a> is praiseworthy as long as he is not haughty and remains established in one-pointedness of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato">AN 6.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised the householder Dārukammika that instead of trying to judge individual monks, he should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato"><b>give alms to the Sangha</b></a> (the community as a whole)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato">AN 6.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a donor gives with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato"><b>serene mind</b></a> to the Sangha, they are destined for a happy rebirth in the heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato">AN 6.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the daily life of a layperson as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato"><b>crowded with children</b></a> and characterized by the use of Kāsi sandalwood and perfumes, which makes it hard to distinguish true spiritual attainments in others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato">AN 6.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that spiritual value is found in being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato"><b>un-haughty</b></a> and restrained, rather than in outward practices like wearing rag-robes or eating only alms-food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.59/en/sujato">AN 6.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika rebuked Ven. Citta Hatthisāriputta for repeatedly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>interrupting senior monks</b></a> while they were engaged in a discussion on the Abhidhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika corrected Ven. Citta Hatthisāriputta, other monks defended Citta, claiming he was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>wise monk</b></a> who was fully capable of taking part in such deep discussions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika taught that it is difficult to truly know another person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>state of mind</b></a> simply by their outward behavior while they are living under the guidance of a teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika, a monk may appear to be exceptionally gentle and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>peaceful</b></a> as long as he lives with his teacher, only to change once he moves away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika explained that a monk who wanders away from his teacher may become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>entangled with laypeople</b></a>, kings, and ministers, leading to the arising of lust that overwhelms his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika compared a monk who appears disciplined but later reverts to the lay life to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>ox that eats crops</b></a> who is only behaved because he is currently tied up or confined in a pen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika used the analogy of rain clearing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>dust on a crossroads</b></a> to explain how a person can attain the first jhāna, yet still have the "dust" of sensuality return if they become negligent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika taught that even a monk who has attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>first jhāna</b></a> can still give up the training and return to a lower life if he becomes muddled and obsessed with worldly talk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika compared the second jhāna to a pond where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>shells and gravel</b></a> are hidden by a heavy rain, but are revealed again once the water is disturbed or the rain stops? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika stated that a monk might be a "gainers of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>second jhāna</b></a>," yet if he over-associates with the public and loses his restraint, he may fall away from the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika compared the temporary loss of interest in sensual pleasures in the third jhāna to a man who has just finished a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>delicious meal</b></a> and has no desire for more food until the "nutritive essence" of that meal fades? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika, a monk who has attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>third jhāna</b></a> can still find himself reverting to the lay life once his initial spiritual inspiration is replaced by worldly attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika compared the stillness of the fourth jhāna to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>pool in a mountain glen</b></a> that is currently windless, but could easily have waves generated if a storm were to blow through? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika warned that even the profound peace of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a> does not guarantee a monk will not leave the Sangha if he fails to guard his mind against the "storms" of worldly interaction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>signless concentration of mind</b></a> (animitta cetosamādhi) can be interrupted by worldly noise just as the song of crickets in a grove disappears when a king’s army arrives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>king's army</b></a> camping in a forest to illustrate how even high meditative attainments can be temporarily overshadowed or lost due to external disturbances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika gave his warning about falling away from the path, Ven. Citta Hatthisāriputta actually did <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>revert to the low life</b></a> of a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika was able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>read Citta’s mind</b></a> and predict that he would leave the monkhood despite his current high meditative attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>devas</b></a> (heavenly beings) approached Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika to inform him that Citta Hatthisāriputta was going to leave the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha was told about Citta Hatthisāriputta leaving the monkhood, he predicted that Citta would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>not long remember</b></a> his time in the lay life and would soon return to renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the phrase "not long remember renunciation" to imply that Citta’s true nature was toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a>, even if he temporarily strayed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after returning to the lay life for a short time, Citta Hatthisāriputta shaved his head, put on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>saffron robes</b></a>, and went forth into homelessness once again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon returning to the monkhood, Citta Hatthisāriputta dwelt <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>solitary and secluded</b></a>, practicing with great diligence to reach the goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his temporary lapse, Ven. Citta Hatthisāriputta ultimately became one of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato"><b>Arahants</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.60/en/sujato">AN 6.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a past teacher named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>Araka</b></a>, who was the founder of a sect and free from sensual lust, who taught his many hundreds of disciples about the brevity of human life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the teacher Araka used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>dewdrop on the tip of a blade of grass</b></a> that quickly vanishes when the sun rises to illustrate how short, fleeting, and full of suffering human life is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the teacher Araka taught his disciples that human life is as fragile as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>water bubble</b></a> that bursts almost immediately when it rains, urging them to do what is good and lead the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that to emphasize the transience of existence, the teacher Araka compared the life of a human being to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>line drawn on water</b></a> with a stick, which disappears instantly and does not last long? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the teacher Araka likened human life to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>mountain river</b></a> that flows swiftly from afar, noting that there is not a single moment or second when the river stops flowing or stays still? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the teacher Araka used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>glob of spit</b></a> that a strong man might easily collect on the tip of his tongue and spit out without effort to describe how little time a human has to live? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the teacher Araka taught that life is like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>piece of meat</b></a> thrown into an iron pan that has been heated all day, which sizzles and vanishes quickly without remaining for long? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the teacher Araka compared a human being's progression toward death to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>cow led to the slaughter</b></a>, where every single step she takes brings her closer to the end of her life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, there was a time in the past when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>lifespan of human beings</b></a> was sixty thousand years, and yet the teacher Araka still taught them that such a life was brief and full of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned an ancient era when humans lived so long that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>girl was marriageable</b></a> only when she reached five hundred years of age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a period in the distant past when humans suffered from only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>six afflictions</b></a>: cold, heat, hunger, thirst, stool, and urine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that if a life of sixty thousand years was considered "brief" by the teacher Araka, it is even more vital for people today to practice the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> since modern lifespans are only around a hundred years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha calculated that a person who lives to the ripe old age of one hundred years actually only experiences <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>three hundred seasons</b></a>: one hundred winters, one hundred summers, and one hundred rainy seasons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha quantified a hundred-year lifespan, he explained that it consists of exactly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>twelve hundred months</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha broke down a century of human life into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>twenty-four hundred fortnights</b></a>, highlighting the limited duration of our existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha counted that a human being living for a hundred years spends only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>thirty-six thousand nights</b></a> on this earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha estimated that over a hundred-year lifespan, a person eats a total of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>seventy-two thousand meals</b></a>, which includes the times they are nursed by their mother? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed several <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>interruptions to eating</b></a> that reduce the total number of meals a person consumes, such as being angry, being in pain, being sick, or observing the Uposatha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told his monks that he had done everything a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>compassionate teacher</b></a> seeking the welfare of his disciples should do by providing them with these detailed instructions on the brevity of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded his teaching by urging his disciples to go to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato"><b>roots of trees</b></a> and empty huts to meditate, so they would not be negligent or live to regret it later? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.74/en/sujato">AN 7.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato"><b>expert in the Vinaya</b></a> as a monk who can accurately distinguish between what is an offense and what is not an offense? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato">AN 7.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is an expert in the Vinaya when he can identify the difference between <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato"><b>light offenses</b></a> and grave offenses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato">AN 7.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an expert in the Vinaya must be virtuous and live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato"><b>restrained by the Patimokkha</b></a>, observing the rules of training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato">AN 7.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that an expert in the Vinaya is a monk who sees <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato"><b>fear in the slightest faults</b></a> while training in the disciplinary codes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato">AN 7.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a true expert in the Vinaya is one who can obtain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a>, which provide a pleasant dwelling in this very life, without any difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato">AN 7.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk's expertise in the Vinaya includes attaining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> and realizing liberation of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato">AN 7.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined an expert in the Vinaya as a monk who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.76/en/sujato"><b>both Patimokkhas</b></a> (the codes of discipline for monks and nuns) well handed down to him in detail and well analyzed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.76/en/sujato">AN 7.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified an expert in the Vinaya as one who knows the disciplinary rules thoroughly in terms of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.76/en/sujato"><b>sutta and its phrasing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.76/en/sujato">AN 7.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who is an expert in the Vinaya <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.79/en/sujato"><b>adorns</b></a> or graces the monastic community when he possesses qualities such as knowing offenses and reaching liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.79/en/sujato">AN 7.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Upali once asked the Buddha for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato"><b>brief teaching</b></a> so that he could dwell alone, diligent and resolute in his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato">AN 7.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha provided criteria to Upali stating that any qualities not leading to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato"><b>utter disenchantment</b></a> should not be considered the true Dhamma or Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato">AN 7.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the true Teacher’s Instruction must lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> (virāga) and the fading of attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato">AN 7.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Upali that teachings which lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato"><b>cessation</b></a> (nirodha) are to be definitively held as the Dhamma and the Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato">AN 7.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the true Dhamma and Vinaya as those qualities that lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato"><b>peace</b></a> (upasama)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato">AN 7.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one should recognize the Teacher's Instruction by whether the practices lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> (abhiññā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato">AN 7.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any quality that leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato"><b>enlightenment</b></a> (sambodha) is truly the Dhamma, the Vinaya, and the Teacher's Instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato">AN 7.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that only teachings leading to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato"><b>Nibbana</b></a> should be regarded as the authentic instruction of the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.83/en/sujato">AN 7.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who is an expert in the Vinaya possesses both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom</b></a> through his own direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.75/en/sujato">AN 7.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was once staying at the foot of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>Naḷeru neem tree</b></a> in the city of Verañja when a Brahmin came to question him about his conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Verañja accused the Buddha of being discourteous because the Buddha did not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>pay homage to elderly Brahmins</b></a> or offer them a seat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that in the entire world of gods, Maras, and Brahmas, he saw no one to whom he should pay homage, warning that if he did, that person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>head would split apart</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha agreed with the Brahmin Verañja that he could be called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>tasteless</b></a>, but only in the sense that he had permanently abandoned and cut off the "tastes" for sights, sounds, smells, tastes, and touches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he could be considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>without possessions</b></a> because he had discarded the enjoyment of the five cords of sensual pleasure so completely that they would never arise again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>teacher of non-doing</b></a> specifically because he teaches the cessation of unwholesome actions through bad bodily, verbal, and mental conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>annihilationist</b></a> only in the sense that he teaches the total annihilation of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>loathsome</b></a> in the sense that he finds bad conduct of the body, speech, and mind to be disgusting and repulsive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined himself as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>disciplinarian</b></a> (venayiko) because he teaches the Dhamma specifically for the purpose of subduing lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called himself an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>ascetic</b></a> (tapassī) because he had abandoned and uprooted all "incinerating" unwholesome states that lead to future suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>not destined for a womb</b></a> because he has destroyed the possibility of future rebirth and the renewal of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>hen brooding over eggs</b></a> to explain how his own disciples, like well-hatched chicks, eventually break out of the shell of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Buddha's hen simile, the chick that breaks the eggshell first with its foot or beak is considered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>eldest</b></a> of them all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to be the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>eldest and best</b></a> in the world because he was the first to break the "shell of ignorance" and attain supreme enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>first jhana</b></a>, a state of rapture and pleasure born of seclusion, by detaching himself from sensual pleasures and unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>second jhana</b></a> as a state of inner tranquility and oneness of mind, free from thought and examination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>third jhana</b></a>, where he lived equanimous, mindful, and clearly aware, experiencing a physical pleasure that the noble ones call "the pleasant dwelling of one who is equanimous and mindful"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhana</b></a>, which is characterized by the purity of mindfulness due to equanimity and the absence of both pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the first watch of the night of his enlightenment, the Buddha attained the knowledge of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>recollecting past lives</b></a>, remembering thousands of previous births in detail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's memory of past lives included specific details such as his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>names, clans, and appearances</b></a> in those previous existences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described seeing the world go through many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>aeons of contraction and expansion</b></a> as he recollected his past lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the middle watch of the night, the Buddha attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a>, allowing him to see beings passing away and being reborn according to their kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that with his divine eye, the Buddha observed that beings who practiced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>bad conduct</b></a> of body, speech, and mind were reborn in states of misery and hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha saw that beings who possessed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> and performed good deeds were reborn in happy destinations, including the heavenly worlds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the last watch of the night, the Buddha attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavas), realizing the Four Noble Truths in their entirety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha realized the truth of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a>, its origin, its cessation, and the path leading to its cessation as the final stage of his enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon his enlightenment, the Buddha knew with certainty that his heart was liberated from the taints of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a>, existence, and ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's final realization included the profound knowledge that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a> and there is no further return to any state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared each of his three great realizations during the night of his enlightenment to a chick <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato"><b>breaking out of an eggshell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.11/en/sujato">AN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>Gabled Hall</b></a> in the Great Wood in Vesālī when General Sīha first heard about him from the prominent Licchavis? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that General Sīha was originally a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>disciple of the Nigaṇṭhas</b></a> (Jains) before he decided to visit the Buddha after hearing high praise from the local community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Jain leader <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>Nigaṇṭha Nātaputta</b></a> twice discouraged General Sīha from visiting the Buddha by claiming that the Buddha taught a "doctrine of non-doing" (akiriyavāda)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he can be called a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>teacher of non-doing</b></a> because he teaches that one should not engage in bad conduct of the body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>teacher of doing</b></a> because he teaches his followers to actively engage in good conduct of body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>annihilationist</b></a> only in the sense that he teaches the total annihilation of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>loathsome</b></a> (jegucchī) in the sense that he loathes and is repulsed by bad conduct and various unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined himself as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>disciplinarian</b></a> because he teaches the Dhamma specifically for the discipline and removal of greed, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said he is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>ascetic</b></a> (tapassī) because he has completely uprooted and "incinerated" all evil, unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>not destined for a womb</b></a> because he has destroyed the potential for future rebirth and the renewal of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>reassurer</b></a> (assāsako) because he provides supreme reassurance and teaches the Dhamma for the sake of reassuring his disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when General Sīha asked to become a lay follower, the Buddha advised him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>investigate thoroughly</b></a> first, as it is better for well-known people to act after careful consideration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that General Sīha was even more impressed with the Buddha when he was told to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>act after investigation</b></a>, noting that other sects would have celebrated his conversion with banners throughout the city? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even after General Sīha converted to Buddhism, the Buddha urged him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>continue giving alms</b></a> to the Nigaṇṭhas (Jains) who had long been supported by his family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that he does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>not teach that gifts</b></a> should only be given to him and his disciples, but rather encouraged Sīha to maintain his generosity toward other ascetics? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's progressive instruction on generosity and virtue, General Sīha attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma-eye</b></a>, realizing that whatever is subject to origination is subject to cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that General Sīha prepared a meal for the Buddha and the Sangha using <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>meat already available</b></a> (pavattamaṃsa), which prompted false rumors that he had slaughtered an animal specifically for the meal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Nigaṇṭhas (Jains) went through the streets of Vesālī shouting that General Sīha had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>slaughtered a large animal</b></a> specifically to feed the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that General Sīha responded to the Nigaṇṭhas' accusations by stating that those critics had long desired to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>discredit the Buddha</b></a>, the Dhamma, and the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that General Sīha declared he would not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>intentionally deprive a living being of life</b></a> even for the sake of his own life, refuting the rumors of slaughter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>Great Wood</b></a> when he delivered a progressive instruction to General Sīha covering giving, virtue, and heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>uplifted mind</b></a>, free from hindrances, he would then reveal the unique teaching of the Buddhas: the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching to General Sīha on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> included the profound topics of suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that General Sīha became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>independent of others</b></a> in the Teacher's instruction after seeing the Dhamma and overcoming all doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>clean cloth</b></a>, free of dark stains, to describe how General Sīha's mind was ready to receive the Dhamma and absorb it correctly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that General Sīha was a high-ranking military official, known as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>general</b></a> (senāpati), who served the Licchavi people of Vesālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that General Sīha visited the Buddha accompanied by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>five hundred chariots</b></a> during the middle of the day, showing his high status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that he taught for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>removal of unwholesome states</b></a>, which is why he could be accurately described as teaching "non-doing" regarding evil actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>practice of virtue</b></a> (sīla) as part of his progressive teaching to General Sīha before discussing the benefits of renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that General Sīha personally served the Buddha and the Sangha with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato"><b>exquisite food</b></a> after his conversion and the realization of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.12/en/sujato">AN 8.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a first point for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> cannot be discerned, such that one could say ignorance did not exist before but has since come into being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> is not without a nutriment (sustenance), but rather is specifically conditioned by a particular source? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>five hindrances</b></a> as the specific nutriment that feeds and sustains ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>three types of misconduct</b></a> (misconduct of body, speech, and mind) serve as the nutriment that brings the five hindrances to fulfillment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>lack of sense restraint</b></a> is the nutriment that leads to the fulfillment of the three types of misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>lack of mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a> is the underlying nutriment that feeds the lack of sense restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>careless attention</b></a> (ayonisomanasikāra) as the nutriment that sustains the lack of mindfulness and clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>lack of faith</b></a> is the nutriment that leads to the fulfillment of careless attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>hearing the untrue Dhamma</b></a> is the specific nutriment that feeds a person's lack of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>association with bad persons</b></a> is the primary nutriment that begins the entire chain of conditions leading to ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>mountain rain</b></a> filling up ravines, pools, and rivers to illustrate how the nutriment of unwholesome qualities eventually flows into and fills the "ocean" of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge and liberation</b></a> (vijjāvimutti) do not exist without a nutriment, but are brought to fulfillment through a sequence of wholesome conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> as the specific nutriment that brings true knowledge and liberation to fulfillment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a> serve as the nutriment that feeds and fulfills the seven factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>three types of good conduct</b></a> (good conduct of body, speech, and mind) are the nutriment for the four establishments of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>sense restraint</b></a> is the essential nutriment that leads to the fulfillment of the three types of good conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a> (satisampajañña) act as the nutriment that feeds sense restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> (yonisomanasikāra) as the nutriment that sustains mindfulness and clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> (saddhā) is the vital nutriment that feeds and fulfills careful attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>hearing the true Dhamma</b></a> is the specific nutriment that leads to the development and fulfillment of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>association with good persons</b></a> is the primary nutriment that begins the causal sequence leading to true knowledge and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a simile of rain falling on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato"><b>mountain top</b></a> to describe how association with good persons sequentially fills up hearing the Dhamma, faith, and mindfulness, eventually filling the great ocean of true knowledge and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.61/en/sujato">AN 10.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.65/en/sujato"><b>rebirth</b></a> (abhinibbatti) is suffering because it makes a person liable to various forms of physical and social distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.65/en/sujato">AN 10.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.65/en/sujato"><b>non-rebirth</b></a> is true happiness, as it ensures one is no longer subject to the pains inherent in existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.65/en/sujato">AN 10.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Sāriputta, the suffering of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.65/en/sujato"><b>cold and heat</b></a>, along with hunger and thirst, are inevitable experiences that one must expect if rebirth occurs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.65/en/sujato">AN 10.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta identified the bodily requirements of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.65/en/sujato"><b>excrement and urine</b></a> as part of the suffering that one should expect to accompany rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.65/en/sujato">AN 10.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta noted that once rebirth has occurred, one must expect the physical pain resulting from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.65/en/sujato"><b>contact with weapons</b></a>, sticks, and fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.65/en/sujato">AN 10.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta stated that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.65/en/sujato"><b>provoked by relatives</b></a> and friends is a specific form of suffering that is to be expected when one undergoes rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.65/en/sujato">AN 10.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.66/en/sujato"><b>dissatisfaction</b></a> (anabhirati) within the Dhamma and Discipline is a state of suffering for a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.66/en/sujato">AN 10.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.66/en/sujato"><b>delight</b></a> (abhirati) in the Dhamma and Discipline as the specific quality that constitutes happiness for a follower of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.66/en/sujato">AN 10.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta explained that a person suffering from dissatisfaction in the spiritual life will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.66/en/sujato"><b>not find ease</b></a> regardless of whether they are walking, standing, sitting, or lying down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.66/en/sujato">AN 10.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Sāriputta, a practitioner lacking delight in the Dhamma will fail to find pleasure even in secluded environments like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.66/en/sujato"><b>forest or empty hut</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.66/en/sujato">AN 10.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that a practitioner who feels delight in the Dhamma will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.66/en/sujato"><b>attain pleasure</b></a> and ease even when they are situated in the middle of a group of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.66/en/sujato">AN 10.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta pointed out that when one is truly satisfied with the Dhamma and Discipline, they find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.66/en/sujato"><b>happiness and pleasure</b></a> in every location, whether they are in a village, the open air, or at the foot of a tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.66/en/sujato">AN 10.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once asked Ven. Sāriputta to give a Dhamma talk to the monks because he wanted to rest his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>aching back</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>lacks faith</b></a> in wholesome states can expect only decline and no growth, whether by day or by night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta compared a person lacking wholesome qualities to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>waning moon</b></a> during the dark fortnight, which diminishes in beauty, roundness, and light? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta identified being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>faithless person</b></a> as a primary state of decline in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>no shame</b></a> (ahirika) regarding unwholesome actions is a condition that leads to certain spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta stated that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>no fear of wrongdoing</b></a> (anottappa) is a specific factor that causes a person to fall away from wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>lazy person</b></a> (kusīto) who lacks energy for wholesome states will only experience a decrease in their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta warned that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>unwise person</b></a> who lacks wisdom in wholesome states will see their spiritual progress steadily diminish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that being an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>angry person</b></a> is a quality of decline that directly hinders one's growth in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta identified being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>resentful person</b></a> (upanāhī) as a detrimental trait that leads to spiritual failure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>evil wishes</b></a> (pāpiccho) is a specific form of decline for a person practicing the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta stated that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>bad friends</b></a> (pāpamitta) is a condition that ensures a person's spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> as a primary factor that causes a person to fall away from wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha affirmed Ven. Sāriputta’s teaching, stating that a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>faith in wholesome states</b></a> can expect only growth and no decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person possessing wholesome qualities to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>waxing moon</b></a> in the bright fortnight, which grows in beauty, roundness, and light? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>shame</b></a> (hirī) regarding unwholesome actions is established in a state of non-decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>fear of wrongdoing</b></a> (ottappa) is a quality that leads to steady spiritual growth and fulfillment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>energetic person</b></a> (āraddhavīriyo) as a key factor in ensuring one's progress in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a> (paññavā) regarding wholesome states is a prerequisite for continuous spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>person without anger</b></a> is a quality of non-decline that supports a practitioner's development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> (kalyāṇamitta) as a vital condition for a person to avoid spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> is a foundational quality of non-decline that leads to the fulfillment of wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.67/en/sujato">AN 10.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta identified the lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>shame and fear of wrongdoing</b></a> (hirī and ottappa) as key factors that lead to the certain decline of a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Sāriputta, a person who lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>energy and wisdom</b></a> regarding wholesome states will see their spiritual progress diminish night and day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta explained that a person who does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>lend an ear</b></a> (sotāvadhāna) to the Dhamma is set for spiritual decline rather than growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta stated that the failure to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>bear the Dhamma in mind</b></a> (dhammadhāraṇā) is a specific condition that leads to the fading away of wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that if a person does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>examine the meaning</b></a> (atthūpaparikkhā) of the teachings they have heard, they will experience a decline in wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta identified the lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>practice in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a> (dhammānudhammappaṭipatti) as a primary cause for a practitioner's spiritual failure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta warned that a lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> (appamāda) regarding wholesome states ensures that a person's spiritual development will only wane? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta compared the spiritual decline of a person lacking wholesome qualities to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>waning moon</b></a> during the dark fortnight, which diminishes in beauty and light? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised and confirmed Ven. Sāriputta's teaching, stating that a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>faith in wholesome states</b></a> can expect only growth and no decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha affirmed that possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>shame and fear of wrongdoing</b></a> are essential qualities that lead to a practitioner's spiritual increase? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>energy and wisdom</b></a> are vital factors that ensure a person's progress and fulfillment in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>lending an ear</b></a> (sotāvadhāna) to the Dhamma as a quality that leads to spiritual growth rather than falling away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>bearing the Dhamma in mind</b></a> (dhammadhāraṇā) is a condition that brings about the growth of wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>examines the meaning</b></a> (atthūpaparikkhā) of the teachings, they can expect only growth in their spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>practice in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a> (dhammānudhammappaṭipatti) as a key quality that prevents spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> (appamāda) regarding wholesome states is a prerequisite for a practitioner to flourish in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the spiritual growth of a person possessing wholesome qualities to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>waxing moon</b></a> during the bright fortnight, which grows in beauty and light? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that whether it is night or day, a person established in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a> should expect only growth and not decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.68/en/sujato">AN 10.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Puṇṇiya once asked the Buddha about the specific cause and reason why a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma talk</b></a> occurs to the Tathāgata at some times and not at others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato">AN 8.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained he is inspired to teach when a bhikkhu is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato"><b>faithful</b></a>, as faith is the first of eight qualities that make a student ready for the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato">AN 8.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he might not feel inspired to give a Dhamma talk if a student, despite having faith, is not one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato"><b>approaches</b></a> the teacher to seek instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato">AN 8.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for a Dhamma talk to occur, the Buddha emphasized that it is necessary for a student to be one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato"><b>attends</b></a> on the teacher (<i>payirupāsitā</i>) rather than simply approaching and leaving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato">AN 8.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato"><b>asks questions</b></a> (<i>paripucchitā</i>) as a key factor that inspires the Tathāgata to provide a formal teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato">AN 8.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is inspired to teach when a student <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato"><b>lends an ear</b></a> (<i>ohitasoto</i>) and listens intently to the Dhamma being shared? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato">AN 8.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a student must be one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato"><b>remembers the Dhamma</b></a> they have heard, as retaining the teachings is a prerequisite for his inspiration to teach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato">AN 8.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that his inspiration to teach arises when a student <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato"><b>examines the meaning</b></a> of the teachings they have already retained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato">AN 8.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said he is inspired to give a Dhamma talk when a student is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato"><b>practicing in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a> after having understood its meaning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato">AN 8.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that only when all eight qualities—from faith to right practice—are present in a student will a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma talk</b></a> occur to the Tathāgata with absolute certainty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.82/en/sujato">AN 8.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught his monks how to respond to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato"><b>wanderers of other sects</b></a> if they were to ask about the fundamental nature and origin of all things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato">AN 8.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's monks expressed that their teachings are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato"><b>rooted in the Blessed One</b></a>, and that he is their guide and their resort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato">AN 8.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that all things are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato"><b>rooted in desire</b></a> (<i>chandamūlakā</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato">AN 8.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, all things come into being through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato"><b>attention</b></a> (<i>manasikārasambhavā</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato">AN 8.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that all things have their origin in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> (<i>phassasamudayā</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato">AN 8.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all things converge in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> (<i>vedanāsamosaraṇā</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato">AN 8.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described all things as being headed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> (<i>samādhippamukhā</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato">AN 8.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all things are dominated by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> (<i>satādhipateyyā</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato">AN 8.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that all things are surpassed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> (<i>paññuttarā</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato">AN 8.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that all things have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a> (<i>vimuttisārā</i>) as their core or final essence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.83/en/sujato">AN 8.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the loud noise made by a crowd of visitors outside his dwelling to the sound of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>fishermen</b></a> hauling in a large catch of fish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha expressed a strong desire to avoid social recognition, stating, "May I not be joined with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>fame</b></a>, and may fame not be joined with me"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who can obtain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>happiness of renunciation</b></a> and the happiness of solitude do not need to seek out inferior worldly pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the pleasure derived from gain, honor, and praise as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>filth-happiness</b></a> and "torpor-happiness"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s attendant, Nāgita, once used the simile of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>heavy rain</b></a> flowing down a slope to describe how people are naturally drawn to follow the Buddha wherever he goes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even certain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>devas</b></a> are unable to obtain the happiness of renunciation, solitude, and enlightenment as easily as he does? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha expressed concern when he saw monks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>socializing</b></a> and tickling each other, as it indicated they were not yet attaining the higher happiness of peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha disapproved of monks who, after <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>stuffing their bellies</b></a> with food, would immediately indulge in the happiness of lying down and sleeping? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha felt a village-dwelling monk sitting in meditation is likely to be disturbed from his concentration by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>monastery attendant</b></a> or a novice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is satisfied with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>forest-dwelling monk</b></a> even if the monk is nodding off, because that monk has the opportunity to soon dispel his drowsiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is pleased with a forest-dwelling monk who is currently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>unconcentrated</b></a>, because the monk is in a position to soon settle his mind through seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is pleased with a forest-dwelling monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>concentrated</b></a>, because that monk is in the ideal state to soon liberate his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that some monks who dwell in villages will abandon their practice of seclusion when they start receiving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>robes and almsfood</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is pleased with a forest-dwelling monk who, despite receiving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>offerings</b></a> of requisites, does not abandon his commitment to solitude and the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he finds ease when traveling a highway alone, specifically when he has no one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>ahead or behind</b></a> him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that even the simple act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>relieving oneself</b></a> is more comfortable for him when he is in complete solitude during his travels? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the happiness of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>enlightenment</b></a> (<i>sambodhasukha</i>) is something he can obtain at will, without trouble or difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>Nāgita</b></a> argued that the Buddha's virtue and wisdom were the reasons why people followed him into the jungle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha discouraged <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>group living</b></a> if it meant monks were constantly socializing rather than pursuing the happiness of renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha believed that a monk who is fond of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>gain, honor, and praise</b></a> will eventually neglect his remote lodgings and return to live in the city? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>peace-happiness</b></a> (<i>upasamasukha</i>) as one of the four higher forms of happiness he values over worldly recognition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, people are naturally drawn to follow him because of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato"><b>virtue and wisdom</b></a>, just as water naturally flows to the low ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.86/en/sujato">AN 8.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerables Mahāmoggallāna and Ānanda went around the monastery to gather the monks because they knew Venerable Sāriputta was about to roar his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>Lion’s Roar</b></a> in the presence of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a certain monk falsely accused Venerable Sāriputta of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>striking him</b></a> and then departing on a tour without seeking forgiveness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta defended himself by stating that only a person who has not established <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> would offend a fellow monk and leave without apologizing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta compared his mind to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>earth</b></a>, which remains unmoved and without disgust whether people throw clean or filthy things upon it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained his mental state is like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>water</b></a>, which washes away both pure and impure substances without being repelled or horrified? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta used the simile of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>fire</b></a>, which burns up clean and dirty things alike without feeling any resentment or repulsion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta stated his mind is like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>wind</b></a>, which blows across both fragrant and foul-smelling items without being troubled or disgusted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta compared his lack of irritation to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>dusting cloth</b></a> that is used to wipe away all kinds of filth without becoming offended? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta described his deep humility by saying he dwells with a mind like an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>outcaste boy or girl</b></a> who enters a village with a very lowly heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta compared his gentle nature to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>bull with horns cut off</b></a>, wandering from street to street without harming anyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta expressed a strong sense of revulsion toward his own physical form, comparing the body to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>carcass</b></a> of a snake or dog hung around the neck of a young person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta told the Buddha he carries his body as if it were a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>pot of fat</b></a> that is leaking and oozing from many cracks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta declared to the Buddha that he dwells with a mind that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>vast and measureless</b></a>, completely free from enmity and ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing Sāriputta’s explanation, the accusing monk confessed that he had acted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>foolishly and confusedly</b></a> by falsely accusing the Elder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha accepted the monk’s confession, stating that it is a mark of growth in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>Noble One’s Discipline</b></a> to see one's mistake and make reparation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that growth in the spiritual life is achieved when one sees a transgression as such and undertakes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>future restraint</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged Sāriputta to forgive the monk so that the monk’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>head would not split</b></a> into seven pieces on the spot? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta expressed his willingness to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>forgive</b></a> the monk who falsely accused him immediately, provided the monk also forgave him for any offense he might have caused? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha sent a monk to summon Sāriputta back specifically because a fellow practitioner had raised a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato"><b>complaint</b></a> against him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.11/en/sujato">AN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Samiddhi</b></a> explained to Venerable Sāriputta that a person's thoughts and intentions arise with name-and-form as their object? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato">AN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Samiddhi</b></a>, the diversity or variety found in a person's thoughts and intentions goes toward the various elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato">AN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Samiddhi</b></a> taught that all mental intentions and thoughts have contact as their specific origin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato">AN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Samiddhi</b></a> stated that a person's thoughts and intentions always converge in the experience of feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato">AN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Samiddhi</b></a> identified concentration as the "head" or foremost quality that leads a person's thoughts and intentions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato">AN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Samiddhi</b></a> told Venerable Sāriputta that mindfulness serves as the authority or dominant factor over all thoughts and intentions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato">AN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Samiddhi</b></a> explained that wisdom is the quality that is superior to or surpasses all other aspects of thoughts and intentions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato">AN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Samiddhi</b></a> declared that liberation is the very core or final essence of a person's thoughts and intentions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato">AN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Samiddhi</b></a> taught that all mental processes and intentions are ultimately grounded in or submerged in the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato">AN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> highly commended Venerable Samiddhi for his skillful answers regarding the nature of the mind but warned him not to become conceited because of his clarity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.14/en/sujato">AN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.15/en/sujato"><b>boil</b></a>" as a designation for the physical body made of the four great elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.15/en/sujato">AN 9.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the human body as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.15/en/sujato"><b>nine openings</b></a> or wound-mouths that are constantly discharging substances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.15/en/sujato">AN 9.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that whatever flows out from the body is inevitably <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.15/en/sujato"><b>impure</b></a>, foul-smelling, and loathsome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.15/en/sujato">AN 9.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the physical body as an accumulation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.15/en/sujato"><b>rice and porridge</b></a> that originated from one's mother and father? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.15/en/sujato">AN 9.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the body as being subject to constant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.15/en/sujato"><b>impermanence</b></a>, as well as to rubbing, pressing, breaking, and eventual dispersal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.15/en/sujato">AN 9.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his monks to develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.15/en/sujato"><b>revulsion</b></a> toward the physical body because of its nature as a festering wound? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.15/en/sujato">AN 9.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda once addressed the monks at Sahajātī regarding a monk who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>boaster and a braggart</b></a> concerning his spiritual attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda warned of monks who falsely claim they can enter and emerge from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>first jhāna</b></a> while actually possessing unwholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahācunda, a monk who brags about entering the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>second jhāna</b></a> will meet with ruin and disaster when cross-examined by a practitioner skilled in reading the minds of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda described how a monk might falsely claim to master the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>third jhāna</b></a>, only to be exposed as immoral and inconsistent in virtue by a wise examiner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda noted that even if a monk claims to enter the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a>, a Tathāgata can discern if that monk is actually faithless and lacking in spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda taught that a monk boasting of attaining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite space</b></a> is revealed as a fraud if they are found to be unlearned and poor in conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda explained that spiritual bragging regarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite consciousness</b></a> is futile when a monk is known by others to be difficult to admonish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda highlighted how a monk might claim the attainment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>base of nothingness</b></a> while secretly associating with bad friends and neglecting their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda stated that claims of reaching the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception</b></a> are proven false when the monk is observed to be lazy and lacking energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda warned that a monk who brags about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception and feeling</b></a> can be identified as unwise and muddled in mindfulness by those with mental penetration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda used a simile of a person who tells a friend to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>dig for treasure</b></a> where none exists to illustrate the empty claims of a spiritual boaster? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda described a situation where a boaster eventually admits their mind was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>deluded and deranged</b></a> after their false claims are repeatedly exposed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>immoral</b></a> and inconsistent in one's virtues as a primary cause for a monk's decline in the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>lack of faith</b></a> is one of the ten qualities that makes it impossible for a monk to achieve growth and expansion in the Buddha's teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda stated that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>unlearned and poorly conducted</b></a> is a state of decline in the Dhamma and Discipline, regardless of a person's outward claims? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda emphasized that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>difficult to admonish</b></a> (dovaca) will face spiritual ruin when scrutinized by the Tathāgata or his disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda warned practitioners that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>having bad friends</b></a> is a quality that leads to disaster rather than the high spiritual attainments a monk might claim? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda classified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>laziness</b></a> (kosajja) as a significant factor that prevents a person from flourishing in the Tathāgata’s Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda described being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>muddled in mindfulness</b></a> as a sign of decline that contradicts any claims of high meditative mastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>deceptive</b></a> (kuhaka) as a quality that utterly blocks a monk from achieving growth, expansion, and fullness in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>difficult to support</b></a> is an unwholesome quality that contributes to a monk's lack of progress in their spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda asserted that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato"><b>unwise</b></a> (duppañño) cannot reach the heights of the Dhamma until they abandon their ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.85/en/sujato">AN 10.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once addressed his monks regarding a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>monk who had passed away</b></a>, using the occasion to teach about the specific qualities that make a person either endearing or disagreeable to their spiritual community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk becomes disagreeable to his peers if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>fond of disputes</b></a> and does not speak in praise of settling legal issues or conflicts within the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one essential quality that leads to being loved and respected by fellow practitioners is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>praising the settlement of disputes</b></a> rather than being litigious? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>desire the training</b></a> and fails to praise the undertaking of the training will not be held in high esteem by his spiritual companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>eager for the training</b></a> and speaking in praise of it are vital qualities for a monk to be cherished and honored by his community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>having evil wishes</b></a> and not speaking in praise of subduing those wishes as a state that prevents a monk from being dear to his fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>of few wishes</b></a> and praises the subduing of wishes possesses a quality that leads to harmony and respect within the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>given to anger</b></a> and failing to praise the removal of anger makes a practitioner disagreeable and uninspiring to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>free from anger</b></a> and speaking in praise of subduing anger are specific behaviors that lead to being honored and esteemed by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>contemptuous</b></a> and does not praise the abandonment of such denigration will find no favor or respect among his peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, not being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>contemptuous or denigrating</b></a> toward others is a key factor in developing spiritual unity and mutual respect in a community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>fraudulent</b></a> and not praising the subduing of fraud as qualities that disqualify a monk from being loved or respected by his companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>without fraud</b></a> is a wholesome quality that contributes to a monk being cherished and developed by his fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>deceitful monk</b></a> who does not speak in praise of subduing deceit will fail to gain the reverence of wise fellow monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>undeceitful</b></a> and praising the removal of deceit are traits that lead to being held in high regard by one's companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>not an investigator</b></a> of the teachings and does not value investigation, he will remain disagreeable to those he lives with? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged being an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>investigator of the teachings</b></a> as a means to become loved, respected, and esteemed by the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>not fond of seclusion</b></a> and does not praise it will not find favor or respect among his spiritual brothers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>devoted to seclusion</b></a> and praising its practice is one of the ten qualities that leads to being loved and honored? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>hospitable kindness</b></a> toward fellow monks as a trait that prevents one from being respected or cherished by the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>hospitable and kind</b></a> to one's fellow practitioners is a quality that leads to spiritual growth, unity, and mutual esteem? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>wild colt</b></a> to illustrate how a monk with unwholesome qualities might wish for respect but will not receive it because wise peers see his unabandoned faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk with wholesome qualities to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato"><b>thoroughbred horse</b></a>, noting that such a person is honored and respected by the community even if they do not explicitly wish for it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.87/en/sujato">AN 10.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha repeatedly corrected the monk Kokālika for falsely accusing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>Sāriputta and Moggallāna</b></a> of having evil wishes, affirming three times that they were actually of good character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that immediately after leaving the Buddha’s presence while harboring ill-will, Kokālika was afflicted with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>boils the size of mustard seeds</b></a> that covered his entire body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how the boils on Kokālika’s body grew progressively larger, expanding from the size of beans to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>size of wood-apples</b></a> before they finally burst? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned the boils on Kokālika’s body eventually burst, causing him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>discharge pus and blood</b></a> as a result of his verbal offenses against noble disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the suffering of the monk Kokālika to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>fish that had swallowed poison</b></a>, as he lay dying on banana leaves due to his spiritual errors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a divinity named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>Turū Paccekabrahmā</b></a> appeared in the air to Kokālika to urge him to have confidence in Sāriputta and Moggallāna, but the monk responded with further insults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person is born, an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>axe is born in their mouth</b></a>, with which the fool cuts themselves whenever they speak badly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that anyone who praises those who should be blamed, or blames those who should be praised, creates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>bad luck with their mouth</b></a> and will not find happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that losing all of one’s wealth and oneself in gambling is a minor misfortune compared to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>greater misfortune</b></a> of harboring ill-will toward those of "rightly gone" (the Sugatas)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>Brahmā Sahampati</b></a> visited the Buddha at the end of the night to formally report that Kokālika had passed away and was reborn in the Paduma hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed the monk Kokālika was reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>Paduma hell</b></a> (the Lotus Hell) as a direct consequence of his hatred toward Sāriputta and Moggallāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the lifespan of a being in the Paduma hell is so long that it is impossible to calculate in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>hundreds of thousands of years</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a simile of a cartload of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>Kosalun sesame seeds</b></a> to illustrate the vast duration of time a being must spend in the lower hell realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the duration of one "Abbuda" hell as the time it would take to empty twenty cartloads of sesame seeds if one seed were removed only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>once every hundred years</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the lifespan in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>Nirabbuda hell</b></a> is twenty times longer than the already immense lifespan of the Abbuda hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>Ababa hell</b></a> as a realm where the lifespan is twenty times the duration of the Nirabbuda hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>Aṭaṭa hell</b></a> represents a period of time equal to twenty durations of the Ababa hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the duration of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>Ahaha hell</b></a> is twenty times the length of the Aṭaṭa hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a sequence of hells including the Kumuda, Sogandhika, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>Uppalaka hells</b></a>, each of which is twenty times longer than the one preceding it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>Puṇḍarīka hell</b></a> has a lifespan twenty times longer than the duration of the Uppalaka hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the longest duration in this specific mathematical series of hells is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>Paduma hell</b></a>, which lasts twenty times longer than the Puṇḍarīka hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that one who reviles the noble ones goes to hell for a period equivalent to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato"><b>one hundred thousand Nirabbudas</b></a> and thirty-six Abbudas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.89/en/sujato">AN 10.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained to the Buddha that there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>ten powers</b></a> belonging to a monk whose taints are destroyed, by which he can rightfully claim his liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk with destroyed taints sees all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>conditioned things</b></a> as impermanent with right wisdom as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, a practitioner who has reached the end of taints views <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> as being like a pit of glowing coals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta described the mind of a liberated monk as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>leaning toward seclusion</b></a> and sloping toward renunciation, away from the causes of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that a monk whose taints are destroyed is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>delighting in renunciation</b></a> and has entirely finished with all things that provide a basis for the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta listed the well-developed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a> as an essential power that allows a monk to know his taints are finished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified the well-developed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>four right strivings</b></a> as a spiritual power that enables a monk to claim "My taints are destroyed"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that having the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>four bases of psychic power</b></a> well developed is a specific hallmark of a monk who has attained the destruction of taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, the well-developed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>five spiritual faculties</b></a> (indriya) constitute one of the ten powers of a fully liberated monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that the well-developed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>five spiritual powers</b></a> (bala) are instrumental for a practitioner to realize the destruction of taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a>, when fully and correctly developed, serve as a power for a monk whose taints are destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified the well-developed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> as the final of the ten powers that support a monk's claim to arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>destruction of taints</b></a> (āsavānaṃ khayaṃ) is a state of realization that a monk arrives at by relying on these ten specific powers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta told the Buddha that seeing things <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>as they really are</b></a> with right wisdom regarding impermanence is the first of the ten powers of a liberated monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta described a monk's mind as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato"><b>completely finished</b></a> with the underlying states that give rise to taints once they are established in renunciation and seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.90/en/sujato">AN 10.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once quoted a verse from the <i>Pārāyana</i> in which he defines a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>great man</b></a> as someone who has understood both "ends" of a spectrum and does not get stuck or "smeared" in the middle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to a monk in Benares, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> constitutes one "end" of human experience, while the origin of that contact constitutes the second end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk explained to his colleagues that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>cessation of contact</b></a> is the true "middle" point between the two ends of contact and its origin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> (taṇhā) as a "seamstress" (sibbinī) because it metaphorically sews a person to the repeated production of new states of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to a specific monk's interpretation, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>past</b></a> is considered one end and the future is the second end, while the present serves as the middle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk suggested that by focusing on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>present</b></a> as the middle ground between the ends of the past and the future, a practitioner can make an end of suffering in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>pleasant feeling</b></a> as one end and painful feeling as the second end, illustrating a dualistic view of sensory experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk defined the "middle" as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>neither-pleasant-nor-painful feeling</b></a>, situated between the ends of pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>name</b></a> (mentality) is one end and form (materiality) is the second end of the conditioned world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk proposed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> acts as the middle between the two ends of name and form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>six internal sense bases</b></a> (eyes, ears, etc.) as one end and the six external sense bases (sights, sounds, etc.) as the second end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when discussing the internal and external sense bases, a monk identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> as the "middle" that exists in relation to both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>identity</b></a> (sakkāya) as one end and the origin of identity as the second end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk interpreted the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>cessation of identity</b></a> as the "middle" point between identity and its origin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha affirmed that all the monks had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>spoken well</b></a> in their own way when they provided various interpretations of what constitutes the "two ends" and the "middle"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically linked the teaching of the "two ends" to his answers provided in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>Metteyya’s Question</b></a> section of the <i>Pārāyana</i>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who directly knows what should be known and fully understands what should be understood becomes an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>ender of suffering</b></a> right here in the present life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically defined the "two ends" as contact and the origin of contact, while asserting that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>middle</b></a> is actually the cessation of contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who transcends the seamstress of craving and is not "smeared" by the middle as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato"><b>great man</b></a> who has outstripped the seamstress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.61/en/sujato">AN 6.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ananda once went to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>Aciravati River</b></a> to bathe and was approached by a monk who questioned how the Buddha had made a definitive declaration regarding the fate of Devadatta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>Devadatta</b></a> to be bound for the plane of misery, destined for hell, and incurable after a thorough scrutiny of his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he had never scrutinized any other individual as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>thoroughly</b></a> as he scrutinized Devadatta before making a declaration about their spiritual destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha only declared Devadatta destined for hell once he saw that there was not even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>tip-of-a-hair's</b></a> amount of wholesome quality left within him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared an incurable person to a man submerged over his head in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>cesspool</b></a> filled with dung, where a rescuer cannot find a single clean spot to grab hold of him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a man in a dung pit to explain why he could not help someone who lacked even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>fraction</b></a> of wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha possesses a specific type of knowledge called the "knowledge of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>people's faculties</b></a>" (purisindriyañāṇāni), which allows him to understand the internal spiritual state of any individual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>reading the minds</b></a> of others, the Buddha can discern if a person currently possesses both wholesome and unwholesome states and predict their future spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a type of person who, despite having unwholesome states at the moment, will not decline because their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>wholesome root</b></a> has not been cut off? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uses the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>non-decline</b></a> (aparihānadhammo) to describe a person whose wholesome roots remain intact, ensuring that wholesome qualities will eventually reappear in them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person of non-decline to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>seeds</b></a> that are not rotten or wind-damaged, which are sown in a well-prepared, fertile field and are certain to grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who, although currently appearing wholesome, is destined for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>decline</b></a> (parihānadhammo) because their unwholesome root remains uncut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person destined for spiritual decline to seeds placed on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>flat rock</b></a>, where they cannot possibly achieve growth, arrival, or fulfillment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who lacks even a tiny amount of wholesome quality and is possessed by entirely unwholesome states is bound for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a> upon the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person destined for hell to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>rotten and broken seeds</b></a> that, even when sown in a good field, will never grow or thrive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>future emergence</b></a> of mental states as something he can directly perceive through his specialized knowledge of how things arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person whose wholesome states disappear and whose wholesome root is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>completely uprooted</b></a>, making them destined for decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>glowing embers</b></a> placed on a flat rock to describe someone whose potential for wholesome growth has been completely extinguished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the transition of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>sunset</b></a> to illustrate how light (wholesome states) disappears and darkness (unwholesome states) appears in a person's mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a state of total spiritual decline to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>midnight hour</b></a>, where all light has vanished and only darkness remains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who, through spiritual progress, sees their unwholesome states disappear and their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome root</b></a> completely uprooted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person with an uprooted unwholesome root to glowing embers placed in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>heap of dry grass</b></a>, suggesting that their wholesome qualities will surely catch fire and grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>sunrise</b></a> to describe the moment when darkness (unwholesome states) vanishes and light (wholesome states) begins to manifest in a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the peak of wholesome development by comparing it to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>midday sun</b></a>, where darkness is entirely gone and only light remains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a rare individual who possesses no unwholesome states whatsoever and is comprised of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>entirely wholesome</b></a>, blameless qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who has eradicated all unwholesome states will attain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>final Nibbana</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who has attained Nibbana to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato"><b>cold embers</b></a> that have been extinguished, which will never again cause a fire even if placed in dry grass or wood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.62/en/sujato">AN 6.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasure</b></a> (kāma) in the Noble Discipline not as the beautiful objects of the world themselves, but as the passion of a person's intentions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beautiful objects remain as they are in the world, but the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>wise</b></a> remove their desire for them to overcome the bondage of sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> (phassa) as the primary source and origin from which all sensual pleasures arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the result of sensual pleasure is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>production of existence</b></a> (attabhāva), which can manifest as either meritorious or demeritorious states depending on one's intentions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>—consisting of right view, right intention, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right concentration—is the only way to the cessation of sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three specific types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>feelings</b></a> (vedanā) that a practitioner must understand: pleasant feeling, painful feeling, and neither-pleasant-nor-painful feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the diversity of feelings by distinguishing between those that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>carnal</b></a> (sāmisā), meaning tied to worldly desires, and those that are spiritual (nirāmisā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as with sensual pleasure, the source and origin of all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>feelings</b></a> is contact (phassa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified six types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> (saññā) based on the senses: the perception of forms, sounds, odors, tastes, tangibles, and mental phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the result of perception is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>conventional usage</b></a> (vohāravepakkaṃ), because a person expresses themselves and communicates based on how they perceive things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that when a person says, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>I was of such a perception</b></a>," they are demonstrating the practical result of how perception shapes their verbal expression? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three specific types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a> (āsavā) that cloud the mind: the taint of sensual desire, the taint of existence, and the taint of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> (avijjā) as the fundamental source and origin of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the diversity of taints leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>rebirth</b></a> in different realms, ranging from hell and the animal realm to the human and heavenly worlds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the result of taints is the production of a new state of existence through an individual's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha famously declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>volition</b></a> (cetanā) is what he calls kamma, for it is only after having willed that one performs an action? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that kamma is performed through three distinct channels: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>body, speech, and mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>timing of results</b></a> for kamma can vary, manifesting either in this very life, in the next life, or in some subsequent life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> as the source and origin of kamma, linking the performance of actions back to sensory experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> (dukkha) as including birth, aging, illness, death, sorrow, and the five aggregates of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> (taṇhā) as the source and origin from which all suffering arises? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>diversity of suffering</b></a> by noting that it can be intense or slight, and it can either fade away slowly or fade quickly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one result of being overcome by suffering is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>bewilderment</b></a> (sammoha), where a person becomes mentally confused by their pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that suffering also results in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>search</b></a> (pariyeṭṭhi) for someone who understands a way to end that suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> as the total cessation of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a noble disciple understands these six themes and their cessation, they understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>penetrative spiritual life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>cessation of ignorance</b></a> is the direct cause for the cessation of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato"><b>cessation of contact</b></a> leads to the cessation of sensual pleasures, feelings, perceptions, and kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.63/en/sujato">AN 6.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato"><b>restrained by the Pātimokkha</b></a>, maintaining good conduct and seeing danger in even the slightest faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato">AN 8.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened a monk’s consumption of food to a noble horse's behavior, stating that a monk should eat whatever is offered—whether coarse or fine—<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato"><b>carefully and without distress</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato">AN 8.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a monk should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato"><b>fastidious regarding misconduct</b></a> of body, speech, and mind, just as a king's noble thoroughbred horse is disgusted by sitting or lying in excrement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato">AN 8.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk’s integrity by stating he should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato"><b>openly reveal his flaws</b></a>, deceit, and crookedness to the Teacher or wise companions so they can be addressed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato">AN 8.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato"><b>gentle and pleasant</b></a> to live with, ensuring that he does not behave in a way that would frighten or upset his fellow monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato">AN 8.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a monk honestly discloses his faults, his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato"><b>Teacher or wise companions</b></a> will then make an active effort to help him suppress and abandon those unwholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato">AN 8.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to have the personal determination to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato"><b>undertake the training</b></a> regardless of whether or not their fellow monks choose to do so? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato">AN 8.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the "straight path" for a monk as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>, ranging from right view to right concentration, comparing it to the straight gait of a noble horse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato">AN 8.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the monk who arouses his energy with the resolve that even if his flesh and blood dry up and only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato"><b>skin, sinews, and bones</b></a> remain, he will not stop until he reaches his goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato">AN 8.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a horse being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato"><b>worthy of a king</b></a> and considered a royal possession to illustrate the high status of a monk who has perfected eight specific spiritual qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato">AN 8.13</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato"><b>disgusted by unwholesome states</b></a> and the attainment of evil qualities, viewing them with the same revulsion a noble horse has for filth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato">AN 8.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted a noble monk displays <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato"><b>steadfast strength</b></a> in his practice until the very end, similar to how a king's thoroughbred horse shows its power until the end of its life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.13/en/sujato">AN 8.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>eight horse scoundrels</b></a> and eight human scoundrels to illustrate the different ways beings react poorly when they are corrected or urged toward progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a type of horse that, when urged by a driver to move forward, actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>pushes the chariot backward</b></a> instead of obeying? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who claims they do not remember their actions when accused of an offense to a horse that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>backs up and retreats</b></a> when it is supposed to go forward? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a horse scoundrel that leaps back and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>breaks the triple pole</b></a> of the chariot when it is prodded by the driver? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened a monk who insults his accuser by calling them ignorant or incompetent to a horse that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>strikes the crossbar</b></a> of its own chariot in a fit of rebellion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a horse that responds to the driver's urge by dropping its thighs on the pole and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>tramples the chariot pole</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who retorts with a counter-accusation when corrected is like a horse that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>collapses onto the pole</b></a> to avoid being driven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a horse scoundrel that, when urged to move, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>takes a wrong path</b></a> and causes the chariot to go off-road? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who prevaricates, diverts the conversation, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>shows anger and malice</b></a> when questioned to a horse that willfully takes a path away from the proper road? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a horse that, when spurred, leaps up and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>raises its forebody</b></a> and front legs in an act of defiance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened a person who makes a scene and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>gesticulates with his arms</b></a> in the middle of the Sangha when accused to a horse that rears up on its hind legs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a horse scoundrel that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>takes the bit in its teeth</b></a> and bolts wherever it wants, completely ignoring the driver and the goad? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who disregards the Sangha and his accuser and simply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>walks away with his offense</b></a> to a horse that runs away with the bit in its teeth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a horse that, when urged to move, neither advances nor retreats but simply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>stands still like a post</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>harasses the Sangha with silence</b></a>, refusing to admit or deny an accusation, is like a horse that stands stubbornly still? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a horse scoundrel that responds to the driver's command by pulling in its legs and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>sits down on all four legs</b></a> right where it is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who spitefully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>gives up the training</b></a> and returns to the low life just to spite those who accused him to a horse that collapses on the ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a "human scoundrel" might mockingly tell the monks to "be happy now" after he has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>renounced the monkhood</b></a> in reaction to being corrected? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a horse <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>destroying its own harness</b></a> with its teeth to describe how some people react with total disregard for the rules and their teachers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>leading the talk aside</b></a> to avoid an accusation as the fourth of eight serious human faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>openly shows ill will</b></a> and resentment when being questioned by their peers as behaving like a horse taking an "off-track" path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that some monks, when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>accused of an offense</b></a>, will try to shift the blame by telling the accuser to "first deal with your own offense"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato"><b>eight human scoundrels</b></a> serves as a warning against being defensive or evasive when one is being held accountable by the spiritual community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.14/en/sujato">AN 8.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.15/en/sujato"><b>ignorance as the ultimate stain</b></a>, declaring it to be far worse than other stains like stinginess or misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.15/en/sujato">AN 8.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.15/en/sujato"><b>non-recital is the stain</b></a> of oral teachings and mantras, as lack of repetition causes them to be lost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.15/en/sujato">AN 8.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.15/en/sujato"><b>non-exertion</b></a> as the stain of a household, suggesting that a lack of initiative and effort ruins the home life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.15/en/sujato">AN 8.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.15/en/sujato"><b>laziness</b></a> is the stain of personal beauty, implying that a lack of energy and care detracts from one's appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.15/en/sujato">AN 8.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.15/en/sujato"><b>heedlessness</b></a> is the stain of one who is meant to be a guard, as a lack of vigilance leads to failure in protection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.15/en/sujato">AN 8.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.15/en/sujato"><b>stinginess</b></a> as the specific stain of a person who gives, acting as a spiritual blemish on the act of generosity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.15/en/sujato">AN 8.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is only fit to go on a mission as a messenger if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.16/en/sujato"><b>not a quarreler</b></a> and possesses the ability to listen and learn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.16/en/sujato">AN 8.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.16/en/sujato"><b>Sāriputta</b></a> as being perfectly endowed with eight qualities that made him worthy of acting as a messenger for the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.16/en/sujato">AN 8.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a Dhamma messenger must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.16/en/sujato"><b>skilled in the relevant</b></a> and the irrelevant, ensuring they focus on what truly matters during their mission? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.16/en/sujato">AN 8.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk acting as a messenger should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.16/en/sujato"><b>not hide the message</b></a> he was sent to deliver, nor should he become angry when he is questioned by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.16/en/sujato">AN 8.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.17/en/sujato"><b>eight ways</b></a> a woman may bind a man, which include her weeping, her laughter, and her speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.17/en/sujato">AN 8.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.17/en/sujato"><b>bound by touch</b></a> are considered "well-bound," identifying physical contact as a particularly powerful form of attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.17/en/sujato">AN 8.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a woman can bind a man through her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.17/en/sujato"><b>appearance and gestures</b></a>, as well as through the use of ornaments and flowers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.17/en/sujato">AN 8.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.17/en/sujato"><b>scents and flavors</b></a> among the various sensory means by which a woman may bind a man's attention and affection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.17/en/sujato">AN 8.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once corrected a group of monks who were gathered in the assembly hall engaging in various kinds of lowly talk—such as talk about kings, thieves, and ministers—by instructing them instead on ten <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato"><b>praiseworthy topics</b></a> of conversation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato">AN 10.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is in a praiseworthy position when he is personally characterized by fewness of wishes and also engages his fellow monks in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato"><b>talk on fewness of wishes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato">AN 10.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk holds a praiseworthy position when he is personally content with what he has and actively leads his peers in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato"><b>talk on contentment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato">AN 10.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified it as a praiseworthy state for a monk to be personally secluded from the world while also providing his fellow practitioners with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato"><b>talk on seclusion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato">AN 10.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered it a praiseworthy quality for a monk to be personally aloof from society and to consistently promote <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato"><b>talk on aloofness from society</b></a> among other monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato">AN 10.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised monks who have personally aroused their energy for spiritual development and who also facilitate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato"><b>talk on arousing energy</b></a> within the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato">AN 10.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described it as a praiseworthy position for a monk to be personally possessed of virtue and to engage his companions in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato"><b>talk on the possession of virtue</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato">AN 10.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is praiseworthy when he is personally possessed of concentration and further supports his fellow monks by giving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato"><b>talk on the possession of concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato">AN 10.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is personally possessed of wisdom and also encourages <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato"><b>talk on the possession of wisdom</b></a> among his peers is holding a praiseworthy position? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato">AN 10.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a praiseworthy monk as one who is personally possessed of liberation and who also conducts <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato"><b>talk on the possession of liberation</b></a> for the benefit of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato">AN 10.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highly valued a monk who is personally possessed of the knowledge and vision of liberation and who additionally engages in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato"><b>talk on the possession of the knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a> with his fellow monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.69/en/sujato">AN 10.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato"><b>faithful</b></a> but lacks virtue is considered incomplete in that factor, and should strive to fulfill both qualities to progress on the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato">AN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a monk to be truly complete in his spiritual life, he must not only have faith but also be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> by possessing good character and following monastic training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato">AN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a>—meaning one who has heard much, retained the teachings, and investigated them with wisdom—as a necessary factor for a monk to be inspiring in all respects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato">AN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who is learned should also strive to be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato"><b>speaker on the Dhamma</b></a> so that he can effectively share the teachings with others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato">AN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato"><b>frequents assemblies</b></a> as possessing one of the ten essential qualities that make a practitioner complete in every way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato">AN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is a specific requirement for a complete monk to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato"><b>confident in teaching</b></a> the Dhamma to an assembly, ensuring the teachings are conveyed with assurance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato">AN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed being an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato"><b>expert in the discipline</b></a> (Vinaya) as a vital characteristic for any monk who wishes to be seen as fully accomplished and inspiring to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato">AN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato"><b>forest dweller</b></a> in remote lodgings as part of the ten qualities that lead to being a fully inspiring and well-rounded practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato">AN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that an inspiring monk should be one who obtains at will and without difficulty the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato"><b>four jhānas</b></a>, which are meditative states providing happiness in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato">AN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk reaches the absolute pinnacle of being "inspiring in all respects" only when he realizes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> and dwells in the stainless liberation of mind and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.8/en/sujato">AN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato"><b>faithful</b></a> but lacks other essential qualities, such as virtue or learning, is considered incomplete in those factors and should strive to fulfill all ten qualities to become truly inspiring in all respects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato">AN 10.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a>—dwelling restrained by the Patimokkha and seeing danger in the slightest faults—as a necessary requirement for a monk to be complete in his spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato">AN 10.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a>—one who has heard much, retained the teachings, and penetrated them with wisdom—as possessing a vital quality for being an inspiring practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato">AN 10.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk should strive to be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato"><b>speaker on the Dhamma</b></a> to effectively share the path to liberation with others as part of his own spiritual completion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato">AN 10.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato"><b>frequents assemblies</b></a> of practitioners possesses one of the ten essential qualities that lead to being a well-rounded and inspiring member of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato">AN 10.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk is praiseworthy when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato"><b>confident in teaching</b></a> the Dhamma to an assembly, ensuring the teachings are conveyed with assurance and clarity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato">AN 10.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed being an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato"><b>expert in the discipline</b></a> (Vinaya) as a fundamental characteristic for any monk who wishes to be seen as fully accomplished and inspiring to his peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato">AN 10.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato"><b>forest dweller</b></a> in remote lodgings fulfills a key criterion for being a truly inspiring practitioner who is complete in all factors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato">AN 10.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should dwell touching with the body those <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato"><b>peaceful liberations</b></a> that transcend forms—the formless meditative attainments—to be considered complete in every respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato">AN 10.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a monk reaches the absolute pinnacle of being "inspiring in all respects" only when he realizes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> and dwells in the stainless liberation of mind and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.9/en/sujato">AN 10.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed noble disciples to observe the Uposatha by consciously <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato"><b>imitating the arahants</b></a>, who for as long as they live abstain from the destruction of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato">AN 9.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the first factor of this observance as dwelling with a mind that is scrupulous and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato"><b>full of compassion</b></a> for the welfare of all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato">AN 9.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a disciple observing the Uposatha follows the example of the arahants by having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato"><b>laid aside the rod</b></a> and the sword while abstaining from taking life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato">AN 9.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from high beds</b></a> and luxurious beds as the eighth factor of the ninefold Uposatha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato">AN 9.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to use a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato"><b>low bed</b></a>, such as a couch or a straw mat, during the Uposatha to mirror the simple lifestyle of the arahants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato">AN 9.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ninth factor of this special Uposatha involves dwelling with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato"><b>mind imbued with loving-kindness</b></a> directed toward all four quarters of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato">AN 9.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a practitioner should pervade the entire world—above, below, and across—with a mind of loving-kindness that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato"><b>vast and exalted</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato">AN 9.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the loving-kindness practiced during the nine-factored Uposatha must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato"><b>measureless and free from hostility</b></a> and ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.18/en/sujato">AN 9.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how certain deities felt intense regret because, as humans, they had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato"><b>risen for monastics</b></a> who visited their homes but had neglected to pay them proper homage or bow, leading to their rebirth in an inferior body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato">AN 9.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a group of deities who felt their spiritual work was incomplete because, while they had paid homage to visiting monastics, they had failed to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato"><b>offer a seat</b></a> to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato">AN 9.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told of deities who experienced remorse after being reborn in an inferior state because they had offered seats to visiting monastics but failed to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato"><b>share their resources</b></a> with them according to their ability and strength? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato">AN 9.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of deities who regretted being reborn in an inferior body because, although they had shared their resources with monastics, they did not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato"><b>sit down to listen</b></a> to the Dhamma being taught? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato">AN 9.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reported that some deities felt remorse for their "incomplete work" because they had sat down to hear the Dhamma but failed to listen with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato"><b>attentive ears</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato">AN 9.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha related how certain deities suffered from regret because, after listening to the Dhamma with attentive ears as humans, they did not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato"><b>remember the teachings</b></a> they had heard? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato">AN 9.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described deities who lamented their rebirth in an inferior state because they had memorized the Dhamma but failed to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato"><b>examine the meaning</b></a> of the teachings they had remembered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato">AN 9.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared the story of deities who felt remorse for their current state because, despite understanding the meaning of the teachings, they had not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato"><b>practiced in accordance</b></a> with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato">AN 9.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between deities reborn in an inferior state and those reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato"><b>superior body</b></a> based on whether they had fulfilled all eight steps of respecting monastics and practicing the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato">AN 9.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a group of deities who were reborn in a "superior body" without regret because they had perfectly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato"><b>fulfilled their work</b></a> by honoring monastics, sharing resources, and practicing the Dhamma in its entirety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato">AN 9.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed to the foot of trees and empty huts, urging his monks to meditate and not be negligent so they would not be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato"><b>remorseful later</b></a>, just as those former deities had been? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.19/en/sujato">AN 9.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a>, good companions, and good comrades as the first prerequisite for developing the factors that lead to enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and restrained by the Pātimokkha, while maintaining good conduct and seeing danger in even the slightest faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that practitioners should engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>effacing talk</b></a>—speech that is conducive to opening up the heart—including talk on wanting little, contentment, and seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>arousing energy</b></a> as a vital prerequisite, specifically for the purpose of abandoning unwholesome states and undertaking wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the prerequisite of wisdom as being endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>noble wisdom</b></a> regarding the arising and passing away of phenomena, which leads to the complete destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>noble friends</b></a> and companions, it can be expected that he will naturally become virtuous and restrained by the rules of the Pātimokkha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk with good companions will easily obtain beneficial speech, such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>talk on liberation</b></a> and the knowledge and vision of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that once established in the five basic prerequisites, a monk should further develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>perception of unattractiveness</b></a> to abandon lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> (metta) as the specific remedy for abandoning ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of breathing</b></a> should be cultivated to effectively cut off discursive and distracting thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>perception of impermanence</b></a> should be developed specifically for the purpose of uprooting the conceit "I am"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one who perceives impermanence, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-self</b></a> becomes firmly established? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that uprooting the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>conceit "I am"</b></a> leads directly to the attainment of Nibbāna in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>resolute and energetic</b></a> as one who does not "throw off the yoke" in the pursuit of wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>penetrative wisdom</b></a> as being "noble" when it leads to the correct ending of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>wings to enlightenment</b></a> (sambodhipakkhikā dhammā) to categorize the specific qualities a practitioner must develop to reach awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>talk on concentration</b></a> as one of the ten types of speech that are helpful for opening up the heart of a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>thoroughly restrained</b></a> by the disciplinary code of the Pātimokkha as a foundation for higher spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that talk regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>non-association</b></a> and seclusion is essential for a monk's progress toward enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that a monk with good friends will inevitably possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> regarding the arising and passing away of all things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.1/en/sujato">AN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who abstains from destroying life causes five types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>fear and animosity</b></a> to subside, protecting themselves from harm in both the present life and the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a noble disciple who possesses confirmed confidence in the Dhamma views the teaching as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>visible here and now</b></a>, timeless, and inviting one to come and see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>noble method</b></a> is well seen and well penetrated by wisdom when a disciple understands the principle of dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>stream-enterer</b></a> as someone who is no longer bound to the nether world and is certain of reaching final enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>taking what is not given</b></a> as a source of fear and mental distress that can be completely overcome through the practice of virtuous restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>community of disciples</b></a>—consisting of the four pairs of persons—is worthy of gifts and hospitality and serves as an unsurpassed field of merit for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a disciple abstains from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>false speech</b></a>, they no longer produce the fear and animosity that arises from being untruthful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the origin of the entire mass of suffering through the formula: "with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> as condition, volitional formations come to be"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the requirements for declaring oneself a stream-enterer is possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>virtues dear to the noble ones</b></a>, which are unbroken, unblemished, and conducive to concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the householder Anāthapiṇḍika that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> leads to both present-life fear and future-life animosity, which are eliminated by those who restrain themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>factors of stream-entry</b></a> as confirmed confidence in the Buddha, the Dhamma, the Sangha, and the possession of perfect virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>abstaining from intoxicants</b></a> like wine and liquor, a person ceases to produce the mental pain and grief that such substances cause? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>cessation</b></a>, where the entire mass of suffering ends through the remainderless fading away of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a stream-enterer has completely finished with the possibility of being reborn in hell, the animal realm, or the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>sphere of afflicted spirits</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the phrase "when this exists, that comes to be" to describe the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>structural principle</b></a> of how all things arise dependently? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that aging, death, sorrow, and lamentation only arise because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a> exists as a necessary condition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence</b></a> in the Tathāgata means acknowledging him as an arahant, perfectly enlightened, and a teacher of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> is the specific condition that leads to the arising of clinging and the continuation of the cycle of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple who has mastered the five virtues and the four factors of stream-entry can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>declare for themselves</b></a> that they have escaped the lower realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> is a dependent link that arises from the six sense bases, which in turn leads to the experience of feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.92/en/sujato">AN 10.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder Anāthapiṇḍika told a group of wanderers that any view that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>dependently arisen</b></a> is impermanent and therefore a form of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Anāthapiṇḍika for correctly refuting followers of other sects, stating that even a monk with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>a hundred years</b></a> of seniority should rebuke them in the same way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked about his own perspective, Anāthapiṇḍika explained that his view is based on seeing that whatever is suffering is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>not mine</b></a>, not what I am, and not my self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Anāthapiṇḍika argued that speculative views, such as whether the world is eternal or not, often arise from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>unwise attention</b></a> or from hearing the reports of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the wanderers of other sects as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>misguided men</b></a> after hearing how Anāthapiṇḍika had logically silenced their various speculative arguments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Anāthapiṇḍika pointed out to the wanderers that by holding onto their dogmatic views, they were actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>clinging to suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wanderers of other sects recognized Anāthapiṇḍika as a disciple of the Buddha and noted that such disciples are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>lovers of quiet</b></a> and disciplined in silence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Anāthapiṇḍika modestly told the wanderers that he did not know <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>all the views</b></a> of the Buddha or of the monks before engaging them in a debate about their own beliefs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that one of the wanderers held the specific view that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>world is eternal</b></a>, claiming this alone is the truth and everything else is fatuous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that another wanderer expressed the opposing view that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>world is not eternal</b></a>, which Anāthapiṇḍika later analyzed as a conditioned and volitionally produced idea? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Anāthapiṇḍika stated that he understood the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> from suffering as it really is, which goes beyond the conditioned views held by other sects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma talk</b></a> to instruct, inspire, and gladden Anāthapiṇḍika after the householder recounted his debate with the wanderers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that some wanderers debated whether the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>soul and the body</b></a> are the same or if the soul is one thing and the body another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Anāthapiṇḍika categorized all the wanderers' speculative theories as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>conditioned</b></a> and volitionally produced, leading to the conclusion that they are inconstant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Anāthapiṇḍika finished his logical refutation, the wanderers became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>silent and crestfallen</b></a>, unable to provide any further rebuttal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder Anāthapiṇḍika initially decided to visit the park of the wanderers because it was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>too early</b></a> in the day to visit the Buddha or the meditating monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any view which is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>volitionally produced</b></a> is ultimately impermanent and subject to cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that refuting others <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>with the Dhamma</b></a>, as Anāthapiṇḍika did, is a commendable way to handle misguided views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Anāthapiṇḍika described the views of the wanderers as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>well-seen</b></a> with right wisdom as actually being sources of distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that Anāthapiṇḍika had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato"><b>rightly refuted</b></a> the misguided wanderers by using the principles of impermanence and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.93/en/sujato">AN 10.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wanderers of other sects silenced themselves when they saw Vajjiyamāhita approaching because they knew the Buddha's disciples were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>lovers of quiet</b></a> and were disciplined in silence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vajjiyamāhita refuted the claim that the Buddha condemns all forms of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>asceticism</b></a>, explaining instead that the Buddha blames what is blameworthy and praises what is praiseworthy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vajjiyamāhita described the Buddha as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>proponent of discriminating speech</b></a> (vibhajjavādo) rather than one who makes one-sided or categorical assertions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a wanderer accused the Buddha of being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>nihilist</b></a> who prescribes nothing, Vajjiyamāhita corrected him by stating the Buddha clearly prescribes what is wholesome and what is unwholesome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vajjiyamāhita is described as having logically and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>rightfully refuted</b></a> the wanderers of other sects, leaving them silent, crestfallen, and unable to respond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Vajjiyamāhita that some forms of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>austerity</b></a> should be practiced while others should not, depending on whether they cause unwholesome states to increase or decrease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>undertaking</b></a> (samādāna) is not worth pursuing if it leads to the decline of wholesome states and the growth of unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>striving</b></a> (padhāna) should only be practiced when it leads to the increase of wholesome states and the waning of unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>relinquishment</b></a> (paṭinissagga) should only be practiced if it results in the growth of wholesome qualities within the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a> (vimutti) should only be pursued if the process of being liberated leads to the subsiding of unwholesome states and the increase of wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>not practice</b></a> any form of austerity if it causes unwholesome states to increase and wholesome states to decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Vajjiyamāhita departed, the Buddha told the monks that even a senior monk who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>little dust in his eyes</b></a> should refute wanderers just as the householder had done? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Vajjiyamāhita with the words "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>Sādhu, sādhu</b></a>," confirming that he had correctly used the Dhamma to restrain the misguided wanderers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified himself as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>proponent of prescriptions</b></a> (sapaññattiko) because he defines the boundaries between what is good and what is bad? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wanderers of other sects were found sitting together engaged in various kinds of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>animal talk</b></a> (tiracchānakathaṃ) before Vajjiyamāhita arrived to debate them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vajjiyamāhita defended the Buddha's teachings by explaining that the Buddha is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>one-sided in his speech</b></a> (ekaṃsavādo) regarding practices like austerity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a> (kusalā dhammā) are the primary metric for determining whether a particular spiritual practice or exertion is beneficial? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.94/en/sujato">AN 10.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to possess six distinct <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>powers of a Tathāgata</b></a> which allow him to roar his "lion’s roar" in assemblies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he is able to set the supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>Wheel of Brahma</b></a> in motion because of the specific spiritual powers he has realized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that one of his powers is the ability to understand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>possible as possible</b></a> and the impossible as impossible as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha possesses the power to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>results of kamma</b></a> undertaken in the past, future, and present by way of their possibilities and causes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha has direct knowledge of the corruption, purification, and emergence regarding various <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>meditative attainments</b></a>, such as the jhānas and liberations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is able to recollect his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>manifold past lives</b></a>, including their specific forms and locations, as a result of his spiritual power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha uses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> to see beings passing away and being reborn according to their kamma, whether they are inferior or superior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>destruction of taints</b></a> to be a supreme power that allows him to dwell in the stainless liberation of mind and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha claimed his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>lordly place</b></a> in the world is justified by his mastery over six specific types of deep knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when others approach the Buddha with questions about what is possible and impossible, he provides answers based on his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>unfailing knowledge</b></a> of those principles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha is able to resolve inquiries regarding how actions lead to specific outcomes by using his power of understanding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>kamma and its results</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha can explain the intricacies of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>meditative purification</b></a> and the process of emerging from deep concentration to any seeker who asks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha provides detailed accounts of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>recollection of past lives</b></a> to those who approach him with questions about their previous existences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha answers questions about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>passing away and rebirth</b></a> of beings by drawing upon his direct vision of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that his knowledge regarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> is a truth he can explain to those seeking liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that the knowledge of what is possible and impossible is only accessible to one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>concentrated</b></a>, not to one who is unconcentrated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that understanding the results of past, future, and present kamma is a feat achieved only by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>concentrated mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the knowledge of how meditative states become corrupted or purified belongs to one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>samāhita</b></a> (concentrated)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the recollection of manifold past lives is a knowledge that is developed by those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>concentrated</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the divine eye, which perceives the rebirth of beings according to their deeds, is a power of one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>concentrated</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>concentration as the path</b></a>, whereas a lack of concentration is considered a "wrong path"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato"><b>lack of concentration</b></a> is a "wrong path" (kummagga) that prevents the realization of the six powers of a Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.64/en/sujato">AN 6.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying at Savatthi in the Palace of Migara’s Mother, he sat in silence throughout the entire night because he refused to recite the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>monastic code</b></a> (Pātimokkha) to an assembly that was not entirely pure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ananda approached the Buddha three times—at the end of the first watch, the middle watch, and the last watch of the night—to request the recitation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>Uposatha</b></a> (observance day) liturgy before the Buddha finally spoke? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha finally broke his silence at dawn, he informed Ananda that he would not recite the Pātimokkha because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>assembly is impure</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Maha Moggallana used his psychic powers to scan the minds of the entire gathering of monks to identify the single <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>immoral person</b></a> whose presence was preventing the Buddha from reciting the monastic code? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a corrupt monk who pretends to be a recluse while being internally rotten as someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>full of dross</b></a> (kasambujāta) and leaking with lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after an immoral monk refused to leave the assembly despite three verbal warnings, the Venerable Maha Moggallana took him by the arm and physically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>ejected him</b></a> from the gate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Maha Moggallana it was "astounding and amazing" that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>empty man</b></a> (moghapuriso) who was being immoral waited until he was physically seized by the arm before he would depart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that from that day forward, he would no longer personally perform the Uposatha or recite the Pātimokkha because it is impossible for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>Tathagata</b></a> to do so in an impure assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used eight wonderful and marvelous qualities of the great ocean to illustrate eight corresponding qualities found in his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma-Vinaya</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that just as the great ocean has a gradual shelf and slope, the Buddha taught that in this Dhamma-Vinaya there is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>gradual training</b></a> and gradual practice, rather than a sudden penetration of final knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the stability of the ocean's shore to the way his disciples do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>transgress a training rule</b></a> even for the sake of their lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the ocean's habit of washing a dead body ashore to the way the Sangha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>casts out</b></a> an immoral or corrupt person who no longer belongs in their community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as the great rivers lose their names when they enter the sea, members of the four castes who go forth into homelessness lose their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>former names</b></a> and clans to be known simply as "recluses"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that no matter how many people attain Nibbāna, the Nibbāna element (the state of liberation) neither <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>increases nor decreases</b></a>, just as the ocean level remains constant regardless of the rivers and rain that flow into it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that just as the great ocean has but one taste—the taste of salt—the Dhamma-Vinaya has but one taste, which is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>taste of liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the "many treasures" found in the ocean, such as pearls and gems, to the many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>treasures of Dhamma</b></a>, such as the four foundations of mindfulness and the seven factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Dhamma-Vinaya as a home for "great beings" like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>stream-enterer</b></a> (sotāpanno) and the Arahant, comparing them to the massive creatures that inhabit the depths of the ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned legendary sea creatures like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>Timi and Timiṅgala</b></a>, some said to be hundreds of yojanas in size, as residents of the great ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>Asuras</b></a> (a class of deities/beings) take great delight in the wonderful and marvelous qualities of the great ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified eight types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>noble persons</b></a> (those on the four paths and those who have attained the four fruits) as the "great beings" who reside within the Dhamma-Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed the monks that they themselves must now perform the Uposatha and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>recite the Pātimokkha</b></a> among themselves, as he would no longer do it personally? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that monks find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato"><b>delight</b></a> in the Dhamma-Vinaya specifically because of its eight wonderful and marvelous qualities, similar to how ocean-dwellers delight in the sea? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.20/en/sujato">AN 8.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>foolish mountain cow</b></a>—who is unskilled and unfamiliar with her terrain—to illustrate a monk who attempts to progress to higher meditation states without first mastering his current one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>unskilled in the field</b></a> of meditation will fail to reach new attainments and will also lose the level of concentration he had previously achieved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a meditator who prematurely attempts the second jhāna without stabilizing the first as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>fallen between two sides</b></a>, much like a cow stuck on a steep mountain path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>cultivate the sign</b></a> of the first jhāna—developing and sustaining it well—before he can safely move on to the stillness of the second jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a wise and clever monk to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>smart mountain cow</b></a> who ensures her front hoof is firmly planted before she dares to lift her back leg to move forward? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who masters the first jhāna can successfully enter the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>second jhāna</b></a>, which is characterized by internal confidence and unification of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that after mastering the second jhāna, a monk can proceed to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>third jhāna</b></a>, where he dwells equanimous and mindful, experiencing a unique happiness with the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a> as a state beyond pleasure and pain, featuring the purity of mindfulness through equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by transcending perceptions of form and resistance, a monk can enter the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite space</b></a>, realizing that "space is infinite"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that after moving beyond the base of infinite space, a meditator can attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite consciousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>base of nothingness</b></a> as a high meditative state reached by a monk who realizes "there is nothing at all"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception</b></a> by completely transcending the base of nothingness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception and feeling</b></a> as the profound attainment reached after mastering all four jhāna and the four immaterial bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said when a monk can both enter and emerge from these nine meditative attainments, his mind becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>malleable and wieldy</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind made malleable through deep meditation is capable of achieving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>boundless concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that a monk with such refined concentration can direct his mind to any phenomenon for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> and realize it for himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ability to exercise <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>manifold psychic powers</b></a>, such as walking on water or traveling to the Brahma-world, as a possible result of this meditative mastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>divine ear element</b></a>, which allows one to hear sounds from both the human and divine realms, as a fruit of advanced concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk with a well-developed mind can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>understand the minds</b></a> of others, knowing whether their thoughts are affected by lust, hatred, or delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>recollect past lives</b></a> in all their modes and details, stretching back through many aeons of world-expansion and contraction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> as the ability to see beings passing away and being reborn according to their kamma (actions)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ultimate realization for a meditator is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavas), leading to the liberation of the mind through wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato"><b>rugged mountains</b></a> to emphasize the difficulty and danger of practicing meditation without a steady, step-by-step foundation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.35/en/sujato">AN 9.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Anāthapiṇḍika that whether a gift is coarse or choice, if it is given carelessly, without respect, and not with one's own hands, it is considered a gift given <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>without regard</b></a> and will not yield great results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a person gives a gift without respect or care, the karmic result is such that their mind will not incline toward enjoying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>fine food</b></a>, choice clothing, or luxury vehicles in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that giving a gift without proper attention results in a future where one's own children, spouse, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>servants</b></a> will not listen to or obey them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when a gift is given with respect, with one's own hands, and with a view to the future, it results in a mind that is able to appreciate and enjoy <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> and high-quality goods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a past life in which he was the brahmin Velāma, who distributed a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>massive gift</b></a> that included eighty-four thousand gold bowls filled with silver and eighty-four thousand silver bowls filled with gold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the story of the brahmin Velāma, the Buddha recounted giving away eighty-four thousand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>elephants</b></a> that were adorned with gold ornaments and banners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once performed a great sacrifice as Velāma where he gave away eighty-four thousand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>chariots</b></a> covered with the skins of lions, tigers, and leopards? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as part of his immense historical offering, the Buddha gave away eighty-four thousand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>milch cows</b></a> equipped with bronze milk pails? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the scale of Velāma's gift as being so large that the abundance of food, drink, and snacks seemed to flow like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>rivers</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the incredible scale of Velāma's generosity, the Buddha noted that there was no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>worthy recipient</b></a> available at that time to purify the gift? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that feeding even one single <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>person with right view</b></a> (a stream-enterer) is more fruitful than the entire, massive gift given by the brahmin Velāma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that feeding one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>once-returner</b></a> (sakadāgāmi) is more fruitful than feeding one hundred people who possess right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, providing a meal to one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>non-returner</b></a> (anāgāmi) yields more merit than feeding one hundred once-returners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that feeding one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>arahant</b></a> is far more fruitful than feeding one hundred non-returners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that feeding one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>paccekabuddha</b></a> is more meritorious than feeding one hundred arahants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that building a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>dwelling</b></a> (vihara) for the use of the Saṅgha of the four quarters is more fruitful than feeding the Buddha and the entire order of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that taking refuge in the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Saṅgha with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>confident heart</b></a> is more fruitful than building a monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that undertaking the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>five training rules</b></a> (precepts) is a more fruitful action than merely taking the three refuges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that developing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>mind of loving-kindness</b></a> for even the briefest moment, such as the time it takes to sniff a fragrance, is more fruitful than keeping the five precepts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that developing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato"><b>perception of impermanence</b></a> for even the time of a finger-snap is the most fruitful action of all, surpassing even the most extensive acts of giving and loving-kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.20/en/sujato">AN 9.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as being an unsurpassed field of merit when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and dwells restrained by the Pātimokkha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk worthy of hospitality is one who sees <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>danger in the slightest faults</b></a> and trains himself in the rules of discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a>—having heard and retained the Dhamma—as a key quality that makes a monk worthy of offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk should master the Dhamma that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>good in the beginning</b></a>, middle, and end, which expresses the entirely complete and pure holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a bhikkhu is an unsurpassed field of merit when he has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>good friends</b></a>, good companions, and good comrades? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> and being endowed with right vision is one of the ten qualities that make a monk worthy of reverential salutation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who can exercise manifold <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>psychic powers</b></a>, such as becoming many from being one and then becoming one again, as being worthy of gifts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that a monk worthy of hospitality can pass through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>walls and mountains</b></a> without obstruction, as if through open space? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk with ten qualities can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>walk on water</b></a> without sinking, as if walking on solid ground, making him an unsurpassed field of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk can travel through the sky <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>cross-legged</b></a> like a winged bird when he possesses the qualities that make him worthy of offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a monk of great merit can touch and stroke the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>sun and moon</b></a> with his hand, showing the extent of his psychic power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk worthy of gifts can hear both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>divine and human</b></a> sounds, whether they are far or near, through the purified divine ear element? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is an unsurpassed field of merit if he can understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>minds of others</b></a>, knowing when a mind is affected by lust or free from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a monk worthy of offerings can discern whether another person's mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>exalted or unexalted</b></a>, concentrated or unconcentrated, and liberated or unliberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is worthy of reverential salutation if he can recollect his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>past lives</b></a> in great detail, including his names, clans, and experiences through many aeons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk possesses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> when he can see beings passing away and being reborn according to their kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a monk with the divine eye sees that beings who engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>misconduct</b></a> are reborn in a state of misery or hell, while those with good conduct are reborn in a happy destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is an unsurpassed field of merit if he has realized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> and dwells in the taintless liberation of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.97/en/sujato">AN 10.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught it is impossible for a monk who associates with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.67/en/sujato"><b>bad friends</b></a> and bad companions to ever fulfill the training in good conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.67/en/sujato">AN 6.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that if a monk has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.67/en/sujato"><b>good friends</b></a> and good companions, it is possible for him to fulfill the training in good conduct and subsequently fulfill the training of a trainee? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.67/en/sujato">AN 6.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that without first fulfilling the training in good conduct, it is impossible for a person to ever fully perfect their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.67/en/sujato"><b>virtues</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.67/en/sujato">AN 6.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk who has successfully perfected his virtues is then capable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.67/en/sujato"><b>abandoning lust</b></a> for sensual existence, the realm of forms, and the formless realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.67/en/sujato">AN 6.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that if a monk delights in company and groups, it is impossible for him to ever find joy in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato"><b>solitude</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato">AN 6.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that only by delighting in solitude can a monk successfully grasp the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato"><b>theme of the mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato">AN 6.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that grasping the theme of the mind is a necessary prerequisite for a person to eventually fulfill <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato">AN 6.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that it is impossible for a monk to fulfill <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> if he has not first fulfilled the requirements of right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato">AN 6.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk must fulfill right concentration before it is possible for him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato"><b>abandon the fetters</b></a> that bind him to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato">AN 6.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that it is impossible for any person to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato"><b>realize Nibbāna</b></a> without first successfully abandoning the spiritual fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato">AN 6.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.67/en/sujato"><b>training of a trainee</b></a> (sekha dhamma) cannot be fulfilled unless one has already completed the training in good conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.67/en/sujato">AN 6.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described a specific sequence of spiritual progress where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato"><b>avoiding company</b></a> leads to solitude, which allows for the development of concentration and the eventual realization of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.68/en/sujato">AN 6.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once declared to his monks that the householder Ugga of Vesālī was endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>eight amazing and astounding qualities</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder Ugga of Vesālī recounted that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>mind became settled</b></a> in faith the very first time he saw the Buddha from a distance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Ugga first sat down to listen, the Buddha gave him a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>gradual teaching</b></a>, which included discourses on giving, virtue, and the heavens? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Ugga the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>danger and degradation</b></a> of sensual pleasures and the various benefits of renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching caused the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>spotless Dhamma-eye</b></a> to arise in Ugga, leading him to realize that whatever is subject to arising is also subject to cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Ugga saw the Dhamma, he told a monk that he became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>independent of others</b></a> in the Teacher’s teaching, having crossed over all doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder Ugga went for refuge to the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha, and immediately undertook <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>celibacy as the fifth</b></a> of his training rules? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ugga told his four young wives they were free to live on his wealth, return to their families, or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>marry another man</b></a> of their choice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Ugga's eldest wife asked to be given to a specific man, Ugga <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>gave her away</b></a> by taking her hand and pouring the water of donation over the hands of the new husband? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ugga claimed that even while giving his beloved wife away to another man, he did not experience any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>alteration of mind</b></a> or resentment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ugga described his household wealth as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>shared impartially</b></a> with those who are virtuous and of good character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ugga of Vesālī stated that whenever he attends to a monk, he always does so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>respectfully</b></a> and never with a lack of respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ugga told a visiting monk that if the monk teaches the Dhamma, Ugga listens <b>attentively and respectfully</b>? And that the householder Ugga mentioned that if a monk he is attending does not teach, then Ugga himself <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>teaches the Dhamma</b></a> to that monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when deities praise the Buddha's teaching to him, Ugga replies that the Dhamma is well-expounded <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>regardless of whether they say so</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ugga maintained he felt no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato"><b>elation of mind</b></a> or conceit from the fact that he was able to converse with celestial beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.21/en/sujato">AN 8.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>—the ultimate goal of Buddhist practice—can be achieved by relying on even the very first jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk training in meditation to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>archer</b></a> or an archer’s apprentice who first practices on a straw man or a mound of clay to eventually become a master who can pierce great bodies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk in the first jhāna should contemplate the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>disease</b></a> and a tumor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a monk turns his mind away from the conditioned phenomena of the jhānas, he should direct it toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>deathless element</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the ultimate peace of Nibbāna as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>stilling of all formations</b></a> and the relinquishing of all acquisitions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if a meditator cannot yet reach the destruction of the taints, they may become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>non-returner</b></a> due to their desire and delight in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the base of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>infinite space</b></a>—where one transcends all perceptions of form—can serve as a meditative foundation for the total destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the base of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>nothingness</b></a>, reached by perceiving "there is nothing at all," is a high meditative state from which one can achieve final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized contemplating the factors of meditation as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>empty</b></a> and non-self in order to turn the mind toward the unconditioned state of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a master archer as one who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>lightning shooter</b></a> and a "piercer of great bodies," using this as a metaphor for an advanced meditator who has mastered the jhānas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the penetration of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>final knowledge</b></a> is possible through contemplative discernment as far as there is "attainment with perception"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even in high immaterial states like the base of infinite consciousness, a monk must see the constituent feelings and perceptions as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>alien</b></a> and disintegrating? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that liberation can be achieved by relying on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a>, a meditative state characterized by the purity of mindfulness through equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the "destruction of craving" and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> are essential components of the peaceful and sublime state he called the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk who has destroyed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>five lower fetters</b></a> becomes one who is "reborn spontaneously" in a higher realm, attaining Nibbāna there without returning to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception and feeling</b></a> as a profound state that, when reached after mastering other attainments, leads to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a meditator must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>skilled in emerging</b></a> from meditative attainments to properly understand and explain the highest states of concentration like the cessation of perception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>desire for the Dhamma</b></a> (dhammarāga) to describe a subtle attachment to meditative states that can lead a practitioner to become a non-returner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should view the mental factors of the second jhāna as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>barb</b></a> and a calamity in order to detach the mind and move toward liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the base of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>neither-perception-nor-non-perception</b></a> as one of the nine progressive meditative attainments that can lead to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the state where one is "no longer subject to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>returning from that world</b></a>" as the destiny of those who master the jhānas but retain some delight in those states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the path to liberation involves seeing the mental components of the third jhāna as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>affliction</b></a> and as suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato"><b>attainments with perception</b></a> (saññāsamāpatti) provide the basis for the analytical contemplation required to reach final knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.36/en/sujato">AN 9.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a chain of nine things rooted in craving, explaining that pursuit leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.23/en/sujato"><b>gain</b></a>, which in turn leads to decision-making, desire, and lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.23/en/sujato">AN 9.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the process of gaining possessions leads to attachment and indulgence, which eventually results in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.23/en/sujato"><b>stinginess</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.23/en/sujato">AN 9.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the act of guarding one's possessions can lead to serious social harms, including the taking up of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.23/en/sujato"><b>rods and weapons</b></a>, quarrels, and strife? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.23/en/sujato">AN 9.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that various evil unwholesome states, such as backbiting and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.23/en/sujato"><b>lying</b></a>, are ultimately rooted in the craving that necessitates the guarding of property? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.23/en/sujato">AN 9.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a monk's mind is thoroughly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.25/en/sujato"><b>cultivated by wisdom</b></a>, he is fit to declare that birth is destroyed and the holy life has been lived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.25/en/sujato">AN 9.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a mind thoroughly cultivated by wisdom as one that is clearly understood to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.25/en/sujato"><b>free from lust</b></a>, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.25/en/sujato">AN 9.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind well-cultivated by wisdom has the nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.25/en/sujato"><b>not returning</b></a> to the sensual realm for future existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.25/en/sujato">AN 9.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a mind thoroughly developed through wisdom is one that will no longer return to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.25/en/sujato"><b>form realm</b></a> of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.25/en/sujato">AN 9.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a mind cultivated by wisdom as having a nature that does not return to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.25/en/sujato"><b>formless realm</b></a> of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.25/en/sujato">AN 9.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that once a monk's mind is fully cultivated by wisdom, he knows for certain that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.25/en/sujato"><b>nothing further</b></a> to be done for this state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.25/en/sujato">AN 9.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned Venerable Upāli that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>remote forest lodgings</b></a> are difficult to endure, and that seclusion is both hard to practice and hard to find delight in? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the forests seem to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>steal away the mind</b></a> of a monk who has not yet attained concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who seeks remote lodgings without having attained concentration is likely to either <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>sink or float away</b></a>, unable to find a firm footing in his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>great elephant</b></a> who can safely bathe and play in a deep lake because his large body allows him to find a footing in the depths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted the giant elephant with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>hare or a cat</b></a>, noting that these small animals would drown if they jumped into a deep lake without considering their own limitations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the early stages of human development by pointing to a young child who plays with his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>urine and excrement</b></a> while lying on his back? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a child matures, the Buddha noted they move on to more refined forms of play, such as playing with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>toy wagons</b></a>, tiny bows, and other children's games? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the worldly enjoyment of adults as being engrossed in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>five strands of sensual pleasure</b></a>, which include agreeable sights, sounds, smells, tastes, and touches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a> arises in the world, he teaches a Dhamma that is perfectly complete and purified, leading to the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Dhamma, a householder may realize that household life is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>crowded path of dust</b></a> and choose to go forth into the homeless life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who has gone forth as one who abandons the taking of life and dwells <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>compassionate</b></a> and kind to all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a virtuous monk, according to the Buddha, stays away from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a> and instead speaks words that promote friendship and reconcile those who are divided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk's speech should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>gentle and affectionate</b></a>, words that go to the heart and are pleasing to many people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a monk should abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a> and instead speak words that are worth recording, timely, and always connected with the goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>bird</b></a> to describe a contented monk, who flies wherever he goes taking only his wings, just as a monk takes only his robes and almsfood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk experiences an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>unblemished happiness</b></a> within himself when he is fully possessed of the noble aggregate of moral discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>restraint of the senses</b></a>, where a monk does not seize upon the signs or features of what he sees so that evil unwholesome states do not overwhelm him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk is said to act with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>clear comprehension</b></a> when he remains fully aware while walking, standing, sitting, eating, and even while performing his bodily functions, as taught by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk must purify his mind of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>five hindrances</b></a>—sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt—before attaining deep meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>first jhāna</b></a> as a state of rapture and pleasure born of seclusion, which is far more excellent than any worldly play? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a monk progresses, the Buddha said he enters the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>second jhāna</b></a>, a state of internal confidence and singleness of mind that is free from thought and examination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, his disciples choose to dwell in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>remote lodgings</b></a> because they see these higher meditative states within themselves, even before they have reached the final goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>formless attainments</b></a>, such as the base of infinite space, as being even more sublime and peaceful than the four jhānas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can eventually reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception and feeling</b></a>, a state where the mental taints are finally destroyed by seeing with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly told Venerable Upāli to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>dwell in the Saṅgha</b></a>, explaining that living within the community would be more comfortable for him than seeking the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared spiritual progress to the way a child's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>toys</b></a> become obsolete as they grow and discover more refined pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who lacks concentration will find the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>solitude of the forest</b></a> to be a struggle rather than a benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>attained their own good</b></a> and destroyed the taints, living in the forest is a natural and appropriate dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> as something "difficult to delight in," particularly for those who have not yet steadied their minds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s advice to Upāli serves as a reminder that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato"><b>spiritual readiness</b></a> is essential before taking on the extreme challenges of remote forest dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an10.99/en/sujato">AN 10.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder Tapussa confessed to the Venerable Ānanda that for laypeople who enjoy and delight in sensual pleasures, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>renunciation</b></a> appears as a terrifying precipice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder Tapussa observed that even very young monks find their minds leaping toward renunciation and seeing it as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>peaceful</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his enlightenment, while still a Bodhisatta, the Buddha found that his own mind did not initially <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>leap toward renunciation</b></a> even though he recognized it was good? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha realized his mind hesitated to renounce because he had not yet seen the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> in sensual pleasures or experienced the benefit of renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once he saw the danger in sensual pleasures and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>cultivated the benefit</b></a> of renunciation, his mind finally grew steady and confident in that state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the experience of the first jhāna as being "afflicted" by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>perceptions and maneuvers</b></a> of the mind associated with sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the intrusion of lower mental states during deep meditation to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>experience of pain</b></a> arising for someone who is otherwise happy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even after attaining the first jhāna, the Buddha’s mind did not initially leap toward the second jhāna because he had not yet seen the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>danger in thought</b></a> and examination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha found that the second jhāna is considered "afflicted" by the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>thought and examination</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha realized the third jhāna—a state of equanimous happiness—is actually "afflicted" by the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>rapture</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the fourth jhāna, characterized by neutral feeling, is "afflicted" by the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>equanimous happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the sphere of infinite space is reached only after completely surmounting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>perceptions of forms</b></a> and the passing away of perceptions of impingement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha found the sphere of infinite space to be "afflicted" by any remaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>perceptions of form</b></a> that might surface? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the sphere of infinite consciousness is "afflicted" by any surfacing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>perceptions of space</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the sphere of nothingness as being "afflicted" by the intrusion of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>perceptions of consciousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the sphere of neither-perception-nor-non-perception is "afflicted" by any surfacing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>perceptions of nothingness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reached the cessation of perception and feeling only after seeing the danger in the sphere of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>neither-perception-nor-non-perception</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>taints were destroyed</b></a> specifically after he reached and saw with wisdom the cessation of perception and feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha did not claim to be "fully enlightened" until he had mastered all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>nine successive attainments</b></a> in both forward and reverse order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> is unshakable and that this current life is his very last birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder Tapussa described himself and other laypeople as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>sensual-pleasure-seekers</b></a> who find the idea of renunciation to be alien and difficult? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he had to see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>benefit in renunciation</b></a> and pursue it repeatedly before his mind would settle into it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha achieved the second jhāna by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>subsiding of thought</b></a> and examination, gaining internal confidence and singleness of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha entered the third jhāna after the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>fading away of rapture</b></a>, dwelling equanimous, mindful, and clearly aware? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reached the fourth jhāna by giving up both pleasure and pain and by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>disappearance of joy</b></a> and grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that to reach the sphere of infinite consciousness, the Buddha had to completely surmount the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>sphere of infinite space</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha entered the sphere of nothingness by realizing that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>there is nothing</b></a> and surmounting the sphere of infinite consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha sought to overcome even the sphere of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>nothingness</b></a> in order to enter the sphere of neither-perception-nor-non-perception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his final achievement as entering and dwelling in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception</b></a> and feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s realization of the "cause and condition" for his mind's initial hesitation led him to investigate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>lack of habituation</b></a> in renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s successful mastery of these states allowed him to announce his awakening to the world, including its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>devas, Māras, and Brahmās</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha achieved the sphere of infinite space by paying no attention to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato"><b>perceptions of diversity</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.41/en/sujato">AN 9.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha initially asked his attendant Meghiya to wait for another monk to arrive before going to meditate alone in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>pleasant mango grove</b></a> he had found by the Kimikāḷā river? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the peaceful setting of a mango grove, the monk Meghiya found himself constantly overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome thoughts</b></a> of sensuality, ill will, and harmfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>good friend</b></a>, companion, and comrade is the first of five things that lead to the maturity of an immature liberation of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a monk has a good friend, it is to be expected that he will be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and live restrained by the rules of the Pātimokkha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>helpful talk</b></a>—specifically speech concerning fewness of wishes, contentment, and seclusion—as a crucial factor for ripening one's spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk with good friends will naturally become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>energetic</b></a> in abandoning unwholesome states and acquiring wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is supported by good friendship will develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> that discerns the arising and passing away of phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that the meditation on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>foulness</b></a> should be specifically developed in order to abandon lustful desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> (mettā) is the essential practice to be developed for the purpose of abandoning ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of breathing</b></a> is the specific tool used to cut off and still discursive thinking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>perception of impermanence</b></a> must be developed by a practitioner to successfully uproot the conceit "I am"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one who perceives impermanence, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-self</b></a> becomes firmly established? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that reaching the uprooting of the conceit "I am" through the perception of non-self is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna in this very life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the monk Meghiya repeatedly asked to leave his duties as an attendant to meditate, the Buddha eventually told him to do what he thought was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>the right time</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>talk on seclusion</b></a> and talk on the knowledge and vision of liberation as types of speech that are truly helpful for opening up the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>steadfast</b></a> and firm in his exertion, never shirking the responsibility of cultivating wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Meghiya believed that a beautiful and charming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>mango grove</b></a> by a river was the perfect place for a clansman intent on striving to practice meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>talk on virtue</b></a>, concentration, and wisdom as essential topics of conversation that a monk should be able to engage in without difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that after grounding oneself in five foundational qualities like virtue and wisdom, one should then practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>four additional meditations</b></a> to further purify the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a mature spiritual life as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>restrained</b></a> by the Pātimokkha and seeing danger in even the slightest of faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Meghiya expressed to the Buddha his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>astonishment</b></a> that even though he had gone forth from the home life out of faith, he was still pursued by evil unwholesome thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wisdom which is noble and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>penetrative</b></a> leads directly to the complete destruction of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who is unable to prosper in his spiritual training to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>cowherd</b></a> who is incapable of looking after and increasing a herd of cattle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>eleven qualities</b></a> that a monk must possess to achieve growth, increase, and fulfillment in the Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk is someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>knowing form</b></a> when he understands as it really is that any kind of form consists of the four great elements and form derived from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>skilled in signs</b></a> when he understands that a fool is characterized by his actions and a wise person is characterized by his actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>removing fly eggs</b></a> to describe a monk who does not tolerate arisen sensual thoughts but instead abandons, dispels, and eliminates them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk "removes the eggs of flies" when he refuses to dwell on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of ill will</b></a> or harmfulness, making them reach non-existence whenever they arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who guards his sense faculties to a cowherd who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>dresses a wound</b></a> in his cattle to prevent infection and further harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk "dresses a wound" when he does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>grasp at signs</b></a> or features of forms seen with the eye, thereby preventing evil unwholesome states from flowing in? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk should practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>restraint of the senses</b></a> regarding the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind to remain spiritually healthy and secure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the expression <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>making smoke</b></a> to describe a monk who teaches the Dhamma to others in detail, exactly as he has heard and mastered it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Buddha's teachings, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>knowing the fords</b></a> refers to a monk who periodically approaches learned elders to ask questions and clarify difficult points of the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to approach those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>learned in the scriptures</b></a>—the bearers of the Dhamma, Vinaya, and Matikas—to reveal what is hidden and dispel doubts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>knows what is drunk</b></a> when he gains inspiration and joy derived from the Dhamma and Vinaya proclaimed by the Tathagata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>knows the path</b></a> is one who truly understands the Noble Eightfold Path as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>skilled in pastures</b></a> to describe a monk who fully understands and practices the four establishments of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>does not milk dry</b></a> is one who knows the proper measure when accepting requisites like robes, almsfood, and medicine from faithful householders? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a capable monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>pays extra honor</b></a> to elder monks who have been ordained for a long time and serve as fathers and leaders of the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, honoring elder monks involves maintaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness in bodily action</b></a> toward them, both in public and in private? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk should maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness in verbal action</b></a> toward the senior elders of the monastic community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness in mental action</b></a> as a vital way for a monk to show reverence to the senior leaders of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a cowherd who does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>know the path</b></a>—the way to lead his cattle to a safe destination—is incapable of causing his herd to prosper? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Buddha's analogy, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>making smoke</b></a> for a cowherd is necessary to keep biting insects away, just as teaching the Dhamma is necessary for the monk's community to thrive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> toward spiritual elders must be maintained consistently, both when one is in their presence and when one is alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>ignorant of form</b></a>—specifically the four great elements—is considered incapable of achieving growth in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk who does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>restrain the mind faculty</b></a> is like a cowherd who fails to dress a wound in his cattle, allowing it to worsen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified "bearers of the Dhamma, bearers of the Vinaya, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>bearers of the Matikas</b></a>" as the specific learned monks that one should consult for spiritual guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk's experience of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>inspiration and joy</b></a> from the Dhamma is analogous to a cowherd knowing exactly what water his cattle have drunk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a> are the "pastures" in which a monk must be skilled to properly nurture his spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>know the measure</b></a> when receiving gifts from faithful donors to avoid exhausting the generosity of the lay supporters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described senior monks who are "fathers of the Sangha" as being like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato"><b>leading bulls</b></a> and sires of a herd, who deserve special honor from a cowherd? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.17/en/sujato">AN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to a deity who addressed the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>reverence for the Teacher</b></a> is the first of six essential factors that prevent a practitioner from falling away from the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity informed the Buddha that a monk's persistent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>reverence for the Dhamma</b></a> serves as a primary protection against spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was told by a deity that maintaining deep <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>reverence for the Saṅgha</b></a> is one of the six specific things that lead to a monk's continued progress without regression? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity highlighted to the Buddha that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>reverence for the training</b></a> is a critical requirement for any monk who wishes to avoid decline in his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>gentleness</b></a>, or being easy to admonish, as a vital quality for a monk to ensure he does not fall away from his attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to a deity's teaching to the Buddha, the cultivation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> is one of the six key conditions that lead to a monk's non-decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after a deity spoke about the qualities for non-decline, the Buddha expressed that he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>in agreement</b></a> with her by remaining silent and then repeating the teaching to his monks the following morning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha addressed the monks to recount how a deity had appeared in the night to list six things that lead to a monk's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>non-falling away</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained to the Buddha that a monk who is personally respectful should also be one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>speaks in praise</b></a> of reverence for the Teacher to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta told the Buddha that a dedicated monk will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>encourage</b></a> those who lack reverence for the Dhamma to develop that quality themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Sāriputta, a monk should identify other monks who already possess reverence for the Saṅgha and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>praise them</b></a> truthfully and at the proper time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta detailed how a monk who values <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>reverence for the training</b></a> will personally embody that respect while also advocating for it among his peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>gentle</b></a> and easy to speak to should actively work to inspire amenability in monks who are difficult to admonish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave his enthusiastic approval by saying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>"Excellent, excellent"</b></a> when Sāriputta provided a detailed breakdown of how to practice and promote the factors for non-decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta emphasized that a monk who values <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> will not only be a good friend himself but will also praise the merits of such associations to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha affirmed that Sāriputta’s detailed explanation—which involved personal practice, encouraging others, and timely praise—is the correct way to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato"><b>meaning</b></a> of the teaching on non-decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.69/en/sujato">AN 6.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a monk to achieve strength in concentration, he must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.72/en/sujato"><b>skilled in entering</b></a> concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.72/en/sujato">AN 6.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.72/en/sujato"><b>skilled in remaining</b></a> in concentration is a necessary quality for a monk to develop power and stability in his meditative states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.72/en/sujato">AN 6.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk cannot reach strength in concentration if he is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.72/en/sujato"><b>skilled in emerging</b></a> from concentration at the proper time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.72/en/sujato">AN 6.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.72/en/sujato"><b>acting with perseverance</b></a> as one of the six key factors that make a monk capable of attaining strength in concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.72/en/sujato">AN 6.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk is incapable of entering the first jhāna unless he succeeds in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.73/en/sujato"><b>abandoning sensual desire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.73/en/sujato">AN 6.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.73/en/sujato"><b>abandoning ill will</b></a> as one of the six prerequisites for a practitioner to be able to enter and dwell in the first jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.73/en/sujato">AN 6.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is unable to attain the first jhāna without first <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.73/en/sujato"><b>abandoning dullness and drowsiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.73/en/sujato">AN 6.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.73/en/sujato"><b>abandoning restlessness and remorse</b></a> is a vital step for any monk who seeks to enter and dwell in the first jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.73/en/sujato">AN 6.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.73/en/sujato"><b>abandoning doubt</b></a> is one of the six things a monk must do to be capable of attaining the first jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.73/en/sujato">AN 6.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the first jhāna can only be attained by someone who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.73/en/sujato"><b>well seen the danger</b></a> in sensual pleasures with right wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.73/en/sujato">AN 6.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is unable to enter and dwell in the first jhāna if he has not abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.74/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of sensual pleasure</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.74/en/sujato">AN 6.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner cannot achieve the first jhāna without first abandoning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.74/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of ill will</b></a> and thoughts of harming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.74/en/sujato">AN 6.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.74/en/sujato"><b>perceptions of sensual pleasure</b></a> as one of the six specific things a monk must abandon to be capable of entering the first jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.74/en/sujato">AN 6.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who successfully abandons <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.74/en/sujato"><b>perceptions of harming</b></a> and perceptions of ill will becomes capable of entering and dwelling in the first jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.74/en/sujato">AN 6.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who remains possessed by thoughts of sensual pleasure, ill will, and harming will dwell in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.75/en/sujato"><b>suffering and despair</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.75/en/sujato">AN 6.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who cultivates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.75/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of renunciation</b></a> is expected to dwell in happiness and peace, and can look forward to a good destination after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.75/en/sujato">AN 6.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, dwelling with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.75/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of non-ill will</b></a> and non-harming leads to a life free from trouble and fever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.75/en/sujato">AN 6.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who maintains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.75/en/sujato"><b>perceptions of renunciation</b></a> and non-harming will experience happiness in this life and a good rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.75/en/sujato">AN 6.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk cannot realize states beyond the human, such as the distinction of noble knowledge and vision, if he possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.77/en/sujato"><b>muddled mindfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.77/en/sujato">AN 6.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.77/en/sujato"><b>lack of clear comprehension</b></a> is one of the six barriers that prevent a monk from achieving superhuman states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.77/en/sujato">AN 6.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk must abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.77/en/sujato"><b>unguarded doors of the senses</b></a> to be capable of realizing the excellence of noble knowledge and vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.77/en/sujato">AN 6.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.77/en/sujato"><b>immoderation in eating</b></a> as a quality that makes a monk incapable of reaching superior spiritual attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.77/en/sujato">AN 6.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.77/en/sujato"><b>deceit</b></a> and flattery are among the six unwholesome qualities that must be abandoned to realize noble knowledge and vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.77/en/sujato">AN 6.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who abandons <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.77/en/sujato"><b>flattery</b></a> (or chatter) becomes capable of realizing states beyond the human? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.77/en/sujato">AN 6.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.78/en/sujato"><b>delighter in the Dhamma</b></a> will dwell with much happiness and joy in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.78/en/sujato">AN 6.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.78/en/sujato"><b>delighter in development</b></a>—meaning mental cultivation—will have a basis for the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.78/en/sujato">AN 6.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.78/en/sujato"><b>delighter in abandoning</b></a> as one of the six qualities that lead to a monk's happiness and the end of mental defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.78/en/sujato">AN 6.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.78/en/sujato"><b>delighter in seclusion</b></a> has a foundation for the destruction of the taints (āsavas)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.78/en/sujato">AN 6.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.78/en/sujato"><b>delighter in non-affliction</b></a> (or non-ill will) allows a monk to dwell with much joy in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.78/en/sujato">AN 6.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.78/en/sujato"><b>delighter in non-proliferation</b></a> (nippapañca) possesses a quality that leads directly to the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.78/en/sujato">AN 6.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Jīvaka Komārabhacca that one is considered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a> simply by going for refuge to the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Saṅgha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato">AN 8.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato"><b>virtuous lay follower</b></a> as one who abstains from destroying life, taking what is not given, sexual misconduct, false speech, and the use of intoxicating drinks and drugs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato">AN 8.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a lay follower practices for both their own benefit and the benefit of others when they are personally accomplished in faith and also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato"><b>encourage others</b></a> in the accomplishment of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato">AN 8.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a lay follower who is personally accomplished in virtue and also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato"><b>encourages others</b></a> in the accomplishment of virtue is practicing for the welfare of both themselves and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato">AN 8.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a lay follower who is personally accomplished in generosity and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato"><b>urges others</b></a> toward the accomplishment of generosity as one practicing for the benefit of both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato">AN 8.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a lay follower who desires to see the monks themselves and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato"><b>inspires others</b></a> to visit the monks is acting for the benefit of both themselves and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato">AN 8.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a lay follower as practicing for the benefit of both when they personally desire to hear the Good Dhamma and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato"><b>encourage others</b></a> to listen to the Good Dhamma as well? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato">AN 8.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a lay follower who is personally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato"><b>practicing in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a> and also encourages others to practice in accordance with the Dhamma is one who practices for the benefit of both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.26/en/sujato">AN 8.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed eight types of powers in the world, identifying the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.27/en/sujato"><b>power of crying</b></a> as the characteristic strength of children? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.27/en/sujato">AN 8.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that while children rely on crying, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.27/en/sujato"><b>power of anger</b></a> is the characteristic strength associated with women? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.27/en/sujato">AN 8.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.27/en/sujato"><b>power of weapons</b></a> as the specific strength upon which thieves and bandits rely for their power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.27/en/sujato">AN 8.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for those who are learned and have much knowledge, their characteristic strength is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.27/en/sujato"><b>power of reflection</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.27/en/sujato">AN 8.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.27/en/sujato"><b>power of patience</b></a> to be the characteristic strength and hallmark of ascetics and brahmins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.27/en/sujato">AN 8.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that the first power of a monk with destroyed taints is seeing all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>conditioned phenomena</b></a> as impermanent with correct wisdom, exactly as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Sāriputta, a monk who has reached the end of taints views all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> as being comparable to a pit of glowing coals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta described the mind of a taintless monk as one that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>inclines toward seclusion</b></a>, slopes toward seclusion, and remains focused on solitary withdrawal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta stated that a monk whose taints are destroyed possesses a mind that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>delights in renunciation</b></a> and is completely detached from all things that provide a basis for taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Sāriputta to clarify exactly how many powers a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>whose taints are destroyed</b></a> must possess to rightfully claim that their taints are finished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a>, when they are well developed and cultivated, as a specific power that supports a monk’s knowledge of their own liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Sāriputta, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>four bases of psychic power</b></a> are among the eight essential strengths that allow a monk to recognize the destruction of their taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that having the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>five faculties</b></a> (such as faith, energy, and wisdom) well developed constitutes a fundamental power for a monk who has destroyed the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta listed the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> as a key strength possessed by those who have attained the final goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> must be fully and properly developed for a monk to have the power to know that their taints are truly gone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta told the Buddha that seeing things <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>as they really are</b></a> regarding the nature of conditioned things is the very first power mentioned for a taintless monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta emphasized that the mind of an Arahant is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>slanted toward renunciation</b></a>, making it one of the eight powers that validate their state of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta described a monk's ability to claim "My taints are destroyed" as being based on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>being possessed of</b></a> these eight specific spiritual strengths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta highlighted that a mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato"><b>completely detached</b></a> from all sources of taints is a defining power of a monk who has finished the spiritual task? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.28/en/sujato">AN 8.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should live in dependence on a teacher or a senior companion while maintaining a keen sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>shame and moral dread</b></a>, which serves as the first cause for obtaining wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>affection and respect</b></a> for one’s teacher or senior companions are essential qualities for a practitioner seeking to develop deep wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to regularly approach their teachers to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>ask questions</b></a> and clarify the meaning of the Dhamma, such as asking, “How is this, Bhante? What is the meaning of this?” <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, teachers help their students by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>dispelling doubts</b></a> regarding various matters that are grounds for doubt when the students inquire about the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that after hearing the Dhamma, a practitioner should practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>two kinds of withdrawal</b></a>: withdrawal of the body and withdrawal of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>virtuous and restrained</b></a> by the Pātimokkha as a critical fourth condition for the growth and fulfillment of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a practitioner should be one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>sees danger</b></a> even in the slightest faults while training in the various rules of conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>learned person</b></a> as one who has heard much Dhamma, remembered it, and thoroughly penetrated it with right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the teachings that lead to wisdom are those that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>good in the beginning</b></a>, middle, and end, and which proclaim a perfectly pure spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for wisdom to grow, a practitioner’s knowledge of the Dhamma must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>retained by speech</b></a>, examined in the mind, and well-penetrated by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>arousing energy</b></a> to abandon unwholesome states and acquire wholesome states is the sixth essential cause for the fulfillment of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner seeking wisdom should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>firm in effort</b></a> and not shirk the responsibility of cultivating wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that when one is in the company of the Sangha, one should avoid <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>animal talk</b></a> (aimless chatter) and instead speak on the Dhamma or invite others to do so? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>noble silence</b></a> as a proper alternative to speaking when a practitioner is gathered with the community of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the eighth cause for wisdom is contemplating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>rise and fall</b></a> of the five aggregates of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically mentioned that observing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>origination and passing away</b></a> of form, feeling, perception, volitional activities, and consciousness is a requirement for spiritual wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that fellow practitioners will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>esteem a monk</b></a> who lives in dependence on a teacher with shame, moral dread, and respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that when a monk asks deep questions about the Dhamma, his fellow practitioners conclude that he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>surely knows and sees</b></a> the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>bodily and mental withdrawal</b></a> after hearing the Dhamma leads to being held in high regard by one’s spiritual companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and trains strictly in the monastic rules earns the respect and appreciation of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>energetic in abandoning</b></a> unwholesome states is a quality that leads to being liked and honored by one's fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that observing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates of clinging</b></a> as they rise and fall is a quality that leads to oneness and esteem within the spiritual community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.2/en/sujato">AN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda taught that the Buddha discovered an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato"><b>attainment of an opening</b></a> in the midst of confinement as a way for the purification of beings and the realization of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato">AN 9.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Ānanda, it is possible for a practitioner to have a functioning eye and see forms, yet remain in a state where they do not experience that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato"><b>visual sense base</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato">AN 9.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda described a meditative achievement where one possesses a functioning ear and hears sounds, but does not actually experience that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato"><b>auditory sense base</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato">AN 9.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda explained that a monk could have a functioning nose and encounter odors, yet still not experience that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato"><b>olfactory sense base</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato">AN 9.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda pointed out a state where one has a functioning tongue and tastes flavors, yet does not experience that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato"><b>gustatory sense base</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato">AN 9.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda stated that a person can have a functioning body and feel tangibles, yet not experience that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato"><b>tactile sense base</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato">AN 9.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Venerable Udāyī asked for clarification, the Venerable Ānanda emphasized that a practitioner who does not experience these sense bases is still <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato"><b>conscious</b></a> rather than unconscious? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato">AN 9.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite space</b></a> as a specific perception where, by surpassing perceptions of form and sensory impact, one no longer experiences the physical sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato">AN 9.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda taught that by entering and dwelling in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite consciousness</b></a>, a monk remains conscious while simultaneously not experiencing the five physical sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato">AN 9.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Ānanda, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato"><b>base of nothingness</b></a> is an attainment where a practitioner is conscious but does not experience the physical sense bases of the eyes, ears, nose, tongue, or body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato">AN 9.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a nun named Jaṭilavāsikā once asked the Venerable Ānanda about a specific type of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> that is steady because of liberation and does not involve trembling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato">AN 9.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda described a high level of concentration as one that is neither leaning forward nor leaning back, and is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato"><b>forcefully suppressed</b></a> by the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato">AN 9.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda shared that the Buddha declared a concentration which is steady and content due to liberation to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato"><b>final knowledge</b></a> as its fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.37/en/sujato">AN 9.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that two Lokāyatika Brahmins informed the Buddha that the teacher Pūraṇa Kassapa claimed to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>all-knowing and all-seeing</b></a>, asserting that he possessed constant and continuous knowledge whether walking, standing, sleeping, or awake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Lokāyatika Brahmins, the teacher Nigaṇṭha Nātaputta claimed to know and see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>infinite world</b></a> through his own infinite knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy of four men who could run as fast as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>skilled archer</b></a> shoots a light arrow across the shadow of a palm tree to illustrate the impossibility of reaching the end of the world by traveling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described four runners whose stride was so great it reached from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>eastern sea</b></a> to the western sea, yet they still could not reach the world's end by walking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if a person with incredible speed traveled for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>hundred years</b></a> without stopping for anything other than basic needs, they would die along the way without ever reaching the physical end of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that the end of the world cannot be known, seen, or reached by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>traveling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the impossibility of reaching it physically, the Buddha insisted that without reaching the end of the world, there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>making an end of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Noble One’s Discipline, the Buddha defines "the world" as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a> consisting of forms, sounds, odors, tastes, and tangibles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>forms cognizable by the eye</b></a> that are agreeable, charming, and pleasing as being part of what is called "the world" in his teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who enters and dwells in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>first jhāna</b></a> is said to have come to the end of the world and to be dwelling at the world's end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even a monk in the first jhāna is still <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>included in the world</b></a> and has not yet fully emerged from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who attains the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>second jhāna</b></a>, characterized by internal confidence and unification of mind, has reached the end of the world but is still not yet emerged from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>third jhāna</b></a>—who dwells in equanimity, mindful and clearly comprehending—as being at the end of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a>, which is the purification of mindfulness by equanimity, is a state where one dwells at the end of the world while still being categorized as part of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by completely surpassing perceptions of form, a monk enters the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite space</b></a> and thereby reaches the end of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that reaching the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite consciousness</b></a> is a way of dwelling at the end of the world, even though the practitioner has not yet fully escaped the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who dwells in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>base of nothingness</b></a> has come to the end of the world, yet remains "not emerged" from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception</b></a> as a state where one is at the end of the world, though still considered "included in the world" by the Enlightened Ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is only by surpassing the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception and entering the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception and feeling</b></a> that one truly transcends the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a monk has truly crossed over attachment to the world when their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>taints are destroyed</b></a> through seeing with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato"><b>not emerged from the world</b></a> to describe practitioners who have reached high meditative states but have not yet achieved the final destruction of mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.38/en/sujato">AN 9.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an ancient <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>battle between the devas and the asuras</b></a> where the asuras initially defeated the devas in three consecutive confrontations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how the devas, after their third defeat by the asuras, fled <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>facing the north</b></a> to take refuge in the City of Devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, once the devas entered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>City of Devas</b></a>, they felt secure and "out of reach" of the asuras? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha also described a different battle where the devas were victorious and the asuras were forced to flee <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>facing the south</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when the asuras retreated to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>City of Asuras</b></a>, they considered themselves "out of reach" of the devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who enters and dwells in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>first jhāna</b></a>—a state of rapture and pleasure born of seclusion—is considered to be living "out of reach" of Mara, the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>Mara the Evil One</b></a> recognizes when a monk has entered the first jhāna and thinks to himself that the monk has become "out of reach" for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the security of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>second jhāna</b></a>—characterized by internal confidence and unification of mind—to a monk being in a state where he is effectively unreachable by Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk dwelling in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>third jhāna</b></a> with equanimity and mindfulness is perceived by Mara as having moved beyond his influence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a>—a state of pure equanimity and mindfulness—as a meditative level where the practitioner is "out of reach" of the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that by entering the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite space</b></a>, a monk is said to have "put an end to Mara" and gone beyond his sight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the vivid metaphor that a monk reaching the base of infinite space has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>blinded Mara</b></a> by depriving Mara’s eye of its opportunity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by surpassing all perceptions of form and entering the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite consciousness</b></a>, a monk becomes invisible to the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>base of nothingness</b></a> as a state where a monk has "struck the trackless" and disappeared from the vision of Mara the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who dwells in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception</b></a> has effectively "put an end to Mara" and crossed over the world's attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception and feeling</b></a> as the meditative state where a monk truly goes beyond the sight of the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk reaches the cessation of perception and feeling and his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>taints are destroyed</b></a> by seeing with wisdom, he has completely put an end to Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the monk who has destroyed his mental taints as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>crossed over the attachment</b></a> in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>out of reach</b></a> (akaraṇīya) to describe how both the monk in jhāna and Mara himself perceive the monk's safety from the Evil One's power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while the jhānas make a monk "out of reach," it is the formless attainments and the destruction of taints that allow a monk to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato"><b>destroy Mara's eye</b></a> and become untraceable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.39/en/sujato">AN 9.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha initially asked his attendant Meghiya to wait for another monk to arrive before going to meditate alone in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>pleasant mango grove</b></a> he had found by the Kimikāḷā river? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the peaceful setting of a mango grove, the monk Meghiya found himself constantly overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome thoughts</b></a> of sensuality, ill will, and harmfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>good friend</b></a>, companion, and comrade is the first of five things that lead to the maturity of an immature liberation of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a monk has a good friend, it is to be expected that he will be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and live restrained by the rules of the Pātimokkha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>helpful talk</b></a>—specifically speech concerning fewness of wishes, contentment, and seclusion—as a crucial factor for ripening one's spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk with good friends will naturally become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>energetic</b></a> in abandoning unwholesome states and acquiring wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is supported by good friendship will develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> that discerns the arising and passing away of phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that the meditation on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>foulness</b></a> should be specifically developed in order to abandon lustful desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> (mettā) is the essential practice to be developed for the purpose of abandoning ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of breathing</b></a> is the specific tool used to cut off and still discursive thinking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>perception of impermanence</b></a> must be developed by a practitioner to successfully uproot the conceit "I am"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one who perceives impermanence, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-self</b></a> becomes firmly established? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that reaching the uprooting of the conceit "I am" through the perception of non-self is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna in this very life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the monk Meghiya repeatedly asked to leave his duties as an attendant to meditate, the Buddha eventually told him to do what he thought was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>the right time</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>talk on seclusion</b></a> and talk on the knowledge and vision of liberation as types of speech that are truly helpful for opening up the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>steadfast</b></a> and firm in his exertion, never shirking the responsibility of cultivating wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Meghiya believed that a beautiful and charming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>mango grove</b></a> by a river was the perfect place for a clansman intent on striving to practice meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>talk on virtue</b></a>, concentration, and wisdom as essential topics of conversation that a monk should be able to engage in without difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that after grounding oneself in five foundational qualities like virtue and wisdom, one should then practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>four additional meditations</b></a> to further purify the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a mature spiritual life as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>restrained</b></a> by the Pātimokkha and seeing danger in even the slightest of faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Meghiya expressed to the Buddha his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato"><b>astonishment</b></a> that even though he had gone forth from the home life out of faith, he was still pursued by evil unwholesome thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.3/en/sujato">AN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the householder Anāthapiṇḍika that a noble disciple who has subsided five dreads and animosities and possesses four factors of stream-entry can declare themselves a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>stream-enterer</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>kills living beings</b></a> creates dread and animosity both in the present life and in the future, while also experiencing mental pain and grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by abstaining from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>taking what is not given</b></a>, a noble disciple ensures that the dread and animosity resulting from theft is completely subsided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> leads to dread and animosity in this life and the next, but one who abstains from it is free from such fear and mental distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>false speech</b></a> as a source of dread and animosity, which is subsided in a noble disciple who practices restraint from lying? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the use of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>intoxicants</b></a> such as wine and liquor produces dread and animosity, and that abstaining from them is a prerequisite for a peaceful mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence</b></a> in the Buddha—recognizing Him as the Tathāgata, the Arahant, and the Perfectly Enlightened One—as a primary factor of stream-entry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a> is well-expounded, directly visible, immediate, and invites all to "come and see" (ehipassiko)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>Saṅgha</b></a> of His disciples as consisting of four pairs of persons or eight types of individuals who are an unsurpassed field of merit for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>virtues dear to the noble ones</b></a>, which are unbroken, unspotted, and lead the practitioner toward concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a stream-enterer is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>fixed in destiny</b></a>, meaning they are no longer subject to falling into lower worlds and are certain to reach enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Anāthapiṇḍika that a noble disciple with confirmed confidence and virtue can declare that they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>finished with hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a noble disciple, rebirth in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>animal realm</b></a> is a destination that has been permanently transcended? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a stream-enterer is no longer subject to rebirth in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>sphere of afflicted spirits</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the virtues of a noble disciple are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>unblemished</b></a>, liberating, and praised by those who are wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>personally experienced</b></a> by the wise for themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Saṅgha of His disciples is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>worthy of gifts</b></a>, hospitality, offerings, and reverential salutation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to one who has attained stream-entry as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato"><b>enlightenment</b></a> as their certain future destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.27/en/sujato">AN 9.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> once declared to a group of monks at the Bamboo Grove in Rājagaha that Nibbāna is true happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked how Nibbāna can be happiness if no feeling is experienced there, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> replied that the very fact that nothing is felt there is itself happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> identified the five cords of sensual pleasure as forms, sounds, smells, tastes, and tangibles that are agreeable, charming, and likable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> defined sensual happiness as the specific pleasure and joy that arises in dependence on the five cords of sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> taught that for a monk residing in the first jhāna, any emergence of perceptions and mental maneuvers associated with sensuality is considered an affliction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> explained that just as pain might arise in a happy person to their distress, unwholesome sensual thoughts arise as an affliction even for one in a meditative state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> cited the Buddha's teaching that whatever is an affliction is necessarily suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> stated that for a monk in the second jhāna, the presence of thought and examination (vitakka-vicāra) is an affliction that disrupts that state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a>, even the feeling of rapture (pīti) is considered an affliction for a monk who has attained the third jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> described how a monk enters the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite space</b></a> by completely transcending perceptions of form and the passing away of perceptions of sensory impingement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> explained that for someone in the base of infinite space, any mental maneuver associated with form perceptions is an affliction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> taught that in the meditative base of infinite consciousness, any perception related to the base of infinite space is an affliction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> noted that for a practitioner in the base of nothingness, any perception associated with the base of infinite consciousness acts as an affliction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> explained that even the refined state of neither-perception-nor-non-perception contains afflictions in the form of perceptions from the base of nothingness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> taught that a monk can reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception and feeling</b></a>, a state where all mental afflictions are stilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> asserted that when a monk sees the cessation of perception and feeling with wisdom, his mental taints (āsavā) are utterly destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Udāyī</b></a> was the monk who specifically asked Sāriputta to clarify how happiness could exist in Nibbāna if there is no feeling there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> used the progression of meditative states to show that happiness is found through the removal of increasingly subtle layers of mental activity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> taught that the highest happiness is not a feeling, but rather the total absence of the "affliction" of feeling and perception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> highlighted that sensual pleasures are essentially tied to the five senses, and thus distinct from the superior happiness of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a>, the state of Nibbāna can be understood as happiness through the perspective of the gradual ending of all suffering and mental disturbance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.34/en/sujato">AN 9.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once waited outside an assembly hall until a teaching session finished, even though his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>back ached</b></a> from standing for so long while waiting for the talk to end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when noble disciples gather, they have two primary duties: either to engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma talk</b></a> or to maintain noble silence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who possesses faith but lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> is considered incomplete in that specific factor of his spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who lacks insight into the Dhamma—despite having faith, virtue, and concentration—to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>four-footed animal</b></a> with one stunted or small foot? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk is only fully complete in his spiritual factors when he simultaneously possesses faith, virtue, internal serenity of mind, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>insight</b></a> through higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka expressed great <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>embarrassment</b></a> when he realized the Buddha had been standing outside the porch waiting for him to finish his long discourse to the monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka taught that one of the five benefits of hearing the Dhamma at the right time is that the practitioner becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>dear and agreeable</b></a> to the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>noble silence</b></a> as one of the two appropriate behaviors for clansmen who have gone forth from the home life into homelessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka explained that through timely Dhamma discussion, a monk is able to experience both the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>meaning and the Dhamma</b></a> itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka stated that hearing the Dhamma at the right time allows a monk to penetrate a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>deep point of meaning</b></a> through the application of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka taught that when a monk skillfully teaches the Dhamma, his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>fellow practitioners</b></a> will hold him in high regard and esteem, recognizing his progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka noted that hearing the Dhamma inspires <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>trainees</b></a>—those still seeking the supreme security from bondage—to arouse their energy to attain the unattained and realize the unrealized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka taught that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>Arahants</b></a> who have finished their task find joy in hearing the Dhamma, as it serves as a happy dwelling in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to actively fulfill their missing spiritual qualities by reflecting on how they might become both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>faithful and virtuous</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>internal serenity of mind</b></a> as a crucial factor that a faithful and virtuous monk must strive to acquire to ensure his spiritual development is not lopsided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the spiritual life he proclaimed as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>perfectly complete</b></a> and pure, particularly when it includes the four factors of faith, virtue, serenity, and insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once stood at the outer porch of an assembly hall, waiting for Venerable Nandaka to finish <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>exhorting</b></a> and gladdening the monks with a Dhamma talk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the act of monks gathering for a talk, stating it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>fitting</b></a> for clansmen who have gone forth out of faith to engage in such discussion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>stunted</b></a> to describe the spiritual state of a monk who may possess concentration but still lacks insight into the Dhamma through higher wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Nandaka, a Dhamma teaching that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>good in the beginning</b></a>, middle, and end is essential for helping practitioners penetrate deep truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>insight</b></a> into the Dhamma through higher wisdom is the fourth and final factor required to make a monk's spiritual qualifications entirely complete? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's long wait outside the assembly hall was only signaled by his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato"><b>clearing his throat</b></a> and knocking on the door bolt once the teaching session had concluded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.4/en/sujato">AN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato"><b>virtue of one beyond training</b></a> (asekha) as one of the essential aggregates that characterizes a monk who has reached the absolute conclusion of the spiritual life and the ultimate security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato">AN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who possesses the aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato"><b>concentration of one beyond training</b></a> is one who has reached the ultimate end and is considered the best among both devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato">AN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato"><b>wisdom of one beyond training</b></a> is a defining quality of a monk who is "ultimate in the spiritual life" and who has attained the final goal of security from all bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato">AN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is endowed with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato"><b>miracle of psychic power</b></a> is considered to have reached the ultimate goal and is the most excellent among devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato">AN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato"><b>miracle of mind-reading</b></a> (or manifestation) as one of the three wondrous qualities that distinguish a monk as having reached the ultimate end of the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato">AN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk endowed with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato"><b>miracle of instruction</b></a>—the ability to guide others toward liberation—is one who has reached the ultimate goal and the ultimate security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato">AN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk who possesses right view, right knowledge, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato"><b>right liberation</b></a> is considered to have reached the absolute conclusion of the spiritual life and is the best among devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato">AN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being fully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato"><b>endowed with true knowledge and conduct</b></a> is what makes a monk the foremost among both devas and humans, reaching the final end of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato">AN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha quoted a verse by Brahma Sanaṅkumāra which claims that while a khattiya (warrior-noble) is the best of those who rely on lineage, the person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato"><b>perfect in knowledge and conduct</b></a> is actually the best among devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato">AN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly endorsed a teaching by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato"><b>Brahma Sanaṅkumāra</b></a>, confirming that the Brahma's verse regarding the excellence of knowledge and conduct was well-spoken, beneficial, and identical to the Buddha's own view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.10/en/sujato">AN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying in Nigrodha’s Park at Kapilavatthu, he was approached by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>Mahānāma the Sakyan</b></a>, who was concerned about how to dwell properly after the Buddha and the monks departed for their travels? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a layperson who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>faithful</b></a> is "one who succeeds" (ārādhako), whereas one who lacks faith does not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> is a necessary quality for a noble disciple to succeed in their spiritual practice, contrasting it with laziness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>established mindfulness</b></a> is considered "one who succeeds," while those who are confused in mindfulness are not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>concentrated</b></a> as a key attribute for a practitioner to be successful, as opposed to being unconcentrated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>wise</b></a> is "one who succeeds" in the Dhamma, whereas one who is unwise fails to do so? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Tathāgata</b></a>, noting that when a disciple does this, their mind is not obsessed by lust, hatred, or delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by recollecting the enlightened qualities of the Teacher, a disciple gains a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>straight mind</b></a> (ujugata), which leads to inspiration in the meaning and the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a sequence where a disciple who gains inspiration in the Dhamma experiences <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>gladness</b></a> (pāmojja), which then leads to the arising of rapture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one who is filled with rapture, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>body becomes tranquil</b></a>, and this physical tranquility leads to the experience of happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mind of one who is happy becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>concentrated</b></a>, completing a meditative progression triggered by holy recollections? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to a disciple who practices the recollection of the Tathāgata as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>entered the stream of the Dhamma</b></a> (dhammasotasamāpanno)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Dhamma</b></a>, which involves reflecting on how the teaching is well-expounded, visible here and now, and to be personally realized by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Saṅgha</b></a>, focusing on the community of disciples who practice well and who constitute an unsurpassed field of merit for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised reflecting on one's own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>virtues</b></a> (sīlānussati), specifically those that are unbroken, untattered, unblemished, and praised by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>recollection of generosity</b></a> (cāgānussati) as reflecting on the good fortune of living at home with a mind free from the stain of stinginess? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>devoted to charity</b></a> and rejoice in giving and sharing, as part of their spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha introduced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>recollection of deities</b></a> (devatānussati), where a practitioner reflects on the faith, virtue, and wisdom that allowed beings to be reborn in heavenly realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that when recollecting deities, a disciple should reflect: "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>Such faith exists in me too</b></a>," comparing their own spiritual qualities to those of the devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by practicing these six recollections, a noble disciple dwells <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>equanimously among a non-equanimous generation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>learning</b></a> (suta) as one of the five key qualities—alongside faith, virtue, generosity, and wisdom—to be reflected upon during the recollection of deities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a disciple who practices these six recollections dwells <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>without affliction</b></a> (abyāpajjo) even among those who are afflicted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed several classes of deities for recollection, ranging from the Devas of the Four Great Kings up to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>Brahmakāyikā devas</b></a> and beyond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that during the moment a disciple recollects the Dhamma, their mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>not obsessed by hatred</b></a>, because it is focused purely on the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Mahānāma to first <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato"><b>establish himself in five qualities</b></a> (faith, virtue, learning, generosity, and wisdom) before further developing the six specific recollections? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.11/en/sujato">AN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>Nandiya the Sakyan</b></a> followed the Buddha from Kapilavatthu to Sāvatthī so that he could manage his business affairs while still having the opportunity to visit the Buddha from time to time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>faithful</b></a> is "one who succeeds" (ārādhako) in the spiritual life, whereas one who lacks faith is one who fails? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> as a key quality for success, stating that a person who is virtuous succeeds in their practice, while one who is immoral does not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> is considered "one who succeeds" in the Dhamma, whereas one who is lazy does not reach success? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>established mindfulness</b></a> is a necessary attribute for a practitioner to succeed, as opposed to being muddle-minded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a disciple who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>concentrated</b></a> is "one who succeeds," while a person who is unconcentrated does not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a> is characterized as "one who succeeds" in the holy life, whereas an unwise person fails to do so? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Nandiya to establish himself in six success-oriented qualities—faith, virtue, energy, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom—and then develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>internal mindfulness</b></a> through five specific recollections? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Tathāgata</b></a>, which involves reflecting on the Buddha's qualities as an Arahant, perfectly enlightened, and the teacher of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Dhamma</b></a>, encouraging disciples to reflect that the teaching is well-expounded, visible here and now, and to be personally realized by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged a specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>recollection of good friends</b></a> (kalyāṇamittā), where a disciple rejoices in having companions who are compassionate, desire their welfare, and provide helpful advice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted the value of having compassionate advisors who provide <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>instruction and exhortation</b></a> as a primary subject for internal mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>recollection of generosity</b></a> (cāgānussati), where a disciple reflects on the blessing of living at home with a mind free from the stain of stinginess? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a noble disciple as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>open-handed</b></a>, delighting in giving and sharing, and always ready to respond to a request for help? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>recollection of deities</b></a> who have surpassed the level of those eating solid food and have been reborn with mind-made bodies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned certain deities with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>mind-made bodies</b></a> (manomayaṃ kāyaṃ) who see no further work to be done for themselves and no need to add to what they have already achieved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the state of high-level deities to a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>liberated through non-attachment</b></a> (asamayavimutto), as both have completed their spiritual tasks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a noble disciple endowed with eleven specific qualities—six successes and five recollections—fully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>abandons evil unwholesome states</b></a> and does not cling to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>overturned pot</b></a> (kumbho nikkujjo) to illustrate how a practitioner with eleven qualities pours out evil states so that they do not return? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the abandonment of unwholesome states to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato"><b>fire</b></a> moving through a grove of dry grass, which only goes forward burning and never turns back to what has already been consumed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.13/en/sujato">AN 11.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk attains greatness and abundance in the Dhamma by being full of light and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.80/en/sujato"><b>application</b></a> in his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.80/en/sujato">AN 6.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk reaches abundance in wholesome states by having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.80/en/sujato"><b>much inspiration</b></a> and spiritual exertion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.80/en/sujato">AN 6.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk achieves spiritual greatness by having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.80/en/sujato"><b>non-satisfaction</b></a> with his current wholesome states, continually striving for higher attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.80/en/sujato">AN 6.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified not laying down the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.80/en/sujato"><b>burden</b></a> regarding wholesome qualities as a requirement for a practitioner to reach greatness in the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.80/en/sujato">AN 6.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.84/en/sujato"><b>large desires</b></a> and who is vexed can only expect a decline in his wholesome qualities day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.84/en/sujato">AN 6.84</a></p></div><hr>
