<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.85/en/sujato"><b>gladdens the mind</b></a> when appropriate and maintains equanimity when needed is capable of reaching the ultimate coolness of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.85/en/sujato">AN 6.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is incapable of entering the fixed course of rightness in wholesome states if they are hindered by an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.86/en/sujato"><b>obstruction of kamma</b></a>, such as a grave past action? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.86/en/sujato">AN 6.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, being hindered by an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.86/en/sujato"><b>obstruction of defilement</b></a> prevents a person from entering the fixed course of rightness, even when they hear the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.86/en/sujato">AN 6.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.86/en/sujato"><b>obstruction of the result</b></a> (the karmic ripening of past deeds) can make a person incapable of entering the fixed course of rightness in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.86/en/sujato">AN 6.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that even if they hear the teachings, a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.86/en/sujato"><b>unwise</b></a> and lacks zeal is incapable of entering the fixed course of rightness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.86/en/sujato">AN 6.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.86/en/sujato"><b>possessing faith</b></a>, zeal, and wisdom as the essential qualities that make a person capable of entering the fixed course of rightness in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.86/en/sujato">AN 6.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.88/en/sujato"><b>grasps what is wrong</b></a> and rejects what is right is incapable of entering the fixed course of rightness while hearing the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.88/en/sujato">AN 6.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.88/en/sujato"><b>receptivity</b></a> (patience) in accordance with the truth is a necessary condition for a person to be capable of entering the fixed course of rightness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.88/en/sujato">AN 6.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.88/en/sujato"><b>lends an ear</b></a> and applies their mind to understand the Dhamma is capable of entering the fixed course of rightness in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.88/en/sujato">AN 6.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person possessed of right view is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.92/en/sujato"><b>incapable</b></a> of living without respect and deference for the Teacher (the Buddha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.92/en/sujato">AN 6.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is impossible for a person who has attained right view to live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.92/en/sujato"><b>without respect</b></a> for the Dhamma, the Saṅgha, or the training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.92/en/sujato">AN 6.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person possessed of right view is incapable of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.92/en/sujato"><b>reborn for an eighth time</b></a>, as they are certain to reach liberation within seven lifetimes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.92/en/sujato">AN 6.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person with right view is incapable of regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.93/en/sujato"><b>any formation</b></a> (conditioned thing) as permanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.93/en/sujato">AN 6.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person possessed of right view is incapable of regarding any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.93/en/sujato"><b>conditioned formation</b></a> as pleasurable or happy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.93/en/sujato">AN 6.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person with right view is incapable of regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.93/en/sujato"><b>any phenomenon</b></a> (dhamma) as self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.93/en/sujato">AN 6.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person with right view is incapable of committing an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.93/en/sujato"><b>immediate deed</b></a>, such as a grave action that brings immediate results in the very next life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.93/en/sujato">AN 6.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person possessed of right view is incapable of seeking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.93/en/sujato"><b>purity through luck</b></a> and omens or superstitious signs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.93/en/sujato">AN 6.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person with right view is incapable of seeking a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.93/en/sujato"><b>person worthy of offerings</b></a> outside of the Buddha's dispensation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.93/en/sujato">AN 6.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.86/en/sujato"><b>zeal</b></a> (desire for the wholesome) as one of the six qualities that make a person capable of entering the fixed course of rightness when they hear the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.86/en/sujato">AN 6.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.88/en/sujato"><b>receptive patience</b></a>—a mind that is compliant with the truth—is a quality that allows a person to enter the fixed course of rightness while listening to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.88/en/sujato">AN 6.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk becomes unloved and disliked by his companions when he is one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.3/en/sujato"><b>praises what is unlovable</b></a> and disparages what is lovable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.3/en/sujato">AN 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.3/en/sujato"><b>moral shame and moral dread</b></a> is considered disagreeable and is not respected by his fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.3/en/sujato">AN 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.3/en/sujato"><b>evil wishes and wrong view</b></a> as two of the eight specific qualities that make a monk unloved and disliked by his fellow celibate practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.3/en/sujato">AN 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk becomes dear and appealing to his companions when he possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.3/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> and is not one who longs for gain or honor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.3/en/sujato">AN 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a monk is often unloved by his peers if he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.4/en/sujato"><b>does not know the right time</b></a> to act or speak and does not know moderation in things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.4/en/sujato">AN 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.4/en/sujato"><b>impure and talkative</b></a> are qualities that lead a monk to be unloved, disliked, and not respected by his fellow spiritual practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.4/en/sujato">AN 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk who is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.4/en/sujato"><b>abuser and reviler</b></a> of his fellow practitioners possesses one of the eight negative qualities that make him unloved and disagreeable to his community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.4/en/sujato">AN 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.4/en/sujato"><b>knows the right time</b></a> and knows moderation is held dear, liked, and respected by those living the spiritual life alongside him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.4/en/sujato">AN 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.5/en/sujato"><b>eight worldly conditions</b></a> that spin after the world: gain and loss, fame and disrepute, blame and praise, and pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.5/en/sujato">AN 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that common worldly conditions like gain, loss, status, and disgrace are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.5/en/sujato"><b>impermanent and subject to change</b></a> among human beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.5/en/sujato">AN 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a wise person as one whose mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.5/en/sujato"><b>not disturbed by agreeable things</b></a> (like gain or praise) and does not feel resistance toward disagreeable things (like loss or blame)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.5/en/sujato">AN 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one who has transcended existence, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.5/en/sujato"><b>attraction and repulsion</b></a> toward worldly conditions are dispelled and no longer exist within them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.5/en/sujato">AN 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.3/en/sujato"><b>longing for gain and honor</b></a> as specific qualities that cause a monk to be unloved and not respected by his fellow practitioners in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.3/en/sujato">AN 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk becomes disagreeable to his peers if he is one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.4/en/sujato"><b>longs for an unearned reputation</b></a> or prominence within the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.4/en/sujato">AN 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk is loved and respected when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.4/en/sujato"><b>pure and not talkative</b></a>, and is careful not to abuse or revile his fellow monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.4/en/sujato">AN 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by understanding the changing nature of worldly conditions, one can realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.5/en/sujato"><b>sorrowless and stainless state</b></a> and truly cross over to the further shore of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.5/en/sujato">AN 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>eight worldly conditions</b></a> that spin after the world: gain and loss, fame and disrepute, blame and praise, and pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>both types of people</b></a>—the uninstructed worldling and the instructed noble disciple—experience the same eight worldly conditions of gain, loss, fame, disrepute, blame, praise, pleasure, and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said an uninstructed worldling does not understand as it really is that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>gain is impermanent</b></a>, suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for an uninstructed worldling, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>mind remains obsessed</b></a> when conditions like loss, disrepute, or blame arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that an uninstructed worldling is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>favorable to gain</b></a> and pleasure but is opposed to loss and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who is bound by attraction and repulsion toward worldly conditions is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>not freed from suffering</b></a>, birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an instructed noble disciple understands that whatever <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>pleasure and pain</b></a> arises is actually impermanent, suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mind of an instructed noble disciple is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>not obsessed</b></a> by the fluctuations of fame and disrepute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said an instructed noble disciple is one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>does not favor</b></a> praise and is not opposed to blame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by abandoning attraction and repulsion toward worldly conditions, a disciple is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>freed from birth</b></a>, aging, death, and all forms of sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>distinguishing factor</b></a> between an uninstructed worldling and a noble disciple as the noble disciple's ability to not let their mind be consumed by worldly conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized all eight worldly conditions as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>subject to change</b></a> and non-eternal among human beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>mindful and wise</b></a> person contemplates the changing nature of gain and loss rather than being swept away by them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>agreeable things</b></a> like fame and pleasure do not disturb the mind of a wise person who truly understands their nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a wise person does not experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>repulsion toward disagreeable</b></a> conditions such as disrepute or blame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>attraction and repulsion</b></a> are dispelled and ended in those who have reached the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ultimate spiritual goal as knowing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>sorrowless, stainless state</b></a> which is beyond the reach of worldly conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said one who fully understands the nature of worldly conditions has truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>crossed to the further shore</b></a> of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>fame and disrepute</b></a> are among the conditions that the world spins after and that spin after the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an uninstructed person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>opposed to loss</b></a> and pain, which keeps them trapped in the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an instructed noble disciple <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato"><b>understands according to reality</b></a> when experiencing blame, seeing it as an impermanent and changing phenomenon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.6/en/sujato">AN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a forest elephant as being deeply annoyed and disgusted when other elephants and calves walk ahead of him and eat the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>tips of grass</b></a> he intended to graze on? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the crowded life of a monk to a forest elephant who is troubled when other elephants eat the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>broken-off branches</b></a> that he himself has already pulled down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a forest elephant feels revolted when he goes to a watering hole and other elephants have already stirred up the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>muddied water</b></a> with their trunks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned a forest elephant is bothered when female elephants <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>rub their bodies</b></a> against him as he is emerging from a bathing place? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk might feel the need to live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>withdrawn from the group</b></a> because he is crowded by other monks, nuns, laypeople, and even royalty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the relief of a forest elephant who, after seeking solitude, is finally able to drink <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>clear water</b></a> that has not been stirred up by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of an elephant "withdrawing his trunk" to symbolize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>satisfaction and contentment</b></a> found in solitary living? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to seek <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>secluded dwellings</b></a> such as the foot of a tree, a mountain cave, or a charnel ground to facilitate their meditation practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk in solitude works to purify his heart from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>covetousness</b></a> by dwelling with a mind free of longing for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk purifies his mind from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>ill will and hatred</b></a> by dwelling with a heart of compassion for the welfare of all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a monk overcomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> by maintaining a "perception of light" and being mindful and clearly acting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a monk in seclusion abandons <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a>, resulting in a mind that is internally peaceful and calm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a monk abandons <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> and dwells certain and undecisive regarding wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by being secluded from sensual pleasures, a monk enters and dwells in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>first jhana</b></a>, characterized by rapture and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha equated the deep satisfaction of entering the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhana</b></a> with the contentment of a solitary elephant who has "withdrawn his trunk"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk can progress beyond physical forms to reach the meditative base of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>infinite space</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the base of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>nothingness</b></a> as a refined state of meditation achieved by completely transcending the base of infinite consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the most advanced meditative attainment mentioned in this discourse is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception and feeling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a monk attains the cessation of perception and feeling and sees with wisdom, his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a> (āsavas) are finally destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the solitary elephant's happiness as a metaphor for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato"><b>liberation of the mind</b></a> that occurs when a practitioner is fully successful in their secluded training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.40/en/sujato">AN 9.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda, while staying at Ghosita's Park in Kosambi, explained that the Buddha identified the five cords of sensual pleasure as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>crowded place</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda quoted a verse by the young deva Pañcālacaṇḍa, which praises the Buddha for discovering an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>opening</b></a> in the crowded place of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Ānanda, the Buddha taught that entering the first jhana is a way to find an opening in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>crowdedness of sensual pleasures</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that even in the first jhana, there is still a sense of being "crowded" because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>thought and examination</b></a> (vitakka and vicāra) have not yet ceased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a> as consisting of forms, sounds, odors, tastes, and tactile objects that are agreeable, charming, and endearing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda stated that a monk attains an opening in the crowded place by entering the second jhana, though it is still "crowded" as long as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>rapture</b></a> (pīti) has not ceased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that while the third jhana provides an opening, it remains crowded so long as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>equanimous happiness</b></a> (upekkhāsukha) has not yet ceased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Ānanda, the fourth jhana is considered an opening, but it is still crowded by the presence of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>perception of form</b></a> (rūpasaññā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that a monk finds an opening by reaching the base of infinite space, yet this state is still crowded by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>perception of the base of infinite space</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda noted that the base of infinite consciousness is an opening, but it remains crowded because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>perception of the base of infinite consciousness</b></a> has not yet ceased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that reaching the base of nothingness is an opening, though it is still considered crowded until the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>perception of the base of nothingness</b></a> ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda described the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception as an opening, yet it remains crowded until the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>perception of that base</b></a> ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda stated that the definitive opening in the crowded place, without further qualification, is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception and feeling</b></a> (saññāvedayitanirodha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Ānanda, it is through seeing with wisdom at the stage of the cessation of perception and feeling that a monk's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a> (āsavas) are finally destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda distinguished between attaining an opening "in a way" through the jhanas and attaining it <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>definitively</b></a> (nippariyāyena) through the destruction of taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda responded to a specific inquiry from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Udāyī</b></a> regarding the definition of a "crowded place" and the "attainment of an opening" as spoken by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that one transcends the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>perceptions of diversity</b></a> and the perceptions of form to reach the formless meditative attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda, referencing the Buddha's teachings, identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>five senses</b></a>—vision, hearing, smell, taste, and touch—as the primary sources of "crowdedness" for a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda described the "seer" mentioned in the verses as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>withdrawn bull among men</b></a> who has understood jhana and found the opening in a crowded place? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that when a monk completely transcends the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception, he enters the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception and feeling</b></a> to find true freedom from all crowded states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.42/en/sujato">AN 9.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are four distinct powers—wisdom, energy, blamelessness, and social unity—that constitute the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>four powers</b></a> a noble disciple uses to overcome all forms of fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>power of wisdom</b></a> as the ability to clearly see and examine which states are wholesome or unwholesome, blameworthy or blameless, and which should be cultivated or abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>power of energy</b></a> as the act of arousing desire and making a persistent effort to abandon unwholesome, dark states while acquiring wholesome, bright ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a noble disciple possesses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>power of blamelessness</b></a> when they are endowed with conduct of body, speech, and mind that is entirely beyond reproach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed four specific bases for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>power of social unity</b></a>, which include generosity, endearing speech, beneficial conduct, and impartiality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the giving of the Dhamma (the teachings) to be the foremost and highest form among all possible types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>generosity</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the foremost form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>endearing speech</b></a> is to repeatedly teach the Dhamma to someone who is attentive and truly wishes to listen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the highest form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>beneficial conduct</b></a> is to encourage and establish a faithless person in faith, or an immoral person in virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the foremost form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>impartiality</b></a> as the mutual recognition and shared state between practitioners at the same stage of awakening, such as one Arahant with another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a noble disciple endowed with the four powers can completely transcend the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>five fears</b></a>, which include the fear of death and the fear of a bad destination after rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that only a person who lacks wisdom or is lazy and indolent has a reason to suffer from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>fear regarding livelihood</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a noble disciple does not experience the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>fear of bad reputation</b></a> because their conduct of body, speech, and mind is blameless and pure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who practice the four bases of social unity—such as generosity and beneficial conduct—are freed from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>fear of embarrassment</b></a> when entering any social assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>fear of death</b></a> is a burden only for those who are foolish, lazy, or have committed blameworthy actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>fear of a bad destination</b></a> as a legitimate worry only for those who lack wisdom and have failed to cultivate wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a noble disciple who possesses the power of wisdom and energy can reflect on their own state and realize they have no cause to fear a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>bad rebirth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that encouraging a stingy person to establish themselves in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>attainment of generosity</b></a> is one of the highest ways to practice beneficial conduct toward others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person's qualities have been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>well-discerned by wisdom</b></a>, they can no longer be overwhelmed by the various terrors that plague the average person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>attainment of wisdom</b></a> is a power that allows one to distinguish between what is noble and what is not, leading to the transcending of all fears? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple who possesses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato"><b>four powers</b></a> of wisdom, energy, blamelessness, and social unity lives with a mind that is firm and courageous in the face of life's uncertainties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.5/en/sujato">AN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a person should be known as twofold—one to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>associated with</b></a> and one not to be associated with—depending on whether wholesome states increase or decline through their company? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta advised that if associating with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>certain person</b></a> causes unwholesome states to increase and wholesome states to decline, one should leave them immediately without even asking for permission? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, even if the requisites like robes and food are easy to obtain, one should not associate with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>person</b></a> if their company causes the goal of the ascetic life to remain unfulfilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>monastic robes</b></a> should be evaluated based on whether wearing them causes unwholesome states to grow or wholesome states to decrease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> leads to the growth of wholesome states and the decline of unwholesome states, it is the kind that should be utilized and associated with? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>lodging</b></a> should not be used if living there causes unwholesome mental states to increase and wholesome ones to decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>village or town</b></a> should only be frequented if doing so helps wholesome states flourish and unwholesome states fade away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that one should even follow a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>person</b></a> for their entire life, despite being told to leave, if that association leads to the fulfillment of the spiritual goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>country or region</b></a> as unsuitable if residing there results in the decline of wholesome states and the growth of unwholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>goal of the ascetic life</b></a> is the primary metric by which a monk should decide whether to stay with or leave a particular individual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta advised that if wholesome states increase while associating with a person, one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>follow that person</b></a> even if basic necessities like medicine and food are difficult to obtain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, one should not use <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>robes</b></a> if they lead to an increase in unwholesome qualities, regardless of how easy or difficult they are to acquire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> that hinders spiritual progress should be avoided, even if it is otherwise readily and easily available? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta suggested that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>lodgings</b></a> are to be judged by their effect on one's mental development rather than their physical comfort or luxury? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta classified a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>village or town</b></a> as "not to be associated with" if visiting it results in the growth of unwholesome states and the abandonment of wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta emphasized that the choice of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>country or region</b></a> to live in should be based on whether it supports the growth of one's meditation and spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome states</b></a> increasing is a definitive sign to stop using a particular requisite or to stop associating with a specific person or location? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a> flourishing is the ultimate standard for determining which persons and environments a practitioner should associate with? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta advised leaving a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>person</b></a> immediately, at any time of day or night, if their company causes spiritual decline and a failure to secure the necessities of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a person who helps one fulfill the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>development of the spiritual life</b></a> should be followed for as long as one lives, even if they were to dismiss you? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>requisites</b></a> like robes and almsfood are only to be used if they support the practitioner in abandoning unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato"><b>external environments</b></a> like villages and countries into two types based entirely on their impact on a practitioner's internal mental state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.6/en/sujato">AN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Subhūti once introduced a monk to the Buddha who was the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>son of Sudatta</b></a> (also known as Anāthapiṇḍika), stating that this monk had gone forth from the household life into homelessness out of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Subhūti requested the Buddha to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>explain the marks</b></a> of a person's faith so that he could determine if a particular monk truly displayed those qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being virtuous and living restrained by the Pātimokkha as a fundamental <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>mark of faith</b></a> for a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk demonstrates faith when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>accomplished in behavior</b></a> and resort, seeing danger in even the slightest faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who has much learning and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>store of learning</b></a> regarding the Dhamma possesses a significant mark of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Dhamma learned by a faithful monk as being good in the beginning, middle, and end, and as proclaiming a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>perfectly pure spiritual life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a mark of faith is found in a monk whose learning of the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>well-penetrated by view</b></a> and well-examined with the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a>, including good companions and comrades, as one of the essential marks of a person's faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>easy to admonish</b></a> and possesses qualities that make him easy to speak to is showing a mark of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that being patient and taking instruction well is a characteristic of a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>easy to admonish</b></a>, which serves as a mark of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a faithful monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>skillful and tireless</b></a> in assisting his fellow monks with their various duties and obligations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk possesses a mark of faith if he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>skillful in management</b></a> and has the ability to organize and carry out tasks for his peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>love for the Dhamma</b></a> and being pleasing in his communication about it is a mark of a monk's faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>delight in the Abhidhamma</b></a> and the Abhivinaya (higher Teaching and Discipline) as a mark of a person's faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a faithful monk as one who lives with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>ardent energy</b></a> for the purpose of abandoning unwholesome states and acquiring wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk shows a mark of faith when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>unfaltering in effort</b></a> and has not "laid down the burden" of cultivating wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that obtaining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>four jhānas</b></a>—which provide a pleasant dwelling in this very life—at will and without difficulty is a mark of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>recollect past lives</b></a>, ranging from one birth to many eons of world expansion and contraction, as a mark of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that possessing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> to see beings passing away and being reborn according to their kamma is a mark of a person's faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato"><b>destruction of taints</b></a> and the personal realization of the taintless liberation of mind as the ultimate mark of a monk's faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.14/en/sujato">AN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda was asked by the householder Dasama if there was a single thing taught by the Buddha that could lead a diligent monk to the destruction of taints and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>supreme security from bondage</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>first jhāna</b></a> is one of the states that a monk can use to attain liberation by reflecting on its conditioned nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda explained that a monk can attain the destruction of taints by realizing that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>second jhāna</b></a> is conditioned and volitionally produced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Ānanda, a monk who recognizes that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>third jhāna</b></a> is inconstant and subject to cessation can reach the end of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a> as a "door to the Deathless" when a monk understands that it is a produced state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda taught that a monk can attain liberation of mind through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> (mettā) if he understands that this state is volitionally produced and therefore inconstant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda stated that the liberation of mind through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> (karuṇā) is a path to the destruction of taints when combined with the insight into impermanence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda taught that a monk can reach the "Deathless" by contemplating the conditioned nature of the liberation of mind through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>altruistic joy</b></a> (muditā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda explained that even the liberation of mind through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> (upekkhā) is an impermanent, produced state that can be used as a basis for full awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda taught that a monk who attains the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite space</b></a> can reach the destruction of taints by reflecting that this attainment is conditioned and subject to cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite consciousness</b></a> as a state from which a diligent monk can achieve final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>base of nothingness</b></a> as the eleventh "door to the Deathless" taught by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda taught that if a monk does not attain the destruction of taints through these meditative states, his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>delight in the Dhamma</b></a> can still lead him to become a non-returner (anāgāmī)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda emphasized that any state that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>conditioned and volitionally produced</b></a> must be understood as inconstant and subject to cessation to reach liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder Dasama compared finding these eleven doors to the Deathless to a man searching for a single hidden treasure but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>finding eleven treasures</b></a> at once? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Dasama used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>house with eleven doors</b></a> to explain that a person could save themselves from a fire through any one of those exits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Dasama the householder was so grateful for the teaching that he offered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>sumptuous meal</b></a> to the entire Sangha of monks from both Vesālī and Pāṭaliputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing Ven. Ānanda's teaching, Dasama presented each individual monk in the assembly with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>pair of cloths</b></a> as an offering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Dasama expressed his devotion by building a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>monastery with five hundred rooms</b></a> for Ven. Ānanda? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda clarified that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>diligent, ardent, and resolute</b></a> can achieve the liberation of a mind that is not yet liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Ānanda, even the most refined meditative states are considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>inconstant</b></a> once a monk realizes they are volitionally produced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda explained that the "one thing" (ekadhammo) leading to liberation is actually a specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato"><b>method of contemplation</b></a> applied to various meditative attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.16/en/sujato">AN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught it is impossible for a person to enter the fixed course of rightness and realize the fruits of awakening if they regard any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.98/en/sujato"><b>conditioned formation as permanent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.98/en/sujato">AN 6.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk must first possess "conformable patience" regarding impermanence before he can ever enter the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.98/en/sujato"><b>fixed course of rightness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.98/en/sujato">AN 6.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that viewing all conditioned formations as impermanent is the necessary foundation for a monk to realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.98/en/sujato"><b>fruit of stream-entry</b></a> or even arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.98/en/sujato">AN 6.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that it is impossible to realize the fruits of a once-returner or a non-returner if one continues to perceive any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.99/en/sujato"><b>conditioned formation as pleasurable</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.99/en/sujato">AN 6.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that only by seeing all conditioned formations as suffering can a practitioner develop the conformable patience required to reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.99/en/sujato"><b>attainment of arahantship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.99/en/sujato">AN 6.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified six distinct benefits that justify a monk establishing the perception of impermanence in all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato"><b>conditioned formations</b></a> without exception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato">AN 6.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the perception of impermanence is fully established, all conditioned formations will appear to the practitioner as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato"><b>unstable</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato">AN 6.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that through the constant perception of impermanence, a practitioner's mind will no longer <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato"><b>delight in the whole world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato">AN 6.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that establishing the perception of impermanence causes the mind to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato"><b>emerge from the whole world</b></a> and detach from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato">AN 6.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the primary fruits of contemplating impermanence is that the mind becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato"><b>inclined toward Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato">AN 6.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the perception of impermanence leads to the total <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of the fetters</b></a> that bind one to saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato">AN 6.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that those who establish the perception of impermanence will be endowed with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato"><b>highest state of recluseship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.102/en/sujato">AN 6.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the necessary perception of suffering in all formations to the revulsion one would feel toward a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.103/en/sujato"><b>murderer with a raised sword</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.103/en/sujato">AN 6.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner who perceives suffering in all formations will eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.103/en/sujato"><b>see peace in Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.103/en/sujato">AN 6.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by perceiving suffering in all formations, a monk's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.103/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendencies</b></a> (anusaya) will be completely uprooted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.103/en/sujato">AN 6.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who develops the perception of suffering in all formations is considered a "doer of what should be done" and one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.103/en/sujato"><b>served the Teacher with love</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.103/en/sujato">AN 6.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that establishing the perception of non-self in all things leads to the total cessation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.104/en/sujato"><b>I-making and mine-making</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.104/en/sujato">AN 6.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who understands non-self in all things becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.104/en/sujato"><b>not made of that</b></a> (<i>atammayo</i>) in relation to the entire world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.104/en/sujato">AN 6.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the perception of non-self endows a practitioner with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.104/en/sujato"><b>uncommon knowledge</b></a> that is not shared by ordinary worldlings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.104/en/sujato">AN 6.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that through the perception of non-self, one will clearly see both the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.104/en/sujato"><b>cause and the phenomena arisen from causes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an6.104/en/sujato">AN 6.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying at the Vulture’s Peak mountain in Rājagaha shortly after Devadatta had departed, he taught that it is good for a monk to engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>periodic review</b></a> of both failure and success from time to time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised his followers that it is beneficial for a monk to periodically reflect upon his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>own failure</b></a> (attavipatti) as a regular spiritual exercise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that a practitioner should periodically reflect on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>failure of others</b></a> (paravipatti) to better understand the nature of worldly conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a monk should periodically reflect upon his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>own success</b></a> (attasampatti) to ensure continued progress and mindfulness on the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his disciples to engage in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>periodic review</b></a> of the success of others (parasampatti) as part of their mental development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that Devadatta was destined for hell, remaining there for an eon as someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>incurable</b></a>, because he allowed his mind to be overcome by eight bad things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>gain</b></a> (lābha) as the first of eight "bad things" that, when it overcomes and possesses a person's mind, leads to their spiritual downfall and a destination of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being overcome and possessed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>loss</b></a> (alābha) can lead a person to be reborn in a state of deprivation and hell, just as it did for Devadatta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>fame</b></a> (yasa) takes possession of one's heart and is not mastered, it becomes a bad thing that results in being bound for hell for an eon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that being overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>disrepute</b></a> (ayasa) was one of the eight specific factors that led to the spiritual ruin and the "incurable" state of Devadatta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>honor</b></a> (sakkāra) as a potentially dangerous state that can possess a person's mind and lead them to a state of spiritual woe if they do not overcome it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>lack of honor</b></a> (asakkāra) can be a "bad thing" (asaddhamma) if it overcomes a person's mind and leads them toward hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>evil desire</b></a> (pāpicchatā) as one of the specific qualities that possessed Devadatta's mind and made him destined for hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>evil friendship</b></a> (pāpamittatā) is a condition that leads to a person being bound for hell and becoming spiritually incurable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>continually overcoming</b></a> worldly conditions like gain, fame, and honor so that they are not possessed by these states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a monk masters and overcomes worldly conditions, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>taints, vexation, and fever</b></a> (āsavā vighātapariḷāhā) that would otherwise arise from those conditions do not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded his discourse by urging the monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato"><b>train themselves</b></a> specifically to master eight conditions, ranging from gain and fame to evil desire and evil friendship, to protect their spiritual welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.7/en/sujato">AN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.63/en/sujato"><b>weaknesses in the training</b></a>—killing, stealing, sexual misconduct, false speech, and the use of intoxicants—and taught that the four establishments of mindfulness must be developed to abandon them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.63/en/sujato">AN 9.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a monk should dwell contemplating the body, feelings, mind, and phenomena, being ardent and clearly comprehending, specifically to overcome the five <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.63/en/sujato"><b>weaknesses in the training</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.63/en/sujato">AN 9.63</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.64/en/sujato"><b>five hindrances</b></a>—sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt—as mental obstacles that should be abandoned through the practice of satipatthana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.64/en/sujato">AN 9.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the practice of removing covetousness and displeasure regarding the world through mindfulness is the primary method for abandoning the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.64/en/sujato"><b>five hindrances</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.64/en/sujato">AN 9.64</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.65/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a> consist of forms, sounds, odors, tastes, and tactile objects that are "agreeable, pleasing, charming, and enticing"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.65/en/sujato">AN 9.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the development of the four establishments of mindfulness is the necessary practice for the abandonment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.65/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.65/en/sujato">AN 9.65</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.66/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates subject to clinging</b></a> as form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.66/en/sujato">AN 9.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that to abandon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.66/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates subject to clinging</b></a>, one must practice contemplation of the body, feelings, mind, and phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.66/en/sujato">AN 9.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.67/en/sujato"><b>five lower fetters</b></a>—identity view, doubt, the distorted grasp of rules and vows, sensual desire, and ill will—are abandoned through the development of the four satipatthanas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.67/en/sujato">AN 9.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed five specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.69/en/sujato"><b>kinds of stinginess</b></a>: stinginess regarding dwellings, families, gains, praise, and the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.69/en/sujato">AN 9.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that the four establishments of mindfulness must be practiced specifically for the abandonment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.69/en/sujato"><b>five kinds of stinginess</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.69/en/sujato">AN 9.69</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.63/en/sujato"><b>false speech</b></a> as one of the five weaknesses in training that can be overcome by dwelling with mindfulness and clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.63/en/sujato">AN 9.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.64/en/sujato"><b>dullness and drowsiness</b></a> as a specific hindrance that requires the cultivation of the four establishments of mindfulness to be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.64/en/sujato">AN 9.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.64/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> among the five hindrances that practitioners must strive to abandon through mindful contemplation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.64/en/sujato">AN 9.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.65/en/sujato"><b>agreeable sounds</b></a> cognizable by the ear as one of the five cords of sensual pleasure that one should aim to abandon via satipatthana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.65/en/sujato">AN 9.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.66/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of perception</b></a> (saññā) as one of the five aggregates subject to clinging that can be abandoned through the four establishments of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.66/en/sujato">AN 9.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.66/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of volitional formations</b></a> (saṅkhāra) is a subject of clinging that practitioners seek to abandon through diligent mindfulness practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.66/en/sujato">AN 9.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.67/en/sujato"><b>identity view</b></a> (sakkāyadiṭṭhi) as the first of the five lower fetters that the four establishments of mindfulness are designed to help abandon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.67/en/sujato">AN 9.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.67/en/sujato"><b>distorted grasp of rules and vows</b></a> (sīlabbataparāmāsa) is a lower fetter that can be successfully abandoned by dwelling mindful and ardent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.67/en/sujato">AN 9.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.69/en/sujato"><b>stinginess regarding the Dhamma</b></a> (dhammamacchariya) as a quality that is abandoned when a monk develops the four establishments of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.69/en/sujato">AN 9.69</a>,</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the wanderer Sutavā that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato"><b>arahant</b></a>—defined as a monk who has destroyed the taints, lived the holy life, and reached the true goal—is inherently incapable of committing nine specific types of transgressions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato">AN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who has fully liberated his mind through final knowledge is fundamentally incapable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato"><b>intentionally taking the life</b></a> of any living being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato">AN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an arahant who has laid down the burden of suffering is incapable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato"><b>taking what is not given</b></a> with the intent to steal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato">AN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk whose taints are destroyed and who has done what was to be done is incapable of engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato"><b>sexual intercourse</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato">AN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a fully liberated person, having reached his own goal, is incapable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato"><b>speaking a conscious lie</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato">AN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who has utterly destroyed the fetters of existence is incapable of consuming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato"><b>stored-up sensual pleasures</b></a> in the way he did previously as a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato">AN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated an arahant is incapable of following a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato"><b>wrong course through desire</b></a>, as his mind is completely liberated through final knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato">AN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who has finished the holy life is incapable of following a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato"><b>wrong course through hatred</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato">AN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha affirmed that a person who has attained final liberation is incapable of following a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato"><b>wrong course through delusion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato">AN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an arahant, having reached the end of all taints, is incapable of following a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato"><b>wrong course through fear</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.7/en/sujato">AN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person possessing virtue, it is not necessary to make an act of will to be free from remorse, because it is a natural law that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato"><b>freedom from remorse</b></a> arises in such a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato">AN 11.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, once freedom from remorse has arisen, a person does not need to intentionally wish for joy to occur, as it is simply the nature of things that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato"><b>joy</b></a> arises for one who is free from remorse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato">AN 11.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that rapture follows naturally from a joyful state of mind, and there is no need for a practitioner to make a conscious act of will to experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato"><b>rapture</b></a> once they are joyful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato">AN 11.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that when a person's mind is filled with rapture, their physical body naturally becomes tranquil, meaning they do not need to consciously strive for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato"><b>tranquil body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato">AN 11.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once the body is tranquil, a person naturally experiences happiness, and therefore they do not need to make a specific act of will to feel <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a> at that stage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato">AN 11.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that for one who is happy, the mind naturally becomes concentrated, and thus no act of will is required for the mind to reach <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato">AN 11.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that when the mind is concentrated, a person naturally knows and sees things as they really are, without needing to make a specific act of will for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision</b></a> to occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato">AN 11.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that for one who knows and sees things as they really are, it is a natural progression to become disenchanted, and such a person does not need to will themselves into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato"><b>disenchantment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato">AN 11.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, dispassion naturally follows disenchantment, and there is no need for an act of will to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato"><b>dispassionate</b></a> once disenchantment is present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato">AN 11.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that for one who is dispassionate, the realization of liberation arises naturally, so that no act of will is needed to attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato">AN 11.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the spiritual path as a process where "qualities flow into qualities," illustrating how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato"><b>wholesome virtue</b></a> naturally leads a person from the near shore to the further shore of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.2/en/sujato">AN 11.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who insults, reviles, or abuses his fellow practitioners is liable to encounter any of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato"><b>eleven misfortunes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato">AN 11.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a practitioner who abuses noble ones may find they are unable to reach spiritual goals that they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato"><b>not yet attained</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato">AN 11.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the dangers of insulting fellow monks is that a person might fall away from the spiritual states they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato"><b>already achieved</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato">AN 11.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that a person who reviles noble ones may develop an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato"><b>overestimation of self</b></a> regarding the true Dhamma, leading to spiritual pride? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato">AN 11.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified dissatisfaction as a consequence for those who abuse their fellow practitioners, noting they may carry out the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato"><b>spiritual life</b></a> without joy or inspiration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato">AN 11.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha cautioned that those who insult noble ones are at risk of committing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato"><b>defiled offense</b></a> or even abandoning the training to return to a lower life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato">AN 11.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned physical and mental health risks for those who revile noble ones, including suffering from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato"><b>severe illness</b></a> or falling into madness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato">AN 11.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a person who is an abuser of noble ones risks facing the end of their life in a state of confusion, being unable to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato"><b>die unconfused</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato">AN 11.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the most severe consequence for reviling noble ones is that, after the breakup of the body, the person may be reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato"><b>plane of misery</b></a> or even in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.6/en/sujato">AN 11.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara taught that it is good for a monk to periodically review his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>personal failure</b></a> (attavipatti) as part of his spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Uttara, a monk should from time to time reflect on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>failure of others</b></a> (paravipatti) to maintain clarity in his training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara advised that a monk should take the time to review his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>personal success</b></a> (attasampatti) from time to time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara suggested that monks should reflect on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>success of others</b></a> (parasampatti) as a beneficial spiritual exercise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Great King Vessavaṇa, after hearing Venerable Uttara’s teaching on success and failure, immediately traveled to the Tāvatiṃsa heaven to report it to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>Sakka, Lord of the Devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, Lord of the Devas, appeared before Venerable Uttara at Mount Saṅkheyyaka to ask if his teaching was his own inspiration or the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>word of the Buddha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara used a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>simile of a grain heap</b></a> to explain that just as people carry grain from a massive pile near a village, all good teachings are drawn from the "heap" of the Buddha’s words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara famously stated that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>whatever is well-spoken</b></a>" is actually the word of the Blessed One, the Arahant, the Perfectly Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka recounted a teaching from the Buddha which explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>Devadatta</b></a> was destined for a state of misery and hell for an entire aeon because his mind was overcome by eight untrue teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>gain</b></a> (lābha) as one of the eight factors that, when it overcomes the mind, can lead a person to be incurable and bound for hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being mastered by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>loss</b></a> (alābha) was one of the key conditions that led to Devadatta's spiritual ruin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the pursuit and obsession with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>fame</b></a> (yasa) can overwhelm a person's heart and lead to their downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, being overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>disrepute</b></a> (ayasa) is one of the eight "untrue teachings" (asaddhamma) that can trap a practitioner in suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that Devadatta's mind was completely possessed by the desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>honor</b></a> (sakkāra), contributing to his eventual rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>dishonor</b></a> (asakkāra), if not overcome, can become a factor that leads to a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>evil desire</b></a> (pāpicchatā) as a primary internal quality that makes a person's spiritual condition incurable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>bad friendship</b></a> (pāpamittatā) as a dangerous external influence that can lead a practitioner to become "destined for hell" (nerayiko)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to train themselves to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>overcome gain</b></a> and loss so that these worldly conditions do not possess their minds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who overcomes fame and disrepute prevents the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a> (āsavas) of vexation and distress from arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by overcoming honor and dishonor, a monk avoids the internal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>vexation</b></a> (vighāta) that would otherwise afflict him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who dwells having overcome evil desire and bad friendship is protected from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>distress</b></a> (pariḷāha) that leads to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka described the teaching on overcoming the eight worldly factors as a discourse that pertains to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato"><b>fundamentals of the holy life</b></a> (ādibrahmacariyako)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an8.8/en/sujato">AN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.71/en/sujato"><b>doubt about the Teacher</b></a> as the first "barrenness of mind," a state where the mind fails to incline toward ardor, devotion, perseverance, or exertion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.71/en/sujato">AN 9.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a practitioner is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.71/en/sujato"><b>perplexed about the Dhamma</b></a>, their heart does not bend toward the dedication and effort required for spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.71/en/sujato">AN 9.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.71/en/sujato"><b>doubting the Sangha</b></a>—the community of practitioners—constitutes a mental obstruction that prevents one's mind from engaging in the necessary spiritual struggle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.71/en/sujato">AN 9.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that having no confidence and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.71/en/sujato"><b>doubting the training</b></a> is a specific form of mental barrenness that stops a person from applying themselves to the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.71/en/sujato">AN 9.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.71/en/sujato"><b>angry with fellow practitioners</b></a> and resentful toward them is the fifth "barrenness of mind," which keeps the heart from inclining toward perseverance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.71/en/sujato">AN 9.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.71/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a> as the specific practice to abandon the five types of barrenness of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.71/en/sujato">AN 9.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato"><b>lust for sensual pleasures</b></a> as the first "shackle of the mind," a condition that prevents a practitioner from dedicating themselves to spiritual exertion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato">AN 9.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner who is not free from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato"><b>lust for the body</b></a> possesses a shackled mind that is unable to incline toward ardor and devotion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato">AN 9.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that remaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato"><b>attached to forms</b></a> or visual objects acts as a mental shackle, hindering the heart from engaging in the path of perseverance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato">AN 9.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that eating as much as one wants and being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato"><b>devoted to sleep</b></a> and lounging is a mental shackle that stops the mind from inclining toward exertion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato">AN 9.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that practicing the spiritual life with the goal of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato"><b>becoming a deity</b></a> in a heavenly realm is actually a "shackle of the mind" that prevents true spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato">AN 9.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that to abandon the five shackles of the mind, a practitioner must dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato"><b>contemplating the body</b></a> as a body, remaining ardent and mindful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato">AN 9.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato"><b>contemplating feelings</b></a> as feelings is one of the four essential methods for removing the shackles that bind the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato">AN 9.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato"><b>contemplating the mind</b></a> as a mind is a necessary practice for any practitioner seeking to overcome mental obstructions and shackles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato">AN 9.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato"><b>contemplating phenomena</b></a> as phenomena allows a person to put away greed and distress for the world and abandon mental shackles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato">AN 9.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a mind as "shackled" when it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato"><b>not free from craving</b></a>, which prevents it from bending toward the hard work of spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato">AN 9.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the purpose of the four establishments of mindfulness is to remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato"><b>ardent and clearly comprehending</b></a> while removing the obstacles that prevent spiritual effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.72/en/sujato">AN 9.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the wanderer Sajjha that an arahant—a monk who has destroyed the taints and lived the holy life—is incapable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato"><b>intentionally killing</b></a> any living being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato">AN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who has done what had to be done and laid down the burden is incapable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato"><b>taking what is not given</b></a> with the intention of stealing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato">AN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated an arahant, who is liberated through right final knowledge and has utterly destroyed the fetters of existence, is completely incapable of engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato"><b>sexual intercourse</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato">AN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified nine specific transgressions that an arahant is incapable of committing, one of which is telling a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato"><b>conscious lie</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato">AN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an arahant is incapable of enjoying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> by storing them up in the manner they might have done previously while living as a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato">AN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed to the wanderer Sajjha that a monk who has attained the final goal of arahantship is incapable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato"><b>renouncing the Buddha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato">AN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted an arahant, having reached the end of the taints, is incapable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato"><b>renouncing the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato">AN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that an arahant, who has attained their own goal and is liberated through right final knowledge, is incapable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato"><b>renouncing the Sangha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato">AN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato"><b>renouncing the training</b></a> as one of the nine actions that an arahant is fundamentally incapable of performing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.8/en/sujato">AN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.10/en/sujato"><b>nine persons</b></a> in the world who are worthy of gifts, hospitality, offerings, and reverential salutation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.10/en/sujato">AN 9.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a group of nine individuals, including those practicing for the fruit of stream-entry and those who have reached arahantship, as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.10/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.10/en/sujato">AN 9.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that among the nine noble persons worthy of offerings, the Buddha included the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.10/en/sujato"><b>gotrabhū</b></a>, a term referring to one who has entered the lineage of the noble ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.10/en/sujato">AN 9.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are individuals at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.10/en/sujato"><b>various stages of awakening</b></a>, from the stream-enterer to the arahant, who all constitute a field of merit for those seeking to make offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an9.10/en/sujato">AN 9.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a meditator who is obsessed with the five hindrances to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>wild colt</b></a> tied to a feeding trough, who thinks only of fodder and never considers the tasks a trainer might require? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>noble thoroughbred</b></a> among men as one who meditates without being dependent on the elements of earth, water, fire, or air? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that Devas and Brahmas pay homage from afar to a supreme person who meditates in such a way that they cannot perceive what that person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>meditating dependent upon</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a "wild colt" meditator remains preoccupied with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a> and does not understand the true escape from it even while they are in seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a thoroughbred person, unlike a wild colt, recognizes the "strike of the goad" as a debt or a bond and meditates with a mind focused on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>task at hand</b></a> rather than immediate gratification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the state of a thoroughbred meditator as one where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>perception of earth</b></a> has been removed or abolished, even as they continue to meditate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a common meditator might meditate while being obsessed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a>, failing to see the reality of how to escape that mental state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an advanced state of meditation where the practitioner is not dependent on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite space</b></a> or any of the other formless spheres? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the "meditation of a wild colt" involves being overwhelmed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> without knowing the actual way to overcome those hindrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a noble thoroughbred person does not meditate dependent on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>this world</b></a> or the world beyond, transcending typical worldly attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that for a supreme meditator, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>perception of water</b></a>, fire, and air is effectively abolished during their unique meditative experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>"fodder, fodder"</b></a> to illustrate the narrow, self-centered focus of an inferior horse, which he likened to an untrained person's approach to meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a thoroughbred person meditates without being dependent on what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>seen, heard, sensed, or cognized</b></a> by the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a meditator like a wild colt is someone whose mind is constantly agitated by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Venerable Sandha that Devas salute the thoroughbred meditator with the words, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>Homage to you</b></a>, O supreme person," because they cannot identify the basis of that person's meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a person meditating like a wild colt is someone who remains stuck in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> and fails to realize the true escape from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that for a noble thoroughbred person, the perception of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite consciousness</b></a> has been removed, making their meditation incomprehensible to ordinary beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a supreme meditator as one who does not even rely on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>base of nothingness</b></a> during their meditative practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a noble person can reach a state where the perception of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception</b></a> is entirely abolished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Venerable Sandha that a thoroughbred person is someone who truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>knows the escape</b></a> from any hindrance that arises in the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the meditation of an inferior person as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>obsessed by hindrances</b></a>, much like a horse obsessed with its feeding trough? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a thoroughbred meditator is one in whom the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato"><b>perception of all worldly things</b></a>—including things searched for and examined by the mind—has been removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an11.9/en/sujato">AN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.1/en/sujato"><b>unloved and disagreeable</b></a> to his fellow practitioners if he is possessed by seven specific qualities, including being motivated by a desire for gain and having wrong view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.1/en/sujato">AN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who is driven by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.1/en/sujato"><b>desire for honor</b></a> and renown is not respected or esteemed by those with whom he lives the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.1/en/sujato">AN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.1/en/sujato"><b>moral shame</b></a> (ahiriko) and a lack of moral dread (anottappī) as two key factors that make a practitioner unloved and not honored by their peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.1/en/sujato">AN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk becomes dear and agreeable to his fellow practitioners when he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.1/en/sujato"><b>free from the desire</b></a> for gain, honor, and renown? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.1/en/sujato">AN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.1/en/sujato"><b>fewness of wishes</b></a> (appiccho) as one of the seven noble qualities that lead a monk to be respected and esteemed by those living the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.1/en/sujato">AN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.1/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> (sammādiṭṭhi) is an essential quality for a monk to be loved, respected, and considered worthy of cultivation by his fellows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.1/en/sujato">AN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified seven distinct spiritual powers, including the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of faith</b></a> (saddhābalaṃ) and the power of energy (vīriyabalaṃ), which allow a monk to live happily? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a wise person who is strengthened by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of mindfulness</b></a> (satibalaṃ) and the power of concentration (samādhibalaṃ) is able to live a life of happiness and spiritual success? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of wisdom</b></a> (paññābalaṃ) is the seventh spiritual power, enabling a practitioner to see the true meaning of things and attain the liberation of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>investigate the Dhamma</b></a> carefully (yoniso vicine dhammaṃ) so that they might see its meaning through wisdom and achieve spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha grouped the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>power of moral shame</b></a> (hirībalaṃ) and the power of moral dread (ottappabalaṃ) as essential strengths for any monk wishing to live a happy and wise life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk's mind attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a> (vimokkho hoti cetaso), it is like the extinguishing of a lamp, having seen the true meaning of the Dhamma through wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/an7.3/en/sujato">AN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once delivered a Dhamma talk specifically concerning Nibbana that was so engrossing that the attending monks listened with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato"><b>eager ears</b></a>, attending to the teaching with their whole minds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato">SN 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a yakkhi (ogress) named Punabbasu’s mother urged her children to be silent because the Buddha was teaching that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato"><b>Nibbana</b></a> is the release from all bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato">SN 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Punabbasu's mother told her children that while a son or a husband is dear in this world, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato"><b>searching for the Dhamma</b></a> was even dearer to her because it leads to the way out of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato">SN 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to a yakkhi mother in the Punabbasusutta, neither a son nor a husband can release a person from suffering in the way that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato"><b>hearing the True Dhamma</b></a> can? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato">SN 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described as the "one with vision" and the "bearer of his final body" who acts as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato"><b>bringer of light</b></a> to gods and humans who are otherwise deluded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato">SN 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Punabbasu’s mother rejoiced in having a son who was "wise and dear to her heart" because he also cherished the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato"><b>pure Dhamma</b></a> taught by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato">SN 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a yakkhi reached a state of great happiness and fulfillment because she <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato"><b>saw the Noble Truths</b></a> for herself after listening to the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato">SN 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the daughter of a yakkhi named Uttara agreed to stay silent so her mother could hear the Buddha, noting that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato"><b>hearing the True Dhamma is pleasant</b></a> and that wandering in suffering is the result of not knowing it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.7/en/sujato">SN 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato"><b>concentration by mindfulness of breathing</b></a> is the one thing that, when developed and cultivated, fulfills the four foundations of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato">SN 54.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how the four foundations of mindfulness, when they are developed and cultivated, fulfill the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato">SN 54.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the seven factors of enlightenment, when fully developed through practice, lead to the fulfillment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge and liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato">SN 54.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identifies <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of breathing</b></a> as the starting point for a chain of spiritual development that eventually completes the entire path to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato">SN 54.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a group of monks specifically asked the Buddha if there was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato"><b>one thing</b></a> that could fulfill four states, which then fulfill seven states, and finally fulfill two states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato">SN 54.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that developing mindfulness of breathing is the key method to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato"><b>fulfill the four foundations of mindfulness</b></a> (satipaṭṭhāna)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato">SN 54.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the sequential fulfillment of the foundations of mindfulness and the factors of enlightenment is what brings <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge and liberation</b></a> to completion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato">SN 54.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato"><b>concentration by mindfulness of breathing</b></a> as a comprehensive path that leads a practitioner from basic meditation all the way to final enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.15/en/sujato">SN 54.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato"><b>Vegabbhari devaputta</b></a> rejected the praise of rival teachers, asserting that a "vile jackal" is never the equal of a "lion" and that teachers of suspect conduct do not resemble the virtuous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato">SN 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato"><b>Vegabbhari devaputta</b></a> characterized certain other sect leaders as "naked liars" whose behavior was suspicious and unworthy of being called "true men"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato">SN 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato"><b>Māra the Evil One</b></a> possessed a devaputta to argue that those who practice penance and seclusion while delighting in the "deva world" are actually providing the right instructions for the next life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato">SN 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> countered Māra's views by explaining that all "forms" (rūpa), whether on earth or in the sky, are actually "bait" cast by the Evil One for the destruction of beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato">SN 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> warned that everything praised by "Namuci" (another name for Māra) is equivalent to bait set for fish, intended to trap and destroy those who are attached to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato">SN 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato"><b>Māṇavagāmiyo devaputta</b></a> used several analogies to describe the Buddha's supremacy, comparing him to the "sun" among things that travel the sky and the "ocean" among bodies of water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato">SN 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato"><b>Māṇavagāmiyo devaputta</b></a> declared that in the world with its devas, the Buddha is rightfully called the "foremost" (aggo) of all beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.30/en/sujato">SN 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the yakkha Āḷavaka asked what a person's best wealth is, the Buddha answered that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> is the best wealth a person can possess in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato">SN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a>, when it is well practiced, is the specific thing that brings a person true happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato">SN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in response to a challenge from a spirit, the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato"><b>truth</b></a> as being the sweetest among all possible tastes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato">SN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a life lived by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> is called the best of all ways to live? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato">SN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person crosses over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato"><b>flood</b></a> of sensual desire, existence, views, and ignorance by means of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato">SN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one crosses the great <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato"><b>ocean</b></a> of samsara through the practice of heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato">SN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person overcomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> through the application of energy and effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato">SN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato"><b>purified</b></a> through the development of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato">SN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a heedful and discerning person gains wisdom by being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato"><b>eager to learn</b></a> and having faith in the Arahants and the Dhamma for the sake of attaining Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato">SN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is dutiful and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato"><b>strives</b></a> with initiative is the one who finds and acquires wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato">SN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that one attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato"><b>fame</b></a> and a good reputation through the consistent practice of truthfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato">SN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato"><b>giving</b></a> to others is the primary way that a person binds and secures friends? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato">SN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a faithful householder who possesses the four qualities of truth, Dhamma, firmness, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato"><b>generosity</b></a> does not sorrow when passing from this world to the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.12/en/sujato">SN 10.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato"><b>four nutriments</b></a> necessary for the maintenance of beings that have already come to be and for the assistance of those seeking a new birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato">SN 12.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato"><b>edible food</b></a>, whether coarse or fine, as the first of four essential nutriments that sustain the lives of all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato">SN 12.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato"><b>mental volition</b></a> as a type of nutriment, alongside sensory contact and consciousness, that provides the support needed for beings to exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato">SN 12.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the four nutriments of food, contact, mental volition, and consciousness are all rooted in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a>, which serves as their source and origin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato">SN 12.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that craving itself does not arise without a cause, but is specifically produced from the condition of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato">SN 12.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> is the necessary condition that leads to the arising of feeling, forming a chain of causes in the process of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato">SN 12.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato"><b>six sense bases</b></a>—the internal foundations for sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, and thought—are themselves conditioned and produced by name-and-form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato">SN 12.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha traced the origin of name-and-form back to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a>, showing how each link in the chain of life is dependent on a prior factor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato">SN 12.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> as being born from ignorance, acting as the prerequisite for the arising of consciousness and the subsequent mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato">SN 12.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the entire mass of human suffering can only be brought to an end through the remainderless fading away and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato"><b>cessation of ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.11/en/sujato">SN 12.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once addressed a group of thirty monks from Pava who were all forest-dwellers and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>wearers of rag-robes</b></a>, teaching them specifically so their minds might be liberated from the taints through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>saṃsāra</b></a> is without a discoverable beginning, and a first point cannot be discerned for beings who wander and roam through the round of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the reason we cannot find the beginning of our existence is that beings are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>obstructed by ignorance</b></a> and fettered by craving as they travel from life to life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the stream of blood you have shed while being decapitated throughout this long course of rebirth is actually greater than the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>water in the four great oceans</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the blood you have shed from being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>slaughtered as cattle</b></a> over your many past lifetimes is far more than all the water found in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the blood you shed while being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>slaughtered as buffaloes</b></a> during your countless previous births is greater in volume than the water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that throughout the long duration of your wandering in rebirth, the blood you shed from being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>slaughtered as rams</b></a> exceeds the amount of water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized the length of our journey through saṃsāra by noting that the blood shed from your neck while being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>slaughtered as goats</b></a> is more than the water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha said that the blood you have shed while being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>killed as deer</b></a> across your infinite past lives is greater than the water contained in the world's four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the blood you lost from being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>slaughtered as chickens</b></a> during your long travel through the round of rebirth is more than the water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the blood you have shed from being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>slaughtered as pigs</b></a> over the vast eons of your existence is greater than the water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the blood you shed when you were caught and executed as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>thieves and highwaymen</b></a> in previous lives is far more than the water found in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the blood you shed from being caught and decapitated as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>adulterers</b></a> throughout your many rebirths is actually greater than the water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon hearing the Buddha's discourse on the immense suffering and length of saṃsāra, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato"><b>thirty monks from Pava</b></a> all attained liberation from the taints through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.13/en/sujato">SN 15.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a yakkha in Rājagaha once praised the Bhikkhunī Sukkā for teaching the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.9/en/sujato"><b>deathless state</b></a> to a large assembly of people at the Bamboo Grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.9/en/sujato">SN 10.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a yakkha, inspired by the teaching of the Bhikkhunī Sukkā, criticized the people of Rājagaha for acting as if they had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.9/en/sujato"><b>drunk honey</b></a> and fallen asleep instead of listening to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.9/en/sujato">SN 10.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a yakkha described the Dhamma taught by the Bhikkhunī Sukkā as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.9/en/sujato"><b>irresistible and delicious</b></a>, suggesting that wise people should drink it in just as a traveler drinks water from a rain cloud? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.9/en/sujato">SN 10.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a yakkha proclaimed that a wise lay follower had produced much merit by providing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.10/en/sujato"><b>gift of food</b></a> to the Bhikkhunī Sukkā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.10/en/sujato">SN 10.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a yakkha traveling through the streets of Rājagaha described the Bhikkhunī Sukkā as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.10/en/sujato"><b>released from all bonds</b></a> while she was staying at the Bamboo Grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.10/en/sujato">SN 10.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if one were to correctly speak of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato"><b>mass of the unwholesome</b></a>, they would be referring specifically to the five hindrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato">SN 47.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the five hindrances—sensual desire, ill will, sloth and torpor, restlessness and remorse, and doubt—as the entirety of what constitutes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome mass</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato">SN 47.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato"><b>mass of the wholesome</b></a> is a complete and accurate description of the four foundations of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato">SN 47.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a monk should dwell contemplating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato"><b>body in the body</b></a> while remaining ardent, clearly comprehending, and mindful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato">SN 47.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the practice of the four foundations of mindfulness is the method by which one removes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato"><b>covetousness and displeasure</b></a> in regard to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato">SN 47.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato"><b>four foundations of mindfulness</b></a> as dwelling in contemplation of the body, feelings, mind, and phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato">SN 47.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that practicing mindfulness with ardor and clear comprehension allows a practitioner to live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato"><b>removing worldly displeasure</b></a> and attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.5/en/sujato">SN 47.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato"><b>four types of self-confidence</b></a> (vesārajja) which allow him to roar his lion’s roar in various assemblies and set the supreme wheel of Dhamma in motion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato">SN 12.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, as a Tathāgata endowed with ten powers, teaches the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato"><b>origin and passing away</b></a> of form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato">SN 12.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha summarized the principle of causality with the formula of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato"><b>specific conditionality</b></a>: "When this exists, that comes to be; with the arising of this, that arises"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato">SN 12.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the entire <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato"><b>mass of suffering</b></a> is generated through a sequence of conditions beginning with ignorance and ending with aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato">SN 12.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato"><b>cessation of the mass of suffering</b></a> occurs through the remainderless fading away and cessation of ignorance, which subsequently leads to the cessation of volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato">SN 12.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato"><b>brahmacakka</b></a> to refer to the supreme wheel of Dhamma that he set in motion after attaining enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato">SN 12.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.27/en/sujato"><b>three types of feeling</b></a>—pleasant, painful, and neither-painful-nor-pleasant—as the primary categories of human experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.27/en/sujato">SN 36.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that only those ascetics and brahmins who understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.27/en/sujato"><b>gratification and danger</b></a> of the three types of feelings as they really are are considered true practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.27/en/sujato">SN 36.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person can only attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.27/en/sujato"><b>goal of asceticism</b></a> in this very life by personally realizing and entering upon it through direct knowledge of feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.27/en/sujato">SN 36.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a true brahmin as one who has understood the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.27/en/sujato"><b>escape from feelings</b></a> as it really is, rather than just possessing theoretical knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.27/en/sujato">SN 36.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Kappa once asked the Buddha how a practitioner can possess a mind that has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato"><b>transcended conceit</b></a> and is free from the tendencies of "I-making" and "mine-making"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato">SN 22.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the way to remove conceit is to see <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato"><b>any kind of form</b></a>—whether past, future, or present, internal or external—as "This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato">SN 22.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a practitioner becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato"><b>liberated through non-clinging</b></a> by viewing every aggregate, including perception and volitional formations, with right wisdom as not-self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato">SN 22.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a mind that has successfully abandoned the labels of "I" and "mine" regarding the conscious body as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato"><b>peaceful and well-liberated</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato">SN 22.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha applied the formula of "not mine, not I, not my self" to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato"><b>all consciousness whatsoever</b></a>, whether it is gross or subtle, inferior or superior, or far or near? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato">SN 22.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Ven. Kappa that right wisdom involves seeing all five aggregates as they really are, which leads to the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato"><b>anupādāvimutto</b></a>, or being liberated through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato">SN 22.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that a Tathāgata’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato"><b>lion's roar</b></a> in assemblies is an expression of his supreme confidence and realization of the truth of the aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.21/en/sujato">SN 12.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that understanding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.27/en/sujato"><b>origin and passing away of feelings</b></a> is a prerequisite for being recognized as a "true ascetic among ascetics"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.27/en/sujato">SN 36.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mind remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato"><b>free from "I-making"</b></a> (ahaṅkāra) when one no longer identifies with the "external signs" or the "conscious body"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato">SN 22.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the path to becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato"><b>well-liberated</b></a> as the process of seeing the truth of non-self in all internal and external phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.125/en/sujato">SN 22.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once told the devaputta Kakudha that he does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.18/en/sujato"><b>rejoice</b></a> because he has gained nothing, and does not sorrow because he has lost nothing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.18/en/sujato">SN 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the devaputta Kakudha visited the Buddha in the Anjana Woods, the Buddha explained that he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.18/en/sujato"><b>neither rejoicing nor sorrowing</b></a> despite his life as an ascetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.18/en/sujato">SN 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to a devaputta that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.18/en/sujato"><b>delight</b></a> (nandī) belongs only to one who has suffering, and that a monk without delight is truly free from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.18/en/sujato">SN 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.18/en/sujato"><b>boredom</b></a> (arati) does not overwhelm him even when he is sitting alone, because he has abandoned the causes of delight and sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.18/en/sujato">SN 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified himself as a "brahmin <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.18/en/sujato"><b>attained to Nibbana</b></a>" (parinibbuta) because he has crossed over the sticky attachment (visattika) of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.18/en/sujato">SN 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that only a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.18/en/sujato"><b>free from delight</b></a> can be truly described as "anagho," or one who is free from the mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.18/en/sujato">SN 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the devaputta Uttara told the Buddha that because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.19/en/sujato"><b>life is swept away</b></a> and youth is brief, a person should perform meritorious deeds that bring happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.19/en/sujato">SN 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha responded to the devaputta Uttara by saying that because life is short and aging has no protection, one seeking peace should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.19/en/sujato"><b>discard the world’s bait</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.19/en/sujato">SN 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the devaputta Uttara emphasized that one should fear death and thus do <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.19/en/sujato"><b>meritorious deeds</b></a>, while the Buddha emphasized that the same fear should lead to the abandonment of worldly cravings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.19/en/sujato">SN 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.19/en/sujato"><b>old age</b></a> provides no shelter for those it has overtaken, making the pursuit of spiritual peace an urgent matter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.19/en/sujato">SN 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato"><b>great ocean</b></a> swelling and filling the rivers to explain how ignorance fuels the entire process of dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato">SN 12.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> (avijjā) increases, it inevitably causes volitional formations to swell and increase as well? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato">SN 12.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the swelling of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> leads directly to the increase and filling up of consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato">SN 12.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the increase of consciousness (viññāṇa) causes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> to swell, which in turn fuels the six sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato">SN 12.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the receding of the great ocean to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato"><b>receding of ignorance</b></a>, which leads to the eventual cessation of the entire mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato">SN 12.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> (taṇhā) is allowed to increase, it inevitably leads to the swelling of clinging, existence, and birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato">SN 12.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the causal chain of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a> by showing how these are the final consequences when the condition of birth increases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato">SN 12.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as a receding ocean makes the mountain streams dry up, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato"><b>cessation of ignorance</b></a> leads to the receding of all volitional formations and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato">SN 12.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato"><b>mountain streams</b></a> (kunnadiyo) and pools to describe the cascading and dependent nature of the factors that lead to rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato">SN 12.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> (vedanā) is the specific condition that, when it increases or "swells," leads directly to the increase of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.69/en/sujato">SN 12.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> taught King Pasenadi of Kosala that it is difficult for a layman who enjoys sensual pleasures and luxuries to know who is an Arahant or who has entered the path to Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> explained that a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> (sīla) can only be truly known by living together with them for a long time, rather than through a brief encounter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> stated that a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>purity</b></a> (soceyya) is to be discovered through dealings and transactions with them over a significant period? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> pointed out that a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>fortitude</b></a> (thāmo) is only truly revealed when they are faced with adversity and misfortune? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> taught that a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> (paññā) can only be known through engaging them in discussion and conversation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> emphasized that judging the spiritual qualities of others requires one to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>attentive</b></a> and wise, rather than inattentive or lacking in discernment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>King Pasenadi</b></a> once encountered a group of thirty-five various ascetics—including Niganthas and naked ascetics—and mistakenly assumed they might be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>Arahants</b></a> based on their appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>King Pasenadi</b></a> revealed to the Buddha that the groups of ascetics he had just honored were actually his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>secret agents</b></a> returning from spying on the countryside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>clay earring</b></a> to describe how some people appear refined and beautiful on the outside while remaining impure within? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> warned that one should not place trust in another person based solely on their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>outward appearance</b></a> or looks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> compared deceptive people to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>copper half-māsa</b></a> coin plated in gold, appearing valuable on the surface but made of base metal underneath? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>King Pasenadi</b></a> described his householder life as "living in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>house crowded with children</b></a>" while using luxury items like Kāsic sandalwood and gold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> observed that some <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained</b></a> people travel through the world wearing the external signs and behaviors of those who are well-restrained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>King Pasenadi</b></a> knelt on the ground and announced his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>name and title</b></a> three times to show respect to a group of passing ascetics who were actually his own spies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> taught that to truly discern someone's character, one must observe them over a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>long time</b></a>, as a short interval is insufficient for a correct judgment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> was staying at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>Eastern Park</b></a> in the Palace of Migāra’s Mother when he delivered the teaching on the four ways to judge a person's character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>King Pasenadi</b></a> explained that after his undercover agents return from their missions, they wash off the "dust and dirt," put on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>white clothes</b></a>, and indulge in sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> noted that some people roam the world with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>impressive entourage</b></a> and beautiful exterior while being internally impure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> explicitly used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>dealings</b></a> (saṃvohārena) to highlight how social and business interactions reveal a person's level of purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>King Pasenadi</b></a> expressed great amazement at how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato"><b>excellently spoken</b></a> the Buddha’s advice was regarding the difficulty of identifying true Arahants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.11/en/sujato">SN 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>King Pasenadi</b></a> of Kosala once led a group of five kings in a discussion to determine which of the five cords of sensual pleasure (kāmaguṇa) is the most supreme? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during a debate between five kings, some argued that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>forms</b></a> (rūpa) constitute the foremost and most excellent of all sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while five kings were debating the hierarchy of the senses, certain individuals claimed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>sounds</b></a> (sadda) are the highest of the five cords of sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a gathering of five kings, some maintained the position that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>odors</b></a> (gandha) are the best and most supreme among the various objects of sensual enjoyment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during a discussion on the five cords of sensual pleasure, some of the five kings proposed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>tastes</b></a> (rasa) represent the peak of sensual experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that some of the five kings argued that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>tactile objects</b></a> (phoṭṭhabba) are the foremost of the five cords of sensual pleasure, surpassing sights, sounds, smells, and tastes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> taught that the "best" of the five cords of sensual pleasure is defined by whatever is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>most agreeable</b></a> (manāpapariyanta) to an individual person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> pointed out the subjectivity of the senses by noting that the same <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>forms</b></a> can be agreeable to one person while being disagreeable to another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> explained that when a person is fully satisfied and has fulfilled their purpose with specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>sounds</b></a>, they do not desire any other sound as being higher or more excellent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> stated that if a person is content and their wish is fulfilled by certain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>odors</b></a>, those specific smells become for them the most supreme and unsurpassed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> taught that a person who is entirely satisfied with certain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>tastes</b></a> will regard those tastes as the highest possible and will not crave anything more refined? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> observed that when a person's purpose is fulfilled by specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>tactile objects</b></a>, those sensations are considered by that person to be the ultimate and unsurpassed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a lay follower named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>Candanaṅgalika</b></a> was present in the assembly of the five kings and requested the Buddha's permission to express his inspired thoughts through a verse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the lay follower <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>Candanaṅgalika</b></a> praised the Buddha by comparing his fragrance and beauty to a hundred-petaled red lotus blooming in the morning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the lay follower <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>Candanaṅgalika</b></a> likened the radiant and glowing presence of the Buddha (Aṅgīrasa) to the sun shining in the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>the five kings</b></a> were so impressed by the lay follower Candanaṅgalika's verse that they each rewarded him by covering him with an upper robe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after receiving five upper robes from the kings, the lay follower <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>Candanaṅgalika</b></a> immediately gifted all five of those robes to the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>King Pasenadi</b></a> of Kosala was the one who initially proposed that the group of five kings should visit the Buddha to resolve their disagreement about sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>the Buddha</b></a> used the phrase "fulfillment of one's purpose" (paripuṇṇasaṅkappo) to explain why a person might perceive a specific sensory object as the highest possible pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato"><b>Aṅgīrasa</b></a> was used by the lay follower Candanaṅgalika to address and praise the Buddha during his inspired poetic delivery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.12/en/sujato">SN 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that King Ajātasattu of Magadha assembled a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato"><b>four-constituent army</b></a> (elephants, cavalry, chariots, and infantry) to march against King Pasenadi of Kosala for the possession of the territory of Kāsī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato">SN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized King Ajātasattu as a man with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato"><b>bad friends</b></a>, bad companions, and bad comrades after the king waged war against King Pasenadi? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato">SN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described King Pasenadi of Kosala as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato"><b>virtuous friend</b></a> (kalyāṇamitto) and a virtuous companion, despite the king suffering a military defeat at the hands of King Ajātasattu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato">SN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that because of his defeat in battle by the Magadhan King, King Pasenadi would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato"><b>sleep in suffering</b></a> that night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato">SN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha famously taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato"><b>victory breeds enmity</b></a>, because the winner of a conflict inevitably creates resentment and hostility in those they have defeated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato">SN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato"><b>peaceful</b></a> (upasanto) sleeps happily because they have completely discarded the desire for both victory and defeat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato">SN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was informed by a group of monks that King Pasenadi was forced to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato"><b>retreat</b></a> to his own capital, Sāvatthī, after his army was crushed by the forces of King Ajātasattu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato">SN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato"><b>evil companionship</b></a> (pāpasampavaṅko) to describe the social and moral environment of King Ajātasattu, who initiated the conflict with Kosala? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato">SN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that true happiness is found by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato"><b>discarding victory and defeat</b></a>, rather than by seeking satisfaction through triumph in war? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato">SN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that while King Ajātasattu won the battle, it was the king who was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato"><b>virtuous companion</b></a>—Pasenadi—who actually suffered the physical defeat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato">SN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato"><b>defeated</b></a> sleep in pain, while the winner of a contest only generates further hatred through their triumph over others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.14/en/sujato">SN 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once told the yakkha Sūciloma that while he was not afraid of him, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato"><b>yakkha’s touch</b></a> was "evil" and unpleasant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato">SN 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the yakkha Sūciloma threatened to shatter his heart or throw him across the Ganges River, the Buddha declared that he saw no one in the world of devas, Maras, or Brahmas who could actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato"><b>shatter his heart</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato">SN 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato"><b>lust and hatred</b></a> originate from the self, just as the trunk-born shoots of a banyan tree grow from the tree itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato">SN 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato"><b>mental thoughts</b></a> that arise from the self to children releasing a crow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato">SN 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the way desires spread out among sense pleasures as being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato"><b>maluva creeper</b></a> spreading through a forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato">SN 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who understand the origin of lust and hatred are able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato"><b>cross the flood</b></a> of existence, which has never been crossed before, and avoid being born again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato">SN 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the yakkha Maṇibhadda observed that it is always auspicious for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.4/en/sujato"><b>mindful person</b></a>, as such a person prospers in happiness and is released from enmity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.4/en/sujato">SN 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person whose mind delights in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.4/en/sujato"><b>harmlessness</b></a> (ahiṃsā) throughout the day and night will have enmity with no one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.4/en/sujato">SN 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who has a share of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.4/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> (metta) for all beings will experience a life where they have enmity with no one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.4/en/sujato">SN 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the yakkhiṇī Piyaṅkaramātā urged her son to be quiet because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.6/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Anuruddha</b></a> was reciting verses of the Dhamma at the break of dawn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.6/en/sujato">SN 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Piyaṅkaramātā suggested to her son that by understanding and practicing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.6/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma verses</b></a> being recited by the monk, they could achieve their own welfare and benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.6/en/sujato">SN 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Piyaṅkaramātā taught her son that they should practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.6/en/sujato"><b>restraint</b></a> toward living beings and avoid harming them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.6/en/sujato">SN 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the yakkhiṇī Piyaṅkaramātā encouraged her son not to speak <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.6/en/sujato"><b>deliberate lies</b></a> as part of their training in good conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.6/en/sujato">SN 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Piyaṅkaramātā hoped that by training in good conduct and virtue, she and her son might eventually be released from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.6/en/sujato"><b>spirit realm</b></a> (pisāca-womb)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.6/en/sujato">SN 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, emotional states like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato"><b>aversion and delight</b></a>, along with horripilation, are self-produced and originate from the same source as lust and hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.3/en/sujato">SN 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>extinguished</b></a> (parinibbuto) is one who always sleeps in comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the yakkha Sivaka encouraged Anāthapiṇḍika to continue his journey by stating that a hundred elephants or horses are not worth a sixteenth part of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>single step</b></a> taken toward seeing the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>unsmeared by sensual pleasures</b></a> is the one who truly sleeps in peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Anāthapiṇḍika set out to see him at dawn, the Buddha was already awake and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>walking in the open air</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha greeted the householder Anāthapiṇḍika by his personal name, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>Sudatta</b></a>, which greatly delighted the householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that once a person has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>severed all attachments</b></a>, they are able to attain peace of mind and sleep happily? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the yakkha Sivaka urged Anāthapiṇḍika to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>advance</b></a> toward the Buddha because moving forward was better for his welfare than turning back? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who has reached the end of suffering as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>cooled</b></a> (sītibhūto) and free from the heat of defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>dispelled anguish</b></a> from their heart is the one who experiences true restful sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the yakkha Sivaka compared the value of a hundred <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>chariots</b></a> drawn by she-mules as being far less than the value of making progress toward the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin</b></a> who is fully liberated as a person who always sleeps in comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>without acquisitions</b></a> (nirūpadhi) is a characteristic of someone who sleeps in total comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as Anāthapiṇḍika approached the cemetery gate to find the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>non-human beings</b></a> (amanussā) opened the gate for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>attained peace</b></a> with their mind sleeps happily because they have removed the distress in their heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the yakkha Sivaka spoke to Anāthapiṇḍika in the darkness, the householder's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>fear and horripilation</b></a> subsided, and light reappeared to guide him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Anāthapiṇḍika was so eager to see the Buddha that he woke up <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>three times</b></a> during the night, mistakenly believing it was already dawn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the yakkha Sivaka mentioned that a hundred thousand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>maidens</b></a> adorned with jeweled earrings are not worth even a fraction of the merit of approaching the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the darkness and fear Anāthapiṇḍika felt, the yakkha Sivaka urged him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato"><b>advance</b></a> three times, ensuring he reached the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.8/en/sujato">SN 10.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a group of virtuous <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.10/en/sujato"><b>isayo</b></a> (rishis) who lived in leaf huts on the shore of the ocean and were respected for their good character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.10/en/sujato">SN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how virtuous rishis once sought a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.10/en/sujato"><b>guarantee of safety</b></a> from Sambara, the Lord of the Asuras, because they feared the unrighteous nature of the asuras during a war with the devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.10/en/sujato">SN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Lord of the Asuras refused to grant safety to virtuous rishis, the Buddha said the rishis warned him that one reaps the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.10/en/sujato"><b>fruit</b></a> of whatever seeds they sow, whether good or evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.10/en/sujato">SN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a story where the Lord of the Asuras, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.10/en/sujato"><b>Sambara</b></a>, was cursed by virtuous rishis and subsequently suffered from such great terror that he woke up in a fright three times every night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.10/en/sujato">SN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the rishis declared that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.10/en/sujato"><b>doer of evil</b></a> would inevitably experience the results of their own unwholesome actions, just as a doer of good reaps good results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.10/en/sujato">SN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Sakka, the Lord of the Devas, attained his high celestial status by undertaking and fulfilling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato"><b>seven vows</b></a> during his previous life as a human being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato">SN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that one of the seven life-long vows mentioned by the Buddha that lead to rebirth as the Lord of the Devas is the commitment to be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato"><b>supporter of one's parents</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato">SN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato"><b>gentle speech</b></a> as an essential life-long vow for those who wish to achieve the same celestial greatness as Sakka, the Lord of the Devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato">SN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that living with a heart free from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato"><b>stain of stinginess</b></a>, being open-handed and devoted to charity, was a vow that helped Sakka reach his position in the Tāvatiṃsa heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato">SN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who vows to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato"><b>truthful speech</b></a> for their entire life is recognized by the devas of Tāvatiṃsa as a truly superior person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato">SN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato"><b>free from anger</b></a>, or immediately subduing any anger that might arise, is one of the seven sacred vows that lead to celestial sovereignty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato">SN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato"><b>superior person</b></a> (sappuriso) as someone who respects the elders in their family and has completely abandoned slanderous speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato">SN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato"><b>devotion to charity</b></a> and the joy of sharing are qualities that mark a person as virtuous and worthy of rebirth in the divine realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.11/en/sujato">SN 11.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared a historical account where virtuous rishis observed that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.10/en/sujato"><b>devas</b></a> are righteous (dhammikā) while the asuras are unrighteous (adhammikā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.10/en/sujato">SN 11.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>four nutriments</b></a>—edible food, contact, mental volition, and consciousness—as necessary for the maintenance of beings that have already come to be? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked who consumes the consciousness-nutriment, the Buddha explained that it is not a valid question because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>he does not say</b></a> that anyone "consumes" it in a personal sense? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the consciousness-nutriment serves as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>condition</b></a> for the production of future renewed existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha rejected the question "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>who touches</b></a>," instructing that the valid way to ask is: "With what as condition is there contact?" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> arises specifically with the six sense bases as its necessary condition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha corrected the monk Moḷiyaphagguna by stating that it is invalid to ask "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>who feels</b></a>," as feeling should instead be understood as being conditioned by contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> is the specific condition that leads to the arising of craving in the chain of dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that asking "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>who craves</b></a>" is an improper question, and that one should focus on how craving arises from feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> (or acquisition) does not belong to a person, but rather arises with craving as its condition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that asking "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>who clings</b></a>" is not a valid question because there is no permanent agent behind the process of acquisition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>edible food</b></a>, whether coarse or fine, as the first of the four essential nutriments that sustain living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>mental volition</b></a> as a type of nutriment, alongside food and contact, that provides support for those seeking a new birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the arising of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>whole mass of suffering</b></a> is a result of a causal chain starting with conditions like craving and clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>six sense bases</b></a> are the foundation which, once present, allow for the arising of contact and subsequent experiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the cessation of contact is achieved through the remainderless fading away and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>cessation of the six</b></a> sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>cessation of craving</b></a>, the condition for clinging is removed, leading toward the end of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>cessation of birth</b></a> is the necessary requirement for the cessation of aging, death, sorrow, and lamentation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the final goal of his teaching as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato"><b>cessation of the mass</b></a> of suffering, which occurs when the entire chain of dependent origination is broken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.12/en/sujato">SN 12.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those ascetics and brahmins who do not understand the origin and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato"><b>cessation of these phenomena</b></a>—referring to the factors of dependent origination—are not considered true ascetics among ascetics? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato">SN 12.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that true ascetics and brahmins are those who clearly understand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato"><b>aging-and-death</b></a> along with its origin, its cessation, and the way leading to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato">SN 12.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one to be considered a true brahmin among brahmins, they must understand the nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a> and the specific path that leads to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato">SN 12.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said those who do not understand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a>, its origin, or the way to its cessation fail to realize the goal of asceticism or brahminhood in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato">SN 12.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that understanding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> and its origin is a fundamental requirement for any ascetic or brahmin to be truly recognized by their peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato">SN 12.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who understands <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> and the way leading to its cessation can personally realize and dwell in the goal of the holy life through direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato">SN 12.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the understanding of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> and its origin as essential knowledge that distinguishes a true seeker from one who is merely an ascetic in name only? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato">SN 12.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who do not understand the nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> and its cessation are not considered to have attained the true purpose of the ascetic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato">SN 12.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato"><b>six sense bases</b></a> as key factors whose origin and cessation must be understood to achieve spiritual realization in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato">SN 12.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who understand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a>, its origin, and its cessation are those who are truly esteemed among both ascetics and brahmins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato">SN 12.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that true spiritual practitioners are those who have directly known the nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a>, its origin, and the way leading to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato">SN 12.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that failing to understand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> and their cessation prevents a person from experiencing the goal of the holy life through their own direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato">SN 12.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by understanding the origin and cessation of factors like birth and craving, an ascetic or brahmin can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato"><b>personally realize</b></a> and dwell in the goal of their practice in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.14/en/sujato">SN 12.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato"><b>middle way</b></a> of Dhamma, which avoids the two extremes of "all exists" and "all does not exist"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato">SN 12.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one who sees the origin of the world with right wisdom, the notion of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato"><b>non-existence</b></a> regarding the world does not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato">SN 12.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one who sees the cessation of the world with right wisdom, the notion of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a> regarding the world does not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato">SN 12.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the world as being mostly fettered by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato"><b>engagement, clinging, and adherence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato">SN 12.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that right view is attained when one does not engage with or cling to the standpoint of "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato"><b>my self</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato">SN 12.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person has right view when they have the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato"><b>independent knowledge</b></a> that what arises and ceases in experience is merely suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato">SN 12.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the belief that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato"><b>all exists</b></a>" as one of the two dogmatic extremes that the world usually depends upon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato">SN 12.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the framework of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a> to teach the Dhamma, starting with ignorance as the condition for volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.15/en/sujato">SN 12.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato"><b>speaker of the Dhamma</b></a> as someone who teaches specifically for the sake of revulsion, fading away, and cessation of aging-and-death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato">SN 12.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato"><b>practicing in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a> if he practices for the sake of revulsion towards birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato">SN 12.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is liberated by non-clinging through revulsion towards existence as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato"><b>attained Nibbāna</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato">SN 12.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk teaches for the purpose of the cessation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a>, he is fit to be called a "speaker of the Dhamma"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato">SN 12.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one is considered to have attained Nibbāna in this very life if they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato"><b>liberated by non-clinging</b></a> through the fading away of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato">SN 12.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is a speaker of the Dhamma if they teach for the cessation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato"><b>feeling and contact</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato">SN 12.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that practicing for the fading away of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> and consciousness is a hallmark of one who is practicing in accordance with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato">SN 12.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk is liberated through revulsion towards <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a>, he is rightly called one who has attained Nibbāna in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.16/en/sujato">SN 12.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> was initially reluctant to visit a nunnery with Venerable Ānanda, twice telling him to go alone because Ānanda was "very busy" and had much to do? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a nun named Thullatissā was so displeased by Venerable Mahākassapa teaching in front of Venerable Ānanda that she compared it to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>needle-peddler</b></a> trying to sell a needle in the presence of a professional needle-maker? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Thullatissā referred to Venerable Ānanda as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>Vedehan sage</b></a> while expressing her offense that Venerable Mahākassapa would think he should teach the Dhamma in Ānanda's presence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Mahākassapa was insulted by a nun, Venerable Ānanda asked for patience and forgiveness on her behalf, telling Kassapa that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>women are foolish</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha publicly introduced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> to the Sangha as being his equal in the ability to attain the first jhana—secluded from sensual pleasures and unwholesome states—whenever he desired? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa warned Venerable Ānanda to stop excusing a nun's behavior, saying, "Wait, friend Ānanda, do not let the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>Sangha</b></a> investigate you further"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared he could attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> and enter the taintless liberation of mind in this very life, and he explicitly stated that Venerable Mahākassapa could do the same? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa stated that anyone who thought they could hide his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> (abhiññā) would be as mistaken as someone thinking they could hide a massive royal elephant with a small palm leaf? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa compared the magnitude of his spiritual attainments to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>royal elephant</b></a> that stands seven or seven-and-a-half cubits high to show they cannot be easily dismissed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after she spoke disrespectfully to Venerable Mahākassapa and heard his response regarding his superior attainments, the nun <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>Thullatissā</b></a> fell away from the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa pointed out that the Buddha never introduced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ānanda</b></a> to the Sangha as being equal to himself in the mastery of the four jhanas and the formless attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa directly challenged Venerable Ānanda's standing by asking him which of them was actually the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>needle-maker</b></a> and which was the "needle-peddler" in the eyes of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recognized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> as having the same capacity as himself to attain the state of the cessation of perception and feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa emphasized that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom were personally verified by him through direct knowledge, just as the Buddha had verified his own? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda acted as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>attendant monk</b></a> (pacchāsamaṇa) when he finally convinced Venerable Mahākassapa to visit the nunnery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>palm leaf</b></a> to illustrate the absurdity of trying to obscure or minimize his high spiritual status and direct knowledges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when he visited the nunnery, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> gave a Dhamma talk that was described as instructing, exhorting, inspiring, and gladdening the nuns who were present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared to the Sangha that Venerable Mahākassapa possessed the same <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>psychic powers</b></a> as himself, such as being able to travel through walls and mountains as if through space? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed Venerable Mahākassapa could exercise the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato"><b>divine ear element</b></a> to hear both human and divine sounds, a mastery the Buddha claimed for himself as well? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.10/en/sujato">SN 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> once rebuked Venerable Ānanda for traveling with a group of young monks who were unrestrained in their senses and did not know moderation in eating, calling Ānanda a "boy" despite his seniority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Venerable Ānanda was wandering in the Southern Hills, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>thirty young monks</b></a> under his care abandoned the training and returned to the lower life of a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha prescribed rules for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>communal meals</b></a> (tikabhojana) for three specific reasons: to restrain ill-behaved people, for the comfort of well-behaved monks, and to prevent those with evil wishes from creating a schism in the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> compared Ānanda’s traveling with untrained monks to "destroying the crops" and "destroying families" because of the burden such a large, unrestrained group placed on the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ānanda</b></a> defended himself against Mahākassapa’s criticism by pointing out that grey hairs had already grown on his head, yet he was still being called a "boy"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>Thullanandā</b></a> was so offended by Mahākassapa calling Ānanda a "boy" that she publicly reminded everyone that Mahākassapa was a "former adherent of another sect"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> declared that from the moment he shaved his hair and put on saffron robes, he never recognized any teacher other than the Blessed One, the Arahant, the Perfectly Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before meeting the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> renounced the world using a robe made of "patchwork cloth" that he had made specifically for the sake of whoever were the Arahants in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Mahākassapa first met the Buddha at the Bahuputta Shrine, he immediately prostrated at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>Blessed One's feet</b></a> and declared himself a disciple for the very first time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned Mahākassapa that if a teacher claims to "know" or "see" when they do not, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>head might split</b></a> apart, but he himself only speaks of what he truly knows and sees? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s first instruction to Mahākassapa was to maintain a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>keen sense of shame</b></a> and fear of wrongdoing (hirottappa) toward all monks, whether they were elders, new, or of middle standing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Mahākassapa to listen to any Dhamma connected with the wholesome by making it a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>matter of vital concern</b></a> and attending to it with his whole mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Mahākassapa that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> (kāyagatāsati) accompanied by joy should never be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> attained final knowledge (Arahantship) on the eighth day after his first meeting with the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for the first seven days after his ordination, Mahākassapa considered himself a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>debtor</b></a> while eating the almsfood of the country, only becoming debt-free on the eighth day when he achieved liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a profound gesture of spiritual intimacy, the Buddha and Venerable Mahākassapa <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>exchanged robes</b></a>, with the Buddha taking Mahākassapa's patchwork robe and Mahākassapa taking the Buddha's worn-out hempen rag-robes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked on how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>soft</b></a> Mahākassapa's patchwork cloth robe was when Mahākassapa offered it to him as a seat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> claimed that if anyone is truly the Buddha’s "own son, born of his mouth," it is he, because he was the one who received the Buddha's rag-robes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> stated he could enter and dwell in the four jhānas and the four formless meditative attainments whenever he desired? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> declared he had personally realized the destruction of the taints and the liberation of mind through his own direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahākassapa compared his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> to a massive royal elephant, stating that anyone who thought they could hide his spiritual attainments was as foolish as someone trying to hide such an elephant with a palm leaf? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after speaking out against Mahākassapa in defense of Ānanda, the nun <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>Thullanandā</b></a> eventually fell away from the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the household life as a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>crowded, dusty path</b></a>" while describing the life of renunciation as being like the open air? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Mahākassapa offered his robe to the Buddha, the Buddha asked him, "Will you wear my <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>hempen rag-robes</b></a>, which are now worn out?" to which Mahākassapa agreed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> recounted his entire spiritual history to Ānanda to demonstrate why he was justified in offering correction, despite Thullanandā’s objections? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.11/en/sujato">SN 16.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa once asked the Buddha why there were fewer training rules in the past when more monks were established in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>final knowledge</b></a>, compared to the present where rules have increased but fewer monks reach that goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that as beings decline and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>true Dhamma</b></a> begins to disappear, the number of training rules increases while the number of monks attaining enlightenment decreases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the true Dhamma does not disappear from the world until a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>counterfeit of the true Dhamma</b></a> arises? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of gold to explain that just as genuine gold only disappears once <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>counterfeit gold</b></a> appears in the world, the true Dhamma is only eclipsed by a counterfeit version of itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that the true Dhamma is not destroyed by external forces like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>earth element</b></a>, the water element, the fire element, or the air element? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>misguided men</b></a> (moghapurisā) within the Buddhist community itself who arise and cause the true Dhamma to disappear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the disappearance of the true Dhamma does not happen all at once, contrasting it with how a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>ship sinks</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five specific qualities among practitioners—monks, nuns, and lay followers—that lead to the confusion and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>disappearance of the true Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that his teachings decline when monks, nuns, and lay followers live without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>respect and deference</b></a> toward the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a lack of respect for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a> itself is one of the five primary factors that lead to the eventual disappearance of the true teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the true Dhamma is at risk of vanishing when practitioners no longer show <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>respect and deference</b></a> toward the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when the community of followers lacks respect for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>training</b></a> (sikkhā), it contributes directly to the loss of the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that maintaining a deep respect for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> (samādhi) is essential for preventing the true Dhamma from being forgotten? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato"><b>endurance of the true Dhamma</b></a> is ensured only when monks, nuns, and lay followers live with consistent respect and deference toward the Teacher, the Dhamma, the Sangha, the training, and concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.13/en/sujato">SN 16.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the devaputta Jantu observed that the earlier disciples of the Buddha were "happy-livers" who sought their alms and lodgings without personal desire and attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.25/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a> by recognizing the impermanence of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.25/en/sujato">SN 2.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the devaputta Jantu criticized certain monks for being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.25/en/sujato"><b>restless and talkative</b></a>, noting that they had become scatter-brained and had lost their clear comprehension and mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.25/en/sujato">SN 2.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the devaputta Jantu compared negligent monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.25/en/sujato"><b>village headmen</b></a>, stating that they simply eat and lie down, remaining infatuated with the homes of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.25/en/sujato">SN 2.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the devaputta Jantu warned that monks who dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.25/en/sujato"><b>negligently</b></a> are considered abandoned and protectorless, whereas he pays homage to those who dwell with diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.25/en/sujato">SN 2.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Nanda devaputta pointed out how time flies and the stages of life leave us, he recommended that one should perform <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.27/en/sujato"><b>meritorious deeds</b></a> because they are productive of happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.27/en/sujato">SN 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha countered Nanda devaputta’s advice on merit by stating that one who is seeking peace should instead <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.27/en/sujato"><b>abandon the world’s bait</b></a> when considering the fear of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.27/en/sujato">SN 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.27/en/sujato"><b>seek peace</b></a> by letting go of worldly attachments, because the passing of time and the decline of life's stages are constant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.27/en/sujato">SN 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the devaputta Nandivisāla described the human body as a "four-wheeled" vehicle with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.28/en/sujato"><b>nine openings</b></a> that is filled with and bound by greed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.28/en/sujato">SN 2.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the "journey" beyond the body is achieved by cutting the thongs of anger and the strap of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.28/en/sujato"><b>evil desire and greed</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.28/en/sujato">SN 2.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that successful progress in the spiritual life is only possible by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.28/en/sujato"><b>uprooting craving</b></a> at its very root? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.28/en/sujato">SN 2.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a monk delights in or welcomes agreeable forms cognizable by the eye, he is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato"><b>bound by Mara</b></a> and falls under the power of the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato">SN 35.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who does not delight in or remain fixated on agreeable tastes as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato"><b>released from Mara’s snare</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato">SN 35.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who delights in agreeable mental phenomena is essentially living in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato"><b>Mara's domain</b></a> and can be dealt with by the Evil One as he pleases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato">SN 35.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato"><b>freed from Mara’s bond</b></a> when he does not welcome or remain fixated on agreeable sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, or thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato">SN 35.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identifies objects "cognizable by the tongue" as potentially alluring and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato"><b>enticing</b></a>, leading to spiritual bondage if one delights in them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato">SN 35.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that not being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato"><b>fixated</b></a> on mental phenomena (manoviññeyyā dhammā) is a key requirement for escaping the snares of the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato">SN 35.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that "agreeable and alluring" forms are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato"><b>provocative of lust</b></a>, and that delighting in them leads directly to falling under the control of Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato">SN 35.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato"><b>Evil One</b></a> can do whatever he wants with a monk who remains fixated on pleasing sensory objects, as such a monk has entered his power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato">SN 35.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who does not delight in agreeable tactile objects is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato"><b>freed</b></a> from the bond of Mara and is no longer subject to his whims? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.115/en/sujato">SN 35.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the devaputta Jantu observed that earlier monks sought alms and lodgings <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.25/en/sujato"><b>without desire</b></a>, contrasting them with later monks who had become unrestrained in their speech and senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.25/en/sujato">SN 2.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed the Licchavis of Vesāli currently dwelling with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato"><b>log pillows</b></a>, remaining diligent and ardent in their archery practice, which prevents King Ajātasattu of Magadha from gaining an opening against them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato">SN 20.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if the Licchavis ever become delicate and begin to sleep on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato"><b>soft beds</b></a> with down pillows until sunrise, King Ajātasattu will then gain an opening and a foothold to conquer them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato">SN 20.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that as long as monks dwell with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato"><b>log pillows</b></a> and remain diligent and ardent in their spiritual striving, Mara the Evil One will not gain an opening or an object for his interference? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato">SN 20.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted that in the future, monks will become delicate and will sleep on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato"><b>cushioned beds</b></a> until sunrise, which will allow Mara the Evil One to gain an opening and a foothold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato">SN 20.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted his disciples to train themselves by vowing to dwell with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato"><b>log pillows</b></a> and to remain diligent and ardent in their striving to prevent spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato">SN 20.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of the Licchavis' <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato"><b>archery practice</b></a> (upāsana) to illustrate the importance of vigilance and physical hardship in maintaining one's defense against enemies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato">SN 20.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that future monks would possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato"><b>delicate hands and feet</b></a>, indicating a shift toward luxury that would make them vulnerable to the power of Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato">SN 20.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the primary defense for both a kingdom and a monk's spiritual path is to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato"><b>diligent</b></a> (appamatta) and ardent (ātāpī) in their respective duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato">SN 20.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato"><b>sleeping until sunrise</b></a> on soft beds as a sign of negligence that invites defeat from external or spiritual adversaries? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato">SN 20.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato"><b>ardent in striving</b></a> (padhānasmiṃ) actively prevents the Evil One from finding any basis to influence or control their mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.8/en/sujato">SN 20.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that the reason why the Buddha left the status of the Tathāgata after death <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato"><b>undeclared</b></a> is directly related to the presence of lust and craving for the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato">SN 44.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that speculative views regarding whether a Tathāgata exists after death arise in those who have not yet <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato"><b>faded away lust</b></a> for the aggregate of form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato">SN 44.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that if a person has not yet faded away their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato"><b>affection for feeling</b></a>, they will continue to hold speculative views about the post-mortem status of the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato">SN 44.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified that views about the Tathāgata exist because a person has not yet abandoned their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato"><b>thirst for perception</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato">SN 44.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that having an unextinguished <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato"><b>fever for volitional formations</b></a> is a specific cause and condition for why metaphysical questions about the Tathāgata remain undeclared? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato">SN 44.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta clarified that the view "the Tathāgata neither exists nor does not exist after death" arises in one who still possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato"><b>craving for consciousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato">SN 44.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that once a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato"><b>rid of lust</b></a> and craving for the aggregate of form, speculative views about the Tathāgata's existence after death no longer occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato">SN 44.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta noted that the cessation of speculative views regarding the Tathāgata depends on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato"><b>fading away</b></a> of desire, affection, thirst, and fever for the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato">SN 44.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta associated the "undeclared" nature of certain philosophical points with the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> (taṇhā) for mental and physical processes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato">SN 44.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained to Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato"><b>cause and condition</b></a> for the Buddha leaving questions undeclared is the fundamental way one relates to form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.5/en/sujato">SN 44.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared an uninstructed worldling being swept away by the "current" of existence to a person in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato"><b>mountain river</b></a> who tries to save themselves by grabbing onto grasses and trees that simply break and tear away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato">SN 22.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that viewing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> as the self is as futile as a drowning man grabbing onto river plants, because form is subject to "breaking" and will inevitably lead to ruin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato">SN 22.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an uninstructed worldling meets with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato"><b>calamity and ruin</b></a> because they mistakenly identify feeling, perception, or volitional formations as their permanent self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato">SN 22.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> as one of the five aggregates that "breaks," meaning that those who rely on it as a permanent self will eventually face spiritual disaster? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato">SN 22.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of various <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato"><b>river grasses</b></a> like kāsa, kusa, and bīraṇa to illustrate the fragile and unreliable nature of the five aggregates when one tries to cling to them for security? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato">SN 22.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the five aggregates are fundamentally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a> (anicca), and that recognizing this truth is the first step toward spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato">SN 22.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a noble disciple who sees the truth of the aggregates becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato"><b>disillusioned</b></a> with form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness, leading to the fading of lust and eventual freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato">SN 22.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that liberation is found when one understands that "there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato"><b>no more for this state of being</b></a>," signifying that the cycle of rebirth based on clinging has ended? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato">SN 22.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the uninstructed worldling lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato"><b>clear comprehension</b></a> of the Dhamma, which is why they continue to view the changing aggregates as "mine" or "myself"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato">SN 22.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato"><b>palujjati</b></a> to describe how the aggregates "break" or "disintegrate," emphasizing that they cannot provide a stable foundation for a permanent identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.93/en/sujato">SN 22.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato"><b>Dependent Origination</b></a> (Paṭiccasamuppāda) as a specific sequence of twelve conditions that lead to the arising of the entire mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato">SN 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> (avijjā) is the primary condition that gives rise to volitional formations, which in turn condition the arising of consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato">SN 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the arising of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> is dependent upon consciousness, establishing the link between awareness and the mental-physical experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato">SN 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato"><b>six sense bases</b></a> (saḷāyatana) arise because of name-and-form, providing the necessary platform for sensory contact to occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato">SN 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> (vedanā) acts as the condition for craving (taṇhā), which is the driving force behind clinging and continued existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato">SN 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a> (jāti) as the direct condition for aging and death, as well as for sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato">SN 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the entire <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato"><b>mass of suffering</b></a> ceases only when ignorance is completely abandoned and extinguished through remainderless fading away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato">SN 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato"><b>cessation of existence</b></a> (bhavanirodha) results directly from the cessation of clinging, which in turn prevents any future birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato">SN 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato"><b>reverse sequence</b></a> of Dependent Origination as the method for dismantling the cycle of rebirth and ending all forms of distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato">SN 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> (saṅkhārā) cease when ignorance ceases, effectively stopping the chain reaction that leads to rebirth and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.1/en/sujato">SN 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a monk asked if there was any form that was stable and eternal, the Buddha clarified that there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.98/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> whatsoever that is permanent, eternal, not subject to change, or that will stay that way forever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.98/en/sujato">SN 22.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.98/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>, perception, or volitional formation that can be considered permanent or eternal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.98/en/sujato">SN 22.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.98/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> that is permanent, stable, eternal, or not subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.98/en/sujato">SN 22.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that not even a single instance of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.98/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> (form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness) remains the same forever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.98/en/sujato">SN 22.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.62/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> (yonisomanasikāra) as the primary quality that ensures a monk will develop and cultivate the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.62/en/sujato">SN 45.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a monk who possesses careful attention, it is to be expected that he will develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.62/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.62/en/sujato">SN 45.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, developing right view as part of the Noble Eightfold Path culminates in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.62/en/sujato"><b>removal of lust</b></a>, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.62/en/sujato">SN 45.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk cultivates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.62/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> specifically for the purpose of the final removal of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.62/en/sujato">SN 45.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato"><b>three kinds of feeling</b></a>: pleasant feeling, painful feeling, and neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato">SN 36.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato"><b>pleasant feeling</b></a> should be viewed as suffering (dukkha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato">SN 36.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato"><b>painful feeling</b></a> should be seen as a dart (salla)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato">SN 36.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato"><b>neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling</b></a> should be regarded as impermanent (anicca)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato">SN 36.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a monk sees feelings correctly—as suffering, as a dart, or as impermanent—he is called a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato"><b>sees rightly</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato">SN 36.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by seeing feelings correctly, a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato"><b>cuts off craving</b></a> and discards the fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato">SN 36.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who has made an end of suffering through the right breakthrough of conceit is one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato"><b>fully understood</b></a> all feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato">SN 36.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk who understands feelings is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato"><b>taintless</b></a> (anāsavo) in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato">SN 36.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that upon the breakup of the body, a master of feelings who is established in Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato"><b>not subject to being reckoned</b></a> (saṅkhyaṃ nopeti)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.5/en/sujato">SN 36.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was informed that King Ajātasattu of Magadha once mobilized a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>fourfold army</b></a> to invade the territory of Kāsi and attack King Pasenadi? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listened as monks reported that King Pasenadi of Kosala successfully defeated King Ajātasattu in battle and managed to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>capture him alive</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was told how King Pasenadi reflected that King Ajātasattu, despite attacking an innocent party, was still his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>nephew</b></a> and should be treated with some leniency? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha heard that King Pasenadi decided to confiscate his enemy's entire elephant, horse, chariot, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>infantry corps</b></a> before releasing him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha received the news of the war between the two kings from a group of monks who had returned from their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>alms round</b></a> in the city of Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a man will continue to plunder others as long as it serves his purpose, but eventually he will be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>plundered in turn</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a fool considers a situation to be an opportunity only as long as their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>evil deed</b></a> has not yet ripened? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a harmful action matures and ripens, the person who committed it inevitably <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>encounters suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that according to the laws of karma, the person who kills will eventually encounter a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>slayer</b></a> of their own? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a person who conquers others will eventually find themselves facing someone who achieves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>victory</b></a> over them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an abuser will, in the course of time, receive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>abuse</b></a> back upon themselves due to their own past actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that an annoyer of others will eventually be met by an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>annoyer</b></a> in return? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described this cycle of reciprocal violence and retribution as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>evolution of kamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's monks identified the aggressor in the battle as King Ajātasattu of Magadha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>son of the Videhan lady</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha heard that the military clash between the forces of Magadha and Kosala took place in the territory of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>Kāsi</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was told that King Pasenadi chose to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>let him go alive</b></a> after completely stripping Ajātasattu of his military resources? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was informed that King Pasenadi was troubled that Ajātasattu chose to attack him even though Pasenadi was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>doing him no harm</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that by the natural laws of consequence, the person who acts as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato"><b>plunderer</b></a> is eventually plundered themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.15/en/sujato">SN 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when King Pasenadi of Kosala became visibly disappointed upon hearing that Queen Mallikā had given birth to a daughter, the Buddha comforted him by explaining that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.16/en/sujato"><b>female offspring</b></a> may actually prove to be better than a male? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.16/en/sujato">SN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a superior woman as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.16/en/sujato"><b>wise and virtuous</b></a>, possessing qualities such as honoring her mother-in-law and being a faithful wife? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.16/en/sujato">SN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a son born to a virtuous and wise woman could eventually grow up to be a hero and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.16/en/sujato"><b>lord of the land</b></a> who governs a kingdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.16/en/sujato">SN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed King Pasenadi that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a> (appamāda) is the one single quality that keeps a person secure in both the good of the present life and the good of the life to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato">SN 3.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato"><b>elephant’s footprint</b></a>—which is considered the best of all footprints because its great size can contain the footprints of all other land animals—to illustrate how diligence encompasses all beneficial aims? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato">SN 3.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that wise people praise <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> in the performance of meritorious deeds because it allows a person to secure both the benefit seen in this life and the benefit of the future life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato">SN 3.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is rightfully called "wise" because they achieve the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato"><b>attainment of the good</b></a> through persistent diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato">SN 3.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that anyone desiring long life, health, beauty, heaven, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato"><b>high birth</b></a> should practice diligence to ensure these various kinds of happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato">SN 3.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed King Pasenadi's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.16/en/sujato"><b>displeasure</b></a> upon receiving the news of his daughter's birth and responded by reciting verses to highlight the potential greatness of women? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.16/en/sujato">SN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.16/en/sujato"><b>faithful wife</b></a> (patibbatā) who is wise and virtuous is a "woman of status" whose child may rule over a great realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.16/en/sujato">SN 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when King Pasenadi asked if there was a single factor that could achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato"><b>both aims</b></a>—the welfare of the present world and the world beyond—the Buddha identified diligence as the definitive answer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato">SN 3.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as the elephant's footprint is preeminent due to its size, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a> is considered the foremost quality for securing one's well-being in all circumstances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.17/en/sujato">SN 3.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed King Pasenadi’s reflection that his well-expounded Dhamma is intended for those who possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a>, good companionship, and good comradeship, rather than those with bad companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha corrected the Venerable Ānanda’s view that good friendship is only half of the holy life, asserting instead that it is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>entire holy life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> can be expected to fully develop and cultivate the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk with good friends develops the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> by cultivating right view and other factors based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, ending in relinquishment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified himself as the supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>good friend</b></a> for all beings, because it is by relying on him that beings subject to birth are liberated from birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that because of his role as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>good friend</b></a>, beings who are subject to aging, illness, and death are able to find liberation from those inevitable sufferings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings subject to sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair are liberated from these states by relying on him as their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>good friend</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged King Pasenadi of Kosala to specifically train himself to become a person who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> and good companionship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that once a person has established good friendship, they should dwell supported by the factor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a> (appamāda) in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to King Pasenadi that when a ruler dwells with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a>, the women of his harem will observe his example and likewise live with diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a king's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a> would inspire his kshatriya vassals and court attendants to also live with heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even a king's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>army</b></a> and military forces would follow the king's lead and strive to live diligently if the king himself is diligent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a king's personal practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a> influences his subjects in both the town and the countryside to also live diligently? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that for a king who dwells with diligence, his own person, his harem, and even his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>treasury</b></a> and storehouses will be effectively guarded and protected? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted a conversation with the Venerable Ānanda that took place in a Sakyan town called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>Nagaraka</b></a> to emphasize the vital importance of good friendship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wise people praise <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a> in the performance of meritorious deeds because it secures long-term welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a wise person is one who, through persistent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a>, manages to secure both the welfare of the present life and the welfare of the life to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>paṇḍita</b></a> (a wise person) to specifically describe those who achieve the "attainment of the good" through steadfast diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that while seeking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>wealth</b></a> and abundance, it is diligence in meritorious deeds that is truly praised by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed King Pasenadi's insight that the holy life is essentially about <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a>, noting that bad companionship is a hindrance to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.18/en/sujato">SN 3.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>King Pasenadi</b></a> of Kosala visited the Buddha and reported that he had just finished moving the estate of a wealthy money-lender, who had died childless, into the royal palace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha heard from King Pasenadi about a money-lender in Sāvatthī who possessed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>eight million</b></a> pieces of gold, and even more silver, yet lived an incredibly austere life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi told the Buddha that the deceased multi-millionaire would only eat <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>red rice bran</b></a> accompanied by sour gruel, despite his immense wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was informed by King Pasenadi that a wealthy merchant chose to wear <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>hempen garments</b></a> made of three pieces rather than luxurious fabrics? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi described to the Buddha how a man of great riches used to travel in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>dilapidated chariot</b></a> with an awning made only of leaves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>unworthy man</b></a> (asappuriso), upon obtaining grand wealth, fails to use it to bring happiness to himself or his parents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an unworthy person with great wealth does not provide for their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>wife and children</b></a>, nor for their workers and servants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that an unworthy man who gains wealth fails to make his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>friends and colleagues</b></a> happy or satisfied? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that an unworthy person does not use their wealth to establish offerings for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>ascetics and brahmins</b></a> that could lead to a heavenly rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if wealth is not properly used, it is eventually taken by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>kings or thieves</b></a>, or destroyed by fire or water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wealth improperly managed by an unworthy person results in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>unloved heirs</b></a> taking it away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>pond</b></a> in an uninhabited, haunted place, where the clear, cool water remains unused and eventually dries up, to describe wasted wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>worthy man</b></a> (sappuriso) as someone who, after acquiring grand wealth, brings happiness to himself, his family, and his associates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a worthy person uses their wealth to establish a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>meritorious offering</b></a> for ascetics and brahmins, which has a happy maturation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the properly utilized wealth of a good person to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>pleasant pond</b></a> located near a village or town where people can actually benefit from the water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a worthy person enjoys and shares their wealth, it goes to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>proper use</b></a> rather than being wasted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to a person who has wealth but neither enjoys it nor gives it away as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>base person</b></a> (kāpuriso) in his verses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>wise and experienced</b></a> person as one who, after obtaining wealth, fulfills their duties and supports their kin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>doer of duties</b></a> with their wealth and remains blameless will reach a heavenly state after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that wealth not correctly enjoyed or shared leads to its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato"><b>depletion</b></a> without any benefit to the owner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.19/en/sujato">SN 3.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the lord of the devas is called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato"><b>Maghavā</b></a> because, in a previous life as a human being, he was a young man named Magha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato">SN 11.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, Sakka is known as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato"><b>Purindada</b></a> because, during his former existence as a human, he was known for giving gifts in towns or cities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato">SN 11.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the king of the gods is specifically called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato"><b>Sakka</b></a> because, when he was a human being, he gave gifts thoroughly and with proper care (sakkaccaṃ)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato">SN 11.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed Sakka is also named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato"><b>Vāsava</b></a> because, in his previous life as a man, he provided rest-houses and dwellings for travelers and those in need? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato">SN 11.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Sakka as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato"><b>Sahassakkha</b></a>, or the "Thousand-Eyed," not because of his physical form, but because he has the mental capacity to think of a thousand matters in a single moment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato">SN 11.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that Sakka is referred to as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato"><b>Sujampati</b></a> because his primary consort is the Asura maiden named Sujā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato">SN 11.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Sakka attained his position as ruler of the Tāvatiṃsa devas by undertaking and perfectly fulfilling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato"><b>seven vows</b></a> throughout his entire human life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato">SN 11.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed "supporting one's parents for life" and "honoring the elders of the family" as the first two foundational commitments that lead a person to the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato"><b>Sakkatta</b></a>, or the sovereignty of the gods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato">SN 11.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one of the vital vows for attaining Sakka's status involves living with a mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato"><b>free from stinginess</b></a>, being one who is delighted in giving, open-handed, and devoted to charity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato">SN 11.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is truthful, speaks gently, avoids malicious speech, and successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato"><b>overcomes anger</b></a> is called a "superior person" by the devas of the Tāvatiṃsa heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.12/en/sujato">SN 11.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted a story about a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato"><b>poor, wretched man</b></a> in Rajagaha who, after practicing faith and virtue, was reborn in the Tavatimsa heaven as a superior being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato">SN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a formerly miserable human being was able to outshine other deities in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato"><b>beauty and glory</b></a> because he had undertaken the training in faith, virtue, learning, generosity, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato">SN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato"><b>Tavatimsa devas</b></a> were initially indignant and complained when they saw a formerly poor and lowly human reborn among them with such great splendor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato">SN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, lord of the devas, defended a newly reborn deity by telling the other gods that the newcomer's status was earned through his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato"><b>unshakable faith</b></a> in the Tathagata while he was a human? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato">SN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared how Sakka defined a person with firm virtue and confidence in the Sangha as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato"><b>not poor</b></a>, declaring that such a person's life is not in vain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato">SN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha quoted Sakka as saying that a wise person should pursue faith, virtue, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma-vision</b></a> while remembering the teachings of the Buddhas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato">SN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the five specific qualities leading a destitute person to heavenly glory are faith, virtue, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato"><b>learning</b></a>, generosity, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato">SN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, lord of the devas, urged his fellow deities not to look down on a new god who had successfully practiced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato"><b>dhammavinaye</b></a> (the Dhamma and Discipline) proclaimed by the Tathagata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato">SN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person possesses a fruitful life when they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato"><b>straight view</b></a> and clear confidence in the community of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.14/en/sujato">SN 11.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha responded to a question from Sakka by stating that human delights like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.15/en/sujato"><b>lotus ponds</b></a> and beautifully designed parks are not worth even a sixteenth part of a truly delightful place? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.15/en/sujato">SN 11.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that whether located in a village, a forest, a valley, or a hill, a place is a truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.15/en/sujato"><b>delightful ground</b></a> only if Arahants dwell there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.15/en/sujato">SN 11.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, lord of the devas, specifically approached the Buddha at Jeta’s Grove in Savatthi to ask what constitutes a truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.15/en/sujato"><b>delightful place</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.15/en/sujato">SN 11.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.15/en/sujato"><b>Arahants</b></a> (enlightened beings) is what gives a location its true spiritual beauty, far surpassing any man-made landscape? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.15/en/sujato">SN 11.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.15/en/sujato"><b>parks and groves</b></a>, while pleasing to ordinary people, cannot compare to the spiritual atmosphere created by those who have attained liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.15/en/sujato">SN 11.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Sakka, lord of the devas, that for humans seeking merit, gifts given to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.16/en/sujato"><b>Sangha</b></a> yield the greatest fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.16/en/sujato">SN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the community of monks as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.16/en/sujato"><b>upright</b></a> and composed in wisdom and virtue, making them the ideal recipients of offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.16/en/sujato">SN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, lord of the devas, approached the Buddha at Vulture’s Peak to ask where a person should give a gift so that it results in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.16/en/sujato"><b>great fruit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.16/en/sujato">SN 11.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, lord of the devas, once praised the Buddha by comparing his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.17/en/sujato"><b>liberated mind</b></a> to the brilliance of the full moon on the fifteenth night of the month? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.17/en/sujato">SN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahma Sahampati suggested that the Buddha should be venerated as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.17/en/sujato"><b>caravan leader</b></a> who is free from debt while wandering the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.17/en/sujato">SN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahma Sahampati urged the Buddha to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.17/en/sujato"><b>teach the Dhamma</b></a> because there would be beings capable of understanding the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.17/en/sujato">SN 11.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how Sakka, lord of the devas, would descend from his Vejayanta Palace and pay homage to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato"><b>various directions</b></a> with joined palms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato">SN 11.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Matali the charioteer was surprised to see Sakka worshipping others, as he believed all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato"><b>kings and gods</b></a> usually pay homage only to Sakka himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato">SN 11.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how Sakka pays homage to those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato"><b>rightly gone forth</b></a> into the homeless life and are devoted to the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato">SN 11.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared that Sakka, the king of the gods, also pays homage to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato"><b>virtuous householders</b></a> who are merit-makers and faithful lay followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato">SN 11.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, Sakka respects those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato"><b>righteously support</b></a> their wives and families while living the life of a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato">SN 11.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Matali the charioteer, upon hearing Sakka's explanation, decided that he too would pay homage to those whom <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato"><b>Sakka venerates</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato">SN 11.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that Sakka refers to his own status as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato"><b>Sujampati</b></a> (husband of Sujā) while performing his acts of worship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato">SN 11.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato"><b>perfect in virtue</b></a> and have been concentrated for a long time are the primary objects of Sakka’s devotion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato">SN 11.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Sakka’s celestial vehicle as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato"><b>chariot yoked</b></a> with a thousand thoroughbreds, which he uses to travel to the gardens of the gods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.18/en/sujato">SN 11.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how Sakka, the lord of the devas, would descend from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato"><b>Vejayanta Palace</b></a> and pay homage with joined palms to the Buddha before traveling to his celestial gardens? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato">SN 11.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how Sakka’s charioteer, Matali, was astonished to see the king of gods paying homage to another, wondering what kind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato"><b>spirit</b></a> could possibly be superior to the lord of the devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato">SN 11.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that Sakka, lord of the devas, specifically pays homage to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato"><b>Fully Enlightened One</b></a>, whom he calls the "teacher of the world" in the presence of his charioteer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato">SN 11.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that Sakka worships <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato"><b>Arahants</b></a>, those in whom lust, hatred, and ignorance have been discarded and who have destroyed all mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato">SN 11.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that Sakka also venerates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato"><b>trainees</b></a>—those diligent practitioners who are still training to overcome lust and hatred—for their unwavering commitment to the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato">SN 11.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Sakka’s celestial vehicle as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato"><b>thousand-yoked thoroughbred chariot</b></a>, which the charioteer Matali prepares whenever the king wishes to visit the pleasure grounds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato">SN 11.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared how Matali the charioteer, after hearing Sakka's explanation, decided to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato"><b>pay homage</b></a> to those whom the lord of the devas himself venerates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.19/en/sujato">SN 11.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted a time when Sakka, lord of the devas, stood in front of his palace and paid homage to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato"><b>Community of Monks</b></a> (the Sangha) with joined palms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato">SN 11.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that Sakka's charioteer questioned why a god would worship humans, whom Matali described as beings dwelling in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato"><b>stinking bodies</b></a> and submerged in carcasses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato">SN 11.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Sakka admires <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato"><b>homeless ones</b></a> because they depart from villages without looking back and without any sense of expectation or craving for what they left behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato">SN 11.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that Sakka respects virtuous monks because they do not store food in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato"><b>granaries, jars, or baskets</b></a>, but instead live righteously on what is provided by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato">SN 11.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described those whom Sakka worships as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato"><b>non-hostile</b></a> even when they are surrounded by people who are full of hostility and aggression? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato">SN 11.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared how Sakka venerates those who have laid down the rod and are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato"><b>peaceful</b></a> among those who are violent or take up weapons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato">SN 11.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Sakka pays homage to those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato"><b>without attachment</b></a> among those who are still caught in the grasp of worldly possessions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato">SN 11.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to Sakka by the title <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato"><b>Sujampati</b></a>, husband of the Asura maiden Sujā, while describing his acts of worship toward the Community of Monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato">SN 11.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that Sakka calls the wise practitioners <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato"><b>sage-like</b></a> and silent, describing them as being at peace even while gods and asuras are at conflict? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato">SN 11.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how Sakka, after paying homage to the Sangha, finally mounted his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato"><b>chariot</b></a> to head to the garden, having placed spiritual reverence before his own royal pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.20/en/sujato">SN 11.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his enlightenment, while he was still an unawakened <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato"><b>Bodhisatta</b></a>, the Buddha reflected on how the world is deeply afflicted by a cycle of birth, aging, death, and rebirth without knowing a way out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato">SN 12.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha discovered the specific cause of suffering by applying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> (yoniso manasikāra), which led to his breakthrough in understanding the causal conditions of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato">SN 12.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha realized through direct insight that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a> is the necessary condition that leads to aging-and-death, noting that where birth exists, aging-and-death must inevitably follow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato">SN 12.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> as being conditioned by ignorance, a realization that forms a critical link in the chain of dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato">SN 12.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the entire sequence of causal conditions—starting from ignorance and leading up to birth and death—as the origin of this <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato"><b>whole mass of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato">SN 12.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha experienced the arising of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato"><b>vision and light</b></a> regarding spiritual truths never heard before once he clearly understood how the cycle of suffering originates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato">SN 12.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha investigated the cessation of suffering and found that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato"><b>aging-and-death</b></a> do not occur when their condition, birth, has been brought to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato">SN 12.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the cessation of the entire chain of suffering begins with the complete fading away and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato"><b>cessation of ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato">SN 12.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> occurs when volitional formations, consciousness, name-and-form, and the other causal links are systematically brought to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato">SN 12.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that regarding the cessation of suffering, knowledge, wisdom, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge</b></a> arose in him concerning things that had remained unheard by him before his awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato">SN 12.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha traced the origin of birth back to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a> (bhava), asserting that birth is a result of the ongoing process of becoming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato">SN 12.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that clinging is conditioned by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a>, showing that our desires are what drive us to attach to the world and maintain the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.10/en/sujato">SN 12.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>Bamboo Grove</b></a> in Rajagaha when he was approached by the naked ascetic Kassapa during his morning alms round? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Acelaka Kassapa first asked to pose a question, the Buddha initially declined, stating it was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>not the right time</b></a> because he had already entered the town for alms food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha rejected the philosophical proposition that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>suffering is self-created</b></a> when he was questioned by the ascetic Kassapa? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned Kassapa against the view that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>suffering is created by another</b></a> person or external force? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha denied that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> is both self-created and created by another, as well as the idea that it arises by chance without any cause at all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Kassapa mistakenly assumed the Buddha did not know or see suffering, the Buddha clarified that he indeed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>knows and sees suffering</b></a> clearly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the assertion "the one who acts is the same as the one who experiences the result" leads to the extreme view of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>eternalism</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the view "one person acts and another experiences the result" leads to the extreme of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>annihilationism</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that instead of following extreme views, the Buddha teaches the Dhamma by the middle way through the principle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the entire mass of suffering originates from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> as its fundamental conditioning factor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the sequence of suffering's origin as starting with ignorance, which conditions <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a>, which in turn condition consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> is achieved through the remainderless fading away and cessation of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches that when volitional formations cease, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> ceases, eventually leading to the ending of the whole mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's explanation of the middle way, Acelaka Kassapa declared that the Dhamma was made clear as if something <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato"><b>overturned</b></a> had been set upright? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.17/en/sujato">SN 12.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the physical bodies of both the fool and the wise person are produced through the conditioning factors of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato"><b>ignorance and craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato">SN 12.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that both the fool and the wise person experience pleasure and pain when they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato"><b>touched by contact</b></a> through the six sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato">SN 12.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that although both have bodies, the fool differs because their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato"><b>ignorance has not been abandoned</b></a> and their craving has not been exhausted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato">SN 12.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a fool remains bound to the cycle of rebirth because they have not lived the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> for the complete destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato">SN 12.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that upon the breakup of the body, a fool is one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato"><b>goes to a body</b></a>, meaning they are subject to further rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato">SN 12.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that because a fool "goes to a body" after death, they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato"><b>not freed from birth</b></a>, aging, death, sorrow, or lamentation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato">SN 12.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a wise person, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> that originally conditioned the production of their body has been completely exhausted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato">SN 12.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a wise person is distinguished by having lived the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> rightly for the purpose of ending all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato">SN 12.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person, having abandoned ignorance, is one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato"><b>does not go to a body</b></a> at the time of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato">SN 12.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who "does not go to a body" after death is finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato"><b>liberated from suffering</b></a>, including the pains of birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato">SN 12.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato"><b>living of the holy life</b></a> as the specific difference and disparity that sets the wise person apart from the fool? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.19/en/sujato">SN 12.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that whether or not Tathagatas (Buddhas) arise in the world, the natural law of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>specific conditionality</b></a> remains a stable and fixed course of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the principle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a> is distinct from the "dependently arisen phenomena" themselves, and he sought to clarify both for his disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>stability of the Dhamma</b></a> as a fundamental reality where aging-and-death is inevitably conditioned by birth, regardless of whether a Buddha is present to teach it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that after he personally discovered and broke through to the truth of conditionality, he then proceeded to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>disclose and analyze</b></a> it so that others could understand the causal links of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the principle of dependent origination through its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>actuality and inerrancy</b></a>, asserting that these causal sequences are unvarying and never otherwise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>aging-and-death</b></a> as a dependently arisen phenomenon that is impermanent, conditioned, and subject to vanishing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a> is a conditioned reality and, like all dependently arisen things, it is of a nature to eventually undergo cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a> (bhava) as a phenomenon that is subject to destruction and fading away because it arises from specific conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> and craving are not permanent states but are "dependently arisen phenomena" that possess the nature of vanishing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> and contact are conditioned experiences that are subject to the law of cessation and fading away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the six sense bases and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> are impermanent and conditioned realities within the chain of dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is a dependently arisen phenomenon, meaning it is not a permanent self but is subject to destruction and vanishing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> (saṅkhārā) as being conditioned by ignorance and having the inherent nature of fading away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> itself as a dependently arisen phenomenon that is impermanent and subject to cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a noble disciple sees dependent origination with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>right wisdom</b></a>, they stop wondering about their past identity or whether they existed in the past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who clearly sees causal conditions will not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>run forward</b></a> to the future, wondering what they will become or how they will exist in times to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who understands the Dhamma of conditionality will not be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>perplexed</b></a> about the present, asking "Am I?" or "How am I?",. <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that seeing the truth of phenomena prevents a disciple from questioning where "this <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>being</b></a>" came from or where it is going? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that it is impossible for a noble disciple who has seen the truth to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>inner doubts</b></a> about their existence across the three periods of time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato"><b>fixed course of the Dhamma</b></a> (dhammaniyāmatā) is an objective reality that he simply uncovers and makes plain for the world to see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.20/en/sujato">SN 12.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Mahā Kassapa for being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato"><b>content with any kind of robe</b></a> and for consistently speaking in praise of such contentment among the monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato">SN 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, Mahā Kassapa never resorts to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato"><b>improper search</b></a> or unseemly behavior for the sake of obtaining a robe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato">SN 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that Mahā Kassapa does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato"><b>fret</b></a> or become anxious when he fails to obtain a robe, maintaining his composure regardless of the outcome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato">SN 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Mahā Kassapa does receive a robe, the Buddha says he uses it without being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato"><b>infatuated</b></a> or blindly attached to it, always remaining aware of the potential danger in such requisites? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato">SN 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha held up Mahā Kassapa as an ideal example because he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato"><b>content with any kind of almsfood</b></a> he receives, using it only after seeing the danger and understanding the escape? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato">SN 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Mahā Kassapa as a monk who consumes his food while possessing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato"><b>wisdom of the escape</b></a>, ensuring he is not overcome by greed or attachment during his meal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato">SN 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha commended Mahā Kassapa for being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato"><b>content with any kind of lodging</b></a> and for refusing to engage in unseemly behavior just to secure a better dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato">SN 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted Mahā Kassapa's practice of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato"><b>content with any medicinal requisites</b></a> provided for the sick, using them without being "tied" to them or intoxicated by them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato">SN 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly instructed all monks to train themselves to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato"><b>content with whatever requisites</b></a> they receive, specifically using Mahā Kassapa as the standard for this training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato">SN 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told his followers he would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato"><b>exhort</b></a> them through Mahā Kassapa—or someone similar to him—to ensure they understand the proper way to use robes, food, and lodgings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato">SN 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when a monk is content with whatever he receives, he uses those items while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato"><b>seeing the danger</b></a> in them, which prevents the arising of unwholesome states of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato">SN 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once monks have been exhorted by a model of contentment like Mahā Kassapa, they should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato"><b>practice accordingly</b></a> to fulfill the training in being satisfied with simple requisites? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.1/en/sujato">SN 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta taught that a monk who is not ardent and lacks scrupulousness is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>incapable of enlightenment</b></a> and incapable of reaching Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Sāriputta, being both ardent and scrupulous makes a practitioner <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>capable of attaining</b></a> the supreme security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta defined a monk as "not ardent" if he fails to exert ardor to prevent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>unarisen evil unwholesome states</b></a> from arising, which would otherwise lead to his harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained that a monk is "unscrupulous" if he does not feel a sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>dread of wrongdoing</b></a> regarding the possibility of unarisen evil states taking root in his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that a monk lacks ardor if he does not strive to ensure that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>arisen evil unwholesome states</b></a> are abandoned, acknowledging that their presence leads to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta described a monk as "unscrupulous" if he lacks concern or dread regarding the fact that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>arisen unwholesome states</b></a>, if not abandoned, will continue to lead to his detriment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta stated a monk is "not ardent" when he does not make an effort toward the goal of having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>unarisen wholesome states</b></a> arise within him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained that a monk is "not ardent" if he does not exert himself to prevent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>arisen wholesome states</b></a> from disappearing or ceasing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta defined an "ardent" monk as one who specifically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>exerts ardor</b></a> to ensure that unarisen evil unwholesome states do not arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that a "scrupulous" monk is one who feels a healthy <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>fear of the consequences</b></a> of unwholesome states, which motivates him to prevent their growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta identified a monk as "ardent" if he makes an active effort to ensure that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states arise</b></a> and that those already arisen do not vanish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained that "scrupulousness" involves feeling dread at the thought of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states ceasing</b></a>, as their disappearance would be to one's own disadvantage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the conversation between Sāriputta and Mahā Kassapa regarding these spiritual qualities took place at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>Deer Park in Isipatana</b></a> near Benares? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta emphasized that being ardent and scrupulous is the essential foundation that makes one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato"><b>capable of Nibbāna</b></a> and the final goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.2/en/sujato">SN 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to approach families <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>like the moon</b></a>, drawing back their body and mind while remaining forever like a newcomer who is never obtrusive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a person looking down into an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>old well</b></a> or a steep mountain gorge to describe the level of restraint a monk should have when approaching lay families? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Mahā Kassapa as an ideal model because he approaches families <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>drawing back his body</b></a> and mind, never becoming over-familiar or bold in his interactions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk’s mind should not be stuck, caught, or bound when visiting families, comparing this mental freedom to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>hand moving in the air</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a monk is fit to visit families if he simply thinks, "Let those who want gain, gain; let those who want merit, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>do merit</b></a>," without personal attachment to the outcome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered a monk worthy of visiting families if he is just as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>glad and happy</b></a> when other people receive gains as he is when he receives something for himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified Mahā Kassapa as a monk whose mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>not attached</b></a> or bound when visiting families, maintaining the same joy for others' success as for his own? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a Dhamma teaching as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>impure</b></a> if the monk gives it with the hope that the audience will become confident in him and show him their confidence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk provides a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>pure Dhamma teaching</b></a> when he speaks solely because the Dhamma is well-expounded and out of a sense of compassion and sympathy for others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a pure teacher primarily wishes for his listeners to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>understand the Dhamma</b></a> and practice for the sake of the truth after hearing it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>to be personally realized</b></a> by the wise, which is the standard a pure monk uses when deciding to teach others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Mahā Kassapa for teaching the Dhamma out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>compassion and kindness</b></a>, rather than for personal gain or recognition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>exhort</b></a> the Sangha through Mahā Kassapa—or someone like him—and that monks should practice according to such a model? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>forever a newcomer</b></a> to describe the ideal attitude of a monk who visits families without becoming overly social or informal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk's teaching is pure when it is motivated by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>sympathy</b></a>, seeking only the welfare of those who hear the well-expounded truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an improper motive for teaching is the desire for the audience to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>be confident</b></a> in the teacher specifically? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the danger of losing one's restraint in social settings to falling into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>river gorge</b></a>, emphasizing the need for a monk to draw back his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>visible here and now</b></a> and timeless, qualities that a pure monk reflects upon before teaching others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha wanted his disciples to train themselves so that their minds would not be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>bound or caught</b></a> by the families they serve? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha held Mahā Kassapa up as the standard for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato"><b>purity of teaching</b></a> because Kassapa's only concern was the listener's understanding and practice of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.3/en/sujato">SN 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>unfit to visit families</b></a> if he visits them with a mind full of expectations regarding the offerings he might receive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a monk as unfit for visiting families if he becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>vexed</b></a> and experiences suffering when people fail to give him anything at all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk lacks the proper qualities for visiting families if he experiences <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>suffering and displeasure</b></a> because a family gave him only a little when he expected much? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk is unfit to associate with families if he becomes mentally disturbed when they provide <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>coarse offerings</b></a> instead of the choice ones he desired? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is unfit to visit families if he becomes vexed when people give to him <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>slowly</b></a> rather than quickly as he had hoped? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk is not worthy of visiting families if he feels displeasure because an offering was given to him <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>carelessly</b></a> instead of with care? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>fit to visit families</b></a> as one who maintains an equanimous mind, thinking, "How could it be obtained here in other families—that they give to me and not 'not give'?" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk worthy of visiting families is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>not vexed</b></a> and does not experience suffering even when he receives no offerings from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as fit for social visits if he remains untroubled by receiving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>little</b></a> instead of much, understanding that giving depends entirely on the family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is spiritually prepared to visit families if receiving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>coarse food</b></a> instead of choice food does not lead to any mental suffering or displeasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is not vexed when families give <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>slowly</b></a> is a monk who truly deserves to be one who visits families? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a monk as fit for visiting families if he remains at peace even when an offering is given <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>without care</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha held up <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>Mahā Kassapa</b></a> as the ideal example of a monk who visits families with a mind free from expectations and vexation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that Mahā Kassapa does not experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>suffering and displeasure</b></a> even when families fail to give him offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Mahā Kassapa for not being vexed when he receives <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>coarse food</b></a> or when offerings are given to him slowly or carelessly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the monks he would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>exhort</b></a> them through Mahā Kassapa—or someone like him—regarding the proper mindset for interacting with laypeople? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his disciples that once they have been exhorted with the example of a monk like Mahā Kassapa, they should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>practice accordingly</b></a> to achieve that same standard? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a monk's reaction to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato"><b>receiving choice food</b></a> versus coarse food as a specific criterion for determining their fitness to visit lay families? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.4/en/sujato">SN 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda once remarked that Ven. Sāriputta’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.1/en/sujato"><b>faculties were serene</b></a> and his complexion was pure and bright because he had spent the day in deep meditative dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.1/en/sujato">SN 28.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even while entering a high meditative state, Ven. Sāriputta stated he never had the thought, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.1/en/sujato"><b>I am attaining</b></a> the first jhāna," or that he had already attained it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.1/en/sujato">SN 28.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda concluded that Ven. Sāriputta was free from self-referential thoughts during meditation because his underlying tendencies toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.1/en/sujato"><b>I-making and mine-making</b></a> had been rooted out for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.1/en/sujato">SN 28.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Ānanda, Ven. Sāriputta did not experience conceit regarding his meditation because his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.1/en/sujato"><b>tendency to conceit</b></a> had been thoroughly destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.1/en/sujato">SN 28.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the spiritual growth of a monk to the way <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato"><b>Nagas in the Himalayas</b></a> grow their bodies and gain strength in small lakes before eventually reaching the great ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato">SN 46.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can only reach <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato"><b>greatness and expansiveness</b></a> in wholesome states by being firmly established in virtue while developing the seven factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato">SN 46.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato"><b>supported by virtue</b></a> is the necessary foundation for a monk to cultivate and make much of the seven factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato">SN 46.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato"><b>factor of mindfulness</b></a> as a quality that must be developed based on seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, eventually ripening in release? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato">SN 46.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato"><b>investigation of states</b></a> as one of the seven enlightenment factors that a monk develops to attain spiritual vastness, similar to a Naga reaching the great ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato">SN 46.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta would typically spend his "day-abiding" meditation under a tree in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.1/en/sujato"><b>Andhavana forest</b></a> after finishing his alms round in Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.1/en/sujato">SN 28.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato"><b>factor of energy</b></a> must be cultivated by a monk to reach the culmination and fulfillment of the seven factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato">SN 46.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato"><b>factor of rapture</b></a> is developed by a monk through a process that leads to the relinquishment of defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato">SN 46.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato"><b>factor of tranquility</b></a> as a specific enlightenment factor that, when made much of, brings a monk to greatness in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato">SN 46.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato"><b>factor of concentration</b></a> as a state that a monk develops while leaning toward seclusion and ripening in total release? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato">SN 46.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato"><b>factor of equanimity</b></a> is developed by a monk as the final of the seven factors of enlightenment, leading to spiritual expansiveness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.1/en/sujato">SN 46.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta achieved his serene state by being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.1/en/sujato"><b>secluded from sensual pleasures</b></a> and secluded from unwholesome states during his meditation in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.1/en/sujato">SN 28.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once stayed in Hatthigāma among the Vajjīs and explained to Ugga the householder that there are specific causes and conditions why some beings <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.125/en/sujato"><b>attain Nibbāna in this life</b></a> while others do not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.125/en/sujato">SN 35.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the householder Ugga asked about the reasons for spiritual success, the Buddha confirmed that there is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.125/en/sujato"><b>specific cause</b></a> and a specific condition for beings to attain final liberation in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.125/en/sujato">SN 35.125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while observing a large log floating down the Ganges River at Kimilā, the Buddha taught Ven. Kimila that a monk who commits a corrupt offense from which there is no rehabilitation is characterized by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.242/en/sujato"><b>inner rottenness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.242/en/sujato">SN 35.242</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.242/en/sujato"><b>inner corruption</b></a> in a monastic context as the commission of a defiled offense where no sign of spiritual emergence or restoration is evident? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.242/en/sujato">SN 35.242</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during a teaching to Ven. Kimila, the Buddha used the metaphor of a log to illustrate that a monk in a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.242/en/sujato"><b>corrupt offense</b></a> will fail to reach the spiritual "ocean" because he is rotten within? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.242/en/sujato">SN 35.242</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Khema observed that foolish people of little wisdom behave like enemies to themselves when they perform <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.22/en/sujato"><b>evil deeds</b></a> that are destined to produce bitter results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.22/en/sujato">SN 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Khema taught that a deed is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.22/en/sujato"><b>not well done</b></a> if its performance causes the doer to later repent and experience the consequences with a tearful face and weeping? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.22/en/sujato">SN 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the deva Khema, a deed is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.22/en/sujato"><b>well done</b></a> when it leads to no future remorse and the person can experience its results with a delighted and happy mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.22/en/sujato">SN 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Khema urged individuals to act with foresight and do what they know to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.22/en/sujato"><b>beneficial to themselves</b></a>, rather than moving forward without wise consideration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.22/en/sujato">SN 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Khema used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.22/en/sujato"><b>cart-driver</b></a> who leaves the smooth main road for a rough path to illustrate the distress one feels when they abandon the Dhamma to follow unrighteousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.22/en/sujato">SN 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Khema warned that those who stray from the Dhamma will eventually fall into the "mouth of Death," lamenting in the same way a driver does when his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.22/en/sujato"><b>axle breaks</b></a> on a rough road? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.22/en/sujato">SN 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.44/en/sujato"><b>Destination</b></a> (Parāyana) as the state achieved through the total destruction of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.44/en/sujato">SN 43.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.44/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> as the specific path that leads directly to the final Destination of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.44/en/sujato">SN 43.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that he had fulfilled his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.44/en/sujato"><b>duty as a teacher</b></a> by showing his disciples the path out of compassion and a desire for their long-term welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.44/en/sujato">SN 43.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his disciples to seek out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.44/en/sujato"><b>roots of trees</b></a> and empty huts to practice meditation so they would not have to suffer from regret in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.44/en/sujato">SN 43.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha issued a stern instruction to his monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.44/en/sujato"><b>meditate</b></a> and avoid being negligent, so as to ensure they do not become remorseful later? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.44/en/sujato">SN 43.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.44/en/sujato"><b>destruction of lust</b></a>, together with the destruction of hatred and delusion, constitutes the ultimate goal known as the Destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.44/en/sujato">SN 43.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that anyone who delights in visual forms, sounds, odors, tastes, or tactile objects actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.20/en/sujato"><b>delights in suffering</b></a>, and that such a person is not freed from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.20/en/sujato">SN 35.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the key to being freed from suffering is to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.20/en/sujato"><b>not delight in</b></a> the six types of sensory objects, including mental phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.20/en/sujato">SN 35.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a concept known as "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.23/en/sujato"><b>the All</b></a>," which consists solely of the six internal sense bases and their six corresponding external objects, such as the eye and forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.23/en/sujato">SN 35.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that anyone who tries to propose an "All" outside of the twelve sense bases would be engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.23/en/sujato"><b>empty speech</b></a> and would be unable to answer questions about it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.23/en/sujato">SN 35.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that attempting to find a reality beyond the senses and their objects leads only to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.23/en/sujato"><b>vexation</b></a> because such a thing lies outside of one's possible range of experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.23/en/sujato">SN 35.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying in the Magadhan village of Pañcasālā, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato"><b>Mara the Evil One</b></a> possessed the brahmin householders to ensure the Buddha would not receive any alms-food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato">SN 4.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the Buddha was forced to leave a village with an empty bowl, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato"><b>Mara the Evil One</b></a> approached him and mockingly asked if he had obtained any food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato">SN 4.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha realized he had been blocked from receiving alms, he told Mara that the Evil One had produced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato"><b>demerit</b></a> by attacking the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato">SN 4.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite having no food, the Buddha declared that he and his followers live in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato"><b>great happiness</b></a> because they possess nothing of their own? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato">SN 4.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the spiritual joy of an enlightened person to that of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato"><b>Radiant Devas</b></a> (Ābhassara gods), who do not need material food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato">SN 4.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha famously stated that even without physical sustenance, those who are liberated shall <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato"><b>feed on rapture</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato">SN 4.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato"><b>Mara the Evil One</b></a> sarcastically suggested that the Buddha should enter the village of Pañcasālā a second time, intending to cause the villagers to mock him again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato">SN 4.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that once <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato"><b>Mara the Evil One</b></a> realized the Buddha had recognized him and his malicious intent, he became miserable and immediately disappeared? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.18/en/sujato">SN 4.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mind and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.23/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> are considered part of "the All," meaning there is no experience that occurs outside the scope of the six senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.23/en/sujato">SN 35.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when King Pasenadi of Kosala asked the Buddha if he claimed to have attained supreme enlightenment, the Buddha explicitly confirmed that he had indeed reached <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>supreme enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi initially questioned the Buddha's claim to enlightenment because he perceived the Buddha as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>young in age</b></a> and relatively new to the monkhood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are four specific things that should not be looked down upon or despised merely because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>small or young</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi pointed out that famous elder teachers like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>Purana Kassapa</b></a>, Makkhali Gosala, and Nigantha Nataputta did not claim to have attained supreme enlightenment despite their seniority and large followings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>noble youth</b></a> should not be despised, as such a person might eventually gain royal power and severely punish those who offended them in their youth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one should never underestimate a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>young serpent</b></a>, because even a small snake possesses a powerful bite that can be fatal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>small fire</b></a> should never be disregarded, as it can quickly find fuel and grow into a massive blaze that consumes forests and property? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>young monk</b></a> should not be looked down upon, as the spiritual power of a virtuous practitioner is formidable regardless of their age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that if a person offends a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>virtuous monk</b></a>, the offender may suffer the loss of their wealth, their family, and their heirs, becoming like the stump of a palm tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>fiery power</b></a> to describe the spiritual potency of a monk's virtue, comparing its impact to the destructive force of a forest fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person, seeking their own welfare, should always show proper respect to a serpent, a fire, a noble, and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>virtuous monk</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's teaching, King Pasenadi declared that the Buddha had made the Dhamma clear, as if he were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>revealing what was hidden</b></a> or showing the way to someone who was lost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi was so impressed by the Buddha’s discourse that he immediately went for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>refuge</b></a> in the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi requested the Buddha to accept him as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a> from that day forward for as long as he lived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a king's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>royal punishment</b></a> can be fierce when he is angry, which is why a person should avoid despising even a young prince to protect their own life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described fire as having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>black trail</b></a>, noting how it can suddenly grow large and burn both men and women who treat it lightly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted a forest fire, after which shoots can grow back, with the spiritual fire of a monk, which can leave a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>without heirs</b></a> or inheritance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>lamp in the darkness</b></a> to describe how the Buddha’s teaching allows those with eyes to see the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s discourse on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato"><b>four small things</b></a> highlights that one should not judge the importance or power of a person or thing solely by its age or size? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.1/en/sujato">SN 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when King Pasenadi of Kosala asked about internal qualities that lead to harm, the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.2/en/sujato"><b>greed, hatred, and delusion</b></a> as the three things that arise within a person to their own suffering and discomfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.2/en/sujato">SN 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the way greed, hatred, and delusion harm a person to how a plant is destroyed by its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.2/en/sujato"><b>own fruit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.2/en/sujato">SN 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that these harmful mental states of greed, hatred, and delusion do not come from the outside but are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.2/en/sujato"><b>born from within</b></a> the individual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.2/en/sujato">SN 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to King Pasenadi that greed, hatred, and delusion specifically overwhelm and harm a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.2/en/sujato"><b>person of evil mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.2/en/sujato">SN 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when King Pasenadi of Kosala inquired if there is anything for one who has been born other than aging and death, the Buddha clarified that for every born being, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato"><b>nothing else</b></a> but aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato">SN 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even wealthy <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato"><b>khattiyas and brahmins</b></a> with vast treasures of gold, silver, and property cannot escape the inevitability of aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato">SN 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato"><b>arahants</b></a>—those who have destroyed all taints and attained liberation—the physical body is still subject to breaking up and being laid down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato">SN 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato"><b>ornate royal chariots</b></a> wearing out to illustrate that the physical body, no matter how well-tended, must eventually reach old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato">SN 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the physical body is destined to decay, the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma of the good</b></a> (satañca dhammo) is the only thing that does not reach old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato">SN 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the body of even the most spiritually advanced practitioners as having a nature characterized by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato"><b>breaking up</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato">SN 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed King Pasenadi that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato"><b>abundant wealth</b></a> and consumer goods provides no protection or alternative path away from the reality of aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato">SN 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the law of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a> applies universally to all, including great householders, kings, and those who have reached the end of the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.3/en/sujato">SN 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala once informed the Buddha about a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>wealthy merchant</b></a> in Savatthi who died childless, resulting in his massive fortune of hundreds of thousands of gold pieces being forfeited to the royal treasury? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi described a merchant who, despite his immense wealth, lived a life of extreme austerity, eating only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>broken rice</b></a> and sour gruel? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a merchant’s past act of providing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> to a Paccekabuddha named Taggarasikhi resulted in him being reborn seven times in the heavenly world and seven times as a wealthy merchant in Savatthi? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that because a wealthy merchant later <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>regretted</b></a> his gift of alms, thinking his servants should have eaten the food instead, his mind in later lives was unable to enjoy fine food, clothing, or luxury? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told King Pasenadi that the merchant had once murdered his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>brother's only son</b></a> for the sake of an inheritance, an evil deed for which he suffered in hell for many hundreds of thousands of years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the merchant's current state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>childless</b></a>—which led to his wealth being seized by the king upon his death—was a lingering kammic result of murdering his nephew in a past life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned King Pasenadi that because the merchant had exhausted his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>old merit</b></a> and failed to accumulate any new merit, he was reborn in the Mahāroruva hell immediately after his death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that material possessions such as grain, wealth, and silver are all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>left behind</b></a> at death, as none of these can be taken with a person to the next world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the only things a person truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>owns</b></a> and takes with them after death are the actions they performed through their body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>shadow</b></a> that never departs to describe how a person's good and evil deeds follow them into their future existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi noted that a millionaire merchant in Savatthi chose to wear only coarse <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>hempen garments</b></a> made of three pieces, despite having the means to afford the finest clothes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi observed that the wealthy but miserly merchant traveled in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>rickety chariot</b></a> and used a leaf as an awning for shade? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the specific Paccekabuddha who received the merchant's fateful gift as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>Taggarasikhi</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a merchant's physical body broke up, his specific destination due to his actions was the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>Mahāroruva hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the performance of good deeds because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato"><b>merit</b></a> is the only true support for living beings as they transition into the future world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.20/en/sujato">SN 3.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted a time when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato"><b>asuras</b></a> (titans) attacked the devas, leading Sakka, the Lord of the Gods, to call upon the young god Suvīra to go out and meet them in battle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato">SN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how the young god Suvīra, despite agreeing to Sakka's command to defend the gods, fell into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato"><b>negligence</b></a> (pamāda) three separate times instead of taking action? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato">SN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Gods, sarcastically told the idle god Suvīra that if he knew of a place where one could find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a> without exerting any effort or undertaking any task, Suvīra should go there and take Sakka with him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato">SN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told of the god Suvīra's request to Sakka to be shown a state where one is idle and does no work, yet lives with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato"><b>all wishes fulfilled</b></a> and is free from sorrow and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato">SN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Gods, told Suvīra that if there truly existed a way to live without working, that would indeed be the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato"><b>path to Nibbāna</b></a>, and Sakka himself would want to be led there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato">SN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted how Sakka, the Lord of the Gods, even while living off the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato"><b>fruit of his merit</b></a> (sakaṃ puññaphalaṃ upajīvamāno) and ruling over the Tavatiṃsa devas, still acted as a commender of effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato">SN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that Sakka, despite his immense celestial power, is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato"><b>commender of arousing energy</b></a> (uṭṭhānavīriyassa vaṇṇavādī), emphasizing the importance of diligence even for the king of gods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato">SN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of Sakka’s valuing of effort to exhort monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato"><b>strive and exert themselves</b></a> within the well-expounded Dhamma-Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato">SN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who have gone forth should put forth effort for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato"><b>attaining the unattained</b></a>, reaching the unreached, and realizing the unrealized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato">SN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Sakka, the Lord of the Gods, as exercising <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato"><b>sovereignty and lordship</b></a> over the Tavatiṃsa devas, yet Sakka still insists on the necessity of work and energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.1/en/sujato">SN 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.21/en/sujato"><b>slaying anger</b></a>, a person is able to sleep in comfort and live without any sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.21/en/sujato">SN 11.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described anger as a state with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.21/en/sujato"><b>poisoned root</b></a> and a honeyed tip, the destruction of which is highly praised by the Noble Ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.21/en/sujato">SN 11.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Sakka, the Lord of the Gods, asked what single thing should be killed to attain happiness, the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.21/en/sujato"><b>anger</b></a> as the only thing whose slaying leads to no regret? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.21/en/sujato">SN 11.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted a story where a small, ugly, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato"><b>dwarfish yakkha</b></a> once sat directly upon the throne of Sakka, the Lord of the Gods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato">SN 11.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a mysterious yakkha who became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato"><b>more handsome and attractive</b></a> every time the Tavatiṃsa gods grew more annoyed and irritated by his presence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato">SN 11.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the strange being sitting on Sakka’s throne was an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato"><b>anger-eating yakkha</b></a> who literally fed on the resentment and fury of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato">SN 11.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told how Sakka, the Lord of the Gods, successfully banished an "anger-eating" yakkha by showing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato"><b>humility and respect</b></a> instead of reacting with hostility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato">SN 11.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Sakka approaching the "anger-eating" yakkha, adjusting his robe, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato"><b>announcing his name</b></a> three times with his hands joined in reverential salutation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato">SN 11.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, as Sakka showed more and more humble respect, the "anger-eating" yakkha became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato"><b>uglier and more dwarfish</b></a> until he finally vanished right there on the spot? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato">SN 11.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Gods, declared that his mind is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato"><b>easily overwhelmed</b></a> by external circumstances and that he is not easily led into anger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato">SN 11.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Gods, stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato"><b>anger does not persist</b></a> in him for a long time, signifying his practice of letting go of resentment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato">SN 11.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Gods, claimed that even when he is angry, he never <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato"><b>speaks harsh words</b></a> or says anything that would violate the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato">SN 11.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Gods, explained that he practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato"><b>self-restraint</b></a> (sanniggaṇhāmi attānaṃ) because he keeps his own long-term welfare and purpose in mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato">SN 11.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of Sakka to teach that even the King of the Gods avoids <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato"><b>disturbing his own mind</b></a> with anger, despite having the power to be wrathful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.22/en/sujato">SN 11.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two kinds of fools: one who fails to see a transgression as a transgression, and one who does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato"><b>rightly accept the confession</b></a> of another person who has admitted their fault? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato">SN 11.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined two kinds of wise people: one who recognizes a transgression as a transgression, and one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato"><b>rightly accepts the confession</b></a> of another in accordance with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato">SN 11.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how Sakka, Lord of the Gods, urged the Tāvatiṃsa devas to never let <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato"><b>anger</b></a> come under their control and to ensure that they do not drift apart from their friends? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato">SN 11.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared a verse by Sakka, Lord of the Gods, warning that a person should never blame someone who is blameless nor should they ever speak <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a> (slander) against others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato">SN 11.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato"><b>anger</b></a> crushes an evil person just as a mountain of stone crushes those beneath it, citing a teaching originally given by Sakka to the assembly of gods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato">SN 11.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once addressed a specific conflict where one monk confessed a transgression to another, but the second monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato"><b>refused to accept</b></a> the apology, prompting a lesson on the behavior of fools versus the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato">SN 11.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted Sakka, Lord of the Gods, advising the devas in the Sudhamma assembly hall that they should not let anger overcome them and should specifically avoid <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.25/en/sujato"><b>getting angry</b></a> with those who are already angry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.25/en/sujato">SN 11.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how Sakka, Lord of the Gods, praised <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.25/en/sujato"><b>non-anger and harmlessness</b></a> (akkodha and avihiṃsā) as the specific path of practice that characterizes the Noble Ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.25/en/sujato">SN 11.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how Sakka, Lord of the Gods, sought to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato"><b>conciliate the devas</b></a> of the Tāvatiṃsa heaven by reciting verses that emphasize the importance of maintaining harmony and restraining one's temper? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.24/en/sujato">SN 11.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.25/en/sujato"><b>non-harming</b></a> (avihiṃsā) is a fundamental quality of the Noble Ones, as illustrated in the ancient instructions Sakka gave to the celestial hosts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.25/en/sujato">SN 11.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato"><b>ten powers</b></a> and four kinds of self-confidence, which allow him to roar his "lion's roar" in assemblies and set the supreme "Brahma-wheel" in motion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato">SN 12.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha sets the "Brahma-wheel" in motion, he explains the specific nature, origin, and passing away of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a>, which consist of form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato">SN 12.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the fundamental principle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato"><b>specific conditionality</b></a>, stating that "when this exists, that comes to be," and "with the arising of this, that arises"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato">SN 12.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the entire mass of suffering arises through a chain of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a> starting with ignorance, which then conditions volitional formations, consciousness, and the rest of the twelve links? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato">SN 12.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his well-expounded Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato"><b>open and manifest</b></a>, clear and "with its patchwork stripped away," making it a sufficient basis for a person of faith to begin their spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato">SN 12.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his disciples to arouse energy by resolving that even if their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato"><b>flesh and blood</b></a> should dry up, leaving only skin, sinews, and bones, they would not stop until they attained what can be reached by manly strength and persistence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato">SN 12.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato"><b>lazy person</b></a> lives in suffering, is frequently "mixed with evil unwholesome states," and misses out on achieving the "great good" of spiritual realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato">SN 12.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> dwells happily, lives secluded from evil unwholesome states, and is capable of fulfilling the "great good" or final goal of the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato">SN 12.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the "supreme" state of enlightenment is never reached by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato"><b>lowly means</b></a>, but rather that the supreme is only attained through supreme effort and practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato">SN 12.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> to a "drink of cream" that is offered when a Teacher is present, urging practitioners to strive for the realization of what they have not yet achieved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato">SN 12.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to train themselves so that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato"><b>requisites</b></a> they use—such as robes, almsfood, and medicine—would yield great fruit and benefit for the donors who provided them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato">SN 12.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a> (appamāda) is the single quality that is sufficient to fulfill one’s own good, the good of others, and the good of both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.22/en/sujato">SN 12.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the destruction of taints is achieved only by one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>knows and sees</b></a>, and not by one who does not know and does not see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that to destroy the taints, one must specifically see the arising and passing away of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a>: form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that even the final knowledge of the destruction of the taints does not arise without a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>proximate cause</b></a> (upanisā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> as the necessary proximate cause that leads to the arising of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> (saddhā) is the proximate cause that leads to the arising of gladness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>gladness</b></a> (pāmojja) as the proximate cause that allows a practitioner to experience rapture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>rapture</b></a> (pīti) is the proximate cause that leads to physical and mental tranquillity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>tranquillity</b></a> (passaddhi) is the proximate cause that results in the experience of happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a> (sukha) is the proximate cause that enables the mind to achieve concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> (samādhi) is the proximate cause for the arising of the knowledge and vision of things as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of things as they really are</b></a> (yathābhūtañāṇadassana) as the proximate cause for the development of revulsion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>revulsion</b></a> (nibbidā) is the proximate cause that leads naturally to dispassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> (virāga) is the proximate cause for attaining liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a> (vimutti) is the final proximate cause required for the knowledge of the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>mountain rain</b></a>—which fills the clefts, then the pools, then the rivers, and finally the great ocean—to illustrate how each stage of the path gradually fulfills the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> (avijjā) as the primary proximate cause that starts the chain leading through volitional formations and consciousness to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> (saṅkhārā) arise with ignorance as their proximate cause? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a> (jāti) is the proximate cause for the arising of the entire mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> (upādāna) is the proximate cause that leads to existence (bhava)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a> and its transcendence to a flow of water that eventually fills the "great ocean" of liberation and knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.23/en/sujato">SN 12.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Bhūmija approached the Venerable Sāriputta to ask how the Buddha would respond to the various theories held by other ascetics and brahmins regarding the origin of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>pleasure and pain</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that some ascetics and brahmins, as noted by Bhūmija, advocate the view that pleasure and pain are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>self-wrought</b></a>, meaning they are created entirely by one's own actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhūmija identified a group of teachers who maintain that pleasure and pain are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>wrought by another</b></a>, implying that external forces or beings are responsible for one's experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Bhūmija, some thinkers propose that pleasure and pain are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>wrought by both</b></a> oneself and another simultaneously? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhūmija described a fourth group of teachers who believe pleasure and pain are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>fortuitously arisen</b></a>, occurring by chance without being created by oneself or others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta clarified that the Buddha describes pleasure and pain as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>dependently arisen</b></a> rather than resulting from the four traditional theories of creation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained that pleasure and pain arise specifically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>dependent on contact</b></a> (phassa), and that without this contact, these feelings cannot be experienced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta argued that even those teachers who believe pleasure and pain are self-wrought or fortuitously arisen are actually experiencing those states <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>through contact</b></a>, whether they realize it or not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Venerable Ānanda reported this discussion to the Buddha, the Buddha praised Sāriputta's answer as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>correct and consistent</b></a> with his own teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that internal pleasure and pain arise because there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>bodily volition</b></a> (kāyasañcetanā), which acts as a cause for these feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>verbal volition</b></a> (vacīsañcetanā) as a condition that leads to the internal experience of pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that internal pleasure and pain can arise through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>mental volition</b></a> (manosañcetanā), and that this entire process is rooted in ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a person might <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>himself prepare</b></a> a bodily formation that results in his own internal pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>others might prompt</b></a> the bodily formation that causes a person to experience internal pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one can prepare a bodily, verbal, or mental formation <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>deliberately</b></a> (sampajāno), leading to the subsequent experience of pleasure or pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that formations can also be prepared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>undeliberately</b></a> (asampajāno), yet they still function as conditions for pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> (avijjā) is "run along with" or underlies all these various ways that formations are prepared? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that with the remainderless fading away and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>nirodha of ignorance</b></a>, the very body, speech, and mind that condition pleasure and pain are no longer present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>field</b></a> (khetta) to illustrate that when ignorance ceases, the productive ground for the arising of pleasure and pain is gone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once ignorance is removed, there is no longer a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>basis</b></a> (vatthu) upon which internal pleasure and pain can be established? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>objective</b></a> (āyatana) or sphere for the arising of pleasure and pain ceases to exist when ignorance is fully eradicated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato"><b>locus</b></a> (adhikaraṇa) or starting point for the production of pleasure and pain is eliminated through the cessation of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.25/en/sujato">SN 12.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once observed that Venerable Mahākassapa had become old and that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>hempen rag robes</b></a> were heavy and worn out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested to the elderly Venerable Mahākassapa that he should wear <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>robes given by householders</b></a> and accept meal invitations rather than continuing his strict ascetic practices? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his advanced age, Venerable Mahākassapa told the Buddha he had long been a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>forest dweller</b></a> and a consistent advocate for the forest-dwelling lifestyle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa explained to the Buddha that he remained an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>almsfood eater</b></a> because he valued and praised the practice of subsisting only on food gathered during alms rounds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa preferred being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>rag-robe wearer</b></a> and told the Buddha that he had spent a long time praising the virtue of wearing robes made from cast-off rags? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha questioned his lifestyle, Venerable Mahākassapa emphasized his commitment to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>fewness of wishes</b></a> and the importance of praising this quality in others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa identified himself to the Buddha as one who was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>contented</b></a> with his basic requisites and who spoke in praise of such contentment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa informed the Buddha that he had long lived <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>secluded</b></a> from the crowd and had consistently praised the practice of physical and mental seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa told the Buddha he valued being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>aloof from society</b></a> and had long spoken in praise of maintaining such independence from social entanglements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa explained to the Buddha that he practiced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>arousal of energy</b></a> and frequently praised the energetic pursuit of the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked why he maintained his rigorous ascetic habits, Venerable Mahākassapa told the Buddha he did so for his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwelling</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa told the Buddha he continued his strict asceticism out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>compassion for future generations</b></a>, hoping to set a standard for those who would follow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa believed that if future generations saw that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>enlightened disciples</b></a> of the Buddha were forest dwellers and rag-robe wearers, they would be inspired to practice the same way for their own long-term happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highly commended Venerable Mahākassapa, stating that he was acting for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>welfare of many</b></a> and out of compassion for the world of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing Venerable Mahākassapa’s reasons for his asceticism, the Buddha ultimately encouraged him to continue to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato"><b>dwell in the forest</b></a> and to wear his rag robes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.5/en/sujato">SN 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once invited Venerable Mahākassapa to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>exhort the bhikkhus</b></a> or give them a Dhamma talk, stating that either he or Mahākassapa should be the ones to instruct the monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa once declined the Buddha's request to teach because he felt that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>bhikkhus were difficult to speak to</b></a> and possessed qualities that made them hard to admonish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa informed the Buddha that contemporary monks were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>impatient</b></a> and did not accept instruction in a way that was respectful or circumspect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa reported seeing two monks, Bhaṇḍa and Abhijika, engaging in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>verbal competition</b></a> to see who could speak more or better regarding their learning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhaṇḍa, a monk who was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>pupil of Venerable Ānanda</b></a>, was called out by Venerable Mahākassapa for competing with another monk over who was the superior speaker? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Abhijika, who was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>pupil of Venerable Anuruddha</b></a>, was observed by Venerable Mahākassapa challenging a fellow monk to see who could speak the longest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing Mahākassapa's report, the Buddha summoned the two monks and asked if it was true they were competing to see who could <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>speak more beautifully</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked the competing monks if they had ever understood him to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>taught the Dhamma</b></a> for the purpose of challenging one another over their learning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to monks who competed over their speaking abilities as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>misguided men</b></a> for acting improperly within a well-expounded discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned the monks on what they could have possibly seen or known that led them to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>out-talk each other</b></a> after having gone forth in his teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon being rebuked by the Buddha, the two monks Bhaṇḍa and Abhijika prostrated at his feet and admitted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>a transgression had overcome them</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks who had been competing confessed to the Buddha that they had acted like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>fools</b></a>, being confused and unskillful in their behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that it was indeed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>a fault</b></a> for monks to compete over who could speak more or better in a discipline that is well-expounded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>growth in the Noble One’s Discipline</b></a> occurs when one sees their transgression as a transgression and makes amends for it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha accepted the apology of the monks once they acknowledged their mistake and resolved to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>restraint in the future</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that seeing one's error and making <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>amends in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a> is the proper way to progress spiritually? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa identified that monks who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>difficult to admonish</b></a> often lack the ability to take advice in a circumspect manner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>moghapurisā</b></a> (misguided men) to describe those who used their knowledge of the Dhamma as a means for competitive speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, acknowledging a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>transgression as a transgression</b></a> is a fundamental requirement for making reparations and moving forward in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks Bhaṇḍa and Abhijika were specifically competing over their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato"><b>learning</b></a> (<i>suta</i>), trying to prove who was the most knowledgeable speaker? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.6/en/sujato">SN 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once requested Venerable Mahākassapa to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>exhort the bhikkhus</b></a> and give them a Dhamma talk, stating that either he or Mahākassapa should be the ones to instruct the monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa told the Buddha that contemporary monks were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>difficult to speak to</b></a> because they possessed qualities that made them hard to admonish and instruct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa observed that many monks were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>impatient</b></a> and did not accept instruction in a way that was respectful or circumspect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa taught that a person who lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> in wholesome states can only expect decline, never growth, whether by day or by night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahākassapa, the absence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>shame</b></a> regarding wholesome states leads a person to spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa explained that a person who lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>fear of wrongdoing</b></a> in wholesome states is destined for spiritual failure and decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa warned that an individual who lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>energy</b></a> in the pursuit of wholesome states will see those qualities diminish over time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa stated that spiritual decline is inevitable for any person who lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> regarding wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa compared the spiritual decline of a person lacking wholesome qualities to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>moon in the dark fortnight</b></a>, which wanes in beauty, size, and light? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa identified that being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>faithless person</b></a> is in itself a state of spiritual decline (<i>parihāna</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa taught that being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>shameless person</b></a> is a condition that leads to the failure of one's spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa and the Buddha both agreed that being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>lazy person</b></a> constitutes a state of decline within the Noble One’s Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa classified being an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>unwise person</b></a> as a primary factor that causes a person to fall away from wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa pointed out that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>angry and hostile</b></a> are qualities that represent a state of decline for a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa emphasized that when there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>no monks who give advice</b></a>, it is a sign of decline for the monastic community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa taught that a person who possesses faith, shame, fear of wrongdoing, energy, and wisdom can expect only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>growth</b></a> in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa compared the spiritual growth of a person possessing wholesome qualities to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>moon in the bright fortnight</b></a>, which increases in beauty, orbit, and light? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that possessing qualities like faith and wisdom is a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>non-decline</b></a> (<i>aparihāna</i>) for a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> in wholesome states will see those qualities wax and grow every day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato"><b>monks who act as exhorters</b></a> is a state of non-decline that supports the spiritual progress of the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.7/en/sujato">SN 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a specific Dhamma for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.60/en/sujato"><b>full understanding</b></a> of all clinging, which involves recognizing how contact and feeling arise from the meeting of the six internal and external sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.60/en/sujato">SN 35.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that eye-consciousness arises dependent on the eye and forms, and that the meeting of these three constitutes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.60/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.60/en/sujato">SN 35.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a well-taught noble disciple who sees the process of sensory experience develops <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.60/en/sujato"><b>revulsion</b></a> toward the eye, forms, consciousness, contact, and feeling, which leads to dispassion and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.60/en/sujato">SN 35.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when the mind and mental phenomena meet, mind-consciousness arises, and the union of these three elements is called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.60/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.60/en/sujato">SN 35.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that being liberated through dispassion allows a person to personally know that they have attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.60/en/sujato"><b>full understanding</b></a> of all clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.60/en/sujato">SN 35.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha advised his followers that the aggregate of form should be seen as it really is with correct wisdom as "this is not mine, this I am not, this is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.119/en/sujato"><b>not my self</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.119/en/sujato">SN 22.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that seeing all five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.119/en/sujato"><b>correct wisdom</b></a> leads to the understanding that there is no further state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.119/en/sujato">SN 22.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha asked his monks to contemplate whether they saw the aggregate of consciousness as "this is not mine, this I am not, this is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.119/en/sujato"><b>not my self</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.119/en/sujato">SN 22.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha distinguished between things that fetter and the fetter itself, explaining that the five aggregates are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.120/en/sujato"><b>things that fetter</b></a>, while the desire and lust for them is the actual fetter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.120/en/sujato">SN 22.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.120/en/sujato"><b>fetter</b></a> is specifically the desire and lust (chandarāga) that a person harbors toward the aggregates of form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.120/en/sujato">SN 22.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.120/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of perception</b></a> is considered a thing that fetters because it is an object toward which desire and lust can arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.120/en/sujato">SN 22.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three distinct types of feelings—pleasant feeling, painful feeling, and neither-pleasant-nor-painful feeling—and taught that every one of them is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato"><b>born of contact</b></a>, rooted in contact, and produced by contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato">SN 36.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a pleasant feeling arises only when there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato"><b>pleasant-feeling-producing contact</b></a>, and that this feeling will cease and subside the moment that specific contact is removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato">SN 36.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato"><b>painful feeling</b></a> depends entirely on painful-feeling-producing contact, and without the presence of that specific contact, the painful sensation cannot persist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato">SN 36.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, even a neutral sensation or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato"><b>neither-pleasant-nor-painful feeling</b></a> is a conditioned experience that arises based on its own specific contact and ceases when that contact disappears? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato">SN 36.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato"><b>simile of two pieces of wood</b></a> being rubbed together to generate heat and fire to illustrate how feelings are produced by the friction of contact and vanish when the contact is "unlocked" or separated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato">SN 36.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that all feelings are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato"><b>rooted in contact</b></a>, meaning they do not arise randomly but are always the result of a meeting between internal and external sensory bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato">SN 36.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by a monk what the "spiritual life" (brahmacariya) truly is, the Buddha defined it as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.6/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>, consisting of factors from Right View to Right Concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.6/en/sujato">SN 45.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the ultimate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.6/en/sujato"><b>consummation</b></a> or goal of the spiritual life is the total destruction of lust, the destruction of hatred, and the destruction of delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.6/en/sujato">SN 45.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha clarified that the phrase "the removal of lust, hatred, and delusion" is a specific designation for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.7/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna-element</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.7/en/sujato">SN 45.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the removal of lust, hatred, and delusion is essentially synonymous with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.7/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavas), which leads to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.7/en/sujato">SN 45.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified the state of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.7/en/sujato"><b>Deathless</b></a> (amata) as being nothing other than the total destruction of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.7/en/sujato">SN 45.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explicitly taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.7/en/sujato"><b>path to the Deathless</b></a> is the Noble Eightfold Path, which provides the framework for practitioners to reach the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.7/en/sujato">SN 45.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha responded to a monk's question about the term "spiritual life" by explaining that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.6/en/sujato"><b>Right View</b></a> is the first factor of the path that leads to the end of lust and hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.6/en/sujato">SN 45.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that once the specific contact produced by a certain condition ceases, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato"><b>corresponding feeling</b></a> that was dependent on it is said to "subside" and "pass away"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.10/en/sujato">SN 36.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.6/en/sujato"><b>spiritual life</b></a> as a journey that is completed only when one achieves the final destruction of the three poisons of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.6/en/sujato">SN 45.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.7/en/sujato"><b>removal of lust, hatred, and delusion</b></a> is the goal toward which all Eightfold Path factors, from Right View to Right Concentration, are directed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.7/en/sujato">SN 45.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once reflected while staying in a forest hut in the Himalayas on whether it was possible to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato"><b>exercise rulership</b></a> righteously, without killing, conquering, or causing grief to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato">SN 4.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One attempted to tempt the Buddha into worldly power by suggesting he use his mastery of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato"><b>four bases of power</b></a> to turn the entire Himalayan mountain range into solid gold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato">SN 4.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that even if a mountain were made of solid gold, it would still be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato"><b>insufficient for one</b></a> person's desires, highlighting the endless nature of human craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato">SN 4.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who has seen the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato"><b>source of suffering</b></a> would never incline toward sensual pleasures, as they understand the "acquisition" of worldly things to be a tangle in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato">SN 4.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato"><b>ruling righteously</b></a> as governing without inflicting harm, without conquering others, and without causing oneself or others to grieve? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato">SN 4.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared how he personally went in search of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato"><b>gratification</b></a>, the danger, and the escape regarding the five aggregates of clinging during his quest for enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato">SN 22.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that he only claimed to have attained full awakening once his knowledge and vision were clear regarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> and the escape from the five aggregates as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato">SN 22.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that he had thoroughly seen with wisdom the gratification, the danger, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato"><b>escape regarding form</b></a> (rūpa) before declaring his liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato">SN 22.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha investigated the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato"><b>danger in feeling</b></a> (vedanā) and perception (saññā) to gain an accurate understanding of how these mental processes contribute to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato">SN 22.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha declared that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato"><b>liberation is unshakeable</b></a> because he had discovered the escape from volitional formations (saṅkhārā) and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato">SN 22.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the understanding of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato"><b>escape from the aggregates</b></a> as the essential factor that allowed him to proclaim that there is no more renewed existence for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato">SN 22.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one should train for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato"><b>removal of acquisitions</b></a> (upadhi) after realizing that these attachments are the very source of suffering in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato">SN 4.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha achieved <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato"><b>unshakeable deliverance of mind</b></a> only after he had fully understood the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato">SN 22.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Mara the Evil One realized the Buddha had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato"><b>recognized his thoughts</b></a> regarding the suggestion of becoming a king, he became miserable and immediately disappeared? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.20/en/sujato">SN 4.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha proclaimed that this was his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato"><b>final birth</b></a> because he had completely understood the gratification, danger, and escape regarding the five aggregates of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.27/en/sujato">SN 22.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while warming his back in the late afternoon sun, explained to Ananda that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.41/en/sujato"><b>old age is inherent in youth</b></a>, sickness is inherent in health, and death is inherent in life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.41/en/sujato">SN 48.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s attendant, Ananda, once expressed shock at seeing the Buddha's physical decline, noting that the Buddha's skin was no longer clear and his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.41/en/sujato"><b>limbs were flaccid and wrinkled</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.41/en/sujato">SN 48.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the process of aging leads to a visible <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.41/en/sujato"><b>alteration in the faculties</b></a>, specifically affecting the eye, ear, nose, tongue, and body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.41/en/sujato">SN 48.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha uttered a verse lamenting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.41/en/sujato"><b>miserable old age</b></a>, describing it as "vile" because it ruins the beauty of the physical form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.41/en/sujato">SN 48.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that even if a person lives for a hundred years, they are still <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.41/en/sujato"><b>headed for death</b></a>, an end that spares nothing and crushes everyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.41/en/sujato">SN 48.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha told the monk Radha that to find liberation, one must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato"><b>abandon desire and lust</b></a> for whatever is "Mara"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato">SN 23.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the physical body or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato"><b>form as Mara</b></a>, instructing Radha that desire and lust for it must be completely discarded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato">SN 23.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined "Mara" as consisting of the five aggregates, teaching that feeling, perception, volitional formations, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato"><b>consciousness are Mara</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato">SN 23.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha advised Radha that practitioners should strive to remove their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato"><b>desire-and-lust</b></a> for anything that is of the nature of Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato">SN 23.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that because all five aggregates are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>, one should abandon all craving and attachment toward them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato">SN 23.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that desire and lust must be abandoned for anything that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato"><b>non-self</b></a>, as these things do not truly belong to a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato">SN 23.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the aggregate of form is essentially <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a>, and therefore one must let go of the "desire-and-lust" that binds them to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato">SN 23.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha instructed Radha to give up desire for anything that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato"><b>subject to destruction</b></a> (khayadhamma) and vanishing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato">SN 23.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that one should abandon attachment to anything <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato"><b>subject to arising</b></a> (samudayadhamma) to successfully live the spiritual life in solitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.23/en/sujato">SN 23.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.41/en/sujato"><b>physical appearance of his own body</b></a>, which had become bent forward and wrinkled, to demonstrate the inescapable reality of decay to his disciple Ananda? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.41/en/sujato">SN 48.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to King Pasenadi of Kosala that there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>four types of persons</b></a> existing in the world, categorized by whether they are moving from darkness to darkness, darkness to light, light to darkness, or light to light? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "dark-to-dark" person as someone born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>low family</b></a>—such as scavengers, hunters, or bamboo-workers—who is poor, physically deformed, and lacks basic necessities like food and clothing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person born into hardship who further engages in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>misconduct</b></a> of body, speech, and mind is considered to be heading from darkness toward even greater darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who moves from a difficult life of poverty into a rebirth in hell to a man who goes from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>darkness to darkness</b></a> or from one bloody stain to another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a "dark-to-light" person as someone born in a low and impoverished family who nevertheless practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>good conduct</b></a> in body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person born in a state of "darkness" who performs meritorious deeds will, after the breakup of the body, reach a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a man climbing from the ground to a couch, then to a horse, an elephant, and finally into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>palace</b></a> to describe the person who moves from a difficult birth to a happy destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "light-to-dark" person as someone born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>high family</b></a>—such as wealthy khattiyas or brahmins—who is handsome, influential, and has an abundance of wealth and pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person born into a life of "light" and privilege becomes "light-to-dark" if they give in to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>evil conduct</b></a> by misbehaving through body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even a person born into a wealthy and prestigious family will fall into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>miserable destination</b></a> after death if they abandon virtue and engage in misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the "light-to-dark" person to someone descending from a palace to an elephant, then to a horse, a couch, the ground, and finally into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>total darkness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the "light-to-light" person as one born into a high, wealthy family who continues to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>virtuous conduct</b></a> in body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is already in the "light" of a fortunate birth and continues to do good will be reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>happy destination</b></a> in the heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the "light-to-light" person to someone moving from one palace to another, or from one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>elephant’s shoulder</b></a> to another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the "dark-to-dark" person as one who is not only poor and stingy but also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>insults and reviles</b></a> ascetics, brahmins, and other mendicants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>nihilist</b></a> and having a disrespectful, annoying attitude contributes to a person's descent from darkness to further darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>prevents others</b></a> from giving food to those who beg is acting in a way that leads to a "dark" future, regardless of their current status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the poor person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>full of faith</b></a> and generous, explaining that such a person is a "dark-to-light" individual heading for a heavenly place? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically mentioned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>rising and saluting</b></a> ascetics and brahmins is a mark of a person who is training in harmonious living and moving toward the "light"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a wealthy person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>miserly</b></a>, possesses wrong views, and is disrespectful to others is heading toward a "light-to-dark" destiny? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha associated a "light-to-light" person with having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>noble intentions</b></a> and a focused, unruffled mind while being born into a position of great wealth and beauty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged training in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato"><b>harmonious living</b></a> as a key characteristic that ensures a person moves toward a heavenly world, whether they start from a low or high social position? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.21/en/sujato">SN 3.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala informed the Buddha that his grandmother, who was <b>one hundred and twenty years old</b>, had passed away after a long life? And that the King told the Buddha he was so devoted to his grandmother that he would have given away an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato"><b>elephant-gem</b></a> or a horse-gem if it could have prevented her death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato">SN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi expressed to the Buddha that he would have surrendered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato"><b>choice village</b></a> or even an entire province if such a gift could have kept his grandmother alive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato">SN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught King Pasenadi that all living beings are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato"><b>subject to death</b></a>, that death is their final end, and that no one can transcend it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato">SN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the mortality of all living beings to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato"><b>pottery vessels</b></a> made by a potter, which all eventually break regardless of whether they are raw or baked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato">SN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that after death, all beings <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato"><b>fare according to their deeds</b></a>, where those who perform evil actions go to hell and those who perform good deeds go to a happy destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato">SN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged King Pasenadi to perform virtuous deeds, stating that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato"><b>meritorious deeds</b></a> are the only true support for living beings in the next world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato">SN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala asked the Buddha to identify which qualities arising in the world lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.23/en/sujato"><b>harm and suffering</b></a> and an uncomfortable life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.23/en/sujato">SN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.23/en/sujato"><b>greed, hatred, and delusion</b></a> as the three specific qualities that arise in the world to the detriment and suffering of a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.23/en/sujato">SN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that greed, hatred, and delusion are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.23/en/sujato"><b>self-born</b></a> qualities that ultimately harm the person of evil mind in whom they arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.23/en/sujato">SN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy stating that an evil-minded person is destroyed by their own internal greed and hatred just as a plant is destroyed by its own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.23/en/sujato"><b>fruit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.23/en/sujato">SN 3.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi expressed to the Buddha that his grandmother was extremely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato"><b>dear and agreeable</b></a> to him, which is why her death caused him such grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato">SN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that life <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato"><b>ends in death</b></a> for every single being, regardless of their age or the affection others have for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato">SN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised King Pasenadi that because death is inevitable, a person should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato"><b>do what is good</b></a> to ensure a positive future beyond this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato">SN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that those who engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato"><b>meritorious conduct</b></a> are headed for a happy destination (suggati) after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.22/en/sujato">SN 3.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when King Pasenadi of Kosala asked the Buddha where a gift should be given, the Buddha replied that one should give <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>where a gift should be given</b></a> according to wherever the mind feels confidence or devotion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha made a clear distinction between where a gift is given out of personal preference and where a gift is given to yield <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>great fruit</b></a>, explaining that the latter depends on the virtue of the recipient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a gift given to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>virtuous person</b></a> is of great fruit, whereas a gift given to an immoral person does not yield the same spiritual reward? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a military analogy to explain merit, asking King Pasenadi if he would employ an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>untrained soldier</b></a> who is cowardly and flees from battle, regardless of whether that man was born into a royal, brahmin, merchant, or menial class? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked King Pasenadi if he would prefer a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>brave and skilled soldier</b></a> regardless of his social origin, to which the King replied that he would certainly employ such a person for his military needs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>social status</b></a> at birth—whether they come from a royal or lowly family—is irrelevant to the merit gained by giving to them, provided they have gone forth into the homeless life and attained spiritual qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a gift is of great fruit when given to a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>abandoned five factors</b></a> of the mind: sensual desire, ill-will, sloth and torpor, restlessness and remorse, and doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a> and ill-will as two of the major mental obstructions that a practitioner must abandon to become a recipient whose presence brings great merit to a donor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a worthy recipient of gifts is one who has abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> along with doubt, ensuring their mind is clear and spiritually settled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a gift yields great fruit when the recipient possesses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>aggregates of one beyond training</b></a>, which include perfected virtue, concentration, and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a recipient who has attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a> and the knowledge and vision of liberation is the ideal field of merit for a donor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a gift is most fruitful when the recipient possesses the aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a>, meaning they fully understand their own release from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged honoring a person of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>low birth</b></a> if they are wise and have established themselves in the noble way of living, just as one would honor a skilled archer regardless of his family background? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who wish for merit should provide food, drink, clothing, and dwellings to those who practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>noble conduct</b></a> and possess an upright mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that meritorious acts can include building <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>bridges</b></a> in difficult places and providing various necessities to those who live righteously? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>thundercloud</b></a> wreathed in lightning that rains down and fills both the high and low land to describe how a faithful person provides for the needy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a> as one who, with a clear mind, prepares food and drink to satisfy wayfarers and beggars, much like a rain cloud providing for the parched earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded his instruction to King Pasenadi by stating that a vast <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato"><b>stream of merit</b></a> showers upon a donor who gives joyfully and generously to the virtuous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.24/en/sujato">SN 3.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala admitted to the Buddha that he was often <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>drunk with sovereignty</b></a> and preoccupied with the duties of kingship, such as conquering territory, before receiving a profound teaching on mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a powerful analogy of four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>massive mountains</b></a>, reaching to the sky, rolling in from the four cardinal directions and crushing all living beings to illustrate the unstoppable advance of aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala told the Buddha that when faced with inevitable destruction and the crushing force of mortality, there is no other duty for a person except <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>righteous living</b></a>, harmonious living, and performing meritorious deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that just as giant rocks would crush everything in their path, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a> are constantly rolling in upon every person regardless of their social status or worldly power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi acknowledged to the Buddha that even the most powerful <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>elephant forces</b></a> used in royal warfare are completely useless and have no place when a person is being overcome by aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>rarity of human birth</b></a> when asking King Pasenadi what should be done if life-threatening danger was imminent and human life was so difficult to obtain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi explained to the Buddha that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>cavalry forces</b></a>, which are vital for a king's defense, provide no protection or escape when aging and death are advancing upon an individual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi noted that neither <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>chariot forces</b></a> nor infantry are effective means of combatting or turning back the inevitable arrival of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a> do not spare anyone, including nobles (khattiyas), brahmins, merchants (vessas), workers (suddas), or even the lowest outcasts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>political counsel</b></a> from clever ministers or strategic schemes are powerless to turn back the advance of aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi confessed to the Buddha that despite the vast <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>wealth and gold</b></a> stored in his royal treasury, such riches cannot be used to bribe or halt the process of dying? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the universality of death to mountains that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>crush everything</b></a> in their path, leaving no survivors from the east, west, north, or south? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a wise person, seeing their own true benefit and the certainty of death, should place <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>firm faith</b></a> in the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that those who practice the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a> through their body, speech, and mind are praised by others in this world during their lifetime? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who lives righteously through body, speech, and mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>rejoices in heaven</b></a> after the breakup of the body and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi described himself as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>obsessed with sensual pleasures</b></a> and the administration of his realm before the Buddha reminded him of the urgency of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described death as a force that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>overwhelms all</b></a> beings, regardless of their beauty, wealth, or power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi argued that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>doing what is good</b></a> is the only meaningful activity left when one realizes that the metaphorical "mountains" of aging and death are closing in? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of mountains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>reaching the sky</b></a> to emphasize the overwhelming and inescapable nature of mortal decay that faces every living being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that neither through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato"><b>wealth nor mantras</b></a> can one conquer death, just as soldiers cannot defeat mountains that are rolling over them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.25/en/sujato">SN 3.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an ancient battle between the devas and asuras where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>lord of the devas</b></a>, Sakka, gave his troops a specific method to overcome fear while in combat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how Sakka instructed the Tavatimsa devas to look upon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>crest of his banner</b></a> to dispel any fear, trepidation, or hair-raising terror that might arise during battle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, Sakka provided alternative options for his troops, suggesting they look at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>banner of Pajapati</b></a>, a king of the devas, if they could not see his own? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted Sakka also recommended looking at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>banner of Varuna</b></a>, a celestial king, as a means for the devas to abandon their fear during the conflict with the asuras? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>Isana, king of devas</b></a>, as the final celestial leader whose banner crest could be used by the devas to find courage in the midst of war? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that looking at the banners of Sakka or the other deva kings <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>might or might not</b></a> actually cause fear to be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that Sakka is still <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>subject to fear</b></a> and trepidation himself because he is not yet free from lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized Sakka, the lord of the devas, as someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>prone to fleeing</b></a> (palāyī), which explains why his banner might not provide ultimate security? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised his monks that if fear arises while they are staying in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>forest or empty huts</b></a>, they should instead recollect the qualities of the Tathagata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by performing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Buddha</b></a>, such as remembering he is an Arahant and perfectly enlightened, a monk's fear will definitely be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>teacher of devas and humans</b></a>, a quality that practitioners should reflect upon to dispel terror when in solitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that if one does not recollect the Enlightened One, they should instead <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>recollect the Dhamma</b></a>, which is well-expounded and visible here and now? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>to be realized</b></a> by the wise for themselves, and focusing on this quality can remove hair-raising terror? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that if a monk cannot recollect the Buddha or the Dhamma, they should then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>recollect the Sangha</b></a> to find relief from fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the community of noble disciples as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world, a thought that brings courage to the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the Sangha of the Blessed One's disciples is composed of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>four pairs of persons</b></a>, or eight individuals, who are worthy of gifts and hospitality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that unlike Sakka, the Tathagata is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>free from lust</b></a>, hatred, and delusion, making his protection reliable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>beyond all trepidation</b></a> (acchambhī) and someone who never flees, providing a perfect object for meditation on fearlessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized in verse that for those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>remember the Buddha</b></a>, the Dhamma, and the Sangha, fear and hair-raising terror will never occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to himself as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato"><b>bull of men</b></a> (narāsabha) and the "eldest in the world" when encouraging monks to find strength in his example? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.3/en/sujato">SN 11.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a battle between the devas and asuras where both Sakka, lord of the devas, and Vepacitti, lord of the asuras, ordered their troops to bind the opposing leader in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato"><b>fivefold bonds</b></a> should they emerge victorious? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato">SN 11.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how the defeated asura lord Vepacitti was brought to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato"><b>Sudhamma hall</b></a> in bonds, where he proceeded to revile and abuse Sakka with harsh words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato">SN 11.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha related a conversation where Matali the charioteer asked Sakka if his patient endurance of Vepacitti’s insults was due to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato"><b>fear or weakness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato">SN 11.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how Sakka refused to engage with the captured asura lord because a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a> should not involve themselves in a verbal conflict with a fool? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato">SN 11.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told of Sakka’s belief that the best way to restrain an angry person is to remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato"><b>mindful and calm</b></a> once you realize the other person is provoked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato">SN 11.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted Sakka’s teaching that there is no higher good than <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato"><b>patience</b></a>, even if a fool mistakenly believes such endurance is motivated by fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato">SN 11.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized supreme patience as the choice of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato"><b>strong person</b></a> to endure the provocations of someone who is weak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato">SN 11.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the strength of a fool is actually a form of weakness, whereas a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato"><b>guarded by Dhamma</b></a> possesses a strength that cannot be successfully opposed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato">SN 11.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato"><b>return anger</b></a> for anger wins a battle that is otherwise incredibly difficult to win? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato">SN 11.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha related how practicing patience in the face of another's rage is actually practicing for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato"><b>benefit of both</b></a> oneself and the other person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato">SN 11.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that people who are unskilled in the Dhamma often misjudge those who practice patience and self-healing, considering such individuals to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato"><b>fools</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato">SN 11.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his monks to be patient and gentle, noting that even Sakka, while ruling a heavenly kingdom, was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato"><b>proponent of patience</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato">SN 11.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the "difficult-to-win battle" is won by the one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato"><b>remains mindful</b></a> and subsides when they see that another has become angry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.4/en/sujato">SN 11.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a story where the devas and asuras, instead of fighting with weapons, agreed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>victory through good speech</b></a> would determine the winner of their battle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how Vepacitti, lord of the asuras, argued that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>heavy stick</b></a> is necessary to restrain fools, otherwise they would only become more unruly and break out even more? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared Sakka’s view that the most effective way to restrain a fool is when one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>mindfully becomes calm</b></a> upon realizing that another person has become angry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how Vepacitti criticized the practice of patience, claiming that if a person endures insults, a fool will simply assume it is out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>fear</b></a> and will attack even more aggressively? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha related how Sakka, lord of the devas, insisted that regardless of what a fool might think, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>no higher good</b></a> than the practice of patience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>supreme patience</b></a> as the specific instance when a person who is actually strong chooses to endure the provocations of someone who is weak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the strength of a fool is actually a form of weakness, whereas the strength of a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>guarded by Dhamma</b></a> is a power that no one can successfully oppose? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by not returning anger for anger, a person wins a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>battle hard to win</b></a> and secures their own welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a person who remains mindful and calm in the face of another's anger is actually practicing for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>benefit of both</b></a> themselves and the other person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that people who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>unskilled in the Dhamma</b></a> often mistake a patient person for a fool, even though that person is actually healing both themselves and the one who is angry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the judges of the deva-asura contest rejected Vepacitti's arguments because his words favored <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>sticks and swords</b></a>, which only lead to further strife and conflict? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how Sakka’s verses were judged superior because they promoted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>no-conflict</b></a> and the absence of quarreling, leading to a peaceful resolution? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how Sakka politely invited Vepacitti to speak first in their debate, acknowledging the asuras as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>ancient devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that Sakka achieved <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>victory through good speech</b></a>, demonstrating that ethical conduct and patience are more powerful than force? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared a metaphor used by Vepacitti where he compared a patient person to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>cow pursuing one who flees</b></a>, arguing that patience only encourages a bully to oppress the patient one further? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that both the devas and asuras appointed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>assemblies</b></a> to act as impartial judges to determine which leader's speech was truly well-spoken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who remain calm when others are angry are effectively <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>healing both</b></a> themselves and their provocateur through their mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how Vepacitti addressed Sakka by the name <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato"><b>Vāsava</b></a> while they were debating the merits of patience versus punishment during their contest of words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.5/en/sujato">SN 11.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was once resting in meditation at Jetavana when both Sakka, the lord of the devas, and Verocana, the lord of the asuras, approached him and stood leaning against a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.8/en/sujato"><b>doorpost</b></a> to engage in a spiritual dialogue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.8/en/sujato">SN 11.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how Verocana, the lord of the asuras, believed a person should strive until their purpose is achieved, asserting that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.8/en/sujato"><b>goal achieved</b></a> is the most beautiful thing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.8/en/sujato">SN 11.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha related how Sakka agreed that striving for a goal is important, but added that nothing is better than <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.8/en/sujato"><b>patience</b></a> once that goal has been attained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.8/en/sujato">SN 11.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a debate where Verocana argued that all beings are driven by their own interests and that the supreme thing in life is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.8/en/sujato"><b>consumption</b></a> (sambhogā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.8/en/sujato">SN 11.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a story about virtuous hermits living in leaf huts in the forest who were visited by both the lord of the devas and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato"><b>lord of the asuras</b></a> to test their conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato">SN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted the behavior of two celestial lords by noting that Vepacitti, the asura lord, entered a hermitage while wearing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato"><b>boots and a sword</b></a>, showing total disrespect to the resident sages? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato">SN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how Sakka showed deep respect to forest hermits by removing his boots, handing his sword and parasol to others, and standing with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato"><b>joined palms</b></a> in homage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato">SN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha related how virtuous hermits warned Sakka to keep his distance because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato"><b>smell of hermits</b></a>, who have long practiced austerities, is typically considered foul to the celestial devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato">SN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared Sakka's response to the hermits, where the deva lord claimed that the gods do not find the scent of virtuous people repulsive, but rather cherish it like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato"><b>garland of flowers</b></a> worn upon the head? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato">SN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned how Sakka specifically stood <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato"><b>downwind</b></a> of the forest hermits as a gesture of humility and reverence while paying his respects to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato">SN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how Vepacitti, the asura lord, arrogantly entered a forest hermitage through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato"><b>main gate</b></a> while being shaded by a parasol, a display of pride toward the virtuous sages dwelling there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn11.9/en/sujato">SN 11.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a> as the process of decrepitude, the graying of hair, the ripening of faculties, and the final breakup of the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato">SN 12.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the origin of aging and death is birth, and that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> is the specific way leading to their cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato">SN 12.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified three distinct types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> that a practitioner must understand: bodily volitional formations, verbal volitional formations, and mental volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato">SN 12.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the origin of volitional formations is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a>, and that by ending ignorance through the Noble Eightfold Path, one brings those formations to cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato">SN 12.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk who thoroughly understands the origin, cessation, and the path leading to the cessation of factors like craving and feeling is called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato"><b>one possessed of view</b></a> by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato">SN 12.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described a person who fully comprehends the cessation of the twelve links of dependent origination as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato"><b>one who has entered the stream of Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato">SN 12.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha said that a practitioner who understands the fourfold nature of volitional formations and existence is a noble one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato"><b>stands knocking at the door of the Deathless</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato">SN 12.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato"><b>aging</b></a> specifically as the "brokenness of teeth," the "wrinkling of skin," and the "decline of the life span" within various orders of beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato">SN 12.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato"><b>death</b></a> is characterized as the completion of time, the disappearance of a being from their current state, and the "laying down of the carcass"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato">SN 12.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refers to a practitioner who possesses a deep, experiential understanding of the path to the cessation of birth and existence as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato"><b>one possessed of vision</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato">SN 12.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha calls a monk who correctly knows the cessation of contact, feeling, and the six sense bases <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato"><b>a noble one with penetrative wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato">SN 12.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk who understands the origin and cessation of consciousness and name-and-form is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato"><b>possessed of a trainee's knowledge</b></a> and a trainee's true knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato">SN 12.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha categorized a person who sees the true Dhamma through the detailed understanding of dependent origination as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato"><b>one who has come to this true Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.28/en/sujato">SN 12.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Venerable Sariputta to provide a detailed explanation for a brief verse regarding the conduct of practitioners that was originally found in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>Questions of Ajita</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sariputta <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>remained silent</b></a> three times when the Buddha initially asked him to explain how the meaning of a briefly stated verse from the Pārāyana should be understood in detail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sariputta explained that the first step in understanding the Dhamma is to see "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>this has come to be</b></a>" (the entity or phenomenon) according to reality with correct wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>trainee</b></a> (sekha) as one who, after seeing the reality of how things have come to be, practices for the sake of revulsion, fading away of lust, and cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Sariputta, one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>fully understood the Dhamma</b></a> (saṅkhātadhammo) is someone who, having seen reality, is liberated through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that seeing the nature of reality involves recognizing that anything which has come to be has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>originated from nutriment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sariputta explained that by seeing how something originated from nutriment, one also correctly sees that with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>cessation of nutriment</b></a>, what has come to be is subject to cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a trainee as one who is practicing for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>revulsion</b></a> toward that which has come to be and its origin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sariputta stated a trainee is practicing for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>fading away of lust</b></a> regarding the fact that things which come to be are subject to cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the practice for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>cessation</b></a> as the proper path for a trainee who correctly sees the reality of phenomena and their dependence on nutriment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sariputta taught that an Arahant, or one who has fully understood the Dhamma, is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>liberated through non-clinging</b></a> (anupādā) because of their complete revulsion and the fading away of lust for what has come to be? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the specific question, "Do you see, Sariputta, that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>this has come to be</b></a>?" to prompt Sariputta to finally break his silence and explain the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sariputta defined the state of one who has fully understood the Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a> from the cycle of origin and cessation through the realization of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha quoted a verse from the Pārāyana inquiring about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>mode of conduct</b></a> (iriya) of the prudent person compared to that of a trainee? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sariputta emphasized that the vision of reality must be through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>correct wisdom</b></a> (sammappaññā) in order to lead a practitioner to the path of cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha expressed his approval of Sariputta’s detailed analysis of the verse by saying, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>Good, good</b></a>, Sariputta!"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sariputta explained that once a person sees that what has come to be is a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>thing subject to cessation</b></a>" (nirodhadhamma), they practice for the fading away of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s inquiry concerned the distinction between those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>fully understood the Dhamma</b></a> and the many trainees present in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sariputta’s method of explaining the verse involved breaking down the understanding of "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>what has come to be</b></a>" into its origin from nutriment and its ultimate cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches that once one sees "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato"><b>this has come to be</b></a>" as it really is, they are on the path toward becoming a noble person who has entered the stream of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.31/en/sujato">SN 12.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that this <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.37/en/sujato"><b>body</b></a> does not belong to you, nor does it belong to others, but should instead be viewed as "old kamma"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.37/en/sujato">SN 12.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the human body as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.37/en/sujato"><b>old kamma</b></a> that has been constructed, willed, and is something to be felt or experienced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.37/en/sujato">SN 12.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a well-taught noble disciple avoids identifying with the body and instead thoroughly attends to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.37/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.37/en/sujato">SN 12.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained the principle of specific conditionality by stating: "When <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.37/en/sujato"><b>this exists</b></a>, that comes to be; with the arising of this, that arises"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.37/en/sujato">SN 12.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that by the remainderless fading away and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.37/en/sujato"><b>cessation of ignorance</b></a>, volitional formations cease, eventually leading to the cessation of the entire mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.37/en/sujato">SN 12.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.37/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> as being conditioned by ignorance, which in turn acts as the condition for consciousness in the cycle of dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.37/en/sujato">SN 12.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that what one intends, what one plans, and whatever one has an underlying tendency for becomes an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.38/en/sujato"><b>object</b></a> for the stationing of consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.38/en/sujato">SN 12.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that when an object for consciousness exists, there is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.38/en/sujato"><b>footing</b></a> for consciousness, which leads to the production of future renewed existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.38/en/sujato">SN 12.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even if one does not consciously intend or plan, consciousness can still gain a footing if there is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.38/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency</b></a> (anuseti) for something? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.38/en/sujato">SN 12.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that once consciousness becomes established and grows, it results in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.38/en/sujato"><b>future renewed existence</b></a>, followed by the arising of birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.38/en/sujato">SN 12.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that if one does not intend, plan, or have any underlying tendency, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.38/en/sujato"><b>no object</b></a> for the stationing of consciousness, preventing its growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.38/en/sujato">SN 12.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.38/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> as occurring when consciousness has no footing and does not grow, thereby preventing future birth, sorrow, and lamentation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.38/en/sujato">SN 12.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that what one intends, what one plans, and whatever one has an underlying tendency for becomes an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato"><b>object for the stationing of consciousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato">SN 12.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when an object for consciousness exists, there is a support for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato"><b>establishment of consciousness</b></a>, which allows it to grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato">SN 12.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that when consciousness is established and grows, it leads specifically to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato"><b>descent of name-and-form</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato">SN 12.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that even if a person does not actively intend or plan, consciousness can still gain a footing and grow if there is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency</b></a> for something? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato">SN 12.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified the origin of the entire mass of suffering—including birth, aging, and death—as starting from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato"><b>stationing of consciousness</b></a> upon an intended or habitual object? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato">SN 12.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when one neither intends, nor plans, nor has any underlying tendency, there is no object for consciousness, which results in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato"><b>cessation of name-and-form</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato">SN 12.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato"><b>cessation of the six sense bases</b></a> and the subsequent cessation of feeling and craving follow directly from the cessation of name-and-form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.39/en/sujato">SN 12.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, once consciousness becomes established and grows, a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato"><b>inclination</b></a> (nati) occurs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato">SN 12.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that when there is an inclination of the mind, it leads to a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato"><b>coming and going</b></a> (āgatigati)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato">SN 12.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato"><b>passing away and reappearing</b></a> (cutūpapāto) is conditioned by the existence of "coming and going"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato">SN 12.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that passing away and reappearing inevitably results in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato"><b>future birth, aging, and death</b></a>, as well as sorrow and lamentation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato">SN 12.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even without active intention or planning, an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency</b></a> alone is enough to establish consciousness and produce an inclination of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato">SN 12.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when consciousness has no object and is not established, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato"><b>no inclination</b></a>, which halts the cycle of coming and going? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato">SN 12.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that by ending the cycle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato"><b>coming and going</b></a>, one prevents the possibility of passing away and reappearing in any future state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato">SN 12.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> occurs when one no longer intends, plans, or maintains underlying tendencies, thereby ending future birth and sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.40/en/sujato">SN 12.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa once informed the Buddha that monks had become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>difficult to speak to</b></a> because they possessed qualities that made them unamenable and impatient when receiving instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed a time when senior monks were exemplary <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>forest dwellers</b></a> who also actively spoke in praise of living in the forest to encourage their peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, early senior monks lived strictly as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>alms-food eaters</b></a> and made it a point to praise the practice of collecting alms-food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha recalled that senior monks of the past were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>rag-robe wearers</b></a> who spoke highly of the practice of wearing robes made from discarded rags? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha noted that in former times, senior monks were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>three-robe wearers</b></a> and advocated for others to limit their possessions to only these three essential robes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the senior monks of old as being characterized by having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>few wishes</b></a> and frequently praising the virtue of wanting little? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that early senior monks were inherently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>content</b></a> with their requisites and spoke in praise of contentment to the rest of the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that senior monks of the past lived <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>secluded</b></a> lives and emphasized the importance of physical and mental seclusion in their teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that early senior monks were described by the Buddha as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>aloof</b></a>, avoiding social entanglements and praising the quality of not being social or entangled with groups? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that senior monks of former times were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>energetic</b></a> in their spiritual practice and spoke in praise of the arousal of energy to achieve higher goals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that in the past, if a monk was energetic and lived ascetically, senior monks would honor him by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>inviting him to a seat</b></a> and commending him as a monk who was "desirous of training"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that when new monks saw seniors honoring those who practiced asceticism and energy, they would strive to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>practice accordingly</b></a>, which led to their long-term welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha lamented that many senior monks eventually stopped being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>forest dwellers</b></a> and ceased praising the qualities of seclusion and energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha observed a shift where senior monks began to honor those who were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>famous and influential</b></a>, inviting them to seats of honor specifically because they were well-known? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that when a monk is a frequent gainer of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>requisites</b></a> like robes and alms-food, he is now often the one mistakenly held up as a "good monk" by his peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha cautioned that when new monks see famous and wealthy monks being honored, they emulate that pursuit of fame, which leads to their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>long-term harm</b></a> and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described the situation where practitioners are overcome by the desire for honor and material gains as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>catastrophe of the holy life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha said that a practitioner who is driven by the longing for fame and offerings is one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>beset</b></a> by the snares and pitfalls of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha invited Venerable Mahākassapa to either <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>exhort the monks</b></a> himself or allow the Buddha to do so, highlighting that both had the authority and responsibility to give a Dhamma talk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākassapa noted that monks had become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato"><b>impatient</b></a> and did not take instruction with proper respect, which discouraged him from teaching them at that time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn16.8/en/sujato">SN 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.1/en/sujato"><b>gain, honor, and praise</b></a> are dreadful, bitter, and harsh, serving as a significant barrier to attaining the unsurpassed security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.1/en/sujato">SN 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his disciples to train themselves to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.1/en/sujato"><b>abandon</b></a> any arisen gain, honor, and praise so that such things do not remain obsessing their minds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.1/en/sujato">SN 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that the pursuit of status and material offerings is a direct obstacle to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.1/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed security from bondage</b></a>, which is the ultimate goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.1/en/sujato">SN 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.2/en/sujato"><b>fisherman</b></a> as a designation for Mara the Evil One, who attempts to catch practitioners with the lure of worldly rewards? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.2/en/sujato">SN 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.2/en/sujato"><b>baited hook</b></a> is a designation for gain, honor, and praise, which are thrown out to trap those who are greedy for material offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.2/en/sujato">SN 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who relishes and desires gain, honor, and praise as being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.2/en/sujato"><b>hooked fish</b></a> that has fallen into calamity and disaster? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.2/en/sujato">SN 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a practitioner who becomes attached to worldly fame and offerings is at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.2/en/sujato"><b>mercy of the Evil One</b></a>, just as a fish that swallows a hook is subject to the fisherman's will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.2/en/sujato">SN 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha characterized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.1/en/sujato"><b>gain, honor, and praise</b></a> with the harsh terms "bitter" and "coarse" to emphasize how dangerous they are to the attainment of true spiritual freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.1/en/sujato">SN 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a teaching about spiritual traps, the Buddha compared the world to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.2/en/sujato"><b>deep pool of water</b></a> where Mara casts a baited hook to catch those who are easily swayed by sensory rewards? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.2/en/sujato">SN 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that even when fame and offerings arise, a practitioner must ensure that such things do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.1/en/sujato"><b>obsess the mind</b></a> or dictate their behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.1/en/sujato">SN 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that a monk who "swallows" the hook of worldly praise has met with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.2/en/sujato"><b>ruin</b></a> and can be dealt with by Mara according to the Evil One's pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.2/en/sujato">SN 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.1/en/sujato"><b>resolute in their training</b></a> to specifically discard any arisen desire for honor, ensuring their progress toward enlightenment remains unhindered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.1/en/sujato">SN 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.12/en/sujato"><b>gain, honor, and praise</b></a> are so dreadful that a person who would never tell a deliberate lie even for a bowl of silver filled with gold powder might eventually do so when their mind is overcome by the desire for status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.12/en/sujato">SN 17.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed he could perceive with his own mind that certain individuals, despite having strong integrity, can have their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.12/en/sujato"><b>minds overwhelmed</b></a> by the pursuit of fame and offerings to the point that they consciously speak falsehoods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.12/en/sujato">SN 17.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a faithful female lay follower should ideally wish for her beloved only son to become like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.23/en/sujato"><b>Citta the Householder</b></a> and Hatthaka of Alavi, who serve as the standard and measure for all male lay disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.23/en/sujato">SN 17.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that if a layperson's son decides to go forth into the homeless life, the Buddha suggested the mother should wish for him to emulate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.23/en/sujato"><b>Sāriputta and Moggallāna</b></a>, as they are the standard and measure for all monks in the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.23/en/sujato">SN 17.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that for a monk who is still a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.23/en/sujato"><b>trainee</b></a> (one who has not yet reached the goal of the spiritual life), the sudden arrival of gain, honor, and praise can act as a significant obstacle to his progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.23/en/sujato">SN 17.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.23/en/sujato"><b>Citta the Householder and Hatthaka of Alavi</b></a> as the two individuals who represent the pinnacle of practice and the proper "measure" for his lay followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.23/en/sujato">SN 17.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that any ascetics or brahmins who do not understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.25/en/sujato"><b>gratification, danger, and escape</b></a> regarding gain, honor, and praise are not considered true spiritual practitioners among their peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.25/en/sujato">SN 17.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who fail to realize the true nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.25/en/sujato"><b>worldly status and offerings</b></a> cannot be said to have realized the goal of asceticism or the goal of brahminhood through their own direct knowledge in this present life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.25/en/sujato">SN 17.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha affirmed that ascetics and brahmins who truly understand the gratification, danger, and escape regarding gain and honor are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.25/en/sujato"><b>rightly considered</b></a> as genuine practitioners who have attained the essence of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.25/en/sujato">SN 17.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a powerful metaphor describing how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.28/en/sujato"><b>gain, honor, and praise</b></a> are so destructive that they metaphorically cut through a person’s outer skin, inner skin, and flesh? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.28/en/sujato">SN 17.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha cautioned that the danger of worldly recognition is so penetrating that it does not stop at the flesh, but proceeds to cut through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.28/en/sujato"><b>sinews and bones</b></a> of a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.28/en/sujato">SN 17.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described gain, honor, and praise as being so "dreadful" that they ultimately strike and remain fixed at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.28/en/sujato"><b>bone marrow</b></a> of those who are attached to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.28/en/sujato">SN 17.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha urged monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.12/en/sujato"><b>train themselves</b></a> specifically to ensure that whenever gain, honor, and praise arise, they do not allow these things to obsess their minds or lead them away from the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.12/en/sujato">SN 17.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the standard for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.23/en/sujato"><b>lay disciples</b></a> by pointing to specific individuals like Hatthaka of Alavi, emphasizing that even laypeople have high spiritual benchmarks to aspire to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.23/en/sujato">SN 17.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that even a mother's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.23/en/sujato"><b>rightful prayer</b></a> for her son should include the hope that he avoids the spiritual pitfalls of fame and material success while he is still a trainee on the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.23/en/sujato">SN 17.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the dreadful nature of gain, honor, and praise to a strong man wrapping a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.29/en/sujato"><b>tough hair-rope</b></a> around a person’s leg and rubbing it until it cuts through the skin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.29/en/sujato">SN 17.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that worldly recognition is so penetrating it does not just stop at the surface, but cuts through the outer skin, the inner skin, the flesh, the sinews, the bones, and finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.29/en/sujato"><b>strikes the marrow</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.29/en/sujato">SN 17.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that for Devadatta, gain, honor, and praise arose specifically for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato"><b>self-destruction</b></a> and downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato">SN 17.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato"><b>banana tree</b></a> bearing fruit to illustrate how some people are destroyed by the very rewards and offerings they receive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato">SN 17.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, just as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato"><b>bamboo plant</b></a> or a reed is destroyed by its own fruit, a "wretched man" is destroyed by the honor and offerings he receives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato">SN 17.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that just as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato"><b>she-mule</b></a> is killed by the foal she conceives, a person's spiritual integrity can be destroyed by the arrival of fame and material offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato">SN 17.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.29/en/sujato"><b>gain, honor, and praise</b></a> as "dreadful" because their influence can permeate a practitioner's being all the way through the flesh to the bone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.29/en/sujato">SN 17.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after Devadatta had left the community, the Buddha addressed the monks to point out that Devadatta's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato"><b>worldly fame</b></a> had actually led to his ruin rather than his benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato">SN 17.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha urged monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.29/en/sujato"><b>train themselves</b></a> specifically to ensure that if gain and honor arise, they do not let these things remain and obsess their minds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.29/en/sujato">SN 17.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha specifically identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato"><b>reed</b></a> as an example of a plant that brings about its own end by producing fruit, much like how status destroys a person lacking in wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato">SN 17.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the influence of honor is so severe it can cut through a person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.29/en/sujato"><b>sinews</b></a>, highlighting the deep spiritual damage it can cause to one's progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.29/en/sujato">SN 17.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato"><b>honor destroys</b></a> a "wretched man" just as the fruit of a bamboo or banana tree brings about the death of the plant itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato">SN 17.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that gain and honor cut through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.29/en/sujato"><b>outer skin</b></a> before proceeding to the inner skin and flesh, indicating a progressive decline in a person's character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.29/en/sujato">SN 17.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha referred to a person who is spiritually ruined by fame and offerings as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato"><b>wretched man</b></a>, emphasizing the moral decay that often follows such worldly success? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato">SN 17.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato"><b>downfall</b></a> to describe the ultimate impact of gain and honor on individuals like Devadatta who allow their minds to be overwhelmed by such things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.35/en/sujato">SN 17.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of breathing</b></a> is fulfilled in detail when a practitioner sits in a secluded place like a forest, keeps their body erect, and remains mindful while breathing in and out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato">SN 54.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ariṭṭha explained his personal practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of breathing</b></a> as having abandoned desire for past and future sensual pleasures and having thoroughly removed the perception of resistance toward internal and external things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato">SN 54.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should train himself to be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato"><b>contemplator of relinquishment</b></a> while breathing in and breathing out as part of the complete development of mindfulness of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato">SN 54.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that while practicing mindfulness of breathing, one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato"><b>discern</b></a> whether they are breathing in a long breath or a short breath? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato">SN 54.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> is not lived to fulfillment by any monk who does not truly know the origin, passing away, gratification, danger, and escape regarding the six bases for contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato">SN 35.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that an unnamed monk once admitted to the Buddha that he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato"><b>at a loss</b></a> because he did not yet understand the five aspects—origin, passing away, gratification, danger, and escape—of the six bases for contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato">SN 35.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a> with correct wisdom as 'This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self' is the path to the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato">SN 35.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for mindfulness of breathing to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato"><b>fulfilled in detail</b></a>, one must go beyond merely abandoning sensual desire and follow a specific sixteen-step training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato">SN 54.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the end of suffering as seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato"><b>tongue</b></a> and the other sense bases as 'This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self' through correct wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato">SN 35.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that sitting down, folding one's legs crosswise, and setting the body erect is the proper <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato"><b>physical posture</b></a> for establishing mindfulness in front of oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato">SN 54.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that anyone who does not understand the six bases for contact as they really are is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato"><b>far from this Dhamma-Vinaya</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato">SN 35.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> as 'This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self' with correct wisdom is essential for reaching the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato">SN 35.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a monk should train to experience the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato"><b>whole body</b></a> and to tranquillize the bodily formation while breathing in and out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato">SN 54.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked his monks whether they developed mindfulness of breathing and then provided a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato"><b>more complete explanation</b></a> than what was initially offered by Venerable Ariṭṭha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.6/en/sujato">SN 54.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato"><b>six bases for contact</b></a> must be understood in five ways—their origin, disappearance, satisfaction, drawback, and the way out—to complete the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.71/en/sujato">SN 35.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the absence of a Tathagata in the world to a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato"><b>blinding darkness</b></a> where seasons, months, and even the distinction between day and night cannot be known? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato">SN 56.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that it is only when a Tathagata, an Arahant and Perfectly Enlightened One, arises in the world that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> are revealed, analyzed, and made plain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato">SN 56.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> should be made by practitioners to truly realize the truth of suffering and the path leading to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato">SN 56.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that as long as the sun and moon do not appear, there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato"><b>manifestation of great light</b></a>, just as there is no spiritual radiance until a Tathagata arises to teach the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato">SN 56.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the process of a Tathagata arising in the world as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato"><b>exposition</b></a> and setting forth of the Four Noble Truths for the benefit of all beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato">SN 56.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the bhikkhunī Vajirā was resting for her day’s abiding in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato"><b>Blind Men’s Grove</b></a> when Mara the Evil One approached her in an attempt to disrupt her concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato">SN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One approached the bhikkhunī Vajirā with the specific intent of causing her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato"><b>fear and consternation</b></a> so that she would fall away from her meditative state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato">SN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One challenged the bhikkhunī Vajirā by questioning who created the "being" and where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato"><b>creator of the being</b></a> was to be found? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato">SN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the bhikkhunī Vajirā used the famous analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato"><b>chariot</b></a> to explain that a "being" is merely a name used when various parts—the aggregates—are assembled together? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato">SN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the bhikkhunī Vajirā argued that what Mara perceived as a "being" was actually just a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato"><b>heap of mere formations</b></a> in which no permanent "being" could actually be found? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato">SN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the bhikkhunī Vajirā clarified that it is only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> that arises, stands, and passes away, and that nothing other than suffering is produced or ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato">SN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that once the bhikkhunī Vajirā recognized Mara and replied with profound wisdom, Mara became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato"><b>sorrowful and disappointed</b></a> and vanished on the spot? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato">SN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that before a Tathagata appears, the world remains in a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato"><b>blinding darkness</b></a> where the path to the end of suffering is neither taught nor revealed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato">SN 56.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the bhikkhunī Vajirā, upon being addressed by an unknown voice, correctly discerned that it was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato"><b>Mara the Evil One</b></a> trying to pull her away from her deep concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato">SN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the bhikkhunī Vajirā explained that the word "being" is merely a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato"><b>convention</b></a> that exists only as long as the five aggregates are present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.10/en/sujato">SN 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the appearance of the sun and moon allows for the discernment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato"><b>seasons and years</b></a>, just as the appearance of a Buddha allows for the clear understanding of the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.38/en/sujato">SN 56.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once visited the Brahmin Aggikabharadvaja in Rajagaha while the Brahmin was preparing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato"><b>milk-rice offering</b></a> for a fire sacrifice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato">SN 7.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is not a true Brahmin merely by birth, but rather one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato"><b>internally pure</b></a> and free from hypocrisy and stains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato">SN 7.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a true Brahmin as one who possesses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a>: knowing one's past lives, seeing heaven and hell, and reaching the destruction of birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato">SN 7.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refused to eat food that had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato"><b>chanted over with verses</b></a>, explaining that such a practice is not the way of those who truly see the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato">SN 7.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one seeking merit should serve food and drink to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato"><b>consummate one</b></a>—a great seer whose taints are destroyed and whose remorse is stilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato">SN 7.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's explanation of a true Brahmin, the Brahmin <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato"><b>Aggikabharadvaja</b></a> went forth into the homeless life and eventually became one of the Arahants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato">SN 7.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that any recluses or Brahmins who do not understand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a>, its origin, its cessation, and the path to its cessation, are not truly considered recluses or Brahmins by him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato">SN 12.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a true recluse must personally realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato"><b>goal of recluseship</b></a> through direct knowledge and dwell in it in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato">SN 12.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that understanding the origin and cessation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> is a requirement for being esteemed among the community of recluses and Brahmins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato">SN 12.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato"><b>esteemed</b></a> among recluses as those who understand the origin, cessation, and path regarding birth, existence, and clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato">SN 12.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that failing to understand the four aspects of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a>—its origin, cessation, and the path to its end—prevents one from attaining the true benefit of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato">SN 12.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the understanding of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> and its cessation as essential knowledge for anyone wishing to be recognized as a true Brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato">SN 12.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that recluses who lack knowledge of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato"><b>six sense bases</b></a> and how they originate cannot be considered true practitioners of the Dhamma-Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato">SN 12.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the understanding of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> and the path leading to its cessation as a defining characteristic of a true spiritual seeker? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato">SN 12.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who truly know the origin and cessation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> are the ones who have realized the purpose of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato">SN 12.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted the importance of understanding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> and its cessation for any ascetic who wishes to be considered a master in the community of recluses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.13/en/sujato">SN 12.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Aggikabharadvaja that a muni (sage) is one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato"><b>finished with higher knowledge</b></a> and has reached the end of all birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato">SN 7.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that providing support to a true practitioner is like planting in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato"><b>field of merit</b></a> for those who are seeking the benefits of good deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.8/en/sujato">SN 7.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vangisa once stood up to praise the Venerable Sariputta's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato"><b>deep wisdom</b></a> and skill in teaching the Dhamma after observing him instruct a group of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato">SN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato"><b>mindful and moderate</b></a> in eating will experience fewer physical pains and will age much more slowly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato">SN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sariputta was described by Vangisa as a master who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato"><b>skilled in the path</b></a> and what is not the path, possessing great wisdom for the benefit of the monastic community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato">SN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala once visited the Buddha while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato"><b>breathing heavily</b></a> because he had just consumed a massive "measure of rice" for his meal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato">SN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vangisa compared the Venerable Sariputta's voice to the sweet and captivating sound of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato"><b>starling</b></a> when he taught the monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato">SN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha composed a specific verse regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato"><b>moderation</b></a> for King Pasenadi to help the king manage his overeating and improve his health? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato">SN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sariputta's Dhamma talks were noted for being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato"><b>courteous and clear</b></a>, enabling his listeners to understand the meaning of the teachings perfectly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato">SN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi hired a young man named Sudassana to recite a verse on temperance at every meal in exchange for a daily wage of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato"><b>one hundred coins</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato">SN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vangisa observed that when Sariputta spoke, the monks would listen with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato"><b>uplifted and joyful minds</b></a>, lending an ear to his every word? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato">SN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that knowing the proper measure of food is a way of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato"><b>protecting life</b></a> and ensuring that one's digestion proceeds smoothly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato">SN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sariputta was known to teach the Dhamma both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato"><b>in brief and in detail</b></a>, showing his versatility and mastery as a speaker? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato">SN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi eventually achieved a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato"><b>slim figure</b></a> by training himself to eat no more than a small cup's measure of rice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato">SN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vangisa stood up and asked the Venerable Sariputta for permission to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato"><b>extol him</b></a> with verses because he was so inspired by Sariputta’s teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato">SN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi expressed that the Buddha had shown him compassion for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato"><b>welfare in two worlds</b></a>—both in this present life and the future—by teaching him about moderation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato">SN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks listening to the Venerable Sariputta were said to focus their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato"><b>entire hearts</b></a> on the teaching, making it their primary mental concern? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato">SN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's verse on mindfulness in eating states that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato"><b>always mindful</b></a> will find their suffering diminished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato">SN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sariputta's speech was described as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato"><b>flawless</b></a> and free from any errors, which helped in communicating the deep essence of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato">SN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato"><b>stroke his limbs</b></a> in satisfaction once he had lost weight and regained his health through the Buddha's guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato">SN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vangisa referred to the Venerable Sariputta as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato"><b>great sage</b></a> (mahāpañño) while praising his ability to explain the Dhamma to the bhikkhus? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.6/en/sujato">SN 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi's initial habit of eating a whole <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato"><b>measure of rice</b></a> caused him so much physical distress that he sought out the Buddha's help while still panting from his meal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.13/en/sujato">SN 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala realized that people who engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato"><b>misconduct</b></a> through body, speech, and mind are actually their own enemies, because they do to themselves exactly what an enemy would do to another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato">SN 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that even if people claim they love themselves, they actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato"><b>dislike themselves</b></a> if they continue to perform bad deeds, as they are creating the same harm for themselves that an adversary would? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato">SN 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala reasoned that those who practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato"><b>good conduct</b></a> in body, speech, and mind are the ones who truly love themselves, because they provide for themselves the same benefits that a dear friend would? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato">SN 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a person truly considers themselves to be dear, they should never <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato"><b>attach themselves to evil</b></a>, because the happiness resulting from wrong-doing is never easily won? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato">SN 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a person is seized by the "End-maker" (death) and must leave their human existence, the only thing they truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato"><b>possess</b></a> and take with them is the merit and evil they have done? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato">SN 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato"><b>shadow</b></a> that never departs to describe how a person's good and bad deeds follow them inevitably into the next life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato">SN 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged people to perform <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato"><b>wholesome deeds</b></a> as an accumulation for the future, noting that happiness is not easily found by those who perform bad actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato">SN 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato"><b>merit</b></a> serves as the essential foundation and support for living beings as they transition into the next world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato">SN 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha's conversation with King Pasenadi, even if someone claims to dislike themselves, they truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato"><b>love themselves</b></a> if they consistently act with integrity in their body, speech, and thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato">SN 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato"><b>merit and evil</b></a> created by a mortal in this life are the only things that follow them as their own personal property after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.4/en/sujato">SN 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala realized that even if a person is guarded by a massive army of elephants, cavalry, chariots, and infantry, they remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato"><b>unprotected</b></a> if they continue to engage in misconduct of body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato">SN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the only true and effective protection for a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato"><b>internal protection</b></a>, which is established by practicing good conduct in body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato">SN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala reasoned that those who practice good conduct of body, speech, and mind are truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato"><b>self-protected</b></a>, even if they have no external military force to guard them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato">SN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that restraint in body, speech, and mind is good, and that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato"><b>restrained everywhere</b></a> and possesses a sense of shame is truly said to be protected? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato">SN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala identified misconduct through body, speech, and mind as the primary reason why a person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato"><b>self is unprotected</b></a>, because military defenses are merely external and do not safeguard the internal self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato">SN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that for those who engage in bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct, they possess an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato"><b>unprotected self</b></a> because external defenses cannot substitute for a lack of internal virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato">SN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala observed that there are very <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato"><b>few people</b></a> in the world who, after acquiring grand and abundant wealth, do not become intoxicated, negligent, or obsessed with sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato">SN 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the vast majority of people who obtain great riches inevitably become intoxicated and negligent, falling into a deep <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato"><b>greed for sensual pleasures</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato">SN 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared those who are infatuated with wealth and sensual pleasures to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato"><b>deer</b></a> that fails to notice a trap that has been set for it until it is too late? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato">SN 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala noted that many people, upon gaining great wealth, begin to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato"><b>mistreat others</b></a>, whereas only a rare few maintain their integrity and kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato">SN 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the eventual result for those who become intoxicated and greedy for wealth is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato"><b>bitter</b></a>, because the fruit of such evil conduct is inherently painful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato">SN 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that those who are obsessed with the enjoyment of sensual pleasures do not realize they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato"><b>overstepped</b></a> proper bounds, much like an animal walking into a snare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato">SN 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala shared his reflection with the Buddha that the majority of beings in the world become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato"><b>negligent</b></a> once they acquire grand possessions and high status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.6/en/sujato">SN 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato"><b>restraint in speech</b></a> is a virtuous quality that contributes to a person being truly protected from internal harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato">SN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato"><b>restraint in mind</b></a> is essential for protection, as mental misconduct leaves a person vulnerable despite the presence of any physical guards or armies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato">SN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala argued that an army of cavalry and chariots constitutes only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato"><b>external protection</b></a>, which ultimately fails to protect those who act unvirtuously? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato">SN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that a person who acts with integrity in body, speech, and mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato"><b>protected</b></a> through internal means, even if they lack a personal guard of soldiers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.5/en/sujato">SN 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala observed that even wealthy people with abundant gold and silver will tell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.7/en/sujato"><b>deliberate lies</b></a> for the sake of sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.7/en/sujato">SN 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that for those who speak lies motivated by greed, their actions will inevitably lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.7/en/sujato"><b>harm and suffering</b></a> for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.7/en/sujato">SN 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala became so disillusioned by the corruption he saw in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.7/en/sujato"><b>judgment hall</b></a> that he told the Buddha he had seen enough of those administrative proceedings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.7/en/sujato">SN 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared people who are infatuated with and greedy for sensual pleasures to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.7/en/sujato"><b>fish in a trap</b></a>, because they fail to realize they have overstepped the bounds of virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.7/en/sujato">SN 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the eventual ripening of evil conduct is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.7/en/sujato"><b>bitter</b></a>, as the consequences of such deeds are inherently painful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.7/en/sujato">SN 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by King Pasenadi of Kosala if anyone was more beloved than her own self, Queen Mallikā replied that there is no one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.8/en/sujato"><b>dearer to her</b></a> than herself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.8/en/sujato">SN 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala admitted to the Buddha that, upon reflection, he found that there was no one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.8/en/sujato"><b>dearer than himself</b></a> in the entire world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.8/en/sujato">SN 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that after searching in every direction with the mind, one can never find anyone <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.8/en/sujato"><b>more beloved</b></a> than one's own self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.8/en/sujato">SN 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that since the self is so precious to everyone, a person who truly loves themselves should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.8/en/sujato"><b>not harm others</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.8/en/sujato">SN 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.8/en/sujato"><b>self is dear</b></a> to all beings equally, this should be the primary reason to refrain from violence against others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.8/en/sujato">SN 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.7/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> as the underlying motive and source that causes people to act dishonestly in legal and social matters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.7/en/sujato">SN 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala sought confirmation from the Buddha regarding a private conversation he had with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.8/en/sujato"><b>Queen Mallikā</b></a> about the nature of self-love? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.8/en/sujato">SN 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that massive rituals involving the slaughter of bulls, goats, and rams are not of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato"><b>great fruit</b></a> and do not lead to significant spiritual benefits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato">SN 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that enlightened sages of right conduct will never approach a sacrifice where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato"><b>living beings</b></a> such as cattle and sheep are being slain for ritual purposes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato">SN 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that when King Pasenadi prepared a great sacrifice, the slaves and workers performed their duties with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato"><b>tearful faces</b></a> because they were driven by the fear of punishment and violence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato">SN 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato"><b>sacrifice without violence</b></a> as an offering where no living beings are killed, and stated that such a peaceful ritual is the kind that wise sages will actually attend? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato">SN 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person should offer non-violent sacrifices because they are productive of vast merit and result in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato"><b>deities being pleased</b></a> with the one making the offering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato">SN 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified certain grand rituals, such as the horse sacrifice and human sacrifice, as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato"><b>not productive</b></a> of great results because of the violence inherent in their performance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato">SN 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that while King Pasenadi had many people bound in ropes and chains, the wise do not consider such <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato"><b>physical bonds</b></a> made of iron or wood to be truly strong? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato">SN 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato"><b>strong bond</b></a> that keeps a person captive is not a metal chain, but rather their own infatuation with jewelry and precious ornaments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato">SN 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the psychological <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato"><b>longing for sons</b></a> and wives as a bond that is far more difficult for a person to break than any physical shackle made of rope or wood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato">SN 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the bonds of attachment and desire as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato"><b>dragging down</b></a> the individual, noting that although these ties might seem loose or lax, they are incredibly difficult to get free of? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato">SN 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that true seekers of freedom succeed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato"><b>cutting the bond</b></a> of household attachment and wandering forth while disregarding sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato">SN 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a non-violent sacrifice is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato"><b>better for the sacrificer</b></a> and never worse, as it avoids the negative consequences associated with taking life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.9/en/sujato">SN 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person manages to break the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato"><b>strong bonds</b></a> of desire for wealth and family, they are finally able to leave behind the pursuit of sensual happiness for a higher spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato">SN 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that even if someone is physically imprisoned with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato"><b>chains and stocks</b></a>, their greatest entrapment remains their inner craving for worldly possessions and relations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn3.10/en/sujato">SN 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>aging</b></a> as the frailty, broken teeth, grey hair, and wrinkling of skin that occurs for various beings within their respective orders? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>death</b></a> as the passing away of beings, the breaking of the aggregates, and the laying down of the carcass? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a> involves the manifestation of the aggregates and the acquisition of the sense bases within various orders of beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are three distinct types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a>: sense-sphere existence, form-sphere existence, and formless-sphere existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four specific types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a>: clinging to sensual pleasures, clinging to views, clinging to rules and observances, and clinging to a doctrine of self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described six classes of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a>, which include craving for forms, sounds, odors, tastes, tangibles, and mental phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> as consisting of six classes born of contact, such as feeling born of eye-contact and feeling born of mind-contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> consists of six classes corresponding to the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>six sense bases</b></a> as the eye-base, ear-base, nose-base, tongue-base, body-base, and mind-base? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>name</b></a> within the context of "name-and-form" as the combination of feeling, perception, volition, contact, and attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> as being composed of the four great elements and the material form derived from those four elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified six classes of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a>, which include eye-consciousness, ear-consciousness, nose-consciousness, tongue-consciousness, body-consciousness, and mind-consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are three types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a>: the bodily volitional formation, the verbal volitional formation, and the mental volitional formation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> as the lack of knowledge regarding suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the way leading to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the entire mass of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> originates through a sequence of conditions starting with ignorance and leading to birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>cessation</b></a> of the entire mass of suffering is achieved through the complete fading away and stopping of ignorance, which subsequently stops volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a> as a condition, aging-and-death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair come into being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha analyzed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> by distinguishing mental factors like perception and intention from the material elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's analysis of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a> illustrates how each factor, such as feeling or craving, serves as a necessary condition for the next factor in the chain of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato"><b>doctrine of self</b></a> as one of the fundamental types of clinging that binds a person to the cycle of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.2/en/sujato">SN 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato"><b>final words</b></a> to his followers were an exhortation that all conditioned things are subject to decay and that they should strive for the goal with diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato">SN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha attained parinibbāna at Kusināra in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato"><b>sal grove</b></a> of the Mallans, specifically while resting between the twin sāl trees? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato">SN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that immediately before his passing, the Buddha entered a meditative state known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception and feeling</b></a> after ascending through all four jhānas and the four formless spheres? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato">SN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha passed away immediately after emerging from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a> for the second time during his final meditative process? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato">SN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahma Sahampati declared that all beings in the world must eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato"><b>lay down the carcass</b></a>, noting that even a peerless Teacher and Tathāgata like the Buddha is subject to this universal law? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato">SN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the lord of the devas, reflected on the Buddha's passing by stating that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato"><b>conditioned things</b></a> are impermanent and that their stilling is bliss? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato">SN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda described the moment of the Buddha's parinibbāna as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato"><b>terrifying event</b></a> that was enough to make one's hair stand on end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato">SN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha observed that there was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato"><b>no more breathing</b></a> in the Buddha as he attained parinibbāna with a steady and unperturbed mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato">SN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha compared the final <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato"><b>deliverance of the mind</b></a> of the Buddha to the extinguishing of a lamp? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato">SN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha stated the Buddha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato"><b>endured pain</b></a> with a mind that did not shrink as he approached his final peace and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato">SN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's final journey involved a complex <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato"><b>meditative sequence</b></a> where he ascended from the first jhāna to the cessation of perception and feeling, descended back to the first jhāna, and then ascended again to the fourth jhāna before passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.15/en/sujato">SN 6.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to a distressed brahmin that he is truly happy because he does not have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>fourteen oxen</b></a> that have been missing for six days, a loss that would otherwise cause great anxiety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the lack of a blighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>sesame field</b></a>—one where the plants have only one or two leaves—as a specific reason for his enduring happiness and peace of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that he is happy because he does not have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>mice</b></a> dancing in an empty granary, a situation that represents the desperation of a householder with no food storage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that his peace comes from not possessing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>seven-month-old pallet</b></a> infested with vermin, which would make rest impossible for a worldly person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a brahmin he is happy because he is not burdened by having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>seven widowed daughters</b></a>, each with children, who require constant support and care? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his freedom from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>squint-eyed wife</b></a> who might disturb his sleep by poking him with her foot as a contributing factor to his tranquil state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he lives happily because he is not harassed at the break of day by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>creditors</b></a> demanding that he pay off his debts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a brahmin of the Bharadvaja clan once came across the Buddha in a wood and observed him sitting cross-legged with his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>body erect</b></a> and mindfulness established before him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s peaceful presence in a forest once prompted a man searching for lost property to compose verses about why a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>secluded ascetic</b></a> might be happier than a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha accepted a brahmin into the Sangha who was so impressed by the Dhamma that he compared the teaching to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>setting upright</b></a> what had been overturned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha granted both the "going forth" and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>higher ordination</b></a> to a member of the Bharadvaja clan who had initially approached him while looking for his lost oxen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a former brahmin, after receiving guidance from the Buddha, lived alone and diligent until he became one of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>arahants</b></a>, realizing the supreme goal of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches that the end of suffering involves the personal realization that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a> and the holy life has been lived to completion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s interaction with a distressed farmer shows how he can turn a conversation about <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>lost property</b></a> into a profound lesson on the benefits of renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remained in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>Kosalan country</b></a> in a certain wood where he manifested a state of peace that stood in stark contrast to the worries of the local villagers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness established</b></a> in front of oneself as a key characteristic of his meditative practice in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.10/en/sujato">SN 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, when challenged by a brahmin farmer to plow and sow for his food, explained that in his spiritual practice, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato"><b>faith is the seed</b></a> and asceticism is the rain that allows his "crops" to grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato">SN 7.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of agricultural tools to describe the path to enlightenment, characterizing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato"><b>wisdom as the yoke and plow</b></a> that he uses to cultivate the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato">SN 7.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the mental faculty of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness as the plowshare and goad</b></a> in his metaphorical spiritual farming, which ultimately leads to the destruction of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato">SN 7.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato"><b>sense of shame</b></a> (hiri) as the "pole" of his spiritual plow and the mind as the "rein" that directs his internal work? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato">SN 7.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato"><b>energy is his draught-ox</b></a>, which carries the practitioner toward "security from bondage," moving forward without ever turning back until the goal is reached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato">SN 7.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the brahmin Kasi Bharadvaja that his method of "farming" results in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato"><b>Deathless as its fruit</b></a>, a state that once attained, liberates a person from all sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato">SN 7.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha strictly maintained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato"><b>food chanted over</b></a> with verses is not to be eaten by him, stating that enlightened ones do not accept a livelihood based on the recitation of verses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato">SN 7.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the importance of ethical restraint in his spiritual "farming" by being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato"><b>guarded in body and speech</b></a> and strictly moderate in his intake of food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato">SN 7.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha metaphorically described his process of clearing mental defilements by stating that he makes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato"><b>truth his weeding</b></a>, using honesty to remove the "weeds" from his field of practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato">SN 7.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that those seeking merit should provide food and drink to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato"><b>consummate Great Seer</b></a> who is free from taints and whose remorse has been stilled, as such an individual is the ideal "field" for those looking for spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.11/en/sujato">SN 7.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> is a conditioned process that begins when a sense faculty like the eye and its corresponding object, such as visual forms, meet to give rise to eye-consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato">SN 12.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the coincidence of the sense base, the sense object, and the resulting sense-consciousness as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a>, which serves as the necessary foundation for the arising of feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato">SN 12.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the sequence leading to human distress involves contact conditioning feeling, and with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato"><b>feeling as condition</b></a>, craving inevitably arises to form the basis of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato">SN 12.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the "passing away" or ending of suffering is initiated through the remainderless fading away and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato"><b>cessation of craving</b></a>, which breaks the chain of dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato">SN 12.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato"><b>cessation of clinging</b></a>—which results from the ending of craving—is the specific cause that leads to the cessation of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato">SN 12.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato"><b>cessation of existence</b></a> occurs, it prevents the arising of birth, thereby stopping the cycle of rebirth and its attendant pains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato">SN 12.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato"><b>cessation of birth</b></a> as the turning point that brings about the complete cessation of aging and death, as well as sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato">SN 12.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato"><b>passing away of suffering</b></a> to describe the total ending of the "whole mass of suffering" that occurs when the internal conditions for that suffering are systematically dismantled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato">SN 12.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that suffering does not only arise from physical senses but also when the mind and mental phenomena meet to produce <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato"><b>mind-consciousness</b></a>, following the same pattern of contact, feeling, and craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato">SN 12.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that the path to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> is found by understanding how craving arises from the various types of consciousness—including ear, nose, tongue, and body consciousness—and learning to bring that craving to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.43/en/sujato">SN 12.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while staying at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato"><b>Brick Hall</b></a> in Ñātika, taught that eye-consciousness arises dependent on the eye and visual forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato">SN 12.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> as the meeting of three things: a sense base (like the ear), its corresponding object (like sounds), and the resulting sense-consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato">SN 12.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> as a chain of conditions where contact leads to feeling, feeling leads to craving, and craving leads to clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato">SN 12.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the total <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato"><b>cessation of craving</b></a> is the necessary step to end clinging, which in turn stops the cycle of existence and birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato">SN 12.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato"><b>birth ceases</b></a>, aging, death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair also cease, ending the entire mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato">SN 12.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once discovered a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato"><b>standing in range</b></a> of his voice while he was reciting a Dhamma discourse to himself in solitary seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato">SN 12.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged a monk to master the discourse on dependent origination because it is highly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato"><b>beneficial</b></a> and fundamental to the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato">SN 12.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato"><b>mind-consciousness</b></a> arises dependent on the mind and mental phenomena, following the same conditional pattern as the physical senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato">SN 12.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed a Lokāyatika Brahmin that the view "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato"><b>all exists</b></a>" is considered the first or primary cosmology of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato">SN 12.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the view "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato"><b>all does not exist</b></a>" as the second major cosmology, which he avoids by teaching a middle path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato">SN 12.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified the belief that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato"><b>all is a unity</b></a>" (oneness) as the third major cosmological theory of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato">SN 12.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the view that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato"><b>all is a plurality</b></a>" as the fourth cosmological theory, which he rejects in favor of the middle way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato">SN 12.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches the Dhamma by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato"><b>middle way</b></a>, which avoids extremes of existence and non-existence by focusing on dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato">SN 12.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> arise with ignorance as their condition, serving as a primary link in the production of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato">SN 12.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato"><b>cessation of ignorance</b></a> leads directly to the cessation of volitional formations, which eventually leads to the ending of the whole mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato">SN 12.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's explanation of the middle way, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato"><b>Lokāyatika Brahmin</b></a> went for refuge and became a lay follower for life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato">SN 12.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the entire <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato"><b>mass of suffering</b></a> arises through a series of conditions beginning with ignorance and ending with aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato">SN 12.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his teaching as "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato"><b>fundamental to the holy life</b></a>" when explaining how the six sense bases lead to the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato">SN 12.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the phrase "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato"><b>passing away</b></a>" (nirodha) to describe the ending of the whole mass of suffering through the systematic cessation of its conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.45/en/sujato">SN 12.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the sequence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a> explains the world's existence without resorting to the extremes of "is" or "is not"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.48/en/sujato">SN 12.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how Vipassī Bodhisatta, before his enlightenment, reflected on how the world is fallen into trouble because it <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>undergoes birth</b></a>, aging, death, and rebirth without knowing the escape from this suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Vipassī Bodhisatta achieved a breakthrough in understanding through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>thorough attention</b></a> (yoniso manasikāra), which allowed him to see the conditional causes of the human predicament? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that Vipassī Bodhisatta realized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a> can only occur when there is birth as a necessary supporting condition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that Vipassī Bodhisatta identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a> (bhava) as the specific condition that must be present for birth to take place? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how Vipassī Bodhisatta discovered that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> (upādāna) is the underlying condition that leads to the entire process of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that Vipassī Bodhisatta found <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> (taṇhā) to be the direct condition that gives rise to clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Vipassī Bodhisatta saw that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> (vedanā) serves as the necessary condition for craving to arise in a being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared that Vipassī Bodhisatta realized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> (phassa) is the essential condition required for the arising of any feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that Vipassī Bodhisatta traced the condition for contact back to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>six sense bases</b></a> (saḷāyatana)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that Vipassī Bodhisatta understood <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> (nāmarūpa) to be the condition that allows the six sense bases to function? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Vipassī Bodhisatta discovered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> (viññāṇa) is the condition upon which name-and-form depends? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how Vipassī Bodhisatta realized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> (saṅkhāra) are the necessary condition that gives rise to consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that Vipassī Bodhisatta identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> (avijjā) as the ultimate condition for the arising of volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that through his discovery of the chain of dependent origination, Vipassī Bodhisatta saw the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> for the whole mass of human distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that for Vipassī Bodhisatta, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>vision arose</b></a> (cakkhuṃ udapādi) regarding things never heard before once he understood the origin of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that Vipassī Bodhisatta also investigated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> by asking what must be absent for aging and death to finally end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Vipassī Bodhisatta realized that with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>cessation of birth</b></a>, aging and death are also brought to an absolute end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that Vipassī Bodhisatta saw how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>cessation of existence</b></a> results in the cessation of birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how Vipassī Bodhisatta understood that through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>cessation of clinging</b></a>, the entire process of existence is halted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that Vipassī Bodhisatta recognized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>cessation of craving</b></a> as the necessary step to end clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Vipassī Bodhisatta realized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>cessation of feeling</b></a> leads directly to the cessation of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how Vipassī Bodhisatta saw that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>cessation of contact</b></a> causes feeling to cease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared that Vipassī Bodhisatta understood the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>cessation of name-and-form</b></a> as the reason why the six sense bases cease to exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that Vipassī Bodhisatta realized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>cessation of ignorance</b></a> results in the cessation of volitional formations, which eventually ends the whole mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that when Vipassī Bodhisatta understood the cessation of suffering, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato"><b>light arose</b></a> (āloko udapādi) in him regarding truths that had never been known before? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.4/en/sujato">SN 12.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should engage in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>thorough investigation</b></a> to understand how various forms of suffering, such as aging and death, originate in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that aging and death arise because of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a> as their necessary condition, and that in the absence of birth, aging and death would not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a> as the specific condition that must be present for birth to take place? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the underlying condition for existence is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a>, which a monk must investigate to understand the path to the destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a>—the mental constructs that drive rebirth—originate from ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if a person under the influence of ignorance constructs a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>meritorious volitional formation</b></a>, their consciousness proceeds to a meritorious destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person constructs a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>demeritorious volitional formation</b></a> while immersed in ignorance, their consciousness will proceed to a demeritorious destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that if a person constructs an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>imperturbable volitional formation</b></a> while in a state of ignorance, their consciousness will proceed to an imperturbable destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk abandons ignorance and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge</b></a> arises, he no longer constructs meritorious, demeritorious, or imperturbable volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who no longer constructs volitional formations does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>cling to anything</b></a> in the world, and by not clinging, he is not agitated and attains personal Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who has reached true knowledge understands that any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>pleasant feeling</b></a> he experiences is impermanent and should not be delighted in or held to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a liberated monk understands a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>painful feeling</b></a> is impermanent and experiences it without being "yoked" to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an arahant views a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling</b></a> as impermanent, experiencing it with full awareness of its conditional nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk with true knowledge experiences all feelings—pleasant, painful, or neutral—while being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>detached</b></a> from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a monk understands he feels a feeling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>terminating with life</b></a>, knowing that with the breakup of the body, all that is felt will become cool? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>simile of a pot</b></a> removed from a kiln to explain how the "heat" of feelings subsides while only the "shards" (the physical body) remain after an arahant passes away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked his monks to confirm that if volitional formations were to cease entirely, then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> would no longer be manifest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that with the total cessation of consciousness, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> would not be manifest at all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed with his disciples that if name-and-form were to cease completely, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>six sense bases</b></a> would no longer be manifest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that with the total cessation of the six sense bases, the phenomenon of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> would no longer be experienced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if contact were to cease entirely, then the arising of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> would not be manifest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that with the total cessation of feeling, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> would no longer be manifest, thereby breaking the chain of dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his monks to have faith and be free from doubt that the cessation of the entire chain of conditions is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.51/en/sujato">SN 12.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato"><b>gain, honor, and praise</b></a> to a "dreadful" and "harsh" obstacle that prevents one from reaching the unexcelled security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato">SN 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the parable of the tortoise, the Buddha explained that the term "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato"><b>hunter</b></a>" is actually a designation for Mara the Evil One, who seeks to capture the unwary? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato">SN 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who relishes and harks after worldly recognition is like a tortoise pierced by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato"><b>harpoon</b></a>, which serves as a metaphor for gain, honor, and praise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato">SN 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the "string" trailing behind a wounded tortoise as a metaphor for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato"><b>delight and lust</b></a>, which allows the "hunter" (Mara) to lead a person to ruin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato">SN 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that any monk whose heart is overcome by gain and fame becomes someone who can be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato"><b>disposed of</b></a> by the Evil One according to his whim? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato">SN 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a story where a tortoise was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato"><b>disowned by his family</b></a> because he ignored their warnings and returned wounded by a harpoon, representing the irreversible damage of being caught in the search for worldly honor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato">SN 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato"><b>train themselves</b></a> specifically to abandon gain, honor, and praise as soon as they arise, so that such desires do not take possession of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.3/en/sujato">SN 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk overwhelmed by the desire for status to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.4/en/sujato"><b>long-haired ewe</b></a> who enters a thorny thicket and becomes hopelessly entangled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.4/en/sujato">SN 17.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk with an "overpowered mind" will find himself <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.4/en/sujato"><b>caught and bound</b></a> in various places when he enters a village or town to collect alms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.4/en/sujato">SN 17.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that gain, honor, and praise are a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.4/en/sujato"><b>bitter obstacle</b></a>" to the attainment of spiritual success, just as thorns are an obstacle to a long-haired sheep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.4/en/sujato">SN 17.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even the act of dressing and taking up one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.4/en/sujato"><b>bowl and robe</b></a> for alms can lead to spiritual disaster if the monk is already "mastered" by the pursuit of fame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.4/en/sujato">SN 17.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly instructed that a monk should practice so that gain, honor, and praise do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.4/en/sujato"><b>take possession</b></a> of his heart, because they are so destructive to the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.4/en/sujato">SN 17.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who is arrogant because of the material gifts he receives to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.5/en/sujato"><b>dung-eating beetle</b></a> that, being full of dung, looks down on other beetles because he has a large pile of dung in front of him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.5/en/sujato">SN 17.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who is "overwhelmed and obsessed in mind" by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.5/en/sujato"><b>gain, honor, and praise</b></a> may look down on other virtuous monks simply because he has received plenty of food and invitations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.5/en/sujato">SN 17.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who, after eating as much as he wants and securing an invitation for the next day, returns to the monastery to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.5/en/sujato"><b>boast</b></a> before his fellow monks about his success in collecting alms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.5/en/sujato">SN 17.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who prides himself on being a recipient of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.5/en/sujato"><b>requisites</b></a>—such as robes, almsfood, and medicine—while disparaging others as having "little merit," is acting in a way that leads to his long-term harm and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.5/en/sujato">SN 17.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who allows himself to be mastered by worldly recognition will eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.5/en/sujato"><b>attain contempt</b></a> for other well-behaved monks, mimicking the behavior of a beetle laden with dung? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.5/en/sujato">SN 17.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.6/en/sujato"><b>thunderbolt</b></a>" as a designation for gain, honor, and praise because of how dangerously they can strike a trainee who has not yet reached the goal of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.6/en/sujato">SN 17.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.6/en/sujato"><b>gain, honor, and praise</b></a> are a "dreadful, harsh, and bitter obstacle" to the attainment of the unexcelled security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.6/en/sujato">SN 17.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers that they must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.6/en/sujato"><b>train themselves</b></a> to abandon any gain or fame that arises so that it does not take possession of their hearts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.6/en/sujato">SN 17.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to gain, honor, and praise as a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.7/en/sujato"><b>poisoned arrow</b></a>," emphasizing the toxic effect these worldly rewards can have on a person's spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.7/en/sujato">SN 17.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that the word "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.7/en/sujato"><b>arrow</b></a>" is a metaphor specifically used to describe the pursuit of gain, honor, and praise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.7/en/sujato">SN 17.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed individuals who, because their minds were "overwhelmed and obsessed" by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato"><b>honor</b></a>, were reborn after death in a state of misery, a bad destination, or even in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato">SN 17.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato"><b>lack of honor</b></a> can lead to rebirth in hell if one's mind becomes obsessed and overcome by the frustration or resentment it causes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato">SN 17.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified that being overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato"><b>both honor and lack of honor</b></a> can lead a person to a "downfall" and rebirth in a "bad destination"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato">SN 17.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato"><b>good man</b></a>" as one whose concentration remains unshaken regardless of whether he is being honored or treated with a lack of honor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato">SN 17.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a true practitioner as one who dwells in the "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato"><b>measureless</b></a>" (appamāṇavihārino), maintaining a steady mind through both success and failure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato">SN 17.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the practitioner who is a constant and subtle <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato"><b>meditator</b></a>, seeing things clearly with insight into views while others are distracted by status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato">SN 17.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the phrase "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato"><b>delight in the destruction of clinging</b></a>" (upādānakkhayārāmaṃ) to describe the primary focus of a person who is not swayed by worldly praise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato">SN 17.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> does not waver in the face of both honor and its absence, they are following the path of the "good man"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato">SN 17.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that he could see into the future of individuals based on their reaction to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato"><b>honor and lack of honor</b></a>, knowing which minds were bound for a "state of misery"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.10/en/sujato">SN 17.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's repeated refrain was that his followers should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.5/en/sujato"><b>abandon</b></a> gain, honor, and praise because these things are "dreadful" and prevent one from reaching spiritual success? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn17.5/en/sujato">SN 17.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was once asked by the Venerable Rahula to provide a brief teaching so that Rahula could dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato"><b>alone and secluded</b></a>, remaining diligent, ardent, and resolute in his spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato">SN 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind are all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>, and because they are impermanent, they are ultimately a source of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato">SN 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Rahula that it is not fit to regard any of the six internal sense bases—such as the eye or the mind—as "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato"><b>this is mine</b></a>," "this I am," or "this is my self," because they are subject to change and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato">SN 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a well-taught noble disciple who truly sees the nature of the sense bases becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato">SN 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, once a practitioner becomes disenchanted with the sense bases, they become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato"><b>dispassionate</b></a>, and through that dispassion, their mind is liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato">SN 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person's mind is liberated, the knowledge arises that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a>," the holy life has been lived, and there is no more returning to any state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato">SN 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was asked by Rahula for a teaching on how to know and see so that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato"><b>I-making and mine-making</b></a> no longer occur regarding one's own body and all external signs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato">SN 18.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that to eliminate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to conceit</b></a>, one must see all forms—whether past, future, or present, internal or external—as "This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato">SN 18.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that every kind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>, whether it is gross or subtle, inferior or superior, or far or near, should be viewed with correct wisdom as not-self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato">SN 18.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the practice of seeing things as not-self must be applied to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a>, volitional formations, and consciousness to prevent the arising of egoistic attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato">SN 18.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato"><b>body with consciousness</b></a>" (saviññāṇake kāye) to describe the physical form that people often mistakenly identify as their permanent identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato">SN 18.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that seeing things as they really are with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato"><b>correct wisdom</b></a> (sammappaññāya) is the specific method required to stop the mental habit of "I-making" regarding external objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato">SN 18.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that anything characterized as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato"><b>subject to change</b></a> (vipariṇāmadhammaṃ) is inherently incapable of being a true, permanent self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.1/en/sujato">SN 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s guidance to Rahula focused on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a>—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—as the key areas where one must practice non-attachment to achieve liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.21/en/sujato">SN 18.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the Venerable Rahula that when one sees all form with correct wisdom as "this is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self," the mind becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.22/en/sujato"><b>peaceful and well liberated</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.22/en/sujato">SN 18.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Rahula that to rid the mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.22/en/sujato"><b>I-making and mine-making</b></a> regarding the body and external signs, one must view all five aggregates as not-self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.22/en/sujato">SN 18.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that whether a form is past, future, or present, internal or external, or even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.22/en/sujato"><b>far or near</b></a>, it should be seen with correct wisdom as "this is not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.22/en/sujato">SN 18.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a state of mind that has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.22/en/sujato"><b>transcended discrimination</b></a> and is rid of the underlying tendency to conceit through the practice of seeing the truth of the aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.22/en/sujato">SN 18.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who sees the true nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.22/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> and the other aggregates as "this is not mine" becomes "liberated through non-clinging"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn18.22/en/sujato">SN 18.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.1/en/sujato"><b>peaked house</b></a> to explain that just as all rafters meet at the roof peak, all unwholesome states are rooted in ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.1/en/sujato">SN 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.1/en/sujato"><b>roof peak</b></a> of a house is pulled down, all the rafters are likewise pulled down, symbolizing how abolishing ignorance destroys all unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.1/en/sujato">SN 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.1/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> as the common convergence point and root for every unwholesome state that arises in the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.1/en/sujato">SN 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because of the danger of unwholesome states rooted in ignorance, the Buddha exhorted his monks to train themselves by vowing, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.1/en/sujato"><b>we will dwell diligent</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.1/en/sujato">SN 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once picked up a tiny amount of soil on the tip of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.2/en/sujato"><b>fingernail</b></a> to illustrate a profound truth about the rarity of human rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.2/en/sujato">SN 20.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the tiny amount of soil on his fingernail to the entire <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.2/en/sujato"><b>great earth</b></a> to show how few beings are actually reborn as human beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.2/en/sujato">SN 20.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are reborn as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.2/en/sujato"><b>human beings</b></a> are as few as the grains of dust on a fingernail, while those reborn in other realms are as numerous as the soil of the earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.2/en/sujato">SN 20.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the comparison of the earth and a fingernail to emphasize that beings reborn <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.2/en/sujato"><b>outside the human realm</b></a> are far more numerous than those who regain human status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.2/en/sujato">SN 20.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because the opportunity for human rebirth is so rare, the Buddha urged his followers to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.2/en/sujato"><b>train themselves</b></a> specifically to live with diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.2/en/sujato">SN 20.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s recurring advice for making the most of a rare human life was that one should practice so they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.2/en/sujato"><b>dwell diligent</b></a> and avoid falling into states of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.2/en/sujato">SN 20.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained he managed to cross the flood of existence specifically by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.1/en/sujato"><b>not standing still</b></a> and not struggling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.1/en/sujato">SN 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if he stood still while in the flood of samsara, he would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.1/en/sujato"><b>sink</b></a>, but if he struggled, he would be swept away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.1/en/sujato">SN 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity praised the Buddha as a "Brahmin" who had attained Nibbana because he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.1/en/sujato"><b>crossed the clinger</b></a> in the world by neither standing still nor struggling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.1/en/sujato">SN 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked about deliverance and detachment, the Buddha stated that it is known through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.2/en/sujato"><b>exhaustion of delight</b></a> in being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.2/en/sujato">SN 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the destruction of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.2/en/sujato"><b>perception and consciousness</b></a> as a key component in understanding the deliverance and seclusion of beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.2/en/sujato">SN 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.2/en/sujato"><b>cessation of feelings</b></a> and their subsiding is how one knows the deliverance and detachment of beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.2/en/sujato">SN 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity cautioned the Buddha that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.3/en/sujato"><b>life is led on</b></a> and the human life span is short, leaving no shelter for one who is being brought to old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.3/en/sujato">SN 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while a deity suggested that one fearing death should perform meritorious deeds, the Buddha taught that one seeking peace should instead <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.3/en/sujato"><b>abandon worldly bait</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.3/en/sujato">SN 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.3/en/sujato"><b>seeking peace</b></a>, observing the fear inherent in death should lead them to let go of the lures of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.3/en/sujato">SN 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity observed to the Buddha that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.4/en/sujato"><b>time passes</b></a> and nights fly past, with the stages of life leaving us in succession? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.4/en/sujato">SN 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.4/en/sujato"><b>mindful of the fear</b></a> in death should give up the "bait" of the world to find lasting peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.4/en/sujato">SN 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.5/en/sujato"><b>cut five</b></a> things (the lower fetters) and abandon another five (the higher fetters) to progress toward liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.5/en/sujato">SN 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a monk should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.5/en/sujato"><b>further develop five</b></a> qualities (the spiritual faculties) to successfully cross the flood of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.5/en/sujato">SN 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is only called "one who has crossed the flood" after he has successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.5/en/sujato"><b>surmounted five attachments</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.5/en/sujato">SN 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha presented a riddle stating that there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.6/en/sujato"><b>five who are asleep</b></a> while others are awake, and five who are awake while others sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.6/en/sujato">SN 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that there are five things through which a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.6/en/sujato"><b>picks up dust</b></a> (defilement) and five things through which they are purified? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.6/en/sujato">SN 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that while most people are awake to worldly concerns, they are actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.6/en/sujato"><b>asleep while awake</b></a> in regard to spiritual realities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.6/en/sujato">SN 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that certain spiritual seekers are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.6/en/sujato"><b>awake while others sleep</b></a> because they have overcome the hindrances that cloud the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.6/en/sujato">SN 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that one is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.6/en/sujato"><b>completely purified</b></a> by five spiritual faculties which counteract the five things that gather mental "dust"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.6/en/sujato">SN 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.1/en/sujato"><b>crossing the flood</b></a> focuses on a "middle way" of effort that avoids both the passivity of standing still and the agitation of over-struggling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.1/en/sujato">SN 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.14/en/sujato"><b>Fully Enlightened One</b></a> to be the best of bipeds, in contrast to a deity's claim that the noble (Khattiya) held that position? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.14/en/sujato">SN 1.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while a deity considered a young bride to be the best of wives, the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.14/en/sujato"><b>service</b></a> (sussūsā) is actually the best quality in a wife? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.14/en/sujato">SN 1.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha felt <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.15/en/sujato"><b>delight</b></a> in the rustling of the great forest at high noon when birds are quiet, whereas a deity found that same silence and sound to be fearful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.15/en/sujato">SN 1.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity pointed out that sleep, drowsiness, yawning, discontent, and food-drunkenness are the reasons the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.16/en/sujato"><b>noble path</b></a> does not manifest to living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.16/en/sujato">SN 1.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by dispelling drowsiness and discontent through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.16/en/sujato"><b>energy</b></a> (vīriya), the noble path is purified and becomes clear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.16/en/sujato">SN 1.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk withdrawing his mental thoughts to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.17/en/sujato"><b>tortoise</b></a> drawing its limbs into its own shell to protect itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.17/en/sujato">SN 1.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that those who are restrained by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.18/en/sujato"><b>shame</b></a> (hiri) and are always mindful are the ones who reach the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.18/en/sujato">SN 1.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a metaphorical dialogue with a deity, the Buddha explained that he considers a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.19/en/sujato"><b>mother</b></a> to be like a "little hut" that a spiritual seeker eventually leaves behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.19/en/sujato">SN 1.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.19/en/sujato"><b>nest</b></a> as a metaphor for a wife when explaining to a deity why he no longer possessed worldly attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.19/en/sujato">SN 1.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described children as metaphorical <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.19/en/sujato"><b>lines</b></a> or "cobwebs" (santānaka) that entangle a person, explaining his own freedom from such ties to a deity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.19/en/sujato">SN 1.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.19/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> (taṇhā) as the actual bond that keeps a person from being truly free, according to his conversation with a deity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.19/en/sujato">SN 1.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi was approached by a deity at dawn while he was drying himself after bathing at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>Tapodā Hot Springs</b></a> in Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity urged Venerable Samiddhi to enjoy food and life, telling him, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>don’t let the time pass</b></a> you by," because he was begging for food without having already eaten? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi explained his urgency in spiritual practice to a deity by stating that he did not know when he would die, as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>time is hidden</b></a> and cannot be seen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity tried to persuade Venerable Samiddhi to leave the monkhood by pointing out he was still young, black-haired, and in the prime of youth, and should therefore enjoy <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>human sensual pleasures</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi told a deity that he was not abandoning the present for the future, but was actually abandoning sensual pleasures because the Buddha described them as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>subject to time</b></a>, full of suffering and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi described the Dhamma (the Buddha's teaching) as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>visible</b></a> here and now, rather than being something one must wait for a future time to experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi argued that the Buddha's Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>timeless</b></a> (akāliko), providing results in the present moment rather than being subject to the passage of time like worldly pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi characterized the Dhamma as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>inviting one to come and see</b></a> (ehipassiko), meaning it is a teaching that welcomes investigation and personal verification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Samiddhi, the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>leading onward</b></a> (opaneyyiko), guiding the practitioner toward the ultimate goal of liberation from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi emphasized that the Dhamma is a path that must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>personally realized by the wise</b></a> for themselves, rather than being something one can experience through another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity told Venerable Samiddhi she found it difficult to approach the Buddha because he was constantly surrounded by other <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>high-ranking deities</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings who are caught up in "the expressible" (akkheyya)—or things that can be named—fall under <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>Death’s yoke</b></a> because they do not fully understand the nature of those things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person fully understands <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>the expressible</b></a>, they no longer identify with the notion of being a "speaker" or an owner of those words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed a deity that if a person does not fully understand the nature of names and concepts, they will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>not conceive of a speaker</b></a> in a way that leads to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that anyone who still considers themselves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>equal, superior, or inferior</b></a> to others would, for that reason, get into disputes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who remains unshakeable in the three modes of conceit (equality, superiority, and inferiority) succeeds in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>avoiding disputes</b></a> with others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a liberated person as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>abandoned reckoning</b></a> (saṅkhaṃ) and has not fallen back into conceit or pride? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a true sage is someone who has cut off craving for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> (nāmarūpe) in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>gods and humans cannot find</b></a> or trace the destination of a person who has cut the bonds of craving and identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's verses, a deity summarized the teaching by saying one should do no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>evil with speech, mind, or body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity, expressing her understanding to the Buddha, concluded that one should abandon sensual pleasures and, being mindful and clearly comprehending, avoid <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato"><b>unbeneficial suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.20/en/sujato">SN 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after his enlightenment, the Buddha reflected on his liberation from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato"><b>austere practices</b></a>, realizing they were unbeneficial and did not lead to the goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato">SN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Mara the Evil One challenged his path, the Buddha declared that any practice of austerity aimed at immortality that is unbeneficial is as useless as an oar or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato"><b>rudder on dry land</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato">SN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Mara that he reached supreme purity not through self-mortification, but by developing the path of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue, concentration, and wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato">SN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One once tried to convince the Buddha that he had missed the true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato"><b>path to purity</b></a> by turning away from his previous life of painful austerities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato">SN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Mara as the "End-maker" and declared that Mara was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato"><b>utterly defeated</b></a> once the Buddha attained enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato">SN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at the foot of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato"><b>Ajapala Banyan tree</b></a> on the bank of the river Nerañjara when he first reflected on his relief at gaining enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato">SN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was once sitting in the open air during the pitch darkness of the night while a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.2/en/sujato"><b>drizzling rain</b></a> was falling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.2/en/sujato">SN 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One once manifested himself as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.2/en/sujato"><b>huge king elephant</b></a> with tusks like pure silver in a failed attempt to cause the Buddha fear and dread? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.2/en/sujato">SN 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recognized Mara's disguise as an elephant and told him that even though Mara has created both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.2/en/sujato"><b>beautiful and ugly forms</b></a> throughout the long course of samsara, he remains defeated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.2/en/sujato">SN 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed Mara manifesting an elephant's trunk that was as large as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.2/en/sujato"><b>giant plow-pole</b></a> to try to intimidate him in the darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.2/en/sujato">SN 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One is known to disappear in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.2/en/sujato"><b>sorrow and dejection</b></a> whenever he realizes the Buddha has seen through his supernatural disguises? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.2/en/sujato">SN 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara once approached the Buddha and displayed various <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.3/en/sujato"><b>beautiful and ugly colors</b></a> and appearances in an attempt to stir up hair-raising terror? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.3/en/sujato">SN 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are well-restrained in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.3/en/sujato"><b>body, speech, and mind</b></a> are not followers of Mara’s power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.3/en/sujato">SN 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that practitioners who maintain perfect restraint are no longer caught in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.3/en/sujato"><b>Mara's bond</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.3/en/sujato">SN 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confronted Mara by stating he has wandered a long time through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.3/en/sujato"><b>samsara</b></a> assuming different appearances, but his efforts to frighten an Enlightened One are in vain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.3/en/sujato">SN 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s first thought after enlightenment was a sense of gratitude for having attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato"><b>bodhi</b></a> and leaving behind fruitless strivings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato">SN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara attempted to tempt the Buddha back into traditional asceticism by claiming that one does not achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato"><b>purity</b></a> by moving away from painful austere practices? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato">SN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was confronted by Mara the Evil One after Mara became aware of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of the Buddha's mind</b></a> regarding his liberation from unbeneficial austerities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.1/en/sujato">SN 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once endured severe, racking, and piercing physical pain from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato"><b>stone splinter</b></a> that had cut his foot, yet he remained mindful and self-possessed without being distressed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato">SN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha's foot was seriously injured by a stone, he lay down on his right side in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato"><b>lion’s posture</b></a>, placing one foot on the other while remaining mindful and clearly self-possessed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato">SN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One once approached the Buddha while he was resting and asked if he was lying down in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato"><b>stupor</b></a> or as if intoxicated by poetry, questioning why he was sleeping alone in a secluded place? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato">SN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Mara that he lies down full of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato"><b>compassion for all beings</b></a>, having reached his goal and become entirely free from sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato">SN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Mara that unlike those whose hearts are constantly pierced by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato"><b>dart</b></a> of craving or suffering, he can sleep peacefully because he has removed that dart entirely? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato">SN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared he does not fear sleeping nor is he troubled by wakefulness, as he sees no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato"><b>loss in the world</b></a> that could cause him regret or anxiety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato">SN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was teaching a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.14/en/sujato"><b>large assembly of laypeople</b></a> in the brahmin village of Ekasālā, Mara the Evil One approached him with the intent to confuse or distract him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.14/en/sujato">SN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One once criticized the Buddha, claiming it was not fitting for him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.14/en/sujato"><b>instruct others</b></a> because doing so might lead to entanglement in likes and dislikes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.14/en/sujato">SN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Mara that the Tathāgata is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.14/en/sujato"><b>completely free</b></a> from both favor and opposition, even when he is compassionately teaching the Dhamma to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.14/en/sujato">SN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself to Mara as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.14/en/sujato"><b>compassionate helper</b></a> who instructs others out of a genuine desire for their welfare, remaining detached from the outcome of his teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.14/en/sujato">SN 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One once threatened the Buddha by claiming he had a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.15/en/sujato"><b>mental snare</b></a> that roams the sky, with which he intended to bind and catch the Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.15/en/sujato">SN 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha responded to Mara's threats by declaring that Mara is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.15/en/sujato"><b>utterly defeated</b></a> because the Buddha has transcended the allure of forms, sounds, tastes, smells, and tangibles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.15/en/sujato">SN 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Mara that because he no longer finds delight in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.15/en/sujato"><b>agreeable sensory objects</b></a> like sights and sounds, he is beyond Mara's power to bind or obstruct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.15/en/sujato">SN 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato"><b>Maddakucchi Deer Park</b></a> in Rajagaha when he had to endure the intense physical pain of a wounded foot caused by a stone splinter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.13/en/sujato">SN 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was once instructing a group of monks specifically on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates of clinging</b></a> when Mara the Evil One attempted to disrupt the teaching by manifesting as a large animal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato">SN 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One once assumed the appearance of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato"><b>ox</b></a> and approached a group of almsbowls left in the open air to startle the monks who were listening to the Buddha's discourse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato">SN 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a monk became alarmed that a stray animal might break their bowls, the Buddha identified the intruder as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato"><b>Mara the Evil One</b></a> and explained that Mara had come specifically to create a "blind" or distraction for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato">SN 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught his disciples that one becomes detached from suffering by realizing that form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato"><b>not mine</b></a> and do not constitute a permanent self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato">SN 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person who is detached and secure has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato"><b>surpassed all fetters</b></a>, making them impossible for even Mara's army to find or track? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato">SN 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks listening to the Buddha's teaching on the aggregates were described as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato"><b>lending an ear</b></a> and applying their whole minds to the Dhamma, which is why Mara felt the need to intervene? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato">SN 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One’s primary goal in appearing near the monks’ almsbowls was to perform a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato"><b>blind</b></a> or a deceptive act to break their concentration on the Buddha's words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato">SN 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was residing in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato"><b>Great Wood</b></a> at the Hall with the Peaked Roof in Vesali when Mara the Evil One attempted to terrify the monks with a supernatural noise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato">SN 4.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was delivering a sermon on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato"><b>six bases for contact</b></a>, Mara the Evil One created a terrifying, shattering sound that made it seem as if the earth were splitting open? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato">SN 4.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk once mistakenly cried out to his companions that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato"><b>earth was splitting</b></a> after hearing a deafening roar created by Mara to distract the assembly from the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato">SN 4.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha corrected a startled monk by clarifying that the terrifying sound was actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato"><b>Mara the Evil One</b></a> trying to obstruct their spiritual progress through fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato">SN 4.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified forms, sounds, tastes, smells, contacts, and mental phenomena as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato"><b>terrible bait of the world</b></a> in which the general populace is infatuated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato">SN 4.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a mindful disciple who surmounts the lures of the senses to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato"><b>sun</b></a> because they shine brightly after having transcended Mara's realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato">SN 4.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One found the Buddha's teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato"><b>six bases for contact</b></a> so threatening to his influence that he used a "shattering" supernatural display to stop the monks from listening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato">SN 4.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once a practitioner realizes the five aggregates are not theirs, they become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato"><b>detached</b></a> from the world and achieve a state of safety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato">SN 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the Buddha revealed his identity and recited a verse on detachment, Mara the Evil One <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato"><b>disappeared on the spot</b></a>, overcome with sorrow and dejection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.16/en/sujato">SN 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that mental phenomena are part of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato"><b>sensory baits</b></a> that trap people in the world's infatuations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato">SN 4.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to be mindful so they could <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato"><b>pass beyond Mara’s realm</b></a> and overcome the terrifying lures of the six senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.17/en/sujato">SN 4.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a brahmin observed the Buddha visiting his home multiple times, the Buddha explained that just as farmers <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato"><b>plow the field</b></a> again and again and seeds are sown again and again, many aspects of life and nature are inherently repetitive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato">SN 7.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that givers continue to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato"><b>give again</b></a> and again, and by doing so, they reach the heavenly state again and again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato">SN 7.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a dairyman <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato"><b>milking a cow</b></a> again and again to illustrate the repetitive nature of activities and the cycles of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato">SN 7.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the cycle of rebirth by stating that a "dullard" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato"><b>enters the womb</b></a> again and again, leading to a continuous loop of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato">SN 7.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that for most beings, they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato"><b>born and die</b></a> again and again, and they are carried to the cemetery again and again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato">SN 7.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that one of vast wisdom, having gained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato"><b>path for non-renewal</b></a> of being, is the one who is finally not born again and again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato">SN 7.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Udaya initially critiqued the Buddha by saying that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato"><b>ascetic Gotama</b></a> keeps coming back again and again, which prompted the Buddha's discourse on the repetitive nature of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato">SN 7.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once suffered from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato"><b>wind ailment</b></a> and requested his attendant, Venerable Upavāṇa, to find him some hot water for treatment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato">SN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Upavāṇa stood silently at the house of the brahmin Devahita, the brahmin asked what the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato"><b>shaven-headed</b></a> monk, clad in an outer robe, was seeking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato">SN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upavāṇa described the Buddha to the brahmin Devahita as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato"><b>Arahant and Sugata</b></a> in the world, a sage who was currently afflicted by a wind ailment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato">SN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Devahita provided a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato"><b>load of hot water</b></a> and a jar of molasses to Venerable Upavāṇa to help treat the Buddha's illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato">SN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the Buddha was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato"><b>bathed with hot water</b></a> and took the molasses mixed with hot water, his physical ailment was subsided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato">SN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Devahita asked the Buddha where a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato"><b>religious gift</b></a> should be given and where a gift, when given, yields the greatest fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato">SN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a gift has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato"><b>great fruit</b></a> when it is given to one who knows their past lives and sees both heaven and the states of deprivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato">SN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a worthy recipient of offerings as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato"><b>sage</b></a> who has reached the destruction of birth and has completed the direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato">SN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a donor’s offering to be successful, it should be given to one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato"><b>attained the destruction</b></a> of birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato">SN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an offering is most effective when the recipient is someone who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato"><b>knows past lives</b></a> and has full spiritual realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato">SN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a sacrificer, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato"><b>offering succeeds</b></a> specifically when it is bestowed upon a muni (sage) who is "finished" with direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato">SN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after receiving a teaching on the most fruitful way to give, the brahmin Devahita declared the Buddha's teaching to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato"><b>excellent</b></a> and went for refuge as a lay follower? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.13/en/sujato">SN 7.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the continuous rain of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato"><b>king of gods</b></a> to other repetitive cycles in the world, such as the repeated sowing of seeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.12/en/sujato">SN 7.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a wealthy brahmin appeared before the Buddha looking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato"><b>shabby and poorly dressed</b></a>, he explained it was because his four sons, at the instigation of their wives, had driven him out of his own home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato">SN 7.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised a mistreated father to learn and recite specific verses in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato"><b>public assembly hall</b></a> when a large crowd and his sons were present to address his family's neglect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato">SN 7.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha provided verses for an elderly man to recite which compared ungrateful sons to people who drive away a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato"><b>swine</b></a> from its own enclosure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato">SN 7.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the verses taught by the Buddha, a neglected father laments that his children, whom he once cherished, have turned out to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato"><b>demons in the form of sons</b></a> who abandon him in his old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato">SN 7.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared an elderly, neglected father to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato"><b>old horse</b></a> that is led away from its feeding trough and deprived of fodder because it is no longer deemed useful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato">SN 7.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s verses suggest that for a man neglected by his children, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato"><b>walking staff</b></a> is actually more helpful than disobedient sons because of the constant support it provides? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato">SN 7.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted the utility of a staff by noting it can ward off a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato"><b>ferocious bull</b></a> or a fierce dog, performing a duty that his ungrateful children failed to do? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato">SN 7.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a walking staff helps an elderly person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato"><b>stand firm</b></a> after stumbling and provides a way to find one's footing in deep water or total darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato">SN 7.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after a father recited the Buddha's verses in public, his sons were so moved by shame and regret that they took him home, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato"><b>bathed him</b></a>, and provided him with new sets of clothes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato">SN 7.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that out of gratitude for the reconciliation with his sons, a wealthy brahmin offered the Buddha a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato"><b>teacher's fee</b></a>, which the Buddha accepted out of compassion for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato">SN 7.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the Buddha’s advice helped restore his family life, the wealthy brahmin declared the teaching excellent and asked to be remembered as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a> who had gone for refuge for life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.14/en/sujato">SN 7.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that there was once a brahmin named Mānatthaddha ("Stubborn with Pride") who was so arrogant that he would not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato"><b>pay homage</b></a> to his own mother, father, teacher, or even his elder brother? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato">SN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Mānatthaddha approached the Buddha with the specific intention that he would only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato"><b>address the Buddha</b></a> if the Buddha spoke to him first? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato">SN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha remained silent after the brahmin Mānatthaddha approached him, the brahmin mistakenly concluded that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato"><b>Ascetic Gotama</b></a> did not know anything? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato">SN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato"><b>reading the thoughts</b></a> of the brahmin Mānatthaddha, addressed him in verse to point out that pride is not beneficial for one who has a goal to achieve? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato">SN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as soon as the brahmin Mānatthaddha realized the Buddha knew his inner thoughts, he immediately threw himself down and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato"><b>prostrated with his head</b></a> at the Buddha’s feet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato">SN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the crowd watching the brahmin Mānatthaddha was amazed to see him show such <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato"><b>extreme humility</b></a> toward the Buddha, as he was known for never honoring even his own parents or teachers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato">SN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the brahmin Mānatthaddha was prostrating, the Buddha told him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato"><b>stand up</b></a> and sit in his own seat because the brahmin's heart had become confident in him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato">SN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Mānatthaddha asked the Buddha to clarify toward whom one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato"><b>not be proud</b></a> and who specifically should be honored and revered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato">SN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should never show pride toward one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato"><b>mother and father</b></a>, and should instead treat them with respect and honor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato">SN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato"><b>elder brother</b></a> and the teacher as two of the four types of people toward whom one should always remain humble and respectful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato">SN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated one should discard all pride to pay homage to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato"><b>the Arahants</b></a>, those who are cooled, have done what was to be done, and are free from taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato">SN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after receiving the Buddha's teaching on humility, the brahmin Mānatthaddha declared the teaching excellent and asked to be remembered as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a> who had gone for refuge for life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.15/en/sujato">SN 7.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person dwells on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato"><b>enjoyment of things</b></a> that can be clung to, their craving increases, leading to the growth of the entire mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato">SN 12.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato"><b>great bonfire</b></a> being fed with loads of wood, grass, and cow dung to describe how craving is sustained when one focuses on gratification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato">SN 12.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the increase of craving is the specific condition that leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato"><b>clinging and existence</b></a>, which eventually results in birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato">SN 12.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by dwelling on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato"><b>danger in things</b></a> that can be clung to, a person can cause their craving to cease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato">SN 12.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato"><b>cessation of craving</b></a> directly results in the cessation of clinging, existence, birth, and all forms of sorrow and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato">SN 12.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the end of suffering to a fire that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato"><b>extinguished through lack of fuel</b></a> because no new wood or grass is added to sustain it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato">SN 12.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a>—including lamentation, displeasure, and despair—as a chain of events triggered by dwelling on the pleasure of worldly objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato">SN 12.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a fire will go out when its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato"><b>previous fuel</b></a> is exhausted and no further sustenance is provided, just as suffering ends when one no longer seeks enjoyment in attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato">SN 12.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a> can be reversed, leading to the total cessation of the whole mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato">SN 12.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato"><b>disadvantage of attachment</b></a> is the key to stopping the cycle of birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.52/en/sujato">SN 12.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person dwells on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato"><b>gratification in things</b></a> that can be fettered, their craving increases, leading to the growth of the entire mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato">SN 12.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato"><b>oil lamp</b></a> burning in dependence on oil and a wick to illustrate how craving is sustained when one focuses on the enjoyment of worldly attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato">SN 12.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if a person continues to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.54/en/sujato"><b>pour oil</b></a> into a lamp and adjust its wick, it will burn for a long time, just as craving persists when it is constantly fed by seeking pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.54/en/sujato">SN 12.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the increase of craving is the direct condition that leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato"><b>clinging and existence</b></a>, which eventually results in birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato">SN 12.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by dwelling on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato"><b>danger in things</b></a> that can be fettered, a practitioner can bring about the total cessation of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato">SN 12.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato"><b>cessation of craving</b></a> leads to the cessation of clinging, which in turn stops the entire cycle of existence and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato">SN 12.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the end of suffering to an oil lamp that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.54/en/sujato"><b>extinguished</b></a> because no new oil is added and the previous fuel has been exhausted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.54/en/sujato">SN 12.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a>—including sorrow, lamentation, and despair—as a chain of events triggered by focusing on the pleasant aspects of things that bind us? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato">SN 12.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an oil lamp becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato"><b>without nutriment</b></a> and goes out when it is no longer supplied with fuel, just as suffering ends when one stops seeking gratification in fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato">SN 12.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for one who sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.54/en/sujato"><b>disadvantage of attachment</b></a>, the craving that fuels the cycle of birth and death is completely destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.54/en/sujato">SN 12.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a> by showing how craving acts as the specific "fuel" or support for the continuation of the entire mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato">SN 12.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a person does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.54/en/sujato"><b>not add oil</b></a> to a lamp, it will eventually go out, symbolizing how the cessation of desire leads to the cessation of birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.54/en/sujato">SN 12.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that dwelling on the "gratification" (<i>assāda</i>) of things is the primary reason why <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato"><b>craving grows</b></a> within a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato">SN 12.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized certain experiences as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.54/en/sujato"><b>things that can be fettered</b></a> (<i>saṃyojaniyesu dhammesu</i>), which serve as the objects of our craving and clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.54/en/sujato">SN 12.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato"><b>cessation of the whole mass</b></a> of suffering is possible simply by changing one's perspective from seeking enjoyment to recognizing the inherent danger in worldly things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.53/en/sujato">SN 12.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the growth of craving to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato"><b>great tree</b></a> that stands for a long time because its roots draw up sap and nutriment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato">SN 12.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person dwells on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato"><b>gratification in things</b></a> that can be clung to, their craving increases, leading to the origin of the entire mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato">SN 12.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the growth of a great tree is supported by its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.56/en/sujato"><b>roots</b></a> which go downwards and across to bring up nutriment, just as craving is fed by focusing on pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.56/en/sujato">SN 12.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that dwelling on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> in things that can be clung to will cause craving to cease, eventually leading to the end of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato">SN 12.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a man <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato"><b>cutting the root</b></a> of a tree and digging it up to describe how a practitioner can completely eliminate the source of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato">SN 12.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person who recognizes the disadvantage of attachment, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.56/en/sujato"><b>cessation of craving</b></a> leads to the cessation of clinging, existence, and birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.56/en/sujato">SN 12.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once the cause of craving is destroyed, it becomes like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato"><b>palm stump</b></a>, rendered unable to grow or arise again in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato">SN 12.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the total destruction of suffering by likening it to a tree being cut into pieces, dried in the sun, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato"><b>burned to ashes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato">SN 12.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the entire <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato"><b>mass of suffering</b></a>—including sorrow, lamentation, and despair—arises specifically because of the condition of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato">SN 12.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that things which are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.56/en/sujato"><b>clung to</b></a> act as the fuel for the continuation of the cycle of birth and death when one sees only enjoyment in them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.56/en/sujato">SN 12.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a tree would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato"><b>without nutriment</b></a> and unable to grow if its roots were destroyed and its wood scattered in a fast-flowing river? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato">SN 12.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that dwelling on "gratification" (<i>assāda</i>) is what causes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.56/en/sujato"><b>craving to grow</b></a>, similar to how a tree grows when its roots are well-nourished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.56/en/sujato">SN 12.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the final state of a person who has ended craving as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato"><b>no longer subject</b></a> to further arising, just like a tree that has been uprooted and reduced to ash? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato">SN 12.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.56/en/sujato"><b>upward-going sap</b></a> of a great tree is the metaphor for how attachment sustains our continued existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.56/en/sujato">SN 12.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato"><b>splitting into pieces</b></a> the delusions of the mind, one can dry up the "moisture" of craving that keeps the cycle of suffering alive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato">SN 12.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the specific sequence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a> to show that the cessation of one link, like craving, inevitably leads to the cessation of the entire chain of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.55/en/sujato">SN 12.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a parable about a cat that swallowed a mouse whole, only to have the mouse eat the cat’s internal organs, as a warning to monks who visit families with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato"><b>unguarded faculties</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato">SN 20.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato"><b>death</b></a> in the Noble One’s Discipline as the act of a monk giving up the training and returning to the lower life of a layperson? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato">SN 20.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a monk commits a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato"><b>defiled offense</b></a> for which a means of rehabilitation is prescribed, he is undergoing what is called "death-like suffering"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato">SN 20.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should enter a village for alms only with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato"><b>guarded body</b></a>, guarded speech, and a guarded mind to ensure he is not overcome by worldly desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato">SN 20.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a monk enters a town with unrestrained senses and sees a woman <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato"><b>poorly dressed</b></a>, lust can invade his mind and lead him to abandon his spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato">SN 20.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a certain monk would not stop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato"><b>visiting families</b></a> excessively despite warnings from his peers, the Buddha used the story of a dying cat to illustrate the severe spiritual danger the monk was facing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato">SN 20.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that monks must train themselves to enter a village for alms only when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness is established</b></a> and their sense faculties are fully restrained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato">SN 20.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the internal pain of a cat whose vitals are being eaten by a swallowed mouse to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> experienced by a monk who fails to guard his mind against lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato">SN 20.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once used the sound of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.12/en/sujato"><b>old jackal</b></a> howling at the break of day to prompt a discussion with his monks about the importance of being thankful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.12/en/sujato">SN 20.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that even an old jackal might have a sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.12/en/sujato"><b>gratitude and thankfulness</b></a>, whereas some humans who claim to be his followers may lack these qualities entirely? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.12/en/sujato">SN 20.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly commanded his disciples to train themselves by resolving: "We will be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.12/en/sujato"><b>grateful</b></a> and thankful"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.12/en/sujato">SN 20.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.12/en/sujato"><b>small thing</b></a> done for a person should never be allowed to perish from their memory or be forgotten? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.12/en/sujato">SN 20.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained senses</b></a> to a predator that hastily swallows its prey, only to find that the prey causes its own destruction from the inside out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.10/en/sujato">SN 20.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared families with many women and few men to monks who have not cultivated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.3/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness deliverance of mind</b></a>, noting that both are easily attacked and harassed by external threats? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.3/en/sujato">SN 20.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who has frequently practiced and developed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.3/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> is as difficult for non-human beings to assault as a family with many men and few women is for thieves to rob? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.3/en/sujato">SN 20.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned his disciples that a monk is susceptible to being harmed by non-human spirits if he has not made the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.3/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind by loving-kindness</b></a> a habit and a foundation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.3/en/sujato">SN 20.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers to train themselves specifically by making the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.3/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness deliverance of mind</b></a> into a vehicle and a foundation, ensuring it is well-undertaken and properly consolidated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.3/en/sujato">SN 20.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that developing a mind of loving-kindness for even the brief duration of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.4/en/sujato"><b>pull of a cow's udder</b></a> three times a day is far more fruitful than giving three hundred pots of food as alms daily? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.4/en/sujato">SN 20.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the merit gained from a moment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.4/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> practiced in the morning, at midday, and in the evening outweighs the merit of massive material donations given at those same times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.4/en/sujato">SN 20.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly urged monks to train until their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.4/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> is so well-developed that it becomes "a vehicle and a foundation," because of the immense spiritual benefits such practice brings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.4/en/sujato">SN 20.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.5/en/sujato"><b>sharp-pointed spear</b></a> to explain that a monk who has fully cultivated the deliverance of mind by loving-kindness becomes spiritually unassailable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.5/en/sujato">SN 20.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as a man cannot bend or crush a sharp spear with his bare hand without only hurting himself, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.5/en/sujato"><b>non-human being</b></a> who tries to upset the mind of a monk established in loving-kindness will only meet with distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.5/en/sujato">SN 20.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a monk has made <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.5/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> his vehicle and foundation, any non-human being attempting to disturb his mind would only experience "weariness and disappointment"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.5/en/sujato">SN 20.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.5/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness deliverance of mind</b></a> as a protective state that must be "frequently practiced, made a vehicle, and properly started" to ensure a monk's safety from non-human interference? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.5/en/sujato">SN 20.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the vulnerability of an unprotected monk to families that are "easy to assault by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.3/en/sujato"><b>thieves and pot-burglars</b></a>" because they lack a strong male presence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.3/en/sujato">SN 20.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted the superiority of mental development over material charity by comparing three hundred pots of food to a single moment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.4/en/sujato"><b>mettacitta</b></a>, or a mind of loving-kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.4/en/sujato">SN 20.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk’s mind becomes as resilient as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.5/en/sujato"><b>sharp spear</b></a> when he has thoroughly cultivated the deliverance of mind by loving-kindness, making it impossible for others to "bend" or "disturb" his mental state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.5/en/sujato">SN 20.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of four highly trained archers shooting arrows in different directions to explain that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.6/en/sujato"><b>life formations</b></a> perish even faster than a man could run to catch all those arrows before they hit the ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.6/en/sujato">SN 20.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while the sun and moon travel with great speed, and certain deities run even faster than them, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.6/en/sujato"><b>wasting away of life</b></a> occurs at an even swifter rate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.6/en/sujato">SN 20.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because of the extreme speed at which life formations vanish, the Buddha exhorted his followers to train themselves by resolving to live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.6/en/sujato"><b>heedfully</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.6/en/sujato">SN 20.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a story about a drum called the Ānaka which, through being repeatedly repaired with new pegs over time, eventually lost its original drumhead and became a mere <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato"><b>conglomeration of pegs</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato">SN 20.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the deep discourses concerning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato"><b>emptiness</b></a> (suññatappaṭisaṃyuttā) would eventually disappear if monks prefer to listen to poetic and literary works composed by outsiders? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato">SN 20.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted a future time when monks would no longer want to listen to or master the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato"><b>supramundane discourses</b></a> spoken by the Tathāgata, leading to the gradual vanishing of these profound teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato">SN 20.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the authentic teachings that must be preserved as being "Tathāgata-uttered, deep, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato"><b>deep in meaning</b></a>, supramundane, and dealing with emptiness"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato">SN 20.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted his own profound teachings with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato"><b>literary compositions</b></a> that are "extra-canonical" and "spoken by disciples," warning that the latter would one day be preferred over the former? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato">SN 20.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his disciples to train themselves by specifically resolving to listen to, pay attention to, and master the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato"><b>discourses of the Tathāgata</b></a> so that the core Dhamma would not be lost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato">SN 20.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.6/en/sujato"><b>strong archers</b></a>, who are "well-trained, practiced, and experienced," to emphasize how difficult it would be to catch their arrows, yet noted this speed is nothing compared to the decay of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.6/en/sujato">SN 20.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, even the speed of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.6/en/sujato"><b>deities</b></a> who run before the sun and moon is surpassed by the swiftness with which life formations waste away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.6/en/sujato">SN 20.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the deterioration of the True Dhamma to an old drum whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato"><b>original drum-head</b></a> eventually vanished because it was repeatedly patched with non-original parts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato">SN 20.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically identified discourses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato"><b>dealing with emptiness</b></a> as the vital teachings that monks must strive to hear and learn to ensure the path remains intact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato">SN 20.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the popularity of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato"><b>beautiful phrases</b></a> and poetic suttas from outside sources would contribute to the neglect of the authentic, deep teachings of the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.7/en/sujato">SN 20.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the necessity of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.6/en/sujato"><b>diligent</b></a> (appamatta) as a direct and urgent response to his realization of the incredible swiftness of mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.6/en/sujato">SN 20.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.21/en/sujato"><b>deity</b></a>, a monk should wander mindfully to abandon sensual lust with the same urgency as if his head were on fire or as if he were being struck by a sword? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.21/en/sujato">SN 1.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should go forth mindfully to abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.21/en/sujato"><b>identity view</b></a> as urgently as if he were being struck by a sword or if his head were currently ablaze? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.21/en/sujato">SN 1.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked who could untangle the "tangle" within and without that enmeshes all beings, the Buddha explained that a wise person established in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.23/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> who develops the mind and wisdom is the one capable of untangling it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.23/en/sujato">SN 1.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.23/en/sujato"><b>Arahants</b></a> as those who have truly untangled the "tangle" of existence because they have destroyed all taints and abandoned lust, hatred, and ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.23/en/sujato">SN 1.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the "tangle" of existence is cut where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.23/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a>, resistance, and the perception of form cease without any remainder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.23/en/sujato">SN 1.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while a deity suggested one should restrain the mind from everything to be free from suffering, the Buddha clarified that one should only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.24/en/sujato"><b>restrain the mind</b></a> from those specific things that are evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.24/en/sujato">SN 1.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for an individual whose mind has attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.24/en/sujato"><b>self-mastery</b></a>, it is not necessary to restrain the mind from everything, but only from those avenues where evil arises? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.24/en/sujato">SN 1.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.25/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a>, who has destroyed the taints and wears his last body, can still use words like "I" or "mine" without being driven by conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.25/en/sujato">SN 1.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when an Arahant says "I speak" or "they speak to me," he is merely using <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.25/en/sujato"><b>conventional speech</b></a> and worldly nomenclature, being skillful in understanding world usage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.25/en/sujato">SN 1.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for one who has abandoned conceit, there are no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.25/en/sujato"><b>fetters</b></a> remaining, as all the "knots" of conceit have been entirely blown away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.25/en/sujato">SN 1.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.26/en/sujato"><b>four lights</b></a> that shine in the world: the sun, the moon, fire, and the Buddha himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.26/en/sujato">SN 1.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that while the sun shines by day and the moon by night, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.26/en/sujato"><b>Enlightened One</b></a> is the supreme light in the world, providing an unsurpassed radiance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.26/en/sujato">SN 1.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a specific state or element where the "stream" of existence turns back and where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.27/en/sujato"><b>earth, water, fire, and air</b></a> find no footing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.27/en/sujato">SN 1.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the cycle of rebirth ceases and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.27/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> are cut off without remainder in the element where the physical four great elements do not find a place to stand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.27/en/sujato">SN 1.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to a deity, even powerful <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.28/en/sujato"><b>Khattiyas</b></a> (nobles) who possess great wealth and territory are still filled with greed and covet one another because they are insatiable in their sensual desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.28/en/sujato">SN 1.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.28/en/sujato"><b>Arahants</b></a> as those who are truly "without anxiety" (anussukā) in the world because they have left home and possessions and have abandoned lust, hatred, and ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.28/en/sujato">SN 1.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity metaphorically described the human body as a vehicle that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.29/en/sujato"><b>four-wheeled</b></a> and nine-gated, which is filled with greed and mired in a swamp? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.29/en/sujato">SN 1.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the "journey" of life proceeds successfully and one escapes the swamp of existence only by cutting the thongs of desire and pulling out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.29/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> by its root? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.29/en/sujato">SN 1.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an Arahant who has transcended <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.25/en/sujato"><b>imagining</b></a> (maññataṃ) and conceit does not have a sense of "I am" even when he uses the word "I" in daily conversation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.25/en/sujato">SN 1.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.26/en/sujato"><b>fire</b></a> is a light that shines both day and night in various places, but it is still surpassed by the peerless light of a Fully Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.26/en/sujato">SN 1.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was delivering a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato"><b>sermon on Nibbāna</b></a> to a group of monks, who were listening with full attention and intent, when Mara the Evil One attempted to interfere? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato">SN 4.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One once disguised himself as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato"><b>farmer</b></a> with disheveled hair, hempen garments, and mud-smeared feet, carrying a large plough and a long goad to approach the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato">SN 4.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One attempted to trick the Buddha by pretending to be looking for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato"><b>lost oxen</b></a> as a pretext to interrupt the Buddha’s teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato">SN 4.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One claimed ownership over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato"><b>eye and forms</b></a>, as well as the sphere of eye-contact and consciousness, asserting that the Buddha could never escape his reach within these domains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato">SN 4.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One asserted that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato"><b>ear and sounds</b></a>, along with the other physical sense bases like the nose, tongue, and body, belong to him and constitute his sphere of influence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato">SN 4.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One insisted that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato"><b>mind and mental phenomena</b></a> are his own property, including the sphere of mind-contact and consciousness, challenging the Buddha to find a way out of his control? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato">SN 4.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refuted Mara's claims of dominance by declaring that where there is no eye, no forms, and no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact</b></a>, the Evil One has no access (agati) whatsoever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato">SN 4.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Mara that there is a state where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato"><b>tongue and tastes</b></a>, and the consciousness associated with them, do not exist, making that state inaccessible to the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato">SN 4.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Mara the Evil One that where there is no mind, no mental phenomena, and no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato"><b>mind-contact</b></a>, the Evil One has no entrance or power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato">SN 4.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One stated in verse that those who use the language of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato"><b>possession</b></a>, saying "this is mine" or "it is me," are the ones who cannot escape his grasp? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato">SN 4.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared to Mara that he does not identify with the things people call "mine," and because he is free from such <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato"><b>attachments</b></a>, Mara cannot even see the path he follows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.19/en/sujato">SN 4.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a well-spoken teaching is not easily understood by a person who possesses a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.16/en/sujato"><b>defiled mind</b></a> and is full of hostility toward others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.16/en/sujato">SN 7.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a Brahmin named Paccanīkasāta approached the Buddha with the express intent of contradicting everything he said, the Buddha pointed out that one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.16/en/sujato"><b>delights in contradicting</b></a> will struggle to comprehend the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.16/en/sujato">SN 7.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person can only truly know what is well-spoken once they have successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.16/en/sujato"><b>abandoned ill will</b></a> and calmed the lack of confidence in their own heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.16/en/sujato">SN 7.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the removal of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.16/en/sujato"><b>hostility</b></a> (sārambha) is a necessary prerequisite for a listener to distinguish whether a teaching has been subhāsita, or "well-spoken"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.16/en/sujato">SN 7.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a Brahmin builder who was overseeing work in a forest asked the Buddha what "work" he was doing while sitting under a tree, the Buddha replied that he had no work to do because he had already <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.17/en/sujato"><b>cut the root</b></a> of the metaphorical "forest" of defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.17/en/sujato">SN 7.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.17/en/sujato"><b>free from the dart</b></a> (visallo), signifying that he had removed the internal stings of craving and suffering that typically plague the human mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.17/en/sujato">SN 7.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the Brahmin Navakammikabhāradvāja that he is able to dwell alone in the forest and experience pleasure because he has successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.17/en/sujato"><b>abandoned discontent</b></a> (arati)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.17/en/sujato">SN 7.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.17/en/sujato"><b>nibbanatho</b></a> (meaning 'free from the forest' or 'free from craving') to explain why he felt no need to engage in the physical labor of clearing a wooded grove like a worldly builder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.17/en/sujato">SN 7.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was observed by a Brahmin builder sitting in a remote forest with his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.17/en/sujato"><b>body erect</b></a> and mindfulness established, demonstrating a state of peaceful meditation that eventually inspired the Brahmin to become a lay follower? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.17/en/sujato">SN 7.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even in a quiet wilderness, one cannot find true peace unless the internal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.17/en/sujato"><b>forest of defilements</b></a> has been cleared away through spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.17/en/sujato">SN 7.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Ānanda remarked that the principle of dependent origination seemed "clear as can be," the Buddha cautioned him that this teaching is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato"><b>deep and appears deep</b></a>, warning him not to underestimate its complexity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato">SN 12.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that because generations of beings do not understand or penetrate the law of dependent origination, they become entangled like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato"><b>matted ball of thread</b></a> and are unable to escape the cycle of rebirth and the plane of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato">SN 12.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person who dwells contemplating the enjoyment or gratification in things that can be clung to, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato"><b>craving increases</b></a>, which then serves as the condition for further clinging and existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato">SN 12.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato"><b>great tree</b></a> whose roots draw up sap to sustain it for a long time to illustrate how craving is nourished when one focuses on the gratification of things that can be clung to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato">SN 12.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person dwells contemplating the danger and disadvantage in things that can be clung to, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato"><b>craving ceases</b></a>, leading to the cessation of clinging and the entire mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato">SN 12.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the total ending of suffering to a man who cuts a tree at its root, digs it up, and burns it to ashes, ensuring that the tree is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato"><b>uprooted</b></a> and unable to grow again in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato">SN 12.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how the "whole mass of suffering" arises through a chain of conditions, starting with craving which leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a>, which then leads to existence, birth, and eventually aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato">SN 12.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a tangled bird's nest to describe the state of beings who have not penetrated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato"><b>law of dependent origination</b></a> and are thus unable to pass beyond the cycle of saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato">SN 12.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the cessation of craving leads to the cessation of clinging, and through this sequence, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato"><b>cessation of the whole mass of suffering</b></a> is finally achieved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato">SN 12.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the way craving is sustained to how a tree's roots, spreading in all directions, draw up <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato"><b>nutritive essence</b></a> to keep the tree alive for a very long duration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato">SN 12.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when one successfully contemplates the danger in clinging, the potential for future suffering becomes like a palm tree stump, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato"><b>rendered non-existent</b></a> and incapable of arising again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.60/en/sujato">SN 12.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a parable about a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato"><b>newly ordained monk</b></a> who visited families excessively and, when corrected by his elders, arrogantly claimed he should be allowed to do so because the senior monks did the same? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato">SN 20.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared senior monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato"><b>great bull elephants</b></a> who can safely eat lotus stalks from a lake because they wash them thoroughly to remove all the mud before consuming them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato">SN 20.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that junior monks who try to imitate their elders without proper discipline are like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato"><b>young elephant calves</b></a> who swallow mud along with their food, leading to their own death or deadly suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato">SN 20.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that elder monks can visit villages and receive offerings safely because they remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato"><b>uninfatuated</b></a> and are not blinded by the gains they receive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato">SN 20.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a monk to truly benefit from the offerings of laypeople, he must consume those gains while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato"><b>seeing the danger</b></a> inherent in attachment to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato">SN 20.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described spiritual failure as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato"><b>blinded by gains</b></a>, comparing monks who are obsessed with offerings to young elephants who die because they cannot distinguish between nourishing food and toxic mud? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato">SN 20.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that successful practice involves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato"><b>understanding the escape</b></a> from the bond of worldly requisites so that one can use offerings for strength without being harmed by greed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato">SN 20.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato"><b>strength and beauty</b></a> to describe the spiritual health of a monk who receives support from the community without becoming mindlessly attached to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato">SN 20.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato"><b>liberating wisdom</b></a> as the reason why some monks experience spiritual destruction when they receive many offerings and honors from lay followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato">SN 20.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his disciples to specifically train themselves to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato"><b>unattached</b></a> so that they can receive the support of a village or town without falling into spiritual ruin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato">SN 20.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that what may be safe for a senior monk with developed self-control can be a cause of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato"><b>deadly suffering</b></a> for a junior monk who lacks the same level of mindfulness and restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato">SN 20.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the proper way to enjoy requisites as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato"><b>not mindless</b></a>, ensuring that a monk uses food and clothing for the sake of the path rather than for sensual indulgence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn20.9/en/sujato">SN 20.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity once described the Buddha as a hero with legs like an antelope (<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.30/en/sujato"><b>antelope-legged</b></a>), noting that he is thin, consumes little food, and roams alone like a lion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.30/en/sujato">SN 1.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that release from suffering is achieved by removing desire for the five <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.30/en/sujato"><b>cords of sensual pleasure</b></a>, with the mind considered as the sixth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.30/en/sujato">SN 1.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying at Jeta's Grove in Sāvatthi, a large group of deities known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato"><b>Satullapakāyika devas</b></a> visited him late at night, illuminating the entire grove with their beauty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato">SN 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity explained to the Buddha that by associating with the good and knowing their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato"><b>true Dhamma</b></a>, a person becomes better and never worse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato">SN 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to a deity's verse to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> is acquired from no other source than the good and the understanding of their true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato">SN 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity informed the Buddha that one who associates with the good and understands the true Dhamma does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato"><b>not sorrow</b></a> even when they are in the midst of those who are grieving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato">SN 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity stated in the presence of the Buddha that an individual who knows the true Dhamma of the good will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato"><b>shine</b></a> with distinction in the midst of their own kinsmen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato">SN 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity told the Buddha that beings who associate with the good and learn the true Dhamma are destined to go to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato"><b>good destination</b></a> after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato">SN 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity suggested to the Buddha that those who understand the true Dhamma of the good are able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato"><b>abide</b></a> in a state of continuous well-being or security? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato">SN 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by a deity whose statement was the best among those present, the Buddha replied that all their statements were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken</b></a> in their own way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato">SN 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that while there are many benefits to associating with the good, the ultimate result of knowing their true Dhamma is being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato"><b>released from all suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.31/en/sujato">SN 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity explained to the Buddha that people fail to give gifts primarily due to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato"><b>stinginess and negligence</b></a>, but those who understand and desire merit should give anyway? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato">SN 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to a deity's teaching to the Buddha, the very things a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato"><b>stingy person</b></a> fears—such as hunger and thirst—are exactly what will afflict them in both this life and the life to come because of their refusal to give? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato">SN 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity told the Buddha that one should conquer the stain of stinginess through giving, as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato"><b>merits</b></a> are the only true support for living beings in the world beyond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato">SN 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity informed the Buddha that the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato"><b>giving from a little</b></a> is an eternal law (sanantana dhamma), and such a modest gift is considered equal in value to a gift of a thousand from those who have much? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato">SN 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity pointed out to the Buddha that because the bad cannot follow the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma of the good</b></a>, which involves giving what is hard to give, their destinations after death differ—the bad go to hell while the good are bound for heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato">SN 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when several deities shared their insights on the virtues of giving, the Buddha affirmed that all of their statements were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken</b></a> in their own specific way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato">SN 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the gift of a righteous person who lives by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato"><b>gleaning</b></a> and supports a family is worth far more than massive sacrifices involving thousands of items offered by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato">SN 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a gift given by those who have acted unjustly—by killing, cutting, or causing sorrow—is a gift given with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato"><b>tears and punishment</b></a> and does not equal the value of a gift given righteously? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato">SN 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity told the Buddha that those who provide for others even when they possess very little themselves are following a path that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato"><b>does not die</b></a> even when they pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato">SN 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that practicing the Dhamma while supporting one's family and giving from a small amount of resources makes one's offerings more precious than <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato"><b>massive sacrifices</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.32/en/sujato">SN 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity explained to the Buddha that while giving is virtuous, many fail to give because of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato"><b>stinginess and negligence</b></a>, though one who understands the value of merit should provide offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato">SN 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity told the Buddha that when a person gives even a small amount from their limited resources, that gift is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato"><b>equal to a thousand</b></a> given by those who have much? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato">SN 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity observed to the Buddha that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato"><b>giving and battle</b></a> are similar, as even a few virtuous people can overcome a great many through the power of their faith and generosity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato">SN 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity taught the Buddha that a person who gives with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a>, even if they have very little to offer, will become happy in the world beyond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato">SN 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity informed the Buddha that a mortal who gives from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato"><b>righteously earned wealth</b></a>—acquired through energetic effort—is able to reach divine stations after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato">SN 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity explained to the Buddha that by giving what is righteously gained, a person is able to cross over <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato"><b>Yama’s Vetaraṇī</b></a> river into a better existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato">SN 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity suggested to the Buddha that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato"><b>discriminating giving</b></a> is specifically praised by the Sugata because gifts yield the greatest fruit when given to those worthy of offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato">SN 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity compared giving to those worthy of offerings in this world to sowing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato"><b>seeds in a good field</b></a> to ensure a bountiful harvest of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato">SN 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity remarked to the Buddha that in addition to giving, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato"><b>restraint toward living beings</b></a> is a noble practice where the virtuous avoid harming others out of a fear of doing evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato">SN 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked which of the deities' various statements on giving was best, the Buddha replied that all their statements were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken</b></a> in their own way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato">SN 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that while faith and giving are highly praised, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato"><b>passage of the Dhamma</b></a> is superior because it is what leads the wise to the ultimate goal of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.33/en/sujato">SN 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity informed the Buddha that sensual pleasures among humans are not permanent, and those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.34/en/sujato"><b>negligent</b></a> regarding them will never return from the realm of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.34/en/sujato">SN 1.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that both misfortune and suffering are born of desire, and that through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.35/en/sujato"><b>removal of desire</b></a>, both misfortune and suffering are likewise removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.35/en/sujato">SN 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that the beautiful things found in the world are not themselves "sensual pleasures," but rather <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.35/en/sujato"><b>passionate intention</b></a> is a person’s true sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.35/en/sujato">SN 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one should abandon anger and conceit and overcome all fetters, for suffering does not overtake one who does not cling to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.35/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.35/en/sujato">SN 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a liberated person who has cut off craving as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.35/en/sujato"><b>untraceable</b></a> by gods or humans, whether they look in this world, the next, or in the heavens? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.35/en/sujato">SN 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mogharāja remarked to the Buddha that those who pay homage to a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.35/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a> and seeks the welfare of others are themselves praiseworthy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.35/en/sujato">SN 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that monks who have understood the Dhamma and discarded <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.35/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a>, thereby moving beyond attachment, are also those who are praiseworthy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.35/en/sujato">SN 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity told the Buddha that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.36/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> acts as a person’s partner, bringing them fame and renown and leading them to heaven after they leave the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.36/en/sujato">SN 1.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity advised the Buddha that by abandoning anger and conceit and overcoming all fetters, one who possesses nothing will not be overtaken by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.36/en/sujato"><b>bonds</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.36/en/sujato">SN 1.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that while foolish and witless people indulge in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.36/en/sujato"><b>negligence</b></a>, a wise person guards diligence as if it were a supreme treasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.36/en/sujato">SN 1.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned against intimacy with sensual delight and negligence, stating that the diligent person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.36/en/sujato"><b>meditates</b></a> is the one who attains supreme happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.36/en/sujato">SN 1.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person should protect <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.36/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a> as their most valuable possession, rather than following the path of the foolish who succumb to heedlessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.36/en/sujato">SN 1.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the appearance of an elderly, wheezing brahmin leaning on a staff as a disguise used by Māra the Evil One to try and confuse a group of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato"><b>diligent monks</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato">SN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra the Evil One once urged young, black-haired monks to enjoy <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato"><b>human sensual pleasures</b></a> in the prime of their youth rather than abandoning the immediate for what involves time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato">SN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks responded to Māra by stating they had not abandoned the immediate for what involves time, but had instead abandoned what involves time for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato"><b>immediate Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato">SN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks, following the Buddha's teachings, described sensual pleasures as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato"><b>involving time</b></a> and being full of suffering and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato">SN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches that the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato"><b>timeless</b></a>, inviting one to "come and see," and is to be personally realized by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato">SN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Māra the Evil One failed to tempt the monks, he was so frustrated that he shook his head, licked his tongue, and produced a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato"><b>three-furrowed frown</b></a> on his forehead before vanishing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato">SN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Māra had appeared to the monks specifically for the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato"><b>blinding</b></a> them or obstructing their spiritual vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato">SN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who has seen <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> and its origin would no longer incline toward sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato">SN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato"><b>acquisition</b></a> (upadhi) as a bond in the world and urged people to train themselves for its removal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato">SN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra’s disguise included being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato"><b>crooked like a rafter</b></a> and carrying a staff made of cluster-fig wood to better impersonate an old man? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato">SN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks described the Dhamma as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato"><b>onward-leading</b></a> (opaneyyiko), contrasting its spiritual rewards with the drawbacks of worldly desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato">SN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that once a person understands the source of pain, they should focus their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato"><b>training</b></a> on dismantling the worldly attachments that keep them bound? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.21/en/sujato">SN 4.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying among the Sakyans at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato"><b>Silāvatī</b></a> when the monk Samiddhi experienced a terrifying supernatural disturbance while meditating? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato">SN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Venerable Samiddhi as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato"><b>diligent</b></a>, ardent, and resolute while he was living in seclusion near the Blessed One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato">SN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Samiddhi reflected on his great <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato"><b>good fortune</b></a> because his teacher was an Arahant and a Perfectly Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato">SN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Samiddhi considered it a significant gain to have been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato"><b>ordained</b></a> in a Dhamma and Discipline that is so well-expounded by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato">SN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Samiddhi expressed deep appreciation for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato"><b>virtuous companions</b></a> in the holy life, seeing their good character as a blessing for his own practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato">SN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a terrifying noise, which sounded as if the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato"><b>earth were splitting</b></a>, was actually a deceptive trick created by Māra the Evil One to frighten a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato">SN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the purpose of Māra’s frightening illusions as an attempt to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato"><b>confuse</b></a> or "blind" the monk to prevent him from achieving spiritual clarity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato">SN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed the monk Samiddhi to return to the same spot where he was frightened and to continue living there <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato"><b>ardently</b></a> despite Māra's attempts to disrupt him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato">SN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Samiddhi declared in verse to Māra that he had gone forth from the household life into homelessness out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a>, which served as his protection against fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato">SN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Samiddhi told Māra that because his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato"><b>mind was well concentrated</b></a> and his wisdom was mature, the Evil One could create whatever forms he liked without causing any distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato">SN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recorded that Māra the Evil One became sad and disappointed and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato"><b>vanished</b></a> the moment he realized the monk Samiddhi had recognized his true identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.22/en/sujato">SN 4.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's disciple Godhika attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>temporary liberation of mind</b></a> six times, only to fall away from it each time before succeeding on his seventh attempt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after falling away from meditative success six times, the Venerable Godhika decided to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>bring out the knife</b></a> to end his life rather than risk losing his spiritual attainment again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra the Evil One used his power to sense the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>thoughts</b></a> of Venerable Godhika and realized the monk was planning to take his own life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra the Evil One approached the Buddha and addressed him as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>Great Hero</b></a> with great wisdom, pleading with him to stop his disciple from committing suicide? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra the Evil One expressed concern to the Buddha that a disciple might die while still a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>trainee</b></a> (sekkha) who had not yet reached the final goal of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Māra that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>steadfast</b></a> do not cling to life and that Godhika had already uprooted all craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared Godhika had attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>final Nibbāna</b></a> after using a weapon on himself, having completely destroyed the root of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha and a group of monks went to visit Godhika, they found him lying on a couch with his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>shoulder turned</b></a>, having already passed away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out a phenomenon of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>smokiness and murkiness</b></a> moving in all directions around the deceased Godhika? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the strange murky smoke was actually Māra the Evil One searching for Godhika's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed Godhika had attained Nibbāna with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>unestablished consciousness</b></a>, meaning Māra could not find where his mind had "landed"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra the Evil One approached the Buddha carrying a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>yellow beluva-wood lute</b></a> while desperately searching for the destination of the monk Godhika? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the steadfast Godhika as one who was always delighted in meditation and had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>conquered the army of Death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed Godhika would never undergo <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>renewed existence</b></a> because he had pulled out craving along with its root? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Māra could not find Godhika's consciousness, he became so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>overcome with sorrow</b></a> that his lute dropped from his arm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that Māra, a "disappointed spirit," <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>vanished</b></a> on the spot after failing to find the liberated monk Godhika? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Godhika was so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>diligent and ardent</b></a> that he persisted in his meditation even after failing to maintain his mental liberation six times in a row? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra used <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>verses</b></a> to address the Buddha, attempting to convince him to forbid his disciple from ending his life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra admitted to the Buddha that he had searched for Godhika in the north, south, east, west, above, below, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato"><b>diagonally</b></a>, but could not find him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.23/en/sujato">SN 4.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was followed by Māra the Evil One for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato"><b>seven years</b></a> as Māra watched for any lapse or opening to exploit, yet he failed to find even a single opportunity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato">SN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at the foot of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato"><b>Ajapāla Banyan Tree</b></a> on the bank of the river Nerañjarā when Māra the Evil One approached him to mockingly ask if he was mourning in the forest because he had lost his wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato">SN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Māra that he meditates peacefully without sorrow because he has completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato"><b>dug out the root</b></a> of sorrow and cut off all longing for existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato">SN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Māra claimed that the Buddha would never escape him if his mind dwelled on things people call "mine," the Buddha replied that he did not possess any such things and that Māra would not even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato"><b>see his path</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato">SN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Māra that he instructs others about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato"><b>deathless realm</b></a> because people who want to reach the "far shore" ask him for guidance, and he provides them with the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato">SN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra the Evil One used a simile of children pulling a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato"><b>crab</b></a> out of a pond and breaking its claws to describe how the Buddha had effectively shattered all of Māra's tricks and distortions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato">SN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra the Evil One admitted that all his "distortions" and "vibrations" had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato"><b>broken and shattered</b></a> by the Buddha, leaving Māra unable to approach him with the hope of finding a lapse again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato">SN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra the Evil One compared his own failure to tempt the Buddha to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato"><b>crow</b></a> attacking a stone that looked like a lump of fat, only to fly away disappointed when it found no soft morsel to eat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato">SN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha addressed Māra as the "friend of the heedless" while declaring that he himself was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato"><b>taintless</b></a> and had destroyed the craving and greed for every state of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato">SN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Māra that his teaching to others was based on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato"><b>truth</b></a> that is free from all acquisitions, which he shares whenever he is questioned by those seeking liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.24/en/sujato">SN 4.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when wood-gatherers discovered the Buddha meditating in a forest grove in Kosala, they observed him sitting with his legs crossed and his body erect, having established <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness in front of him</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato">SN 7.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a Brahmin once questioned why the Buddha chose to meditate in a deep, frightening, and desolate forest, wondering if he was seeking the companionship of a world-ruler or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato"><b>the highest heaven</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato">SN 7.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to a visiting Brahmin that he meditates alone in the forest because he has completely uprooted all cravings and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato"><b>delights</b></a> that are born from the root of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato">SN 7.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as having attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato"><b>supreme enlightenment</b></a>, which allows him to meditate in solitude with total confidence and fearlessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato">SN 7.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the Brahmin Bhāradvāja that he possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato"><b>purified vision</b></a> regarding all things, having abandoned all doubts and attachments while dwelling in the wilderness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato">SN 7.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Bhāradvāja was so moved by the Buddha's explanation of his forest meditation that he declared the teaching to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato"><b>excellent</b></a> and took refuge in the Buddha for life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato">SN 7.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Brahmin Bhāradvāja first saw the Buddha in a desolate forest where there was no music or song, he was amazed that a sage could live there so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato"><b>joyfully</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.18/en/sujato">SN 7.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a Brahmin named Mātuposaka once asked the Buddha if he was doing the right thing by seeking alms righteously to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato"><b>support his parents</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato">SN 7.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that any person who righteously seeks alms and uses them to support their mother and father is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato"><b>generating much merit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato">SN 7.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who fulfill the duty of caring for their parents are praised by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato"><b>wise people</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato">SN 7.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who provides for their parents through righteous means will pass away and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato"><b>rejoice in heaven</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato">SN 7.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly told a Brahmin that by supporting his parents, he was indeed a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato"><b>acting properly</b></a> and fulfilling his duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato">SN 7.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for the specific service rendered to one's mother and father, a person is regarded as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato"><b>living righteously</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato">SN 7.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's discourse on the merit of supporting one's parents, the Brahmin Mātuposaka asked to be recognized as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a> from that day forward? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.19/en/sujato">SN 7.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a Brahmin mendicant argued he was equal to the Buddha because they both begged for food, the Buddha replied that one does not become a monk simply by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato"><b>begging from others</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato">SN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has merely adopted a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato"><b>foul Dhamma</b></a> (vissaṃ dhammaṃ) is not truly a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato">SN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a true monk as someone who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato"><b>set aside both merit and evil</b></a> to live a life of spiritual purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato">SN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a genuine monk is one who lives a holy life and wanders through the world with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato"><b>understanding</b></a> (saṅkhāya)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato">SN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a Brahmin named Saṅgārava, who lived in Sāvatthī, believed that he could achieve spiritual purification by being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato"><b>believer in water</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato">SN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Saṅgārava practiced a ritual of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato"><b>water immersion</b></a> every single morning and evening to cleanse himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato">SN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda, out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> for the Brahmin Saṅgārava, requested that the Buddha visit the Brahmin to teach him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato">SN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha consented to visit the Brahmin Saṅgārava's home by remaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato"><b>silent</b></a>, which was his customary way of accepting an invitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato">SN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Saṅgārava explained to the Buddha that his evening bath was intended to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato"><b>wash away</b></a> any evil deeds he had committed during the day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato">SN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Saṅgārava believed that by bathing in the morning, he could <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato"><b>purify</b></a> any evil deeds he had done throughout the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato">SN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the true "lake" for purification is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a>, which possesses the "shore of virtue" (sīlatittho)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato">SN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the lake of the Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato"><b>unclouded</b></a> and praised by those who are good? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato">SN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that "knowers of the Veda" bathe in the Dhamma and cross to the other shore with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato"><b>dry limbs</b></a>, implying they are not wet by ritual water but cleansed by truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato">SN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's explanation of internal purification, the Brahmin Saṅgārava declared the teaching <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato"><b>excellent</b></a> and took refuge in the Triple Gem? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato">SN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin mendicant in the Bhikkhaka Sutta was so moved by the Buddha's words that he asked to be recognized as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a> from that day forward? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato">SN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally visited the residence of the Brahmin Saṅgārava and sat on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato"><b>prepared seat</b></a> to have a dialogue about the Brahmin's ritual practices? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato">SN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a true monk is someone who leads a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> (brahmacariya) rather than just someone who receives alms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato">SN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Saṅgārava practiced his water purification rituals <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato"><b>every evening and morning</b></a> as a religious duty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato">SN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, crossing to the "other shore" of liberation is done by those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato"><b>cleansed</b></a> by the unclouded Dhamma rather than by physical water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.21/en/sujato">SN 7.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the Brahmin mendicant that one is not a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato"><b>to that extent</b></a> merely by adopting an outward form or begging for food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.20/en/sujato">SN 7.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Brahmin lady Dhanañjānī tripped while serving her husband food, she spontaneously shouted her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato"><b>homage</b></a> to the Buddha, the Arahant, and the Perfectly Enlightened One three times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato">SN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Bhāradvājagotta was so irritated by his wife’s devotion to the Buddha that he called her a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato"><b>wretched woman</b></a> and threatened to refute the doctrine of her "shaven-headed" teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato">SN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin lady Dhanañjānī confidently challenged her husband to visit the Buddha, telling him that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato"><b>no one in the world</b></a>—including gods, Maras, or Brahmas—could successfully refute the Buddha's doctrine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato">SN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Brahmin Bhāradvājagotta first approached the Buddha in an angry mood, he still observed the etiquette of exchanging <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato"><b>courteous greetings</b></a> before sitting down to speak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato">SN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Bhāradvājagotta asked the Buddha a riddle in verse, questioning what a person must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato"><b>cut off</b></a> in order to sleep in comfort and live without sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato">SN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the Brahmin Bhāradvājagotta that having cut off <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato"><b>anger</b></a> is the specific action that allows a person to sleep in comfort and remain free from sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato">SN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha vividly described anger as something that possesses a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato"><b>poisoned root</b></a> but has a honeyed tip? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato">SN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Noble Ones praise the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato"><b>slaying of anger</b></a> because once it is destroyed, one no longer experiences sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato">SN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha’s teaching on anger, the Brahmin Bhāradvājagotta declared the Dhamma to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato"><b>excellent</b></a> and compared the Buddha's explanation to lighting a lamp in the dark? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.1/en/sujato">SN 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was in the Sakyan town of Khomadussa, a group of Brahmins and householders mocked him as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato"><b>shaven-headed ascetic</b></a> when he approached their assembly hall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato">SN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an assembly is not truly an assembly unless it contains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato"><b>good people</b></a> who speak the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato">SN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who have abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato"><b>lust, hatred, and delusion</b></a> are the ones who truly qualify as "good people" (santo) when they speak the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato">SN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmins of Khomadussa were so impressed by the Buddha's verses that they compared his teaching to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato"><b>uncovering what was concealed</b></a> or lighting a lamp in the dark? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato">SN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was walking for alms in Khomadussa while it was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato"><b>drizzling</b></a>, which led him to seek shelter at a local assembly hall where the townspeople were gathered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato">SN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmins and householders of Khomadussa eventually went for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato"><b>refuge</b></a> to the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Sangha after their initial mockery turned to profound admiration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato">SN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa, while still a newly ordained monk, felt a surge of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato"><b>discontent</b></a> and lust after seeing a group of well-dressed women visiting the Aggāḷava Shrine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato">SN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa realized it was his own responsibility to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato"><b>dispel his own lust</b></a> rather than relying on another person to remove the mental disturbance for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato">SN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa described his intrusive sensual thoughts as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato"><b>impudent thoughts</b></a> (pagabbhā vitakkā) that were pursuing him while he lived the homeless life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato">SN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa asserted that even if a thousand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato"><b>trained archers</b></a> (uggaputtā mahissāsā) surrounded him, they could not make him flee because he was firmly established in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato">SN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa declared that his mind was firmly set upon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato"><b>path to Nibbāna</b></a>, which he had heard about directly from the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato">SN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa referred to the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato"><b>Kinsman of the Sun</b></a> (ādiccabandhu) while reflecting on the teaching he had received regarding the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato">SN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa confidently challenged <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato"><b>the Evil One</b></a> (Mara), stating that the tempter would not even be able to see his path because of his dedication to spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato">SN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa was acting as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato"><b>temple guardian</b></a> (vihārapālo) at the Aggāḷava Shrine when he faced his internal struggle with desire while his preceptor was away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato">SN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the Brahmins of Khomadussa that sages are those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato"><b>speak the Dhamma</b></a> after having abandoned passion and ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.22/en/sujato">SN 7.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa emphasized that even if many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato"><b>more women</b></a> arrived at the monastery, they would not be able to upset his composure once he was properly established in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.1/en/sujato">SN 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>uninstructed worldling</b></a> might easily become disenchanted with the physical body because its growth and decay are so obvious, yet they struggle to find disenchantment with the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the physical body, which is composed of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>four great elements</b></a>, can be seen to persist for ten, twenty, fifty, or even a hundred years or more? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described that which is called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a>, mentality, or consciousness as something that arises as one thing and ceases as another by day and by night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the famous analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>monkey</b></a> roaming through a forest who grabs one branch, lets it go, and then seizes another to illustrate how quickly consciousness changes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued it would actually be better for an uninstructed person to take the physical body as a self rather than the mind, because the body is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>more stable</b></a> over time while the mind is in constant flux? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that people remain deeply attached to the mind because they have long held the mistaken view: "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>This is mine</b></a>, this I am, this is my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>instructed noble disciple</b></a> avoids identifying with the mind by closely and carefully attending to the principle of dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the entire <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>mass of suffering</b></a> originates through a specific causal chain, where the presence of ignorance leads to the arising of volitional formations and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the cessation of the entire mass of suffering is achieved only through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>remainderless fading away</b></a> and cessation of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a noble disciple who truly understands dependent origination becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that through disenchantment one becomes dispassionate, and it is through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> that the mind is finally liberated from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the specific knowledge that arises upon liberation as the realization that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a>, the holy life has been lived, and there is no more returning to any state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> as the specific condition that gives rise to consciousness within the framework of dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that while the body clearly shows <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato"><b>increase and decline</b></a> over many years, the mind's extreme rapidity of transformation is what makes it so difficult for the uninstructed to let go of it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.61/en/sujato">SN 12.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>uninstructed worldling</b></a> might be able to become disenchanted, dispassionate, and liberated regarding the physical body, yet finds it nearly impossible to do so regarding the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the physical body, composed of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>four great elements</b></a>, clearly exhibits growth and decline, making its true nature easier for ordinary people to perceive than the nature of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that people struggle to let go of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a>, mentality, or consciousness because they have long clung to it with the mistaken view: "This is mine, this I am, this is my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it would actually be better for an uninstructed person to take the physical body as a self rather than the mind, because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>body</b></a> is far more stable and long-lasting than the ever-changing mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that while the body can persist for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>one hundred years</b></a> or more, what is called consciousness arises as one thing and ceases as another by day and by night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>instructed noble disciple</b></a> avoids falling into the trap of identifying with the mind by closely and carefully attending to the principle of dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the core principle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>conditionality</b></a> with the formula: "When this exists, that comes to be; with the arising of this, that arises; when this does not exist, that does not come to be; with the cessation of this, that ceases"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>pleasant feeling</b></a> arises only in dependence on a contact that is to be experienced as pleasant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> that produces a pleasant feeling ceases, the resulting feeling also ceases and subsides immediately? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>painful feeling</b></a> arises dependent on a contact experienced as painful, and it ceases once that particular contact is no longer present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling</b></a> follows the same law of conditionality, arising and ceasing based on the presence or absence of its corresponding contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of rubbing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>two sticks</b></a> together to show that just as heat and fire are generated by friction, feelings are generated solely through contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the cessation of feelings by explaining that just as the heat generated by friction is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>extinguished</b></a> when the two sticks are separated, feelings vanish when contact ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a well-taught disciple becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> not just with the body, but with contact, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that through disenchantment, a practitioner becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>dispassionate</b></a>, and it is through this dispassion that the mind is finally liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the realization of a liberated person as the clear <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>knowledge</b></a> that "Birth is destroyed, the holy life has been lived, what had to be done has been done"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person who has achieved liberation, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>no more returning</b></a> to any state of being in the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the terms mind, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>mentality</b></a>, and consciousness interchangeably to describe the internal process that is in a state of constant flux? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> are among the things that a noble disciple must become disenchanted with to reach final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato"><b>extreme rapidity</b></a> of the mind's change by noting it arises as one thing and ceases as another throughout every day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.62/en/sujato">SN 12.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>four nutriments</b></a> that are necessary for the maintenance of beings that have already come into being and for the support of those seeking a new existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>edible food</b></a>, whether coarse or subtle, is the first of four nutriments required for the sustenance of living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a graphic simile of a couple in a desert forced to eat their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>only son's flesh</b></a> just to survive and complete their journey to illustrate how edible food should be regarded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that edible food should not be taken for fun, indulgence, or personal beauty, but solely for the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>crossing the desert</b></a> of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the nutriment of edible food is fully understood, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>lust for the five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a> is also fully understood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a noble disciple fully understands the lust for sensual pleasure, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>no fetter</b></a> remaining that could bind them to return to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> as the second nutriment, which he compared to the constant suffering of a flayed cow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>flayed cow</b></a>—attacked by insects and creatures wherever she stands—to illustrate the pervasive and painful nature of the nutriment of contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if the nutriment of contact is fully understood, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>three feelings</b></a> (pleasant, painful, and neither-painful-nor-pleasant) are also fully understood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that once the three feelings are fully understood by a noble disciple, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>nothing further</b></a> to be done to achieve liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>mental volition</b></a> as the third nutriment, comparing its dangerous nature to a man being dragged toward a pit of glowing coals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the danger of mental volition through the simile of two strong men dragging a person toward a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>pit of glowing coals</b></a> from which they desperately want to escape? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the nutriment of mental volition is fully understood, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>three cravings</b></a> (sensual craving, craving for existence, and craving for extermination) are also fully understood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is the fourth nutriment, which should be viewed with the same dread as a criminal undergoing severe daily punishment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a simile of a criminal being stabbed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>three hundred spears</b></a> a day—one hundred in the morning, one hundred at noon, and one hundred in the evening—to illustrate the nature of the nutriment of consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if the nutriment of consciousness is fully understood, then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> is also fully understood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the purpose of understanding these nutriments is to realize there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>nothing more</b></a> for a noble disciple to do once name-and-form and consciousness are fully comprehended? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a couple eating their child's flesh in the desert would feel <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>distress and grief</b></a>, emphasizing that edible food should be viewed with a similar lack of attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the nutriment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>mental volition</b></a> is a source of great suffering because it leads to death or death-like pain, just like falling into a pit of charcoal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person being stabbed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato"><b>one hundred spears</b></a> three times a day, the experience is one of pure pain and displeasure, paralleling the suffering inherent in consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.63/en/sujato">SN 12.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>four nutriments</b></a>—edible food, contact, mental volition, and consciousness—which are necessary for the maintenance of beings and for the support of those seeking a new existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if there is lust, delight, and craving for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>edible food</b></a>, consciousness becomes established and grows, leading to the continuation of the cycle of birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when consciousness becomes established and grows in any of the four nutriments, there is a subsequent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>descent of name-and-form</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the descent and presence of name-and-form inevitably leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>growth of volitional formations</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wherever there is the growth of volitional formations, there is the production of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>renewed existence</b></a> in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the production of future renewed existence leads directly to the experience of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>future birth, aging, and death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that any state resulting in future birth, aging, and death is inherently accompanied by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>sorrow, despair, and anguish</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the nutriment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a>, if accompanied by lust and craving, provides a platform for consciousness to become established and flourish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the nutriment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>mental volition</b></a> is a condition for the growth of consciousness when there is underlying delight and attachment to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> itself as a nutriment that, when craved, leads to its own further establishment and the subsequent descent of name-and-form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>painter or a drawer</b></a> to illustrate how existence is "created" when the "dyes" of lust and craving are present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the process of becoming to a painter using various <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>dyes and pigments</b></a> like crimson, turmeric, indigo, or madder to create a form on a polished board or wall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a painter can only create the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>image of a man or a woman</b></a> if the necessary materials and pigments are available, just as existence arises only when the nutriments and craving coexist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if there is no lust, delight, or craving for the four nutriments, consciousness becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>unestablished</b></a> and does not grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when consciousness is not established, there is no descent of name-and-form, effectively <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>stopping the cycle</b></a> of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that where there is no future renewed existence, the state is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>without sorrow</b></a>, despair, and anguish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>sunbeam</b></a> entering a window to explain how consciousness seeks a place to land or "establish" itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted in his analogy that a sunbeam entering through an eastern window would normally land on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>western wall</b></a> of a house? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if there were no western wall for a sunbeam to land on, the light would then land <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>on the earth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha further illustrated that if there were no earth for a sunbeam to land on, the light would land <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>on the water</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that if there were no wall, earth, or water, the sunbeam would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>not established anywhere</b></a>, just as consciousness remains unestablished when there is no lust for the nutriments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the four nutriments provide the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato"><b>support</b></a> needed for beings seeking a new existence to actually come into being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.64/en/sujato">SN 12.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato"><b>noble silence</b></a> as the attainment of the second jhāna, which is characterized by the subsiding of thought and examination and the presence of internal confidence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato">SN 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even while practicing the second jhāna, Venerable Mahāmoggallāna found that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato"><b>perceptions and maneuvers</b></a> associated with thought still occurred to him until he received further guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato">SN 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his psychic power to approach Venerable Mahāmoggallāna and urged him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato"><b>steady the mind</b></a> and unify it specifically within the state of noble silence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato">SN 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna identified the second jhāna as a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato"><b>unification of mind</b></a> that is born of concentration and accompanied by rapture and pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato">SN 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically warned Venerable Mahāmoggallāna not to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato"><b>negligent</b></a> regarding the practice of noble silence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato">SN 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna credited his attainment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato"><b>greatness of direct knowledge</b></a> to being a disciple who was personally assisted and encouraged by the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato">SN 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahāmoggallāna, noble silence is a meditative state that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato"><b>free from thought</b></a> and examination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato">SN 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna, while in seclusion, contemplated the meaning of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato"><b>noble silence</b></a> before the Buddha intervened to clarify its practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.1/en/sujato">SN 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out Venerable Lakuṇḍakabhaddiya to the monks, noting that although he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato"><b>ugly and unsightly</b></a>, he possessed immense spiritual power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato">SN 21.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared Venerable Lakuṇḍakabhaddiya to be a monk of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato"><b>great psychic power</b></a> and mighty influence despite his stunted physical appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato">SN 21.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that Venerable Lakuṇḍakabhaddiya had achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato"><b>supreme goal</b></a> of the holy life, for which clansmen rightly go forth from the household life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato">SN 21.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the meditative attainments mastered by Venerable Lakuṇḍakabhaddiya are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato"><b>not easy to gain</b></a> for most practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato">SN 21.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato"><b>lion</b></a>, whom all other animals fear regardless of their own size, to illustrate that spiritual power is not determined by physical stature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato">SN 21.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that among human beings, a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato"><b>wise</b></a> is considered truly great, even if they are young or physically small? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato">SN 21.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that a person is not great simply by being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato"><b>fool with a large body</b></a>, as wisdom is the true measure of greatness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato">SN 21.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that Venerable Lakuṇḍakabhaddiya was often <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato"><b>looked down upon</b></a> by other monks because of his dwarfish and unsightly physical appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.6/en/sujato">SN 21.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa, who was the Buddha's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato"><b>paternal cousin</b></a>, once approached the Buddha in a state of distress and tears because he felt mistreated by other monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato">SN 21.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha corrected Venerable Tissa by pointing out that although he was quick to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato"><b>criticize others</b></a>, he was not patient or enduring when others spoke to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato">SN 21.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a clansman who has gone forth out of faith, it is only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato"><b>proper and fitting</b></a> to be both a speaker and one who can endure the speech of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato">SN 21.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised Tissa that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato"><b>non-anger</b></a> is always better than giving in to anger when provoked by the words of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato">SN 21.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> is lived specifically for the purpose of removing anger, conceit, and hypocrisy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato">SN 21.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a verse to ask Tissa why he was angry, reminding him that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato"><b>removal of conceit</b></a> is a core goal of a monk’s training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato">SN 21.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa complained to the Buddha that other monks were stinging him with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato"><b>sharp words</b></a> and completely surrounding him with their verbal attacks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato">SN 21.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that there was a monk named Thera who was famous among the community because he lived as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato"><b>solitary dweller</b></a> and constantly spoke in praise of living alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato">SN 21.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Thera defined "living alone" to the Buddha as entering the village for alms alone, returning alone, and practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato"><b>walking meditation</b></a> in total solitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato">SN 21.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha acknowledged Venerable Thera's physical solitude, he taught that there is a way that living alone is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato"><b>fulfilled in detail</b></a> that goes beyond just being physically apart from others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato">SN 21.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that true solitary dwelling involves ensuring that whatever is of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato"><b>past is abandoned</b></a> so that it no longer burdens the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato">SN 21.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who "dwells alone" is one for whom the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato"><b>future is relinquished</b></a>, meaning they do not cling to expectations or anxieties about what is to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato">SN 21.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the perfection of living alone as a state where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato"><b>desire and lust</b></a> for present forms of individual existence have been thoroughly removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato">SN 21.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ideal solitary dweller as someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato"><b>unsullied</b></a> by any of the factors of existence (dhammas)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato">SN 21.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato"><b>all-conqueror</b></a> to describe a person who has truly mastered the art of living alone by overcoming all internal and external bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato">SN 21.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person is truly a solitary dweller when they are liberated through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato">SN 21.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a> who has given up everything and knows all, identifying such a person as the true "one who dwells alone"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato">SN 21.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Thera was specifically summoned by the Buddha after other monks reported his habit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato"><b>living in seclusion</b></a> to the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato">SN 21.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Venerable Tissa that his behavior of being a speaker who was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato"><b>not patient under criticism</b></a> was not appropriate for someone who had left the household life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.9/en/sujato">SN 21.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's explanation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato"><b>living alone</b></a> shifted the focus from mere physical isolation to the internal psychological state of being free from clinging to the three periods of time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn21.10/en/sujato">SN 21.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.15/en/sujato"><b>form is impermanent</b></a>, and that anything which is impermanent is inherently suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.15/en/sujato">SN 22.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, because feelings and perceptions are impermanent and suffering, they must be viewed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.15/en/sujato"><b>proper wisdom</b></a> as "this is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.15/en/sujato">SN 22.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that whatever is suffering is also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.15/en/sujato"><b>non-self</b></a>, meaning it should not be regarded as one’s soul or essential identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.15/en/sujato">SN 22.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.16/en/sujato"><b>form is suffering</b></a>, and that seeing this truth clearly leads a noble disciple to become disenchanted with the physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.16/en/sujato">SN 22.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.16/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> are suffering, and because they are suffering, they are non-self and should not be identified with? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.16/en/sujato">SN 22.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.16/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is suffering, and once a person truly understands this, they realize there is no further state of existence after this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.16/en/sujato">SN 22.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that not only is form impermanent, but the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.18/en/sujato"><b>cause and condition</b></a> for the arising of form are also impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.18/en/sujato">SN 22.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked his followers how form could possibly be permanent if it is produced from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.18/en/sujato"><b>impermanent sources</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.18/en/sujato">SN 22.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.18/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> is impermanent because the very conditions that allow feelings to arise are themselves unstable and changing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.18/en/sujato">SN 22.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.18/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> is impermanent since it originates from causes that are themselves subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.18/en/sujato">SN 22.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.18/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> cannot be permanent because it arises based on conditions that are fundamentally impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.18/en/sujato">SN 22.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that form is suffering because its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.19/en/sujato"><b>cause and condition</b></a> are also characterized by suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.19/en/sujato">SN 22.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha raised the question of how form could ever lead to true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.19/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a> if it has been produced from that which is inherently suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.19/en/sujato">SN 22.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.19/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is suffering, and seeing this truth as it really is leads to the end of the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.19/en/sujato">SN 22.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a noble disciple who sees the aggregates as suffering becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.15/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a>, and through that disenchantment, they attain liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.15/en/sujato">SN 22.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed the elderly householder Nakulapitā to train himself so that even though his physical body was afflicted, his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>mind would not be afflicted</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder Nakulapitā approached the Buddha for advice because he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>old and frail</b></a>, describing himself as one whose body was constantly ailing and who frequently saw the physician? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that anyone carrying around a physical body who claims to be healthy even for a moment is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>fool</b></a>, because the body is inherently fragile and encumbered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta noticed Nakulapitā’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>serene appearance</b></a> and pure complexion after the householder had listened to a brief teaching from the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulapitā told Venerable Sāriputta that he felt he had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>sprinkled with nectar</b></a> by the Dhamma teaching he received from the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>afflicted in mind</b></a> when they regard physical form as their self or as belonging to their self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>untaught worldling</b></a> who is not disciplined in the Dhamma is the one who suffers mentally when their physical body changes and alters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that mental affliction arises when a person remains obsessed with the notion <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>I am form</b></a>, especially when that form begins to decay or change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that sorrow and despair arise for an individual when their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>feeling changes</b></a> and alters, provided they have identified that feeling as their self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta described a person as being both afflicted in body and mind if they regard <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> as their self and then suffer when that perception inevitably changes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, identifying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> as one's self leads to deep mental pain and distress when those formations undergo transformation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a person becomes afflicted in mind when they are obsessed with the idea that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is their self, only to face lamentation when that consciousness alters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>taught noble disciple</b></a> as someone who does not regard form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, or consciousness as being their self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that a noble disciple is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>afflicted in mind</b></a> even when their physical body changes, because they do not identify with the form as "mine"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> alters and changes for a well-taught disciple, sorrow and lamentation do not arise because they lack the "I am feeling" obsession? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a person is not afflicted in mind if they do not regard <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> as self and therefore remain untroubled when perception transforms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta asserted that the absence of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>I am volitional formations</b></a> obsession prevents the arising of grief when those mental formations inevitably change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that a disciple who does not see <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> as self remains free from mental affliction and despair even when that consciousness alters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the householder Nakulapitā was delighted and satisfied by Venerable Sāriputta’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>detailed explanation</b></a> of how one can be afflicted in body but not in mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta questioned Nakulapitā on whether he had the presence of mind to ask the Buddha for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>deeper explanation</b></a> regarding the difference between bodily and mental affliction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nakulapitā stated he would travel a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>great distance</b></a> just to hear the meaning of the Buddha’s brief instructions clarified by Venerable Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that identifying the self as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>possessing form</b></a> is one of the specific ways an untaught person becomes mentally afflicted when their body decays? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the physical body is like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato"><b>frail egg</b></a>, being inherently weak, bound to break, and covered with a shell of infirmity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.1/en/sujato">SN 22.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was at Kapilavatthu, deities from ten world-systems gathered in great numbers to see both him and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato"><b>unconquered Sangha</b></a> of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato">SN 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that four deities from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato"><b>Pure Abodes</b></a> (Suddhāvāsa) used their spiritual power to appear instantly before the Buddha, intending to each recite a verse in his presence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato">SN 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the first deity to speak described the gathering in the grove as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato"><b>Great Assembly</b></a> (Mahāsamaya) where celestial beings had come together to witness the community of enlightened monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato">SN 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity praised the monks' mental discipline, observing that they had made their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato"><b>minds straight</b></a> and concentrated, much like a charioteer holding the reins to stay on course? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato">SN 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity noted that the wise monks under the Buddha’s guidance effectively <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato"><b>guarded their senses</b></a>, comparing their control to that of a skilled driver managing a team of horses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato">SN 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity described the monks who have attained enlightenment as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato"><b>cut the stake</b></a> and the bar, and uprooted the pillar, signifying their complete freedom from mental fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato">SN 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity compared the monks to "young noble bulls" or "elephants" (susunāgā), describing them as being pure, spotless, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato"><b>well-tamed</b></a> by the Buddha, the "One with Vision"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato">SN 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity proclaimed that those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato"><b>gone for refuge</b></a> to the Buddha will be protected from falling into the plane of misery (apāyabhūmi) after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato">SN 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity stated that upon discarding the human body, those who follow the Buddha will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato"><b>fill the host</b></a> of devas, signifying a fortunate rebirth in the celestial realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.37/en/sujato">SN 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha's foot was once cut by a stone splinter at Maddakucchi in the Deer Park, he endured the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>severe physical pain</b></a> mindfully and clearly comprehending, without becoming distressed or unperturbed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after sustaining a painful injury to his foot, the Buddha composed himself by spreading out his outer robe folded in four and lying down on his right side in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>lion’s posture</b></a>, placing one foot on the other while remaining mindful and clearly comprehending? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Sakalika Sutta, seven hundred deities belonging to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>Satullapa group</b></a> visited the Buddha at Maddakucchi late at night, illuminating the entire park with their radiant beauty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity compared the Buddha’s endurance of sharp and agonizing physical pain to that of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>tusker</b></a> (nāga), because he remained unperturbed and mindful despite the intensity of the bodily sensation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity praised the Buddha’s resilience by comparing him to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>lion</b></a> (sīha), noting how he bore distressing and unpleasant bodily feelings with mindfulness and without being shaken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity likened the Buddha to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>thoroughbred</b></a> (ājānīya), highlighting his ability to endure painful and bitter physical sensations with perfect mindfulness and clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity described the Buddha’s strength of mind by comparing him to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>bull</b></a> (nisabha), observing that even when faced with sharp and agonizing pain, he remained unharassed and composed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity compared the Buddha to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>draft-ox</b></a> (dhorayha) for his capacity to carry the burden of intense physical suffering without losing his focus or mental stability? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity characterized the Buddha as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>tamed one</b></a> (danta), because he could endure even the most unpleasant and harsh bodily feelings while maintaining full awareness and equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity invited others to behold the Buddha’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>well-developed concentration</b></a> and liberated mind, which was neither leaning forward nor backward, nor held back by the forceful suppression of volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity declared that only someone who lacks spiritual vision would think they could surpass the Buddha, whom they described as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>tusker among men</b></a> and a lion among men? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity remarked that Brahmins who study the five Vedas for a hundred years while practicing austerities still possess an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>inferior nature</b></a> if their minds have not been properly liberated from attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity pointed out that those overcome by craving and bound by rules and vows, who practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>harsh asceticism</b></a> for a hundred years, will still fail to reach the "far shore" of final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity taught that there is no true self-discipline for one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>desirous of status</b></a>, nor is there sagehood for one who has an unconcentrated mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity warned that even if a person lives alone in the forest, if they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>negligent</b></a> (pamatta), they will be unable to cross beyond the realm of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>discarding conceit</b></a> and being well-concentrated and mentally liberated, a practitioner can truly cross beyond the realm of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>everywhere liberated</b></a> as someone who has attained a pure mind and is no longer bound by any mental fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato"><b>diligent</b></a> (appamatta) while dwelling alone in the forest and has a mind free from fetters is the one who successfully transcends the power of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.38/en/sujato">SN 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity named Cūḷakokanadā, daughter of Pajjunna, once visited the Buddha at the Gabled Hall in Vesālī, illuminating the entire Mahāvana wood with her beauty while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.40/en/sujato"><b>paying homage</b></a> to the Buddha and the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.40/en/sujato">SN 1.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deity Cūḷakokanadā declared that one should not do <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.40/en/sujato"><b>evil with speech</b></a>, mind, or body in any part of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.40/en/sujato">SN 1.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deity Cūḷakokanadā taught that after abandoning sensual desires, a person should remain mindful and clearly comprehending to ensure they do not follow <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.40/en/sujato"><b>unprofitable suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.40/en/sujato">SN 1.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity explained to the Buddha that just as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.41/en/sujato"><b>vessel pulled out</b></a> of a burning house is the only one that remains of use, so too is wealth that is rescued from the "burning" world through giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.41/en/sujato">SN 1.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity compared the world to a house on fire because it is constantly being consumed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.41/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.41/en/sujato">SN 1.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity stated that what is given is "well-rescued" because it yields <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.41/en/sujato"><b>happy results</b></a>, whereas wealth that is kept may be lost to thieves, kings, or fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.41/en/sujato">SN 1.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity encouraged the wise to both use and give their wealth, noting that one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.41/en/sujato"><b>not blamed</b></a> for stinginess in this life will reach a heavenly destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.41/en/sujato">SN 1.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who gives food is actually a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.42/en/sujato"><b>giver of strength</b></a> to the recipient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.42/en/sujato">SN 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the giver of a vehicle (such as a cart or transport) is considered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.42/en/sujato"><b>giver of happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.42/en/sujato">SN 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the person who provides a dwelling or shelter is effectively a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.42/en/sujato"><b>giver of everything</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.42/en/sujato">SN 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the person who instructs others in the Dhamma is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.42/en/sujato"><b>giver of the Deathless</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.42/en/sujato">SN 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity observed that both devas and humans <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.43/en/sujato"><b>delight in food</b></a>, prompting the Buddha to explain the karmic benefits of sharing it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.43/en/sujato">SN 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when people give food with faith and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.43/en/sujato"><b>placid mind</b></a>, they find that very gift waiting for them as a benefit in both this world and the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.43/en/sujato">SN 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.43/en/sujato"><b>subdue stinginess</b></a> and give gifts because merit is the only true support for living beings in the future world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.43/en/sujato">SN 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the noble path to liberation as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.46/en/sujato"><b>straight way</b></a> and the final destination as "the fearless" (Nibbāna)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.46/en/sujato">SN 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened the spiritual journey to riding a silent chariot equipped with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.46/en/sujato"><b>wheels of Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.46/en/sujato">SN 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that moral shame (hiri) serves as the leaning-board of the spiritual chariot, while truth acts as its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.46/en/sujato"><b>protective upholstery</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.46/en/sujato">SN 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that anyone, whether woman or man, who possesses the "vehicle" of the Dhamma with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.46/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> as its runner, will certainly draw near to Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.46/en/sujato">SN 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that merit increases day and night for those who plant gardens and groves, as well as for those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.47/en/sujato"><b>build bridges</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.47/en/sujato">SN 1.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, people who provide wells, water tanks, and shelters are considered to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.47/en/sujato"><b>established in Dhamma</b></a> and virtuous, eventually heading to a heavenly destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.47/en/sujato">SN 1.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity proclaimed in the Jetavanasutta that mortals are purified by action, knowledge, Dhamma, and virtue, and notably <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.48/en/sujato"><b>not by lineage</b></a> or wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.48/en/sujato">SN 1.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity praised the Venerable Sāriputta, stating that any monk who has reached the "far shore" could at most only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.48/en/sujato"><b>equal Sāriputta</b></a> in wisdom, virtue, and peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.48/en/sujato">SN 1.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that stingy people who insult others or hinder them from giving are reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.49/en/sujato"><b>hell or the animal realm</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.49/en/sujato">SN 1.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a stingy person happens to be reborn as a human, they will be born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.49/en/sujato"><b>poor family</b></a> where necessities like food and clothing are difficult to obtain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.49/en/sujato">SN 1.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are generous and have firm faith in the Triple Gem—the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha—will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.49/en/sujato"><b>appear in heaven</b></a> after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.49/en/sujato">SN 1.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that generous people who are reborn in the human world find themselves in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.49/en/sujato"><b>wealthy families</b></a> where they easily enjoy sensual pleasures and abundance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.49/en/sujato">SN 1.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.51/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> as the one thing that is good to possess even until old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.51/en/sujato">SN 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.51/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> is the quality that is best when well-established in a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.51/en/sujato">SN 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.51/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> as the true treasure of human beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.51/en/sujato">SN 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that while material wealth can be stolen, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.51/en/sujato"><b>merit</b></a> is the one thing that is difficult for thieves to take away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.51/en/sujato">SN 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, when asked what remains good even when one is not aging, replied that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.52/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> remains good? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.52/en/sujato">SN 1.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.53/en/sujato"><b>caravan</b></a> (or travel companions) as the friend of a person who is traveling abroad? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.53/en/sujato">SN 1.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.53/en/sujato"><b>mother</b></a> is the person's best friend within their own home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.53/en/sujato">SN 1.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.53/en/sujato"><b>merits made by oneself</b></a> are the only true friends that follow and support a person in the future life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.53/en/sujato">SN 1.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.55/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> is the primary force that gives birth to a person and keeps them in the cycle of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.55/en/sujato">SN 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, it is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.55/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> of a person that "runs about" or wanders through various experiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.55/en/sujato">SN 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a "being" (satta) as that which <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.55/en/sujato"><b>enters samsara</b></a>, the round of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.55/en/sujato">SN 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.55/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> is the greatest fear or danger that a person faces in existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.55/en/sujato">SN 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remained completely unmoved by the temptations of Māra’s daughters because he was liberated in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed destruction of acquisitions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra described the Buddha as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> and a Sugata in the world who cannot be easily led by passion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the daughters of Māra—Craving, Discontent, and Passion—initially promised their father they would bring the Buddha back under his control by binding him with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>snare of passion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained he meditates alone in the forest because he has conquered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>army of pleasant and agreeable forms</b></a> and won peace of heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the daughters of Māra reflected that if they had directed their seductive charms toward an unliberated person, that person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>heart would burst</b></a> or they would have gone mad? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a bhikkhu who has crossed the "floods" of existence as one who possesses a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>tranquil body</b></a> and a well-liberated mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the Tathāgatas lead people to the far shore by means of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>True Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after failing to defeat the Buddha, Māra was found by his daughters sitting dejectedly and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>scratching the ground</b></a> with a stick? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in an attempt to cater to the diverse preferences of men, the daughters of Māra manifested themselves as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>one hundred young girls</b></a> to try and tempt the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even when the daughters of Māra transformed themselves into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>one hundred middle-aged women</b></a>, the Buddha paid them no attention at all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is mindful and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>beyond formations</b></a> does not get angry and remains unbothered by sensual perceptions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra mocked his daughters’ failed mission by comparing their attempt to provoke the Buddha to trying to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>split a mountain</b></a> with lily stalks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the daughter of Māra named Craving (Taṇhā) that he does not seek social intimacy because he has already experienced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>bliss of enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the daughters of Māra realized an unliberated person under their influence would wither away like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>green reed</b></a> that has been cut and laid in the sun? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sources describe the Buddha as having scattered the three daughters of Māra just as the wind blows away a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>tuft of wool</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the daughters of Māra manifested as women who had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>given birth once</b></a> and women who had given birth twice as part of their exhaustive attempt to seduce the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra warned his daughters that the Buddha had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>transcended the realm of Māra</b></a> and was no longer subject to his influence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the daughter Passion (Ragā) observed that many would follow the Buddha's path, the Buddha replied that those who understand the Dhamma have no reason to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>envious</b></a> of the Tathāgatas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>not a laggard</b></a> and understands the Dhamma can cross the "sixth flood" of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra compared his daughters’ attempt to attack the Buddha to trying to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato"><b>dig into a mountain</b></a> with one’s bare fingernails? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.25/en/sujato">SN 4.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed his monks that he attained unsurpassed deliverance specifically through the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.4/en/sujato"><b>wise reflection</b></a> and wise effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.4/en/sujato">SN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his disciples to also strive for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.4/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed deliverance</b></a> by applying the same wise reflection and wise effort that he used to achieve liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.4/en/sujato">SN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra the Evil One once confronted the Buddha in the Deer Park, claiming that the Buddha was still <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.4/en/sujato"><b>bound by the snare</b></a> of Māra, whether divine or human? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.4/en/sujato">SN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared himself completely freed from Māra's snare and told the "Exterminator" (Māra) that he had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.4/en/sujato"><b>defeated</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.4/en/sujato">SN 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly confirmed to his monks that both he and they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato"><b>freed from all snares</b></a>, encompassing both divine and human bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato">SN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave a famous missionary command to his monks to wander for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato"><b>good of the many</b></a> and the happiness of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato">SN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his monks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato"><b>not to go two by the same way</b></a> when they first set out to spread the Dhamma, in order to reach as many people as possible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato">SN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Dhamma he wanted his monks to teach as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato"><b>good in the beginning</b></a>, good in the middle, and good in the end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato">SN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to proclaim the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a>, which is utterly perfect and pure in both its meaning and its phrasing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato">SN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there are beings with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato"><b>little dust in their eyes</b></a> who will perish if they do not hear the Dhamma, but will understand it if it is taught to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato">SN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after sending his monks out to teach, the Buddha himself traveled to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato"><b>Uruvelā</b></a> at Senānigama to personally engage in teaching the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato">SN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even when Māra insisted the Buddha was held by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato"><b>great bond</b></a>, the Buddha maintained that he was freed from all snares and that Māra's influence had been terminated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.5/en/sujato">SN 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was sitting in the open air during a drizzling night, Māra the Evil One transformed himself into a giant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato"><b>serpent king</b></a> with a body as large as a boat to try and terrify him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato">SN 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a sage who frequents <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato"><b>empty houses</b></a> as one who should remain self-controlled and not tremble, even when faced with terrifying creatures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato">SN 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that even if the sky were to split or the earth were to shake, Buddhas would never seek <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato"><b>protection in acquisitions</b></a> because they have transcended fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato">SN 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra attempted to intimidate the Buddha by manifesting a serpent with eyes like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato"><b>large bronze plates</b></a> from Kosala and a tongue that flickered like flashes of lightning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato">SN 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remained completely unmoved when Māra manifested a serpent whose hood was as large as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato"><b>brewer’s sieve</b></a> and whose breath sounded like a smith's bellows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato">SN 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha practiced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato"><b>lion's posture</b></a>—lying on his right side with one foot overlapping the other—after spending much of the night walking in the open air? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato">SN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Māra mocked the Buddha for sleeping like a "misfortunate one" after the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato"><b>sun had risen</b></a>, the Buddha replied that he sleeps peacefully because he is free from the sting of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato">SN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he can sleep without concern because he has achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato"><b>destruction of all acquisitions</b></a> and is no longer entangled in the "ensnaring" net of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato">SN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra argued that a person with children <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.8/en/sujato"><b>delights in children</b></a> and a cattle owner delights in cattle, claiming that acquisitions are the source of human joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.8/en/sujato">SN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha countered Māra’s view on joy by stating that a person actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.8/en/sujato"><b>sorrows because of children</b></a> and cattle, as acquisitions are actually the source of human sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.8/en/sujato">SN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.8/en/sujato"><b>without acquisitions</b></a> is the only one who truly does not sorrow, refuting Māra's claim that such a person lacks delight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.8/en/sujato">SN 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after failing to frighten the Buddha with a serpent disguise, Māra became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato"><b>miserable and disappointed</b></a> and vanished as soon as he realized the Buddha had recognized him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato">SN 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha maintained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a> even while lying down to sleep, keeping the "thought of rising" firmly in his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato">SN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to craving as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato"><b>viscous attachment</b></a> that leads one anywhere, and confirmed that he had entirely removed it from his life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato">SN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato"><b>great sage</b></a> who has gone to an empty hut does not even stir a hair on his head when faced with many terrors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.6/en/sujato">SN 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra questioned whether the Buddha was sleeping in an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato"><b>empty house</b></a> because he had nothing to do or because he was in a state of misfortune? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato">SN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized his rest as the sleep of one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato"><b>crossed to the far shore</b></a>, free from the burdens of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.7/en/sujato">SN 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.9/en/sujato"><b>life of humans</b></a> is very short and that for anyone who is born, death is absolutely certain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.9/en/sujato">SN 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his disciples to perform wholesome deeds and lead a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.9/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> because no one who is born can avoid mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.9/en/sujato">SN 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Māra the Evil One claimed that human life is long and should not be despised, the Buddha countered that one should live as though their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.9/en/sujato"><b>head is on fire</b></a> because death is always approaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.9/en/sujato">SN 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that even those who live a long time generally only live for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.10/en/sujato"><b>hundred years</b></a> or perhaps a little more? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.10/en/sujato">SN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra unsuccessfully tried to tempt the Buddha by arguing that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.10/en/sujato"><b>days and nights</b></a> do not pass and that life does not truly come to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.10/en/sujato">SN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refuted Māra's claims by stating that days and nights do pass and that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.10/en/sujato"><b>life of mortals</b></a> is steadily exhausted like the water of small streams? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.10/en/sujato">SN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra once attempted to strike the Buddha with fear and dread by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.11/en/sujato"><b>splitting large rocks</b></a> while the Buddha was sitting outdoors in the dark and rain at Vulture’s Peak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.11/en/sujato">SN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that even if one were to move the entire <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.11/en/sujato"><b>Vulture’s Peak mountain</b></a>, a fully liberated Buddha would remain completely unmoved and without commotion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.11/en/sujato">SN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.11/en/sujato"><b>rightly liberated</b></a> through perfect knowledge have no inner trembling or instability, even when faced with terrifying external events? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.11/en/sujato">SN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Māra questioned if the Buddha "roared like a lion" because he believed he had no opponent, the Buddha replied that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.12/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgatas</b></a> roar because they have attained spiritual power and crossed over the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.12/en/sujato">SN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.12/en/sujato"><b>great hero</b></a> who speaks with confidence and fearlessness while teaching the Dhamma to large assemblies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.12/en/sujato">SN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the attachments of the world as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.12/en/sujato"><b>viscous attachment</b></a> that he has successfully crossed over and transcended? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.12/en/sujato">SN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized to his monks that because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.9/en/sujato"><b>beyond must be faced</b></a> after death, it is essential to prioritize wholesome actions in the present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.9/en/sujato">SN 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra would often become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.10/en/sujato"><b>sorrowful and dejected</b></a> and vanish immediately upon realizing that the Buddha had recognized his deceptive intentions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.10/en/sujato">SN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.9/en/sujato"><b>no immortality</b></a> for anyone who has been born, highlighting the temporary nature of all human existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.9/en/sujato">SN 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha demonstrated extreme equanimity by sitting in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.11/en/sujato"><b>open air</b></a> during a drizzling night of "blinding darkness" while Māra tried to intimidate him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.11/en/sujato">SN 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra once approached the Buddha while he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.12/en/sujato"><b>teaching the Dhamma</b></a> to a large assembly, specifically intending to confuse or blind the eyes of those listening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.12/en/sujato">SN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha affirmed that "great heroes" are those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.12/en/sujato"><b>fully empowered</b></a> and capable of speaking with spiritual authority in any assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.12/en/sujato">SN 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha countered Māra’s false view that life continues indefinitely by stating that human life is always being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.10/en/sujato"><b>cut off</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.10/en/sujato">SN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Māra compared the life of mortals to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.10/en/sujato"><b>rim of a chariot wheel</b></a> that moves without end, the Buddha corrected him by explaining how life is actually fleeting and finite? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn4.10/en/sujato">SN 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Akkosakabhāradvāja initially approached the Buddha while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato"><b>angry and displeased</b></a> because he had heard that another member of his clan had left the household life to become a monk under the Buddha's guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato">SN 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked the Brahmin Akkosakabhāradvāja if he ever offered food to visiting friends and relatives, using this to illustrate that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato"><b>unaccepted insults</b></a> remain with the person who spoke them, just as unaccepted food remains with the host? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato">SN 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who returns abuse for abuse, or anger for anger, is essentially <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato"><b>sharing a meal</b></a> with their attacker, whereas those who refuse to retaliate do not participate in that exchange? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato">SN 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Brahmin Akkosakabhāradvāja once questioned the Buddha, asking if he ever <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato"><b>felt anger</b></a> even though the public and the King recognized him as an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato">SN 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that anger can find no footing in one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato"><b>tamed and lives in balance</b></a>, having been freed by right knowledge and perfect peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato">SN 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato"><b>retaliates with anger</b></a> against someone who is already angry makes the situation worse for themselves than the original person who was angry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato">SN 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that a person who does not get angry back at someone who is angry wins a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato"><b>battle hard to win</b></a>, benefitting both themselves and the other person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato">SN 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that when a person remains mindful and calm in the face of another's anger, they are acting as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato"><b>healer of both</b></a> parties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato">SN 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that only those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato"><b>unskilled in the Dhamma</b></a> mistake a person's patient and calm endurance for foolishness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato">SN 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha's former critic, Akkosakabhāradvāja, eventually attained the supreme goal of the holy life and became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato"><b>one of the Arahants</b></a> after practicing diligently and alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato">SN 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that for someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato"><b>freed by right knowledge</b></a> and remains poised, anger is a state that simply cannot occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.2/en/sujato">SN 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while a fool believes they have won a battle by using <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.3/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a>, the true victory belongs to the one who understands and practices patient endurance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.3/en/sujato">SN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who retaliates with anger against someone who is already angry makes the situation <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.3/en/sujato"><b>worse for themselves</b></a> than for the original person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.3/en/sujato">SN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by staying mindful and calm when another person is angry, an individual acts as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.3/en/sujato"><b>healer of both</b></a> parties, benefiting both themselves and the other? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.3/en/sujato">SN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.3/en/sujato"><b>unskilled in the Dhamma</b></a> often mistake a person's patient endurance and calm behavior for a sign of foolishness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.3/en/sujato">SN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Brahmin Asurindakabhāradvāja insulted him, the Buddha remained silent, later explaining that winning a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.3/en/sujato"><b>battle hard to win</b></a> involves not getting angry in return? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.3/en/sujato">SN 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the Brahmin Ahiṃsakabhāradvāja that to truly live up to the name "Non-harmer," one must be a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.5/en/sujato"><b>does not harm</b></a> others through body, speech, or mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.5/en/sujato">SN 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.5/en/sujato"><b>non-harmer</b></a> as someone who refrains from violence and injury toward any living being across all three doors of action? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.5/en/sujato">SN 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was asked by the Brahmin Jaṭābhāradvāja how to untangle the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato"><b>tangle inside</b></a> and the tangle outside that enmeshes the entire population? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato">SN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person, firmly established in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> and developing both mind and wisdom, is the one who can untangle the web of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato">SN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ideal practitioner as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato"><b>ardent and discerning bhikkhu</b></a> who is capable of unraveling the complex "tangle" of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato">SN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the Arahants, who have destroyed lust, hatred, and ignorance, are the ones for whom the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato"><b>tangle is untangled</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato">SN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the "tangle" of existence is completely cut in the state where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> and the perception of resistance cease without remainder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato">SN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the complex web of life is severed where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato"><b>perception of form</b></a> and all mental and physical phenomena are brought to an absolute end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato">SN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after receiving a teaching from the Buddha on how to resolve the complications of life, Jaṭābhāradvāja requested the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato"><b>going forth</b></a> and eventually attained Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.6/en/sujato">SN 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying on the bank of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>Sundarikā River</b></a> in the Kosalan country when the Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja was performing a fire sacrifice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after finishing his fire sacrifice, the Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja looked around in all directions to see who was worthy to eat the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>remains of the offering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja first approached the Buddha, he saw him sitting at the foot of a tree with his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>head covered</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja initially wanted to turn back because the Buddha was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>shaven-headed</b></a>, but he changed his mind after reflecting that some Brahmins are also shaven-headed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked about his birth or lineage, the Buddha told the Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>Do not ask about birth</b></a>, but ask about conduct," explaining that a sage can be born from a low family just as fire is born from wood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person worthy of receiving offerings as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>tamed by truth</b></a>, endowed with self-mastery, and has completed the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refused to eat the food offered by the Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja because it had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>chanted over with verses</b></a>, stating that Buddhas do not accept food obtained in such a manner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed the Brahmin to instead offer his food to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>consummate one</b></a> or a great seer whose taints are destroyed, as such a person is the proper "field" for those seeking merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that in the entire world, including the realms of devas and Maras, there is no one who can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>properly digest</b></a> the remains of that specific sacrificial offering except a Tathāgata or a disciple of a Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised the Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja to discard the sacrificial remains either where there was little grass or to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>plunge them into water</b></a> that contained no living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the sacrificial remains were thrown into the water, they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>hissed and smoked</b></a> intensely, just like a plowshare that has been heated all day and then plunged into water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja was so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>shocked and terrified</b></a> by the supernatural reaction of the food in the water that his hair stood on end before he approached the Buddha for guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that true purification is not found by burning wood externally, but by kindling an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>internal fire</b></a> within oneself through constant concentrated attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a series of metaphors about the spiritual life, the Buddha compared a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a> to the heavy shoulder-load carried by one who tends a sacrificial fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha metaphorically described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>anger</b></a> as the "smoke" and "falsehood" as the "ashes" of a fire, while the "tongue" serves as the sacrificial ladle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>heart</b></a> as the hearth or fireplace where the light of a well-tamed self is maintained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma as a lake</b></a> and "virtue" as its ford (the safe place to enter and bathe), where wise people wash to cross to the other shore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who remain centered in truth, Dhamma, and self-restraint reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>state of Brahma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja was so impressed by the Buddha's explanation of the internal holy life that he requested to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>receive the going forth</b></a> and higher ordination as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the Buddha's guidance, the former ritual-bound Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja eventually attained the supreme goal and became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato"><b>one of the Arahants</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn7.9/en/sujato">SN 7.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa compared the Buddha’s glorious appearance to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.11/en/sujato"><b>moon in a cloudless sky</b></a>, describing how the Great Sage outshines the entire world in fame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.11/en/sujato">SN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was said to outshine even thousands of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.11/en/sujato"><b>deities</b></a> in both beauty and glory when he stayed on the banks of the Gaggarā Lotus Pond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.11/en/sujato">SN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a compassionate deity once exhorted a bhikkhu dwelling in a forest grove to guard his mind because it was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato"><b>wandering outside</b></a> toward worldly unwholesome thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato">SN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity advised a monk to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato"><b>dispel desire</b></a> for the company of people so that he could be truly happy and free from passion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato">SN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity warned a bhikkhu that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato"><b>dust of passion</b></a> is difficult to cross, and urged him not to let sensual lust carry him away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato">SN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity used the metaphor of a bird shaking off <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato"><b>clinging dust</b></a> to describe how a mindful and energetic bhikkhu should shake off unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato">SN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity once woke up a sleeping monk by asking him what use there is in sleep, comparing a monk's urgency to one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato"><b>pierced by an arrow</b></a> and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato">SN 9.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity urged a bhikkhu to live up to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> with which he originally went forth from the household life into homelessness, rather than succumbing to sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato">SN 9.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a bhikkhu explained to a deity that since sensual desires are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato"><b>impermanent and unstable</b></a>, one who is freed from them should not be troubled by the "heat" of worldly concerns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato">SN 9.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a bhikkhu declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato"><b>removal of lust</b></a> and the overcoming of ignorance result in a knowledge that is supremely pure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato">SN 9.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a bhikkhu explained that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato"><b>cutting off ignorance</b></a> through true knowledge and the destruction of taints, a practitioner reaches a state that is sorrowless and free from despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato">SN 9.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a bhikkhu told a deity that a person who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato"><b>steady energy</b></a> and is resolute in seeking Nibbāna should not be disturbed by the "heat" of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato">SN 9.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa addressed the Buddha as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.11/en/sujato"><b>Aṅgīrasa</b></a>, a name used to emphasize the Buddha's radiance, when praising him in front of a large assembly in Campā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.11/en/sujato">SN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa compared the Buddha to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.11/en/sujato"><b>sun</b></a> (the "beaming one") shining in the sky, free from stains, as a way to illustrate the Buddha's supreme fame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.11/en/sujato">SN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity in a forest grove noticed a bhikkhu was having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome thoughts</b></a> related to the household life and intervened to "stir him up" out of compassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato">SN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity taught that one who enters the forest seeking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> must ensure their mind does not continue to drift toward external worldly objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato">SN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that at the Gaggarā Lotus Pond, the Buddha's audience included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.11/en/sujato"><b>seven hundred laywomen</b></a> and seven hundred laymen, in addition to five hundred monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.11/en/sujato">SN 8.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha (as referenced through the deity's teaching) emphasizes that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato"><b>mindful</b></a> is the key to dispelling unwholesome states that arise while living in a secluded forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.1/en/sujato">SN 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a bhikkhu asserted that one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato"><b>destroyed the taints</b></a> (āsavas) and is freed from sorrow is beyond the reach of the "heat" that affects worldly beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.2/en/sujato">SN 9.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his enlightenment, while he was still a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>Bodhisatta</b></a>, the Buddha reflected on how the world is afflicted by birth, aging, and death, yet does not know the escape from this suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, through careful attention, realized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a> occur only when there is birth, and that they are caused by birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha discovered birth itself is caused by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a>, forming a crucial link in the chain of dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> as the specific condition that leads to existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha traced the origin of clinging back to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a>, which serves as its necessary condition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that craving arises because of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha realized feeling is dependent on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> between the senses and their objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that contact occurs because of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>six sense bases</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha found that the six sense bases are conditioned by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha discovered a reciprocal relationship where name-and-form is conditioned by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a>, and consciousness in turn is conditioned by name-and-form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha realized consciousness <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>turns back</b></a> from name-and-form and does not go further, marking the limit of the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the sequence of dependent origination as the way the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>whole mass of suffering</b></a> comes to arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that regarding things unheard of before, vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>light</b></a> arose in him when he understood the origin of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha discovered the way to end suffering by realizing that with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>cessation of birth</b></a>, aging and death also cease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the cessation of consciousness leads to the cessation of name-and-form, which eventually leads to the cessation of this <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>entire mass of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared his discovery of the path to enlightenment to a man wandering in a forest who finds an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>ancient path</b></a> traveled by people in former times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>ancient city</b></a>, once inhabited and beautiful with parks and groves, to describe the destination one reaches by following the ancient path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>ancient path</b></a> followed by the Rightly Enlightened Ones of the past as the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that by following the ancient path, the Buddha gained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> of aging and death, their origin, their cessation, and the way leading to their cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to have gained direct knowledge of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a>, including their origin and the path to their cessation, by following the ancient road? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his discovery, the Buddha taught the Dhamma so that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> became successful, prosperous, and well-proclaimed among both gods and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.65/en/sujato">SN 12.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught his monks a method of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>internal exploration</b></a> to understand the root causes of suffering while he was staying among the Kurus? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after a certain monk failed to satisfy the Buddha's inquiry about internal exploration, the Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>Ananda</b></a> requested the Buddha to explain the topic himself so the monks could remember it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the first step of internal exploration as investigating the origin of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>aging-and-death</b></a> to see what conditions must exist for them to occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>acquisitions</b></a> (upadhi) as the specific source and cause from which the various types of suffering in the world, such as aging-and-death, arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is practicing for the complete destruction of suffering when he understands aging-and-death, their origin, their cessation, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>way leading to their cessation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>acquisitions</b></a> themselves are not causeless, but are actually conditioned and brought into being by craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> arises and settles wherever there is anything in the world that is agreeable and pleasurable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a> as one of the primary places in the world where craving arises and takes root because it is perceived as agreeable and pleasurable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>ear</b></a>, the nose, the tongue, and the body are all locations in the world where craving can arise and settle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> is a place in the world where craving typically arises and establishes itself whenever it encounters something agreeable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that past ascetics and brahmins who viewed agreeable things as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>permanent</b></a> or as a source of pleasure only succeeded in increasing their craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who view the world's agreeable things as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>self</b></a> or as being "healthy" actually increase their acquisitions and, consequently, their suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a beautiful, fragrant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>beverage mixed with poison</b></a> to describe the agreeable things of the world that lead to death or deadly suffering when consumed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who impulsively pursues the world's pleasures as being like a thirsty man who drinks a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>poisoned drink</b></a> without reflection, leading to his own ruin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who see the agreeable things of the world as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a> and as suffering are able to abandon craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by seeing the world as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>non-self</b></a>, a person can stop the growth of acquisitions and thereby stop the growth of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged viewing the agreeable and pleasurable things of the world as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>disease</b></a> or a danger in order to abandon craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who abandon craving also abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>acquisitions</b></a>, which leads to freedom from birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a wise person to a thirsty man who, upon being warned that a beautiful beverage is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>mixed with poison</b></a>, chooses not to drink it after careful reflection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that a wise person would rather quench their thirst with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>water or buttermilk</b></a> than drink a pleasurable beverage that they know contains deadly poison? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that anyone in the present who views the agreeable things of the world as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> (bhayato) will be freed from sorrow, lamentation, and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the chain of suffering is broken when one understands that without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a>, acquisitions cannot exist, and without acquisitions, aging-and-death cannot occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>internal exploration</b></a> (antaraṃ sammasaṃ) as a practice that involves tracing suffering back to its origin in craving and acquisitions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that those who abandon suffering through right understanding are truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a> from the whole mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that viewing agreeable things as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato"><b>secure</b></a> (khemato) is a misconception that leads to the continued increase of craving and acquisitions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.66/en/sujato">SN 12.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika once approached the Venerable Sāriputta in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>Deer Park at Isipatana</b></a> after emerging from his evening meditation to discuss the nature of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta clarified that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>aging-and-death</b></a> is not self-wrought, nor is it wrought by another, nor by both, nor does it arise by chance without a cause? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Sāriputta, aging-and-death is actually conditioned by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a>, rather than being created by oneself or others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a> itself is not self-wrought or created by chance, but is instead conditioned by existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a> (bhava) is a condition that arises from clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his dialogue with Mahākoṭṭhika, the Venerable Sāriputta identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> as being conditioned by craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> arises due to the condition of feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> is conditioned by contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> is dependent upon the six sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta revealed a reciprocal relationship where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> is conditioned by consciousness, and consciousness is simultaneously conditioned by name-and-form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta used the famous analogy of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>two sheaves of reeds</b></a> (naḷakalāpiyo) to describe how consciousness and name-and-form support each other by leaning against one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta explained that just as one sheaf of reeds would fall if the other were pulled away, the cessation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> leads to the cessation of consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta taught that when consciousness ceases, name-and-form ceases, which eventually leads to the cessation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>whole mass of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika praised Sāriputta’s explanation, stating that a monk is worthy of being called a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>speaker on the Dhamma</b></a>" (dhammakathiko) if he teaches for the purpose of revulsion toward aging-and-death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika, a monk is "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>practicing in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a>" (dhammānudhammappaṭipanno) if he practices for the fading away and cessation of aging-and-death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika defined a monk who is "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>attained to Nibbāna in this very life</b></a>" (diṭṭhadhammanibbānappatto) as one who is liberated through non-clinging due to revulsion toward birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika applied his criteria for a "speaker on the Dhamma" to the teaching of the cessation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> (avijjā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>thirty-six grounds</b></a> or items through which he expressed his approval of the Venerable Sāriputta's comprehensive explanation of dependent origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta emphasized that the chain of suffering—including contact, feeling, and craving—originates from the mutual dependence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato"><b>consciousness and name-and-form</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.67/en/sujato">SN 12.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was so highly honored in Rājagaha that he and his monks received abundant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>offerings</b></a> of robes, almsfood, and medicine, while other wanderers were neglected? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wanderer <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>Susima</b></a> was encouraged by his group to ordain under the Buddha specifically to learn the Dhamma so they could teach it themselves to gain respect and support? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha allowed Susima to receive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>ordination</b></a> after the Venerable Ananda introduced him and conveyed Susima's desire to lead the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a group of monks declared their final knowledge to the Buddha, stating that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a>, the holy life has been lived, and there is no more coming back to any state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Susima questioned the enlightened monks, they revealed that they did not possess various <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>psychic powers</b></a>, such as walking on water or traveling through walls? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the enlightened monks told Susima they did not possess the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>divine ear</b></a> element, which would have allowed them to hear distant or heavenly sounds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks who had reached the end of suffering admitted to Susima that they could not necessarily read the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>minds</b></a> of other beings or persons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite being fully liberated, the monks explained to Susima that they did not remember their various <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>past lives</b></a> or previous existences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the enlightened monks stated they did not have the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> used to see beings passing away and being reborn according to their karma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks told Susima they did not dwell in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>formless liberations</b></a> that transcend material form, even though they were already liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks clarified to a confused Susima that they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>liberated by wisdom</b></a> (paññāvimuttā) rather than through supernormal psychic attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Susima that knowledge of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>stability of the Dhamma</b></a> (dhammaṭṭhitiñāṇa) must arise first, followed only later by knowledge of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Susima that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> (rūpa) is impermanent, and because it is impermanent, it is suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness are all impermanent and should not be regarded as "mine" or "my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a learned noble disciple becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with the five aggregates when they see them as they really are with correct wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> arises through disenchantment, and through dispassion, the mind is liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha guided Susima to see that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>aging-and-death</b></a> is conditioned by birth, and that this understanding is a form of knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Susima on how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a> (bhava) is the necessary condition for birth to occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the link where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> (upādāna) acts as the specific condition that leads to existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha showed Susima that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> is the condition for clinging, and feeling is the condition for craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha traced the chain of suffering all the way back to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> (avijjā), which serves as the condition for volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>cessation of birth</b></a>, aging-and-death are also brought to a stop? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Susima eventually confessed to the Buddha that he had entered the order as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>thief of the Dhamma</b></a> with the wrong intention of stealing the teaching for material gain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who steals the Dhamma to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>criminal</b></a> who is captured and sentenced by a king to a painful and humiliating execution? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>karmic results</b></a> of being a "Dhamma-thief" are even more painful and bitter than the punishment of a captured criminal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha accepted Susima's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>confession</b></a> of his transgression, noting that it is growth in the Noble One's discipline to see one's error and make amends? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a person recognizes their transgression and practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>restraint</b></a> in the future, they can experience true growth in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching to Susima demonstrates that one can realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a> through the understanding of dependent origination without needing any psychic powers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of a criminal being led through the streets with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>harsh-sounding drum</b></a> to illustrate the gravity of misusing the holy life for gain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is conditioned by volitional formations, a realization that constitutes part of the "stability of the Dhamma"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.70/en/sujato">SN 12.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates of clinging</b></a>—specifically form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—as the heavy "burden" that beings carry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato">SN 22.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato"><b>carrier of the burden</b></a> is the person, described as the individual of a certain name and clan who bears the weight of the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato">SN 22.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the "taking up of the burden" as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> that leads to renewed existence, which is accompanied by delight and lust and seeks enjoyment in various states of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato">SN 22.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato"><b>laying down of the burden</b></a> is the remainderless fading away and cessation of craving, which involves its total relinquishment, release, and rejection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato">SN 22.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that while taking up the burden of the five aggregates results in suffering in the world, the actual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato"><b>laying down of the burden</b></a> is the path to true happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato">SN 22.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a person has laid down the heavy burden and pulled out craving by its very root, they become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato"><b>quenched</b></a> (parinibbuto) and are no longer driven by the hunger of desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato">SN 22.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly categorized the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—as the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.23/en/sujato"><b>things to be fully understood</b></a> by a practitioner on the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.23/en/sujato">SN 22.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the attainment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.23/en/sujato"><b>full understanding</b></a> (pariññā) specifically as the complete destruction of lust, the destruction of hatred, and the destruction of delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.23/en/sujato">SN 22.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has discarded the heavy burden and refuses to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato"><b>take up another burden</b></a> has successfully ended the cycle of rebirth and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.22/en/sujato">SN 22.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.23/en/sujato"><b>full understanding</b></a> is not merely intellectual, but is the actual experiential destruction of the underlying defilements of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.23/en/sujato">SN 22.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his full enlightenment, while he was still a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato"><b>Bodhisatta</b></a>, the Buddha sought to understand the gratification, the danger, and the escape regarding the five aggregates of form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato">SN 22.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato"><b>gratification</b></a> of form as the pleasure and joy that arise dependent on it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato">SN 22.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> in form is the fact that it is impermanent, suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato">SN 22.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> from form consists in the removal and abandonment of desire and lust for it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato">SN 22.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato"><b>gratification</b></a> of feeling as whatever pleasure and joy arise dependent on feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato">SN 22.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> from volitional formations is achieved through the removal and abandonment of desire and lust for those formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato">SN 22.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he did not claim to have awakened to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato"><b>supreme enlightenment</b></a> in this world until he had fully understood the gratification, danger, and escape of the five aggregates as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato">SN 22.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that once the Buddha gained direct knowledge of the gratification, danger, and escape of the five aggregates, he realized his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a> was unshakeable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato">SN 22.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared this current life to be his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato"><b>last birth</b></a> and that there would be no further renewed existence after he fully understood the nature of the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato">SN 22.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato"><b>full understanding</b></a> of the five aggregates (form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness) includes seeing their gratification, their danger, and the escape from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.26/en/sujato">SN 22.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings are infatuated with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> only because there is a gratification to be found in it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato">SN 22.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, beings would never become disenchanted with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> if there were no inherent danger associated with it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato">SN 22.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings are able to escape from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> only because an actual escape from it exists? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato">SN 22.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> did not have an aspect of gratification, beings would not be infatuated with them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato">SN 22.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that beings would not become disenchanted with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> if there were no danger involved in it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato">SN 22.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato"><b>escape from the five aggregates</b></a> is possible, and that is why practitioners are able to transcend them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato">SN 22.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that beings remain bound to the world until they understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato"><b>gratification, danger, and escape</b></a> of the five aggregates as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato">SN 22.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who have fully understood the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates of clinging</b></a> in terms of gratification, danger, and escape are released and dwell with a boundless mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato">SN 22.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that it is because of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato"><b>gratification in consciousness</b></a> that beings are drawn into infatuation with the mental experience of awareness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.28/en/sujato">SN 22.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that anyone who finds delight in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>—the physical aspect of existence—is actually finding delight in suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato">SN 22.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who delights in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a>—by taking pleasure in the five aggregates—is not released from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato">SN 22.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that delighting in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> is equivalent to delighting in suffering, which keeps a person bound to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato">SN 22.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that whoever delights in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> is delighting in suffering and will not be freed from the weight of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato">SN 22.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha equated delighting in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a>—the mental activities that construct our reality—with delighting in suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato">SN 22.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who delights in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is not released from suffering because they are delighting in the very thing that is suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato">SN 22.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that by not delighting in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>, one does not delight in suffering and is thus released from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato">SN 22.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the key to being released from suffering is to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato"><b>not delight</b></a> in feeling, perception, and volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato">SN 22.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who does not delight in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> does not delight in suffering and has attained release? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.29/en/sujato">SN 22.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>home of consciousness</b></a> consists of the elements of form, feeling, perception, and volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahākaccāna, a person is considered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>home-wanderer</b></a> when their consciousness is shackled by lust and attachment to the elements of form, feeling, perception, and volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna taught that the Buddha is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>wanderer without a home</b></a> because he has entirely abandoned the desire, lust, delight, and craving for the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna defined an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>abode-wanderer</b></a> as one who is shackled by the "abodes" of signs—specifically the signs of forms, sounds, smells, tastes, tangibles, and mental phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is called one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>wanders without an abode</b></a> because he has abandoned all involvement with the signs of the six senses, making those attachments like a palm stump that can no longer grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna explained that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>intimate with the village</b></a> is a layperson or practitioner who lives in close association with householders, sharing their joys and sorrows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahākaccāna, a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>not intimate with the village</b></a> avoids social entanglement and does not involve himself in the personal business or emotional states of laypeople? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>empty of sense pleasures</b></a> involves being completely free from lust, desire, affection, and craving for worldly enjoyments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna described a person as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>not empty of sense pleasures</b></a> if they still harbor desire, thirst, and fever for the objects of the senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahākaccāna, a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>preoccupied</b></a> (purakkharāno) when they dwell on the future, thinking, "May I have such form or such consciousness in the time to come"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna explained that one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>not preoccupied</b></a> has no expectations or mental projections regarding what their future aggregates—such as feeling or perception—will be? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna characterized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>engaging in disputes</b></a> as participating in verbal conflicts where one claims to know the Dhamma and Vinaya better than others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> as a primary element that serves as a "home" where consciousness dwells when it is bound by attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahākaccāna, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> is one of the four mental and physical elements that provide a "home" for the wandering of consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> acts as a "home" for consciousness, anchoring it through the force of underlying tendencies and lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> serve as a "home" for consciousness, shackling it to the cycle of existence through craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna noted that the Buddha is free from being a "home-wanderer" because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendencies</b></a> of his mind toward the aggregates have been utterly destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>not engaging in disputes</b></a> as a monk's refusal to use competitive speech like "You have fallen into error" or "Your argument is refuted"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna taught that a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>intimate with the village</b></a> if they become "happy when others are happy and sad when others are sad," losing their meditative detachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahākaccāna, to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>released from abodes</b></a> means the shackles to sense objects like tastes and smells have been cut off at the root, unable to arise again in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna explained that the Buddha's instruction to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>give up the home</b></a> specifically refers to abandoning the craving for the four aggregates that consciousness inhabits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.56/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> is the very thing that gives birth to a person and causes them to enter the rounds of saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.56/en/sujato">SN 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, it is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.56/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> of a person that roams about, leading them through existence while they remain unreleased from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.56/en/sujato">SN 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.57/en/sujato"><b>kamma</b></a> as the ultimate destination for every being who has been brought into existence by craving and led by the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.57/en/sujato">SN 1.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.58/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> as the "wrong path" that leads people away from spiritual progress and the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.58/en/sujato">SN 1.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.58/en/sujato"><b>age</b></a> represents the exhaustion of our days and nights, marking the continuous depletion of a person's lifespan? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.58/en/sujato">SN 1.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once remarked that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.58/en/sujato"><b>woman</b></a> is the stain of the holy life, a state of existence to which the common people are often deeply attached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.58/en/sujato">SN 1.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to austerity and the holy life as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.58/en/sujato"><b>waterless bath</b></a> because these practices purify the practitioner internally without the use of water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.58/en/sujato">SN 1.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.59/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> serves as a person's partner, accompanying them as a companion throughout their life's journey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.59/en/sujato">SN 1.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.59/en/sujato"><b>governed by wisdom</b></a>, which provides the necessary guidance and direction for their actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.59/en/sujato">SN 1.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a mortal can be freed from all suffering by being one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.59/en/sujato"><b>delights in Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.59/en/sujato">SN 1.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.61/en/sujato"><b>name</b></a> has overwhelmed everything and that no single thing exists in the world that is greater than it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.61/en/sujato">SN 1.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.61/en/sujato"><b>all things</b></a> have come under the control of "name," a single principle that pervades the entire world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.61/en/sujato">SN 1.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the entire world is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.62/en/sujato"><b>led by the mind</b></a> and is constantly being dragged around by it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.62/en/sujato">SN 1.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.63/en/sujato"><b>everything has come under the control of craving</b></a>, which acts as the force leading and dragging the world along? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.63/en/sujato">SN 1.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the world as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.64/en/sujato"><b>fettered by delight</b></a>, while identifying thought as the means through which the world wanders? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.64/en/sujato">SN 1.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.64/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> is the name given to the state attained through the total abandoning of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.64/en/sujato">SN 1.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.65/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of craving</b></a>, a person is able to cut every bond and tie that binds them to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.65/en/sujato">SN 1.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the world as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.66/en/sujato"><b>struck by death</b></a> and surrounded on all sides by the inevitability of old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.66/en/sujato">SN 1.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the world is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.66/en/sujato"><b>pierced by the dart of craving</b></a> and is constantly "smoldering" with the heat of desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.66/en/sujato">SN 1.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the world is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.67/en/sujato"><b>established in suffering</b></a> and remains shut in by death while being snared by craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.67/en/sujato">SN 1.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the entire world is bound by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.69/en/sujato"><b>desire</b></a>, and it is only through the removal of this desire that every bond and tie can be cut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.69/en/sujato">SN 1.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked what one must kill to live happily, the Buddha replied that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.71/en/sujato"><b>slaying anger</b></a> allows a person to sleep soundly and live without sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.71/en/sujato">SN 1.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described anger as having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.71/en/sujato"><b>honeyed tip</b></a> but a poison root, explaining that the Noble Ones praise its destruction because its removal leads to a state beyond grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.71/en/sujato">SN 1.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.73/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> is a person's best wealth in this world, and that following the Dhamma faithfully is what brings true happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.73/en/sujato">SN 1.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.73/en/sujato"><b>truth</b></a> as the sweetest of all tastes, and stated that a life lived through wisdom is considered the most excellent way to live? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.73/en/sujato">SN 1.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.74/en/sujato"><b>knowledge</b></a> is the best of all things that rise or increase, while ignorance is the thing most worthy of being allowed to fall away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.74/en/sujato">SN 1.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.74/en/sujato"><b>Sangha</b></a> is the most excellent of those who go forth on the spiritual journey, while the Buddha himself is the best of those who speak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.74/en/sujato">SN 1.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who practices right speech and mind, avoids evil deeds, and remains a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.75/en/sujato"><b>generous giver</b></a> in their own home need not fear the next world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.75/en/sujato">SN 1.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.75/en/sujato"><b>firmly grounded</b></a> in the four qualities of faith, gentleness, generosity, and benevolence removes the fear of what lies beyond death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.75/en/sujato">SN 1.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that while the physical <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.76/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> of mortals is subject to decay, their name and clan (their identity and reputation) do not decay? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.76/en/sujato">SN 1.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the phrase <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.76/en/sujato"><b>waterless bath</b></a> to describe austerity and the holy life, because these practices purify the practitioner internally without the need for water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.76/en/sujato">SN 1.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.76/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> as the "wrong path" (uppatho) and labeled woman as the "stain" or impurity that can hinder the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.76/en/sujato">SN 1.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.76/en/sujato"><b>age</b></a> represents the constant exhaustion of our days and nights, marking the steady depletion of a person's lifespan? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.76/en/sujato">SN 1.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.76/en/sujato"><b>laziness and negligence</b></a> as two of the six "holes" in the world where wealth and merit fail to stay, leading to a person's spiritual and material decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.76/en/sujato">SN 1.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.76/en/sujato"><b>lack of restraint</b></a>, sleepiness, and lethargy are fissures that must be completely avoided if one wishes to preserve their spiritual gains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.76/en/sujato">SN 1.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.77/en/sujato"><b>authority</b></a> (or self-mastery) is the greatest lordship in the world, while anger is the "rust" that ruins one's internal strength? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.77/en/sujato">SN 1.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that while people stop a thief from taking their belongings, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.77/en/sujato"><b>recluse</b></a> who "takes" alms is loved, and wise people welcome their repeated return? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.77/en/sujato">SN 1.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.77/en/sujato"><b>thieves</b></a> as the affliction or "tumor" of the world, contrasting them with the wise practitioner who brings benefit to society? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.77/en/sujato">SN 1.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a person should never <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.78/en/sujato"><b>give away themselves</b></a> or surrender their own integrity, highlighting the importance of self-protection in spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.78/en/sujato">SN 1.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should always <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.78/en/sujato"><b>release beautiful speech</b></a> that is beneficial to others, while being careful never to utter speech that is evil or harmful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.78/en/sujato">SN 1.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.7/en/sujato"><b>well-penetrated the Dhamma</b></a> are not led away by the conflicting views of others, but instead walk evenly amidst an uneven world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.7/en/sujato">SN 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared those who do not understand the Dhamma to people who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.7/en/sujato"><b>asleep</b></a>, noting that it is always the right time for them to wake up to the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.7/en/sujato">SN 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.8/en/sujato"><b>unconfused</b></a> for a person, they are considered "fully awake" and are no longer susceptible to being misled by the teachings of other sects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.8/en/sujato">SN 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity once told the Buddha that there is no possibility of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.9/en/sujato"><b>taming</b></a> or sagehood for a person who still harbors a desire for conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.9/en/sujato">SN 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.9/en/sujato"><b>abandoned conceit</b></a> and remains diligent while living alone in the forest can successfully cross to the far shore of death’s realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.9/en/sujato">SN 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that forest-dwelling practitioners who live the holy life and eat only one meal a day have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.10/en/sujato"><b>serene complexions</b></a> because they do not sorrow over the past or yearn for the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.10/en/sujato">SN 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that "fools" wither away like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.10/en/sujato"><b>green reed</b></a> that has been cut down because they waste their lives grieving over things that have passed and longing for things that have not yet come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.10/en/sujato">SN 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.10/en/sujato"><b>living in the present</b></a> as the specific practice that allows spiritual seekers to maintain a clear and radiant appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.10/en/sujato">SN 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while a deity once claimed that true happiness is only known by those who see the celestial Nandana Grove, the Buddha taught that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.11/en/sujato"><b>formations are impermanent</b></a> and their stilling is the only true happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.11/en/sujato">SN 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha corrected a deity's view of celestial pleasure by stating that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.11/en/sujato"><b>peace</b></a> is found only when one understands the nature of arising and passing away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.11/en/sujato">SN 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity once suggested that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.11/en/sujato"><b>Nandana Grove</b></a> was the ultimate abode of the famous Thirty-three gods, but another deity responded with the Buddha’s teaching on the cessation of all constructed things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.11/en/sujato">SN 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person with sons or cattle actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.12/en/sujato"><b>sorrows</b></a> because of them, as acquisitions are the primary source of human grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.12/en/sujato">SN 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the only person who truly does not sorrow is the one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.12/en/sujato"><b>no acquisitions</b></a> to cling to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.12/en/sujato">SN 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a deity claimed that a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.12/en/sujato"><b>rejoices</b></a> because of their children and possessions, the Buddha countered by saying those same things are actually the cause of their suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.12/en/sujato">SN 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared there is no love in this world that can ever be equal to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.13/en/sujato"><b>love for one’s self</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.13/en/sujato">SN 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while people value cattle as wealth, there is actually no wealth equal to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.13/en/sujato"><b>grain</b></a> for sustaining life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.13/en/sujato">SN 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that no light in the universe—not even the sun—is as great as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.13/en/sujato"><b>light of wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.13/en/sujato">SN 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.13/en/sujato"><b>rain</b></a> as the paramount body of water, even surpassing the vastness of the ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.13/en/sujato">SN 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha challenged the popular idea that love for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.13/en/sujato"><b>son</b></a> is the supreme form of love, pointing instead to the inherent love for one's own self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.13/en/sujato">SN 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.13/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> provides a radiance that is unmatched by any physical light source in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.13/en/sujato">SN 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.79/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> is what binds a person's provisions for their spiritual journey, while splendor (sirī) serves as the abode of their wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.79/en/sujato">SN 1.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.79/en/sujato"><b>desire</b></a> (icchā) as the force that drags a person around and is the single hardest thing in the world to give up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.79/en/sujato">SN 1.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the many beings who are bound by desire to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.79/en/sujato"><b>bird in a snare</b></a>, unable to find freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.79/en/sujato">SN 1.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.80/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> (paññā) is the primary light in the world, whereas mindfulness (sati) is what keeps a person truly wakeful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.80/en/sujato">SN 1.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.80/en/sujato"><b>cattle</b></a> (gāvo) as the essential work or support for the lives of living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.80/en/sujato">SN 1.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.80/en/sujato"><b>rain</b></a> (vuṭṭhi) nurtures both the lazy and the energetic just like a mother nurtures her child? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.80/en/sujato">SN 1.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.81/en/sujato"><b>ascetics</b></a> (samaṇā) are the "faultless" ones in the world because they have fully understood desire and achieved perpetual independence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.81/en/sujato">SN 1.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.81/en/sujato"><b>nobles</b></a> (khattiyas) will bow down to an ascetic who is well-established in the Dhamma, regardless of whether that ascetic was born of low status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.81/en/sujato">SN 1.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.81/en/sujato"><b>ascetics</b></a>, the holy life they have lived is never lost or destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn1.81/en/sujato">SN 1.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha approved of the teaching by the deva Kassapa that one should train in well-spoken speech, attending to ascetics, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.1/en/sujato"><b>stilling of the mind</b></a> (cittavūpasama)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.1/en/sujato">SN 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Kassapa advised that practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.1/en/sujato"><b>secluded sitting</b></a> is a vital part of the training for those seeking spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.1/en/sujato">SN 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Kassapa stated that a monk should be a meditator with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.2/en/sujato"><b>liberated mind</b></a> (vimuttacitto) if they wish to attain the heart's goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.2/en/sujato">SN 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Kassapa taught that a practitioner should be one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.2/en/sujato"><b>not dependent</b></a> (anissito) on anything, having known the arising and passing away of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.2/en/sujato">SN 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.4/en/sujato"><b>four lights</b></a> that illuminate the world: the sun, the moon, fire, and the Fully Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.4/en/sujato">SN 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that among all things that shine, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.4/en/sujato"><b>Fully Enlightened One</b></a> (Sambuddho) is the most excellent, providing an unsurpassed radiance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.4/en/sujato">SN 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the deva Māgadha that while the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.4/en/sujato"><b>sun</b></a> shines by day and the moon by night, the Buddha's radiance is the greatest in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.4/en/sujato">SN 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One attempted to instill <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato"><b>fear and horripilation</b></a> in the nun Āḷavikā to drive her away from her seclusion in the Blind Men’s Grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato">SN 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One asserted to the nun Āḷavikā that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato"><b>no escape</b></a> in the world and urged her to enjoy sensual delights? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato">SN 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Āḷavikā countered Mara’s claims by stating that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato"><b>escape from the world</b></a> does exist and that she had fully realized it through her own wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato">SN 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Āḷavikā described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> as being like swords and stakes, and the five aggregates as a chopping block? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato">SN 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Āḷavikā rebuked Mara by calling him the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato"><b>Kinsman of the Negligent</b></a> after he tried to tempt her with worldly delights? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato">SN 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Āḷavikā declared that the "delight in sensual pleasure" praised by Mara had actually become a source of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato"><b>disgust</b></a> for her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato">SN 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One vanished <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato"><b>sorrowful and dejected</b></a> once he realized the nun Āḷavikā had recognized his true identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.1/en/sujato">SN 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Somā entered the Blind Men’s Grove at Sāvatthī and sat at the foot of a tree for her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato"><b>day’s abiding</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato">SN 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One claimed that the state attained by sages is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato"><b>difficult to achieve</b></a> and beyond the capacity of a woman? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato">SN 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One tried to disparage the nun Somā by suggesting that women possess only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato"><b>two-fingered wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato">SN 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Somā argued that womanhood is irrelevant to spiritual attainment when the mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato"><b>well-concentrated</b></a> and knowledge is present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato">SN 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Somā stated that gender does not matter for one who is correctly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato"><b>seeing the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato">SN 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Somā told Mara that he is only worthy of addressing people who still identify themselves as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato"><b>a woman or a man</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato">SN 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One’s primary goal in approaching the nun Somā was to make her fall away from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato">SN 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Somā recognized that the one trying to cause her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato"><b>fear and terror</b></a> was not a human but Mara the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.2/en/sujato">SN 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Kisāgotamī sought seclusion in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato"><b>Blind Men’s Grove</b></a> at Sāvatthī to spend her day in meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato">SN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One approached the nun Kisāgotamī with the intention of instilling fear and trepidation to make her fall away from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato">SN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One questioned the nun Kisāgotamī as to whether she was weeping in the woods because she had a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato"><b>dead son</b></a> or was out searching for a man? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato">SN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Kisāgotamī responded to Mara by stating that she is finished with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato"><b>dead sons</b></a> and that men are a thing of the past for her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato">SN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Kisāgotamī declared she does not fear Mara because her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato"><b>delight</b></a> has been destroyed everywhere and the mass of darkness has been shattered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato">SN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Kisāgotamī proclaimed that she lives <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato"><b>taintless</b></a> after having successfully conquered the army of Death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato">SN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One immediately <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato"><b>vanished</b></a> in a state of sorrow and dejection once he realized that the nun Kisāgotamī had recognized him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.3/en/sujato">SN 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Vijayā, after returning from her alms round in Sāvatthī, went to sit at the foot of a tree for her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato"><b>day’s abiding</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato">SN 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One tried to seduce the nun Vijayā by praising her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato"><b>youth and beauty</b></a> while identifying himself as a youth as well? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato">SN 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One invited the nun Vijayā to delight in the forest with him accompanied by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato"><b>fivefold music</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato">SN 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Vijayā told Mara that she hands over all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato"><b>delightful forms</b></a>, sounds, tastes, odors, and tangibles back to him because she has no use for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato">SN 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Vijayā expressed that she felt repelled and humiliated by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato"><b>foul body</b></a>, which she described as fragile and subject to breaking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato">SN 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Vijayā informed Mara that her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato"><b>craving for sensual pleasures</b></a> had been completely struck out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato">SN 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Vijayā declared that for her, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato"><b>darkness</b></a> has been destroyed in all cases, including for those in form and formless realms and those in peaceful attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato">SN 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Vijayā caused Mara the Evil One to disappear by recognizing his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato"><b>true identity</b></a> as he tried to disturb her seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.4/en/sujato">SN 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Cālā explained that she does not approve of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.6/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a> because for one who is born there is death, suffering, and various forms of torment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.6/en/sujato">SN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Cālā credited the Buddha for teaching her the Dhamma for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.6/en/sujato"><b>surpassing of birth</b></a> and the abandoning of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.6/en/sujato">SN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Cālā warned that beings who have reached the form or formless realms but do not understand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.6/en/sujato"><b>cessation</b></a> are destined to return to the renewal of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.6/en/sujato">SN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One tried to entice the nun Upacālā to set her heart on rebirth in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.7/en/sujato"><b>heavens</b></a>, such as those of the Tāvatiṃsa or Tusita gods, to experience delight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.7/en/sujato">SN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Upacālā rejected rebirth in the celestial realms because those gods remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.7/en/sujato"><b>bound by sensuality</b></a> and continue to fall under the control of Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.7/en/sujato">SN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Upacālā described the spiritual state she attained as being where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.7/en/sujato"><b>Mara has no footing</b></a>, a place that is unshakable and unblazing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.7/en/sujato">SN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Upacālā famously declared to Mara that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.7/en/sujato"><b>whole world is ablaze</b></a>, smoking, and quaking with the fires of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.7/en/sujato">SN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One vanished sorrowfully once he realized the nun Upacālā recognized his identity after he failed to tempt her with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.7/en/sujato"><b>celestial rebirth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.7/en/sujato">SN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Sīsupacālā stated she does not approve of any religious systems outside the Buddha’s teaching because their followers are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.8/en/sujato"><b>unskilled in the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.8/en/sujato">SN 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Sīsupacālā identified herself as a follower of the Buddha, whom she described as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.8/en/sujato"><b>unrivalled person</b></a> born in the Sakyan clan? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.8/en/sujato">SN 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Sīsupacālā praised the Buddha as the one who is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.8/en/sujato"><b>conqueror of all</b></a>, who has pushed back Mara and is everywhere undefeated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.8/en/sujato">SN 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Sīsupacālā declared that the Buddha has reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.8/en/sujato"><b>destruction of all kamma</b></a> and is liberated through the destruction of acquisitions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.8/en/sujato">SN 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara the Evil One questioned the nun Sīsupacālā about why she had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.8/en/sujato"><b>shaved her head</b></a> and looked like a nun if she did not approve of any sectarian system? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.8/en/sujato">SN 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Sīsupacālā told Mara that she approves of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.8/en/sujato"><b>teaching of the Blessed One</b></a>, who sees all and is free from everything? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.8/en/sujato">SN 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Cālā sat at the foot of a tree for her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.6/en/sujato"><b>day’s abiding</b></a> after finishing her alms round in Sāvatthī, only to be confronted by Mara the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.6/en/sujato">SN 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nun Upacālā characterized the world as being in a state where it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.7/en/sujato"><b>quaking</b></a>, contrasting this with the peace she found in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn5.7/en/sujato">SN 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned the bhikkhu Kokālika not to harbor ill will toward Sāriputta and Moggallāna, describing the two chief disciples as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for every person born, an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>axe is born in the mouth</b></a> with which the fool cuts themselves when they speak badly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the lifespan in the Paduma hell using an analogy involving a cartload of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>sesame seeds</b></a>, suggesting it is so long it cannot be easily counted in years or centuries? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the bhikkhu Kokālika was reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>Paduma hell</b></a> specifically because he harbored resentment in his heart toward Sāriputta and Moggallāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>Brahmā Sahampati</b></a> visited him at Jeta's Grove in the middle of the night to report that Kokālika had passed away and fallen into hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a person removed one single sesame seed every hundred years from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>cartload of twenty Kosalan bushels</b></a>, the seeds would be exhausted before a single Abbuda hell period ended? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that harboring ill will toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>Well-Gone Ones</b></a> is a far greater "losing throw" at dice than losing all of one's wealth along with one's own life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how the bhikkhu Kokālika's body became covered in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>boils</b></a> that grew from the size of mustard seeds to the size of bael fruits before bursting with pus and blood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that anyone who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>praises the blameworthy</b></a> or blames the praiseworthy stores up bad luck with their mouth and will not find happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that twenty Abbuda hells are equal to one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>Nirabbuda hell</b></a>, and this factor of twenty continues through a series of progressively longer-lasting hells? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the final and longest-lasting hell in the exponential series mentioned to the monks as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>Paduma hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>Brahmā Sahampati</b></a> possessed an exquisite beauty that illuminated the entirety of Jeta's Grove when he came to deliver news about the death of Kokālika? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the duration of suffering for one who reviles the noble ones as lasting for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>thirty-six Abbudas</b></a> plus five, along with hundreds of thousands of Nirabbudas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha twice urged the bhikkhu Kokālika to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>place confidence</b></a> in Sāriputta and Moggallāna after the monk accused them of being under the control of evil wishes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the physical decline of the bhikkhu Kokālika, stating he died from an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>affliction</b></a> that caused his skin to break out in large, oozing pustules as a result of his resentment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that directing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>evil words</b></a> and a wicked mind against the virtuous leads a person directly to a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned the names of various hells like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>Ababa and Ahaha</b></a> to illustrate the vast, exponential increase in the lifespan of beings suffering in those realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the bhikkhu Kokālika persisted in his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>slander</b></a> against Sāriputta and Moggallāna even after being corrected and warned three times by the Blessed One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the length of one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>Abbuda hell</b></a> to the time it would take for a person to empty a massive container of sesame seeds by picking out just one seed every hundred years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who directs their mind to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato"><b>hostility</b></a> toward the Sugatas (the Buddhas) commits a spiritual error far more significant than losing all of one's wealth at dice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.10/en/sujato">SN 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa, while staying at the Aggāḷava Shrine with his preceptor, once felt <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato"><b>discontent and lust</b></a> and realized that he alone was responsible for dispelling these unwholesome states of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato">SN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa declared that a monk who has completely abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato"><b>household-based thoughts</b></a> and has no desire for the "jungle" of cravings is one who lives without the extremes of dislike or delight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato">SN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa observed that everything on earth and in the sky is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a> and subject to decay, and that those with a liberated mind live with this constant understanding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato">SN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato"><b>sage</b></a> is one who remains unstained by desire regarding anything that is seen, heard, sensed, or cognized, and who has successfully dispelled all longing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato">SN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa described a monk who is focused and wise as one who has attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato"><b>peaceful state</b></a> and remains calmly awaitful of his final passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato">SN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa once looked down on other virtuous monks because of his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.3/en/sujato"><b>ready wit</b></a> and poetic inspiration, but he eventually realized this arrogance was a loss for him and felt deep remorse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.3/en/sujato">SN 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa exhorted himself to abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.3/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a> and the path that leads to it, warning that those obsessed with pride would suffer long-term remorse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.3/en/sujato">SN 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa warned that people who are smirched by hypocrisy and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.3/en/sujato"><b>struck by conceit</b></a> are destined to fall into hell and experience long-lasting sorrow there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.3/en/sujato">SN 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa taught that a monk who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.3/en/sujato"><b>victor on the path</b></a> and practices correctly never sorrows, but instead enjoys both a good reputation and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.3/en/sujato">SN 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa identified an energetic and pure-hearted person as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.3/en/sujato"><b>seer of the Dhamma</b></a> if they have successfully abandoned all of the mental hindrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.3/en/sujato">SN 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa proclaimed that by completely abandoning pride and the five hindrances, one becomes an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.3/en/sujato"><b>ender of suffering</b></a> through the power of true knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.3/en/sujato">SN 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa taught that people are often <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato"><b>stuck in acquisitions</b></a>, but a muni should dispel desire for all worldly things they encounter through their senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato">SN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato"><b>common people</b></a> as being entangled in many unrighteous thoughts, unlike the monk who remains focused and sincere in his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.2/en/sujato">SN 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda taught that a person's mind burns with lust primarily due to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.4/en/sujato"><b>distortion of perception</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.4/en/sujato">SN 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda advised those struggling with passion to intentionally avoid the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.4/en/sujato"><b>sign of the beautiful</b></a> that is associated with lustful desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.4/en/sujato">SN 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda instructed that one should view all conditioned formations as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.4/en/sujato"><b>alien</b></a> and as suffering, rather than identifying with them as a self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.4/en/sujato">SN 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda suggested that to extinguish a great fire of lust, one should focus the mind on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.4/en/sujato"><b>unattractive</b></a> nature of things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.4/en/sujato">SN 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda emphasized that a practitioner should remain frequently full of revulsion toward worldly desires while maintaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.4/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.4/en/sujato">SN 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda taught that by developing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.4/en/sujato"><b>signless</b></a> state of mind and discarding the underlying tendency to conceit, one can live in peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.4/en/sujato">SN 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken speech</b></a> as having four essential factors: it is well-spoken not ill-spoken, righteous not unrighteous, pleasant not unpleasant, and true not false? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato">SN 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that speech which is true, pleasant, righteous, and beneficial is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato"><b>faultless</b></a> and beyond reproach by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato">SN 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa stated that speech is only truly well-spoken when it does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato"><b>trouble oneself</b></a> or cause harm to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato">SN 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa observed that a person should speak only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato"><b>pleasant words</b></a> that are welcomed by others, avoiding the mention of others' faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato">SN 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato"><b>truth</b></a> is the "immortal word" and that the virtuous are those firmly established in truth, purpose, and the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato">SN 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa praised the speech of the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato"><b>best of words</b></a> because it leads directly to security and the attainment of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato">SN 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato"><b>truthful speech</b></a> is the fourth factor of well-spoken language, emphasizing that one must speak only what is true and never what is false? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato">SN 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa believed the specific words spoken by the Buddha were uniquely designed for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato"><b>ending of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.5/en/sujato">SN 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, during a special gathering at the palace of Migāra’s mother, invited five hundred monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>reproach him</b></a> for any bodily or verbal misconduct they might have observed during the invitation ceremony (<i>pavāraṇā</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta proclaimed that the Buddha is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>originator of the path</b></a> that was previously unarisen, the producer of the path that was unproduced, and the one who is uniquely skilled in and knows the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the Venerable Sāriputta for his extraordinary intelligence, describing him as being possessed of great, wide, joyous, swift, sharp, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>penetrative wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the Venerable Sāriputta to the eldest son of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>wheel-turning monarch</b></a> because Sāriputta correctly keeps the supreme Wheel of Dhamma rolling just as the Buddha first set it in motion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that an entire assembly of five hundred monks were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>arahants</b></a> who had successfully destroyed their taints, lived the holy life, and liberated their minds through final knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified sixty monks within a specific assembly of five hundred arahants as being possessors of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>three true knowledges</b></a> (<i>tevijjā</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that sixty monks in a holy gathering were possessors of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> (<i>chaḷabhiññā</i>), which are high spiritual attainments beyond normal human perception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished sixty monks in a pure assembly as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>liberated both ways</b></a> (<i>ubhatobhāgavimutta</i>), meaning they achieved liberation through both the formless attainments and through wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa described the five hundred monks gathered for the invitation ceremony as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>sages</b></a> who had cut the bonds of fetters, were free from suffering, and had completely exhausted the possibility of future rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa compared the Buddha to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed caravaneer</b></a> who has won the battle and is now attended by disciples who have overcome death and possess the three true knowledges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa referred to an assembly of five hundred arahants as all being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>sons of the Blessed One</b></a>, declaring that there was not a single "chaff" or defective person among them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa paid homage to the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>slayer of the dart of craving</b></a> and the "Kinsman of the Sun" who successfully removed the suffering of his disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha held a special <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>invitation ceremony</b></a> on the fifteenth-day Uposatha, a day of purification, where he invited his disciples to critique his conduct to ensure the purity of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa likened the Buddha to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato"><b>wheel-turning king</b></a> who travels around the earth bounded by the ocean, just as the Buddha is surrounded by his enlightened disciples who serve and honor him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.7/en/sujato">SN 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa once praised the Buddha with spontaneous verses that were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato"><b>not thought out before</b></a>, prompting the Buddha to invite him to speak even more inspired verses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato">SN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa described the Buddha as being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato"><b>great cloud</b></a> that rains down upon his disciples as he teaches them the stainless and fearless Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato">SN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa characterized the Buddha as the "seventh of the sages" who has successfully overcome the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato"><b>devious path of Mara</b></a> and broken through all mental barrenness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato">SN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa identified the Buddha as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato"><b>liberator from bonds</b></a> who remains unattached while clearly distinguishing and analyzing the various components of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato">SN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa proclaimed that the Buddha has pointed out a manifold path specifically for the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato"><b>crossing the flood</b></a> of worldly suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato">SN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa taught that when the Deathless is proclaimed by the Buddha, those who truly see the Dhamma stand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato"><b>unshakeable</b></a> in their realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato">SN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa referred to the Buddha as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato"><b>light-maker</b></a> who has seen the passing of all mental standpoints and has realized the foremost state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato">SN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa warned that once the Dhamma has been so well taught by the Buddha, there should be no room for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato"><b>negligence</b></a> among those who understand it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato">SN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa stated that more than a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato"><b>thousand monks</b></a> were present to wait upon the Buddha, listening to the stainless Dhamma that leads to the state of "no-fear-from-any-quarter"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato">SN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa emphasized that a practitioner should always be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato"><b>ever-diligent</b></a> and training respectfully within the teaching of the Blessed One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.8/en/sujato">SN 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who does not know or see <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.82/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a> as it really is should seek a teacher specifically to gain knowledge of it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.82/en/sujato">SN 12.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a teacher should be sought to gain knowledge of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.82/en/sujato"><b>origin of aging and death</b></a> if one does not already see it clearly according to reality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.82/en/sujato">SN 12.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized seeking a teacher for the sake of obtaining knowledge of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.82/en/sujato"><b>cessation of aging and death</b></a> for those who have not yet realized it as it truly is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.82/en/sujato">SN 12.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a teacher must be sought to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.82/en/sujato"><b>way leading to the cessation</b></a> of aging and death if that path is not yet known or seen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn12.82/en/sujato">SN 12.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once used a tiny amount of soil on the tip of his fingernail to show that it is insignificantly small compared to the vastness of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn13.1/en/sujato"><b>great earth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn13.1/en/sujato">SN 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of fingernail soil versus the earth to explain that for a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn13.1/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>, the mass of suffering already destroyed is far greater than the little that remains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn13.1/en/sujato">SN 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a noble disciple who has attained the fruit of stream-entry, the amount of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn13.1/en/sujato"><b>suffering remaining</b></a> is so small it does not even equal a thousandth part of the suffering that has been eliminated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn13.1/en/sujato">SN 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the state of a stream-enterer as being one where a person has at most <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn13.1/en/sujato"><b>seven more births</b></a> remaining before reaching the final end of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn13.1/en/sujato">SN 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted the immense importance of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn13.1/en/sujato"><b>breakthrough to the Dhamma</b></a> by comparing the reduction in suffering it brings to the size of the entire world compared to a speck of dust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn13.1/en/sujato">SN 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn13.1/en/sujato"><b>obtaining of the eye of Dhamma</b></a> is of great benefit because it means the massive mountain of one's past suffering has been almost entirely exhausted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn13.1/en/sujato">SN 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings come together and associate by way of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.14/en/sujato"><b>elements</b></a>, meaning those with similar natures naturally gravitate toward one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.14/en/sujato">SN 14.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.14/en/sujato"><b>inferior inclinations</b></a> will always congregate and associate with others who also possess inferior inclinations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.14/en/sujato">SN 14.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that beings with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.14/en/sujato"><b>good inclinations</b></a> consistently come together and associate with others who share those same virtuous inclinations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.14/en/sujato">SN 14.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.14/en/sujato"><b>past</b></a>, beings always associated with one another based on their shared elements and dispositions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.14/en/sujato">SN 14.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.14/en/sujato"><b>future</b></a>, beings will continue to come together and associate according to their specific internal elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.14/en/sujato">SN 14.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.14/en/sujato"><b>present</b></a>, just as in all other times, beings associate with those who match their own elements and inclinations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.14/en/sujato">SN 14.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>Vulture’s Peak Mountain</b></a> in Rājagaha when he observed various senior monks walking back and forth with their respective groups? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the monks walking with the Venerable Sāriputta as all being monks of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>great wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the monks accompanying the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna were all monks of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>great power</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the group of monks walking with the Venerable Mahākassapa as all being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>proponents of ascetic practices</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the monks walking alongside the Venerable Anuruddha were all possessors of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the monks following the Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāniputta were all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>speakers on the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the monks walking with the Venerable Upāli were all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>experts in the Vinaya</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that the monks accompanying the Venerable Ānanda were all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a> ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the monks walking with Devadatta were all monks of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>evil wishes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings come together and associate by way of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>elements</b></a>, which explains why similar types of people naturally congregate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>inferior inclinations</b></a> naturally come together and associate with others who also have inferior inclinations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>good inclinations</b></a> will naturally come together and associate with others who share those same virtuous inclinations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that throughout the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>past</b></a>, beings have always associated and come together according to their shared elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>future</b></a>, beings will continue to come together and associate based on their specific internal elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that even in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>present</b></a>, beings associate and come together primarily by way of their shared elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the physical observation of senior monks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>walking back and forth</b></a> with their followers to demonstrate the psychological principle of attraction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the noble groups of monks led by Sāriputta and Moggallāna to the group led by Devadatta to show how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato"><b>dispositions</b></a> determine one's social circle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.15/en/sujato">SN 14.15</a></p></div><hr>
