<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings naturally come together and associate by way of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato"><b>elements</b></a>, meaning that those of similar character and disposition gravitate toward one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato">SN 14.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato"><b>inferior inclinations</b></a> will always associate and match with others who also possess inferior inclinations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato">SN 14.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the graphic simile of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato"><b>dung and urine</b></a> associating with their own kind to illustrate how people of low character naturally congregate together? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato">SN 14.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, just as spit, pus, and blood associate with their own kind, beings of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato"><b>low character</b></a> will inevitably find and stay with others like themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato">SN 14.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato"><b>good inclinations</b></a> consistently come together and associate with others who share those same virtuous inclinations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato">SN 14.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the association of virtuous people to the way that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato"><b>milk and oil</b></a> naturally blend and associate with their own kind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato">SN 14.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphors of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato"><b>ghee and honey</b></a> associating with their own kind to demonstrate how noble-minded beings naturally match and agree with one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato">SN 14.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the principle of beings associating by elements is a constant law that held true in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato"><b>past, future, and present</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato">SN 14.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the "jungle" of defilements is born from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato"><b>association</b></a> with others, whereas it is through non-association that this jungle is cut down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato">SN 14.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a person trying to cross the ocean on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato"><b>tiny piece of wood</b></a> to describe how a person of good life can still sink by relying on the wrong company? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato">SN 14.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even an upright person will sink in their spiritual journey if they associate with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato"><b>lazy person</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato">SN 14.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha strongly advised that one should avoid those of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato"><b>inferior energy</b></a> to avoid being dragged down by their lack of spiritual commitment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato">SN 14.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended dwelling with those who are noble, secluded, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato"><b>consistently energetic</b></a> in order to succeed in the wise and meditative life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.16/en/sujato">SN 14.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings naturally come together and associate by way of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.17/en/sujato"><b>elements</b></a>, which means that those with similar character traits and spiritual dispositions gravitate toward one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.17/en/sujato">SN 14.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that individuals who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.17/en/sujato"><b>faithless</b></a> will consistently associate and match with others who also lack conviction in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.17/en/sujato">SN 14.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.17/en/sujato"><b>shameless</b></a> regarding their conduct will naturally come together and associate with others who similarly lack a sense of moral shame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.17/en/sujato">SN 14.17</a></p></div><hr>
