<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed several monks who were preparing to travel to the western districts to first go and see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> before departing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highly praised Sāriputta, describing him as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>wise</b></a> and a helpful supporter of his fellow monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta warned monks that wise people from various social classes—such as khattiyas, brahmins, householders, and ascetics—often ask monks to explain their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>Teacher's doctrine</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta emphasized the importance of monks having a deep and clear understanding of the Dhamma so they can answer questions accurately without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>misrepresenting the Buddha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that when asked about the Buddha's core message, monks should explain that the Teacher is one who proclaims the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>removal of desire and lust</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta specified that the Buddha teaches the removal of desire and lust regarding the physical aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained that the Teacher's message includes the removal of desire and lust for the aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> as one of the key aggregates for which the Buddha teaches the removal of desire and lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that the Buddha’s instruction focuses on removing desire and lust for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta clarified that the Buddha’s path involves the removal of desire and lust regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained the danger of lust by stating that if one is not free from desire for form, the inevitable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>change and alteration</b></a> of that form leads to sorrow and lamentation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta warned that for someone who has not removed lust for feeling, its change results in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>pain and despair</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that harboring thirst and fever for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> leads directly to mental suffering when that perception undergoes change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta noted that a person who has not discarded desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> will experience sorrow and displeasure when those formations change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained that the danger in having desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is that its change and alteration inevitably produce despair for the unreleased mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that when one is free from desire and lust for form, no sorrow or pain arises even when that form undergoes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>alteration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained that for those who have removed lust for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>, the change of that feeling does not lead to sorrow or lamentation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta highlighted that the benefit of being free from lust for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> is the absence of mental suffering when those formations change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta declared that one who has discarded desire and lust for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> will not experience sorrow or despair when consciousness alters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explained the Buddha encourages the abandonment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome states</b></a> because they lead to suffering, trouble, and despair in the present life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta stated the Buddha recommends giving up unwholesome qualities because they lead to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>bad destination</b></a> after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that the Buddha commends the undertaking of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a> because they lead to happiness in this life and a good destination after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.2/en/sujato">SN 22.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the arising, continuance, and manifestation of the physical aggregate of form is actually the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.30/en/sujato"><b>arising of suffering</b></a> and the manifestation of disease, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.30/en/sujato">SN 22.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the cessation, stilling, and passing away of consciousness is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.30/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> and the passing away of aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.30/en/sujato">SN 22.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the arising of the aggregates of feeling, perception, and volitional formations is synonymous with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.30/en/sujato"><b>continuance of disease</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.30/en/sujato">SN 22.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.31/en/sujato"><b>misery</b></a> as consisting of the five aggregates: form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.31/en/sujato">SN 22.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.31/en/sujato"><b>root of misery</b></a> as the craving that leads to renewed existence, which is accompanied by delight and lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.31/en/sujato">SN 22.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that craving—the root of misery—takes three forms: craving for sensual pleasures, craving for existence, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.31/en/sujato"><b>craving for non-existence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.31/en/sujato">SN 22.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato"><b>abandon what is not yours</b></a>, explaining that letting go of things that do not belong to you will lead to your long-term welfare and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato">SN 22.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> is not yours, and therefore it should be discarded so that its absence can lead to your benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato">SN 22.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> and perception do not belong to you, they should be abandoned for the sake of your happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato">SN 22.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the aggregates of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> and consciousness are not yours and should be given up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato">SN 22.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy where people gathered and burned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato"><b>grass and wood</b></a> in Jeta’s Grove to show that just as monks don't feel "I am being burned" when wood is taken, they shouldn't identify with the aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato">SN 22.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha asked if monks felt they were being carried away when wood was collected from the grove, they replied it was not so because the wood was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato"><b>not self</b></a> and did not belong to self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato">SN 22.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that abandoning the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—is necessary because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato"><b>not yours</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.33/en/sujato">SN 22.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato"><b>measured</b></a> by whatever they have an underlying tendency for, and through that measurement, they are reckoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato">SN 22.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when one does not have an underlying tendency for something, they are not measured by it and therefore cannot be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato"><b>reckoned</b></a> by it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato">SN 22.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a certain monk clarified to the Buddha that if one has an underlying tendency for the aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>, one is measured and reckoned by it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato">SN 22.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the dialogue between the Buddha and a monk, having an underlying tendency for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> leads to being measured and defined by that feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato">SN 22.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that when a monk has an underlying tendency for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a>, he is measured and reckoned based on that perception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato">SN 22.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a certain monk explained that by not having an underlying tendency for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a>, one avoids being measured or reckoned by them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato">SN 22.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if one has an underlying tendency for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a>, then one is measured and reckoned by it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.36/en/sujato">SN 22.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato"><b>practicing in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a> as one who dwells often contemplating revulsion toward the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato">SN 22.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk truly practicing the Dhamma should dwell often contemplating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato"><b>revulsion</b></a> toward the physical aggregate of form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato">SN 22.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who dwells contemplating revulsion toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> is following the practice that is in accordance with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato">SN 22.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the proper practice for a monk involves dwelling with a sense of revulsion toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato">SN 22.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that practicing in accordance with the Dhamma requires a monk to dwell often contemplating revulsion toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato">SN 22.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that contemplating revulsion toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is a key aspect of practicing the Dhamma correctly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato">SN 22.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by dwelling with revulsion toward the aggregates, a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato"><b>fully understands</b></a> form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato">SN 22.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a person fully understands the five aggregates, they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a> from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato">SN 22.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that one who is liberated from the five aggregates is also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato"><b>liberated from birth</b></a>, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato">SN 22.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ultimate result of practicing in accordance with the Dhamma is to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato"><b>liberated from suffering</b></a>, including sorrow, lamentation, and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.39/en/sujato">SN 22.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted his followers to live as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>islands unto themselves</b></a>, being their own refuge and seeking no other external refuge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to live with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma as their island</b></a> and the Dhamma as their refuge, rather than looking to any other teaching for protection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who lives as their own island and refuge should carefully investigate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>origin of sorrow</b></a>, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair to understand how they are produced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>uninstructed worldling</b></a> suffers because they regard the physical aggregate of form as self, or identify self with form in various ways? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a person identifies their self with the aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>, they experience sorrow and lamentation when that feeling inevitably changes and alters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that identifying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> as being the self leads to mental distress and pain when that perception undergoes change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that sorrow and displeasure arise for those who regard <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> as self, especially when those formations change from their original state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an uninstructed person suffers from despair when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> they regard as their self undergoes its natural process of change and alteration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>impermanence of form</b></a> and its nature to change and fade away with right wisdom, one can successfully abandon sorrow and lamentation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a monk understands the impermanence, change, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>fading away of feeling</b></a>, the sorrow that would have arisen from it is abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to recognize that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a>, whether past or present, is impermanent and subject to suffering to prevent the arising of mental pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who correctly perceives the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>cessation of volitional formations</b></a> with right wisdom no longer suffers from the sorrow and despair associated with them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by knowing the impermanence and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>change of consciousness</b></a> as it really is, one can live without anxiety and remain at peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who has abandoned sorrow by understanding the nature of the five aggregates as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>dwells happily</b></a> and unagitated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the specific term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>extinguished in that respect</b></a> (tadaṅganibbuto) to describe a monk who no longer feels anxiety after abandoning the mental states that lead to sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>right wisdom</b></a> involves seeing that all five aggregates, both in the past and the present, are impermanent, suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the origin of suffering lies in how an uninstructed person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>regards the aggregates</b></a> as self, or as being contained within a self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the absence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato"><b>agitation</b></a> and anxiety comes specifically from abandoning the identification with form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.43/en/sujato">SN 22.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the devaputta Dīghalaṭṭhi described an ideal bhikkhu as a meditator with a liberated mind who understands the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.13/en/sujato"><b>arising and passing away</b></a> of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.13/en/sujato">SN 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the devaputta Dīghalaṭṭhi, a monk who is virtuous and unattached should specifically aspire to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.13/en/sujato"><b>attainment of the heart</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.13/en/sujato">SN 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by the devaputta Nandana about who is truly wise, the Buddha explained that such a person is concentrated, mindful, and finds <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.14/en/sujato"><b>delight in meditation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.14/en/sujato">SN 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who has exhausted all sorrows and destroyed all mental taints as an individual who is currently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.14/en/sujato"><b>wearing his last body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.14/en/sujato">SN 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the devaputta Candana that a person who is perfect in virtue, wise, and resolute is the one who successfully crosses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.15/en/sujato"><b>flood</b></a> of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.15/en/sujato">SN 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who is detached from sensual perceptions and has completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.15/en/sujato"><b>transcended the fetter of form</b></a> will not sink in the deep waters of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.15/en/sujato">SN 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the devaputta Vāsudatta suggested that a bhikkhu should practice mindfulness to abandon sensual lust with the same urgency as someone whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.16/en/sujato"><b>head was on fire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.16/en/sujato">SN 2.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a bhikkhu must be mindful to abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.16/en/sujato"><b>identity view</b></a> as if his very clothes or turban were ablaze? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.16/en/sujato">SN 2.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the devaputta Subrahma once expressed to the Buddha that his mind was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.17/en/sujato"><b>constantly terrified</b></a> and anxious regarding troubles that had not yet even arisen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.17/en/sujato">SN 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that safety for living beings cannot be found anywhere except through the factors of enlightenment, spiritual austerity, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.17/en/sujato"><b>restraint of the senses</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.17/en/sujato">SN 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in response to a deity's fear, the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.17/en/sujato"><b>relinquishing of everything</b></a> as a primary means for achieving true security? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.17/en/sujato">SN 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the devaputta Dīghalaṭṭhi visited the Buddha at the Bamboo Grove, his exquisite presence was so bright that it <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.13/en/sujato"><b>illuminated the entire grove</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.13/en/sujato">SN 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying at Vulture Peak just after Devadatta had departed, Brahmā Sahampati appeared and compared the way <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.12/en/sujato"><b>honor</b></a> (sakkāra) destroys a person of bad character to the way a plantain tree is killed by its own fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.12/en/sujato">SN 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati used the vivid biological analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.12/en/sujato"><b>mule’s embryo</b></a>, which leads to the death of the mother, to explain to the Buddha how the pursuit of gain and fame can bring about the spiritual and personal ruin of an inferior man? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.12/en/sujato">SN 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati pointed out to the Buddha that just as the bamboo and the reed are brought to their end by their own fruit, so too is a "bad man" such as Devadatta destroyed by the very <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.12/en/sujato"><b>veneration</b></a> and status he has sought? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.12/en/sujato">SN 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was sitting in the open air during the blinding darkness of the night at Andhakavinda with a light drizzle falling, Brahmā Sahampati appeared to him and encouraged the practice of resorting to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato"><b>remote lodgings</b></a> to facilitate liberation from the fetters of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato">SN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati advised the Buddha that a monk should live within the Sangha while remaining self-guarded and mindful, and should wander for alms among families while staying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato"><b>guarded in the sense faculties</b></a> and prudent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato">SN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati described a state of advanced spiritual training where a bhikkhu can sit without his hair standing on end (vigatalomahaṃso) in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato"><b>terrifying places</b></a> filled with snakes, lightning, and thunder, because he has become liberated in the fearless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato">SN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati testified to the Buddha that he had personally witnessed a thousand "conquerors of death"—those who have fully attained the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a>—living the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato">SN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati informed the Buddha that there were more than five hundred <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato"><b>sekhas</b></a> (those still in training) and numerous other groups of practitioners who were actively progressing toward the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato">SN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati noted that many followers within the Buddha's dispensation had attained the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato"><b>stream-entry</b></a> (sotasamāpanna), which ensures they are no longer subject to rebirth in the animal realm or other lower planes of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato">SN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati told the Buddha he could not even begin to calculate the number of other people who were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato"><b>partakers of merit</b></a> (puññabhāgā) because the amount was so vast that he feared speaking a falsehood if he attempted to count them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.13/en/sujato">SN 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aññāsikoṇḍañño, after a long absence, paid homage to the Buddha by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato"><b>kissing his feet</b></a> and massaging them with his hands? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato">SN 8.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aññāsikoṇḍañño identified himself to the Buddha by repeatedly announcing, "I am Koṇḍañña, Blessed One; I am Koṇḍañña, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato"><b>Fortunate One</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato">SN 8.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa requested the Buddha's permission to praise Venerable Aññāsikoṇḍañño in his presence, to which the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato"><b>Buddha replied</b></a>, "Let it occur to you, Vaṅgīsa"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato">SN 8.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa described the elder Koṇḍañña as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato"><b>enlightened after the Buddha</b></a> (Buddhānubuddho) and a striver of intense energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato">SN 8.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa praised Venerable Koṇḍañña as an attainer of solitude and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwellings</b></a> who is constantly diligent in his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato">SN 8.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa declared that Venerable Koṇḍañña has attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato"><b>everything a disciple</b></a> following the Teacher's instruction should attain through heedful training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato">SN 8.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa described Venerable Koṇḍañña as a monk of great power who possesses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato">SN 8.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa highlighted Venerable Koṇḍañña's expertise in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato"><b>knowledge of others' minds</b></a> (cetopariyāyakovido)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato">SN 8.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa referred to the elder Koṇḍañña as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato"><b>heir to the Buddha</b></a> who bows down at the feet of the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.9/en/sujato">SN 8.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was present when Venerable Mahāmoggallāna used his mind to scan the minds of five hundred arahants, finding them <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.10/en/sujato"><b>liberated and free from acquisitions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.10/en/sujato">SN 8.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa requested permission from the Buddha to praise Venerable Mahāmoggallāna in verse, saying, "It occurs to me, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.10/en/sujato"><b>Fortunate One</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.10/en/sujato">SN 8.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa described the disciples of the Buddha as possessors of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.10/en/sujato"><b>triple knowledge</b></a> who have left death behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.10/en/sujato">SN 8.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa praised Venerable Mahāmoggallāna as a monk of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.10/en/sujato"><b>great psychic power</b></a> who encompasses the liberated minds of other monks with his own? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.10/en/sujato">SN 8.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa depicted the disciples of the Buddha sitting upon a mountain slope, attending to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.10/en/sujato"><b>Sage who has gone to the further shore of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.10/en/sujato">SN 8.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa described the Buddha, Gotama, as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.10/en/sujato"><b>Sage of many qualities</b></a> (anekākārasampannaṃ) who is served by his enlightened disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn8.10/en/sujato">SN 8.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings only become enamored with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato"><b>earth element</b></a> because there is a certain gratification to be found in it, and if this gratification did not exist, beings would never become attached to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato">SN 14.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, beings would not become disenchanted with the earth element if there were no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> inherent in it, but because this danger does exist, they are able to develop a sense of world-weariness toward it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato">SN 14.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> from the earth element is possible, and it is precisely because this escape exists that beings are eventually able to transcend their attachment to the physical world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato">SN 14.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that if there were no gratification in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato"><b>air element</b></a>, beings would never feel infatuated or enamored with it, yet they remain bound to it because they experience its pleasurable aspects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato">SN 14.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that beings only seek to leave the air element because there is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> in it, and without the presence of such a drawback, beings would never become disenchanted or seek a way out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato">SN 14.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that until beings understand the gratification, the danger, and the escape regarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato"><b>four elements</b></a>—earth, water, fire, and air—as they really are, they cannot truly be liberated from the world of gods and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato">SN 14.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the only reason beings can actually move beyond the four elements is that an actual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> exists, and without this possibility, the goal of spiritual liberation would be unattainable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato">SN 14.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a state where one dwells with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato"><b>mind without barriers</b></a>, a state achieved only after one has fully comprehended the gratification, danger, and escape of the physical elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato">SN 14.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato"><b>water and fire</b></a> elements follows the same logic as earth and air, showing that our attraction and eventual repulsion from all physical matter are based on the existence of both pleasure and suffering within them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato">SN 14.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that it is only when beings have understood the elements according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato"><b>reality</b></a> that they are said to be detached, released, and fully liberated from the entire world including its gods and Maras? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.33/en/sujato">SN 14.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.45/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>, and that anything subject to impermanence is inherently suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.45/en/sujato">SN 22.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that whatever is suffering is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.45/en/sujato"><b>non-self</b></a>, and because of this, one should regard such things as "This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.45/en/sujato">SN 22.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person sees the five aggregates as they really are with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.45/en/sujato"><b>correct wisdom</b></a>, their mind becomes detached and liberated from the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.45/en/sujato">SN 22.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind that has become detached and liberated through wisdom is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.45/en/sujato"><b>liberated from the taints</b></a> through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.45/en/sujato">SN 22.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a person who is "engaged" with the five aggregates as unliberated, whereas one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato"><b>not engaged</b></a> is liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato">SN 22.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that consciousness can only persist, grow, and increase as long as it has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato"><b>form as its object</b></a> and its support? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato">SN 22.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted it is impossible for anyone to point out a coming, going, or rebirth of consciousness if it is viewed as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato"><b>apart from volitional formations</b></a> and the other three aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato">SN 22.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when lust for the five aggregates is abandoned, the support for consciousness is cut off, leaving it without a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato"><b>base for growth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato">SN 22.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described consciousness that is unsupported, unconditioned, and does not grow as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato">SN 22.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that because a liberated mind is steady, it becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato"><b>contented</b></a>, which prevents further agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato">SN 22.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is not agitated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato"><b>personally attains Nibbāna</b></a>, realizing for themselves that the goal of the holy life has been reached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato">SN 22.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner who has attained liberation knows through direct experience that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a> and what had to be done is finished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato">SN 22.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the final realization of an enlightened being as the knowledge that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato"><b>no more for this state of being</b></a> after the holy life has been lived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato">SN 22.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the growth of consciousness by saying it attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato"><b>increase and abundance</b></a> only when it is sprinkled with the "moisture" of delight in the aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.53/en/sujato">SN 22.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Anāthapiṇḍika described the Jetavana Monastery as a place frequented by the community of seers and as a source of joy for him because it was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato"><b>dwelt in by the King of the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato">SN 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Anāthapiṇḍika taught that mortal beings are purified by their own actions, spiritual knowledge, righteousness, and virtue, rather than through their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato"><b>lineage or wealth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato">SN 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Anāthapiṇḍika identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato"><b>action, knowledge, and righteousness</b></a>, along with moral virtue, as the components of the "excellent life" that lead to purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato">SN 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Anāthapiṇḍika advised that a wise person, seeing his own benefit, should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato"><b>examine the Dhamma systematically</b></a> to achieve purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato">SN 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Anāthapiṇḍika held the Venerable Sāriputta as the ultimate standard for any bhikkhu who has crossed over, due to his supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato"><b>wisdom, virtue, and peace</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato">SN 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed Venerable Ānanda’s intuition that the radiant deity who visited the monastery at night and praised Sāriputta was actually the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato"><b>young deva Anāthapiṇḍika</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato">SN 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deity Siva declared that by associating only with the good and becoming intimate with them, one gains knowledge of the True Dhamma and becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato"><b>better, not worse</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato">SN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the deity Siva, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato"><b>wisdom is gained</b></a> from no other source than from the good through the knowledge of the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato">SN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deity Siva emphasized that one who knows the True Dhamma through associating with the good <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato"><b>does not sorrow</b></a> even when in the midst of those who are sorrowing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato">SN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deity Siva taught that knowing the True Dhamma through association with the good allows a person to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato"><b>shine among kinsmen</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato">SN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deity Siva stated that beings who learn the True Dhamma from the good will eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato"><b>go to a good destination</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato">SN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deity Siva claimed that those who associate with the good and understand the True Dhamma <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato"><b>stand in constancy</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato">SN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha added to the deity Siva’s praise of the good by stating that through the knowledge of the True Dhamma, one is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato"><b>released from all suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato">SN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Anāthapiṇḍika described the Jetavana Grove as a place that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato"><b>generates joy</b></a> for him because it was the residence of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato">SN 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Anāthapiṇḍika taught that a person is purified in this world through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato"><b>moral virtue</b></a> and spiritual knowledge, explicitly excluding lineage as a means of purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.20/en/sujato">SN 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deity Siva repeatedly urged that one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato"><b>make intimacy with the good</b></a> because knowledge of the True Dhamma is only found through such association? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato">SN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deity Siva suggests that the benefits of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato"><b>knowing the True Dhamma</b></a> of the good include both social recognition among relatives and a fortunate rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato">SN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that the ultimate benefit of associating with the good and understanding the True Dhamma is that a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato"><b>released from all suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.21/en/sujato">SN 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that both gods and humans <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>rejoice in food</b></a>, as it is a fundamental source of delight for both realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Serī asked the Buddha to identify the "yakkha," or spirit, who does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>not rejoice in food</b></a>, unlike the rest of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when people give food with faith and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>confident heart</b></a>, that very gift serves them as a benefit in both this life and the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the practice of giving by advising that one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>subdue stinginess</b></a> and overcome the "stain" of miserliness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>meritorious deeds</b></a> are the essential support and foundation for living beings in the world beyond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Serī revealed to the Buddha that in a previous existence he was a king who was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>master donor</b></a> and an advocate for the practice of giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his past life as a king, the deva Serī established donation centers at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>four gates</b></a> to provide for recluses, brahmins, the poor, wayfarers, beggars, and mendicants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Serī’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>women’s quarters</b></a> once approached him to ask if they could also give gifts and perform meritorious deeds under his patronage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Serī graciously gave the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>first gate</b></a> of his charity network to the women of the palace so they could manage their own giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>nobles</b></a> (khattiyas) following King Serī requested to have their own share in the practice of giving so they could earn merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Serī handed over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>second gate</b></a> of his donation centers to the nobles to fulfill their desire for independent merit-making? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>troops</b></a> and military personnel under King Serī asked for the opportunity to give gifts and perform deeds of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Serī assigned the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>third gate</b></a> to his soldiers so they could participate in the royal charity program? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>brahmins and householders</b></a> requested to be included in King Serī's giving, prompting the king to grant them the final gate for their charity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because other groups took over the donation gates, King Serī was eventually told that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>his own gifts</b></a> were no longer being distributed at those specific locations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in response to his charity gates being occupied by others, King Serī ordered that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>half the revenue</b></a> from his outlying provinces be distributed locally as gifts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Serī told the Buddha that he cannot find the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>limit of the merit</b></a> he earned through his extensive and long-term commitment to generosity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Serī stated he could not calculate the full <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>ripening of his merit</b></a> or exactly how long he was destined to dwell in the heavenly realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches that one who acts as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>conqueror of the stain</b></a> of miserliness ensures that their merit becomes a stable foundation in the hereafter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Serī praised the Buddha's teaching on the benefits of faith-based giving as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato"><b>perfectly well-spoken</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.23/en/sujato">SN 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who has attained Nibbana to someone who has reached the shore and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.5/en/sujato"><b>no longer struggles</b></a>, unlike a person still in the river who must use all their limbs to stay afloat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.5/en/sujato">SN 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a brahmin who has finished his task and destroyed the taints has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.5/en/sujato"><b>no further work</b></a> to do, having already reached the end of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.5/en/sujato">SN 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Dāmali taught that a spiritual seeker should strive tirelessly and that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.5/en/sujato"><b>giving up sensual desires</b></a>, one no longer yearns for future existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.5/en/sujato">SN 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even though it is difficult, "trainees" established in virtue are able to do what is hard, and that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.6/en/sujato"><b>contentment</b></a> brings happiness to one who has entered the homeless life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.6/en/sujato">SN 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who delight in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.6/en/sujato"><b>stilling of the mind</b></a> and spend their days and nights in meditation are able to obtain the contentment that others find so rare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.6/en/sujato">SN 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even a mind difficult to settle can be concentrated by those who delight in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.6/en/sujato"><b>stilling of the faculties</b></a>, allowing them to cut through the net of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.6/en/sujato">SN 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the path of the Noble Ones as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.6/en/sujato"><b>even</b></a>, even when they travel through the difficult and uneven terrain that causes others to stumble? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.6/en/sujato">SN 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.6/en/sujato"><b>ignoble</b></a> fall headlong on the uneven path, while the Noble Ones remain "even" among the "uneven"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.6/en/sujato">SN 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Pañcālacaṇḍa praised the Buddha as a sage of broad wisdom who discovered an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.7/en/sujato"><b>opening in a confined space</b></a> through his realization of meditation (jhana)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.7/en/sujato">SN 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who have regained mindfulness and are well-concentrated can find the Dhamma for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.7/en/sujato"><b>attainment of Nibbana</b></a> even when they are in a confined space? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.7/en/sujato">SN 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Tāyana, a former sect-founder, taught that a spiritual seeker must strive to cut the stream and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato"><b>discard sensual desires</b></a> because a sage cannot reach oneness without letting them go? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato">SN 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Tāyana warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato"><b>lax wandering</b></a> or half-hearted spiritual practice only serves to scatter more "dust" of defilements rather than clearing them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato">SN 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Tāyana emphasized that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato"><b>well-done deed</b></a> is far superior because it is an action that one does not have to regret after performing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato">SN 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Tāyana used the analogy of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato"><b>kusa grass</b></a> to explain that a spiritual life wrongly handled drags a person toward a bad destination, just as grass cuts the hand that grasps it incorrectly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato">SN 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Tāyana stated that any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato"><b>slack work</b></a>, corrupt vows, or suspicious spiritual living will never yield great fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato">SN 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the visitor who illuminated the entire Jetavana monastery as Tāyana, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato"><b>former sect-founder</b></a> who had passed away and been reborn as a deva? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato">SN 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly commanded his monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato"><b>master the verses</b></a> spoken by the deva Tāyana, urging them to learn and remember them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato">SN 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the verses of Tāyana as being highly beneficial and as constituting the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato"><b>fundamentals of the holy life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato">SN 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Candimasa declared that those who achieve the meditative absorptions and remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.11/en/sujato"><b>unified in mind</b></a> will safely reach their destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.11/en/sujato">SN 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a diligent practitioner who escapes suffering to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.11/en/sujato"><b>fish breaking a net</b></a> to find freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.11/en/sujato">SN 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are diligent and master the jhānas will eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.11/en/sujato"><b>reach the far shore</b></a> of Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.11/en/sujato">SN 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described those who abandon the "thicket" of defilements through meditation as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.11/en/sujato"><b>abandoners of conflict</b></a> (raṇañjahā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.11/en/sujato">SN 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Veṇḍu proclaimed that those humans are truly happy who choose to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.12/en/sujato"><b>worship the Sugata</b></a> and follow his guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.12/en/sujato">SN 2.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Veṇḍu stated that happiness belongs to those who apply themselves to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.12/en/sujato"><b>diligent training</b></a> within the Buddha's teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.12/en/sujato">SN 2.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who follow his well-expounded instructions and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.12/en/sujato"><b>practice jhana</b></a> will not fall under the power of Death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.12/en/sujato">SN 2.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha addressed the deva Veṇḍu to explain that those who are not negligent will succeed through his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.12/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken instructions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.12/en/sujato">SN 2.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Tāyana taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato"><b>badly done deed</b></a> is better left unperformed, as it invariably leads to torment later on? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato">SN 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Tāyana advised that if there is a task to be done, one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato"><b>strive firmly</b></a> and with great effort rather than being half-hearted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato">SN 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young deva Tāyana believed that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato"><b>corrupt spiritual life</b></a> is entirely fruitfulness and does not lead to any high spiritual attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.8/en/sujato">SN 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deva Candimasa described practitioners who will find safety as those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.11/en/sujato"><b>heedful and mindful</b></a> while entering the jhānas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn2.11/en/sujato">SN 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, shortly after his enlightenment under the Ajapāla Banyan Tree, initially reflected that the Dhamma he had discovered was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>deep and difficult to see</b></a>, being subtle and beyond the scope of mere logic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the majority of people are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>attached to worldliness</b></a> and delight in such attachments, which makes the principle of dependent origination extremely difficult for them to perceive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>stilling of all formations</b></a> and the relinquishment of all acquisitions as concepts that are particularly hard for a world obsessed with craving to understand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha initially hesitated to teach because he felt that if he shared the Dhamma and others did not understand it, the effort would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>wearisome and troublesome</b></a> for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Dhamma as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>going against the stream</b></a>, noting that those blinded by the mass of darkness and lust would fail to see its subtle and profound nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Brahmā Sahampati realized the Buddha was inclined to "dwell in inaction" rather than teaching, he became alarmed and lamented that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>world is perishing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati vanished from the Brahma-world and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>appeared before the Buddha</b></a> as quickly as a strong man might extend his arm, just to plea for the world's benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati formally requested the Buddha to teach by kneeling on one knee and stating there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>beings with little dust</b></a> in their eyes who would otherwise waste away if they did not hear the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati urged the Buddha to teach because an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>impure Dhamma</b></a> devised by those still stained had previously appeared among the people of Magadha, and a pure teaching was now needed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati pleaded with the Buddha to open the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>door to the Deathless</b></a> so that people could hear the Dhamma realized by the "Stainless One"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati used the simile of a man standing on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>rocky mountain peak</b></a> looking down at the people below to encourage the Buddha to look upon the world overcome by sorrow and birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing Brahmā Sahampati’s plea, the Buddha surveyed the world with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>Buddha-eye</b></a> and saw the varying spiritual capacities of all beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while surveying the world, the Buddha observed beings with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>sharp and dull faculties</b></a>, as well as those who were easy or difficult to instruct regarding the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha saw that some beings live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>perceiving fear and blame</b></a> regarding the consequences of actions in the other world, while others remain heedless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the different spiritual levels of people to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>blue, red, or white lotuses</b></a> in a pond, noting that they all exist in different stages of growth relative to the water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Buddha’s analogy of the lotus pond, some beings are like lotuses that are born and grow in the water but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>remain submerged</b></a>, thriving entirely within the water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha saw some beings as lotuses that rise up out of the water and stand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>untouched by the water</b></a>, symbolizing those ready to transcend worldly suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha ultimately accepted the request to teach by declaring that the "doors to the Deathless" are open and those with ears should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>release their faith</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Brahmā Sahampati that he had initially refrained from speaking the sublime Dhamma because he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>perceived wearying trouble</b></a> in trying to communicate such a profound truth to humanity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Brahmā Sahampati successfully requested the Buddha to teach, he paid homage by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato"><b>keeping his right side</b></a> toward the Buddha before vanishing from the spot? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.1/en/sujato">SN 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted the story of a brahmin’s son named Brahmadeva who went forth into homelessness and, through diligent practice, soon realized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato"><b>supreme goal of the holy life</b></a> to become an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato">SN 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how the Venerable Brahmadeva, after attaining enlightenment, entered the city of Sāvatthī for alms and eventually approached his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato"><b>mother’s residence</b></a> while she was performing a ritual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato">SN 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati, upon seeing a brahmin woman making a constant ritual offering to a deity, decided to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato"><b>arouse a sense of urgency</b></a> in her so she would direct her generosity toward a more worthy recipient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato">SN 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati informed a woman that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato"><b>Brahma-world is far away</b></a> and that the deity she was honoring does not consume the type of ritual food she was offering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato">SN 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati identified the monk Brahmadeva as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato"><b>attainer of the stage beyond the gods</b></a>, noting that he was free from acquisitions and did not depend on others for his support? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato">SN 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati praised the enlightened monk as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato"><b>worthy of offerings</b></a> from both humans and gods, describing him as a knower of the Vedas who had fully developed himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato">SN 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati explained that an Arahant is one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato"><b>discarded all evil</b></a>, remains unsoiled by the world, and moves about seeking alms while being perfectly "cooled" or at peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato">SN 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati characterized a peaceful sage as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato"><b>without smoke or distress</b></a>, having laid down the rod of violence toward all living beings, whether they are weak or strong? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato">SN 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati compared a monk with a liberated mind to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato"><b>tamed tusker</b></a> elephant that wanders about calmly, free from the agitation and cravings of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato">SN 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati urged a woman to place her offering with someone <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato"><b>worthy of gifts</b></a>, promising that such a virtuous act would lead to her long-term happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato">SN 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted how a brahmin woman, after hearing Brahmā Sahampati’s instructions, became confident and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato"><b>established a gift</b></a> in her son, the sage who had crossed the flood of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.2/en/sujato">SN 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while staying at the bank of the Nerañjarā River shortly after his supreme enlightenment, reflected that it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>painful to live without respect</b></a> and deference toward another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered living in dependence on another ascetic or brahmin for the purpose of fulfilling an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>incomplete aggregate of virtue</b></a>, but found no one superior to himself in the entire world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha searched for someone more spiritually advanced to honor, yet saw no one in the world of devas, Maras, or Brahmas who possessed a more <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>complete aggregate of concentration</b></a> than his own? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, upon surveying the world of beings, could not find any person more <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>accomplished in wisdom</b></a> than himself whom he could live in dependence upon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha looked for a teacher superior to himself in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of liberation</b></a> but found that no such person existed among the ascetics and brahmins of his time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha determined there was no one in the world of gods and humans who surpassed him in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that he should live honoring, respecting, and depending on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma to which he had awakened</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati, having known the reflection in the Buddha's mind, vanished from the Brahma-world and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>appeared before the Buddha</b></a> as quickly as a strong man might extend his arm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati confirmed to the Buddha that all the Arahants and Fully Enlightened Buddhas of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>past lived in dependence</b></a> on the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati stated that all the Fully Enlightened Buddhas who will arise in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>future will live in dependence</b></a> on the Dhamma, honoring it above all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati encouraged the current Buddha to follow the path of his predecessors and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>live honoring the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati described the current Buddha as one who acts as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>destroyer of sorrow for many</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati declared that it is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>nature of the Buddhas</b></a> (dhammatā) to dwell honoring and respecting the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's reflection on the importance of living with respect took place while he was staying under the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>Ajapāla Banyan Tree</b></a> at Uruvelā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's survey of potential teachers included the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>entire world with its gods</b></a>, Maras, and Brahmas to ensure he found anyone superior to himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati advised that a person who loves themselves and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>desires greatness</b></a> should hold the True Dhamma in high esteem? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha initially sought a teacher because he believed one should live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>honoring and respecting</b></a> another person who was more accomplished in spiritual qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati showed his respect by arranging his upper robe over one shoulder and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>extending his joined palms</b></a> toward the Buddha before speaking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's decision to rely on the Dhamma was motivated by the fact that he was already <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>perfect in virtue</b></a>, concentration, and wisdom compared to everyone else? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati urged the Buddha to remember the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato"><b>instruction of the Buddhas</b></a> and to always keep the True Dhamma as his foremost authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.3/en/sujato">SN 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while staying at Sāvatthī, became aware of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>pernicious view</b></a> held by Baka the Brahma, who believed his realm was permanent, eternal, and the highest possible state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, sensing the thoughts of Baka the Brahma, disappeared from the Jeta Grove and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>instantly appeared</b></a> in the Brahma-world to address Baka's misconceptions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Baka the Brahma initially welcomed the Buddha by asserting that his own Brahma-realm was a place that was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>not subject to passing away</b></a> and that no higher escape existed beyond it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared Baka the Brahma was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>immersed in ignorance</b></a> because he described things that are actually impermanent and unstable as being permanent and stable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha corrected Baka the Brahma’s claim that there was no higher state by explicitly stating there is indeed a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>superior escape</b></a> beyond the Brahma-world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Baka the Brahma claimed that seventy-two individuals who had performed meritorious deeds and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>transcended birth and decay</b></a> were now residing in his realm for their final rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed Baka the Brahma that although Baka perceived his own lifespan as being exceptionally long, the Buddha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>knew the full extent</b></a> of Baka's life and its eventual end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, describing himself as one with infinite vision, recounted Baka the Brahma’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>past meritorious deeds</b></a> as clearly as if he were awakening from sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reminded Baka the Brahma of a past life where Baka had compassionately provided <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>water for many people</b></a> who were suffering from thirst and heat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recalled a time when Baka the Brahma rescued people who had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>taken captive</b></a> on the banks of the Eṇi River, setting them free from their captors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how Baka the Brahma once used his great strength to rescue a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>boat seized by a naga</b></a> in the Ganges River, saving the people on board who were in danger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed to Baka the Brahma that in a previous life, the Buddha had actually been Baka's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>companion named Kappa</b></a> and considered him to be wise and observant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the Buddha revealed his knowledge of the past, Baka the Brahma acknowledged the Buddha’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>blazing majesty</b></a> and the way his presence illuminated the entire Brahma-world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Baka the Brahma ultimately conceded to the Buddha’s wisdom, admitting that the Buddha indeed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato"><b>knew his lifespan</b></a> and possessed knowledge that surpassed his own? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn6.4/en/sujato">SN 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity living in a forest grove once warned a monk that he was being "devoured by thoughts" because of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.11/en/sujato"><b>unwise attention</b></a> (<i>ayoniso manasikāra</i>), specifically the unwholesome thoughts of sensual desire, ill will, and harmfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.11/en/sujato">SN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity encouraged a struggling monk to abandon improper thinking and instead practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.11/en/sujato"><b>wise attention</b></a> (<i>yoniso manasikāra</i>) by reflecting on the Teacher, the Dhamma, and the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.11/en/sujato">SN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity explained to a monk that reflecting on one's own virtues and the Triple Gem would lead to a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.11/en/sujato"><b>unwavering joy</b></a> and rapture, which ultimately helps one make an end to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.11/en/sujato">SN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity once felt a sense of dread during the stillness of noon, describing the sound of the great forest as a terrifying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.12/en/sujato"><b>creaking</b></a> or moaning when all the birds had settled down to rest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.12/en/sujato">SN 9.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a deity expressed fear of the forest's silence at high noon, a monk countered by saying that for him, that very same silence and rustling of the woods was a source of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.12/en/sujato"><b>spiritual delight</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.12/en/sujato">SN 9.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity once rebuked a group of monks for being restless, talkative, and having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.13/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained faculties</b></a> (<i>pākatindriyā</i>), noting that they were forgetful and lacked situational awareness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.13/en/sujato">SN 9.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity recalled how the "disciples of Gotama" in the past lived happily because they were content with whatever alms or lodgings they received and truly understood the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.13/en/sujato"><b>impermanence</b></a> of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.13/en/sujato">SN 9.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity compared monks who are overly focused on food and living comfortably in others' homes to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.13/en/sujato"><b>village headmen</b></a>, suggesting they have become infatuated with worldly life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.13/en/sujato">SN 9.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity harshly criticized negligent monks by comparing them to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.13/en/sujato"><b>ghosts</b></a> (<i>petā</i>) who have been cast aside and are without a protector? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.13/en/sujato">SN 9.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity living in a forest grove stated that while they criticize those who live neglectfully, they pay <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.13/en/sujato"><b>homage</b></a> to those who live diligently? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.13/en/sujato">SN 9.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's early disciples were described by a deity as having no personal desires for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.13/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> or lodgings, which allowed them to successfully make an end to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.13/en/sujato">SN 9.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity successfully stirred a sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.11/en/sujato"><b>spiritual urgency</b></a> (<i>saṃvega</i>) in a monk by observing his unwholesome thoughts and speaking verses to correct his behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.11/en/sujato">SN 9.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity once accused a monk of being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.14/en/sujato"><b>scent thief</b></a> (<i>gandhattheno</i>) simply because he sniffed a lotus flower in a pond that had not been given to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.14/en/sujato">SN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity explained to a monk that for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.14/en/sujato"><b>stainless person</b></a> (<i>anaṅgaṇassa posassa</i>) who is always seeking purity, a sin the size of a hair's tip appears as large as a cloud? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.14/en/sujato">SN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after being rebuked for sniffing a flower, a monk asked a deity to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.14/en/sujato"><b>warn him</b></a> again in the future whenever the deity observed him acting in such an improper way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.14/en/sujato">SN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity told a monk they had no words for a person who is "thickly smeared" with evil, comparing such a person to a stained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.14/en/sujato"><b>nurse's cloth</b></a> (<i>dhāticelaṃ</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.14/en/sujato">SN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the very first stage of human development in the womb as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.1/en/sujato"><b>embryonic spot</b></a> (<i>kalala</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.1/en/sujato">SN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.1/en/sujato"><b>solid mass</b></a> (<i>ghana</i>) is the specific stage of embryonic development from which the limbs, hair, and nails arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.1/en/sujato">SN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a human being in the mother's womb is sustained by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.1/en/sujato"><b>food and drink</b></a> that the mother consumes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.1/en/sujato">SN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the yakkha Indaka asked how a being "finds this body," the Buddha replied by detailing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.1/en/sujato"><b>sequence of development</b></a> from an embryonic spot to a swelling, a piece of flesh, and finally a solid mass? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.1/en/sujato">SN 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a yakkha named Sakka once challenged the Buddha, arguing that it is not proper for a monk who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.2/en/sujato"><b>abandoned all bonds</b></a> and is fully liberated to spend time instructing others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.2/en/sujato">SN 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that if one instructs others with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.2/en/sujato"><b>placid mind</b></a> (<i>pasannena manasā</i>) out of compassion, they are not bound or tied by that act of sympathy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.2/en/sujato">SN 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted to a yakkha that a wise person living among others should still be able to feel <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.2/en/sujato"><b>compassion and sympathy</b></a> for them without losing their spiritual independence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn10.2/en/sujato">SN 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk who was criticized by a deity for his minor negligence was immediately stirred with a sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.14/en/sujato"><b>spiritual urgency</b></a> (<i>saṃvega</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.14/en/sujato">SN 9.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity once told Venerable Kassapagotta that his attempt to exhort a dull-witted hunter was like a fool's errand because the hunter lacked the wisdom to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.3/en/sujato"><b>meaning</b></a> of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.3/en/sujato">SN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity used the metaphor of holding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.3/en/sujato"><b>ten lamps</b></a> to explain that even with perfect external lighting, a person without the "eye" of wisdom cannot perceive the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.3/en/sujato">SN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity successfully inspired a sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.3/en/sujato"><b>spiritual urgency</b></a> (<i>saṃvega</i>) in Venerable Kassapagotta after observing him spend time instructing a person who was incapable of understanding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.3/en/sujato">SN 9.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity living in a forest grove once lamented the departure of many monks, feeling a sense of discontent because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.4/en/sujato"><b>seating places</b></a> were now empty of the Buddha’s learned disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.4/en/sujato">SN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity praised the disciples of the Buddha as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.4/en/sujato"><b>learned</b></a> (<i>bahussutā</i>) and skilled speakers who brought life to the forest groves where they resided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.4/en/sujato">SN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity described the monks who left the forest as wandering <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.4/en/sujato"><b>like deer</b></a>, signifying their lack of attachment and their freedom in moving through the lands of Magadha, Kosala, and Vajji? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.4/en/sujato">SN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity characterized the lifestyle of liberated monks as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.4/en/sujato"><b>homeless</b></a> (<i>aniketā</i>), living without a fixed residence and free from the bonds of domestic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.4/en/sujato">SN 9.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity once rebuked Venerable Ānanda for spending an excessive amount of time <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.5/en/sujato"><b>instructing the laity</b></a> instead of focusing on his own meditation in the seclusion of the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.5/en/sujato">SN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity urged Venerable Ānanda to go to the foot of a tree and focus on placing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.5/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> in his heart rather than being negligent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.5/en/sujato">SN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity dismissively referred to the social interactions and teaching of laypeople as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.5/en/sujato"><b>chatter</b></a> (<i>biḷibiḷikā</i>) when it interfered with a monk's primary goal of meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.5/en/sujato">SN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda was moved to a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.5/en/sujato"><b>spiritual urgency</b></a> after being admonished by a compassionate deity who wanted him to prioritize his own enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.5/en/sujato">SN 9.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity named Jālinī, a former wife of Venerable Anuruddha from a previous life, once tried to tempt him to return to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato"><b>Tāvatiṃsa heaven</b></a> to be surrounded by celestial maidens? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato">SN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha rejected the offer of rebirth in heaven, stating that celestial maidens are actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato"><b>ill-fated</b></a> (<i>duggatā</i>) because they remain established in the view of identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato">SN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deity Jālinī believed that those who have not seen the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato"><b>Nandana Grove</b></a> of the Tāvatiṃsa devas do not truly understand what happiness is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato">SN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha told the deity Jālinī that she did not understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato"><b>word of the Arahants</b></a> when she tried to entice him with the pleasures of heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato">SN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha taught the deity Jālinī that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato"><b>conditioned things</b></a> (<i>sabbasaṅkhārā</i>) are impermanent, having the nature to arise and then vanish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato">SN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato"><b>stilling</b></a> (<i>vūpasamo</i>) of conditioned things is the only true and lasting happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato">SN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha informed his former wife that he had exhausted the cycle of births and would have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato"><b>no more renewed existence</b></a> in the company of devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.6/en/sujato">SN 9.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity once warned the monk Venerable Nāgadatta that entering a village <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.7/en/sujato"><b>too early</b></a> and returning too late led to him being overly entangled with householders? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.7/en/sujato">SN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity cautioned a monk that by sharing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.7/en/sujato"><b>joys and sorrows</b></a> of laypeople, he was putting himself at risk of falling under the power of King Death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.7/en/sujato">SN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity expressed fear for Venerable Nāgadatta's spiritual progress because the monk had become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.7/en/sujato"><b>overly bold</b></a> and too closely bound to families? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.7/en/sujato">SN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after being rebuked by a deity for his improper schedule and social entanglements, Venerable Nāgadatta was successfully stirred with a sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.7/en/sujato"><b>spiritual urgency</b></a> (<i>saṃvega</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.7/en/sujato">SN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity, acting out of compassion, once assumed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.8/en/sujato"><b>appearance of the mistress</b></a> of a house in order to admonish a monk who was spending an excessive amount of time with her family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.8/en/sujato">SN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity warned a monk that when people gather in assembly halls or on riverbanks, they often gossip about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.8/en/sujato"><b>private matters</b></a> and relationships between monks and householders? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.8/en/sujato">SN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk argued to a deity that a practitioner (<i>tapassī</i>) must be able to endure many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.8/en/sujato"><b>conflicting sounds</b></a> without becoming troubled or defiled by them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.8/en/sujato">SN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk stated that anyone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.8/en/sujato"><b>frightened by noise</b></a> like a "deer of the wind" in the forest is considered light-minded and unable to fulfill their spiritual vows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.8/en/sujato">SN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk asserted that even in the face of public talk or external distractions, a serious practitioner should remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.8/en/sujato"><b>unflustered</b></a> and mentally unstained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.8/en/sujato">SN 9.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a Vajjiputtan monk living in the forest once compared himself to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.9/en/sujato"><b>discarded piece of wood</b></a> because he felt lonely while others in the city of Vesali were enjoying a festival? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.9/en/sujato">SN 9.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity encouraged a lonely monk by telling him that many beings actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.9/en/sujato"><b>envy him</b></a> for his secluded life, just as those in hell envy those on the path to heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.9/en/sujato">SN 9.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity described a monk practicing in the forest as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.9/en/sujato"><b>heaven-goer</b></a>, contrasting his spiritual path with the worldly noise of those celebrating in the city? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.9/en/sujato">SN 9.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a Vajjiputtan monk's dejection was transformed into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.9/en/sujato"><b>spiritual urgency</b></a> (<i>saṃvega</i>) after a deity reminded him of the precious value of his forest seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.9/en/sujato">SN 9.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity once questioned a monk as to why he had stopped <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.10/en/sujato"><b>reciting the Dhamma</b></a> (<i>sajjhāya</i>) after being very diligent in his chanting for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.10/en/sujato">SN 9.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity pointed out that by reciting the Dhamma verses, a monk gains spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.10/en/sujato"><b>inspiration</b></a> (<i>pasāda</i>) and receives praise in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.10/en/sujato">SN 9.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk explained to a deity that his desire to recite verses ceased once he had attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.10/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> (<i>virāga</i>) and spiritual realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.10/en/sujato">SN 9.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk told a deity that once he had fully understood all that is seen, heard, or sensed, he reached the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.10/en/sujato"><b>relinquishment</b></a> (<i>nikkhepana</i>) which the peaceful ones speak of? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.10/en/sujato">SN 9.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk who had previously been very vocal in his studies told a deity that he had now become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.10/en/sujato"><b>unconcerned</b></a> and silent because he had attained the goal of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.10/en/sujato">SN 9.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity warned a monk that wandering at improper times was a sign of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.7/en/sujato"><b>suppagabbha</b></a> (overly bold or impudent) which hinders spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.7/en/sujato">SN 9.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity informed a monk that those who are free from worldly ties are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.9/en/sujato"><b>longed for</b></a> by others who are still trapped in the suffering of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn9.9/en/sujato">SN 9.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.34/en/sujato"><b>earth element</b></a> were exclusively suffering and not also associated with pleasure, sentient beings would never delight in it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.34/en/sujato">SN 14.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, beings are able to experience disenchantment with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.34/en/sujato"><b>earth element</b></a> only because it is inherently suffering and not exclusively pleasant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.34/en/sujato">SN 14.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.34/en/sujato"><b>water element</b></a> were entirely suffering without any pleasure, sentient beings would never feel infatuation or delight toward it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.34/en/sujato">SN 14.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated beings only become disenchanted with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.34/en/sujato"><b>water element</b></a> because it is fundamentally a source of suffering and not purely pleasant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.34/en/sujato">SN 14.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that beings only delight in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.34/en/sujato"><b>fire element</b></a> because it contains an element of pleasure, for if it were exclusively painful, they would not become enamored with it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.34/en/sujato">SN 14.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings would never find a way to become disenchanted with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.34/en/sujato"><b>air element</b></a> if it were exclusively pleasant and not also a source of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.34/en/sujato">SN 14.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that anyone who delights in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.35/en/sujato"><b>earth element</b></a> is actually delighting in suffering, and such a person is not liberated from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.35/en/sujato">SN 14.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by not delighting in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.35/en/sujato"><b>earth element</b></a>, a person does not delight in suffering and is therefore released from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.35/en/sujato">SN 14.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said those who find delight in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.35/en/sujato"><b>water element</b></a> are essentially delighting in suffering, and as a result, they remain unreleased from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.35/en/sujato">SN 14.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that to delight in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.35/en/sujato"><b>fire element</b></a> is to delight in suffering itself, making one a person who is not freed from the cycle of pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.35/en/sujato">SN 14.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that whoever delights in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.35/en/sujato"><b>air element</b></a> is delighting in suffering and consequently will not be liberated from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.35/en/sujato">SN 14.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the arising, persistence, and manifestation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.36/en/sujato"><b>earth element</b></a> is equivalent to the arising of suffering, the persistence of disease, and the manifestation of aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.36/en/sujato">SN 14.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the cessation and passing away of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.36/en/sujato"><b>earth element</b></a> constitutes the cessation of suffering, the calming of disease, and the passing away of aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.36/en/sujato">SN 14.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how the arising and production of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.36/en/sujato"><b>water element</b></a> is the very arising of suffering and the manifestation of aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.36/en/sujato">SN 14.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the cessation and subsiding of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.36/en/sujato"><b>fire element</b></a> marks the cessation of suffering and the calming of disease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.36/en/sujato">SN 14.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the arising and manifestation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.36/en/sujato"><b>air element</b></a> is the arising of suffering and the persistence of disease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.36/en/sujato">SN 14.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the cessation and passing away of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.36/en/sujato"><b>air element</b></a> to be the cessation of suffering and the passing away of aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn14.36/en/sujato">SN 14.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato"><b>saṃsāra</b></a> has no discoverable beginning, and that a first point cannot be discerned for beings wandering through the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato">SN 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings continue to wander and roam through countless lives because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato"><b>obstructed by ignorance</b></a> and fettered by craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato">SN 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy stating that if a person were to cut up all the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato"><b>grass and wood</b></a> in India into small blocks to count their maternal lineage, the vegetation would be exhausted before the line of mothers and grandmothers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato">SN 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for an inconceivable amount of time, we have experienced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato"><b>suffering and disaster</b></a> while repeatedly filling the cemeteries in the long course of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato">SN 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the sheer duration of our journey through rebirth is enough to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with all conditioned things and to seek liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato">SN 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato"><b>tears</b></a> you have shed while weeping over the loss of loved ones throughout your many past lives are far greater than the volume of water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato">SN 15.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the tears shed specifically due to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato"><b>death of a mother</b></a> over the course of your countless past lives exceed the amount of water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato">SN 15.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that throughout the long, beginningless course of rebirth, the tears you have shed from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato"><b>death of a father</b></a> are more voluminous than all the water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato">SN 15.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the tears shed due to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato"><b>death of a brother</b></a> or sister throughout the vast duration of samsara are significantly more than the water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato">SN 15.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the tears shed when experiencing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato"><b>death of a son</b></a> or daughter over countless aeons are more than the water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato">SN 15.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that the tears shed from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato"><b>loss of wealth</b></a> over the long period of wandering in rebirth exceed the water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato">SN 15.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the tears shed due to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato"><b>loss through illness</b></a> over the long course of samsara are more than the water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato">SN 15.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said the tears shed while being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato"><b>united with the unloved</b></a> and separated from the loved throughout the long journey of rebirth are more than the water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato">SN 15.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that because of the immense suffering experienced in the past, it is sufficient to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato"><b>dispassionate</b></a> toward all formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato">SN 15.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his followers to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a> from all conditioned things after realizing the vast extent of suffering endured in the beginningless cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato">SN 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the infinity of past lives by saying the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato"><b>line of mothers</b></a> for a single person would never be exhausted, even if all the vegetation in the world were used to count them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato">SN 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> as one of the primary fetters that keeps sentient beings wandering and revolving in the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato">SN 15.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that our long journey in samsara has resulted in us having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato"><b>swollen the cemeteries</b></a> through countless deaths across many aeons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato">SN 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato"><b>first point</b></a> of beings wandering in samsara is not known, as they have been traveling for an inconceivable amount of time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.1/en/sujato">SN 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the tears shed due to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato"><b>loss of relatives</b></a> over the long course of wandering in samsara are greater than the water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.3/en/sujato">SN 15.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the duration of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato"><b>eon</b></a> as being so long that it is impossible to calculate its length in terms of hundreds, thousands, or even hundreds of thousands of years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato">SN 15.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the length of a single <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato"><b>eon</b></a> to the time it would take for a person to wear away a solid rock mountain that is one yojana (roughly seven miles) high, wide, and long? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato">SN 15.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy stating that if a person wiped a massive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato"><b>mountain</b></a> once every hundred years with a fine cloth from Kasi, the mountain would be worn away before the end of a single eon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato">SN 15.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that throughout the beginningless cycle of rebirth, not just one, but many hundreds, thousands, and even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato"><b>hundreds of thousands of eons</b></a> have already passed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato">SN 15.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the first point of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato"><b>samsara</b></a> is not known, and because it has no discoverable beginning, beings have wandered through the cycle of rebirth for an inconceivable amount of time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato">SN 15.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with all conditioned things (formations) because of the immense duration of suffering they have already endured in the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato">SN 15.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the purpose of reflecting on the length of eons is to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato"><b>dispassionate</b></a> and seek liberation from the cycle of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato">SN 15.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by a brahmin how many eons have passed, the Buddha stated that the number of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato"><b>elapsed eons</b></a> is so vast that it is not easy to count them even in hundreds of thousands? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato">SN 15.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the number of past eons to the grains of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato"><b>sand</b></a> in the Ganges River, from where it originates to where it enters the great ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato">SN 15.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the number of eons that have already passed by and elapsed is actually far greater than all the grains of sand in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato"><b>Ganges River</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato">SN 15.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings continue to wander and roam through countless lives because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato"><b>obstructed by ignorance</b></a> and fettered by craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato">SN 15.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that over the long course of beginningless rebirth, we have experienced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato"><b>suffering and disaster</b></a> and have repeatedly filled the cemeteries? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato">SN 15.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the duration of our journey through samsara has been so long that it is sufficient to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a> from all conditioned things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato">SN 15.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha describe the inconceivable number of past eons, a certain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato"><b>brahmin</b></a> was so impressed that he immediately took refuge in the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato">SN 15.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the image of a "solid, massive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato"><b>mountain of rock</b></a>" without any holes or cracks to help a monk visualize the massive scale of time involved in a single eon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato">SN 15.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for an inconceivable amount of time, sentient beings have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato"><b>swollen the cemeteries</b></a> due to the sheer number of deaths experienced across countless past lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato">SN 15.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato"><b>first point</b></a> for beings wandering in rebirth is not discerned, emphasizing the "beginningless" nature of our existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato">SN 15.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that because of the vast amount of time spent in samsara, one should aim to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a> from all conditioned things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.5/en/sujato">SN 15.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's comparison involving the grains of sand in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato"><b>Ganges</b></a> was specifically addressed to a brahmin who was curious about the number of eons that had passed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.8/en/sujato">SN 15.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the way beings wander through rebirth to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.9/en/sujato"><b>stick</b></a> thrown into the air that sometimes falls on its base, sometimes on its middle, and sometimes on its tip? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.9/en/sujato">SN 15.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings continue to wander and roam through the cycle of rebirth because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.9/en/sujato"><b>obstructed by ignorance</b></a> and fettered by craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.9/en/sujato">SN 15.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, beings in the beginningless cycle of rebirth travel from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.9/en/sujato"><b>this world to another</b></a> and from that world back to this one, just as a stick thrown upward lands in various positions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.9/en/sujato">SN 15.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.10/en/sujato"><b>heap of bones</b></a> left by a single person wandering through rebirth for just one eon would be as massive as a mountain, provided the bones were collected and did not decay? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.10/en/sujato">SN 15.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.10/en/sujato"><b>Mount Vepulla</b></a> in Rajagaha as the specific landmark whose size would be equaled by the pile of bones of a single individual reborn over the course of an entire eon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.10/en/sujato">SN 15.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the beginning of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.10/en/sujato"><b>saṃsāra</b></a> is inconceivable, and that a first point of beings wandering through the cycle cannot be discerned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.10/en/sujato">SN 15.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a person sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.10/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> with correct wisdom, they will wander for a maximum of seven more times before making an end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.10/en/sujato">SN 15.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the path leading to the stilling of suffering as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.10/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>, which must be practiced to reach the end of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.10/en/sujato">SN 15.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the end of suffering is achieved by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.10/en/sujato"><b>destruction of all fetters</b></a>, a goal that becomes possible once the truth of suffering and its origin are fully understood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.10/en/sujato">SN 15.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised his followers that whenever they see a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.11/en/sujato"><b>destitute</b></a> and in a miserable condition, they should conclude that they themselves have also experienced such states in the long course of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.11/en/sujato">SN 15.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that we have all experienced a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.11/en/sujato"><b>miserable condition</b></a> at some point during the inconceivable amount of time we have been wandering through the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.11/en/sujato">SN 15.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the immense duration of our past suffering is a sufficient reason to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.11/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with all conditioned things (formations) and to seek liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn15.11/en/sujato">SN 15.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato"><b>five kinds of seeds</b></a>—those born from roots, stems, joints, cuttings, and seeds themselves—to illustrate how consciousness requires specific conditions to grow and flourish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato">SN 22.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato"><b>earth element</b></a> to the four "stations of consciousness," acting as the foundational soil where the mind takes root? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato">SN 22.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato"><b>water element</b></a> as a metaphor for delight and lust, explaining that just as seeds need moisture to sprout, consciousness needs emotional attachment to expand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato">SN 22.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> should be viewed as a seed that, when provided with its proper nutriment and "sprinkled" with delight, attains growth, increase, and expansion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato">SN 22.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when consciousness is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato"><b>involved with form</b></a> and supported by form as its object, it finds a footing and grows, much like a seed in fertile soil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato">SN 22.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted it is impossible for anyone to describe the coming, going, or growth of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato"><b>consciousness apart from form</b></a>, feeling, perception, and volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato">SN 22.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that consciousness reaches growth and expansion specifically when it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato"><b>involved with volitional formations</b></a>, using them as a station and foundation for its continued existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato">SN 22.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a monk abandons lust for the elements of form, feeling, perception, and formations, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato"><b>footing of consciousness</b></a> is cut off, preventing it from taking further root? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato">SN 22.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a state where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato"><b>consciousness is unestablished</b></a> and becomes liberated, because it no longer strives to produce further existence or "growth"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato">SN 22.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once consciousness is liberated and steady, one becomes content and attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato"><b>final Nibbāna</b></a> within oneself, understanding that birth has been destroyed and there is no more coming back to this state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.54/en/sujato">SN 22.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> because a concentrated monk is able to understand things as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined understanding things "as they really are" as seeing both the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>origin and passing away</b></a> of the five aggregates of form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the origin of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> arises when one delights in it, welcomes it, and remains fastened to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the origin of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> occurs specifically when a person delights in, welcomes, and remains fastened to their sensations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the origin of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> as the process of delighting in, welcoming, and remaining fastened to how one perceives the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the origin of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> is found in the act of delighting in and remaining fastened to one's mental constructions and intentions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> originates when one delights in it, welcomes it, and remains fastened to it as an object? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that delight in the five aggregates is the direct cause of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when clinging is present, it acts as a condition for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a> to arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that existence leads to birth, which then results in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a>, sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the total <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> as a causal chain beginning with delighting in the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the "passing away" of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> is achieved when one no longer delights in it, no longer welcomes it, and no longer remains fastened to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by not delighting in or remaining fastened to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>, the delight that was once there ceases to exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the cessation of delight as the key factor in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>passing away of perception</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>passing away of volitional formations</b></a> occurs when a monk does not welcome them or remain fastened to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>passing away of consciousness</b></a> results from the cessation of the delight that comes from not welcoming or fastening to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>cessation of delight</b></a> in the aggregates, clinging also ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>cessation of clinging</b></a> leads to the cessation of existence, which in turn stops the cycle of birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when birth ceases, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>whole mass of suffering</b></a>—including aging, death, and sorrow—is finally brought to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha summarized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> as the natural result of no longer delighting in or remaining fastened to any of the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.5/en/sujato">SN 22.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>agitation through clinging</b></a> occurs when an individual regards the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—as the self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>uninstructed worldling</b></a> as someone who has no regard for noble ones, is unskilled in their Dhamma, and views their physical form as being their self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when an uninstructed person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>form changes</b></a>, their consciousness becomes preoccupied with that change, leading to a mind seized by agitation and distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four ways an uninstructed worldling wrongly regards the aggregates, such as seeing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>self as possessing form</b></a> or form as being inside the self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who identifies with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>feeling as self</b></a> will inevitably become anxious and full of longing when those feelings undergo change and become otherwise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>perception as self</b></a> causes the mind to be overwhelmed by states born of change, resulting in a state of mental exhaustion and agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if an individual views <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> as their self, they will experience distress and agitation whenever those mental constructions change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when one regards <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>consciousness as self</b></a>, the inevitable transformation of that consciousness leads to an agitated mind that is full of longing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>full of longing</b></a> and distressed as someone whose mind has been seized by the agitation born of the change in the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>non-agitation through non-clinging</b></a> is achieved when a person no longer regards the five aggregates as their self or as belonging to a self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>instructed noble disciple</b></a> as one who has regard for the noble ones, is skilled in their Dhamma, and does not see form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, or consciousness as self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for an instructed noble disciple, even when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>form changes</b></a> and becomes otherwise, their consciousness does not remain preoccupied with that change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by not identifying with feelings, a noble disciple remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>not distressed</b></a> and does not agitate through clinging when those feelings change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a noble disciple who does not regard <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>perception as self</b></a> avoids the mental agitation that typically follows the change of how one perceives the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that because a noble disciple does not regard <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations as self</b></a>, they remain unperturbed and without longing even when those formations alter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>consciousness changes</b></a>, the noble disciple’s mind is not seized by agitation because they do not cling to consciousness as being their self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>non-agitation</b></a> to describe the mental state of a disciple whose mind is not overwhelmed by the inevitable instability of the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>mental preoccupation</b></a> with the change of the aggregates that directly causes the experience of anxiety and distress in uninstructed people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a noble disciple is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>not anxious</b></a> regarding the future or distressed by the present change of the aggregates because they have abandoned the view of self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha summarized the path to ending suffering in this sutta as the transition from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato"><b>clinging to the aggregates</b></a> to a state of non-clinging that prevents mental agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.7/en/sujato">SN 22.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato"><b>agitation through clinging</b></a> as the sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair that arise for an uninstructed worldling when they regard the five aggregates as their self and those aggregates inevitably change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato">SN 22.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an uninstructed worldling experiences suffering because they regard physical <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> with the view, "This is mine, this I am, this is my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato">SN 22.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when an uninstructed worldling's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> changes and becomes otherwise, their mind becomes preoccupied with that change, leading to distress and agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato">SN 22.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> as "this is my self" causes an individual to suffer from agitation when that perception undergoes alteration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato">SN 22.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an uninstructed person remains bound to sorrow and despair as long as they view <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> as "This is mine, this I am, this is my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato">SN 22.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that sorrow and lamentation arise when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> changes for a person who has identified that consciousness as their very self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato">SN 22.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato"><b>non-agitation through non-clinging</b></a> as the state where an instructed noble disciple does not regard the aggregates as "mine" or "self," and thus remains untroubled when they change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato">SN 22.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an instructed noble disciple avoids sorrow when their physical <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato"><b>form changes</b></a> because they have already realized, "This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato">SN 22.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by viewing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> as "not mine," a noble disciple prevents the arising of pain and displeasure when those feelings inevitably alter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato">SN 22.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato"><b>instructed noble disciple</b></a> as one who sees perception, volitional formations, and consciousness as "not self," thereby remaining free from mental agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.8/en/sujato">SN 22.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.9/en/sujato"><b>form is impermanent</b></a> in all three periods of time—the past, the future, and the present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.9/en/sujato">SN 22.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an instructed noble disciple should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.9/en/sujato"><b>indifferent to the past</b></a> regarding form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.9/en/sujato">SN 22.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.9/en/sujato"><b>not delight in the future</b></a> regarding the five aggregates because they recognize their inherent impermanence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.9/en/sujato">SN 22.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to practice for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.9/en/sujato"><b>fading away of lust</b></a> and the cessation of the five aggregates as they exist in the present moment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.9/en/sujato">SN 22.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that since past and future <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.9/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> are impermanent, one should seek revulsion toward those of the present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.9/en/sujato">SN 22.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.10/en/sujato"><b>form is suffering</b></a> in the past and future, making it even more vital to understand its nature in the present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.10/en/sujato">SN 22.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an instructed noble disciple practices for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.10/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> by seeking revulsion and fading away of present feelings and perceptions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.10/en/sujato">SN 22.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that because the five aggregates are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.10/en/sujato"><b>suffering in the three times</b></a>, a disciple should not delight in the future of any of these aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.10/en/sujato">SN 22.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.10/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> of the past and future is suffering, one must practice for the fading away of consciousness in the present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.10/en/sujato">SN 22.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.10/en/sujato"><b>anapekkho</b></a> (indifferent or without expectation) toward the past aggregates of perception and volitional formations because they are inherently suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.10/en/sujato">SN 22.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>saṃsāra</b></a> has no discoverable beginning, and the first point of beings wandering on—obstructed by ignorance and fettered by craving—cannot be seen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared an uninstructed worldling to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>leashed dog</b></a> bound to a strong post or pillar, who can only walk, stand, sit, or lie down right by that very post? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the uninstructed worldling is just like a bound dog because they stay close to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> subject to clinging, regarding them as "This is mine, this I am, this is my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>frequently reflect</b></a> on one's own mind, recognizing that for a long time it has been defiled by lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that beings are defiled by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>defilement of the mind</b></a>, and conversely, they are purified by the purification of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of a masterpiece of painting called a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>traveling show</b></a>" to show that even though such art is diverse, the mind that created it is even more diverse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the mind is more diverse than any other class of beings, even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>animal kingdom</b></a>, whose members are so varied because they were "thought up" by the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the uninstructed worldling to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>painter</b></a> or dyer who creates the figure of a man or woman by using various dyes on a polished board, wall, or cloth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the uninstructed worldling, through their own mental activity, produces their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked his followers to contemplate whether form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>permanent</b></a> or impermanent, leading them to realize they are all impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings continue to wander through the round of rebirths because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>fettered by craving</b></a> and obstructed by ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that just as a dog bound to a pillar remains tied to it, the uninstructed worldling is tied to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> and the other aggregates whether they are walking, standing, sitting, or lying down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a long time the mind has been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>defiled</b></a>, and it is this very defilement that prevents beings from reaching purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that the diversity found in the world, such as the many different types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>animals</b></a>, is actually a reflection of the incredible diversity and creativity of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>rebirth</b></a> and the creation of existence by comparing it to a dyer using lacquer, turmeric, indigo, or crimson to paint a figure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that a well-taught noble disciple who sees the truth of the aggregates becomes disenchanted with them and attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that one who understands the impermanence of the five aggregates knows that "there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato"><b>no more</b></a> for this state of being," signaling the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.100/en/sujato">SN 22.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> occurs only for one who knows and sees, and not for one who does not know or see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that knowledge and vision leading to the end of taints consists of understanding the origin and passing away of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk does not dwell devoted to mental development, even if he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>wishes</b></a> for his mind to be liberated from the taints through non-clinging, it will not be liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who lacks devotion to mental development to a hen whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>eggs</b></a> fail to hatch because she did not sit on them, warm them, or incubate them properly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the mind remains unliberated specifically due to the lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>development</b></a> of the four establishments of mindfulness and the four right strivings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mind cannot reach liberation if one has neglected to cultivate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>four bases for spiritual power</b></a>, the five faculties, and the five powers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the systematic cultivation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> and the Noble Eightfold Path as essential for the mind to be freed from the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a monk is fully devoted to mental development, his mind will be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a> from the taints even if he does not have the explicit wish for it to happen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a hen whose chicks safely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>break the eggshell</b></a> with their claws and beaks to illustrate the natural result of consistent and proper mental cultivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the gradual progress of mental development to the way a carpenter sees his fingerprints on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>handle of an adze</b></a>, knowing it is wearing down even if he cannot tell exactly how much was worn away that day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a carpenter knows his tool handle is thinning over time, a monk devoted to development knows his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>taints are being destroyed</b></a> even if the daily incremental progress is not immediately perceptible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>ocean-going ship</b></a> whose rattan bindings, after being weathered by the sun and rain, eventually rot and waste away easily when left on the shore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the image of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>rotting fetters</b></a> on a ship to describe how the spiritual bonds of a monk devoted to mental development eventually subside and waste away with little difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one must clearly see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>origin and passing away</b></a> of the five aggregates of clinging in order to achieve the final destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that mere <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>intent</b></a> or desire for liberation is insufficient if one is not actively engaged in the 37 factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> as a quality that, when developed, ensures the mind will be liberated from the taints through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even without a conscious wish for liberation, the mind of a practitioner <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>devoted to development</b></a> will naturally find freedom from the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who neglects mental development is like a hen whose eggs <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>rot</b></a> because she failed to provide the necessary warmth and care for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>five faculties</b></a> (faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom) among the essential practices that must be "developed" to secure mental liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a monk devoted to development, his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato"><b>fetters</b></a> subside and waste away just as bindings on a ship rot when exposed to the elements during the winter and rainy seasons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.101/en/sujato">SN 22.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>perception of impermanence</b></a>, when developed and cultivated, is capable of wearing out all sensual lust, lust for form, and lust for existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the power of the perception of impermanence to a farmer using a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>large plough</b></a> to tear up all the roots in a field as he ploughs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the perception of impermanence, when frequently practiced, can completely uproot the deep-seated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>conceit 'I am'</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the image of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>reaper of coarse grass</b></a> who shakes and cleans his harvest to describe how the perception of impermanence removes all ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as all mangoes in a bunch come away when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>stalk is cut</b></a>, the perception of impermanence causes all unwholesome lusts and ignorance to be exhausted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>peak of a peaked house</b></a>—which is the highest point and the place where all rafters meet—to the perception of impermanence in its ability to exhaust all lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the perception of impermanence to be the foremost of qualities for exhausting conceit, much like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>black orris</b></a> is considered the best of all root fragrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the supremacy of the perception of impermanence to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>red sandalwood</b></a>, which is regarded as the best of all heartwood fragrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>jasmine</b></a> is the foremost of all flower fragrances, the perception of impermanence is the foremost practice for removing ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all petty kings are followers of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>Wheel-turning Monarch</b></a>, just as all wholesome practices that exhaust lust are subordinate to the perception of impermanence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>radiance of the moon</b></a> outshines the light of all the stars, just as the perception of impermanence surpasses other practices in exhausting all types of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>autumn sun</b></a> rising into a clear sky to dispel darkness to the way the perception of impermanence dispels all ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that cultivating the perception of impermanence specifically involves reflecting on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>origin and passing away</b></a> of the five aggregates of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers to develop the perception of impermanence by contemplating the nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>, including how it arises and how it vanishes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that understanding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>passing away of feeling</b></a> is a core part of the practice that leads to the exhaustion of all lust for existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the exhaustion of ignorance is achieved by observing: "Such is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a>, such its origin, such its passing away"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one uproots the conceit "I am" by frequently reflecting on the arising and vanishing of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when one sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>passing away of consciousness</b></a> through the perception of impermanence, all ignorance and the conceit "I am" are removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>sensual lust</b></a> as one of the primary mental taints that can be completely exhausted through the consistent cultivation of the perception of impermanence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stressed that for the perception of impermanence to effectively uproot defilements, it must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato"><b>developed and cultivated</b></a> extensively? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.102/en/sujato">SN 22.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.104/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> (dukkha) as the five aggregates subject to clinging, which include form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.104/en/sujato">SN 22.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.104/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> is the craving that leads to renewed existence, accompanied by delight and lust, seeking enjoyment here and there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.104/en/sujato">SN 22.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.104/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> is the remainderless fading away and cessation of that very craving, its abandonment, relinquishment, and release? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.104/en/sujato">SN 22.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.104/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>—consisting of right view, right intention, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right concentration—as the way leading to the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.104/en/sujato">SN 22.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.114/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge</b></a> (vijjā) as the understanding of the nature of the five aggregates, including their origin, their cessation, and the way leading to their cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.114/en/sujato">SN 22.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person is considered "possessed of true knowledge" when they clearly understand the four truths regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.114/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.114/en/sujato">SN 22.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an uninstructed worldling, who has no regard for noble ones, views <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> as self, or self as possessing form, or form as in self, or self as in form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato">SN 22.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an uninstructed worldling as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato"><b>bound by the bond</b></a> of form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato">SN 22.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those who are bound to the five aggregates are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato"><b>bound to birth</b></a>, aging, and death, and they continue to move from this world to the next while still in bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato">SN 22.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized an uninstructed worldling as one who is bound by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato"><b>internal and external bonds</b></a>, unable to see either the near shore or the far shore of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato">SN 22.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an instructed noble disciple, who is well-trained in the Dhamma, does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato"><b>not regard</b></a> form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, or consciousness as self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato">SN 22.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a noble disciple is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato"><b>not bound</b></a> by the bond of the five aggregates, unlike the uninstructed worldling who remains fettered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato">SN 22.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a noble disciple to be one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato"><b>sees the shore</b></a> (tīradassī) and the far shore (pāradassī), meaning they have gained the vision necessary for liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato">SN 22.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly said of the instructed noble disciple who is not bound by the aggregates: "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato"><b>I say he is freed from suffering</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato">SN 22.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that true knowledge includes knowing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.114/en/sujato"><b>origin of volitional formations</b></a> and the specific practice that leads to their cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.114/en/sujato">SN 22.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that being "bound" means one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato"><b>lives bound</b></a>, dies bound, and goes from this world to the next while still in a state of bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato">SN 22.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that the difference between a worldling and a noble disciple lies in their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato"><b>view of the self</b></a> in relation to the five aggregates of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato">SN 22.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.104/en/sujato"><b>craving for non-existence</b></a> (vibhavataṇhā) is one of the specific forms of craving that constitutes the origin of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.104/en/sujato">SN 22.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that knowing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.114/en/sujato"><b>cessation of consciousness</b></a> is a critical component of what it means to be a person who has attained true knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.114/en/sujato">SN 22.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a disciple who does not regard <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> as self is no longer bound by internal or external bonds and is thus released from the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.117/en/sujato">SN 22.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught his disciples to abandon whatever is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato"><b>not yours</b></a>, explaining that letting go of these things will lead to your long-term welfare and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato">SN 35.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a> is not yours, and therefore advised that it should be abandoned for the sake of your own benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato">SN 35.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that external <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato"><b>forms</b></a> are not yours, and that by abandoning them, you will find lasting happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato">SN 35.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato"><b>eye-consciousness</b></a> is something that does not belong to you, and letting go of it is for your own good? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato">SN 35.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact</b></a> as not being yours, instructing that its abandonment results in your own welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato">SN 35.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>—whether pleasant, painful, or neither-pleasant-nor-painful—arising from eye-contact is not yours and should be relinquished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato">SN 35.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the monks that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato"><b>tongue</b></a> is not theirs, and that its abandonment would be for their welfare and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato">SN 35.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato"><b>tastes</b></a> are not yours, and once they are abandoned, it will lead to your happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato">SN 35.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> itself is not yours, and that by abandoning it, you will experience lasting welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato">SN 35.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> (dhammā) are not yours, and discarding the notion of ownership over them is for your joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato">SN 35.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato"><b>mind-consciousness</b></a> is not yours, and its abandonment is a path to your long-term benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato">SN 35.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato"><b>any feeling</b></a>—whether pleasant, painful, or neutral—that arises based on mind-contact is not yours and should be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato">SN 35.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of people carrying away <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato"><b>grass and sticks</b></a> from Jeta’s Grove to show that since the senses are not "self" or "belonging to self," we should not feel they are "us" being taken away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.101/en/sujato">SN 35.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the physical <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato"><b>body</b></a>, made of the four great elements and nourished by food, as being equivalent to a "boil" (gaṇḍa) that is subject to breaking and scattering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato">SN 35.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> (taṇhā) as the "root of the boil" and taught that a monk truly digs out this root only when craving is abandoned and cut off like a palm stump? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato">SN 35.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha corrected the claims of Udaka Rāmaputta, stating that one is a true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato"><b>knowledge-master</b></a> (vedagū) only if they understand the origin, passing away, gratification, danger, and escape of the six bases for contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato">SN 35.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is called an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato"><b>all-conqueror</b></a> (sabbajī) when they have understood the six bases for contact and have become liberated through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato">SN 35.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha criticized the teacher <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato"><b>Udaka Rāmaputta</b></a> for claiming to be an "all-conqueror" and a "knowledge-master" without actually possessing the true realization of those states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato">SN 35.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato"><b>six bases for contact</b></a> must be understood as they really are in order for a person to claim they have truly "dug out the root of the boil"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato">SN 35.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the human body as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato"><b>heap of boiled rice</b></a> and bread (odanakummāsūpacaya), emphasizing its conditioned and fragile nature in his teaching on the "boil"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato">SN 35.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the expression <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato"><b>cut off at the root</b></a> to describe the state of craving in a monk who has successfully removed the underlying cause of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato">SN 35.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who speaks rightly would only claim to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato"><b>dug out</b></a> the root of the boil if they had actually ended the cycle of craving and rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato">SN 35.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the body as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato"><b>born of mother and father</b></a> and subject to constant rubbing, massaging, and eventual destruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato">SN 35.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared a specific teaching called the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato"><b>exposition on the secure from bondage</b></a> (yogakkhemipariyāya) to explain how one reaches true spiritual safety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato">SN 35.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato"><b>secure from bondage</b></a> (yogakkhemī) because he has completely abandoned and uprooted all enticing forms cognizable by the eye? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato">SN 35.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a> has abandoned all sounds, odors, tastes, and tactile objects that are agreeable, pleasing, and connected with sensual desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato">SN 35.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> (dhammā) that are charming and lovely have been abandoned and cut off at the root by one who is secure from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato">SN 35.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the process of ending bondage as making unwholesome states <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato"><b>incapable of arising</b></a> again in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato">SN 35.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato"><b>has shown the way</b></a> to abandon the bondage of the six senses, leading to the title of being "secure from bondage"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato">SN 35.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, objects that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato"><b>agreeable and pleasing</b></a> are abandoned by a Tathāgata so that they can never take root or arise again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.104/en/sujato">SN 35.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato"><b>knowledge-master</b></a> (vedagū) specifically to denote someone who has mastered the understanding of the five aspects (origin, passing away, gratification, danger, and escape) of the sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato">SN 35.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato"><b>liberation through non-clinging</b></a> (anupādāvimutto) as the final step in becoming a true "all-conqueror"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato">SN 35.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once the "root of the boil" (craving) is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato"><b>annihilated</b></a>, it is made into something that no longer exists (anabhāvaṅkatā) for that individual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.103/en/sujato">SN 35.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato"><b>internal pleasure and pain</b></a> arise only when there is something to cling to, specifically the internal sense bases such as the eye, the ear, or the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato">SN 35.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a> as impermanent, suffering, and subject to change, explaining that anything with such a nature cannot be a reliable foundation for lasting internal happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato">SN 35.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> is itself impermanent and that internal pleasure or pain only occurs because one clings to the mind as a source of identity or stable experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato">SN 35.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a well-taught noble disciple becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with the sense faculties—the eye, tongue, and mind—once they truly see their impermanent and suffering nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato">SN 35.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by becoming disenchanted with the senses, a practitioner becomes dispassionate, and it is through this <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> that the mind is finally liberated from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato">SN 35.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a person is liberated, they possess the knowledge that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a>, the holy life has been lived to its completion, and there is no more returning to any state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato">SN 35.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a person does not possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.112/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> and full understanding of external forms (rūpa), they are fundamentally incapable of reaching the destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.112/en/sujato">SN 35.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.112/en/sujato"><b>abandoning</b></a> and becoming dispassionate toward external sounds, odors, and tastes, a person remains "unfit" to end the cycle of pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.112/en/sujato">SN 35.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly taught that only by having direct knowledge and full understanding of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.112/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> (dhammā) is a person capable of finally destroying all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.112/en/sujato">SN 35.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.112/en/sujato"><b>capable of destroying suffering</b></a> is strictly reserved for those who have developed dispassion for and successfully let go of the six external sense objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.112/en/sujato">SN 35.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato"><b>tongue</b></a> is impermanent and that the internal pleasure or pain one experiences through it arises solely through the act of clinging to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.105/en/sujato">SN 35.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the meeting of the eye, forms, and eye-consciousness is called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> (phassa), which then serves as the necessary condition for feeling to arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato">SN 35.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> (vedanā) acts as the condition for craving, which sets in motion the entire sequence leading to the mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato">SN 35.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the entire mass of suffering—including aging, death, sorrow, and despair—ceases through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato"><b>remainderless fading away</b></a> and cessation of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato">SN 35.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the same causal process of dependent origination leading to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> applies to the mind and mental phenomena just as it does to the physical senses like the eye and tongue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato">SN 35.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was reciting a teaching in private, a certain monk was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato"><b>standing listening</b></a> (upassuti) to him, which led the Buddha to ask if the monk had heard the discourse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato">SN 35.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically urged a monk to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato"><b>learn and master</b></a> the Dhamma exposition on the six bases of contact, declaring it to be fundamental to the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato">SN 35.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the sequence of dependent origination and cessation as a teaching that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato"><b>beneficial</b></a> (atthasañhito) and constitutes the beginning of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato">SN 35.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a> (jāti) is the condition for aging and death, and that by the cessation of birth, the entire mass of suffering is brought to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato">SN 35.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> (upādāna) as being conditioned by craving, and explained that the cessation of clinging directly leads to the cessation of existence (bhava)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.113/en/sujato">SN 35.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a specific discourse known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>Exposition on Burning</b></a> to illustrate the extreme dangers of becoming attached to sensory experiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated it would be better for one’s eye-faculty to be lacerated by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>red-hot iron pin</b></a> than for one to grasp at the signs and features of forms cognizable by the eye? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that if a person dies while their consciousness is bound by gratification in the signs and features of forms, they may be reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>hell or the animal realm</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that it is better to have the ear-faculty lacerated by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>sharp iron spike</b></a> that is glowing and ablaze than to grasp at the signs and features of sounds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha cautioned that consciousness tethered to the enjoyment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>sounds</b></a> and their secondary characteristics can lead a person to a miserable rebirth after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha said it is better for the nose-faculty to be lacerated by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>sharp nail-cutter</b></a> that is burning and ablaze than to grasp at the signs and features of odors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that if one's consciousness remains fixed on the gratification of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>odors</b></a> at the time of death, it serves as a cause for rebirth in the lower realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that it is better for the tongue-faculty to be lacerated by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>sharp razor</b></a> that is red-hot and glowing than to grasp at the signs and features of tastes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that a mind obsessed with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>gratification of tastes</b></a> at the moment of passing can result in a destiny of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated it is better for the body-faculty to be lacerated by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>sharp spear</b></a> that is ablaze and glowing than to grasp at the signs and features of tactile objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha regarded <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>sleep</b></a> as barren, fruitless, and dull for one's life, yet still considered it preferable to thinking thoughts that would cause a schism in the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that thoughts leading to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>schism in the Sangha</b></a> are so dangerous that even a state of "barren" sleep is a better alternative? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha instructed that a noble disciple should reflect on the fact that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>eye is impermanent</b></a>, along with forms and eye-consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that any feeling—whether pleasant, painful, or neutral—that arises from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact</b></a> is essentially impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha encouraged practitioners to contemplate that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>ear and sounds</b></a> are impermanent as a way to transcend the dangers of sensory grasping? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>nose and odors</b></a>, as well as the nose-consciousness and contact that depend on them, are all subject to the law of impermanence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a disciple should recognize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>tongue and tastes</b></a> as impermanent, as well as any feeling born of tongue-contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>mind is impermanent</b></a>, along with mental phenomena, mind-consciousness, and mind-contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when a noble disciple sees the impermanence of all sense bases and their objects, they become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with the entire process of sensory experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that through disenchantment, one becomes dispassionate, and through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a>, the mind is finally liberated from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.235/en/sujato">SN 35.235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the arising of experience through physical analogies, stating that just as picking up and setting down occurs when there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.236/en/sujato"><b>hands</b></a>, internal pleasure and pain arise only when the eye-faculty exists? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.236/en/sujato">SN 35.236</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that just as coming and going is discerned when there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.236/en/sujato"><b>feet</b></a>, the experience of internal pleasure and pain depends on the existence of the tongue-faculty and its corresponding contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.236/en/sujato">SN 35.236</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the example of limbs to show conditionality, noting that just as bending and stretching happen when there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.236/en/sujato"><b>limbs</b></a>, internal pleasure and pain arise conditioned by mind-contact when the mind-faculty is present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.236/en/sujato">SN 35.236</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that just as hunger and thirst are known only when there is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.236/en/sujato"><b>belly</b></a>, internal feelings of pleasure and pain can only arise when there is a sense faculty like the eye or mind to facilitate contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.236/en/sujato">SN 35.236</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized the necessity of sense bases by explaining that when there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.236/en/sujato"><b>no hands</b></a>, picking up and setting down are not known, and similarly, without the eye-faculty, no internal pleasure or pain arises from eye-contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.236/en/sujato">SN 35.236</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that just as coming and going are not discerned when there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.236/en/sujato"><b>no feet</b></a>, internal pleasure and pain do not arise when the mind-faculty and mind-contact are absent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.236/en/sujato">SN 35.236</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the presence of the eye-faculty is the prerequisite for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.237/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact</b></a> to produce internal pleasure and pain, much like how physical actions require the presence of hands? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.237/en/sujato">SN 35.237</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used a second discourse on the same analogies to reinforce that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.237/en/sujato"><b>tongue-faculty</b></a> exists, internal pleasure and pain arise from tongue-contact, just as coming and going occur when there are feet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.237/en/sujato">SN 35.237</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that internal pleasure and pain arise based on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.237/en/sujato"><b>mind-contact</b></a> only when the mind exists, comparing this relationship to how hunger and thirst exist only when there is a belly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.237/en/sujato">SN 35.237</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha illustrated the cessation of suffering by noting that when there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.237/en/sujato"><b>no limbs</b></a>, bending and stretching do not occur, just as internal pleasure and pain do not arise when the eye-faculty is absent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.237/en/sujato">SN 35.237</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that just as hunger and thirst are not found when there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.237/en/sujato"><b>no belly</b></a>, internal pleasure and pain do not arise in the absence of the tongue-faculty and tongue-contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.237/en/sujato">SN 35.237</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha concluded that if the mind-faculty does not exist, then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.237/en/sujato"><b>internal pleasure and pain</b></a> conditioned by mind-contact do not arise, just as physical movements are absent when the corresponding body parts are missing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.237/en/sujato">SN 35.237</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>four vipers</b></a> of fierce heat and deadly venom to represent the four great elements—earth, water, fire, and air—that constitute the physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that if any one of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>four vipers</b></a> representing the body’s physical elements becomes provoked, a person faces death or deadly suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> subject to clinging—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—are like five murderous enemies who follow a person with the intent to take their life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha compared the psychological states of delight and lust to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>sixth murderous housebreaker</b></a> who follows behind a person with a sword raised, ready to strike off their head? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>six internal sense bases</b></a> are like an empty village, where a wise person examining them will find every "house" to be void, hollow, and empty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha referred to the six external sense objects—forms, sounds, odors, tastes, tactile objects, and mental phenomena—as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>village-plundering robbers</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the eye is constantly "struck" by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>agreeable and disagreeable forms</b></a>, just as robbers might attack an inhabited village? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the tongue is struck by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>agreeable and disagreeable tastes</b></a>, symbolizing how external objects can overwhelm the senses like plundering robbers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>great flood</b></a> to represent the four spiritual obstacles of sensual desire, existence, views, and ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described the "near shore" of the great ocean of existence as a designation for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>identity</b></a>, characterizing it as perilous and frightening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha referred to the "far shore" as a designation for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a>, describing it as a place of safety that is free from fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> is the "raft" one must build and use to cross over the dangerous waters of worldly existence to reach the safety of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>arousal of energy</b></a> is symbolized by a person striving with their hands and feet to move their raft across a great body of water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>brahmin</b></a> as a title for an Arahant who has successfully crossed the flood of suffering and now stands safely on dry land? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha compared the constant physical maintenance of the body—feeding, bathing, and resting—to the high-risk task of tending to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>fierce vipers</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a wise practitioner must flee from the "vipers" (elements), the "enemies" (aggregates), and the "robbers" (external sense bases) to find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>true safety</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that crossing the flood to reach Nibbāna requires <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>personal effort</b></a>, rather than relying on an external ferry or a bridge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the mind is struck by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>agreeable and disagreeable mental phenomena</b></a>, just as a village is besieged by robbers when its defenses are down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described the "raft" of the path as being constructed from the "grass, sticks, branches, and foliage" of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>, ranging from right view to right concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato"><b>internal sense bases</b></a> like the eye and the mind appear void and empty when inspected by a person who is clever, proficient, and wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.238/en/sujato">SN 35.238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>three qualities</b></a>—guarding the sense doors, moderation in eating, and devotion to wakefulness—as the foundation for a monk to dwell in happiness and successfully strive for the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>guarded in the sense doors</b></a> does not grasp at the signs or secondary features of forms, sounds, or other sensory objects to prevent unwholesome states from entering the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>skilled charioteer</b></a> who can drive an aristocratic chariot in any direction he chooses to illustrate how a monk should train in the taming and composure of his six sense faculties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a monk trains in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>restraint of the mind-faculty</b></a> so that unwholesome states like covetousness and displeasure do not overwhelm him when he cognizes mental phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>moderation in eating</b></a> involves wisely reflecting that food is consumed not for play or intoxication, but simply for the maintenance and endurance of the physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that one should not consume food for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>ornament or beauty</b></a>, but rather to support the holy life and end the discomfort of hunger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha compared the wise consumption of food to a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>anointing a wound</b></a> solely for the purpose of helping it heal and close? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the analogy of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>greasing an axle</b></a> to explain that a monk eats only to provide the necessary support to "transport the load" of spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that through moderate eating, a practitioner seeks to strike out old feelings of hunger and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>prevent new feelings</b></a> of over-satiety from arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>devotion to wakefulness</b></a> as a practice where a monk purifies his mind of obstructive states through walking and sitting during the day and the first watch of the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha instructed that during the middle watch of the night, a monk should lie down on his right side in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>lion's posture</b></a> with one foot over the other? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that even when resting, a monk should remain mindful and clearly comprehending while keeping the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>perception of rising</b></a> fixed in his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk should resume purifying his mind of obstructive states through walking and sitting during the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>last watch of the night</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha noted that a practitioner who is devoted to wakefulness uses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>walking and sitting</b></a> as primary methods to cleanse the mind of unwholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the ultimate goal of these three practices is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavas), which is facilitated by a life lived in happiness and mental clarity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that by being guarded in the sense doors, a monk practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>restraint for the sake of composure</b></a> and inner peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha mentioned that eating for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>blamelessness and comfort</b></a> allows a practitioner to maintain the physical health necessary for the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha's simile of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>thoroughbred chariot</b></a> standing at a crossroads illustrates the readiness and control a monk has over his mental and sensory responses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that guarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato"><b>eye-faculty</b></a> involves specifically avoiding the habit of being a "grasper of signs" or a "grasper of features" when seeing forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.239/en/sujato">SN 35.239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defines the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.1/en/sujato"><b>unconditioned</b></a> as the destruction of lust, the destruction of hatred, and the destruction of delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.1/en/sujato">SN 43.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the specific path that leads to the unconditioned state is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.1/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.1/en/sujato">SN 43.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized his own compassion as a teacher by stating he has done for his disciples everything that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.1/en/sujato"><b>compassionate teacher</b></a> seeking their welfare should do? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.1/en/sujato">SN 43.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identifies <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.2/en/sujato"><b>serenity and insight</b></a> together as the path that leads practitioners toward the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.2/en/sujato">SN 43.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to seek out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.1/en/sujato"><b>empty huts</b></a> and the foot of trees for their practice to ensure they would not be negligent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.1/en/sujato">SN 43.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned his monks to meditate diligently so they would not have to live as those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.2/en/sujato"><b>remorseful</b></a> in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.2/en/sujato">SN 43.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.11/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> is the definitive way leading to the unconditioned, which is characterized by the total destruction of greed, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.11/en/sujato">SN 43.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's primary instruction to his disciples to avoid spiritual failure was the exhortation, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.11/en/sujato"><b>do not be negligent</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.11/en/sujato">SN 43.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly links the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.2/en/sujato"><b>destruction of lust</b></a> to the attainment of the unconditioned state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.2/en/sujato">SN 43.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha frequently concluded his teachings on the path by telling his disciples that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.11/en/sujato"><b>roots of trees</b></a> and secluded places are the proper settings for the meditation that leads to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.11/en/sujato">SN 43.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>unconditioned</b></a> as the total destruction of lust, the destruction of hatred, and the destruction of delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>serenity</b></a> (samatha) as a path that leads a practitioner toward the attainment of the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>insight</b></a> (vipassanā) is a fundamental path leading to the unconditioned state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that he had fulfilled his duty as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>compassionate teacher</b></a> by showing his disciples the path to the unconditioned out of a desire for their welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted his followers to seek out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>empty huts</b></a> and the roots of trees to practice meditation so they would not be negligent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>emptiness concentration</b></a> as a specific meditative path leading to the realization of the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>signless concentration</b></a> is a path that leads the practitioner to the destruction of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>undirected concentration</b></a> as a method for reaching the state of the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that concentration accompanied by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>thought and examination</b></a> serves as a path leading toward the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who dwells contemplating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>body in the body</b></a>, being ardent and clearly comprehending, is on the path to the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the contemplation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>feelings in feelings</b></a> as a path that leads to the destruction of greed and distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the contemplation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>mind in the mind</b></a> as a component of the path leading to the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that generating the desire to prevent the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>arising of evil</b></a>, unwholesome states is a form of right striving that leads to the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the effort to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>abandon unwholesome states</b></a> that have already arisen as a path to the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the base for power consisting of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>concentration due to desire</b></a> and volitional formations of striving is a path leading to the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>faculty of faith</b></a>, when it is based upon seclusion and dispassion, as a path leading to the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>faculty of wisdom</b></a> as one of the essential faculties that lead a practitioner toward the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>power of energy</b></a> is one of the five spiritual powers that constitute the path to the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>enlightenment factor of mindfulness</b></a> as a specific factor to be developed for the realization of the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>enlightenment factor of equanimity</b></a>, when ending in self-surrender, is a path leading to the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> as the first factor of the Noble Eightfold Path that leads to the unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>right speech</b></a> is a component of the path that practitioners must develop to reach the destruction of delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> as a path leading to the unconditioned when it is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned his disciples not to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato"><b>negligent</b></a> so that they would not have to live with remorse in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn43.12/en/sujato">SN 43.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remained silent when asked by the wanderer Vacchagotta whether a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.10/en/sujato"><b>self exists</b></a>, because affirming it would have aligned him with those practitioners who hold the view of eternalism? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.10/en/sujato">SN 44.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refused to answer Vacchagotta's question about whether a self does not exist, later explaining to Ānanda that such an answer would have aligned him with those who hold the view of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.10/en/sujato"><b>annihilationism</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.10/en/sujato">SN 44.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Ānanda that if he had answered Vacchagotta by saying "there is a self," it would not have been consistent with the arising of the knowledge that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.10/en/sujato"><b>all phenomena are nonself</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.10/en/sujato">SN 44.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refrained from telling the wanderer Vacchagotta that "there is no self" because he was concerned it would cause <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.10/en/sujato"><b>greater confusion</b></a> to an already bewildered man, leading him to think a self he previously possessed had now vanished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.10/en/sujato">SN 44.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> as the precursor to the arrival of unwholesome states, and that it is immediately followed by a lack of moral shame and moral dread? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato">SN 45.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for an ignorant person who lacks true vision, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> is the first unwholesome state to spring up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato">SN 45.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato"><b>wrong intention</b></a> arises as a direct consequence for a person who is established in wrong view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato">SN 45.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato"><b>wrong speech</b></a> originates in one who is already possessed of wrong intention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato">SN 45.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato"><b>wrong action</b></a> is the unwholesome state that follows naturally from wrong speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato">SN 45.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato"><b>wrong livelihood</b></a> as the state that arises for a person who has already fallen into wrong action? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato">SN 45.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato"><b>wrong effort</b></a> and wrong mindfulness are part of a sequential chain of unwholesome states that eventually leads to wrong concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato">SN 45.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge</b></a> (vijjā) as the precursor to the arrival of wholesome states, which is followed by the qualities of moral shame and moral dread? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato">SN 45.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for one who has attained true knowledge, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> arises as the foundation for developing the rest of the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato">SN 45.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato"><b>right intention</b></a> springs up in a practitioner who has already attained right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato">SN 45.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato"><b>right speech</b></a>, right action, and right livelihood as the successive wholesome states that arise for someone possessed of right intention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato">SN 45.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the arrival of knowledge eventually leads to the completion of the Noble Eightfold Path, culminating in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.1/en/sujato">SN 45.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> was a bhikkhuni disciple of the Buddha who was renowned for being wise, experienced, intelligent, and a brilliant speaker? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when King Pasenadi asked if the Tathāgata exists after death, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> explained that the Blessed One has left this matter undeclared? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>great ocean</b></a> to describe the Tathāgata, noting that both are deep, immeasurable, and hard to fathom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> informed King Pasenadi that the Buddha has not declared that the Tathāgata does not exist after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the physical <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> by which one might describe a Tathāgata has been abandoned, cut off at the root, and made like a palm stump? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> explained that the Tathāgata is freed from being reckoned in terms of form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked King Pasenadi if there was any accountant who could count the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>grains of sand</b></a> in the Ganges River to illustrate the immeasurable nature of the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> told King Pasenadi that the question of whether the Tathāgata "both exists and does not exist" after death is a point the Buddha has not declared? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> in a Tathāgata has been obliterated and is no longer subject to future arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> used the same word-for-word explanation as the Buddha to explain why the fate of a Tathāgata after death remains undeclared? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the impossibility of counting the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>water in the ocean</b></a> to the impossibility of defining the Tathāgata after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> noted that once the aggregates are abandoned and cut off at the root, they are made non-existent for the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> is one of the five aggregates that a Tathāgata has abandoned so thoroughly it can never arise again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> and the Buddha both asked King Pasenadi if anyone could measure the ocean in terms of buckets of water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> as an aggregate from which the Tathāgata is completely liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> was staying at Toranavatthu when King Pasenadi of Kosala visited her to ask about the existence of the Tathāgata after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> in a Tathāgata is "annihilated" in the sense of being obliterated and made subject to non-arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> explained that the Tathāgata is freed from reckoning (saṅkhā) because the basis for such reckoning has been removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the phrase <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>made like a palm stump</b></a> to describe how the five aggregates are destroyed and cannot grow again in a Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi expressed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>amazement</b></a> at how the teacher (the Buddha) and the disciple (Sister Khemā) agreed perfectly in both meaning and phrasing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> stated that the question of whether the Tathāgata "neither exists nor does not exist" after death is one of the four undeclared points? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked King Pasenadi if the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>ocean</b></a> could be counted as having so many hundreds or thousands of buckets of water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> was specifically sought out by King Pasenadi because he could not find another ascetic or brahmin in Toranavatthu to answer his questions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>undeclared</b></a> (abyākata) refers to questions that do not lead to a definite "yes" or "no" because the subject is beyond measurement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> used the term "freed from reckoning by way of form" to describe the liberated state of the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Tathāgata as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable</b></a> (appameyyo) specifically because the aggregates that define a person have been cut off? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> was described as having "ready wit" (kalyāṇapaṭibhānā) by the King's messenger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the term "Tathāgata exists after death" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>does not apply</b></a> when the five aggregates have been completely abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>Sister Khemā</b></a> explained that the reason the Buddha did not declare these points was that the Tathāgata is as hard to fathom as the depths of the sea? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that he has not declared whether a Tathāgata <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato"><b>both exists and does not exist</b></a> after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.1/en/sujato">SN 44.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared a parable about an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato"><b>acrobat</b></a> and his apprentice to illustrate how practicing mindfulness serves to protect both oneself and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato">SN 47.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato"><b>protecting oneself</b></a> through the practice and development of mindfulness, one effectively protects others as well? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato">SN 47.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato"><b>protects others</b></a> by practicing patience, non-harming, loving-kindness, and compassion, which ultimately results in protecting oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato">SN 47.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a> should be cultivated both with the thought, "I will protect myself," and the thought, "I will protect others"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato">SN 47.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha agreed with an apprentice named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato"><b>Medakathālikā</b></a>, who argued that each person must look after themselves to ensure mutual safety while performing on a bamboo pole? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato">SN 47.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that self-protection is achieved through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato"><b>repeated exercise</b></a> and development of the mind, which creates a positive impact on those around us? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato">SN 47.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that qualities such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> and compassion are the specific means by which protecting others leads to the protection of oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato">SN 47.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the setting of a town named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato"><b>Sedaka</b></a> among the Sumbhans to deliver a profound teaching on the interconnectedness of individual practice and social harmony? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato">SN 47.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato"><b>patience</b></a> and non-harming as essential virtues that protect the practitioner while they are focused on looking after the welfare of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato">SN 47.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato"><b>bamboo pole</b></a> suggests that the best way to ensure the safety and success of a group is for each individual to maintain their own internal balance and mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.19/en/sujato">SN 47.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> to a man carrying a bowl filled to the brim with oil through a dense crowd distracted by a beautiful woman? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato">SN 47.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the image of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato"><b>most beautiful girl in the land</b></a> to represent the intense worldly distractions one must navigate while maintaining unwavering mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato">SN 47.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a parable told by the Buddha, a man’s life depended on not spilling a single drop from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato"><b>bowl of oil</b></a> while walking between a festive crowd and a dancing celebrity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato">SN 47.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should be as diligent as a man followed by an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato"><b>executioner with a drawn sword</b></a> ready to behead him if he loses focus for even a moment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato">SN 47.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered the Janapadakalyāṇī Sutta while staying at a town called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato"><b>Sedaka</b></a> in the territory of the Sumbhans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato">SN 47.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> should be developed, cultivated, and made a basis for one's spiritual life to avoid the dangers of negligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato">SN 47.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked his monks if a man fearing for his life would dare to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato"><b>negligent</b></a> and look at external distractions while carrying a precarious vessel of oil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato">SN 47.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ananda explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato"><b>True Dhamma</b></a> does not last long after the Buddha's passing if the four establishments of mindfulness are not developed and cultivated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato">SN 47.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Ananda, the primary cause for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato"><b>long endurance</b></a> of the Buddha’s teachings after his parinibbāna is the persistent practice of the four establishments of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato">SN 47.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ananda defined the cultivation of mindfulness as dwelling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato"><b>contemplating the body</b></a> in the body, being ardent, clearly comprehending, and mindful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato">SN 47.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ananda taught that a monk must work to remove <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato"><b>covetousness and displeasure</b></a> in regard to the world while practicing mindfulness of feelings, the mind, and phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato">SN 47.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhadda approached Venerable Ananda to specifically ask about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato"><b>cause and condition</b></a> for why the True Dhamma might decline following the Tathāgata’s final nirvana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato">SN 47.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's parable illustrates that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato"><b>internal focus</b></a> on the body is more vital for safety than any external spectacle occurring in one's environment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato">SN 47.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ananda listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a>—body, feelings, mind, and phenomena—as the essential pillars for the survival of the Buddha's dispensation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato">SN 47.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a scenario where a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato"><b>huge crowd</b></a> gathers to see a woman who is extremely skilled in dancing and singing, serving as a metaphor for the world's most alluring distractions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato">SN 47.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ananda praised Venerable Bhadda’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato"><b>sagacity</b></a> and kalyāṇī (beautiful) inquiry regarding the longevity of the Buddha’s teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato">SN 47.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato"><b>well-undertaken</b></a> to describe how mindfulness directed to the body should be thoroughly practiced and established like a vehicle or a foundation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato">SN 47.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Ananda, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata’s parinibbāna</b></a> does not inevitably lead to the disappearance of the Dhamma, provided the community remains dedicated to satipaṭṭhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato">SN 47.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the man with the oil must not neglect it to look at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato"><b>external things</b></a>, just as a practitioner must remain focused on their internal mental state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.20/en/sujato">SN 47.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ananda specified that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato"><b>contemplating phenomena</b></a> as one of the four establishments of mindfulness is vital for ensuring the Buddha's teachings remain for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.21/en/sujato">SN 47.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light arose in him regarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato"><b>contemplation of the body</b></a> in the body, a practice he described as being previously unheard of? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato">SN 47.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that his insight into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato"><b>contemplation of feelings</b></a> in feelings was a realization that "should be developed" and was subsequently fully "developed" by him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato">SN 47.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato"><b>contemplation of the mind</b></a> in the mind as one of the four areas where eye, knowledge, and wisdom arose in him concerning things never before heard? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato">SN 47.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato"><b>light</b></a> (āloko) arose in him when he realized the contemplation of phenomena in phenomena and understood that this practice must be cultivated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato">SN 47.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the arising of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge</b></a> (vijjā) within himself specifically in relation to the four establishments of mindfulness being successfully developed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato">SN 47.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave a formal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato"><b>instruction</b></a> (anusāsanī) to his monks, exhorting them to always dwell mindful and clearly comprehending? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato">SN 47.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato"><b>mindful</b></a> (sato) as dwelling ardent, clearly comprehending, and mindful, while having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato">SN 47.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato"><b>clearly comprehending</b></a> (sampajāno) when feelings are known by him as they arise, known as they persist, and known as they vanish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato">SN 47.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato"><b>thoughts</b></a> (vitakkā) must be known as they arise, persist, and disappear for a practitioner to be established in clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato">SN 47.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk remains clearly comprehending by being aware of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato"><b>perceptions</b></a> (saññā) as they appear, stay, and pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato">SN 47.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato"><b>contemplating the body</b></a> in the body requires the monk to be ardent (ātāpī) and to remove longing and grief for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato">SN 47.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato"><b>vision</b></a> (cakkhu) arose in him regarding things "unheard of before" concerning the necessity of developing mindfulness of feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato">SN 47.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk stays mindful by dwelling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato"><b>contemplating phenomena</b></a> in phenomena, maintaining clear comprehension and removing worldly covetousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato">SN 47.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato"><b>ardent</b></a> (ātāpī) is a core component of the four establishments of mindfulness, alongside being mindful and clearly comprehending? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato">SN 47.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's realization of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a> was described as the arising of knowledge (ñāṇa) regarding matters that were previously unknown to him through traditional hearing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.31/en/sujato">SN 47.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly defined the state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato"><b>mindful and clearly comprehending</b></a> as the fundamental training he wanted his followers to maintain in their daily practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.35/en/sujato">SN 47.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that those for whom you have compassion—such as friends, colleagues, relatives, or kinsmen—should be exhorted, settled, and established in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.48/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.48/en/sujato">SN 47.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when you wish to benefit those who think they should listen to you, you should encourage them to dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.48/en/sujato"><b>contemplating the body</b></a> in the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, and mindful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.48/en/sujato">SN 47.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that helping loved ones involves establishing them in mindfulness of feelings, the mind, and phenomena, while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.48/en/sujato"><b>removing covetousness and displeasure</b></a> in regard to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.48/en/sujato">SN 47.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five specific faculties related to experience, known as the faculties of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato"><b>pleasure, pain, joy, displeasure, and equanimity</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato">SN 48.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato"><b>faculty of pleasure</b></a> specifically as bodily pleasure and bodily pleasantness that is felt as a result of body-contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato">SN 48.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato"><b>faculty of pain</b></a> consists of bodily pain and bodily unpleasantness born of body-contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato">SN 48.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato"><b>faculty of joy</b></a> refers to mental pleasure and mental pleasantness born specifically of mind-contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato">SN 48.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato"><b>faculty of displeasure</b></a> is defined as mental pain and mental unpleasantness that arises from mind-contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato">SN 48.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato"><b>faculty of equanimity</b></a> as a feeling that is neither pleasant nor painful, whether it is experienced bodily or mentally? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato">SN 48.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that both the faculty of pleasure and the faculty of joy should be collectively regarded as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato"><b>pleasant feeling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato">SN 48.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the faculty of pain and the faculty of displeasure are both to be categorized and seen as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato"><b>painful feeling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato">SN 48.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the faculty of equanimity is the only one among the five feeling-faculties to be regarded as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato"><b>neither-pleasant-nor-painful feeling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.37/en/sujato">SN 48.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>virtuous bhikkhu</b></a> should carefully attend to the five aggregates of clinging as being impermanent, suffering, and non-self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta identified eleven specific ways to contemplate the aggregates, including viewing them as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>disease, a tumor, and a dart</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Sāriputta, a virtuous monk who consistently views the five aggregates as alien and disintegrating can eventually realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>fruit of stream-entry</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta explained that even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>stream-enterer</b></a> must continue to attend carefully to the five aggregates of clinging as impermanent and suffering to reach the next stage of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta stated that by contemplating the five aggregates as a misfortune and an affliction, a stream-enterer can realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>fruit of once-returning</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>once-returner</b></a> should continue the same method of careful attention toward the aggregates to further their spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta pointed out that when a once-returner views the five aggregates as empty and non-self, they can realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>fruit of non-returning</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta advised that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>non-returner</b></a> should still attend to the five aggregates of clinging as impermanent and suffering in order to attain the final goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta explained that through the continued contemplation of the aggregates as a disease and a dart, a non-returner can realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>fruit of arahantship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta clarified that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>arahant</b></a> has no further work to do and no more accumulation of deeds, yet they still maintain careful attention toward the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta noted that for an enlightened arahant, the contemplation of the aggregates as impermanent and empty leads to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwelling in this life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that the practice of attending to the five aggregates as non-self helps an arahant maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta defined the objects of this careful attention as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates of clinging</b></a>, which are form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that this entire discourse on spiritual progress was prompted by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>Ven. Mahākoṭṭhika</b></a> questioning Ven. Sāriputta at the Deer Park in Isipatana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> (yoniso manasikāra) is the essential mental quality required to analyze the aggregates correctly at every stage of the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.122/en/sujato">SN 22.122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined ignorance as the failure of an untaught worldling to understand, as it really is, that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> has the nature of arising and the nature of vanishing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person is considered to be "immersed in ignorance" when they do not truly understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>nature of arising and vanishing</b></a> regarding the aggregate of feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that ignorance involves not perceiving the nature of arising and vanishing of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who does not understand the nature of arising and vanishing in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> is described as being in a state of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that ignorance exists when someone fails to recognize, as it really is, that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> has the nature of arising, the nature of vanishing, and the nature of both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined true knowledge (<i>vijjā</i>) as the capacity of an instructed noble disciple to understand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> as having the nature of arising and the nature of vanishing as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a noble disciple possesses true knowledge when they accurately perceive the nature of arising and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>nature of vanishing</b></a> regarding the aggregate of feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, true knowledge is attained when one understands the nature of arising and vanishing in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> exactly as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an instructed noble disciple is "possessed of true knowledge" when they understand that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> have the nature of arising and vanishing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that true knowledge consists in understanding, as it really is, the nature of arising and vanishing of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>instructed noble disciple</b></a> (<i>sutavā ariyasāvako</i>) as the specific type of person who possesses the true knowledge of the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted the noble disciple with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>untaught worldling</b></a> (<i>assutavā puthujjano</i>), who remains in ignorance because they lack real understanding of the aggregates' nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that both ignorance and true knowledge are defined by whether or not one understands the nature of things <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>as it really is</b></a> (<i>yathābhūtaṃ</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato"><b>nature of both arising and vanishing</b></a> (<i>samudayavayadhamma</i>) must be understood across all five aggregates to overcome ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.126/en/sujato">SN 22.126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the five aggregates of form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.136/en/sujato"><b>glowing ember</b></a> to illustrate their inherent nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.136/en/sujato">SN 22.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an instructed noble disciple who sees the five aggregates as glowing embers experiences <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.136/en/sujato"><b>revulsion</b></a>, which leads to the fading of lust and eventual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.136/en/sujato">SN 22.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, once a practitioner is liberated through revulsion toward the aggregates, they possess the knowledge that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.136/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a>, the holy life has been lived, and what had to be done has been done? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.136/en/sujato">SN 22.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that because the five aggregates are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.137/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>, a practitioner should abandon all desire for form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.137/en/sujato">SN 22.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for whatever is discovered to be impermanent, the primary task of the monk is to ensure that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.137/en/sujato"><b>desire is abandoned</b></a> toward those things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.137/en/sujato">SN 22.137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a clansman who has gone forth out of faith, the proper way to live in accordance with the Dhamma is to dwell often in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.146/en/sujato"><b>revulsion</b></a> toward the five aggregates of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.146/en/sujato">SN 22.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by dwelling often in revulsion toward form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness, a monk eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.146/en/sujato"><b>fully understands</b></a> those five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.146/en/sujato">SN 22.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who fully understands the five aggregates through persistent revulsion becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.146/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a> from the cycles of birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.146/en/sujato">SN 22.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, being liberated from the five aggregates means that a person is also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.146/en/sujato"><b>liberated from suffering</b></a>, including all sorrow, lamentation, and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.146/en/sujato">SN 22.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that dwelling often in revulsion toward the aggregates is the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.146/en/sujato"><b>practice in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a> (<i>anudhammo</i>) for those who have dedicated themselves to the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.146/en/sujato">SN 22.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>cut the five lower fetters</b></a> by being resolved on the inspired utterance: "It might not be, and it might not be mine; it will not be, and it will not be mine"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>untaught worldling</b></a> remains in bondage because they regard the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—as self or as belonging to a self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the lack of understanding of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>impermanent form</b></a> "as it really is" as a primary reason why an untaught person fails to achieve spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an untaught worldling does not truly perceive that feeling, perception, and volitional formations are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that common ignorance includes failing to recognize that consciousness is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>non-self</b></a> as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a worldling fails to understand that all five aggregates are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>conditioned</b></a> (<i>saṅkhata</i>) and therefore subject to change and instability? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that an untaught person does not understand, as it really is, that form and the other aggregates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>will be annihilated</b></a> (<i>vibhavissati</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>instructed noble disciple</b></a> as one who does not regard form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, or consciousness as self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a noble disciple who understands the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>nature of the aggregates</b></a> as impermanent and suffering can effectively move toward the destruction of the fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a monk understands the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>annihilation of form</b></a> and the other aggregates as it really is, they can become resolved on non-attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that an untaught worldling experiences <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>terror</b></a> when faced with the prospect of non-existence or the cessation of the "mine"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted the fearful worldling with the noble disciple, who feels <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>no terror</b></a> at the thought "It might not be, and it might not be mine"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> can only persist and grow when it has form as a means, an object, and a support? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that consciousness finds <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>growth, increase, and abundance</b></a> by being "sprinkled with delight" in relation to feeling, perception, and volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared it impossible for anyone to describe the coming, going, or rebirth of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>consciousness apart from</b></a> the other four aggregates of form, feeling, perception, and volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if lust for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>form element</b></a> is abandoned, the support for consciousness is cut off, and its object is removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once lust for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>consciousness element</b></a> itself is abandoned, consciousness becomes unestablished and ceases to grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>unestablished consciousness</b></a> as being unconditioned, liberated, and steady? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner with liberated consciousness is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>content</b></a>, and because they are content, they are no longer agitated by anything in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that being unagitated leads a monk to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>attain Nibbāna</b></a> personally and directly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (<i>āsavānaṃ khayo</i>) occurs immediately for one who knows and sees the unestablished nature of consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.55/en/sujato">SN 22.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a monk delights in and remains fastened to agreeable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato"><b>forms cognizable by the eye</b></a>, he is considered to have entered Mara’s lair and is bound by Mara’s snare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato">SN 35.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who welcomes and clings to tantalizing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> cognizable by the mind is said to have come under the control of the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato">SN 35.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who remains fastened to lovely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato"><b>tastes cognizable by the tongue</b></a> is bound by Mara’s bond and can be dealt with by the Evil One as he pleases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato">SN 35.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk is freed from Mara’s snare when he does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato"><b>not delight in forms</b></a> that are otherwise agreeable, pleasing, and enticing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato">SN 35.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who does not welcome or remain fastened to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato"><b>agreeable mental phenomena</b></a> is considered to have not entered Mara’s lair and is not under the control of the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato">SN 35.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk as being released from Mara’s bond if he does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato"><b>not welcome tastes</b></a> that are charming, lovely, and tantalizing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato">SN 35.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined entering Mara’s lair specifically as the act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato"><b>delighting in and clinging</b></a> to sense objects that are enticing and connected with sensual desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato">SN 35.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that any monk who remains fastened to sense objects that are agreeable and lovely is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato"><b>bound by Mara’s snare</b></a> and is at the mercy of the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato">SN 35.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who does not delight in enticing sense objects is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato"><b>not under the control</b></a> of the Evil One and has cast off Mara's snare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato">SN 35.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified certain objects <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato"><b>cognizable by the mind</b></a> as being just as potentially binding as physical objects if one chooses to delight in and welcome them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.114/en/sujato">SN 35.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one cannot know, see, or reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>end of the world</b></a> by traveling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that in the Noble One’s Discipline, the "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>world</b></a>" is defined as that by which one is a perceiver and conceiver of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a> as one of the things in the world by which one is a perceiver and conceiver of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that it is by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>ear</b></a> that one is a perceiver of the world and a conceiver of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that one is a perceiver and conceiver of the world through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>nose</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda clarified that it is by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>tongue</b></a> that one is a perceiver of the world and a conceiver of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda stated that one is a perceiver and conceiver of the world through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that it is by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> that one is a perceiver of the world and a conceiver of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda compared monks who bypass the Buddha to ask him questions to a man who ignores the root of a tree to search for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>heartwood</b></a> among the branches and leaves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described by Venerable Ānanda as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>elucidator of meaning</b></a> and the giver of the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Ānanda, the six sense bases are what is called the "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>world</b></a>" in the Noble One's Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once gave a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>brief summary</b></a> about the end of the world and then immediately entered his dwelling without explaining it in detail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is referred to as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a>, who is the lord of the Dhamma and the one who knows and sees? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda was highly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>esteemed</b></a> by his spiritual companions as someone capable of elaborating on the Buddha's brief teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>dhammabhūto</b></a>, meaning he has become the Dhamma, as stated by Venerable Ānanda? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described by Venerable Ānanda as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>lord of the Dhamma</b></a>, the one to whom monks should go for the definitive meaning of a teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>conceiver of the world</b></a> to explain how we identify with and create notions about the world through our senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>ñāṇabhūto</b></a>, meaning he has become knowledge, according to the description by Venerable Ānanda? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the end of the world is not something to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>known or seen</b></a> by physical travel to a distance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>brahmabhūto</b></a>, meaning he has become the holy one, in the praise offered by Venerable Ānanda? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified Venerable Ānanda as a monk of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato"><b>great wisdom</b></a> because of his ability to perfectly detail a short instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.116/en/sujato">SN 35.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his enlightenment, the Buddha observed that the mind frequently wanders toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>five chords of sensual pleasure</b></a> that were previously contacted, even after they have ceased and changed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that regarding past sensual pleasures that have ceased and changed, one should maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a>, mindfulness, and the protection of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even when sensual pleasures are in the past, a person’s mind may still frequently go toward them, requiring a dedicated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>protection of the mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that there is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>base to be known</b></a> where the eye ceases and the perception of forms ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there is a specific dimension or base where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>ear ceases</b></a> and the perception of sounds ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out the existence of a base where the nose ceases and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>perception of odors</b></a> ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a base that should be known where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>tongue ceases</b></a> and the perception of tastes ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a practitioner should understand the base where the body ceases and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>perception of tactile objects</b></a> ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one must come to know the base where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>mind ceases</b></a> and the perception of mental phenomena ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha gave a brief summary about the cessation of the sense bases and then left without explanation, the monks turned to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ānanda</b></a> to elaborate on the meaning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained that the "base to be known" mentioned by the Buddha refers specifically to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>cessation of the six sense bases</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that Venerable Ānanda possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>great wisdom</b></a> for his ability to detail a brief teaching in the same way the Buddha himself would have? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mind may wander to past <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> much more frequently than it does to present or future ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, before his enlightenment, realized that past sensual pleasures are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>niruddhā</b></a>, meaning they have already ceased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the five chords of sensual pleasure as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>vipariṇatā</b></a>, which means they have undergone change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his monks to maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> as a way to protect the mind from being pulled toward past sensual experiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the five chords of sensual pleasure as things that had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>previously contacted</b></a> by the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda used a simile about a man <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>seeking heartwood</b></a> to illustrate how monks should ideally go directly to the Buddha for the highest meanings of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, even as a Bodhisatta, he had to guard his mind against the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato"><b>influence</b></a> of past sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.117/en/sujato">SN 35.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a story about a tortoise that, upon seeing a jackal, withdrew its head and four limbs into its shell to remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato"><b>motionless and silent</b></a> so that the predator could not find an opening to devour it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato">SN 35.240</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared Mara, the Evil One, to a jackal waiting by a riverbank, noting that Mara is constantly watching for an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato"><b>opportunity for access</b></a> through the six sense doors of a person, such as the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, or mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato">SN 35.240</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers to dwell as one with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato"><b>guarded doors</b></a> in the sense faculties, explaining that if they do not provide an opening through their senses, Mara will eventually become disappointed and leave, just as the jackal left the tortoise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato">SN 35.240</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when a person perceives an object with the eye, they should not grasp at its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato"><b>signs or features</b></a>, because if they leave the eye faculty unguarded, evil unwholesome states like covetousness and grief might overwhelm them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato">SN 35.240</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the same practice of restraint used for the physical senses—like sight and sound—must also be applied to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato"><b>mind faculty</b></a>, ensuring that one does not become captivated by mental phenomena and thus remains protected from unwholesome influences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato">SN 35.240</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the successful practitioner as a monk who, like a tortoise withdrawing into its shell, pulls his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato"><b>mental thoughts</b></a> inward, remaining independent and causing no harm to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato">SN 35.240</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato"><b>quenched</b></a> (parinibbuto) to describe the state of a monk who has successfully withdrawn from external distractions and mental proliferations, reaching a state where he no longer reviles anyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato">SN 35.240</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that Mara is "constantly and localizingly" waiting for a person to fail in their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato"><b>sense restraint</b></a>, hoping to find an entry point through the tongue's taste, the body's touch, or the ear's hearing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato">SN 35.240</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the primary reason to practice restraint of the sense faculties is to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato"><b>thwart Mara</b></a>, the Evil One, who loses his footing and his ability to influence a practitioner when their "doors" are securely guarded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato">SN 35.240</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the process of guarding the sense doors to a tortoise that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato"><b>carefree and silent</b></a> within its own shell, illustrating that true safety comes from internal mental discipline rather than external conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.240/en/sujato">SN 35.240</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>large log</b></a> floating down the Ganges River to illustrate the journey a practitioner takes toward Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a log will reach the ocean if it does not get stuck on either bank, a monk will slant toward Nibbana if he avoids <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>spiritual obstacles</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>near bank</b></a> in his river metaphor as a designation for the six internal sense bases, which a practitioner must move beyond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>far bank</b></a> in his teaching as a designation for the six external sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>sinking in the middle</b></a> of the river of existence is a metaphor for a practitioner being overcome by delight and lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>stranded on high ground</b></a> as a designation for the conceit "I am" (asmimāna), which prevents a person from progressing toward enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>seized by humans</b></a> occurs when a monk lives in close association with laypeople, becoming entangled in their joys, sorrows, and personal affairs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>seized by non-humans</b></a> as the act of living the holy life specifically for the purpose of being reborn in a heavenly deva host? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>caught in an eddy</b></a> to describe a practitioner who is trapped by the five cords of sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>rotten within</b></a> if they are immoral, of evil character, and only pretending to be a true practitioner of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>Right View</b></a> is the specific quality that causes a person's life to slant, slope, and incline toward Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying at Kosambi on the bank of the Ganges, a cowherd named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>Nanda</b></a> listened to the Buddha's teaching and immediately requested to be ordained as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha required the cowherd Nanda to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>return the cows</b></a> to their owners before allowing him to join the monastic order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the cowherd Nanda initially argued that the cows would return to their owners by themselves because they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>attached to their calves</b></a>, though the Buddha still insisted he fulfill his responsibility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after practicing in solitude and diligence, the former cowherd Nanda eventually attained the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>nibbānaninnā</b></a> to describe how a monk's path naturally flows toward the cessation of suffering when obstacles are removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "sham" monk who is inwardly corrupt as being like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>rubbish</b></a> (kasambujāto) that has no place in the true community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as the current of the Ganges flows to the sea, a practitioner's mind will reach Nibbana if it is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>diverted or corrupted</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that sharing both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>joys and sorrows</b></a> with laypeople can be a spiritual trap that prevents a monk from reaching his ultimate goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s metaphor highlights that Nibbana is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato"><b>natural destination</b></a> for those who maintain pure conduct and avoid mental entanglements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.241/en/sujato">SN 35.241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha was staying among the Sakyans at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>Kapilavatthu</b></a> in Nigrodha’s Park, the local Sakyans requested that he be the first to use their newly built assembly hall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Sakyans of Kapilavatthu specifically asked the Buddha to use their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>new assembly hall</b></a> before any other ascetic, brahmin, or human, believing his use of it would lead to their long-term welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha accepted the Sakyans' invitation to inaugurate their hall by remaining silent, which was his traditional way of giving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>consent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before the Buddha arrived at the new assembly hall, the Sakyans prepared it by spreading mats, setting out seats, establishing a water jar, and lighting an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>oil lamp</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon entering the new hall, the Buddha washed his feet and sat down against the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>middle pillar</b></a>, facing the east? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spent much of the night instructing, urging, and encouraging the Sakyans with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma talk</b></a> before finally dismissing them as the night grew late? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the Sakyans departed, the Buddha mentioned to Ven. Mahāmoggallāna that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>back pained him</b></a> and that he needed to stretch it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha requested Ven. Mahāmoggallāna to give a Dhamma talk to the monks because the Buddha himself was tired and needed to lie down in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>lion’s posture</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna addressed the monks with a teaching he called the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>exposition on the corrupt</b></a> and the uncorrupt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna defined a "corrupt" person as someone who, upon seeing a form with the eye, becomes obsessed with pleasing forms or struck by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>displeasing forms</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Mahāmoggallāna, a person is considered corrupt if they live without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> and possess a restricted mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna taught that a person with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>restricted mind</b></a> (parittacetaso) does not truly understand the liberation of mind or liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna warned that if a monk's mind is not properly guarded, Mara will find an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>opportunity for access</b></a> through any of the six sense doors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna compared a corrupt person to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>dry reed hut</b></a> that easily catches fire no matter which side a person approaches with a torch? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna explained that a practitioner is "corrupt" in regards to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>six sense bases</b></a> if they allow external objects to dominate their internal state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna described a corrupt monk as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>overcome by forms</b></a> rather than one who overcomes forms himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna taught that unwholesome states are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>leading to renewed existence</b></a> and are productive of future suffering, birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna defined an "uncorrupt" person as one who does not become obsessed with pleasing forms or resentful toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>displeasing forms</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna stated that an uncorrupt monk dwells with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable mind</b></a> (appamāṇacetaso) because his mindfulness of the body is firmly established? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Mahāmoggallāna, an uncorrupt monk truly understands the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom where unwholesome states cease without remainder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>Mara</b></a> cannot find an entry point or an object of support in a monk who dwells with his sense faculties well-guarded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna used the simile of a hall with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>thick clay plaster</b></a> to describe an uncorrupt mind that cannot be ignited by the "fire" of Mara’s temptations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna taught that an uncorrupt practitioner has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>vanquished</b></a> evil, unwholesome states that are defiling and lead to future suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Mahāmoggallāna emphasized that by becoming uncorrupt, a monk overcomes the states that lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>birth, aging, and death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Ven. Mahāmoggallāna finished his exposition, the Buddha got up and praised him, saying "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato"><b>well said</b></a>" (sādhu sādhu) for his teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.243/en/sujato">SN 35.243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk truly understands the nature of existence when he perceives the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>origin and passing away</b></a> of all things that constitute suffering, specifically the five aggregates of form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the danger of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> to a charcoal pit deeper than a man's height, filled with glowing coals that are free from flames and smoke, illustrating how a wise practitioner views them as a source of deadly pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a simile of a man being dragged toward a pit of fire by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>two strong men</b></a> to describe how a person would struggle and twist their body to escape, much like a monk must struggle to avoid being consumed by sensual desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk’s careful movement through the world as being like a man entering a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>forest of thorns</b></a>, where he must remain constantly mindful of every step—front, back, left, and right—to avoid being pricked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Noble One’s Discipline, the Buddha specifically defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>thorn</b></a> as anything in the world that is agreeable and pleasurable, because such things can "prick" and entangle a person’s mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>non-restraint</b></a> occurs when a monk sees a form and becomes obsessed with it if it is agreeable, or resentful toward it if it is disagreeable, thereby losing his mental balance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a person who lacks mindfulness as having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>restricted mind</b></a> (parittacetaso), which prevents them from understanding the true liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>restraint</b></a> (saṃvara) is achieved when a monk remains neither obsessed by pleasing sights nor struck by displeasing ones, maintaining equanimity through all six sense doors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a practitioner who maintains established <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> (kāyagatāsati) dwells with an immeasurable mind, making it impossible for unwholesome states to remain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable mind</b></a> (appamāṇacetaso) as the result of a monk successfully guarding his senses and understanding the liberation by wisdom where evil, unwholesome states cease without remainder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a diligent monk happens to experience a lapse in mindfulness that leads to unwholesome thoughts, he will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>quickly abandon</b></a> and dispel those thoughts immediately rather than letting them linger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the speed with which a mindful monk destroys unwholesome thoughts to water droplets falling onto a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>red-hot iron cauldron</b></a>, where the water vanishes and dries up instantly upon contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is firm in his practice cannot be enticed back to a lay life by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>kings or ministers</b></a> offering him wealth and sensual enjoyments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>Ganges River</b></a>, which flows, slants, and inclines toward the east, to illustrate that a mind slanting toward seclusion cannot be forced to flow in the opposite direction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that trying to force a devoted monk to return to the lay life is as futile as a crowd trying to make the Ganges flow <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>toward the west</b></a> using only shovels and baskets? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a monk's mind has long been slanting toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> (vivekaninna), it is impossible for him to return to the "low life" of worldly possessions and desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> (form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness) as the essential "things that constitute suffering" which must be understood in terms of their origin and cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who sees the danger in sensual pleasures experiences no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>lust or fever</b></a> for them, even when he is observing or experiencing agreeable objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that living without mindfulness of the body leads to a state where a practitioner does not know <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> (cetovimutti) as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>hīnāyāvattitvā</b></a> to refer to the "returning to the low life," which happens when someone gives up the monastic training to pursue worldly wealth and merit-making as a layperson? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>Noble One’s Discipline</b></a> as a training where one learns to identify and avoid the "thorns" of agreeable and pleasurable mental objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome states</b></a> like covetousness and displeasure (abhijjhādomanassa) will not follow or overwhelm a monk who is mindful and restrained in his conduct and resort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who has truly developed his mind is so committed to the path that even if his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato"><b>relatives or friends</b></a> try to persuade him to leave the monkhood, he will remain steadfast in his training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.244/en/sujato">SN 35.244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying at Vesālī in the Great Wood, he was visited by the Venerable Anurādha who sought clarification on how to correctly describe the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a> to wandering ascetics of other sects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that wandering ascetics once questioned the Venerable Anurādha on whether a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>supreme person</b></a>, the highest of persons, is described by the Buddha as existing after death, not existing, both, or neither? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Venerable Anurādha told wandering ascetics that a Tathāgata is described apart from the four traditional positions regarding life after death, the ascetics dismissed him as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>foolish elder</b></a> or a novice who was ignorant and incompetent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the inquiry of the Venerable Anurādha to emphasize that he only teaches <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> and the cessation of suffering, rather than speculative views about the state of a Tathāgata after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked the Venerable Anurādha whether form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness are permanent or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a> to help him understand the nature of the self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, anything that is impermanent is ultimately <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> and subject to change, and therefore should not be regarded as "mine," "I," or "my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should view all form, whether past, future, or present, internal or external, as "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>not mine</b></a>" through the application of true wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a learned noble disciple who sees the five aggregates as they really are becomes disenchanted with form and develops <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a>, which leads to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a practitioner's mind is liberated through dispassion, the Buddha states they possess the knowledge that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a>" and there is no further state of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned Anurādha on whether he regarded the Tathāgata as being identical to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>, to which Anurādha correctly replied "No, venerable sir"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that the Tathāgata is not to be found within feeling, perception, volitional formations, or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the Tathāgata is not to be regarded as existing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>apart from</b></a> the five aggregates (form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Tathāgata cannot be identified as the combination of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> (form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Anurādha if he regarded the Tathāgata as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>without form</b></a>, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness, which Anurādha also denied? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that since the Tathāgata is not apprehended as a truth or reality even in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>present life</b></a>, it is improper to describe him using the four speculative positions regarding death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Anurādha that the Tathāgata is not truly findable or traceable as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>self-entity</b></a> within the five aggregates even while he is still alive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha affirmed that he only proclaims <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>cessation</b></a> of suffering, both in the past and in the present, as the core of his teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anurādha was motivated to visit the Buddha because he wanted to answer the questions of wandering ascetics in a way that was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>consistent</b></a> with the Buddha's actual teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly warned against <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>misrepresenting</b></a> him with what is groundless or false when answering the philosophical queries of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the term "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>highest attainment</b></a>" was used by wandering ascetics to describe the spiritual status of a Tathāgata during their debate with Anurādha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that viewing any of the five aggregates as "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>this is my self</b></a>" is an incorrect way of seeing according to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a Tathāgata as the "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>highest person</b></a>" (paramapuriso) who has reached the pinnacle of spiritual achievement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato"><b>disenchantment</b></a> (nibbidā) with the five aggregates is a necessary step before one can achieve liberation from the cycle of birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.2/en/sujato">SN 44.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta once approached the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika at Bārāṇasī in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>Deer Park at Isipatana</b></a> to discuss why the Buddha left certain philosophical questions about the Tathāgata undeclared? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika explained that the question of whether a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata exists after death</b></a> is one of the four positions that the Buddha left as undeclared (<i>abyākata</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika, a person who delights in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> and does not know its cessation as it really is will hold speculative views about the state of a Tathāgata after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika taught that speculative views about the Tathāgata arise in those who are fascinated by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> and fail to see its cessation with true wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika identified enchantment with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> as a reason why people speculate whether a Tathāgata both exists and does not exist after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika explained that those who do not see the cessation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> are prone to the view that a Tathāgata neither exists nor does not exist after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika stated that being fascinated by and delighting in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> leads one to hold speculative views about the Tathāgata's existence after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika told Sāriputta that for one who knows and sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>cessation of form</b></a> as it really is, speculative views regarding the Tathāgata do not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika clarified that views about the Tathāgata's post-mortem state are absent in those who do not find joy in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> and understand its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika explained that one who sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception</b></a> as it really is will no longer formulate the view that a Tathāgata exists after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika taught that wisdom regarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>cessation of volitional formations</b></a> prevents the mind from dwelling on the four speculative positions about the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika, a practitioner who sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>cessation of consciousness</b></a> does not engage in speculations about whether a Tathāgata neither exists nor does not exist after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika provided a further explanation that speculative views arise for those who delight in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a> (<i>bhava</i>) and are ignorant of its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika explained that the Buddha's silence on the state of the Tathāgata after death is linked to whether a person still finds joy in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> (<i>upādāna</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika taught that delight in and fascination with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> (<i>taṇhā</i>) is a fundamental cause for the arising of speculative views about the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika stated that for one who sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>cessation of existence</b></a> as it really is, the speculative view that a Tathāgata both exists and does not exist after death does not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika pointed out that knowledge of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>cessation of clinging</b></a> removes the basis for wondering if a Tathāgata does not exist after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika explained that by knowing and seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>cessation of craving</b></a>, a person is freed from the need to declare the Tathāgata's post-mortem state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika taught that when craving is destroyed, there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato"><b>cycle of existence</b></a> (<i>vaṭṭa</i>) remaining to designate or describe the state of such a liberated being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.6/en/sujato">SN 44.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wanderer Vacchagotta once visited the Buddha to discuss a debate he overheard in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>Debating Hall</b></a> regarding the various religious teachers of the time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vacchagotta observed that famous teachers like Pūraṇa Kassapa and Makkhali Gosāla would always declare the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>rebirth destination</b></a> of their deceased disciples, regardless of their level of attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Vacchagotta, the Buddha was unique because he would declare the rebirth of ordinary disciples but would not declare a destination for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>supreme person</b></a> who had reached the highest attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a disciple who is a "supreme person," he simply declares that they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>cut off craving</b></a>, unwound the fetters, and made an end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Vacchagotta that he only proclaimes rebirth for those with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> (or sustenance), and not for those who are without clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>burning fire</b></a> to explain rebirth, pointing out that fire burns only as long as it has fuel and not when it is without fuel? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Vacchagotta asked about a flame blown a long distance by the wind, the Buddha clarified that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>wind</b></a> itself serves as the fuel or sustenance for that flame in that moment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> as the fuel or sustenance for a being who has laid down one body but has not yet been reborn in another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wanderer Vacchagotta described the Buddha and other contemporary teachers as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>heads of orders</b></a>, famous founders of sects, and highly regarded by the public? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha validated Vacchagotta's confusion, stating it was appropriate to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>perplexed</b></a> when considering deep matters like the cessation of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the liberation of his highest disciples as being achieved through the correct <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>breaking through of conceit</b></a>, specifically the "I am" notion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vacchagotta mentioned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>Ajita Kesakambala</b></a> as one of the six famous teachers who claimed to know the future rebirths of their followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>sustenance</b></a>—meaning fuel or clinging—the process of rebirth simply cannot occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the teacher <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta</b></a> was listed by Vacchagotta as one of the influential leaders who provided specific details on where their disciples were reborn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the state of an Arahant as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>unwound the fetter</b></a>, which means they are no longer bound to any form of future existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>highest person</b></a> (<i>paramapuriso</i>) to describe those disciples for whom rebirth is no longer applicable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vacchagotta specifically questioned the Buddha on what serves as the "fuel" for a person in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>interval</b></a> between death and a new rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>taṇhūpādāna</b></a>, or "craving as sustenance," is what carries a being into a new life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vacchagotta sought to understand how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a> should be known when the Buddha's declarations about his disciples differed so much from other teachers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that just as fire is sustained by wood, the continuation of life is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato"><b>sustained by clinging</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn44.9/en/sujato">SN 44.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once spent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato"><b>three months in solitary retreat</b></a> at Sāvatthī, instructing his monks that no one should visit him except for the single person who brought him his almsfood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato">SN 45.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after emerging from a three-month retreat, the Buddha told his monks that he had been dwelling in the same state of mind he occupied when he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato"><b>first enlightened</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato">SN 45.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> (<i>vedayita</i>) can be experienced as a result of either wrong view or the subsiding of wrong view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato">SN 45.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>, as well as the subsiding of right view, can both serve as conditions for the arising of feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato">SN 45.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that feeling can arise based on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato"><b>wrong concentration</b></a> or its subsiding, just as it can arise from right concentration or its subsiding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato">SN 45.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato"><b>desire</b></a> (<i>chanda</i>), thought, and perception as specific conditions that lead to the experience of feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato">SN 45.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that even when desire, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato"><b>thought</b></a>, and perception have subsided, that very state of subsiding also serves as a condition for feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato">SN 45.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there is a specific type of feeling conditioned by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato"><b>striving for the un-attained</b></a>, which occurs when a practitioner has not yet reached their goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.12/en/sujato">SN 45.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the eight factors of the Noble Eightfold Path as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.16/en/sujato"><b>pure and radiant</b></a>, free from blemishes, and devoid of corruptions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.16/en/sujato">SN 45.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the eight factors of the Noble Eightfold Path only arise in the world when a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.16/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a>, an Arahant, a Perfectly Enlightened One, makes his appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.16/en/sujato">SN 45.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.27/en/sujato"><b>pot</b></a> to explain that just as a pot without a support is easily overturned, a mind without a support is also easily unsettled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.27/en/sujato">SN 45.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the Noble Eightfold Path as the essential <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.27/en/sujato"><b>support for the mind</b></a> that prevents it from being easily disturbed or "overturned" by worldly conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.27/en/sujato">SN 45.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a mind lacking the practice of the Noble Eightfold Path to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.27/en/sujato"><b>unsupported vessel</b></a>, which is prone to being tipped over and losing its stability? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.27/en/sujato">SN 45.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind provided with its proper support—namely, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.27/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>—is difficult to overturn and remains steady? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.27/en/sujato">SN 45.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five distinct spiritual faculties related to feeling, which are the faculty of pleasure, the faculty of pain, the faculty of joy, the faculty of displeasure, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>faculty of equanimity</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the faculty of pleasure arises specifically dependent on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>contact to be felt as pleasant</b></a>, and without that contact, the pleasure cannot emerge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person experiences pleasure, they possess the self-awareness to recognize and understand, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>"I am happy"</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>rubbing two sticks together</b></a> to generate heat to illustrate how sensory contact produces the various faculties of feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that just as heat is quenched when two sticks are separated, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>faculty of pain</b></a> ceases and subsides as soon as the specific contact that caused it is removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>faculty of joy</b></a> as something that arises dependent on a contact to be felt as joyful, leading a person to understand that they are in a state of gladness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>faculty of displeasure</b></a> arises from a contact to be felt as unpleasant, and that once that contact ceases, the displeasure itself is extinguished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the faculty of equanimity arises dependent on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>neutral contact</b></a> (one to be felt as neither-painful-nor-pleasant), which the person then recognizes as being equanimous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a specific feeling arises from a corresponding contact, that feeling is considered to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>subside</b></a> only when that underlying contact has ceased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a person in pain understands their immediate experience by internally acknowledging, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>"I am pained"</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>faculty of pleasure</b></a> is not permanent, as it must cease and vanish once the pleasant contact that conditioned it is no longer present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>joy and displeasure</b></a> as two of the five specific faculties of feeling that are entirely dependent on sensory contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>contact to be felt as joyful</b></a> ceases, any feeling that arose from it—specifically the faculty of joy—immediately comes to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>subsiding</b></a> (<i>vūpasammati</i>) to describe the natural resolution and fading away of the faculty of equanimity when its causal contact disappears? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato"><b>faculty of pain</b></a> arises only when there is a contact to be felt as painful, and that the person experiencing it is fully aware of being in a state of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.39/en/sujato">SN 48.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Uṇṇābha observed to the Buddha that the five sense faculties—the eye, ear, nose, tongue, and body—each have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato"><b>different objective domains</b></a> and do not experience each other's specific range? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato">SN 48.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the Brahmin Uṇṇābha that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> is the common resort for the five physical sense faculties and is the faculty that experiences all their diverse objective domains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato">SN 48.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Brahmin Uṇṇābha asked about the foundation for the mental faculty, the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> as the specific resort of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato">SN 48.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the Brahmin Uṇṇābha that the ultimate resort of mindfulness is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato">SN 48.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified for the Brahmin Uṇṇābha that the resort of liberation is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato">SN 48.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the Brahmin Uṇṇābha that he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato"><b>gone beyond the range</b></a> of a question when the Brahmin tried to ask what the resort of Nibbāna itself was? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato">SN 48.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the spiritual or "holy" life as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato"><b>immersed in Nibbāna</b></a>, having Nibbāna as its ground, its destination, and its final goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato">SN 48.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the Brahmin Uṇṇābha departed, the Buddha compared the Brahmin's unwavering faith to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato"><b>sunbeam</b></a> entering through a window and resting firmly on the western wall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato">SN 48.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> of the Brahmin Uṇṇābha to be so firmly rooted and established that it could not be uprooted by any ascetic, brahmin, deva, Mara, or Brahma in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato">SN 48.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed his monks that if the Brahmin Uṇṇābha were to pass away at that time, he would be a non-returner, as there was no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato"><b>fetter</b></a> remaining that could bring him back to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato">SN 48.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the five sense faculties, such as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato"><b>eye faculty</b></a> and the ear faculty, do not experience each other's objective domains but instead converge in the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato">SN 48.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the holy life concludes in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a>, which serves as its final termination and ultimate purpose? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.42/en/sujato">SN 48.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once resided at Rājagaha on the mountain called Vulture Peak in a specific location known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato"><b>Boar's Cave</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato">SN 48.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained to the Buddha that a bhikkhu whose taints are destroyed shows supreme deference to the Teaching because he sees <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed security from bondage</b></a> within it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato">SN 48.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato"><b>faculty of faith</b></a> as a primary way a monk achieves the unsurpassed security from bondage that leads to peace and enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato">SN 48.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, a monk whose taints are destroyed attains unsurpassed security by specifically developing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato"><b>faculty of wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato">SN 48.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta defined the "supreme deference" shown by an arahant as living with deep <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato"><b>reverence and respect for the Teacher</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato">SN 48.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha heard from Venerable Sāriputta that a monk who has destroyed the taints maintains a state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato"><b>reverent toward the Dhamma</b></a> as a core expression of supreme deference? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato">SN 48.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that showing supreme deference to the Buddha’s dispensation involves living with reverence and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato"><b>respect toward the Saṅgha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato">SN 48.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta listed being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato"><b>respectful toward the training</b></a> as one of the five essential ways a bhikkhu with destroyed taints demonstrates supreme deference? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato">SN 48.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that a monk who has attained the end of taints lives with profound reverence and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato"><b>respect toward concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato">SN 48.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha fully endorsed Venerable Sāriputta’s explanation that the spiritual faculties—including energy and mindfulness—are what lead a bhikkhu toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato"><b>peace and enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn48.58/en/sujato">SN 48.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his enlightenment, while still a Bodhisatta, the Buddha investigated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>cause and condition</b></a> for developing the bases for spiritual power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk develops the basis for spiritual power consisting of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>concentration due to desire</b></a> and volitional formations of striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for desire to lead to spiritual power, it must not be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>too lax</b></a>, too tense, constricted internally, or distracted externally? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the basis for spiritual power involving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>concentration due to energy</b></a> as a state where one's effort and volitional formations of striving are perfectly balanced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when developing the basis for spiritual power consisting of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>concentration due to mind</b></a>, the mind must remain unenveloped and open? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the basis for spiritual power consisting of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>concentration due to investigation</b></a> involves volitional formations of striving that are neither too tense nor too lax? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised practitioners to develop a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>luminous mind</b></a> by maintaining an open and unenveloped awareness while cultivating spiritual powers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a practice called being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>"percipient of before and after"</b></a>, where the practitioner views the past and future with the same quality of awareness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that one should develop the mind so that it is the same <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>by day and by night</b></a>, ensuring a consistent and unbroken level of concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by frequently developing the four bases for spiritual power, one can experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>manifold spiritual power</b></a>, such as appearing as many from being one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a practitioner who has mastered the bases for spiritual power can pass through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>walls and mountains</b></a> without being obstructed, as if moving through open space? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who develops these spiritual bases can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>walk on water</b></a> without sinking, just as if they were walking on solid ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that a person with developed spiritual power can even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>touch the sun and moon</b></a> with their hand because of their great might and majesty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>divine ear element</b></a>, which is purified and surpasses the human, one can hear both divine and human sounds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by perfecting the bases for spiritual power, one can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>know the minds</b></a> of other beings, distinguishing between a mind with lust and one without lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that through mental penetration, a practitioner can recognize whether another person’s mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>concentrated or distracted</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>recollect past lives</b></a> in great detail, including names, social standing, and experiences of pleasure and pain across many eons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a>, one can see beings passing away and being reborn according to their kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that beings who engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>bodily misconduct</b></a> and revile the noble ones are reborn after death in states of misery or hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that beings with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> who perform good deeds are reborn in a happy destination, even in the heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ultimate result of developing the four bases for spiritual power is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> as being realized through direct knowledge after the taints have been destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk who masters the four bases for spiritual power can exercise mastery with the body even as far as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato"><b>Brahmā world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.11/en/sujato">SN 51.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered the <i>Anattalakkhaṇasutta</i> to the group of five monks while staying at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>Deer Park</b></a> in Benares? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that form is nonself because if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> were truly the self, it would not lead to affliction and one could command it to be exactly how they wished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> is nonself because it is subject to affliction and does not obey a person's desire to remain in a specific state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> is nonself, as it inevitably leads to suffering and cannot be willed to change at one's command? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> are nonself, since they lead to affliction and do not allow a person to say, "Let my volitional formations be thus"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is nonself because it is subject to change and affliction, rather than being under one's absolute control? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked the group of five monks to determine if form was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>permanent or impermanent</b></a>, to which they correctly identified it as impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that anything which is impermanent is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is not fit to regard that which is impermanent and subject to change as "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>this is my self</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that every kind of form—whether past, future, or present—should be seen with correct wisdom as "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>this is not mine</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that all consciousness, whether <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>internal or external</b></a>, must be viewed as "This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a learned noble disciple who sees the five aggregates clearly becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that through disenchantment, a disciple becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>dispassionate</b></a>, and through dispassion, the mind is liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the mind is liberated, the knowledge arises: "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a>, the holy life has been lived, what had to be done has been done"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that once a disciple is liberated, they understand there is "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>no more for this state of being</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha required that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>gross or subtle</b></a> feelings must be viewed with correct wisdom as not being one's self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that perceptions, whether <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>far or near</b></a>, are to be regarded as "netaṃ mama" (this is not mine)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's explanation of the nonself characteristic, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>group of five monks</b></a> were delighted and rejoiced in his words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's Anattalakkhaṇa discourse was so effective that while it was being spoken, the minds of the five monks were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>liberated from the taints</b></a> through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a method of analytical questioning to show that no part of our <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>physical or mental</b></a> experience can be considered a permanent self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato"><b>correct wisdom</b></a> consists of seeing all five aggregates as they really are: "This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.59/en/sujato">SN 22.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha directly challenged the view of Pūraṇa Kassapa, who claimed that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>no cause</b></a> or condition for the defilement or purification of beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings become defiled because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> is not exclusively suffering but contains pleasure, leading beings to become infatuated with it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> were entirely painful and devoid of pleasure, beings would never become enamored with it or suffer defilement because of it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the reason beings become bound and defiled is that they find a form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>pleasure</b></a> in perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that beings are purified through a specific cause, namely becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with the five aggregates after recognizing the suffering inherent in them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> were exclusively pleasurable and free from suffering, beings would never seek liberation or become purified from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the cause for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>defilement</b></a> as the process where beings become infatuated with the aggregates, which leads to fetters and subsequent corruption? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is a cause for defilement because it is not solely suffering but is "steeped in pleasure," causing beings to cling to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that purification occurs because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> is ultimately suffering, which eventually leads a practitioner to experience disenchantment and dispassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that beings are purified when they become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>dispassionate</b></a>, a state that arises only because the five aggregates are subject to suffering and change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>infatuated</b></a> (sārajjanti) to describe the primary psychological reaction to the pleasure found in the aggregates that prevents spiritual purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that if the aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> were "exclusively suffering," it would be impossible for beings to ever become attached to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that beings undergo <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>purification</b></a> precisely because the five aggregates are not "exclusively pleasurable" and are "steeped in suffering"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the existence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>fetters</b></a> (saṃyoga) as the link between a being's infatuation with the aggregates and their resulting defilement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that there is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>condition</b></a> for purification: when one experiences suffering in the aggregates, they reach a state of dispassion and are thereby cleansed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> is possible only because the aggregates are not exclusively pleasurable, allowing for the arising of disenchantment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the phrase "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>steeped in pleasure</b></a>" to explain why beings naturally gravitate toward and become stuck in the cycle of form, feeling, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha showed how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>disenchantment</b></a> (nibbindanti) acts as the bridge between recognizing the suffering in consciousness and attaining final purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato"><b>purification</b></a> is a causal process, where the realization of suffering leads to dispassion, which in turn leads to the cleansing of the being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.60/en/sujato">SN 22.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.61/en/sujato"><b>form is burning</b></a>, along with the other four aggregates of feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.61/en/sujato">SN 22.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a learned noble disciple who sees the five aggregates as burning becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.61/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.61/en/sujato">SN 22.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that once a disciple becomes disenchanted with the aggregates, they become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.61/en/sujato"><b>dispassionate</b></a>, and through that dispassion, the mind is liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.61/en/sujato">SN 22.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the mind is liberated, the knowledge arises that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.61/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a>" and the holy life has been fully lived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.61/en/sujato">SN 22.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a liberated disciple understands there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.61/en/sujato"><b>no more for this state of being</b></a> after the current life is completed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.61/en/sujato">SN 22.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.61/en/sujato"><b>consciousness is burning</b></a>, just like the physical body and mental feelings, and that recognizing this is key to the path of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.61/en/sujato">SN 22.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a certain monk that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.63/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> to the aggregates, one remains bound by Mara, the personification of death and the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.63/en/sujato">SN 22.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.63/en/sujato"><b>not clinging</b></a> to the five aggregates, a person is freed from the power of the "Evil One" (Mara)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.63/en/sujato">SN 22.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.63/en/sujato"><b>clinging to form</b></a> is effectively enslaved by the conditions of suffering and the cycle of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.63/en/sujato">SN 22.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha approved of the explanation that not clinging to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.63/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a>, perception, or consciousness leads to final freedom from the influence of Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.63/en/sujato">SN 22.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who dwells alone and remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.63/en/sujato"><b>diligent and ardent</b></a> can realize the supreme goal of the holy life in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.63/en/sujato">SN 22.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's instruction on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.63/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> highlights that grasping at our mental intentions and activities is a primary way we remain bound to Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.63/en/sujato">SN 22.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a monk that one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.64/en/sujato"><b>conceiving</b></a> (maññamāno) is bound by Mara, whereas one who does not conceive is free from the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.64/en/sujato">SN 22.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.64/en/sujato"><b>not conceiving form</b></a>, a person becomes liberated from the power of Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.64/en/sujato">SN 22.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.64/en/sujato"><b>not conceiving feeling</b></a>, perception, volitional formations, or consciousness is released from the "Evil One"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.64/en/sujato">SN 22.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a certain monk that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.64/en/sujato"><b>conceiving consciousness</b></a> as belonging to oneself or as a self results in being bound by Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.64/en/sujato">SN 22.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's brief teaching on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.64/en/sujato"><b>not conceiving</b></a> the five aggregates led a monk to practice diligently until he eventually became an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.64/en/sujato">SN 22.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was asked by the Venerable Radha how to eliminate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.71/en/sujato"><b>I-making and mine-making</b></a> (ahaṅkāramamaṅkāra) regarding both the conscious body and external signs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.71/en/sujato">SN 22.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.71/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to conceit</b></a> (mānānusaya) is removed when one sees all five aggregates with correct wisdom as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.71/en/sujato">SN 22.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Radha that any kind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.71/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>—whether past, future, present, internal, or external—should be seen as "This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.71/en/sujato">SN 22.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.71/en/sujato"><b>gross or subtle</b></a> feelings, perceptions, and volitional formations must be viewed as not-self to end the arising of conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.71/en/sujato">SN 22.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that seeing all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.71/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a>, whether far or near, as "not mine" and "not my self" is the path to overcoming I-making? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.71/en/sujato">SN 22.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s explanation on the nature of the aggregates to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.71/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Radha</b></a> led him to realize the supreme goal of the holy life and become one of the Arahants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.71/en/sujato">SN 22.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato"><b>uninstructed worldling</b></a> remains in suffering because they do not understand the origin and passing away of form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato">SN 22.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato"><b>instructed noble disciple</b></a> by their ability to see the origin and passing away of the five aggregates as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato">SN 22.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that knowing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato"><b>gratification</b></a> (assāda) of the five aggregates is necessary to understand why beings are attracted to and infatuated with them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato">SN 22.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> (ādīnava) in the five aggregates as they really are is a vital characteristic of a noble disciple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato">SN 22.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> (nissaraṇa) from the five aggregates as a critical piece of knowledge for reaching final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato">SN 22.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that correctly knowing the origin, passing away, gratification, danger, and escape regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> is essential for spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato">SN 22.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that understanding the five factors—origin, passing away, gratification, danger, and escape—regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> is what truly defines a knowledgeable disciple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.74/en/sujato">SN 22.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that a monk who fails to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>guard the doors of the sense faculties</b></a>, is not moderate in eating, and is not devoted to wakefulness is prone to giving up the training and returning to the "lower life" of a layperson? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Sāriputta, it is impossible for a monk to maintain a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>perfectly pure holy life</b></a> for as long as he lives if he lacks sense restraint, moderation in food, and devotion to wakefulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta defined guarding the sense faculties as seeing a form with the eye but not grasping at its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>signs and features</b></a>, which prevents evil unwholesome states like covetousness and displeasure from entering the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that a monk practices restraint of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>mind faculty</b></a> by not grasping at signs or features when cognizing a mental phenomenon, ensuring that unwholesome states do not take hold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>moderate in eating</b></a> involves reflecting wisely that food is taken not for amusement or intoxication, but only for the endurance and maintenance of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta advised that food should be consumed for the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>terminating old feelings</b></a> of hunger while ensuring that new feelings of discomfort from overeating are not created? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta emphasized that a monk should eat primarily for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>support of the holy life</b></a>, rather than for physical beauty, fattening, or vanity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>devotion to wakefulness</b></a> as a daily practice where a monk purifies his mind of obstructive states during the day and the first watch of the night through walking and sitting meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta instructed monks to rest in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>lion’s posture</b></a>—lying on the right side with one foot placed over the other—during the middle watch of the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta taught that even when lying down to sleep, a monk should remain mindful and clearly comprehending, while keeping the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>thought of rising</b></a> firmly in mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta urged practitioners to rise in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>last watch of the night</b></a> to continue purifying the mind from obstructive states through further walking and sitting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta explicitly listed the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>the mind</b></a> as the specific doors through which a monk must practice restraint to prevent the influx of evil unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>obstructive states</b></a> as the mental hindrances that a practitioner must actively purify from their mind during both the day and the watches of the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta concluded his teaching by exhorting the monks that they must specifically train themselves to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>guarded at the sense doors</b></a>, moderate in eating, and devoted to wakefulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta’s teaching on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>wise reflection</b></a> regarding food specifies that a practitioner should eat only to prevent the "harm" of hunger and to maintain health and blamelessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta warned that failing to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato"><b>moderate in eating</b></a> is one of the three primary behavioral failures that leads a monk to abandon his spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.120/en/sujato">SN 35.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally invited his son, the Venerable Rāhula, to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>Andhavana forest</b></a> for the day's abiding because he perceived that Rāhula’s qualities maturing for liberation were ready? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha decided to specifically lead Rāhula to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavānaṃ khaye) after reflecting on Rāhula's spiritual progress during a period of solitary retreat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that thousands of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>deities</b></a> followed the Buddha and Rāhula to the forest, aware that the Buddha was about to give a teaching that would lead Rāhula to final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Rāhula to consider whether the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a> is permanent or impermanent as the starting point for his teaching on the nature of reality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Rāhula correctly answered the Buddha that anything <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a> (anicca) is inherently suffering (dukkha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Rāhula that it is not fit to regard things that are impermanent, suffering, and subject to change as "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>This is mine</b></a>," "This I am," or "This is my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha extended his inquiry beyond the sense organs to include <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>external forms</b></a>, asking Rāhula to see them as impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Rāhula that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>eye-consciousness</b></a> is also impermanent and therefore not a suitable basis for a permanent identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact</b></a> is impermanent, emphasizing that even the meeting of the eye and forms is transient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Rāhula that any feeling, perception, or mental formation that arises <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>conditioned by eye-contact</b></a> is itself impermanent and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha applied the same rigorous analysis of impermanence to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>tongue</b></a> and tastes to help Rāhula let go of sensory attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned Rāhula on whether the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> (mano) is permanent, leading him to realize it is also subject to change and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> (dhammā) as objects of the mind that are impermanent and should not be viewed as "self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>mind-consciousness</b></a> (manoviññāṇa) must be seen with wisdom as impermanent and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that everything arising <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>conditioned by mind-contact</b></a>, including feelings and volitional formations, is subject to the same laws of impermanence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>well-taught noble disciple</b></a> as one who becomes disenchanted with the senses and their objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that disenchantment with the eye, forms, and consciousness leads to a mind that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>becomes disenchanted</b></a> with all internal and external sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s method of teaching Rāhula involved a step-by-step <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>analytical inquiry</b></a> into the six internal and external sense bases to reveal their true nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>subject to change</b></a> (vipariṇāmadhamma) is a key reason why sensory experience cannot be considered a permanent self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a monk who sees the truth of impermanence becomes disenchanted even with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> (viññāṇa) itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.121/en/sujato">SN 35.121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Udena asked the Venerable Piṇḍolabhāradvāja why young monks in the prime of their youth are able to lead a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>perfectly pure holy life</b></a> for their entire lives instead of indulging in sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, according to Venerable Piṇḍolabhāradvāja, suggested that monks should maintain their celibacy by regarding women the age of their mothers as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>mothers</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piṇḍolabhāradvāja explained that a monk can sustain his practice by viewing women of a similar age to himself as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>sisters</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised monks to regard women younger than themselves as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>daughters</b></a> as a method to overcome lustful thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Udena observed that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>mind is fickle</b></a>, noting that lustful states can sometimes arise even toward women who are regarded as mothers, sisters, or daughters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piṇḍolabhāradvāja cited the Buddha’s instruction to contemplate the body from the soles of the feet up to the hair on the head as being full of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>various kinds of impurity</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught monks to reflect on thirty-two parts of the body, including the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>liver, heart, and lungs</b></a>, to help them remain detached from physical attraction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Udena argued that for those with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>undeveloped mind</b></a>, contemplating the body as foul can be difficult because beauty may still appear to them despite their efforts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piṇḍolabhāradvāja shared the Buddha's teaching on being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>guarded at the doors of the sense faculties</b></a> as a primary defense against the arising of unwholesome mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when seeing a form with the eye, one should not grasp at its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>signs and features</b></a> to prevent covetousness and displeasure from flowing into the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piṇḍolabhāradvāja explained that one should practice restraint of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>mind faculty</b></a> when cognizing mental phenomena so that evil unwholesome states do not take hold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Udena admitted that whenever he enters his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>inner palace</b></a> with an unguarded body, speech, and mind, he is intensely overwhelmed by states of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Udena found that when he entered his quarters with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>established mindfulness</b></a> and restrained senses, lustful thoughts did not trouble him in the same way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Udena praised the Buddha’s teaching on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>sense restraint</b></a> as the most effective "reason and cause" for young monks to persevere in their spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piṇḍolabhāradvāja used the phrase "the one who knows and sees" to describe the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>Blessed One</b></a>, the Arahant, the Perfectly Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's method of preventing unwholesome states involves actively <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>guarding the eye faculty</b></a> so that "evil unwholesome states" like greed do not follow the act of seeing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that at the end of his discussion with Venerable Piṇḍolabhāradvāja, King Udena officially went for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato"><b>refuge</b></a> to the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Saṅgha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.127/en/sujato">SN 35.127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna explained to the householder Hāliddikāni that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato"><b>diversity of elements</b></a> (dhātunānatta) is the primary condition that leads to a diversity of contact and subsequently a diversity of feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato">SN 35.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahākaccāna, when a monk sees a form with the eye and recognizes it as agreeable, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato"><b>pleasant feeling</b></a> arises dependent on the resulting eye-contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato">SN 35.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna taught that if a monk perceives a form with the eye as disagreeable, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato"><b>eye-consciousness</b></a> produced is of a nature to be felt as painful, leading to a painful feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato">SN 35.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato"><b>neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling</b></a> arises when a practitioner perceives a sensory object, such as a form, as an occasion for equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato">SN 35.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna clarified that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato"><b>diversity of contact</b></a> (phassanānatta) is the causal link between the initial variety of elements and the variety of feelings an individual experiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato">SN 35.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna was staying at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato"><b>Kuraraghara</b></a> on the Precipice Mountain in Avanti when he delivered this detailed explanation on the origin of feelings to the householder Hāliddikāni? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato">SN 35.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna taught that the same process of diverse feelings arising from contact applies to all senses, including hearing a sound, smelling an odor, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato"><b>tasting a taste</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato">SN 35.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna explained that when the mind cognizes a mental phenomenon as agreeable, it results in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato"><b>mind-consciousness</b></a> that is felt as pleasant, illustrating how the mind functions as a sense base? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato">SN 35.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha originally stated the principle that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato"><b>diversity of feeling</b></a> arises dependent on diversity of contact, a teaching which Venerable Mahākaccāna later elaborated on in detail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato">SN 35.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākaccāna emphasized that a monk must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato"><b>know and understand</b></a> whether a mental phenomenon is agreeable, disagreeable, or an occasion for equanimity to understand how specific feelings are produced in the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.130/en/sujato">SN 35.130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the lord of devas, once visited the Buddha at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato"><b>Vulture’s Peak Rock</b></a> in Rājagaha to ask about the specific cause and reason why some beings do not attain Nibbāna in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato">SN 35.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Sakka that a person fails to attain Nibbāna when they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato"><b>delight in</b></a> and welcome agreeable forms, sounds, and tastes that are connected with sensual desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato">SN 35.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person remains holding to pleasing sensory objects, their consciousness becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato"><b>dependent</b></a> on those objects, leading to the presence of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato">SN 35.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato"><b>having clinging</b></a> (saupādāno) is the primary reason why many beings do not attain final liberation in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato">SN 35.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified certain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> (dhammā) as being agreeable, charming, and provocative of lust, which can bind a practitioner to the cycle of suffering if they are welcomed and held to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato">SN 35.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Sakka that a person attains Nibbāna in this very life if they do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato"><b>remain holding to</b></a> objects that are agreeable, pleasing, and charming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato">SN 35.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a practitioner does not delight in or welcome pleasing sensory experiences, their consciousness <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato"><b>is not dependent</b></a> on them and has no clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato">SN 35.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the path to attaining Nibbāna in this very life as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato"><b>without clinging</b></a> (anupādāno) toward the six types of sensory objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato">SN 35.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly described objects cognized by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato"><b>tongue</b></a> and the other sense bases as being "connected with sensual desire" (kāmūpasaṃhitā) when they are found to be agreeable and pleasing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato">SN 35.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a practitioner who does not delight in sensory objects, the resulting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato"><b>lack of dependency</b></a> in their consciousness is the causal basis for their final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.118/en/sujato">SN 35.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk's vision is considered well purified when he understands, as they really are, the origin and passing away of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>six bases for contact</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that one monk in the sources explained that a bhikkhu’s vision is well purified when he understands the origin and passing away of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates of clinging</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk claimed vision is well purified once a practitioner understands the origin and passing away of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>four great elements</b></a> as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted a monk's teaching that purified vision consists of understanding that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>whatever is subject to origination</b></a> is all subject to cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>kimsuka tree</b></a> to illustrate why different enlightened individuals might describe the path to purified vision in various ways? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who sees a kimsuka tree when it is black and leafless, comparing its appearance to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>charred stump</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a simile told by the Buddha, a person who sees a kimsuka tree when it is blossoming with red flowers might describe it as being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>piece of meat</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that a person seeing a kimsuka tree with its bark stripped and its pods bursting would compare its appearance to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>sirisa tree</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of a kimsuka tree with thick foliage and deep shade to show how it might be compared to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>banyan tree</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the way various "good persons" explain purified vision depends on the specific way their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>vision is well purified</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the human body—which is born of mother and father and sustained by food—to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>frontier city</b></a> with strong foundations and six gates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Buddha's simile of the city, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>six gates</b></a> are a designation for the six internal sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>gatekeeper</b></a> of the frontier city as a designation for mindfulness (sati)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>swift pair of messengers</b></a> arriving at a city as a designation for the dual qualities of serenity (samatha) and insight (vipassana)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>lord of the city</b></a> sitting at the central square as a designation for consciousness (viññāṇa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>central square</b></a> in the middle of the city is a designation for the four great elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>message of reality</b></a> delivered by the messengers to the lord of the city is a designation for Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>path</b></a> by which the messengers of serenity and insight arrive and depart is a designation for the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the human body as being subject to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>breaking up and dispersal</b></a>, even though it is nourished by boiled rice and bread? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>wise gatekeeper</b></a> as one who is smart and keeps out strangers while admitting only those who are known? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha's simile, the messengers deliver their message to the lord and then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>return by the same path</b></a> they came? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the four great elements as consisting specifically of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>earth element</b></a>, the water element, the heat element, and the air element? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the body is an accumulation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>boiled rice and bread</b></a> and is subject to constant rubbing, massage, and eventual destruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the Noble Eightfold Path, starting with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> and ending with right concentration, as the vehicle for delivering the message of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.245/en/sujato">SN 35.245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that if desire, lust, hatred, or delusion arises regarding forms cognizable by the eye, a monk should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>restrain the mind</b></a> from them because such a path is considered fearful, thorny, and used only by "unworthy men"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the pursuit of sensual desires as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>wrong path</b></a> (ummaggo) that is not frequented by "superior men"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>uninstructed worldling</b></a> who lacks restraint over the six bases for contact to an ox that wanders into an unguarded cornfield and becomes intoxicated with the crop? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of an ox being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>beaten with a stick</b></a> by a diligent watchman to explain how a monk can train his mind to no longer return to the allure of the five strings of sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk's mind is no longer drawn toward the six bases for contact, it becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>concentrated internally</b></a>, steady, and well-settled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a story about a king who, upon hearing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>sound of a lute</b></a> for the first time, found it so enthralling and intoxicating that he demanded his ministers bring him the sound itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the sound of a lute does not exist independently, but is produced through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>many components</b></a> including the box, skin, neck, head, strings, plectrum, and human effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in one of the Buddha's similes, a king was so disappointed that he could not find the "sound" within the instrument that he ordered the lute to be broken <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>into a hundred pieces</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a king searching for the source of music by reducing a lute to splinters, burning it to ashes, and then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>winnowing the ashes</b></a> in a strong wind to prove the sound could not be grasped? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the story of the broken lute to illustrate how a person might realize that the instrument is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>mere name</b></a> and that people are generally deluded by it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the king's analytical destruction of the lute to the way a monk should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>investigate form</b></a> (rūpa) as far as its ultimate destination goes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who wishes to find the truth must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>investigate feeling</b></a> (vedanā) as far as the destination of feeling reaches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by thoroughly investigating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> (saññā), a practitioner can see through the illusions of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> (saṅkhārā) must be investigated to the very limit of their existence to gain liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>investigate consciousness</b></a> (viññāṇa) as far as its scope goes to understand its true nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a monk investigates the five aggregates and finds nothing to grasp, the notions of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>I, mine, or I am</b></a> no longer occur to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>floating ashes</b></a> down a swift river to show how the components of a lute—and by extension, the self—cannot be recovered once they are analyzed and dispersed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>unguarded field</b></a> of ripening corn is a metaphor for the lack of restraint over the six sense bases, which leads to spiritual intoxication? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>five strings of sensual pleasure</b></a> as the "crop" that an unrestrained person mindlessly consumes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato"><b>subdued the mind</b></a> will find that it no longer wanders back toward sensual objects, just as a beaten ox stays away from a cornfield? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.246/en/sujato">SN 35.246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who lacks restraint to a man with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>raw and diseased body</b></a> walking through a forest of reeds, where every thorn and leaf that pricks or scratches him causes intense pain and distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described verbal criticism and insults encountered by a monk in a village or forest as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>thorns in the village</b></a>, which can only be managed through the practice of restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>lack of restraint</b></a> occurs when a person sees a pleasant form and becomes attached to it, or sees an unpleasant form and becomes irritated by it, while living with unestablished mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>limited mind</b></a> (parittacetaso) to describe the state of a person who lives without established mindfulness of the body and fails to understand the liberation of mind and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the internal conflict of the senses by comparing them to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>six animals</b></a> of different species and habitats—a snake, crocodile, bird, dog, jackal, and monkey—tied together by a single rope? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Buddha's simile of the six animals tied together, the snake represents a sense faculty that instinctively tries to crawl into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>termite mound</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened one of the sense faculties to a crocodile that, when tied to other animals, constantly tries to pull toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>water</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included a bird in his sensory simile, noting that its natural instinct is to fly up into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>sky</b></a> even while being restrained by its ties to other animals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared one of the sense faculties to a dog that constantly pulls toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>village</b></a> in search of its own familiar domain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a jackal as part of a group of six animals tied together representing the senses, with the jackal constantly trying to enter a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>charnel ground</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a monkey in a simile to represent a sense faculty that persistently tries to pull its companions toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>forest</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when mindfulness of the body is not developed, the senses pull the practitioner in different directions, and they will eventually follow whichever sense faculty is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>most powerful</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>restraint</b></a> (saṃvaro) as the state where a practitioner is not attached to agreeable sense objects and not repelled by disagreeable ones, while maintaining established mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who practices mindfulness of the body lives with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>measureless mind</b></a> (appamāṇacetaso), which allows him to truly understand the liberation of mind and wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the power of mindfulness by comparing it to tying six different animals to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>strong post</b></a> or pillar so they can no longer wander off to their respective domains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that the phrase "strong post or pillar" is a designation for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> (kāyagatāsati)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that when six different animals are tied to a post, they eventually stop pulling in different directions and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>lie down</b></a> right next to it once they become exhausted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted his followers to train themselves so that mindfulness of the body is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>cultivated and developed</b></a>, made into a vehicle, and well-undertaken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk with developed mindfulness of the body finds that pleasant forms do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>pull at the eye</b></a>, and unpleasant forms do not cause him repulsion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a monk's mindfulness of the body is not established, pleasant and unpleasant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> will continue to distract and pull the mind toward unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that understanding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom is essential for ensuring that arisen unwholesome states cease without remainder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.247/en/sujato">SN 35.247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.1/en/sujato"><b>three kinds of feeling</b></a>—pleasant, painful, and neither-pleasant-nor-painful—and taught that a concentrated disciple must understand their origin and cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.1/en/sujato">SN 36.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is concentrated, mindful, and clearly comprehending understands the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.1/en/sujato"><b>path leading to the destruction</b></a> of all feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.1/en/sujato">SN 36.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to a monk who has reached the end of feelings and is free from longing as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.1/en/sujato"><b>attained to Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.1/en/sujato">SN 36.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that any feeling—whether pleasant, painful, or neutral, and whether <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.2/en/sujato"><b>internal or external</b></a>—should be understood as suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.2/en/sujato">SN 36.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized all felt experiences as being of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.2/en/sujato"><b>perishable nature</b></a> and deceptive by their very essence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.2/en/sujato">SN 36.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by seeing the constant decay and passing away of feelings, a practitioner becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.2/en/sujato"><b>detached</b></a> from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.2/en/sujato">SN 36.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to lust</b></a> (rāgānusayo) must be abandoned regarding pleasant feelings to achieve liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato">SN 36.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to aversion</b></a> (paṭighānusayo) is what typically needs to be abandoned in relation to painful feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato">SN 36.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that even neutral feelings can harbor an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to ignorance</b></a> (avijjānusayo) if they are not rightly understood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato">SN 36.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato"><b>bhikkhu without underlying tendencies</b></a> to describe a monk who has abandoned the latent habits of lust, aversion, and ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato">SN 36.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk has discarded the fetters and achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato"><b>right breakthrough of conceit</b></a>, he has effectively made an end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato">SN 36.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who experiences pleasant feeling without seeing the way out of it as being bound by an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato">SN 36.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato"><b>ardent monk</b></a> who maintains clear comprehension can fully understand all feelings and become taintless in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato">SN 36.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that after the breakup of the body, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a> who has mastered the nature of feelings cannot be defined or categorized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.3/en/sujato">SN 36.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that while people talk about a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.4/en/sujato"><b>bottomless abyss</b></a> in the ocean, the term actually designates painful bodily feelings in the Noble One's Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.4/en/sujato">SN 36.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.4/en/sujato"><b>uninstructed worldling</b></a> who is touched by painful bodily feeling often sorrows, wails, and falls into total confusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.4/en/sujato">SN 36.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is overwhelmed by pain as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.4/en/sujato"><b>not gained a footing</b></a> in the "bottomless abyss" of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.4/en/sujato">SN 36.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.4/en/sujato"><b>noble disciple</b></a> as one who does not sorrow or weep even when experiencing life-threatening bodily pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.4/en/sujato">SN 36.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.4/en/sujato"><b>endures painful feelings</b></a> without trembling has truly gained a footing and emerged from the abyss? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.4/en/sujato">SN 36.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.4/en/sujato"><b>life-threatening</b></a> bodily feelings can cause an uninstructed person to weep simply because they lack the strength to withstand them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.4/en/sujato">SN 36.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a>, the Arahant, the Perfectly Enlightened One, to be the chief among all living beings, whether they are footless, two-footed, or many-footed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a> are rooted in diligence and converge in diligence, just as the Tathāgata is the chief of all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a> as the foremost of all wholesome states, comparable to how the Tathāgata is considered the highest among all classes of beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk who is diligent can be expected to develop and cultivate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> to its fullest extent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the development of the Noble Eightfold Path as being based on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a>, dispassion, and cessation, ultimately maturing in release? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a diligent monk develops right view and right concentration based on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> and cessation, leading to the final relinquishment of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Noble Eightfold Path, when practiced diligently, has the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>removal of lust</b></a> as its final goal and destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the cultivation of the Noble Eightfold Path by a diligent monk results in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>removal of hatred</b></a> as its ultimate conclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the practice of the Noble Eightfold Path leads directly to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>removal of delusion</b></a> for those who are diligent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Noble Eightfold Path as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>grounded in the deathless</b></a>, having the deathless as its destination and final culmination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Noble Eightfold Path is a practice that is slanting, sloping, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>inclining toward Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the supremacy of diligence among wholesome states to the supremacy of the Tathāgata over all beings, whether they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>footless</b></a>, two-footed, four-footed, or many-footed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the Tathāgata is the chief of all beings, including those with form and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>formless</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is considered the chief of all living beings, ranging from the percipient to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>non-percipient</b></a> and those neither percipient nor non-percipient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all wholesome states <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>converge in diligence</b></a>, which is declared to be the best among them just as the Tathāgata is the best among beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a monk develops the Noble Eightfold Path by practicing right view and other factors so that they result in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>maturing in release</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared himself, the Tathāgata, to be the foremost of all beings, even those in the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>neither percipient nor non-percipient</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a diligent monk develops the path by relying on seclusion, dispassion, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>cessation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a diligent monk, the development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> involves its inclining and sloping toward Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the primacy of diligence to his own rank as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>foremost</b></a> among all classes of living beings, regardless of their number of feet or form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato"><b>diligent</b></a> will cultivate the Noble Eightfold Path so that it terminates in the removal of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.139/en/sujato">SN 45.139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a> to the footprint of an elephant, stating that just as the elephant’s footprint is the chief of all animal footprints due to its great size, diligence is the chief of all wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato">SN 45.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a> are rooted in diligence and converge in diligence, making it the most important foundation for spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato">SN 45.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who is diligent can be expected to successfully cultivate and fulfill the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato">SN 45.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a diligent monk develops right view by ensuring it is based on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a>, dispassion, and cessation, ultimately maturing in release? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato">SN 45.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the supremacy of diligence among wholesome states to how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.146/en/sujato"><b>moonlight</b></a> is the most brilliant and foremost of all the light provided by the stars? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.146/en/sujato">SN 45.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the image of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.147/en/sujato"><b>sun</b></a> rising in a cloudless autumn sky to illustrate how diligence outshines and surpasses all other wholesome states in its brilliance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.147/en/sujato">SN 45.147</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.148/en/sujato"><b>Benares cloth</b></a> is considered the best of all woven fabrics, diligence is declared to be the best of all wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.148/en/sujato">SN 45.148</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a diligent monk’s practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato"><b>right concentration</b></a> as being supported by dispassion and cessation, leading to the total relinquishment of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato">SN 45.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that just as all animal tracks are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato"><b>encompassed</b></a> by the footprint of an elephant, all wholesome qualities are encompassed by the single quality of diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato">SN 45.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a monk to truly develop the Noble Eightfold Path, he must be established in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.148/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a>, as it is the root from which all path factors grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.148/en/sujato">SN 45.148</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the light of all the stars combined does not equal even a fraction of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.146/en/sujato"><b>radiance</b></a> of the moon, much like how other wholesome states compare to the power of diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.146/en/sujato">SN 45.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato"><b>maturing in release</b></a> as the natural outcome for any monk who diligently applies themselves to the factors of the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.140/en/sujato">SN 45.140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as all seeds and plants reach growth, increase, and expansion by depending on the earth, a monk reaches growth in wholesome states by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato"><b>depending on virtue</b></a> while cultivating the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato">SN 45.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the foundation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> to the earth, explaining that it is the essential basis upon which a monk must stand to successfully develop and frequently practice the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato">SN 45.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a monk to reach growth, increase, and expansion in the Dhamma, he must develop the factors of the Noble Eightfold Path while being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato"><b>established in virtue</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato">SN 45.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk develops right view and right concentration by relying on seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, ultimately <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato"><b>maturing in release</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato">SN 45.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the development of the Noble Eightfold Path is a process that results in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato"><b>total relinquishment</b></a> of suffering, provided the path factors are grounded in virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato">SN 45.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the successful cultivation of the Noble Eightfold Path as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato"><b>based on cessation</b></a> and resulting in release, which can only be achieved by one established in virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato">SN 45.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a tree that leans, slopes, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.152/en/sujato"><b>inclines toward the east</b></a> to illustrate that when such a tree is cut at the root, it will inevitably fall in that same direction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.152/en/sujato">SN 45.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who frequently practices the Noble Eightfold Path becomes one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.152/en/sujato"><b>inclining toward Nibbāna</b></a>, just as a tree leaning in a certain direction falls that way when cut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.152/en/sujato">SN 45.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the cultivation of the Noble Eightfold Path leads a monk to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.152/en/sujato"><b>slanting toward Nibbāna</b></a>, ensuring his spiritual trajectory is directed toward the final goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.152/en/sujato">SN 45.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the development of the Noble Eightfold Path as a process where a monk becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.152/en/sujato"><b>sloping toward Nibbāna</b></a> through the dedicated practice of right view, right intention, and the other path factors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.152/en/sujato">SN 45.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who develops right resolve and right effort while relying on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.152/en/sujato"><b>seclusion and dispassion</b></a> becomes someone who is naturally sloping and inclining toward Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.152/en/sujato">SN 45.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a tree's physical lean to show how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.152/en/sujato"><b>practice of the path</b></a> fundamentally alters a person's spiritual direction toward final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.152/en/sujato">SN 45.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato"><b>spike of rice</b></a> or barley to explain that only when it is well-pointed can it pierce the hand or foot and draw blood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato">SN 45.154</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato"><b>well-directed view</b></a> and well-directed path-development will be able to pierce ignorance, arouse true knowledge, and realize Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato">SN 45.154</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, just as an ill-directed spike of grain cannot pierce the skin, an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato"><b>ill-directed view</b></a> cannot pierce ignorance or lead to the realization of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato">SN 45.154</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato"><b>piercing ignorance</b></a> and arousing true knowledge is only possible for a monk who has properly directed his view and his development of the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato">SN 45.154</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato"><b>realizing Nibbāna</b></a> is a possible outcome for a monk, provided that his view and his cultivation of the Noble Eightfold Path are well-directed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato">SN 45.154</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato"><b>arousing true knowledge</b></a> requires a well-pointed and well-directed spiritual practice, much like a spike of grain needs to be well-positioned to pierce a surface? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato">SN 45.154</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that just as seeds depend on the earth for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato"><b>growth and expansion</b></a>, a monk's success in the Noble Eightfold Path is grounded in his commitment to virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.150/en/sujato">SN 45.150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato"><b>pierce ignorance</b></a> is not accidental, but the direct result of a monk’s well-directed view and persistent cultivation of the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.154/en/sujato">SN 45.154</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk practicing the Noble Eightfold Path to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.160/en/sujato"><b>River Ganges</b></a>, which flows, slants, and inclines toward the east and cannot be made to flow in the opposite direction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.160/en/sujato">SN 45.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a great crowd of people cannot make the Ganges flow toward the west, a monk whose mind has long <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.160/en/sujato"><b>slanted toward seclusion</b></a> cannot be forced to return to a lower life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.160/en/sujato">SN 45.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk is frequently practicing the Noble Eightfold Path, even if kings, ministers, or relatives try to entice him with wealth to return to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.160/en/sujato"><b>lay life</b></a>, it is impossible for him to abandon his training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.160/en/sujato">SN 45.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the cultivation of the Noble Eightfold Path as a practice that is based on seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, ultimately <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.160/en/sujato"><b>maturing in release</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.160/en/sujato">SN 45.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.162/en/sujato"><b>discriminations</b></a> (vidhā) or conceits that a practitioner must overcome: the thoughts "I am better," "I am equal," and "I am worse"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.162/en/sujato">SN 45.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the Noble Eightfold Path must be developed for the purpose of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.162/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> and full understanding of the three forms of conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.162/en/sujato">SN 45.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the thought <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.162/en/sujato"><b>"I am better"</b></a> is a form of discrimination that is abandoned when a monk successfully cultivates factors like right view and right concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.162/en/sujato">SN 45.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the Noble Eightfold Path is the specific means for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.162/en/sujato"><b>abandonment</b></a> and destruction of the various types of conceit and discrimination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.162/en/sujato">SN 45.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three distinct types of suffering, the first being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.165/en/sujato"><b>suffering due to pain</b></a> (dukkhadukkhatā), which the holy path is designed to overcome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.165/en/sujato">SN 45.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there is a type of unsatisfactoriness called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.165/en/sujato"><b>suffering due to formations</b></a> (saṅkhāradukkhatā), which refers to the inherent instability of all conditioned things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.165/en/sujato">SN 45.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.165/en/sujato"><b>suffering due to change</b></a> (vipariṇāmadukkhatā) as one of the three primary forms of suffering that can be fully understood and abandoned through the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.165/en/sujato">SN 45.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the Noble Eightfold Path should be developed for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.165/en/sujato"><b>full understanding</b></a>, exhaustion, and abandonment of all three types of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.165/en/sujato">SN 45.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three fundamental miseries (nīghā) that afflict beings, declaring that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.168/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> is one of these primary sources of distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.168/en/sujato">SN 45.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.168/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> is a form of misery that a monk must aim to abandon by cultivating the eight path factors such as right view and right intention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.168/en/sujato">SN 45.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.168/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> as one of the three miseries that keep a mind in bondage until the Noble Eightfold Path is fully developed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.168/en/sujato">SN 45.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the Noble Eightfold Path is specifically practiced for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.168/en/sujato"><b>destruction</b></a> and abandonment of the miseries of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.168/en/sujato">SN 45.168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of the Ganges' natural eastward flow to explain that a monk who cultivates the path becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.160/en/sujato"><b>inclined toward Nibbāna</b></a> just as the river is inclined toward the ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.160/en/sujato">SN 45.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk develops right concentration and other path factors while ensuring the practice is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.162/en/sujato"><b>based on dispassion</b></a> and cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.162/en/sujato">SN 45.162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the Noble Eightfold Path leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.160/en/sujato"><b>total relinquishment</b></a> of conditioned states, preventing a practitioner from ever sliding back into worldly ways? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.160/en/sujato">SN 45.160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>four bases for spiritual power</b></a> are developed and cultivated, they lead to great fruit and great benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the first basis for spiritual power as the development of concentration due to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>desire</b></a> combined with volitional formations of striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, desire is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>overly lax</b></a> when it is accompanied by and associated with sloth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that desire is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>overly exerted</b></a> when it is accompanied by and associated with restlessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described desire as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>constricted internally</b></a> when it is accompanied by and associated with sloth and torpor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated desire is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>scattered externally</b></a> when it repeatedly wanders away and is dispersed among the five cords of sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to dwell with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>perception of before and after</b></a>, where one perceives the future state the same as the past and vice versa? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the practice of "as below, so above," which involves reviewing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>body</b></a> from the soles of the feet up and from the hair of the head down as full of various impurities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed thirty-one parts of the body, including head hairs, skin, flesh, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>internal organs</b></a>, to be contemplated for spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised practicing "as by day, so by night," meaning one should develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>bases for spiritual power</b></a> at night using the same aspects, signs, and features as used during the day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught practitioners to develop a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>bright mind</b></a> that is open and unshrouded by using the perception of light? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the second basis for spiritual power as the development of concentration due to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>energy</b></a> combined with volitional formations of striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, energy is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>overly lax</b></a> when it is accompanied by sloth, hindering spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that energy is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>overly exerted</b></a> when it is joined with restlessness, which should be avoided for balanced development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the third basis for spiritual power as the development of concentration due to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> combined with volitional formations of striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> as being constricted internally when it is weighed down by sloth and torpor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the fourth basis for spiritual power as the development of concentration due to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>investigation</b></a> combined with volitional formations of striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>investigation</b></a> becomes scattered externally when it is diverted toward the five cords of sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>perception of day</b></a> well-resolved is a key component in developing an open and bright mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that through the cultivation of the four bases for spiritual power, a monk can experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>manifold spiritual power</b></a>, such as becoming many from being one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that by developing these four bases, a practitioner can pass through walls and mountains as if through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>space</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the four bases of spiritual power allow a person to exercise mastery with the body as far as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>Brahma world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the ultimate goal of developing the four bases for spiritual power is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who cultivates these four bases realizes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the four bases of power are developed through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato"><b>perception of light</b></a>, ensuring the mind is always bright and never shrouded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn51.20/en/sujato">SN 51.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha once stayed at Sāvatthī in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato"><b>Salaḷā tree hall</b></a> while teaching the monks about the transformative power of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato">SN 52.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha compared the unwavering direction of a monk's mind to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato"><b>Ganges river</b></a>, which naturally flows, slopes, and inclines toward the east? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato">SN 52.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha taught that just as a massive crowd with shovels and baskets cannot make the Ganges flow westward, no one can force a monk who practices the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a> to return to a lower life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato">SN 52.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha explained that even if kings, royal ministers, or friends offered wealth to a monk, it would be impossible to entice him to give up the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato"><b>training</b></a> and return to the lay life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato">SN 52.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha stated that a monk who frequently develops mindfulness has a mind that has long been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato"><b>inclined toward seclusion</b></a>, making it impossible for him to revert to the pursuit of worldly goods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato">SN 52.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha defined the proper practice of mindfulness as dwelling ardent, clearly comprehending, and mindful, having removed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato"><b>covetousness and displeasure</b></a> in regard to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato">SN 52.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha taught that a monk should dwell contemplating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato"><b>body as a body</b></a> as a primary method for establishing the mind firmly in spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato">SN 52.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha identified the four areas of contemplation as the body, feelings, the mind, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato"><b>phenomena</b></a>, which together form the basis for spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato">SN 52.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha once resided in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.10/en/sujato"><b>Blind Men’s Grove</b></a> at Sāvatthī while he was gravely ill and experiencing racking physical pains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.10/en/sujato">SN 52.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when fellow monks asked how he endured severe illness, the Venerable Anuruddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.10/en/sujato"><b>painful bodily feelings</b></a> did not take possession of his mind or overwhelm it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.10/en/sujato">SN 52.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha attributed his ability to remain mentally unperturbed by physical suffering to having a mind well-established in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.10/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.10/en/sujato">SN 52.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Anuruddha, a monk who dwells contemplating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.10/en/sujato"><b>feelings as feelings</b></a> can effectively prevent physical affliction from dictating his mental state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.10/en/sujato">SN 52.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha highlighted being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.10/en/sujato"><b>ardent</b></a> and clearly comprehending as essential qualities for a monk to maintain stability during extreme physical distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.10/en/sujato">SN 52.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha demonstrated through his own illness that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.10/en/sujato"><b>contemplation of the mind</b></a> as the mind is a practical tool for ensuring that bodily pain does not lead to mental agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.10/en/sujato">SN 52.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha used the metaphor of the Ganges to illustrate that it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato"><b>not possible</b></a> to turn back a person who has committed themselves to the path of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato">SN 52.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Anuruddha emphasized that for a monk who has made the four establishments of mindfulness a habit, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato"><b>no case</b></a> where he would abandon the training to return to the lay life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn52.8/en/sujato">SN 52.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha asked the Venerable Kimila how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>concentration by mindfulness of breathing</b></a> is developed to be of great fruit and benefit, Kimila remained silent three times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ananda invited the Buddha to explain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of breathing</b></a>, noting that after hearing it from the Blessed One, the monks would remember it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a monk should go to a forest, the foot of a tree, or an empty hut, sit down cross-legged, and set <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness in front of him</b></a> before beginning the practice of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk breathes in and out long or short, or trains to tranquilize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>bodily formation</b></a>, he is dwelling as a contemplator of the body in the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>in-and-out breathing</b></a> as a certain kind of body among the bodies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk contemplator of feelings in feelings is one who trains to experience rapture, pleasure, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>mental formation</b></a> while breathing in and out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that giving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>close attention</b></a> to in-and-out breathing is regarded as a certain kind of feeling among the feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk dwells as a contemplator of the mind in the mind when he trains to gladden, concentrate, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>liberate the mind</b></a> while breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that he does not recommend the development of concentration by mindfulness of breathing for one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>muddle-minded</b></a> and lacks clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk contemplator of phenomena in phenomena as one who breathes while contemplating impermanence, fading away, cessation, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>relinquishment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while contemplating the four establishments of mindfulness through breathing, a monk dwells ardent and mindful, having removed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>covetousness and displeasure</b></a> in regard to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a monk sees with wisdom the abandoning of covetousness and displeasure, he becomes one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>looks on with equanimity</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the effect of mindfulness of breathing to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>mound of dust</b></a> at a crossroads that is struck down by any cart or chariot approaching from any of the four directions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a simile to explain that a monk who dwells contemplating the body, feelings, mind, or phenomena <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>strikes down</b></a> evil unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that before practicing mindfulness of breathing, one should sit down and hold the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>body erect</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner should train with the thought, ‘I will breathe in experiencing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>whole body</b></a>’? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk develops mindfulness of breathing by training to breathe in and out while contemplating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>fading away</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to train themselves to breathe in and out while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>tranquilizing the mental formation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should breathe in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>mindfully</b></a> and breathe out mindfully as a foundational step of the practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk practicing mindfulness of breathing trains to breathe in and out while contemplating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato"><b>cessation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.10/en/sujato">SN 54.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once spent a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato"><b>three-month retreat</b></a> in solitary seclusion at the wood of Icchānaṅgala, during which he requested that no one visit him except for the single monk who brought him his almsfood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato">SN 54.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his monks to tell wanderers of other sects, if they should ask, that he spent the majority of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato"><b>rains residence</b></a> dwelling in the concentration by mindfulness of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato">SN 54.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato"><b>concentration by mindfulness of breathing</b></a> as a "noble dwelling" (ariyavihāra), indicating its status as a superior and refined state of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato">SN 54.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the practice of mindfulness of breathing as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato"><b>divine dwelling</b></a> (brahmavihāra), a term often associated with the boundless states like loving-kindness, but here applied to this specific breath meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato">SN 54.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called the concentration by mindfulness of breathing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata’s dwelling</b></a> (tathāgatavihāra), signifying that it is the characteristic meditative state in which a Fully Enlightened One frequently resides? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato">SN 54.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for those monks who are still "trainees" (sekhā) and have not yet reached the goal, the development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of breathing</b></a> is a primary tool for the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato">SN 54.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, even for Arahants who have already destroyed the taints and fulfilled the spiritual life, concentration by mindfulness of breathing serves as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwelling</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato">SN 54.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for those who have already attained liberation, the continued practice of breath concentration contributes to their ongoing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato">SN 54.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his own meditative practice as being mindfully aware of breathing in and out, whether the breath is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato"><b>long or short</b></a>, while clearly understanding the nature of each breath? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato">SN 54.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even while breathing in and out, one should train to be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato"><b>contemplator of relinquishment</b></a>, letting go of all worldly attachments through the breath? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.11/en/sujato">SN 54.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness are all impermanent and suffering, and should therefore be viewed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato"><b>correct wisdom</b></a> as "This is not mine, this I am not, this is not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato">SN 22.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a well-taught noble disciple becomes disenchanted with the five aggregates, they reach a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> through which they are finally liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato">SN 22.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified Arahants as those who have reached the end of rebirth, having lived the holy life and accomplished <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato"><b>what had to be done</b></a>, with no further state of being ahead of them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato">SN 22.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared Arahants to be the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato"><b>foremost and best</b></a> beings in the entire world, including all abodes of beings up to the very peak of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato">SN 22.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Arahants as truly happy because they have utterly destroyed craving and have burst through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato"><b>net of delusion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato">SN 22.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that Arahants have a mind that is imperturbable and unclouded, and they dwell in the world completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato"><b>unsoiled</b></a> by its worldly conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato">SN 22.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha affectionately referred to Arahants as his own "breast-born sons" because they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato"><b>fully understood</b></a> the five aggregates and follow the path of the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato">SN 22.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized Arahants as "great nagas" who are well-concentrated, free from fear and dread, and have perfectly completed their training in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato"><b>three trainings</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato">SN 22.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Arahants possess the "knowledge of one beyond training" and have successfully reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato"><b>essence of the holy life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato">SN 22.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that Arahants, having conquered the world and eliminated all delight in future existence, roar an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato"><b>unrivaled lion's roar</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.76/en/sujato">SN 22.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared his teaching to the king of beasts, explaining that when a lion comes out of his lair and roars three times in the evening, all the other animals are struck with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato"><b>fear and terror</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato">SN 22.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how even royal elephants, though secured by strong leather bonds, will break their chains and flee in fear when they hear the roar of a lion because the lion possesses such <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato"><b>great power</b></a> and authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato">SN 22.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a Tathāgata arises in the world as a fully enlightened teacher, he explains the nature of the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—including their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato"><b>origin and passing away</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato">SN 22.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when long-lived and beautiful gods who dwell for ages in high celestial mansions hear the Dhamma, they are often overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato"><b>a sense of urgency</b></a> and fear regarding their own existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato">SN 22.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that celestial beings, despite their long lives and happiness, eventually realize through his teaching that they are actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato"><b>impermanent and unstable</b></a>, even though they previously believed themselves to be eternal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato">SN 22.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that high-ranking gods come to the realization that they are still <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato"><b>included within identity</b></a> and have not yet escaped the cycle of existence when they hear the words of the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato">SN 22.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that his spiritual influence over the world of gods and humans is so vast and impactful that he is described as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato"><b>great majesty</b></a> and authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato">SN 22.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, as the peerless teacher of gods and men, set in motion the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato"><b>Wheel of Dhamma</b></a> through his own direct knowledge, revealing truths that even the gods had not realized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato">SN 22.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the specific path leading to the subsiding of all suffering and the cessation of identity is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato">SN 22.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the terrified reaction of long-lived, famous gods hearing his words to the reaction of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato"><b>other beasts</b></a> when they hear the roar of a lion in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato">SN 22.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that celestial beings only recognize their own state of being non-eternal after they hear the voice of an Arahant who has become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato"><b>fully liberated</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.78/en/sujato">SN 22.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that any recluse or brahmin who recollects their manifold <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>past lives</b></a> is actually recollecting the five aggregates subject to clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined form as that which is "deformed" because it is affected and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>deformed by cold</b></a>, heat, hunger, thirst, and the touch of insects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that feeling is so called because it feels, specifically feeling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>pleasure and pain</b></a>, as well as neither-pain-nor-pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, perception is named for its function of perceiving qualities such as the colors <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>blue, yellow</b></a>, red, and white? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described volitional formations as those things which <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>construct the conditioned</b></a>, including the construction of form, feeling, perception, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined consciousness as that which cognizes diverse tastes, such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>sour and bitter</b></a>, pungent, sweet, alkaline, and salty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his disciples to reflect on how they are currently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>being devoured</b></a> by form, just as they were devoured by form in the past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if one delights in future feeling, they will be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>devoured by feeling</b></a> in the future just as they are in the present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a well-taught noble disciple reflects on being devoured by perception, they become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>unconcerned with the past</b></a> and do not delight in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a practitioner should be one who is practicing for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>cessation of volitional formations</b></a> in the present so as not to be devoured by them in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as one is devoured by present consciousness, one would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>devoured by consciousness</b></a> in the future if they were to delight in it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned his monks on whether the five aggregates are permanent or impermanent, and they concluded that they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that whatever is impermanent is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a>, and therefore should not be regarded as "mine" or "my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that all form—whether past, future, or present, internal or external—should be seen with correct wisdom as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>not mine</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a noble disciple as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>discards</b></a> the five aggregates (form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness) rather than accumulating them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the noble disciple <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>abandons</b></a> the five aggregates and does not grasp after them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the spiritual process of a noble disciple to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>scattering</b></a> the five aggregates rather than piling them up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a well-taught noble disciple <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>extinguishes</b></a> the five aggregates rather than kindling them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>revulsion</b></a> toward the five aggregates leads directly to dispassion and subsequent liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person's mind is liberated, they possess the knowledge that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a> and the holy life has been lived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called a monk an Arahant when he neither discards nor accumulates, but stands having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>finished discarding</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Arahant as one who neither abandons nor grasps, but stands having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>finished abandoning</b></a> everything that was to be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that gods including <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>Indra, Brahma, and Pajapati</b></a> pay homage from afar to a monk whose mind is thus liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the gods pay homage to the liberated monk by calling him the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>thoroughbred of men</b></a> and the supreme among men? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the gods admit they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato"><b>do not understand</b></a> what the liberated monk is meditating in dependence upon, because his mind is so deeply free from all dependencies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.79/en/sujato">SN 22.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was once staying among the Sakyans at Kapilavatthu in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>Banyan Tree Park</b></a> when he entered into a period of solitary retreat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that on one occasion, the Buddha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>dismissed the Sangha</b></a> of monks for a certain reason, only to later reflect with compassion on how the new monks might fare without his guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared newly ordained monks who are unable to see their teacher to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>young calf</b></a> that might undergo a change or transformation if it cannot see its mother? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>young seeds</b></a> failing to receive water to describe the potential spiritual decline of new monks who do not have the opportunity to see the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>Brahmā Sahampati</b></a> realized the Buddha’s compassionate reflection regarding the dismissed monks, he instantly vanished from the Brahma-world and appeared before the Buddha to urge him to welcome the Sangha back? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after being requested by Brahmā Sahampati to favor the Sangha, the Buddha performed a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>psychic feat</b></a> that caused the monks to approach him one by one, looking somewhat embarrassed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the practice of alms-gathering, or "scraps-gathering," as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>lowly way of life</b></a> and a form of "curse" in the eyes of the world, yet one undertaken by clansmen for a noble purpose? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that true clansmen do not go forth into homelessness because of debt, fear, or lack of livelihood, but specifically for the purpose of reaching the end of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>mass of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who is covetous, full of ill-will, and lacking mindfulness to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>firebrand from a pyre</b></a> that is burning at both ends and smeared with dung in the middle, making it useless for wood in both the village and the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained senses</b></a> and a confused mind has failed both in the enjoyments of a householder and in fulfilling the purpose of asceticism? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome thoughts</b></a>—sensual thought, thought of ill-will, and thought of harmfulness—which can only be completely ceased through specific mental cultivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that unwholesome thoughts cease without remainder for a person who dwells with a mind well-established in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>signless concentration</b></a> as a primary method for the total cessation of unwholesome mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that signless concentration, when developed and cultivated, is of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>great fruit</b></a> and brings great benefit to the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are two primary views—the view of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>existence and non-existence</b></a>—and that a noble disciple must investigate if there is anything in the world they can cling to without fault? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that if one were to engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> to form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, or consciousness, it would inevitably lead to the arising of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the entire origin of the mass of suffering as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>conditioned by clinging</b></a>, which leads to existence, birth, and then to aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned his monks on the nature of the five aggregates, leading them to conclude that form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness are all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, whatever is impermanent is inherently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a>, and therefore should not be regarded as "mine" or "my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any aggregate—whether past, future, or present—is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>not self</b></a>, and this should be seen as it really is with correct wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that by seeing the aggregates with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>correct wisdom</b></a>, a well-taught noble disciple becomes disenchanted and reaches dispassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that once a disciple is liberated through dispassion, they understand that birth is destroyed and there is nothing further for this <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato"><b>state of being</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.80/en/sujato">SN 22.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once left the Ghosita Monastery in Kosambi to wander <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>alone and companionless</b></a>, without even notifying his attendants or the Sangha of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda explained that when the Buddha packs his lodging and departs alone without informing anyone, he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>wishes to dwell in solitude</b></a> and should not be followed by anyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while on a solitary wander, the Buddha eventually settled in Pālileyyaka and stayed there at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>foot of the Bhaddasāla tree</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a group of monks approached Venerable Ānanda because they had not heard a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma talk</b></a> from the Buddha in person for a long time and wished to visit him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the Dhamma in "analytical portions," which included the four establishments of mindfulness, the four right strivings, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>noble eightfold path</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha addressed a monk's specific question regarding how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>knowing and seeing</b></a> leads to the immediate destruction of the taints (<i>āsava</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>uninstructed worldling</b></a> as one who has no regard for noble ones and is unskilled in their Dhamma, leading them to misinterpret the nature of their own existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>regards form as self</b></a>, that very act of regarding is itself a conditioned volitional formation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the volitional formation of regarding an aggregate as "self" originates from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the craving which produces unwholesome volitional formations is born of feeling produced by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>contact with ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the volitional formation, the craving, the feeling, and the contact are all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>impermanent and conditioned</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>self as possessing form</b></a> is a mental formation that eventually leads to the arising of the taints if not understood correctly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the view that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>form is in self</b></a> as a dependently arisen formation that one must see with wisdom to be liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mental act of regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>self as in form</b></a> is a conditioned state born from ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that even if a monk does not regard form as self, but instead <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>regards feeling as self</b></a>, they are still caught in a conditioned volitional formation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>perception as self</b></a> is a product of craving that must be seen as impermanent for the taints to be destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations as self</b></a> is itself a formation that arises through contact with ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>consciousness as self</b></a> is a conditioned view that originates from feeling born of contact with ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the eternalist view "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>the self and the world are one</b></a>; after death I shall be permanent" is actually a conditioned volitional formation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even the annihilationist view "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>I might not be</b></a>, and it might not be for me" is a volitional formation produced by craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>perplexed and doubtful</b></a> regarding the True Dhamma as a mental formation that is impermanent and dependently arisen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>immediate destruction of the taints</b></a> occurs when one knows and sees that even deep-seated views and doubts are merely impermanent, conditioned formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>four right strivings</b></a> as one of the analytical portions of the Dhamma he specifically taught to the monks at Pālileyyaka? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>five faculties</b></a> and the five powers as essential components of the Dhamma that he had analytically explained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that he had analytically taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> to help his disciples reach liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha traced the origin of all identity views and doubts back to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a>, specifically feelings produced by contact with ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato"><b>knowledge of others' minds</b></a> to perceive the internal reflection of a monk and then taught the assembly how to end the taints based on that monk's thought? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato">SN 22.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> was once staying in a forest hut at Makkarakata in Avanti when he was insulted by young brahmin students who called him a "shaveling" and a "menial"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>brahmin students</b></a> once derided Venerable Mahākaccāna by calling him "dark-skinned" and the "offspring of the Kinsman’s feet" while walking around his forest dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>ancient brahmins</b></a>, according to Venerable Mahākaccāna, were once supreme in virtue because they guarded their sense doors and successfully overcame their anger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> described modern brahmins as being intoxicated by their lineage and walking a path of violence while neglecting to guard their sense doors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> compared the spiritual wealth of a person who does not guard their sense doors to wealth found by a man in a dream, which disappears upon waking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that rituals such as reciting the three Vedas, wearing matted hair, and ritual bathing are described by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> as mere outward signs and shams rather than true spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> taught that the true path to the state of Brahma is a mind that is well-concentrated, serene, and shows no hostility toward any being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a person is considered to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>unguarded doors</b></a>, as explained by Venerable Mahākaccāna, if they become enamored with agreeable forms or filled with ill will toward disagreeable ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> explained that someone who lacks mindfulness of the body when seeing a form is considered to have a "limited mind"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> defined an "unguarded door" as reacting with lust to pleasant sounds and with aversion to unpleasant ones while lacking proper mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a person who encounters a smell and becomes obsessed with it if it is pleasant, or repelled if it is unpleasant, is described by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> as having unguarded sense doors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> taught that failing to maintain mindfulness of the body while tasting flavors leads to a state where unwholesome states do not cease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a>, reacting emotionally to tactile objects without self-awareness results in a limited mind that cannot understand the liberation of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> defined "unguarded doors" regarding the mind as being fascinated by agreeable mental phenomena or bothered by disagreeable ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> explained that a person with "guarded doors" is one who does not become enamored with agreeable forms and does not feel ill will toward disagreeable ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that maintaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> was highlighted by Venerable Mahākaccāna as a key characteristic of one who guards their sense doors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> described the mind of a person who guards their sense doors as "immeasurable," in contrast to the limited mind of those who are unguarded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> taught that guarding the door of the tongue involves not being obsessed with pleasant tastes and not being disturbed by unpleasant ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a>, a person with "guarded doors" correctly knows the liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom where unwholesome states cease without remainder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Lohicca</b></a>, after hearing Mahākaccāna’s explanation, compared the Dhamma to a lamp being carried into the darkness so that those with eyes could see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato"><b>Lohicca the brahmin</b></a> went for refuge to the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Sangha specifically because of the way Venerable Mahākaccāna clarified the meaning of guarding the sense doors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.132/en/sujato">SN 35.132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>young brahmin</b></a> student, after being instructed and gladdened by Venerable Udāyī, successfully convinced the lady Verahaccānī to invite the monk for a meal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Udāyī</b></a> was described by a student as a teacher who explains the holy life that is perfectly complete and pure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the lady Verahaccānī first asked for a teaching while sitting on a high seat with her head covered and wearing sandals, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Udāyī</b></a> politely declined by saying, "The time will come, sister," and then departed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Udāyī</b></a> initially refused to preach because, as the student later explained, he and other noble disciples are "reverent toward the Dhamma" and require the listener to show proper respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the lady <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Verahaccānī</b></a> eventually realized her lack of etiquette and invited Venerable Udāyī a second time, specifically choosing to sit on a low seat with her head uncovered to hear the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Udāyī</b></a> taught that Arahants describe the existence of pleasure and pain when the eye exists, but they do not describe them when the eye is absent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Udāyī</b></a>, the designation of pleasure and pain by the Arahants is dependent on the existence of the ear and its corresponding auditory experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Udāyī</b></a> explained that Arahants only designate pleasure and pain when the nose sense base is present, and not when it is absent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Udāyī</b></a> taught that without the existence of the body as a sense base, Arahants would not designate or describe the experience of pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Udāyī</b></a> emphasized that Arahants describe pleasure and pain when the mind is present, but they do not do so when the mind is absent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the lady <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Verahaccānī</b></a> asked Venerable Udāyī a specific question about when Arahants designate pleasure and pain and when they do not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Udāyī</b></a> accepted the meal invitation from the lady Verahaccānī by remaining silent, which was the traditional manner of acceptance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the explanation of the sense bases, the lady <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Verahaccānī</b></a> declared that the teaching was as clear as if someone had set upright what had been overturned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Udāyī</b></a> used the same teaching framework for all six internal sense bases to explain how Arahants designate pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>brahmin student</b></a> acted as a mediator between Venerable Udāyī and the lady Verahaccānī to ensure the Dhamma was taught under the correct conditions of respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Udāyī</b></a> demonstrated the importance of monastic discipline by refusing to teach a layperson who sat on a seat higher than his own? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.133/en/sujato">SN 35.133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while Arahants have already completed their work, monks who are still trainees must continue to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> regarding the six bases of contact to reach the unsurpassed security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato">SN 35.134</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that Arahants, those who have destroyed the taints and lived the holy life, are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato"><b>incapable of being negligent</b></a> and therefore do not need to be told to practice heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato">SN 35.134</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a trainee, agreeable and disagreeable forms can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato"><b>obsess the mind</b></a> if they do not maintain constant heedfulness over their sense of sight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato">SN 35.134</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered a discourse in the Sakyan town of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato"><b>Devadaha</b></a> specifically to clarify which monks need to practice heedfulness regarding the senses and which do not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato">SN 35.134</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk practices heedfulness and prevents sense objects from overwhelming the mind, his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> becomes constant and his mindfulness becomes established? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato">SN 35.134</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato"><b>tranquil body</b></a> and a concentrated, one-pointed mind as the direct fruits of a trainee's heedfulness regarding the six bases of contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato">SN 35.134</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized mental phenomena as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato"><b>cognizable by the mind</b></a> and warned that these, whether agreeable or disagreeable, require heedfulness so they do not take possession of a trainee's heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato">SN 35.134</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Arahants as those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato"><b>laid down the burden</b></a> and reached their own goal, meaning they no longer have a task to perform regarding heedfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato">SN 35.134</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the practice of heedfulness is essential for anyone still seeking the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed security from bondage</b></a>, a state already achieved by the Arahants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato">SN 35.134</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato"><b>fruit of heedfulness</b></a>—such as an unconfused mind and unshakable energy—he encourages trainees to remain diligent in guarding their six sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.134/en/sujato">SN 35.134</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that devas and humans delight in and are enchanted by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato"><b>forms</b></a>, yet they dwell in suffering when those forms change, fade away, or cease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato">SN 35.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he dwells in happiness when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato"><b>forms change</b></a>, fade away, and cease, because he has fully understood their origin, danger, and the escape from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato">SN 35.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that devas and humans find delight in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a>, but experience suffering whenever those phenomena undergo change or cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato">SN 35.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out a radical difference in perception, where what the rest of the world calls happiness, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato"><b>Noble Ones</b></a> call suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato">SN 35.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato"><b>cessation of personality</b></a> is seen as true happiness by the Noble Ones, even though this view is the complete opposite of how the rest of the world sees it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato">SN 35.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the Dhamma to a light for those who can see, while noting it remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato"><b>darkness</b></a> and obscured for those who are blinded by ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato">SN 35.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are possessed by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato"><b>lust for existence</b></a> and follow its stream cannot easily understand the Dhamma, as they remain within Mara’s realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato">SN 35.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked his monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato"><b>abandon</b></a> whatever does not belong to them, explaining that letting go of such things will lead to their long-term welfare and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato">SN 35.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a> is not yours and urged that it should be abandoned for one's own benefit and joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato">SN 35.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> is not yours, and therefore it should be relinquished to ensure one's future welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato">SN 35.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of people carrying away <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato"><b>grass and sticks</b></a> from Jeta’s Grove to show that we shouldn't identify with the sense bases, since they are not our "self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato">SN 35.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha asked if monks would feel they were being "carried away" if someone took foliage from the grove, they replied "No," because it was neither <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato"><b>self nor what belongs to self</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato">SN 35.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that devas and humans delight in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato"><b>sounds</b></a> and odors, but find themselves in distress when these sensory experiences change or end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato">SN 35.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified himself as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato"><b>Tathagata</b></a>, a perfectly enlightened one who does not delight in tastes or tactile objects because he has realized the escape from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato">SN 35.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato"><b>tongue</b></a> and the sensations of taste are not yours and should be abandoned for the sake of long-lasting happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato">SN 35.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that only those who have attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato"><b>state of the Noble Ones</b></a> are worthy of truly understanding the path where one becomes taintless and attains Parinibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato">SN 35.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato"><b>obstructed</b></a> by ignorance, the truth of the Dhamma remains as hidden as objects in the pitch black of night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato">SN 35.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the physical <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato"><b>body</b></a> is not yours, and just as one would not grieve over the removal of sticks from a forest, one should abandon attachment to the physical sense base? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.138/en/sujato">SN 35.138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that devas and humans are "enchanted" by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato"><b>tastes</b></a> and tactile objects, which makes the inevitable change of these things a source of pain for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato">SN 35.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he dwells in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a> specifically through the change, fading away, and cessation of all mental phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.136/en/sujato">SN 35.136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato"><b>old kamma</b></a> as the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind, describing them as being constructed and willed in the past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato">SN 35.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato"><b>new kamma</b></a> refers to any action a person performs in the present moment through body, speech, or mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato">SN 35.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato"><b>cessation of kamma</b></a> is the liberation achieved when one touches the ending of bodily, verbal, and mental actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato">SN 35.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>—starting with Right View and ending with Right Concentration—as the specific way leading to the cessation of kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato">SN 35.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his disciples to meditate under <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato"><b>tree roots</b></a> and in empty huts to ensure they would not be negligent or live with later regret? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato">SN 35.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> is lived under him for the primary purpose of reaching the full understanding of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato">SN 35.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a> is a form of suffering, and spiritual practice is aimed at its full understanding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato">SN 35.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato"><b>forms</b></a> are considered suffering, and for their full understanding, the holy life is lived under the Blessed One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato">SN 35.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato"><b>eye-consciousness</b></a> as suffering, noting that the goal of the monk's training is to understand it fully? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato">SN 35.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact</b></a> is suffering, and his disciples live the holy life to achieve a complete understanding of this contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato">SN 35.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that any feeling—whether pleasant, painful, or neutral—that arises with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact as condition</b></a> is actually a form of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato">SN 35.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato"><b>tongue</b></a> and the experience of tastes as suffering that must be fully understood through the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato">SN 35.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> itself is suffering, and the purpose of spiritual training is the full understanding of the mental faculty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato">SN 35.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha classified all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> (dhamma) as suffering, stating that the holy life is lived to fully comprehend them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato">SN 35.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato"><b>mind-contact</b></a> as suffering, and emphasized that understanding it is a core goal of his teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato">SN 35.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that any feeling arising from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato"><b>mind-contact</b></a>—be it pleasant, painful, or neutral—is suffering that requires full understanding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato">SN 35.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his monks to tell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato"><b>wanderers of other sects</b></a> that they follow his path specifically for the full understanding of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.152/en/sujato">SN 35.152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as a teacher who acts out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> and seeks the welfare of his disciples by providing them with essential instructions for liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato">SN 35.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the six internal sense bases, including the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a>, are old kamma that should be seen as things to be felt and understood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato">SN 35.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned his followers that failing to meditate and being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato"><b>negligent</b></a> would lead to future remorse, urging them to practice while they have the opportunity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.146/en/sujato">SN 35.146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified his teaching that "whatever is felt is included in suffering" refers specifically to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>impermanence of formations</b></a>, rather than denying the existence of pleasant or neutral feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that all feelings are considered suffering because formations are of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>nature to vanish</b></a> and are subject to change and fading away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, for one who has attained the first jhāna, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>speech has ceased</b></a> as part of the process of successive cessation of formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>thought and examination</b></a> (vitakka and vicāra) cease for a practitioner who has successfully attained the second jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>rapture</b></a> (pīti) as the specific formation that ceases when a practitioner enters and dwells in the third jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>in-and-out breathing</b></a> ceases for a monk who has attained the fourth jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>perception of forms</b></a> ceases when a practitioner reaches the base of infinite space? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the perception of the base of infinite space ceases for those who attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite consciousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the perception of the base of infinite consciousness ceases when one enters the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>base of nothingness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the perception of the base of nothingness ceases for one who has attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>perception and feeling</b></a> cease for a practitioner who has successfully entered the attainment of the cessation of perception and feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a monk whose taints are destroyed, the mental defilements of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>greed, hatred, and delusion</b></a> have completely ceased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a process of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>successive stilling</b></a> (vūpasama) of formations, where speech is stilled in the first jhāna and thought and examination are stilled in the second? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>six tranquillities</b></a> (passaddhi), ranging from the tranquillity of speech in the first jhāna to the tranquillity of greed, hatred, and delusion in one who has destroyed the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>neutral feelings</b></a> (neither-pleasant-nor-painful) are ultimately classified as suffering because of the inherent nature of all formations to change and pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (khīṇāsava) as the state where greed, hatred, and delusion are finally and fully tranquillized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.11/en/sujato">SN 36.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the various feelings arising in the body to the many different <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.12/en/sujato"><b>winds blowing</b></a> in the sky, coming from the east, west, north, and south? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.12/en/sujato">SN 36.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as some winds are dusty and others dustless, some hot and others cold, so too do pleasant, painful, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.12/en/sujato"><b>neutral feelings</b></a> arise within the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.12/en/sujato">SN 36.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is ardent and does not neglect clear comprehension can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.12/en/sujato"><b>fully understand</b></a> all feelings that arise within the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.12/en/sujato">SN 36.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a practitioner fully understands feelings, they become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.12/en/sujato"><b>taintless</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.12/en/sujato">SN 36.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a wise person who understands feeling as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.12/en/sujato"><b>knower of the Vedas</b></a> (vedagū) who, standing in the Dhamma, does not come to be reckoned after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.12/en/sujato">SN 36.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>three types of feeling</b></a> to Venerable Ananda as pleasant feeling, painful feeling, and neither-pleasant-nor-painful feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the origin of all feelings is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a>, and that feeling ceases specifically when contact ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>—starting with right view and ending with right concentration—as the way leading to the cessation of feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the gratification of feeling as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>pleasure and joy</b></a> that arises depending on that feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the danger of feeling lies in the fact that feeling is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>impermanent and suffering</b></a>, possessing a nature that is subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the escape from feeling is the removal and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>abandonment of desire</b></a> and lust for feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a practitioner who has successfully attained the first jhāna, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>speech has ceased</b></a> as part of the successive cessation of formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that for a monk who has destroyed the taints, the underlying mental defilements of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>greed, hatred, and delusion</b></a> have completely ceased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a process of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>successive stilling</b></a> of formations, noting that speech is stilled in the first jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a practitioner who has attained the base of infinite space, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>perception of forms</b></a> has been tranquilized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the perception of the base of infinite space is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>tranquilized</b></a> for a practitioner who enters and dwells in the base of infinite consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the perception of the base of infinite consciousness is tranquilized for one who attains the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>base of nothingness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the perception of the base of nothingness is tranquilized for one who attains the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato"><b>perception and feeling</b></a> are tranquilized for one who has entered and dwells in the cessation of perception and feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.15/en/sujato">SN 36.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato"><b>three types of feeling</b></a>, which are pleasant feeling, painful feeling, and neither-pleasant-nor-painful feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato">SN 36.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato"><b>origin of feeling</b></a> is contact, meaning that when contact ceases, feeling also ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato">SN 36.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>—consisting of factors from right view to right concentration—to be the practice that leads to the cessation of feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato">SN 36.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato"><b>gratification</b></a> of feeling as the pleasure and joy that arises depending on that feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato">SN 36.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> of feeling is its inherent nature of being impermanent, suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato">SN 36.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> from feeling is found in the removal and abandonment of desire and lust for it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato">SN 36.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato"><b>successive cessation</b></a> of formations, noting that for one who has attained the first jhāna, speech has ceased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato">SN 36.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a process for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato"><b>successive stilling</b></a> of formations, where different mental and physical activities become quieted as one progresses through higher states of meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato">SN 36.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha outlined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato"><b>six tranquillities</b></a>, stating that for a practitioner who reaches the fourth jhāna, in-and-out breathing becomes tranquilized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.17/en/sujato">SN 36.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the Dhamma through various <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>modes of presentation</b></a>, explaining that feelings can be classified as two, three, five, six, eighteen, thirty-six, or even one hundred and eight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the carpenter Pañcakaṅga disagreed with Venerable Udāyī, insisting that the Buddha taught only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>two feelings</b></a>—pleasant and painful—rather than three? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that those who do not concede to what is well-spoken by others regarding the different modes of the Dhamma will likely live in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>strife and quarrels</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that practitioners who accept the Dhamma taught in its various modes will dwell in harmony and concord, "blending like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>milk and water</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasure</b></a> as the pleasure and joy that arises dependent on the five cords of sensual pleasure, such as agreeable forms and sounds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a> as forms, sounds, odors, flavors, and tangibles that are desirable, charming, and liked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>first jhāna</b></a>, which is born of seclusion and accompanied by thought and examination, is a pleasure more excellent than sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>second jhāna</b></a> is more sublime than the first because it features internal confidence and singleness of mind without thought or examination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>third jhāna</b></a> as a superior pleasure where one dwells equanimous and mindful, experiencing a happiness that the Noble Ones praise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a> is an even more excellent pleasure, characterized by neither-pain-nor-pleasure and the purity of equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite space</b></a>, reached by transcending all perceptions of form and sensory impingement, is a higher pleasure than the fourth jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception</b></a> is an even more refined and excellent state of pleasure than the base of nothingness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception and feeling</b></a> to be the most sublime and excellent pleasure possible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha anticipated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>other sectarians</b></a> might question how the cessation of perception and feeling can be called "pleasure" if no feeling is experienced there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that he does not describe a state as "pleasure" solely based on pleasant feeling, but rather <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>wherever pleasure is found</b></a>, he describes it as such? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda reported the entire conversation between the carpenter Pañcakaṅga and Venerable Udāyī to the Buddha after <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>overhearing their dispute</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that both Pañcakaṅga and Udāyī were correct in their own way, as they were referring to different <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>valid presentations</b></a> of his teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the carpenter Pañcakaṅga argued that what others call "neither-pleasant-nor-painful feeling" was actually described by the Buddha as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>peaceful and sublime</b></a> kind of pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>first jhāna</b></a> involves rapture and pleasure that is specifically "born of seclusion"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>second jhāna</b></a> involves a "singleness of mind" (ekodibhāva) that is born of concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>third jhāna</b></a> as the state where the Noble Ones say: "He is equanimous, mindful, one who dwells happily"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a> is reached through the abandoning of both pleasure and pain and the previous disappearance of joy and grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite space</b></a> is attained by the non-attention to "perceptions of diversity" (nānattasaññā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>complete surmounting</b></a> of the base of nothingness as the prerequisite for entering the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma should be used to bring people together so they look at each other with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>affectionate eyes</b></a> rather than arguing over terminology? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a>, there is a "purity of mindfulness" that is specifically brought about by equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.19/en/sujato">SN 36.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between different levels of mental states, specifically identifying those that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>carnal</b></a> (sāmisa) and those that are spiritual (nirāmisa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>carnal rapture</b></a> (sāmisā pīti) is the joy that arises specifically in dependence on the five cords of sensual pleasure, such as agreeable forms and sounds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>spiritual rapture</b></a> (nirāmisā pīti) as the joy experienced by a monk who has entered the first or second jhāna, characterized by seclusion and concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a state called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>more spiritual than spiritual rapture</b></a>, which occurs when a taintless monk reviews their mind as being liberated from greed, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>carnal happiness</b></a> (sāmisaṃ sukhaṃ) consists of the pleasure and joy that arises depending on the five cords of sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>spiritual happiness</b></a> (nirāmisaṃ sukhaṃ) as the pleasure experienced within the first, second, or third jhānas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>more spiritual than spiritual happiness</b></a> arises when a monk whose taints are destroyed reflects on how their mind is free from the roots of greed, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a> as forms, sounds, odors, flavors, and tangibles that are desirable, charming, and liked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>carnal equanimity</b></a> (sāmisā upekkhā) as the neutral feeling that arises in connection with the five cords of sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>spiritual equanimity</b></a> (nirāmisā upekkhā) is the purified mindfulness and neutral feeling reached when a monk enters the fourth jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>more spiritual than spiritual equanimity</b></a> arises for a taintless monk who contemplates their own mind's liberation from greed, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>carnal deliverance</b></a> (sāmiso vimokkho) is a type of liberation that remains connected with material form (rūpa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>spiritual deliverance</b></a> (nirāmiso vimokkho) as a type of liberation specifically connected with the formless or immaterial (arūpa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>more spiritual than spiritual deliverance</b></a> is the liberation that arises when a monk with destroyed taints reviews their mind as free from greed, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reserved the highest classification of spiritual states—"more spiritual than spiritual"—specifically for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>taintless monk</b></a> (khīṇāsava) who reflects on their own liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically linked the rapture and pleasure born of seclusion and concentration in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>first and second jhānas</b></a> to spiritual rapture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the state where a practitioner experienced a happiness the Noble Ones praise—the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>third jhāna</b></a>—under the category of spiritual happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the state beyond pleasure and pain, known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhāna</b></a>, as the source of spiritual equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the act of a liberated monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>reviewing</b></a> (paccavekkhato) their mind's freedom from greed, hatred, and delusion generates a unique and refined level of rapture and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that mental states like rapture, happiness, and equanimity have three distinct levels: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato"><b>carnal, spiritual, and more spiritual</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.31/en/sujato">SN 36.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Ānanda suggested that good friendship was half of the holy life, the Buddha corrected him by stating that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a>, good companionship, and good comradeship actually constitute the entire holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato">SN 45.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses good friendship can be expected to develop and frequently practice the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> as a direct result of that companionship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato">SN 45.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk with good friends develops <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato"><b>Right View</b></a> in a specific way that is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, and which matures in release? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato">SN 45.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the cultivation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato"><b>Right Concentration</b></a> by a practitioner with good companions must be grounded in seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, eventually maturing in release? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato">SN 45.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the entirety of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> is defined by the presence of good friendship, good companionship, and good comradeship among practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato">SN 45.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified himself as the supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato"><b>good friend</b></a>, explaining that it is by relying upon him that beings who are subject to birth are finally delivered from birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato">SN 45.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that beings subject to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a> are liberated from these conditions specifically by coming to him as their good friend and spiritual guide? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato">SN 45.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those subject to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato"><b>sorrow and lamentation</b></a> find freedom from these painful states by relying on the Buddha as their good friend? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato">SN 45.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that reliance on a good friend allows beings who are subject to pain, displeasure, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato"><b>despair</b></a> to be released from all such forms of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato">SN 45.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Venerable Ānanda that it is through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato"><b>method</b></a> of observing how beings are freed from suffering through his guidance that one can understand why good friendship is the whole of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.2/en/sujato">SN 45.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when eight things—the Noble Eightfold Path—are developed and cultivated, they lead a person from the near shore to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato"><b>far shore</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato">SN 45.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that among human beings, only a few people reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato"><b>far shore</b></a>, while the vast majority of people merely run up and down the near bank? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato">SN 45.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who follow the Dhamma when it is well-expounded will cross over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato"><b>realm of Death</b></a>, a crossing that is otherwise extremely difficult to achieve? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato">SN 45.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the wise person to leave the dark state behind and instead develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato"><b>bright state</b></a> by moving from the home life into homelessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato">SN 45.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person should seek delight in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a>—a state where it is often considered difficult to find joy—after they have given up sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato">SN 45.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a wise person should strive to cleanse themselves of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato"><b>mental defilements</b></a> by abandoning all sensual desires and having no possessions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato">SN 45.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those whose minds are well-developed in the factors of enlightenment and who delight in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato"><b>relinquishing grasping</b></a> are the ones who reach final liberation and are extinguished in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.34/en/sujato">SN 45.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his monks to tell wanderers of other sects that the holy life is lived under his guidance specifically for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.41/en/sujato"><b>fading away of lust</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.41/en/sujato">SN 45.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked if there is a path for the fading away of lust, the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.41/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> as the specific way and practice to achieve that goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.41/en/sujato">SN 45.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.50/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in virtue</b></a> to the dawn, describing it as the forerunner and omen for the arising of the Noble Eightfold Path in a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.50/en/sujato">SN 45.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.51/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in desire</b></a> (the zeal to practice) as a primary precursor and sign that leads to the development and cultivation of the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.51/en/sujato">SN 45.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.54/en/sujato"><b>accomplishment in heedfulness</b></a> is an essential forerunner, much like the dawn appearing before the sun rises, for the arising of the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.54/en/sujato">SN 45.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta told the Buddha that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a>, good companionship, and good comradeship actually constitute the entire holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato">SN 45.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha fully endorsed Venerable Sāriputta’s statement, confirming that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a>, good companionship, and good comradeship are indeed the entirety of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato">SN 45.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> can be expected to develop and frequently practice the Noble Eightfold Path as a result of that companionship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato">SN 45.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk with good friends develops <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato"><b>Right View</b></a> in a way that is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, and which matures in release? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato">SN 45.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk with good companions develops <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato"><b>Right Concentration</b></a> by basing it upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, ultimately maturing in release? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato">SN 45.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified himself as the supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato"><b>good friend</b></a>, stating that by relying upon him, beings who are subject to birth are delivered from birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato">SN 45.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Venerable Sāriputta that beings subject to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a> are liberated from these states by relying upon the Buddha as their spiritual guide and good friend? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato">SN 45.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those subject to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato"><b>sorrow and lamentation</b></a> find freedom from these painful conditions through their reliance on the Buddha as a good friend? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato">SN 45.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that reliance on a good friend allows beings who are subject to pain, displeasure, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato"><b>despair</b></a> to be released from all such forms of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato">SN 45.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Venerable Sāriputta that the fact that beings are freed from suffering through his guidance is the specific reason why <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> is considered the whole of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.3/en/sujato">SN 45.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda witnessed the Brahmin Jāṇussoṇi driving out of Sāvatthī in a magnificent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>all-white chariot</b></a>, featuring white horses, white ornaments, and even a white sunshade? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the public saw the Brahmin Jāṇussoṇi’s impressive white carriage, they described it as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>divine vehicle</b></a> (brahmayāna) because of its celestial appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>divine vehicle</b></a> is actually a designation for the Noble Eightfold Path, rather than a physical chariot? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the Noble Eightfold Path as the supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma vehicle</b></a> (dhammayāna) that leads practitioners to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the Noble Eightfold Path as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed victory in battle</b></a>, signifying its power to overcome spiritual defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>Right View</b></a>, when fully developed and cultivated, results in the complete removal of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the frequent practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>Right Intention</b></a> (Sammāsaṅkappa) leads a practitioner to the final end of lust and aversion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>Right Speech</b></a> is a critical component of the spiritual vehicle that culminates in the destruction of delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>Right Action</b></a>, when cultivated, has the removal of lust, hatred, and delusion as its ultimate destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>Right Livelihood</b></a> as one of the eight factors that make up the divine vehicle leading out of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>Right Effort</b></a> is a necessary part of the Dhamma vehicle that concludes with the fading away of all mental defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>Right Mindfulness</b></a> is a factor of the unsurpassed victory in battle that leads to the removal of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>Right Concentration</b></a> (Sammāsamādhi) is the factor that brings the development of the Noble Eightfold Path to its final goal of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a chariot metaphor to explain the holy life, where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>faith and wisdom</b></a> are qualities yoked together to lead the way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Buddha's spiritual chariot metaphor, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>sense of shame</b></a> (hirī) acts as the pole and the mind acts as the connecting strap? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described mindfulness as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>watchful charioteer</b></a> who safely guides the divine vehicle of the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> (sīla) to the protective equipment of the chariot, while jhana acts as the axle and energy as the wheels? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> (upekkhā) as the "yoke-load concentration" that keeps the spiritual journey balanced and steady? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>weapons</b></a> such as non-ill will, non-harming, and seclusion are used by the wise to achieve victory over the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato"><b>patience</b></a> (titikkhā) serves as the protective armor for those traveling toward the supreme security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.4/en/sujato">SN 45.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> by not only listing its factors but also providing a detailed analysis (vibhaṅga) of each component for his followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Right View</b></a> specifically as the knowledge of suffering, the knowledge of the origin of suffering, the knowledge of the cessation of suffering, and the knowledge of the way leading to the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the "knowledge of suffering" is one of the essential components that constitutes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Right View</b></a> within the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that having knowledge of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> is a fundamental requirement for a practitioner to possess Right View? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Right Intention</b></a> consists of three specific qualities: the intention of renunciation, the intention of non-ill will, and the intention of harmlessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Right Speech</b></a> as the deliberate abstinence from four verbal wrongdoings: lying, malicious speech, harsh speech, and idle chatter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Right Action</b></a> is practiced by abstaining from the destruction of life, from taking what is not given, and from sexual misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Right Livelihood</b></a> involves a noble disciple completely abandoning wrong forms of livelihood and earning their living through a righteous and ethical trade? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Right Effort</b></a> as a fourfold process where a monk generates desire and exerts energy to prevent unarisen evil unwholesome states from ever arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Right Effort</b></a> includes the persistent striving to abandon evil unwholesome states that have already arisen within the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Right Effort</b></a> as the mental resolve and exertion used to bring about the arising of wholesome states that have not yet arisen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Right Effort</b></a> involves the cultivation, maintenance, and perfection of wholesome states that are already present in a practitioner's mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Right Mindfulness</b></a> as dwelling as a contemplator of the body, feelings, mind, and phenomena, while remaining ardent, clearly aware, and mindful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Right Mindfulness</b></a> by successfully removing covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world while contemplating their own mind and feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>Right Concentration</b></a> is achieved through the progressive development of the four meditative jhanas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>first jhana</b></a> as a state born of seclusion from sensual pleasures and unwholesome states, accompanied by thought, examination, rapture, and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>second jhana</b></a> is characterized by internal confidence, singleness of mind, and rapture and happiness born of concentration, entirely free from thought and examination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>third jhana</b></a> experiences a physical happiness that the noble ones describe as "dwelling happily with equanimity and mindfulness"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhana</b></a> as a state of neither-pain-nor-pleasure, where mindfulness is purified specifically by the presence of equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.8/en/sujato">SN 45.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Lomasakaṅgiya, trainees who have not yet reached the goal but are striving for the unsurpassed security from bondage dwell having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato"><b>abandoned the five hindrances</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato">SN 54.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Lomasakaṅgiya explained that for Arahants, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato"><b>five hindrances</b></a> are not just abandoned but are cut off at the root like a palm stump, so they are no longer subject to future arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato">SN 54.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once spent a three-month rainy season retreat in the Icchānaṅgala forest grove and requested that no one approach him except the person who brought his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato"><b>alms-food</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato">SN 54.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told his monks that he spent the bulk of his three-month retreat dwelling in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato"><b>concentration by mindfulness of breathing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato">SN 54.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if one were to speak rightly of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato"><b>noble dwelling</b></a>, one could rightly refer to the concentration by mindfulness of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato">SN 54.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the practice of mindfulness of breathing as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato"><b>divine dwelling</b></a> (brahmavihāro)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato">SN 54.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the concentration by mindfulness of breathing as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata’s dwelling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato">SN 54.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for trainees who have not yet reached the goal, the concentration by mindfulness of breathing, when developed and cultivated, leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato">SN 54.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for Arahants who have already finished their task, the Buddha explained that the concentration by mindfulness of breathing leads to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwelling</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato">SN 54.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that practicing the concentration by mindfulness of breathing helps Arahants maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato">SN 54.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his practice of mindfulness of breathing as breathing in and out while being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato"><b>ever mindful</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato">SN 54.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while dwelling in the concentration by mindfulness of breathing, the Buddha would clearly understand when he was breathing in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato"><b>long breath</b></a> or a short breath? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato">SN 54.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s practice of mindfulness of breathing included training to breathe in and out while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato"><b>contemplating relinquishment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.12/en/sujato">SN 54.12</a></p></div><hr>
