<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that mindfulness of breathing involves training to breathe in and out while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.1/en/sujato"><b>experiencing rapture</b></a> and pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.1/en/sujato">SN 54.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should train to breathe in and out while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.1/en/sujato"><b>tranquillizing the mental formation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.1/en/sujato">SN 54.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's final instruction in the sixteen steps of mindfulness of breathing is to train while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.1/en/sujato"><b>contemplating relinquishment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.1/en/sujato">SN 54.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered this specific teaching while staying at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>Migāramātā’s Mansion</b></a> in Savatthi, seated in the open air and surrounded by a large assembly of monks during a full-moon night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by a monk, the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates subject to clinging</b></a> as the aggregates of form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the five aggregates subject to clinging are fundamentally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>rooted in desire</b></a> (chanda)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that clinging is neither identical to the five aggregates nor separate from them, but rather it is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>desire and lust</b></a> (chandarāga) for those aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there can be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>diversity of desire and lust</b></a> regarding the five aggregates, such as when a person wishes to possess a specific kind of form, feeling, perception, volitional formation, or consciousness in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>aggregate</b></a> as any instance of that category—whether past, future, or present; internal or external; gross or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>four great elements</b></a> are the cause and condition for the description of the aggregate of form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> (phassa) as the primary cause and condition for the manifestation of the aggregates of feeling, perception, and volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> (nāmarūpa) is the essential cause and condition for the description of the aggregate of consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>identity view</b></a> (sakkāyadiṭṭhi) arises when an untaught ordinary person regards any of the five aggregates as self, or self as possessing the aggregate, or the aggregate as in the self, or the self as in the aggregate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a well-taught noble disciple <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>avoids identity view</b></a> by not regarding form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, or consciousness as self or related to a self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>gratification</b></a> of the five aggregates as the pleasure and joy that arises in dependence on them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> of the five aggregates as the fact that they are impermanent, suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> from the five aggregates is the removal and abandonment of desire and lust for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for one who sees with correct wisdom that no aggregate is "mine," "I," or "my self," there are no more underlying tendencies to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>I-making and mine-making</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed a monk's private thought questioning how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>deeds done by non-self</b></a> could ever affect a self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>misguided man</b></a> might think he can overstep the teacher's instruction by using a mind dominated by craving to question the nature of non-self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a method of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>cross-examination</b></a> to lead the monks to understand the nature of the aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked the monks if the five aggregates were permanent or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>, to which they correctly replied they were impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that whatever is impermanent is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> (dukkha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that it is not fit to regard anything that is impermanent, suffering, and subject to change as "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>this is mine</b></a>, this I am, this is my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged viewing all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>past, future, and present</b></a> aggregates with correct wisdom as "not mine"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple who sees the aggregates as they really are experiences <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato"><b>revulsion</b></a> (nibbidā) toward them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.82/en/sujato">SN 22.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s paternal cousin, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Tissa</b></a>, once confessed to his fellow monks that he was dissatisfied with the holy life and felt his mind was plagued by doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for someone who has not yet abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>lust and desire</b></a> for the five aggregates (form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness), sorrow and despair will inevitably arise when those aggregates change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that sorrow and lamentation do not arise when the five aggregates change, provided that a person has already <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>discarded lust</b></a> and craving for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a simile of a man <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>unskilled in the way</b></a> to represent the "worldling" (puthujjana), who is someone that does not know the correct path to spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the Tathagata—the Fully Enlightened One—as the person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>skilled in the way</b></a> and capable of giving accurate directions to those seeking the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a detailed simile about a journey, the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>fork in the road</b></a> as a metaphor for the state of "doubt" (vicikicchā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned against taking the "left-hand path," which he defined as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>wrong eightfold path</b></a> consisting of wrong view, wrong intention, and other incorrect practices? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to take the "right-hand path," which is a specific designation for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> leading to the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> (avijjā) to a "thick wood" that a traveler must successfully navigate to find liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>great marsh</b></a> to represent the trapping and dangerous nature of "sensual pleasures" (kāmā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>anger and despair</b></a> (kodhūpāyāsa) to a "deep abyss" or a "precipice" that poses a significant danger to those on the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ultimate goal of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>Nibbana</b></a> as a "delightful level ground" reached after navigating through various spiritual obstacles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Tissa felt dejected, he described his condition to the Buddha by saying his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>body felt heavy</b></a> and unresponsive, as if he were drugged? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha addressed Venerable Tissa’s struggle with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> (thinamiddha), which had overwhelmed Tissa's mind and hindered his ability to perceive the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa told the Buddha that "the quarters were not clear" to him, indicating a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>mental disorientation</b></a> where the Dhamma no longer seemed clear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a method of questioning to help Venerable Tissa realize that the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed with Venerable Tissa that whatever is impermanent is fundamentally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> (dukkha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Venerable Tissa that because the aggregates are impermanent and suffering, it is not fit to regard them as "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>this is mine</b></a>, this I am, this is my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha offered personal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>exhortation and instruction</b></a> to Venerable Tissa specifically to help him overcome his dissatisfaction with the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically encouraged Venerable Tissa to "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato"><b>be glad</b></a>" (abhirama) and reassured him that he was there to provide guidance and assistance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.84/en/sujato">SN 22.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once pointed out the monk Mahākappina to a group of other monks, asking them if they ever noticed any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato"><b>shaking or trembling</b></a> in Mahākappina’s body while he sat in meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato">SN 54.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha asked about Mahākappina’s posture, the monks replied that whether that venerable was sitting in the middle of the assembly or alone in seclusion, they never saw any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato"><b>movement of the body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato">SN 54.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a certain type of concentration is developed and cultivated, there is neither shaking or trembling of the body nor any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato"><b>shaking or trembling of the mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato">SN 54.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato"><b>concentration by mindfulness of breathing</b></a> as the specific practice that, when developed and cultivated, leads to a state where both the body and mind remain perfectly still? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato">SN 54.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a master of meditation as a monk who is a frequent and effortless obtainer of concentration, receiving such <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato"><b>deep stillness</b></a> at will and without any difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato">SN 54.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the practice of mindfulness of breathing begins with a monk seeking out a secluded location, such as a forest, the root of a tree, or an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato"><b>empty hut</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato">SN 54.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner of mindfulness of breathing should sit down cross-legged and keep their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato"><b>body erect</b></a> while establishing mindfulness in front of them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato">SN 54.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that a meditator should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato"><b>ever mindful</b></a> when breathing in and ever mindful when breathing out as the core foundation of their training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato">SN 54.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the detailed steps of mindfulness of breathing taught by the Buddha, a monk eventually trains himself to breathe in and out while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato"><b>contemplating relinquishment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato">SN 54.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of Mahākappina to demonstrate that the frequent practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of breathing</b></a> results in an unwavering physical and mental state where no agitation occurs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.7/en/sujato">SN 54.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>concentration by mindfulness of breathing</b></a>, when developed and cultivated, is of great fruit and great benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to practice mindfulness of breathing in secluded places like a forest, the root of a tree, or an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>empty hut</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk practicing mindfulness of breathing should sit down, fold his legs crosswise, and keep his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>body erect</b></a> while establishing mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that even before his enlightenment, while he was still an unawakened <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>Bodhisatta</b></a>, he frequently dwelt in the concentration by mindfulness of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by frequently dwelling in the concentration by mindfulness of breathing, his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>body and eyes</b></a> did not tire, and his mind was liberated from the taints through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a monk desires his mind to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>liberated from the taints</b></a> through non-clinging, he should closely attend to concentration by mindfulness of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that concentration by mindfulness of breathing should be practiced by any monk who wishes to abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>memories and intentions</b></a> based on the household life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed mindfulness of breathing can help a monk who wishes to dwell perceiving the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>repulsive in the unrepulsive</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk can train to perceive the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>unrepulsive in the repulsive</b></a> through the steady practice of mindfulness of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that mindfulness of breathing can lead a monk to perceive the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>repulsive in both</b></a> the unrepulsive and the repulsive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that through mindfulness of breathing, a monk can train himself to perceive the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>unrepulsive in both</b></a> the repulsive and the unrepulsive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk can train to avoid both the repulsive and unrepulsive and dwell in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a>, mindful and clearly comprehending, by practicing mindfulness of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that mindfulness of breathing can lead to the attainment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>first jhana</b></a>, which is characterized by rapture and pleasure born of seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who masters mindfulness of breathing can enter and dwell in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>second jhana</b></a>, featuring internal confidence and singleness of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>third jhana</b></a>, where one dwells in equanimity and experiences happiness with the body, as an attainment reachable through mindfulness of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that mindfulness of breathing can lead to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>fourth jhana</b></a>, a state of neither-pain-nor-pleasure and purity of mindfulness through equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that mindfulness of breathing allows a monk to transcend perceptions of form and reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite space</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by developing mindfulness of breathing, a monk can transcend the base of infinite space and enter the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite consciousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed a monk can reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>base of nothingness</b></a> by transcending the base of infinite consciousness through concentration by mindfulness of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that mindfulness of breathing can lead a monk to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception</b></a> after transcending the base of nothingness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the highest reach of concentration by mindfulness of breathing is the attainment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>cessation of perception and feeling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato"><b>oil lamp</b></a> going out when its fuel is exhausted to describe how all feelings become "cool" and cease upon the breakup of the body for one who has mastered mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.8/en/sujato">SN 54.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a bhikkhu sees the eye as it really is—as impermanent—this realization constitutes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.156/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.156/en/sujato">SN 35.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the destruction of delight leads to the destruction of lust, and conversely, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.156/en/sujato"><b>destruction of lust</b></a> leads to the destruction of delight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.156/en/sujato">SN 35.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a mind as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.156/en/sujato"><b>well liberated</b></a> when both delight and lust have been completely destroyed through seeing the impermanence of the sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.156/en/sujato">SN 35.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that seeing the tongue as it truly is—<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.156/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>—is the path for a bhikkhu to achieve right view and subsequent revulsion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.156/en/sujato">SN 35.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mind itself is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.156/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>, and that seeing it this way is necessary for a bhikkhu to develop right view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.156/en/sujato">SN 35.156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha advised the Venerable Mahākoṭṭhika that one should abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.163/en/sujato"><b>desire</b></a> for whatever is suffering, specifically identifying the eye as a source of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.163/en/sujato">SN 35.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha instructed Mahākoṭṭhika that because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.163/en/sujato"><b>forms</b></a> are suffering, one must abandon all desire and lust toward them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.163/en/sujato">SN 35.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.163/en/sujato"><b>eye-consciousness</b></a> is suffering, and therefore a practitioner should abandon desire for it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.163/en/sujato">SN 35.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained to Mahākoṭṭhika that any feeling—whether pleasant, painful, or neither-painful-nor-pleasant—arising from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.163/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact</b></a> is suffering and should be met with the abandonment of desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.163/en/sujato">SN 35.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified the mind as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.163/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> and urged Mahākoṭṭhika to abandon desire for it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.163/en/sujato">SN 35.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha clarified that because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.163/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> are suffering, desire for them must be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.163/en/sujato">SN 35.163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.165/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> is abandoned when one knows and sees the eye and forms as impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.165/en/sujato">SN 35.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught a bhikkhu that knowing and seeing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.165/en/sujato"><b>eye-consciousness</b></a> and eye-contact as impermanent is how wrong view is successfully abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.165/en/sujato">SN 35.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that knowing and seeing any feeling arising from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.165/en/sujato"><b>mind-contact</b></a> as impermanent is the method for abandoning wrong view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.165/en/sujato">SN 35.165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha declared that all external sense bases, including sounds, odors, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.225/en/sujato"><b>tastes</b></a>, are impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.225/en/sujato">SN 35.225</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that by seeing external phenomena like tactile objects and mental phenomena as impermanent, a practitioner becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.225/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> and reaches liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.225/en/sujato">SN 35.225</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that while ordinary people call a great body of water an ocean, in the Noble One's Discipline the true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.228/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a> is an ocean because its rushing impetus consists of forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.228/en/sujato">SN 35.228</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described crossing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.228/en/sujato"><b>ocean of the eye</b></a> as successfully enduring the rushing impetus of forms, along with its metaphorical waves, whirlpools, sharks, and demons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.228/en/sujato">SN 35.228</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.228/en/sujato"><b>tongue</b></a> as an ocean whose rushing impetus is made of tastes, and he stated that one who endures this impetus has reached the shore and stands on dry land? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.228/en/sujato">SN 35.228</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.228/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> is an ocean in the Noble One's Discipline, with mental phenomena acting as the rushing impetus that a practitioner must learn to endure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.228/en/sujato">SN 35.228</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a person who has crossed the difficult-to-traverse sensory ocean is called a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.228/en/sujato"><b>world-ender</b></a> who has lived the holy life and gone to the far shore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.228/en/sujato">SN 35.228</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the true "ocean" in his discipline consists of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato"><b>forms cognizable by the eye</b></a> that are agreeable, pleasing, charming, and provocative of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato">SN 35.229</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the world of devas and humans is mostly submerged in the ocean of sensory objects, becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato"><b>tangled like a matted ball</b></a> of thread and unable to escape the round of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato">SN 35.229</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the sensory "ocean" includes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> that are agreeable, charming, and connected with sensual desire, causing the world to remain stuck in suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato">SN 35.229</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha declared that a practitioner who has successfully faded out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato"><b>lust, hatred, and ignorance</b></a> has crossed the dangerous and fearful ocean of sensory attraction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato">SN 35.229</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ocean of agreeable sensory objects as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato"><b>difficult to traverse</b></a> and full of sharks and demons, yet it can be overcome by those who have surpassed all attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato">SN 35.229</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that by abandoning suffering and ensuring there is no further renewal of being, a person is said to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato"><b>befuddled the King of Death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato">SN 35.229</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that those who remain submerged in the "ocean" of agreeable sensory objects are unable to transcend <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato"><b>samsara</b></a>, the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.229/en/sujato">SN 35.229</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how an uninstructed worldling who experiences a painful feeling suffers twice, as if they were struck by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>two darts</b></a>—one physical and one mental? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a well-taught noble disciple, when touched by a painful feeling, does not sorrow or lament, and is therefore struck by only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>one dart</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an uninstructed worldling reacts to physical pain by weeping and becoming distraught, which causes them to experience both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>bodily and mental</b></a> pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that when an uninstructed worldling is touched by a painful feeling, they harbor resistance toward it, and thus the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to aversion</b></a> regarding that pain lies within them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha observed that an uninstructed worldling seeks delight in sensual pleasure when in pain because they do not know any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>escape from painful feeling</b></a> other than sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when someone delights in sensual pleasure to escape pain, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to lust</b></a> regarding pleasant feeling lies within them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that an uninstructed worldling does not understand the origin, the passing away, the gratification, the danger, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>escape regarding feelings</b></a> as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that for one who does not understand feelings as they really are, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to ignorance</b></a> regarding neither-painful-nor-pleasant feelings persists within them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described an uninstructed worldling as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>fettered to feelings</b></a>, whether they are experiencing pleasure, pain, or a neutral state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha said that a person who is fettered to feelings is consequently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>fettered to birth</b></a>, aging, death, and all forms of sorrow and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a well-taught noble disciple, when experiencing physical pain, remains calm and composed, thereby feeling only the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>bodily feeling</b></a> and not the mental feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a well-taught noble disciple does not harbor resistance toward pain, which prevents the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to aversion</b></a> from taking root? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a well-taught noble disciple does not seek sensual pleasure as an escape from pain because they understand an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>escape from feelings</b></a> that is independent of sensual gratification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that because a noble disciple does not delight in sensual pleasure, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to lust</b></a> regarding pleasant feelings is eventually abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a noble disciple who truly understands the origin and passing away of feelings is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>unfettered by ignorance</b></a> when experiencing neither-painful-nor-pleasant feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha declared that a well-taught noble disciple is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>detached from suffering</b></a>, which includes liberation from the cycle of birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that for a wise person who has mastered the Dhamma, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>agreeable things</b></a> do not churn or agitate the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha said that a wise person does not feel resistance toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>disagreeable things</b></a>, as they have moved beyond both attraction and repulsion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the enlightened person as someone who has understood the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>spotless and sorrowless state</b></a> and has successfully reached the further shore of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato"><b>great difference</b></a> between a wise person and an ordinary person as the ability to experience feelings without being bound or overwhelmed by them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.6/en/sujato">SN 36.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk should await his time of death being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>mindful and clearly comprehending</b></a>, calling this his specific instruction to his followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined a monk as being mindful when he dwells contemplating the body, feelings, mind, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>phenomena</b></a>, while remaining ardent and clearly comprehending? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk is "clearly comprehending" when he acts with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>full awareness</b></a> during daily activities such as walking, looking, stretching, and even eating or drinking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha instructed that a monk should maintain clear comprehension even during basic physical functions like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>excreting and urinating</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when a mindful monk experiences a pleasant feeling, he understands it has arisen in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>dependence on the body</b></a>, rather than being causeless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that because the physical body is impermanent, conditioned, and dependently arisen, any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>pleasant feeling</b></a> that arises based on it cannot possibly be permanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who dwells contemplating the impermanence and fading away of pleasant feelings is able to abandon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to lust</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that if a monk understands that painful feelings arise in dependence on an impermanent body, he can successfully abandon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to aversion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that by contemplating the cessation and relinquishment of neither-painful-nor-pleasant feelings, a practitioner abandons the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described an advanced practitioner as someone who feels a pleasant, painful, or neutral feeling but remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>detached from it</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a monk to recognize when they are experiencing a feeling that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>limited by the body</b></a>, understanding its boundaries clearly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a practitioner should understand when they are experiencing a feeling that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>limited by life</b></a> as they approach the end of their lifespan? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the simile of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>oil lamp</b></a> that burns in dependence on oil and a wick to illustrate how feelings depend on the physical body to exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that just as an oil lamp goes out when its fuel is exhausted, feelings will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>become cool right here</b></a> after the breakup of the body for one who does not delight in them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha highlighted that a monk is mindful only when he has removed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>covetousness and displeasure</b></a> regarding the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk should practice clear comprehension even when performing simple movements like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>bending and stretching</b></a> his limbs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a monk should remain clearly comprehending while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>falling asleep</b></a> and while waking up to maintain constant awareness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a well-practiced monk understands that a pleasant feeling is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a> and should not be held to or relished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk who contemplates the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>vanishing of feelings</b></a> (vayānupassī) is practicing in a way that leads to the total abandonment of latent defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described the state after death for an enlightened one as a state where all that is felt, being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>unrelished</b></a>, simply settles into coolness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that clear comprehension must be maintained even while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato"><b>speaking or keeping silent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn36.7/en/sujato">SN 36.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a wrongly directed path of practice to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato"><b>spike of rice</b></a> or barley that, if stepped on, could never pierce the hand or foot or draw blood because of its poor orientation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato">SN 45.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught it is impossible for a monk with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato"><b>wrongly directed view</b></a> and wrongly directed path development to shatter ignorance, arouse true knowledge, and realize Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato">SN 45.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato"><b>rightly directed spike</b></a> of rice or barley to illustrate how a monk with the correct orientation of practice can successfully pierce through ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato">SN 45.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is entirely possible for a monk to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato"><b>shatter ignorance</b></a> and arouse true knowledge if they possess a rightly directed view and engage in rightly directed path development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato">SN 45.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato"><b>realize Nibbana</b></a> specifically through the power of a rightly directed view combined with the development of the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato">SN 45.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a monk to succeed in his practice, he must develop factors like Right View and Right Concentration while they are based upon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a>, dispassion, and cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato">SN 45.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the development of the Noble Eightfold Path must ultimately be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato"><b>maturing in release</b></a> in order for a practitioner to arouse true knowledge and achieve liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato">SN 45.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that path development must be based on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> (virāga) and cessation (nirodha) for it to lead to the shattering of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.9/en/sujato">SN 45.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once expressed a desire to the monks to go into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato"><b>solitary retreat</b></a> for a period of half a month, during which time he was not to be visited by anyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato">SN 45.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was in his half-month retreat, he requested that the only person allowed to approach him was the one who brought him his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato"><b>alms-food</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato">SN 45.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after emerging from a period of seclusion, the Buddha revealed that he had been dwelling in the same state of mind as when he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato"><b>first enlightened</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato">SN 45.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha realized through direct experience that feeling (vedayita) can arise conditioned by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato"><b>Right View</b></a> just as it can be conditioned by Wrong View? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato">SN 45.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that feeling is experienced based on various factors of the path, including <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato"><b>Wrong Concentration</b></a> and Right Concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato">SN 45.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that feeling occurs when internal factors such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato"><b>desire</b></a> (chanda), thought (vitakka), and perception (saññā) have not yet been stilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato">SN 45.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that even when desire, thought, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato"><b>perception are stilled</b></a>, feeling can still be experienced as a result of that stilled condition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato">SN 45.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there is a certain effort or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato"><b>stretching out</b></a> required for the attainment of what has not yet been attained, and that feeling arises based on that very pursuit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn45.11/en/sujato">SN 45.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the dawn, as the forerunner of the sun’s rising, to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.12/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a>, which serves as the precursor for the arising of the seven factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.12/en/sujato">SN 46.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses good friends can be expected to develop and cultivate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.12/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.12/en/sujato">SN 46.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk develops the seven factors of enlightenment, such as mindfulness and equanimity, by relying on seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, while ultimately <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.12/en/sujato"><b>maturing in release</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.12/en/sujato">SN 46.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a monk with good friendship, the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.12/en/sujato"><b>enlightenment factor of equanimity</b></a> is to be expected as part of his spiritual progression? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.12/en/sujato">SN 46.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally visited <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahakassapa</b></a> at the Pipphali Cave when the latter was afflicted with a grave illness and experiencing great pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato">SN 46.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha showed concern for the sick by asking Venerable Mahakassapa if his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato"><b>painful feelings</b></a> were subsiding or if they were instead increasing and not tolerable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato">SN 46.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked about his condition, Venerable Mahakassapa informed the Buddha that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato"><b>pains were increasing</b></a> and that their subsiding was not apparent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato">SN 46.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the seven factors of enlightenment are "well expounded" and, when developed, lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a>, enlightenment, and Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato">SN 46.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato"><b>enlightenment factor of mindfulness</b></a> is one of the seven factors he has perfectly taught for the purpose of achieving liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato">SN 46.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha visited the ailing Mahakassapa in the evening, immediately after emerging from his own period of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato">SN 46.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the Buddha finished teaching the seven factors of enlightenment, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahakassapa</b></a> delighted in his words and was immediately cured of his grave illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato">SN 46.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the seven factors of enlightenment, including the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato"><b>enlightenment factor of equanimity</b></a>, are specifically designed to lead a practitioner toward Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.14/en/sujato">SN 46.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Yamaka initially held the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>pernicious view</b></a> that an arahant whose taints are destroyed is annihilated and perishes with the breakup of the body, failing to exist after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that several monks urged Ven. Yamaka not to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>misrepresent the Blessed One</b></a>, as the Buddha would never say that a monk whose taints are destroyed is annihilated upon the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta visited Ven. Yamaka to help him abandon his wrong view after other monks were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>unable to dissuade</b></a> him from his position regarding the post-mortem status of an arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta began his instruction to Yamaka by asking whether the five aggregates, starting with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>material form</b></a>, are permanent or impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta questioned Yamaka on whether <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> is permanent or impermanent, leading him to conclude that what is impermanent is suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta clarified that because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> is impermanent and suffering, it is not fit to be regarded as "This is mine, this I am, this is my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> are impermanent, and seeing this leads an instructed noble disciple to become disenchanted with them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is impermanent, and through disenchantment with it, one becomes dispassionate and liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta led Yamaka to admit that he does not regard <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>form as the Tathāgata</b></a>, dismantling the identification of the person with the physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta asked Yamaka if he regarded the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata as in feeling</b></a>, or as apart from feeling, to show the Tathāgata's independence from mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta questioned if the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata is apart from perception</b></a>, volitional formations, or consciousness, showing that the Tathāgata cannot be defined by these aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta asked whether the Tathāgata is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>collection of the five aggregates</b></a> (form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness), to which Yamaka replied "No"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta questioned if the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata is someone without</b></a> form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, or consciousness, illustrating that the Tathāgata is not found outside of experience either? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta argued that since the Tathāgata is not found as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>truth or reality</b></a> even in this present life, it is improper to claim he is annihilated after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Yamaka, after hearing Sāriputta's teaching, realized that when an arahant dies, it is simply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>impermanent suffering</b></a> that has ceased and come to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>murderous henchman</b></a> who disguises himself as a devoted servant to illustrate how the five aggregates can be dangerous when wrongly clung to as a self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in Sāriputta's simile, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>wealthy householder</b></a> is deceived by a man who pretends to be a loyal attendant but is actually waiting for a private moment to kill him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta described the assassin as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>always a murderer</b></a>, whether he is serving the master politely or in the act of taking the master's life with a sharp sword? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta compared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>uninstructed worldling</b></a> to the deceived householder because they trust the five aggregates as being their own "self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta explained that an uninstructed person wrongly regards <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>form as self</b></a>, or the self as possessing form, or form as being in the self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta pointed out that an uninstructed person does not understand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>impermanent feeling as it really is</b></a>, leading to identification and clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that one who does not understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>conditioned nature</b></a> of volitional formations will approach and cling to them as their "self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>form as a murderer</b></a> because identifying with it leads to long-term harm and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta stated that when one clings to the five aggregates and settles upon them as "mine," it results in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>suffering for a long time</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta explained that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>instructed noble disciple</b></a>, unlike the worldling, does not regard form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, or consciousness as a self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta emphasized that a noble disciple understands <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>non-self perception</b></a> as it really is, thereby avoiding the trap of the "murderous" aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that by not clinging to the five aggregates, they become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>conducive to happiness</b></a> and welfare for a long time because they are no longer viewed as a self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while listening to Sāriputta’s discourse on the "murderous" nature of the aggregates, Ven. Yamaka’s mind was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>liberated from the taints</b></a> through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Yamaka expressed that it is a great benefit for monks to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>compassionate companions</b></a> like Ven. Sāriputta who provide such clear and life-changing instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta used the comparison of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato"><b>murderous servant</b></a> to explain that the aggregates are inherently dangerous to those who fail to see their true nature as impermanent and non-self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.85/en/sujato">SN 22.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the tempting objects of the five senses and the mind to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato"><b>baited hook</b></a> cast by a fisherman into a deep lake to trap unsuspecting fish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato">SN 35.230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato"><b>six hooks</b></a> in the world—corresponding to the six senses—that lead to the calamity, slaughter, and ruin of living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato">SN 35.230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described forms cognizable by the eye that are agreeable, pleasing, and charming as potential traps for those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato"><b>delight in them</b></a> and remain fastened to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato">SN 35.230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who welcomes and remains fastened to agreeable tastes is said to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato"><b>swallowed the hook</b></a> of Mara, the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato">SN 35.230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that any person who becomes attached to delightful mental phenomena falls into the power of Mara and becomes one who can be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato"><b>done with as he pleases</b></a> by the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato">SN 35.230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the act of "remaining fastened to" (ajjhosāya tiṭṭhati) sensual pleasures as the specific behavior that leads a person to meet with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato"><b>calamity and ruin</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato">SN 35.230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato"><b>broken the hook</b></a> of Mara if he does not delight in, welcome, or remain fastened to agreeable sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, or thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato">SN 35.230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a fish that has not swallowed a fisherman’s bait to describe a practitioner who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato"><b>not at the mercy</b></a> of the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato">SN 35.230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even though certain mental phenomena are "connected with sensual desire" and "enticing," a monk is safe from harm if he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato"><b>does not welcome</b></a> them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato">SN 35.230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that by maintaining restraint toward delightful sense objects, a practitioner effectively <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato"><b>destroys the hook</b></a> of the Mara, ensuring they do not fall into his grasp? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.230/en/sujato">SN 35.230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a woman who lives at home in the morning with a mind obsessed by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.4/en/sujato"><b>stain of stinginess</b></a> possesses one of the three qualities that often lead to rebirth in a state of misery or hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.4/en/sujato">SN 37.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a woman who dwells at home during the middle of the day with a mind obsessed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.4/en/sujato"><b>envy</b></a> is practicing a quality that leads to rebirth in the plane of misery and the lower worlds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.4/en/sujato">SN 37.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified living at home in the evening with a mind obsessed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.4/en/sujato"><b>sensual lust</b></a> as a key factor that leads a woman, after the breaking up of the body, to be reborn in a state of deprivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.4/en/sujato">SN 37.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how three specific mental states—stinginess, envy, and sensual lust—occurring at different times of the day, are the reasons why a woman might be reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.4/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.4/en/sujato">SN 37.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is very difficult for a woman who has not made merit to be born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato"><b>suitable family</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato">SN 37.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a woman who has not performed meritorious actions, it is a rare and difficult thing to marry into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato"><b>suitable family</b></a> once she has reached the appropriate age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato">SN 37.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha counted dwelling at home <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato"><b>without a co-wife</b></a> as one of the five specific things that are difficult to obtain for a woman who lacks merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato">SN 37.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the station of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato"><b>blessed with children</b></a> is a condition that is easy to attain for a woman who has made merit, but difficult for one who has not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato">SN 37.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato"><b>exercise influence over a husband</b></a> as the fifth difficult thing to achieve for a woman who has not cultivated merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato">SN 37.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that five specific stations—birth in a good family, marriage into a good family, no co-wife, having children, and influence over a husband—are all easily obtained by a woman who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato"><b>made merit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato">SN 37.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato"><b>growth in faith</b></a> as the first of five ways a female noble disciple grows in a manner that is truly beneficial? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato">SN 37.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when a female noble disciple increases her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a>, she is growing with a "noble growth" and takes hold of what is essential? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato">SN 37.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged women to increase their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato"><b>learning</b></a> (spiritual knowledge) as one of the five factors that ensure they grow in a way that provides the best for their own person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato">SN 37.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that growth in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato"><b>generosity</b></a> was highlighted by the Buddha as a key quality that makes a female noble disciple one who "takes the essence"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato">SN 37.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a female noble disciple who increases her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> grows in a noble way and secures the best possible outcome for herself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato">SN 37.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a woman who grows in faith, virtue, wisdom, generosity, and learning as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato"><b>virtuous lay follower</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato">SN 37.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato"><b>taking the essence</b></a> to describe how a virtuous woman secures the true core of benefit in this very life through her spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato">SN 37.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted the "meritless woman" with the "meritorious woman," showing that the latter finds <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato"><b>success in family life</b></a> to be a natural result of her past good deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.32/en/sujato">SN 37.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a woman's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.4/en/sujato"><b>obsessions</b></a> at different times of the day—specifically stinginess, envy, and lust—directly correlate to her future destination after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.4/en/sujato">SN 37.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by growing in five specific qualities, a woman becomes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato"><b>noble disciple</b></a> who provides what is excellent for herself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn37.34/en/sujato">SN 37.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha was once seriously ill and in pain at the Bamboo Grove, he requested Venerable Mahācunda to recite the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.16/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.16/en/sujato">SN 46.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's grave illness was cured and subsided immediately after he expressed his approval of Venerable Mahācunda’s recitation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.16/en/sujato"><b>factors of enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.16/en/sujato">SN 46.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda, addressing the Buddha, stated that the seven factors of enlightenment are well-expounded and, when developed and cultivated, lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.16/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a>, to enlightenment, and to Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.16/en/sujato">SN 46.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the seven factors of enlightenment, when developed and cultivated, are what lead a practitioner from the near shore to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato"><b>far shore</b></a> of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato">SN 46.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that only a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato"><b>few people</b></a> among human beings actually reach the far shore, while the vast majority of people merely run up and down the near bank? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato">SN 46.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who follow the Dhamma in accordance with the well-expounded Dhamma are the ones who will cross over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato"><b>realm of Death</b></a>, which is otherwise exceedingly difficult to cross? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato">SN 46.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the wise to abandon the dark state and develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato"><b>bright state</b></a> by leaving the home life for homelessness and seeking delight in seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato">SN 46.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person should give up sensual pleasures and, having nothing of their own, seek to cleanse themselves from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato"><b>defilements of the mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato">SN 46.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described those who have fully developed the factors of enlightenment and who delight in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato"><b>relinquishment of grasping</b></a> as being liberated in this very world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato">SN 46.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato"><b>destroyed the taints</b></a> and possess spiritual splendor as having attained Nibbāna right here in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.17/en/sujato">SN 46.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying in the Hall with the Peaked Roof in Vesālī, he spent much of his time giving various discourses to the monks on the subject of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>foulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once requested to enter a period of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>solitude</b></a> for half a month, during which no one was allowed to visit him except the person who brought his alms-food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because the Buddha spoke in praise of the meditation on foulness, many monks became so repelled and disgusted with their own bodies that they sought a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>weapon-wielder</b></a> to take their lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that as many as ten, twenty, or even thirty monks took their own lives in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>single day</b></a> due to their intense repulsion toward the physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha emerged from his two-week retreat, he immediately noticed that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>Sangha of bhikkhus</b></a> appeared to be significantly depleted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda explained to the Buddha that the monks had been so diligently practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>meditation on foulness</b></a> that many had chosen to commit suicide? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda requested the Buddha to teach another method of practice so that the remaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>community of monks</b></a> might be established in knowledge and safety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Venerable Ānanda to assemble all the monks living in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>vicinity of Vesālī</b></a> at the assembly hall to receive a new teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>concentration by mindfulness of breathing</b></a> is a practice that is peaceful, sublime, and unadulterated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described mindfulness of breathing as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>happy dwelling</b></a> that causes evil unwholesome states to vanish on the spot as soon as they arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the effectiveness of mindfulness of breathing to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>great rain cloud</b></a> that suddenly settles and dispels a cloud of dust raised in the last month of the hot season? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk practicing mindfulness of breathing should find a secluded spot, such as the foot of a tree or an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>empty hut</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that during breath meditation, one should sit down cross-legged and keep the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>body erect</b></a> while establishing mindfulness in front? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the core of the practice is to remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>ever mindful</b></a> while breathing in and ever mindful while breathing out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that as a monk develops concentration through the breath, he trains himself to breathe in and out while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>contemplating relinquishment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified mindfulness of breathing as a method that "makes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>evil unwholesome states</b></a> disappear" instantly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>unadulterated</b></a> (asecanako) to describe the pure and exquisite nature of the experience of concentration through mindfulness of breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s instruction on breathing meditation was given specifically to provide a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>peaceful alternative</b></a> for monks who had become overwhelmed by the practice of foulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when mindfulness of breathing is developed and cultivated, it serves as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>sublime dwelling</b></a> for the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically praised the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato"><b>meditation on foulness</b></a> before his retreat, which led to the monks' intense focus on the repulsiveness of their own bodies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn54.9/en/sujato">SN 54.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Venerable Vakkali was staying at a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>potter’s shed</b></a> while being gravely ill and in pain, he sent fellow monks to the Buddha to pay homage on his behalf and request a visit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha arrived to visit the sick Venerable Vakkali, he specifically told the monk not to struggle to rise from his bed, as there were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>prepared seats</b></a> available for him to sit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha inquired whether the sick monk Vakkali’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>painful feelings</b></a> were subsiding or increasing, to which Vakkali replied that they were only worsening and not subsiding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked the dying monk Vakkali if he had any remorse or guilt, and Vakkali admitted he felt <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>great remorse</b></a> because his physical weakness had long prevented him from coming to see the Buddha in person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha dismissed the importance of his own physical appearance, asking Vakkali what the use was of seeing his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>foul body</b></a> when the real priority should be the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha famously taught the ailing Vakkali that "he who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>sees the Dhamma</b></a> sees me; he who sees me sees the Dhamma"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Vakkali that because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of form</b></a> is impermanent and suffering, it is not fit to be regarded as "mine," "I," or "my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Vakkali that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of feeling</b></a> is impermanent and subject to change, and therefore should not be identified as one's self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Vakkali that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of perception</b></a> is not-self because it is impermanent and inherently involves suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a>—the mental factors that shape our actions—are impermanent and should not be clung to as a permanent self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of consciousness</b></a> is impermanent, and a person who sees this correctly becomes disenchanted and liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after receiving a teaching from the Buddha, the Venerable Vakkali requested to be carried to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>Black Rock</b></a> on the side of Mount Isigili, stating he did not wish to die in a residential area? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity informed the Buddha that the monk Vakkali was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>intent on liberation</b></a>, signaling his advanced spiritual state even while facing death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha sent monks to the dying Vakkali with a message of reassurance, telling him, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>Do not fear</b></a>, Vakkali, your death will not be an evil one"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the monk Vakkali heard the Buddha’s messengers approaching, he showed extreme respect by asking to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>lowered from his bed</b></a> to the ground, feeling it was inappropriate to hear the Buddha's words while sitting on a high seat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his death, Vakkali declared to the Buddha’s messengers that he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>no doubt</b></a> that the five aggregates (form, feeling, perception, formations, and consciousness) are impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the dying Vakkali stated he had no remaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>desire or lust</b></a> for things that are impermanent, suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after the Buddha’s messengers left his side, the Venerable Vakkali <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>used the knife</b></a> to end his own life while struggling with his terminal illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, upon visiting Vakkali's body, pointed out a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>dark, smoky cloud</b></a> or swirl of light that was moving in all directions around the corpse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the mysterious smoky aura near Vakkali's body as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>Mara the Evil One</b></a>, who was searching for the monk's consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his efforts, Mara could not find where Vakkali’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>consciousness was established</b></a> because the monk had attained full enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha officially declared that the "clansman Vakkali" had attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>final Nibbāna</b></a> (parinibbuto) with unestablished consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vakkali’s liberation was characterized by the Buddha as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>unestablished consciousness</b></a>, meaning he was no longer subject to further rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any monk who sees that the five aggregates are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>subject to change</b></a> and does not feel lust or affection for them is truly free from doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Vakkali’s passing as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato"><b>non-evil death</b></a> (apāpakaṃ maraṇaṃ) because he died as an arahant, fully liberated from the cycle of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.87/en/sujato">SN 22.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once declared to a thousand monks at Gayasisa that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato"><b>entire world is burning</b></a>, specifically meaning that the internal and external sense bases, such as the eye and forms, are aflame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato">SN 35.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha spoke of the senses being on fire, he explained they are burning with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato"><b>fire of lust</b></a>, the fire of hatred, and the fire of delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato">SN 35.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that every sense organ and its corresponding experience, including the tongue, tastes, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato"><b>tongue-consciousness</b></a>, are burning with the fires of birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato">SN 35.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato"><b>pleasant feeling</b></a> that arises dependent on sense-contact is "burning" because it is subject to sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato">SN 35.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha extended the metaphor of fire to the mind itself, stating that the mind, mental phenomena, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato"><b>mind-contact</b></a> are all burning with the fires of greed and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato">SN 35.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a well-taught noble disciple who perceives this "burning" nature of the senses becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato">SN 35.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a disciple becomes disenchanted with the senses, they become dispassionate, and through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a>, their mind is liberated from the cycle of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato">SN 35.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a liberated person as someone who possesses the knowledge: "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a>, the holy life has been lived, what had to be done has been done, and there is no more for this state of being"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato">SN 35.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was delivering this famous "Fire Sermon," the minds of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato"><b>thousand monks</b></a> listening were liberated from the taints through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato">SN 35.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of fire to illustrate that our internal experiences of pleasure, pain, and neutral feelings are all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato"><b>aflame</b></a> with the same existential sufferings of birth and decay? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.28/en/sujato">SN 35.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> as the destruction of lust, the destruction of hatred, and the destruction of delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato">SN 38.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>—consisting of right view, right intention, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right concentration—as the way to realize Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato">SN 38.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by the wanderer Jambukhādaka, Venerable Sāriputta stated that the holy life is lived under the Buddha for the purpose of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.4/en/sujato"><b>full understanding of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.4/en/sujato">SN 38.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that a person is considered to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.5/en/sujato"><b>attained to consolation</b></a> when they understand the origin, the passing away, the gratification, the danger, and the escape regarding the six bases of contact as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.5/en/sujato">SN 38.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato"><b>destruction of lust</b></a> is one of the essential characteristics that constitutes Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato">SN 38.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wanderer Jambukhādaka, after hearing the explanation of the path to Nibbāna, praised it to Venerable Sāriputta as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato"><b>excellent path</b></a> and an excellent way for the realization of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato">SN 38.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.4/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> is the specific practice and way leading to the full understanding of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.4/en/sujato">SN 38.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta resided among the Magadhans at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato"><b>Nālaka village</b></a> when he engaged in these profound discussions with the wanderer Jambukhādaka? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato">SN 38.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato"><b>Right View</b></a> and Right Intention as the leading factors of the Noble Eightfold Path that lead to Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato">SN 38.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that Nibbāna is attained through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato"><b>destruction of delusion</b></a> along with the destruction of lust and hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato">SN 38.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wanderer Jambukhādaka told Venerable Sāriputta that the path to Nibbāna is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato"><b>sufficient for diligence</b></a> (appamāda)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato">SN 38.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta clarified that the same <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.5/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> used to realize Nibbāna is also the way to reach consolation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.5/en/sujato">SN 38.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, one who understands the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.5/en/sujato"><b>origin and passing away</b></a> of the six bases of contact has reached a state of having "attained to consolation"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.5/en/sujato">SN 38.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta specifically listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato"><b>Right Speech</b></a>, Right Action, and Right Livelihood as the ethical components of the path to Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato">SN 38.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato"><b>Right Concentration</b></a>, supported by Right Effort and Right Mindfulness, is the culmination of the path to Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato">SN 38.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wanderer Jambukhādaka acknowledged that the path for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.4/en/sujato"><b>full understanding of suffering</b></a> is an auspicious and beneficial practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.4/en/sujato">SN 38.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato"><b>destruction of hatred</b></a> is a defining element of the Nibbāna element? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.1/en/sujato">SN 38.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta emphasized that one must know the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.5/en/sujato"><b>gratification and danger</b></a> of the six bases of contact to be truly considered one who has "attained to consolation"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.5/en/sujato">SN 38.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta insisted that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.4/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> is the only way for the full understanding of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.4/en/sujato">SN 38.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, understanding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.5/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> from the six bases of contact is a vital component of attaining consolation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.5/en/sujato">SN 38.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that frequently giving attention to things that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato"><b>bases for sensual desire</b></a> causes unarisen sensual desire to arise and existing sensual desire to increase and expand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato">SN 46.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, frequently giving attention to things that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato"><b>bases for ill will</b></a> causes unarisen ill will to arise and existing ill will to grow and reach fullness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato">SN 46.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that frequently giving attention to things that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato"><b>bases for sloth and torpor</b></a> leads to the arising of unarisen sloth and torpor and the increase of those already present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato">SN 46.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that giving frequent attention to things that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato"><b>bases for restlessness and remorse</b></a> results in the arising of these states and causes existing restlessness and remorse to further expand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato">SN 46.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that frequently giving attention to things that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato"><b>bases for doubt</b></a> causes unarisen doubt to arise and existing doubt to grow and multiply? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato">SN 46.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged frequently giving attention to things that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato"><b>bases for the mindfulness factor of enlightenment</b></a> as this leads to its arising and eventually to its fulfillment by development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato">SN 46.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that frequently giving attention to things that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato"><b>bases for the equanimity factor of enlightenment</b></a> is what allows this factor to arise and reach fulfillment through development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.23/en/sujato">SN 46.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that for one who attends inappropriately, unarisen <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a> arises and existing sensual desire increases and expands? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato">SN 46.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato"><b>inappropriate attention</b></a> is the primary cause for unarisen ill will to arise and for existing ill will to grow and reach fullness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato">SN 46.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for someone attending inappropriately, the unarisen <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness factor of enlightenment</b></a> does not arise and, if it has already arisen, it ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato">SN 46.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato"><b>inappropriate attention</b></a> prevents the unarisen equanimity factor of enlightenment from arising and causes the existing factor to cease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato">SN 46.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato"><b>appropriate attention</b></a>, unarisen sensual desire does not arise and any sensual desire that has already arisen is abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato">SN 46.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that for one who attends appropriately, unarisen <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> does not arise and existing ill will is abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato">SN 46.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato"><b>appropriate attention</b></a> ensures that unarisen doubt does not arise and that any existing doubt is abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato">SN 46.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one who attends appropriately, the unarisen <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness factor of enlightenment</b></a> arises and, once arisen, goes on to fulfillment by development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato">SN 46.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato"><b>appropriate attention</b></a> leads to the arising of the unarisen equanimity factor of enlightenment and helps it reach fulfillment through development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.24/en/sujato">SN 46.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers to encourage those for whom they have compassion—including friends, colleagues, relatives, and kinsmen—to become established in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.16/en/sujato"><b>four factors of stream-entry</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.16/en/sujato">SN 55.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when a noble disciple possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.17/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence in the Buddha</b></a>, it is impossible for them to ever be reborn in hell, the animal realm, or the sphere of afflicted spirits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.17/en/sujato">SN 55.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should settle and establish their loved ones in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.16/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence in the Dhamma</b></a> as a core part of practicing compassion toward them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.16/en/sujato">SN 55.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, while it is possible for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.17/en/sujato"><b>four great elements</b></a>—earth, water, fire, and air—to undergo change or deviation, a noble disciple with confirmed confidence in the Buddha will never undergo such a change that would lead to a lower rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.17/en/sujato">SN 55.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.16/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence in the Sangha</b></a> as one of the four essential qualities that one should help their friends and relatives to cultivate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.16/en/sujato">SN 55.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.16/en/sujato"><b>virtues dear to the noble ones</b></a> as being unbroken, unblemished, and ultimately leading to the development of concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.16/en/sujato">SN 55.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted there is "no such thing" as a noble disciple who possesses the virtues dear to the noble ones falling into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.17/en/sujato"><b>hell or the animal realm</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.17/en/sujato">SN 55.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically mentioned that we should encourage those who "think they ought to listen"—such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.16/en/sujato"><b>friends and colleagues</b></a>—to be settled in the qualities that lead to stream-entry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.16/en/sujato">SN 55.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the earth, water, fire, and air elements as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.17/en/sujato"><b>four great elements</b></a> (catunnaṃ mahābhūtānaṃ), and noted that even their fundamental nature is less stable than the destiny of a noble disciple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.17/en/sujato">SN 55.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the virtues a noble disciple should be established in are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.16/en/sujato"><b>praised by the wise</b></a> and are not grasped by wrong views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.16/en/sujato">SN 55.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.17/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence</b></a> (aveccappasāda) to describe the unwavering faith a noble disciple has in the Enlightened One, which serves as a protection against rebirth in states of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.17/en/sujato">SN 55.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha maintained that a noble disciple’s possession of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.17/en/sujato"><b>unblemished virtue</b></a> provides a certainty of progress that surpasses the stability of the physical world's elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.17/en/sujato">SN 55.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha visited the monk Assaji in Rājagaha after being told that the monk was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>afflicted, suffering, and gravely ill</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha saw the sick Assaji attempting to rise from his bed, he instructed the monk to remain still and instead sat on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>prepared seats</b></a> that were already available? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha inquired if Assaji's painful feelings were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>subsiding</b></a> and not increasing, but the monk replied that his pains were only getting worse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his physical suffering, Assaji confessed to the Buddha that he felt no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>self-reproach regarding virtue</b></a>, indicating he had maintained his moral conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha discovered Assaji was suffering from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>remorse and regret</b></a> because his illness prevented him from maintaining the bodily calm necessary to attain concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Assaji feared he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>falling away</b></a> from the path because he could no longer gain the meditative concentration he had previously practiced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that recluses who view concentration as the essence of their practice often feel they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>falling away</b></a> when they cannot attain it due to illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Assaji that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness are all impermanent and should not be regarded as "mine"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a well-taught noble disciple who sees the impermanence of the aggregates becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> and dispassionate, leading to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if a monk experiences a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>pleasant feeling</b></a>, he should understand it as impermanent and not remain attached to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that when a monk experiences a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>painful feeling</b></a>, he should recognize it as impermanent and not delight in it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that even when experiencing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>neither-painful-nor-pleasant feeling</b></a>, a monk should see it as impermanent and remain unattached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the liberated state as feeling feelings while remaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>detached</b></a> from them, whether they are pleasant, painful, or neutral? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can clearly discern when he is experiencing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>feeling limited to the body</b></a> as opposed to other types of feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can clearly discern when he is experiencing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>feeling limited to life</b></a>, understanding the nature of his own vitality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that after the breakup of the body and the end of life, all that is felt by a monk—being no longer delighted in—will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>become cool</b></a> right there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>oil lamp</b></a> to illustrate how feelings cease when their supporting conditions are exhausted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the ending of a monk's feelings to a lamp going out through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>lack of fuel</b></a> when the oil and wick are both used up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching on the ending of feelings emphasizes that with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>breakup of the body</b></a>, all experienced feelings will eventually reach a state of cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha helped the sick Assaji overcome his anxiety about losing concentration by redirecting him toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato"><b>insight into impermanence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.88/en/sujato">SN 22.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the internal sense base of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato"><b>eye is consumed</b></a> by birth, aging, death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato">SN 35.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, all external <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato"><b>forms are consumed</b></a> by birth, aging, and death, just as the sense organs that perceive them are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato">SN 35.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the mental process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato"><b>eye-consciousness</b></a> is considered "consumed" by the various forms of suffering inherent in existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato">SN 35.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato"><b>pleasant or painful feeling</b></a> that arises dependent on eye-contact is ultimately consumed by birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato">SN 35.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the tongue, the tastes it perceives, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato"><b>tongue-consciousness</b></a> are all "consumed" by the inevitability of sorrow and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato">SN 35.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the mind, mental phenomena, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato"><b>mind-consciousness</b></a> as being consumed by the fires of birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato">SN 35.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a learned noble disciple becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with the sense bases and their contacts when they truly see how these elements are consumed by suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato">SN 35.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how disenchantment leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a>, which in turn leads to liberation and the knowledge that "birth is destroyed"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.29/en/sujato">SN 35.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a monk asked how to abandon ignorance, the Buddha replied that one must know and see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato"><b>eye as impermanent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato">SN 35.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that seeing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato"><b>forms as impermanent</b></a> is a direct method for a practitioner to abandon ignorance and allow true knowledge to arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato">SN 35.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that knowing and seeing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact</b></a> as impermanent is an essential requirement for the arising of true knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato">SN 35.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that any feeling—whether pleasant, painful, or neutral—arising from eye-contact must be seen as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a> for ignorance to be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato">SN 35.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato"><b>tongue and tastes</b></a> as impermanent is how a monk abandons ignorance and gains true knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato">SN 35.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that seeing the body and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato"><b>body-contact</b></a> as impermanent is the way for a practitioner to cultivate true knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato">SN 35.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato"><b>mind as impermanent</b></a> is a fundamental step for any monk seeking to abandon ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato">SN 35.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that true knowledge (vijjā) arises only for one who knows and sees <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> (dhammā) as impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato">SN 35.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that abandoning ignorance involves the direct realization that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato"><b>mind-consciousness</b></a> is an impermanent phenomenon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato">SN 35.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha clarified that any feeling arising from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato"><b>mind-contact</b></a>, whether it be pleasant, painful, or neither-painful-nor-pleasant, must be viewed as impermanent to destroy ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.53/en/sujato">SN 35.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.16/en/sujato"><b>going forth</b></a> from the household life into homelessness is the most difficult thing to do in this Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.16/en/sujato">SN 38.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Sāriputta, for one who has already gone forth as a monk, the most difficult challenge is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.16/en/sujato"><b>finding delight</b></a> in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.16/en/sujato">SN 38.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta identified that for a monk who has found delight in his lifestyle, the next great difficulty is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.16/en/sujato"><b>practice in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.16/en/sujato">SN 38.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta encouraged practitioners by stating it would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.16/en/sujato"><b>not be long</b></a> before a monk practicing correctly in accordance with the Dhamma becomes an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn38.16/en/sujato">SN 38.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a dialogue regarding the challenges of the holy life, the Venerable Sāriputta emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn39.16/en/sujato"><b>going forth</b></a> is a primary hurdle for those entering the Buddha's path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn39.16/en/sujato">SN 39.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta noted that even after a person becomes a monk, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn39.16/en/sujato"><b>attaining delight</b></a> in that state is a significant difficulty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn39.16/en/sujato">SN 39.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta considered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn39.16/en/sujato"><b>true practice of Dhamma</b></a> to be the ultimate difficulty for a monk who has already found joy in his vocation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn39.16/en/sujato">SN 39.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato"><b>first jhāna</b></a> as a state secluded from sensual pleasures and unwholesome qualities, accompanied by thought, examination, rapture, and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato">SN 40.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna was practicing in seclusion, he was troubled by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato"><b>perceptions of sensuality</b></a> that occurred even as he sought the first jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato">SN 40.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna struggled with his meditation, the Buddha used <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato"><b>supernormal power</b></a> to appear before him and provide direct personal guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato">SN 40.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically exhorted the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna not to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato"><b>negligent</b></a> regarding the first jhāna but to steady his mind within it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato">SN 40.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato"><b>unify the mind</b></a> and concentrate it specifically within the state of the first jhāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato">SN 40.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna considered himself a disciple who reached <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato"><b>great direct knowledge</b></a> because he was sustained and assisted by the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato">SN 40.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha addressed the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna by the name <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato"><b>Moggallāna</b></a> twice when urging him to establish his mind firmly in his meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.1/en/sujato">SN 40.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the way the physical body subsists on food to the way the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>five hindrances</b></a> require their own specific mental "nutriments" to arise and grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> are similar to the physical body in that they require specific nutriments to be maintained and brought to fulfillment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that making much of unwise attention toward the "attractive feature" of things serves as the nutriment for the arising and growth of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified unwise attention to the "repulsive feature" as the specific nutriment that causes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> to arise and increase? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the hindrance of sloth and torpor is fed by unwise attention to listlessness, lassitude, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>mental sluggishness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>drowsiness after meals</b></a>, when combined with unwise attention, is one of the nutriments that leads to the arising and growth of sloth and torpor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>unsettled mind</b></a>, when given unwise attention, acts as the primary nutriment for the hindrance of restlessness and remorse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the hindrance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> arises and flourishes when a person frequently applies unwise attention to things that are bases for doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that the factor of enlightenment called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> is nourished and brought to fulfillment through the frequent application of wise attention to things that are a basis for mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified the investigation of wholesome and unwholesome, or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>blameable and blameless</b></a> states, as a primary nutriment for the enlightenment factor of investigation of phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the enlightenment factor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>investigation of phenomena</b></a> is fed by wise attention to the contrast between inferior and superior states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the "element of arousal" and the "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>element of exertion</b></a>" are the nutriments that cause the enlightenment factor of energy to arise and develop? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the enlightenment factor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>energy</b></a> is sustained and fulfilled by the "element of endeavor" when combined with frequent wise attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>rapture</b></a> as a factor of enlightenment arises and reaches fulfillment when one frequently applies wise attention to things that are a basis for rapture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the enlightenment factor of tranquillity is nourished by wise attention to both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>physical tranquillity</b></a> and mental tranquillity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified the "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>sign of serenity</b></a>" as a key nutriment that, through wise attention, leads to the arising and growth of the enlightenment factor of concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>sign of non-disarray</b></a>" is an essential nutriment for developing the factor of enlightenment known as concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the factor of enlightenment called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> reaches the fullness of its development through the frequent application of wise attention to things that are a basis for equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that just as a physical body cannot stand without food, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>five hindrances</b></a> cannot be maintained or increased without their specific mental "nutriments"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>unwise attention</b></a> (ayonisomanasikāra) is the common factor that "feeds" all five hindrances, allowing them to arise and flourish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>wise attention</b></a> (yonisomanasikāra) as the fundamental "nutriment" required for the arising and completion of all seven factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that sloth and torpor can be fed by the simple physical act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato"><b>stretching</b></a> (vijambhitā) when it is accompanied by unwise attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.2/en/sujato">SN 46.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahānāma the Sakyan once expressed his fear to the Buddha that dying amidst the chaotic streets of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>Kapilavatthu</b></a> might cause him to lose his mindfulness of the Triple Gem? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reassured Mahānāma that a person who has long cultivated spiritual qualities need not fear, as their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>death will not be a bad one</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>long fortified</b></a> by faith, virtue, learning, generosity, and wisdom will inevitably head toward a high distinction after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a jar of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>ghee or oil</b></a> broken in a deep lake to illustrate how a developed mind rises "upward" while the physical body sinks and dissolves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the physical body is merely a form composed of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>four great elements</b></a>, born of mother and father, and sustained by food like rice and porridge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that even if the physical body is devoured by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>crows, vultures, or dogs</b></a> after death, a mind steeped in spiritual practice remains directed toward a higher state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahānāma the Sakyan was concerned that encountering <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>elephants, horses, and chariots</b></a> in a crowded city might distract his mind from the Buddha at the moment of his passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five specific qualities—faith, virtue, learning, generosity, and wisdom—that cause the mind to go <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>upward to distinction</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahānāma the Sakyan specifically asked the Buddha what his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>future state</b></a> (abhisamparāya) would be if he were to die in a state of muddled mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the physical body as a thing subject to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>breaking and dissolution</b></a>, contrasting its fragile nature with the enduring direction of a well-trained mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the city of Kapilavatthu, Mahānāma the Sakyan felt the environment was so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>prosperous and populous</b></a> that it created a "crowded" atmosphere that threatened his spiritual focus? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when a vessel of oil is broken in water, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>stones and gravel</b></a> sink to the bottom, just as the material elements of the body remain behind while the mind ascends? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>destination</b></a> of one's life is determined by the long-term habitual cultivation of the mind rather than a singular moment of confusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahānāma the Sakyan would often enter Kapilavatthu in the evening after <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>paying homage</b></a> to the Buddha or other venerable monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly told Mahānāma, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato"><b>Do not be afraid</b></a>," reassuring him that his long-term devotion to the Dhamma secured a safe passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.21/en/sujato">SN 55.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka, while staying at Badarika Park, was once gravely ill and afflicted with severe pain, leading a group of elder monks to send a messenger to inquire if his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>affliction was personal</b></a> and if his painful feelings were subsiding rather than increasing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the elder monks at Ghosita’s Park utilized Ven. Dasaka as a messenger to repeatedly relay questions to Ven. Khemaka regarding his realization of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates of clinging</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka explicitly stated to the elder monks that he did not regard any of the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, or consciousness—as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>self or belonging to self</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite not seeing a self in the five aggregates, Ven. Khemaka admitted to the elder monks that he was not yet an Arahant because he still had a residual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>notion of "I am"</b></a> (<i>asmi</i>) regarding the aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka made a subtle distinction between having the notion "I am" (<i>asmi</i>) and the view <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>"This I am"</b></a> (<i>ayamahamasmīti</i>), claiming he possessed the former but had abandoned the latter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the elder monks pressed him for clarification on his state of realization, Ven. Khemaka decided to overcome his physical illness and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>leaned on a staff</b></a> to personally travel and speak with the elders himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka used the famous analogy of a lotus flower to explain that the notion "I am" does not reside in the petals, the color, or the pollen, but is rather a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>scent of the flower</b></a> as a whole? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka argued that the notion "I am" is not identical to form, feeling, perception, formations, or consciousness, yet it is not found <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>apart from these five aggregates</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka explained that even after a noble disciple has abandoned the five lower fetters, there can still remain a residual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>"I am" conceit</b></a>, "I am" desire, and "I am" underlying tendency that has not yet been uprooted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka compared the residual "I am" conceit to the lingering smell of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>cleaning agents</b></a> like salt, lye, or cow dung on a piece of cloth that has already been washed clean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka taught that the way to remove the final traces of the "I am" conceit is by dwelling upon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>rise and fall</b></a> of the five aggregates of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka stated that when a person contemplates "Such is form, such its origin, such its passing away," the residual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency</b></a> of "I am" eventually goes to destruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka's analogy of the soiled cloth explains how a washerman uses various substances to clean it, yet the owner must still place it in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>scented casket</b></a> to fully eradicate the smell of the cleaning agents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka identified the "I am" notion as a combination of conceit (<i>māna</i>), desire (<i>chanda</i>), and an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency</b></a> (<i>anusaya</i>) that persists even in advanced practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Ven. Khemaka finished his detailed explanation of the "I am" notion, the minds of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>sixty elder monks</b></a> were liberated from the taints through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka himself attained full liberation and his mind was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>freed from the taints</b></a> at the very same time he was delivering his discourse to the elder monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the elder monks clarified that they did not question Ven. Khemaka to trouble him, but because they believed he was capable of explaining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's teaching in detail</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka emphasized that he did not say "I am form" nor did he say "I am <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>apart from form</b></a>," illustrating the difficulty of pinpointing the "I am" notion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a>—form, feeling, perception, formations, and consciousness—served as the framework for the entire dialogue between Ven. Khemaka and the elder monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka's explanation involved revealing, uncovering, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>making plain</b></a> the Dhamma in a way that led to the mass enlightenment of his peers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Dasaka had to travel back and forth between the Ghosita Park and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>Badarika Park</b></a> multiple times to facilitate the conversation between the monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the elder monks specifically asked if Ven. Khemaka saw anything as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>self or belonging to self</b></a> within the five aggregates of clinging as a test of his level of awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>"khīṇāsavo"</b></a> refers to an Arahant whose taints are destroyed, a status that the elder monks initially thought Ven. Khemaka had achieved since he saw no self in the aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka explained that even though the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>five lower fetters</b></a> (which bind one to the sensual world) are abandoned by a noble disciple, the subtle scent of "I" can still remain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the "scent of the flower" analogy highlights that the "I am" notion is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>emergent property</b></a> of the aggregates rather than being located in any single one of them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the elder monks were described as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>delighted</b></a> and rejoiced in Ven. Khemaka's words after he clarified the subtle points of the "I am" conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka used the example of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>blue lotus</b></a> (<i>uppala</i>), the red lotus (<i>paduma</i>), and the white lotus (<i>puṇḍarīka</i>) to make his point about the nature of scent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Khemaka's teaching reveals that the final stage of liberation involves the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>uprooting</b></a> of the most subtle levels of ego-conceit that persist beyond the initial stages of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the entire discussion in the Khemaka Sutta demonstrates how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato"><b>communal inquiry</b></a> and the sharing of Dhamma insights can lead to the simultaneous liberation of many practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.89/en/sujato">SN 22.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato"><b>Migajāla</b></a> once approached the Buddha to specifically ask for a definition of what it truly means to be "one who dwells alone" versus "one who dwells with a companion"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato">SN 35.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that "dwelling with a companion" is not about physical proximity to others, but about whether a person remains bound by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato"><b>fetter of delight</b></a> regarding agreeable sensory objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato">SN 35.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> is a person’s true "companion," and as long as it has not been abandoned, a person is considered to be "dwelling with a companion" even if they are physically isolated in a forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato">SN 35.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk is considered to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato"><b>dwelling alone</b></a> if he has abandoned craving, even if he lives in a village crowded with monks, nuns, laypeople, and even kings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato">SN 35.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when a monk delights in and welcomes agreeable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato"><b>forms cognizable by the eye</b></a>, delight arises, leading to the lust and fetters that constitute "dwelling with a companion"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato">SN 35.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a specific causal chain where delighting in sensory objects leads to delight, delight leads to lust, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato"><b>lust leads to a fetter</b></a>, which prevents one from truly being alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato">SN 35.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even in remote forest dwellings and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato"><b>solitary lodgings</b></a> sequestered from people, a person is still "dwelling with a companion" if they have not yet abandoned the internal companion of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato">SN 35.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a monk does not delight in or welcome agreeable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato"><b>tastes cognizable by the tongue</b></a>, delight ceases, and he is consequently freed from the bond of a companion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato">SN 35.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato"><b>unbound from the fetter of delight</b></a> is called "one who dwells alone," regardless of their physical environment or social surroundings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato">SN 35.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> cognizable by the mind as a sensory field where, if delight is abandoned and ceases, a monk can truly be said to dwell alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato">SN 35.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that if a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato"><b>remains fastened</b></a> to sensory experiences that are charming and provocative of lust, he remains a "dweller with a companion" due to his internal attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato">SN 35.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the state of "dwelling alone" as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato"><b>disjoined from the fetter of delight</b></a>, which is achieved by not welcoming or delighting in agreeable sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, and thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato">SN 35.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato"><b>unabandoned craving</b></a> is the specific reason why a person living in a quiet, secluded place is still technically considered to be "dwelling with a companion"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.63/en/sujato">SN 35.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna, using his psychic power, vanished from the Jetavana monastery and appeared among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>Tāvatiṃsa gods</b></a> as quickly as a strong man might stretch out his flexed arm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Sakka, the Lord of the Devas, first approached the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna in the heavenly realm, he was accompanied by an entourage of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>five hundred deities</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna taught Sakka that going for refuge to the Buddha is an excellent practice because it leads beings to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Devas, confirmed to Mahāmoggallāna that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>taking refuge</b></a> in the Dhamma is indeed excellent and results in rebirth in a happy destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna emphasized that going for refuge to the Sangha is a cause for certain beings to be reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>good destination</b></a> upon their death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as the conversation in heaven progressed, the number of deities accompanying Sakka to see Mahāmoggallāna increased from hundreds to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>eighty thousand</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna described the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed trainer</b></a> of persons to be tamed and the teacher of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna taught that having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence</b></a> in the Buddha allows a person to be reborn in a heavenly world where they surpass other gods in ten respects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna, those with confirmed confidence in the Dhamma surpass other deities in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>divine life-span</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Devas, agreed that possessing confirmed confidence in the Sangha leads to surpassing other gods in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>divine beauty</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna stated that individuals endowed with confirmed confidence in the Triple Gem surpass other devas in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>divine happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Devas, acknowledged that those who take refuge in the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha will surpass other gods in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>divine fame</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna explained that beings with confirmed confidence in the Buddha surpass other gods in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>divine authority</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Devas, confirmed that followers of the Buddha surpass other heavenly beings in five types of sensory experiences, including <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>divine forms</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna noted that those who go for refuge to the Dhamma surpass other gods in the experience of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>divine sounds</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Devas, agreed that confirmed confidence in the Sangha leads to surpassing other deities in the enjoyment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>divine smells</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna taught that those who possess the virtues loved by the noble ones surpass other gods in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>divine tastes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Devas, recognized that virtuous followers of the Buddha surpass other deities in experiencing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>divine touches</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna described the Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>visible here and now</b></a>, timeless, and inviting one to "come and see"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna defined the Sangha of the Blessed One's disciples as consisting of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>four pairs of persons</b></a> or the eight types of individuals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Devas, agreed that the Sangha is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world, worthy of gifts and hospitality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna emphasized the importance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>virtues loved by the noble ones</b></a>, which he described as being unbroken, unblemished, and liberating? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna taught that proper virtue is that which is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>praised by the wise</b></a> and leads to concentration of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Devas, used the respectful term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>dear sir</b></a> (mārisa) when addressing the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna during their meeting in the Tāvatiṃsa heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna explained that the Dhamma is to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>personally experienced</b></a> by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna stated that the disciples of the Buddha are practicing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>good way</b></a>, the straight way, the true way, and the proper way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Devas, acknowledged that having confirmed confidence in the Buddha's qualities—such as being an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> and perfectly enlightened—is a cause for a fortunate rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna specifically identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence in the Dhamma</b></a> as a primary reason for some beings to arise in a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Devas, confirmed that those who possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>unblemished virtues</b></a> will surpass other gods in their divine experience of happiness and fame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>Triple Gem</b></a>—the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha—provides a secure refuge that leads to a good destination after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the Lord of the Devas, personally greeted the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>paying homage</b></a> to him before standing to one side to listen to his teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna used the phrase <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>Lord of the Devas</b></a> when addressing Sakka while teaching him about the benefits of refuge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato"><b>virtues leading to concentration</b></a> are a key factor in ensuring a deity surpasses others in the ten divine respects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn40.10/en/sujato">SN 40.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the mind to gold, explaining that just as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato"><b>iron</b></a> is a corruption that makes gold brittle and not luminous, there are specific mental corruptions that prevent the mind from being properly concentrated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato">SN 46.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato"><b>copper</b></a> as one of the five impurities of gold that prevents the metal from being malleable or fit for fine work, using this as an analogy for how certain states hinder the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato">SN 46.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, gold becomes brittle and lacks its natural luster when it is defiled by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato"><b>tin</b></a>, just as the mind loses its radiance when affected by various mental corruptions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato">SN 46.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato"><b>lead</b></a> is a corruption that makes gold lose its flexibility and prevents it from being used by a goldsmith, much like how mental hindrances make the mind rigid? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato">SN 46.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato"><b>silver</b></a> as one of the five corruptions of gold that stop it from being radiant and easy to work with, comparing these to the five hindrances of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato">SN 46.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when gold is corrupted by various metal impurities, it is no longer <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato"><b>malleable</b></a> or wieldy, illustrating how a corrupted mind lacks the flexibility needed for spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato">SN 46.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a mind corrupted by the five hindrances as being not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato"><b>luminous</b></a> and not rightly concentrated for the ultimate goal of the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato">SN 46.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the primary purpose of purifying the mind from its five corruptions is to achieve a state where it is rightly concentrated for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato">SN 46.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a> as a corruption of the mind that, when present, prevents the mind from being soft, wieldy, and radiant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato">SN 46.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> is an obstruction and a hindrance that acts as a corruption of the mind specifically to weaken a person's wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato">SN 46.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> are corruptions of the mind that act as obstructions, making wisdom weak and ineffective? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato">SN 46.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> as one of the five obstructions that defile the mind and hinder the development of deep wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato">SN 46.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> as a specific corruption of the mind that serves as an obstruction and a hindrance, ultimately weakening one's wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato">SN 46.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that the five hindrances—sensual desire, ill will, sloth and torpor, restlessness and remorse, and doubt—are collectively known as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato"><b>corruptions of the mind</b></a> because they actively weaken wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato">SN 46.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted the five hindrances with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a>, describing the latter as being non-obstructive and non-corrupting to the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato">SN 46.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness enlightenment factor</b></a> is developed and cultivated, it leads to the realization of the fruit of true knowledge and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato">SN 46.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato"><b>equanimity enlightenment factor</b></a> as one of the seven non-obstructive factors of the mind that, when practiced frequently, lead to true liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato">SN 46.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that the seven factors of enlightenment, when developed and cultivated, lead directly to the realization of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge and liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato">SN 46.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato"><b>non-obstructions</b></a> to describe the seven factors of enlightenment, distinguishing them from the five hindrances that block the mind's clarity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.37/en/sujato">SN 46.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a mind free from hindrances becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato"><b>wieldy</b></a>, meaning it is ready and capable of being used for spiritual development, much like purified gold is ready for a goldsmith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.33/en/sujato">SN 46.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahānāma the Sakyan expressed concern to the Buddha about losing mindfulness when entering the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato"><b>crowded city</b></a> of Kapilavatthu, which he described as being filled with elephants, horses, chariots, and carts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato">SN 55.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahānāma the Sakyan worried about his future destination after death because he found that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the Triple Gem</b></a>—the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha—would often become muddled when navigating the busy and congested streets of his home city? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato">SN 55.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reassured Mahānāma the Sakyan not to fear the timing or circumstances of his passing, stating that his death would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato"><b>not evil</b></a> because he had already cultivated the essential qualities of a noble disciple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato">SN 55.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple who possesses four specific spiritual factors is naturally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato"><b>inclined toward Nibbāna</b></a>, just as a tree that leans in a certain direction will inevitably fall in that direction when its roots are cut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato">SN 55.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one of the four factors that secures a person's spiritual path is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato"><b>verified confidence</b></a> in the Buddha, recognizing him as the "Teacher of gods and humans"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato">SN 55.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato"><b>virtues loved by the noble ones</b></a>—which are described as unbroken, unblemished, and leading to concentration—as one of the four essential qualities that determine a disciple's spiritual trajectory? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato">SN 55.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato"><b>leaning tree</b></a> to explain that a person's long-term spiritual "slant" or "inclination" is more important than the immediate chaos they might face at the moment of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato">SN 55.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly told Mahānāma that a noble disciple (ariyasāvako) who is possessed of verified confidence in the Triple Gem is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato"><b>sloping toward Nibbāna</b></a>, regardless of the distractions found in a busy city? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato">SN 55.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mahānāma the Sakyan described the city of Kapilavatthu as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato"><b>prosperous and populous</b></a>, yet he feared this very congestion would hinder his ability to keep the Buddha's teachings in mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato">SN 55.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the state of a noble disciple's mind as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato"><b>leaning toward Nibbāna</b></a> (nibbānaninno) when they are firmly established in the four factors of stream-entry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.22/en/sujato">SN 55.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the elder monks instructed Venerable Channa that form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>consciousness are impermanent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the elder monks taught Venerable Channa that the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>not-self</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the elder monks summarized the Dhamma for Venerable Channa by stating that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>all formations are impermanent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the elder monks emphasized a fundamental tenet of Buddhism to Venerable Channa, declaring that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>all phenomena are non-self</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Channa initially struggled because, although he understood the teachings on impermanence, his mind would not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>leap forward to Nibbāna</b></a> or settle into the stilling of all formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Channa felt a sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>agitation and clinging</b></a> when his mind recoiled from the idea of the cessation of craving, leading him to wonder who his "self" truly was? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Channa sought out Venerable Ānanda for guidance because Ānanda was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>esteemed by the Buddha</b></a> and highly respected by wise fellow monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda encouraged Channa by telling him he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>capable of understanding</b></a> the Dhamma and had already cleared the obstacles to his progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that people in this world generally rely upon a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>duality of existence</b></a> and non-existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when one sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>origin of the world</b></a> with right wisdom, the notion of the world's "non-existence" does not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when one sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>cessation of the world</b></a> with right wisdom, the notion of the world's "existence" does not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the world is mostly shackled by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>engagement and clinging</b></a>, yet the noble disciple does not take a stand on the idea of a "self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined Right View as the understanding that what arises is merely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>suffering arising</b></a>, and what ceases is merely suffering ceasing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the realization of the arising and ceasing of suffering as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>knowledge independent of others</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the view that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>all exists</b></a>" as one of the two extreme views that a practitioner should avoid? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the view that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>all does not exist</b></a>" as the second extreme view that deviates from the middle way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the Dhamma by the middle way, explaining that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>ignorance is the condition</b></a> that leads to volitional formations and the entire chain of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the entire mass of suffering—including aging and death—has its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>origin through conditions</b></a> such as craving and clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> is achieved through the complete fading away and cessation of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after receiving Venerable Ānanda's teaching on the middle way and dependent origination, Venerable Channa declared that he had finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>attained the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Channa found relief and clarity because he had the support of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato"><b>compassionate fellow monks</b></a> like Ānanda who were willing to exhort and instruct him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.90/en/sujato">SN 22.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the monk Migajāla that delighting in and welcoming agreeable forms leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato">SN 35.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk does not delight in or hold to charming and dear forms, delight ceases, and this brings about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato">SN 35.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the same principle of delight causing suffering applies to all sense bases, including <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato"><b>flavors cognizable by the tongue</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato">SN 35.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who remains focused on agreeable phenomena cognizable by the mind will experience the arising of delight, which he calls the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato">SN 35.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by not welcoming or remaining focused on phenomena cognizable by the mind, a monk experiences the cessation of delight and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato">SN 35.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha’s brief teaching, Venerable Migajāla lived alone and diligent, eventually attaining the supreme goal of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato">SN 35.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Migajāla, upon attaining liberation, realized for himself that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a> and that there was no further state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato">SN 35.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed Samiddhi that wherever the eye, forms, and eye-consciousness exist, there exists the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato"><b>world</b></a> or the description of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato">SN 35.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the presence of the eye and things to be cognized by eye-consciousness is the basis for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato"><b>description of the world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato">SN 35.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Samiddhi that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato"><b>world</b></a> exists in the presence of the tongue, flavors, and tongue-consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato">SN 35.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the existence of the mind, mental phenomena, and mind-consciousness is what allows for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato"><b>description of the world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato">SN 35.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that where there is no eye, no forms, and no eye-consciousness, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato"><b>no world</b></a> and no description of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato">SN 35.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Samiddhi that if there is no tongue, no flavors, and no tongue-consciousness, then there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato"><b>no description of the world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato">SN 35.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that in the absence of the mind, phenomena, and mind-consciousness, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato"><b>no world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato">SN 35.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the presence of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato"><b>six sense bases</b></a> and their corresponding objects as the condition for the existence of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato">SN 35.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the arising of delight (<i>nandī</i>) in the six spheres of sense contact is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato">SN 35.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by abandoning delight in sense objects, a practitioner achieves the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato">SN 35.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the definition of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato"><b>world</b></a> as being entirely dependent on the internal and external sense bases and the consciousness that arises from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato">SN 35.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Migajāla became one of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato"><b>Arhats</b></a> after successfully practicing the Buddha's instructions on non-delight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.64/en/sujato">SN 35.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Samiddhi that the absence of things to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato"><b>cognized</b></a> by the six types of consciousness means there is no description of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.68/en/sujato">SN 35.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>Citta the householder</b></a> once clarified a technical point of doctrine for a group of senior monks by explaining that "fetters" and "the things that can be fettered" are different in both meaning and phrasing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a group of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>senior monks</b></a> in the Wild Mango Grove were debating whether the term "fetter" and "things that can be fettered" referred to the same thing or held different meanings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder used the famous simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>black ox and a white ox</b></a> to demonstrate that objects of the senses are not inherently fetters to the senses themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder explained how the eye is not the fetter of forms, nor are forms the fetter of the eye, but the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>desire and lust</b></a> (chandarāgo) that arises in dependence on both is the actual fetter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when two oxen are joined by a single harness or yoke-strap, Citta the householder pointed out that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>single strap</b></a> is what constitutes the fetter, rather than one ox being a fetter to the other? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the senior monks praised Citta the householder for possessing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>eye of wisdom</b></a> (paññācakkhu), which allowed him to understand and explain the deep meaning of the Buddha's teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder taught that the ear is not the fetter of sounds, nor are sounds the fetter of the ear, but rather the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>fetter</b></a> is the desire and lust that arises when they meet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder emphasized that the nose is not a fetter to odors, nor are odors a fetter to the nose, but the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>bond</b></a> is the desire and lust that arises in dependence on both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the senior monks were sitting in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>circular pavilion</b></a> (maṇḍalamāḷe) after their alms round when Citta the householder arrived to discuss the nature of fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder explained that the tongue is not the fetter of tastes, nor are tastes the fetter of the tongue, but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>lustful desire</b></a> is the actual link that binds them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Citta the householder, the body is not the fetter of tactile objects, nor are tactile objects the fetter of the body, because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>fetter</b></a> is specifically the desire and lust that arises from their contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder clarified that the mind is not the fetter of mental phenomena, nor are mental phenomena the fetter of the mind, but the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>desire and lust</b></a> that arises in dependence on both is what constitutes the fetter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the senior monks acknowledged that Citta the householder was fortunate to have gained a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>thorough understanding</b></a> of the profound words of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks initially held divided opinions, with some believing "fetter" and "things that can be fettered" were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>identical in meaning</b></a> while others believed they were different? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>saṃyojana</b></a>, which Citta the householder analyzed, is the Pāli word for "fetter" used in the Buddha's teachings to describe what binds beings to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder argued that if a black ox were the fetter of a white ox, it would be impossible to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>separate them</b></a> without the harness, illustrating that the bond is an external factor (lust)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks were having an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>intercurrent talk</b></a> (antarākathā) about Buddhist philosophy when Citta the householder joined their assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder’s explanation shows that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>sense bases</b></a> (like the eye and ear) and their objects (like sights and sounds) are not the problem, but rather the emotional reaction of lust is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder is one of the few laypeople in the Sutta Piṭaka shown to have such deep <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma insight</b></a> that he could resolve complex doctrinal questions for senior monastics? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.1/en/sujato">SN 41.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared certain spiritual failures to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato"><b>giant trees</b></a> with tiny seeds, such as the banyan or the sacred fig, which grow so large that they eventually overspread, encircle, and cause other trees to collapse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato">SN 46.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of parasitic trees to describe how a person who has gone forth from the home life can be ruined and brought to ruin by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato">SN 46.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified five specific mental obstructions, known as "overgrowths of the mind," which are responsible for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato"><b>weakening wisdom</b></a> and preventing spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato">SN 46.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a> is an obstruction and a hindrance that overspreads the consciousness, making it one of the primary factors that enfeebles a practitioner's wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato">SN 46.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> as a mental hindrance that acts like an "overgrowth," cluttering the mind and rendering one's wisdom weak and ineffective? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato">SN 46.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> is not merely tiredness but is a specific mental obstruction that overpowers the mind and diminishes the power of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato">SN 46.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> function as hindrances that overspread the mind, preventing a person from achieving the clarity needed for deep insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato">SN 46.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> is an "overgrowth of the mind" that serves as a hindrance and an obstruction, actively working to weaken a person's faculty of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato">SN 46.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted the five hindrances with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a>, which he described as "non-obstructions" and "non-hindrances" that do not overspread the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato">SN 46.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when the seven factors of enlightenment are frequently cultivated, they lead directly to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato"><b>realization of the fruit</b></a> of true knowledge and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.39/en/sujato">SN 46.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta, accompanied by Ven. Ānanda, visited the householder Anāthapiṇḍika when he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>afflicted, suffering, and gravely ill</b></a> to offer spiritual guidance and support? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by Ven. Sāriputta about his condition, the layman Anāthapiṇḍika confessed that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>painful feelings</b></a> were increasing rather than subsiding, showing no sign of the end of their increase? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta explained that an uninstructed worldling is reborn in a state of deprivation or hell because of a lack of confidence in the Buddha, but a noble disciple like Anāthapiṇḍika possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence in the Buddha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that if a disciple reflects upon their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence in the Buddha</b></a>, their physical pains can subside on the spot? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta identified that the uninstructed worldling faces a bad destination after death due to a lack of confidence in the Dhamma, whereas a noble disciple possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence in the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta emphasized that the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>well expounded by the Blessed One</b></a>, to be realized by the wise for themselves, and that reflecting on this quality can alleviate suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta reminded Anāthapiṇḍika that while others lack faith in the community of disciples, he possessed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence in the Saṅgha</b></a>, which is the unsurpassed field of merit for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Ven. Sāriputta, seeing one's own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence in the Saṅgha</b></a> is a powerful reflection that can cause intense physical pain to subside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that the uninstructed worldling is reborn in hell because of immoral conduct, but a noble disciple possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>virtues dear to the noble ones</b></a> that are conducive to concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta instructed Anāthapiṇḍika to reflect on his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>virtues dear to the noble ones</b></a> as a means to find relief from the painful feelings of his grave illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta explained that holding wrong view leads an uninstructed worldling to hell, while a noble disciple like Anāthapiṇḍika is established in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>Right View</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta pointed out that seeing one's own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>Right View</b></a> within oneself is a factor that can lead to the immediate subsiding of painful bodily sensations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta distinguished a noble disciple from an uninstructed worldling by their possession of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>Right Intention</b></a>, which serves as a protection against rebirth in the lower realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>Right Speech</b></a> as one of the ten specific qualities a noble disciple can reflect upon to experience a cessation of pain during illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta noted that a noble disciple does not possess the wrong action that leads to misery, but instead possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>Right Action</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that reflecting on one's own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>Right Livelihood</b></a> is a spiritual practice that can lead to the subsiding of intense physical affliction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta contrasted the "wrong effort" of the uninstructed worldling with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>Right Effort</b></a> possessed by a noble disciple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta encouraged Anāthapiṇḍika to see that he was endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>Right Mindfulness</b></a>, a quality that counteracts the factors leading to a bad rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta explained that while wrong concentration leads to the downfall of a worldling, a noble disciple possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>Right Concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta taught that a disciple can experience the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>subsiding of pain</b></a> by reflecting on the presence of Right Concentration within themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>Right Knowledge</b></a> as one of the advanced factors possessed by a noble disciple that can be used as a basis for healing and reflection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta stated that a noble disciple possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>Right Liberation</b></a>, and seeing this quality within oneself can cause painful feelings to cease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that immediately after Ven. Sāriputta gave this detailed teaching on the ten qualities, the householder Anāthapiṇḍika’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>pains subsided on the spot</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Sāriputta recited verses stating that a person with unshakable faith in the Tathāgata and virtues dear to the noble ones is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>not poor</b></a> and their life is not in vain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Ven. Sāriputta as "wise" and of "great wisdom" for his ability to analyze the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato"><b>four factors of stream-entry</b></a> in ten ways? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.26/en/sujato">SN 55.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed he does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>not dispute with the world</b></a>, but rather it is the world that disputes with him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>proponent of the Dhamma</b></a> does not dispute with anyone in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he only says something does not exist if it is already <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>held not to exist</b></a> by the wise in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha agrees with the wise in the world that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> which is permanent, stable, eternal, and not subject to change is something that does not exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> which is permanent and eternal is agreed by the wise in the world to be non-existent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha agrees with the wise that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> is not something that can be permanent, stable, or eternal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> are recognized by the wise as being not permanent and not eternal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> which is permanent, stable, and eternal is held by the wise in the world to not exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that whatever is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>held to exist</b></a> by the wise in the world, he also says exists? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha agrees with the wise in the world that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> is actually impermanent, suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> is recognized by the wise as being impermanent, suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha has awakened to and perfectly realized a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>world-condition</b></a> (lokadhammo) in the world, which he then discloses and explains to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha describes the five aggregates—form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness—as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>world-condition</b></a> that he analyzes and makes clear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refers to anyone who does not know or see the nature of the aggregates when they are being explained as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>blind foolish worldling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared himself to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>lotus</b></a> that is born and grows in the water but rises above it to stand unsullied? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that although he was born and grew up in the world, he has overcome the world and dwells <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato"><b>unsullied by the world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.94/en/sujato">SN 22.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> to a lump of foam floating down the Ganges River, which appears substantial from afar but is revealed to be empty and hollow upon close examination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> is like a water bubble appearing on the surface during an autumn rain, lasting only a brief moment before vanishing without leaving any core substance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> to a mirage shimmering in the midday heat of the last month of summer, which deceives the observer and possesses no reality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> are like the trunk of a plantain tree, which a person might peel apart looking for heartwood only to find that it has no core at all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> to a magician’s trick performed at a crossroads, illustrating that it is a deceptive phenomenon without any genuine essence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a bhikkhu examines the five aggregates with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>thorough attention</b></a>, they appear to him as being empty, hollow, and void of substance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a well-taught noble disciple who sees the empty nature of the aggregates becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that through disenchantment with the five aggregates, a practitioner becomes dispassionate, and through dispassion, their mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered this famous discourse on the emptiness of the five aggregates while he was staying at Ayojjha on the bank of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>Ganges River</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>man with good sight</b></a> observing a lump of foam to show how a wise person should scrutinize the nature of physical form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, referred to in this text as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>Kinsman of the Sun</b></a>, used five distinct similes to illustrate that there is no essence to be found in the five aggregates of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when life, heat, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> depart from the physical body, it lies rejected and cast away like a mindless log? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the human <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>body</b></a> as a deceptive illusion that prattles to fools and as a "murderer" because no internal core or permanent self can be found within it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged monks to review the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> with energy and clear comprehension, whether by day or by night, to understand their true nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a practitioner should abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>all fetters</b></a> and make a refuge for themselves by correctly contemplating the empty nature of the aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to strive for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>unshakeable state</b></a> with the same sense of urgency as someone whose head is on fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that just as a plantain tree has no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>heartwood</b></a>, there is no substance to be found in any of the volitional formations that make up our experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the image of a magician’s apprentice displaying a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>magical illusion</b></a> at a crossroads to demonstrate the deceptive and hollow nature of consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the body becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>mindless</b></a> and serves as food for others once the vital functions of life and heat have ceased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a bhikkhu with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> should constantly dwell mindful and clearly comprehending while observing the rise and fall of the aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that no matter which of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> one examines—whether past, future, or present—they are all equally devoid of any permanent core? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.95/en/sujato">SN 22.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> whatsoever that is permanent, stable, eternal, and not subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that not even a single <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> can be found that lasts forever without being subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> that remains permanent or stable like an eternal thing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> exist that are eternal and not liable to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha affirmed that there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> that is permanent and would remain exactly the same forever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once picked up a small <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>clump of cow dung</b></a> to illustrate that not even a tiny amount of self-existence is permanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if even a tiny <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>acquisition of self-existence</b></a> were permanent, the holy life for the complete destruction of suffering would not be possible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that it is precisely because no amount of self-existence is permanent that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> can be lived for the right destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recalled a past life in which he was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>consecrated kshatriya king</b></a> who possessed 84,000 cities, with Kusāvatī as his royal capital? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a former life as a great king, the Buddha owned 84,000 palaces, the chief of which was called the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma Palace</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, in a previous existence as a monarch, had 84,000 gabled halls, including the magnificent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>Mahābyūha gabled hall</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a king in a past life, the Buddha possessed 84,000 <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>couches</b></a> made of ivory, heartwood, gold, and silver, draped in expensive fabrics and furs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted having 84,000 elephants in a past life, led by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>state elephant Uposatha</b></a>, all adorned with gold ornaments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once had 84,000 horses as a royal monarch, including the famous <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>state horse Valāhaka</b></a>, the king of horses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha possessed 84,000 chariots in a former life, the chief of which was the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>Vejayanta chariot</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a great king, the Buddha was attended by 84,000 women, with the beautiful <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>Queen Subhaddā</b></a> as their chief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that as a king he had 84,000 <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>garments</b></a> of the finest linen, silk, wool, and cotton? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite having 84,000 cities, the Buddha as a king could only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>inhabit</b></a> one city at a time, which was the royal capital of Kusāvatī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that although he had 84,000 dishes of food served to him, he could only eat <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>one measure of rice</b></a> and the accompanying soup? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his past royal grandeur to show that all those <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>formations</b></a> are now past, ceased, and changed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that all conditioned things are so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>impermanent and unstable</b></a> that one should be disenchanted and liberated from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is enough to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato"><b>dispassionate</b></a> toward all formations because they are ultimately insecure and subject to cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.96/en/sujato">SN 22.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once used a tiny bit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>soil on his fingernail</b></a> to illustrate to a monk that not even a minuscule amount of form is permanent, stable, or eternal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if even a tiny amount of form were permanent, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> for the complete destruction of suffering would not be possible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that it is precisely because not even a fingernail’s worth of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> is permanent that the holy life can be lived successfully for the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> whatsoever that is stable, eternal, and not subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> exist that remain forever without being subject to the law of change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> that is permanent, stable, or eternal, appearing exactly the same forever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was asked by a monk if there was any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> at all that would stand forever as an eternal thing, to which he replied there is none? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>destruction of suffering</b></a> is the primary goal of the holy life, and this goal is achievable only because the aggregates are impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a monk that if even a tiny amount of consciousness were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>permanent</b></a>, the path to liberation would be fundamentally closed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple who truly sees the impermanent nature of the five aggregates becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a practitioner sees the nature of the aggregates correctly and becomes liberated, they realize there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>no more coming</b></a> to any state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha held a small amount of soil on his fingernail to show that there isn't even that much perception that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>stable</b></a> and unchanging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that the holy life is only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>possible</b></a> because everything, including volitional formations, is subject to change and cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any form—whether past, future, or present—is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a> and should be viewed with right wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that liberation occurs when a practitioner becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>dispassionate</b></a> through the clear seeing of the impermanence of the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that neither feeling nor any other aggregate is permanent, stable, or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>eternal</b></a> like an unchanging thing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while staying at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>Savatthi</b></a>, used a physical demonstration of soil on his fingernail to answer a monk's deep inquiry about the existence of any permanent aggregate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the correct seeing of the five aggregates leads one to understand that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato"><b>nothing more</b></a> remains to be done for the sake of complete liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.97/en/sujato">SN 22.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena, after being bitten by a venomous snake, instructed his fellow monks to carry him outside because he knew his body would soon scatter like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato"><b>handful of chaff</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato">SN 35.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sariputta once observed that even though Venerable Upasena had been bitten by a deadly viper, there was no apparent alteration in his body or any change in his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato"><b>faculties</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato">SN 35.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena explained to Sariputta that an alteration of the body or the senses only occurs for one who thinks "I am the eye" or "the eye is mine," whereas he had long ago uprooted such <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato"><b>I-making</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato">SN 35.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena maintained his composure while dying from snake venom because he no longer possessed the underlying tendency to identify with the notion that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato"><b>the tongue is mine</b></a>" or "I am the tongue"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato">SN 35.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Upasena, a person who does not identify with the thought "I am the mind" or "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato"><b>the mind is mine</b></a>" will not experience a visible change in their physical appearance even when faced with a fatal snake bite? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato">SN 35.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena was able to face death by a viper without physical distress because his underlying tendency to the conceit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato"><b>I-making and mine-making</b></a> had been thoroughly uprooted for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato">SN 35.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the incident where Venerable Upasena was bitten by a venomous snake and then calmly instructed his fellow monks to carry him out took place at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato"><b>Snake’s Hood Grotto</b></a> in the Cool Grove at Rajagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato">SN 35.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as soon as the monks carried Venerable Upasena outside of the cave as he had requested, his body <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato"><b>scattered</b></a> right then and there like a handful of chaff due to the snake's venom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato">SN 35.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena told Sariputta that only those who identify with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a> as "I" or "mine" would show signs of physical or mental alteration when suffering from a deadly encounter with a viper? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato">SN 35.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sariputta was surprised to see that Venerable Upasena's appearance remained serene and his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato"><b>faculties</b></a> showed no sign of change even though a venomous snake had just fallen on his body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.69/en/sujato">SN 35.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>visible here and now</b></a> when a person experiences a form with the eye and internally recognizes whether lust for that form is present or absent within them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Upavaṇa asked how the Dhamma is "timeless" and "inviting one to come and see," the Buddha responded by describing the mindful recognition of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>lust for forms</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma is "to be experienced by the wise for themselves" when a monk tastes a flavor and clearly understands: "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>there is lust for tastes</b></a> within me"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>inviting one to come and see</b></a> specifically through the act of a person knowing their own internal state when they cognize a mental phenomenon and feel lust for it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the Dhamma becomes "visible here and now" when a monk recognizes that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>there is no lust</b></a> for forms within me" after seeing an object with the eye? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upavaṇa sought clarification from the Buddha on the meaning of the Dhamma being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>onward-leading</b></a> and how that quality can be understood through personal experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>timeless</b></a> nature of the Dhamma by showing that the immediate awareness of one's own internal desire—or lack thereof—is the key to understanding the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma is "to be experienced by the wise for themselves" when a monk knows: "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>there is no lust for tastes</b></a> within me" after experiencing a flavor with the tongue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person is "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>experiencing lust</b></a>" for mental phenomena when they cognize an idea with the mind and simultaneously feel the presence of that lust within themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>visible here and now</b></a> because a practitioner can directly perceive the absence of lust for mental phenomena when their mind is clear and detached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that knowing "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>there is lust for sounds</b></a> within me" is a way to personally validate the description of the Dhamma as "inviting one to come and see"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>to be experienced by the wise</b></a> to describe the specific internal realization a monk has when he knows for certain that lust for odors is not present within him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Dhamma as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>onward-leading</b></a> because it encourages the direct observation of how the mind reacts to tactile objects and whether lust arises? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Dhamma is "visible here and now" when someone hears a sound and understands: "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>no lust for sounds</b></a> exists within me"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>experiencing the flavor</b></a> and also experiencing the lust for that flavor, the Buddha considers this a direct, personal verification of the Dhamma's visibility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the quality of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>timeless</b></a> applies to the Dhamma because the truth of one's internal state regarding lust for smells can be seen immediately upon contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Venerable Upavaṇa that the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>inviting one to come and see</b></a> because it points to the verifiable reality of whether lust for tactile objects is present in the heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one knows the Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato"><b>visible here and now</b></a> by recognizing the internal absence of lust even while one is fully experiencing a mental phenomenon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.70/en/sujato">SN 35.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha heard of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>new and unknown monk</b></a> who was gravely ill and in pain, he visited him out of compassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha showed great kindness by telling a sick monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>not to rise</b></a> from his bed when the Buddha came to visit him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a sick monk confessed to the Buddha that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>painful feelings</b></a> were only increasing and showed no sign of subsiding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk admitted to the Buddha that he felt a great deal of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>remorse and regret</b></a> while he was suffering from a serious illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even though a sick monk felt remorse, he clarified to the Buddha that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>conscience</b></a> did not reproach him regarding his virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk told the Buddha he understood the Dhamma was taught not merely for the purification of virtue, but for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>fading away of lust</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highly commended a monk for correctly identifying that the purpose of the Dhamma is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>fading away of lust</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned a sick monk on whether the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a> is permanent or impermanent to help him gain insight into the nature of reality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that anything <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a> is actually suffering and therefore should not be grasped as "mine" or "my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked a monk whether it was fit to regard the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> as "This is mine, this I am, this is my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a learned noble disciple experiences <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>revulsion</b></a> toward the six sense bases, including the ear, nose, tongue, and body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>dispassionate</b></a> is liberated, and in liberation, there comes the knowledge that birth is destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was giving a teaching on the sense bases, a sick monk attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>vision of the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk realized the profound truth that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>whatever is subject to origination</b></a> is all subject to cessation while listening to the Buddha speak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha visited a sick monk at the request of another monk who reported that the patient was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato"><b>newly ordained</b></a> and unknown? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.74/en/sujato">SN 35.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised Venerable Rādha that one should abandon desire for whatever is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.76/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.76/en/sujato">SN 35.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Venerable Rādha that because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.76/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a>, forms, and eye-consciousness are impermanent, one should let go of desire for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.76/en/sujato">SN 35.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that any feeling—whether pleasant, painful, or neither-pleasant-nor-painful—that arises from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.76/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact</b></a> is impermanent and therefore desire for it should be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.76/en/sujato">SN 35.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Venerable Rādha to abandon desire for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.76/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> and mental phenomena because they are inherently impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.76/en/sujato">SN 35.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rādha once asked the Buddha for a brief teaching so that he could dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.76/en/sujato"><b>alone and withdrawn</b></a>, remaining diligent and ardent in his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.76/en/sujato">SN 35.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should abandon desire for whatever is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.77/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a>, including the eye, forms, and eye-consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.77/en/sujato">SN 35.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.77/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact</b></a> and the resulting feelings as suffering, advising Rādha that desire for them must be given up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.77/en/sujato">SN 35.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Venerable Rādha that the mind, mental phenomena, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.77/en/sujato"><b>mind-consciousness</b></a> are all states of suffering for which desire should be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.77/en/sujato">SN 35.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that even a feeling that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.77/en/sujato"><b>neither-pleasant-nor-painful</b></a> is considered suffering when it arises from mind-contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.77/en/sujato">SN 35.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Ānanda asked the Buddha why the term "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato"><b>the world</b></a>" is used, the Buddha replied that it refers to that which is of a nature to disintegrate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato">SN 35.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Noble One's Discipline, the Buddha defined "the world" as whatever is of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato"><b>nature to disintegrate</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato">SN 35.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Venerable Ānanda that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a>, forms, and eye-consciousness are all part of "the world" because they are subject to disintegration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato">SN 35.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato"><b>tongue</b></a>, flavors, and tongue-consciousness are considered "the world" because they possess the nature to disintegrate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato">SN 35.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a>, mental phenomena, and mind-contact are all described as "the world" because they are of a nature to disintegrate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato">SN 35.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Ānanda that any feeling arising from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato"><b>tongue-contact</b></a> is of a nature to disintegrate and is therefore classified as "the world"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato">SN 35.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato"><b>mind-consciousness</b></a> and any feeling arising from mind-contact are part of the "nature to disintegrate" that defines the world in the Noble One's Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.84/en/sujato">SN 35.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by Venerable Kāmabhū to explain a brief verse by the Buddha, Citta the householder identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato"><b>moral virtue</b></a> as the true meaning of the term "faultless" (nelaṅga)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato">SN 41.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder explained to Venerable Kāmabhū that the "white canopy" of the spiritual chariot mentioned by the Buddha is a metaphor for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato">SN 41.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his detailed explanation of the Buddha’s verse, Citta the householder interpreted the "one-horse" (ekāro) steering the chariot as a representation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato">SN 41.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder taught that the term "chariot" in the Buddha's verse refers to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato"><b>physical body</b></a>, which is composed of the four great elements and is subject to breaking up and destruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato">SN 41.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder explained that the "rolling" (vattatī) of the spiritual chariot signifies the monk's practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato"><b>moving forward and returning</b></a> with clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato">SN 41.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Citta the householder, an Arahant is described as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato"><b>trouble-free</b></a> (anīgha) because they have completely abandoned the "troubles" of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato">SN 41.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder defined the "stream" mentioned in the Buddha's verse as a metaphor for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a>, and noted that an Arahant is "stream-cut" because this craving has been uprooted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato">SN 41.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder clarified that lust, hatred, and delusion are the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato"><b>bonds</b></a> from which an Arahant is totally freed, making them "one without bonds"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato">SN 41.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing Citta the householder's profound interpretation of the Buddha's words, Venerable Kāmabhū exclaimed that Citta was fortunate to possess the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato"><b>eye of wisdom</b></a> regarding the deep teachings of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato">SN 41.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's verse about a "faultless chariot" with a "white canopy" was explained by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato"><b>Citta the householder</b></a> to mean that an Arahant has cut the stream of craving and is free from all spiritual bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.5/en/sujato">SN 41.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū explained to Citta the householder that there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>three formations</b></a>: the bodily formation, the verbal formation, and the mental formation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Kāmabhū, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>bodily formation</b></a> is defined as in-breathing and out-breathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū identified thought and examination as the specific components that make up the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>verbal formation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū taught that perception and feeling are mental states that constitute the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>mental formation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū explained that in-breathing and out-breathing are called the bodily formation because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>bodily states</b></a> that are bound up with the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū noted that thought and examination are called the verbal formation because one first thinks and examines before one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>breaks into speech</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū clarified that perception and feeling are called the mental formation because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>mental states</b></a> bound up with the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a monk enters the attainment of the cessation of perception and feeling, Venerable Kāmabhū stated that the monk does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>not have the intention</b></a> or thought that they are currently entering or have entered that state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū explained that a monk is led into the state of cessation because their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>mind has been previously developed</b></a> in a way that leads them to that specific attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Kāmabhū, when a monk enters the attainment of cessation, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>verbal formation</b></a> ceases first, followed by the bodily formation, and finally the mental formation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū pointed out a key difference between death and cessation: in a dead person, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>vitality</b></a> is exhausted, while in a monk in cessation, the vitality is not exhausted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū taught that whereas the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>heat</b></a> of the body is extinguished in one who has died, it is not extinguished in a monk who has entered the cessation of perception and feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū explained that while a dead person's faculties are broken up, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>faculties</b></a> of a monk in the attainment of cessation become exceptionally clear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū stated that a monk emerging from the attainment of cessation does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>not think</b></a> "I am emerging" or "I have emerged," as their prior mental development naturally leads them out of the state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Kāmabhū, when a monk emerges from the attainment of cessation, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>mental formation</b></a> arises first, followed by the bodily formation, and then the verbal formation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>three types of contact</b></a>—void contact, signless contact, and undirected contact—that touch a monk who has emerged from the cessation of perception and feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū described the mind of a monk who has emerged from cessation as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>slanted toward seclusion</b></a>, sloping and inclining toward it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked which qualities are most helpful for the attainment of the cessation of perception and feeling, Venerable Kāmabhū identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>serenity</b></a> (samatha) as one of the two key factors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that along with serenity, Venerable Kāmabhū named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>insight</b></a> (vipassanā) as the second essential quality for reaching the attainment of cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāmabhū explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>mental formation</b></a> of perception and feeling ceases last when entering cessation but arises first when emerging from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder was praised by Venerable Kāmabhū for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato"><b>insightful questioning</b></a> regarding the internal processes and benefits of the attainment of cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.6/en/sujato">SN 41.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder explained to Venerable Godatta that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>measureless liberation of mind</b></a> is achieved by a monk who pervades the entire world with a mind imbued with loving-kindness, compassion, altruistic joy, and equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Citta the householder, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind through nothingness</b></a> occurs when a monk completely transcends the base of infinite consciousness and dwells in the base of nothingness, perceiving that "there is nothing"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind through emptiness</b></a> as the state where a monk reflects in a forest or at the foot of a tree that "this is empty of self or what belongs to self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>signless liberation of mind</b></a> as the attainment reached when a monk enters the signless concentration of mind by not giving attention to any signs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder identified lust, hatred, and delusion as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>makers of measurement</b></a> (pamāṇakaraṇo), noting that these are completely destroyed and uprooted in a monk who has reached Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder taught that among all the various measureless liberations of mind, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>unshakeable liberation of mind</b></a> (akuppā cetovimutti) is considered the chief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder clarified that the unshakeable liberation of mind is specifically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>empty of lust</b></a>, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>somethings</b></a> (kiñcana) to describe lust, hatred, and delusion, explaining that an Arahant's unshakeable liberation is the chief among liberations through nothingness because these "somethings" have been abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder explained that lust, hatred, and delusion are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>makers of signs</b></a> (nimittakaraṇo), and because an Arahant has uprooted them, their unshakeable liberation is the chief among signless liberations of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Godatta asked Citta the householder whether the four types of liberation of mind—measureless, nothingness, emptiness, and signless—were different in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>both meaning and phrasing</b></a> or only different in phrasing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder taught that there is a specific method (pariyāya) by which these four liberations are different in meaning and phrasing, but another method where they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>the same in meaning</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>four divine abidings</b></a>—loving-kindness, compassion, altruistic joy, and equanimity—as the practical application of the measureless liberation of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder compared the abandoned defilements of an Arahant to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>palm stumps</b></a>, meaning they are cut off at the root and incapable of ever arising again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Godatta praised Citta the householder's deep understanding, stating that Citta possessed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>eye of wisdom</b></a> which ranges over the profound words of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>unshakeable liberation of mind</b></a> as the most excellent state among the various signless liberations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder explained that for a monk whose taints are destroyed, the makers of measurement—lust, hatred, and delusion—are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>annihilated</b></a> and made non-existent for the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when explaining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>liberation through emptiness</b></a>, Citta the householder emphasized reflecting on the lack of self or anything belonging to self in all things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder noted that the four types of liberation are "one in meaning" because they all culminate in a state that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>empty of defilements</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Citta the householder used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato"><b>kiñcana</b></a> (something/impediment) specifically to refer to the three root poisons of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn41.7/en/sujato">SN 41.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>gold and silver</b></a> are not allowable for the Sakyaputtan contemplatives, and that they do not accept, seek, or consent to them in any way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Maṇicūḷaka the headman defended the Buddha’s monks in a royal assembly by asserting that they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>laid aside gems and gold</b></a> and are entirely apart from the use of money? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for whomever gold and silver are allowable, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>five strands of sensual pleasure</b></a> are also necessarily allowable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a person consents to the five strands of sensual pleasure, they should be unequivocally considered as following the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>way of a non-ascetic</b></a> rather than the discipline of a true contemplative? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that while a monk may seek out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>grass</b></a> if he has a practical need for it, he is strictly forbidden from seeking or consenting to gold and silver under any circumstances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Maṇicūḷaka the headman asked if he had misrepresented the Buddha's position to others, the Buddha confirmed that he had spoken <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>according to the Dhamma</b></a> and had correctly represented the monastic discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha allowed for those who need <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>timber</b></a> to seek it out for their requirements, but emphasized that this allowance never extends to the acquisition or seeking of gold and silver? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk has a practical need for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>cart</b></a>, he is permitted to seek one, yet he must remain completely detached from the acceptance of gold and silver? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk may seek out a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>workman</b></a> or an assistant if he has need of one, but there is no valid method or reason for him to seek out gold and silver? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the controversy regarding whether <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>Sakyaputtan contemplatives</b></a> accepted gold and silver originally arose during a discussion among the royal assembly gathered in the king’s palace at Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Maṇicūḷaka the headman was able to successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>convince the assembly</b></a> of the purity of the monks' conduct before later visiting the Buddha to ensure his defense was perfectly accurate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha resided at Rājagaha in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>Bamboo Grove</b></a>, the Squirrels' Sanctuary, when he provided the definitive teaching clarifying that his monks do not accept money? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Maṇicūḷaka the headman used the specific phrase "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>without gold and silver</b></a>" to describe the lifestyle of the Buddha's monks, distinguishing them from those who still engage with wealth and jewelry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha insisted that he would never say, through any method or interpretation, that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>gold and silver</b></a> should be accepted or sought after by a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Maṇicūḷaka the headman was concerned that his defense of the monks might lead to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>reprehensible position</b></a> in a Dhamma-based argument, but the Buddha reassured him that his statement was blameless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the acceptance of gold and silver as being fundamentally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato"><b>not the way of a son of the Sakyans</b></a>, thereby defining the core identity of his monastic followers by their renunciation of money? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.10/en/sujato">SN 42.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that simply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>seeing monks</b></a> who are accomplished in virtue, concentration, wisdom, and liberation is of great benefit to a person's spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>hearing the Dhamma</b></a> from monks who have attained liberation and the knowledge and vision of liberation provides immense assistance to those on the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to pursue <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>visiting monks</b></a> who are perfected in virtue and concentration, as such meetings are highly beneficial? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>attending upon</b></a> and associating with monks who are accomplished in wisdom and liberation is a fundamental practice for spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>recollecting</b></a> the qualities and teachings of monks who have attained liberation serves as a powerful support for one's own practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>going forth</b></a> into the homeless life in imitation of monks who are accomplished in virtue is a highly fruitful and beneficial action? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that hearing the Dhamma from accomplished monks leads a practitioner to dwell in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>twofold seclusion</b></a>, consisting of both bodily seclusion and mental seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a practitioner lives in seclusion and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>reflects on the Dhamma</b></a> they have heard, they begin the process of awakening the factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that as a monk reflects on the Dhamma while in seclusion, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness enlightenment factor</b></a> is aroused and eventually brought to the completion of its development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a practitioner who investigates and examines the Dhamma with wisdom arouses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>investigation-of-states</b></a> enlightenment factor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that tirelessly and vigorously investigating the Dhamma with wisdom leads to the arousal of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>energy enlightenment factor</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that for a practitioner whose energy is aroused, there arises a non-worldly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>spiritual rapture</b></a>, which marks the arousal of the rapture enlightenment factor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person's mind is filled with spiritual rapture, both their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>body and mind</b></a> become tranquil, signifying the arousal of the tranquillity enlightenment factor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one whose body is tranquil and who experiences happiness, the mind becomes concentrated, fulfilling the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>concentration enlightenment factor</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once the mind is concentrated, the practitioner looks on with care at the concentrated mind, thereby arousing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>equanimity enlightenment factor</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that those who cultivate and develop these seven factors of enlightenment can expect to achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>seven fruits</b></a> and benefits in their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that one of the seven potential fruits of the enlightenment factors is the attainment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>final knowledge</b></a> early in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if final knowledge is not attained early in life, a practitioner of the seven enlightenment factors may instead reach it at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>time of death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a specific benefit where a practitioner becomes a Non-returner who attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>final Nibbāna</b></a> in the interval between death and rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a practitioner of the enlightenment factors might become a Non-returner who attains final Nibbāna shortly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"><b>upon landing</b></a> in a new existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato">SN 46.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified unwise attention to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>sign of the beautiful</b></a> as the "nutriment" that causes unarisen sensual desire to arise and existing sensual desire to increase and expand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wise attention to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>sign of the unattractive</b></a> serves as the "non-nutriment" that prevents the arising of sensual desire and leads to the abandoning of existing sensual desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that frequently giving unwise attention to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>sign of repulsion</b></a> acts as a nutriment for the growth and fulfillment of ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind by loving-kindness</b></a> through wise attention is the primary way to starve or prevent the growth of ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed boredom, laziness, stretching, drowsiness after meals, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>mental sluggishness</b></a> as factors that, when combined with unwise attention, nourish sloth and torpor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended wise attention to the element of arousal, the element of endeavor, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>element of exertion</b></a> as the means to prevent the arising of sloth and torpor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that unwise attention to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>unpeaceful mind</b></a> provides the necessary nutriment for the arising and increase of restlessness and remorse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that wise attention to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>peace of mind</b></a> is the specific non-nutriment that prevents restlessness and remorse from overwhelming a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that frequently giving unwise attention to things that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>grounds for doubt</b></a> is what allows spiritual doubt to arise and gain strength? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to use wise attention to distinguish between <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>wholesome and unwholesome</b></a> states as a way to eliminate doubt and prevent its recurrence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>wise attention</b></a> to things that are grounds for the mindfulness enlightenment factor as the nutriment that brings that factor to the completion of its development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>lack of attention</b></a> to grounds for mindfulness acts as a "non-nutriment," meaning it prevents the mindfulness enlightenment factor from arising or being fulfilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>investigation-of-states</b></a> enlightenment factor is nourished by wise attention to blamable and blameless, and inferior and superior qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that failing to attend to the distinction between <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>dark and bright qualities</b></a> prevents the enlightenment factor of investigation of states from reaching fulfillment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>energy enlightenment factor</b></a> is nourished by wise attention to the elements of arousal, endeavor, and exertion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>lack of frequent attention</b></a> to the element of endeavor is a non-nutriment that keeps the energy enlightenment factor from arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified wise attention to things that are grounds for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>rapture enlightenment factor</b></a> as the essential nutriment for its arising and full development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>rapture enlightenment factor</b></a> cannot be fulfilled if a practitioner neglects to give frequent attention to the qualities that ground it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>tranquillity of body</b></a> and mind is nourished by wise attention, leading to the completion of the tranquillity enlightenment factor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the enlightenment factor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>tranquillity</b></a> is "starved" and prevented from arising when there is a lack of attention to both bodily and mental calm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wise attention to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>sign of serenity</b></a> and the sign of non-dispersal is the specific nutriment for the concentration enlightenment factor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a lack of attention to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>sign of non-dispersal</b></a> prevents the concentration enlightenment factor from being successfully developed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the nutriment for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>equanimity enlightenment factor</b></a> as wise attention to things that are grounds for equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that failing to attend to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>grounds for equanimity</b></a> serves as a non-nutriment, hindering the arising of the equanimity enlightenment factor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the concept of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>nutriments and non-nutriments</b></a> to explain how we can intentionally choose to feed or starve specific mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha summarized the spiritual path as the starving of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato"><b>five hindrances</b></a> and the feeding of the seven factors of enlightenment through the power of attention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.51/en/sujato">SN 46.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that wanderers of other sects once claimed they taught the same Dhamma as the Buddha, specifically the abandonment of the five hindrances and the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished his teaching from other sects by providing a specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>method of exposition</b></a> where the five hindrances are expanded to ten and the seven factors of enlightenment are expanded to fourteen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a> can be understood as twofold, consisting of both internal sensual desire and external sensual desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> is categorized as two distinct hindrances when viewed as internal ill will and external ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the hindrance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> actually consists of two separate components: sloth (thina) as one and torpor (middha) as the other? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> are considered two separate hindrances in his detailed method of exposition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the hindrance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> as having two aspects: doubt regarding internal states and doubt regarding external states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness enlightenment factor</b></a> can be divided into two categories: mindfulness of internal states and mindfulness of external states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>investigation-of-states</b></a> enlightenment factor is twofold, involving the wise examination of both internal and external phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two forms of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>energy enlightenment factor</b></a>, which are bodily energy and mental energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>rapture enlightenment factor</b></a> can be experienced in two ways: with initial and sustained thought, or without initial and sustained thought? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>tranquillity enlightenment factor</b></a> as consisting of both tranquillity of body and tranquillity of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>concentration enlightenment factor</b></a> can be categorized as concentration with initial and sustained thought and concentration without initial and sustained thought? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>equanimity enlightenment factor</b></a> is twofold, covering equanimity regarding internal states and equanimity regarding external states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that no one in the world—including gods, Maras, or Brahmas—could correctly answer how the 5 hindrances become 10 and the 7 factors become 14, except for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>Tathagata</b></a> or his disciple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the five hindrances as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>defilements of the mind</b></a> that weaken wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his monks that if wanderers of other sects claim to teach the same path, they should be asked about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>specific distinction</b></a> of how the hindrances and enlightenment factors are further divided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that wanderers of other sects would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>confounded and frustrated</b></a> if asked to explain the deeper division of the hindrances and enlightenment factors, as it is beyond their scope? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the seven factors of enlightenment should be developed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>as they really are</b></a> (yathābhūtaṃ) after abandoning the five hindrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>initial and sustained thought</b></a> (vitakka and vicāra) play a role in distinguishing different levels of the rapture and concentration enlightenment factors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>pariyāya</b></a> to describe this detailed way of explaining how five mental states become ten and seven become fourteen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s explanation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>ten hindrances</b></a> illustrates that obstacles to spiritual progress can arise from both within oneself and from external objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's division of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>fourteen enlightenment factors</b></a> shows that the path to awakening involves balancing bodily and mental qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> is weakened by the presence of the five hindrances, making their abandonment essential for developing the enlightenment factors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato">SN 46.52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if wanderers of other sects are asked about the right and wrong time to develop the enlightenment factors, they would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>confounded and frustrated</b></a> because the answer is beyond their scope? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>sluggish</b></a> (līna), it is the wrong time to develop the enlightenment factors of tranquillity, concentration, and equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared trying to rouse a sluggish mind with calming factors to a man trying to start a small fire by throwing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>wet grass and wood</b></a> onto it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the mind is sluggish, it is the right time to develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>investigation-of-states</b></a> enlightenment factor to help rouse the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>energy enlightenment factor</b></a> is specifically appropriate for development when the mind has become sluggish or inactive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended developing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>rapture enlightenment factor</b></a> as a way to effectively arouse and uplift a sluggish mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of throwing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>dry grass and wood</b></a> onto a small fire to illustrate how investigation, energy, and rapture can successfully rouse a sluggish mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>restless</b></a> (uddhata), it is the wrong time to develop the enlightenment factors of investigation-of-states, energy, and rapture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared trying to calm a restless mind with arousing factors to a man trying to extinguish a large bonfire by throwing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>dry wood and grass</b></a> into it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>tranquillity enlightenment factor</b></a> is the correct quality to develop when the mind has become restless and excited? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>concentration enlightenment factor</b></a> is highly effective at settling the mind when it is in a state of restlessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>equanimity enlightenment factor</b></a> as a necessary quality to develop when one needs to subside a restless and overactive mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of throwing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>wet grass and soil</b></a> onto a large bonfire to illustrate how tranquillity, concentration, and equanimity can successfully calm a restless mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha famously declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> (sati) is always useful (sabbatthika) regardless of whether the mind is sluggish or restless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>tranquillity</b></a> enlightenment factor is difficult to arouse when the mind is already sluggish because the mind is already too close to a state of inactivity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that only a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>Tathagata</b></a> or his disciple could correctly explain the appropriate timing for developing each of the seven factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>sluggish mind</b></a> is easy to rouse specifically by using the qualities of investigation, energy, and rapture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>restless mind</b></a> is easy to subside specifically by using the qualities of tranquillity, concentration, and equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>blowing with the mouth</b></a> (mukhavāta) on a fire to represent the active rousing of the mind through investigation and energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that using investigation and energy on an already <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>restless mind</b></a> is counterproductive, as it makes the mind even harder to calm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the enlightenment factor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> is particularly "untimely" when the mind is sluggish, as it does nothing to provide the necessary stimulation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato">SN 46.53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.31/en/sujato"><b>streams of merit</b></a> and streams of the wholesome that serve as nourishment for happiness and lead to the attainment of what is wished for? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.31/en/sujato">SN 55.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.31/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence</b></a> in the Buddha, recognizing him as the Enlightened One and the Teacher of gods and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.31/en/sujato">SN 55.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.31/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence</b></a> in the Dhamma—the teaching that is well-expounded by the Blessed One and to be personally realized by the wise—as a significant stream of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.31/en/sujato">SN 55.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that having confirmed confidence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.31/en/sujato"><b>Sangha</b></a>, the community of disciples who have practiced well, is an unsurpassed field of merit for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.31/en/sujato">SN 55.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.31/en/sujato"><b>virtuous behavior</b></a> which is unbroken, unspotted, and leads to concentration is considered one of the four primary sources of merit and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.31/en/sujato">SN 55.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple who dwells at home with a mind free from the stain of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.32/en/sujato"><b>stinginess</b></a>, being open-handed and delighting in giving, possesses a powerful stream of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.32/en/sujato">SN 55.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.32/en/sujato"><b>devoted to charity</b></a> and rejoicing in sharing and giving as an alternative fourth stream of merit that provides nourishment for happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.32/en/sujato">SN 55.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of heavy rain falling on a mountaintop to explain how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.38/en/sujato"><b>spiritual qualities</b></a> flow downward to fill mountain clefts, rifts, and gullies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.38/en/sujato">SN 55.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that just as small rivers eventually fill the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.38/en/sujato"><b>great ocean</b></a>, the streams of merit from confidence and virtue flow together toward a grand spiritual goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.38/en/sujato">SN 55.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that having unshakeable confidence in the Triple Gem and practicing virtuous behavior are factors that flow together and lead to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.38/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.38/en/sujato">SN 55.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the cumulative effect of a disciple's confidence and virtue as a movement that goes to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.38/en/sujato"><b>further shore</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.38/en/sujato">SN 55.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.31/en/sujato"><b>nourishment for happiness</b></a> as the direct result for any noble disciple who consistently practices the four streams of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.31/en/sujato">SN 55.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the process of spiritual filling by describing how heavy rain fills <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.38/en/sujato"><b>mountain rifts</b></a> and pools before eventually flowing into the sea? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.38/en/sujato">SN 55.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a noble disciple, the qualities of confidence and morality are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.38/en/sujato"><b>flowing things</b></a> that lead toward the ultimate goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.38/en/sujato">SN 55.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha resided at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>Nigrodha Park</b></a> in Kapilavatthu when he explained the specific behaviors that distinguish a disciple who lives negligently from one who lives diligently? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Nandiya the Sakyan asked about a disciple who lacks the four factors of stream-entry, the Buddha identified such a person as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>outsider</b></a> who stands on the side of worldlings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>living negligently</b></a> if they possess confirmed confidence in the Buddha but are satisfied with that alone and do not strive for seclusion by day or meditation by night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence</b></a> in the Enlightened One as a factor that, when coupled with complacency instead of further effort, leads to a negligent life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a disciple is negligent, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>gladness</b></a> does not arise, which in turn prevents the development of rapture and tranquility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a disciple lacks rapture and tranquility, they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>dwell in suffering</b></a>, making it impossible for their mind to become concentrated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person's mind is not concentrated, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>phenomena</b></a> do not become manifest to them, and they are thus reckoned as living negligently? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>living diligently</b></a> as the state where a noble disciple has confirmed confidence in the Buddha but remains unsatisfied with that alone, striving further for spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a disciple who lives diligently and strives further, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>gladness is born</b></a>, which serves as the foundation for rapture and physical tranquility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a diligent disciple’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>body becomes tranquil</b></a>, they experience happiness, which then allows the mind to reach concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for one with a concentrated mind, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>spiritual phenomena</b></a> become manifest, confirming that the disciple is living diligently? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being satisfied with having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>virtues dear to the noble ones</b></a> without further practice in meditation leads a disciple to be categorized as one who lives negligently? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>seclusion by day</b></a> and meditation by night as the necessary efforts a noble disciple must make to avoid falling into negligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a disciple as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>unsatisfied</b></a> with their current attainment of virtue or confidence if they continue to exert effort for higher spiritual states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>rapture</b></a> arises in one who is filled with gladness, and this rapture is a critical link in the chain toward mental concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>happy person</b></a> finds it much easier to concentrate the mind compared to one who is dwelling in suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Nandiya the Sakyan that confirmed confidence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>Sangha</b></a> is one of the four factors that can either lead to negligence if one is complacent, or diligence if one strives further? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even with confirmed confidence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a>, a noble disciple is still considered negligent if they do not pursue further mental development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>worldling</b></a> to describe anyone who completely lacks the four factors of stream-entry, distinguishing them from noble disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the sequence from gladness to rapture to tranquility to happiness to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> is the path by which spiritual phenomena become manifest to the diligent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.40/en/sujato">SN 55.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato"><b>streams of merit</b></a> and streams of the wholesome that provide "nourishment for happiness" and lead to the attainment of what is longed for and wished for? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato">SN 55.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple who possesses confirmed confidence in the Triple Gem along with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato"><b>virtues dear to the noble ones</b></a> has a mass of merit that is incalculable and immeasurable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato">SN 55.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the vast merit of a noble disciple to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato"><b>water in the ocean</b></a>, noting that it is impossible to measure such merit in specific quantities, just as one cannot count every gallon of the sea? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato">SN 55.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of many rivers flowing into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato"><b>great ocean</b></a> to illustrate how streams of merit flow toward a wise person who is a giver of food, drink, and clothing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato">SN 55.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato"><b>great ocean</b></a> as a fearsome and immeasurable reservoir that serves as the home for many jewels, comparing its abundance to the spiritual wealth of a virtuous person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato">SN 55.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence</b></a> in the Buddha involves recognizing him as the Enlightened One, the Teacher of gods and humans, and the Blessed One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato">SN 55.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato"><b>unbroken virtue</b></a>—which is untainted, liberating, and leads to concentration—as one of the four essential sources of merit and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato">SN 55.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a disciple who provides <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato"><b>bedding and mats</b></a> to others, streams of merit flow toward them just as rivers flow toward the sea? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato">SN 55.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described merit not just as a future reward but as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato"><b>nourishment for happiness</b></a> that supports a disciple's spiritual growth and well-being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato">SN 55.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the merit belonging to a noble disciple with the four factors of stream-entry is simply reckoned as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato"><b>great mass of merit</b></a> because it is beyond human calculation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.41/en/sujato">SN 55.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Tatiyaabhisanda Sutta, the Buddha identified possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato"><b>noble wisdom</b></a> regarding the arising and passing away of phenomena as a primary stream of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato">SN 55.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wisdom which is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato"><b>penetrative</b></a> and leads to the complete destruction of suffering is a fundamental stream of the wholesome that leads to happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato">SN 55.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is established in the wholesome and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato"><b>develops the path</b></a> for the attainment of the Deathless will not tremble when death approaches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato">SN 55.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described one who has attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato"><b>essence of the Dhamma</b></a> and delights in the destruction of taints as a "seeker of merit" who has achieved a state of peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato">SN 55.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence</b></a> in the Dhamma—the teaching that is to be personally realized by the wise—is a source of immeasurable merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato">SN 55.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that confidence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato"><b>Sangha</b></a>—the community that has practiced well and is an unsurpassed field of merit—is a stream of the wholesome that provides nourishment for happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato">SN 55.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato"><b>King of Death</b></a> to emphasize that those who have correctly developed the path do not fear the inevitable arrival of mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato">SN 55.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato"><b>noble wisdom</b></a> specifically as the understanding that discerns the arising and passing away of things, ultimately leading to the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato">SN 55.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato"><b>fixed in the wholesome</b></a> and desires merit as one who is actively cultivating the path to the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato">SN 55.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the four <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato"><b>streams of merit</b></a> are so vast they are "incalculable" (asaṅkhyeyyo), representing a spiritual wealth that cannot be measured by standard human units? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.43/en/sujato">SN 55.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>Nigrodha Park</b></a> in Kapilavatthu when he gave instructions on how a wise lay follower should spiritually console a sick and afflicted friend? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise lay follower should console a peer who is sick with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>four comforting assurances</b></a> regarding confirmed confidence in the Triple Gem and noble virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged a dying person to let go of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>concern for parents</b></a> by reminding them that every person is subject to death regardless of whether they maintain such concern? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that if a dying person still feels concern for their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>wife and children</b></a>, they should be urged to abandon that attachment because death will occur whether they worry or not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that heavenly sensual pleasures are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>more excellent</b></a> and refined than human ones, and encouraged a dying person to direct their mind away from human desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a lay follower’s mind should be progressively directed from human desires to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>Devas of the Four Great Kings</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>Tāvatiṃsa devas</b></a> as having a more sublime and excellent existence than the Devas of the Four Great Kings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>Yāma devas</b></a> as a higher destination for the mind to be directed toward than the Tāvatiṃsa devas during spiritual counseling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mind should be led to value the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>Tusita devas</b></a> over the Yāma devas as a person moves through progressive stages of letting go? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>Nimmānarati devas</b></a> as being more refined and excellent in their existence than the Tusita devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>Paranimmitavasavatti devas</b></a> represent a higher and more excellent state of being than the Nimmānarati devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised directing the mind toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>Brahma-world</b></a> as it is considered even more excellent and refined than the realm of the Paranimmitavasavatti devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>Brahma-world is impermanent</b></a>, unstable, and still included within the sphere of "identity" (sakkāya)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ultimate spiritual goal for a dying lay follower is to withdraw the mind even from the Brahma-world and resolve it upon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>cessation of identity</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>no difference</b></a> in the quality of liberation between a lay follower whose mind is liberated from the taints and a monk whose mind is likewise liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specified that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>confirmed confidence in the Buddha</b></a>—recognizing him as the Enlightened One and Teacher of gods and humans—is the first comfort for a sick disciple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a sick person should be comforted by their possession of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>virtues dear to the noble ones</b></a>, which are unbroken, untainted, and conducive to concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised Mahānāma the Sakyan that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>wise lay follower</b></a> is fully capable of providing the highest spiritual guidance to another lay person in their final moments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s guidance for the dying involves a systematic process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>detaching the mind</b></a> from human ties, then from heavenly realms, and finally from all forms of conditioned existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the reality that all beings are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato"><b>subject to death</b></a>, using this as a logical foundation to help a dying person relinquish worldly attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.54/en/sujato">SN 55.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>saṃsāra is without a discoverable beginning</b></a> for beings who are obstructed by the hindrance of ignorance and fettered by the bond of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, even when a time comes that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>great ocean dries up</b></a>, evaporates, and is no more, there is still no end to suffering for beings who continue to roam and wander in saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that although a time eventually arrives when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>Mount Sineru is burned and destroyed</b></a>, the suffering of beings bound by craving and ignorance does not come to an end through that cosmic destruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha declared that even the eventual time when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>great earth is consumed by fire</b></a> and ceases to exist does not bring about an end to the suffering of beings who have not yet reached liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the striking analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>dog tied to a pillar</b></a> with a leash to describe how an uninstructed worldling constantly runs and circles around the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>ignorance as the hindrance</b></a> and craving as the fetter that causes beings to keep revolving through the cycle of birth and death without finding a finish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that an uninstructed worldling who regards <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>form as self</b></a> is like a leashed dog that can only run and circle around the post to which it is tied? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by revolving around <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>feeling, perception, and volitional formations</b></a> as if they were the self, a person fails to be liberated from birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an uninstructed person may regard <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>consciousness as self</b></a>, or see the self as possessing consciousness, which keeps them bound to the cycle of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that because a person "runs and circles" around the five aggregates of clinging, they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>not liberated from sorrow</b></a>, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>taught noble disciple</b></a> as one who does not regard form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, or consciousness as self and is therefore not bound to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when a practitioner <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>stops running and circling</b></a> around the five aggregates, they finally become liberated from the entire mass of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that the uninstructed worldling is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>not liberated from birth and death</b></a> precisely because they continue to identify with and revolve around the changing nature of the aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that liberation occurs only when one is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>released from the five aggregates</b></a>, meaning they no longer view these mental and physical processes as "mine" or "myself"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the image of a dog tied to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato"><b>strong post or pillar</b></a> to illustrate the psychological entrapment of those who are undisciplined in the Dhamma and see the aggregates as their identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn22.99/en/sujato">SN 22.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sariputta and Venerable Mahacunda visited the Venerable Channa when he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>gravely ill</b></a> and experiencing such intense pain that he felt his condition was only worsening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Channa described his extreme head pain to Sariputta by comparing it to a strong man splitting his head open with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>sharp sword</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when describing his physical agony, the Venerable Channa told Sariputta that the pain in his head felt like a strong man tightening a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>leather strap</b></a> around his skull as a headband? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Channa compared the violent "winds" cutting through his stomach to the sensation of a skilled butcher carving a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>cow’s belly</b></a> with a sharp knife? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Channa described the burning sensation in his body as being like two strong men grabbing a weaker man by the arms and roasting him over a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>pit of coals</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that due to his unbearable physical suffering, the Venerable Channa informed Sariputta that he intended to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>use the knife</b></a> and end his own life, stating he had no desire to live? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sariputta, out of compassion, begged Channa not to use the knife and offered to personally search for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>suitable food</b></a> for him if he lacked it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sariputta offered to go out and find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>suitable medicine</b></a> for the Venerable Channa to help him endure his illness and prevent him from taking his own life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sariputta told the ailing Channa that if he lacked a proper caregiver, Sariputta himself would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>wait upon him</b></a> to ensure his needs were met? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Channa claimed he would end his life in a way that was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>blameless</b></a> (<i>anupavajja</i>), asserting he had served the Buddha with love for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Sariputta questioned him on his views, the Venerable Channa stated he did not regard the eye, eye-consciousness, or things cognizable by the eye as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>mine or self</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Channa explained to Sariputta that he practiced non-attachment toward the tongue and tastes because he had seen and understood their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>cessation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Channa affirmed to Sariputta that he viewed the mind and mind-consciousness with the wisdom that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>this is not mine</b></a>, this I am not, this is not my self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahacunda advised Channa to constantly reflect on the Buddha's teaching that for one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>dependent</b></a>, there is wavering, but for one who is independent, there is no wavering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahacunda taught that when there is no wavering, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>stillness</b></a> (<i>passaddhi</i>), and when there is stillness, there is no leaning toward worldliness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the teaching Mahacunda gave to Channa, when there is no leaning, there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>coming and going</b></a> in terms of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahacunda explained that when there is no coming and going, there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>passing away</b></a> and being reborn in a new state of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahacunda described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a> as a state that exists neither here, nor beyond, nor in between the two? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after Sariputta and Mahacunda finished their exhortation and departed, the Venerable Channa carried out his intention and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>used the knife</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Sariputta asked the Buddha about Channa's future destination after his suicide, the Buddha declared that Channa had actually behaved <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>blamelessly</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he defines a person as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>blameful</b></a> (<i>saupavajja</i>) only if they lay down one body and take up another body through craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that because the Venerable Channa did not take up a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>new body</b></a> after death, his act of using the knife was not accompanied by spiritual blame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sariputta noted that Channa had many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>friendly families</b></a> and supporters in the village of Vajjigama, which originally led Sariputta to worry about Channa's attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite Channa's strong social ties in Vajjigama, the Buddha insisted that Channa was blameless because he had attained the state where he no longer <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato"><b>clung</b></a> to further existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.87/en/sujato">SN 35.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying in the town of Uruvelakappa among the Mallans when he was approached by the headman Bhadraka, who requested a teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>origin and passing away</b></a> of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declined to teach the headman Bhadraka about the origin of suffering using examples from the past because it might lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>doubt and perplexity</b></a> for the listener? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he would not teach the headman Bhadraka about the origin of suffering using examples from the future, as it could likewise result in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>doubt and perplexity</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha chose to teach the headman Bhadraka about the origin of suffering by focusing on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>present moment</b></a> while they were both sitting right there in Uruvelakappa? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the people of Uruvelakappa as an example to show that the death or imprisonment of certain neighbors would cause the headman <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>sorrow and lamentation</b></a>, while the same events happening to others would not affect him at all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the headman Bhadraka admitted to the Buddha that his sorrow over the misfortune of others was entirely dependent on whether or not he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>desire and lust</b></a> for those specific individuals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by understanding the present cause of suffering, one can infer that all suffering in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>past and future</b></a> is also rooted in desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the headman Bhadraka to use the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>timeless Dhamma</b></a> he had just realized to understand that all suffering is desire-rooted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>desire is the root</b></a> of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the headman Bhadraka expressed how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>wonderful and marvelous</b></a> it was that the Buddha's teaching on the root of suffering so perfectly matched his own personal experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the headman Bhadraka told the Buddha about his son Ciravāsi, explaining that even a slight delay in news about the boy would cause him <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>extreme anxiety</b></a> and change of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the headman Bhadraka confessed to the Buddha that if any harm came to his son Ciravāsi, it might cause such grief that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>very life</b></a> would be altered or endangered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the headman’s intense love for his son to illustrate that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>suffering arises</b></a> specifically from desire and attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned the headman about a time before he had met his wife, pointing out that before he saw or heard of her, he had no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>desire or love</b></a> for her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that it was only through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>seeing or hearing</b></a> of his wife that desire and love arose in the headman Bhadraka? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha showed that if the headman’s wife were to face execution or blame, he would experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>despair and displeasure</b></a> solely because of his pre-existing desire for her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any suffering that arises is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>rooted in desire</b></a> and has desire as its source? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the lack of sorrow for some people in Uruvelakappa was due to the headman having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>no desire</b></a> for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's conversation with Bhadraka demonstrates a teaching method that moves from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>specific and personal</b></a> (like a son or wife) to a universal principle about suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that suffering does not just happen randomly, but is always <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato"><b>connected to desire</b></a> as its underlying cause? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.11/en/sujato">SN 42.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the liberation of the mind by loving-kindness culminates in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>beautiful</b></a> for a monk who has not yet reached a higher liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the liberation of the mind by compassion culminates in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite space</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the liberation of the mind by appreciative joy has the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>base of infinite consciousness</b></a> as its culmination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the liberation of the mind by equanimity culminates in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>base of nothingness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha challenged his followers to ask wanderers of other sects what the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>destination</b></a>, excellence, fruit, and final goal of their meditation practices were, implying they would not be able to answer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that no one in the world—including gods, Maras, or Brahmas—could satisfactorily answer questions about the specific fruits of the divine abodes unless they were a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>Tathagata</b></a> or a disciple of the Tathagata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to develop the liberation of the mind by loving-kindness specifically in conjunction with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when practicing the divine abodes, the Buddha said a monk can train himself to perceive the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>repulsive as unrepulsive</b></a> if he so desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when practicing the divine abodes, the Buddha said a monk can train himself to perceive the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>unrepulsive as repulsive</b></a> if he so desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a monk to avoid both the repulsive and unrepulsive and dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>equanimous</b></a>, mindful and clearly aware, while practicing the divine abodes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the four divine abodes (loving-kindness, compassion, appreciative joy, and equanimity) should be developed with a mind that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>boundless</b></a> and free from hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the process of spreading loving-kindness to the whole world, above, below, and across, as making it <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>all-encompassing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the factors of enlightenment, when joined with the divine abodes, must be based upon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a>, fading away, and cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the factors of enlightenment developed through these meditations should result in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>relinquishment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that wanderers of other sects questioned the monks about what the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>distinction</b></a> was between their own teaching and the teaching of the Buddha regarding the abandonment of hindrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the five hindrances as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>weakeners of wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the liberation of the mind by compassion is particularly effective when accompanied by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness factor of enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>investigation of phenomena</b></a> factor of enlightenment as one of the key components to be developed alongside appreciative joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the development of the liberation of the mind by equanimity can be integrated with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>rapture factor of enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>tranquillity factor of enlightenment</b></a> is essential when developing the heart's release through the four divine abodes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>concentration factor of enlightenment</b></a> should be developed in conjunction with boundless states like loving-kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>equanimity factor of enlightenment</b></a> as a final factor to be cultivated alongside the liberation of the mind by equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying in the town of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>Haliddavasana</b></a> among the Koliyans when he gave this teaching on the divine abodes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's disciples visited a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>park of wanderers</b></a> of other sects because it was too early in the morning to go for alms in the town? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks, after being questioned by wanderers about the difference in their teachings, did not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>rejoice in or reject</b></a> their words but instead went to the Buddha to learn the true meaning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the liberation of the mind by loving-kindness as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>free from ill will</b></a> and hostility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk can train himself to perceive both the unrepulsive and repulsive as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato"><b>repulsive</b></a> while developing the divine abodes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.54/en/sujato">SN 46.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmins and householders of Veludvara visited the Buddha because they had heard his excellent reputation as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>Arhat</b></a> and a Perfectly Enlightened One who is a teacher of both gods and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmins of Veludvara specifically asked the Buddha for a teaching that would allow them to live a comfortable householder life with families and wealth while still attaining a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha responded to the householders by offering a specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>Dharma discourse</b></a> that can be applied to oneself, based on reflecting on one's own desires for happiness and aversion to pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that because we ourselves wish to live and not die, we should reflect that since being killed would be unpleasant to us, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>others</b></a> also find it unpleasant to be killed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a noble disciple purifies their conduct regarding life by abstaining from killing, encouraging others to abstain, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise</b></a> of such abstinence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that just as we would find it unpleasant if someone took our property by way of theft, we should realize that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>stealing</b></a> from another person would be equally unpleasant to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described bodily conduct as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>purified in three ways</b></a> when a person personally avoids taking what is not given, urges others to avoid it, and praises the act of not stealing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that since we would find it unpleasant if someone committed adultery with our own wives, we should likewise refrain from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> with the wives of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a noble disciple’s bodily conduct is purified when they abstain from sexual misconduct, motivate others to abstain, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>speak in praise</b></a> of such abstinence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a reflexive logic for speech, noting that if we dislike our own interests being ruined by lies, we should conclude that we must refrain from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>false speech</b></a> toward others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that verbal conduct is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>purified in three ways</b></a> when one personally avoids lying, encourages others to avoid lying, and praises the avoidance of lies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that because it is unpleasant for our friends to be turned against us by someone's talk, we should never use <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a> to cause division among others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the purification of speech as including a personal commitment to avoid divisive talk, urging others to stop such talk, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>speaking in praise</b></a> of avoiding it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested reflecting that since we dislike being addressed with harsh words, we should conclude that we should abstain from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a> directed at anyone else? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that verbal conduct is purified when one abandons harsh language, encourages others to abandon it, and speaks in praise of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>gentle speech</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that if we find it unpleasant when others address us with frivolous or pointless talk, we should personally refrain from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described speech as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>thrice-purified</b></a> when a person abandons idle chatter, urges others to abandon it, and speaks in praise of such abandonment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple possesses confirmed confidence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>Buddha</b></a>, recognizing him as the Blessed One, the Teacher of gods and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a noble disciple has confirmed confidence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a>, seeing it as a teaching that is well-expounded and to be personally experienced by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple has confirmed confidence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>Sangha</b></a>, the community of practitioners who are worthy of gifts and are an unsurpassed field of merit for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a noble disciple as possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>virtues loved by the Noble Ones</b></a>, which are unbroken, unblemished, and lead to concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a noble disciple who possesses seven wholesome qualities and the four factors of stream-entry may <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>declare of themselves</b></a> that they are finished with rebirth in hell or the animal realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato"><b>stream-enterer</b></a> (Sotāpanna) is one who is no longer subject to falling into lower worlds and is certain of eventually attaining full enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn55.7/en/sujato">SN 55.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the Venerable Radha that where there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a>, there is Māra, or a killer, or one who is dying, and thus one should view form as a disease, a tumor, and a dart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato">SN 23.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that seeing the aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> as Māra, as a source of misery, and as misery itself constitutes seeing rightly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato">SN 23.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one should view <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato"><b>perception</b></a> as a killer and as a dart in order to achieve right viewing and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato">SN 23.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if one views <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> as Māra and as a tumor, they are practicing the path toward revulsion and dispassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato">SN 23.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> should be seen as a killer and as a source of misery, for where consciousness exists, there is Māra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato">SN 23.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed the Venerable Radha that the ultimate purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato"><b>right viewing</b></a> is to lead a practitioner to a state of revulsion toward the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato">SN 23.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the purpose of achieving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato"><b>revulsion</b></a> toward the aggregates of form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness is to reach dispassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato">SN 23.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> is to attain liberation from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato">SN 23.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a> as the attainment of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato">SN 23.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Venerable Radha asked what lies beyond Nibbāna, the Buddha replied that he had overstepped the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato"><b>limit of the question</b></a> because the holy life is lived for the sake of Nibbāna as its final destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato">SN 23.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato"><b>"a being"</b></a> (satta) as one who is "stuck" and "strongly stuck" through desire, lust, delight, and craving for the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato">SN 23.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of children playing with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato"><b>sandcastles</b></a> to describe how beings cherish and possess the five aggregates as long as they have not lost their lust and craving for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato">SN 23.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the process of letting go of attachment to children who, once they lose interest in their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato"><b>sandcastles</b></a>, scatter and demolish them with their hands and feet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato">SN 23.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted his followers to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato"><b>shatter and demolish</b></a> their attachment to form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness to practice for the destruction of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato">SN 23.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly defined Nibbāna as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato">SN 23.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is called "a being" specifically because of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato"><b>strong attachment</b></a> they feel toward feeling, perception, and volitional formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato">SN 23.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> is grounded in Nibbāna, has Nibbāna as its destination, and Nibbāna as its final culmination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.1/en/sujato">SN 23.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one should practice the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a> by treating the five aggregates like toys that must be discarded once desire for them has vanished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.2/en/sujato">SN 23.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Puṇṇa specifically approached the Buddha to request a brief teaching so that he could dwell in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>solitude</b></a> while remaining diligent, ardent, and resolute in his spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> arises when a monk relishes, welcomes, and remains fastened to agreeable sense objects, causing delight to arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> is achieved when a monk does not relish, welcome, or remain fastened to sense objects, thereby causing delight to cease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Ven. Puṇṇa announced his intention to live in Sunāparanta, the Buddha warned him that the people there were notoriously <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>fierce and rough</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Puṇṇa told the Buddha he would consider the people of Sunāparanta to be "very kind" even if they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>abusing and reviling</b></a> him, as long as they did not strike him with their hands? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Puṇṇa resolved to think highly of the people of Sunāparanta even if they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>striking with hands</b></a>, provided they did not attack him with clods of earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Puṇṇa informed the Buddha he would maintain a positive view of the people of Sunāparanta even if they hit him with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>clods of earth</b></a>, so long as they did not strike him with sticks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Puṇṇa expressed to the Buddha that he would still perceive his attackers as kind if they beat him with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>sticks</b></a>, provided they refrained from using knives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Puṇṇa was so determined that he told the Buddha he would be grateful if people attacked him with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>knives</b></a>, as long as they did not actually take his life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Puṇṇa explained to the Buddha that even if the people of Sunāparanta were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>taking his life</b></a> with a sharp knife, he would feel he had gained an end to life without having to seek it out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Puṇṇa observed to the Buddha that some disciples, feeling humiliated and revolted by the body, actually search for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>bringer of the knife</b></a> to end their lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highly commended Ven. Puṇṇa for his extraordinary <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>self-control and peace</b></a>, giving him permission to live among the fierce people of Sunāparanta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed that after Ven. Puṇṇa traveled to Sunāparanta, he successfully established five hundred <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>laymen</b></a> in the practice during a single rainy season? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed Ven. Puṇṇa also successfully established five hundred <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>laywomen</b></a> in the practice during that same rainy season in Sunāparanta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that Ven. Puṇṇa realized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>three true knowledges</b></a> during the very same rainy season he spent in Sunāparanta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared Ven. Puṇṇa had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>attained Parinibbāna</b></a> after passing away in the Sunāparanta region? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the late Ven. Puṇṇa as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>wise man</b></a> who had practiced in accordance with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly noted to his monks that Ven. Puṇṇa did not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>harass him</b></a> with unnecessary disputes or problems concerning the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a monk relishes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>agreeable forms</b></a> cognizable by the eye, delight arises, which leads to the origin of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the origin of suffering is linked to relishing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> cognizable by the mind, just as it is with the physical senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the mental actions of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>relishing and welcoming</b></a> sense objects are the specific causes for the arising of delight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Puṇṇa’s commitment to being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato"><b>ardent and resolute</b></a> was his primary reason for seeking a concise summary of the Dhamma from the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.88/en/sujato">SN 35.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Rasiya the headman asked if the Buddha condemned all forms of asceticism, the Buddha clarified that he only censures those who engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>self-mortification</b></a> which is painful, ignoble, and unbeneficial? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are two extremes to be avoided by one who has gone forth: devotion to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> and devotion to self-mortification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>Middle Way</b></a> as a path he discovered that gives rise to vision and knowledge, and leads to peace and Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>—starting with right view and ending with right concentration—as the specific Middle Way that avoids both indulgence and self-torment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized one type of wealth-seeker as blameworthy because they seek wealth <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>unrighteously and violently</b></a>, yet fail to even make themselves happy with it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained a consumer of wealth is blameworthy on three grounds if they seek wealth unrighteously, do not please themselves, and do <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>not share</b></a> it or do merit with it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who seeks wealth unrighteously and violently but uses it to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>please themselves</b></a> is praiseworthy on one ground while remaining blameworthy on two others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a consumer of sensual pleasures who seeks wealth unrighteously and violently, yet still <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>shares it</b></a> and performs meritorious deeds, as being praiseworthy on two grounds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if wealth is sought both righteously and unrighteously, a person is still blameworthy for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>unrighteous seeking</b></a> and for failing to use the wealth for merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave praise to wealth-seekers who obtain their means <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>without violence</b></a>, even if they are still criticized for how they spend or fail to share that wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a specific type of person who seeks wealth righteously and without violence but is blameworthy because they do not make themselves happy or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>do merit</b></a> with it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised a consumer of sensual pleasures on two grounds if they seek wealth righteously and make themselves happy, even if they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>neglect to share</b></a> that wealth with others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who seeks wealth righteously, makes themselves happy, and shares it for merit can still be blameworthy if they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>infatuated</b></a> and blind to the danger of those riches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the "foremost, best, and supreme" consumer of wealth as one who seeks it righteously, shares it, and uses it <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>without infatuation</b></a>, fully aware of the peril? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>seeing the peril</b></a> (ādīnavadassāvī) to describe the wise attitude a person should have while enjoying the wealth they have earned righteously? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the importance of possessing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>wisdom of escape</b></a> when consuming sensual pleasures, so that one does not become enslaved by their possessions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized three types of ascetics who lead a rough life, the first being those who torment themselves but fail to attain any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered an ascetic blameworthy on three grounds if they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>torment themselves</b></a>, fail to achieve wholesome states, and do not realize any superhuman knowledge or vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised an ascetic who leads a rough life on one ground if they actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>attain wholesome states</b></a>, despite the fact that they are still blamed for self-torment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an ascetic who realizes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>superhuman knowledge</b></a> and vision (alamariyañāṇadassanavisesa) is praiseworthy for that achievement, even if their practice involves painful self-torment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even the most successful self-mortifying ascetic is still <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>blameworthy on one ground</b></a> according to the Buddha, because they unnecessarily torment and burn themselves up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha introduced the concept of three types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>wearing away</b></a> (nijjarā) that are visible here and now, which do not involve self-torment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> is abandoned, a person no longer intends for their own affliction or the affliction of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the abandonment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> as a form of "wearing away" that is immediate and to be personally experienced by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the abandonment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> as the third type of righteous "wearing away" that leads to the end of suffering without ignoble pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the destruction of greed, hatred, and delusion is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>timeless</b></a> (akālikā), meaning its benefits and the truth of its abandonment can be realized without delay? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the abandonment of defilements as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>inviting one to come and see</b></a> (ehipassika), because the results are verifiable through practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the "wearing away" of unwholesome states is to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>personally experienced</b></a> by the wise (paccattaṃ veditabbā viññūhi)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that seeking wealth <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>righteously</b></a> (dhammena) is the first of several praiseworthy qualities for a layperson? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly praised those who seek wealth <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>without coercion</b></a> or violence (asahasena) as part of their righteous livelihood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person's enjoyment of wealth is only truly praiseworthy if they also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>perform meritorious deeds</b></a> with that wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>captivated</b></a> (mucchito) by one's wealth is a ground for being blamed, even if that wealth was acquired honestly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an ascetic who strives with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>burning zeal</b></a> but fails to achieve the goal is blameworthy for their useless suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the true goal of the holy life is not the "rough life" itself, but the attainment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>distinction in knowledge</b></a> and vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>uninfatuated</b></a> (amucchito) to describe the ideal state of a layperson who uses wealth while remaining spiritually free? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that acting out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>lust, hatred, or delusion</b></a> leads a person to intend for their own affliction and the affliction of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the true "wearing away" as a practice that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>visible here and now</b></a> (sandiṭṭhikā), rather than a promise of future benefit only? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded his teaching to Rasiya by highlighting that the three forms of erosion—of lust, hatred, and delusion—are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato"><b>onward-leading</b></a> (opaneyyikā) to the final goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.12/en/sujato">SN 42.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained why even hymns that have been long-studied may not occur to the mind when it is overwhelmed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a mind obsessed with sensual desire to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>bowl of water</b></a> mixed with various dyes like lac, turmeric, indigo, or madder, in which a person cannot see their own reflection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a person does not understand the escape from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> as it really is, they cannot clearly see their own good or the good of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a bowl of water being heated by a fire, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>boiling and bubbling</b></a>, to describe a mind overcome by ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> act like water-plants and algae covering a bowl of water, preventing a person from seeing things as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a mind filled with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> to a bowl of water stirred by the wind, rippling and swirling, making it impossible to see one's own reflection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a mind hindered by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> as being like a bowl of water that is turbid, muddy, and murky, and then placed in the dark? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when the mind is not obsessed with sensual desire, even hymns that have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>not been studied</b></a> for a long time will occur to the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the absence of hindrances allows a person to understand their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>own good</b></a>, the good of others, and the good of both? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the image of a bowl of water that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>not dyed</b></a> by lac or turmeric to illustrate a mind free from the hindrance of sensual desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a mind free from ill will is like water that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>not boiling</b></a> or bubbling, allowing for clear vision and understanding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a mind free from sloth and torpor to a bowl of water that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>not covered</b></a> by water-plants or algae? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind free from restlessness and remorse is like a bowl of water <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>not stirred</b></a> by the wind, remaining calm and still? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a mind free from doubt as being like a bowl of water that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>clear and bright</b></a>, placed in the light so one can see their reflection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> as being non-obstructive and non-hindering to the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the factors of enlightenment are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>not corruptions</b></a> of the mind, unlike the five hindrances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when the seven factors of enlightenment are developed and cultivated, they lead to the realization of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>fruit of true knowledge</b></a> and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Saṅgārava asked the Buddha why <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>studied hymns</b></a> sometimes do not occur to the mind while unstudied ones sometimes do? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> factor of enlightenment is one of the qualities that leads to liberation when properly cultivated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> as one of the seven factors of enlightenment that are non-obstructive to the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Saṅgārava the Brahmin was so impressed by the Buddha’s explanation that he went for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>refuge</b></a> to the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Saṅgha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the five hindrances prevent one from knowing things <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>as they really are</b></a> (yathābhūta)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically mentioned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>lac, turmeric, and indigo</b></a> are substances that can dye water, just as sensual desire dyes the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>good sight</b></a> (cakkhumā puriso) cannot see their own reflection if the water is dyed, boiling, covered, stirred, or muddy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>realization of liberation</b></a> (vimutti) is a direct result of cultivating the factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the five hindrances as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>obstructions and coverings</b></a> that prevent the mind from functioning clearly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Saṅgārava expressed his appreciation by calling the Buddha's teaching <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>excellent</b></a> and asking to be accepted as a lay follower? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>factors of enlightenment</b></a> (bojjhaṅgā) highlights their role in overcoming the corruptions of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that knowing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> (nissaraṇa) from the hindrances is essential for attaining true knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"><b>darkness</b></a> combined with muddy water to describe the state of a mind paralyzed by doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato">SN 46.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk develops and cultivates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.1/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a>, he is able to understand the nature of reality and see things as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.1/en/sujato">SN 56.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, it is only when a monk is concentrated that he can truly understand, as it really is, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.1/en/sujato"><b>Noble Truth of Suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.1/en/sujato">SN 56.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a practitioner who possesses mental concentration is capable of discerning the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.1/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> exactly as it is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.1/en/sujato">SN 56.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that developing a concentrated mind is essential for a monk to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.1/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> as a lived reality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.1/en/sujato">SN 56.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that through the power of concentration, a monk gains the clarity to see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.1/en/sujato"><b>way leading to the cessation of suffering</b></a> as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.1/en/sujato">SN 56.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any clansmen in the past who rightly went forth from the household life into homelessness did so specifically for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.3/en/sujato"><b>realization of the Four Noble Truths</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.3/en/sujato">SN 56.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that all clansmen who will go forth from the household life into homelessness in the future will do so for the sake of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.3/en/sujato"><b>breakthrough to the Four Noble Truths</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.3/en/sujato">SN 56.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, any clansmen currently leaving the household life to lead a holy life are doing so for the purpose of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.3/en/sujato"><b>true realization</b></a> of suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path to its end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.3/en/sujato">SN 56.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.3/en/sujato"><b>exertion should be made</b></a> to understand the truth "This is suffering," as this realization is the core objective of the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.3/en/sujato">SN 56.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that all ascetics and brahmins who fully awakened to things as they really are in the past did so by awakening to these <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.5/en/sujato"><b>four Noble Truths</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.5/en/sujato">SN 56.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that no ascetic or brahmin in the future will achieve full awakening to things as they really are unless they awaken to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.5/en/sujato"><b>Noble Truth of Suffering</b></a> and its associated truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.5/en/sujato">SN 56.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, all current ascetics and brahmins who are presently attaining full awakening are doing so through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.5/en/sujato"><b>complete breakthrough</b></a> to the four facts of suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.5/en/sujato">SN 56.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that because awakening is tied to the Four Noble Truths, a person must conclude that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.5/en/sujato"><b>exertion should be made</b></a> to understand the Noble Truth of the origin of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.5/en/sujato">SN 56.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.1/en/sujato"><b>exertion should be made</b></a> to understand the Noble Truth of the way leading to the cessation of suffering, as this is the practice that follows the attainment of concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.1/en/sujato">SN 56.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.3/en/sujato"><b>purpose of homelessness</b></a> for a rightly gone-forth clansman is solely to achieve the breakthrough to the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.3/en/sujato">SN 56.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha equated the state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.5/en/sujato"><b>fully awakened</b></a> with the comprehensive and direct understanding of the Four Noble Truths as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.5/en/sujato">SN 56.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed he understands <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>magic</b></a> and the results of deceptive practices, even though he is not a magician himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>long-haired warriors</b></a> of the Koliyans to explain how one can be familiar with immoral people without being immoral oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Pāṭaliya the headman initially approached the Buddha to ask if the report that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>Ascetic Gotama</b></a> knows magic was true or a slander? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he clearly understands the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>way to hell</b></a> for those who practice magic and deception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that after the breakup of the body, a deceptive person who practices magic is reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>state of misery</b></a>, even in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>destruction of life</b></a> as one of the specific unwholesome actions whose consequences he fully understands? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained he knows exactly what kind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>rebirth</b></a> awaits those who take what is not given? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, he possesses knowledge of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a> and the specific destination of those who indulge in it after they die? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that he knows the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>result of lying</b></a> and the downward path it creates for a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a> as a behavior that leads to rebirth in a state of deprivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he understands <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a> and the suffering that follows such actions in future lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a> as a path leading to a bad destination and a lower world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>covetousness</b></a> is a mental state that he fully understands along with its kammic results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>ill will</b></a> as a condition that leads to rebirth in hell after the death of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated he knows the consequences of holding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> and where such views lead a person after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refuted teachers who claim that anyone who kills a living being always experiences <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>pain and grief</b></a> in the present life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out how a person might kill an enemy of a king and be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>rewarded</b></a> with jewels and honors, experiencing pleasure despite their act of killing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted a rewarded killer with one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>executed</b></a> by a king for murder to show that the immediate visible results of karma can vary? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha criticized teachers who claim that stealing always leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>visible suffering</b></a> in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a thief might be rewarded by a king for stealing from an enemy, appearing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>bathed and anointed</b></a> while enjoying sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a thief being led to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>execution</b></a> with his head shaved and hands bound behind his back to illustrate the visible punishment for theft? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a person committing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>adultery</b></a> with a king's enemy's wife might be honored by that king in the present life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that some who commit sexual misconduct are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>severely punished</b></a> by kings, contradicting the idea that karma always yields a uniform visible result? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha showed how a liar who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>amuses a king</b></a> with false words might receive a great gift instead of immediate suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a liar who ruins a householder's interests being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>paraded through the streets</b></a> to the sound of a harsh drum before being beheaded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered teachers who make sweeping, false claims about karma to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>immoral</b></a> and of wrong view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Pāṭaliya the headman mentioned he maintained a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>rest house</b></a> where he provided supplies to any wandering ascetics or brahmins who visited? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Pāṭaliya once hosted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>four teachers</b></a> with completely different views on the existence of another world and the fruits of karma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that one teacher Pāṭaliya encountered taught the nihilist view that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>no result of karma</b></a> and no world beyond this one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a second teacher Pāṭaliya met argued the opposite, asserting that there is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>future world</b></a> and that good and bad deeds do bear fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a third teacher Pāṭaliya met claimed that even acts like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>murder and torture</b></a> do not actually constitute evil or carry any kammic weight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a fourth teacher Pāṭaliya met insisted that when one acts, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>evil is done</b></a>, and that merit is truly gained through giving and self-control? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Pāṭaliya confessed to the Buddha that he was filled with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>perplexity and doubt</b></a> because of these conflicting teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Pāṭaliya it was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>proper to doubt</b></a> when faced with such contradictory doctrines? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha introduced a concept called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma-concentration</b></a> as a way for a disciple to abandon all doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a noble disciple as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>abandoned the ten unwholesome paths</b></a> of action? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a noble disciple dwells pervading the entire world with a mind imbued with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a noble disciple, according to the Buddha, practices pervading the four quarters with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> that is vast and measureless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a noble disciple as one who dwells pervading the world with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>altruistic joy</b></a>, free from hostility and ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the fourth dwelling of a noble disciple is pervading the world with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reasoned that if a teacher's view of "no other world" is true, a virtuous person is still <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>safe</b></a> because they have lived without harming others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that if the view that "there is a future world" is true, the noble disciple is safe because they will be reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a "safe bet" logic, stating that a disciple who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>restrained</b></a> in body, speech, and mind wins on both counts regardless of which teacher is right? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a noble disciple reflects on their own virtuous and harmless life, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>gladness</b></a> is born within them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, from gladness comes rapture, and for one with a rapturous mind, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>body becomes tranquil</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that one who feels happiness finds their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>mind concentrated</b></a>, which is the essence of Dhamma-concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the noble disciple is free from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>covetousness and ill will</b></a>, remaining unconfused and mindful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that even if a teacher claims that killing does not create evil, a disciple is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>praiseworthy</b></a> for not harming any living being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even if a teacher claims there is no merit in giving, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>virtuous person</b></a> is still considered wise by their peers for their restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's explanation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma-concentration</b></a> applies to the practice of all four Divine Abodes (Loving-kindness, Compassion, Altruistic Joy, and Equanimity)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching emphasizes that a disciple should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>restrained in body, speech, and mind</b></a> to achieve peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind filled with equanimity should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>abundant and measureless</b></a>, extended to all beings in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha successfully removed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> of Pāṭaliya the headman through the systematic teaching on virtue and concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.13/en/sujato">SN 42.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that those who have gone forth should avoid the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>two extremes</b></a> of sensual indulgence and self-mortification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>Middle Way</b></a> as a path that avoids extremes and leads specifically to vision, knowledge, peace, and Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the Middle Way as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>, which consists of right view, right intention, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the Noble Truth of Suffering by stating that, in brief, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates subject to clinging</b></a> are suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>Noble Truth of Suffering</b></a> includes not just birth, aging, and death, but also association with the unpleasant and separation from the loved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the Noble Truth of the Origin of Suffering as craving that leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>renewed existence</b></a>, accompanied by delight and lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized craving into three types: craving for sensual pleasures, craving for existence, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>craving for extermination</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the Noble Truth of the Cessation of Suffering as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>remainderless fading away</b></a> and cessation of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Noble Truth of Suffering is a reality that must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>fully understood</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the Noble Truth of the Origin of Suffering—which is craving—is something that must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>abandoned</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the Noble Truth of the Cessation of Suffering is a realization that must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>personally experienced</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Noble Truth of the Way Leading to the Cessation of Suffering is a path that must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>developed</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha only claimed to have attained supreme enlightenment once his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision</b></a> of the Four Noble Truths in their twelve aspects was thoroughly purified? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after attaining enlightenment, the Buddha declared that his liberation was unshakeable and that there would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>no more rebirth</b></a> for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was giving this first sermon, the venerable Kondañña attained the "stainless Dhamma Eye," realizing that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>whatever is subject to origination</b></a> is also subject to cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deities of the earth and various heavens cried out in celebration that the Buddha had set in motion the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>unexcelled Wheel of Dhamma</b></a> at the Deer Park in Benares? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the text, the sound of the deities celebrating the Buddha's first sermon reached as far as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>Brahma world</b></a> in a single moment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha set the Wheel of Dhamma in motion, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>ten-thousandfold world system</b></a> shook, trembled, and quaked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>measureless glorious light</b></a> appeared in the world during the Buddha's first sermon, surpassing even the divine majesty of the gods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave the monk Kondañña the name <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>Aññāsikoṇḍañño</b></a> because he was the first to truly understand the teaching of the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the pursuit of sensual pleasures as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>low and vulgar</b></a>, belonging to ordinary people rather than the noble ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the practice of self-mortification as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato"><b>painful and unworthy</b></a>, leading to no spiritual benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.11/en/sujato">SN 56.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.3/en/sujato"><b>conduit to existence</b></a> as the desire, lust, delight, and craving for form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.3/en/sujato">SN 23.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.3/en/sujato"><b>cessation of the conduit to existence</b></a> is achieved specifically through the cessation of all desire, lust, delight, and craving for the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.3/en/sujato">SN 23.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that mental standpoints, adherences, and underlying tendencies towards the five aggregates are fundamental components of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.3/en/sujato"><b>conduit to existence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.3/en/sujato">SN 23.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his disciple Rādha to abandon desire and lust for form so that it might be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.9/en/sujato"><b>cut off at the root</b></a>, made like a palm stump, and rendered incapable of future arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.9/en/sujato">SN 23.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when desire, lust, delight, and craving for feeling are abandoned, that feeling becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.9/en/sujato"><b>obliterated</b></a> and no longer subject to future arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.9/en/sujato">SN 23.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by abandoning desire and lust for consciousness, one ensures that such consciousness is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.9/en/sujato"><b>anabhāvaṅkataṃ</b></a> (obliterated), meaning it is eradicated and will not be produced again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.9/en/sujato">SN 23.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by the monk Rādha to define who or what "Māra" is, the Buddha answered that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.11/en/sujato"><b>form is Māra</b></a>, as are feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.11/en/sujato">SN 23.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a learned noble disciple who sees the five aggregates as Māra becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.11/en/sujato"><b>disenchanted</b></a> with form, feeling, perception, volitional formations, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.11/en/sujato">SN 23.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that through disenchantment with the aggregates, lust fades away, and with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.11/en/sujato"><b>fading of lust</b></a>, the mind is liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.11/en/sujato">SN 23.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a liberated person possesses the knowledge that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.11/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a>, the holy life has been lived, and there is no more coming back to any state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.11/en/sujato">SN 23.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.11/en/sujato"><b>consciousness is Māra</b></a>, illustrating that even our core awareness of experience is part of the cycle of death and entrapment if clung to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.11/en/sujato">SN 23.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that abandoning desire and lust for volitional formations results in those formations being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.9/en/sujato"><b>made like a palm stump</b></a>, which serves as a metaphor for something that can never grow or arise again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn23.9/en/sujato">SN 23.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the monk Bāhiya that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato"><b>eye is impermanent</b></a>, and because it is subject to change, it should not be regarded as "mine," "I," or "myself"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato">SN 35.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, whatever is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato"><b>impermanent is suffering</b></a>, and that which is suffering and subject to change is not fit to be considered one’s own soul or self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato">SN 35.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato"><b>learned noble disciple</b></a> experiences revulsion toward the eye, forms, eye-consciousness, and eye-contact when they see their true, impermanent nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato">SN 35.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that any feeling—whether pleasant, painful, or neutral—that arises with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato"><b>mind-contact as a condition</b></a> is actually impermanent and should be viewed with dispassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato">SN 35.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a disciple experiences <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato"><b>revulsion toward the senses</b></a>, they become dispassionate, and through that dispassion, their mind is liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato">SN 35.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person is liberated, the knowledge arises that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato"><b>birth is destroyed</b></a>, the holy life has been lived, and there is no more returning to any state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato">SN 35.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bāhiya specifically requested the Buddha to teach him the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma in brief</b></a> so that he could go and dwell alone, withdrawn, and diligent in his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato">SN 35.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that eye-consciousness and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact</b></a> are just as impermanent as the eye itself, and realizing this is a key step toward spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato">SN 35.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for anything to be considered "mine" or "my self," it must not be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato"><b>subject to change</b></a>, yet all elements of sensory experience are constantly changing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.89/en/sujato">SN 35.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the four establishments of mindfulness as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato"><b>one-way path</b></a> for the purification of beings and the realization of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato">SN 47.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that practicing the four establishments of mindfulness is the method for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato"><b>overcoming of sorrow</b></a> and lamentation, as well as the disappearance of pain and displeasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato">SN 47.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers to dwell contemplating the body in the body while remaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato"><b>ardent</b></a>, clearly comprehending, and mindful to remove covetousness and displeasure regarding the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato">SN 47.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato"><b>attainment of the true way</b></a> and the realization of Nibbāna are achieved specifically through the development of the four establishments of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato">SN 47.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the second establishment of mindfulness as dwelling and contemplating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato"><b>feelings in feelings</b></a> while being clearly comprehending and mindful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato">SN 47.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the practice of contemplating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato"><b>mind in the mind</b></a> as an essential component of the "one-way path" to purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato">SN 47.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should dwell contemplating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato"><b>phenomena in phenomena</b></a>, putting away the covetousness and displeasure that arise from the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato">SN 47.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered his discourse on the one-way path to purification while he was staying among the people of Vesālī in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato"><b>Ambapālī’s Grove</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.1/en/sujato">SN 47.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's specific definition of how a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.2/en/sujato"><b>mindful</b></a> is to live contemplating the body, feelings, mind, and phenomena with ardor and clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.2/en/sujato">SN 47.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk acts with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.2/en/sujato"><b>clear comprehension</b></a> when he maintains full awareness while going forward and returning, as well as while looking ahead and looking aside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.2/en/sujato">SN 47.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that clear comprehension should be applied even to physical movements such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.2/en/sujato"><b>flexing and extending</b></a> one's limbs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.2/en/sujato">SN 47.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's instructions on clear comprehension include being fully aware while performing basic daily tasks like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.2/en/sujato"><b>wearing robes</b></a> and carrying an alms bowl? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.2/en/sujato">SN 47.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that clear comprehension is necessary even during biological functions such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.2/en/sujato"><b>eating, drinking</b></a>, tasting, and even when urinating or defecating? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.2/en/sujato">SN 47.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly instructed that a monk must maintain clear comprehension whether he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.2/en/sujato"><b>walking, standing, sitting</b></a>, falling asleep, waking up, speaking, or keeping silent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.2/en/sujato">SN 47.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the speculative view of the world being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>steady as a pillar</b></a>—where nothing truly changes—arises only when there is clinging to the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned his monks on what must exist for the irrational view to arise that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>winds do not blow</b></a> and rivers do not flow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>clinging to form</b></a> (the physical body or material world) as a primary reason why a person might adopt the view that the sun and moon do not actually rise or set? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>clinging to feeling</b></a>, perception, or volitional formations, they can fall into the wrong view that pregnant women do not actually give birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the view of a static world arises because of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>clinging to consciousness</b></a>, which leads one to misinterpret the fundamental nature of reality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha lead his monks to understand that because form is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a> and subject to change, it is logically impossible for such a changing thing to produce a view that the world is eternally "steady as a pillar" without clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that whatever is impermanent is inherently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a>, and that clinging to this suffering is what fuels the arising of various wrong views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the five aggregates as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>subject to change</b></a>, emphasizing that if one did not cling to these changing factors, speculative views about the world's stillness would never occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>seen, heard, sensed, and cognized</b></a> by the mind is impermanent and should not be a basis for clinging to views about the world's fixedness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>stream-enterer</b></a> as a noble disciple who has successfully abandoned all doubt regarding the five aggregates and their relationship to wrong views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a disciple has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>abandoned doubt</b></a> regarding the reality of suffering and its origin, they are no longer capable of falling into the view that the world is an unchanging pillar? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>no longer subject to downfall</b></a> to describe the security of a noble disciple who has gained clear insight into the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha assured that a noble disciple who has overcome doubt about the path to the cessation of suffering is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>destined for enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that views such as "rivers do not flow" are discarded by a noble disciple when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>perplexed doubt</b></a> is removed through right understanding of the aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the resolution of doubt regarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> is the hallmark of a noble disciple who has reached the stage of stream-entry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn24.1/en/sujato">SN 24.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>, subject to change, and always becoming otherwise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato">SN 25.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato"><b>faith-follower</b></a> (saddhānusārī) as a person who has sufficient faith and conviction in the truth that the six sense bases are impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato">SN 25.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma-follower</b></a> (dhammānusārī) as one for whom the teachings on the impermanence of the senses are accepted after being pondered with a degree of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato">SN 25.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that both faith-followers and Dhamma-followers have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato"><b>transcended the plane of worldlings</b></a> (puthujjanabhūmi) and have entered the plane of superior persons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato">SN 25.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who has entered the "fixed course of rightness" is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato"><b>incapable of performing any deed</b></a> that would lead to rebirth in hell, the animal realm, or the sphere of afflicted spirits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato">SN 25.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a faith-follower or Dhamma-follower is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato"><b>incapable of passing away</b></a> until they have realized the fruit of stream-entry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato">SN 25.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato"><b>stream-enterer</b></a> (sotāpanno) as a person who truly knows and sees the reality of the six sense bases being impermanent and changing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato">SN 25.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a stream-enterer is a noble disciple who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato"><b>no longer subject to downfall</b></a>, is fixed in destiny, and is certain to attain full enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn25.1/en/sujato">SN 25.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the arising, persistence, and production of the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind is effectively the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn26.1/en/sujato"><b>arising of suffering</b></a> and the manifestation of aging-and-death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn26.1/en/sujato">SN 26.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn26.1/en/sujato"><b>cessation and subsiding</b></a> of the six internal sense bases (such as the eye and the mind) is equivalent to the cessation of suffering and the subsiding of disease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn26.1/en/sujato">SN 26.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn27.1/en/sujato"><b>desire and lust</b></a> (chandarāga) for the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, or mind as a "corruption of the mind" (upakkileso)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn27.1/en/sujato">SN 27.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn27.1/en/sujato"><b>corruption of the mind</b></a> regarding the six sense bases is abandoned, a monk’s mind becomes inclined toward renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn27.1/en/sujato">SN 27.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn27.1/en/sujato"><b>inclined toward renunciation</b></a> (nekkhammaninna) becomes workable and wieldy for the realization of truths through direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn27.1/en/sujato">SN 27.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn27.1/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of mental corruption</b></a> in the six sense bases is necessary for a monk to reach the state where their mind is fortified by renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn27.1/en/sujato">SN 27.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> was once residing at the Bamboo Grove in Rājagaha when he was approached by a female wanderer who questioned his manner of eating alms food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a female wanderer named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>Sucimukhī</b></a> asked Venerable Sāriputta if he was "eating downward-facing," he clarified that he did not eat in such a manner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta defined "eating downward-facing" as the practice of recluses and brahmins who earn their living through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>the art of sites</b></a> (geomancy), which is considered a wrong livelihood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāriputta, recluses who earn their living through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>the art of stars</b></a> (astrology) are described as those who are "eating upward-facing"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta explained that "eating sideways-facing" refers to recluses and brahmins who support themselves by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>running errands</b></a> and messages for others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that "eating intermediate-facing" refers to those who earn their livelihood through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>the art of limbs</b></a>, a form of fortune-telling or palmistry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta emphasized that he does not engage in any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>debased arts</b></a> (tiracchānavijjā) to earn his living, but instead seeks his food in a righteous manner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that he seeks his alms food <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>righteously</b></a> (dhammena) and eats only what he has obtained through proper means? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing Venerable Sāriputta’s explanation of right livelihood, the wanderer Sucimukhī proclaimed throughout Rājagaha that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>Sakyaputtan ascetics</b></a> eat righteous and blameless food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta rejected four metaphorical directions of "eating" because they all represented various forms of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>wrong livelihood</b></a> practiced by other spiritual seekers of his time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wanderer Sucimukhī went from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>street to street</b></a> and square to square in Rājagaha to encourage people to give alms to the disciples of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta's definition of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>blameless food</b></a> centers on the moral purity of how the food was obtained rather than the physical direction one faces while eating? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>tiracchānavijjā</b></a>, translated as "debased arts," was used by Venerable Sāriputta to categorize various forms of divination and worldly services used for gain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sutta records Venerable Sāriputta eating his alms food while leaning against <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>a certain wall</b></a> in Rājagaha after having walked for alms in a house-to-house manner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta insisted that his practice of seeking alms is always <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato"><b>in accordance with the Dhamma</b></a>, free from the corruptions of fortune-telling or performing errands? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn28.10/en/sujato">SN 28.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified four types of meditators based on whether they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.1/en/sujato"><b>skilled in concentration</b></a> and whether they are skilled in attainment in concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.1/en/sujato">SN 34.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that among various practitioners, the meditator who is both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.1/en/sujato"><b>skilled in concentration</b></a> and skilled in attainment in concentration is considered the foremost and best? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.1/en/sujato">SN 34.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an analogy of milk products—progressing from milk to curd, butter, and ghee—to explain that a meditator skilled in both concentration and attainment is like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.1/en/sujato"><b>cream of ghee</b></a>, the finest of them all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.1/en/sujato">SN 34.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, some meditators may be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.1/en/sujato"><b>skilled in attainment</b></a> in concentration but lack the necessary skill in concentration itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.1/en/sujato">SN 34.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted there are meditators who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.1/en/sujato"><b>skilled in concentration</b></a> but are not skilled in the specific attainment in concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.1/en/sujato">SN 34.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a specific category of meditator who is neither skilled in concentration nor <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.1/en/sujato"><b>skilled in attainment</b></a> in concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.1/en/sujato">SN 34.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between the ability to enter a meditative state and being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.11/en/sujato"><b>skilled in steadiness</b></a> in concentration, which refers to the ability to maintain that state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.11/en/sujato">SN 34.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a meditator who is both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.11/en/sujato"><b>skilled in attainment</b></a> and skilled in steadiness in concentration to be the most excellent and supreme among those who practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.11/en/sujato">SN 34.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a meditator might be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.11/en/sujato"><b>skilled in steadiness</b></a> in concentration without being particularly skilled in the actual attainment or entering of that concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.11/en/sujato">SN 34.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used five distinct superlatives—foremost, best, chief, supreme, and most excellent—to describe the practitioner who masters both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.11/en/sujato"><b>attainment and steadiness</b></a> in concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn34.11/en/sujato">SN 34.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, reflecting on his time as a Bodhisatta before his enlightenment, investigated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato"><b>gratification</b></a>, the danger, and the escape regarding the six external sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato">SN 35.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato"><b>gratification</b></a> of forms, sounds, odors, tastes, tactile objects, and mental phenomena as the pleasure and joy that arise in dependence on them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato">SN 35.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> in external sense objects lies in the fact that they are impermanent, suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato">SN 35.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> from external sense objects as the removal and abandonment of desire and lust for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato">SN 35.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he did not claim to have awakened to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato"><b>supreme enlightenment</b></a> until he had directly known the gratification, danger, and escape regarding the six external sense bases as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato">SN 35.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his realization of enlightenment as the arising of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision</b></a>, which confirmed for him that his deliverance of mind was unshakable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato">SN 35.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared upon his enlightenment that this was his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato"><b>final birth</b></a> and that there would be no further renewal of being for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato">SN 35.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically looked for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato"><b>nissaraṇa</b></a> (escape) from the world of senses, which he discovered to be the disciplining of desire and lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato">SN 35.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> (dhammā), as the sixth external sense base, are impermanent and subject to change, posing a danger to those who cling to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato">SN 35.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato"><b>unshakable deliverance</b></a> is only possible once one has fully understood the true nature of sensory gratification and its inherent limitations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.14/en/sujato">SN 35.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha sought out the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato"><b>gratification of the eye</b></a> and, having discovered what it was, saw it clearly with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato">SN 35.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha investigated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato"><b>danger of the eye</b></a> as part of his pre-enlightenment quest and saw it clearly through his own wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato">SN 35.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha searched for and discovered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato"><b>escape from the eye</b></a> before he attained full awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato">SN 35.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha undertook a quest to understand the gratification, danger, and escape regarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato"><b>tongue</b></a> as one of the six internal sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato">SN 35.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his realization as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato"><b>unshakable deliverance</b></a> (akuppā me vimutti) after fully understanding the nature of the six internal sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato">SN 35.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that until he understood the six internal sense bases "as they really are," he did not declare himself to have attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato"><b>supreme enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato">SN 35.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha realized this was his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato"><b>final birth</b></a> only after he saw the gratification, danger, and escape of the internal sense base of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato">SN 35.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha applied his search for gratification, danger, and escape to all the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato"><b>internal sense bases</b></a>, which include the ear, nose, and body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato">SN 35.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision</b></a> (ñāṇadassana) arose within him once he accurately understood the three aspects of internal sensory experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato">SN 35.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato"><b>no renewal of being</b></a> (natthi dāni punabbhavo) once the escape from the internal sense bases is fully realized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.15/en/sujato">SN 35.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha investigated the gratification, danger, and escape of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato"><b>external forms</b></a> (rūpa) during his pursuit of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato">SN 35.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha sought to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato"><b>gratification of sounds</b></a> and saw it clearly with wisdom as part of his spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato">SN 35.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha scrutinized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato"><b>danger of odors</b></a> as an external sense base to gain insight into the nature of reality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato">SN 35.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha discovered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato"><b>escape from tastes</b></a> by seeking out their true nature with his wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato">SN 35.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he sought the gratification, danger, and escape of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato"><b>tactile objects</b></a> (phoṭṭhabbā) before he attained his awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato">SN 35.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explored the nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> (dhammā) as external sense bases to find their inherent gratification and danger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato">SN 35.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato"><b>deliverance of mind</b></a> became unshakable only after he understood the six external sense bases in three ways (gratification, danger, escape)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato">SN 35.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that knowing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato"><b>six external sense bases</b></a> as they really are is essential for attaining the end of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato">SN 35.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> (paññā) to describe the means by which he saw the danger and escape regarding external sensory objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato">SN 35.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> (nissaraṇa) as a key element he sought concerning all external sense objects like forms and sounds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.16/en/sujato">SN 35.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato"><b>perturbation</b></a> as being equivalent to a disease, a tumor, and a dart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato">SN 35.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato"><b>Tathagata</b></a> (an enlightened one) dwells unperturbed and with the "dart" of craving removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato">SN 35.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised any monk who wishes to live unperturbed to not "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato"><b>conceive</b></a>" (create a sense of self or ownership) regarding the eye, nor conceive in the eye, nor conceive from the eye, nor conceive "the eye is mine"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato">SN 35.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should not conceive in any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato"><b>pleasant, painful, or neutral feeling</b></a> that arises from eye-contact if they wish to remain unperturbed and free from the "dart"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato">SN 35.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers that to attain a state of non-perturbation, one should not conceive in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato"><b>tongue</b></a>, its tastes, tongue-consciousness, or the feelings that arise from tongue-contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato">SN 35.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an unperturbed practitioner should not conceive in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a>, mental phenomena, or the specific consciousness and contact related to the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato">SN 35.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk should not "conceive" in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato"><b>the all</b></a> (sabba), meaning they should not create a sense of identity or ownership in the totality of sensory experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato">SN 35.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person does not "conceive" in the senses or their objects, they do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato"><b>cling</b></a> to anything in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato">SN 35.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who does not cling to anything in the world will not be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato"><b>agitated</b></a>, and being unagitated, they personally attain Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato">SN 35.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the final realization of a liberated monk as knowing that birth is destroyed and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> has been lived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato">SN 35.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.92/en/sujato"><b>dyad</b></a> (a pair) as the meeting of a sense faculty and its corresponding object, such as the eye and forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.92/en/sujato">SN 35.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.92/en/sujato"><b>mind and mental phenomena</b></a> together constitute one of the essential dyads that define human experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.92/en/sujato">SN 35.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that if anyone tried to propose a different <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.92/en/sujato"><b>dyad</b></a> than the pairs of senses and objects he taught, they would be unable to explain it and would only meet with vexation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.92/en/sujato">SN 35.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on the "dyad" includes the ears and sounds, the nose and odors, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.92/en/sujato"><b>body and tactile objects</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.92/en/sujato">SN 35.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to dwell "unperturbed" and with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato"><b>dart removed</b></a> by letting go of the mental tendency to "conceive" (maññana) in relation to all sense experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.90/en/sujato">SN 35.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> arises only in dependence on a dyad, such as the meeting of a sense organ and its corresponding object? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the eye and visual forms as a dyad that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>moving and tottering</b></a>, impermanent, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>eye-consciousness</b></a> is itself impermanent because it arises from causes and conditions that are also impermanent and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact</b></a> is defined as the meeting, conjunction, and coalescence of three factors: the eye, forms, and eye-consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person is "contacted" through the meeting of the senses and their objects, they then proceed to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>feel, intend, and perceive</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>tongue</b></a> and tastes form a dyad that is fundamentally impermanent and subject to "becoming otherwise"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>tongue-consciousness</b></a> cannot be permanent because the very conditions that allow it to arise are themselves unstable and changing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>tongue-contact</b></a> specifically as the conjunction and meeting of the tongue, tastes, and tongue-consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> and mental phenomena together constitute a dyad through which mind-consciousness is able to arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the mind and mental phenomena as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a> and subject to constant change and transformation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>mind-consciousness</b></a> is an impermanent phenomenon because it originates from a cause that is itself not permanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>mind-contact</b></a> is the result of the meeting and coming together of the mind, mental phenomena, and mind-consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a> is a physical faculty that is impermanent and subject to "becoming otherwise," or losing its current state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>cause or condition</b></a> required for the arising of eye-consciousness is also marked by impermanence and change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>tongue-contact</b></a> is an impermanent experience because the meeting of the tongue, tastes, and consciousness is itself a fleeting event? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> as a "moving and tottering" faculty that is inherently unstable and subject to variation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> (dhammā) are not fixed or permanent, but are instead changing and subject to "becoming otherwise"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>perceiving</b></a> (sañjānāti) is triggered once an individual is "contacted" through the conjunction of a sense faculty and its object? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>intending</b></a> or volitionalizing (ceteti) is a mental process that occurs as a consequence of being "contacted" via the sensory systems? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> (vedeti) is a conditional experience that arises only after the "meeting, conjunction, and coalescence" of a sense base, its object, and consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.93/en/sujato">SN 35.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the six bases of contact, when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato"><b>untamed and unguarded</b></a>, are vehicles for suffering, whereas when they are well-tamed and well-guarded they bring happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato">SN 35.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared practitioners who know how to restrain their senses to being "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato"><b>unleakable</b></a>" (anavassuta), living with faith as their companion and preventing the "leakage" of taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato">SN 35.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that upon seeing an agreeable form, one should dispel the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato"><b>path of lust</b></a>, and upon seeing a disagreeable form, one should not let the mind be corrupted by ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato">SN 35.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that when hearing sounds, whether pleasant or unpleasant, one should not be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato"><b>enthralled</b></a> by the pleasant nor allow the mind to be corrupted by aversion toward the unpleasant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato">SN 35.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when experiencing a foul or disagreeable odor, a practitioner should dispel <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato"><b>resistance</b></a> (paṭigha) and avoid being led away by desire for fragrant smells? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato">SN 35.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that when tasting something delicious, one should not consume it with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato"><b>greed</b></a> or attachment, nor should one show opposition or anger when tasting something unpleasant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato">SN 35.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that when contacted by pleasure, one should not be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato"><b>intoxicated</b></a> by it, and when contacted by pain, one should not tremble or be shaken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato">SN 35.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> toward the "dyad" of pleasure and pain, remaining neither attracted to one nor repelled by the other? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato">SN 35.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that most people are obsessed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato"><b>diversified perceptions</b></a> (papañcasaññā) and suggested that a monk should instead dispel all mind-made, household-related thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato">SN 35.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once the mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato"><b>well-developed</b></a> regarding the six senses, it does not waver anywhere regardless of what external stimuli it contacts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato">SN 35.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by overcoming lust and hatred through sense restraint, monks reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato"><b>far shore</b></a> of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.94/en/sujato">SN 35.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mālukyaputta, despite being an elderly monk, requested the Buddha to provide a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>brief teaching</b></a> so that he could practice in seclusion and reach enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha initially questioned Mālukyaputta's request for a short exhortation, jokingly asking what he should say to the younger monks if an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>old and aged</b></a> monk like him was still asking for brief instructions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Mālukyaputta if he could have desire, lust, or affection for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>visual forms</b></a> that he had never seen, never seen before, and did not currently see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of unheard sounds and unexperienced tastes to illustrate that one cannot have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> for sensory objects that have never been encountered or cognized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that in the act of seeing, there should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>merely the seen</b></a>, meaning one should not add mental projections or a sense of "self" to the experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that in the act of hearing, there should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>merely the heard</b></a> to prevent the mind from becoming entangled in desire or aversion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when the sensed is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>merely the sensed</b></a> (including smells, tastes, and touches), the practitioner is no longer "by that" or "therein"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a person is not "therein" (not identifying with the sensory experience), they are neither here nor beyond nor in between, which constitutes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mālukyaputta explained in verse that seeing a form with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>forgotten mindfulness</b></a> leads a person to focus on "agreeable signs," which triggers a lustful mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mālukyaputta taught that for a person with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>lustful mind</b></a> who clings to what they feel, many painful feelings born of forms will continue to grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Mālukyaputta, a mind troubled by covetousness and distress is described as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>far from Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mālukyaputta warned that hearing a sound with a "muddled" state of mindfulness and dwelling on its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>agreeable sign</b></a> causes one to experience and remain attached to that sound? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mālukyaputta highlighted that becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>infatuated</b></a> with odors, tastes, or tactile contacts leads to an accumulation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha approved of Mālukyaputta's detailed verses, which explained that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>unlustful mind</b></a> allows one to experience sense objects without clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mālukyaputta observed that when a person is mindful and does not lust after forms, their suffering is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>exhausted and not heaped up</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mālukyaputta described a person who acts with mindfulness and does not cling to what they feel as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>near Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mālukyaputta taught that when one knows a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomenon</b></a> (dhamma) but remains mindful and detached, they do not experience the accumulation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha reiterated Mālukyaputta’s verses to emphasize that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> is lived by reducing suffering through the exhaustion of attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on the "seen merely the seen" is meant to eliminate the mental tendency to create a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>self</b></a> in relation to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mālukyaputta became one of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>arahants</b></a> (fully enlightened ones) after following the Buddha's brief exhortation on sense restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s instruction to Mālukyaputta covers all six senses, including the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>mind-consciousness</b></a> and mental phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that in the cognized there should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato"><b>merely the cognized</b></a> (viññāte viññātamattaṃ) to ensure the practitioner does not become "entrenched" in thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.95/en/sujato">SN 35.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato"><b>state of decline</b></a> as occurring when a monk sees a form and, upon the arising of evil unwholesome memories and intentions, tolerates and fails to abandon them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato">SN 35.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should recognize he is declining from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato"><b>wholesome states</b></a> if he does not dispel or eliminate evil intentions conducive to fetters that arise through his sensory experiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato">SN 35.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato"><b>state of non-decline</b></a> as a condition where a monk, after seeing a form, does not tolerate but instead abandons and brings to an end any evil unwholesome memories and intentions that arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato">SN 35.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk can understand he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato"><b>not declining</b></a> from wholesome states if he actively dispels and eliminates any evil unwholesome thoughts triggered by the mind or other senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato">SN 35.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato"><b>six bases of mastery</b></a> are achieved when evil unwholesome memories and intentions do not arise at all when a monk experiences sensory objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato">SN 35.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk should realize a sense base has been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato"><b>mastered</b></a> if, upon seeing a form with the eye, no evil unwholesome memories or intentions conducive to fetters arise in response? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato">SN 35.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that even the sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato"><b>taste</b></a> can lead to a state of decline if a monk experiences a taste and chooses to tolerate rather than dispel the unwholesome thoughts that follow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato">SN 35.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato"><b>memories and intentions</b></a> that are "conducive to fetters" as the specific type of unwholesome mental activity that one must abandon to avoid a state of spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato">SN 35.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that mastery over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> is demonstrated when a monk cognizes mental phenomena and no evil unwholesome states or intentions arise during that cognition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato">SN 35.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to recognize they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato"><b>mastered</b></a> their sensory experience when the "bases of mastery" are active and no unwholesome thoughts are triggered by external stimuli like sounds or smells? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato">SN 35.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato"><b>state of decline</b></a> to warn practitioners that failing to eliminate evil unwholesome thoughts as soon as they arise is a clear sign of regressing from the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato">SN 35.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato"><b>six bases of mastery</b></a>, the crucial factor is that evil unwholesome states do not even begin to arise during the contact between a sense organ and its object? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.96/en/sujato">SN 35.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that "dwelling negligently" (pamādavihārī) begins when a person lives with the eye faculty <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained</b></a>, allowing the mind to be "soiled" or defiled by visual forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato">SN 35.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato"><b>mind is soiled</b></a> by sensory objects, gladness does not arise, which prevents the development of rapture and tranquility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato">SN 35.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a lack of tranquility leads to suffering, and the mind of one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato"><b>suffers</b></a> cannot attain the concentration necessary for spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato">SN 35.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for one who dwells negligently, spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato"><b>phenomena</b></a> (dhammā) do not become manifest because their mind is not concentrated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato">SN 35.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato"><b>dwelling vigilantly</b></a>" (appamādavihārī) as a state where a practitioner lives with the sense faculties restrained, ensuring the mind is not soiled by sensory experiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato">SN 35.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a practitioner's mind is not soiled by sensory objects, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato"><b>gladness</b></a> (pāmojja) is born, which then leads to the arising of rapture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato">SN 35.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one who is filled with rapture, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato"><b>body becomes tranquil</b></a>, and one who is tranquil experiences a happiness that supports mental concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato">SN 35.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when the mind is concentrated, spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato"><b>phenomena become manifest</b></a>, and the practitioner is then reckoned as one who "dwells vigilantly"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato">SN 35.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that restraining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato"><b>tongue faculty</b></a> from being soiled by tastes is a critical component of dwelling vigilantly and avoiding spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato">SN 35.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato"><b>mind faculty</b></a> (manindriya) must be restrained from being soiled by mental phenomena to ensure that concentration and the manifestation of truth can occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.97/en/sujato">SN 35.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person is reckoned as "violent" when they have unabandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a>, which causes them to manifest anger when others irritate them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato">SN 42.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the reason a person is considered "violent" is due to unabandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a>, leading them to show anger whenever they are provoked by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato">SN 42.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> is not yet abandoned, they are easily irritated by others and consequently manifest the anger that defines them as "violent"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato">SN 42.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the abandonment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> as a primary reason why a person is reckoned as "gentle," as they no longer manifest anger even when others might attempt to irritate them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato">SN 42.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person is known as "gentle" because they have abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a>, and because of this, others do not irritate them into manifesting anger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato">SN 42.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person is considered "gentle" when they have abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a>, which prevents them from manifesting anger even when being provoked by those around them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato">SN 42.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a headman named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato"><b>Caṇḍa</b></a> approached the Buddha to specifically ask about the causes and conditions that lead one person to be called "violent" and another to be called "gentle"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato">SN 42.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the internal state of one's mind—specifically whether greed, hatred, and delusion are abandoned—is what determines whether one will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato"><b>manifest anger</b></a> when dealing with other people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato">SN 42.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the headman Caṇḍa was so impressed by the Buddha’s explanation of violence and gentleness that he compared the Dhamma to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato"><b>lamp</b></a> held up in the darkness so that those with eyesight might see forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato">SN 42.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's teaching on the roots of personal conduct, the headman Caṇḍa immediately sought refuge in the Triple Gem and asked to be recognized as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a> for life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.1/en/sujato">SN 42.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the stage manager <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>Tālapuṭa</b></a> asked the Buddha if actors are reborn in a "Heaven of Laughter," the Buddha initially refused to answer him three times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how actors who present things that provoke <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> in an audience already bound by desire are actually creating the conditions for their own suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an actor who incites <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> in audiences already bound by aversion is acting in a way that leads to a miserable rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that beings are already bound by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a>, and actors who further increase that delusion through their performances face a painful destination after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an actor who is himself intoxicated and negligent, and who causes others to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>intoxicated and negligent</b></a>, is reborn in the "Hell of Laughter"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that the belief that actors go to a special "Heaven of Laughter" is actually a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that for a person holding a wrong view, there are only two possible destinations: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>hell or the animal realm</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the stage manager Tālapuṭa heard the Buddha's teaching on the fate of actors, he began to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>weep</b></a> because he realized he had been deceived by his former teachers for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Tālapuṭa lamented to the Buddha that he had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>cheated</b></a> and lured by old traditions claiming that making people laugh on stage led to a heavenly rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's explanation, Tālapuṭa immediately sought <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>refuge</b></a> in the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the stage manager Tālapuṭa was so moved by the Buddha's teaching that he requested <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>ordination</b></a> and the higher ordination as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the former actor Tālapuṭa eventually attained the highest goal of the holy life, becoming an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>arahant</b></a> shortly after his ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered this teaching regarding the dangers of entertainment while staying at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>Bamboo Grove</b></a> in Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Tālapuṭa described how actors use a mixture of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>truth and falsehood</b></a> on stage to amuse and entertain their audiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that actors reinforce the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>bond of lust</b></a> in people who are not yet free from desire by presenting alluring things on stage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted how actors intensify the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>bond of hatred</b></a> in those who are not yet free from aversion by presenting provocative things in their performances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, actors increase the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>bond of delusion</b></a> in those who are already confused by presenting delusive things on the stage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Tālapuṭa compared the Buddha’s teaching to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>setting upright</b></a> what had been overturned or revealing what was hidden? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Tālapuṭa realized his previous teachers had misled him into thinking that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>sacrificing truth</b></a> for the sake of amusement would result in a divine life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s refusal to answer Tālapuṭa’s initial questions was an attempt to spare him the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato"><b>distress</b></a> of learning the grim reality of his profession's karmic consequences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.2/en/sujato">SN 42.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the warrior headman <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato"><b>Yodhājīva</b></a> approached the Buddha to ask if it was true, as his traditional teachers claimed, that a soldier who dies striving in battle is reborn in a heavenly realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato">SN 42.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato"><b>initially refused</b></a> to answer Yodhājīva’s question about the afterlife of soldiers three times, advising the headman to set the matter aside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato">SN 42.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a warrior's mind during battle is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato"><b>ill-directed</b></a> because it is seized by the cruel thought that other beings should be slaughtered, bound, or annihilated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato">SN 42.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a soldier who dies while striving in battle with a mind set on the destruction of others is actually reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato"><b>Battle-Slain Hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato">SN 42.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the belief that a warrior dying in combat is reborn among the "devas of the realm of those fallen in battle" is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato">SN 42.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that for a person holding a wrong view, such as the idea that killing in battle leads to heaven, there are only two possible destinations: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato"><b>hell or the animal realm</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato">SN 42.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the headman Yodhājīva heard the Buddha's explanation of the karmic consequences of warfare, he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato"><b>broke into tears</b></a> and wept? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato">SN 42.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Yodhājīva told the Buddha he was weeping because he realized he had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato"><b>deceived and cheated</b></a> for a long time by his former teachers who promised a heavenly rebirth for dying in battle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato">SN 42.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's explanation revealed that the traditional teaching given to warriors—that their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato"><b>exertion in battle</b></a> would lead to divine status—was a dangerous falsehood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato">SN 42.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the truth about the fate of those who die in combat, Yodhājīva took refuge in the Triple Gem and asked the Buddha to accept him as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a> from that day forward? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato">SN 42.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha indicated that the mental state of wishing that beings be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato"><b>annihilated or destroyed</b></a> is a "low and misdirected" state of mind that leads to suffering rather than divinity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato">SN 42.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's initial reluctance to answer Yodhājīva was intended to avoid causing the headman <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato"><b>distress</b></a> by overturning his long-held religious beliefs about his profession? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.3/en/sujato">SN 42.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the headman <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>Asibandhakaputto</b></a> once approached the Buddha to ask if He, like certain Brahmins, could perform a ritual to ensure that the entire world would be reborn in a happy destination or a heavenly world after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the headman Asibandhakaputto, there were certain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>Brahmins of the west</b></a> who claimed they could "direct" a deceased person toward heaven through their specific prayers and invocations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>heavy rock</b></a> being thrown into a deep lake to illustrate that an immoral person's actions naturally lead them downward, regardless of the prayers of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that if a man has destroyed life, taken what is not given, and engaged in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>sexual misconduct</b></a>, no amount of collective prayer from a large crowd can cause him to be reborn in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>prayers and praise</b></a> of a large group are as ineffective for saving a person with wrong views from hell as they would be at making a submerged stone float to the surface of a pond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a>, harsh speech, and divisive speech as unwholesome verbal actions that contribute to a person's rebirth in a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, even if a massive crowd were to assemble and pray for a virtuous person to be reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a>, their prayers would be useless because the person's own good conduct ensures a fortunate rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a pot of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>ghee or oil</b></a> broken in a deep lake to show that while the physical body (like shards) sinks, the refined mind of a virtuous person naturally "rises" toward a higher destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person who abstains from killing and holds <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>, the inevitable destination after the breakup of the body is a happy, heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that in his broken-pot analogy, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>shards and gravel</b></a> of the vessel represent the coarser elements that sink, just as unwholesome habits weigh a person down at death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who has abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>covetousness</b></a> and ill will cannot be forced into a bad rebirth by the curses or negative wishes of a large crowd? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that no matter how many people walk around a person with their hands in prayer, they cannot make a virtuous man <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>sink</b></a> into a state of deprivation after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the headman Asibandhakaputto described certain Brahmins as "attendants on the fire" and "those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>descend into water</b></a>," referring to their specific ritualistic practices intended to save the deceased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching on rebirth suggests that one's personal destination is determined by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>breakup of the body</b></a> following a life of either wholesome or unwholesome conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s use of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>counter-questioning</b></a> led the headman Asibandhakaputto to realize for himself that external rituals cannot override the internal law of kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the futility of wishing for things against their nature by asking if a stone could be told to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>rise to the surface</b></a> of water simply through the power of verbal commands? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's analogies of the rock and the oil, the headman Asibandhakaputto declared the teaching was like "setting upright what was overturned" and asked to be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching confirms that the ultimate factor for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato"><b>happy destination</b></a> is one's own ethical integrity, rather than any posthumous ritual performed by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.6/en/sujato">SN 42.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the headman <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>Asibandhakaputta Gāmaṇi</b></a> once questioned the Buddha about whether He truly dwells as one who is compassionate toward all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by a headman, the Buddha confirmed that he indeed dwells as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>compassionate</b></a> toward all living beings without exception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the headman Asibandhakaputta asked the Buddha why, if He is compassionate to all, He teaches the Dhamma <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>thoroughly</b></a> to some people but not as thoroughly to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a farmer with three fields, explaining that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>superior field</b></a> is the first one where a wise farmer would choose to sow his seeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared bhikkhus and bhikkhunis to a superior field, noting that he teaches them a Dhamma that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>good in the beginning</b></a>, middle, and end, with the right meaning and phrasing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he prioritizes teaching monks and nuns because they live with Him as their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>islands</b></a>, their shelters, and their refuges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened lay followers (men and women) to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>middling field</b></a>, which a farmer sows only after he has finished sowing his best field? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared ascetics and Brahmins of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>other sects</b></a> to an inferior field of salty soil and poor ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches the Dhamma even to those of other sects in the hope that they might understand even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>one word</b></a> or phrase, which would lead to their long-term welfare and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared an inferior field to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>cattle fodder</b></a>, explaining that a farmer might still sow seeds there just to provide food for his animals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a second analogy involving three water jars, where the best jar is one that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>non-leaking</b></a> and does not exude or seep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared bhikkhus and bhikkhunis to water jars that do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>exude or seep</b></a>, making them the primary recipients of His teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened lay followers to water jars that do not leak but still "sweat" or exude water, representing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>order of priority</b></a> in His teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared those of other sects to a water jar that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>leaking and cracked</b></a>, making it the least efficient vessel for holding the water of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that even a cracked and leaking water jar might still be used for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>washing dishes</b></a>, just as He teaches other sects for whatever small benefit they may gain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defines the "holy life" (<i>brahmacariya</i>) that He proclaims as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>completely perfect</b></a> and pure in its entirety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato"><b>monastics</b></a> (bhikkhus and bhikkhunīs) are those who dwell with Him as their source of protection and final resort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.7/en/sujato">SN 42.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered the teaching known as the "Conch Blower" while he was staying at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>Pāvarika’s Mango Grove</b></a> in the city of Nāḷandā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the headman <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>Asibandhakaputta</b></a>, who was a disciple of the Jain leader Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta, approached the Buddha to discuss the fate of those who violate ethical precepts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned the Jain teaching that anyone who kills, steals, or lies is destined for a state of misery and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a> based on the frequency of their actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Jain leader Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta taught that a person is "led by what they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>habitually do</b></a>," meaning their most frequent actions determine their rebirth? But that the Buddha pointed out that even a person who kills actually spends a <b>greater amount of time</b> during the day and night <i>not</i> killing than they do actually killing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used logic to show that if rebirth were based strictly on what one "habitually" does, then a person who occasionally steals would not go to hell because they spend <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>more time</b></a> not stealing than stealing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a disciple who becomes convinced they are destined for hell because of a past mistake is held back by that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a>, which can actually lead them to a bad rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who does not abandon a fixed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>harmful view</b></a> about their own spiritual doom is as good as "dropped into hell" because of their own mental state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the "Tathāgata" as one who censures killing and stealing but encourages disciples to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>abandon</b></a> those actions and vow to abstain from them in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a disciple feels <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>remorse</b></a> for a past bad deed, they should realize that simply worrying about it cannot undo what was done? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the way to transcend past bad karma is to recognize the mistake and commit to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>future restraint</b></a>, thereby abandoning the unwholesome path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a noble disciple, after abandoning unwholesome deeds, is described by the Buddha as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>free from covetousness</b></a> and ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a disciple should dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>undeluded</b></a> and clearly comprehending while actively practicing the four "Divine Abidings"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> as pervading one's mind through the entire world—above, below, and across—without any hostility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the expansive reach of a mind filled with loving-kindness to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>strong conch blower</b></a> who easily makes himself heard in all four directions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that "measured karma"—or karma that is limited and restrictive—does not remain in a person who has developed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> by loving-kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the liberation of mind through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> is so vast that any past "measured karma" cannot persist within it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, developing the liberation of mind by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>altruistic joy</b></a> allows a practitioner to pervade the world with a boundless and immeasurable mental state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> as a powerful state of mind that, when fully developed, prevents limited past actions from taking root? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>divisive speech</b></a> as one of the unwholesome actions a noble disciple must abandon before developing a boundless heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a> and idle chatter as behaviors that must be discarded to achieve a state of mental purity and mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a noble disciple becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>right-viewed</b></a> by abandoning wrong views and letting go of ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>immeasurable</b></a> to describe the heart of a disciple who dwells in the Four Divine Abidings, contrasting it with the "limited" nature of bad deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a mind developed through the four "Brahmavihāras" becomes so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>vast and great</b></a> that it overwhelms the effects of minor past unwholesome karma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>measured karma</b></a> (<i>pamāṇakataṃ kammaṃ</i>) suggests that expansive spiritual practice can "wash away" or neutralize the weight of past errors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing this teaching, the headman Asibandhakaputta declared it was as if the Buddha had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>revealed what was hidden</b></a> or held up a lamp in the dark? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the headman Asibandhakaputta was so moved by the "Simile of the Conch Blower" that he immediately asked to be accepted as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s conversation with Asibandhakaputta shows that even those who were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato"><b>disciples of other teachers</b></a> could find clarity and refuge in the Dhamma through logical and compassionate discussion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.8/en/sujato">SN 42.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>Jetavana</b></a>, Anāthapiṇḍika’s Monastery at Sāvatthī, when he received the news of the passing of his chief disciple, Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta passed away while he was staying at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>Nālakagāmaka</b></a> in the Magadhan country, after falling seriously ill? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a novice named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>Cunda</b></a> served as the attendant to Venerable Sāriputta during his final illness and subsequent passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Sāriputta's death, the novice Cunda took the deceased monk's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>bowl and robes</b></a> to Sāvatthī to inform Venerable Ānanda of the loss? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda confessed to the Buddha that upon hearing of Sāriputta’s death, his body felt as if it were drugged and he felt <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>disoriented</b></a>, losing his sense of direction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Ānanda if Sāriputta had taken away the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of virtue</b></a> when he attained final nibbāna, reminding him that the Dhamma remains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned whether Sāriputta had taken the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of concentration</b></a> with him, illustrating that internal realizations are not lost to the world when a teacher passes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that Sāriputta did not take the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of wisdom</b></a> with him, encouraging Ānanda to see that the teachings are still accessible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha comforted Ānanda by noting that Sāriputta’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of liberation</b></a> was not removed from the community of practitioners upon his death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked if Sāriputta had taken the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a> with him, emphasizing that these qualities are results of the practice that others can still achieve? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda praised the late Sāriputta as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>exhorter</b></a> who was constantly informing, instructing, inciting, and gladdening his fellow monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda remembered Sāriputta as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>indefatigable</b></a> in teaching the Dhamma and a great benefactor to his companions in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is impossible for anything that is born, come to be, and conditioned not to perish, describing the inevitability of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>separation</b></a> from all that is dear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>great tree</b></a> to describe the monkhood, stating that Sāriputta’s passing was like the largest branch falling off a tree possessed of heartwood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted his followers to dwell as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>islands unto yourselves</b></a>, with yourselves as a refuge and no other refuge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a monk should dwell with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma as an island</b></a> and the Dhamma as a refuge, seeking no external source of security? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that being an island unto oneself is achieved by dwelling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>contemplating the body</b></a> in the body, remaining ardent, clearly comprehending, and mindful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one finds refuge in the Dhamma by dwelling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>contemplating feelings</b></a> in feelings, having removed greed and distress regarding the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>contemplating the mind</b></a> and phenomena as the method for a monk to live as his own refuge and island? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that those who live as islands unto themselves will be at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato"><b>very top</b></a> of those who are desirous of training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.13/en/sujato">SN 47.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a monk requested a short teaching from the Buddha so that he could live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato"><b>withdrawn</b></a>, diligent, ardent, and self-controlled in his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato">SN 47.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha initially remarked that some <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato"><b>empty men</b></a> merely ask him for a brief teaching but then continue to follow him around rather than putting the Dhamma into practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato">SN 47.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato"><b>well-purified virtue</b></a> and straight view as the essential starting point for the development of all wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato">SN 47.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once a practitioner has established purified virtue and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato"><b>straight view</b></a>, they should then proceed to develop the four establishments of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato">SN 47.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that the four establishments of mindfulness should be practiced in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato"><b>three ways</b></a>: internally, externally, and both internally and externally? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato">SN 47.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the practice of contemplating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato"><b>body in the body</b></a> while remaining ardent, clearly comprehending, and mindful as a core part of mental development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato">SN 47.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that while contemplating the body, feelings, mind, or phenomena, one must work on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato"><b>removing greed</b></a> and distress with regard to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato">SN 47.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that contemplating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato"><b>feelings in feelings</b></a> internally and externally is one of the four ways to establish mindfulness based on a foundation of virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato">SN 47.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers to contemplate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato"><b>mind in the mind</b></a>, remaining mindful and clearly comprehending, after they have purified their virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato">SN 47.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the contemplation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato"><b>phenomena in phenomena</b></a> as the final of the four establishments of mindfulness used to ensure growth in wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato">SN 47.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that for anyone who builds upon a foundation of virtue with the four establishments of mindfulness, only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato"><b>growth</b></a> in wholesome states is to be expected, not decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.3/en/sujato">SN 47.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that monks who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato"><b>newly ordained</b></a> and recently arrived in the Dhamma and Vinaya should be encouraged and established in the four establishments of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato">SN 47.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed new monks to practice mindfulness of the body specifically for the purpose of attaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato"><b>knowledge as it really is</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato">SN 47.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that practitioners should remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato"><b>ardent and clearly comprehending</b></a> while contemplating the body, feelings, mind, and phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato">SN 47.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ideal mental state for practicing mindfulness as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato"><b>unified</b></a>, serene, and concentrated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato">SN 47.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato"><b>trainees</b></a> (sekhas) as those who have not yet reached the goal but live aspiring to the unsurpassed security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato">SN 47.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for those still in training, the practice of contemplating feelings, mind, and phenomena is done for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato"><b>full understanding</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato">SN 47.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato"><b>arahants</b></a> as those who have destroyed the taints, lived the holy life, and reached their own goal with the destruction of the fetters of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato">SN 47.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even arahants continue to practice the four establishments of mindfulness, though they do so while being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato"><b>detached</b></a> from the body and feelings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato">SN 47.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should dwell with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato"><b>one-pointed mind</b></a> when contemplating the mind and phenomena to gain true insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato">SN 47.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching in the Sāla Sutta illustrates that the four establishments of mindfulness are the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato"><b>same practice</b></a> used by beginners, trainees, and fully enlightened beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato">SN 47.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged that those who have recently entered the training should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato"><b>settled</b></a> and established in mindfulness to ensure their proper development in the Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato">SN 47.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an arahant dwells contemplating phenomena in phenomena while remaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato"><b>mindful</b></a> and clearly comprehending, despite having already completed the task to be done? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.4/en/sujato">SN 47.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a story about a hawk that pounced on and seized a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato"><b>quail</b></a> to illustrate the dangers of wandering into improper territory? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato">SN 47.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a captured quail lamented its misfortune, realizing it was caught because it had wandered into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato"><b>foreign territory</b></a>, which was the domain of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato">SN 47.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the quail's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato"><b>ancestral domain</b></a> as a field turned up by the plow, a place filled with clods of earth where it could remain safe from predators? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato">SN 47.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a hawk, overconfident in its own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato"><b>strength</b></a>, released a captured quail to let it return to its home ground, thinking it could simply catch it again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato">SN 47.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how the quail cleverly lured the hawk into an attack while it hid inside a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato"><b>clod of earth</b></a>, causing the hawk to strike the ground and die? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato">SN 47.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned his monks that Māra will find an opportunity and an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato"><b>object for attack</b></a> if they wander into an improper resort that is foreign territory? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato">SN 47.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato"><b>wrong resort</b></a> for a monk as the five cords of sensual pleasure, which lead the mind away from liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato">SN 47.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the five cords of sensual pleasure as forms, sounds, odors, tastes, and tactile objects that are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato"><b>agreeable</b></a>, pleasing, and provocative of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato">SN 47.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised monks to dwell in their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato"><b>ancestral resort</b></a> so that Māra, the Evil One, cannot find a footing or an opening to influence them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato">SN 47.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a> as the proper ancestral resort and domain for every monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato">SN 47.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk stays in his proper domain by dwelling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato"><b>contemplating the body</b></a> in the body, remaining ardent, clearly comprehending, and mindful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato">SN 47.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by dwelling in the four establishments of mindfulness, a monk effectively removes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato"><b>covetousness and distress</b></a> with regard to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.6/en/sujato">SN 47.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described certain regions of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato"><b>Himalayas</b></a>, the king of mountains, as being so rugged and uneven that they are inaccessible to both monkeys and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato">SN 47.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a story of hunters who set <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato"><b>birdlime</b></a> (a sticky trap) on monkey tracks to catch those that are foolish and greedy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato">SN 47.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato"><b>foolish monkey</b></a> who, upon seeing birdlime, grabs it with its hand and gets stuck, then gets both hands, both feet, and eventually its snout trapped while trying to free itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato">SN 47.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how a monkey trapped in five places by birdlime falls into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato"><b>misfortune and ruin</b></a>, becoming completely subject to the hunter's will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato">SN 47.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a monk wanders into "foreign territory" that is not his own resort, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato"><b>Māra</b></a> (the Evil One) will find an opening and an object for attack? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato">SN 47.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a>—consisting of agreeable and lust-provoking forms, sounds, odors, tastes, and tactile objects—as the "foreign territory" monks should avoid? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato">SN 47.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to dwell in their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato"><b>ancestral domain</b></a> so that Māra cannot find any footing or opportunity to influence them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato">SN 47.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a monk's proper ancestral domain as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato">SN 47.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk stays in his proper resort by dwelling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato"><b>contemplating the body</b></a> in the body, remaining ardent, clearly comprehending, and mindful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato">SN 47.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that while practicing the four establishments of mindfulness, one should strive to remove <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato"><b>covetousness and distress</b></a> with regard to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato">SN 47.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the image of a hunter spearing a monkey and carrying it away to illustrate what happens when a person is caught by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato"><b>sensual objects</b></a> in the wrong territory? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato">SN 47.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato"><b>wise monkeys</b></a> who are not greedy by nature will see the hunter's trap from a distance and keep far away to remain safe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.7/en/sujato">SN 47.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who fails to observe the progress of his own meditation to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>unskilled cook</b></a> who prepares various dishes for a king but never notices which flavors his master actually prefers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a variety of flavors a cook might serve—including <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>sour, bitter, and pungent</b></a>—to illustrate the different "signs" a servant must learn to recognize to be successful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an incompetent cook fails to receive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>wages and bonuses</b></a> because he is too foolish to discern the specific preferences and tastes of his master? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that even if a monk is ardent and mindful, his mind will not become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>concentrated</b></a> if he fails to "pick up the sign" of his own mental state during practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a foolish monk who does not observe the signs of his mind, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>defilements</b></a> are not abandoned, even while he is technically practicing the establishments of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a wise cook as one who receives <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>clothing</b></a> and other gifts because he observes whether his master reaches for the salty or the sweet dishes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a skilled monk becomes a "taker" of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>pleasant dwellings</b></a> in this very life by correctly observing which meditation subjects lead his mind to serenity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>picking up the sign</b></a> (uggaṇhāti) to describe the essential skill of a meditator who notices what actually helps their mind become unified? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>alkaline and non-alkaline</b></a> flavors as part of the diverse menu a cook uses to test and understand his master’s palate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a wise monk gains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a> because he specifically "picks up the sign" of his own mind while contemplating phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a cook is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>foolish</b></a> if he does not notice that his master praises a certain dish or takes a large portion of it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk contemplates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>feelings in feelings</b></a>, he must remain ardent and clearly comprehending while also observing how his mind responds to the practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>sweet and salty</b></a> tastes as part of the sensory feedback a skilled cook must evaluate to please a king or a royal minister? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>unskilled monk</b></a> misses the benefits of the holy life because he treats his meditation like a task without observing its internal effects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>sign of the mind</b></a> as the crucial indicator that tells a practitioner whether their meditation is moving toward concentration or not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a wise monk is like a cook who prepares <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>many kinds of soup</b></a> but always keeps his eye on his master's reaction to each one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a monk's mind does not become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>unified</b></a> while contemplating the mind, it is a sign that he is not correctly observing his mental state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>corruptions</b></a> are only abandoned when a practitioner is wise and competent enough to pick up the signs of their own mental development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>king or royal minister</b></a> as the "employer" to whom a cook must cater, comparing them to the monk's own mind in the context of meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato"><b>competent</b></a> is one who successfully removes greed and distress from the world by knowing exactly how to handle his own mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.8/en/sujato">SN 47.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the four noble truths—suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path—as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.20/en/sujato"><b>real</b></a>, not unreal, and not otherwise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.20/en/sujato">SN 56.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because the four noble truths are factual and unalterable, the Buddha taught that a spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.20/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> should be made by every person to understand that "this is suffering"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.20/en/sujato">SN 56.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he and his disciples had to wander and roam through a long course of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.21/en/sujato"><b>saṃsāra</b></a> precisely because they had not yet penetrated or discovered the four noble truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.21/en/sujato">SN 56.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once the four noble truths are fully understood and penetrated, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.21/en/sujato"><b>craving for existence</b></a> is cut off and the root of suffering is destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.21/en/sujato">SN 56.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that when the four noble truths are properly realized as they really are, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.21/en/sujato"><b>no more renewed existence</b></a> or future rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.21/en/sujato">SN 56.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the truth of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.20/en/sujato"><b>way leading to the cessation of suffering</b></a> is an immutable reality that is "not otherwise" and "not unreal"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.20/en/sujato">SN 56.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.21/en/sujato"><b>conduit to existence</b></a> (bhavanetti) to describe the force that is finally exhausted and destroyed when the four noble truths are clearly seen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.21/en/sujato">SN 56.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to make an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.20/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> specifically to realize the truth of the origin of suffering and the truth of its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.20/en/sujato">SN 56.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while addressing monks at Koṭigāma, the Buddha reflected that the failure to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.21/en/sujato"><b>penetrate</b></a> the four noble truths was the direct cause of the long journey through various births for both himself and his audience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.21/en/sujato">SN 56.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the truth of suffering as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.20/en/sujato"><b>not unreal</b></a> (avitatha), highlighting that the four noble truths are constant and unchanging laws of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.20/en/sujato">SN 56.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that any ascetics or brahmins who do not understand the four noble truths as they really are are not considered by him to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato"><b>true ascetics</b></a> or true brahmins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato">SN 56.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who lack a real understanding of suffering and its origin have not realized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato"><b>goal of the holy life</b></a> through their own direct knowledge in this present life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato">SN 56.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified those who truly understand the four noble truths as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato"><b>true brahmins</b></a> who dwell having realized the benefit of their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato">SN 56.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that people who do not know the path leading to the cessation of suffering are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato"><b>deficient in liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato">SN 56.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who are ignorant of where suffering ceases entirely are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato"><b>incapable of making an end</b></a> to suffering and remain subject to birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato">SN 56.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who possess a clear understanding of the four noble truths are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato"><b>endowed with liberation</b></a> of mind and are capable of ending the cycle of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato">SN 56.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that those who understand the four noble truths are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato"><b>not subject to birth and aging</b></a> in the same way as those who remain ignorant of them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato">SN 56.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a handful of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato"><b>simsapa leaves</b></a> to illustrate the difference between the vast amount of knowledge he possessed and the specific truths he chose to teach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato">SN 56.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the things he has known through direct knowledge but has not taught are as numerous as the leaves in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato"><b>simsapa grove</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato">SN 56.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed he withheld many things he knew because they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato"><b>not connected with the goal</b></a> of spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato">SN 56.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha chose not to teach certain topics because they do not lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato"><b>revulsion</b></a>, dispassion, or the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato">SN 56.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that his vast untaught knowledge is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato"><b>fundamental to the holy life</b></a> and does not lead to Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato">SN 56.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha focused his teaching exclusively on the four noble truths because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato"><b>connected with the goal</b></a> and lead to enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato">SN 56.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the four noble truths—suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path—to be the essential teachings that lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> and awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato">SN 56.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to make an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> to understand the truth of suffering, as this knowledge is vital for spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato">SN 56.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while staying in Kosambī, used a simple physical metaphor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato"><b>leaves in his hand</b></a> to contrast his limited public teaching with his unlimited private wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato">SN 56.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the truth of the way leading to the cessation of suffering specifically because it conduces to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato"><b>nibbana</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato">SN 56.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an ascetic who does not understand the four noble truths lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato"><b>liberation by wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.22/en/sujato">SN 56.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the only things he teaches are those that lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato"><b>peace</b></a> and enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.31/en/sujato">SN 56.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared beings wandering through rebirth to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.33/en/sujato"><b>stick thrown into the air</b></a>, which unpredictably falls sometimes on its base and sometimes on its tip? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.33/en/sujato">SN 56.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings are driven to wander from world to world because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.33/en/sujato"><b>obstructed by ignorance</b></a> and fettered by craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.33/en/sujato">SN 56.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that even if one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.34/en/sujato"><b>head were on fire</b></a>, one should prioritize the breakthrough to the four noble truths over even the urgent task of putting out those flames? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.34/en/sujato">SN 56.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it would be worth being struck by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.35/en/sujato"><b>three hundred spears</b></a> every single day for a hundred years if it meant one would finally realize the four noble truths at the end of that century? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.35/en/sujato">SN 56.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the primary reason beings roam through a long course of rebirth is that they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.33/en/sujato"><b>not seen</b></a> the four noble truths as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.33/en/sujato">SN 56.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for the realization of the four noble truths, one must develop extraordinary <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.34/en/sujato"><b>zeal and effort</b></a>, comparable to the intensity used to extinguish burning clothes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.34/en/sujato">SN 56.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the breakthrough to the four noble truths is not achieved through misery, but is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.35/en/sujato"><b>accompanied by happiness</b></a> and joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.35/en/sujato">SN 56.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that in the beginningless cycle of rebirth, the amount of pain one has already suffered from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.35/en/sujato"><b>weapons</b></a> like swords and arrows is far greater than the pain of being struck by hundreds of spears daily for a century? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.35/en/sujato">SN 56.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.34/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a> as essential qualities that must be applied for the realization of the truth of the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.34/en/sujato">SN 56.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged spiritual seekers by saying it is fitting for a person of good family to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.35/en/sujato"><b>undergo</b></a> even extreme physical pain if it leads to the direct penetration of the four noble truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.35/en/sujato">SN 56.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings constantly transition from this world to the next and back again because they have not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.33/en/sujato"><b>penetrated</b></a> the truth of the origin of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.33/en/sujato">SN 56.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.34/en/sujato"><b>burning clothes</b></a> to illustrate the supreme urgency required for a person to understand the truth of the path leading to the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.34/en/sujato">SN 56.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the cycle of rebirth, or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.35/en/sujato"><b>saṃsāra</b></a>, as being without a discoverable beginning, meaning its starting point cannot be perceived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.35/en/sujato">SN 56.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to make a spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.33/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> to understand the reality of suffering, comparing the aimless wandering of beings without this knowledge to a stick falling on either end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.33/en/sujato">SN 56.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that while the path may be difficult, the actual moment of breakthrough to the four noble truths is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.35/en/sujato"><b>accompanied by joy</b></a> rather than by displeasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.35/en/sujato">SN 56.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda was highly impressed by the skill of several <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato"><b>Licchavi youths</b></a> in Vesālī who were so well-trained in archery that they could shoot arrows through a narrow keyhole from a distance, one after another, without missing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato">SN 56.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Ven. Ānanda whether it was more difficult to shoot arrows through a narrow keyhole from a distance or to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato"><b>pierce a hair-tip</b></a> with another hair-tip after the first had been split seven times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato">SN 56.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that as difficult as it is to pierce a split hair-tip with another hair-tip, it is even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato"><b>harder to penetrate</b></a> the Four Noble Truths as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato">SN 56.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that those who truly realize the truth that "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato"><b>this is suffering</b></a>" are achieving a breakthrough that is far more subtle and difficult than the most expert feats of physical precision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato">SN 56.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because the Four Noble Truths are so difficult to realize, the Buddha urged his followers that a spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> must be made to understand the origin and cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato">SN 56.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato"><b>Gabled Hall</b></a> in the Great Wood at Vesālī when he used a comparison between expert archery and spiritual insight to instruct Ven. Ānanda on the importance of the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato">SN 56.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato"><b>hair split seven times</b></a> to illustrate the extreme subtlety and difficulty involved in truly penetrating the reality of the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato">SN 56.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda, after observing youths practicing archery at a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato"><b>public hall</b></a>, remarked to the Buddha on how well-trained and skilled they were for being able to hit such a tiny target from afar without missing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato">SN 56.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that those who penetrate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato"><b>way leading to the cessation of suffering</b></a> as it really is are performing a feat of understanding that is "harder to penetrate than the hardest thing"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato">SN 56.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that one must apply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato"><b>spiritual exertion</b></a> specifically to realize the Four Noble Truths because their penetration is the most difficult achievement possible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.45/en/sujato">SN 56.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato"><b>world-interstices</b></a> as regions of blinding, gloomy darkness where the light of the sun and moon cannot reach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato">SN 56.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there is a darkness <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato"><b>more fearsome</b></a> and greater than the pitch-black voids between world systems? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato">SN 56.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the ultimate darkness as the failure of ascetics and brahmins to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato"><b>four noble truths</b></a> as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato">SN 56.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those who do not understand suffering delight in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> that lead to future birth and continued existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato">SN 56.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who are ignorant of the truth of suffering fall into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato"><b>darkness of birth</b></a>, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato">SN 56.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that people who fail to penetrate the four noble truths are not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato"><b>freed from suffering</b></a> or from sorrow and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato">SN 56.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who truly understand the four noble truths do not delight in the formations that lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato"><b>renewed existence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato">SN 56.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that by realizing the four noble truths, one is liberated and does not fall into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato"><b>darkness of sorrow</b></a> and lamentation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato">SN 56.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his disciples to make a spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> specifically to understand the truth: "This is the way leading to the cessation of suffering"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.46/en/sujato">SN 56.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the famous simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato"><b>blind turtle</b></a> in the ocean to illustrate how difficult it is for a person to regain human status once it is lost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato">SN 56.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked his monks if a turtle surfacing once every hundred years would likely put its neck through a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato"><b>single-holed yoke</b></a> floating on the sea? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato">SN 56.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the chance of a blind turtle hitting a floating yoke is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato"><b>quicker</b></a> than the chance of a person in a state of woe regaining human birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato">SN 56.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that in the states of woe, there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato"><b>righteous conduct</b></a>, no living in harmony, and no wholesome activity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato">SN 56.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that in the lower realms, the prevailing behavior is for beings to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato"><b>devouring one another</b></a>, with the strong preying on the weak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato">SN 56.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha attributed the extreme difficulty of escaping lower realms to the fact that those beings have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato"><b>not seen</b></a> the four noble truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato">SN 56.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that human birth is incredibly rare because there is no opportunity for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato"><b>meritorious deeds</b></a> once one has fallen into the downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato">SN 56.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should make a great <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> to understand the truth of suffering to avoid the cycle of aimless wandering in lower states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato">SN 56.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that the primary reason for the "devouring of the weak" in lower realms is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato"><b>absence</b></a> of the four noble truths in those states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato">SN 56.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to realize the truth of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> as a matter of supreme urgency given the rarity of human life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato">SN 56.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha contrasted the physical impossibility of a turtle hitting a tiny target in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato"><b>great ocean</b></a> with the even greater spiritual difficulty of regaining a human existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.47/en/sujato">SN 56.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the rarity of obtaining human birth by comparing the world to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato"><b>vast ocean</b></a> where a single-holed yoke is tossed about by the winds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato">SN 56.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato"><b>one-eyed turtle</b></a> surfacing once every hundred years to illustrate how difficult it is for a person to happen upon a human life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato">SN 56.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the appearance of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a>, an Arhat, a Perfectly Enlightened One in the world, is as rare as a specific turtle accidentally putting its neck through a floating yoke hole? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato">SN 56.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the presence of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma and Vinaya</b></a> in the world is an extraordinary coincidence, occurring as rarely as a one-eyed turtle hitting a tiny floating target in the great sea? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato">SN 56.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that because human birth and the teaching of a Buddha are so rare, one should make a spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> to understand the truth: "this is suffering"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato">SN 56.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once compared the tiny amount of soil on his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato"><b>fingernail</b></a> to the entire great earth to show how little suffering remains for a noble disciple who has realized the Four Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato">SN 56.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person with right view who has achieved breakthrough, the suffering already <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato"><b>destroyed</b></a> is like the vast earth, while the remainder is like a few grains of sand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato">SN 56.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who truly knows "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato"><b>this is suffering</b></a>" as someone for whom the previous mass of suffering has been completely exhausted and finished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato">SN 56.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a noble disciple who has penetrated the truths, the maximum number of future lives remaining is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato"><b>seven</b></a>, which is a negligible amount compared to the suffering of the past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato">SN 56.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the comparison of soil on a fingernail versus the earth to urge monks that a spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> must be made to realize the path leading to the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato">SN 56.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that those beings who pass away as humans and are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.102/en/sujato"><b>reborn as humans</b></a> are as few as the grains of soil on a fingernail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.102/en/sujato">SN 56.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the number of beings who pass away from the human realm and are reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.102/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a> is as vast as the entire great earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.102/en/sujato">SN 56.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized obtaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato"><b>human status</b></a> as a matter of extraordinary chance, given the sheer physical difficulty of a turtle hitting a single hole in a world-wide ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato">SN 56.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even if a yoke were blown by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato"><b>four winds</b></a> across the entire world-ocean, the rarity of a one-eyed turtle poking its head through it is the same as the rarity of a Buddha appearing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato">SN 56.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, once you possess human birth and a Tathāgata has appeared, you should focus your efforts on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato"><b>way leading to the cessation of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.48/en/sujato">SN 56.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato"><b>noble disciple</b></a> who has attained vision as having already finished nearly all their suffering, leaving only a "fingernail's worth"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato">SN 56.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato"><b>understand</b></a> the four noble truths as they really are have successfully overcome a "mass of suffering" equivalent to the size of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato">SN 56.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha frequently used physical objects like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.102/en/sujato"><b>soil</b></a> to illustrate metaphysical truths about the destinations of beings after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.102/en/sujato">SN 56.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for those who do not understand the Four Noble Truths, the cycle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.102/en/sujato"><b>hellish rebirth</b></a> is far more common than returning to human form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.102/en/sujato">SN 56.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> as a reality that can be known "as it really is" through direct breakthrough and insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.51/en/sujato">SN 56.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings are infatuated with external forms only because there is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato"><b>gratification in forms</b></a>, and if such gratification did not exist, beings would not be enamored with them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato">SN 35.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings experience revulsion toward forms because there is an inherent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato"><b>danger in forms</b></a>, and without this danger, they would not feel weary or disenchanted with what they see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato">SN 35.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that beings are capable of transcending the influence of the visual world because there is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato"><b>escape from forms</b></a>, a liberation that would be impossible to achieve if such an escape did not exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato">SN 35.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that beings are only infatuated with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato"><b>sounds</b></a> because they provide gratification, but they also contain a danger that eventually leads to revulsion and an escape that allows for final release? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato">SN 35.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, if there were no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato"><b>gratification in odors</b></a> or tastes, beings would never become attached to them, yet it is only through understanding their danger and escape that one can be liberated from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato">SN 35.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that beings only experience a sense of revulsion toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato"><b>tactile objects</b></a> because of their inherent danger, and that recognizing this alongside their gratification and escape is necessary for spiritual freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato">SN 35.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that beings would never be able to leave behind the influence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato"><b>mental phenomena</b></a> if an escape did not exist, but because an escape is available alongside gratification and danger, liberation is possible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato">SN 35.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that until beings understand the gratification, danger, and escape regarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato"><b>six external bases</b></a> (forms, sounds, odors, tastes, tactile objects, and mental phenomena) as they really are, they cannot dwell with a mind that is detached and liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.18/en/sujato">SN 35.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that anyone who seeks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato"><b>delight in the eye</b></a> is actually seeking delight in suffering, and those who delight in suffering are not released from the cycle of pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato">SN 35.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that to find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato"><b>delight in the ear</b></a> and the sounds it perceives is equivalent to finding delight in suffering, which prevents a person from ever being released from that suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato">SN 35.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that one who takes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato"><b>delight in the nose</b></a> and odors is effectively taking delight in suffering, and as a result, such an individual remains unreleased from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato">SN 35.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha equated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato"><b>delight in the tongue</b></a> and the flavors it tastes with delighting in suffering, and explicitly stated that such a person is not released from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato">SN 35.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when a person finds <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato"><b>delight in the body</b></a> and tactile sensations, they are delighting in suffering, and are therefore not liberated from the burden of dukkha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato">SN 35.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that anyone who takes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato"><b>delight in the mind</b></a> and mental phenomena is actually delighting in suffering, making them "unreleased from suffering" in his view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato">SN 35.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato"><b>does not delight in the eye</b></a> is also not delighting in suffering, and is consequently released from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato">SN 35.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that by not delighting in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato"><b>six internal bases</b></a>—the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind—one avoids delighting in suffering and thereby attains true liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.19/en/sujato">SN 35.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.98/en/sujato"><b>lack of restraint</b></a> occurs when a monk delights in, welcomes, and remains cleaving to agreeable forms cognizable by the eye, which are provocative of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.98/en/sujato">SN 35.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk should understand he is experiencing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.98/en/sujato"><b>decline from wholesome states</b></a> if he finds himself delighting in and cleaving to agreeable sense objects like sights, tastes, or mental phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.98/en/sujato">SN 35.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.98/en/sujato"><b>restraint</b></a> as the state where a monk does not delight in, welcome, or remain cleaving to agreeable and charming sense objects that are provocative of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.98/en/sujato">SN 35.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk practices restraint by not cleaving to sense objects, he can confidently know that he is in a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.98/en/sujato"><b>non-decline</b></a> regarding his wholesome spiritual qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.98/en/sujato">SN 35.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically warned that remaining focused on and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.98/en/sujato"><b>delighting</b></a> in agreeable mind-objects is a form of lack of restraint that leads a practitioner away from wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.98/en/sujato">SN 35.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.99/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> because a monk who is concentrated is able to understand things as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.99/en/sujato">SN 35.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who is concentrated understands "as it really is" that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.99/en/sujato"><b>eye is impermanent</b></a>, along with the forms it perceives and the eye-consciousness that arises from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.99/en/sujato">SN 35.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that through concentration, a monk understands that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.99/en/sujato"><b>eye-consciousness</b></a>, eye-contact, and any feelings—whether pleasant, painful, or neutral—arising from that contact are all impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.99/en/sujato">SN 35.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a concentrated monk sees that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.99/en/sujato"><b>mind is impermanent</b></a>, as are the mental phenomena, mind-consciousness, and mind-contact associated with it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.99/en/sujato">SN 35.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the realization that whatever feeling arises from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.99/en/sujato"><b>mind-contact</b></a> is impermanent as a core part of understanding reality through concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.99/en/sujato">SN 35.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.100/en/sujato"><b>devote themselves to seclusion</b></a> (or solitude), explaining that a monk in seclusion is able to understand the true nature of things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.100/en/sujato">SN 35.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.100/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> allows a practitioner to clearly see that the eye and the forms it sees are inherently impermanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.100/en/sujato">SN 35.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who is secluded and practicing meditation understands as it really is that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.100/en/sujato"><b>eye-contact</b></a> and the resulting feelings of pleasure or pain are not permanent states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.100/en/sujato">SN 35.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the practice of seclusion leads to the direct knowledge that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.100/en/sujato"><b>mind-consciousness</b></a> is impermanent, just like the physical sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.100/en/sujato">SN 35.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even the most subtle <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.100/en/sujato"><b>feelings</b></a>—whether pleasant, painful, or neither-painful-nor-pleasant—that arise from mental contact are seen as impermanent by one who is secluded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.100/en/sujato">SN 35.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the phrase <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.99/en/sujato"><b>understands as it really is</b></a> to describe the specific insight into the impermanence of the six internal and external sense bases gained through meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.99/en/sujato">SN 35.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.98/en/sujato"><b>spiritual decline</b></a> as being directly linked to how one responds to "agreeable, charming, and provocative" sensory experiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.98/en/sujato">SN 35.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded his teachings on concentration and seclusion by repeating that a practitioner who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.100/en/sujato"><b>concentrated and secluded</b></a> will inevitably perceive the truth of impermanence in all aspects of experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn35.100/en/sujato">SN 35.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while staying at Nāḷandā in Pāvārika’s mango grove, was once accused of causing the ruin of families because he traveled with a large community of monks during a period of severe <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>famine</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta, a rival teacher, attempted to discredit the Buddha by sending a disciple to pose a "two-horned question" designed to trap the Buddha in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>logical dilemma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly confirmed to the headman Asibandhakaputta that he encourages and praises <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a>, protection, and care for families in many different ways? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta promised the headman Asibandhakaputta that he would gain a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>good reputation</b></a> if he could successfully refute the claims of the powerful and famous ascetic Gautama? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to have the ability to recall ninety-one eons of past history and stated that he never once knew a family to have been harmed simply by giving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> to a mendicant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that families who possess great wealth and abundant resources have acquired them through past acts of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>giving</b></a>, truthfulness, and self-restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified eight specific causes for the destruction of families, one of which is the seizure of wealth by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>kings</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the actions of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>thieves</b></a> as one of the primary external factors that lead to the ruin and downfall of a family's fortune? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that natural disasters, specifically mentioning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>fire</b></a>, are among the eight reasons why a family’s accumulated wealth might be destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the destructive power of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>water</b></a>, such as floods, as a major cause for families losing their status and riches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a family may face ruin if their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>stored treasure</b></a> or hidden assets are lost or vanish from their known location? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>mismanaged business</b></a> or poorly executed work ventures are significant internal factors that lead to the economic failure of a household? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the birth of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>spendthrift</b></a>—someone who squanders, scatters, and destroys the family’s wealth—is a specific cause for a family’s decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>impermanence</b></a> itself as the eighth and ultimate reason why families and their fortunes eventually undergo destruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that anyone who falsely accuses him of working for the ruin of families, and does not abandon that speech or view, will be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>reborn in hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the headman Asibandhakaputta was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>disciple of the Nigaṇṭhas</b></a> before he was sent to challenge the Buddha’s conduct during a famine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's explanation of the eight causes of ruin, the headman Asibandhakaputta declared the Buddha's teaching to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>magnificent</b></a> and clear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>two-horned question</b></a> to describe a dilemma that is so difficult it is like something a person can neither spit out nor swallow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the mere act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato"><b>giving alms</b></a> to virtuous renunciants is not a cause for a family's poverty, but rather a practice associated with prosperity and truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn42.9/en/sujato">SN 42.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his final years, the Buddha fell so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>seriously ill</b></a> while staying at Beluvagāmaka that he experienced excruciating, life-threatening pains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha was struck by a deadly illness, he endured the intense physical pain mindfully and with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>clear comprehension</b></a>, remaining completely undisturbed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha decided it was not proper to pass away into final Nibbāna without first addressing his attendants and the community of monks, and thus he suppressed his illness by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>determination</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that he chose to maintain his life by suppressing a severe affliction through the application of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>energy</b></a> and a resolved will to live? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda was so distressed by the Buddha’s sickness that he felt as though his own body was drugged and he could no longer see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>directions</b></a> clearly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ānanda found a small measure of comfort during the Buddha's illness by thinking that the Buddha would not pass away until he had left instructions for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>Bhikkhu Saṅgha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha clarified that he had taught the Dhamma completely, without making any distinction between inner and outer teachings, because he possessed no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>teacher’s fist</b></a> that holds back secrets? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a Tathāgata does not have the thought that he should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>lead the Saṅgha</b></a> or that the community of monks is dependent on him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described himself in his old age as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>eighty years old</b></a> and having reached the final stage of his life’s journey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used a striking simile to describe his aging body, comparing it to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>old cart</b></a> that can only be kept going with the help of makeshift repairs and straps? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha shared that his body only felt truly at ease when he withdrew from all external objects and entered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>signless concentration</b></a> of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha famously exhorted his followers to live as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>islands unto themselves</b></a> and to be their own refuge, rather than seeking security in anything external? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to take the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma as an island</b></a> and the Dhamma as their refuge, emphasizing that no other spiritual authority should be relied upon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha defined being a "refuge to oneself" as dwelling in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>contemplation of the body</b></a>, remaining ardent, clearly comprehending, and mindful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk should practice being his own island by contemplating feelings, mind, and phenomena while removing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>covetousness and grief</b></a> regarding the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those monks who live as their own islands and refuges after his passing will reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>highest peak</b></a>, provided they are truly keen on training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even when he was recovering from a life-threatening illness, the Buddha's primary instruction to Ānanda was to maintain the practice of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>four establishments of mindfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that spiritual progress is a matter of personal responsibility by telling his disciples they should live with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>no other refuge</b></a> except themselves and the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that his physical body was only comfortable when he reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato"><b>cessation of certain feelings</b></a> through advanced states of mental concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn47.9/en/sujato">SN 47.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised his followers not to think <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.7/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome thoughts</b></a>—such as thoughts of sensual desire, ill will, and cruelty—because they do not lead to disenchantment, direct knowledge, or Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.7/en/sujato">SN 56.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that dwelling on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.7/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of ill will</b></a> is unbeneficial and irrelevant to the fundamentals of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.7/en/sujato">SN 56.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to focus their thinking on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.7/en/sujato"><b>the nature of suffering</b></a>, as this type of reflection leads to enlightenment and the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.7/en/sujato">SN 56.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that reflecting on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.7/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> is a spiritually productive activity that is conducive to peace and awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.7/en/sujato">SN 56.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that one should make an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.7/en/sujato"><b>exertion to understand</b></a> the way leading to the cessation of suffering rather than engaging in thoughts that bring no spiritual benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.7/en/sujato">SN 56.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha prohibited engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.9/en/sujato"><b>contentious speech</b></a>, where practitioners argue over who understands the Dhamma and Vinaya correctly and who does not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.9/en/sujato">SN 56.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that claiming "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.9/en/sujato"><b>I am practicing rightly</b></a>" while accusing others of practicing wrongly is unbeneficial speech that fails to lead to Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.9/en/sujato">SN 56.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha regarded debates where one tells another "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.9/en/sujato"><b>You are refuted</b></a>" or "Your speech is disconnected" as obstacles to the goal of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.9/en/sujato">SN 56.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that instead of arguing about doctrines, one should speak about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.9/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a> to ensure that conversation remains focused on spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.9/en/sujato">SN 56.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged practitioners to speak about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.9/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> because such talk is connected with the spiritual goal and leads to awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.9/en/sujato">SN 56.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato"><b>low topics</b></a>" (tiracchānakathā) as talk about kings, thieves, ministers, and armies, and advised his followers to avoid such conversation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato">SN 56.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised against talking about <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato"><b>battles and wars</b></a> because these subjects do not contribute to disenchantment, fading of lust, or peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato">SN 56.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed conversation regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato"><b>food, drink, and clothing</b></a> as unbeneficial speech that does not lead to higher knowledge or Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato">SN 56.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that talk about <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato"><b>villages, towns, and cities</b></a> is considered "low topics" that do not lead to the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato">SN 56.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha discouraged practitioners from discussing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato"><b>women and heroes</b></a>, as such talk is irrelevant to the development of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato">SN 56.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized talk about <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato"><b>speculations regarding the world</b></a> and the sea as unbeneficial for those seeking spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato">SN 56.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that conversation should focus on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato"><b>path to the cessation of suffering</b></a> rather than petty worldly matters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato">SN 56.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when monks converse, they should ensure their speech is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato"><b>connected with the goal</b></a> and leads to direct knowledge and enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato">SN 56.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, focusing speech on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato"><b>truth of suffering</b></a> is the correct way to engage in dialogue that actually leads to Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato">SN 56.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato"><b>exertion should be made</b></a> to fully understand the Four Noble Truths as the ultimate priority of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/sn56.10/en/sujato">SN 56.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the human body as being held together by bones and nerves, covered in skin and flesh, and filled with various <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato"><b>internal organs</b></a> such as the intestines, liver, heart, lungs, kidneys, and spleen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato">Snp 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato"><b>impurities</b></a> like eye-secretion, ear-filth, mucus, saliva, sweat, and blood constantly flow from the body’s nine openings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato">Snp 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that while a foolish person, overcome by ignorance, considers the body beautiful, its hollow head is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato"><b>filled with brains</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato">Snp 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a body lies dead, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato"><b>swollen and blue</b></a> in a cemetery, even one's own relatives eventually abandon it and lose interest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato">Snp 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that after death, a corpse is typically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato"><b>devoured by animals</b></a> such as dogs, jackals, wolves, worms, crows, and vultures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato">Snp 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a wise monk, having heard his teachings, understands the body <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato"><b>as it really is</b></a> and sees its true, impure nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato">Snp 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to discard <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato"><b>desire for the body</b></a>, whether it be regarding their own physical form or the bodies of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato">Snp 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who is detached from lust and desire can attain the "deathless peace" known as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato"><b>Nibbana</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato">Snp 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the human body as a foul-smelling, two-footed thing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato"><b>full of carcasses</b></a> that is nonetheless carried about and cherished by people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.11/en/sujato">Snp 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "peaceful person" as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato"><b>free from craving</b></a> even before the body breaks up at death, and who does not depend on the past or future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato">Snp 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a sage is "restrained in speech" because they are not angry, not boastful, and speak with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato"><b>counsel</b></a> without being arrogant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato">Snp 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a peaceful person as someone who has no attachment to the future and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato"><b>does not sorrow</b></a> over the things of the past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato">Snp 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a peaceful person is one who is not led by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato"><b>dogmatic views</b></a> and has seen the value of seclusion from sensory contacts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato">Snp 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a truly peaceful person is not greedy for gains, does not get angry at a lack of profit, and is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato"><b>addicted to pleasant things</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato">Snp 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described a sage as one who remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato"><b>equanimous and mindful</b></a> at all times, without considering themselves equal, superior, or inferior to others in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato">Snp 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a person is truly "peaceful" when they have no dependencies, having understood the Dhamma and become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato"><b>free from attachment</b></a> to existence or non-existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato">Snp 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that for a peaceful person, there are no "bonds" or internal ties, and such an individual has successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato"><b>crossed over attachment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato">Snp 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha noted that a sage does not grieve over what <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato"><b>does not exist</b></a> and does not claim anything in the world as their own personal property? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.10/en/sujato">Snp 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who is skilled in their own good and wishes to attain the state of peace should be capable, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato"><b>perfectly upright</b></a>, easy to speak to, gentle, and not arrogant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato">Snp 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised practitioners to lead a simple life, being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato"><b>easy to support</b></a>, having few duties, and maintaining a light livelihood with peaceful faculties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato">Snp 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged people to develop a heart of universal goodwill toward all living beings, wishing that they be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato"><b>happy and secure</b></a>, whether they are weak or strong, seen or unseen, or living far or near? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato">Snp 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that one should not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato"><b>deceive or despise</b></a> another person anywhere, nor wish harm upon anyone out of anger or ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato">Snp 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that one should cherish all living beings with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato"><b>boundless mind</b></a>, just as a mother would protect her only child with her own life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato">Snp 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the practice of radiating loving-kindness without limit in all directions—upward, downward, and across—as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato"><b>divine abiding</b></a> in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato">Snp 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is virtuous and endowed with right vision, by discarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a>, will never again return to a womb for rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.8/en/sujato">Snp 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Cunda the smith asked the Buddha about the different types of ascetics, the Buddha replied that there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.5/en/sujato"><b>four types of ascetics</b></a> in the world and no fifth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.5/en/sujato">Snp 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a "Way-Conqueror" (Maggajina) as an ascetic who is free from doubt, has removed the arrows of suffering, and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.5/en/sujato"><b>delighted by Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.5/en/sujato">Snp 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a "Way-Expounder" (Maggadesaka) as a sage who has realized the supreme truth and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.5/en/sujato"><b>explains the Dhamma</b></a> to others to resolve their uncertainties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.5/en/sujato">Snp 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a "Way-Liver" (Maggajivi) as one who lives according to the well-taught path of Dhamma, remaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.5/en/sujato"><b>self-restrained and mindful</b></a> while following faultless practices? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.5/en/sujato">Snp 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned against "Way-Corrupters" (Maggadusi), describing them as people who put on a false front of virtue but are actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.5/en/sujato"><b>boastful and uncontrolled</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.5/en/sujato">Snp 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a "Way-Corrupter" is often <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.5/en/sujato"><b>deceptive and a family-defiler</b></a>, yet they walk about pretending to be a true practitioner among the virtuous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.5/en/sujato">Snp 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that not associating with fools, but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato"><b>associating with the wise</b></a> and honoring those worthy of honor, is considered a supreme blessing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato">Snp 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato"><b>living in a suitable locality</b></a>, having performed meritorious deeds in the past, and setting oneself in the right direction are among the highest protections a person can have? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato">Snp 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified great learning, proficiency in a craft, a well-trained discipline, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken speech</b></a> as factors that constitute a supreme blessing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato">Snp 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the support of one's mother and father, the cherished care of one's wife and children, and engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato"><b>unconfused work</b></a> are essential elements of a blessed life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato">Snp 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed giving, living by the Dhamma, providing assistance to relatives, and performing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato"><b>blameless actions</b></a> as supreme blessings for any individual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato">Snp 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that abstaining from evil, restraint from intoxicants, and maintaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness in all things</b></a> are vital for a person's highest protection and welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato">Snp 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that showing respect and humility, being content and grateful, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato"><b>hearing the Dhamma</b></a> at the proper time are key components of the supreme blessing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato">Snp 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that patience, being easy to speak to, visiting ascetics, and engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato"><b>timely religious discussion</b></a> are practices that lead to a person's ultimate well-being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato">Snp 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that self-restraint, a holy life, seeing the Noble Truths, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato"><b>realization of Nibbāna</b></a> are the pinnacles of what constitutes a supreme blessing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato">Snp 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the mind of a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato"><b>unshaken by worldly conditions</b></a>, remaining sorrowless, stainless, and secure, as having achieved the highest possible blessing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.4/en/sujato">Snp 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed how various thinkers claim that purity is found only in their own system and not in other teachings, remaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato"><b>entrenched in their own truths</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato">Snp 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that those who enter assemblies to debate often label others as "fools" and engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato"><b>mutual accusations</b></a> while seeking only praise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato">Snp 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a person seeking praise in a debate becomes distressed if their argument is refuted, becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato"><b>dejected and angry</b></a> while searching for flaws in others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato">Snp 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a person whose argument is judged inferior and defeated in a public debate will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato"><b>lament and grieve</b></a>, sorrowing that they have been outdone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato">Snp 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised practitioners to refrain from debates and disputes, noting that there is no other benefit in them except the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato"><b>temporary gain of praise</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato">Snp 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if one is praised for their argument in an assembly, the resulting pride and conceit become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato"><b>ground for distress</b></a> and mental agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato">Snp 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a debater looking for an opponent to a brave hero <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato"><b>nourished by royal food</b></a> who goes about roaring in search of a rival to fight in battle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato">Snp 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that those who have relinquished all views and do not take up any side in a conflict have no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato"><b>opponent to fight with</b></a> in the arena of intellectual debate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato">Snp 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for those who have overcome all views and do not oppose any specific doctrine, there is nothing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato"><b>clutched or taken up</b></a> that could lead to further dispute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato">Snp 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that a person who ponders dogmatic views in their mind is essentially trying to challenge an enlightened "cleansed one," which is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato"><b>futile endeavor</b></a> because the sage has moved beyond all views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.8/en/sujato">Snp 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who quells <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>arisen anger</b></a>—as one would use medicine to counteract spreading snake venom—to a snake shedding its worn-out skin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who entirely cuts off <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a>, similar to a person picking a lotus flower from a pond, leaves behind both the near and far shores of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the total removal of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> as being like drying up a fast-flowing stream, allowing a practitioner to shed their old self like a snake sheds its skin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the total destruction of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a> to a great flood sweeping away a very weak bridge made of reeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who finds no essence in any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>existences</b></a>, just as one would find no flowers on a fig tree, leaves behind both the near and far shores? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the recurring imagery of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>snake shedding its worn-out skin</b></a> to illustrate how an enlightened monk abandons the cycle of rebirth and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk who has burnt away all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>distracting thoughts</b></a> and internal mental constructs has reached a state where they leave behind both the near and far shores of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who does not overshoot or lag behind in practice and has surpassed all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>mental proliferation</b></a> as being like a snake shedding its skin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who understands everything in the world is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>false</b></a>, and thus remains free from greed, lust, and hate, has successfully left behind the near and far shores? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendencies</b></a> and unwholesome roots have been completely uprooted is like a snake shedding its old, worn-out skin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who has abandoned the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>five hindrances</b></a> and is free from doubt and distress as one who leaves behind both the near and far shores? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a monk who has no inward anger and has transcended the cycle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>existence and non-existence</b></a> as someone who sheds their old skin like a snake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who knows the world is false and is completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>free from delusion</b></a> leaves behind the near and far shores, just as a snake sheds its skin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who has no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato"><b>distress</b></a> or underlying causes that would lead back to "this shore" has successfully left behind the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.1/en/sujato">Snp 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the yakkha Āḷavaka repeatedly ordered him to leave and enter his dwelling, the Buddha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>initially complied</b></a> three times to show his cooperative nature before eventually refusing on the fourth attempt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the yakkha Āḷavaka that no one in the world of devas, Māras, or Brahmās could <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>scatter his mind</b></a> or split his heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> as the finest wealth a person can possess in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a>, when it is well practiced, is the specific thing that brings true happiness to a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>truth</b></a> is the sweetest of all tastes that one can experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a life lived by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> is the kind of life that is called the best? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that an individual crosses over the flood of worldly existence by means of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that one crosses the great ocean of suffering through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified that a person overcomes suffering through the application of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>energy</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha declared that a person is purified from defilements through the power of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that one gains wisdom by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>listening</b></a> attentively and being heedful of the Dhamma of the Arahants for the sake of attaining Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha said that a person who is an industrious and enduring <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>doer of what is fit</b></a> is the one who finds wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>truth</b></a> as the quality by which a person achieves lasting fame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that one binds and maintains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>friendships</b></a> through the practice of giving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a faithful householder who possesses the four qualities of truth, Dhamma, firmness, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato"><b>generosity</b></a> does not sorrow after passing away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.10/en/sujato">Snp 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that fear is born from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato"><b>intimacy</b></a> and that the true sage sees the benefit in being homeless and free from social attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato">Snp 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described a sage as one who has crushed the seeds of future existence and refuses to supply the moisture of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> that would allow them to grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato">Snp 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a sage is one who has understood all dwellings and has no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato"><b>desire</b></a> for any of them, having already reached the further shore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato">Snp 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha characterized a sage as someone who is all-conquering, all-knowing, and completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato"><b>unsullied</b></a> by any phenomena or mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato">Snp 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a sage possesses the power of wisdom, is well-endowed with virtue, and is constantly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato"><b>mindful</b></a> and delighted in meditative absorption (jhana)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato">Snp 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha compared a sage to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato"><b>lion</b></a> that does not tremble at sounds and to the wind that cannot be captured by a net? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato">Snp 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the image of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato"><b>lotus</b></a> which is not smeared by water to describe how a sage remains unpolluted by the world around them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato">Snp 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha said a sage remains as firm as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato"><b>pillar</b></a> in a pool even when others speak to them with harsh or provocative language? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato">Snp 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a sage is as straight as a shuttle, loathes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato"><b>evil deeds</b></a>, and carefully examines what is even and uneven in conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato">Snp 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a sage who lives on alms remains equanimous, neither praising the food they receive nor <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato"><b>disparaging</b></a> the person who gives it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato">Snp 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha pointed out that a householder and a sage are far apart in their conduct, specifically because a householder is unrestrained regarding the life of others, whereas a sage constantly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato"><b>protects</b></a> living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato">Snp 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha compared the difference between a householder and a sage to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato"><b>crested peacock</b></a> that can never match the speed of a swan, illustrating how a layperson cannot easily imitate a sage meditating in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.12/en/sujato">Snp 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>fifteenth-day Uposatha</b></a> night, the yakkha Sātāgira invited his friend Hemavata to visit the Buddha, whom he described as a teacher of excellent name? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the yakkha Sātāgira observed that the Buddha's mind is always well-directed and that he has attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>mastery over his thoughts</b></a> regarding both pleasant and unpleasant things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is recognized by the yakkhas as one who never takes what is not given, is restrained toward all living beings, and never <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>neglects meditation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the yakkha Sātāgira, the Buddha never utters a lie, avoids divisive and harsh speech, and instead speaks with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>wisdom and meaning</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described as being completely free from lust toward sensual pleasures, possessing an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>unclouded mind</b></a>, and having transcended all delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the yakkha Sātāgira testified that the Buddha is endowed with true knowledge and pure conduct, his taints are destroyed, and for him there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>no more rebirth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the yakkha Hemavata describes the Buddha as a brave sage with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>antelope-like legs</b></a>, who is thin, eats little food, and meditates alone in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the yakkhas compare the Buddha to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>solitary lion</b></a> and an elephant who has no regard for sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked about the origin of the world, the Buddha taught that the world has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>arisen in six</b></a> (the six sense bases), makes its acquaintance in six, and is afflicted in six? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>six sense bases</b></a> as the specific things through which the world experiences clinging and affliction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that the way one is released from suffering is by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>discarding desire</b></a> for the five cords of sensual pleasure, with the mind as the sixth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a person who is always endowed with virtue, possesses wisdom, and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>well-concentrated</b></a> is able to cross the flood that is so difficult to cross? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>reflective within</b></a> and mindful is capable of crossing over the great ocean of worldly existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that an individual who is detached from the perception of sensual pleasure and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>surpassed all fetters</b></a> does not sink in the deep waters of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha identified that one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>exhausted delight in existence</b></a> is the one who remains stable and does not sink into the depths of samsara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the yakkha Hemavata praised the Buddha as a Great Seer (Mahesi) who possesses deep wisdom and is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>seer of the subtle meaning</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the yakkhas recognized the Buddha as an "all-knower" who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>without possessions</b></a> and unattached to the realm of sensual existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described by the yakkhas as a Great Seer walking on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>divine path</b></a>, having been liberated in every way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the yakkha Hemavata called the Buddha a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>giver of wisdom</b></a> who is not attached to the "lurking-place" of sensual desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha is described as the All-knower and the Wise One who walks specifically upon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>noble path</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the yakkhas rejoiced in seeing the Buddha, proclaiming him as the "Enlightened One" (Sambuddha) who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>crossed the flood</b></a> and is free from all taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a group of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato"><b>one thousand yakkhas</b></a>, possessed of psychic power and fame, all went for refuge to the Buddha as their unsurpassed teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.9/en/sujato">Snp 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Rāhula if he honored the wise man, whom he described as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato"><b>torch-bearer</b></a> for humanity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato">Snp 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted Rāhula to become an ender of suffering by first abandoning the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato">Snp 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha instructed Rāhula to specifically associate with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato"><b>good friends</b></a> (kalyāṇa mitte) and seek out remote, quiet lodgings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato">Snp 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha advised Rāhula to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato"><b>moderate in eating</b></a> (mattaññū) to support his spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato">Snp 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha cautioned Rāhula not to develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> for requisites such as robes, almsfood, or lodgings, to avoid returning to the world of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato">Snp 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught Rāhula to remain restrained by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato"><b>Pātimokkha</b></a> and the five sense faculties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato">Snp 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha encouraged Rāhula to maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> and to cultivate a strong sense of disillusionment with worldly things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato">Snp 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha advised Rāhula to avoid beautiful signs that lead to lust and instead meditate on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato"><b>unattractive</b></a> (asubha) to gain a concentrated and well-composed mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato">Snp 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught Rāhula that by discarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to conceit</b></a> (mānānusaya), he would be able to live in peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.11/en/sujato">Snp 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a monk wanders rightly in the world when he has completely uprooted the practice of interpreting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato"><b>omens and dreams</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato">Snp 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk wanders rightly when he removes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato"><b>lust</b></a> for both human and divine sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato">Snp 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a monk wanders rightly by abandoning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato"><b>slander and anger</b></a> and remaining free from both compliance and opposition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato">Snp 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha declared that a monk wanders rightly when he abandons both the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato"><b>pleasant and unpleasant</b></a> and remains unattached to anything in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato">Snp 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described a monk who wanders rightly as one who does not see essence in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato"><b>acquisitions</b></a> (upadhi) and has disciplined his desire for grasping? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato">Snp 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk wanders rightly when he is unopposed in word, mind, and deed and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato"><b>intent on Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato">Snp 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha said a monk wanders rightly when he does not become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato"><b>puffed up</b></a> if someone pays him homage, nor resentful if reviled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato">Snp 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk wanders rightly by abandoning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato"><b>greed and existence</b></a>, thereby becoming one who has crossed over doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato">Snp 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a monk wanders rightly when he understands his own suitability and does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato"><b>harm anyone</b></a> in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato">Snp 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that a monk wanders rightly when he has no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendencies</b></a> (anusaya) and has uprooted all unwholesome roots? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato">Snp 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk wanders rightly when he has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato"><b>destroyed the taints</b></a> (āsava), abandoned conceit, and surpassed the path of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato">Snp 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha said a monk wanders rightly when he is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato"><b>person of faith</b></a> who has heard the Dhamma and does not follow factions among those who are partisan? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato">Snp 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described the monk who wanders rightly as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato"><b>conqueror</b></a> who is pure, has removed the veil of ignorance, and is skilled in the knowledge of the cessation of volitional activities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.13/en/sujato">Snp 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the lay follower Dhammika approached the Buddha at Jetavana to ask about the specific conduct that makes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>disciple excellent</b></a>, whether they are a monk or a layperson? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the lay follower Dhammika praised the Buddha as the one who knows the destination and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>ultimate goal</b></a> for the entire world, including the devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Dhammika, even the great <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>Naga king Erāvaṇa</b></a> sought out the Buddha after hearing of his victory and departed satisfied with the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the lay follower Dhammika noted that Kuvera, the king also known as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>Vessavaṇa</b></a>, consulted the Buddha about the Dhamma and left feeling gratified? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the lay follower Dhammika claimed that no rival debaters, such as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>Ajivikas or Niganthas</b></a>, could ever surpass the Buddha in wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised monks that they should not wander into a village at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>wrong time</b></a> (afternoon or night), as those who do so often fall into worldly entanglements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to subdue their desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>sensory objects</b></a>—such as forms, sounds, and scents—before entering a village to collect their morning meal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk, after receiving his alms, should return to sit alone in a secluded place and remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>internally contemplative</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that when a monk converses with others, he should only discuss the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>sublime Dhamma</b></a> and never engage in malicious speech or finding fault? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a wise monk who remains unattached to his food, lodging, and robes to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>drop of water</b></a> that does not stick to a lotus leaf? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha acknowledged to Dhammika that it is not possible for a layperson living in a household with possessions to fully touch the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>monastic Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught laypeople that they should neither <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>kill any living being</b></a> nor cause others to kill, showing restraint toward all creatures in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his lay followers to avoid taking anything <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>not given</b></a> and to refrain from encouraging or approving of theft by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a wise person should avoid <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>unchastity</b></a> as if it were a pit of glowing coals, or at the very least, never violate another person's spouse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a layperson should not speak <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>falsehoods</b></a> in any setting, whether they are in a public assembly or a private gathering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned laypeople against indulging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>intoxicating drinks</b></a> because such consumption leads to madness and causes foolish people to commit many evil deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>eight-factored Uposatha</b></a> as a special practice he proclaimed for laypeople to bring an end to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that during the Uposatha observance, a layperson should refrain from eating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>food at night</b></a> or at any "wrong time" outside of the morning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as part of the Uposatha practice, the Buddha taught that laypeople should refrain from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>wearing garlands</b></a> or using perfumes and should sleep on a simple mat on the ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended observing the Uposatha on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>fourteenth, fifteenth, and eighth</b></a> days of the lunar fortnight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged laypeople to share food and drink with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>Sangha of monks</b></a> with a clear and confident mind after successfully completing their Uposatha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a diligent layperson should support their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>mother and father</b></a> in accordance with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised laypeople to engage only in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>righteous trade</b></a> as part of their virtuous life as a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that a householder who follows these moral practices diligently will be reborn among the devas called the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>Self-Luminous</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Dhammika, the Buddha's Dhamma is described as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato"><b>subtle and pleasant</b></a>, a well-proclaimed teaching that all the gathered monks and laypeople were eager to hear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.14/en/sujato">Snp 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda narrated how the Buddha-to-be renounced his life as a householder because he saw it as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato"><b>crowded and a place of dust</b></a>, whereas he viewed the life of renunciation as being like the open air? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato">Snp 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda described the Buddha-to-be, after his renunciation, as one who completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato"><b>avoided bodily misconduct</b></a> and purified his livelihood by giving up verbal misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato">Snp 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda recounted how the Buddha-to-be entered the Magadhan capital of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato"><b>Giribbaja in Rajagaha</b></a> to collect alms while bearing the distinguished marks of a great man? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato">Snp 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Bimbisāra, while standing in his palace, observed the Buddha-to-be and noted that he was tall, pure, and possessed of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato"><b>mindful behavior</b></a>, looking only a yoke-length ahead as he walked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato">Snp 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Bimbisāra suspected the Buddha-to-be was not from a low-class family due to his appearance and commanded his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato"><b>royal messengers</b></a> to follow the monk to see where he would stay? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato">Snp 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda described the Buddha-to-be as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato"><b>guarded at the sense doors</b></a> and well-restrained while he mindfully filled his alms bowl from house to house? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato">Snp 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a royal messenger reported to King Bimbisāra that the Buddha-to-be was staying at Mount Paṇḍava, where he sat like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato"><b>lion in a mountain cave</b></a> or a tiger among bulls? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato">Snp 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Bimbisāra personally visited the Buddha-to-be at Mount Paṇḍava and offered him <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato"><b>wealth and enjoyments</b></a>, even inviting him to lead a wing of the army because of his noble appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato">Snp 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha (as the Bodhisatta) informed King Bimbisāra that he was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato"><b>Sun-kinsman</b></a> (Ādiccā) by clan and a Sākiyan by birth, originating from a wealthy and heroic people near the Himalayas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato">Snp 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha (as the Bodhisatta) explained to King Bimbisāra that he did not renounce his home out of a desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a>, but rather because he had seen the peril inherent in them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato">Snp 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha (as the Bodhisatta) told King Bimbisāra that his mind found delight in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato"><b>striving</b></a> (padhāna) because he recognized renunciation as a state of safety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato">Snp 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha (as the Bodhisatta) clarified to King Bimbisāra that his lineage was from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato"><b>Kosala country</b></a>, and that he had no interest in worldly desires when he left his family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.1/en/sujato">Snp 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was striving for the sake of attaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>security from bondage</b></a> by the banks of the river Nerañjarā when Mara approached him with words of false compassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara attempted to discourage the Buddha from his spiritual exertion by telling him that he was emaciated and near death, urging him to live and perform <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>meritorious deeds</b></a> like the fire sacrifice instead? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha responded to Mara's temptations by stating that he had no need for even the smallest amount of merit, as such <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>merit</b></a> is only sought by those whom Mara can influence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared to Mara that he possessed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>faith, energy, and wisdom</b></a>, which sustained him even as his physical body was wasting away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that as his blood dried up and his flesh wasted away, his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>mind became more serene</b></a> and his mindfulness, wisdom, and concentration became more firmly established? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> as the first army of Mara, which a spiritual seeker must overcome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the second, third, and fourth armies of Mara consist of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>discontent, hunger and thirst, and craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> as the fifth army of Mara and fear as the sixth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the seventh and eighth armies of Mara as being composed of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>doubt</b></a> and the combination of contempt and obstinacy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that Mara's ninth army consists of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>gain, honor, and fame</b></a> that is wrongly acquired? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the tenth army of Mara as the tendency for one to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>exalt oneself</b></a> while disparaging others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha expressed his resolve by stating that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>death in battle</b></a> against Mara would be better for him than living on in defeat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha vowed to smash Mara's army with wisdom, comparing the act to breaking an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>unbaked clay pot</b></a> with a stone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha intended to wander from kingdom to kingdom training many disciples who, being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>diligent and resolute</b></a>, would reach the state where there is no more sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara admitted he had followed the Buddha step by step for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>seven years</b></a> but failed to find any opening or lapse in the mindful and Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara compared his failed attempt to find a weakness in the Buddha to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato"><b>crow</b></a> that pecks at a stone looking for fat, only to fly away disappointed when it finds none? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.2/en/sujato">Snp 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that speech is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken</b></a>, blameless, and beyond criticism by the wise only when it possesses four specific factors: it is well-spoken, righteous, pleasing, and true? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato">Snp 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for speech to be considered truly excellent, a person should speak only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a> and never what is contrary to the Dhamma (adhamma)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato">Snp 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified speaking only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato"><b>pleasing words</b></a>—and avoiding what is unpleasing—as the third essential factor of well-spoken speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato">Snp 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person must only speak what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato"><b>true</b></a> and never utter falsehoods for their words to be recognized as well-spoken by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato">Snp 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that the virtuous consider <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken speech</b></a> to be the highest, provided it is also righteous, pleasing, and truthful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato">Snp 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa, in a verse of praise, explained that well-spoken speech is that which does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato"><b>torment oneself</b></a> nor cause harm to other people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato">Snp 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa taught that one should speak <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato"><b>pleasing speech</b></a> that is welcomed by others, especially words that do not bring along evil states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato">Snp 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa described truth as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato"><b>immortal word</b></a> and noted that this has been considered the eternal law since ancient times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato">Snp 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa declared that the virtuous are firmly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato"><b>established in truth</b></a>, in the ultimate meaning, and in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato">Snp 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa praised the speech of the Buddha as the best of all words because it leads to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato"><b>security and Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato">Snp 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Vaṅgīsa highlighted that the Buddha’s speech is supreme specifically because it is aimed at making an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato"><b>end to suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato">Snp 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught his monks that speech possessing the four qualities of being well-spoken, righteous, pleasing, and true is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato"><b>beyond reproach</b></a> by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.3/en/sujato">Snp 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned the monk Kokālika that harboring ill will toward the noble disciples Sāriputta and Moggallāna was dangerous, advising him to instead <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>inspire confidence</b></a> in them because they were of an amiable nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the Buddha's repeated warnings, the monk Kokālika insisted three times that Sāriputta and Moggallāna were possessed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>evil desires</b></a>, leading to a disastrous physical and spiritual outcome for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after Kokālika ignored the Buddha’s advice and left his presence, his entire body became covered in boils that initially appeared as small as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>mustard seeds</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the rapid progression of Kokālika’s illness, where his boils grew from the size of mustard seeds to the size of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>mung beans</b></a> and eventually much larger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sources describe how Kokālika’s boils continued to swell until they reached the size of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>jujube stones</b></a> and then the size of the jujube fruit itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha’s account, Kokālika’s boils grew to the size of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>myrobalan fruits</b></a> before reaching the size of unripe wood-apples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the final stage of Kokālika’s physical affliction involved his boils growing to the size of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>bael fruits</b></a>, at which point they burst open, discharging pus and blood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that after Kokālika died from his illness, he was reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>Paduma hell</b></a> because of the hatred he harbored in his heart toward Sāriputta and Moggallāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Brahmā Sahampati visited the Buddha in the middle of the night to report that Kokālika had passed away and had indeed been reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>Paduma hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked about the lifespan of a being in the Paduma hell, the Buddha explained that it is so long it cannot be easily calculated in terms of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>hundreds of thousands</b></a> of years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>Kosalan cartload</b></a> containing twenty khārikas of sesame seeds to help explain the immense duration of life in the lowest realms of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if a person were to remove a single sesame seed from a massive cartload only once every <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>hundred years</b></a>, the cart would be emptied before a single term in the Abbuda hell would end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a sequence of hells where the lifespan in each is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>twenty times</b></a> longer than the one preceding it, starting from the Abbuda hell and moving through Nirabbuda, Ababa, and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>Paduma hell</b></a> as the realm where Kokālika was reborn, which represents a duration of time vastly longer than the preceding Kumuda, Sogandhika, Uppalaka, and Puṇḍarīka hells? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that every person is born with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>ax in their mouth</b></a>, a metaphor for the tongue with which a fool cuts himself by speaking badly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that one who praises those who deserve blame, or blames those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>deserve praise</b></a>, accumulates "bad luck" with their mouth and will find no happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered harboring ill will toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>Well-Gone Ones</b></a> (sugatas) to be a far greater misfortune than losing all one's wealth and oneself in a game of dice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who slanders noble ones by directing evil speech and thoughts toward them is destined for a hellish term lasting for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>countless nirabbudas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>speaker of falsehoods</b></a>, or one who denies a deed they have actually done, both face the same miserable fate in the next world as people of "base actions"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a person offends an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>innocent man</b></a> who is pure and flawless, that evil will return to the fool like fine dust thrown against the wind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an individual attached to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>quality of greed</b></a> as being likely to disparage others with their speech, being faithless, stingy, and given to backbiting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a person who slanders others and performs evil deeds as a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>destroyer of growth</b></a>" (bhūnahu) and a person of ill-birth who should not speak much lest they face hellish consequences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that those who engage in many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>misdeeds</b></a> and revile the virtuous will eventually fall into a "precipice" for a very long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that no person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>kamma</b></a> is ever lost, and every doer will eventually meet the results of their own actions, seeing the pain they have caused in the next world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a hellish punishment where a wrongdoer is struck with iron stakes and forced to eat <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>glowing iron balls</b></a> that resemble fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of those in hell who are unable to find refuge and are forced to lie on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>spread-out embers</b></a> or enter into a blazing mass of fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a terrifying realm where beings are caught in a net, beaten with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>iron hammers</b></a>, and cast into a blinding darkness as thick as mist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>iron cauldron</b></a> where wrongdoers are cooked for a long time, bobbing up and down in the midst of blazing fires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the misery of being cooked in a mixture of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>pus and blood</b></a>, where the wrongdoer suffers wherever they happen to stay within that realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a hellish forest of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>sharp-edged leaves</b></a> (asipattavana) where beings enter and have their limbs mangled and cut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that in hell, scavenger animals like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>black and spotted dogs</b></a>, jackals, and flocks of crows devour those who are weeping and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that since life in the Paduma hell is so long and painful, one should always <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato"><b>guard speech and mind</b></a> toward those who are pure and virtuous? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.10/en/sujato">Snp 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sage Asita, during his midday rest, witnessed the gods of the Thirty-Three heaven rejoicing and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>tossing their garments</b></a> in celebration of a monumental event? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the gods explained to the sage Asita that their joy was due to the birth of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>peerless Bodhisatta</b></a> in the Sakyan village of Lumbini, born for the welfare and happiness of the human world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the gods described the newborn Bodhisatta to Asita as the "bull among men" who would eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>roar like a lion</b></a> and set the wheel of Dhamma in motion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon hearing of the birth, the sage Asita immediately visited King Suddhodana’s palace and asked the Sakyans where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>young prince</b></a> was, as he desired to see him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Sakyans showed Asita the child, the sage observed that the prince was as brilliant as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>burnished gold</b></a> and as radiant as the sun emerging from clouds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the sage Asita looked at the child, the gods held a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>thousand-rimmed parasol</b></a> in the air above him, though the holders of the parasol remained invisible to human eyes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sage Asita, who was an expert in the marks of a Great Man, joyfully recognized the infant as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>best of bipeds</b></a> and a supreme being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sage Asita began to weep after seeing the child, which caused the Sakyans to worry that some <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>impending danger</b></a> might befall the young prince? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Asita reassured the Sakyans that he was not weeping for the child, but for himself, because he realized he would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>pass away</b></a> before he could hear the Dhamma of the future Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sage Asita predicted the child would reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>summit of enlightenment</b></a> and turn the wheel of Dhamma out of compassion for the many? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that out of compassion for his nephew Nālaka, the sage Asita urged him to go and lead a holy life under the future Buddha as soon as he heard the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>shout of "Buddha"</b></a> in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nālaka, after being instructed by Asita, waited for the Buddha’s appearance while living with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>guarded senses</b></a> and accumulating merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Nālaka finally met the Buddha, he asked him to explain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>supreme state of sagehood</b></a> and the practice of an ascetic who seeks alms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Nālaka that the practice of sagehood is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>difficult to endure</b></a> and hard to achieve, requiring one to be firm and strong? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a sage should maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> when encountering both abuse and reverence while walking through a village? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that various temptations, like "tongues of fire," arise in the world, and specifically cautioned the sage not to let <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>women</b></a> seduce or allure him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the principle of universal compassion, stating that one should not kill or cause others to kill by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>comparing others to oneself</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a sage as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>moderate in eating</b></a> and has few wishes, having abandoned the greed and desire that typical people are attached to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that after collecting alms, a sage should go to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>edge of the forest</b></a> and sit at the foot of a tree to practice meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a sage should not be eager for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>invitations</b></a> from the village and should instead return to the forest to find delight in solitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a sage entering a village should not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>prattle</b></a> or engage in idle talk, but should instead wander in search of food with focused intent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a sage should be indifferent to results, thinking "it is good" if they receive food and "<b>it is well</b>" if they do not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that a sage wandering with an almsbowl should appear to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>mute</b></a>, even if they are not, and should never despise a small gift or the person giving it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the practice of sagehood to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>razor's edge</b></a>, requiring the sage to press the tongue against the palate and be restrained in stomach and speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a person finds delight in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>living alone</b></a> and practices solitude, they will "shine in the ten directions" as a true ascetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of water to describe wisdom, noting that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>small creeks</b></a> flow noisily while the great ocean moves in silence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a fool to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato"><b>half-filled jar</b></a> and a wise man to a full lake, illustrating that those who are full of wisdom remain calm and quiet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.11/en/sujato">Snp 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha delivered the discourse on the contemplation of dyads while sitting in the open air at the Eastern Park, surrounded by the Sangha on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>full-moon night</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that hearing wholesome and noble teachings is intended to lead to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>knowledge of dyads</b></a> exactly as they are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the first dyad for contemplation as knowing "This is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a>" and "This is the origin of suffering"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the second part of the first dyad involves contemplating "This is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a>" and the path leading to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk practicing the contemplation of dyads with diligence can expect either final knowledge in this life or the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>non-returning</b></a> (anāgāmitā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that those who do not understand suffering and the path to its cessation are far from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any suffering that arises in the world is caused by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>acquisitions</b></a> (upadhi), and without these acquisitions, suffering cannot occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a fool, being ignorant, creates acquisitions and thus encounters <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>suffering again and again</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that all suffering arises due to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> (avijjā), and with the total fading away and cessation of ignorance, suffering ceases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to ignorance as a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>great delusion</b></a>" that causes beings to wander in saṃsāra for a very long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings who have attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge</b></a> (vijjā) and understand the end of rebirth do not go to any further existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that all suffering originates from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>volitional formations</b></a> (saṅkhāra), and with their cessation, there is no more suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that suffering ends by realizing the danger in volitional formations and reaching the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>calming of all formations</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that wise ones who have overcome the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>yoke of Māra</b></a> through right knowledge do not return to future rebirths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> (viññāṇa) as a condition for the arising of suffering, and taught that its total cessation brings suffering to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk whose consciousness has subsided and who is free from hunger as being "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>fully extinguished</b></a>" (parinibbuto)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that suffering arises due to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> (phassa), and those overwhelmed by contact follow the stream of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who have fully understood contact and find delight in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>subsiding of contact</b></a> are liberated from the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that whether a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> (vedanā) is pleasant, painful, or neutral, it should be seen as suffering, deceptive, and perishable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk becomes extinguished by seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>passing away</b></a> of feelings and reaching the end of all feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> (taṇhā) as the "companion" of a person that keeps them wandering in saṃsāra and unable to transcend existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a monk should wander mindfully, being free from craving and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> (anādāno), after realizing that craving is the origin of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a> (bhava) results from clinging, and for one who has come into existence, suffering and death are inevitable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described wise people as those who do not return to rebirth because they have realized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>destruction of birth</b></a> through the ending of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>exertion</b></a> (ārambha) or undertakings as a condition for suffering, and taught that its cessation prevents suffering's arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a monk who has cut off the craving for existence and whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>mind is peaceful</b></a>, the cycle of birth is finished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all suffering arises from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>nutriment</b></a> (āhāra), and that by fully understanding and becoming independent of all nutriment, one reaches spiritual health? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who stands on the Dhamma and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>exhausted the taints</b></a> (āsava) does not fall into any worldly classification or reckoning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that suffering arises from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>perturbation</b></a> (iñjita), and that by relinquishing agitation and suppressing volitional formations, one becomes unperturbed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a dyad stating that one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>dependent</b></a> wavers, while one who is independent and without clinging does not waver? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged monks to recognize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>great danger</b></a> in dependencies and to wander mindfully without clinging to any support? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while formless states are more peaceful than form states, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>cessation</b></a> is even more peaceful than formless states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that those who do not understand cessation are destined to return to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>repeated rebirth</b></a> in either the form or formless realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the world with its gods views as truth that which the Noble ones, through right wisdom, have seen to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>false</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the world incorrectly perceives an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>atman or self</b></a> in what is actually non-self, remaining stuck in name-and-form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Nibbāna as having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>non-deceptive nature</b></a> (amosadhamma), a truth recognized as truth by the Noble ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that what the rest of the world considers <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>pleasure</b></a> (such as forms, sounds, and tastes), the Noble ones see as suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>cessation of identity</b></a> (sakkāya) is viewed as pleasure by the Noble ones, even though this view opposes the entire world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that the Dhamma is difficult to understand for those blinded by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato"><b>darkness of ignorance</b></a> and overwhelmed by the passion for existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.12/en/sujato">Snp 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a person’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.1/en/sujato"><b>sensual desires</b></a> are lost after they have become intensely attached to them, that person suffers as if they had been pierced by a dart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.1/en/sujato">Snp 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who mindfully avoids sensual pleasures to someone who avoids treading on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.1/en/sujato"><b>head of a snake</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.1/en/sujato">Snp 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is greedy for possessions like land, gold, cattle, and servants will be overwhelmed by troubles, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.1/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> will follow them like water entering a broken boat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.1/en/sujato">Snp 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one should always be mindful and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.1/en/sujato"><b>abandon sensual pleasures</b></a> to cross the flood of existence, just as one bails water out of a boat to reach the far shore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.1/en/sujato">Snp 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is attached to the "cave" of the body and steeped in delusion as being far from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a>, noting that sensual pleasures in the world are not easily abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato">Snp 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that those bound to the sweetness of existence find it difficult to be released, as they are constantly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato"><b>longing</b></a> for past pleasures or obsessing over future ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato">Snp 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that people who are greedy for sensual pleasures often lament when they face death, anxiously crying out, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato"><b>What will we become</b></a> after we pass away from here?" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato">Snp 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato"><b>fish in a dried-up stream</b></a> to describe how people struggle and tremble when they are attached to their belongings and existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato">Snp 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person should practice being "without mine-making" (amamo) and should not form <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato"><b>attachments to existence</b></a> after seeing the suffering of those who cling to things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato">Snp 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a wise person should remove desire for "both ends" (the past and the future) and, by fully understanding contact, remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato"><b>undefiled</b></a> by what they see or hear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato">Snp 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a sage who has understood perception and crossed the flood is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato"><b>smeared by possessions</b></a> and does not long for this world or the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato">Snp 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that life is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato"><b>short</b></a>, and therefore one should learn to recognize what is harmful in the world and never act wrongly because of it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.2/en/sujato">Snp 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a sage does not enter into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato"><b>disputes</b></a> that have arisen, regardless of whether others speak with a corrupt mind or a truthful one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato">Snp 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned how a person who is led by their own desires and preferences could ever overcome their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato"><b>own views</b></a>, especially if they hold their own opinions to be perfect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato">Snp 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that experts call it an "un-Noble quality" when a person praises their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato"><b>virtuous conduct</b></a> and vows to others without being asked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato">Snp 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a "Noble quality" as being a monk who is peaceful and fully extinguished, who does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato"><b>not boast</b></a> of his virtues and has no arrogance anywhere in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato">Snp 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who relies on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato"><b>constructed and prejudiced</b></a> teachings for the sake of some perceived personal benefit only finds an unstable peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato">Snp 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is not easy to transcend <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato"><b>entrenched views</b></a> once a person has judged among various teachings and adopted one as their own? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato">Snp 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the "Cleansed One" (dhona) has no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato"><b>constructed view</b></a> regarding various existences, having successfully abandoned both illusion and conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato">Snp 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who is "independent" (anūpaya) cannot be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato"><b>judged or spoken of</b></a> because they have no "self" or "non-self" to cling to and have discarded all views in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.3/en/sujato">Snp 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed how people who live entrenched in their own views often argue that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>this alone is truth</b></a>, yet these very disputes lead to little peace and bring about both praise and blame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised against engaging in intellectual arguments because there are only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>two fruits of dispute</b></a>—praise and blame—and that one should instead seek the safe ground where no such disputes exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who relies on external rules and vows for purity to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>traveler far from home</b></a>, who trembles with fear and anxiety if they happen to fail in the performance of their rituals or lose their standing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a sage should abandon all attachment to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>virtue and vows</b></a> as a primary means of purification, choosing instead to remain detached and peaceful without actively seeking either purity or impurity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when people claim their own path is perfect while dismissing another person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>teaching as deficient</b></a>, they are merely arguing based on their own personal conventions and subjective preferences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha argued that if a spiritual path were truly "low" simply because of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>rival’s disparagement</b></a>, then no teaching at all could ever be considered superior, as every sect speaks poorly of the doctrines held by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that people often praise their own paths as the ultimate truth because they consider <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>purity to be personal</b></a>, believing that spiritual validation is found only within their own specific group or tradition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a true brahmin—an enlightened one—is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>led by others</b></a> or dependent on their opinions, having judged the various teachings and moved beyond the need to see any single doctrine as "the best"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned those who claim to have found <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>purity through vision</b></a>—using the phrase "I know, I see"—by pointing out that if they still rely on something external for their status, they haven't truly transcended craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even when a person perceives things, they are only seeing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a>, and the wise do not believe that true spiritual purity can be achieved merely by seeing such external or internal phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>dogmatic person</b></a> is very difficult to discipline because they are guided by preconceived views and insist that whatever they currently depend upon is the only source of "the beautiful"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a sage does not enter into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>mental reckonings</b></a> or follow specific dogmas, but instead understands various common conventions and remains equanimous toward them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a sage as someone who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>unbound the knots</b></a> of the world and remains neutral, refusing to take sides or join factions even when surrounded by people who are embroiled in disputes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a wise person is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>equanimous onlooker</b></a> among the restless, as they do not grasp at the idea that they must "gain" anything from the disputes or conventions of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a wise person is one who abandons <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>former taints</b></a> and does not create new ones, remaining completely freed from the influence of preconceived views and self-censure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized an emancipated sage as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato"><b>laid down the burden</b></a>, being detached from all things seen, heard, or sensed, and no longer yearning for any form of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.13/en/sujato">Snp 4.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should completely uproot the root of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>conceptual proliferation</b></a>, specifically the notion "I am," and should constantly train to discipline any craving that arises within? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a practitioner should not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>conceptualize oneself</b></a> as being superior, inferior, or even equal to others, regardless of the various experiences they encounter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk should seek peace only within himself rather than looking to others, because for one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>peaceful within</b></a>, there is no "self" to be found, let alone something that is "not-self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a stable person to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>middle of the ocean</b></a>, where no wave arises and the water remains still, suggesting that a monk should remain unmoved and not create any "bulge" of pride or agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers to restrain their eyes, shut their ears against <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>village talk</b></a>, avoid greed for flavors, and refuse to claim anything in the world as "mine"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk is affected by physical contact or hardship, he should not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>lament anywhere</b></a>, nor should he long for different states of existence or tremble in the face of terrifying things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a practitioner should not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>store up</b></a> food, drink, or clothing, and should not be filled with anxiety or distress if they happen to go without such necessities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that a monk be a meditator who is not "foot-loose" or restless, avoids remorse and negligence, and chooses to dwell in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>quiet places</b></a> with minimal noise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised against excessive sleep and instead encouraged a life of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>vigilance</b></a>, urging his followers to abandon laziness, deceit, laughter, sexual intercourse, and physical ornaments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha forbade the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>occult arts</b></a>, such as interpreting dreams and physical marks, practicing astrology, interpreting animal cries, or performing fertility rites and medicine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should not tremble when criticized nor become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>puffed up</b></a> when praised, and should actively work to dispel greed, stinginess, anger, and slander? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk should not engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>buying and selling</b></a> or find fault with others, and should avoid being attached to social life or flattering people in hopes of gaining material rewards? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned against being boastful or using <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>calculated speech</b></a>, and instructed that one should not learn to be arrogant or engage in contentious, argumentative conversation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person should not be led into lying and should not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>look down</b></a> on others based on their lifestyle, wisdom, or their practice of virtue and vows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even when provoked by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato"><b>harsh words</b></a> of other recluses or ordinary people, a monk should not respond with bitterness, because those who are truly peaceful do not retaliate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.14/en/sujato">Snp 4.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha felt a profound sense of spiritual urgency and fear when he observed people struggling and quarreling with one another, comparing them to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato"><b>fish in little water</b></a> who are at odds in a world that feels coreless and shaken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato">Snp 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha realized the root of human suffering and restlessness is a hidden <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato"><b>dart in the heart</b></a>, and once this dart is pulled out, a person no longer runs in all directions seeking satisfaction or sinks into despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato">Snp 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described greed as a "great flood" and yearning as a swift "current," teaching that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato"><b>mud of sensuality</b></a> is a trap that is extremely difficult for a person to cross over? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato">Snp 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that to live in peace, one should wither up the past and let there be nothing for the future, because by not grasping at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato"><b>middle</b></a> (the present), one can move through the world with a tranquil mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato">Snp 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person is truly free from grief when they have no sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato"><b>mine-making</b></a> regarding "name-and-form" (the mind and body) and do not sorrow over what does not exist or what is lost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato">Snp 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a "peaceful" person as one who does not deviate from the truth, has relinquished all attachments, and remains an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato"><b>unshakable muni</b></a> (sage) who no longer envies anyone in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato">Snp 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a sage who has attained inner peace does not participate in social comparisons and refuses to view themselves as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato"><b>equal, inferior, or superior</b></a> to anyone else? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato">Snp 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged those seeking liberation to overcome <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato"><b>sleep, sloth, and torpor</b></a>, to avoid living with negligence, and to never stand in conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato">Snp 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a practitioner should be truthful, humble, and free from deceit or slander, always keeping their mind set on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato"><b>attaining Nibbana</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato">Snp 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one who understands the Dhamma and is free from yearning, there are no more mental constructions, and such a person sees <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato"><b>safety everywhere</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato">Snp 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who has crossed over sensual pleasures—a bond he described as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato"><b>hard to cross</b></a>—no longer grieves or worries because they have cut the stream and are without bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato">Snp 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a wise person should not be led by falsehood, should not be attached to physical form, and should live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato"><b>restrained from violence</b></a> (the rod)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.15/en/sujato">Snp 4.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta praised the Buddha as a teacher of sweet speech who had come from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>Tusita heaven</b></a> to dispel all the darkness in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta once asked the Buddha about the specific training a monk should undertake to blow away his own mental stains, just as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>smith cleans silver</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sāriputta inquired about the various terrors a monk might encounter while living in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>secluded lodgings</b></a>, such as at the root of a tree, in a cemetery, or within mountain caves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mindful monk should not fear five specific things: gadflies, stinging insects, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>crawling things</b></a>, human contact, and four-footed beasts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a practitioner should not be terrified by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>followers of other teachings</b></a>, even when witnessing many frightening things from them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk seeking the good should endure hunger, cold, and heat, and should put forth <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>firm energy</b></a> even when afflicted by various physical hardships? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should not commit theft, should not speak lies, and should touch both the trembling and the steady with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to dispel any mental turbulence, identifying such states as part of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>Dark One's faction</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that one should not fall under the power of anger or conceit, but should instead <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>uproot their very foundation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a wise person should prioritize wisdom and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>wholesome joy</b></a> to suppress the various dangers found in remote lodgings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a trainee should discipline thoughts of lamentation regarding basic needs, such as wondering where they will eat or where they will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>find a place to sleep</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that a monk should be moderate in food and clothing, and even if provoked in a village, he should not respond with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that a practitioner keep their eyes downcast, avoid wandering aimlessly, and remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>devoted to meditation</b></a> while staying highly vigilant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should be mindful and rejoice when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>admonished by others</b></a>, using the opportunity to break down any stubbornness toward his fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there are "five dusts" in the world—sights, sounds, tastes, smells, and touches—and that a mindful monk must train to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>subdue lust</b></a> for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by subduing desire and becoming one-pointed in mind, a monk who rightly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato"><b>investigates the Dhamma</b></a> can completely dispel spiritual darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.16/en/sujato">Snp 4.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the herdsman Dhaniya boasted that his rice was cooked and his cows were milked, the Buddha replied that he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>free from anger</b></a> and had completely removed the stubbornness of his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in contrast to the herdsman Dhaniya who felt secure because his hut was thatched and his fire was kindled, the Buddha declared that his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>hut was open</b></a> and the fire of his passions was extinguished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that whereas the herdsman Dhaniya took comfort in living permanently on the banks of the Mahī river with his family, the Buddha stated that he was only staying for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>single night</b></a> on those same banks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the herdsman Dhaniya described his wife as obedient and faithful with no evil heard of her, the Buddha countered by explaining that his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>liberated mind</b></a> was obedient, well-tamed, and free from any evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a well-constructed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>raft</b></a> to explain how he had already crossed the flood of worldly existence and reached the far shore, making the raft no longer necessary? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that although the herdsman Dhaniya took pride in being self-employed and supporting his family, the Buddha asserted that he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>no one’s hired servant</b></a> and wandered through the whole world with only what he had spiritually earned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the herdsman Dhaniya listed his many types of livestock, including heifers and bulls, as a sign of wealth, but the Buddha replied that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>no such cattle</b></a> existed for him at all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the herdsman Dhaniya believed his cattle were securely tied with new ropes of muñja grass that even a bull could not break, the Buddha spoke of having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>broken all bonds</b></a> of existence like a bull or an elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared his freedom from the cycle of rebirth to an elephant breaking a rotted creeper, declaring that he would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>never again</b></a> enter a mother's womb? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that immediately after the dialogue between the herdsman and the Buddha, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>great cloud</b></a> rained down and filled the lowlands and highlands, prompting Dhaniya to place his faith in the Buddha's words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the herdsman Dhaniya and his wife chose to lead the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>spiritual life</b></a> under the Buddha, whom they called the "One with Vision," in order to reach the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra the Evil One claimed that a person with sons and cattle finds delight in them, but the Buddha corrected him by stating that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>acquisitions</b></a> are actually the source of a person's sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>without acquisitions</b></a> does not sorrow, which was the direct opposite of the view held by Māra the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the herdsman Dhaniya referred to the Buddha as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>Great Sage</b></a> and requested that he become their teacher after witnessing the contrast between worldly and spiritual security? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his mind as having been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>long-cultivated</b></a> and well-tamed, which allowed him to remain unaffected by the worldly concerns that worried the herdsman Dhaniya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person with sons <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato"><b>sorrows over sons</b></a> and a person with cattle sorrows over cattle, because the very things people think bring joy are often the cause of their distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.2/en/sujato">Snp 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught one should lay down the rod against all beings and refrain from harming any of them, choosing instead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>wander alone</b></a> like a rhinoceros horn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, affection arises from socializing, and it is this very <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>affection</b></a> that leads to the arising of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who feels excessive sympathy for friends and companions becomes fettered in heart and may eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>lose the goal</b></a> of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared attachment to one’s wife and children to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>spreading bamboo</b></a> tree whose branches are entangled, suggesting that a seeker should remain unattached like a single bamboo shoot? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the wise to value freedom of movement, much like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>deer in the forest</b></a> that is not bound and goes wherever it wants for pasture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that social interactions often involve invitations and obligations regarding staying or traveling, which is why a sage seeks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>unattached freedom</b></a> by wandering alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that while there is delight and great love found in the company of children, a person should wander alone to avoid the inevitable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>loathing of separation</b></a> from the beloved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true sage as someone who is friendly toward all four directions and remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>content</b></a> with whatever comes their way, enduring troubles without fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that even some renunciants, as well as householders living in homes, are difficult to support, which is why a sage should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>unconcerned</b></a> with the children of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised seekers to shed the marks of a householder, comparing this act to a koviḷāra tree <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>shedding its leaves</b></a>, in order to cut the bonds of domestic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if one finds a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>wise companion</b></a> who is firm and lives well, one should wander with them joyfully and mindfully to overcome all troubles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that if a wise companion cannot be found, one should wander alone like a king leaving behind a conquered kingdom or like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>Mātaṅga elephant</b></a> in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha praised the value of having superior or equal friends, he maintained that if such friends are unavailable, one should eat <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>blameless food</b></a> and wander alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>gold bracelets</b></a> clashing on a single arm to illustrate how companionship can lead to conflict and noise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being with a second person leads to idle chatter and annoyance, and it is to avoid this <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> in the future that a sage chooses solitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described sensual pleasures as sweet and delightful but warned that they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>churn the mind</b></a> with their diverse forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the strands of sensual pleasure are equivalent to a calamity, a tumor, a disease, and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>dart</b></a> that brings fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to endure cold, heat, hunger, thirst, and the bites of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>gadflies and reptiles</b></a> while wandering alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a sage living in the forest to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>great elephant</b></a> that leaves the herd behind to live in solitude according to its own desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is impossible for one who delights in company to reach even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>temporary liberation</b></a> of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as one who has overcome the contortions of views, attained the path, and possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>arisen knowledge</b></a> that is not dependent on others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the solitary sage as being without greed, without deceit, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>without thirst</b></a>, and without hypocrisy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised avoiding an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>evil companion</b></a> who is established in what is wrong and intent on negligence, as they see no spiritual benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended associating with a learned, noble, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>intuitive friend</b></a> in order to understand the goal and remove one's doubts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of abstaining from games, delights, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>embellishment</b></a> of the body, choosing instead to be a speaker of truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should abandon wealth, grain, kinsmen, and sensual pleasures, leaving them all behind as if they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>ordinary things</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called sensual pleasures a bond with little satisfaction and much suffering, referring to them as a metaphorical <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>throat-cutter</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared breaking one's fetters to a fish <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>breaking a net</b></a> in the water or a fire not returning to what it has already burnt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a sage, according to the Buddha, should keep their eyes downcast, their senses guarded, and their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>mind protected</b></a> to avoid being "leaky" with defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the act of going forth as shedding householder marks like a pārichatta tree shedding its leaves and donning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>saffron robes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a seeker should not be greedy for flavors, should beg for food in order from house to house, and should have a heart <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>unbound to families</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a sage should abandon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>five hindrances</b></a> of the mind and remove all defilements to live independently? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by leaving behind pleasure, pain, joy, and dejection, a practitioner attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>purified serenity</b></a> and equanimity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true seeker as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> for the attainment of the supreme goal and possesses firm strength and vigor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphors of a lion not startled by sounds, the wind not caught in a net, and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato"><b>lotus</b></a> not smeared by water to describe the ideal solitary sage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.3/en/sujato">Snp 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at the brahmin village of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>Ekanāḷā</b></a> in Magadha during the sowing season when he encountered a farmer using five hundred plows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the brahmin Kasibhāradvāja told the Buddha that he should plow and sow in order to eat, the Buddha replied that he, too, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>plows and sows</b></a> for his sustenance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> as the seed in his metaphorical spiritual farm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>austerity</b></a> or penance to the rain that allows his spiritual crops to grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the Buddha's explanation of his spiritual practice, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> serves as the yoke and the plow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>sense of shame</b></a> as the pole of his spiritual plow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> acts as the strap that holds his spiritual plowing equipment together? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> to represent both the plowshare and the goad in his spiritual labor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the importance of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>guarded in body</b></a> and speech to protect one's spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>restrained in the stomach</b></a> regarding food is a vital component of his spiritual "farming"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>truth</b></a> as the tool he uses for "weeding" or clearing away the unnecessary from his spiritual field? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>gentleness</b></a>, or freedom from roughness, as the "release" or unyoking from his spiritual work? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>energy</b></a> to a draft-ox that carries him toward the state of security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated his spiritual effort goes forward without turning back until it reaches a place where one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>does not sorrow</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called the result of his spiritual plowing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>deathless fruit</b></a>, which leads to liberation from all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's metaphors, the brahmin Kasibhāradvāja offered him <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>milk-rice</b></a> in a large bronze bowl as a sign of respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refused to eat the milk-rice, explaining that it is not the custom of Buddhas to eat food obtained by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>reciting verses</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, as long as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a> exists, enlightened beings will reject livelihood gained through the performance of chanting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one should instead provide food and drink to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>great seer</b></a> who has destroyed all mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an enlightened person whose remorse has been stilled is the ideal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>field</b></a> for someone looking to earn spiritual merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the brahmin asked how to dispose of the rejected milk-rice, the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>no one in the world</b></a>, including gods or Maras, could properly digest it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed the brahmin to discard the milk-rice in a place with little grass or into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>water without living beings</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the milk-rice was thrown into the water, it <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>hissed and smoked</b></a> just like a plowshare that has been heated all day and then plunged into water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brahmin Kasibhāradvāja was so shocked by the miracle of the sizzling rice that his <b>hair stood on end</b> in awe? And that after witnessing the miracle, the brahmin fell at the Buddha's feet and compared the teaching to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato"><b>lamp held in the dark</b></a> so those with eyes could see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.4/en/sujato">Snp 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>lover of the Dhamma</b></a> is one who prospers, while one who hates the Dhamma is destined for downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>hater of the Dhamma</b></a> as the very first cause of decline and downfall in a person's life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person faces downfall when they find themselves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>loving the wicked</b></a> and failing to hold the virtuous dear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>disliking the good</b></a> and preferring the ways of the wicked is a significant sign of a person's spiritual and social decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who chooses to follow and take pleasure in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>wicked teachings</b></a> rather than the Dhamma is heading toward downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>fond of sleep</b></a> and laziness as traits that lead a person toward their own ruin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>fond of company</b></a> and socializing, rather than being diligent in their duties, is on the path to downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>lacking initiative</b></a> and living an idle, lazy life as a major cause of failure and decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>prone to anger</b></a> and displays a bad temper is exhibiting a clear sign of downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who is wealthy but refuses to support their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>aging parents</b></a> whose youth has passed is courting downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>deceiving</b></a> a brahmin, an ascetic, or any other mendicant with lies is a major factor in a person's decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a man who has much wealth and property but eats <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>delicious food</b></a> all alone without sharing is heading toward downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>proud of birth</b></a> and using one's status to look down on others is a cause of ruin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a man who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>proud of wealth</b></a> and lineage, and consequently despises his own relatives, is inviting downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>despising his own kinsmen</b></a> out of arrogance is displaying a trait that leads to total decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>womanizer</b></a>, a drunkard, or a gambler as habits that cause a person to waste their gains and fall into ruin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a man who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>unfaithful</b></a> to his own wife and is seen with prostitutes is on the path to downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who is seen with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>wives of others</b></a> commits a deed that leads directly to their own decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that an old man who marries a young girl and cannot sleep due to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>jealousy</b></a> over her is experiencing a cause of downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that placing a woman or man who is a drunkard and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>spendthrift</b></a> in a position of authority and wealth is a cause of downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person born into a noble family who has little wealth but a great <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato"><b>craving for power</b></a> and desires a kingdom as someone heading for downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.6/en/sujato">Snp 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at Anāthapiṇḍika’s Park in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>Jeta’s Grove</b></a> when he approached the house of the brahmin Aggikabhāradvāja during a fire sacrifice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the brahmin Aggikabhāradvāja saw the Buddha approaching, he initially insulted him by calling him a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>shaveling</b></a> and a menial outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in response to being called an outcast, the Buddha asked the brahmin if he truly knew what an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>outcast</b></a> is or the qualities that make one so? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>angry and resentful</b></a>, possessing wicked and hypocritical traits, should be recognized as an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, someone who harms living beings and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>no compassion</b></a> for life is considered an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>oppressor</b></a> who besieges and destroys villages or towns as a person to be known as an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any person who takes through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>theft</b></a> what belongs to others, whether in a settlement or a forest, is an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized anyone who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>denies a debt</b></a> and flees when pressured to pay, claiming they owe nothing, as an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who kills or robs a traveler on the road for even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>small trifle</b></a> as an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a man who tells a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>lie</b></a> when questioned as a witness, whether for his own sake or for another's, is an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified someone who commits misconduct with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>wives of relatives</b></a> or friends, whether by force or by mutual affection, as an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who is wealthy but does <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>not support</b></a> their aging parents when they are past their youth is to be known as an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that anyone who strikes or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>verbally abuses</b></a> their mother, father, brother, sister, or mother-in-law is an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who, when asked for beneficial advice, instead <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>counsels harm</b></a> and speaks covertly is an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described someone who performs an evil deed and then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>conceals it</b></a> so others won't know as an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who receives high-quality hospitality at another's house but does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>honor their guest</b></a> in return when they visit as an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that anyone who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>deceives</b></a> a brahmin, an ascetic, or a mendicant with falsehoods is an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>insults</b></a> a brahmin or ascetic when they arrive for a meal and refuses to give them food is an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that someone who exalts themselves and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>disparages others</b></a> out of their own conceit is an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized those who are irritating, stingy, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>unafraid of wrongdoing</b></a> as outcasts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that anyone who reviles the Buddha, his disciples, or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>renunciant wanderers</b></a> is to be considered an outcast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called the person who falsely claims to be an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> when they are not the "lowest of outcasts" and a thief of the universe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha famously declared that one does not become an outcast or a brahmin by birth, but only through one's own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>actions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cited the example of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>Mātaṅga</b></a>, the son of an outcast "dog-eater," who achieved the highest possible fame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that many nobles and brahmins came to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>serve</b></a> Mātaṅga because of his spiritual purity, even though he was of low birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, Mātaṅga attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>Brahma world</b></a> after letting go of sensual passion, showing that his birth did not prevent his spiritual attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that brahmins born into families of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>Vedic scholars</b></a> are frequently found committing evil deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that high-caste birth does not prevent someone from being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato"><b>censured</b></a> in this life or falling into a bad destination in the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp1.7/en/sujato">Snp 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha addressed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>spirits</b></a> gathered from the earth and sky, wishing them happiness and urging them to listen closely to his words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called upon all spirits to be kind to the human race, specifically because humans bring them <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>tributes</b></a> day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared there is no treasure found in this world or the heavens that is equal to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state he attained as being characterized by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>extinction and dispassion</b></a>, a deathless and excellent state that has no equal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised a pure concentration called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>immediate concentration</b></a>, noting that no other state of focus is equal to it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>eight persons</b></a> (forming four pairs) who are praised by the good as the disciples of the Sugata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that gifts given to his disciples, the Sangha, yield <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>great fruit</b></a> and spiritual benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described those well-employed in Gotama’s teaching as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>free from desire</b></a> and having attained the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said those who have reached the goal enjoy <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a>, a state of cooling and peace, having gained it for free? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a "superior person" who sees the Noble Truths to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>stone pillar</b></a> fixed in the earth that cannot be shaken by winds from the four quarters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those who clearly understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>Noble Truths</b></a>, even if they become negligent, will never take an eighth birth in saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that upon the achievement of vision (stream-entry), a person abandons three things: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>identity view</b></a>, doubt, and attachment to rituals and vows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person who has attained vision is permanently freed from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>four planes of misery</b></a> (the lower realms)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared it is impossible for a person who has seen the path to hide any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>evil deed</b></a> they might commit by body, speech, or mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the excellent Dhamma he taught to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>forest grove</b></a> in full blossom during the first month of summer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described as the "knower of the excellent" and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>giver of the excellent</b></a> who brought the unsurpassed Dhamma to beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the wise, whose old kamma is exhausted and who have no desire for future birth, go out just like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>lamp</b></a> when the oil and wick are finished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to those whose "old seed" of kamma is destroyed and have no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>desire for growth</b></a> in future existence as "the wise" (dhīrā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha invited all assembled spirits to join in paying homage to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>Buddha</b></a>, who is revered by both gods and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha invited the gathered spirits to pay homage to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a>, the teaching that leads to safety and well-being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha invited the spirits to pay homage to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>Sangha</b></a>, the community of noble disciples, to ensure their own well-being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato"><b>truth</b></a> of the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha is the source of true happiness and security? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.1/en/sujato">Snp 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Kassapa Buddha taught that "raw stench" is not the consumption of meat, fish, or grain, but rather the commission of immoral acts like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato"><b>killing and stealing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato">Snp 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Kassapa Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato"><b>lying and fraud</b></a> as the true "raw stench" that defiles a person, rather than the type of food they choose to eat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato">Snp 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Kassapa Buddha explained that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained in desires</b></a> and greedy for tastes constitutes a greater spiritual impurity than any physical meal provided by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato">Snp 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Kassapa Buddha, individuals who hold <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato"><b>nihilistic views</b></a> and follow a crooked path are characterized as having a "raw stench" regardless of their diet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato">Snp 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Kassapa Buddha taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato"><b>merciless and arrogant</b></a>, or betraying one's friends, is what truly constitutes a foul spiritual odor in a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato">Snp 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Kassapa Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato"><b>anger and pride</b></a>, along with envy and boastfulness, as the actual "raw stench" that one must abandon to be pure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato">Snp 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Kassapa Buddha asserted that those who are of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato"><b>bad character</b></a>, such as debt-jumpers and slanders, are the ones who truly suffer from the "raw stench" of defilement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato">Snp 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Kassapa Buddha warned that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato"><b>aggressive and disrespectful</b></a> toward other living beings fall headlong into hell, a fate caused by their conduct rather than their food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato">Snp 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Kassapa Buddha taught that external ascetic practices like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato"><b>nakedness or matted hair</b></a> cannot purify a person who has not yet overcome their internal spiritual doubts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato">Snp 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Kassapa Buddha emphasized that neither <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato"><b>sacrifices nor hymns</b></a> can cleanse a mortal who remains stuck in uncertainty and hasn't reached the state of peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato">Snp 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Kassapa Buddha described a wise and stable person as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato"><b>guarded in the senses</b></a> and stands firm in the Dhamma, remaining untainted by what is seen or heard? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato">Snp 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing Kassapa Buddha’s teaching on the true meaning of "raw stench," the Brahmin listener was so moved that he immediately requested <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato"><b>ordination</b></a> as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.2/en/sujato">Snp 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who flouts a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato"><b>sense of shame</b></a> (hiri) and claims friendship while refusing to help with possible tasks is not a true friend? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato">Snp 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that wise people recognize someone who offers <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato"><b>affectionate words</b></a> but fails to follow through with actions as being a "friend" in name only? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato">Snp 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a true friend as someone who cannot be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato"><b>alienated by others</b></a>, comparing the security of that friendship to a child resting on its parent's breast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato">Snp 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged individuals to bear the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato"><b>human burden</b></a> of responsibility and effort, as this brings about joy and the fruit of praise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato">Snp 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who drinks the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato"><b>taste of seclusion</b></a> and the taste of peace becomes free from distress and sin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato">Snp 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who drink the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato"><b>taste of joy in the Dhamma</b></a> are cleansed of evil and experience a profound inner stillness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato">Snp 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who is always <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato"><b>apprehensive of a split</b></a> in a relationship or constantly looking for flaws is not a genuine companion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato">Snp 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true friend as one upon whom another can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato"><b>depend</b></a> with total confidence, knowing they are inseparable even when others try to cause division? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.3/en/sujato">Snp 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while staying at the residence of the yakkha Sūciloma in Gaya, was tested by the yakkha who pressed his body against him to see if the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato"><b>Teacher would flinch</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato">Snp 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to the yakkha Sūciloma that although he was not afraid, he moved his body away because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato"><b>yakkha’s touch</b></a> was "evil" or unpleasant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato">Snp 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the yakkha Sūciloma that he did not see anyone in the entire world—including gods, Maras, and Brahmas—who could <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato"><b>confound his mind</b></a> or split his heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato">Snp 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that intense emotions like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato"><b>lust and hatred</b></a> are not external forces but are actually born from within one's own self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato">Snp 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the origin of aversion, delight, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato"><b>horripilation</b></a> (the hair standing on end) as being rooted in the individual's own self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato">Snp 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared mental thoughts that "toss one about" to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato"><b>children releasing a crow</b></a>, illustrating how thoughts can become erratic and difficult to control when they arise from the self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato">Snp 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato"><b>banyan tree's trunks</b></a> to describe how defilements are self-born and deeply rooted within the personality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato">Snp 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described defilements as being widely attached to sense pleasures, similar to how a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato"><b>māluvā creeper</b></a> spreads and entangles itself throughout a forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato">Snp 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that those who understand the true origin of defilements are able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato"><b>dispel them</b></a> and cross the difficult flood of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato">Snp 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once the source of inner turmoil is understood and dispelled, a practitioner will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato"><b>never again</b></a> face the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.5/en/sujato">Snp 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that living according to the Dhamma and the holy life is considered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato"><b>supreme asset</b></a> for anyone who has gone forth from the home life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato">Snp 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato"><b>talkative and aggressive</b></a>, their life becomes spiritually worse and they only succeed in increasing their own internal "dust" or defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato">Snp 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato"><b>fond of quarreling</b></a> and shrouded in delusion remains ignorant of the true Dhamma even when it is clearly explained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato">Snp 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those who harm others while being dominated by ignorance are actually following a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato"><b>path to hell</b></a> without even realizing it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato">Snp 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the fate of a corrupt monk as falling into a cycle of misery, moving from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato"><b>darkness to darkness</b></a> and from one womb to another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato">Snp 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk with a corrupt and impure character to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato"><b>cesspool</b></a> that has been filled for many years and is extremely difficult to cleanse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato">Snp 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged the community of monks to act in unity to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato"><b>expel</b></a> those who possess evil desires, evil thoughts, and evil conduct from their group? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato">Snp 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used agricultural metaphors, advising monks to blow away the "chaff" and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato"><b>rubbish</b></a> of the community—referring to those who only pretend to be true monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato">Snp 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his disciples to live only with those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato"><b>pure and mindful</b></a>, ensuring that the community remains harmonious and wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato">Snp 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when pure and mindful practitioners <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato"><b>live together in harmony</b></a>, they can successfully bring an end to all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.6/en/sujato">Snp 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how ancient brahmins were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>restrained and ascetic</b></a>, having abandoned the five cords of sensual pleasure to pursue their own spiritual goals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that brahmins of old possessed no cattle, gold, or grain, as they considered their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>recitation and study</b></a> to be their true treasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how ancient brahmins would only accept <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>food prepared with faith</b></a> when it was offered at a householder's door? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that prosperous states and kingdoms used to revere ancient brahmins by providing them with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>colorful clothes</b></a>, beds, and residences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that ancient brahmins were considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>inviolable and invincible</b></a> because they were protected by the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that ancient brahmins practiced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>celibacy for forty-eight years</b></a> in their dedicated quest for knowledge and conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that ancient brahmins did not buy their wives, but lived together in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>mutual affection</b></a> after meeting through choice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that ancient brahmins strictly restrained themselves from sexual intercourse except during the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>fertile period</b></a> of their wives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that ancient brahmins highly praised <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>virtue and patience</b></a> along with non-violence, gentleness, and austerity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even the most supreme and diligent among the ancient brahmins did not engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>sexual intercourse</b></a> even in their dreams? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how ancient brahmins would perform sacrifices using <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>ghee and oil</b></a> collected righteously, without ever killing cattle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that ancient brahmins regarded cattle as their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>supreme friends</b></a>, placing them in the same category as one's mother, father, or relatives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that ancient brahmins valued cattle specifically because they are the vital source of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>medicines</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said ancient brahmins recognized cattle as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>givers of strength</b></a>, beauty, and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that as long as brahmins lived by their ancient standards, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>people in the world</b></a> lived happily and in peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>corruption of values</b></a> occurred when brahmins began to envy the king's splendor, his wealth, and his adorned women? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that corrupt brahmins <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>composed mantras</b></a> to persuade King Okkaka to perform elaborate sacrifices so that they could gain material wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how King Okkaka performed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>horse sacrifice</b></a> and human sacrifice after being persuaded by brahmins seeking to acquire riches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that King Okkaka gave corrupt brahmins <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>adorned women</b></a>, chariots, and delightful mansions as sacrificial fees? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that as brahmins began to accumulate wealth, they became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>overcome by craving</b></a> and sought even more through corrupt means? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said corrupt brahmins eventually urged the king to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>slaughter cattle</b></a> by claiming cows were a resource for humans just like water, gold, or grain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how King Okkaka had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>hundreds of thousands</b></a> of cows slaughtered in sacrifices after being misled by corrupt brahmins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that cows are as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>gentle as sheep</b></a> and do not harm anyone with their feet or horns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha related that when the king's weapon fell upon the innocent cows, gods, ancestors, and Indra cried out that it was an act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>unrighteousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that there were only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>three diseases</b></a>—desire, hunger, and decay—before the practice of animal sacrifice began to spread? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that as a direct consequence of the slaughter of animals, the number of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>diseases increased to ninety-eight</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that when the Dhamma is destroyed, the social order breaks down and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>wives disregard their husbands</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that kshatriyas and brahmins who abandon their noble lineage for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>sake of sensual pleasures</b></a> fall under the power of desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha's explanation of the ancient Brahmin code, a group of wealthy brahmins took <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>refuge in the Triple Gem</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching was praised by listeners as being like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato"><b>turning upright what was overturned</b></a> or carrying a lamp into the darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.7/en/sujato">Snp 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the act of learning from a teacher to how a deity might <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.8/en/sujato"><b>honor Indra</b></a>, suggesting that one should deeply respect the person from whom they learn the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.8/en/sujato">Snp 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by associating with a wise and learned teacher and practicing the Dhamma accordingly, a person becomes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.8/en/sujato"><b>skillful and discerning</b></a> practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.8/en/sujato">Snp 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that serving a mean or ignorant person leads to one facing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.8/en/sujato"><b>death with doubt</b></a> still intact, having failed to understand the Dhamma in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.8/en/sujato">Snp 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as a person swept away by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.8/en/sujato"><b>swift current</b></a> cannot help others cross a river, a person who has not realized the Dhamma for themselves cannot help others attain enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.8/en/sujato">Snp 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.8/en/sujato"><b>strong boat</b></a> equipped with oars and a rudder to describe a skilled teacher who, having reached the other shore, is capable of helping many others cross the flood of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.8/en/sujato">Snp 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised seekers to associate with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.8/en/sujato"><b>good person</b></a> (sappurisa) who is wise and learned, as understanding the meaning of their teachings leads to true happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.8/en/sujato">Snp 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.9/en/sujato"><b>honoring elders</b></a> and being free from envy are essential qualities for a person who wishes to be firmly established in the Dhamma and reach the highest goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.9/en/sujato">Snp 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the importance of being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.9/en/sujato"><b>knower of the proper time</b></a> for visiting one's teachers and listening respectfully to well-spoken teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.9/en/sujato">Snp 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to be humble and set aside stubbornness, focusing instead on reflecting upon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.9/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> and self-restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.9/en/sujato">Snp 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised his followers to delight in the Dhamma and avoid <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.9/en/sujato"><b>defiling speech</b></a> or malicious talk, guided instead by truthful and well-spoken words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.9/en/sujato">Snp 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.9/en/sujato"><b>laughter and pride</b></a>, along with greed, hypocrisy, and ill-will, as unwholesome states that one must abandon to live with a steady and composed mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.9/en/sujato">Snp 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that wisdom and learning do not grow for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.9/en/sujato"><b>reckless person</b></a> who is negligent and lacks concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.9/en/sujato">Snp 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged people to "Arise and sit up," questioning what use there is in sleep for those afflicted by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.10/en/sujato"><b>arrow of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.10/en/sujato">Snp 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted his followers to train hard for peace so that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.10/en/sujato"><b>King of Death</b></a> (Mara) does not find them negligent and take them under his power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.10/en/sujato">Snp 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that individuals should strive to cross over the attachment that binds both gods and humans, warning them not to let the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.10/en/sujato"><b>opportune moment</b></a> for practice pass them by? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.10/en/sujato">Snp 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that those who let the right moment pass will eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.10/en/sujato"><b>sorrow</b></a> when they find themselves in a state of misery or the lower realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.10/en/sujato">Snp 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.10/en/sujato"><b>negligence</b></a> as "dust" and explained that continuous negligence only leads to more spiritual defilement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.10/en/sujato">Snp 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.10/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a> and knowledge, a person can pluck out the arrow of suffering from themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp2.10/en/sujato">Snp 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised the Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja not to inquire about a person’s birth, but instead to inquire about their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>conduct</b></a>, as even from low-quality wood a sacrificial fire is born? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a sage can be born into a family of low status but still be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>steadfast</b></a> and noble if they are restrained by a sense of shame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person worthy of the sacrificial cake as one who is tamed by truth, endowed with self-mastery, and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>reached the end of the Vedas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has abandoned sensual pleasures and wanders as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>homeless one</b></a> with a well-restrained self is the one to whom an offering should be made? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified those who are free from passion and have their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>senses composed</b></a> as being like the moon released from the eclipse of Rahu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who wander in the world unattached, always mindful, and having abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>possessiveness</b></a> are truly worthy of receiving a sacrificial offering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Tathagata as one who has overcome sensual pleasures, knows the end of birth and death, and is as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>cooled</b></a> as a pool of water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that a Tathagata is equal to the equal, far from the unequal, and possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>infinite wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the one who is free from deceit, pride, and greed, and who has discarded the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>stain of sorrow</b></a>, is the one who deserves the sacrificial cake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a person worthy of an offering is one who has abolished the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>dwelling of the mind</b></a> and has no graspings whatsoever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an ideal recipient as one who is concentrated and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>crossed the flood</b></a> of existence, understanding the Dhamma with supreme vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a person who has destroyed their taints and is wearing their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>last body</b></a> as the most suitable recipient for a sacrifice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a Tathagata, the taints of existence and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a> have been totally incinerated and no longer exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a "knower of the Vedas" (vedagū) as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>liberated everywhere</b></a> and is no longer attached to the things of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a person who has gone beyond bonds and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>no attachments</b></a> among those who are attached is worthy of the sacrificial cake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that understanding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> along with its "field and ground" is a quality of one who is worthy of an offering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a sage as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>seer of seclusion</b></a> who does not depend on hope and has transcended the views held by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a person who has understood both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>high and low states</b></a> of existence and has seen them extinguished is peaceful and liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who has seen the end of the destruction of birth and has completely removed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>path of passion</b></a> is pure and stainless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who does not perceive a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>self in the self</b></a>, is concentrated, and remains steady is free from agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person with no stains of delusion and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision</b></a> in all things as having reached the purity of a spirit (yakṣa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha initially refused to eat the sacrificial cake offered by the Brahmin because he does not eat food over which <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>verses have been chanted</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that it is not the practice of those with vision to accept food gained by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>chanting verses</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed the Brahmin to serve his food to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>great seer</b></a> whose taints are destroyed and whose remorse has been stilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a great seer who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>field for merit</b></a> is the appropriate person for one who is seeking merit to provide with food and drink? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized an ideal recipient of offerings as one whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>anger has vanished</b></a> and whose mind is unclouded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended offering food to a sage who is free from lust and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>removed sloth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that one should honor a sage who is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>expert in birth and death</b></a> with food and drink to make a sacrifice successful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja eventually recognized the Buddha as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>unexcelled field of merit</b></a> and an object of sacrifice for the whole world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha’s discourse, the Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja compared the Dhamma to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>lamp in the darkness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Brahmin Sundarikabhāradvāja requested <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>ordination</b></a> and the higher status of a monk after being impressed by the Buddha's words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the Buddha's guidance, Sundarikabhāradvāja eventually attained the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a>, living the holy life to its conclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>upright</b></a> and has a steady mind, without any doubt, is worthy of the sacrificial cake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>attainment of enlightenment</b></a> makes a person a "Tathagata" who is truly deserving of religious offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>liberated through the destruction of clinging</b></a> are peaceful and beyond the reach of the world’s attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that for a truly liberated person, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>objects of attachment</b></a> no longer exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>transcended views</b></a> known by others is a "seer of seclusion"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that an offering given to a person who has reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>supreme enlightenment</b></a> brings great fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a worthy recipient as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>pure and stainless</b></a>, having removed all traces of passion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that an offering is successful when one honors a sage by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato"><b>removing their frown</b></a> and saluting them with joined palms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.4/en/sujato">Snp 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed to the young man Māgha that a donor who seeks wealth righteously and provides for many people generates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>much merit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who wander the world unattached, possessing nothing, and are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>self-controlled</b></a> are the ideal recipients for timely offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified those who have severed all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>fetters and bonds</b></a> as being truly worthy of sacrificial offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described worthy recipients of offerings as those who are tamed, liberated, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>without anguish</b></a> or expectations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who have abandoned lust, hatred, and delusion, and whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>taints are destroyed</b></a>, are the ones to whom a seeker of merit should give? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those in whom <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>deceit and pride</b></a> no longer dwell are the most suitable individuals to receive a religious sacrifice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are free from greed, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>without possessiveness</b></a>, and without hope are ideal for a donor's offering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described worthy recipients as those who do not fall into cravings and have successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>crossed the flood</b></a> of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who have no craving for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>existence or non-existence</b></a> anywhere in the world are the right people to receive gifts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that those who have abandoned sensual pleasures, wander homeless, and are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>well-restrained</b></a> should receive offerings from a seeker of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared those with passion removed and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>composed senses</b></a> to the moon when it is released from the eclipse of Rahu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are peaceful, free from passion, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>without anger</b></a> are the ones in whom an offering should be placed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described worthy recipients as those who have left behind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>birth and death</b></a> entirely and have overcome all doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who wander the world as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>islands to themselves</b></a> and are liberated in every way are deserving of timely offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified those who truly know that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>this is their last birth</b></a> and that there is no more rebirth as ideal recipients for a sacrifice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a "knower of the Vedas" who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>delighted in meditation</b></a> and mindful is a person to whom one should offer a sacrificial gift? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the one who has attained supreme enlightenment as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>refuge for many</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised Māgha that while performing a sacrifice, a donor should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>gladden the mind</b></a> in all respects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person making an offering, the sacrifice serves as a basis to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>abandon ill will</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has removed passion should also cultivate a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>mind of loving-kindness</b></a> that is immeasurable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged being diligent day and night in pervading <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>all directions</b></a> with immeasurable loving-kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who offers a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>threefold successful sacrifice</b></a> to worthy recipients will be reborn in the Brahma-world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young man Māgha addressed the Buddha as a sage who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>Brahma-like</b></a> and can see the Brahma-world directly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that giving food and drink to others is a way for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>householder seeking merit</b></a> to practice sacrifice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a worthy recipient as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>straight as a shuttle</b></a> and well-restrained in their conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified those who wander without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>possessiveness or craving</b></a> as having successfully crossed the flood of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those for whom there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>destination after death</b></a> (meaning they are no longer subject to rebirth) are ideal candidates for religious gifts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>liberated everywhere</b></a> and possesses nothing is the exact type of person in whom a donor should place an offering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a sacrifice is truly successful when it is offered with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato"><b>purified mind</b></a> to those who have attained the highest states of spiritual perfection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.5/en/sujato">Snp 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person is called a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>bhikkhu</b></a> when they have attained Nibbāna by a path they have made for themselves, crossed over doubt, and abandoned both existence and non-existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>gentle person</b></a> (sorata) as one who is equanimous everywhere, mindful, does not harm anyone in the world, and is free from spiritual "protuberances" or defects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>tamed</b></a> (danta) when their faculties are developed both internally and externally throughout the whole world, and they await their time while having penetrated this and the other world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one is called a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>Buddha</b></a> because they have analyzed all eons, the entire cycle of wandering, and the passing away and rebirth of beings, becoming pure and stainless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>brahmin</b></a> as one who has expelled all evils, is stainless, well-concentrated, and has transcended the cycle of wandering while remaining independent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>samana</b></a> is one who is calmed, has abandoned both merit and evil, is dustless, and has understood this world and the next to transcend birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>one who has bathed</b></a> (nhataka) as someone who has washed away all evils both internally and externally throughout the whole world and no longer undergoes "eons" or rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>nāga</b></a> is a person who does no wrong in the world, has released all fetters and bonds, and is not attached to anything while being liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>conqueror of fields</b></a> (khettajina) as one who has analyzed and conquered all fields—divine, human, and the field of Brahma—and is freed from the root bonds of all fields? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>skillful person</b></a> (kusala) is one who has analyzed all "treasures"—divine, human, and the treasure of Brahma—and is freed from the root bonds of all treasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a> (paṇḍita) as one who has analyzed both internal and external dualities, is pure in wisdom, and has transcended both the dark and the bright? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>sage</b></a> (muni) is one who knows the Dhamma of the good and the bad both internally and externally, and has crossed the net of attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>knower of the Vedas</b></a> (vedagu) as one who has analyzed all the Vedas of recluses and brahmins, is detached from all sensations, and has transcended all knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>one who has understood</b></a> (anuvidita) is someone who has investigated the mental proliferation of name-and-form—the root of disease—and is freed from all such root bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>energetic person</b></a> (viriyavā) is one who is repelled by all evils, has transcended the suffering of hell, and is steadfast in their exertion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>thoroughbred</b></a> (ājāniya) as one whose bonds—the roots of attachment both internal and external—have been severed, making them free from all such root bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>learned person</b></a> (sottiya) is one who has heard and understood all the Dhammas in the world, is free from fault, and is liberated without questioning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>noble one</b></a> (ariya) as someone who has cut off all taints and attachments, no longer enters a womb, and has cleared the three types of perception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>one of good conduct</b></a> (caraṇavā) as a person who has attained the goal of conduct, always understands the Dhamma, and has a mind liberated from all irritation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>wanderer</b></a> (paribbājaka) is one who has avoided any action that results in suffering, has fully understood and discarded deceit, conceit, greed, and anger, and has reached the end of name-and-form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wanderer Sabhiya initially approached famous teachers like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>Pūraṇa Kassapa</b></a> and Makkhali Gosāla, but they could not answer his questions and instead showed anger and resentment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>deity</b></a> who was a former relative of Sabhiya gave him a set of questions and told him to live the holy life under whoever could answer them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sabhiya initially hesitated to visit the Buddha because the Buddha was <b>young and newly ordained</b> compared to other long-established teachers? And that when Sabhiya finally approached him, the Buddha encouraged him to ask <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>whatever he wished</b></a>, promising to put an end to his doubts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha’s answers, Sabhiya praised the Buddha for having crossed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>flood of existence</b></a> and for having no taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sabhiya addressed the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>kinsman of the sun</b></a> (ādiccabandhu) and a gentle sage who had reached the goal of the silent ways? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sabhiya declared that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>no counterpart</b></a> to the Buddha in the world of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>lion</b></a> who is without clinging and has abandoned all fear and dread? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sabhiya compared the Buddha to a beautiful <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>white lotus</b></a> that remains unsullied by the water it grows in, as the Buddha is unsullied by merit and evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha’s discipline, a former member of another sect must undergo a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>four-month probation</b></a> (parivāsa) before receiving full ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sabhiya was so impressed that he told the Buddha he was willing to undergo probation for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>four years</b></a> instead of just four months? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after receiving ordination, Sabhiya lived alone and diligent, eventually becoming one of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>Arahants</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sabhiya praised the Buddha for being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>teacher</b></a> who has overcome Māra and severed all underlying tendencies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is pure when they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>discarded attachment</b></a> and have no more "eons" to undergo? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sabhiya noted that the Buddha had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>destroyed all troubles</b></a> and was cooled, tamed, and steadfast? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of a liberated person as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>free from irritation</b></a> (paṭighā) and having a mind that is everywhere unattached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the root of disease as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>mental proliferation</b></a> (papañca) of name-and-form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a person worthy of worship is one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>transcended attachment</b></a> and the "net" of worldly things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sabhiya found that only the Buddha could explain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>sixty-three views</b></a> based on recluses' assertions and free him from the flood of doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato"><b>reached the end</b></a> of birth and death is a true recluse (samana) and a true Brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.6/en/sujato">Snp 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the life of mortals in this world is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>unpredictable</b></a> and unknown, characterized by being brief, difficult, and bound up with suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained there is no means by which those who are born can avoid dying, as reaching old age and eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>death</b></a> is the inherent nature of all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>ripe fruits</b></a>, which always face the danger of falling in the morning, to describe how mortals who are born always live in the constant fear of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the lives of mortals to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>clay vessels</b></a> made by a potter, all of which eventually end in being broken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that whether people are young or old, fools or wise, everyone eventually comes under the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>universal power</b></a> of death and has death as their final destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when people are overcome by death and pass to the other world, even a father cannot protect his son, nor can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>kinsmen</b></a> protect their relatives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that while relatives are looking on and lamenting deeply, mortals are led away one by one, much like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>cattle</b></a> being led to the slaughter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that because the world is afflicted by death and aging, the wise do not grieve, having understood the true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>way of the world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that no one attains peace of mind through weeping and grief, but instead, such actions only cause more suffering and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>physical harm</b></a> to the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that lamentation is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>useless</b></a> for the deceased, as those who have passed away are not saved or protected by the weeping of those they left behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who does not abandon sorrow only falls deeper into suffering, following the power of grief as they continue to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>lament</b></a> the dead? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that whatever people expect to happen, things often turn out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>differently</b></a>, which illustrates the profound uncertainty and separation inherent in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise and skillful person should blow away suddenly arisen grief, just as the wind blows away a tuft of cotton or water extinguishes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>burning house</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that anyone seeking their own happiness should pull out the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>dart</b></a> of lamentation, vain desire, and despair from themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a person has pulled out the dart of sorrow and becomes independent, they attain true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato"><b>peace of mind</b></a> and become sorrowless and extinguished (nibbuto)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.8/en/sujato">Snp 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a dispute arose between the young men Vāseṭṭha and Bhāradvāja regarding whether a person becomes a Brahmin by birth or by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>action</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhāradvāja argued that one is a Brahmin simply by being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>well-born</b></a> on both the mother’s and father’s side for seven generations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vāseṭṭha contended that a person is a Brahmin because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and possess good conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>grass and trees</b></a> have distinct physical characteristics that define their species, humans do not have such diverse birth-based markers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that insects, such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>moths and ants</b></a>, possess distinct physical marks of their birth, unlike the uniform physical nature of humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>four-footed animals</b></a>, both small and large, have physical differences based on their species that are not found among human beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>serpents</b></a> and long-backed snakes have physical characteristics determined by their birth, which distinguishes them from humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>fish</b></a> living in the water have birth-based physical marks that are entirely absent in the human species? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>birds</b></a> that fly through the air have distinct physical features of their species, whereas humans are physically similar to one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that humans do not differ from one another in their hair, head, ears, eyes, mouth, or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>nose</b></a> in a way that would define them as separate species? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that among humans, the distinctions made between people are merely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>designations</b></a> and not based on inherent physical birth-marks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a person who lives by the protection of cows as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>farmer</b></a> and not as a Brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who makes a living through various crafts as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>artisan</b></a> rather than a Brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that one who lives by trade is known as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>merchant</b></a> and is not defined as a Brahmin by their birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who earns a living by serving others is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>servant</b></a> and not a Brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who lives by taking what is not given is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>thief</b></a> and not a Brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who makes a living by archery and weapons is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>soldier</b></a> and not a Brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a person who lives by performing priestly duties as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>sacrificer</b></a> and not a Brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that one who rules over a village or a kingdom is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>king</b></a> and not a Brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refused to call someone a Brahmin just because they were born from a Brahmin mother, calling such a person a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>"sir-denoter"</b></a> (bhovādi) if they still have attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a true Brahmin as someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>possessionless</b></a> and unattached to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a Brahmin as one who has cut all fetters, does not tremble with fear, and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>unfettered</b></a> and detached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a true Brahmin is someone who endures abuse, beating, and imprisonment without resentment, possessing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>strength of patience</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a Brahmin as one who is without anger, is dutiful, virtuous, and has reached their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>last body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of water on a lotus leaf or a mustard seed on the tip of a needle to describe a Brahmin who does not cling to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a Brahmin as one who understands the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>destruction of suffering</b></a> right here in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a Brahmin as one with profound wisdom, who is an expert in what is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>path and the non-path</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a Brahmin does not associate closely with either <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>householders or the homeless</b></a> and wanders with few desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a Brahmin as one who has laid down the rod of violence toward all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>living beings</b></a>, neither killing nor causing others to kill? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a Brahmin as someone who is friendly among the hostile, peaceful among the violent, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>unattached</b></a> among those who cling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that in a true Brahmin, lust, hatred, pride, and contempt have fallen away like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>mustard seed</b></a> from the tip of a needle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a Brahmin as one who speaks words that are gentle, informative, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>truthful</b></a>, by which they do not offend anyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a Brahmin is one who does not take anything in the world that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>not given</b></a>, whether it be long or short, fine or coarse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a Brahmin as one who has no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>yearnings</b></a> for this world or the next, being detached and liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a Brahmin has no "whys" (doubts) because of their knowledge and has reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>plunge into the deathless</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a Brahmin has transcended the attachment to both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>merit and evil</b></a> and is sorrowless and pure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the world turns and beings proceed by means of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>action</b></a> (kamma), just as a linchpin holds a moving chariot? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha concluded that one is a Brahmin not by birth, but by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato"><b>austerity</b></a>, the holy life, restraint, and taming? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp3.9/en/sujato">Snp 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that quarrels, disputes, lamentation, and grief, along with stinginess, arise from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>dear things</b></a> that people hold onto in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the "dear things" and the greed that circulate in the world have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>desire</b></a> (chanda) as their ultimate source and origin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that desire itself arises in the world based on the way people perceive things as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>pleasant and unpleasant</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person makes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>judgments</b></a> in the world after observing the existence and nonexistence of various forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified anger, lying, and doubt as mental states that can only exist when the dualities of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>pleasant and unpleasant</b></a> are present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a person who is full of doubt should train in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>path of knowledge</b></a> after understanding the teachings proclaimed by the Ascetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the feelings of pleasant and unpleasant have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>contact</b></a> (phassa) as their necessary condition, and without contact, these feelings would not arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that contact in the world is dependent upon the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a> (nāmarūpa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that possessions and the sense of ownership are rooted in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>wish and desire</b></a> (icchā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that when wish is absent, the sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>mine-making</b></a> (mamatta) does not exist for a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a state where contacts no longer touch a person once <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> has been "disappeared" or transcended? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the disappearance of "form" as occurring in a unique state of consciousness where one is neither normally perceiving nor deranged in perception, and neither <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>without perception</b></a> nor perceiving what has disappeared? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mental classifications known as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>conceptual proliferation</b></a> (papañcasaṅkhā) have perception as their fundamental source? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that while some sages speak of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>purity of the spirit</b></a> (yakkhassa suddhi) as the ultimate goal, others teach a state of non-attachment without remainder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a sage who understands the nature of dependencies and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a> does not enter into disputes and does not go along with existence or nonexistence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.11/en/sujato">Snp 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>truth is one</b></a> and not two, and that if people truly understood this singular truth, they would not engage in disputes with one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that if a person were considered a fool simply because they did not accept another person’s teaching, then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>all people</b></a> would be fools because everyone holds views that differ from someone else's? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that there are not many diverse, eternal truths in the world, but rather people formulate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>dualistic views</b></a> of "truth" and "falsehood" based on their own perceptions and logical reasoning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that people often rely on what they have seen, heard, or their own virtuous conduct to reach a fixed conclusion, subsequently viewing others with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>contempt</b></a> and calling them "unskilled fools"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by the very same standard a person uses to label another a fool, they concurrently label themselves an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>expert</b></a>, praising their own intelligence while disparaging others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is intoxicated by their own fixed views often considers themselves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>perfectly wise</b></a> and believes their own opinion is the only one that is completely substantiated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that different sectarians are often infatuated with their own views because of a deep <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>attachment to their own perspectives</b></a>, which leads them to proclaim their own path as the only one leading to purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how proponents of various schools of thought claim that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>purity</b></a> exists only within their own system and not in the teachings of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person is firmly attached to their own specific path and labels others' teachings as impure, they inevitably bring about <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>strife</b></a> and verbal disputes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>left behind all fixed decisions</b></a> and dogmatic views does not create strife or engage in disputes in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that if simply being called "low" or "inferior" by another person actually made one a fool, then no one among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>recluses</b></a> would be considered wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that people often apply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>logic and reasoning</b></a> (takka) to their views to justify their personal version of "truth" and to dismiss others' claims as false? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was asked why various experts, each claiming to be skilled, disagree and maintain that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>only their own view</b></a> is correct while others are incomplete? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that standing on a fixed position and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>measuring others</b></a> against oneself leads a person directly into conflict and disputes in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the perception of many different "truths" in the world is not because many truths actually exist, but because of the way people <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato"><b>perceive and conceptualize</b></a> things differently based on their views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.12/en/sujato">Snp 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if purity or the removal of suffering could be achieved simply through seeing or knowledge alone, a person would still be encumbered by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato"><b>acquisitions</b></a> (upadhi)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato">Snp 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true Brahmin as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato"><b>untainted by merit</b></a> or evil, having abandoned the notion of "self" and not creating any new kammic formations in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato">Snp 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared people who abandon one teaching only to cling to another to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato"><b>monkey</b></a> that lets go of one branch only to immediately grab another, never finding true peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato">Snp 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that while many people fluctuate between "high and low" states by strictly adhering to various vows, a person of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato"><b>vast wisdom</b></a> who has understood the Dhamma does not undergo such fluctuations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato">Snp 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a sage who has become independent regarding all things seen, heard, or sensed is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato"><b>unattached</b></a> and cannot be characterized or judged by anyone in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato">Snp 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who have released the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato"><b>bond of grasping</b></a> do not formulate personal views, do not put anything forward as absolute, and do not claim "ultimate purity" for their own doctrines? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato">Snp 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the true Brahmin as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato"><b>gone beyond boundaries</b></a> and, having understood the nature of grasping, is neither attached to passion nor to the desire for dispassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.4/en/sujato">Snp 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that when a person dwells on one view as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato"><b>supreme</b></a> and considers all other perspectives in the world as "low," they are unable to overcome the cycle of disputes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato">Snp 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who are skilled in the Dhamma call it a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato"><b>bond</b></a> when a person relies on what they have seen, heard, or sensed, or on their own virtue, and uses that as a basis to look down on others as inferior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato">Snp 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a monk should not depend on things seen, heard, or sensed, nor on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato"><b>virtue and vows</b></a>, as a means to formulate fixed views or dogmas in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato">Snp 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person should not consider themselves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato"><b>equal</b></a>, inferior, or superior to others based on their own knowledge or their adherence to certain religious rules? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato">Snp 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a wise person as one who does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato"><b>take sides</b></a> among different factions and does not rely on any particular view or knowledge as a source of ultimate security? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato">Snp 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for a person who has no desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato"><b>being or non-being</b></a>, whether in this life or the next, there are no fixed "dwellings" or dogmatic attachments to specific teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato">Snp 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a true Brahmin, having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato"><b>gone to the far shore</b></a>, is not someone who can be led by external virtue and vows, and does not return to the use of fixed views to define reality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.5/en/sujato">Snp 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that human life is extremely brief, and even those who manage to live past a hundred years eventually die of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato"><b>old age</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato">Snp 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that people often grieve over things they consider "mine," even though <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato"><b>possessions</b></a> are never permanent and separation is an inevitable reality of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato">Snp 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that at the moment of death, a person must abandon every single thing they previously <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato"><b>claimed as their own</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato">Snp 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared seeing a deceased loved one to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato"><b>dream</b></a>, noting that just as one does not see the people from a dream upon waking, one no longer sees those who have passed away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato">Snp 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that when people pass away, only their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato"><b>name remains</b></a> behind to be spoken of by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato">Snp 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that sages who see security (khema) wander after <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato"><b>abandoning possessions</b></a>, because those who are greedy for "mine-making" never truly escape sorrow, lamentation, and stinginess? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato">Snp 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a sage as being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato"><b>lotus leaf</b></a>, which is not tainted by water, just as a sage is not tainted by what is seen, heard, or sensed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato">Snp 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a "cleansed one" (dhona) does not form <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato"><b>mental opinions</b></a> or conceit based on things seen or heard, nor do they seek purity through any other external means? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.6/en/sujato">Snp 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that for someone devoted to sexual intercourse, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato"><b>teaching is forgotten</b></a> and they begin to practice in a way that is wrong and ignoble? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato">Snp 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who once lived alone but later indulges in sexual intercourse to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato"><b>swerving carriage</b></a> that has gone off track and is considered a vile, common person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato">Snp 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato"><b>fame and reputation</b></a> a person previously earned will diminish if they abandon their solitary life to give in to sexual intercourse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato">Snp 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person overwhelmed by sensual intentions as being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato"><b>miserable person</b></a> who broods and becomes dejected when hearing the criticism of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato">Snp 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even someone known as a "wise man" who has committed to a solitary life will suffer like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato"><b>fool</b></a> if they become entangled in sexual intercourse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato">Snp 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged practitioners to train in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> (viveka), calling it the supreme practice for the noble ones, as it leads one close to Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato">Snp 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that people who are still obsessed with sensual pleasures actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato"><b>envy the sage</b></a> who is "empty" of desire and has successfully crossed over the flood of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.7/en/sujato">Snp 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refused an offer of a beautiful woman for marriage, stating that after seeing Craving, Discontent, and Passion, he had no desire for sexual intercourse with a body he described as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>filled with excrement and urine</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained he attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>inner peace</b></a> (ajjhattasanti) by not grasping at any specific views among the various doctrines he had observed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>purity</b></a> is not achieved through views, learning, knowledge, or even virtue and vows alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that purity is also not achieved by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>absence of views</b></a>, learning, knowledge, or virtue, but by letting go of all these things without clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who has let go of dependencies and is at peace will not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>yearn for existence</b></a> (bhavaṃ na jappe) in any form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when a person is attached to their own preconceived views, they end up <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>confused</b></a> (momuhato) when they inquire about the truth of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that anyone who considers themselves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>equal, superior, or inferior</b></a> to others will inevitably engage in disputes because of those comparisons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is unshakeable in the face of comparisons like "equal" or "distinguished" has no reason to engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>disputes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a sage (muni) as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>left home</b></a> to wander without a fixed abode, avoiding the creation of intimate ties with people in the village? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a sage to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>lotus</b></a>, which remains unsullied by water and mud just as a sage remains unsullied by sensual pleasures and the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a "master of knowledge" (vedagū) does not become conceited through views or thoughts and is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>led by deeds</b></a> (kammunā) or learning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there are no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>fetters</b></a> (ganthā) for someone who is completely detached from perception (saññā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>liberated by wisdom</b></a> (paññāvimutta), there are no more delusions or confusions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato"><b>grasp at perception and views</b></a> are the very ones who go about clashing and creating friction in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp4.9/en/sujato">Snp 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Udaya that the shattering of ignorance and liberation through final knowledge are achieved through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of sensual desires</b></a> and dejection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato">Snp 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a mind is purified by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato"><b>equanimity and mindfulness</b></a> when such purification is preceded by the investigation of states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato">Snp 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato"><b>delight</b></a> as the primary fetter that binds the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato">Snp 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the wandering or investigation of the world as being driven by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato"><b>thought</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato">Snp 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> as the state attained specifically through the abandoning of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato">Snp 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato"><b>consciousness ceases</b></a> for a person who acts mindfully and does not delight in feelings, whether they are internal or external? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato">Snp 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to understand all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.15/en/sujato"><b>stations of consciousness</b></a> and to know exactly how a liberated person remains established in their final goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.15/en/sujato">Snp 5.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who has transcended the perception of form and sees "there is nothing" must further understand that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.15/en/sujato"><b>delight is a fetter</b></a> in order to gain true insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.15/en/sujato">Snp 5.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.15/en/sujato"><b>consummate brahmin</b></a> as one who has lived the holy life and possesses the knowledge that comes from correctly contemplating the origin of states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.15/en/sujato">Snp 5.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Mogharāja to look upon the world as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.16/en/sujato"><b>empty</b></a> and to remain always mindful in order to transcend death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.16/en/sujato">Snp 5.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one can only cross over beyond the reach of death by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.16/en/sujato"><b>uprooting the view of self</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.16/en/sujato">Snp 5.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.16/en/sujato"><b>King of Death</b></a> (Mara) cannot see the person who views the world as empty and lives with constant mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.16/en/sujato">Snp 5.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged Piṅgiya to be diligent and discard <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.17/en/sujato"><b>form</b></a> so that he might avoid the renewal of being in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.17/en/sujato">Snp 5.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that people are often <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.17/en/sujato"><b>harmed by forms</b></a> and that heedless individuals suffer because they are oppressed by these physical appearances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.17/en/sujato">Snp 5.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the student Piṅgiya described himself to the Buddha as old, weak, and fading, seeking a way to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.17/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of birth and old age</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.17/en/sujato">Snp 5.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the student Piṅgiya declared that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.17/en/sujato"><b>nothing unknown</b></a>, unseen, or unfelt by the Buddha in the entire world across all ten directions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.17/en/sujato">Snp 5.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Piṅgiya that human beings are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.17/en/sujato"><b>obsessed with craving</b></a> and tormented by the approach of old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.17/en/sujato">Snp 5.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one should discard <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.17/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> with diligence in order to achieve the non-renewal of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.17/en/sujato">Snp 5.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Udaya that the removal of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato"><b>sloth and remorse</b></a> is a necessary component for the shattering of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.14/en/sujato">Snp 5.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Posāla that a person who has discarded all form and sees "there is nothing" is someone whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.15/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a> is fully understood by a Tathagata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.15/en/sujato">Snp 5.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Ajita that the world is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato"><b>shrouded by ignorance</b></a> and does not shine because of the presence of stinginess and heedlessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato">Snp 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato"><b>longing</b></a> as the smear that stains the world, with suffering being its greatest source of fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato">Snp 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Ajita that while the "streams" of the senses flow everywhere in the world, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> is the quality that acts as their necessary restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato">Snp 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the flowing streams of sensory experience in the world are effectively <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato"><b>shut by wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato">Snp 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the mental and physical phenomena known as "name-and-form" cease completely through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato"><b>cessation of consciousness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato">Snp 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a "trainee" (sekha) or anyone who has understood the Dhamma should not be greedy for sensual pleasures and should maintain an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato"><b>unperturbed mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato">Snp 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Tissametteyya that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.3/en/sujato"><b>Great Man</b></a> is one who is content in the world and possesses no "commotions" or agitations of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.3/en/sujato">Snp 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a Great Man as one who has understood "both ends" (extremes) and is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.3/en/sujato"><b>smeared by the middle</b></a> through the application of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.3/en/sujato">Snp 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who leads a holy life amidst sensual pleasures but remains free from craving and always mindful is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.3/en/sujato"><b>liberated through reflection</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.3/en/sujato">Snp 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.3/en/sujato"><b>overcome the seamstress</b></a> (a metaphor for craving that "sews" lives together) as one who has successfully crossed over birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.3/en/sujato">Snp 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Puṇṇaka that sages, kshatriyas, and brahmins offer sacrifices in this world because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato"><b>dependent on aging</b></a> and hope for further existence in the human state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato">Snp 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that those who diligently follow the "path of sacrifice" have not crossed birth and aging because they remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato"><b>infatuated with the passion for being</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato">Snp 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has evaluated the "near and far" in the world and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato"><b>no commotions</b></a> anywhere in the world is the one who truly crosses birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato">Snp 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who has crossed over the cycle of birth and aging as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato"><b>peaceful and without smoke</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato">Snp 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato"><b>without distress and without hope</b></a> (regarding future existence) as one who has successfully transcended birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato">Snp 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the student Ajita asked the Buddha what <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato"><b>obstructs the world</b></a> and prevents it from being illuminated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato">Snp 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Ajita that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> is the specific quality that provides the necessary restraint for the world's flowing streams of sensory experience? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato">Snp 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who have understood the Dhamma and are still "trainees" should live as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato"><b>skillful bhikkhu</b></a> who wanders mindfully? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.2/en/sujato">Snp 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Puṇṇaka asked the Buddha if the various sacrifices performed by humans and deities actually allow them to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato"><b>transcend birth and aging</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato">Snp 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that people who offer sacrifices are merely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato"><b>praying for sensual pleasures</b></a> because they are driven by the hope for future gain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.4/en/sujato">Snp 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Mettagū that the many different forms of suffering found in the world all originate from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato"><b>acquisitions</b></a> (upadhi)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato">Snp 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an ignorant person who continues to create <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato"><b>acquisitions</b></a> (upadhi) will repeatedly encounter suffering, and therefore one who sees the origin of suffering should refrain from creating them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato">Snp 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised to teach a Dhamma that can be experienced in this very life, which allows a person to wander mindfully and cross over <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato"><b>attachment in the world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato">Snp 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that one should dispel <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato"><b>delight and involvement</b></a> in whatever one understands—whether it is above, below, across, or in the middle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato">Snp 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that in order to end the cycle of existence, a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> should not remain established in "being" (bhava)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato">Snp 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who lives mindfully and diligently, having given up <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato"><b>mine-making</b></a> and attachments, can abandon birth, aging, sorrow, and lamentation right here in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato">Snp 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the student Mettagū declared that those who receive the constant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato"><b>advice of the Buddha</b></a> would surely be able to abandon their suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato">Snp 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato"><b>reached the end of knowledge</b></a> (vedagū) as someone who is possessionless and unattached to sensual existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato">Snp 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who has crossed over the flood of existence is one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato"><b>without doubt</b></a> and has reached the "far shore"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato">Snp 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person who discards <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato"><b>attachment to states of being</b></a> (bhavābhave) is one who has effectively crossed over the cycle of birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato">Snp 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato"><b>free from craving</b></a> and undistressed as one who has successfully transcended the suffering of birth and old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato">Snp 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato"><b>possessionless</b></a> and unattached to sensual existence is the one who has truly crossed the "flood" of worldly suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.5/en/sujato">Snp 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised Upasīva to cross the great flood of existence by being mindful, looking to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato"><b>nothingness</b></a>, and relying on the support of "there is nothing"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato">Snp 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should cross the flood by abandoning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a>, refraining from various talks, and watching day and night for the destruction of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato">Snp 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is liberated in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato"><b>highest release of perception</b></a> and established in the state of nothingness would remain there without moving on? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato">Snp 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato"><b>simile of a flame</b></a> blown by a gust of wind to explain how a sage, once liberated from the "name-body," goes out and can no longer be defined? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato">Snp 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person who has "gone out" and reached the goal, there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato"><b>measure</b></a> or criteria by which they can be spoken of? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato">Snp 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato"><b>all phenomena</b></a> (dhammas) are removed for a person, all pathways of speech and description are also removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato">Snp 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Upasīva that the state of one who has reached the end is beyond description because that by which they could be categorized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato"><b>no longer exists</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato">Snp 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Nanda that experts do not call someone a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato"><b>sage</b></a> simply because of their view, learning, or knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato">Snp 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined true sages as those who wander <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato"><b>without anguish</b></a> and without hope, having discarded the "armies" of defilement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato">Snp 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that recluses and brahmins who claim purity solely through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato"><b>precepts and vows</b></a> have not actually crossed over birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato">Snp 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even those who claim purity through what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato"><b>seen and heard</b></a> have failed to transcend birth and aging, despite their diligent practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato">Snp 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato"><b>abandoned craving</b></a> and become taintless (anāsava) as the ones who have truly crossed the flood of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato">Snp 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that crossing birth and aging requires a person to abandon everything <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato"><b>seen, heard, or sensed</b></a>, as well as all attachments to precepts and vows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato">Snp 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the student Nanda praised the Buddha's words as being well-spoken and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato"><b>free from acquisitions</b></a> (upadhi)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato">Snp 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Upasīva asked the Buddha for a specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato"><b>support</b></a> (ārammaṇa) that would allow him to cross the great flood alone and unsupported? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato">Snp 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a sage who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato"><b>liberated from the name-body</b></a> as someone who has reached an end and can no longer be reckoned in terms of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato">Snp 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly denied that all recluses and brahmins are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato"><b>shrouded by birth and aging</b></a>, noting that those who abandon all attachments have indeed crossed over? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato">Snp 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato"><b>fully understood craving</b></a> are the only ones who can truly be called "crossers of the flood"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato">Snp 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged Upasīva to remain mindful while relying on the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato"><b>nothingness</b></a> to successfully cross the flood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato">Snp 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who wander as true sages are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato"><b>without hope</b></a> for future existence or any state of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato">Snp 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Upasīva questioned whether the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato"><b>consciousness</b></a> of one who remains for many years in the state of nothingness would eventually pass away or disappear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.7/en/sujato">Snp 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that even if people lead a holy life while adhering to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato"><b>diverse ways of purity</b></a>, they still do not cross birth and aging if they remain dependent on views and vows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.8/en/sujato">Snp 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Hemaka that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.9/en/sujato"><b>removal of desire and lust</b></a> for pleasant things seen, heard, sensed, and cognized is the deathless state of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.9/en/sujato">Snp 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the student Hemaka complained to the Buddha that teachings he heard previously were merely based on oral tradition and served as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.9/en/sujato"><b>increasers of speculation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.9/en/sujato">Snp 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are mindful and have attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.9/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna in this very life</b></a> are always at peace and have crossed the sticky attachments of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.9/en/sujato">Snp 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Todeyya that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.10/en/sujato"><b>no further liberation</b></a> for a person in whom sensual desires do not dwell, who is free from craving, and who has crossed over doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.10/en/sujato">Snp 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true sage to Todeyya as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.10/en/sujato"><b>without hope or expectation</b></a> and does not engage in mental speculations regarding wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.10/en/sujato">Snp 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a sage as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.10/en/sujato"><b>possessionless</b></a> and unattached to the existence of sensual pleasures in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.10/en/sujato">Snp 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.11/en/sujato"><b>possessionlessness and non-attachment</b></a> as the "island" for those overwhelmed by the flood of aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.11/en/sujato">Snp 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined Nibbāna as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.11/en/sujato"><b>destruction of aging and death</b></a>, which serves as a refuge for those standing in the middle of the lake of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.11/en/sujato">Snp 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who are mindful and cooled in this life do not become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.11/en/sujato"><b>subjects of Mara</b></a>, the personification of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.11/en/sujato">Snp 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged Jatukaṇṇi to subdue greed for sensual pleasures by recognizing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.12/en/sujato"><b>renunciation as security</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.12/en/sujato">Snp 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one should let whatever was in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.12/en/sujato"><b>past</b></a> (pubbe) wither away and ensure there is nothing for the future in order to wander at peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.12/en/sujato">Snp 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a person does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.12/en/sujato"><b>grasp in the middle</b></a> (the present moment), they will be able to wander through life at peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.12/en/sujato">Snp 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for one who is entirely without greed for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.12/en/sujato"><b>name-and-form</b></a>, there are no taints that lead to the power of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.12/en/sujato">Snp 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Bhadrāvudha that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.13/en/sujato"><b>Mara follows a person</b></a> through whatever specific things they grasp at in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.13/en/sujato">Snp 5.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that one should dispel the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.13/en/sujato"><b>craving for grasping</b></a> in all directions—above, below, across, and in the middle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.13/en/sujato">Snp 5.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that beings are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.13/en/sujato"><b>stuck in the realm of death</b></a> because they are attached to grasping at things in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.13/en/sujato">Snp 5.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mindful monk should not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.13/en/sujato"><b>grasp at anything</b></a> in the whole world, knowing that such attachment allows Mara to follow them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.13/en/sujato">Snp 5.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Hemaka asked the Buddha for a teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.9/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a> that he could practice mindfully to cross over the sticky attachments of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.9/en/sujato">Snp 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Todeyya asked the Buddha to describe the nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.10/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a> for someone who is already free of sensual desires and craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.10/en/sujato">Snp 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the student Kappa requested the Buddha to describe an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.11/en/sujato"><b>island</b></a> that would provide refuge for those afflicted by aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.11/en/sujato">Snp 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Jatukaṇṇi addressed the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.12/en/sujato"><b>all-seeing one</b></a> (sahajanetta) while asking for a way to understand the abandoning of birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.12/en/sujato">Snp 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhadrāvudha praised the Buddha as a sage who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.13/en/sujato"><b>abandoned his home</b></a> (okañjahaṃ), cut off craving, and crossed the flood of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/snp5.13/en/sujato">Snp 5.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.2/en/sujato"><b>mothers of Bodhisattas</b></a> pass away exactly seven days after the birth of their child? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.2/en/sujato">Ud 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda once expressed his amazement to the Buddha that the Buddha's mother was reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.2/en/sujato"><b>Tusita heaven</b></a> following her death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.2/en/sujato">Ud 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all beings who exist now or will exist in the future will eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.2/en/sujato"><b>leave their bodies</b></a> and pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.2/en/sujato">Ud 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged that a wise person, recognizing the inevitability of death, should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.2/en/sujato"><b>ardent</b></a> in practicing the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.2/en/sujato">Ud 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once asked a group of boys who were catching fish whether they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.4/en/sujato"><b>afraid of suffering</b></a> and if they found suffering to be unpleasant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.4/en/sujato">Ud 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if a person is afraid of suffering, they should not perform an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.4/en/sujato"><b>evil deed</b></a> either openly or in secret? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.4/en/sujato">Ud 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there is no escape from suffering, even if one attempts to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.4/en/sujato"><b>run away</b></a>, if they continue to perform evil deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.4/en/sujato">Ud 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a lay follower’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato"><b>only son</b></a> died, he visited the Buddha in broad daylight with his hair and clothes still wet as a sign of his intense grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato">Ud 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that many humans and devas are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato"><b>bound by attachment</b></a> to what is "dear" in form, which leads them into the power of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato">Ud 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are heedful day and night and abandon what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato"><b>dear</b></a> are the ones who can truly overcome their misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato">Ud 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that people who cling to "dear things" become afflicted and fall under the sway of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato"><b>King of Death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato">Ud 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by letting go of what is dear, a person effectively <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato"><b>digs up the root</b></a> of their own misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato">Ud 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the successful release from attachment as overcoming the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato"><b>bait of death</b></a>, which is otherwise extremely difficult to transcend? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato">Ud 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encountered a group of boys <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.4/en/sujato"><b>harming fish</b></a> while he was walking into the city of Sāvatthī to collect alms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.4/en/sujato">Ud 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed it is a standard occurrence for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.2/en/sujato"><b>Bodhisatta's mother</b></a> to pass away shortly after giving birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.2/en/sujato">Ud 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that if someone has already done or is currently doing an evil deed, they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.4/en/sujato"><b>no escape</b></a> from suffering even if they try to flee? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.4/en/sujato">Ud 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that people who are obsessed with the enjoyment of "dear things" eventually become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato"><b>worn out</b></a> and fall victim to death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato">Ud 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, upon reflecting on the short life of his mother, uttered a verse urging followers to practice the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.2/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> with diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.2/en/sujato">Ud 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.4/en/sujato"><b>suffering is unpleasant</b></a> to all beings, using the boys' own feelings to illustrate why they should avoid harming others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.4/en/sujato">Ud 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha met a lay follower who was so overwhelmed by the death of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato"><b>beloved son</b></a> that he sought the Buddha's guidance while still in the middle of his mourning rites? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.7/en/sujato">Ud 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once sat in meditation specifically reviewing his own abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.3/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome states</b></a> alongside the completion of his wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.3/en/sujato">Ud 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uttered a verse describing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.3/en/sujato"><b>nature of existence</b></a> by stating that what existed before may no longer be, and what did not exist before can come to be? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.3/en/sujato">Ud 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed Venerable Cūḷapanthaka sitting nearby with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.10/en/sujato"><b>steady body</b></a> and his mindfulness established directly in front of him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.10/en/sujato">Ud 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who maintains a steady mind while standing, sitting, or lying down can reach a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.10/en/sujato"><b>distinction</b></a> in his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.10/en/sujato">Ud 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by resolving upon mindfulness in all postures, a practitioner can eventually go beyond the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.10/en/sujato"><b>King of Death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.10/en/sujato">Ud 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha witnessed a monk who was able to endure <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato"><b>bitter feelings</b></a> and sharp pain born of the ripening of old kamma without becoming distressed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato">Ud 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a perfected monk as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato"><b>shaking off</b></a> the "dust" of the past by abandoning all kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato">Ud 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a monk who is "without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato"><b>mine-making</b></a>," meaning he has no sense of possessiveness or ego-attachment toward his experiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato">Ud 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that for a monk who stands firm in his spiritual attainment, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato"><b>no need</b></a> to go about telling people or boasting of his status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato">Ud 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Visākhā, a famous lay follower, once visited the Buddha in the middle of the day because a business matter she had with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.9/en/sujato"><b>King Pasenadi</b></a> of Kosala was not settled to her satisfaction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.9/en/sujato">Ud 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared to Visākhā that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.9/en/sujato"><b>dependence on others</b></a> is ultimately a form of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.9/en/sujato">Ud 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.9/en/sujato"><b>self-mastery</b></a>, or spiritual independence, is the true source of happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.9/en/sujato">Ud 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that people are often <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.9/en/sujato"><b>distressed</b></a> by what they share with others, as such worldly bonds are extremely difficult to overcome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.9/en/sujato">Ud 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha sat at Jetavana Monastery specifically reflecting on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.3/en/sujato"><b>fulfilled</b></a> wholesome states he had developed through his own practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.3/en/sujato">Ud 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a monk who sits with an erect body and established mindfulness as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.10/en/sujato"><b>resolute</b></a> in his training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.10/en/sujato">Ud 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato"><b>undistressed</b></a> while experiencing intense physical pain is practicing with mindfulness and clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato">Ud 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato"><b>such a one</b></a> (tādino) to describe a stable and liberated monk who has transcended the need for worldly validation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato">Ud 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.9/en/sujato"><b>difficulty of bonds</b></a> (yogā) is what makes it so hard for people to escape from the suffering inherent in dependence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.9/en/sujato">Ud 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that spiritual progress involves maintaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.10/en/sujato"><b>established mindfulness</b></a> regardless of whether a monk is sitting or even sleeping? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.10/en/sujato">Ud 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that suffering born of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato"><b>past kamma</b></a> can be faced with equanimity by those who have developed their minds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.1/en/sujato">Ud 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the monk Dabba Mallaputta as attaining final nibbāna by rising into the air and entering the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato"><b>fire element</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato">Ud 8.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that when Dabba Mallaputta’s body was consumed by fire during his final passing, not even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato"><b>ashes or soot</b></a> remained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato">Ud 8.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the total disappearance of a liberated monk's body after death to the way <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato"><b>ghee or oil</b></a> burns away without leaving any residue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato">Ud 8.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a glowing piece of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato"><b>iron struck by a hammer</b></a> to explain that the destination of those who are fully liberated cannot be known after they pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato">Ud 8.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that those who have crossed the "flood of the bond of sensuality" and are well-released have attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato"><b>unshakable happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato">Ud 8.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as the path of a spark is unknown once it cools, there is no way to point out the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato"><b>destination</b></a> of those who have attained right liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato">Ud 8.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.3/en/sujato"><b>unborn, unbecome, unmade, and unconditioned</b></a> state, which is the necessary requirement for an escape from the world of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.3/en/sujato">Ud 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that if the unconditioned state did not exist, there would be no possible <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.3/en/sujato"><b>escape</b></a> for beings from what is born, become, made, and conditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.3/en/sujato">Ud 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha held a specific discourse for the monks focused on the topic of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.3/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> to inspire, urge, and gladden them in their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.3/en/sujato">Ud 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once observed that his monks were listening to his teachings with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.3/en/sujato"><b>focused ears</b></a>, having applied their whole minds to understanding the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.3/en/sujato">Ud 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato"><b>dependent</b></a> on things there is "wavering" (instability), but for one who is independent there is no wavering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato">Ud 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when there is no wavering of the mind, there is a profound state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato"><b>tranquility</b></a> (passaddhi) within the practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato">Ud 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when tranquility is present, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato"><b>inclination</b></a> (the leaning of the mind toward existence) does not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato">Ud 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when inclination is absent, there is no more <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato"><b>coming and going</b></a>, which refers to the restless movement toward rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato">Ud 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that without the cycle of "coming and going," there is no longer any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato"><b>passing away and reappearing</b></a> in different states of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato">Ud 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a> as a state that is neither "here," nor "beyond," nor "in between" the two? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato">Ud 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Dabba Mallaputta as performing the feat of sitting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato"><b>cross-legged in the air</b></a> before his final passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.10/en/sujato">Ud 8.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato"><b>independent</b></a> and unsupported by attachments, there is no wavering of the mind, which leads to the cessation of all misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.4/en/sujato">Ud 8.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was once traveling with the monk Nagasamala when they reached a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato"><b>fork in the road</b></a> and disagreed on which path they should take? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato">Ud 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha witnessed the monk Nagasamala place the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's bowl and robe</b></a> on the ground and walk away because he insisted on taking a different route? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato">Ud 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's attendant, Nagasamala, was attacked by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato"><b>bandits</b></a> who struck him with their hands and feet after he chose to travel a path against the Buddha's advice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato">Ud 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha heard from Nagasamala how bandits had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato"><b>broken his bowl</b></a> and torn his outer robe while he was traveling alone on a separate path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato">Ud 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato"><b>swan</b></a> separating milk from water to describe how a wise person, even while living among others, abandons what is evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato">Ud 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato"><b>knower of the Vedas</b></a> (vedagū) in the spiritual sense is one who knows how to abandon unwholesome states while living in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato">Ud 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed Venerable Piṇḍolabhāradvāja sitting nearby and characterized him as a monk who was an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato"><b>alms-food eater</b></a> and a forest-dweller? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato">Ud 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified Venerable Piṇḍolabhāradvāja as a practitioner of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato"><b>dhutanga observances</b></a>, including wearing rag-robes and possessing only three robes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato">Ud 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the "Teaching of the Buddhas" as consisting of not insulting, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato"><b>not harming</b></a>, and practicing restraint according to the monastic code (Patimokkha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato">Ud 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato"><b>moderation in eating</b></a> and staying in secluded lodgings are core principles of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato">Ud 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato"><b>devotion to the higher mind</b></a> (adhicitte ca āyogo) is a fundamental instruction shared by all Buddhas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato">Ud 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that Venerable Piṇḍolabhāradvāja was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato"><b>aloof from society</b></a> and energetic in his pursuit of spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato">Ud 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha saw Venerable Sāriputta sitting in meditation with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.7/en/sujato"><b>straight body</b></a> and recognized him as one who had few desires and was content? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.7/en/sujato">Ud 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that sorrows do not exist for a sage who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.7/en/sujato"><b>peaceful and mindful</b></a> while training in the ways of sagacity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.7/en/sujato">Ud 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.7/en/sujato"><b>stable one</b></a> (tādino) to describe a sage who is heedful and no longer subject to the influence of grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.7/en/sujato">Ud 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.7/en/sujato"><b>training in sagacity</b></a> (monapathesu sikkhato) remains undistracted and focused on the higher mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.7/en/sujato">Ud 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that Venerable Sāriputta was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.7/en/sujato"><b>secluded</b></a> and energetic, qualities that are essential for achieving spiritual peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.7/en/sujato">Ud 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato"><b>restraint in the Patimokkha</b></a> is a vital safeguard for those seeking to follow the path of the Buddhas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato">Ud 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's attendant Nagasamala <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato"><b>thrice requested</b></a> to take a specific path, only for the Buddha to repeatedly advise that the other path was the correct one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.7/en/sujato">Ud 8.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the value of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato"><b>secluded lodging</b></a> as a necessary condition for a practitioner to attain the higher mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.6/en/sujato">Ud 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his full awakening, the Buddha spent seven days sitting in a single posture at the foot of the Mucalinda tree, experiencing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato"><b>bliss of liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato">Ud 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a great unseasonable storm arose, the Naga King Mucalinda protected the Buddha by wrapping his body seven times around him and spreading a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato"><b>large hood</b></a> over the Buddha’s head to shield him from the cold and rain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato">Ud 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Naga King Mucalinda took it upon himself to protect the Buddha from heat, cold, and the touch of gadflies and mosquitoes by using his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato"><b>serpent body</b></a> as a shield for seven days? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato">Ud 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the storm subsided, the Naga King Mucalinda vanished his serpent form and assumed the appearance of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato"><b>young man</b></a> to stand before the Buddha and pay him homage with joined palms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato">Ud 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato"><b>seclusion is happiness</b></a> for one who is content, who has heard the Dhamma, and who sees the truth of things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato">Ud 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described happiness in this world as involving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato"><b>non-ill will</b></a> and restraint toward all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato">Ud 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato"><b>overcoming of sensual desires</b></a> and dispassion toward worldly things constitutes true happiness in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato">Ud 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato"><b>removal of the conceit 'I am'</b></a> to be the supreme happiness of all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato">Ud 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remained in deep meditation for an entire week, experiencing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato"><b>secluded bliss</b></a>, immediately following his enlightenment near the bank of the Nerañjarā River? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato">Ud 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's inspired utterance regarding happiness was prompted by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato"><b>Naga King's devotion</b></a> and the end of a week-long unseasonable storm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.1/en/sujato">Ud 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once overheard his monks debating which of two regional rulers, King Bimbisāra of Magadha or King Pasenadi of Kosala, possessed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato"><b>greater wealth</b></a> and power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato">Ud 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that discussing the property, armies, and influence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato"><b>worldly kings</b></a> is an unsuitable topic of conversation for monks who have left the household life out of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato">Ud 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his followers that whenever they gather, they should practice only one of two things: engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma talk</b></a> or maintaining noble silence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato">Ud 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato"><b>noble silence</b></a> as a primary duty for monks when they are not actively discussing the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato">Ud 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that all the sensual happiness in the world and even heavenly happiness are not worth one-sixteenth of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato"><b>happiness of the destruction of craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato">Ud 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the temporary pleasures of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato"><b>heavenly happiness</b></a> to the ultimate peace of ending desire, finding the former to be vastly inferior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato">Ud 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha corrected monks who were comparing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato"><b>military power</b></a> and vehicle fleets of various kings, telling them such talk does not belong to the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato">Ud 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emerged from his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato"><b>evening seclusion</b></a> specifically to address the assembly of monks who were engaged in "aimless talk" about worldly politics? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato">Ud 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato"><b>sons of good family</b></a> who have gone forth from the home life, worldly gossip about kings is inappropriate conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.2/en/sujato">Ud 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once summoned a monk named Bhaddiya because he was frequently overheard exclaiming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>“Oh, what happiness!”</b></a> while staying in the forest and in empty huts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s monks initially suspected that Bhaddiya was dissatisfied with the holy life and was longing for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>royal happiness</b></a> he enjoyed before becoming a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned Bhaddiya, the son of Kāḷīgodhā, to discover what benefit he saw in making such a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>joyful exclamation</b></a> repeatedly in solitary places? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhaddiya explained to the Buddha that when he was a secular ruler, he lived in constant fear despite having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>well-organized defense</b></a> inside and outside his palace and city? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha heard Bhaddiya confess that even with guards protecting his entire district, he formerly lived <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>fearful and anxious</b></a>, always suspecting danger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhaddiya told the Buddha that since ordaining as a monk and living in the forest, he has become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>fearless and unagitated</b></a>, even though he is now alone and unprotected? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was told by Bhaddiya that he now dwells with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>mind like a wild deer</b></a>, symbolizing a state of total freedom and lack of concern for worldly safety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhaddiya informed the Buddha that his current bliss arises from being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>unburdened</b></a> by the responsibilities of kingship and living simply on what others provide? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhaddiya described his state of peace to the Buddha by saying he lives with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>hair of his body at rest</b></a>, which indicates he is no longer susceptible to terror or horror? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, upon hearing Bhaddiya's explanation, uttered an inspired verse stating that true happiness belongs to one who has no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>internal anger</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has transcended the cycle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>existence and non-existence</b></a> is truly happy and beyond all sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>gods cannot see</b></a> the track or find the destination of a person who has achieved such supreme, sorrowless liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhaddiya preferred to exclaim "Oh, what happiness!" specifically when he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>under the root of a tree</b></a>, demonstrating his deep appreciation for the monastic life in nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha utilized a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>messenger monk</b></a> to bring Bhaddiya before him so he could address the rumors circulating among the other monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhaddiya is frequently referred to in the sutta as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>Kāḷīgodhā’s son</b></a>, highlighting his noble background before he renounced his status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>Mango Grove</b></a> at Anupiyā when he gave this teaching on the difference between worldly and spiritual security? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha asked Bhaddiya if he was indeed shouting about happiness, Bhaddiya <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>openly admitted</b></a> to the behavior and explained his spiritual motivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhaddiya contrasted his former life as a ruler, where he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>always suspicious</b></a> and alarmed, with his current life of total fearlessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used Bhaddiya’s experience to show that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>liberation from fear</b></a> is a much higher form of happiness than the protection offered by armies and wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the a Buddha's inspired utterance emphasizes that the person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato"><b>beyond fear</b></a> is the one who has mastered their own mind and overcome the craving for rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.10/en/sujato">Ud 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once observed a group of young boys between Sāvatthī and Jeta's Grove who were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.3/en/sujato"><b>hitting a snake</b></a> with a stick? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.3/en/sujato">Ud 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who seek their own happiness by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.3/en/sujato"><b>harming other beings</b></a> with a stick will not find happiness after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.3/en/sujato">Ud 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.3/en/sujato"><b>seeking one's own happiness</b></a> without using violence against living beings who also desire happiness leads to bliss in the next life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.3/en/sujato">Ud 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.3/en/sujato"><b>inspired utterance</b></a> regarding the use of force was prompted by seeing creatures being harassed on the road while he was entering Sāvatthī for alms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.3/en/sujato">Ud 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.3/en/sujato"><b>do not harm</b></a> living beings who desire happiness will themselves attain happiness hereafter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.3/en/sujato">Ud 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his time in Sāvatthī, the Buddha and his community of monks were so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato"><b>honored and respected</b></a> that they received abundant requisites such as robes, almsfood, and medicine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato">Ud 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because the Buddha was highly revered, wanderers of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato"><b>other sects</b></a> became jealous because they were not respected and lacked the necessary support for their daily needs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato">Ud 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s monks were frequently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato"><b>abused and reviled</b></a> with harsh and insulting words by rival ascetics who could not endure the success of the Buddhist Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato">Ud 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his monks that whether they are in a village or a forest, they should not attribute <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato"><b>pleasure and pain</b></a> to themselves or to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato">Ud 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that sensory <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato"><b>contacts touch a person</b></a> only because of their "acquisitions" (upadhi), or the underlying conditions of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato">Ud 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha posed a rhetorical question asking how external contact could possibly affect one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato"><b>without acquisitions</b></a> (nirūpadhi)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato">Ud 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha responded to the news of his monks being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato"><b>harassed by rivals</b></a> by teaching a profound lesson on how to remain unmoved by external circumstances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato">Ud 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato"><b>Anāthapiṇḍika’s Monastery</b></a> in Jeta’s Grove when he addressed the monks' concerns about being insulted by wanderers of other sects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato">Ud 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the psychological impact of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato"><b>harsh words</b></a> depends entirely on the presence of internal acquisitions, implying that an enlightened person is beyond such impact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.4/en/sujato">Ud 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once greeted a lay follower from Icchanangala who had not visited in a long time because he was frequently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.5/en/sujato"><b>busy with chores</b></a> and various duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.5/en/sujato">Ud 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.5/en/sujato"><b>true happiness</b></a> does not belong to someone who possesses things, but rather to the person who is learned and has understood the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.5/en/sujato">Ud 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that people who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.5/en/sujato"><b>bound to others</b></a> and attached to possessions often find themselves afflicted and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.5/en/sujato">Ud 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who has understood the Dhamma as one for whom <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.5/en/sujato"><b>possessions</b></a> are no longer a source of ultimate concern or happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.5/en/sujato">Ud 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's inspired utterance in the Upāsaka Sutta emphasizes that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.5/en/sujato"><b>learned person</b></a> who has nothing (akiñcana) is the one who truly knows peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.5/en/sujato">Ud 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once encountered a wanderer whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato"><b>young wife</b></a> was pregnant and repeatedly asked him to go and fetch oil for her impending delivery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato">Ud 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the time of the Buddha, King Pasenadi of Kosala allowed recluses and brahmins to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato"><b>drink as much oil</b></a> or ghee as they wanted at his storehouse, but prohibited them from taking any away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato">Ud 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told of a wanderer who tried to circumvent a king's rule by drinking a large amount of oil, intending to return home and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato"><b>vomit it up</b></a> for his wife? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato">Ud 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha saw a wanderer suffering from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato"><b>intense and sharp pain</b></a> because he was unable to digest or expel the excessive amount of oil he had consumed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato">Ud 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato"><b>without possessions</b></a> (akiñcana) are truly happy, as they are not weighed down by the burden of material attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato">Ud 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified people who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato"><b>masters of knowledge</b></a> (vedaguno) as those who have given up all possessions and worldly clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato">Ud 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato"><b>bound to other people</b></a> and to worldly things is a mind that will inevitably face affliction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato">Ud 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha witnessed a man <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato"><b>rolling back and forth</b></a> in agony on the streets of Sāvatthī because of his desperate attempt to provide oil for his wife? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato">Ud 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the suffering of the wanderer who over-consumed oil to the general state of those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato"><b>clinging to possessions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato">Ud 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha states that people with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato"><b>possessions</b></a> are the very ones seen being afflicted in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato">Ud 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that true security is found when a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato"><b>unburdened</b></a> by the constant and often painful need to acquire and protect material things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato">Ud 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted how the wanderer’s desire to help his wife led to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato"><b>terrible physical suffering</b></a> because of his unwise and desperate actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato">Ud 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.5/en/sujato"><b>understood the Dhamma</b></a>, worldly possessions are not a prerequisite for happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.5/en/sujato">Ud 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of a suffering wanderer to teach that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato"><b>bound by the mind</b></a> to others and their needs can be a source of great distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.6/en/sujato">Ud 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, after sitting for seven days in the bliss of liberation following his enlightenment, used his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato"><b>Buddha-eye</b></a> to survey the world and saw beings everywhere being tormented by the "fevers" of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato">Ud 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the world is so overcome by sensory contact that people mistakenly identify what is actually a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato"><b>disease</b></a> as being a "self"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato">Ud 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out a fundamental irony of worldly perception: that in whatever way a person conceives something to be, that thing inevitably becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato"><b>otherwise</b></a> than what was imagined? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato">Ud 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the things people delight in are actually a source of fear, and that which people <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato"><b>fear</b></a> is essentially the nature of suffering itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato">Ud 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the spiritual or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> is lived specifically for the purpose of abandoning existence (<i>bhava</i>) entirely? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato">Ud 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that any recluses or brahmins who claim that one can achieve liberation from existence by means of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato"><b>existence</b></a> itself are not actually freed from the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato">Ud 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that recluses or brahmins who seek an escape from existence by focusing on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato"><b>non-existence</b></a> (or extermination) have also failed to truly exit the cycle of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato">Ud 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that suffering arises specifically dependent upon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato"><b>acquisitions</b></a>, and that suffering cannot possibly occur once all clinging is destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato">Ud 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that when a person views the world with right wisdom, they see that all forms of existence are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>, characterized by suffering, and subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato">Ud 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato"><b>Nirvana</b></a> as the remainderless fading away and cessation of all cravings, a state where a monk has conquered Māra and will never face the renewal of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.10/en/sujato">Ud 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha corrected a group of monks who were discussing how being an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>alms-food eater</b></a> allows one to see agreeable sights with the eye from time to time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that some monks mistakenly believed that a primary benefit of wandering for alms was the opportunity to hear <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>agreeable sounds</b></a> with the ear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that several monks once gathered to discuss how an alms-food eater might frequently encounter <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>agreeable odors</b></a> with the nose while walking through the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's disciples were once overheard discussing the sensory pleasure of tasting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>agreeable flavors</b></a> with the tongue as a reason to become alms-food eaters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that some monks suggested that wandering for alms was desirable because it provided the chance to feel <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>agreeable tangibles</b></a> with the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a group of monks desired to become alms-food eaters specifically so they could be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>honored and respected</b></a> and worshipped by the laypeople? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, after emerging from his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>evening seclusion</b></a>, sat with the monks to address their inappropriate conversation about sensory gains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the monks that their talk about the pleasures of alms-gathering was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>inappropriate</b></a> for those who had gone forth from home to homelessness out of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the two proper duties for monks who have gathered together as either engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma talk</b></a> or maintaining noble silence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>noble silence</b></a> is one of the two appropriate actions for a monk to practice when in the company of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>devas</b></a> envy a monk who is an alms-food eater, provided that he is not dependent on fame and praise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a true monk should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>self-supported</b></a> and not reliant on others for his spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk is truly worthy of being envied by the gods when he supports himself and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>not nurturing others</b></a>, meaning he is free from the burdens of domestic dependents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk loses his spiritual worth if he becomes dependent on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>fame and praise</b></a> while performing his duties as an alms-food eater? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the monks who leave the household life as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>clansmen</b></a> who have gone forth out of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>interrupted discussion</b></a> to describe the conversation the monks were having about sensory pleasures before he arrived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a monk should not wander for alms with the goal of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>esteemed</b></a> or worshipped by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's inspired utterance in this sutta highlights that a monk who is truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato"><b>independent</b></a> of worldy acclaim is an object of envy for the gods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.8/en/sujato">Ud 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>Anāthapiṇḍika’s Park</b></a> at Jeta’s Grove when he overheard a group of monks debating which worldly skill was the most superior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encountered a group of monks who had gathered after their meal to discuss which specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>craft</b></a> should be considered the "foremost" among all human occupations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha heard monks arguing that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>elephant craft</b></a>, or the skill of training and managing elephants, was the highest of all possible crafts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that some monks suggested to the Buddha that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>horse craft</b></a> was the most excellent skill, while others argued in favor of the chariot craft? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during a debate overheard by the Buddha, some monks claimed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>archery</b></a> was the pinnacle of crafts, while others insisted it was swordsmanship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed monks debating the merits of intellectual skills, such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>finger-calculation</b></a> and accounting, as being the best crafts to master? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's disciples once held a discussion on whether the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>craft of writing</b></a> or the art of poetry should be ranked as the foremost craft? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was told that some monks believed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>cosmology</b></a> (lokāyata) or the specialized "knowledge of the kshatriyas" was the most important craft a person could learn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emerged from his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>evening seclusion</b></a> to visit the assembly hall and specifically asked the monks what topic of conversation they had been discussing in his absence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told his monks that debating the merits of worldly crafts was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>inappropriate</b></a> for clansmen who had gone forth from home to homelessness out of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there are only two proper duties for monks who have gathered together: engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma talk</b></a> or maintaining noble silence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>noble silence</b></a> is a required practice for monks when they are not actively discussing the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true monk in an inspired utterance as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>does not live by a craft</b></a>, but is instead unburdened and seeking the spiritual goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a liberated monk as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>completely released</b></a> in every way, with his sensory faculties fully restrained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who truly follows the path is one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>wandering homeless</b></a> and has no sense of "mine-making" regarding worldly things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that a monk is one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>abandoned conceit</b></a> and is without hope or longing for future existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>faring alone</b></a> to describe the spiritual independence of a monk who has let go of all worldly attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha viewed the monks' debate about vocational skills as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato"><b>interrupted discussion</b></a> that needed to be replaced with talk regarding the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.9/en/sujato">Ud 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Meghiya, while walking along the banks of the Kimikāḷā River, became enamored with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>mango grove</b></a> that he found to be exceptionally pleasant, delightful, and suitable for spiritual striving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Meghiya first asked for permission to go to the mango grove to meditate, the Buddha initially asked him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>wait</b></a> until another monk arrived since they were currently alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Meghiya argued to the Buddha that while the Blessed One had nothing further to do or achieve, he himself still had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>further work</b></a> to do in his spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Meghiya requested permission three times, the Buddha eventually relented, stating that he could not say anything to someone speaking of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>striving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while sitting in the mango grove he had so desired, Venerable Meghiya was overwhelmed by three unwholesome states: sensual thoughts, thoughts of ill will, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of harming</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Meghiya expressed great astonishment to the Buddha that despite going forth from home out of faith, he was still pursued by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>evil unwholesome thoughts</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> is immature, there are five specific things that lead to its maturity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a>—having good companions and comrades—as the first essential factor for maturing the liberation of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk must be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>virtuous</b></a> and live restrained by the Pātimokkha as a primary requirement for spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner should see <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>danger in the slightest fault</b></a> and train themselves strictly in the rules of conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed ten specific types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>effacing talk</b></a>, such as talk on fewness of wishes and talk on contentment, that are conducive to opening the heart and attaining Nirvana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged talk on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> and the avoidance of social entanglement as a way to support the development of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, engaging in talk regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>knowledge and vision of liberation</b></a> is a vital support for maturing one's spiritual faculty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk should be able to engage in wholesome, liberating talk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>at will</b></a>, without difficulty or trouble? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk must maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>aroused energy</b></a> for abandoning unwholesome states and acquiring wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a "stable" practitioner as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>firm in effort</b></a> and does not throw off the burden of cultivating wholesome qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>noble wisdom</b></a>—specifically the wisdom that discerns the arising and passing away of things—as the fifth factor for maturing liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that when a monk has a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>good friend</b></a>, it is expected that they will naturally become virtuous and restrained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once a monk is grounded in the five preliminary factors, they should then develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>unattractiveness</b></a> (asubha) specifically to abandon lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended the cultivation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> (mettā) as the specific antidote for abandoning ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of breathing</b></a> should be developed in order to cut off the tendency toward distracting or obsessive thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>perception of impermanence</b></a> should be cultivated specifically to uproot the conceit "I am" (asmimāna)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one who perceives impermanence, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>perception of non-self</b></a> becomes firmly established? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that uprooting the conceit "I am" leads directly to the attainment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>Nirvana</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described unwholesome thoughts as being like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>floating things</b></a> (uppilāva) that follow and haunt the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an ignorant person whose mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>unsteady</b></a> will wander aimlessly from life to life, pursued by these wandering thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>ardent</b></a> (ātāpī) to describe a mindful practitioner who restrains and understands the nature of their own mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that a truly awakened one (a "Buddha") has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>abandoned without remainder</b></a> all the subtle and distracting thoughts that agitate the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's dialogue with Meghiya highlights that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> in a beautiful environment is not enough for progress if the mind lacks the proper supporting conditions like good friendship and virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.1/en/sujato">Ud 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at Rajagaha in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>Bamboo Grove</b></a> when he encountered a leper named Suppabuddha, who was described as a poor, pitiable, and wretched human being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Suppabuddha the leper initially approached the large crowd surrounding the Buddha because he mistakenly thought that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>food</b></a> was being distributed there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Suppabuddha realized the Buddha was teaching the Dhamma rather than giving out food, he decided to stay and listen, thinking, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>I too will listen</b></a> to the Dhamma"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his mind to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>scan the assembly</b></a> to identify who among the crowd was capable of understanding the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically identified Suppabuddha the leper as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>capable</b></a> of understanding the teaching among the entire assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave a progressive instruction to Suppabuddha, which included talk on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>giving</b></a>, virtue, and the heavens? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his teaching to the leper, the Buddha explained the danger, degradation, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>defilement</b></a> of sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>benefits of renunciation</b></a> as part of his progressive instruction to prepare Suppabuddha’s mind for deeper truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that only after the Buddha perceived that Suppabuddha’s mind was ready, soft, and free of hindrances did he reveal the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a>: suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the way Suppabuddha’s mind received the Dhamma to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>clean cloth</b></a>, free of dark stains, that easily takes dye? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while sitting in his seat, Suppabuddha experienced the arising of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma-eye</b></a>, realizing that "whatever is subject to arising is all subject to cessation"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha’s teaching, Suppabuddha became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>independent of others</b></a> in the Teacher’s instruction, having seen and reached the Dhamma for himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Suppabuddha praised the Buddha's teaching for making the Dhamma clear, comparing it to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>setting upright</b></a> what had been overturned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after leaving the Buddha’s presence, Suppabuddha the leper was tragically killed by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>cow with a young calf</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Suppabuddha was born a leper because in a past life as a merchant's son, he had insulted a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>Paccekabuddha</b></a> named Tagarasikhi? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that Suppabuddha’s past-life act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>spitting</b></a> and insulting a holy person led to him suffering in hell for many hundreds of thousands of years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>residual result</b></a> of Suppabuddha's past unwholesome karma was what caused him to be a poor leper in his final human birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his physical condition as a leper, the Buddha noted that Suppabuddha had undertaken faith, virtue, learning, generosity, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> in the Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uttered an inspired verse stating that a wise person in the world should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato"><b>avoid evil</b></a> deeds just as a person with sight avoids dangerous paths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.3/en/sujato">Ud 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at Savatthi in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>Migāramāta’s Mansion</b></a> when he was asked by the Venerable Ānanda to recite the Patimokkha to the assembly of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda approached the Buddha during the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>first watch</b></a> of the night to request the recitation of the monastic code, but the Buddha remained silent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even after the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>middle watch</b></a> of the night had passed, the Buddha refused to recite the Patimokkha despite a second request from the Venerable Ānanda? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha waited until the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>dawn was breaking</b></a> and the night had reached its end before explaining why he would not recite the monastic code? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha finally informed the Venerable Ānanda that he would not recite the Patimokkha because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>assembly was impure</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna used his psychic powers to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>scan the mind</b></a> of every monk in the assembly to identify the individual who was causing the impurity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna discovered a person sitting in the assembly who was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>immoral</b></a>, of evil character, and a "fake" ascetic who was "rotten within"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna commanded the immoral person to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>get up and leave</b></a> three times, but the person remained silent and refused to move? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna eventually took the immoral man <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>by the arm</b></a>, pulled him out of the assembly, and locked the door with a bolt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna for his actions, noting how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>amazing</b></a> it was that the immoral man waited until he was physically grabbed before leaving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared he would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>never again</b></a> perform the Uposatha or recite the Patimokkha himself, leaving that duty to the monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>impossible</b></a> and "unthinkable" for a Tathāgata to recite the Patimokkha in an impure assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used eight metaphors of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>great ocean</b></a> to illustrate the wonderful qualities of his Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as the ocean has a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>gradual shelf</b></a> and slope, the Dhamma-Vinaya involves a gradual training and practice rather than an abrupt penetration of final knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that just as the ocean is stable and does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>overstep its bounds</b></a>, his disciples will not transgress the training rules even for the sake of their lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the ocean’s refusal to harbor a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>corpse</b></a>—washing it quickly to the shore—to the Sangha’s refusal to live in communion with immoral persons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an immoral monk is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>far from the Sangha</b></a> and the Sangha is far from him, even if he is physically sitting in the middle of the assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the way great rivers like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>Ganges and Yamuna</b></a> lose their names in the ocean to how people of all castes lose their previous status when joining the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when members of the four castes go forth into homelessness, they are simply known as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>Sakyaputtiya recluses</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna-element</b></a> does not increase or decrease regardless of how many monks attain final liberation, just as the ocean level remains constant despite all the world's rain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that his Dhamma-Vinaya has only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>one taste</b></a>, which is the taste of liberation, just as the great ocean has only the taste of salt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the many treasures of the ocean, such as pearls and gems, to the "treasures" of the Dhamma like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>four foundations of mindfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>four right strivings</b></a> as one of the "many treasures" found within his teaching and discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha included the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>four bases of psychic power</b></a> as part of the spiritual wealth available in the Dhamma-Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>five spiritual faculties</b></a> and the five powers as treasures that monks delight in within the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> as a key set of "gems" found in the training of a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha regarded the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> as one of the most precious treasures within the Dhamma-Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that the great ocean is the home of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>giant beings</b></a> such as Timi and Timingala, some of which are hundreds of yojanas long? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the great beings of the ocean to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>noble persons</b></a> in his Dhamma, such as Stream-enterers and Arahants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically included those <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>practicing for the fruit</b></a> of Stream-entry as part of the "great beings" who dwell within the Dhamma-Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>Once-returners</b></a> and those practicing for that stage are among the wonderful "beings" that make the Dhamma-Vinaya magnificent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>Non-returners</b></a> as one of the eight types of individuals who inhabit and find joy in the Dhamma-Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> to be the highest of the "great beings" dwelling within his teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that asuras, nagas, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>gandhabbas</b></a> are among the supernatural beings that delight in the great ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>moghapurisa</b></a> (empty man) to describe the immoral person who sat silently in the assembly despite being impure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>covered</b></a> is what the rain of suffering leaks into, whereas what is open does not suffer such leakage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave an inspired verse encouraging people to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>open up</b></a> what is covered so that the "rain" (of defilement) will not leak in? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the immoral monk as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>inner-rotting</b></a> and full of "leaks," despite his outward appearance as a recluse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's metaphors about the ocean were specifically intended to show why <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>monks delight</b></a> in the Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's decision to stop reciting the Patimokkha personally was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato"><b>pivotal moment</b></a> that shifted the responsibility of the Uposatha ceremony to the Sangha as a whole? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.5/en/sujato">Ud 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>Jeta's Grove</b></a> in Savatthi when he personally shared a dwelling with the monk Soṇa for a night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa was originally a lay follower and an attendant to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> while he was living on the Overhanging Mountain in Avanti? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākaccāna initially twice discouraged Soṇa from ordaining, suggesting instead that he practice the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> as a householder because its lifelong requirements were difficult? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākaccāna described the holy life as a practice that is as pure and perfect as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>polished shell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākaccāna warned Soṇa that the monastic life involves practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>one meal a day</b></a> and sleeping alone for the rest of one's life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that it took three years for Soṇa to receive his Higher Ordination because monks were so scarce in the Southern District of Avanti that it was difficult to assemble a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>ten-member group</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after spending the rainy season in seclusion, the monk Soṇa expressed a deep desire to the Venerable Mahākaccāna to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>see the Buddha</b></a> in person, as he had only heard of his greatness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākaccāna described the Buddha as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>serene</b></a>, with calmed faculties and a calmed mind, having reached the supreme restraint of a "great tusker"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahākaccāna instructed Soṇa to bow his head to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's feet</b></a> on his behalf and inquire about the Buddha's health and vigor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Soṇa arrived at Jeta's Grove, the Buddha specifically asked the Venerable Ānanda to prepare a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>lodging</b></a> for the visiting monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda realized the Buddha wanted to stay in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>same dwelling</b></a> with Soṇa because the Buddha himself had designated the space? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha and the monk Soṇa spent much of the night sitting in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>open air</b></a> before eventually entering their shared dwelling to rest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that at the time of dawn, the Buddha invited the monk Soṇa to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>recite the Dhamma</b></a> for the assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Soṇa recited the sixteen parts of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>Aṭṭhakavagga</b></a> in their entirety before the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Soṇa recited the Dhamma with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>musical intonation</b></a> (sarabhañña) that was highly commended by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Soṇa for having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>grasped well</b></a> and reflected properly on the sixteen parts of the Aṭṭhakavagga? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Soṇa as being endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>faultless speech</b></a> that is clear, articulate, and effective in communicating the meaning of the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Soṇa how many years it had been since his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>Higher Ordination</b></a>, to which Soṇa replied that he had been a monk for only one year? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Soṇa told the Buddha he had long seen the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>danger</b></a> in sensual pleasures but found home life too crowded and full of duties to ordain sooner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>sambādha</b></a> (crowded or confined) when Soṇa described why it was difficult to live the holy life as a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uttered an inspired verse stating that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>noble person</b></a> (ariya) does not delight in evil after seeing the danger in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>pure person</b></a> (suci) will not find pleasure in evil once they have known the Dhamma that is free from acquisitions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically inquired whether Soṇa’s long journey to Savatthi was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>comfortable</b></a> and if he had faced fatigue or difficulty getting food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Soṇa confirmed to the Buddha that he had arrived with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>little fatigue</b></a> and without suffering from lack of almsfood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Soṇa set out for Savatthi after <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>packing away</b></a> his lodgings and taking his bowl and robes, showing the simplicity of a monk's travel? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha showed great <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>hospitality</b></a> to the visiting monk Soṇa by ensuring he was well-lodged despite being a junior monk of only one year? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk Soṇa delivered the message from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākaccāna</b></a> as his first act upon meeting the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.6/en/sujato">Ud 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while staying at Jeta's Grove, observed the Venerable Kaṅkhārevata sitting cross-legged and reviewing his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.7/en/sujato"><b>purification by overcoming doubt</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.7/en/sujato">Ud 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that ardent meditators practicing the holy life are able to abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.7/en/sujato"><b>all doubts</b></a>, whether they concern this world, the next world, or things perceived by themselves or others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.7/en/sujato">Ud 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a meditator who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.7/en/sujato"><b>ardent</b></a> and devoted to the holy life, all varieties of doubt are eventually abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.7/en/sujato">Ud 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Venerable Ānanda was walking for alms in Rajagaha, Devadatta approached him and declared his intention to perform the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato"><b>Uposatha ceremony</b></a> and Sangha acts completely apart from the Buddha and the Bhikkhu Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato">Ud 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, upon being informed by the Venerable Ānanda of Devadatta's separatist declaration, realized that Devadatta intended to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato"><b>split the Sangha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato">Ud 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that doing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato"><b>what is good</b></a> is easy for a good person to do, but it is extremely difficult for an evil person to perform? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato">Ud 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato"><b>evil deeds</b></a> are easy for an evil person to commit, yet they are very difficult for Noble Ones (Ariyas) to perform? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato">Ud 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that while evil is easy for the evil, performing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato"><b>good deed</b></a> is difficult for those of bad character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato">Ud 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato"><b>Noble Ones</b></a>, committing an evil act is something that is inherently difficult to do? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato">Ud 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was traveling in Kosala with a large group of monks, he observed a group of young men passing by who were making a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.9/en/sujato"><b>great noise</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.9/en/sujato">Ud 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described certain people as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.9/en/sujato"><b>forgetful</b></a> and only appearing to be wise while actually being mere talkers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.9/en/sujato">Ud 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that those who speak excessively and delight in the "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.9/en/sujato"><b>stretching of mouths</b></a>" often do not even know what is leading them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.9/en/sujato">Ud 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are led away by their own talkative nature and lack of mindfulness remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.9/en/sujato"><b>ignorant</b></a> of the forces guiding them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.9/en/sujato">Ud 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stayed at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato"><b>Bamboo Grove</b></a> in Rajagaha when he received the troubling news that Devadatta was moving to create a schism in the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.8/en/sujato">Ud 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that meditators (<i>jhāyino</i>) who are dedicated to the practice abandon all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.7/en/sujato"><b>internal and external doubts</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.7/en/sujato">Ud 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.9/en/sujato"><b>moghapurisa</b></a> (though implied by the context of those "pretending to be wise") to contrast the noisy, forgetful young men with those who truly understand the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.9/en/sujato">Ud 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda observed that other <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>sectarians</b></a> are honored and receive requisites like robes and almsfood only as long as Fully Enlightened Ones do not appear in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>glow-worm</b></a> to describe how other teachers shine only until the sun of a Tathāgata rises and outshines them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>logicians</b></a> and their disciples are never truly purified from suffering as long as they hold onto wrong views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda noted that the appearance of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a> causes other teachers to lose their respect and their supply of medicinal requisites and dwellings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the radiance of a Fully Enlightened One to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>sun</b></a>, which makes the puny light of all other thinkers disappear when it rises? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>wrong views</b></a> are unable to be released from the cycle of suffering, regardless of how much they shine in the absence of a Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda highlighted that once a Buddha arises, only the Buddha and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>Saṅgha of monks</b></a> are the ones who receive the highest honor and support from the public? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the intellectual light of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>thinkers</b></a> appears significant only because the sun-like radiance of a Sammāsambuddha has not yet illuminated the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the honor given to other teachers is temporary and entirely dependent on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>absence</b></a> of a Fully Enlightened One in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that no matter how much they are honored, logicians cannot achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>purification</b></a> from their taints through their own speculative theories? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta was responsible for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>instructing</b></a>, urging, and gladdening the Venerable Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya with a detailed Dhamma talk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed the Venerable Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya achieving liberation from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a> through non-clinging while he was being taught by Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a liberated person as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>crossed the flood</b></a> of existence, reaching a shore that had never been crossed before? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person is truly free from the cycle of birth when they no longer view any aspect of existence with the conceit <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>"I am this"</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>liberated everywhere</b></a>—above, below, and in every direction—is the hallmark of one who has escaped further rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the specific guidance of Sāriputta, Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya's mind was completely released from all taints <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>without clinging</b></a> to anything in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an inspired utterance to celebrate the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>non-rebirth</b></a> attained by a monk who has abandoned all self-centered perceptions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta's method of teaching involved <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>rousing</b></a> and inspiring his students to help them realize the ultimate goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches that one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>liberated above</b></a> and below has effectively transcended all the bonds that lead to suffering and renewal of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recognized the exact moment when Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>mind was freed</b></a> and subsequently spoke a verse about the crossing of the previously uncrossed flood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed the Venerable Subhūti sitting in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato"><b>thoughtless concentration</b></a> with his body held erect, leading the Buddha to utter a verse about the transcendence of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato">Ud 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a liberated person as one whose internal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato"><b>speculations</b></a> have been completely shredded and incinerated, leaving no remainder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato">Ud 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an individual who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato"><b>surpassed the four yokes</b></a> and has transcended attachment to forms will never again return to the cycle of birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato">Ud 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato"><b>incinerated thoughts</b></a> to describe the mental state of a monk who has successfully overcome the inner turmoil of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato">Ud 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke on the dangers of desire after monks reported that two gangs in Rājagaha were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>killing each other</b></a> because they were both enamored with the same prostitute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that both what a person has already attained and what they still hope to attain are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>covered in dust</b></a>—meaning they are defiled—for those who remain stuck in the training of the worldly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the extreme view that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>"there is no fault in sensual pleasures"</b></a> as a primary obstacle that prevents people from realizing the truth of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who place the core of their holy life solely in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>rules and vows</b></a> are following an extreme that fails to lead to true liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that both the indulgence in sensual pleasures and the rigid adherence to external vows are paths that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>increase the cemetery</b></a> by fueling the cycle of death and rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that some people <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>lag behind</b></a> the truth while others over-run it because they fail to have direct knowledge of the two extremes of dogmatism and indulgence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for those who have direct knowledge of reality and do not identify with their views, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>cycle of existence</b></a> can no longer be described or sustained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared people who are obsessively intent on what they see and hear to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>moths</b></a> that fly into an oil lamp, unaware that the light will lead to their destruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while sitting in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>darkness of the night</b></a> with oil lamps burning, the Buddha witnessed insects meeting their ruin and used the event to teach about the dangers of human attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who "over-run" the Dhamma and fail to find its essence only succeed in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>increasing new bondage</b></a> for themselves in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that some people are so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>intent on dogmas</b></a> derived from what is seen and heard that they fall into ruin just like insects falling into a flame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who do not reach the core essence of the spiritual life are essentially <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>over-running</b></a> the very path that could lead them to freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>enamored</b></a> with worldly objects or people leads to quarrels, strife, and even physical violence among those who are bound by their hearts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>blazing lamp</b></a> to illustrate how alluring but dangerous sensory distractions can be for those without wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that only through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> of the two extremes can a practitioner truly avoid being caught in the "dust" of both current and future attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>falling into disaster</b></a> as something that happens to those who are simply following traditional hearsay or visual appearances without deeper insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that five hundred women, including Sāmāvatī, who perished in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato"><b>palace fire</b></a> in Kosambi had actually attained various stages of enlightenment, such as stream-entry, once-returning, and non-returning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato">Ud 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the world as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato"><b>bound by delusion</b></a>, appearing capable and attractive to the foolish while it is actually surrounded by darkness and bound by acquisitions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato">Ud 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one who truly knows and sees reality, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato"><b>nothing</b></a> (no "thing") to be found, even though the world appears permanent to those caught in ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato">Ud 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when monks asked about the destination of the laywomen who died in King Utena’s harem, the Buddha assured them that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato"><b>none of those women</b></a> died without fruit, meaning they had all achieved a level of spiritual realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato">Ud 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>base</b></a> or dimension where there is no earth, water, fire, or air, nor any of the formless spheres like infinite space or infinite consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that in the state of Nibbana, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>neither this world</b></a> nor another world, and neither the sun nor the moon provides light there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the end of suffering as a state that has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>no coming or going</b></a>, no staying, no passing away, and no arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ultimate liberation is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>unestablished</b></a>, non-proliferating, and without an object, and that this alone constitutes the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that Nibbana is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>without an object</b></a> (anārammaṇa), meaning it does not rely on any sensory or mental phenomena for its existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.2/en/sujato"><b>uninclined</b></a> (anata)—referring to the state of Nibbana—is extremely difficult to see, and that the ultimate truth is not easily perceived by those still caught in craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.2/en/sujato">Ud 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that for one who knows and sees the truth, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.2/en/sujato"><b>craving is penetrated</b></a>, and for such a person, there is "nothing" left to grasp? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.2/en/sujato">Ud 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while the world appears permanent or eternal (sassato) to the ignorant, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato"><b>nothing to be found</b></a> in it for the one who sees with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato">Ud 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Nibbana as a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>non-proceeding</b></a> (appavattaṃ), which serves as the final cessation of the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.2/en/sujato"><b>truth</b></a> is not "well-seen" (sudassanaṃ) by those who have not yet overcome the internal drive of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.2/en/sujato">Ud 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an inspired utterance to emphasize that Nibbana is neither a place of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>arising</b></a> nor a place of passing away, but rather the absolute end of all distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed the Venerable Sāriputta instructing the Venerable Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya under the impression that he was still a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato"><b>trainee</b></a>, even though Bhaddiya had already reached the final goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato">Ud 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ultimate state of liberation as one where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato"><b>cycle is severed</b></a> and the stream of craving has dried up, meaning it no longer flows toward further existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato">Ud 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that once a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato"><b>hope is gone</b></a> regarding the round of rebirth, the cycle of suffering is broken and does not continue to turn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato">Ud 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uttered an inspired verse explaining that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato"><b>stream</b></a> of craving has dried up and no longer flows, that very state marks the absolute end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato">Ud 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the Venerable Sāriputta used <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato"><b>many methods</b></a> of Dhamma talk to instruct, urge, and gladden Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya, unaware that the monk was already fully liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato">Ud 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that people who are excessively attached to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> and the bond of fetters are incapable of ever crossing the great flood of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato">Ud 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the masses of people in Sāvatthī as being so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato"><b>infatuated</b></a> and blinded by lust that they fail to see any fault or danger in the fetters that bind them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato">Ud 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato"><b>stuck in attachment</b></a> to worldly enjoyments can never hope to cross the vast and great flood of samsara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato">Ud 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after a group of monks reported how the townspeople were obsessed with pleasure, the Buddha explained that such <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato"><b>bondage to fetters</b></a> is the primary obstacle to reaching the further shore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato">Ud 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared people who are blinded by sensual pleasures and covered by the shroud of craving to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato"><b>fish in a trap</b></a> (a funnel-net)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato">Ud 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that individuals who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato"><b>blinded by desire</b></a> are inevitably pursued by old age and death, much like a calf follows its mother? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato">Ud 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato"><b>suckling calf</b></a> following its mother to illustrate how relentlessly old age and death follow those who remain caught in the veil of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato">Ud 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being bound by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato"><b>Careless One</b></a>—a reference to Māra—leads beings into a state of ruin similar to fish being caught in the mouth of a net? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato">Ud 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed townspeople who were so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato"><b>shrouded by craving</b></a> and blinded by their own lusts that they were heading straight toward further suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato">Ud 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato"><b>blinded by sensual things</b></a> are essentially covered by a veil that prevents them from seeing the spiritual freedom that lies beyond old age and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato">Ud 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, immediately after his enlightenment, spent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato"><b>seven days</b></a> sitting in a single posture on the bank of the Nerañjarā River? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato">Ud 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his initial seven-day period of meditation under the Bodhi tree as time spent experiencing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato"><b>bliss of liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato">Ud 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the first watch of the night after his enlightenment, the Buddha gave thorough attention to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a> in forward order, contemplating how suffering arises? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato">Ud 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained the principle of conditionality by stating, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato"><b>when this exists, that comes to be</b></a>; with the arising of this, that arises"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato">Ud 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the entire sequence leading to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato"><b>origin of this whole mass of suffering</b></a> begins with ignorance as the primary condition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato">Ud 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato"><b>phenomena become manifest</b></a> to an ardent, meditating brahmin, all of that person's doubts vanish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato">Ud 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a meditator's doubts are dispelled once he understands <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato"><b>things with their causes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato">Ud 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the middle watch of the night following his enlightenment, the Buddha reflected on dependent origination in reverse order to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.2/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.2/en/sujato">Ud 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the principle of cessation by stating, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.2/en/sujato"><b>when this does not exist, that does not come to be</b></a>; with the cessation of this, that ceases"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.2/en/sujato">Ud 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the total fading away and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.2/en/sujato"><b>cessation of ignorance</b></a> is the necessary first step for the cessation of volitional formations and the end of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.2/en/sujato">Ud 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the doubts of an ardent, meditating brahmin pass away when he has understood the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.2/en/sujato"><b>destruction of causes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.2/en/sujato">Ud 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while sitting at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.1/en/sujato"><b>foot of the Bodhi tree</b></a>, concluded that the cessation of birth directly results in the cessation of aging, death, sorrow, and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.2/en/sujato">Ud 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth, before he met the Buddha, was a highly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>respected and honored</b></a> figure living on the seashore at Suppāraka who received many offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth once mistakenly believed that he was one of the <b>arahants</b> or those on the path to arahantship in the world? And that a deity, acting out of compassion for Bāhiya, informed him that he was actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>not an arahant</b></a> and did not yet possess the practice to become one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity told Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth that the Buddha, a true <b>arahant</b> and fully enlightened one, was currently staying at Sāvatthī and teaching the Dhamma? And that after being moved by the deity’s words, Bāhiya traveled from Suppāraka to Sāvatthī, staying only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>one night</b></a> at each location along his long journey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth perceived the Buddha, while he was walking for alms, as having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>calm mind</b></a> and perfectly controlled senses, like a noble elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth approached the Buddha in the street and requested a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma teaching</b></a> that would lead to his long-term welfare and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha initially declined Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth’s request to teach him in the street, explaining that it was an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>improper time</b></a> since he was in the middle of his alms round? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth persisted with his request to the Buddha because he knew that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>dangers to life</b></a> are uncertain and one's lifespan is difficult to know? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even after Bāhiya’s second request, the Buddha still maintained it was an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>improper time</b></a> for a teaching while entering houses for alms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that on Bāhiya’s third request, the Buddha taught him a practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>sensory restraint</b></a> where in the seen there should be only the seen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth that in what is heard, sensed, and cognized, there should only be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>just the heard</b></a>, just the sensed, and just the cognized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when one does not identify with the seen, heard, or sensed, then one is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>not therein</b></a>, and thus not "here," "beyond," or "in between"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth that the state of being neither "here," "beyond," nor "in between" is itself the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the Buddha’s brief instruction, Bāhiya’s mind was immediately <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>liberated from the taints</b></a> through non-clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after the Buddha finished giving his brief advice and departed, Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth was attacked and killed by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>young cow</b></a> with a calf? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha discovered Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth’s body, he told the monks to cremate the remains and build a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>stupa</b></a> to honor him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha officially declared that Bāhiya of the Bark-cloth had attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>final Nibbāna</b></a> before he passed away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a profound state where the elements of water, earth, fire, and air find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>no footing</b></a>, and where the sun and moon do not shine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a sage knows the truth through silence, he is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato"><b>freed from form</b></a> and formlessness, as well as from pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.10/en/sujato">Ud 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, during the last watch of the night after his enlightenment, thoroughly contemplated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a> in both forward and reverse order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato">Ud 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the fundamental principle of conditionality by stating: "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato"><b>when this exists, that comes to be</b></a>; with the arising of this, that arises"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato">Ud 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the entire "mass of suffering" originates from a chain of conditions beginning with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato">Ud 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the cessation of the entire mass of suffering is achieved through the total fading away and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato"><b>cessation of ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato">Ud 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared an ardent, meditating brahmin to whom phenomena become manifest to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato"><b>sun illuminating the sky</b></a> while scattering the army of Māra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato">Ud 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his enlightenment, the Buddha spent another <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.4/en/sujato"><b>seven days</b></a> sitting in a single posture at the foot of the Ajapāla Banyan tree experiencing the bliss of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.4/en/sujato">Ud 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a certain "humhumka" brahmin, known for his grumbling or haughty nature, once approached the Buddha to ask what <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.4/en/sujato"><b>qualities</b></a> actually make someone a true brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.4/en/sujato">Ud 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared a true brahmin is one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.4/en/sujato"><b>discarded evil qualities</b></a> and is free from grumbling and stains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.4/en/sujato">Ud 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true brahmin as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.4/en/sujato"><b>self-controlled</b></a> and has reached the end of knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.4/en/sujato">Ud 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, only a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.4/en/sujato"><b>lived the holy life</b></a> can rightly speak a word about Brahman (the supreme)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.4/en/sujato">Ud 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a true brahmin is someone for whom there are no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.4/en/sujato"><b>swellings</b></a> of passion or pride anywhere in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.4/en/sujato">Ud 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha sat at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato"><b>foot of the Bodhi tree</b></a> on the bank of the Nerañjarā River when he first attained full enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato">Ud 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato"><b>clinging</b></a> as the specific condition that leads to existence and the subsequent cycle of birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato">Ud 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that with the cessation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato"><b>birth</b></a>, then aging, death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure, and despair also cease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato">Ud 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's inspired utterance in the Tatiyabodhisutta emphasizes that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato"><b>doubts vanish</b></a> for a brahmin when they understand the nature of phenomena and their causes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.3/en/sujato">Ud 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a monk of brahmin birth asked the Buddha what qualities make a person a true brahmin, the Buddha explained that it is the act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.5/en/sujato"><b>excluding evil qualities</b></a> and wandering always mindful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.5/en/sujato">Ud 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once observed several of his most eminent disciples—including Sāriputta, Mahāmoggallāna, and Mahākassapa—approaching him and referred to all of them as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.5/en/sujato"><b>brahmins</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.5/en/sujato">Ud 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are "buddhas" in the sense of being awakened and have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.5/en/sujato"><b>destroyed their fetters</b></a> are the ones who are truly brahmins in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.5/en/sujato">Ud 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying at Rājagaha, Venerable Mahākassapa resided in the Pipphali Cave while he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.6/en/sujato"><b>afflicted and gravely ill</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.6/en/sujato">Ud 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after recovering from a serious illness, Venerable Mahākassapa <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.6/en/sujato"><b>rejected the offerings</b></a> of five hundred deities who were eager to provide him with alms food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.6/en/sujato">Ud 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha witnessed Venerable Mahākassapa choosing to seek alms specifically in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.6/en/sujato"><b>streets of the poor</b></a>, the wretched, and the weavers, rather than accepting divine offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.6/en/sujato">Ud 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true brahmin as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.6/en/sujato"><b>tamed and established</b></a> in the essential core of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.6/en/sujato">Ud 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized an enlightened person as a brahmin who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.6/en/sujato"><b>destroyed the taints</b></a> (āsavas) and has "vomited out" all faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.6/en/sujato">Ud 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.6/en/sujato"><b>not supporting others</b></a> to describe the spiritual independence of a sage like Mahākassapa, who remains unburdened by social entanglements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.6/en/sujato">Ud 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a true brahmin is someone who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.5/en/sujato"><b>discarded evil qualities</b></a> and lives a life of constant mindfulness and spiritual awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.5/en/sujato">Ud 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while staying at the Jeta Grove, declared Venerable Saṅgāmaji to be a true brahmin because he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato"><b>does not rejoice</b></a> at the arrival of his former wife nor sorrow at her departure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato">Ud 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Saṅgāmaji’s former wife brought their young son to him while he was meditating and asked him to support them, the Buddha observed that the monk remained in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato"><b>perfect silence</b></a> and did not respond even after three requests? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato">Ud 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a>—which is purified and surpasses the human eye—to witness a woman abandoning her young child in front of the monk Saṅgāmaji to test his detachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato">Ud 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato"><b>freed from ties</b></a> (saṅga), specifically referring to the lack of clinging shown by Venerable Saṅgāmaji toward his family, is what he defines as a true brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato">Ud 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed Saṅgāmaji’s former wife realize that the monk was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato"><b>not even interested</b></a> in his own son, which led her to eventually take the child back and leave him in peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato">Ud 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of a liberated person like Saṅgāmaji as someone who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato"><b>neither welcomes</b></a> those who come to him nor laments the departure of those who go? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato">Ud 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while staying in Anāthapiṇḍika’s Monastery, the Buddha witnessed Venerable Saṅgāmaji <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato"><b>conquer the battle</b></a> of domestic ties through his total lack of physical or verbal reaction to his former family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato">Ud 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically noted that Venerable Saṅgāmaji <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato"><b>did not look</b></a> at or speak to the child his wife had left before him, illustrating a mind completely free from worldly identification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.8/en/sujato">Ud 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while staying at Gayā Head, saw many matted-hair ascetics plunging into the river during freezing winter nights believing that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato"><b>purification comes through this</b></a> ritual bathing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato">Ud 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that one is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato"><b>not made pure</b></a> by water, despite the fact that many people bathe in it for religious reasons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato">Ud 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a true brahmin and a pure person as one in whom <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato"><b>truth and Dhamma</b></a> are found, rather than one who relies on external rites? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato">Ud 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encountered matted-hair ascetics at Gayā who were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato"><b>sacrificing to the fire</b></a> and repeatedly immersing themselves in water to achieve spiritual cleansing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato">Ud 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that while "many a person" bathes in water for purification, he taught that only the presence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato"><b>virtue and truth</b></a> makes a person truly clean in the spiritual sense? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato">Ud 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stayed at Gayā during the cold "between-the-eights" winter period and saw ascetics practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato"><b>ascetic immersion</b></a> in the river even as the snow was falling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato">Ud 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, upon seeing the matted-hair ascetics at Gayā, uttered an inspired exclamation declaring that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato"><b>ritual water-bathing</b></a> is insufficient to bring about genuine spiritual purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud1.9/en/sujato">Ud 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was residing in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>Kuṇḍadhāna Wood</b></a> at Kuṇḍikā when a Koliyan woman named Suppavāsā experienced a pregnancy that lasted for seven years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, Suppavāsā the Koliyan daughter suffered through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>obstructed labor</b></a> for seven agonizing days following her seven-year pregnancy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while in intense pain, Suppavāsā reflected that the Buddha teaches the Dhamma specifically for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of suffering</b></a> such as she was experiencing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Suppavāsā the Koliyan daughter found comfort in the thought that the Saṅgha of the Buddha's disciples practices for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>removal of pain</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during her obstructed labor, Suppavāsā praised <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> as a state of supreme happiness where such intense suffering does not exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Suppavāsā sent her husband to the Buddha to offer respects and report her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>severe illness</b></a> and painful labor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave a formal blessing, wishing that Suppavāsā be happy and give birth to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>healthy son</b></a> in good health? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that at the very moment the Buddha spoke his blessing, Suppavāsā gave birth to her son <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>without further difficulty</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Suppavāsā’s husband marveled at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>great power</b></a> and might of the Tathāgata after witnessing his wife's safe delivery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that following her delivery, Suppavāsā invited the Buddha and the Saṅgha to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>seven-day meal</b></a> at her home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna to intervene when another lay follower had already <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>invited the Saṅgha</b></a> for a meal on the same day Suppavāsā wished to start her offering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha prioritized Suppavāsā’s invitation because she had carried her child for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>seven years</b></a> and suffered greatly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a lay follower asked the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna to provide a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>guarantee</b></a> for three things—wealth, life, and faith—before he would agree to postpone his meal for the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna told the lay follower he could guarantee his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>wealth and life</b></a>, but that the man had to be the guarantor of his own faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the lay follower agreed to postpone his meal invitation after Mahāmoggallāna <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>guaranteed</b></a> his life and fortune? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Suppavāsā personally served the Buddha and the Saṅgha with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>choice food</b></a> for seven consecutive days? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Suppavāsā had her newborn son <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>pay respects</b></a> to the Buddha and the entire Saṅgha of monks during the seven-day festival? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta, known as the General of the Dhamma, asked the newborn child if his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>experience in the womb</b></a> was bearable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the newborn son of Suppavāsā described his seven years in the womb as living in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>copper pot</b></a> filled with blood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Suppavāsā was filled with joy and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>elation</b></a> when she saw her young son conversing with the Venerable Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Suppavāsā if she would like to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>another son</b></a> like the one she had just struggled seven years to deliver? And that Suppavāsā told the Buddha she would gladly have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>seven more sons</b></a> like him, despite the intense suffering of her first pregnancy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uttered an inspired verse explaining how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>unattractive</b></a> often appears in the form of the attractive to those who are heedless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>unlovely</b></a> can take the form of the lovely, causing people to misperceive the nature of their experiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that suffering often overcomes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>heedless person</b></a> by masquerading as happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s disciple <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>Mahāmoggallāna</b></a> was used as a mediator to settle meal invitations between two different lay followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the child born to Suppavāsā was able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>speak and reason</b></a> with the Venerable Sāriputta shortly after his birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the story of Suppavāsā to illustrate how people can remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>attached</b></a> to things that cause them great suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>General of the Dhamma</b></a> is a title used in the sources to refer to the Buddha's chief disciple, Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a Buddha’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato"><b>inspired utterance</b></a> highlights how the appearance of pleasure can mask the reality of pain for those who are not mindful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud2.8/en/sujato">Ud 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was residing at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>Anāthapiṇḍika’s Monastery</b></a> in Jeta’s Grove when his half-brother, Venerable Nanda, first admitted to other monks that he was dissatisfied with the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's half-brother Nanda informed his fellow monks that he was unable to endure the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> and intended to return to the lower life of a layperson? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the primary reason Nanda struggled with his monastic training was his memory of a Sakyan girl, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>"Belle of the Country"</b></a>, who had asked him to return home quickly as he was leaving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his psychic power to take Venerable Nanda to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>Tāvatiṃsa heaven</b></a>, appearing there as instantly as a strong man might stretch out his folded arm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while in the Tāvatiṃsa heaven, the Buddha showed Nanda five hundred <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>dove-footed nymphs</b></a> who were serving Sakka, the ruler of the devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Nanda to compare the beauty of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan girl</b></a> he desired to the beauty of the five hundred celestial nymphs he saw in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nanda told the Buddha that, compared to the celestial nymphs, the beautiful Sakyan girl looked like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>mutilated monkey</b></a> with its ears and nose cut off? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave Nanda a personal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>guarantee</b></a> that he would obtain those five hundred dove-footed nymphs if he would continue to find delight in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nanda explicitly told the Buddha he would continue to practice the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> solely because of the Buddha's promise that he would receive celestial nymphs as a result? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the other monks heard that Nanda was practicing for the sake of nymphs, they began to mock him by calling him a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>"hireling"</b></a> of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Nanda’s fellow monks ridiculed him as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>"menial"</b></a> because they believed he was living the spiritual life only to gain a reward of heavenly women? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s plan to motivate Nanda through heavenly rewards eventually led Nanda to feel <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>disgusted and humiliated</b></a> by the criticism of his fellow monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after being mocked by his peers, Nanda lived <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>secluded and ardent</b></a>, eventually reaching the supreme goal of the holy life through his own direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>deity</b></a> visited the Buddha in the middle of the night to report that Venerable Nanda had attained liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha knew through his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>encompassed mind</b></a> that Nanda had attained Arahantship even before a deity came to inform him of the news? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that once Nanda attained enlightenment, he approached the Buddha and asked to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>released</b></a> from the Buddha’s earlier promise regarding the five hundred nymphs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uttered an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>inspired verse</b></a> describing a monk who has successfully crossed the mire and crushed the "thorn of sensual desire"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who has reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>destruction of delusion</b></a> remains unshaken by both pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nanda was not only a disciple but also the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>maternal aunt’s son</b></a> and half-brother of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s promise of nymphs is used in the sources to show how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>sensual motivation</b></a> can be redirected toward the ultimate goal of spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when a monk's mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>liberated from taints</b></a>, he is naturally freed from all worldly or celestial bonds and promises? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato"><b>hireling</b></a> (bhatako) was specifically used by monks as a derogatory term for Nanda because he practiced the holy life for a "wage" of celestial nymphs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.2/en/sujato">Ud 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the chaotic noise made by five hundred visiting monks to the sound of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>fishermen</b></a> hauling in a large catch of fish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>dismissed</b></a> five hundred monks from his presence at Jeta's Grove because their loud behavior while settling in was inappropriate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the five hundred monks led by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>Yasoja</b></a> traveled to the Vajjian country to practice in seclusion after being sent away by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the 500 monks who were dismissed by the Buddha settled in leaf huts along the banks of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>Vaggumudā River</b></a> to spend their rains retreat in diligent practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Yasoja exhorted his fellow monks to practice with such dedication that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>Buddha would be pleased</b></a> with their conduct despite their initial mistake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that all five hundred monks who were dismissed by the Buddha attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>three true knowledges</b></a> during a single rains retreat by the Vaggumudā River? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying at Vesālī, he told the Venerable Ānanda that the direction where the monks were staying appeared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>radiant and luminous</b></a> to his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the messenger monk sent by the Buddha traveled by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>psychic power</b></a>, vanishing from the Great Wood and appearing before the monks by the river as fast as a man stretches his arm? And that the five hundred monks responded to the Buddha's summons by using their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>psychic power</b></a> to vanish from their river dwellings and appear instantly before him in Vesālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon their arrival in Vesālī, the five hundred monks found the Buddha sitting in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>imperturbable concentration</b></a> and immediately entered that same state themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remained in deep silence even during the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>middle watch</b></a> of the night, despite the Venerable Ānanda's second request for him to acknowledge the visiting monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed Ānanda that both he and all five hundred monks had spent the entire night sitting together in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>imperturbable concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the Venerable Ānanda that if he had truly understood the state of the monks' minds, he would not have even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>thought to speak</b></a> or interrupt their meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uttered an inspired verse describing a monk who has successfully conquered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>thorn of sensual desire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who stands <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>unshaken</b></a> by pleasure or pain is like a motionless mountain standing firm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who has conquered sensual desire is also unaffected by abuse, physical harm, or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>imprisonment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's dismissal of the noisy monks was done out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>compassion</b></a> and a desire for their long-term spiritual welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monks recognized that the Buddha was dwelling in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>imperturbable state</b></a> and realized that was the appropriate meditation for them to join him in? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>tisso vijjā</b></a> refers to the three true knowledges—recollection of past lives, knowledge of the passing away and rebirth of beings, and knowledge of the destruction of the taints—attained by all five hundred monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that an enlightened monk does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato"><b>tremble</b></a> when faced with the fluctuations of worldly conditions like pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.3/en/sujato">Ud 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Venerable Sāriputta as sitting nearby with his legs crossed, his body erect, and his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.4/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness established</b></a> in front of him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.4/en/sujato">Ud 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who has achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.4/en/sujato"><b>destruction of delusion</b></a> to a rock mountain that remains unshakable and well-settled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.4/en/sujato">Ud 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is firm like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.4/en/sujato"><b>rock mountain</b></a> does not tremble, a state reached through the ending of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.4/en/sujato">Ud 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed the Venerable Mahāmoggallāna sitting nearby with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.5/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> well-established within him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.5/en/sujato">Ud 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.5/en/sujato"><b>restrained</b></a> in the six bases of contact and maintains body-centered mindfulness might realize his own Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.5/en/sujato">Ud 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.5/en/sujato"><b>constantly concentrated</b></a> and possesses established mindfulness is capable of direct spiritual knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.5/en/sujato">Ud 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was residing at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.5/en/sujato"><b>Jeta’s Grove</b></a> in Sāvatthī when he uttered an inspired verse regarding Mahāmoggallāna's internal mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.5/en/sujato">Ud 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was approached by several monks who complained that the Venerable Pilindavaccha was addressing them with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato"><b>term "outcast"</b></a> (vasala)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato">Ud 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, after reviewing the past lives of Pilindavaccha, revealed that the monk had been born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato"><b>brahmin family</b></a> for five hundred consecutive births? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato">Ud 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Pilindavaccha’s use of the word "outcast" was not due to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato"><b>malicious heart</b></a> but was a long-standing habit from his many previous lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato">Ud 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato"><b>habitual behavior</b></a> practiced over a very long time can remain in a person's speech even after they have attained spiritual purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato">Ud 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato"><b>true brahmin</b></a> as someone in whom no deceit or conceit dwells and who is entirely free from greed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato">Ud 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true monk as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato"><b>without "mine-making"</b></a> (amamo) and who has no longings or expectations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato">Ud 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato"><b>Bamboo Grove</b></a> in Rājagaha when he addressed the issue of a monk's seemingly offensive language? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato">Ud 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato"><b>dispelled anger</b></a> and whose inner self is "extinguished" or cooled (abhinibbutatto) is a true recluse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato">Ud 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha sent another monk to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato"><b>summon</b></a> the Venerable Pilindavaccha to investigate the reports of his "outcast" talk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato">Ud 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged the monks not to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato"><b>offended</b></a> by Pilindavaccha's speech, as it was simply a result of long-practiced conduct from his past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato">Ud 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato"><b>vītalobho</b></a> (free from greed) to characterize the state of a true brahmin and monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato">Ud 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is free from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato"><b>deceit and conceit</b></a> is the one who truly earns the titles of brahmin, recluse, and monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.6/en/sujato">Ud 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>Bamboo Grove</b></a> in Rājagaha while the Venerable Mahākassapa was residing at the Pipphali Cave, where he spent seven days in a single meditative posture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how five hundred deities were eager to provide <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>alms food</b></a> to Mahākassapa upon his emergence from meditation, yet he rejected them to seek alms in the streets of Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, Sakka, the ruler of the devas, disguised himself as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>weaver</b></a> to successfully offer alms to Mahākassapa when the monk would not accept food from other deities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that Sujā, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>Asura maiden</b></a> and wife of Sakka, assisted her husband by filling the weaver's shuttle while he carried out his disguise to offer food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how Mahākassapa received a meal from a disguised Sakka that was extraordinarily rich, consisting of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>various flavors</b></a> and many different soups and sauces? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Mahākassapa recognized the giver was actually Sakka because of the "potency of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>psychic power</b></a>" and the divine majesty reflected in the alms food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recorded how Mahākassapa addressed Sakka by the clan name <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>"Kosiya"</b></a> when rebuking him for using a disguise to perform an act of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka told Mahākassapa that even deities like himself have a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>need for merit</b></a> and that performing meritorious deeds is something they are responsible for doing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha heard Sakka flying through the sky and shouting three times that his gift was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>"supreme gift"</b></a> because it had been accepted by the Venerable Mahākassapa? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>divine ear-element</b></a>, which is purified and surpasses human hearing, to listen to Sakka’s inspired exclamation from the air? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uttered an inspired verse stating that even the devas envy a monk who lives on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>alms food</b></a> and is self-reliant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is peaceful and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>always mindful</b></a> is the kind of person whom the gods hold in high esteem? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an ideal monk as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>self-reliant</b></a> (attabharassa) and does not have the burden of nourishing others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even a high-ranking deity like Sakka, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato"><b>ruler of devas</b></a>, values the opportunity to earn merit by serving enlightened disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud3.7/en/sujato">Ud 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while staying at Kusinārā in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato"><b>Upavattana Sal Grove</b></a> of the Mallas, the Buddha observed a group of monks who were restless, puffed up, and talkative? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato">Ud 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha once described a group of monks as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato"><b>loose-tongued</b></a>, muddle-headed, and lacking mindfulness because they had wandering minds and uncontrolled faculties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato">Ud 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that an individual with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato"><b>unguarded body</b></a> and wrong view falls under the power of Mara, the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato">Ud 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> according to the Buddha, a person who is overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> and governed by wrong view is essentially walking into the control of Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato">Ud 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that a monk with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato"><b>guarded mind</b></a>, who dwells within the range of right intention and right view, can successfully abandon all miserable states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato">Ud 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that spiritual progress requires prioritizing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> and maintaining an understanding of the arising and passing away of all things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato">Ud 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha noted that monks with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato"><b>uncontrolled faculties</b></a> and wandering minds were unable to maintain the concentration necessary for their training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato">Ud 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha was once traveling through the Kosalan country with a large <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato"><b>Sangha of monks</b></a> when he pulled off the road to rest at the root of a tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato">Ud 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> a certain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato"><b>cowherd</b></a> once approached the Buddha, paid homage, and was subsequently instructed and gladdened by the Buddha's Dhamma talk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato">Ud 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> a cowherd prepared a special meal of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato"><b>thick milk rice</b></a> and fresh ghee in his own home to offer to the Buddha and the community of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato">Ud 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha accepted the cowherd's invitation for a meal through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato"><b>silence</b></a>, which was his traditional way of consenting to such requests? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato">Ud 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> a cowherd <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato"><b>personally served</b></a> the Buddha and the Sangha with his own hands, ensuring they were fully satisfied with the milk rice and fresh ghee he had prepared? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato">Ud 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> shortly after the Buddha finished his meal and departed, the generous cowherd was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato"><b>murdered</b></a> by another man at a boundary? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato">Ud 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha was informed by a group of monks that the cowherd who had just provided them with a meal had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato"><b>deprived of life</b></a> by a man between boundaries? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato">Ud 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha declared that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato"><b>wrongly directed mind</b></a> can cause a person even more harm than an enemy could ever do to another enemy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato">Ud 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that whatever harm one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato"><b>hater</b></a> may do to another hater, a wrongly directed mind does even worse harm to itself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato">Ud 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha’s inspired utterance following the cowherd's death emphasized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato"><b>internal danger</b></a> of a wrongly directed mind over the external danger of an enemy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.3/en/sujato">Ud 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha warned that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato"><b>muddle-headed</b></a> and lacking mindfulness were traits of monks who were not properly concentrated or composed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato">Ud 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that a monk can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato"><b>abandon all miserable states</b></a> by guarding his mind and clearly knowing the arising and passing away of things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.2/en/sujato">Ud 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Venerable Sāriputta was sitting in the open air on a moonlit night with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>freshly shaved head</b></a>, a malicious yakkha struck him with a blow so powerful it could have split a mountain peak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once recounted how a yakkha, after ignoring three warnings from his companion and striking a powerful monk, immediately fell into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>great hell</b></a> while crying out, “I am burning!”? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahāmoggallāna used his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> to witness a yakkha deliver a massive blow to the head of Venerable Sāriputta while the latter was deep in meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite being hit with a force capable of felling a giant elephant, Venerable Sāriputta told Venerable Mahāmoggallāna that he was comfortable and only had a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>slight headache</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, using his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>divine ear element</b></a>, overheard a conversation between his two chief disciples where they expressed mutual admiration for each other's psychic powers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Mahāmoggallāna marveled at his companion's endurance, Venerable Sāriputta modestly replied that he was so lacking in power he could not even see a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>mud-sprite</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a well-developed mind to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>rock</b></a>, stating that such a mind stands steady and does not tremble when faced with suffering or provocation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mind that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>detached</b></a> from things that provoke lust and remains unperturbed by things that provoke anger will never be reached by suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a yakkha's blow to a monk in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> was described by the Buddha as being large enough to sink a giant elephant seven or eight cubits tall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once observed two "Great Nāgas" (referring to Sāriputta and Moggallāna) and celebrated their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>conversation</b></a> regarding a supernatural attack by uttering an inspired verse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while staying at Pigeon's Cave, a malicious spirit was warned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>three times</b></a> by his friend not to assault a monk of great authority and psychic power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>steady like a mountain</b></a> does not shake or get angry at things that are normally considered provocative? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta’s survival of a supernatural assault was considered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>wonderful and marvelous</b></a> event by his fellow monk, Venerable Mahāmoggallāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a mind that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>well-cultivated</b></a> through meditation becomes impervious to the types of pain that would destroy an ordinary person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that two yakkha friends were traveling from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>north to the south</b></a> when they encountered the meditating Venerable Sāriputta and one of them decided to attack him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the mind that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>unmoved</b></a> as a primary defense against the arising of suffering in one's life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while stay at the Bamboo Grove in Rājagaha, the Buddha used his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>psychic abilities</b></a> to perceive a conversation happening between two of his disciples at a distant cave? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a yakkha was described as being of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>great psychic power</b></a> himself, yet his attack failed to cause significant harm to a monk who had mastered his own mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s inspired utterance in the Yakkhapahāra Sutta emphasizes that a mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>free from anger</b></a> is a state of being that is deeply developed and stable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even though Venerable Sāriputta was sitting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato"><b>unguarded</b></a> in an open space, his internal mental state of concentration acted as a profound protection against physical trauma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.4/en/sujato">Ud 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once left <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>Ghosita’s Park</b></a> in Kosambi because he felt crowded and uncomfortable living among monks, nuns, laypeople, kings, and even wandering sectarians? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha found it <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>uncomfortable</b></a> and lacking in ease to live surrounded by a large multitude, which prompted his decision to seek solitude away from the crowd? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha departed for his solitary retreat <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>alone and unannounced</b></a>, without informing his personal attendant or the community of monks about his departure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after leaving Kosambi, the Buddha settled in Pālileyyaka at the foot of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>beautiful sal tree</b></a> in the Protected Forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a certain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>bull elephant</b></a> also felt crowded by his herd and decided to seek the same Protected Forest where the Buddha was residing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha noted that a bull elephant suffered because the other elephants in his herd would eat the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>broken-off branches</b></a> he had laboriously prepared for himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha observed that a bull elephant was forced to drink <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>turbid water</b></a> because the rest of the herd would muddy the water sources whenever he went to drink? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the bull elephant chose to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>leave the herd</b></a> to find peace and seclusion, mirroring the Buddha’s own choice to leave the crowded human community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while in the Protected Forest, the bull elephant attended to the Buddha by using his trunk to provide both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>drinking and washing water</b></a> for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while deep in solitary meditation, reflected that he was now living <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>in comfort and ease</b></a> specifically because he was no longer crowded by various groups of people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the bull elephant in the Protected Forest reflected that he was finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>happy and at ease</b></a> because he could eat untrampled grass and drink clear water away from his herd? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his own mind to perceive the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>thoughts and reflections</b></a> of the bull elephant who was looking after him in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the elephant as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>tusked one</b></a> whose mind harmonized with his own in their mutual appreciation for solitary living? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha declared that the mind of a "Great One" (nāga) meets the mind of another <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>Great One</b></a> when they both find joy in the seclusion of the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while living in the forest, the bull elephant would use his trunk to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>clear the grass</b></a> around the area where the Buddha was staying to make it more suitable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha remarked that living <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>apart from the crowd</b></a> allowed both him and the elephant to escape the stress and misery caused by constant social interaction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha experienced a profound sense of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>un-crowded</b></a> once he reached Pālileyyaka, a sharp contrast to his previous life in Kosambi? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized both himself and the bull elephant as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>solitary</b></a> in their shared preference for the peaceful forest over a crowded existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's inspired utterance in the Nāga Sutta highlights how his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a> was in complete agreement with the elephant's mind regarding the delight of being alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.5/en/sujato">Ud 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was residing in Sāvatthī at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>Anāthapiṇḍika’s Monastery</b></a> when he and his community of monks were highly honored and provided with the four requisites, causing jealousy among other sectarians? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha and the Sangha received abundant offerings of robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicinal requisites, while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>other sectarians</b></a> were neglected and received no such honors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how jealous sectarians approached a female wanderer named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>Sundarī</b></a> and manipulated her into helping them discredit the Buddhist monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by sectarians to help her "relatives," Sundarī replied that she was willing to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>sacrifice her life</b></a> for their benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sectarians instructed Sundarī to visit <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>Jeta’s Grove</b></a> frequently so that people would see her constantly heading toward the Buddha’s monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Sundarī was seen by many people visiting the grove, the sectarians <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>murdered her</b></a> and hid her body in a ditch near Jeta’s Grove to frame the Buddha’s disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sectarians went to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>King Pasenadi of Kosala</b></a> to report that Sundarī was missing and specifically suggested searching Jeta’s Grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after "finding" Sundarī's body in the ditch, the sectarians paraded her on a litter through the streets of Sāvatthī to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>incite the public</b></a> against the monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sectarians accused the Buddha’s disciples of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>shameless</b></a>, immoral, and liars, claiming they murdered a woman after engaging in sexual misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s monks were subjected to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>harsh verbal abuse</b></a> and reviling from the people of Sāvatthī who believed the false accusations spread by the sectarians? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the monks reported the public hostility to him, the Buddha predicted that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>uproar</b></a> would not last long and would vanish after exactly seven days? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed his monks to respond to their accusers with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>specific verse</b></a> about the karmic consequences of lying? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>speaks untruth</b></a> goes to hell, as does one who denies having committed an action they actually performed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that people of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>base conduct</b></a>, such as liars and murderers, meet the same miserable fate in the next world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as the monks began reciting the Buddha's verse in response to the abuse, the people of Sāvatthī realized the monks were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>innocent</b></a> and had not committed the crime? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the false rumor against the Sangha disappeared after <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>seven days</b></a>, exactly as the Buddha had prophesied? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared unrestrained people who use harsh speech to soldiers in battle shooting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>arrows</b></a> at a war elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk should endure harsh words from others with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>uncorrupted mind</b></a>, just as an elephant in battle endures arrows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained</b></a> sting others with their speech, making patience a vital quality for a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>inspired utterance</b></a> to describe the verses he spoke after realizing the full significance of the events surrounding Sundarī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sectarians were described as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>unable to endure</b></a> the success and honor bestowed upon the Buddha and the Sangha, leading to their desperate plot? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>non-celibate</b></a> (abrahmacārino) was a false label being used by the public to attack the virtuous Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even when being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>reviled and harassed</b></a>, a monk must maintain his composure and spiritual integrity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato"><b>honored and respected</b></a> by society as the catalyst for the sectarian's malicious conspiracy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.8/en/sujato">Ud 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while meditating in seclusion at the Bamboo Grove, Venerable Upasena Vaṅgantaputta reflected on his great fortune to have the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato"><b>Buddha as his teacher</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato">Ud 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha confirmed that a wise person who has "seen the state" does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato"><b>grieve at death</b></a> because their life is no longer scorched by the fires of defilement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato">Ud 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk with a peaceful mind and destroyed rebirth as someone who has successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato"><b>cut off craving</b></a> for existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato">Ud 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> while staying at the Bamboo Grove in Rājagaha, the Buddha used his mind to perceive the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato"><b>private thoughts</b></a> of Venerable Upasena as he reflected on his successful spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato">Ud 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> Venerable Upasena considered it a great gain that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato"><b>spiritual companions</b></a> were virtuous and possessed of good character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato">Ud 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha noted that for an arahant like Upasena, who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato"><b>possessed of great power</b></a> and influence, both life and death are considered excellent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato">Ud 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha observed Venerable Sāriputta sitting in Jeta’s Grove with a straight body, focused on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.10/en/sujato"><b>reviewing his own inner peace</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.10/en/sujato">Ud 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who has a peaceful mind and has cut the "guide-cable" of craving is completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.10/en/sujato"><b>freed from Mara’s bonds</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.10/en/sujato">Ud 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha stated that when a monk's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.10/en/sujato"><b>wandering in rebirth</b></a> is exhausted, they have truly attained the state of one who is internally at peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.10/en/sujato">Ud 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> Venerable Sāriputta was sitting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.10/en/sujato"><b>near the Buddha</b></a> in Sāvatthī when the Buddha recognized his profound state of composure and uttered an inspired verse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.10/en/sujato">Ud 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> Queen Mallikā told King Pasenadi of Kosala that after careful reflection, she could find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato"><b>no one dearer</b></a> to her than her own self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato">Ud 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> King Pasenadi of Kosala admitted to Queen Mallikā that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato"><b>he too</b></a> loved himself more than anyone else in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato">Ud 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that after searching all directions with the mind, one will find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato"><b>nothing dearer than self</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato">Ud 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha used the conversation between King Pasenadi and Queen Mallikā to explain that because the self is dear to everyone, one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato"><b>should not harm others</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato">Ud 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> King Pasenadi sought out the Buddha at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato"><b>Anāthapiṇḍika’s Monastery</b></a> specifically to report a private conversation he had with his queen regarding the nature of self-love? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato">Ud 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha emphasized that for one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato"><b>loves themselves</b></a>, the logical spiritual conclusion is to refrain from hurting other beings who also value their own lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato">Ud 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha described a monk with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.10/en/sujato"><b>peaceful mind</b></a> as one for whom the stressful cycle of birth and death has been completely exhausted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.10/en/sujato">Ud 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> Venerable Upasena Vaṅgantaputta rejoiced in the fact that he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato"><b>perfected in virtue</b></a> and possessed a one-pointed, well-concentrated mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud4.9/en/sujato">Ud 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha uttered an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato"><b>inspired verse</b></a> after King Pasenadi related how both he and the queen realized that their own selves were the most precious things to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud5.1/en/sujato">Ud 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that anyone who has fully developed and cultivated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>four bases of power</b></a> could, if they so desired, remain alive for an entire aeon or for the remainder of an aeon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was staying at the Cāpāla Shrine, he consciously and mindfully decided to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>relinquish the life-formation</b></a>, determining that his final passing would occur in three months? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s act of relinquishing his will to live caused a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>great earthquake</b></a> that was described as terrifying and hair-raising, accompanied by the bursting of celestial drums? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave a broad hint to Ven. Ānanda about his ability to live for an aeon, but Ānanda failed to request him to stay because his mind was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>possessed by Māra</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Māra the Evil One that he would not attain final Nibbāna until his monks were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>competent and well-trained</b></a>, capable of explaining the Dhamma and refuting counter-doctrines? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked on the beauty of several locations in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>Vesālī</b></a>, including the Udena, Gotamaka, Sattamba, Bahuputta, Sārandada, and Cāpāla shrines? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refused to pass away until he was certain that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> (the Sasana) was successful, prosperous, widespread, and well-proclaimed among both gods and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Māra to be at ease because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>final passing</b></a> of the Tathāgata would take place exactly three months from the time of their conversation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the relinquishing of the "formation of being" to a sage breaking his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>shell of existence</b></a> like a coat of mail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha insisted his female lay followers must become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>capable disciples</b></a>, learned and well-versed in the Dhamma, before he would consider entering parinibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a sage who is inwardly delighted and concentrated is able to give up the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>formation of being</b></a>, whether measurable or immeasurable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha required his disciples to be able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>rightly refute</b></a> any regular counter-doctrine that might arise, using the Dhamma to demonstrate its wonders? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Ven. Ānanda to take a sitting mat and accompany him to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>Cāpāla Shrine</b></a> for his "day-abiding" or afternoon meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra the Evil One approached the Buddha and specifically urged him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>attain parinibbāna</b></a> now, claiming that all the conditions the Buddha previously set for his passing had been met? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's monks are expected to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>bearers of the Dhamma</b></a> (dhammadharā) who practice in accordance with the teachings and follow the proper path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha repeated his statement about the Four Bases of Power to Ven. Ānanda <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>three times</b></a>, yet Ānanda still did not perceive the hint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>nuns</b></a> (bhikkhuniyo) must also be competent, trained, and courageous in the Dhamma before he would depart from the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even while setting aside his life-formation, the Buddha was described as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>mindful and clearly comprehending</b></a> (sato sampajāno)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Māra that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> had become "prosperous and widespread" and was now well-proclaimed among the masses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized his male lay followers as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>competent disciples</b></a> who should be capable of teaching and explaining the Dhamma to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Ven. Ānanda was eventually told by the Buddha to go and sit at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>foot of a tree</b></a> nearby after failing to ask the Buddha to stay? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s inspired utterance at the Cāpāla Shrine describes the sage as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>concentrated</b></a> (samāhito) while letting go of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>Sārandada Shrine</b></a> as one of the delightful places located in Vesālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra used the Buddha's own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>past words</b></a> as an argument to convince him that it was finally time for him to pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s disciples are tasked with taking what they have learned from their teacher and making it <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>clear and open</b></a> for others to understand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the earth-shaking event following his decision as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>terrifying</b></a> (bhiṃsanako)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that his monks should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>courageous</b></a> (visāradā) and well-versed in the scriptures before his work was considered complete? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Māra that the Tathāgata would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>pass away</b></a> with the passing of three months? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that his teaching should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato"><b>popularized</b></a> (bāhujaññaṃ) and well-proclaimed among all people before his departure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.1/en/sujato">Ud 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while King Pasenadi was visiting the Buddha, he saw thirty-five ascetics of various sects—including matted-hair ascetics, Nigaṇṭhas, and naked ascetics—passing by with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>long hair and nails</b></a> while carrying their equipment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala showed such deep respect to a passing group of ascetics that he knelt on one knee and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>announced his name</b></a> three times to them before returning to sit with the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi asked the Buddha if the groups of passing matted-hair ascetics and wanderers were among those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>Arahants</b></a> or those who have entered the path to Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained it is difficult for a layman who enjoys sense pleasures and uses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>gold and silver</b></a> to accurately judge whether someone else has attained Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught King Pasenadi that a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> is to be known by living together with them, and even then, only after a long time and by one who is attentive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>purity</b></a> is to be known through one's dealings and transactions with them, provided one observes them for a long period with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>fortitude</b></a> is only truly known in times of adversity, and not by someone who is lacking in wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> is to be known through discussion and conversation, but it requires a long duration of observation to be certain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told King Pasenadi that judging spiritual attainments requires one to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>attentive</b></a> rather than inattentive, and wise rather than dull-witted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after listening to the Buddha's criteria for judging others, King Pasenadi revealed that the ascetics he had just honored were actually his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>spies</b></a> returning from gathering intelligence in the countryside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi explained that his secret agents would soon wash off the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>dust and dirt</b></a> of their travels, put on white clothes, and return to enjoying the five strands of sense pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, upon understanding the true nature of the King's spies, gave an inspired utterance advising that a person should not be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>another's man</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that one should not live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>depending on another</b></a> or trade in the Dhamma for personal gain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a person who lives in a home <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>crowded with children</b></a> and enjoys fine Kāsī sandalwood faces obstacles in recognizing true spiritual experts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's criteria for assessing someone's character explicitly requires a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>long time</b></a> and cannot be determined by a brief or superficial encounter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi's spies were disguised as five different types of renunciants, including <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>single-garment ascetics</b></a> and wanderers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that true virtue, purity, fortitude, and wisdom are traits that must be observed by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>wise person</b></a> rather than one who is foolish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala praised the Buddha's teaching as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato"><b>wonderful and marvelous</b></a> because it correctly identified the difficulty he faced in judging the spiritual quality of his own men? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.2/en/sujato">Ud 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed various sectarians in Sāvatthī who were "blind and eyeless," meaning they did not know the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>beneficial and the harmful</b></a> or the Dhamma and what is not the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described sectarians who held the view that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>world is eternal</b></a>, asserting that this alone is the truth and everything else is empty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted some recluses and brahmins argued that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>world is not eternal</b></a>, leading to constant disputes and quarrels with those of opposing views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned thinkers who believed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>world is finite</b></a>, while others insisted with equal certainty that the world is infinite? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a group of sectarians who claimed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>soul and the body</b></a> are the same thing, while another group argued they are entirely different? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained how various teachers quarreled over whether a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata exists after death</b></a>, does not exist, both exists and does not, or neither exists nor does not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>mouth-swords</b></a> to describe how sectarians would wound one another with their bitter and divisive arguments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told a story about a former king of Sāvatthī who ordered a man to assemble all the people in the city who had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>blind from birth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how a king instructed that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>elephant</b></a> be presented to a group of blind men, with each man being allowed to feel only one specific part of the animal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the blind men who felt the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>elephant's head</b></a> told the king that an elephant was exactly like a water jar? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how the blind men who felt the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>elephant's ear</b></a> insisted to the king that the animal was like a winnowing basket? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the blind men who touched the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>elephant's tusk</b></a> were convinced that an elephant was nothing other than a plowshare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared that the blind men who felt the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>elephant's trunk</b></a> described the beast to the king as being like a plow pole? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted the blind men who felt the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>elephant's body</b></a> were certain that an elephant was shaped like a granary? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those blind men who felt the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>elephant's foot</b></a> reported to the king that the animal was like a pillar? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recounted how the blind men who felt the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>elephant's hindquarters</b></a> claimed the animal was just like a mortar? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said the blind men who felt the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>elephant's tail</b></a> described it as being like a pestle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned the blind men who felt the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>tuft of the tail</b></a> were convinced an elephant was like a broom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the blind men in the parable as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>fighting with their fists</b></a> because they each believed their own limited experience was the only truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that the king in the parable was actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>delighted</b></a> by the spectacle of the blind men fighting over their different perceptions of the elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the sectarians in Sāvatthī to the blind men in the parable because they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>attached</b></a> to their own partial views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>one-sided vision</b></a> (ekaṅgadassino) to describe people who only see a portion of the truth and then argue that it is the whole? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the sectarians were "blind and eyeless" specifically because they did not know <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>the Dhamma</b></a> or what constitutes a lack of Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's inspired utterance (Udāna) for this sutta highlights how some people <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>quarrel and dispute</b></a> when they are stuck in their own limited perspectives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the sectarians as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>obsessed with their own views</b></a> (nānādiṭṭhinissayanissitā), which led them into endless conflict? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that while the blind men shouted "the elephant is like this, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>not like that</b></a>," they were all technically correct about the small part they had touched? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even though the blind men were shouting and fighting, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>truth of the elephant</b></a> was something that none of them fully understood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that sectarians <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>wound one another</b></a> because they fail to see the overarching reality that encompasses all their partial truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching on the blind men and the elephant serves as a warning against being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato"><b>stubbornly attached</b></a> to personal opinions without understanding the full scope of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.4/en/sujato">Ud 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described various sectarians as being "blind and eyeless" because they fail to recognize what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>beneficial and harmful</b></a>, as well as what is Dhamma and what is not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted some recluses and brahmins held the dogmatic view that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>self and the world</b></a> are eternal, asserting that this alone is the truth and everything else is empty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified certain thinkers who argued that the self and the world are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>not eternal</b></a>, leading them into constant quarrels with those holding opposing views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed teachers who claimed that the self and the world are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>self-made</b></a>, while others insisted they are created by another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a philosophical position where the self and the world are considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>fortuitously arisen</b></a>, meaning they are neither self-made nor made by another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified sectarians who believed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>pleasure and pain</b></a> (sukhadukkhaṃ) are eternal, while others argued they are transient and not eternal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned some recluses believed pleasure and pain were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>self-made</b></a>, whereas others argued they were the result of someone else's actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described sectarians who held the view that the self and the world are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>both eternal and not eternal</b></a> at the same time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed recluses who claimed the self and the world are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>neither eternal nor not eternal</b></a>, rejecting both categorical positions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha witnessed various teachers wounding one another with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>mouth-swords</b></a>, which refers to the sharp and bitter verbal arguments they used to defend their conflicting doctrines? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after walking for alms in Sāvatthī, a group of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>monks</b></a> approached the Buddha to report the disputes and various views they had encountered among the city's religious practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that because sectarians do not know the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a>, they live in a state of constant contention, arguing about whose teaching is correct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that people who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>attached to views</b></a> often fall into quarrels and disputes because they cling to their own preferences and inclinations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, some thinkers believed that pleasure and pain were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>both self-made and other-made</b></a>, combining two different theories of causality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a group who held that pleasure and pain are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>fortuitously arisen</b></a>, occurring without any specific cause from oneself or another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that wanderers of other sects are "blind and eyeless" specifically because they lack <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>spiritual vision</b></a> and do not understand the true nature of things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that sectarians would often shout at one another, saying "The Dhamma is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>like this</b></a>, it is not like that!" as they fought over their respective beliefs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an inspired utterance to warn that those who get <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>stuck</b></a> in speculative views will flounder before they ever reach the end of the "flood" of darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s description of sectarians as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>nānādiṭṭhinissayanissitā</b></a> means they are completely dependent upon and supported by their various speculative views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s teaching implies that true understanding requires one to move beyond being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>quarrelsome and disputatious</b></a> regarding philosophical theories? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that some recluses and brahmins <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato"><b>sink in the middle</b></a> of their spiritual journey because they remain obsessed with attachment to their own viewpoints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.5/en/sujato">Ud 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was staying at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>Anāthapiṇḍika’s Monastery</b></a> in Jeta’s Grove when he was informed about the many different sectarian teachers in Sāvatthī who were constantly arguing over their conflicting views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the various recluses and brahmins of Sāvatthī as being "blind and eyeless" because they did not know the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>beneficial and the harmful</b></a> or the difference between Dhamma and non-Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed sectarians who held the dogmatic view that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>self and the world</b></a> are eternal, claiming that this alone is the truth and all other views are empty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, some religious thinkers in Sāvatthī insisted that the self and the world are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>not eternal</b></a>, which led them into bitter disputes with those who believed the opposite? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a group of teachers who claimed the self and the world are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>both eternal and not eternal</b></a>, attempting to combine two contradictory philosophical positions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted some sectarians argued the self and the world are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>neither eternal nor not eternal</b></a>, rejecting both standard categories of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the view held by some recluses that the self and the world are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>self-made</b></a> (sayaṅkato), meaning they are created by one's own actions or essence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned thinkers who believed the self and the world are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>made by another</b></a> (paraṅkato), attributing existence to an external creator or force? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a philosophical position where the self and the world are considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>both self-made and made by another</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of sectarians who argued that the self and the world are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>fortuitously arisen</b></a>, meaning they are neither self-made nor made by another but occur by chance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that some brahmins held the view that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>pleasure and pain</b></a> (sukhadukkhaṃ) along with the self and the world are eternal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the view that pleasure and pain are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>self-made</b></a>, a theory where one's current experiences of happiness or suffering are entirely produced by oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified thinkers who claimed that pleasure and pain are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>made by another</b></a>, suggesting that our internal states are governed by external agents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that sectarians in Sāvatthī would wound one another with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>mouth-swords</b></a>, which was his metaphor for their sharp and aggressive verbal arguments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that wanderers of other sects are "blind" because they do not understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a>, leading them to live in constant contention and strife? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that people who are obsessed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>I-making</b></a> (ahaṅkāra) and "other-making" are unable to see the truth of their situation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an inspired utterance to point out that most people do not see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>dart</b></a> (salla), which refers to the hidden source of their suffering and attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one who sees the "dart" of suffering, the thought "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>I am the doer</b></a>" or "another is the doer" does not occur? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that humanity is bound by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>fetter of pride</b></a> (mānupetā), which keeps them tied to their speculative views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that those who are obsessed with their views and engage in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>contentious talk</b></a> cannot transcend the cycle of saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was approached by a group of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>monks</b></a> who had just returned from alms-round and were troubled by the constant fighting they saw among the city's various religious groups? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized the sectarians as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato"><b>dependent on views</b></a> (nānādiṭṭhinissayanissitā), which caused them to argue that "The Dhamma is like this, it is not like that"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.6/en/sujato">Ud 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda observed that other <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>sectarians</b></a> are honored and receive requisites like robes and almsfood only as long as Fully Enlightened Ones do not appear in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>glow-worm</b></a> to describe how other teachers shine only until the sun of a Tathāgata rises and outshines them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>logicians</b></a> and their disciples are never truly purified from suffering as long as they hold onto wrong views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda noted that the appearance of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a> causes other teachers to lose their respect and their supply of medicinal requisites and dwellings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the radiance of a Fully Enlightened One to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>sun</b></a>, which makes the puny light of all other thinkers disappear when it rises? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>wrong views</b></a> are unable to be released from the cycle of suffering, regardless of how much they shine in the absence of a Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda highlighted that once a Buddha arises, only the Buddha and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>Saṅgha of monks</b></a> are the ones who receive the highest honor and support from the public? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the intellectual light of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>thinkers</b></a> appears significant only because the sun-like radiance of a Sammāsambuddha has not yet illuminated the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the honor given to other teachers is temporary and entirely dependent on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>absence</b></a> of a Fully Enlightened One in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that no matter how much they are honored, logicians cannot achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato"><b>purification</b></a> from their taints through their own speculative theories? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.10/en/sujato">Ud 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta was responsible for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>instructing</b></a>, urging, and gladdening the Venerable Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya with a detailed Dhamma talk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed the Venerable Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya achieving liberation from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a> through non-clinging while he was being taught by Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a liberated person as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>crossed the flood</b></a> of existence, reaching a shore that had never been crossed before? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person is truly free from the cycle of birth when they no longer view any aspect of existence with the conceit <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>"I am this"</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>liberated everywhere</b></a>—above, below, and in every direction—is the hallmark of one who has escaped further rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the specific guidance of Sāriputta, Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya's mind was completely released from all taints <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>without clinging</b></a> to anything in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an inspired utterance to celebrate the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>non-rebirth</b></a> attained by a monk who has abandoned all self-centered perceptions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Sāriputta's method of teaching involved <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>rousing</b></a> and inspiring his students to help them realize the ultimate goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches that one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>liberated above</b></a> and below has effectively transcended all the bonds that lead to suffering and renewal of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recognized the exact moment when Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato"><b>mind was freed</b></a> and subsequently spoke a verse about the crossing of the previously uncrossed flood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.1/en/sujato">Ud 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed the Venerable Subhūti sitting in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato"><b>thoughtless concentration</b></a> with his body held erect, leading the Buddha to utter a verse about the transcendence of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato">Ud 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a liberated person as one whose internal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato"><b>speculations</b></a> have been completely shredded and incinerated, leaving no remainder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato">Ud 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an individual who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato"><b>surpassed the four yokes</b></a> and has transcended attachment to forms will never again return to the cycle of birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato">Ud 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato"><b>incinerated thoughts</b></a> to describe the mental state of a monk who has successfully overcome the inner turmoil of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.7/en/sujato">Ud 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke on the dangers of desire after monks reported that two gangs in Rājagaha were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>killing each other</b></a> because they were both enamored with the same prostitute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that both what a person has already attained and what they still hope to attain are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>covered in dust</b></a>—meaning they are defiled—for those who remain stuck in the training of the worldly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the extreme view that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>"there is no fault in sensual pleasures"</b></a> as a primary obstacle that prevents people from realizing the truth of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who place the core of their holy life solely in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>rules and vows</b></a> are following an extreme that fails to lead to true liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that both the indulgence in sensual pleasures and the rigid adherence to external vows are paths that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>increase the cemetery</b></a> by fueling the cycle of death and rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that some people <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>lag behind</b></a> the truth while others over-run it because they fail to have direct knowledge of the two extremes of dogmatism and indulgence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for those who have direct knowledge of reality and do not identify with their views, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>cycle of existence</b></a> can no longer be described or sustained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared people who are obsessively intent on what they see and hear to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>moths</b></a> that fly into an oil lamp, unaware that the light will lead to their destruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while sitting in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>darkness of the night</b></a> with oil lamps burning, the Buddha witnessed insects meeting their ruin and used the event to teach about the dangers of human attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who "over-run" the Dhamma and fail to find its essence only succeed in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>increasing new bondage</b></a> for themselves in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that some people are so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>intent on dogmas</b></a> derived from what is seen and heard that they fall into ruin just like insects falling into a flame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who do not reach the core essence of the spiritual life are essentially <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>over-running</b></a> the very path that could lead them to freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>enamored</b></a> with worldly objects or people leads to quarrels, strife, and even physical violence among those who are bound by their hearts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>blazing lamp</b></a> to illustrate how alluring but dangerous sensory distractions can be for those without wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that only through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> of the two extremes can a practitioner truly avoid being caught in the "dust" of both current and future attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.8/en/sujato">Ud 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato"><b>falling into disaster</b></a> as something that happens to those who are simply following traditional hearsay or visual appearances without deeper insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud6.9/en/sujato">Ud 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that five hundred women, including Sāmāvatī, who perished in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato"><b>palace fire</b></a> in Kosambi had actually attained various stages of enlightenment, such as stream-entry, once-returning, and non-returning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato">Ud 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the world as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato"><b>bound by delusion</b></a>, appearing capable and attractive to the foolish while it is actually surrounded by darkness and bound by acquisitions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato">Ud 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one who truly knows and sees reality, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato"><b>nothing</b></a> (no "thing") to be found, even though the world appears permanent to those caught in ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato">Ud 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when monks asked about the destination of the laywomen who died in King Utena’s harem, the Buddha assured them that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato"><b>none of those women</b></a> died without fruit, meaning they had all achieved a level of spiritual realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato">Ud 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>base</b></a> or dimension where there is no earth, water, fire, or air, nor any of the formless spheres like infinite space or infinite consciousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that in the state of Nibbana, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>neither this world</b></a> nor another world, and neither the sun nor the moon provides light there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the end of suffering as a state that has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>no coming or going</b></a>, no staying, no passing away, and no arising? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ultimate liberation is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>unestablished</b></a>, non-proliferating, and without an object, and that this alone constitutes the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that Nibbana is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>without an object</b></a> (anārammaṇa), meaning it does not rely on any sensory or mental phenomena for its existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.2/en/sujato"><b>uninclined</b></a> (anata)—referring to the state of Nibbana—is extremely difficult to see, and that the ultimate truth is not easily perceived by those still caught in craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.2/en/sujato">Ud 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that for one who knows and sees the truth, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.2/en/sujato"><b>craving is penetrated</b></a>, and for such a person, there is "nothing" left to grasp? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.2/en/sujato">Ud 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while the world appears permanent or eternal (sassato) to the ignorant, there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato"><b>nothing to be found</b></a> in it for the one who sees with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.10/en/sujato">Ud 7.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Nibbana as a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>non-proceeding</b></a> (appavattaṃ), which serves as the final cessation of the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.2/en/sujato"><b>truth</b></a> is not "well-seen" (sudassanaṃ) by those who have not yet overcome the internal drive of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.2/en/sujato">Ud 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an inspired utterance to emphasize that Nibbana is neither a place of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato"><b>arising</b></a> nor a place of passing away, but rather the absolute end of all distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.1/en/sujato">Ud 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed the Venerable Sāriputta instructing the Venerable Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya under the impression that he was still a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato"><b>trainee</b></a>, even though Bhaddiya had already reached the final goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato">Ud 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ultimate state of liberation as one where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato"><b>cycle is severed</b></a> and the stream of craving has dried up, meaning it no longer flows toward further existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato">Ud 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that once a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato"><b>hope is gone</b></a> regarding the round of rebirth, the cycle of suffering is broken and does not continue to turn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato">Ud 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uttered an inspired verse explaining that when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato"><b>stream</b></a> of craving has dried up and no longer flows, that very state marks the absolute end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato">Ud 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that the Venerable Sāriputta used <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato"><b>many methods</b></a> of Dhamma talk to instruct, urge, and gladden Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya, unaware that the monk was already fully liberated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.2/en/sujato">Ud 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that people who are excessively attached to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> and the bond of fetters are incapable of ever crossing the great flood of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato">Ud 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the masses of people in Sāvatthī as being so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato"><b>infatuated</b></a> and blinded by lust that they fail to see any fault or danger in the fetters that bind them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato">Ud 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato"><b>stuck in attachment</b></a> to worldly enjoyments can never hope to cross the vast and great flood of samsara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato">Ud 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after a group of monks reported how the townspeople were obsessed with pleasure, the Buddha explained that such <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato"><b>bondage to fetters</b></a> is the primary obstacle to reaching the further shore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.3/en/sujato">Ud 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared people who are blinded by sensual pleasures and covered by the shroud of craving to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato"><b>fish in a trap</b></a> (a funnel-net)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato">Ud 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that individuals who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato"><b>blinded by desire</b></a> are inevitably pursued by old age and death, much like a calf follows its mother? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato">Ud 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato"><b>suckling calf</b></a> following its mother to illustrate how relentlessly old age and death follow those who remain caught in the veil of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato">Ud 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being bound by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato"><b>Careless One</b></a>—a reference to Māra—leads beings into a state of ruin similar to fish being caught in the mouth of a net? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato">Ud 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed townspeople who were so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato"><b>shrouded by craving</b></a> and blinded by their own lusts that they were heading straight toward further suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato">Ud 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato"><b>blinded by sensual things</b></a> are essentially covered by a veil that prevents them from seeing the spiritual freedom that lies beyond old age and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.4/en/sujato">Ud 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the monk Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya as possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.5/en/sujato"><b>great power</b></a> and might, even though his physical appearance was unsightly, dwarfish, and often held in contempt by other monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.5/en/sujato">Ud 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that the monk Lakuṇḍaka Bhaddiya had successfully attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.5/en/sujato"><b>supreme goal</b></a> of the holy life, having directly known and realized liberation for himself in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.5/en/sujato">Ud 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the poetic imagery of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.5/en/sujato"><b>flawless carriage</b></a> with a white canopy and a single spoke to describe a liberated being who has severed the stream of existence and is completely free from bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.5/en/sujato">Ud 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly asked his monks if they noticed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.5/en/sujato"><b>unsightly monk</b></a> walking behind the group, only to clarify that this individual had actually reached the highest state of spiritual attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.5/en/sujato">Ud 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who has reached the end of the holy life is beyond suffering and attachment, regardless of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.5/en/sujato"><b>physical form</b></a> or how they are perceived by those who judge by outward appearances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.5/en/sujato">Ud 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed the Venerable Aññāsikoṇḍañña sitting nearby with his body held erect, reviewing his own liberation through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.6/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.6/en/sujato">Ud 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a botanical metaphor to describe a wise person, stating that if there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.6/en/sujato"><b>no root</b></a> in the ground and no leaves, a "creeper" of craving has no basis to grow and cause further suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.6/en/sujato">Ud 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a wise person who is freed from all bonds is so pure that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.6/en/sujato"><b>devas and Brahmā</b></a> praise them, and no one in the world is worthy of blaming them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.6/en/sujato">Ud 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that once the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.6/en/sujato"><b>root of craving</b></a> is removed, there is no longer a source for the mental entanglements that bind a person to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.6/en/sujato">Ud 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a monk who has attained the absolute <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.6/en/sujato"><b>liberation from craving</b></a> sits with mindfulness established, reviewing the freedom he has won from the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.6/en/sujato">Ud 7.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while sitting in meditation at Sāvatthī, reviewed his own success in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.7/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of objectification</b></a> and the mental tendencies that lead to conceptual proliferation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.7/en/sujato">Ud 7.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a sage who has no craving and has moved beyond <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.7/en/sujato"><b>conceptual proliferation</b></a> is respected and not looked down upon by anyone, including the devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.7/en/sujato">Ud 7.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a liberated person as one who has crossed over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.7/en/sujato"><b>fetter and the bar</b></a>, signifying the complete removal of all mental obstructions and deep-seated attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.7/en/sujato">Ud 7.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one who has attained the end of craving, there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.7/en/sujato"><b>staying place</b></a> or conceptual foothold left within the entire world of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.7/en/sujato">Ud 7.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a person without "objectification" (papañca) is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.7/en/sujato"><b>true muni</b></a> (sage) who wanders through the world without being affected by the desires that trap others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.7/en/sujato">Ud 7.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed the Venerable Mahākaccāna sitting nearby with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.8/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> well established within him, leading the Buddha to utter a verse about the power of constant awareness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.8/en/sujato">Ud 7.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.8/en/sujato"><b>clinger</b></a> (visattikā) to describe the pervasive and sticky nature of craving that a practitioner must eventually cross over through the power of mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.8/en/sujato">Ud 7.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.8/en/sujato"><b>gradual dwelling</b></a>, where a monk uses consistent and constant mindfulness to eventually cross over the deep attachment of craving at the proper time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.8/en/sujato">Ud 7.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uttered a verse about the one who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.8/en/sujato"><b>constant mindfulness</b></a> of the body, noting that such a person is on the path to transcending all future states of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.8/en/sujato">Ud 7.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha alluded to a profound meditative reflection, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.8/en/sujato"><b>"It might not be"</b></a>, as a way to understand the cessation of the "me" and "mine" that leads to total spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud7.8/en/sujato">Ud 7.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically instructed Cunda the smith to serve him with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>sūkaramaddava</b></a> (a special dish) while serving the rest of the monks with other choice foods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Cunda that he saw no one in the world—including devas, Māras, and Brahmās—who could properly digest <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>sūkaramaddava</b></a> except for a Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha requested that any remaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>leftovers</b></a> of the sūkaramaddava be buried in a hole rather than given to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after eating the meal provided by Cunda, the Buddha was stricken with a severe illness involving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>bloody dysentery</b></a> and near-fatal pains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even while experiencing intense, life-threatening pain after his final meal, the Buddha remained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>mindful and clearly comprehending</b></a> without becoming distressed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his severe illness, the Buddha set out on foot toward the town of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>Kusinārā</b></a> accompanied by the Venerable Ānanda? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his final journey, the Buddha felt weary and asked Ānanda to spread out his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>fourfold outer robe</b></a> so he could sit down and rest under a tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Ānanda for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>drinking water</b></a> from a nearby stream, even though Ānanda initially warned him that the water was muddy and disturbed by 500 carts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha had to request water from Ānanda <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>three times</b></a> before Ānanda finally agreed to go to the shallow, muddy stream? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a stream that had been made shallow and turbid by passing carts miraculously became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>clear and serene</b></a> as soon as Ānanda approached it to fetch water for the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Venerable Ānanda marveled at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>psychic power</b></a> and majesty of the Tathāgata when he saw the muddy water transform into a clear, flowing stream? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spent some of his final hours resting in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>lion's posture</b></a> (lying on the right side) while the monk Cundaka sat before him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha showed great compassion by instructing Ānanda to prevent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>remorse</b></a> from arising in Cunda the smith regarding the meal that preceded the Buddha's Parinibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that there are two meals that provide <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>equal fruit</b></a> and far exceed all others in merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the meal eaten before a Tathāgata attains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>supreme enlightenment</b></a> is one of the two most meritorious offerings possible? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>final meal</b></a> a Tathāgata eats before passing away into the Nibbāna element without remainder is equal in merit to the meal of his enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly stated that Cunda the smith had accumulated karma leading to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>long life</b></a> because of the final almsfood he provided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted Cunda the smith would gain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>beauty</b></a> (vaṇṇa) as a specific result of his merit in serving the Tathāgata's last meal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Ānanda that Cunda's deed would lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a> (sukha) and the fulfillment of his spiritual potential? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that serving the Buddha's final meal was said by the Buddha to lead Cunda to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>heaven</b></a> (sagga)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that Cunda the smith’s offering would result in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>fame</b></a> (yasa) for the generous donor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that among the blessings for Cunda the smith, the Buddha included the attainment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>authority</b></a> (ādhipateyya) in future lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used an inspired utterance to teach that for one who gives, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>merit increases</b></a>, and for one who is self-restrained, no enmity is accumulated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the skilled person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>abandons evil</b></a> and reaches total Nibbāna through the destruction of greed, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>restraint</b></a>, one ensures that no "vera" (enmity or vengeance) is piled up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>quenched</b></a> (nibbuto) as the result of the complete exhaustion of lust, hate, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while the Buddha was extremely exhausted and ill, he continued to teach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a> to Cunda to inspire and gladden him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s final travel from Pāva to Kusinārā is a key event where he displayed both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato"><b>human fragility</b></a> through illness and superhuman resolve through meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.5/en/sujato">Ud 8.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person of poor virtue suffers a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>great loss of wealth</b></a> due to negligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an immoral person faces the disadvantage of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>bad reputation</b></a> spreading abroad? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an unvirtuous person enters any assembly—whether of nobles, brahmins, or householders—feeling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>diffident and confused</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who lacks virtue dies in a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>confusion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that after death, a person with poor virtue is reborn in a state of misery, a bad destination, or even in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a virtuous person gains a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>great mass of wealth</b></a> through the foundation of diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a person of good virtue enjoys a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>good reputation</b></a> that is widely proclaimed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a virtuous person enters any assembly with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>confidence</b></a> and without embarrassment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who has perfected virtue passes away with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>unconfused mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a virtuous person, after the breakup of the body, is reborn in a good destination, even in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed with his divine eye thousands of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>deities</b></a> taking up residence in Pāṭaligāma while the city was being built? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed that powerful deities influence the minds of powerful kings and ministers to build their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>dwellings</b></a> in the specific areas where those deities reside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the planning of the city at Pāṭaligāma by the ministers Sunidha and Vassakāra to a consultation with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>Tāvatiṃsa devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>Pāṭaliputta</b></a> would become the chief city and a center for the exchange of goods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha foretold that Pāṭaliputta would face three major dangers: from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>fire</b></a>, from water, and from internal dissension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>internal dissension</b></a> would be one of the three catastrophic events to strike the future city of Pāṭaliputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person should dedicate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>merit</b></a> of a meal given to virtuous practitioners to the local deities of the place where they live? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that deities who are honored and respected by humans will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>reciprocate</b></a> that honor and respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the protection of deities as being like a mother's love for her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>own son</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>befriended by the deities</b></a> always sees auspicious sights? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Magadhan ministers named a city gate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>Gotama Gate</b></a> to commemorate the exit of the Buddha from the city? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Magadhan ministers intended to name a crossing point the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>Gotama Ford</b></a> in honor of the Buddha crossing the Ganges River? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha arrived at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>Ganges River</b></a> when it was so full that a crow could drink from it while standing on the bank? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while other people were looking for boats or building rafts, the Buddha and the Saṅgha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>vanished</b></a> from one bank of the Ganges and instantly appeared on the other? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared his miraculous crossing of the river to a strong man <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>extending his arm</b></a> or drawing it back? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uttered an inspired verse about wise people who cross the ocean and the flood by making a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>bridge</b></a> while leaving the marshes behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that while ordinary people were still <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>binding a raft</b></a>, those who are truly wise have already crossed over to the other side? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>"breaking of seed-boxes"</b></a> (puṭabhedana) to describe Pāṭaliputta's future status as a major trade city? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told the lay followers of Pāṭaligāma that the third disadvantage of being unvirtuous is appearing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>diffident and embarrassed</b></a> before any group of people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spent the night in an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>empty house</b></a> after the lay followers of Pāṭaligāma had departed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that ministers build their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato"><b>residences</b></a> in places where high-ranking deities have already taken possession of the land? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.6/en/sujato">Ud 8.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha was visited by Visākhā, Migāra’s mother, while she was in a state of deep mourning, characterized by her having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>wet clothes and hair</b></a>, because her beloved granddaughter had recently passed away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked Visākhā if she would truly desire to have as many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>children and grandchildren</b></a> as there are people living in the entire city of Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha inquired about the mortality rate of Sāvatthī, Visākhā explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>people die daily</b></a> in the city—sometimes ten, sometimes one—meaning the population is never free from the occurrence of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha helped Visākhā realize that if she had as many relatives as the population of Sāvatthī, she would never be with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>dry hair</b></a> or dry clothes because someone she loved would be dying every single day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for those who possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>one hundred dear ones</b></a>, there are exactly one hundred sufferings, demonstrating that grief is numerically proportional to one's attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha illustrated the weight of attachment by explaining that the number of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>sufferings</b></a> an individual faces decreases steadily as they have fewer dear ones, whether that number be ninety, fifty, or even just ten? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even possessing just <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>one dear one</b></a> inevitably results in one suffering, showing that any degree of attachment to the "dear" leads to potential pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>nothing dear</b></a> anywhere in the world have no suffering and are free from the burdens of grief and lamentation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of being liberated from all dear ones as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>dustless</b></a> (viraja), meaning one is free from the "dust" of defilements that arise through attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>despairless</b></a> (anupāyāsa) to characterize the mental state of an individual who has successfully transcended the need to hold people or things as dear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha uttered an inspired verse stating that whatever <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>sorrows or lamentations</b></a> of many kinds exist in the world, they all arise solely in dependence on someone or something held dear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if there are no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>dear ones</b></a>, then sorrows, lamentations, and various forms of pain simply do not come into existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined those who are truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>happy</b></a> (sukhino) and free from sorrow as those who have no dear ones anywhere in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly advised that anyone seeking the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>sorrowless</b></a> (asoka) state should refrain from making anything dear to them in any part of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the profound dialogue regarding the nature of grief between the Buddha and Visākhā occurred at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>Mansion of Migāra’s Mother</b></a> in the Eastern Park at Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Visākhā approached the Buddha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>during the day</b></a> while still physically showing the signs of her distress to seek his guidance on her recent loss? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s realization about the connection between attachment and suffering prompted him to speak an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>inspired utterance</b></a> (Udāna) specifically addressing the origin of worldly pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all forms of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato"><b>pain in the world</b></a>, which take on many different shapes, are ultimately rooted in the existence of things we hold dear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/ud8.8/en/sujato">Ud 8.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while there are two kinds of giving—material giving and the giving of the Dhamma—the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti98/en/sujato"><b>giving of the Dhamma</b></a> is the supreme of the two? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti98/en/sujato">Iti 98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two types of sharing, material sharing and sharing the Dhamma, and declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti98/en/sujato"><b>sharing the Dhamma</b></a> is the foremost form of sharing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti98/en/sujato">Iti 98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between material assistance and assistance through the Dhamma, stating that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti98/en/sujato"><b>assistance through the Dhamma</b></a> is the best kind of support one can provide? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti98/en/sujato">Iti 98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged wise people to sacrifice at the proper time because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti98/en/sujato"><b>giving</b></a> is considered a supreme and unsurpassed action when directed toward the right field of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti98/en/sujato">Iti 98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for those who are diligent in his teaching, both those who speak and those who hear the Dhamma with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti98/en/sujato"><b>confident heart</b></a> find their ultimate goal becomes purely clarified? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti98/en/sujato">Iti 98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that for a person who has overstepped one specific quality—<a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti25/en/sujato"><b>deliberate lying</b></a>—there is no evil deed they might not potentially commit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti25/en/sujato">Iti 25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a person has disregarded the next world and transgressed the single factor of truthfulness by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti25/en/sujato"><b>lying</b></a>, there is no sin they are incapable of doing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti25/en/sujato">Iti 25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that if beings knew the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato"><b>result of giving</b></a> and sharing as clearly as he did, they would never eat a meal without first having shared some of it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato">Iti 26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the reason beings eat without giving and allow the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato"><b>stain of stinginess</b></a> to obsess their minds is simply because they do not understand the fruit of sharing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato">Iti 26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that if people truly understood the great fruit of sharing, they would not let even their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato"><b>last morsel</b></a> of food pass their lips without sharing it, provided there was someone to receive it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato">Iti 26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that giving to the Noble Ones with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato"><b>clear heart</b></a> at the proper time is an action that yields great fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato">Iti 26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that donors who provide food to those worthy of offerings will, after passing away from the human state, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato"><b>go to heaven</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato">Iti 26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who reach heaven through their generosity experience joy and enjoy the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato"><b>result of sharing</b></a> while being free from stinginess? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato">Iti 26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato"><b>stain of stinginess</b></a> would not be able to take hold of a person's mind if they truly understood the karmic consequences of giving as he did? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato">Iti 26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that giving should be done <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato"><b>in due time</b></a> to the Noble Ones to ensure the gift yields its most magnificent results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti26/en/sujato">Iti 26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha guaranteed the state of non-returning for any person who successfully abandons the single mental quality of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti1/en/sujato"><b>greed</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti1/en/sujato">Iti 1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings who are infatuated with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti1/en/sujato"><b>greed</b></a> are destined for a state of misery, while those with insight abandon it and never return to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti1/en/sujato">Iti 1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha acted as a spiritual guarantor for his followers, promising they would reach the stage of a non-returner if they let go of the hindrance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti1/en/sujato"><b>greed</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti1/en/sujato">Iti 1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by abandoning the single quality of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti2/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a>, a practitioner is assured of attaining the fruit of non-returning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti2/en/sujato">Iti 2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that beings corrupted by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti2/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> travel to a bad destination after death, but those who fully understand it and give it up do not return to this human existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti2/en/sujato">Iti 2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti2/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> as a specific "single thing" that, when discarded, prevents a person from being reborn again in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti2/en/sujato">Iti 2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti3/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> as a primary factor that leads beings to rebirth in unhappy realms, and promised non-returning to those who abandon it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti3/en/sujato">Iti 3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person with insight clearly understands the nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti3/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> and abandons it, they are freed from ever returning to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti3/en/sujato">Iti 3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti3/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> is a quality that causes beings to be "bewildered" and fall into states of misery if it is not overcome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti3/en/sujato">Iti 3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a person can overcome the single obstacle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti4/en/sujato"><b>anger</b></a>, they are guaranteed to reach the state of non-returning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti4/en/sujato">Iti 4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that beings provoked by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti4/en/sujato"><b>anger</b></a> fall into bad destinations, but those who abandon it through insight never return to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti4/en/sujato">Iti 4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the complete abandonment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti4/en/sujato"><b>anger</b></a> as a necessary condition for ensuring one will never be reborn in this realm again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti4/en/sujato">Iti 4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by discarding the single quality of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti6/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a>, a disciple can be certain they have attained the status of a non-returner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti6/en/sujato">Iti 6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that those intoxicated by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti6/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a> are headed for a state of misery, whereas those who abandon it do not return to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti6/en/sujato">Iti 6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti6/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a> as a major hindrance to spiritual progress that must be understood and abandoned to end the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti6/en/sujato">Iti 6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person is incapable of ending all suffering if they do not directly know and fully understand the nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti7/en/sujato"><b>the all</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti7/en/sujato">Iti 7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that only by fully understanding and becoming detached from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti7/en/sujato"><b>the all</b></a> is one actually capable of the destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti7/en/sujato">Iti 7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who has mastered "the knowledge of the all" as one who does not get attached to anything and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti7/en/sujato"><b>overcome all suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti7/en/sujato">Iti 7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti7/en/sujato"><b>abandoning the all</b></a> involves both direct knowledge and the fading away of lust for every aspect of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti7/en/sujato">Iti 7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who knows <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti7/en/sujato"><b>the all</b></a> from every perspective and does not delight in it has truly gone beyond all sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti7/en/sujato">Iti 7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti16/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> as the single most helpful internal factor for a trainee who has not yet reached the goal but is striving for the supreme security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti16/en/sujato">Iti 16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a monk practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti16/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a>, he is able to successfully abandon unwholesome qualities and develop wholesome ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti16/en/sujato">Iti 16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated there is no other internal quality as beneficial as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti16/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> for a trainee seeking to attain the ultimate goal of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti16/en/sujato">Iti 16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk who strives with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti16/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> will eventually reach the destruction of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti16/en/sujato">Iti 16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti17/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> as the most helpful external factor for a trainee who is still striving for the supreme security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti17/en/sujato">Iti 17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti17/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> is able to abandon unwholesome states and cultivate wholesome states more effectively than through any other external means? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti17/en/sujato">Iti 17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti17/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> as one who is deferential and respectful, carefully following the advice of his wise and virtuous companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti17/en/sujato">Iti 17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who values <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti17/en/sujato"><b>good friendship</b></a> and acts mindfully will gradually reach the destruction of all fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti17/en/sujato">Iti 17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti18/en/sujato"><b>schism in the Sangha</b></a> is the one thing that, when it arises in the world, leads to the harm and suffering of many people, including both devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti18/en/sujato">Iti 18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that when a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti18/en/sujato"><b>schism in the Sangha</b></a> occurs, the community falls into mutual quarreling, abuse, and even the total exclusion of one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti18/en/sujato">Iti 18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti18/en/sujato"><b>schism in the Sangha</b></a> prevents those without confidence in the Dhamma from gaining it and causes some who already have confidence to undergo a change for the worse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti18/en/sujato">Iti 18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that anyone who causes a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti18/en/sujato"><b>schism in the Sangha</b></a> is destined for a state of misery and will be boiled in hell for an entire aeon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti18/en/sujato">Iti 18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who breaks the unity of the community as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti18/en/sujato"><b>established in un-Dhamma</b></a> and as one who has destroyed their own path to security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti18/en/sujato">Iti 18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti19/en/sujato"><b>concord in the Sangha</b></a> is the one thing that, when it arises in the world, leads to the welfare and happiness of many people, including devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti19/en/sujato">Iti 19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that when there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti19/en/sujato"><b>concord in the Sangha</b></a>, the community remains free from mutual quarreling, abuse, and exclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti19/en/sujato">Iti 19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti19/en/sujato"><b>concord in the Sangha</b></a> helps those without confidence to gain it and causes those who already have confidence to experience an increase in their faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti19/en/sujato">Iti 19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who promotes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti19/en/sujato"><b>concord in the Sangha</b></a> and delights in the unity of the community will rejoice in heaven for an entire aeon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti19/en/sujato">Iti 19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the person who unifies a broken community as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti19/en/sujato"><b>established in Dhamma</b></a> and as one who does not fall away from the supreme security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti19/en/sujato">Iti 19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that if a person were to die while their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti20/en/sujato"><b>mind is corrupt</b></a>, they would be reborn in hell as if they had been carried there and set down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti20/en/sujato">Iti 20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that he could understand another person’s heart with his own and knew that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti20/en/sujato"><b>corrupted mind</b></a> leads directly to a state of misery after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti20/en/sujato">Iti 20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti20/en/sujato"><b>corruption of mind</b></a> as the specific cause for beings to be reborn in a bad destination or a state of suffering after the body breaks up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti20/en/sujato">Iti 20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as a person might be carried and dropped somewhere, one with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti20/en/sujato"><b>depraved mind</b></a> is similarly deposited in hell upon death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti20/en/sujato">Iti 20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti20/en/sujato"><b>corrupt mental state</b></a> at the moment of death is the determining factor that results in their rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti20/en/sujato">Iti 20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a person were to die while their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti21/en/sujato"><b>mind is confident</b></a> and clear, they would be reborn in heaven as if they had been carried there and set down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti21/en/sujato">Iti 21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that he could perceive another's heart and recognized that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti21/en/sujato"><b>serene mind</b></a> ensures a person will be reborn in a happy destination after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti21/en/sujato">Iti 21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti21/en/sujato"><b>confidence of mind</b></a> as the primary reason beings are reborn in a happy destination or the heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti21/en/sujato">Iti 21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that someone who dies with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti21/en/sujato"><b>confident heart</b></a> is reborn in the heavenly world just as if they had been physically transported and placed there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti21/en/sujato">Iti 21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that it is because of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti21/en/sujato"><b>confidence of their mind</b></a> that some people, after death, arise in a happy world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti21/en/sujato">Iti 21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti33/en/sujato"><b>good virtue</b></a> and good view is, as if carried there, deposited in heaven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti33/en/sujato">Iti 33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a wise person who is endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti33/en/sujato"><b>good view</b></a> and good virtue will, after the breakup of the body, be reborn in a happy destination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti33/en/sujato">Iti 33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two specific qualities—<a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti33/en/sujato"><b>bhaddakena sīlena</b></a> (good virtue) and good view—as the factors that lead a person to rebirth in the heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti33/en/sujato">Iti 33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti33/en/sujato"><b>good virtue</b></a> and good view would not be deposited in heaven as if carried there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti33/en/sujato">Iti 33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti34/en/sujato"><b>not ardent</b></a> and lacks fear of wrongdoing is incapable of attaining enlightenment, Nibbana, or the supreme security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti34/en/sujato">Iti 34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is lazy, low in energy, and full of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti34/en/sujato"><b>sloth and torpor</b></a> as being incapable of reaching the highest awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti34/en/sujato">Iti 34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti34/en/sujato"><b>ardent</b></a> and afraid of wrongdoing (ottāpī) is capable of attaining Nibbana and the unsurpassed security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti34/en/sujato">Iti 34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said one who is mindful, discerning, meditative, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti34/en/sujato"><b>heedful</b></a> can cut the bond of birth and old age to touch the unsurpassed awakening right in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti34/en/sujato">Iti 34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the holy life (brahmacariya) is not lived for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti35/en/sujato"><b>deceiving people</b></a>, wheedling them, or for the benefit of gains, honor, and fame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti35/en/sujato">Iti 35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the true purpose of living the holy life is for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti35/en/sujato"><b>restraint</b></a> and abandoning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti35/en/sujato">Iti 35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the path of the holy life as one that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti35/en/sujato"><b>plunges into Nibbana</b></a> and is followed by great seekers and sages? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti35/en/sujato">Iti 35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that the holy life is not lived with the motive of wanting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti35/en/sujato"><b>people to know</b></a> one’s status, but for the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti35/en/sujato">Iti 35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the holy life is lived specifically for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti36/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> (abhiññā) and full understanding (pariññā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti36/en/sujato">Iti 36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who practice the holy life as he described will make an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti36/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a> by following the path of the Great Sages? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti36/en/sujato">Iti 36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that anyone who lives the holy life for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti36/en/sujato"><b>gains and honor</b></a> is not practicing according to the true purpose of the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti36/en/sujato">Iti 36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the path he taught as one that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti36/en/sujato"><b>not based on hearsay</b></a> (anītihaṃ) but leads directly to the goal of Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti36/en/sujato">Iti 36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses two qualities—a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti37/en/sujato"><b>sense of urgency</b></a> regarding things that should evoke urgency and wise effort—lives with much happiness and joy in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti37/en/sujato">Iti 37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, when a person feels a sense of urgency and applies <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti37/en/sujato"><b>wise effort</b></a> (yoniso padhāna), they establish a basis for the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti37/en/sujato">Iti 37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a wise monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti37/en/sujato"><b>ardent</b></a>, prudent, and peaceful in conduct as one who can reach the end of suffering by applying himself to mental calm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti37/en/sujato">Iti 37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti37/en/sujato"><b>un-agitated</b></a> and possesses mental calm is capable of reaching the destruction of all pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti37/en/sujato">Iti 37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two specific thoughts that frequently occur to a Tathāgata: the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato"><b>thought of safety</b></a> (khemo vitakko) and the thought of seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato">Iti 38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato"><b>thought of safety</b></a> as the reflection that one does not harm any being, whether frail or firm, through their behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato">Iti 38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato"><b>delights in non-ill will</b></a> (abyāpajjhārāma) and frequently thinks of not harming others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato">Iti 38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato"><b>thought of seclusion</b></a> (paviveko vitakko) is the realization that whatever is unwholesome has been abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato">Iti 38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato"><b>dwell in seclusion</b></a> so that they too might frequently experience thoughts regarding what is unwholesome and what remains to be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato">Iti 38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of someone standing on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato"><b>rocky mountain peak</b></a> to describe how he, free from sorrow, observes the world overwhelmed by birth and old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato">Iti 38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato"><b>all-seeing</b></a> sage who has ascended the palace of the Dhamma to look down upon people immersed in grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato">Iti 38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized himself as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato"><b>Great Sage</b></a> (mahesi) who has crossed to the beyond, dispelled darkness, and is liberated through the destruction of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato">Iti 38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to himself as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato"><b>sage</b></a> (muni) who is bearing his final body and has conquered Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato">Iti 38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato"><b>delight in non-ill will</b></a>, the recurring thought will be that they do not harm any being, whether frail or firm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti38/en/sujato">Iti 38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti37/en/sujato"><b>prudent</b></a> (nipako) and mindful can attain the end of suffering by contemplating with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti37/en/sujato">Iti 37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that his guidance consists of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti39/en/sujato"><b>two expositions of the Dhamma</b></a> delivered in sequence: first, to see evil as evil, and second, to become disenchanted and liberated from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti39/en/sujato">Iti 39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, acting out of compassion for all beings, explained that once a person has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti39/en/sujato"><b>seen evil as evil</b></a> and become detached from it, they are able to make a complete end to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti39/en/sujato">Iti 39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti40/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> (avijjā) as the primary precursor for the arrival of unwholesome states, which is immediately followed by a lack of shame and a lack of fear of wrongdoing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti40/en/sujato">Iti 40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti40/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge</b></a> (vijjā) is the precursor for the arrival of wholesome states, and it is followed closely by the presence of shame and fear of wrongdoing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti40/en/sujato">Iti 40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that all the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti40/en/sujato"><b>miserable destinations</b></a> (duggati) in this world and the next are rooted in ignorance and are built up through desire and greed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti40/en/sujato">Iti 40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti40/en/sujato"><b>evil wishes</b></a>, lacks shame, and has no regard for the consequences of their actions, they produce evil that leads them to a state of deprivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti40/en/sujato">Iti 40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged practitioners to dispel desire and ignorance so that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti40/en/sujato"><b>arousing knowledge</b></a>, they might abandon all miserable destinations and the cycle of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti40/en/sujato">Iti 40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti41/en/sujato"><b>devoid of noble wisdom</b></a> (ariyā paññā) dwell in suffering and despair in this very life and can expect a bad destination after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti41/en/sujato">Iti 41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti41/en/sujato"><b>not devoid of noble wisdom</b></a> live happily and without anxiety in the present life, with a happy destination awaiting them after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti41/en/sujato">Iti 41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti41/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> (paññā) as the supreme quality in the world because it is the faculty that leads to the correct understanding of the destruction of birth and existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti41/en/sujato">Iti 41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the world, including its gods, is largely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti41/en/sujato"><b>settled in name-and-form</b></a> (nāmarūpa) and mistakenly imagines that this conditioned reality is the ultimate truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti41/en/sujato">Iti 41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that both gods and humans envy the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti41/en/sujato"><b>fully enlightened ones</b></a> who are mindful, possess great wisdom, and are currently bearing their final physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti41/en/sujato">Iti 41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two "bright qualities" (<a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti42/en/sujato"><b>sukkā dhammā</b></a>)—shame (hirī) and fear of wrongdoing (ottappa)—as the essential protectors of the world's social order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti42/en/sujato">Iti 42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti42/en/sujato"><b>shame and fear of wrongdoing</b></a> did not protect the world, human society would collapse into chaos and become indistinguishable from animals like goats, chickens, or dogs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti42/en/sujato">Iti 42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that it is specifically because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti42/en/sujato"><b>shame and fear of wrongdoing</b></a> exist that people show respect to their elders and recognize familial relationships like mothers and aunts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti42/en/sujato">Iti 42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who completely lack <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti42/en/sujato"><b>shame and fear of wrongdoing</b></a> are considered to have lapsed from the "bright roots" and are destined to continue in the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti42/en/sujato">Iti 42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those in whom <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti42/en/sujato"><b>shame and fear of wrongdoing</b></a> are always properly established will flourish in the holy life and become peaceful sages who have exhausted the need for rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti42/en/sujato">Iti 42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, referred to as the "Tathagata," emphasized that his teachings are meant to help beings <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti39/en/sujato"><b>become disenchanted</b></a> with evil so they can eventually attain liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti39/en/sujato">Iti 39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti41/en/sujato"><b>noble wisdom</b></a> (ariyā paññā), a practitioner can live without the "fever" of unwholesome states and find peace right in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti41/en/sujato">Iti 41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti42/en/sujato"><b>shame and fear of wrongdoing</b></a> as being one whose practice of the holy life has reached "full growth" and leads to the end of all renewed existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti42/en/sujato">Iti 42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there is an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti43/en/sujato"><b>unborn, unbecome, unmade, and unconditioned</b></a> state, and that without its existence, there would be no possibility of escape for those who are born and conditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti43/en/sujato">Iti 43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described that which is born, become, and conditioned as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti43/en/sujato"><b>nest of disease</b></a> that is unstable and subject to crumbling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti43/en/sujato">Iti 43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the escape from the conditioned world is a peaceful state that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti43/en/sujato"><b>beyond the reach of reasoning</b></a> (atakkāvacaraṃ) and permanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti43/en/sujato">Iti 43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the cessation of suffering as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti43/en/sujato"><b>stillness of formations</b></a> (saṅkhārūpasamo), which he identified as a state of true happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti43/en/sujato">Iti 43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ultimate goal as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti43/en/sujato"><b>sorrowless, stainless state</b></a> where all "states of suffering" (dukkhadhammānaṃ) are extinguished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti43/en/sujato">Iti 43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato"><b>two elements of Nibbana</b></a>: the Nibbana-element with residue and the Nibbana-element without residue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato">Iti 44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato"><b>Nibbana-element with residue</b></a> (saupādisesā nibbānadhātu) as the state of an Arahant who has destroyed lust, hatred, and delusion but still experiences pleasure and pain through the five senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato">Iti 44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an Arahant is a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato"><b>destroyed the taints</b></a>, lived the holy life to completion, and is liberated through final knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato">Iti 44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato"><b>Nibbana-element without residue</b></a> (anupādisesā nibbānadhātu) as the state where all things that are felt and experienced will "become cool" in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato">Iti 44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the Nibbana-element without residue is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato"><b>pertaining to the future</b></a> (samparāyikā) and is the state where all types of existence cease entirely? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato">Iti 44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato"><b>unconditioned state</b></a> and have destroyed the "cord of existence" have reached the true essence of his teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato">Iti 44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, referred to as "the one with vision," proclaimed that Nibbana with residue is a state that can be realized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato"><b>in this very life</b></a> (diṭṭhadhammikā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato">Iti 44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to dwell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti45/en/sujato"><b>devoted to seclusion</b></a> (paṭisallānaratā) and to find joy in remaining alone for meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti45/en/sujato">Iti 45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that practitioners should be committed to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti45/en/sujato"><b>internal mental calm</b></a> (ajjhattaṃ cetosamathaṃ) and should never neglect the practice of jhana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti45/en/sujato">Iti 45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti45/en/sujato"><b>possessed of insight</b></a> (vipassanāya samannāgatā) and to foster the use of empty huts for their spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti45/en/sujato">Iti 45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that two fruits can be expected for those devoted to seclusion: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti45/en/sujato"><b>final knowledge</b></a> (aññā) in this very life or, if there is a residue of clinging remaining, the state of non-returning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti45/en/sujato">Iti 45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti45/en/sujato"><b>peaceful in mind</b></a>, prudent, and mindful as being capable of seeing the Dhamma correctly while remaining indifferent to sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti45/en/sujato">Iti 45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti45/en/sujato"><b>delight in heedfulness</b></a> (appamādaratā) and see danger in negligence are incapable of falling away and are very close to Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti45/en/sujato">Iti 45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that because there is an escape that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti43/en/sujato"><b>peaceful and permanent</b></a> (santaṃ... dhuvaṃ), the exit from the world of birth and death can be clearly discerned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti43/en/sujato">Iti 43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato"><b>unattached</b></a> (anissitena), proclaimed two distinct Nibbana elements to help practitioners achieve the destruction of the cord of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti44/en/sujato">Iti 44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that anyone who has not yet abandoned lust, hatred, and delusion is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato"><b>bound by Mara</b></a>, caught in the Evil One's snare and subject to his will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato">Iti 68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who has successfully abandoned the three defilements of lust, hatred, and delusion is described as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato"><b>unbound from Mara</b></a>, having completely slipped the Evil One's snare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato">Iti 68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha refers to an individual who has cleared away the mental stains of lust, hatred, and ignorance as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato"><b>one of developed self</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato">Iti 68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha describes a practitioner who has fully removed lust, hatred, and delusion from their mind as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato"><b>become divine</b></a> (brahmabhūta)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato">Iti 68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches that by abandoning the three roots of unwholesomeness, a person is recognized as a Buddha who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato"><b>gone beyond enmity</b></a> and fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato">Iti 68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identifies the "Tathagata" as the "abandoner of all," specifically referring to one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato"><b>relinquished all lust</b></a>, hatred, and ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato">Iti 68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared any monk or nun who has not abandoned lust, hatred, and delusion to someone who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti69/en/sujato"><b>not crossed the ocean</b></a>, remaining trapped amidst its dangerous waves, whirlpools, and monsters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti69/en/sujato">Iti 69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha states that a practitioner who successfully abandons lust, hatred, and delusion is like a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti69/en/sujato"><b>crossed the ocean</b></a> and now stands safely on the dry land of the far shore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti69/en/sujato">Iti 69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha describes one who has crossed the difficult sea of defilements as a true "brahmin" who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti69/en/sujato"><b>overcome attachment</b></a> and abandoned death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti69/en/sujato">Iti 69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of an ocean filled with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti69/en/sujato"><b>monsters and demons</b></a> to describe the spiritual dangers and fears faced by those who have not yet abandoned their inner defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti69/en/sujato">Iti 69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha says a person who has reached the end of suffering and birth is "beyond measure" and is said to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti69/en/sujato"><b>bewildered the King of Death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti69/en/sujato">Iti 69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha points out that a person who has abandoned lust, hatred, and delusion has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti69/en/sujato"><b>abandoned suffering</b></a> in such a way that there is no more renewal of being in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti69/en/sujato">Iti 69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed to have personally seen beings reborn in a state of misery and hell because they practiced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato"><b>wrong conduct</b></a> of body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato">Iti 70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato"><b>reviling noble ones</b></a> (Ariyas) and holding wrong views are specific actions that lead to rebirth in the lower worlds after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato">Iti 70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that his knowledge of beings being reborn in hell for their misdeeds was not based on hearsay from others, but was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato"><b>personally known</b></a> and seen by himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato">Iti 70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> and undertakes actions based on that view will experience rebirth in the plane of misery and the lower world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato">Iti 70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person of poor wisdom who directs their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato"><b>mind wrongly</b></a> and speaks wrongly is destined for rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato">Iti 70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identifies <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato"><b>wrongful actions</b></a> performed with the body as a primary cause for a foolish person to be reborn in a state of deprivation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato">Iti 70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato"><b>unlearned</b></a> in the Dhamma and fail to make merit face a grim destination after their short life in this world is over? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti70/en/sujato">Iti 70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha teaches that a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato"><b>transcended fear</b></a> and enmity is one who has successfully cleared the mind of lust, hatred, and ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti68/en/sujato">Iti 68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that beings who practice good conduct of body, speech, and mind, while also possessing right view and refraining from reviling noble ones, are reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti71/en/sujato"><b>heavenly world</b></a> after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti71/en/sujato">Iti 71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that his knowledge regarding the rebirth of virtuous beings in heaven was not based on hearsay from others, but was something he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti71/en/sujato"><b>personally seen</b></a> and directly known for himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti71/en/sujato">Iti 71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a wise person who directs their mind rightly and speaks and acts correctly is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti71/en/sujato"><b>maker of merit</b></a> who, despite the shortness of human life, secures a place in heaven after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti71/en/sujato">Iti 71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti72/en/sujato"><b>renunciation</b></a> (nekkhamma) as the specific element of escape for those seeking to free themselves from the entrapment of sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti72/en/sujato">Iti 72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti72/en/sujato"><b>formless</b></a> state (āruppa) serves as the necessary element of escape for those who wish to transcend the limitations of the realm of forms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti72/en/sujato">Iti 72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti72/en/sujato"><b>cessation</b></a> (nirodha) is the ultimate element of escape for everything that is dependently arisen, conditioned, and has come into being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti72/en/sujato">Iti 72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is "liberated there"—meaning they have understood the escape from form and desire—as a sage who has successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti72/en/sujato"><b>crossed over bonds</b></a> (yoga) and attained peaceful higher knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti72/en/sujato">Iti 72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the formless states are inherently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti73/en/sujato"><b>more peaceful</b></a> than the states of form, representing a deeper level of spiritual refinement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti73/en/sujato">Iti 73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti73/en/sujato"><b>cessation</b></a> is even more peaceful than the formless states, marking it as the highest possible tranquility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti73/en/sujato">Iti 73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that beings who remain in the form realm or the formless realm but fail to understand cessation are destined to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti73/en/sujato"><b>return to rebirth</b></a> and the cycle of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti73/en/sujato">Iti 73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized those who fully comprehend form, do not settle even in the formless states, and find liberation in cessation as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti73/en/sujato"><b>death-abandoners</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti73/en/sujato">Iti 73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by realizing the "Deathless element" with the body and relinquishing all acquisitions, one attains a state that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti73/en/sujato"><b>sorrowless and stainless</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti73/en/sujato">Iti 73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that only by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti73/en/sujato"><b>touching cessation</b></a> can a person truly move beyond the reach of Death and the constant renewal of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti73/en/sujato">Iti 73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti71/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> (sammādiṭṭhi) as a core component of the path that leads a person to a happy destination and a heavenly world after they die? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti71/en/sujato">Iti 71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti72/en/sujato"><b>ardent practitioner</b></a> should always strive to experience the "stilling of all formations" as the only true escape from the conditioned world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti72/en/sujato">Iti 72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three distinct types of sons (or children) found in the world: the superiorly born, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>similarly born</b></a>, and the inferiorly born? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>superiorly born son</b></a> (atijāto) as one who is virtuous and has taken refuge in the Triple Gem, even though his parents are unvirtuous and have not taken refuge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>similarly born son</b></a> (anujāto) as one who is virtuous and takes refuge in the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha, just like his virtuous parents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>inferiorly born son</b></a> (avajāto) is one who is unvirtuous and of evil character, despite having parents who are virtuous and devoted to the Triple Gem? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wise people desire sons who are either superiorly born or similarly born, but they do not desire an inferiorly born son who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>stainer of the family</b></a> (kulagandhano)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a son is "superiorly born" if he observes the five precepts—abstaining from killing, stealing, sexual misconduct, lying, and intoxicants—even if his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>parents are unvirtuous</b></a> and immoral? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a son as "inferiorly born" if he fails to go for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>refuge in the Triple Gem</b></a> (the Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha) while his parents are faithful practitioners who have done so? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized lay followers who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>possessed of faith</b></a> and virtue as being like the moon when it is freed from a cloud, shining brightly in any assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a son who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>maker of merit</b></a> and virtuous is considered superior if his moral conduct exceeds that of his unvirtuous mother and father? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that lay followers (<i>upāsakā</i>) should strive to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>bountiful givers</b></a> and remain free from stinginess to truly shine within the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>atijāto</b></a> to specifically honor a child who transcends the immoral lifestyle of their family by committing to the path of Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a son is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>similarly born</b></a> when he matches his parents' commitment to virtue, such as abstaining from taking what is not given and from fermented and distilled liquors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the lack of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>moral discipline</b></a> and the failure to take refuge as the defining traits of a son who is inferior to his parents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that lay followers who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato"><b>virtuous and wise</b></a> are highly valued because they sustain the family's spiritual integrity rather than causing its decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti74/en/sujato">Iti 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described three types of people in the world using the metaphor of rain: the rainless-like, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato"><b>local-pourer</b></a>, and the universal-pourer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato">Iti 75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a person who gives nothing to anyone—neither food, drink, clothing, nor lodging—as being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato"><b>rainless cloud</b></a> that does not rain anywhere? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato">Iti 75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato"><b>universal-pourer</b></a> to describe a generous person who gives to everyone, including the poor, travelers, and beggars? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato">Iti 75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a truly generous person to a great cloud that thunders, galls, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato"><b>rains everywhere</b></a>, saturating both the highlands and the lowlands? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato">Iti 75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato"><b>wretch among men</b></a> as a person who fails to share their food and drink with ascetics, brahmins, and the needy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato">Iti 75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a person who is compassionate toward all beings and, like a rain-filled cloud, delights in distributing wealth by saying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato"><b>"Give, give!"</b></a> to those in need? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato">Iti 75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the individual who, after collecting wealth righteously through their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato"><b>effort and initiative</b></a>, uses it to satisfy the needs of those who seek help? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato">Iti 75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a "local-pourer" is someone who is selective in their generosity, choosing to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato"><b>give to some</b></a> while refusing to give to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato">Iti 75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed several specific items that a "universal-pourer" provides to others, including food, drink, clothing, vehicles, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato"><b>scented ointments</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti75/en/sujato">Iti 75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person should guard their virtue (<i>sīla</i>) because they desire three specific types of happiness: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato"><b>praise, wealth, and heaven</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato">Iti 76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that even if a person does not commit evil themselves, they will be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato"><b>suspected of evil</b></a> if they associate with those who are known to perform evil deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato">Iti 76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a person becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato"><b>just like the friend</b></a> they make and the person they serve, because the nature of living together inevitably influences one's character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato">Iti 76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the influence of bad companions to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato"><b>poisoned arrow</b></a> that, when placed in a quiver, smears and contaminates even the unpoisoned arrows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato">Iti 76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato"><b>rotting fish</b></a> wrapped in kusa grass to illustrate how associating with a fool makes a person's reputation and character "smell" foul? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato">Iti 76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that associating with a wise person is like wrapping <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato"><b>fragrant incense</b></a> in a leaf, which causes the leaf itself to become fragrant and sweet-smelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato">Iti 76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a wise person should understand that they are like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato"><b>leaf-wrapper</b></a>, taking on the qualities of whatever—good or bad—they choose to contain or associate with? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato">Iti 76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a wise person should not associate with the wicked because the wicked lead others <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato"><b>toward hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato">Iti 76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that associating with virtuous people is the path to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato"><b>happy destination</b></a>, as the virtuous guide their associates toward spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato">Iti 76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that even if one avoids wrongdoing, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato"><b>bad reputation</b></a> will still grow if one continues to spend time with those who lack virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato">Iti 76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a wise person avoids "evil friends" because they are aware of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato"><b>danger of smearing</b></a> their own character through such associations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti76/en/sujato">Iti 76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as a supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti100/en/sujato"><b>physician and surgeon</b></a> who is always ready to be asked for help and who bears his final body? [Iti 100]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated his disciples are his "true-born children," born of the Dhamma and created by the Dhamma, making them <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti100/en/sujato"><b>heirs of the Dhamma</b></a> rather than heirs of material things? [Iti 100]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha distinguished between two kinds of giving—material giving and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti100/en/sujato"><b>gift of the Dhamma</b></a>—and declared the latter to be the foremost of the two? [Iti 100]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are two ways of sharing, material sharing and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti100/en/sujato"><b>sharing of the Dhamma</b></a>, with the sharing of the Dhamma being superior? [Iti 100]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two forms of assistance, material assistance and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti100/en/sujato"><b>assistance through the Dhamma</b></a>, and designated the Dhamma-based version as supreme? [Iti 100]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of two types of sacrifice, material sacrifice and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti100/en/sujato"><b>sacrifice of the Dhamma</b></a>, emphasizing that the sacrifice of the Dhamma is the highest? [Iti 100]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, referred to as the Tathāgata, is said to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti100/en/sujato"><b>compassionate toward all beings</b></a> and free from stinginess in his spiritual sacrifice? [Iti 100]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there are four things that are both easy to find and blameless, one of which is wearing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti101/en/sujato"><b>robes made of rags</b></a> found on a dustheap? [Iti 101]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti101/en/sujato"><b>scraps of alms food</b></a> to be one of the four trifles that are easy to obtain and ethically blameless for a monk? [Iti 101]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his followers to see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti101/en/sujato"><b>foot of a tree</b></a> as a blameless and easily accessible lodging? [Iti 101]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti101/en/sujato"><b>putrid urine</b></a> (used as medicine) as one of the four easy-to-get, blameless items that a monk can use? [Iti 101]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti101/en/sujato"><b>content with trifles</b></a> and easily found, blameless things possesses a factor of monkhood? [Iti 101]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha taught that when a monk is content with simple, blameless requisites, his mind faces no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti101/en/sujato"><b>obstruction in any direction</b></a>? [Iti 101]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti102/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavā) is possible only for one who knows and sees, not for one who does not know or see? [Iti 102]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that it is by "knowing and seeing" the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti102/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a>—suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path—that the taints are destroyed? [Iti 102]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> the Buddha explained that for a trainee (sekha) following the straight path, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti102/en/sujato"><b>knowledge of destruction</b></a> of the taints arises first, followed immediately by final knowledge? [Iti 102]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said once final knowledge arises in one who is liberated, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti102/en/sujato"><b>knowledge of liberation</b></a> arises, confirming that the fetters have been destroyed? [Iti 102]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that Nibbāna, which is the release from all bonds, can never be attained by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti102/en/sujato"><b>lazy person</b></a> or a fool? [Iti 102]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, acting as a "Brahmin," described himself as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti100/en/sujato"><b>ready to be asked</b></a> for help and always having hands clean for giving? [Iti 100]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is diligent and content with blameless, simple things has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti101/en/sujato"><b>mastered the qualities</b></a> that are in accordance with monkhood? [Iti 101]</p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three types of persons who appear in the world for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato"><b>welfare of many</b></a>, out of compassion for the world and for the benefit of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato">Iti 84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Tathāgata as the first type of person who appears in the world to proclaim a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato"><b>perfectly complete holy life</b></a> that is good in the beginning, middle, and end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato">Iti 84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato"><b>Arahant disciple</b></a>—one who has destroyed the taints and lived the holy life—is the second type of person who appears for the happiness and benefit of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato">Iti 84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato"><b>trainee (sekha)</b></a> who is learned and virtuous is the third type of person who appears in the world to teach the Dhamma for the welfare of devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato">Iti 84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is referred to as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato"><b>first great seer</b></a> (mahesi) in the world, followed by his accomplished disciples and trainees? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato">Iti 84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described these three types of noble persons as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato"><b>light-makers</b></a> (pabhaṅkarā) who proclaim the Dhamma and open the door to the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato">Iti 84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that these three noble persons <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato"><b>release many from bonds</b></a> (yoga) by showing the path to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato">Iti 84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to himself as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed caravan-leader</b></a> (satthavāhena) whose followers can make an end of suffering by being diligent in his teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato">Iti 84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that dwelling in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato"><b>contemplation of the unattractive</b></a> in the body allows a monk to abandon the underlying tendency to lust for the element of beauty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato">Iti 85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of breathing</b></a> be well-established internally to prevent the arising of external discursive thoughts that lead to vexation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato">Iti 85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that dwelling in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato"><b>contemplation of impermanence</b></a> in all conditioned things leads to the total abandonment of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato">Iti 85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for those who dwell contemplating impermanence in all conditioned things, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge (vijjā)</b></a> arises as ignorance is discarded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato">Iti 85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is mindful of breathing and contemplates the unattractive in the body as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato"><b>sage (muni)</b></a> who has crossed over the bond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato">Iti 85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is always ardent and sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato"><b>stilling of all conditioned things</b></a> is one who truly sees rightly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato">Iti 85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato"><b>liberated through direct knowledge</b></a> (abhiññā) has reached a state of peace and transcended all spiritual bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato">Iti 85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the Dhamma he proclaims is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato"><b>meaningful and well-phrased</b></a>, intended to guide beings toward purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato">Iti 84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an Arahant disciple is one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato"><b>laid down the burden</b></a> and completely destroyed the fetters of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato">Iti 84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considers those who follow his well-taught path with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a> to be capable of ending suffering right here in this life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato">Iti 84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared these three types of persons (himself, the Arahant, and the trainee) to be the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato"><b>best among devas and humans</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti84/en/sujato">Iti 84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that establishing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness in front</b></a> during breathing meditation helps one remain focused and undisturbed by external thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti85/en/sujato">Iti 85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk practicing in accordance with the Dhamma should speak <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti86/en/sujato"><b>only Dhamma</b></a> and never non-Dhamma when engaged in conversation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti86/en/sujato">Iti 86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that someone truly practicing the Dhamma should only think <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti86/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of Dhamma</b></a> rather than unwholesome or non-Dhamma thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti86/en/sujato">Iti 86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that if a monk is not speaking or thinking of Dhamma, he should remain an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti86/en/sujato"><b>equanimous onlooker</b></a> who is mindful and clearly comprehending? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti86/en/sujato">Iti 86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk who delights in, rejoices in, and reflects upon the Dhamma will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti86/en/sujato"><b>not fall away</b></a> from the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti86/en/sujato">Iti 86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that whether walking, standing, sitting, or lying down, a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti86/en/sujato"><b>stills the mind</b></a> internally can attain true peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti86/en/sujato">Iti 86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that there are three types of unwholesome thoughts—sensual desire, ill will, and harming—which he described as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato"><b>causing blindness</b></a> and a lack of vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato">Iti 87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of sensual desire</b></a> as a primary factor that stifles wisdom and fails to lead a person toward Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato">Iti 87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of ill will</b></a> are obstructive because they cause vexation, lead to a lack of knowledge, and prevent the cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato">Iti 87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of harming</b></a> are considered unwholesome because they cause a lack of vision and do not lead to the ultimate goal of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato">Iti 87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of three wholesome thoughts—renunciation, non-ill will, and non-harming—which serve as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato"><b>providers of vision</b></a> and knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato">Iti 87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of renunciation</b></a> are essential for spiritual progress as they increase wisdom and lead one toward Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato">Iti 87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha designated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato"><b>thoughts of non-harming</b></a> as a way to cultivate wisdom and move closer to the final peace of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato">Iti 87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a heavy rain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato"><b>stilling a cloud of dust</b></a> to describe how wholesome thoughts can settle and calm unwholesome mental activity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato">Iti 87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by stilling unwholesome thoughts and focusing the mind, a person can attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato"><b>state of peace</b></a> right here in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti87/en/sujato">Iti 87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified greed, hatred, and delusion as three <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato"><b>inner stains</b></a> that act as internal enemies, foes, and murderers within a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato">Iti 88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato"><b>greed</b></a> is a producer of harm and a provoker of the mind, yet people often fail to recognize the internal fear that is born from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato">Iti 88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person is overcome by greed, they become blind to their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato"><b>good (attha)</b></a> and lose the ability to see the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato">Iti 88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato"><b>drop of water</b></a> falling off a lotus leaf to describe how greed is abandoned by one who does not lust for desirable things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato">Iti 88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> produces harm and mental agitation, creating an internal danger that most people do not perceive until it is too late? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato">Iti 88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person blinded by hatred cannot see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a>, and their mental state becomes one of utter darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato">Iti 88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the abandonment of hatred to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato"><b>ripe palm fruit</b></a> falling away from its stalk once the bond that held it has been broken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato">Iti 88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> is a provoker of the mind and an internal adversary that prevents people from recognizing the harm they are causing themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato">Iti 88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who dispels delusion dispels all spiritual darkness, much like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato"><b>rising sun</b></a> illuminates the world and removes the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato">Iti 88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that Devadatta was destined for the plane of misery and hell for an entire eon because his mind was overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome things</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato">Iti 89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato"><b>evil ambition</b></a> as one of the primary factors that led Devadatta to be overcome by unwholesome states and destined for hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato">Iti 89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato"><b>evil friendship</b></a> was a contributing factor in Devadatta becoming spiritually incurable and falling into the Avici hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato">Iti 89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that Devadatta's downfall was partly caused by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato"><b>stopping halfway</b></a> in his spiritual progress because of a mere trifling distinction or small attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato">Iti 89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how Devadatta, though once famous and considered a wise man, ended up in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato"><b>dreadful Avici hell</b></a> after attacking the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato">Iti 89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that anyone who tries to harm an innocent person with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato"><b>corrupt mind</b></a> will find that the evil they intended only returns to touch themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato">Iti 89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a person trying to pollute the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato"><b>great ocean</b></a> with a pot of poison to show how verbal abuse cannot affect the peaceful mind of the Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato">Iti 89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised wise people to associate with and serve a friend whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato"><b>path</b></a> a monk can follow to reach the final end of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato">Iti 89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that greed, hatred, and delusion are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato"><b>internal enemies</b></a> and adversaries that people often do not recognize as the source of their own inner fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato">Iti 88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that when greed, hatred, or delusion take hold of a man, his world becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato"><b>utterly dark</b></a> and he loses the ability to perceive the truth of things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti88/en/sujato">Iti 88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that even someone considered a sage or a "developed self" can fall into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato"><b>four-doored Avici hell</b></a> if they harbor evil intentions against a peaceful Tathāgata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti89/en/sujato">Iti 89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an ascetic or brahmin is only considered a "true ascetic among ascetics" or a "true brahmin among brahmins" if they understand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato"><b>suffering as it really is</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato">Iti 103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who do not understand the origin of suffering are unable to realize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato"><b>goal of the spiritual life</b></a> through their own direct knowledge in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato">Iti 103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that individuals who lack knowledge of the path leading to the cessation of suffering are also considered deficient in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato">Iti 103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who fail to see where suffering ceases without remainder are unfit to reach the end of all suffering and are instead destined to continue undergoing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato"><b>birth and aging</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato">Iti 103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha regards those who truly understand the cessation of suffering as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato"><b>endowed with liberation by wisdom</b></a> and capable of making an end to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato">Iti 103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one to be recognized as a true brahmin among brahmins, they must possess a factual understanding of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato"><b>way leading to the subsiding of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato">Iti 103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that practitioners who understand the Four Noble Truths can dwell in this life having personally realized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato"><b>goal of asceticism</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato">Iti 103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the achievement of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> as a primary characteristic of those who have understood the origin and cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato">Iti 103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that practitioners who do not know the path to end suffering are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato"><b>unfit to make an end</b></a> of suffering, remaining trapped in the cycle of birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato">Iti 103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that only those who understand the Four Noble Truths as they really are have successfully achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> required to live as true spiritual practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti103/en/sujato">Iti 103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even the act of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato"><b>seeing accomplished monks</b></a> who are endowed with virtue, concentration, and wisdom is of great benefit to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato">Iti 104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato"><b>visiting and attending on</b></a> monks who have achieved liberation can help a practitioner bring their own incomplete development of virtue and concentration to fulfillment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato">Iti 104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described monks who are accomplished in knowledge and vision of liberation as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato"><b>teachers and caravan-leaders</b></a> who guide others toward spiritual safety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato">Iti 104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified certain noble monks as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato"><b>shedders of conflict</b></a> because they have abandoned the inner "wars" and conflicts of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato">Iti 104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to wise monks who inform and inspire others as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato"><b>dispellers of darkness</b></a> and makers of light for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato">Iti 104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by associating with accomplished monks, a practitioner's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of wisdom</b></a> can reach full development through the influence of their example? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato">Iti 104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called monks who possess liberation of mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato"><b>torch-bearers and luminaries</b></a> because they illuminate the True Dhamma for those around them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato">Iti 104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said those who hear the teachings of wise "Seeing ones" and understand them correctly will achieve the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato"><b>destruction of birth</b></a> and will not return to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato">Iti 104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato"><b>remembering accomplished monks</b></a> (anussaraṇa) and following them into the spiritual life are practices that provide immense assistance to a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato">Iti 104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato"><b>four arisings of craving</b></a> where craving can take hold of a monk, specifically concerning physical needs and existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato">Iti 105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that craving in a monk can specifically arise for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato"><b>robes and alms-food</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato">Iti 105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that craving can arise in a practitioner for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato"><b>lodging</b></a> or for the sake of "being and non-being" (future existence or non-existence)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato">Iti 105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato"><b>craving as their companion</b></a> as someone who wanders for a very long time through the cycle of rebirths without finding an escape? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato">Iti 105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that once a person recognizes craving as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a>, they should strive to live as a monk who is mindful and free from attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato">Iti 105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato"><b>free from craving</b></a> and without grasping is the one who can truly wander through the spiritual life having understood the danger of desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato">Iti 105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that failing to overcome craving means a person will continue <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato"><b>wandering in saṃsāra</b></a>, moving between this state of being and other states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti105/en/sujato">Iti 105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha designated monks who are "Noble ones" as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato"><b>illuminators and radiance-givers</b></a> for those who seek to understand the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti104/en/sujato">Iti 104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that families where parents are honored by their children in the home are said to dwell with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato"><b>Brahma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato">Iti 106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha designated parents as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato"><b>early deities</b></a> for their children because of the immense help and nourishment they provide? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato">Iti 106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to parents as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato"><b>early teachers</b></a> of their children since they are the ones who first show them the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato">Iti 106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that parents are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato"><b>worthy of offerings</b></a> from their children because they are the nourishers and bringers-up of their offspring? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato">Iti 106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged wise children to honor their parents with practical service, such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato"><b>washing their feet</b></a> and bathing them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato">Iti 106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who serve their parents with food, drink, and clothing are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato"><b>praised in this life</b></a> and will rejoice in heaven after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato">Iti 106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato"><b>showers of this world</b></a> to describe parents because they are the ones who introduce their children to human existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti106/en/sujato">Iti 106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato"><b>mutual dependence</b></a> between laypeople and monks, as both groups work together to practice the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato">Iti 107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that lay brahmins and householders are very helpful to monks when they provide <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato"><b>robes and alms-food</b></a> to sustain them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato">Iti 107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said monks are very helpful to householders by teaching them the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a>, which is good in its beginning, middle, and end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato">Iti 107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by relying on one another, householders and monks can successfully cross over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato"><b>flood of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato">Iti 107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the spiritual life is lived for the express purpose of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato"><b>making a final end of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato">Iti 107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that householders who have faith in the Arahants and practice the path will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato"><b>rejoice in the celestial world</b></a> after they pass away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato">Iti 107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that through the support of laypeople, monks are able to ward off the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato"><b>hardships of the homeless life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti107/en/sujato">Iti 107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that monks who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti108/en/sujato"><b>fraudulent and haughty</b></a> are not considered his true disciples and have fallen away from the Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti108/en/sujato">Iti 108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that monks who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti108/en/sujato"><b>not deceitful</b></a> and remain well-composed are his true followers who belong to his teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti108/en/sujato">Iti 108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that talkative, stubborn, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti108/en/sujato"><b>uncomposed monks</b></a> will never achieve growth, increase, or expansion in his discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti108/en/sujato">Iti 108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described wise monks who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti108/en/sujato"><b>not stubborn</b></a> as those who will truly flourish in the Dhamma taught by the Samma-Sambuddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti108/en/sujato">Iti 108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti108/en/sujato"><b>arrogant and flashy</b></a> monks as individuals who do not truly belong to his spiritual community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti108/en/sujato">Iti 108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that only practitioners who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti108/en/sujato"><b>steadfast and composed</b></a> can reach the full potential of the Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti108/en/sujato">Iti 108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the simile of a person being swept away by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato"><b>river current</b></a> to illustrate how craving carries individuals toward spiritual danger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato">Iti 109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the six internal sense bases as being "delightful and agreeable," yet warned that following them is like being carried downstream toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato"><b>death or deadly suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato">Iti 109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha equated a "lake downstream" filled with waves and whirlpools to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato"><b>five lower fetters</b></a> that bind a person to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato">Iti 109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the "fear of waves" in his river parable is a metaphor for the psychological dangers of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato"><b>anger and despair</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato">Iti 109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the "whirlpools" one must avoid in the river of existence represent the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato"><b>five strands of sensual pleasure</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato">Iti 109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the context of a monk’s discipline, the Buddha used the metaphor of "monsters and demons" to describe the potential for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato"><b>women</b></a> to be a source of attachment and distraction on the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato">Iti 109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that "going against the current" in the river of craving is a metaphor for the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato"><b>renunciation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato">Iti 109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that "striving with hands and feet" to swim against the flow of desire represents the necessary <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato"><b>arousing of energy</b></a> for spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato">Iti 109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified himself as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato"><b>man with sight</b></a> standing on the riverbank who warns others of the hidden perils of following their cravings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato">Iti 109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner who abandons sensual pleasures and attains a "fully liberated mind" is one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato"><b>reached the far shore</b></a> of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti109/en/sujato">Iti 109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that "merit" is actually a synonym for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a>, encompassing everything that is pleasing, charming, and dear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato">Iti 22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recalled that by developing a mind of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> for seven years, he did not return to this world for seven aeons of cosmic contraction and expansion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato">Iti 22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, during cycles of cosmic contraction, he would often be reborn in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato"><b>Abhassara</b></a> realm, a plane of streaming radiance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato">Iti 22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how, during cycles of cosmic expansion, he would reappear in an empty <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato"><b>Brahma-palace</b></a> as the Great Brahma, the unconquered Overlord? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato">Iti 22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated he had held the position of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato"><b>Sakka</b></a>, the ruler of the devas, thirty-six times as a result of his past meritorious actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato">Iti 22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once reigned many hundreds of times as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato"><b>wheel-turning monarch</b></a>, a righteous king who conquered the four quarters and possessed the seven treasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato">Iti 22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha attributed his immense power and majesty in previous lives to three specific practices: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato"><b>giving</b></a>, self-mastery, and self-restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato">Iti 22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to train in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato"><b>merit</b></a>, specifically by practicing giving, harmonious living, and the development of a mind of loving-kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato">Iti 22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person who develops qualities that produce happiness will be reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato"><b>happy world</b></a> that is free from affliction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato">Iti 22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato"><b>self-mastery</b></a> (dama) was one of the three primary causes for his attainment of great supernormal power and authority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti22/en/sujato">Iti 22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there is one single quality, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti23/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> (appamāda), which when developed, secures welfare in both the present life and the life to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti23/en/sujato">Iti 23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the wise highly praise <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti23/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> in the performance of meritorious deeds because it ensures the attainment of one's goals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti23/en/sujato">Iti 23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a "wise" person as one who, through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti23/en/sujato"><b>steadfastness</b></a> and heedfulness, manages to achieve both the mundane benefits of this world and the spiritual benefits of the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti23/en/sujato">Iti 23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a striking image to describe the length of saṃsāra, stating that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato"><b>pile of bones</b></a> of a single person wandering for one aeon would be as large as a mountain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato">Iti 24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the accumulated bones of one person's rebirths over an aeon to the size of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato"><b>Mount Vepulla</b></a>, located near the Magadhan capital of Giribbaja? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato">Iti 24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the cycle of rebirth only begins to end when a person sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> with right wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato">Iti 24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato"><b>Eightfold Path</b></a> as the specific spiritual journey that leads directly to the calming of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato">Iti 24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person who gains insight into the Noble Truths will wander in saṃsāra for a maximum of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato"><b>seven more times</b></a> before making an end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato">Iti 24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the ultimate end of suffering is achieved through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato"><b>destruction of all fetters</b></a> that bind a person to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato">Iti 24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato"><b>heap of bones</b></a> was intended to emphasize how much physical remains a single individual would leave behind if their bones did not decay during a single cosmic age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti24/en/sujato">Iti 24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all other forms of merit-making aimed at a better rebirth are not worth even a sixteenth part of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind by loving-kindness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato">Iti 27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the way loving-kindness outshines other merits to how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato"><b>radiance of the moon</b></a> outshines the light of all the stars? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato">Iti 27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the mind liberated by love as being like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato"><b>autumn sun</b></a>, which rises into a clear sky and dispels all darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato">Iti 27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha likened the brilliance of a mind well-developed in loving-kindness to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato"><b>morning star</b></a> as it beams and shines at the break of dawn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato">Iti 27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who develops immeasurable loving-kindness while seeing the "end of acquisitions" finds that their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato"><b>fetters</b></a> are worn thin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato">Iti 27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a person has a mind of love for even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato"><b>single living being</b></a>, they become wholesome through that act alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato">Iti 27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a "Noble One" (ariya) who develops a mind of compassion for the entire world of beings creates <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato"><b>abundant merit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato">Iti 27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha claimed that ancient royal sages who performed massive sacrifices, such as the horse sacrifice, did not achieve a sixteenth part of the value of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato"><b>well-developed mind of love</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato">Iti 27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a person who does not kill, conquer, or cause others to do so, and who has love for all beings, has no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato"><b>enmity</b></a> toward anyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato">Iti 27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> through loving-kindness is the practice that truly outshines, beams, and radiates over all other meritorious works? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti27/en/sujato">Iti 27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who possesses two specific unwholesome qualities will live in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> even in this present life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato">Iti 28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato"><b>unguarded sense doors</b></a> as one of the two primary reasons a practitioner experiences vexation, despair, and fever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato">Iti 28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato"><b>immoderation in eating</b></a>, combined with a lack of sense restraint, leads to a state of misery and distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato">Iti 28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who fails to guard their sense doors can expect a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato"><b>bad destination</b></a> or rebirth after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato">Iti 28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed the six doors that a monk must guard to avoid suffering as the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato">Iti 28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the internal state of a person who is immoderate in eating as living with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato"><b>burning body</b></a> and a burning mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato">Iti 28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is unrestrained in his senses and immoderate in food will live in suffering <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato"><b>day and night</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato">Iti 28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato"><b>bodily pain</b></a> and mental pain are the inevitable outcomes for one who does not practice restraint of the sense faculties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato">Iti 28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that failing to restrain the senses and being immoderate with food leads to a life filled with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato"><b>vexation</b></a> and despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato">Iti 28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a practitioner who remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained</b></a>, the experience of "living in suffering" includes both present-life distress and the threat of a future painful rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti28/en/sujato">Iti 28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti29/en/sujato"><b>guarding the sense doors</b></a> and moderation in eating can live happily and without distress in the present life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti29/en/sujato">Iti 29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti29/en/sujato"><b>moderate in eating</b></a> and restrains their senses can expect a happy destination after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti29/en/sujato">Iti 29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the six doors that must be well-guarded by a monk as the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti29/en/sujato"><b>mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti29/en/sujato">Iti 29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person who is restrained in their senses and moderate in food experiences both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti29/en/sujato"><b>bodily pleasure</b></a> and mental pleasure throughout the day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti29/en/sujato">Iti 29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a practitioner who is guarded in their senses and restrained in eating lives without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti29/en/sujato"><b>burning</b></a> (apariḷāhaṃ) in either body or mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti29/en/sujato">Iti 29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti30/en/sujato"><b>remorse</b></a> (literally "burning") arises in a person when they realize they have failed to do what is wholesome and have instead committed evil deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti30/en/sujato">Iti 30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who has done evil, cruel, and wicked deeds without creating a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti30/en/sujato"><b>refuge</b></a> of good actions will experience regret at the end of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti30/en/sujato">Iti 30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified misconduct of body, speech, and mind as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti30/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome actions</b></a> that lead an unwise person to rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti30/en/sujato">Iti 30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti30/en/sujato"><b>regret</b></a> is fueled by two specific thoughts: "I have not done what is good" and "I have done what is evil"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti30/en/sujato">Iti 30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who performs harmful deeds associated with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti30/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> and fails to do good is reborn in a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti30/en/sujato">Iti 30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has practiced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti31/en/sujato"><b>wholesome deeds</b></a> and refrained from evil does not suffer from remorse or "burning" of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti31/en/sujato">Iti 31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, one who abandons misconduct of body, speech, and mind and performs many good deeds is reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti31/en/sujato"><b>heaven</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti31/en/sujato">Iti 31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti31/en/sujato"><b>non-remorse</b></a> (atapanīya) to describe the peaceful internal state of someone who has not done wrong and has done much good? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti31/en/sujato">Iti 31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a person who avoids bad conduct and does what is wholesome as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti31/en/sujato"><b>wise</b></a> and destined for a happy state after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti31/en/sujato">Iti 31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person is "cast into hell" if they possess both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti32/en/sujato"><b>bad virtuous conduct</b></a> and bad view? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti32/en/sujato">Iti 32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti32/en/sujato"><b>bad view</b></a> (pāpikā diṭṭhi) as one of the two specific qualities that ensure an unwise person's rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti32/en/sujato">Iti 32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that being possessed of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti32/en/sujato"><b>evil virtue</b></a> (pāpaka sīla) leads a person to a painful destination after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti32/en/sujato">Iti 32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti32/en/sujato"><b>unwise person</b></a> (duppañño) who holds wrong views and practices bad conduct will inevitably experience rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti32/en/sujato">Iti 32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that as long as a person does not fully understand and abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti8/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a>, they are incapable of making an end to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti8/en/sujato">Iti 8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described beings who are delighted by existence and bound by the bond of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti8/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a> as those destined to return to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti8/en/sujato">Iti 8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who have overcome the bond of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti8/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a> and are liberated by its destruction have transcended all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti8/en/sujato">Iti 8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti11/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> as the specific mental state by which beings are confused and led to a bad destination or state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti11/en/sujato">Iti 11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that "seeing ones" (vipassino) are those who abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti11/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> through right knowledge and consequently never return to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti11/en/sujato">Iti 11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that unless a person's mind becomes dispassionate toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti11/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> and abandons it, they remain incapable of reaching the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti11/en/sujato">Iti 11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that beings who are stained by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti13/en/sujato"><b>denigration</b></a> (makkha) are led by that quality to experience a bad destination after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti13/en/sujato">Iti 13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that only by fully understanding and letting go of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti13/en/sujato"><b>denigration</b></a> can a practitioner become capable of achieving the destruction of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti13/en/sujato">Iti 13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those with insight who correctly understand and abandon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti13/en/sujato"><b>denigration</b></a> will never have to return to this world of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti13/en/sujato">Iti 13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that there is no other single hindrance that causes people to wander and roam in saṃsāra for so long as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti14/en/sujato"><b>hindrance of ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti14/en/sujato">Iti 14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the world as being "shrouded" by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti14/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a>, which causes beings to drift through the cycle of rebirth both day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti14/en/sujato">Iti 14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who shatter the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti14/en/sujato"><b>mass of darkness</b></a> (tamokhandha) by abandoning delusion no longer have any cause to continue wandering in the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti14/en/sujato">Iti 14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, beings who are obstructed by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti14/en/sujato"><b>hindrance of ignorance</b></a> have been wandering and roaming through saṃsāra for an incredibly long duration of time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti14/en/sujato">Iti 14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that no other single fetter binds beings to the cycle of rebirth for as long as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti15/en/sujato"><b>fetter of craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti15/en/sujato">Iti 15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti15/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> as a person's "companion" (dutiyo) that keeps them wandering in saṃsāra for a long time, moving between various states of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti15/en/sujato">Iti 15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to live mindfully and without grasping, having recognized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti15/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> as the very origin and birth of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti15/en/sujato">Iti 15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti15/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> as the specific bond that prevents a person from transcending the movement between "this state and other states" in the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti15/en/sujato">Iti 15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti15/en/sujato"><b>free from craving</b></a> (vītataṇho) and without acquisitions is the one who truly wanders through the world with mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti15/en/sujato">Iti 15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti8/en/sujato"><b>full understanding</b></a> (pariññā) to describe the necessary insight into conceit that allows one to finally be capable of ending all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti8/en/sujato">Iti 8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti11/en/sujato"><b>right knowledge</b></a> (sammadaññāya) regarding delusion are able to abandon it and ensure they never return to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti11/en/sujato">Iti 11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that if a practitioner lives devoted to the training with wisdom as the outcome and liberation as the core, they can expect either <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti46/en/sujato"><b>final knowledge</b></a> in this life or the state of non-returning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti46/en/sujato">Iti 46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true sage as one who is complete in training, has seen the end of birth, and is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti46/en/sujato"><b>conqueror of Mara</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti46/en/sujato">Iti 46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged monks to be constant in meditation and resolute so they might overcome Mara's army and go <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti46/en/sujato"><b>beyond birth and death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti46/en/sujato">Iti 46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the spiritual life should be lived with wisdom as its outcome, liberation as its core, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti46/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness as its authority</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti46/en/sujato">Iti 46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called a person a "muni" (sage) if they are the bearer of their final body and have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti46/en/sujato"><b>gone beyond old age</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti46/en/sujato">Iti 46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who lives wakeful, mindful, and clearly comprehending can attain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti47/en/sujato"><b>supreme enlightenment</b></a> in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti47/en/sujato">Iti 47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti47/en/sujato"><b>no fear</b></a> for those who are wakeful and alert? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti47/en/sujato">Iti 47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to investigate the Dhamma at the right time to become unified in mind and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti47/en/sujato"><b>destroy darkness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti47/en/sujato">Iti 47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a wakeful monk as one who is glad, serene, and consistently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti47/en/sujato"><b>discerning wholesome states</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti47/en/sujato">Iti 47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that by devoting oneself to wakefulness and cutting the fetters of birth and old age, a wise monk can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti47/en/sujato"><b>touch enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti47/en/sujato">Iti 47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified two types of people who are bound for a state of misery: the one who falsely claims to be celibate and the one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti48/en/sujato"><b>falsely accuses</b></a> a pure practitioner of misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti48/en/sujato">Iti 48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti48/en/sujato"><b>untruthful person</b></a> who denies their own wrongdoings will face the same destination in the afterlife as a person of base actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti48/en/sujato">Iti 48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that many who wear the ochre robe are actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti48/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained</b></a> and will go to hell because of their evil deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti48/en/sujato">Iti 48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is better to swallow a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti48/en/sujato"><b>red-hot iron ball</b></a> like a flame of fire than for an immoral, unrestrained person to live off the alms-food provided by the country? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti48/en/sujato">Iti 48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti48/en/sujato"><b>immoral and unrestrained</b></a> yet accept offerings from the people are heading toward a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti48/en/sujato">Iti 48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that many people are "held back" or "fall short" because they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti49/en/sujato"><b>delight in existence</b></a> and find that their minds do not leap forward when the Dhamma for the cessation of existence is taught? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti49/en/sujato">Iti 49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described some people as "overshooting" the truth because, out of disgust for existence, they mistakenly praise <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti49/en/sujato"><b>annihilation</b></a> and believe the self is destroyed at death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti49/en/sujato">Iti 49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that "those with vision" see "what has come to be" exactly as it is and practice for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti49/en/sujato"><b>fading away</b></a> and cessation of that state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti49/en/sujato">Iti 49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a monk has fully understood "what has come to be" and has exhausted the craving for existence, he does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti49/en/sujato"><b>return to birth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti49/en/sujato">Iti 49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those who see the nature of what has come to be and go beyond it are liberated through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti49/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a> for existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti49/en/sujato">Iti 49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified greed, hatred, and delusion as the three primary <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti50/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome roots</b></a> that can overwhelm a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti50/en/sujato">Iti 50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared unwholesome states like greed and hatred to a fruit that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti50/en/sujato"><b>harms the reed</b></a> from which it was produced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti50/en/sujato">Iti 50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that greed, hatred, and delusion arise within a person of evil mind and proceed to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti50/en/sujato"><b>harm that person</b></a> directly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti50/en/sujato">Iti 50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there are three distinct <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti52/en/sujato"><b>types of feeling</b></a> that humans experience: pleasant, painful, and neither-pleasant-nor-painful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti52/en/sujato">Iti 52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a mindful and concentrated disciple must understand not only the feelings themselves but also the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti52/en/sujato"><b>origin of feelings</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti52/en/sujato">Iti 52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who has attained the destruction of feelings as being "hungerless" and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti52/en/sujato"><b>attained to Nibbana</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti52/en/sujato">Iti 52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a practitioner should understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti52/en/sujato"><b>path leading to the destruction</b></a> of feelings to reach liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti52/en/sujato">Iti 52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that a practitioner should view pleasant feeling as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato"><b>painful</b></a> to understand its underlying nature of change and unsatisfactoriness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato">Iti 53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that painful feeling should be seen as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato"><b>dart</b></a> (salla) that should be recognized and removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato">Iti 53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a neutral feeling (neither-pleasant-nor-painful) should be regarded as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato"><b>impermanent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato">Iti 53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk who sees all three types of feeling correctly is a "noble one" who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato"><b>cut off craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato">Iti 53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk becomes a "right-seer" when he has made an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a> through the full understanding of conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato">Iti 53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to a practitioner who has gone beyond the bonds of the world as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato"><b>peaceful sage</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato">Iti 53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who has mastered the understanding of feelings is one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato"><b>removed the fetters</b></a> of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato">Iti 53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by seeing pleasant things as painful and painful things as a dart, a monk becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a> through direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti53/en/sujato">Iti 53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three types of searches that people engage in: the search for sensual desire, the search for existence, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti54/en/sujato"><b>search for the spiritual life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti54/en/sujato">Iti 54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a mindful disciple of the Enlightened One should understand not only the three types of searches but also the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti54/en/sujato"><b>origin of searches</b></a> and the path leading to their cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti54/en/sujato">Iti 54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a monk becomes "hungerless" and fully extinguished once he has attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti54/en/sujato"><b>destruction of searches</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti54/en/sujato">Iti 54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the searches for sensual desire and existence, along with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti55/en/sujato"><b>dogmatic adherence to truth</b></a>, are linked to the accumulation of various viewpoints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti55/en/sujato">Iti 55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who has destroyed the three searches as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti55/en/sujato"><b>free from doubt</b></a> and has abandoned the longing for future existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti55/en/sujato">Iti 55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one who is detached from all passion and liberated by the destruction of craving, all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti55/en/sujato"><b>views have been uprooted</b></a> and searches relinquished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti55/en/sujato">Iti 55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha categorized three specific taints (āsavā) that affect sentient beings: the taint of sensuality, the taint of existence, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti56/en/sujato"><b>taint of ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti56/en/sujato">Iti 56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a disciple who is concentrated and mindful understands the taints, where they cease, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti56/en/sujato"><b>path leading to their destruction</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti56/en/sujato">Iti 56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti56/en/sujato"><b>exhaustion of taints</b></a>, a practitioner becomes "hungerless" and reaches Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti56/en/sujato">Iti 56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that one who has destroyed the taint of sensuality, dispelled ignorance, and exhausted the taint of existence is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti57/en/sujato"><b>liberated and without acquisitions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti57/en/sujato">Iti 57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a practitioner who has destroyed the three taints is one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti57/en/sujato"><b>conquered Mara</b></a> and his entire army? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti57/en/sujato">Iti 57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha referred to the person who has completely exhausted the taints as the one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti57/en/sujato"><b>bears the final body</b></a>, indicating they will not be reborn again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti57/en/sujato">Iti 57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three distinct forms of craving: sensual craving, craving for existence, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti58/en/sujato"><b>craving for extermination</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti58/en/sujato">Iti 58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that those bound by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti58/en/sujato"><b>yoke of craving</b></a> are subject to the power of Mara and continue to travel the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti58/en/sujato">Iti 58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who abandon craving for existence and have attained the destruction of the taints have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti58/en/sujato"><b>reached the far shore</b></a> in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti58/en/sujato">Iti 58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who possesses the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti59/en/sujato"><b>virtuous conduct</b></a> of one beyond training (an asekha) shines like the sun after transcending the realm of Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti59/en/sujato">Iti 59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a practitioner must be endowed with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti59/en/sujato"><b>aggregate of concentration</b></a> of one beyond training to successfully transcend Mara's domain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti59/en/sujato">Iti 59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when the aggregate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti59/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> is fully developed in one beyond training, that person surpasses the reach of the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti59/en/sujato">Iti 59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk who has perfected virtue, concentration, and wisdom to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti59/en/sujato"><b>shining sun</b></a> because they have gone beyond the power of Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti59/en/sujato">Iti 59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three distinct <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti60/en/sujato"><b>bases of meritorious activity</b></a> that a person should cultivate: giving, ethical conduct, and mental development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti60/en/sujato">Iti 60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged people to train in merit because it brings about an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti60/en/sujato"><b>excellent happiness</b></a> that has lasting results for the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti60/en/sujato">Iti 60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one should cultivate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti60/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> as one of the three essential practices that lead to happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti60/en/sujato">Iti 60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a wise person who develops the three sources of merit will be reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti60/en/sujato"><b>happy world</b></a> that is free from affliction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti60/en/sujato">Iti 60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that an unwise person who engages in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti64/en/sujato"><b>bodily misconduct</b></a>, verbal misconduct, and mental misconduct is bound for rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti64/en/sujato">Iti 64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti64/en/sujato"><b>mental misconduct</b></a>, along with other actions driven by fault, leads a person to misery after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti64/en/sujato">Iti 64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that failing to perform wholesome deeds while performing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti64/en/sujato"><b>many unwholesome actions</b></a> is the path that leads an unwise person to a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti64/en/sujato">Iti 64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person who abandons bodily and verbal misconduct and practices <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti65/en/sujato"><b>good conduct</b></a> is reborn in a heavenly world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti65/en/sujato">Iti 65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that by abandoning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti65/en/sujato"><b>verbal misconduct</b></a> and other faulty actions, a practitioner ensures a positive destination after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti65/en/sujato">Iti 65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person of wisdom who performs <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti65/en/sujato"><b>many wholesome deeds</b></a> will arise in heaven after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti65/en/sujato">Iti 65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti66/en/sujato"><b>three types of purification</b></a>: purification of body, purification of speech, and purification of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti66/en/sujato">Iti 66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who is pure in body and speech and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti66/en/sujato"><b>taintless in mind</b></a> as one who has truly abandoned all unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti66/en/sujato">Iti 66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti66/en/sujato"><b>endowed with purification</b></a> in their heart and conduct is one who is considered truly pure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti66/en/sujato">Iti 66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti67/en/sujato"><b>three types of sagacity</b></a>, which are the sagacity of body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti67/en/sujato">Iti 67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called a person a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti67/en/sujato"><b>sage</b></a> (muni) if they possess sagacity of body and speech and a mind that is free from taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti67/en/sujato">Iti 67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti67/en/sujato"><b>endowed with sagacity</b></a> in all three ways—body, speech, and mind—is someone who has washed away their evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti67/en/sujato">Iti 67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the human body as inherently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti77/en/sujato"><b>fragile</b></a> and taught that consciousness is of a nature that is subject to fading away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti77/en/sujato">Iti 77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti77/en/sujato"><b>acquisitions</b></a> (upadhī) are impermanent, characterized by suffering, and are subject to change? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti77/en/sujato">Iti 77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that by recognizing the danger in acquisitions and understanding the breaking nature of the body, a practitioner can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti77/en/sujato"><b>transcend birth and death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti77/en/sujato">Iti 77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, an individual who has developed their mind and attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti77/en/sujato"><b>supreme peace</b></a> simply waits for the appropriate time for their life to conclude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti77/en/sujato">Iti 77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings naturally come together and associate with one another based on their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato"><b>elements</b></a> (dhātu), or their underlying dispositions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato">Iti 78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that throughout the past, present, and future, beings with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato"><b>low inclinations</b></a> (hīnādhimuttikā) have always associated and converged with others of low inclinations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato">Iti 78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that beings with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato"><b>beautiful inclinations</b></a> (kalyāṇādhimuttikā) consistently associate and come together with others who share those same positive dispositions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato">Iti 78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person of good life can "sink" spiritually, much like a person clinging to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato"><b>small piece of wood</b></a> in the great ocean, if they associate with those who are lazy and indolent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato">Iti 78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato"><b>entanglement</b></a> is born from social association, and that this entanglement is only cut off through non-association and solitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato">Iti 78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised practitioners to avoid the lazy and those of low energy, and instead always live with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato"><b>wise</b></a> who are energetic and meditative? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato">Iti 78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified three specific behaviors—delighting in activities, delighting in talk, and delighting in sleep—that lead to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato"><b>spiritual decline</b></a> of a monk in training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato">Iti 79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk in training (sekha) will avoid decline if he does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato"><b>delight in talk</b></a> or find pleasure in social chatter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato">Iti 79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato"><b>restless</b></a> and preoccupied with work and talk is incapable of reaching supreme enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato">Iti 79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato"><b>unrestless</b></a> (anuddhato) and having few duties are essential qualities for a monk to be capable of attaining supreme enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato">Iti 79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato"><b>delight in work</b></a> (kammārāmatā) is a factor that causes a practitioner to fall away from the path of training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato">Iti 79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly listed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato"><b>delight in sleep</b></a> (niddārāmatā) as one of the three primary obstacles to the spiritual progress of a trainee? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato">Iti 79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha recommended associating with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato"><b>noble ones</b></a> (ariyehi) who are secluded, energetic, and intent on spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato">Iti 78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that even a person of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato"><b>good life</b></a> (sādhujīvī) can fail if they rely on or associate with those who are lazy and lack energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti78/en/sujato">Iti 78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that recognizing the body as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti77/en/sujato"><b>breaking</b></a> and consciousness as fading is key to attaining the supreme peace of Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti77/en/sujato">Iti 77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato"><b>monk in training</b></a> (sekha) to refer to those who must specifically guard against delighting in work, talk, and sleep to prevent their spiritual downfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti79/en/sujato">Iti 79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti80/en/sujato"><b>three unwholesome thoughts</b></a>—those connected with not being despised, those connected with gain and honor, and those connected with fondness for associates—that can seriously hinder a monk’s spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti80/en/sujato">Iti 80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a practitioner who delights in the company of associates and is preoccupied with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti80/en/sujato"><b>gain and honor</b></a> remains far from reaching the destruction of the fetters that bind one to rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti80/en/sujato">Iti 80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is able to abandon worldly ties, such as children, livestock, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti80/en/sujato"><b>marriages</b></a>, is capable of attaining supreme enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti80/en/sujato">Iti 80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically cautioned against the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti80/en/sujato"><b>thought of not being despised</b></a>, which refers to an unwholesome concern for one's social status and reputation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti80/en/sujato">Iti 80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti80/en/sujato"><b>fond of associates</b></a> (sahanandī amaccehi) is a type of unwholesome thought that keeps the mind tied to worldly concerns rather than spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti80/en/sujato">Iti 80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared he had personally seen beings who were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato"><b>overcome by honor</b></a> and renown, whose minds were so obsessed that they were reborn in hell after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato">Iti 81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato"><b>overcome by a lack of honor</b></a> is just as dangerous as being obsessed with praise, as both states of mind can lead to rebirth in a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato">Iti 81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that his teachings on the destinies of those obsessed with honor were based on what he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato"><b>directly known and seen</b></a> for himself, rather than on hearsay from other teachers or contemplatives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato">Iti 81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a "good person" is defined by having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a> that does not waver regardless of whether they are being honored or are facing a total lack of public respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato">Iti 81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the ideal meditator as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato"><b>subtle visionary</b></a> who remains constantly heedful and unshaken by the ups and downs of worldly gain or loss? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato">Iti 81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato"><b>delights in the destruction of clinging</b></a> is the person truly worthy of being called a "good man" (sappuriso)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato">Iti 81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato"><b>mental obsession</b></a> resulting from both honor and its absence can lead beings to a downward path and rebirth in the underworld? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti81/en/sujato">Iti 81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a noble disciple decides to shave their hair and beard and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato"><b>go forth from home to homelessness</b></a>, a cry rings out among the devas announcing that this person intends to do battle with Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato">Iti 82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the devas proclaim that a noble disciple is actively <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato"><b>battling Mara</b></a> whenever that practitioner dwells devoted to the development of the seven factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato">Iti 82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a third "divine cry" rings out among the gods when a noble disciple has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato"><b>destroyed the taints</b></a> and realized for themselves the taintless liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato">Iti 82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once a noble disciple attains final liberation in this very life, the devas announce that this practitioner is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato"><b>victorious in battle</b></a> and has conquered the very peak of the spiritual battlefield? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato">Iti 82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even the devas pay homage to a disciple who is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato"><b>sublime winner of the battle</b></a> and has overcome all spiritual dread? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato">Iti 82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared a verse where the devas salute a liberated disciple as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato"><b>thoroughbred among men</b></a> because they have conquered the difficult-to-defeat army of Death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato">Iti 82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the state of a fully liberated person as having conquered the army of Death (Maccu) through an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato"><b>unobstructed liberation</b></a> (vimokkhena anāvaraṃ)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato">Iti 82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato"><b>three specific occasions</b></a> when the "cries of the gods" (devasaddā) ring out to celebrate the progress of a noble disciple toward Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato">Iti 82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the devas honor a liberated practitioner because they no longer see in that person any trace of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato"><b>basis for rebirth</b></a> that would lead them back under the power of Death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti82/en/sujato">Iti 82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a deva is about to pass away from a heavenly realm, five premonitory signs appear, including that their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato"><b>flowers wither</b></a> and their celestial clothes become soiled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato">Iti 83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato"><b>sweat from the armpits</b></a> and a loss of physical beauty as two of the five specific omens that manifest when a deva is due to pass away from their divine state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato">Iti 83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a deva who is about to pass away no longer finds <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato"><b>delight in their throne</b></a>, which serves as a clear sign of their impending death and departure from the heavens? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato">Iti 83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a deva passing away from their celestial world, the "good destination" (sugati) that their fellow devas encourage them to seek is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato"><b>human birth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato">Iti 83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the best gain a former deva can achieve upon being reborn as a human is to acquire <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> (saddha) in the Dhamma and Discipline proclaimed by the Tathagata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato">Iti 83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined "well-established" faith as a conviction that is so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato"><b>firmly rooted</b></a> and stable that it cannot be shaken by any ascetic, brahmin, deva, Mara, or Brahma in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato">Iti 83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha shared that devas use three specific phrases to cheer on a fellow deva who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato"><b>passing away</b></a>, hoping they reach the human world, gain faith, and become well-established in the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato">Iti 83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that after attaining a human birth and gaining supreme faith, one should abandon bodily, verbal, and mental misconduct and perform <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato"><b>meritorious deeds</b></a> through abundant giving (dana)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato">Iti 83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned that devas compassionately urge those departing to the human world to not only practice the Dhamma themselves but also to establish other mortals in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> (brahmacariya)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato">Iti 83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a noble disciple with unshakable faith as being "well-established" in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato"><b>true Dhamma</b></a>, a state which even death cannot destroy once that person has abandoned unwholesome actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti83/en/sujato">Iti 83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there are three supreme kinds of confidence, and for those with confidence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato"><b>foremost</b></a>, the result of their merit is also foremost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato">Iti 90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified himself, the Tathagata, as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato"><b>supreme being</b></a> among all creatures, whether they are footless, two-footed, four-footed, or many-footed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato">Iti 90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> (virāga) to be the supreme of all things, whether those things are conditioned or unconditioned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato">Iti 90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the supreme state of dispassion as the crushing of pride, the removal of thirst, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato"><b>uprooting of attachment</b></a>, and the cessation of suffering known as Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato">Iti 90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the community of his disciples (the Sangha) is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato"><b>foremost assembly</b></a> among all groups or flocks in existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato">Iti 90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the Sangha of disciples—consisting of the four pairs or eight types of individuals—as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato">Iti 90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a wise person gives a gift to what is supreme, they gain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato"><b>supreme merit</b></a>, which leads to a long life, beauty, fame, and happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato">Iti 90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, those who have confidence in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato"><b>supreme Dhamma</b></a> and the peace of dispassion will experience the highest form of happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato">Iti 90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person who gives to the foremost and is concentrated on the supreme Dhamma will rejoice in reaching a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato"><b>supreme state</b></a>, whether as a deva or a human? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato">Iti 90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified those who are confident in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato"><b>unrivaled Buddha</b></a>, who is the most worthy of gifts, as those who have established themselves in the supreme? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti90/en/sujato">Iti 90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized the livelihood of alms-gathering (piṇḍolya) as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato"><b>lowest form</b></a> of living, often accompanied by the social stigma of being a beggar with a bowl in hand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato">Iti 91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that noble sons go forth into the homeless life not out of fear of kings or debt, but with the goal of reaching the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a> and its entire mass of birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato">Iti 91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who remains full of longing for sensual pleasures and possesses a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato"><b>corrupt mind</b></a> is failing in his spiritual purpose despite having gone forth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato">Iti 91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a corrupt, immoral monk to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato"><b>funeral firebrand</b></a> (chavālāta) that is burning at both ends and smeared with dung in the middle, making it useless for any purpose? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato">Iti 91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an unsuccessful monk as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato"><b>fallen away</b></a> from the enjoyments of a householder while also failing to fulfill the goal of the monkhood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato">Iti 91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that many people who wear the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato"><b>saffron robe</b></a> around their necks are actually of evil character and lack self-restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato">Iti 91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who are evil and commit wicked deeds will, as a result of their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato"><b>evil actions</b></a>, be reborn in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato">Iti 91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it would be better for a person to swallow a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato"><b>red-hot iron ball</b></a> than to eat the alms-food provided by the country while being immoral and unrestrained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato">Iti 91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk who is muddle-minded, lacks alertness, and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato"><b>uncomposed</b></a> is like a piece of wood from a pyre that serves no good in the village or the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato">Iti 91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those who go forth because they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato"><b>overwhelmed by suffering</b></a> should strive to ensure that the entire mass of that suffering can be brought to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti91/en/sujato">Iti 91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if a monk were to follow closely behind him, grasping the corner of his robe and stepping in his footprints, that monk is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti92/en/sujato"><b>far from the Buddha</b></a> if he remains full of desire, ill-will, and a corrupt mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti92/en/sujato">Iti 92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated a person does not truly see him unless they see the Dhamma, and that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti92/en/sujato"><b>not seeing the Dhamma</b></a> is the reason why a person with a confused and uncomposed mind remains distant from him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti92/en/sujato">Iti 92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk living even a hundred leagues away is considered to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti92/en/sujato"><b>close to him</b></a> if that monk is free from sensual desire, possesses a mind of non-ill will, and is mindful and composed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti92/en/sujato">Iti 92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha famously declared that one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti92/en/sujato"><b>sees the Dhamma</b></a> sees him, because it is through the realization of the truth that one truly draws near to the Tathagata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti92/en/sujato">Iti 92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a wise person who has fully understood the Dhamma and is free from agitation to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti92/en/sujato"><b>calm lake</b></a> in a windless place? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti92/en/sujato">Iti 92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that one who is full of longing and agitation is far from the state of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti92/en/sujato"><b>one who is quenched</b></a> (nibbuta), just as the greedy is far from the one who is free from greed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti92/en/sujato">Iti 92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato"><b>three fires</b></a> that burn within beings: the fire of lust (rāgaggi), the fire of hatred (dosaggi), and the fire of delusion (mohaggi)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato">Iti 93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the fire of lust burns those mortals who are obsessed and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato"><b>infatuated with sensual pleasures</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato">Iti 93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the fire of hatred as that which burns malicious people who are prone to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato"><b>taking life</b></a> and harming others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato">Iti 93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned the fire of delusion burns those who are confused and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato"><b>unskilled in the Noble Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato">Iti 93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that those who do not understand these three internal fires and delight in self-identity continue to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato"><b>increase the realms</b></a> of hell, the animal world, and the sphere of hungry ghosts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato">Iti 93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the fire of lust is quenched by those who are constantly devoted to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato"><b>perception of foulness</b></a> (asubhasaññā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato">Iti 93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated the fire of hatred is quenched by the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> (mettā) by the best of men? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato">Iti 93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the fire of delusion is quenched by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato"><b>penetrative wisdom</b></a> (paññā) that leads to the total destruction of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti93/en/sujato">Iti 93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to investigate in such a way that their consciousness is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti94/en/sujato"><b>scattered or distracted</b></a> outwardly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti94/en/sujato">Iti 94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should ensure his mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti94/en/sujato"><b>not settled inwardly</b></a> (stuck or fixated) and that by not clinging, he remains free from agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti94/en/sujato">Iti 94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when consciousness is neither scattered outwardly nor stuck inwardly, the future <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti94/en/sujato"><b>origin of birth</b></a>, aging, death, and suffering is brought to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti94/en/sujato">Iti 94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that for a monk who has abandoned the seven fetters and cut the cord of craving, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti94/en/sujato"><b>wandering through births</b></a> is finished and there is no more rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti94/en/sujato">Iti 94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described three distinct types of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti95/en/sujato"><b>rebirth in the sensual realm</b></a>, which include those who experience pleasures as they are presented, those who delight in their own creations, and those who control others' creations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti95/en/sujato">Iti 95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even those reborn as deities who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti95/en/sujato"><b>control the creations of others</b></a> (paranimmitavasavattino) have not yet transcended the wandering through the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti95/en/sujato">Iti 95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a wise person, recognizing the danger in sensual pleasures, should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti95/en/sujato"><b>abandon all desires</b></a>, whether they are human or divine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti95/en/sujato">Iti 95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that by cutting the difficult-to-cross stream of craving, a person can reach Nibbāna and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti95/en/sujato"><b>surpass all suffering</b></a> without any residue remaining? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti95/en/sujato">Iti 95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified those who possess the highest knowledge and see the Noble Truths as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti95/en/sujato"><b>wise ones</b></a> who, having understood the end of birth, do not return to any state of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti95/en/sujato">Iti 95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any person bound by both the bond of sensual pleasure and the bond of existence is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti96/en/sujato"><b>returner</b></a> (āgāmī) who will come back to this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti96/en/sujato">Iti 96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti96/en/sujato"><b>non-returner</b></a> (anāgāmī) as a person who has completely untied the bond of sensual pleasure but remains tied by the bond of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti96/en/sujato">Iti 96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that an Arahant is one who has destroyed the taints and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti96/en/sujato"><b>liberated from both bonds</b></a>: the bond of sensual pleasure and the bond of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti96/en/sujato">Iti 96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that beings who are fettered by both sensual and existential bonds continue to travel through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti96/en/sujato"><b>birth and death</b></a> within the cycle of Saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti96/en/sujato">Iti 96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described those who have abandoned sensual desires but are still bound by the bond of existence as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti96/en/sujato"><b>not yet reached</b></a> the final destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti96/en/sujato">Iti 96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said those who have reached the destruction of taints and have cut off pride and rebirth are the ones who have truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti96/en/sujato"><b>crossed to the other shore</b></a> of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti96/en/sujato">Iti 96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato"><b>good character</b></a> (kalyāṇasīlo) is one who lives restrained by the rules of the Pātimokkha, is perfect in conduct, and sees danger in even the smallest faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato">Iti 97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a monk of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato"><b>good nature</b></a> (kalyāṇadhammo) as one who is devoted to the practice of the seven groups of qualities that contribute to enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato">Iti 97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato"><b>good wisdom</b></a> (kalyāṇapañño) is one who, through the destruction of taints, has realized the taintless liberation of mind and liberation by wisdom in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato">Iti 97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is complete in character, nature, and wisdom is called a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato"><b>supreme person</b></a> (uttamapuriso) who has lived the spiritual life to its completion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato">Iti 97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato"><b>conscientious mind</b></a> as one of "good character" because they commit no evil deeds through body, speech, or mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato">Iti 97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato"><b>good nature</b></a> to describe a monk whose qualities that lead to awakening have been well-developed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato">Iti 97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who understands the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato"><b>destruction of suffering</b></a> within themselves in this very life is recognized as a person of "good wisdom"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato">Iti 97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who has abandoned all things, is free from doubt, and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato"><b>unattached to the whole world</b></a> as one who has reached the pinnacle of spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato">Iti 97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is considered <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato"><b>consummate</b></a> (kevalī) when they have fulfilled the training in good character, good nature, and good wisdom within the Dhamma and Discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti97/en/sujato">Iti 97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a true "threefold knowledge brahmin" as someone who has achieved specific spiritual realizations through the Dhamma, rather than someone who merely engages in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>vocal recitation</b></a> of hymns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the first of the three knowledges involves the ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>recollect past lives</b></a>, ranging from a single birth to hundreds of thousands of existences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a practitioner can remember their previous lives across many aeons of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>world-contraction and expansion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when one recollects past lives, they can remember specific personal details such as their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>name and clan</b></a> in those previous states of being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that in the recollection of past lives, a person remembers their physical appearance, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>food they consumed</b></a>, and the specific lifespan they had in each birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the first knowledge allows one to recall the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>pleasure and pain</b></a> they experienced in past lives and the circumstances of their passing away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for a diligent and ardent practitioner, the attainment of the first knowledge means that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>ignorance is destroyed</b></a> and spiritual light arises? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the second knowledge as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a>, a purified vision surpassing that of humans, which allows one to see beings passing away and being reborn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that with the divine eye, one understands how beings are reborn into inferior or superior, beautiful or ugly states according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>their kamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that beings who possess bad conduct of body, speech, and mind, and who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>revile noble ones</b></a>, are reborn in states of misery and hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a> and the undertaking of actions based on wrong view as primary reasons for rebirth in a bad destination after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described how those with good conduct and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> are reborn in happy destinations, including the heavenly worlds, after the breakup of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that through the attainment of the second knowledge, darkness is dispelled and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>light arises</b></a> for the monk who dwells heedful and resolute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the third knowledge is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavānaṃ khaya), which a practitioner realizes for themselves in this very life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that attaining the third knowledge involves reaching the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>liberation of mind</b></a> and liberation by wisdom through direct realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that once the third knowledge is attained, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>ignorance is vanquished</b></a> just as light appears when darkness is removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term "threefold knowledge brahmin" to describe a sage (muni) who has reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>end of birth</b></a> and is a master of direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who has seen both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato"><b>heaven and hell</b></a> and has finished the cycle of rebirth is the one he truly calls a person of threefold knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti99/en/sujato">Iti 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who tolerates a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>thought of sensual desire</b></a> while walking is considered to be lacking ardor and consistently lazy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, if a monk does not abandon a thought of ill will while he is standing, he is described as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>unafraid of wrongdoing</b></a> and of low energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who fails to dispel a thought of harmfulness while sitting is regarded as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>lacking ardor</b></a> and failing in his spiritual effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that even while lying down awake, a monk who tolerates evil worldly thoughts is called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>consistently lazy</b></a> and a person of low energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised a monk who, while walking, does not tolerate thoughts of sensual desire but instead abandons them, calling such a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>ardent</b></a> and resolute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a monk who dispels thoughts of ill will while standing as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>afraid of wrongdoing</b></a> (scrupulous) and consistently energetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who abandons thoughts of harmfulness while sitting is described as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>consistently resolute</b></a> and dedicated in his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who dispels unwholesome thoughts while lying down awake is considered to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>energy stirred up</b></a> and to be truly ardent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that any monk who indulges in evil, worldly thoughts in any of the four postures is following a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>wrong path</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person who is infatuated with things that cause delusion is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>incapable</b></a> of attaining supreme enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who quiets his unwholesome thoughts is someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>capable</b></a> of reaching supreme enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk should delight in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>subsiding of thoughts</b></a> (vitakkūpasame) whether he is walking, standing, sitting, or lying down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>thought of harmfulness</b></a> (vihiṃsāvitakko) to describe one of the three specific unwholesome mental states that must be abandoned for spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>striving</b></a> (pahitatto) as one who actively dispels and eliminates any unwholesome thoughts that arise during his daily activities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who tolerates a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>thought of ill will</b></a> (byāpādavitakko) is considered to be of "low energy" (hīnavīriyo)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined the state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>ottāpī</b></a> (scrupulous or afraid of wrongdoing) by one's refusal to dwell on evil or unwholesome thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that spiritual discipline should be maintained even in the posture of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>lying down</b></a>, provided the monk is awake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the constant effort to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>dispel</b></a> (vinodeti) unwholesome thoughts is what defines a monk as being "consistently resolute"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>infatuated</b></a> (mucchito) with delusional thoughts is effectively blocked from reaching the highest goal of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato"><b>ardent</b></a> (ātāpī) involves a continuous process of not tolerating, but rather abandoning and abolishing unwholesome mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti110/en/sujato">Iti 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted monks to live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>possessed of virtue</b></a> and the Pātimokkha, urging them to see danger in even the slightest of faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, a monk who walks while free from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>covetousness and ill will</b></a>, and who maintains a concentrated mind, is described as being ardent and resolute? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk who stands with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>energy stirred up</b></a> and with mindfulness established is considered to be "afraid of wrongdoing" and "constantly resolute"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who sits with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>tranquil body</b></a> and unsluggish energy is regarded as one who is truly dedicated to the spiritual path and consistently energetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that even while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>lying down awake</b></a>, a monk is called "ardent" if he has abandoned the five hindrances—such as sloth and torpor—and maintains a concentrated, one-pointed mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a monk as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>ardent</b></a> (ātāpī) if he maintains continuous energy and mindfulness regardless of whether he is walking, standing, sitting, or lying down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>afraid of wrongdoing</b></a> (ottāpī) to describe a monk who has abandoned spiritual doubt and whose mind remains focused and collected during all his physical activities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>constantly resolute</b></a> (pahitatto) as someone who has successfully dispelled restlessness and remorse in every posture he assumes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk should practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>restraint</b></a> and mindfulness not only in the four main postures but also specifically when bending or stretching his limbs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that a monk should live observing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>rise and fall</b></a> of the five aggregates (khandhas) throughout the world, whether looking above, across, or below? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who is always mindful and striving as being a person of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>peaceful conduct</b></a> who remains unexcited and composed in his spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who is constantly resolute and mindful is the one who is truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>fit for tranquility</b></a> of mind (cetosamatha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly listed the abandonment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>restlessness and remorse</b></a> as a vital requirement for a monk to be considered "ardent" and "energetic" while in a sitting or standing posture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged monks to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato"><b>scrupulous</b></a> by training themselves to see fear in even the slightest faults while remaining restrained by the rules of the Pātimokkha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti111/en/sujato">Iti 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato"><b>Tathagata</b></a> because he has fully awakened to the nature of the world, its origin, its cessation, and the path leading to its cessation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato">Iti 112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that he is called the Tathagata because he has fully understood everything that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato"><b>seen, heard, sensed, and cognized</b></a> by all beings, including devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato">Iti 112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that everything he says or teaches from the night of his enlightenment until his final passing is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato"><b>exactly so</b></a> and never otherwise, which is why he is called the Tathagata? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato">Iti 112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is referred to as the Tathagata because he perfectly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato"><b>acts as he speaks</b></a> and speaks only as he acts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato">Iti 112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described himself as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato"><b>conqueror</b></a> who is unconquered by any unwholesome states, the all-seeing one who wields mastery over himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato">Iti 112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by understanding the entire world as it really is, he became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato"><b>detached from the world</b></a> and attained a state of supreme peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato">Iti 112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is celebrated as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed lion</b></a> who set in motion the "Wheel of Brahma" for the benefit of both devas and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato">Iti 112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is considered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato"><b>best of those who tame</b></a> because he is perfectly self-tamed and can effectively lead others to the same state of discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato">Iti 112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is described as the "one whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato"><b>taints are destroyed</b></a>" (khīṇāsavo), having reached the end of all suffering and the destruction of all karmic activity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato">Iti 112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha is honored as the one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato"><b>crossed over</b></a> the flood of existence and, out of compassion, helps others to cross over to the shore of safety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/iti112/en/sujato">Iti 112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised making haste in doing good because if one is slow in performing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp116/en/sujato"><b>merit</b></a>, the mind begins to delight in evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp116/en/sujato">Dhp 116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught if a person should do evil, they should not repeat it or find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp117/en/sujato"><b>delight in evil</b></a>, as the accumulation of evil leads to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp117/en/sujato">Dhp 117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged people who do merit to do it again and again and find delight in it, because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp118/en/sujato"><b>accumulation of merit</b></a> leads to happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp118/en/sujato">Dhp 118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that an evil-doer may see good fortune as long as their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp119/en/sujato"><b>evil deed</b></a> has not yet ripened, but once it does, they truly see the painful results of their actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp119/en/sujato">Dhp 119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted a good person may experience bad results as long as their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp120/en/sujato"><b>good deed</b></a> has not yet ripened, but when it eventually matures, they see the true fruits of their goodness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp120/en/sujato">Dhp 120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned not to underestimate evil by thinking it won't affect you, just as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp121/en/sujato"><b>water jar</b></a> is filled by falling drops of water, a fool becomes full of evil even by gathering it little by little? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp121/en/sujato">Dhp 121</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a wise person becomes filled with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp122/en/sujato"><b>merit</b></a> even by gathering it little by little, just as a jar fills with water drop by drop? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp122/en/sujato">Dhp 122</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a wealthy merchant avoids a dangerous road or a person who loves life avoids poison, one should avoid <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp123/en/sujato"><b>evil deeds</b></a> with the same caution? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp123/en/sujato">Dhp 123</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as poison cannot enter a hand that has no wound, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp124/en/sujato"><b>evil</b></a> does not affect one who does not perform it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp124/en/sujato">Dhp 124</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that whoever offends a harmless, pure, and faultless person will find that their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp125/en/sujato"><b>evil</b></a> returns to them, like fine dust thrown against the wind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp125/en/sujato">Dhp 125</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while evil-doers are reborn in hell and the virtuous go to heaven, those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp126/en/sujato"><b>taintless</b></a> attain final Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp126/en/sujato">Dhp 126</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared there is no place on earth—not in the sky, the ocean, or a mountain cleft—where one can hide and escape the consequences of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp127/en/sujato"><b>bad deed</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp127/en/sujato">Dhp 127</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated there is no place in the world—neither in the sky, nor the ocean, nor mountain caves—where one can stay and not be overcome by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp128/en/sujato"><b>death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp128/en/sujato">Dhp 128</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught one should abandon anger and give up conceit to overcome all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp221/en/sujato"><b>fetters</b></a>, as suffering does not befall those who do not cling to name and form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp221/en/sujato">Dhp 221</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considers a person who can restrain rising anger like a rolling chariot to be a true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp222/en/sujato"><b>charioteer</b></a>, while others merely hold the reins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp222/en/sujato">Dhp 222</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised conquering anger with non-anger, badness with goodness, the stingy with giving, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp223/en/sujato"><b>liar with truth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp223/en/sujato">Dhp 223</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by speaking the truth, not becoming angry, and giving even a little when asked, one can go into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp224/en/sujato"><b>presence of the gods</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp224/en/sujato">Dhp 224</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described sages who are harmless and always restrained in body as reaching the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp225/en/sujato"><b>deathless state</b></a>, where they no longer sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp225/en/sujato">Dhp 225</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for those who are always vigilant, training day and night, and are intent on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp226/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a>, the taints reach their end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp226/en/sujato">Dhp 226</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha mentioned an ancient saying that there is no one in the world who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp227/en/sujato"><b>not blamed</b></a>, whether they remain silent, speak much, or speak in moderation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp227/en/sujato">Dhp 227</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out there never was, nor will there be, nor is there now, a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp228/en/sujato"><b>wholly blamed</b></a> or wholly praised? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp228/en/sujato">Dhp 228</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if the wise praise a person after observing them day by day to be of flawless conduct and endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp229/en/sujato"><b>wisdom and virtue</b></a>, that person is truly beyond reproach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp229/en/sujato">Dhp 229</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked who would dare blame a person who is as pure as a coin of gold, whom even the gods and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp230/en/sujato"><b>Brahmā</b></a> praise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp230/en/sujato">Dhp 230</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised one to guard against physical agitation and be restrained in body, abandoning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp231/en/sujato"><b>bodily misconduct</b></a> to practice good conduct with the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp231/en/sujato">Dhp 231</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised one to guard against verbal agitation and be restrained in speech, abandoning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp232/en/sujato"><b>verbal misconduct</b></a> to practice good conduct with speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp232/en/sujato">Dhp 232</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised one to guard against mental agitation and be restrained in mind, abandoning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp233/en/sujato"><b>mental misconduct</b></a> to practice good conduct with the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp233/en/sujato">Dhp 233</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the wise who are restrained in body, speech, and mind are indeed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp234/en/sujato"><b>perfectly restrained</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp234/en/sujato">Dhp 234</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that both the liar and the person who denies having committed an action they actually performed are equal after death, as both are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp306/en/sujato"><b>people of base deeds</b></a> who end up in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp306/en/sujato">Dhp 306</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that many who wear the monastic robe but remain evil and unrestrained will be reborn in hell because of their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp307/en/sujato"><b>evil deeds</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp307/en/sujato">Dhp 307</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is better to swallow a red-hot iron ball than for an immoral and unrestrained person to subsist on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp308/en/sujato"><b>alms-food</b></a> provided by the people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp308/en/sujato">Dhp 308</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that four misfortunes befall a heedless man who consorts with another’s wife: the acquisition of demerit, disturbed sleep, blame, and rebirth in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp309/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp309/en/sujato">Dhp 309</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised against consorting with another’s wife, noting it leads to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp310/en/sujato"><b>evil destination</b></a> and heavy penalties from the law, all for a very brief moment of pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp310/en/sujato">Dhp 310</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monastic life that is wrongly handled to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp311/en/sujato"><b>kusa grass</b></a> that cuts the hand when gripped incorrectly, as both lead to harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp311/en/sujato">Dhp 311</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that any slack deed, corrupt practice, or dubious holy life is never of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp312/en/sujato"><b>great fruit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp312/en/sujato">Dhp 312</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged doing what needs to be done with firm effort, as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp313/en/sujato"><b>slack ascetic</b></a> only increases their own defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp313/en/sujato">Dhp 313</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp314/en/sujato"><b>evil deed</b></a> is best left undone because it causes later regret, while a good deed is better done as it brings no remorse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp314/en/sujato">Dhp 314</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged followers to guard themselves as thoroughly as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp315/en/sujato"><b>border city</b></a>, warning that those who let the opportunity for spiritual practice pass will sorrow in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp315/en/sujato">Dhp 315</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that those who feel shame for what is not shameful but no shame for what is shameful go to a state of woe because of their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp316/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp316/en/sujato">Dhp 316</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that beings who see fear where there is none and no fear where it actually exists are led to a state of woe by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp317/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp317/en/sujato">Dhp 317</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that imagining a fault in the faultless and seeing no fault in the faulty leads beings to a state of woe through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp318/en/sujato"><b>wrong view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp318/en/sujato">Dhp 318</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who recognize a fault as a fault and the faultless as faultless attain a happy destination by following <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp319/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp319/en/sujato">Dhp 319</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared himself to an elephant in battle, stating he would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp320/en/sujato"><b>endure abuse</b></a> from others because many people are of bad character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp320/en/sujato">Dhp 320</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp321/en/sujato"><b>tamed person</b></a> as the best among humans because, like a tamed elephant mounted by a king, they can endure verbal abuse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp321/en/sujato">Dhp 321</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while tamed mules and noble horses are excellent, a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp322/en/sujato"><b>tamed himself</b></a> is even more excellent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp322/en/sujato">Dhp 322</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that one cannot reach the "untrodden land" (Nibbāna) using animal-drawn vehicles, but only through a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp323/en/sujato"><b>self-tamed</b></a> mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp323/en/sujato">Dhp 323</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a captive elephant named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp324/en/sujato"><b>Dhanapāla</b></a> who, in a state of rut, would refuse to eat because he longed for his home in the elephant grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp324/en/sujato">Dhp 324</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a sluggish glutton who sleeps and rolls around to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp325/en/sujato"><b>great hog</b></a> fed on swill, noting such a person enters the womb again and again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp325/en/sujato">Dhp 325</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha resolved to thoroughly restrain his mind—which formerly wandered wherever it pleased—just as a mahout uses a hook to restrain an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp326/en/sujato"><b>elephant in rut</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp326/en/sujato">Dhp 326</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged followers to delight in heedfulness and lift themselves out of the "bog" of defilements like an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp327/en/sujato"><b>elephant</b></a> pulling itself out of the mud? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp327/en/sujato">Dhp 327</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that if one finds a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp328/en/sujato"><b>prudent companion</b></a> of good conduct and wisdom, one should walk with them joyfully and mindfully to overcome all dangers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp328/en/sujato">Dhp 328</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a prudent companion cannot be found, one should walk alone like a king leaving a conquered kingdom or a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp329/en/sujato"><b>Matanga elephant</b></a> in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp329/en/sujato">Dhp 329</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated it is better to live alone than to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp330/en/sujato"><b>companionship with a fool</b></a>, encouraging one to walk alone and do no evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp330/en/sujato">Dhp 330</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while friends are a joy in times of need, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp331/en/sujato"><b>abandoning of all suffering</b></a> is the ultimate joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp331/en/sujato">Dhp 331</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed motherhood, fatherhood, the state of an ascetic, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp332/en/sujato"><b>state of a brahmin</b></a> as sources of joy in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp332/en/sujato">Dhp 332</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that virtue lasting until old age, firmly established faith, the attainment of wisdom, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp333/en/sujato"><b>non-doing of evil</b></a> are all sources of joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp333/en/sujato">Dhp 333</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that suffering follows a person who speaks or acts with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp1/en/sujato"><b>corrupted mind</b></a>, just as the wheel follows the hoof of the ox? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp1/en/sujato">Dhp 1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that happiness follows a person who speaks or acts with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp2/en/sujato"><b>pure mind</b></a>, just as their own shadow never leaves them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp2/en/sujato">Dhp 2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that hatred is never stilled in those who harbor <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp3/en/sujato"><b>resentful thoughts</b></a>, such as "he abused me, he struck me, he defeated me, he robbed me"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp3/en/sujato">Dhp 3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that hatred is stilled in those who do not harbor <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp4/en/sujato"><b>resentful thoughts</b></a> about being abused, struck, defeated, or robbed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp4/en/sujato">Dhp 4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that hatred is never quenched by hatred in this world, but is only quenched by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp5/en/sujato"><b>non-hatred</b></a>, which he called an eternal law? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp5/en/sujato">Dhp 5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that many people do not realize we must all die, but for the wise who understand this <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp6/en/sujato"><b>inevitability of death</b></a>, their quarrels quickly cease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp6/en/sujato">Dhp 6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that Mara easily overthrows a person who lives looking only for pleasure and remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp7/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained in the senses</b></a>, just as the wind topples a weak tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp7/en/sujato">Dhp 7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that Mara cannot overthrow a person who lives contemplating the unlovely and remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp8/en/sujato"><b>restrained in the senses</b></a>, just as the wind cannot move a rocky mountain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp8/en/sujato">Dhp 8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one who is still impure and lacks self-restraint and truthfulness is not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp9/en/sujato"><b>worthy of the saffron robe</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp9/en/sujato">Dhp 9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who has purged away all impurities, is well-established in virtue, and possesses self-restraint and truthfulness is truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp10/en/sujato"><b>worthy of the saffron robe</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp10/en/sujato">Dhp 10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that people who perceive the unessential as essential and the essential as unessential never arrive at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp11/en/sujato"><b>essential</b></a>, as they are lost in wrong intentions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp11/en/sujato">Dhp 11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who recognize the essential as essential and the unessential as unessential arrive at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp12/en/sujato"><b>essential</b></a> by following right intentions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp12/en/sujato">Dhp 12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as rain leaks into an ill-thatched house, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp13/en/sujato"><b>passion</b></a> penetrates an undeveloped mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp13/en/sujato">Dhp 13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as rain does not leak into a well-thatched house, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp14/en/sujato"><b>passion</b></a> cannot penetrate a well-developed mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp14/en/sujato">Dhp 14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the evil-doer grieves both in this life and the next, feeling distressed when they see their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp15/en/sujato"><b>defiled deeds</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp15/en/sujato">Dhp 15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the person who has done merit rejoices both in this life and the next, feeling great joy when they see their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp16/en/sujato"><b>pure deeds</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp16/en/sujato">Dhp 16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that the evil-doer suffers in both worlds, tormented by the thought "I have done evil," and suffers even more when reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp17/en/sujato"><b>state of woe</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp17/en/sujato">Dhp 17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the maker of merit is happy in both worlds, delighted by the thought "I have done merit," and is even more delighted when reborn in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp18/en/sujato"><b>happy destination</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp18/en/sujato">Dhp 18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who recites many scriptures but is heedless and does not practice them to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp19/en/sujato"><b>cowherd</b></a> counting another's cattle, noting they do not share in the fruits of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp19/en/sujato">Dhp 19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if a person recites only a little scripture but lives in accordance with the Dhamma, abandoning greed, hatred, and delusion, they share in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp20/en/sujato"><b>fruits of the spiritual life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp20/en/sujato">Dhp 20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp21/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> as the path to the Deathless, while warning that those who are heedless are as if they are already dead? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp21/en/sujato">Dhp 21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wise people who clearly understand the importance of vigilance <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp22/en/sujato"><b>delight in heedfulness</b></a> and find joy in the spiritual domain of the Noble Ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp22/en/sujato">Dhp 22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who are constant in meditation and always possess firm endeavor will eventually touch <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp23/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a>, which is the supreme security from bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp23/en/sujato">Dhp 23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person who is energetic, mindful, pure in deed, and lives according to the Dhamma, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp24/en/sujato"><b>fame increases</b></a> significantly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp24/en/sujato">Dhp 24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged the wise person to use effort, heedfulness, and self-control to create an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp25/en/sujato"><b>island</b></a> for themselves that no flood of defilements can overwhelm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp25/en/sujato">Dhp 25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that while fools and ignorant people indulge in heedlessness, the wise person treasures and guards <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp26/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> as their greatest wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp26/en/sujato">Dhp 26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised people not to indulge in heedlessness or seek intimacy with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp27/en/sujato"><b>sensual delight</b></a>, because the person who is heedful and meditative attains great happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp27/en/sujato">Dhp 27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a wise person who dispels heedlessness through heedfulness as one who ascends the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp28/en/sujato"><b>palace of wisdom</b></a> and looks down upon the grieving world without sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp28/en/sujato">Dhp 28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a wise person who is heedful among the heedless and awake among those who sleep to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp29/en/sujato"><b>swift horse</b></a> that leaves a weak one behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp29/en/sujato">Dhp 29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that through heedfulness, Maghavā attained the status of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp30/en/sujato"><b>lord of the gods</b></a>, emphasizing that heedfulness is always praised while heedlessness is always censured? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp30/en/sujato">Dhp 30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who delights in heedfulness and fears heedlessness moves through the world like a fire, burning up all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp31/en/sujato"><b>fetters</b></a> whether they are great or small? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp31/en/sujato">Dhp 31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a monk who delights in heedfulness and sees danger in heedlessness is incapable of falling away and is in the very <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp32/en/sujato"><b>presence of Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp32/en/sujato">Dhp 32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person straightens their flickering and fickle mind just as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp33/en/sujato"><b>fletcher straightens an arrow</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp33/en/sujato">Dhp 33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the quivering of the mind to a fish thrashed out of water and thrown on the ground, struggling to escape <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp34/en/sujato"><b>Māra's realm</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp34/en/sujato">Dhp 34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that it is good to subdue the mind because it is difficult to restrain and lands wherever it pleases, and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp35/en/sujato"><b>tamed mind</b></a> is what brings happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp35/en/sujato">Dhp 35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged the wise to guard their minds, which are extremely subtle and difficult to see, because a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp36/en/sujato"><b>guarded mind</b></a> brings true happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp36/en/sujato">Dhp 36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that those who restrain their mind—which wanders far, moves alone, and is bodiless—will be freed from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp37/en/sujato"><b>Māra's bonds</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp37/en/sujato">Dhp 37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that for a person with an unsteady mind who does not understand the True Dhamma, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp38/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> will never reach fulfillment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp38/en/sujato">Dhp 38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for one whose mind is free from lust and who has moved beyond the accumulation of both merit and evil, there is no fear for such a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp39/en/sujato"><b>vigilant person</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp39/en/sujato">Dhp 39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed followers to view the body as fragile like a jar, establish the mind like a fortified city, and fight Māra with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp40/en/sujato"><b>weapon of wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp40/en/sujato">Dhp 40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that before long this body will lie upon the ground, cast aside and devoid of consciousness, just like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp41/en/sujato"><b>useless log</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp41/en/sujato">Dhp 41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that whatever harm an enemy may do to an enemy, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp42/en/sujato"><b>wrongly directed mind</b></a> can cause a person even greater harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp42/en/sujato">Dhp 42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that neither a mother, nor a father, nor any other relative can do a person as much good as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp43/en/sujato"><b>well-directed mind</b></a> can? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp43/en/sujato">Dhp 43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even if a person speaks a thousand words, if they are a collection of meaningless sentences, they are far inferior to a single <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp100/en/sujato"><b>meaningful word</b></a> that brings peace to the listener? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp100/en/sujato">Dhp 100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a single verse of poetry that leads to a state of peace when heard is much better than a collection of a thousand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp101/en/sujato"><b>meaningless verses</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp101/en/sujato">Dhp 101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that reciting a single <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp102/en/sujato"><b>word of the Dhamma</b></a> which brings composure to the heart is superior to reciting a hundred verses that lack any meaningful benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp102/en/sujato">Dhp 102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha considered the person who conquers their own self to be the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp103/en/sujato"><b>supreme victor</b></a> in battle, even when compared to a warrior who might defeat a million men on the battlefield? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp103/en/sujato">Dhp 103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp104/en/sujato"><b>conquering oneself</b></a> is truly better than defeating all other people, especially for an individual who is self-tamed and consistently practices restrained conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp104/en/sujato">Dhp 104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that the victory achieved by a person who has mastered themselves is so absolute that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp105/en/sujato"><b>not even a god</b></a>, a celestial musician, Māra, or even Brahmā has the power to turn that person's victory back into a defeat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp105/en/sujato">Dhp 105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that honoring a person who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp106/en/sujato"><b>developed themselves</b></a> for just a single moment is a more spiritually fruitful act than performing a thousand ritual sacrifices every month for an entire century? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp106/en/sujato">Dhp 106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that paying homage to a person who has truly cultivated their mind for one moment is far superior to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp107/en/sujato"><b>tending a sacrificial fire</b></a> in the forest for a hundred years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp107/en/sujato">Dhp 107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that any ritual offering or sacrifice made in the world for a full year to gain merit is not worth even a fourth of the value of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp108/en/sujato"><b>paying respect</b></a> to those who are upright and straight in their conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp108/en/sujato">Dhp 108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for those who are habitually respectful and consistently honor their elders, four specific qualities will increase: <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp109/en/sujato"><b>longevity, beauty, happiness, and strength</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp109/en/sujato">Dhp 109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha maintained that a single day of life for someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp110/en/sujato"><b>virtuous and meditative</b></a> is of much greater value than living for a hundred years while remaining immoral and lacking mental composure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp110/en/sujato">Dhp 110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that living for just one day as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp111/en/sujato"><b>wise and meditative</b></a> person is more significant than living a hundred years as someone who is foolish and possesses an uncontrolled mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp111/en/sujato">Dhp 111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted that a single day of life spent being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp112/en/sujato"><b>resolute in energy</b></a> and effort is superior to a hundred years of life spent in laziness and a total lack of vigorous endeavor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp112/en/sujato">Dhp 112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that living for one day while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp113/en/sujato"><b>perceiving arising and falling</b></a>—the transient nature of all things—is better than a hundred years of life for a person who does not see this truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp113/en/sujato">Dhp 113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a single day of life for one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp114/en/sujato"><b>perceives the Deathless state</b></a> is far more important than a hundred years spent without ever seeing this ultimate spiritual reality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp114/en/sujato">Dhp 114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that living for just one day while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp115/en/sujato"><b>perceiving the supreme Dhamma</b></a> is better than a century of life for a person who remains blind to this highest truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp115/en/sujato">Dhp 115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked who would investigate this earth and the realm of Yama together with its devas, just as a skilled person would pick a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp44/en/sujato"><b>well-taught path of Dhamma</b></a> like a flower? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp44/en/sujato">Dhp 44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a trainee in the higher discipline is the one who will investigate this earth and the world of Yama, picking the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp45/en/sujato"><b>well-taught path of Dhamma</b></a> just as a skilled garland-maker selects the best flowers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp45/en/sujato">Dhp 45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged his followers to recognize that the body is as ephemeral as foam and as insubstantial as a mirage, thereby breaking the flowery shafts of Māra to reach a state <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp46/en/sujato"><b>beyond the sight</b></a> of the King of Death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp46/en/sujato">Dhp 46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared death to a great flood that sweeps away a sleeping village, noting that it carries off the person whose mind is fixated only on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp47/en/sujato"><b>picking the flowers</b></a> of sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp47/en/sujato">Dhp 47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the "Ender," or death, brings under his absolute sway the person who is insatiable in sensual desires and whose mind is distracted by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp48/en/sujato"><b>picking the flowers</b></a> of worldly life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp48/en/sujato">Dhp 48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a sage should move through a village just <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp49/en/sujato"><b>as a bee</b></a> takes nectar from a flower and flies away without damaging its beautiful color or its fragrance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp49/en/sujato">Dhp 49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed that instead of focusing on the faults of others or what they have done or left undone, one should only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp50/en/sujato"><b>examine one's own</b></a> deeds and omissions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp50/en/sujato">Dhp 50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that well-spoken words are as fruitless as a beautiful, colorful flower that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp51/en/sujato"><b>lacks fragrance</b></a> for a person who does not actually practice what they say? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp51/en/sujato">Dhp 51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that well-spoken words are highly fruitful, like a beautiful flower that is both colorful and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp52/en/sujato"><b>possesses fragrance</b></a>, for the person who acts according to those words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp52/en/sujato">Dhp 52</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that just as many garlands can be made from a large heap of flowers, a person born into this mortal life should perform <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp53/en/sujato"><b>many good deeds</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp53/en/sujato">Dhp 53</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that while the scent of flowers like sandalwood or jasmine cannot travel against the wind, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp54/en/sujato"><b>fragrance of the virtuous</b></a> travels in all directions, even against the wind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp54/en/sujato">Dhp 54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that among all the various types of perfumes such as sandalwood, tagara, lotus, or jasmine, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp55/en/sujato"><b>fragrance of virtue</b></a> is by far the most excellent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp55/en/sujato">Dhp 55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the scent of common perfumes like sandalwood is relatively faint, whereas the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp56/en/sujato"><b>scent of the virtuous</b></a> is supreme and wafts even among the gods in the celestial realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp56/en/sujato">Dhp 56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that Māra, the personification of evil and death, cannot even find the path of those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp57/en/sujato"><b>perfect in virtue</b></a>, live mindfully, and have been liberated through right knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp57/en/sujato">Dhp 57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a beautiful, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp58/en/sujato"><b>sweet-smelling lotus</b></a> growing out of a heap of refuse discarded on a high road to describe how a wise person can arise from a lowly environment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp58/en/sujato">Dhp 58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a lotus grows in refuse, a disciple of the Fully Enlightened One <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp59/en/sujato"><b>outshines with wisdom</b></a> the blind worldlings who are like the garbage of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp59/en/sujato">Dhp 59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that just as a night is long to one who is awake and a league is long to one who is weary, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp60/en/sujato"><b>saṃsāra is long</b></a> to fools who do not know the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp60/en/sujato">Dhp 60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that if a traveler cannot find a companion who is their equal or better, they should resolutely pursue a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp61/en/sujato"><b>solitary life</b></a> because there is no true fellowship with a fool? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp61/en/sujato">Dhp 61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a fool is constantly troubled by the thoughts "these sons are mine" and "this wealth is mine," even though one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp62/en/sujato"><b>self is not one's own</b></a>, let alone sons or riches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp62/en/sujato">Dhp 62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that a fool who recognizes their own foolishness is wise to that extent, but a fool who considers themselves <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp63/en/sujato"><b>to be wise</b></a> is truly called a fool? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp63/en/sujato">Dhp 63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a fool who associates with a wise person for a lifetime without understanding the Dhamma to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp64/en/sujato"><b>spoon that cannot taste</b></a> the flavor of the soup? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp64/en/sujato">Dhp 64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an intelligent person who associates with a wise person for even a moment will quickly understand the Dhamma, just as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp65/en/sujato"><b>tongue tastes the flavor</b></a> of the soup? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp65/en/sujato">Dhp 65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that fools of poor intelligence act as their own enemies by performing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp66/en/sujato"><b>evil deeds</b></a> that ultimately bring about bitter results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp66/en/sujato">Dhp 66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a deed is not well done if one later regrets performing it and must experience its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp67/en/sujato"><b>painful results</b></a> with a tearful face and weeping? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp67/en/sujato">Dhp 67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a deed is well done when one does not regret performing it afterwards and can experience its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp68/en/sujato"><b>pleasant results</b></a> while being delighted and glad at heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp68/en/sujato">Dhp 68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a fool thinks an evil deed is as sweet as honey as long as it has not yet ripened, but once that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp69/en/sujato"><b>evil ripens</b></a>, they must undergo suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp69/en/sujato">Dhp 69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that even if a fool practices extreme asceticism by eating food only with the tip of a blade of kusa grass, they are not worth a sixteenth part of those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp70/en/sujato"><b>comprehended the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp70/en/sujato">Dhp 70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that an evil deed does not bring immediate results like fresh milk curdling, but instead follows the fool like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp71/en/sujato"><b>fire covered with ashes</b></a>, smoldering as it burns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp71/en/sujato">Dhp 71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any knowledge or skill acquired by a fool only leads to their harm, as it destroys their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp72/en/sujato"><b>good fortune</b></a> and cleaves their head? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp72/en/sujato">Dhp 72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned against the desires of a fool, who might seek undue reputation, precedence among monks, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp73/en/sujato"><b>authority in monasteries</b></a>, and honor among families? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp73/en/sujato">Dhp 73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a fool's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp74/en/sujato"><b>desire and conceit</b></a> increase when they think that both laypeople and monks should credit them for every success and depend on them in all tasks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp74/en/sujato">Dhp 74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the path to worldly gain is entirely different from the path leading to Nibbāna, and therefore a monk should not delight in worldly honors but should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp75/en/sujato"><b>cultivate seclusion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp75/en/sujato">Dhp 75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one should associate with a wise person who reveals one's faults, viewing them as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp76/en/sujato"><b>pointer to hidden treasure</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp76/en/sujato">Dhp 76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who gives advice, instructs others, and restrains them from what is unseemly is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp77/en/sujato"><b>loved by the good</b></a> but disliked by the wicked? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp77/en/sujato">Dhp 77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers not to associate with evil friends or base men, but instead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp78/en/sujato"><b>associate with noble people</b></a> and good friends? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp78/en/sujato">Dhp 78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that one who drinks in the Dhamma lives happily with a serene mind, and that the wise always find delight in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp79/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma revealed by the Noble Ones</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp79/en/sujato">Dhp 79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the self-discipline of the wise to how irrigators lead water, fletchers bend arrows, and carpenters shape wood, noting that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp80/en/sujato"><b>wise tame themselves</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp80/en/sujato">Dhp 80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as a solid rock is not shaken by the wind, the wise remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp81/en/sujato"><b>unmoved by blame or praise</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp81/en/sujato">Dhp 81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that after hearing the teachings of the Dhamma, the wise become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp82/en/sujato"><b>perfectly serene</b></a>, just like a deep, clear, and still lake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp82/en/sujato">Dhp 82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that good people practice renunciation in all circumstances and do not speak with a desire for sensual pleasures, remaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp83/en/sujato"><b>steadfast through happiness or pain</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp83/en/sujato">Dhp 83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who does not desire sons, wealth, or a kingdom for themselves or others through unrighteous means is truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp84/en/sujato"><b>virtuous, wise, and righteous</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp84/en/sujato">Dhp 84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that among all humans, only a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp85/en/sujato"><b>few reach the far shore</b></a> of liberation, while the rest of the people merely run along the near bank? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp85/en/sujato">Dhp 85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who live according to the well-expounded Dhamma will be able to cross over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp86/en/sujato"><b>difficult realm of Death</b></a> and reach the far shore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp86/en/sujato">Dhp 86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a wise person should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp87/en/sujato"><b>abandon the dark way</b></a> and cultivate the bright way, moving from the life of a householder to the state of homelessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp87/en/sujato">Dhp 87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that in seclusion, a wise person should seek a delight that is hard to find, giving up sensual pleasures and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp88/en/sujato"><b>cleansing oneself</b></a> of all mental defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp88/en/sujato">Dhp 88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that those who have well-developed their minds in the factors of enlightenment and have abandoned all clinging are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp89/en/sujato"><b>fully extinguished</b></a> in this world with their taints destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp89/en/sujato">Dhp 89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for one who has completed the spiritual journey and is liberated from all ties, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp90/en/sujato"><b>fever of passion</b></a> no longer exists? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp90/en/sujato">Dhp 90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared mindful practitioners to swans leaving a lake, noting that they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp91/en/sujato"><b>abandon every settled abode</b></a> and do not delight in a permanent home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp91/en/sujato">Dhp 91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the path of those who have no accumulation and focus on emptiness and signless liberation is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp92/en/sujato"><b>hard to track</b></a>, much like the path of birds in the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp92/en/sujato">Dhp 92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the "footprint" or path of an individual whose taints are destroyed and who is not attached to food is as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp93/en/sujato"><b>difficult to follow</b></a> as that of birds flying through the air? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp93/en/sujato">Dhp 93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that even the gods envy a person whose senses are stilled like well-tamed horses and who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp94/en/sujato"><b>abandoned all pride</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp94/en/sujato">Dhp 94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a disciplined and upright person to the earth and a pillar, stating that for such a person, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp95/en/sujato"><b>cycles of rebirth</b></a> no longer exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp95/en/sujato">Dhp 95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the mind, speech, and actions of one who is perfectly tranquil and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp96/en/sujato"><b>liberated through right knowledge</b></a> are entirely peaceful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp96/en/sujato">Dhp 96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the supreme person as one who is free from blind faith, knows the Unconditioned, has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp97/en/sujato"><b>cut all ties</b></a>, and has renounced all desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp97/en/sujato">Dhp 97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that any place—be it a village, a forest, a valley, or a plain—becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp98/en/sujato"><b>truly delightful</b></a> when Arahants dwell there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp98/en/sujato">Dhp 98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that forests are delightful to those <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp99/en/sujato"><b>free from passion</b></a>, even though ordinary people seeking sensual pleasures find no delight in them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp99/en/sujato">Dhp 99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that because all beings tremble at punishment and fear death, one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp129/en/sujato"><b>identify with others</b></a> and refrain from killing or causing others to kill? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp129/en/sujato">Dhp 129</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that since life is dear to everyone, one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp130/en/sujato"><b>compare oneself to others</b></a> and never strike or slay another living being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp130/en/sujato">Dhp 130</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that anyone who harms living beings while seeking their own happiness will <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp131/en/sujato"><b>not find happiness</b></a> after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp131/en/sujato">Dhp 131</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who does not harm other beings while seeking their own happiness will surely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp132/en/sujato"><b>attain happiness</b></a> in the life to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp132/en/sujato">Dhp 132</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised against speaking harshly to anyone, as such words can lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp133/en/sujato"><b>retaliatory speech</b></a> and painful consequences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp133/en/sujato">Dhp 133</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared reaching Nibbāna to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp134/en/sujato"><b>broken gong</b></a> that no longer vibrates, signifying a state where one no longer engages in contentious or angry speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp134/en/sujato">Dhp 134</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a cowherd driving cattle with a staff to illustrate how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp135/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a> drive the life span of all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp135/en/sujato">Dhp 135</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a fool does not realize the gravity of their evil deeds, and is eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp136/en/sujato"><b>burned by their own actions</b></a> as if by a fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp136/en/sujato">Dhp 136</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that anyone who inflicts punishment on the harmless and innocent will quickly fall into one of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp137/en/sujato"><b>ten dire states</b></a> of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp137/en/sujato">Dhp 137</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed severe pain, catastrophic loss, bodily injury, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp138/en/sujato"><b>grievous illness</b></a> as some of the immediate consequences for those who harm the innocent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp138/en/sujato">Dhp 138</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who harms the harmless may suffer loss of mind, trouble from kings, terrifying accusations, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp139/en/sujato"><b>loss of relatives</b></a>, or the destruction of their wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp139/en/sujato">Dhp 139</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person of poor wisdom who harms the innocent may see their house burned by fire and, after the breakup of the body, be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp140/en/sujato"><b>reborn in hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp140/en/sujato">Dhp 140</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha clarified that external practices like nakedness, matted hair, or sleeping on the ground cannot <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp141/en/sujato"><b>purify a mortal</b></a> who has not yet overcome doubt and craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp141/en/sujato">Dhp 141</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that even if a person is finely adorned, they are a true monk and Brahmin if they are peaceful, disciplined, and have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp142/en/sujato"><b>laid aside the stick</b></a> toward all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp142/en/sujato">Dhp 142</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked if there is any man in the world so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp143/en/sujato"><b>restrained by shame</b></a> that he avoids reproach as a thoroughbred horse avoids the whip? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp143/en/sujato">Dhp 143</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged practitioners to be like a thoroughbred horse touched by the whip—ardent and stirred—and to abandon great suffering through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp144/en/sujato"><b>faith and virtue</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp144/en/sujato">Dhp 144</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared self-mastery to craftsmanship, noting that just as irrigators lead water and fletchers shape arrows, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp145/en/sujato"><b>virtuous tame themselves</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp145/en/sujato">Dhp 145</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned how one can find laughter and joy when the world is constantly burning, and why those shrouded in darkness do not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp146/en/sujato"><b>seek a light</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp146/en/sujato">Dhp 146</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged looking at the body as a "painted image"—a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp147/en/sujato"><b>heap of sores</b></a> and a diseased thing with many thoughts, but no permanent stability? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp147/en/sujato">Dhp 147</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the human form as worn out, a nest of disease, and brittle, emphasizing that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp148/en/sujato"><b>life ends in death</b></a> as this putrid body breaks apart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp148/en/sujato">Dhp 148</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asked what delight there could be in looking at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp149/en/sujato"><b>bleached bones</b></a> that have been cast away like gourds in autumn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp149/en/sujato">Dhp 149</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the body as a "city made of bones" plastered with flesh and blood, serving as a dwelling place for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp150/en/sujato"><b>aging and death</b></a>, pride, and malice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp150/en/sujato">Dhp 150</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even well-decorated royal chariots wear out and the body inevitably ages, but the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp151/en/sujato"><b>Teaching of the Good</b></a> never grows old? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp151/en/sujato">Dhp 151</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person of little learning to an ox, stating that while their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp152/en/sujato"><b>flesh increases</b></a>, their wisdom does not grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp152/en/sujato">Dhp 152</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his long journey through many births in Saṃsāra, seeking the "house-builder" of craving, and declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp153/en/sujato"><b>repeated birth is painful</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp153/en/sujato">Dhp 153</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, upon attaining enlightenment, addressed the "house-builder" and declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp154/en/sujato"><b>ridgepole is shattered</b></a> and the mind has reached the end of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp154/en/sujato">Dhp 154</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha lamented that those who have not lived the holy life or gained wealth in their youth waste away like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp155/en/sujato"><b>old herons</b></a> in a pond without fish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp155/en/sujato">Dhp 155</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that those who fail to live the holy life in their youth lie like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp156/en/sujato"><b>spent arrows</b></a> shot from a bow, sighing over things of the past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp156/en/sujato">Dhp 156</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a person holds themselves dear, they should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp157/en/sujato"><b>protect themselves well</b></a> by keeping watch over their conduct during at least one of the three watches of the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp157/en/sujato">Dhp 157</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that a wise person should first <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp158/en/sujato"><b>establish themselves</b></a> in what is proper before attempting to instruct others, thereby avoiding defilement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp158/en/sujato">Dhp 158</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha suggested that if one acts in the same way as one instructs others, then being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp159/en/sujato"><b>well-tamed</b></a> themselves, they can truly tame others, though the self is indeed difficult to master? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp159/en/sujato">Dhp 159</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one is one's own protector, for what other protector could there be, and that through a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp160/en/sujato"><b>well-tamed self</b></a>, one gains a protector that is hard to find? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp160/en/sujato">Dhp 160</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that evil done by oneself, born of oneself and caused by oneself, crushes a person of poor intelligence just as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp161/en/sujato"><b>diamond grinds</b></a> a hard gem? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp161/en/sujato">Dhp 161</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person of extreme corruption to a tree overcome by a maluva creeper, noting that such a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp162/en/sujato"><b>brings upon themselves</b></a> exactly what an enemy would wish for them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp162/en/sujato">Dhp 162</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that deeds which are harmful and wrong for oneself are easy to perform, while that which is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp163/en/sujato"><b>beneficial and good</b></a> is truly difficult to do? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp163/en/sujato">Dhp 163</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person of poor wisdom who scorns the teaching of the Arahants and the Noble ones due to evil views brings about their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp164/en/sujato"><b>own destruction</b></a>, much like the fruit of the bamboo kills the plant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp164/en/sujato">Dhp 164</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that by oneself alone is evil done and oneself defiled, and by oneself alone is evil left undone and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp165/en/sujato"><b>oneself purified</b></a>, as no one can purify another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp165/en/sujato">Dhp 165</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one should not neglect one's own spiritual welfare for the sake of another's, no matter how great, but should instead be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp166/en/sujato"><b>intent on one's own goal</b></a> after realizing what it truly is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp166/en/sujato">Dhp 166</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged followers not to practice low qualities, live in negligence, or embrace wrong views, so as not to be one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp167/en/sujato"><b>prolongs the world</b></a> of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp167/en/sujato">Dhp 167</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted practitioners to rouse themselves and not be negligent, but to live a life of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp168/en/sujato"><b>righteous conduct</b></a>, which leads to happiness in this world and the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp168/en/sujato">Dhp 168</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should practice the Dhamma of good conduct and avoid bad conduct, because the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp169/en/sujato"><b>practitioner of Dhamma</b></a> lives happily both here and hereafter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp169/en/sujato">Dhp 169</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that if one looks upon the world as a mere bubble or a mirage, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp170/en/sujato"><b>King of Death</b></a> will not be able to see them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp170/en/sujato">Dhp 170</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha invited people to look at this world, which is like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp171/en/sujato"><b>decorated royal chariot</b></a> where fools flounder, but where the wise have no attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp171/en/sujato">Dhp 171</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said that a person who was formerly negligent but later becomes diligent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp172/en/sujato"><b>illuminates this world</b></a>, just like the moon when freed from clouds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp172/en/sujato">Dhp 172</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that one whose evil deed is covered by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp173/en/sujato"><b>wholesome deed</b></a> illuminates this world like the moon emerging from behind the clouds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp173/en/sujato">Dhp 173</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described this world as being blind, where only a few see clearly, and compared those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp174/en/sujato"><b>go to heaven</b></a> to the few birds that manage to escape from a net? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp174/en/sujato">Dhp 174</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while swans fly on the path of the sun and the powerful fly through the air, the wise are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp175/en/sujato"><b>led away from the world</b></a> after conquering Mara and his army? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp175/en/sujato">Dhp 175</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that for a person who has transgressed the one law of truth, who speaks lies, and who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp176/en/sujato"><b>scorned the next world</b></a>, there is no evil deed they will not do? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp176/en/sujato">Dhp 176</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that misers do not go to the world of the gods and fools do not praise giving, but the wise person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp177/en/sujato"><b>rejoices in giving</b></a> becomes happy in the world beyond? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp177/en/sujato">Dhp 177</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp178/en/sujato"><b>fruit of stream-entry</b></a> is far superior to having sole sovereignty over the earth, going to heaven, or having lordship over all the worlds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp178/en/sujato">Dhp 178</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an enlightened one as having a victory that cannot be undone and possessing an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp179/en/sujato"><b>infinite range</b></a> of wisdom that leaves no track for others to follow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp179/en/sujato">Dhp 179</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an awakened one is completely free from the entangling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp180/en/sujato"><b>mesh of craving</b></a>, which otherwise leads people astray through various rebirths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp180/en/sujato">Dhp 180</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even the gods envy those wise and mindful individuals who are devoted to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp181/en/sujato"><b>meditative absorption</b></a> and delight in the peace of renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp181/en/sujato">Dhp 181</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized the rarity of spiritual opportunity by stating that it is difficult to be born as a human, difficult to live as a mortal, difficult to hear the True Dhamma, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp182/en/sujato"><b>difficult for Buddhas</b></a> to arise in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp182/en/sujato">Dhp 182</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha summarized the fundamental teaching of all Buddhas as being the non-doing of any evil, the performance of what is wholesome, and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp183/en/sujato"><b>purification of one's own mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp183/en/sujato">Dhp 183</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that patient endurance is the highest austerity and that Nibbāna is supreme, further explaining that one who harms or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp184/en/sujato"><b>oppresses others</b></a> is not a true renunciant or monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp184/en/sujato">Dhp 184</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha listed several core practices of the awakened, including not insulting or harming others, restraint according to the monastic code, moderation in eating, living in secluded places, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp185/en/sujato"><b>devotion to higher mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp185/en/sujato">Dhp 185</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that sensual desires are never satisfied even by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp186/en/sujato"><b>rain of gold coins</b></a>, teaching that such pleasures actually provide little enjoyment and lead to much suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp186/en/sujato">Dhp 186</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a true disciple of the Fully Enlightened One finds no delight even in heavenly pleasures, as they instead find joy in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp187/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp187/en/sujato">Dhp 187</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that people driven by fear often seek refuge in external things such as mountains, forests, parks, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp188/en/sujato"><b>trees, and shrines</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp188/en/sujato">Dhp 188</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that seeking refuge in nature or shrines is not a secure or supreme refuge, as such places cannot <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp189/en/sujato"><b>release a person</b></a> from all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp189/en/sujato">Dhp 189</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who goes for refuge to the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Sangha gains the wisdom to clearly see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp190/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp190/en/sujato">Dhp 190</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the path to the end of suffering as the Noble Eightfold Path, which allows one to understand suffering, its origin, and its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp191/en/sujato"><b>total overcoming</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp191/en/sujato">Dhp 191</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared the Triple Gem to be the only secure and supreme refuge, because it is by arriving at this refuge that a person is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp192/en/sujato"><b>freed from all suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp192/en/sujato">Dhp 192</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that a person of noble birth and wisdom is rare to find, but wherever such a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp193/en/sujato"><b>thoroughbred man</b></a> is born, that family prospers in happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp193/en/sujato">Dhp 193</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the birth of Buddhas is a cause for happiness, as is the teaching of the True Dhamma, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp194/en/sujato"><b>concord of the Sangha</b></a>, and the shared spiritual practice of those in harmony? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp194/en/sujato">Dhp 194</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that it is impossible to calculate the merit of a person who pays homage to those worthy of homage—namely the Buddhas or their disciples—who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp195/en/sujato"><b>transcended all obstacles</b></a> and crossed beyond sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp195/en/sujato">Dhp 195</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that no one can measure the merit gained by honoring those who are enlightened, peaceful, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp196/en/sujato"><b>fearless</b></a>, as they have reached a state of complete extinguishment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp196/en/sujato">Dhp 196</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that we can live most happily and free from hatred even among those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp197/en/sujato"><b>filled with hatred</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp197/en/sujato">Dhp 197</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that it is possible to live in great happiness and free from affliction, even while dwelling among people who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp198/en/sujato"><b>troubled by affliction</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp198/en/sujato">Dhp 198</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated we can live happily and free from anxious greed among those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp199/en/sujato"><b>filled with anxiety</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp199/en/sujato">Dhp 199</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a way of living happily with nothing to call one's own, where one feeds on joy like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp200/en/sujato"><b>Abhassara gods</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp200/en/sujato">Dhp 200</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that victory breeds hatred because the defeated live in pain, whereas the peaceful person lives happily by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp201/en/sujato"><b>relinquishing both victory and defeat</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp201/en/sujato">Dhp 201</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught there is no fire like lust, no crime like hatred, no suffering like the Five Aggregates, and no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp202/en/sujato"><b>happiness higher than peace</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp202/en/sujato">Dhp 202</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified hunger as the ultimate illness and conditioned things as the greatest suffering, concluding that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp203/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna is the highest happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp203/en/sujato">Dhp 203</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared health as the greatest gain and contentment as the greatest wealth, while stating that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp204/en/sujato"><b>trust is the best kinsman</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp204/en/sujato">Dhp 204</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said once a person has tasted the flavor of seclusion and the flavor of peace, they become free from fear and evil while drinking the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp205/en/sujato"><b>joy of the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp205/en/sujato">Dhp 205</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that meeting Noble Ones is good and living with them is always happy, while one would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp206/en/sujato"><b>constantly happy</b></a> if they never had to see fools? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp206/en/sujato">Dhp 206</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that anyone who consorts with fools will grieve for a long time, as living with a fool is as painful as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp207/en/sujato"><b>living with an enemy</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp207/en/sujato">Dhp 207</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised following the wise, the discerning, and the noble—just as the moon follows the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp208/en/sujato"><b>path of the stars</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp208/en/sujato">Dhp 208</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that one who abandons the spiritual goal for the sake of the pleasant will eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp209/en/sujato"><b>envy the self-exerted</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp209/en/sujato">Dhp 209</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned against forming attachments to the dear or the undear, as not seeing the dear is painful and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp210/en/sujato"><b>seeing the undear</b></a> is also painful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp210/en/sujato">Dhp 210</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one should not hold anything as dear because the loss of the dear is painful, and those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp211/en/sujato"><b>neither dear nor undear</b></a> are free from bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp211/en/sujato">Dhp 211</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that sorrow and fear are born from what is dear, and that for one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp212/en/sujato"><b>freed from the dear</b></a>, there is no sorrow and therefore no fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp212/en/sujato">Dhp 212</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that affection gives rise to sorrow and fear, but for one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp213/en/sujato"><b>freed from affection</b></a>, sorrow and fear no longer exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp213/en/sujato">Dhp 213</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that sorrow and fear arise from attachment, yet one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp214/en/sujato"><b>freed from attachment</b></a> has neither sorrow nor fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp214/en/sujato">Dhp 214</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that sorrow and fear are produced by lust, but for one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp215/en/sujato"><b>freed from lust</b></a>, there is no sorrow and no fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp215/en/sujato">Dhp 215</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that craving is the source of sorrow and fear, and that one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp216/en/sujato"><b>freed from craving</b></a> is without sorrow or fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp216/en/sujato">Dhp 216</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person who is perfect in virtue and insight, established in the Dhamma, and a speaker of truth is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp217/en/sujato"><b>held dear by the world</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp217/en/sujato">Dhp 217</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person whose mind is set on the Ineffable and whose heart is not bound to sensual pleasures as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp218/en/sujato"><b>one who flows upstream</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp218/en/sujato">Dhp 218</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the analogy of a man returning from a long journey to show how kinsmen and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp219/en/sujato"><b>friends welcome</b></a> a loved one who has arrived safely? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp219/en/sujato">Dhp 219</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as kinsmen welcome a returning loved one, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp220/en/sujato"><b>merits of a person</b></a> who has done good welcome them as they go from this world to the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp220/en/sujato">Dhp 220</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person nearing the end of their life to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp235/en/sujato"><b>withered leaf</b></a>, noting that while the messengers of death await them, they stand at the threshold of the journey without any spiritual provisions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp235/en/sujato">Dhp 235</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged individuals to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp236/en/sujato"><b>make an island</b></a> for themselves by striving quickly to become wise and stainless, which allows one to reach the divine land of the Ariyas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp236/en/sujato">Dhp 236</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that when a person's life has reached its end and they are in the presence of the King of Death, they have no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp237/en/sujato"><b>resting place</b></a> along the way and no provisions for the journey ahead? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp237/en/sujato">Dhp 237</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that by striving quickly to become a wise person who has removed their spiritual impurities, one will reach a state where they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp238/en/sujato"><b>will not come again</b></a> to birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp238/en/sujato">Dhp 238</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a wise person should remove their own impurity gradually, moment by moment, just as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp239/en/sujato"><b>smith</b></a> removes the dross from silver? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp239/en/sujato">Dhp 239</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the results of evil deeds to rust arising from iron, which then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp240/en/sujato"><b>eats away</b></a> the very iron from which it grew, leading the transgressor to a state of woe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp240/en/sujato">Dhp 240</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified various types of "stains," stating that non-repetition is the stain of mantras, non-exertion is the stain of houses, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp241/en/sujato"><b>heedlessness</b></a> is the stain of one who guards? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp241/en/sujato">Dhp 241</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while unchastity is the stain of a woman and stinginess is the stain of a donor, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp242/en/sujato"><b>evil deeds</b></a> are stains that affect a person in both this world and the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp242/en/sujato">Dhp 242</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp243/en/sujato"><b>ignorance</b></a> to be the ultimate and worst of all stains, and he urged the bhikkhus to abandon this stain to become truly stainless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp243/en/sujato">Dhp 243</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that life is easy for a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp244/en/sujato"><b>shameless</b></a> and as bold as a crow, but such a life is spiritually corrupt and arrogant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp244/en/sujato">Dhp 244</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that life is difficult for a modest person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp245/en/sujato"><b>always seeking purity</b></a> and who lives a life of detachment and humble sincerity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp245/en/sujato">Dhp 245</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that any person who destroys life, tells lies, or commits <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp246/en/sujato"><b>adultery</b></a> is actively undermining their own spiritual foundation in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp246/en/sujato">Dhp 246</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a person addicted to intoxicating drinks is essentially <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp247/en/sujato"><b>digging up his own root</b></a> even while still living in this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp247/en/sujato">Dhp 247</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that those who are unrestrained and of an evil nature will be subject to long-lasting suffering if they allow <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp248/en/sujato"><b>greed and unrighteousness</b></a> to rule them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp248/en/sujato">Dhp 248</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a person who becomes disgruntled or envious of the food and drink given to others will not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp249/en/sujato"><b>attain concentration</b></a> by day or by night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp249/en/sujato">Dhp 249</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that only when the feeling of resentment or envy toward others' gains is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp250/en/sujato"><b>uprooted</b></a> and destroyed can a person truly attain concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp250/en/sujato">Dhp 250</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha famously stated that there is no fire like lust, no grip like hatred, no net like delusion, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp251/en/sujato"><b>no river like craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp251/en/sujato">Dhp 251</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed how easy it is to see the faults of others while hiding one's own, comparing this behavior to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp252/en/sujato"><b>cheating gambler</b></a> who conceals an unlucky die? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp252/en/sujato">Dhp 252</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that for a person who constantly focuses on the faults of others and is always irritable, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp253/en/sujato"><b>taints increase</b></a> and they move further away from the destruction of those taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp253/en/sujato">Dhp 253</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as there is no track in the sky, there is no true ascetic outside the Dhamma, because while common people <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp254/en/sujato"><b>delight in proliferation</b></a>, the Tathāgatas are free from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp254/en/sujato">Dhp 254</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted that there are no eternal conditioned things and that there is no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp255/en/sujato"><b>instability</b></a> to be found in the Buddhas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp255/en/sujato">Dhp 255</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught a person is not established in Dhamma merely because they decide a case arbitrarily, but rather a wise person is one who carefully discriminates between <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp256/en/sujato"><b>right and wrong</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp256/en/sujato">Dhp 256</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp257/en/sujato"><b>righteous person</b></a> as one who leads others not by force but through Dhamma, acting as an impartial and wise guardian of the law? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp257/en/sujato">Dhp 257</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a person is not truly wise just because they speak a lot, but rather the one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp258/en/sujato"><b>peaceful, friendly, and fearless</b></a> is called wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp258/en/sujato">Dhp 258</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that an upholder of the Dhamma is not defined by how much they talk, but by whether they experience the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp259/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma personally</b></a> and do not neglect it, even if they have heard only a little? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp259/en/sujato">Dhp 259</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a person is not an "elder" simply because their hair has turned grey, noting that one who is merely advanced in age without virtue is said to have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp260/en/sujato"><b>aged in vain</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp260/en/sujato">Dhp 260</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a true elder as a wise person who has cast out all impurities and in whom truth, Dhamma, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp261/en/sujato"><b>non-violence, restraint, and self-control</b></a> abide? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp261/en/sujato">Dhp 261</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who is envious, stingy, and deceitful cannot become a person of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp262/en/sujato"><b>good character</b></a> merely through eloquent speech or a beautiful appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp262/en/sujato">Dhp 262</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a person is called "refined" or handsome in character only when they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp263/en/sujato"><b>uprooted and destroyed</b></a> their inner faults and cast out all hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp263/en/sujato">Dhp 263</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned how a person who is undisciplined, speaks lies, and is full of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp264/en/sujato"><b>desire and greed</b></a> could ever be considered a true ascetic, regardless of having a shaven head? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp264/en/sujato">Dhp 264</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person is truly called an "ascetic" (samaṇa) because they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp265/en/sujato"><b>stilled all evils</b></a>, whether those evils are small or large? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp265/en/sujato">Dhp 265</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that one is not a monk simply by begging for alms, and that one does not become a monk merely by adopting an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp266/en/sujato"><b>outward form</b></a> of practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp266/en/sujato">Dhp 266</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true monk as one who has moved beyond both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp267/en/sujato"><b>merit and evil</b></a>, lives a holy life, and moves through the world with careful discernment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp267/en/sujato">Dhp 267</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person does not become a sage through silence if they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp268/en/sujato"><b>foolish and ignorant</b></a>, but rather a wise person is like one who holds a scale and chooses only what is best? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp268/en/sujato">Dhp 268</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a true sage as one who avoids evils and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp269/en/sujato"><b>weighs both worlds</b></a> with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp269/en/sujato">Dhp 269</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a person is not "noble" (ariya) if they harm living beings, but is instead called noble because of their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp270/en/sujato"><b>non-violence toward all</b></a> living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp270/en/sujato">Dhp 270</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised monks that they should not rest content or feel secure merely because of their moral habits, much learning, or the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp271/en/sujato"><b>attainment of concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp271/en/sujato">Dhp 271</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged monks not to be confident in their spiritual progress until they have reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp272/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>, even if they have experienced the bliss of renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp272/en/sujato">Dhp 272</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp273/en/sujato"><b>Eightfold Path</b></a> to be the best of all paths, the Four Noble Truths the best of all truths, and dispassion (virāga) the best of all states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp273/en/sujato">Dhp 273</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the Eightfold Path is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp274/en/sujato"><b>only path</b></a> leading to the purification of vision, and that by following it, one can completely bewilder and escape the influence of Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp274/en/sujato">Dhp 274</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that once you have entered upon this path, you will make an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp275/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a>, for he has shown the way to extract the "dart" of sorrow once he gained the knowledge to do so? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp275/en/sujato">Dhp 275</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that you must make the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp276/en/sujato"><b>personal effort</b></a> yourself, as the Tathāgatas only point the way, and only those who meditate and enter the path are freed from the bonds of Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp276/en/sujato">Dhp 276</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that when a person perceives with wisdom that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp277/en/sujato"><b>"all conditioned things are impermanent,"</b></a> they become weary of suffering, which is the direct path to purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp277/en/sujato">Dhp 277</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the path to purification is found when one sees through wisdom that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp278/en/sujato"><b>"all conditioned things are suffering,"</b></a> leading to a profound turning away from worldly distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp278/en/sujato">Dhp 278</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that when a person realizes through wisdom that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp279/en/sujato"><b>"all things are non-self,"</b></a> they grow weary of suffering, marking the true path to purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp279/en/sujato">Dhp 279</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a person who is young and strong but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp280/en/sujato"><b>remains lazy</b></a> and fails to strive when it is time to exert effort will never find the path to wisdom due to their lethargic mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp280/en/sujato">Dhp 280</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who is guarded in speech, restrained in mind, and avoids doing evil with the body can purify these <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp281/en/sujato"><b>three pathways of action</b></a> and successfully win the path known by the seers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp281/en/sujato">Dhp 281</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that wisdom arises from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp282/en/sujato"><b>spiritual application</b></a>, and that by knowing the two-fold path of gain and loss, one should conduct themselves so that their wisdom continues to grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp282/en/sujato">Dhp 282</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged his followers to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp283/en/sujato"><b>cut down the forest</b></a> of lust rather than just a single tree, because danger arises from that "forest," and by clearing both the forest and its undergrowth, one reaches the state of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp283/en/sujato">Dhp 283</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that as long as even the slightest <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp284/en/sujato"><b>lustful desire</b></a> of a man for a woman remains uncut, his mind stays bound, just like a suckling calf is tied to its mother? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp284/en/sujato">Dhp 284</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp285/en/sujato"><b>pluck out self-love</b></a> as easily as an autumn lotus is plucked by the hand, and instead cultivate the path of peace leading to the Nibbāna he taught? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp285/en/sujato">Dhp 285</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a fool as someone who plans where they will live during the rainy season or winter without realizing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp286/en/sujato"><b>deadly danger</b></a> that could end their life unexpectedly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp286/en/sujato">Dhp 286</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared death to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp287/en/sujato"><b>great flood</b></a> that sweeps away a sleeping village, carrying off any person who is overly doting on their children and cattle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp287/en/sujato">Dhp 287</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that for a person overcome by the King of Death, children, fathers, and kinsmen can provide <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp288/en/sujato"><b>no protection</b></a> or shelter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp288/en/sujato">Dhp 288</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that a wise person, understanding that relatives cannot save them from death, should be restrained by virtue and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp289/en/sujato"><b>quickly clear the path</b></a> leading to Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp289/en/sujato">Dhp 289</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that if a wise person sees an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp290/en/sujato"><b>abundant happiness</b></a> to be gained by giving up a small pleasure, they should abandon that lesser pleasure for the sake of the greater one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp290/en/sujato">Dhp 290</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that one who seeks their own happiness by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp291/en/sujato"><b>inflicting pain on others</b></a> becomes entangled in the bonds of hatred and will never be freed from that enmity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp291/en/sujato">Dhp 291</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that for those who are arrogant and heedless, neglecting what should be done while performing what should not be done, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp292/en/sujato"><b>taints increase</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp292/en/sujato">Dhp 292</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the taints of mindful and clearly comprehending people come to an end when they persistently practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp293/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> and avoid doing what should not be done? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp293/en/sujato">Dhp 293</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a striking metaphor to state that a true brahmin goes untroubled even after having "killed" <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp294/en/sujato"><b>mother and father</b></a> (representing craving and conceit) and two warrior kings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp294/en/sujato">Dhp 294</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a brahmin as going untroubled after "killing" mother, father, and two learned kings, along with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp295/en/sujato"><b>tiger as the fifth</b></a> hindrance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp295/en/sujato">Dhp 295</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said the disciples of Gotama always wake up well-awakened when their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp296/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the Buddha</b></a> is constantly maintained both day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp296/en/sujato">Dhp 296</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that his disciples are truly well-awakened when they keep their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp297/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the Dhamma</b></a> constantly active throughout the day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp297/en/sujato">Dhp 297</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that the disciples of Gotama are always well-awakened if they maintain constant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp298/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the Saṅgha</b></a> every day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp298/en/sujato">Dhp 298</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that his followers wake up well-awakened when they practice constant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp299/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> throughout their daily lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp299/en/sujato">Dhp 299</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that his disciples remain well-awakened when their minds <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp300/en/sujato"><b>delight in non-violence</b></a> (ahimsa) toward all beings, day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp300/en/sujato">Dhp 300</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that the disciples of Gotama wake up well-awakened when their minds find constant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp301/en/sujato"><b>delight in meditation</b></a> throughout the day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp301/en/sujato">Dhp 301</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that while renunciation is difficult and householder life is painful, one should strive not to be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp302/en/sujato"><b>traveler in saṃsāra</b></a> pursued by suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp302/en/sujato">Dhp 302</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that a person who has faith, is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp303/en/sujato"><b>endowed with virtue</b></a>, and possesses fame and wealth is honored in every land they visit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp303/en/sujato">Dhp 303</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared good people to the Himalayan mountains because they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp304/en/sujato"><b>shine from afar</b></a>, whereas the wicked are as invisible as arrows shot in the dark of night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp304/en/sujato">Dhp 304</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person who sits, sleeps, and walks alone unwearied, while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp305/en/sujato"><b>subduing themselves</b></a>, will find true delight in the solitude of the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp305/en/sujato">Dhp 305</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp334/en/sujato"><b>craving</b></a> grows like a creeper in a person who lives negligently, causing them to leap from life to life like a monkey seeking fruit in a forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp334/en/sujato">Dhp 334</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that when a person is overcome by the wretched, clinging <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp335/en/sujato"><b>craving in the world</b></a>, their sorrows grow like birana grass after a heavy rain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp335/en/sujato">Dhp 335</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that for one who overcomes the wretched <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp336/en/sujato"><b>craving that is difficult to surpass</b></a>, sorrows fall away like drops of water from a lotus leaf? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp336/en/sujato">Dhp 336</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised those gathered to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp337/en/sujato"><b>dig up the root of craving</b></a> just as one digs up the root of birana grass, so that Mara may not break them repeatedly like a reed in a stream? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp337/en/sujato">Dhp 337</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the persistence of suffering to a tree, noting that just as a tree grows again if its root is firm and undamaged even if cut, so too <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp338/en/sujato"><b>this suffering arises</b></a> again and again if the underlying tendency of craving is not uprooted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp338/en/sujato">Dhp 338</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that for a person of wrong view, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp339/en/sujato"><b>thirty-six streams of craving</b></a> that flow toward pleasant objects carry them away with thoughts based on passion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp339/en/sujato">Dhp 339</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that the streams of craving flow everywhere, and when one sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp340/en/sujato"><b>creeper of craving</b></a> sprouting and standing, its root should be cut with wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp340/en/sujato">Dhp 340</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha remarked that those beings who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp341/en/sujato"><b>bound by attachment to pleasures</b></a> and who seek happiness are the ones who undergo birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp341/en/sujato">Dhp 341</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared people driven by craving to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp342/en/sujato"><b>snared hare</b></a>, noting that being fettered by bonds and attachments, they undergo suffering for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp342/en/sujato">Dhp 342</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because people are driven by craving like a snared hare, the Buddha urged practitioners to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp343/en/sujato"><b>dispel craving</b></a> if they desire their own dispassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp343/en/sujato">Dhp 343</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of the irony of a person who, having left the "forest" of household life for the "forest-less" state of a monk, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp344/en/sujato"><b>runs back to that same forest</b></a> of desire even after being freed from its bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp344/en/sujato">Dhp 344</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that wise people do not consider bonds made of iron, wood, or hemp to be strong, but rather the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp345/en/sujato"><b>strong attachment</b></a> to precious jewelry, children, and wives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp345/en/sujato">Dhp 345</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described attachment to sensual pleasures as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp346/en/sujato"><b>strong bond that pulls one down</b></a> and is difficult to loosen, yet the wise renounce it and wander forth without longing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp346/en/sujato">Dhp 346</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared those overcome by passion to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp347/en/sujato"><b>spider caught in its own web</b></a>, but noted that the wise cut this bond and depart, leaving behind all suffering and longing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp347/en/sujato">Dhp 347</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised seekers to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp348/en/sujato"><b>let go of the past, future, and middle</b></a>, reaching the further shore of existence with a mind liberated in every way, so as not to undergo birth and aging again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp348/en/sujato">Dhp 348</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha cautioned that for a person troubled by thoughts, possessing fierce passion, and contemplating only what is attractive, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp349/en/sujato"><b>craving grows even more</b></a>, making their bond truly strong? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp349/en/sujato">Dhp 349</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one who delights in calming thoughts and constantly meditates on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp350/en/sujato"><b>unattractive nature of the body</b></a> will bring an end to craving and cut the bond of Mara? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp350/en/sujato">Dhp 350</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a person who has reached the final goal, is fearless, without craving, and free from blemish, as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp351/en/sujato"><b>broken the thorns of existence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp351/en/sujato">Dhp 351</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified the one who is without craving and grasping, and is skilled in language and the sequence of letters, as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp352/en/sujato"><b>person of great wisdom</b></a> who bears their final body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp352/en/sujato">Dhp 352</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared himself the conqueror of all and the knower of all, liberated through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp353/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a>, having realized everything by himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp353/en/sujato">Dhp 353</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp354/en/sujato"><b>gift of the Dhamma excels all gifts</b></a>, the taste of the Dhamma excels all tastes, and the destruction of craving overcomes all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp354/en/sujato">Dhp 354</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp355/en/sujato"><b>riches destroy the foolish</b></a> but not those who seek the further shore, as the fool destroys themselves through craving for wealth just as they would destroy others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp355/en/sujato">Dhp 355</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that just as weeds are the bane of fields, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp356/en/sujato"><b>lust is the bane of people</b></a>, and therefore gifts given to those free from lust yield great fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp356/en/sujato">Dhp 356</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that weeds ruin fields while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp357/en/sujato"><b>hatred ruins people</b></a>, which is why offerings made to those who have overcome hatred result in great merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp357/en/sujato">Dhp 357</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that just as fields are damaged by weeds, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp358/en/sujato"><b>delusion is the ruin of people</b></a>, and thus gifts given to those without delusion are highly fruitful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp358/en/sujato">Dhp 358</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that as weeds are the blight of fields, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp359/en/sujato"><b>desire is the blight of mankind</b></a>, and consequently, giving to those free from desire brings great fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp359/en/sujato">Dhp 359</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp360/en/sujato"><b>restraint in the eye</b></a>, ear, nose, and tongue is good for a practitioner's discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp360/en/sujato">Dhp 360</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp361/en/sujato"><b>restrained in every way</b></a>—including restraint in body, speech, and mind—is liberated from all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp361/en/sujato">Dhp 361</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true monk as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp362/en/sujato"><b>controlled in hands, feet, and speech</b></a>, finds delight in inward reflection, and remains solitary and content? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp362/en/sujato">Dhp 362</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp363/en/sujato"><b>controlled in speech</b></a>, speaks with wisdom rather than arrogance, and clarifies the meaning of the Dhamma, possesses speech that is sweet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp363/en/sujato">Dhp 363</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp364/en/sujato"><b>dwells in the Dhamma</b></a>, delights in it, and constantly ponders and remembers the teaching, does not fall away from the true Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp364/en/sujato">Dhp 364</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned that a monk who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp365/en/sujato"><b>envies others</b></a> or underestimates his own gains will fail to attain concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp365/en/sujato">Dhp 365</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted that even if a monk's gains are small, the gods will praise him if he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp366/en/sujato"><b>does not underestimate his gain</b></a> and remains pure in his livelihood and energetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp366/en/sujato">Dhp 366</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a true monk as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp367/en/sujato"><b>no sense of 'mine' in name-and-form</b></a> and does not grieve over what does not exist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp367/en/sujato">Dhp 367</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha promised that a monk dwelling in loving-kindness who is pleased with the teaching will attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp368/en/sujato"><b>state of peace</b></a>, which is the bliss of the stilling of formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp368/en/sujato">Dhp 368</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the metaphor of a boat, advising a monk to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp369/en/sujato"><b>bail out the boat</b></a> of the self, because once emptied of lust and hatred, it will travel swiftly toward Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp369/en/sujato">Dhp 369</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a monk who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp370/en/sujato"><b>crossed the flood</b></a> as one who has cut off five lower fetters, abandoned five higher ones, and developed five further spiritual faculties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp370/en/sujato">Dhp 370</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted monks to meditate and not be negligent, warning that those who let their minds delight in sensual pleasures might one day <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp371/en/sujato"><b>swallow a red-hot iron ball</b></a> through their own carelessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp371/en/sujato">Dhp 371</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp372/en/sujato"><b>no meditative absorption for the unwise</b></a> and no wisdom for those who do not meditate, but the one who possesses both is close to Nibbana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp372/en/sujato">Dhp 372</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha said a monk with a peaceful mind who enters an empty house experiences a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp373/en/sujato"><b>superhuman delight</b></a> through clearly seeing the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp373/en/sujato">Dhp 373</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that whenever a practitioner contemplates the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp374/en/sujato"><b>rise and fall of the aggregates</b></a>, they experience joy and happiness, which is the Deathless for those who know it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp374/en/sujato">Dhp 374</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha established that for a wise monk, the practice begins with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp375/en/sujato"><b>guarding the senses</b></a>, contentment, and restraint according to the monastic code (Patimokkha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp375/en/sujato">Dhp 375</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised practitioners to associate with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp376/en/sujato"><b>noble friends</b></a> of pure life and to be refined in conduct and behavior in order to end suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp376/en/sujato">Dhp 376</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the imagery of a jasmine plant, saying that monks should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp377/en/sujato"><b>shed lust and hatred</b></a> just as the flower sheds its withered petals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp377/en/sujato">Dhp 377</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha characterized a monk as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp378/en/sujato"><b>at peace</b></a> when they are tranquil in body, speech, and mind, well-concentrated, and have rejected the bait of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp378/en/sujato">Dhp 378</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp379/en/sujato"><b>urge themselves by themselves</b></a> and examine themselves, because a self-guarded and mindful monk will live happily? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp379/en/sujato">Dhp 379</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp380/en/sujato"><b>self is the lord of self</b></a> and self is the goal of self, urging practitioners to restrain themselves like a merchant training a noble horse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp380/en/sujato">Dhp 380</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a monk who is full of joy and confident in the teaching will reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp381/en/sujato"><b>state of peace</b></a>, which is the bliss of the stilling of formations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp381/en/sujato">Dhp 381</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp382/en/sujato"><b>young monk</b></a> who applies himself to the teaching illuminates the world like the moon when it is freed from a cloud? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp382/en/sujato">Dhp 382</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged practitioners to strive and cut the stream of craving, teaching that by knowing the destruction of conditioned things, one becomes a knower of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp383/en/sujato"><b>unconditioned</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp383/en/sujato">Dhp 383</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained that when a brahmin has gone to the far shore of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp384/en/sujato"><b>two states</b></a>—namely, calm and insight—all their fetters vanish through their understanding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp384/en/sujato">Dhp 384</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a true brahmin as one for whom there is neither a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp385/en/sujato"><b>far shore</b></a> nor a near shore, and who is free from distress and worldly ties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp385/en/sujato">Dhp 385</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true brahmin as one who is meditative, stainless, has done what was to be done, is free from taints, and has reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp386/en/sujato"><b>highest goal</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp386/en/sujato">Dhp 386</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha observed that while the sun shines by day and the moon by night, the Awakened One <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp387/en/sujato"><b>shines by his glory</b></a> all through the day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp387/en/sujato">Dhp 387</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that one is called a brahmin because they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp388/en/sujato"><b>discarded evil</b></a>, a recluse because they live in equanimity, and a "gone-forth one" because they have banished their own stains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp388/en/sujato">Dhp 388</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp389/en/sujato"><b>should not strike</b></a> a brahmin, and a brahmin should not vent wrath upon their striker, for woe follows the one who strikes and even more woe follows the one who vents wrath? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp389/en/sujato">Dhp 389</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha noted it is of no small advantage to a brahmin when they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp390/en/sujato"><b>restraining the mind</b></a> from pleasant things, for as the intention to harm recoils, suffering is stilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp390/en/sujato">Dhp 390</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called a person a brahmin if they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp391/en/sujato"><b>restrained in three ways</b></a>—body, speech, and mind—and have committed no wrong through any of these? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp391/en/sujato">Dhp 391</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised that one should reverently honor the person from whom they learn the Dhamma taught by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp392/en/sujato"><b>Fully Enlightened One</b></a>, just as a brahmin honors the sacrificial fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp392/en/sujato">Dhp 392</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a person does not become a brahmin by matted hair, lineage, or birth, but rather the one in whom <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp393/en/sujato"><b>truth and Dhamma</b></a> exist is considered pure and a true brahmin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp393/en/sujato">Dhp 393</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned the value of external asceticism, like matted hair or deerskin garments, for those who have a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp394/en/sujato"><b>jungle within</b></a> while only polishing their outside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp394/en/sujato">Dhp 394</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called that person a brahmin who wears rags, is lean with veins showing, and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp395/en/sujato"><b>meditating alone</b></a> in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp395/en/sujato">Dhp 395</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that birth from a certain mother does not make one a brahmin, but rather a person is a true brahmin when they are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp396/en/sujato"><b>without attachment</b></a> and without clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp396/en/sujato">Dhp 396</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a brahmin as one who has cut <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp397/en/sujato"><b>all fetters</b></a>, who does not tremble with fear, and who has gone beyond all ties and attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp397/en/sujato">Dhp 397</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described an enlightened person as one who has cut the strap of hatred, the thong of craving, and the rope of wrong views, and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp398/en/sujato"><b>lifted the crossbar</b></a> of ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp398/en/sujato">Dhp 398</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called a person a brahmin if they endure abuse, beating, and imprisonment without anger, using the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp399/en/sujato"><b>strength of patience</b></a> as their army? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp399/en/sujato">Dhp 399</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke of a brahmin as one who is free from anger, observant of vows, virtuous, subdued, and who is bearing their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp400/en/sujato"><b>final body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp400/en/sujato">Dhp 400</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person who does not cling to sensual pleasures to water on a lotus leaf or a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp401/en/sujato"><b>mustard seed</b></a> on the point of a needle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp401/en/sujato">Dhp 401</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a brahmin as one who knows for themselves the destruction of suffering and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp402/en/sujato"><b>laid down the burden</b></a> of the aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp402/en/sujato">Dhp 402</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a true brahmin as one of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp403/en/sujato"><b>deep wisdom</b></a> who is skilled in distinguishing the right path from the wrong path and has reached the ultimate goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp403/en/sujato">Dhp 403</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a brahmin is one who does not associate with householders or the homeless and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp404/en/sujato"><b>few in desires</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp404/en/sujato">Dhp 404</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called a person a brahmin if they have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp405/en/sujato"><b>laid aside the rod</b></a> toward all beings and neither kill nor cause others to kill? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp405/en/sujato">Dhp 405</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a brahmin as one who is friendly among the hostile, peaceful among the violent, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp406/en/sujato"><b>unattached</b></a> among those who cling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp406/en/sujato">Dhp 406</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a brahmin to a needle's point from which a mustard seed has fallen, because their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp407/en/sujato"><b>lust and hatred</b></a>, along with conceit and hypocrisy, have completely fallen away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp407/en/sujato">Dhp 407</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a brahmin as one who utters <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp408/en/sujato"><b>speech that is true</b></a>, gentle, and instructive, ensuring they do not offend anyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp408/en/sujato">Dhp 408</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called a person a brahmin if they take <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp409/en/sujato"><b>nothing not given</b></a> in the world, whether it is long or short, small or large, beautiful or ugly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp409/en/sujato">Dhp 409</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a brahmin is one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp410/en/sujato"><b>without longing</b></a> for this world or the next and has no worldly attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp410/en/sujato">Dhp 410</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a brahmin as one who has no attachments, has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp411/en/sujato"><b>reached certainty</b></a> through knowledge, and has plunged into the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp411/en/sujato">Dhp 411</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called a person a brahmin if they have gone beyond the bond of both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp412/en/sujato"><b>merit and evil</b></a> and are sorrowless, stainless, and pure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp412/en/sujato">Dhp 412</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a true brahmin to the moon, describing them as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp413/en/sujato"><b>pure like the moon</b></a>, clear, undisturbed, and having exhausted the delight in existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp413/en/sujato">Dhp 413</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a brahmin as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp414/en/sujato"><b>passed beyond</b></a> the difficult path of samsara and delusion, is meditative, and is free from doubt and clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp414/en/sujato">Dhp 414</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught that a brahmin is one who, having abandoned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp415/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a>, wanders forth as a homeless one with the craving for existence exhausted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp415/en/sujato">Dhp 415</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a brahmin as one who has abandoned craving and wandered forth, having seen their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp416/en/sujato"><b>thirst for existence</b></a> entirely destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp416/en/sujato">Dhp 416</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called a person a brahmin who, having abandoned the human bond, has also <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp417/en/sujato"><b>transcended the divine bond</b></a> and is detached from all bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp417/en/sujato">Dhp 417</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified a brahmin as a hero who has abandoned both delight and boredom, is cooled and without acquisitions, and is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp418/en/sujato"><b>conqueror of all worlds</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp418/en/sujato">Dhp 418</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called a person a brahmin if they know the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp419/en/sujato"><b>passing away and rebirth</b></a> of beings in every way and are unattached, happy, and enlightened? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp419/en/sujato">Dhp 419</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha stated that a brahmin is an Arahant whose path is unknown to gods, gandhabbas, or humans, and whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp420/en/sujato"><b>taints are destroyed</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp420/en/sujato">Dhp 420</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha defined a brahmin as one who has nothing in the past, future, or middle, and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp421/en/sujato"><b>clinging to nothing</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp421/en/sujato">Dhp 421</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described a brahmin as one who is noble, excellent, a hero, a great sage, a conqueror, and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp422/en/sujato"><b>passionless and washed</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp422/en/sujato">Dhp 422</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called a person a brahmin if they know their previous lives, see heaven and hell, and have reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp423/en/sujato"><b>end of birth</b></a> through the perfection of direct knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/dhp423/en/sujato">Dhp 423</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gayā-Kassapa</b></a> used to believe he could wash away his past evil deeds by bathing in the Gayā River three times every day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato">Thag 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gayā-Kassapa</b></a> eventually realized that true spiritual purification is achieved by plunging into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> rather than physical water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato">Thag 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gayā-Kassapa</b></a> declared himself a legitimate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>son of the Buddha</b></a> after he attained the Three Knowledges and successfully completed the Teacher's instructions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato">Thag 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his conversion to the Buddha's teaching, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gayā-Kassapa</b></a> led a community of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>two hundred disciples</b></a> while living as a Brahmin ascetic at Gayā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato">Thag 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gayā-Kassapa</b></a> attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>true knowledge</b></a> (aññā) by observing the Dhamma with right reflection and understanding its profound meaning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato">Thag 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gayā-Kassapa</b></a> specifically identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>Gaya-Phalgu River</b></a> as the location where he performed his morning, noon, and evening ritual washings to rid himself of sin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato">Thag 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gayā-Kassapa</b></a> claimed that by following the path, he became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>pure and stainless</b></a>, inheriting the spiritual legacy of the "Pure One" (the Buddha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato">Thag 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gayā-Kassapa</b></a> was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin family</b></a> and shared a very similar background and spiritual quest to his relative, Nadī-Kassapa? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.7/en/sujato">Thag 5.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Uttiya</b></a>, a former prince of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan royal family</b></a> in Kapilavatthu, decided to join the Sangha after being inspired by the Buddha's spiritual power during a visit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato">Thag 1.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Uttiya</b></a> warned that hearing a woman’s song without mindfulness can cause <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>sensual lust</b></a> to arise and destroy one's concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato">Thag 1.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Uttiya</b></a> attained the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> while practicing insight meditation at midday, shortly after overcoming a period of mental agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato">Thag 1.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Uttiya</b></a> taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>increase of taints</b></a> (asavas) occurs when a practitioner loses mindfulness and dwells on thoughts of affection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato">Thag 1.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Uttiya</b></a> emphasized that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>obsessed by sounds</b></a> and losing one's presence of mind leads directly to the mental defilements that fuel the round of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato">Thag 1.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Uttiya</b></a> overcame a lapse in his spiritual concentration by using the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>power of reflection</b></a> to regain control over his agitated mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato">Thag 1.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Uttiya</b></a> initially developed faith in the Buddha's teaching after witnessing the Master visiting his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>family relatives</b></a> in his hometown of Kapilavatthu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato">Thag 1.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Uttiya</b></a> realized that focusing on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato"><b>attractive signs</b></a> leads to a mind captivated by passion, which only serves to increase one's suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.99/en/sujato">Thag 1.99</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> attained the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>total liberation</b></a> at the exact moment the razor touched his hair during his ordination ceremony? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato">Thag 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> squandered his family's wealth on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>prostitute</b></a> before feeling deep regret for his unrighteous lifestyle and seeking out the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato">Thag 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a>'s spiritual transformation began with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>vivid dream</b></a> of the Buddha sitting peacefully and understanding the Venerable's internal state of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato">Thag 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> declared he is now completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>finished with cravings</b></a> and stated that he no longer owes anything to the desires that once ruled him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato">Thag 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>happiness of recluses</b></a> as being rooted in their complete freedom from the bonds and control of women? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato">Thag 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> confessed to a layperson that his former life was characterized by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>lack of seriousness</b></a> and a failure to live a pure holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato">Thag 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> successfully reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>state of Nibbāna</b></a>, which he describes as the sorrowless realm where craving cannot reach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato">Thag 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> was born into a Brahmin family in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>Rājagaha</b></a> and initially associated with bad company before his ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato">Thag 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> experienced the bliss of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato"><b>meditative absorption</b></a> and liberation even while being questioned by others about his worldly possessions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.9/en/sujato">Thag 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāpanthaka</b></a> was the grandson of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>wealthy official</b></a> in Rājagaha and decided to go forth into homelessness after listening to the Buddha preach the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato">Thag 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāpanthaka</b></a> first encountered the Buddha, whom he called the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>Fearless Teacher</b></a>, he was deeply stirred by the sight of the "Supreme Person"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato">Thag 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāpanthaka</b></a> achieved his spiritual goal through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>diligent effort</b></a>, having renounced his wife, children, money, and grain to follow the monastic path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato">Thag 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāpanthaka</b></a> made a resolute vow that he would not sit down even for a single minute until he had successfully extracted the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>arrow of craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato">Thag 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāpanthaka</b></a> successfully attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā), which include the recollection of past lives and the purification of the divine eye? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato">Thag 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāpanthaka</b></a> described his liberation from all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>acquisitions</b></a> (upadhi) as being similar to the sun rising to dispel the darkness of the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato">Thag 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāpanthaka</b></a> became a master of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>four jhanas</b></a> and the Teacher's instructions before finally attaining the fruit of Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato">Thag 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāpanthaka</b></a> pointed out that those who rely on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>external gestures</b></a> and pleas to deities for luck cannot achieve their true spiritual aspirations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato">Thag 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after all his cravings had dried up, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāpanthaka</b></a> entered and remained in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>cross-legged position</b></a>, dwelling in the bliss of fruition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato">Thag 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāpanthaka</b></a> lived with well-restrained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato"><b>sense faculties</b></a>, remaining unvanquished while paying homage to the Sambuddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.3/en/sujato">Thag 8.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lakuṇṭaka-Bhaddiya</b></a>, despite being born into a wealthy family in Sāvatthī, was nicknamed "Lakuṇṭaka" because he was exceptionally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>short in stature</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato">Thag 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lakuṇṭaka-Bhaddiya</b></a> first attained the stage of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>Non-Returner</b></a> (anāgāmī) by taking the sight of a laughing woman’s teeth as his subject for insight meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato">Thag 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lakuṇṭaka-Bhaddiya</b></a> reached the ultimate goal of Arahantship only after receiving personal instruction on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness directed to the body</b></a> (kāyagatāsati) from the Venerable Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato">Thag 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lakuṇṭaka-Bhaddiya</b></a> was a great scholar and eloquent speaker who was particularly famous for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>sweet voice</b></a> when teaching others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato">Thag 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while others might find joy in music from drums and flutes, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lakuṇṭaka-Bhaddiya</b></a> declared that he found his true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>delight in the Buddha's teaching</b></a> while sitting under a tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato">Thag 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lakuṇṭaka-Bhaddiya</b></a> described himself as a happy and fortunate meditator because he had completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>abandoned the roots of craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato">Thag 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lakuṇṭaka-Bhaddiya</b></a> warned that "fools" are easily led astray by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>mere sound</b></a> because they fail to understand the internal state and only perceive the external? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato">Thag 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lakuṇṭaka-Bhaddiya</b></a> gained a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>clear vision</b></a> of both the internal and external, which allowed him to remain unhindered and unmoved by the voices of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato">Thag 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lakuṇṭaka-Bhaddiya</b></a> observed that those who judge a person based solely on their physical appearance or voice are actually dominated by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>desire and lust</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato">Thag 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lakuṇṭaka-Bhaddiya</b></a> found great peace sitting in meditation within the woods of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato"><b>Ambātaka garden</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.2/en/sujato">Thag 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Devasabha</b> was born into the royal Sakyan family and first developed faith when the Buddha intervened to settle a dispute between the Sakyan and Koliyan clans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.100/en/sujato">Thag 1.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Devasabha</b> describes the path to liberation as being accomplished through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.100/en/sujato"><b>right exertion</b></a> and established mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.100/en/sujato">Thag 1.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Devasabha</b> used the beautiful metaphor of being "filled with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.100/en/sujato"><b>flowers of liberation</b></a>" to describe his state before reaching final Nirvana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.100/en/sujato">Thag 1.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Devasabha</b> attained the state of an Arahant, which he characterizes as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.100/en/sujato"><b>taintless</b></a> and dwelling in the bliss of release? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.100/en/sujato">Thag 1.100</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Belaṭṭhakāni</b> was originally a Brahmin from Sāvatthī who, despite ordaining, initially struggled with laziness and the use of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.101/en/sujato"><b>harsh speech</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.101/en/sujato">Thag 1.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, seeing that <b>Venerable Belaṭṭhakāni</b> had insight that was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.101/en/sujato"><b>ripe</b></a>, personally exhorted him with a verse to stir his diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.101/en/sujato">Thag 1.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Belaṭṭhakāni</b> was cautioned by the Buddha against being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.101/en/sujato"><b>large pig</b></a> fattened on grain, which represents a lazy person who only seeks to eat and sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.101/en/sujato">Thag 1.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the lazy person's habit of using their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.101/en/sujato"><b>mouth like a plow</b></a>—meaning they are only concerned with filling their belly—to a life that leads only to further rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.101/en/sujato">Thag 1.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Belaṭṭhakāni</b> achieved Arahantship after being deeply moved by the Buddha's teaching that a lazy man goes to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.101/en/sujato"><b>repeated rebirth</b></a> again and again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.101/en/sujato">Thag 1.101</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Setuccha</b> was once the son of a local ruler who suffered great sorrow after he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.102/en/sujato"><b>lost his throne</b></a> and could no longer maintain his independence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.102/en/sujato">Thag 1.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Setuccha</b> warns that those who are deceived by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.102/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a> and have defiled minds cannot truly progress in their actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.102/en/sujato">Thag 1.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Setuccha</b> taught that people who are constantly wavering between <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.102/en/sujato"><b>gain and loss</b></a> fail to achieve the stability of the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.102/en/sujato">Thag 1.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Setuccha</b> observed that those preoccupied with worldly fluctuations and ego are unable to attain true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.102/en/sujato"><b>concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.102/en/sujato">Thag 1.102</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Bandhura</b> was born in the city of Sīlavatī as the son of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato"><b>senator</b></a> (councilor) before he traveled to Sāvatthī and heard the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato">Thag 1.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that out of gratitude to his former king, <b>Venerable Bandhura</b> returned to his hometown of Sīlavatī to teach the king the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato">Thag 1.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the king of Sīlavatī was so inspired by the teachings of <b>Venerable Bandhura</b> that he built a great monastery named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato"><b>Sudassana</b></a> and offered it to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato">Thag 1.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite being offered a grand monastery, <b>Venerable Bandhura</b> chose to give everything to the Sangha and continued to live simply by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato"><b>gathering alms</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato">Thag 1.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Bandhura</b> declined the offer of fellow monks to provide for his needs, stating that he was completely satisfied with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato"><b>taste of the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato">Thag 1.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Bandhura</b> compared the Dhamma to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato"><b>supreme nectar</b></a>, noting that once one has tasted this sweetness, they have no need for anything else? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato">Thag 1.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Bandhura</b> viewed worldly attractions and sensual pleasures as being like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato"><b>poison</b></a>, which he would never return to after attaining enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato">Thag 1.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Bandhura</b> declared he had no "need" for specific people or objects because he lived in constant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a> through his spiritual realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.103/en/sujato">Thag 1.103</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vītāsoka</b></a> was the younger brother of the famous Emperor Dhammāsoka and lived in India during the 218th year after the Buddha's passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato">Thag 2.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before he ordained as a monk, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vītāsoka</b></a> was a dedicated disciple of Venerable Giridatta and had already gained a deep understanding of both the Sutta and Abhidhamma Pitakas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato">Thag 2.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vītāsoka</b></a> attained the first stage of enlightenment, known as Stream-entry, while sitting in a barber's chair after seeing gray hairs in a mirror? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato">Thag 2.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon seeing gray hairs during a haircut, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vītāsoka</b></a> was struck by a powerful sense of spiritual urgency that led him to develop deep insight into the nature of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato">Thag 2.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after his formal ordination under his teacher Giridatta, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vītāsoka</b></a> successfully attained the final goal of Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato">Thag 2.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vītāsoka</b></a> described his spiritual awakening as the shattering of "the darkness of night" and the realization that the body he once cherished was actually empty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato">Thag 2.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vītāsoka</b></a> declared that all of his spiritual "wrappings" or bonds were severed, ensuring that he would never face rebirth again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato">Thag 2.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his royal upbringing as a prince, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vītāsoka</b></a> chose to abandon his worldly status to seek the path of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.25/en/sujato">Thag 2.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> was born into a family of landowners in Sāvatthī and made the life-changing decision to ordain as a monk immediately after the birth of his first child? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato">Thag 2.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his diligent practice while living near a village, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> attained the six supernormal knowledges (<i>abhiññā</i>), marking his mastery over the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato">Thag 2.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when his wife and a large retinue tried to convince him to return to lay life to save their inheritance, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> manifested the power to stand in mid-air to preach to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato">Thag 2.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> attained a state of spiritual safety by completely abandoning the five hindrances that typically obstruct the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato">Thag 2.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> taught that by holding up the "Mirror of the Dhamma," a practitioner is able to truly know and see the nature of their own self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato">Thag 2.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> realized through profound meditation that the human body is empty of a permanent core, whether viewed from the inside or the outside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato">Thag 2.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the death of his child and his wife's pleas to reclaim their family property, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> remained committed to his liberation and renounced all worldly possessions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato">Thag 2.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> once resided in a cemetery in Sāvatthī after paying his respects to the Buddha, demonstrating his dedication to the practice of forest asceticism? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.26/en/sujato">Thag 2.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin was born on the exact <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>same day as the Buddha</b></a> in the city of Kapilavatthu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin was one of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>seven co-natals</b></a> (those born at the same time as the Bodhisatta), a group which also included the Bodhi tree, the mother of Rāhula, the horse Kanthaka, and the attendant Channa? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite being named Udāyin, this Venerable was commonly known as Kāludāyin because of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>dark skin</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin grew up as a close <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>childhood companion</b></a> of the Bodhisatta before the latter left the palace to seek enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Suddhodana specifically chose Venerable Kāludāyin to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>invite the Buddha</b></a> back to his home city after many other messengers had failed to return? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before Venerable Kāludāyin was successful, the King had sent several ministers with thousands of followers, but they all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>attained Arahantship</b></a> after hearing the Dhamma and remained with the Buddha instead of delivering the King's message? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin promised King Suddhodana he would bring the Buddha home even if he himself chose to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>become a monk</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin waited until the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>end of the rainy season</b></a> to invite the Buddha to travel, observing that the roads were finally dry and suitable for the journey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin used poetic verses to describe trees with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>crimson blossoms</b></a> that looked like blazing fires as a way to praise the beauty of the season for travel? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin noted the season was perfect for the Buddha’s journey because the trees were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>shedding old leaves</b></a> and preparing to bear fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin described the weather during the Buddha's homecoming as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>neither too cold nor too hot</b></a>, making it a pleasant time for the journey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin encouraged the Buddha to travel so that the two clans, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>Sakyans and Koliyans</b></a>, could have the opportunity to see him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin mentioned the Buddha crossing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>Rohinī River</b></a> while traveling toward the west to visit his father? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin used the metaphor of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>hope of a plowman</b></a> sowing seeds to illustrate how the Buddha's return would fulfill the hopes of his family and followers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin compared the spiritual journey to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>merchant crossing the sea</b></a> with the hope of bringing back wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin spoke of how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>continued sowing</b></a> and timely rain lead to an abundance of grain for a nation, mirroring how persistent spiritual effort leads to great results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin praised the merit of giving, stating that a generous donor who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>continues to give</b></a> is one who will ascend to the heavenly realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin highlighted the Buddha's noble lineage, claiming that family purity was maintained for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>seven generations</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin referred to the Buddha’s father, King Suddhodana, by the honorific title of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>Great Seer</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin confirmed that the Buddha’s mother, Queen Māyā, passed away and was reborn to enjoy the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>delights of heaven</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin accompanied the Buddha on his journey to Kapilavatthu along with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>twenty thousand Arahants</b></a>, traveling one league each day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kāludāyin declared himself to be the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato"><b>son of the Buddha</b></a>, the one who is the "unconquered conqueror" and the peerless Angīrasa? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.1/en/sujato">Thag 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a>, born into a prominent Brahmin family in Sāvatthī, was a master of the Vedas and a champion orator before he encountered the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> achieved the ultimate goal of Arahantship at the very moment his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>hair was being shaved</b></a> to enter the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> taught that the first step in the ascetic's practice is to know one's own happiness and observe the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>teachings one has heard</b></a> to see what is suitable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> identified associating with good friends in the path and engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>extensive learning</b></a> of the Dhamma as essential for a true ascetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> emphasized that a monk's duty includes listening carefully to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Teacher</b></a> (the Buddha) as a primary component of their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> taught that an ascetic should have a heart full of devotion toward the Buddha and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>reverence for the Dhamma</b></a> as it really is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> highlighted that a true practitioner of the path must cherish and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>esteem the Saṅgha</b></a> as part of the ascetic's training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> instructed that maintaining a pure and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>blameless livelihood</b></a> is a fundamental requirement for the ascetic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a>, an ascetic's duty involves keeping one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>thoughts well-stabilized</b></a> through consistent mindfulness and posture? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> taught that a monk should be established in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>higher mind</b></a> (adhicitta) while always being mindful of what is appropriate to do? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> advocated for seeking out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>remote forest lodgings</b></a> that are quiet and far from noise to better associate with silent sages? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> defined the ascetic's duty as being virtuous and well-learned while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>investigating the Dhamma</b></a> according to reality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> encouraged practitioners to engage in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>meditation on truths</b></a> as a core part of their daily spiritual routine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> taught that meditating on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>nature of impermanence</b></a> and the perception of non-self are vital practices for an ascetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> advised monks to cultivate the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>perception of foulness</b></a> (asubha) and to avoid finding any delight in worldly affairs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> identified the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> (bojjhaṅgas) as a necessary part of the ascetic’s path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> taught that an ascetic should master the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>four bases of power</b></a> (iddhipādas) and the spiritual faculties to progress in the training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a>, the complete undertaking of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> is the definitive duty of those following the Buddha's guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> described the silent sage (muni) as one who has successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>abandoned all craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> taught that the pinnacle of the ascetic life is reached when a person has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>smashed the taints</b></a> (āsavas) at their very root? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> concluded his teaching by urging practitioners to always <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>dwell in liberation</b></a> as the final goal of the ascetic's practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gotama</b></a> explain the true nature of the ascetic life, many of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin relatives</b></a> were so inspired that they accepted the training and established themselves in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.7/en/sujato">Thag 10.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūti</b></a> was the younger brother of the famous householder Anāthapiṇḍika and was born into the wealthy family of the counselor Sumana in Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūti</b></a> decided to renounce the world and ordain as a monk after witnessing the historic donation of Jeta’s Grove to the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūti</b></a> achieved a deep mastery of monastic training, becoming thoroughly proficient in both categories of the code of discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūti</b></a> attained the state of an Arahant after retiring to the forest to practice a specific meditation theme given to him by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūti</b></a> to be the foremost among monks who dwell in "limitless loving-kindness" (metta)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūti</b></a> was recognized as the foremost monk among those "worthy of offerings" due to his unique habit of meditating while on his alms round? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when going for alms, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūti</b></a> would enter into a meditative absorption and only accept food after emerging, thereby maximizing the merit for the donors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a severe drought once threatened the city of Rājagaha because <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūti</b></a> was meditating in the open air due to a lack of shelter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Bimbisāra of Magadha once invited <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūti</b></a> to stay in his city but inadvertently forgot to construct the promised dwelling for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the rain began to fall only after King Bimbisāra realized his oversight and built a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>leaf hut</b></a> for Venerable Subhūti to stay in? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūti</b></a> addressed the "Rain-god" in his inspired verses, welcoming the rain to fall because his mind was already well-concentrated and at peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūti</b></a> used the metaphor of a "well-roofed hut" to signify a mind that is protected from the "winds" of desire and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūti</b></a> declared that he lived without any fear of external threats or internal mental disturbances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the citizens of Rājagaha once gathered at the royal palace to complain about a drought, unaware it was linked to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>meditative practice</b></a> of Venerable Subhūti? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūti</b></a> described his inner state as being "liberated" and living with "ardent energy" even while dwelling in a simple leaf hut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.1/en/sujato">Thag 1.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> was the son of a Brahmin named Samiddhi and hailed from the city of Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato">Thag 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> chose to renounce his home life and ordain as a monk shortly after his wife gave birth to their son? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato">Thag 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> achieved Arahantship by using the "Four Noble Truths" as his primary theme for meditation under the Buddha's guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato">Thag 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the former wife of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> attempted to tempt him back to the lay life by dressing in beautiful ornaments and visiting him with their young child? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato">Thag 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his wife's emotional appeals, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> remained completely unattached and free from any longing for his past family life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato">Thag 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> declared that he had no expectations or desires for future existence in "this world or the next"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato">Thag 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> identified the marks of an enlightened being as someone who is "peaceful," "self-controlled," and possesses wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato">Thag 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> attained a profound insight that allowed him to see the "rising and falling" (sanh diệt) of all things in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato">Thag 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> described the state of a sage as being "untainted by all things," signifying his absolute detachment from worldly phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato">Thag 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the former wife of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Punnamāsa</b></a> eventually realized her efforts were futile and left him alone after seeing his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato"><b>total liberation</b></a> from all household ties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.10/en/sujato">Thag 1.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a> was inspired to go forth after hearing of the great psychic powers of Venerable Mahā Moggallāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato">Thag 1.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through diligent practice of serenity and insight, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a> attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato"><b>six supernormal knowledges</b></a> in a very short time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato">Thag 1.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a> utilized his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato"><b>psychic powers</b></a> and telepathy to perform acts of service and provide benefits to other living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato">Thag 1.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a> described his physical state after reaching high spiritual attainments as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato"><b>light like cotton</b></a> wafted by the wind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato">Thag 1.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to his verse, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a> experienced a sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato"><b>great rapture</b></a> that made his body feel as though it were floating? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.104/en/sujato">Thag 1.104</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Malytavambha</b></a> was motivated to renounce the world after being converted by the teachings of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Bacchabhu</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato">Thag 1.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Malytavambha</b></a> purposely chose to live in places where alms food and requisites were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato"><b>difficult to obtain</b></a> to strengthen his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato">Thag 1.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Malytavambha</b></a> would immediately depart from any location where requisites were easy to find if it did not serve his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato"><b>spiritual goal</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato">Thag 1.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Malytavambha</b></a> stated he had no desire to remain in any place where he felt <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato"><b>discontented</b></a> or where his spiritual purpose was not being met? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato">Thag 1.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Malytavambha</b></a>, a person of wisdom—referred to as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato"><b>person with eyes</b></a>—will only dwell in a place where the spiritual goal is present? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato">Thag 1.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Malytavambha</b></a> would move away from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato"><b>places of pleasure</b></a> if they were not conducive to his higher aim of Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.105/en/sujato">Thag 1.105</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Suhemanta</b></a>, the son of a wealthy Brahmin, traveled from a frontier region to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato"><b>Sankassa</b></a> specifically to hear the Buddha preach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato">Thag 1.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Suhemanta</b></a> became a renowned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato"><b>reciter of the Three Pitakas</b></a> (Tepiṭaka) after going forth in the Buddha's dispensation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato">Thag 1.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after attaining the six supernormal knowledges, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Suhemanta</b></a> dedicated himself to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato"><b>serving the Sangha</b></a> by solving difficult problems for other monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato">Thag 1.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Suhemanta</b></a> taught that while a fool may only see one aspect of a purpose, a wise person can see a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato"><b>hundred parts</b></a> of it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato">Thag 1.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verse of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Suhemanta</b></a> emphasizes the vast difference in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato"><b>analytical wisdom</b></a> between those who are ignorant and those who are enlightened? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato">Thag 1.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Suhemanta</b></a> was recognized for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato"><b>analytical skills</b></a> and his ability to explain the nuances of spiritual goals to the assembly of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.106/en/sujato">Thag 1.106</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammasava</b></a> was born in Magadha and decided to leave the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato"><b>household life</b></a> once he felt his spiritual conditions had fully ripened? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato">Thag 1.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammasava</b></a> specifically sought out the Buddha at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato"><b>South Mountain</b></a> to hear the Dhamma and request ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato">Thag 1.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammasava</b></a> declared that he had attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā) after carefully reflecting on the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato">Thag 1.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his verse of joy, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammasava</b></a> stated he had successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato"><b>completed the task</b></a> set out by the Buddha’s instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato">Thag 1.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammasava</b></a> only went forth into the homeless life after having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato"><b>carefully considered</b></a> and pondered the decision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.107/en/sujato">Thag 1.107</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammasava's Father</b></a> was so moved by his son's youthful renunciation that he decided to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato"><b>follow the example</b></a> and become a monk himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato">Thag 1.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammasava's Father</b></a> did not go forth into the homeless life until he had reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato"><b>age of one hundred and twenty</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato">Thag 1.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his advanced age, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammasava's Father</b></a> attained Arahantship and achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato">Thag 1.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammasava's Father</b></a> proved that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's teaching</b></a> can be successfully practiced and completed even by those who start in very old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato">Thag 1.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammasava's Father</b></a> expressed his realization with the same phrase as his son, noting that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's word</b></a> had been fulfilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato">Thag 1.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammasava's Father</b></a> sought out the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato"><b>Blessed One</b></a> specifically to request ordination after seeing his son's dedication? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.108/en/sujato">Thag 1.108</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka and Venerable Bharata were brothers born into a merchant family in the ancient city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>Campā</b></a> before they both chose to renounce the world and join the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka was inspired to go forth into the homeless life after hearing a sermon by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a>, a man known for his delicate upbringing who nonetheless successfully practiced the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while his brother attained spiritual goals quickly, Venerable Nandaka initially struggled to master his mind because it was frequently occupied by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome thoughts</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bharata achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>six supernormal powers</b></a> (six abhiññās) immediately after his ordination, whereas his brother Nandaka required more time and guidance to reach the same level of attainment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that to support his brother’s spiritual progress, Venerable Bharata acted as a mentor and had Venerable Nandaka serve as his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>attendant</b></a> so they could practice together constantly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bharata provided crucial instructions on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> (vipassanā) to his brother while they were resting by the side of the road during their travels? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the turning point in Venerable Nandaka’s practice occurred when he observed a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>caravan bull</b></a> that had collapsed in deep mud but was eventually able to pull its heavy load once it was cared for? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka watched as a collapsed bull was unyoked, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>fed, and watered</b></a>, gaining the renewed strength necessary to haul its wagon out of a difficult mire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka used the image of the rejuvenated bull as a meditation subject, resolving that he too must pull himself out of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>mire of rebirth</b></a> through his own effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka compared a diligent monk to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>noble thoroughbred</b></a> that, even after falling, stands back up and refuses to abandon its heavy burden of spiritual duty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that by applying the lesson of the bull to his own mind, Venerable Nandaka successfully attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> and declared his final knowledge to his brother? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka described himself as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>legitimate son</b></a> of the Buddha, born of the Dhamma, after he had thoroughly purified his mind through practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka emphasized that a true disciple of the Buddha is one who has become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>complete in right view</b></a> (sammādiṭṭhi) and is well-trained in the path to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bharata invited his brother to go before the Buddha so they could both utter their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.28/en/sujato"><b>lion's roar</b></a>, a bold declaration of their spiritual freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.28/en/sujato">Thag 2.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bharata referred to the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.28/en/sujato"><b>supreme Instructor</b></a> whose guidance had allowed them both to reach the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.28/en/sujato">Thag 2.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bharata expressed deep gratitude toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.28/en/sujato"><b>compassionate sage</b></a> who had encouraged them to leave the household life for the sake of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.28/en/sujato">Thag 2.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bharata joyfully declared that the very <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.28/en/sujato"><b>purpose of their renunciation</b></a> had been completely realized through their attainment of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.28/en/sujato">Thag 2.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bharata confirmed that their spiritual work was finished because all of their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.28/en/sujato"><b>fetters were destroyed</b></a>, leaving nothing more to be done for their liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.28/en/sujato">Thag 2.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the story of Venerable Nandaka and Venerable Bharata highlights the power of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>spiritual friendship</b></a> between brothers who supported each other until both attained the final goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka identified as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>disciple of the Awakened One</b></a>, having mastered his faculties just as a well-bred animal is trained for service? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after achieving Arahantship, Venerable Nandaka used verses of poetry to declare his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato"><b>final knowledge</b></a>, comparing his inner strength to that of a powerful bull? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.27/en/sujato">Thag 2.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bharata urged his brother to stand with him before the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.28/en/sujato"><b>highest of Buddhas</b></a> to report on the success of their spiritual journey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.28/en/sujato">Thag 2.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> was the youngest brother of the famous Emperor Dhammāsoka and chose to renounce his royal status as vice-regent to live as a forest-dwelling monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> was moved to seek the ascetic life after witnessing the serene presence and supernormal powers of the Elder Mahā Dhammarakkhita during a royal hunting expedition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> described the ultimate "great happiness" as living completely alone in the forest, with no one else either in front of him or behind him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> specifically sought out the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Sītavana forest</b></a>, a place known for its cool mountain caves and refreshing waters, as the ideal setting for his solitary meditation practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> made a firm vow to "gird his armor" of spiritual energy and not leave the depths of the forest until he had achieved the total destruction of all mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> found delight in the wilderness, comparing the forest to a place where wild elephants roam freely, which he found most conducive to the joy of a meditator? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> practiced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>walking meditation</b></a> (caṅkama) alone by the cool streams of mountain caves to maintain his mindfulness and attain spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> aspired to sit upon a mountain peak, cooled by fragrant breezes, for the purpose of "shattering ignorance" (avijjā) through deep concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> compared the fulfillment of his spiritual goal to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>full moon</b></a> on the fifteenth day, indicating that his task was complete and all his taints were eradicated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> lived during a significant era, exactly 218 years after the Parinibbāna of the Buddha, when his brother King Dhammāsoka unified India under one empire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> experienced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>bliss of liberation</b></a> while sitting on a "carpet" of fallen forest flowers within a mountain cave, finding a joy that surpassed any palace luxury? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> remained steadfast in his decision to become a monk even when King Dhammāsoka personally tried to persuade him to stay in the royal court? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> used the phrase "living as one, not as two" to emphasize the profound <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>spiritual independence</b></a> required to reach the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> was specifically named "Ekavihāriya" because of his dedication to the practice of solitary dwelling in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> declared that because he had attained the end of all mental taints, he would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>never be reborn</b></a> into any state of existence again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> believed that through the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>solitary meditation</b></a> in the beautiful forest, he would achieve a state where no one could ever cause him harm or disturbance again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> viewed the forest as an environment that had been highly praised by the Buddha himself for those who wish to live with energetic striving and persistence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> sought to live a life governed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> and the accomplishment of his own spiritual potential, free from the influence and control of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> found the "fragrance of the forest" combined with the cool mountain air to be a profound support for his final breakthrough to enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ekavihāriya</b></a> successfully completed his spiritual journey, attaining the state of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato"><b>taintless arahant</b></a> through his unwavering commitment to the life of a forest recluse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.2/en/sujato">Thag 10.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> was born into a prominent Brahmin family and was miraculously saved from his mother’s funeral pyre after she passed away during childbirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the commentary on the verses of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a>, a person in their final birth cannot die before attaining Arahantship, even if they were to fall from the heights of Mount Sineru? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>young age</b></a> of seven, Venerable Saṅkicca felt a deep sense of spiritual urgency and decided to ordain as a monk after learning that his mother had died giving birth to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> attained the highest stage of enlightenment, Arahantship, at the very moment his hair was being shaved for his ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> was a direct disciple of the Buddha’s chief disciple, Venerable Sāriputta, who performed his ordination ceremony? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> once demonstrated extraordinary compassion by offering his own life to a band of robbers to protect a group of three thousand fellow monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a lay follower questioned why he chose to live in the deep forest during the monsoon season, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> replied by praising the beauty of remote mountain caves for the practice of meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> compared the movement of his meditative thoughts in the solitude of the forest to the way the monsoon wind spreads clouds across the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that seeing a crow in a cemetery, which had been born from an egg and made its home among the dead, served as a powerful meditation object for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> to develop detachment from the physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> taught that a monk lives in true peace when he does not need to guard others and is not guarded by others, remaining free from all expectations for sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> found the most suitable environment for his practice to be among clear waters, rocks, and wild animals like monkeys and timid deer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> explicitly stated his preference for living in secluded mountain caves and forest haunts frequented by wild beasts rather than staying in populated areas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> maintained a mind free from "un-Ariyan" thoughts, which he described as intentions to harm, kill, or cause suffering to other sentient beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> declared he had completed all the tasks set by the Buddha and had successfully laid down the heavy burden of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that by uprooting the very <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>source of existence</b></a>, Venerable Saṅkicca ensured he would no longer be subject to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> successfully achieved the goal for which he left the household life by completely destroying all mental fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> possessed such total equanimity that he neither delighted in the prospect of death nor clung to the continuation of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> likened his wait for the end of his life to a worker who, having finished his job, simply waits for his wages or the appointed time to depart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> emphasized that a monk should remain both alert and mindful while waiting for the natural conclusion of his lifespan? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the term "un-Ariyan" was used by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> to describe thoughts rooted in anger and ill will toward other living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> found spiritual delight in the "secluded residence" of the forest, where the absence of people allowed for deep concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the story of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkicca</b></a> is preserved in the Dhammapāla commentary as an example of a monk who offered his life for the benefit of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag11.1/en/sujato">Thag 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kunda Dhāna</b></a> was born into a Brahmin family in Sāvatthī and had mastered the three Vedas before he heard the Buddha's teaching and chose to ordain later in life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato">Thag 1.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Pasenadi of Kosala held <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kunda Dhāna</b></a> in such high regard that he provided him with all necessary requisites, sparing him the need to go on daily alms rounds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato">Thag 1.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kunda Dhāna</b></a> demonstrated his spiritual powers and attainment of fruit when Subhaddā invited the Buddha and the community of monks for a meal, an event recorded in the Anguttara Nikāya commentary? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato">Thag 1.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kunda Dhāna</b></a> taught that a monk who has successfully "crossed the flood" of existence is one who has severed five lower fetters and abandoned five higher fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato">Thag 1.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kunda Dhāna</b></a> urged practitioners to cultivate five "higher" faculties, such as faith and energy, to reach the ultimate spiritual goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato">Thag 1.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kunda Dhāna</b></a> describes a liberated monk as one who has overcome five specific attachments, thereby earning the title of one who has "crossed the flood"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato">Thag 1.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his advanced age at the time of ordination, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kunda Dhāna</b></a> was able to achieve enlightenment through diligent effort and the Buddha's guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato">Thag 1.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verse of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kunda Dhāna</b></a> emphasizes a structured path of fivefold spiritual development to transcend the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.15/en/sujato">Thag 1.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Belaṭṭhasīsa</b></a> was once a fire-worshiper in the group of Kassapa of Uruvelā before encountering the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato">Thag 1.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Belaṭṭhasīsa</b></a> achieved Arahantship, the highest stage of enlightenment, upon hearing the Buddha preach the <i>Ādittapariyāya Sutta</i>, also known as the Fire Sermon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato">Thag 1.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Belaṭṭhasīsa</b></a> served as the preceptor and instructor for Venerable Ānanda, the Buddha’s faithful attendant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato">Thag 1.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Belaṭṭhasīsa</b></a> compared his tireless dedication to the spiritual life to a well-bred ox with horns that pulls a plow without feeling fatigue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato">Thag 1.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Belaṭṭhasīsa</b></a> declared he could walk and stand day and night without weariness because he was sustained by unworldly bliss? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato">Thag 1.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Belaṭṭhasīsa</b></a> was one of the thousand former matted-hair ascetics who attained liberation together under the Buddha's guidance at Uruvelā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato">Thag 1.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Belaṭṭhasīsa</b></a> used the metaphor of a "good breed" of draft animal to illustrate the strength and endurance required in the monastic training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato">Thag 1.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Belaṭṭhasīsa</b></a>, reflecting on his early practice and current pure attainment, expressed his joy through a verse that celebrates a life free from worldly burdens? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.16/en/sujato">Thag 1.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dāsaka</b></a> was born as a slave in the household of Anāthapiṇḍika and was tasked with guarding the gate at the Jetavana Monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato">Thag 1.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dāsaka</b></a> was granted his freedom from slavery by Anāthapiṇḍika after the merchant recognized his virtuous qualities and desire to become a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato">Thag 1.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his ordination, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dāsaka</b></a> initially struggled with sloth and torpor, often overeating and sleeping through the Buddha's Dhamma talks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato">Thag 1.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally admonished <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dāsaka</b></a> by comparing a lazy, overfed monk to a "great hog" that rolls around in sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato">Thag 1.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dāsaka</b></a> that those who succumb to drowsiness and excessive eating are "fools" who will continue to "enter the womb" for repeated rebirths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato">Thag 1.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's sharp comparison to a "great hog" caused <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dāsaka</b></a> to feel a profound sense of spiritual urgency, which motivated him to develop insight and attain Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato">Thag 1.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dāsaka</b></a> later repeated the same verse of admonition spoken by the Buddha as a way to declare his own final knowledge and victory over suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato">Thag 1.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the spiritual transformation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dāsaka</b></a> serves as a testament to the Buddha's skill in using direct, even harsh, metaphors to guide a student toward enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.17/en/sujato">Thag 1.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkharakkhita</b></a>, who was born into a wealthy family in Sāvatthī, was inspired to seek liberation after observing a mother deer in a forest thicket whose love for her fawn prevented her from finding food and water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato">Thag 1.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkharakkhita</b></a> realized that the entire world is bound by the fetters of craving (<i>taṇhā</i>) and lives in suffering because it cannot sever the ties of attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato">Thag 1.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkharakkhita</b></a> attained the state of an Arahant by using his observations of a starving mother deer as a primary subject for his insight meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato">Thag 1.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkharakkhita</b></a> composed a verse to admonish a fellow monk who was struggling with wrong thoughts, comparing a monk who ignores the Teacher's instructions to a weak, young deer lost in the woods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato">Thag 1.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkharakkhita</b></a>, living in solitude is insufficient for spiritual progress if one does not diligently attend to the Buddha's teachings regarding the "Supreme Happiness" of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato">Thag 1.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkharakkhita</b></a> used the metaphor of a "weak deer" to describe a practitioner whose sense faculties remain unrefined and "raw" because they have neglected the path of the Master? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato">Thag 1.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the spiritual exhortation given by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkharakkhita</b></a> was so effective that his companion monk was deeply stirred, eventually developing enough insight to also achieve Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato">Thag 1.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Saṅkharakkhita</b></a> identified the inability to cut through the "tangle of craving" as the reason the world remains trapped in a cycle of pain and thirst? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.109/en/sujato">Thag 1.109</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷagavaccha</b></a>, a former Brahmin from Kosambī, chose to remain neutral and loyal to the Buddha’s word while the rest of the monastic community in Kosambī was embroiled in bitter disputes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato">Thag 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷagavaccha</b></a> viewed the quarrels of his fellow monks as a significant cause for spiritual decline and the loss of progress in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato">Thag 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷagavaccha</b></a> attained Arahantship by avoiding partisan factions and focusing exclusively on the wisdom and instructions declared by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato">Thag 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷagavaccha</b></a> taught that a monk who finds constant joy in the Dhamma will eventually reach the peaceful state, which is characterized by the total stilling of conditioned formations (<i>saṅkhārupasama</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato">Thag 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷagavaccha</b></a> expressed his enlightenment through a verse emphasizing that "stilling" and "tranquility" are the results of a practitioner's dedication to the Buddha's path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato">Thag 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷagavaccha</b></a> believed that loyalty to the Dhamma, rather than siding with any particular group, is what leads a monk to reach the "unconditioned" or the "state of peace"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato">Thag 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷagavaccha</b></a> described the experience of the Dhamma as being filled with "profound joy", which serves as a foundation for reaching the highest peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato">Thag 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a>, a man from a wealthy family in Kosala, first developed faith in the Buddha's teaching during the historic occasion when the Master accepted the donation of the Jetavana Monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato">Thag 1.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> lived as a forest-dweller in a cave at the foot of a mountain, where he spent his time in intensive meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato">Thag 1.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> found spiritual inspiration during the rainy season when he saw the withered trees and reeds transformed into lush, green foliage by the falling rain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato">Thag 1.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> practiced a form of contemplation where he reasoned that if mindless plants could flourish in favorable weather, he—endowed with consciousness—should be able to grow in virtuous qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato">Thag 1.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> compared the spiritual growth of a practitioner to trees reaching high toward the sky after being refreshed by moisture from drifting clouds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato">Thag 1.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> emphasized that a deep "delight in seclusion" (<i>viveka</i>) is a necessary condition for a monk to cultivate a "multitude of good things" in his heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato">Thag 1.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> attained Arahantship and declared his "Final Knowledge" through a verse that highlighted the beauty of the forest and mountains as catalysts for his internal growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato">Thag 1.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> used his "energy and determination" during the most favorable seasons to ensure his spiritual qualities grew as vigorously as the forest during the rains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.110/en/sujato">Thag 1.110</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷagavaccha</b></a> demonstrated that true "joy" in the Buddha's Dhamma is the antidote to the social decay and loss of spiritual direction caused by monastic infighting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.11/en/sujato">Thag 1.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Bhāradvāja</b></a>, born into a Brahmin family in Rājagaha, ordained after hearing the Buddha preach at the Bamboo Grove and eventually attained the state of an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato">Thag 2.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Bhāradvāja</b></a> compared the roar of wisdom to the sound of lions residing in mountain caves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato">Thag 2.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Bhāradvāja</b></a> described a true hero as one who has achieved victory in the battle against Mara and his army? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato">Thag 2.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Bhāradvāja</b></a> expressed great joy and happiness upon seeing his own son, Kanhadinna, attain the taintless state of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato">Thag 2.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Bhāradvāja</b></a> affirmed his successful completion of the spiritual life by declaring his devoted service to the Teacher and his reverence for the Dhamma and the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato">Thag 2.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Bhāradvāja</b></a> was inspired to declare his final knowledge when his son, already an Arahant, asked him if he had reached the goal of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato"><b>spiritual life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato">Thag 2.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Bhāradvāja</b></a> used the metaphor of a lion's roar to signify the power and fearlessness of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> he gained through enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato">Thag 2.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Bhāradvāja</b></a> lived to see his son Kanhadinna become a disciple of the Buddha first, which eventually led to his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato"><b>ordination</b></a> and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.29/en/sujato">Thag 2.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Valliya</b></a> used the metaphor of a monkey entering a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>five-doored cottage</b></a> to describe how the mind wanders restlessly through the senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato">Thag 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Valliya</b></a> commanded his own mind to stop its restless wandering, declaring that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> now held it firmly in check? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato">Thag 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Valliya</b></a>, after becoming an Arahant, noted that his mind could no longer "go far" as it did before because it was now restrained by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>spiritual insight</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato">Thag 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Valliya</b></a> was born in Sāvatthī and made it a priority to associate with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>virtuous friends</b></a> once he reached adulthood and mastered his senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato">Thag 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Valliya</b></a> attained liberation from worldly desires and attachments through the development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> under the guidance of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato">Thag 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Valliya</b></a> viewed his past life of worldly cravings as something he had successfully escaped through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>Noble Path</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato">Thag 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Valliya</b></a> emphasizes that once the mind is captured by wisdom, it no longer has the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato"><b>freedom</b></a> to roam into unwholesome states as it did in the past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.3/en/sujato">Thag 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kanhadinna</b></a>, the son of Bhāradvāja, entered the monkhood under the guidance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> after hearing the Dhamma in Takkasilā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato">Thag 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kanhadinna</b></a> declared that by serving true persons and constantly listening to the True Dhamma, he was able to walk upon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>path to the Deathless</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato">Thag 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kanhadinna</b></a> described his state of liberation as one where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>craving for existence</b></a> (bhavataṇhā) has been completely cut off? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato">Thag 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kanhadinna</b></a> proclaimed that for an enlightened being, there is no attachment or "existence" regarding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>past</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato">Thag 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kanhadinna</b></a> stated that in his liberated mind, there is no concept of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>future</b></a> that he clings to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato">Thag 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kanhadinna</b></a> taught that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>present</b></a> does not exist as a point of attachment for one who has fully realized the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato">Thag 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kanhadinna</b></a> successfully attained the fruit of Arahantship through the development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>insight</b></a> (vipassanā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato">Thag 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kanhadinna</b></a> achieved his goal of liberation and then returned to Rājagaha, where he had the joy of seeing his father, Bhāradvāja, also sitting as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato"><b>enlightened disciple</b></a> of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.30/en/sujato">Thag 2.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> was originally a king in the border region at a city named Kukkuta before he renounced the world to become a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> was so dedicated to learning that he sent messengers out of the four city gates every morning specifically to find and bring scholars to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> rewarded merchants from Sāvatthī with one hundred thousand gold coins just for the joy of hearing the word "Buddha" for the first time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing about the Triple Gem, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> and his ministers renounced their royal lives and set out to find the Buddha, eventually reaching the banks of the Ganges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> and his followers crossed the Ganges and the Candabhāgā rivers on horseback without the horses' hooves getting wet by making an act of truth regarding the Buddha's supremacy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his divine eye to see <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> approaching and traveled through the air to meet him at the bank of the Candabhāgā River? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> and his entire retinue achieved Arahantship immediately after hearing their first sermon from the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> received his ordination through the miraculous "Ehi Bhikkhu" (Come, monk) pronouncement from the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his very first sermon to the community of monks, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> helped more than one thousand monks achieve the state of Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha officially designated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> as the foremost among the monks who are preachers or exhorters of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> taught that a person with foresight who can see things before they happen will not be found with flaws, even under close observation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> emphasized the importance of distinguishing between what is beneficial and what is harmful, as well as between friends and foes, as a mark of true understanding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> compared a person who has perfected the mindfulness of breathing (ānāpānasati) to the moon that shines brightly when it emerges from behind the clouds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a>, the mindfulness of breathing, when fully developed and practiced as taught by the Buddha, illuminates this world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> described his own mind as being "exceedingly white" (pure), boundless, and thoroughly well-developed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> declared that a mind that is thoroughly restrained and clear can illuminate every direction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> taught that a wise person truly "lives" even if their material wealth is exhausted, whereas a wealthy person without wisdom does not truly live at all? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> viewed wisdom as the essential tool for discerning the truth of what one hears and for increasing one's reputation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> stated that a person endowed with wisdom is able to find happiness and joy even in the midst of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> observed that death is an inescapable and ordinary law for everything that is born, noting that there is nothing extraordinary about this process? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> taught that birth and death follow one another without interruption, and this constant cycle is the nature of all sentient beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> argued that weeping for the dead is entirely useless because it provides no benefit to the deceased and brings no purity to the living? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> pointed out that recluses and Brahmins do not praise the act of mourning or lamenting for those who have passed away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> warned that excessive weeping actually harms one's physical body, ruins one's eyesight, and degrades one's complexion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a>, lamentation for the dead is harmful because it drains a person's physical strength and dulls their wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> taught that if one maintains a joyful and happy mind, they will find that all directions around them become pleasant and joyful as well? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> advised that there is no lasting peace to be found simply by searching for external happiness through mourning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> believed that families should seek to surround themselves with those who are wise and have heard much Dhamma (the learned)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> compared wise and learned people to a sturdy boat that is capable of crossing a river overflowing with water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> taught that it is through the power of wisdom that individuals are able to successfully fulfill their duties and navigate the challenges of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> once delivered a specific sermon to the community of nuns, emphasizing the inevitability of death and the value of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally invited <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> to preach to the assembly, recognizing his profound ability to explain the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> taught that what is beneficial for the living is often of no use to those who have already died? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a>'s name was given to him because he was born into the family of a king in Kukkuta and inherited the throne before his spiritual quest began? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kappina</b></a> once demonstrated the power of faith by stating that if there is a Supreme Buddha in the world, then the river they were crossing would not even wet their horses' hooves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.3/en/sujato">Thag 10.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat, the son of King Bimbisāra, taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> is the mother of all good qualities and should be thoroughly purified as it stands at the head of all wholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> as a supreme fragrance and an ointment that, unlike worldly scents, has the power to spread in all four directions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> serves as the highest provision and the supreme travel gear for a person journeying through the rounds of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat taught that a person established in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> gains many good friends through self-restraint, while those who are immoral lose their friends by performing evil deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat, after surviving assassination attempts by his brother King Ajātasattu, taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> is the landing place of the Buddhas and a means to brighten one's mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat declared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> to be a peerless strength and the supreme weapon for a practitioner to utilize in their spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sīlavat, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> is the first and foremost ornament, as well as a rare type of armor that protects the practitioner from spiritual harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> is a bridge of great strength that allows a person to cross over the difficulties and dangers of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat pointed out that while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> is supreme in this world, wisdom is the ultimate, and one who possesses both is victorious among both gods and humans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat stated that a person who lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> receives a bad reputation and lacks wealth, whereas a virtuous person is always praised and attains a good name? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat taught that an immoral person who does not practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> suffers from anxiety in all places and faces a miserable destination after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat explained that the wise should protect their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> if they desire three specific types of happiness: fame, wealth, and the joy of heaven after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> as the best vehicle for transportation, as it enables a person to travel successfully and safely in every direction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat, an Arahant and former prince of Rājagaha, taught that well-learned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> is the foundation that brings about every kind of success in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat taught that a wise person who is well-established in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> finds joy in all places and eventually attains liberation from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat successfully converted the very assassins his brother Ajātasattu sent to kill him by teaching them the profound benefits of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat encouraged all people to keep their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> pure because it acts as a necessary restraint and defense that leads one toward the enlightenment of the Buddhas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat was originally a prince who renounced his royal life to become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>monk</b></a> after the Buddha sent Venerable Mahā Moggallāna to invite him to the Order for his own safety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> of a wise person who is well-composed leads to praise in this life and the attainment of liberation in the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīlavat emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> is the most important thing to be studied in this world, as it is the primary factor leading to all forms of achievement and prosperity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.1/en/sujato">Thag 12.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Singālapitar</b></a> was born into a wealthy family in Sāvatthī and received his name, which means "Singāla’s father," because he had a son named Singālaka? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato">Thag 1.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Singālapitar</b></a> went forth into homelessness, the Buddha specifically assigned him the human skeleton as his subject of meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato">Thag 1.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while practicing his meditation, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Singālapitar</b></a> lived in the Bhesakalā Forest at Sumsumāragira among the Sakyan people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato">Thag 1.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato"><b>forest deity</b></a> who knew Venerable Singālapitar was close to enlightenment recited a verse to encourage him to complete his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato">Thag 1.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Singālapitar</b></a> is described as an "heir to the Buddha’s teaching" who lived alone in the forest to master his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato">Thag 1.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his diligent meditation, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Singālapitar</b></a> was able to spread the "perception of a skeleton" throughout his entire body to overcome worldly attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato">Thag 1.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the forest deity predicted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Singālapitar</b></a> would quickly and completely abandon all sensual desires through his focused practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato">Thag 1.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after attaining Arahantship, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Singālapitar</b></a> repeated the verse of the forest deity as a declaration of his own final knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato">Thag 1.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the meditation on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato"><b>human skeleton</b></a> was the specific technique that led Venerable Singālapitar to achieve the highest stage of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.18/en/sujato">Thag 1.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kula</b></a> was born into a Brahmin family but initially struggled with an unstable and unbalanced mind after ordaining? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato">Thag 1.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kula</b></a> gained deep spiritual insight by observing how irrigators lead water through channels to where it is needed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato">Thag 1.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sight of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato"><b>fletchers</b></a> (arrow-makers) straightening their arrows with heat and skill served as a metaphor for Venerable Kula to straighten his own mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato">Thag 1.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kula</b></a> watched carriage makers and carpenters shaping wood for wheels and axles to understand how he could shape his own character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato">Thag 1.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after returning from an alms round with a full bowl, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kula</b></a> spent his midday rest reflecting on the techniques of craftsmen to tame his own spirit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato">Thag 1.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kula</b></a> achieved Arahantship by applying the lessons of three worldly crafts—irrigation, fletching, and carpentry—to his mental training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato">Thag 1.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the central theme of the verse by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kula</b></a> is that while others tame external elements like water or wood, the wise focus on taming themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato">Thag 1.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kula</b></a> used these "three methods of subduing" as personal encouragement to focus his mind on a single meditation subject? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato">Thag 1.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kula</b></a> declared his final enlightenment by noting that "the self-tamed tame themselves," just as craftsmen master their materials? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.19/en/sujato">Thag 1.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita</b></a> was a master of the three Vedas and a highly accomplished Brahmin in Sāvatthī before he ever met the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato">Thag 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita</b></a> to be the foremost among all disciples in the mastery of "analytical insight" (paṭisambhidā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato">Thag 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita</b></a> used the vivid metaphor of "leaves being blown away by the wind" to describe how a practitioner can discard all evil qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato">Thag 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita</b></a> was known for his habit of frequently questioning the Buddha and other senior Elders to deepen his understanding of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato">Thag 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his great learning, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita</b></a> emphasized that a peaceful mind—one that is "calm and quiet"—is essential for reciting words of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato">Thag 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita</b></a> attained Arahantship almost immediately after going forth, gaining complete mastery over the meaning and the law of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato">Thag 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically praised the realizations of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita</b></a> that were recorded in the Vedalla Sutta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato">Thag 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita</b></a> taught that a mind that is "not agitated" is the foundation for successfully casting off all unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato">Thag 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita</b></a> came from a very wealthy Brahmin background in the city of Sāvatthī before dedicating his life to the pursuit of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.2/en/sujato">Thag 1.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jenta</b></a>, a son of a local king in Magadha, was born in a village that shared his name before he felt the karmic urge to renounce the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato">Thag 1.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jenta</b></a> achieved Arahantship through diligent practice and finding joy in insight meditation (vipassanā) after hearing the Buddha’s teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato">Thag 1.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jenta</b></a> reflected on how difficult it is to live as a monk, but also noted that the life of a householder is equally challenging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato">Thag 1.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jenta</b></a> described the True Dhamma (Saddhamma) as being "profound" and observed that spiritual "wealth" is something very difficult to obtain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato">Thag 1.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jenta</b></a> found the choice between different ways of living to be extremely difficult when the mind is constantly reflecting on the nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato"><b>impermanence</b></a> (anicca)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato">Thag 1.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jenta</b></a> expressed great excitement when reflecting on his spiritual attainments, remembering the many doubts he had to overcome during his journey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato">Thag 1.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jenta</b></a> emphasized that once the mind is set on the Dhamma, the struggle of deciding between a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato"><b>monastic life</b></a> and a lay life becomes a central spiritual challenge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.111/en/sujato">Thag 1.111</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vacchagotta</b></a> was born into a wealthy Brahmin family in Rājagaha and was distinguished as one of four Elders who shared the name Vaccha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato">Thag 1.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vacchagotta</b></a> became a wanderer (paribbājaka) because he could not find the "heartwood" or spiritual essence within the traditional Brahminical scriptures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato">Thag 1.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vacchagotta</b></a> attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato"><b>Six Direct Knowledges</b></a> (chaḷabhiññā) after finding satisfaction in the answers provided to him by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato">Thag 1.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vacchagotta</b></a> joyfully declared that he had attained the "Three Knowledges" (tevijjā) and reached the ultimate goal of the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato">Thag 1.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vacchagotta</b></a> achieved a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato"><b>great concentration</b></a> (samādhi) and became highly skilled in mental tranquility as part of his liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato">Thag 1.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vacchagotta</b></a> felt he had completely fulfilled the Buddha's instructions and finished all that was to be done for his enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato">Thag 1.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vacchagotta</b></a> celebrated his new life and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato"><b>spiritual fruits</b></a> he attained through his verse, signifying his complete satisfaction with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.112/en/sujato">Thag 1.112</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vanavaccha</b></a> first gained faith in the Buddha's teaching during the historical encounter between the Teacher and King Bimbisāra of Magadha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato">Thag 1.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vanavaccha</b></a> literally means "Forest Vaccha," a title he earned due to his deep preference for living in secluded forest environments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato">Thag 1.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vanavaccha</b></a> lived in a small, humble dwelling when he visited his relatives in Rājagaha, specifically to share his way of life with them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato">Thag 1.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vanavaccha</b></a> delighted in the natural beauty of the mountains, especially the rocky areas where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato"><b>clear spring water</b></a> flows freely? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato">Thag 1.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vanavaccha</b></a> found great joy in being surrounded by forest animals such as monkeys and deer that lived near his mountain retreat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato">Thag 1.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vanavaccha</b></a> described his favorite scenery as mountains covered with trees and greenery, where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> of the wilderness supported his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato">Thag 1.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the poem composed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vanavaccha</b></a> about the crags and streams is considered a declaration of his final knowledge and enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.113/en/sujato">Thag 1.113</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Adhimutta</b></a> was born in Sāvatthī and initially felt that the Brahmin teachings he grew up with lacked any fundamental or substantial basis? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato">Thag 1.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Adhimutta</b></a> was inspired to ordain after witnessing the majestic deportment of the Buddha during the dedication ceremony of the Jetavana Monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato">Thag 1.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Adhimutta</b></a> attained Arahantship very shortly after his ordination and spent time instructing other monks on the importance of renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato">Thag 1.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Adhimutta</b></a> specifically exhorted monks who were physically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato"><b>burly and strong</b></a> to avoid being weighed down by physical indulgence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato">Thag 1.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Adhimutta</b></a> questioned how anyone could achieve the true qualities of a recluse (samaṇa) if they remained attached to the comforts and pleasures of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato">Thag 1.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Adhimutta</b></a> taught that the path of a seeker must be focused on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato"><b>attainment of renunciation</b></a>, rather than being distracted by the coarse nature of the physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.114/en/sujato">Thag 1.114</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> was born into a Brahmin family in Kosala and was named after the auspicious star under which he was born? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his conversion, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> practiced a "skull-spell" where he could determine a deceased person's place of rebirth by simply tapping on their skull with his fingernail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> claimed to have the supernatural power to declare the destination of those who had been dead for as long as three years using his ritualistic Brahminical mantras? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> traveled to Sāvatthī specifically to challenge the Buddha’s authority by boasting about his ability to see the rebirth of the dead? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha tested the skills of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> by presenting him with the skull of a monk who had attained Nibbāna and asking him to identify that person's next life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his extensive knowledge of spells, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> was unable to find any destination for the Arahant’s skull, which caused him to stand in shamed silence while dripping with sweat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> admitted his exhaustion and failure, the Buddha revealed that He knew the "destination" was actually the cessation of rebirth, as the monk had reached Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> was so intrigued by the Buddha's superior knowledge that he initially asked for the "secret mantra" that allowed the Buddha to know where the Arahant had gone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha informed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> that he would have to go forth and ordain as a monk if he truly wished to learn the "secret" of ending birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after ordaining and being instructed in meditation, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> eventually attained the fruit of Arahantship and declared that he had successfully surpassed the sensual realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> described his liberation as "unshakeable" and confirmed that through his practice, every single one of his fetters had been completely destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> attributed his final liberation to the "Brahmin's observation," a term reflecting the profound insight and meditation that led to his awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sivaka</b></a> was born into a Brahmin family in the capital city of Rājagaha and received a comprehensive education in various subjects before becoming a wanderer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato">Thag 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sivaka</b></a> was inspired to ordain as a Buddhist monk after having the opportunity to listen to the Buddha's Dhamma, which sparked deep faith in his heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato">Thag 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sivaka</b></a> used the poetic metaphor of "houses" being built again and again to describe the repetitive and wearying nature of rebirth in the cycle of saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato">Thag 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sivaka</b></a> taught that the search for the "house-builder"—representing craving and ignorance—is driven by the realization that repeated birth is inherently suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato">Thag 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon attaining Arahantship, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sivaka</b></a> addressed the "house-builder" directly, declaring that the builder had been discovered and would never build a house of rebirth for him again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato">Thag 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sivaka</b></a> described the destruction of his defilements by saying his "rafters" (walls) were broken and his "ridgepole" (roof) was shattered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato">Thag 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sivaka</b></a> proclaimed his mind had been "pushed away from the stream," signifying his total liberation from the currents of worldly existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato">Thag 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the spiritual breakthrough of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sivaka</b></a> is described as his mind being "blown to pieces" here, which refers to the complete disintegration of craving and conditioned existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato">Thag 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> initially only sought ordination as a means to acquire a more powerful "secret," but he ended up finding the ultimate peace of Nibbāna instead? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sivaka</b></a> attained the highest goal of the holy life by following his natural spiritual inclination and carefully listening to the teachings of the Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato">Thag 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the realization of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a> demonstrates that even the most advanced mundane supernatural powers are inferior to the Buddha's knowledge of the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sivaka</b></a>, after becoming an Arahant, celebrated the fact that the "walls" of his house of existence had collapsed, meaning he would never again be bound by the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.32/en/sujato">Thag 2.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Migasīra</b></a>, once a famous wanderer revered for his skull-divination, eventually found that the only thing worth knowing was the path to the end of all fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.31/en/sujato">Thag 2.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> was once expelled from the monastery by his own brother because he was considered slow and dim-witted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> stood grieving at the gate of the monastery after being turned away, the Buddha personally came to him and stroked his head in comfort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a simple <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>foot-wiping cloth</b></a> as a meditation object to help Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka focus his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> achieved the "supreme goal" of Arahantship by practicing meditation on a clean object as instructed by the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his enlightenment, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> attained the "three true knowledges" (tevijjā) and the "divine eye," allowing him to see beyond the physical realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> possessed the psychic power to manifest himself in a thousand different forms simultaneously within a mango grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha sent a messenger to summon him, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> traveled through the air to meet the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> is described as a "field of merit" for humanity, meaning that offerings made to him bring great spiritual fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his initial struggles with learning, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> eventually declared that he had "completed the Buddha's bidding" through his spiritual success? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> specifically attributed his success to the compassion of the Buddha, who took him by the arm and led him back into the monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> gained the ability to remember his own past lives after following the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explicitly told <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> to settle his mind on a "clean object" to overcome his mental dullness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> sat in the presence of the Buddha with a fully concentrated mind after arriving through the air to pay homage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his transformation, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> was so disheartened by his brother's rejection that he stood longing for the Buddha's words at the monastery gate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cūḷa-Panthaka</b></a> described the Buddha as the "one who receives offerings from the whole world"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.4/en/sujato">Thag 10.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a> was a king in the kingdom of Magadha who had been deeply immersed in sensual pleasures before meeting the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha traveled through the air specifically to save <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a>, recognizing his potential to attain Arahantship through the meditation on foulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a> used the metaphor of a "stagnant pool" and a "large wound" to describe the inherent impurity of the human body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a>, the body is a vessel filled with blood and pus, constantly leaking various impurities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a> described the body as being bound by sixty tendons and plastered with flesh to give it shape? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a> compared the skin of the body to an "armor" that covers a foul-smelling and useless interior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a> taught that ordinary people who follow their desires are essentially walking toward the "King of Death" to be thrown away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a> warned that the body is shrouded by ignorance and caught in the "net of latent tendencies" (anusaya)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a> described the cycle of rebirth as being driven by the "chariot of kamma," leading to both success and failure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a> characterized a "foolish worldling" as someone who mistakenly clings to the body with the thought "this is mine"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a> advised wise practitioners to avoid the physical body as one would avoid a "snake smeared with dung"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a> attained Nibbāna by abandoning the root of existence and becoming "taintless" (anāsava)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a> spoke of the body as being "fettered by the five hindrances" and obsessed by craving and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon hearing the Buddha's teaching on the foulness of the body, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a> felt a sense of "dread and revulsion" that led him to seek immediate ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kappa</b></a> successfully attained Arahantship after being assigned ten subjects for meditation by his preceptor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.5/en/sujato">Thag 10.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> was born into a lowly family and worked as a humble street-sweeper, often struggling with hunger because he did not earn enough to eat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while scanning the world with compassion during the first watch of the night, saw that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> possessed the potential for Arahantship, shining like a lamp inside a jar? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> used to gather withered flowers into heaps and carry them away in a basket as his daily labor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> first saw the Buddha entering the city of Rājagaha with a company of monks, he felt such awe that he tried to hide himself against a wall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his low social standing, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> showed great respect by discarding his carrying pole and greeting the Buddha with palms joined in prayer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally invited <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> to leave the household life after asking him with sweet words if he could endure the life of a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> received his ordination directly from the Buddha through the formal invitation, "Come, monk" (<i>ehi bhikkhu</i>)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> described his former life as being "despised and reviled," yet he kept his heart humble toward the public while he performed his menial work? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after joining the Sangha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> lived alone in the forest and practiced the Buddha’s instructions with unwavering diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> successfully mastered the eight meditative attainments and five mundane superknowledges during his spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> attained the "sixth superknowledge," which refers to the total destruction of all mental taints and the realization of Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the first watch of the night of his enlightenment, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> recollected his past lives in great detail? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the middle watch of the night, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> purified his divine eye, allowing him to see the passing away and rebirth of other beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the final watch of the night, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> shattered the "mass of darkness" known as ignorance and liberated his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as the sun began to rise after his enlightenment, the king of gods Sakka and the Great Brahmā arrived to pay homage to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that seven hundred deities accompanied the Great Brahmā and Sakka to celebrate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> as a "Noble Victor" who had transcended old age and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the high deities addressed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> as a "Great Man" (<i>uttamapurisa</i>) and a "Supreme Man" because he had completely ended his mental fermentations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha saw the deities surrounding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a>, he smiled and praised the monk's spiritual achievement? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the example of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> to teach that one becomes a "true Brahmin" through austerity, the spiritual life, self-control, and restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that many other monks were curious about <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> and asked him about his family background and how he attained his high spiritual fruits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> did not hide his past, openly declaring that he came from a poor home where he performed a very "lowly trade"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> described himself as having once been a person who was "shunned, mistreated, and held in contempt" by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> was specifically sweeping the streets of Rājagaha, the capital of Magadha, when the Buddha first sought him out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deities recognized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> as being "worthy of offerings" after he had attained full liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> attained his enlightenment by following the Teacher's instructions "just as the Victor had taught"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his difficult beginnings, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> rose to become one of the most respected figures in the Sangha, eventually being honored by the highest celestial beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the story of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> serves as a powerful reminder that spiritual liberation is available to everyone, regardless of their social class or birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sunīta</b></a> was described by the Buddha as a "supreme Brahmin" because of his perfection in the spiritual life and self-discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag12.2/en/sujato">Thag 12.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ajita</b></a> was the son of a Brahmin who served as an assessor of goods for King Pasenadi of Kosala in the city of Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato">Thag 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before meeting the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ajita</b></a> was a student of the learned Brahmin Bāvarī in the Kapittha grove on the banks of the Godhāvarī River? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato">Thag 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ajita</b></a> was sent to see the Buddha by his teacher Bāvarī, along with fellow students Tissa and Metteyya, after which he was converted and ordained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato">Thag 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ajita</b></a> attained Arahantship by choosing a specific subject for meditation and developing his insight while the Master was still living? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato">Thag 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ajita</b></a> declared he had neither a fear of death nor a delight in life, but would discard his body with mindfulness and clear comprehension? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato">Thag 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ajita</b></a> attained enlightenment and expressed his victory through a verse emphasizing his state of being perfectly mindful and alert? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato">Thag 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ajita</b></a>, who began his spiritual journey as a hermit on the Godhāvarī, eventually found the ultimate peace of the Dhamma in Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.20/en/sujato">Thag 1.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nigrodha</b></a> was born into a prominent and famous Brahmin family in the city of Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato">Thag 1.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nigrodha</b></a> was inspired to enter the monastic life after seeing the majestic and dignified appearance of the Buddha during the dedication of the Jetavana Monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato">Thag 1.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nigrodha</b></a> attained the six higher knowledges (abhiññā) after developing deep insight and reflecting on the bliss of the fruits of the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato">Thag 1.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nigrodha</b></a> proclaimed that fear did not make him afraid because he had gained a profound understanding of the path to the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato">Thag 1.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nigrodha</b></a> stated that in the teaching of the Master, fear finds no footing for a bhikkhu who walks that path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato">Thag 1.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nigrodha</b></a> used his enlightenment verse to declare that a monk who walks the right path does not encounter fear, as the path itself is free from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato">Thag 1.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nigrodha</b></a> emphasized the great benefit of the Dhamma in helping a practitioner escape from the cycle of rebirth and attain the supreme peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.21/en/sujato">Thag 1.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cittaka</b></a> was born into a wealthy Brahmin family in Rājagaha before being moved to ordained life by the Buddha's discourse at the Bamboo Grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato">Thag 1.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cittaka</b></a> reached Arahantship by retreating to a forest and using the training rules (discipline) as the foundation for his meditation practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato">Thag 1.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cittaka</b></a> spoke of beautiful, blue-necked peacocks with bright crests that called out to one another in the forest of Kāramviyā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato">Thag 1.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cittaka</b></a> mentioned that the cries of the peacocks and the cool breezes of the forest would stir a sleeping meditator to wakefulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato">Thag 1.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cittaka</b></a> declared his final knowledge (aññā) after his fellow practitioners asked him if he had been diligent while living in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato">Thag 1.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cittaka</b></a> used the imagery of the forest and the natural calls of the peacocks to describe the environment that supported his spiritual awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato">Thag 1.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Cittaka</b></a>, after hearing the Master at the Veḷuvana (Bamboo Grove), immediately gained faith and left his wealthy life to enter the order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.22/en/sujato">Thag 1.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gosāla</b></a> was motivated to renounce his wealthy life in Magadha after hearing that his acquaintance, Soṇakuṭikaṇṇa, had already gone forth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato">Thag 1.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gosāla</b></a> attained the highest concentration and Arahantship while focusing his meditation on the rise and fall of the five aggregates (khandhas)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato">Thag 1.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gosāla</b></a> was supported during his time as a seeker by his mother, who provided him with daily alms in a village not far from his practice site? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato">Thag 1.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gosāla</b></a> achieved enlightenment after consuming a meal of rice porridge mixed with honey and sugar, which he ate under the shade of a bamboo grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato">Thag 1.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gosāla</b></a> expressed his intention to ascend a mountain to further increase his practice of seclusion (viveka) after fully accepting the Master's teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato">Thag 1.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gosāla</b></a> attained Arahantship with the specific analytical knowledges of both the meaning and the doctrine (paṭisambhidā) after reflecting on the nature of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato">Thag 1.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Gosāla</b></a> described his meal at the bamboo clump as the catalyst that allowed him to gain the strength to breakthrough to the final truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.23/en/sujato">Thag 1.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahānāma</b></a>, though born into a wealthy Brahmin family in Sāvatthī, once became so distressed by his inability to stop persistent sensual thoughts that he contemplated suicide by leaping off a mountain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato">Thag 1.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahānāma</b></a> sought solitude for his meditation practice on a specific hill known as Nesādaka before attaining the ultimate goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato">Thag 1.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahānāma</b></a>, in a moment of deep self-disgust over his defiled mind, climbed a mountain peak and addressed his own life as if speaking to another person, declaring, "I will kill it!"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato">Thag 1.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahānāma</b></a> described the famous mountain Nesādaka as being "craggy with many peaks" and "thickly overgrown with grass and trees" in the verse that later became his declaration of final knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato">Thag 1.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahānāma</b></a> attained the state of an Arahant through insight meditation at the very moment he was prepared to end his life out of frustration with his unwholesome thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato">Thag 1.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahānāma</b></a> initially felt his life was meaningless because his mind was "soiled with impurities," showing the intense psychological struggle some practitioners faced before enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato">Thag 1.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahānāma</b></a> used his own verse, which originally reflected his despair on the mountain, to proclaim his spiritual victory and the end of his cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato">Thag 1.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the transformation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahānāma</b></a> highlights how a practitioner can turn a moment of utter renunciation and "weariness with the world" into a catalyst for total liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato">Thag 1.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahānāma</b></a> was inspired to go forth into the homeless life after hearing the Blessed One preach the Dhamma in his home city of Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.115/en/sujato">Thag 1.115</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Pārāpariya</b></a> was a highly learned Brahmin from Rājagaha who was a master of the Three Vedas and a teacher of spells before he became a Buddhist monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato">Thag 1.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Pārāpariya</b></a> derived his name from the Pārāpara clan, emphasizing his distinguished family lineage before he renounced the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato">Thag 1.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Pārāpariya</b></a> was moved to seek ordination after witnessing the majestic and dignified deportment of the Buddha in the city of Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato">Thag 1.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Pārāpariya</b></a> described his path to enlightenment as "abandoning the six sense bases" and "well-restraining the sense doors"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato">Thag 1.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Pārāpariya</b></a> emphasized that he "uprooted the roots of evil" in order to reach the complete destruction of all taints (āsavakkhaya)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato">Thag 1.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after his ordination, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Pārāpariya</b></a> achieved Arahantship and expressed his spiritual joy through a verse summarizing his mastery over his senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato">Thag 1.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Pārāpariya</b></a> is a prime example of a Vedic scholar who found the Buddha's teaching more profound than the traditional spells and mantras he once taught? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato">Thag 1.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the declaration of final knowledge by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Pārāpariya</b></a> focuses on the internal discipline of the mind rather than the external rituals of his Brahmin upbringing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato">Thag 1.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Pārāpariya</b></a> achieved his goal by purifying the very "gates" of perception, which he refers to as "well-guarded" in his final verse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.116/en/sujato">Thag 1.116</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasa</b></a> was a young man of such immense wealth in Benares that he lived in three different palaces specially built for the three seasons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato">Thag 1.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasa</b></a> was motivated to renounce his life of luxury after waking up one night and seeing the unseemly and "impure" appearance of his sleeping female attendants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato">Thag 1.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasa</b></a> fled his home in the middle of the night wearing golden slippers, deities (devas) were said to have opened the city gates to facilitate his departure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato">Thag 1.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasa</b></a> wandered toward the Deer Park at Isipatana while crying out, "Alas, what distress! Alas, what danger!" before meeting the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato">Thag 1.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally comforted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasa</b></a> by saying, "Come here, Yasa! Here there is no distress, here there is no danger!"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato">Thag 1.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasa</b></a> attained full Arahantship while listening to the Buddha preach the Dhamma to Yasa’s own father, who had come looking for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato">Thag 1.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha formally ordained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasa</b></a> with the words "Come, Bhikkhu," his hair was instantly cut short and he was miraculously provided with the eight monk requisites? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato">Thag 1.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasa</b></a> declared in his verse that he attained the Three Knowledges (tevijjā) and completed the Buddha's training despite being "well-anointed and well-dressed" in fine garments at the time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato">Thag 1.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasa</b></a> first became a lay follower after hearing the gradual instruction on the Four Noble Truths, before progressing to full liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.117/en/sujato">Thag 1.117</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Upavāna</b></a> was born into a prominent Brahmin family in Sāvatthī and was inspired to ordain after witnessing the majesty of the Buddha during the dedication of the Jetavana monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato">Thag 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his ordination, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Upavāna</b></a> dedicated himself to the development of insight meditation and eventually attained the six direct knowledges (chaḷabhiññā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato">Thag 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Upavāna</b></a> served as a personal attendant to the Buddha and once had to seek medical assistance when the Blessed One was suffering from a wind ailment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato">Thag 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha was in physical pain, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Upavāna</b></a> approached a lay disciple named Devahita to request hot water and medicine for the "Sage" and "Arahant"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato">Thag 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Upavāna</b></a> referred to the Buddha as the "Sugata" (the Happy One) in his verses when explaining the need for medicinal hot water to treat the Buddha's illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato">Thag 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Upavāna</b></a> taught that one should bring offerings to the Buddha because he is a person who is truly worthy of offerings, honor, and respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato">Thag 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the efforts of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Upavāna</b></a> in obtaining the necessary medicine, the Buddha’s wind ailment was relieved, after which the Blessed One expressed his gratitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato">Thag 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Upavāna</b></a> used his verses to highlight the importance of "honoring those worthy of honor" (pūjanīye pūjayato), a principle that guided his service to the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato">Thag 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the lay disciple Devahita immediately recognized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Upavāna</b></a> needed a specific item for the Buddha’s health just by seeing him arrive with his bowl and robes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato">Thag 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Upavāna</b></a> attained the highest spiritual goal of the holy life, becoming an Arahant, before taking up his role as the Buddha's attendant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.33/en/sujato">Thag 2.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Isidinna</b></a> was born in Sunāparanta to a family of officials and first encountered the Dhamma after witnessing the Buddha perform the Twin Miracle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato">Thag 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Isidinna</b></a> achieved the stage of a Stream-enterer (Sotāpanna) while he was still living as a layperson, before he eventually decided to ordain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato">Thag 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Isidinna</b></a> was still a layman, a deity (deva) visited him to challenge his attachment to worldly possessions like jewelry and rings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato">Thag 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Isidinna</b></a> was urged toward ordination by a deity who pointed out the hypocrisy of laypeople who claim "sensual pleasures are impermanent" yet remain deeply attached to them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato">Thag 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity once critiqued the lifestyle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Isidinna</b></a> by noting that many who speak of the Dhamma do not actually know "the Dhamma as it really is" because they cannot let go of their families? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato">Thag 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deity who spoke to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Isidinna</b></a> observed that laypeople often lack the "strength to cut off craving" (taṇhā), leading them to remain bound to their wives and children? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato">Thag 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Isidinna</b></a> was so deeply moved (saṃvega) by the deity's pointed words regarding his attachments that he decided to leave the household life immediately? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato">Thag 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after ordaining, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Isidinna</b></a> quickly attained Arahantship and then repeated the very verses the deity had used to stir him as a declaration of his enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato">Thag 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Isidinna</b></a> lived in the region of Sunāparanta, where the offering of a sandalwood monastery provided the occasion for him to see the Buddha's supernatural powers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato">Thag 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verses of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Isidinna</b></a> serve as a powerful reminder that merely speaking about the impermanence of desires is not the same as having the strength to abandon them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.34/en/sujato">Thag 2.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Upasena</b></a>, the son of Vaṅganta and the brother of the Buddha's chief disciple Sāriputta, was initially rebuked by the Buddha for prematurely ordaining a disciple but eventually attained Arahantship through diligent meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>secluded places</b></a>, free from noise and inhabited by wild beasts, were recommended by Venerable Upasena as the ideal environment for a monk to arrange his lodgings and practice the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena lived a life of extreme simplicity, advocating for the wearing of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>rag-robes</b></a> gathered from rubbish heaps or the highway as part of his commitment to the ascetic path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when going on an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>alms round</b></a> from house to house, Venerable Upasena taught that a monk should maintain a humble heart and keep his senses well-restrained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena warned that a practitioner who is greedy for flavors and constantly seeks out diverse tastes will find that their mind does not take <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>delight in meditation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Upasena, an ascetic living in solitude should be content with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>coarse food</b></a> and have few desires to support their practice of mental purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena advised those seeking liberation to focus on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>avoiding association</b></a> with both householders and those who have gone forth into the homeless life, preferring the life of a recluse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena suggested that a wise person in the Sangha should act as if they were "dumb" or "mute" to ensure <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>moderation in speech</b></a> and avoid making long, unnecessary discourses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the path taught by Venerable Upasena emphasizes the principle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>non-harming</b></a>, instructing monks never to criticize or cause harm to any living being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena emphasized that a monk must be fully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>restrained in the Pātimokkha</b></a>, the fundamental monastic code, as the basis for their spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>moderation in eating</b></a> was considered essential by Venerable Upasena for any practitioner who wishes to properly develop the mind and attain higher states of peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena taught that a monk should be skilled in understanding how the mind emerges and how to correctly grasp the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>meditation signs</b></a> (nimittas) during their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the balanced development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>serenity and insight</b></a> (samatha and vipassanā) was a key teaching of Venerable Upasena for those aiming to end their suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena encouraged monks to possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>energy and endurance</b></a>, staying constantly devoted to their duties until the goal of the holy life is reached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena stated that a wise person who has confidence will continue to press forward in their training until they achieve the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>destruction of taints</b></a> (āsavas)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that by following the disciplined lifestyle and mental training he described, Venerable Upasena declared that a monk can attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>highest peace</b></a>, which is Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>Vaṅganta's son</b></a>, Venerable Upasena, became so successful in his practice and teaching that the Buddha recognized him as a prominent disciple who was highly regarded by many? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena’s commitment to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>ascetic practices</b></a> (dhutaṅgas) was so effective that his advice led many other monks to adopt a similar lifestyle of simplicity and seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upasena delivered these instructions on reclusive living to a monk who was seeking guidance during a period of dissension in the Sangha at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>Kosambī</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato"><b>Rūpasārī's son</b></a>, Venerable Upasena, mastered the three Vedas in his youth before finding true liberation under the teachings of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag10.6/en/sujato">Thag 10.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> was born in the city of Campā into the family of a prominent city council member, and his birth was celebrated by the entire city? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because of his past merit from making offerings to a Paccekabuddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> possessed a complexion that resembled fine gold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> had a unique physical characteristic where fine, golden hair grew on the palms of his hands and the soles of his feet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before ordaining, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> lived a life of extreme luxury, being provided with three different palaces specifically suited for each of the three seasons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> first went to see the Buddha at Rājagaha as part of a large retinue invited by King Bimbisāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha preach the Dhamma, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> gained deep faith and sought his parents' permission to leave the household life and ordain as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> initially struggled to achieve concentration in the forest because he was constantly distracted by the many people who sought him out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> practiced walking meditation so intensely that his feet became swollen and bled, yet he still failed to reach any spiritual attainments during that period of over-exertion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that out of frustration with his lack of progress despite his extreme effort, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> contemplated disrobing and returning to the lay life to make merit through wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, knowing the thoughts of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a>, visited him to teach him the "Simile of the Lute" to explain the importance of balanced effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the spiritual practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> to tuning a lute, explaining that if the strings are too tight or too loose, the instrument will not produce the right sound? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that by following the Buddha’s advice on balancing his energy, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> eventually attained Arahantship while staying at Vulture Peak Mountain (Gijjhakūṭa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> reflected on his transformation by noting that he was once "high" in the kingdom serving the King of Anga, but was now "highest" in the Dhamma having crossed all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> described a monk who has crossed the "flood" of existence as one who has cut the five lower fetters and abandoned the five higher fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> warned that a monk who is vain, heedless, and focused on external appearances will never reach the perfection of virtue, concentration, or wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a>, the "taints" (āsavas) of a person increase when they neglect what should be done and instead focus on what should not be done? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> taught that the taints of those who are diligent and constantly practice "mindfulness of the body" (kāyagatāsati) will eventually reach their end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> encouraged practitioners to walk the "straight path" that has been taught and to avoid crooked ways while constantly admonishing oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> described the Buddha as the "Peerless Teacher" who used the example of the lute to guide him when his energy was too tense? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that by listening to and delighting in the Buddha’s instructions, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> was able to achieve the "three true knowledges" (tevijja) and complete the Buddha's teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> emphasized that a liberated mind is one that is focused on "renunciation" (nekkhamma) and the seclusion of the heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> declared that the path to liberation involves being dedicated to non-ill will (vô sân) and the total eradication of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> taught that when a person is dedicated to the destruction of craving and their mind is free from delusion, they truly understand the arising and passing of the sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for a monk like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> who is truly liberated and at peace, there is no longer any accumulation of past deeds and no more work left to be done for his own salvation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> used the simile of a "solid rock" to describe a mind that remains unmoved by the winds of worldly conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a>, neither sights, sounds, smells, tastes, nor touches can agitate the mind of one who is truly stable and detached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> described the mind of an Arahant as being "unbound" and "stable," even when confronted with both pleasant and unpleasant experiences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> achieved his goal by practicing with a calm mind, after learning to temper the "heat" of his excessive effort as instructed by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> stated that he has completed "the Buddha's bidding", signifying he has fulfilled the entire training of a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the final realization of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Koḷivisa</b></a> centered on "watching the passing away" of all phenomena, which allowed his mind to remain free and unattached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag13.1/en/sujato">Thag 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.24/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sugandha</b></a> received his name because a wonderful fragrance filled his entire house at the moment of his birth into a wealthy family in Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.24/en/sujato">Thag 1.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.24/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sugandha</b></a> attained the full realization of an Arahant just seven days after being inspired to go forth by the preaching of the elder Mahā Sela? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.24/en/sujato">Thag 1.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.24/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sugandha</b></a> declared that he had attained the three true knowledges and completed the Buddha's instructions after a single rainy season? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.24/en/sujato">Thag 1.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his ordination, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.24/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sugandha</b></a> achieved liberation by seeing the true nature of all phenomena, also known as the Dhamma-nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.24/en/sujato">Thag 1.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nandiya</b></a> was a member of the Sakyan royal clan in Kapilavatthu whose parents named him "Nandiya" because they believed his birth brought joy to their home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato">Thag 1.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nandiya</b></a> chose to enter the monkhood at the same time that his famous friend, Venerable Anuruddha, and several other companions were ordained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato">Thag 1.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while living in the Eastern Bamboo Grove, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nandiya</b></a> was targeted by Mara, who manifested in a terrifying form to frighten him, but the monk remained completely unmoved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato">Thag 1.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nandiya</b></a> boldly dismissed Mara by stating that the Evil One only meets with failure and misfortune when trying to oppose those who have transcended his realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato">Thag 1.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nandiya</b></a> described a monk whose heart is "constantly and strongly directed toward the Dhamma" as one who cannot be shaken by the "Black One" (Mara)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato">Thag 1.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nandiya</b></a> attained the state of an Arahant through his dedicated study and the power of spiritual aspirations he had made in past lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato">Thag 1.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nandiya</b></a> taught that the Dhamma generates a radiant light and is the path that leads directly to the attainment of the Noble Fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato">Thag 1.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Abhaya</b></a> was a prince and the son of King Bimbisāra who initially encountered the Buddha through a debate scheme orchestrated by the Jain leader Nātaputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.26/en/sujato">Thag 1.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Abhaya</b></a> decided to go forth as a monk only after the death of his father, King Bimbisāra, having been impressed by the Buddha's wisdom and insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.26/en/sujato">Thag 1.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Abhaya</b></a> first realized the stage of a Stream-enterer (Sotāpanna) after hearing a specific teaching known as the "Simile of the Hole in the Log"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.26/en/sujato">Thag 1.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Abhaya</b></a> compared his piercing insight into the Dhamma to the skill of an archer who is able to split a single hair with an arrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.26/en/sujato">Thag 1.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Abhaya</b></a> used the poetic epithet "Kinsman of the Sun" (Ādiccabandhu) to refer to the Buddha when praising the well-spoken words he had heard? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.26/en/sujato">Thag 1.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.26/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Abhaya</b></a> eventually reached the ultimate goal of Arahatship through persistent and energetic effort after his initial realization as a prince? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.26/en/sujato">Thag 1.26</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.24/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sugandha</b></a> was so wealthy and well-born in Sāvatthī that his decision to renounce the world for the homeless life was a significant act of faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.24/en/sujato">Thag 1.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nandiya</b></a> explicitly identified Mara as the "Black One" (Kaṇha) while warning him that opposing a practitioner of the Dhamma leads only to pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.25/en/sujato">Thag 1.25</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.24/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sugandha</b></a> emphasized that his spiritual success was a result of observing the true nature of things (Dhammatā) and following the Buddha's guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.24/en/sujato">Thag 1.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kimbila</b></a>, before entering the monkhood, was a member of the Sakyan royal family in Kapilavatthu and lived a life surrounded by immense wealth and luxury? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato">Thag 1.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally intervened to help <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kimbila</b></a> by manifesting a vision of a beautiful young woman transforming into an old woman, specifically because he saw that the monk's insight was maturing while staying at Anupiya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato">Thag 1.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after witnessing a divine vision of physical decay, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kimbila</b></a> was so deeply moved by the truth of impermanence that he sought out the Buddha to hear the Dhamma and subsequently ordained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato">Thag 1.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kimbila</b></a> compared the onset of old age to being under a heavy curse, noting how it completely overwhelms and alters one's previous beauty and physical form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato">Thag 1.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his attainment of Arahantship, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kimbila</b></a> realized that while his external form had changed through aging, he had successfully abandoned the "self" or ego-notion that he once mistakenly believed to be permanent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato">Thag 1.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verses spoken by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kimbila</b></a> serve as a declaration of his "correct knowledge" (aññā), emphasizing his shift from a view of permanence to a deep realization of the transience of all things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato">Thag 1.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kimbila</b></a> used his own spiritual shock at the sight of aging as a catalyst to transcend the world of material form and achieve total liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato">Thag 1.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vajjiputta</b></a> was the son of a Licchavī king in the city of Vesālī and was particularly skilled in the art of training and managing elephants during his youth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato">Thag 1.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha preach at a monastery, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vajjiputta</b></a> was so motivated by his "mature conditions for liberation" that he ordained and eventually mastered the six higher powers (abhiññā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato">Thag 1.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that following the Buddha’s final passing (Parinibbāna), <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vajjiputta</b></a> worked tirelessly with other elders, traveling from place to place to ensure that the True Dhamma would not decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato">Thag 1.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vajjiputta</b></a> once directly exhorted Venerable Ānanda to stop engaging in "bustling activity" and instead devote himself to the solitary practice of meditation in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato">Thag 1.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vajjiputta</b></a> encouraged his fellow monk to let the state of Nibbāna sink deep into the heart by seeking out the seclusion provided by the "thicket of tree roots"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato">Thag 1.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite Venerable Ānanda's role in preaching to large crowds, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vajjiputta</b></a> felt it was vital to urge him toward the higher fruits of the path through more intensive jhāna practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato">Thag 1.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vajjiputta</b></a> literally identifies him as a "son of the Vajjis," reflecting his noble heritage within the Vajjian confederacy of Vesālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato">Thag 1.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vajjiputta</b></a> questioned the utility of external social activities for a monk, suggesting that the primary work should be the inward cultivation of silence and concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato">Thag 1.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāgavaccha</b></a> was the son of a Brahmin named Sammiddhi from the village of Nālaka in Magadha, the same village associated with several of the Buddha’s chief disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato">Thag 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the primary motivation for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāgavaccha</b></a> to ordain as a monk was his deep respect for the wisdom of Venerable Sāriputta, whom he chose to follow into the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato">Thag 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāgavaccha</b></a> identified a specific combination of qualities—the power of wisdom, virtue, concentration, and the bliss of jhāna—as the essential components for spiritual success? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato">Thag 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāgavaccha</b></a> taught that a monk should be extremely mindful of their food intake, consuming only those "món ăn" (almsfood) that support the body in a way that yields good results for the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato">Thag 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that having attained the highest fruit of liberation, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāgavaccha</b></a> described his state of mind as being completely detached from craving while simply "waiting for the time of death"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato">Thag 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāgavaccha</b></a> shared his verses specifically to encourage his "fellow companions in the holy life" to maintain diligence and energetic effort in their own practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato">Thag 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāgavaccha</b></a> highlights that a practitioner who has successfully "distanced themselves from craving" can remain peaceful and composed even when facing the end of their lifespan? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.12/en/sujato">Thag 1.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sambula-Kaccāna</b></a>, a citizen of Magadha from the Kaccāna clan, attained Arahantship along with the six higher powers while meditating in a terrifying cave during a violent thunderstorm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato">Thag 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite staying alone in a cave named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato"><b>Bheravāyana</b></a> (which means "scary path") during a terrifying storm, Venerable Sambula-Kaccāna remained completely free from fear and trembling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato">Thag 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while wild animals like gấu (bears), dã can (jackals), buffaloes, and elephants were screaming in terror during a heavy rainstorm, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sambula-Kaccāna</b></a> remained in a state of calm concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato">Thag 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sambula-Kaccāna</b></a> attributed his lack of fear and his hair not standing on end during a frightening storm to his understanding of the "nature of the Dhamma"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato">Thag 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sambula-Kaccāna</b></a> found that the cooling air brought by a heavy rainstorm actually helped his mind reach stillness, leading to the development of insight and full liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato">Thag 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sambula-Kaccāna</b></a> practiced intensive insight meditation in a cave located in the vicinity of the Himalayas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato">Thag 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sambula-Kaccāna</b></a> declared his attainment of supreme knowledge by reciting verses about staying alone in a "scary cave" without any horror or hair-raising fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato">Thag 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the "scary cave" where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sambula-Kaccāna</b></a> resided was filled with the sounds of crashing thunder and flashing lightning, yet he remained unmoved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato">Thag 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sambula-Kaccāna</b></a> did not care for his own life or safety when the unseasonal storm broke out, focusing instead on his spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato">Thag 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sambula-Kaccāna</b></a> successfully used the solitary and frightening environment of a mountain cave to master his mind and achieve the six higher powers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.35/en/sujato">Thag 2.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a>, who was born into a Brahmin family in Kosala, lived in the forest after ordaining under the Buddha and quickly attained the stage of an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato">Thag 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a> used the metaphor of a "solid rock" to describe a mind that is steady, unmoving, and no longer subject to agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato">Thag 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after experiencing the bliss of liberation, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a> dedicated himself to encouraging and inspiring other forest-dwelling monks in their spiritual efforts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato">Thag 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a> taught that suffering cannot come to someone whose mind is like a stone, remaining without lust for pleasant objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato">Thag 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a>, the secret to ending suffering is to cultivate a mind that is neither attracted to the agreeable nor moved by things that cause agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato">Thag 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a> attained Arahantship while dwelling in the solitude of the forest, eventually finding complete happiness in the "bliss of Nirvana"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato">Thag 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a> specifically visited other monks living in the wild to share his verses of enlightenment and to help them find spiritual joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato">Thag 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a> emphasized that a mind developed through meditation becomes firm and "unmoving" even when faced with objects that typically cause desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato">Thag 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verses of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a> highlight the transformation of the human heart into something as stable and unshakable as a mountain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato">Thag 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Khitaka</b></a> taught that when the mind is properly cultivated and free from attachment, there is no source from which suffering can arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.36/en/sujato">Thag 2.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasadatta</b></a> was born into the royal family of the Mallas and completed his traditional education at the famous ancient center of learning, Takkasilā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato">Thag 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before meeting the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasadatta</b></a> traveled as a wanderer alongside the ascetic Sabhiya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato">Thag 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasadatta</b></a> initially listened to the Buddha's teachings with a "fault-finding mind," specifically intending to point out flaws in the Buddha's answers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato">Thag 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a person like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasadatta</b></a>, who listens to the Dhamma with a fault-finding attitude, to someone who is as far from the True Teaching as the earth is from the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato">Thag 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasadatta</b></a> that a fool who listens to the Victor's teaching with a critical heart will see their progress in the Dhamma diminish like the moon during the dark fortnight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato">Thag 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasadatta</b></a> recorded the Buddha's analogy that an individual with a fault-finding mind dries up in the Excellent Dhamma just like a fish stranded in shallow water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato">Thag 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the verses of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasadatta</b></a>, a fool who listens to the Buddha's teaching with a desire to criticize will fail to grow in the Dhamma, much like a rotten seed planted in a field? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato">Thag 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasadatta</b></a> explained that one who listens to the teachings of the Noble Ones with a "satisfied mind" is able to abandon all taints and achieve unshakable realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato">Thag 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasadatta</b></a> described the ultimate goal of listening correctly to the Dhamma as reaching supreme peace and the attainment of Nibbāna free from all taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato">Thag 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Yasadatta</b></a> became an Arahant after the Buddha addressed his specific mental state of wanting to find faults in others' words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.10/en/sujato">Thag 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> was born into a very wealthy family in the region of Avanti and was originally known simply as Soṇa? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name "Kuṭikaṇṇa," given to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a>, literally means "one with an expensive ear" because he wore a pair of earrings worth a fortune? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before ordaining, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> was a prosperous landowner who supported the great teacher Mahā Kaccāna with all necessary requisites? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> felt a deep sense of spiritual urgency after hearing the Dhamma from Mahā Kaccāna, which led him to seek entry into the monkhood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> initially faced difficulties in being ordained because it was hard to gather the required assembly of ten monks in the remote region where he lived? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> traveled all the way to Sāvatthī specifically to pay his respects to the Buddha after receiving permission from his preceptor, Mahā Kaccāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> was granted the rare privilege of sleeping in the same dwelling or porch area as the Buddha during his visit to Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally invited <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> to recite the Dhamma, and he proceeded to chant the sixteen Aṭṭhakas (the Chapter of Octads) with great skill? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while reciting the Dhamma before the Buddha, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> developed deep insight into the dangers of secular life and attained the state of an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> was sent back to his teacher Mahā Kaccāna with three specific questions or matters that the Buddha had approved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> rejoiced in his higher ordination and the fact that he was finally free from all mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> observed the Buddha spending many days living in the open air before finally entering his meditation cell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> described seeing the Buddha, referred to as the Teacher Gotama, lying down on his double-layered robe (saṅghāṭi) with the composure of a lion in a cave? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> noted that the Buddha had completely abandoned all forms of fear and anxiety in his daily life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> was praised for choosing "good and beautiful words" when he preached the Dhamma in the presence of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> emphasized that his spiritual success came from the full understanding of the Five Aggregates of clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> credited his attainment of supreme peace to his diligent practice of the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the story of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> and his recitation of the Dhamma is also recorded in detail in other Buddhist scriptures like the Udāna and the Aṅguttara commentary? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Soṇa Kuṭikaṇṇa</b></a> expressed great joy in having achieved the "supreme peace" of Nibbāna, which he described as being without any taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.11/en/sujato">Thag 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> was once falsely accused of theft and brought before King Pasenadi after a thief dropped stolen goods near him in the forest, an event that prompted him to declare his spiritual purity through these verses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> maintained that since the day he left the household life, he never once entertained an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>unnoble thought</b></a> associated with hatred or ill will? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that throughout his many years as a monk, <b>Venerable Revata</b> never knew a single thought of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>harming or hurting</b></a> any living being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> successfully cultivated a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>infinite loving-kindness</b></a> (metta) by following the gradual training taught by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> considered himself a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>friend to all</b></a> and remained compassionate toward every living creature, living a life free from resentment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> took great delight in a mind that had become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>unmoving and unshakable</b></a> through his dedicated practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> was a practitioner of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>divine abodes</b></a> (Brahmavihāras), states of mind that are practiced by the most virtuous of beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a disciplined disciple of the Buddha, <b>Venerable Revata</b> attained a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>concentration without thought</b></a> (avitakka), where discursive thinking is stilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> achieved a realization of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>Noble Silence</b></a>, finding a profound internal stillness through his meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> compared a monk who has destroyed delusion to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>solid mountain</b></a> of rock that cannot be shaken by the wind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> taught that for a person seeking absolute purity, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>fault as light as a hair</b></a> is viewed as being as heavy as a dark cloud? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>frontier fortress</b></a>, guarded both inside and out, to explain how a practitioner should guard their own mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> warned that one must not let even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>single moment</b></a> pass by without being vigilant in one's spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> lived with total equanimity, stating that he did not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>delight in life</b></a> and did not delight in death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> described his wait for his final passing as being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>worker waiting for his wages</b></a> at the end of a job? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while waiting for the end of his life, <b>Venerable Revata</b> remained perfectly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>mindful and alert</b></a>, having completed everything the Buddha instructed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> successfully laid down the heavy burden of the five aggregates and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>uprooted the fuel</b></a> of existence that leads to rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> declared he had attained the very goal for which he became a monk: the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>destruction of all fetters</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the final exhortation of <b>Venerable Revata</b> was for practitioners to strive with diligence and never be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>negligent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Revata</b> reached his final goal and declared his entry into Nibbana, having achieved total <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato"><b>freedom from all acquisitions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.1/en/sujato">Thag 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lomasakaṅgīya</b></a> was born into a Sakyan family in Kapilavatthu and received his name due to his fine body hair and weak physical constitution? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato">Thag 1.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lomasakaṅgīya</b></a> initially chose not to join other young Sakyan princes, such as Anuruddha, when they first left home to ordain as monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato">Thag 1.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lomasakaṅgīya</b></a> was prompted to seek the Buddha's guidance after his friend Candana asked him about the discourse on the "Auspicious Night" (<i>Bhaddekaratta</i>), which he could not then explain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato">Thag 1.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lomasakaṅgīya</b></a> went to his mother for permission to ordain, she was deeply concerned that his delicate health would not withstand the rigors of the ascetic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato">Thag 1.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lomasakaṅgīya</b></a> vowed to push aside the various sharp grasses like <i>dabba</i>, <i>kusa</i>, <i>muñja</i>, and <i>pabbaja</i> that might prick his chest while he lived in the wild? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato">Thag 1.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his physical frailty, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lomasakaṅgīya</b></a> expressed a firm determination to increase his practice of the "joy of solitude" or secluded living in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato">Thag 1.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that fellow monks tried to dissuade <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lomasakaṅgīya</b></a> from entering the mountains for meditation, fearing his body was too weak to endure such a demanding environment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato">Thag 1.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lomasakaṅgīya</b></a> eventually attained Arahantship along with the six higher powers while living in the forest, proving his spiritual strength surpassed his physical weakness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato">Thag 1.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lomasakaṅgīya</b></a> used the same verse he spoke to his mother and his fellow monks as his final declaration of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato">Thag 1.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally advised <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Lomasakaṅgīya</b></a> to ordain and seek his parents' permission after the monk had approached him to ask about the <i>Bhaddekaratta</i>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.27/en/sujato">Thag 1.27</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jambugāmikaputta</b></a>, whose name means "the son of Jambugāmika," was born in the city of Campā to a lay follower and was known by his father's name? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato">Thag 1.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while serving as a novice in the Añjana Grove at Sāketa, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jambugāmikaputta</b></a> received a verse from his father who was testing his commitment to the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato">Thag 1.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the father of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jambugāmikaputta</b></a> questioned whether his son was still finding satisfaction in worldly things like fine robes, ornaments, and perfumes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato">Thag 1.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verse sent by the father of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jambugāmikaputta</b></a> emphasized that the true fragrance of virtue comes from the precepts one keeps, rather than external scents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato">Thag 1.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jambugāmikaputta</b></a> felt a deep sense of spiritual urgency when he realized his father still viewed him as someone potentially swayed by worldly desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato">Thag 1.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jambugāmikaputta</b></a> used the very verse his father sent him as a subject for his meditation practice to transcend his status as an ordinary person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato">Thag 1.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through diligent exertion, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jambugāmikaputta</b></a> attained Arahantship and the six higher powers, realizing the ultimate goal of the monkhood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato">Thag 1.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jambugāmikaputta</b></a> recited his father's verse both to announce his enlightenment and to offer praise to his father for providing the motivation for his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato">Thag 1.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the father of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jambugāmikaputta</b></a> was a lay follower who feared his son might lack the persistence to remain in the monkhood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato">Thag 1.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the spiritual breakthrough of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Jambugāmikaputta</b></a> was directly catalyzed by his father's probing questions about his satisfaction with his robes and his commitment to virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.28/en/sujato">Thag 1.28</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sona Potiriyaputta</b></a> served as a commander-in-chief for the Sakyan King Bhaddiya before he decided to follow the king's example and ordain as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato">Thag 2.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his military background, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sona Potiriyaputta</b></a> initially struggled with laziness and failed to practice meditation after his ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato">Thag 2.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once stayed at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato"><b>Anupiya Mango Grove</b></a> and emitted a radiant light to personally encourage Venerable Sona Potiriyaputta to develop mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato">Thag 2.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sona Potiriyaputta</b></a> was exhorted by the Buddha to stay awake and diligent during the star-lit night, as a wise person does not spend such time in sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato">Thag 2.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after receiving the Buddha's encouragement, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sona Potiriyaputta</b></a> felt deeply stirred and chose to practice insight meditation in the open air to overcome his faults? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato">Thag 2.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sona Potiriyaputta</b></a> declared that dying in battle after falling from an elephant would be better than living a life of spiritual defeat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato">Thag 2.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the dedicated practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a>, Venerable Sona Potiriyaputta eventually attained the state of Arahantship and declared his final knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato">Thag 2.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sona Potiriyaputta</b></a> was the son of a landowner named Potiriya from the city of Kapilavatthu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato">Thag 2.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sona Potiriyaputta</b></a> expressed his spiritual breakthrough by repeating the very verses the Buddha used to wake him from his sloth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.37/en/sujato">Thag 2.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nisabha</b></a> decided to renounce the world after witnessing the majestic power and virtue of the Buddha during a conflict between the Sakyans and the Koliyans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato">Thag 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nisabha</b></a> admonished a fellow monk who was wasting his time, reminding him that the purpose of faith-led ordination is to end all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato">Thag 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nisabha</b></a> taught that a monk must abandon the "five cords of sensual pleasure" and all delightful foods that entice the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato">Thag 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nisabha</b></a> lived with a balanced mind, stating that he neither delighted in death nor in life, but simply waited for the right time to pass? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato">Thag 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nisabha</b></a> emphasized the importance of remaining "alert and mindful" while waiting for the natural end of one's life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato">Thag 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nisabha</b></a> was born into a family of the Koliya people and attained Arahantship shortly after joining the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato">Thag 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nisabha</b></a>, a true practitioner should only consume food that is necessary, letting go of things that the mind finds "agreeable and pleasing"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato">Thag 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nisabha</b></a> effectively used his own realization to encourage others to stop wasting their precious time in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato">Thag 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nisabha</b></a> described the path to ending suffering as starting with the renunciation of worldly desires through faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.38/en/sujato">Thag 2.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a>, a member of the Sakyan royal family, was initially negligent in his monastic duties, spending his days socializing and his nights in sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato">Thag 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> had a transformative dream where he saw himself riding an elephant into a village for alms while wearing his monastic robes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato">Thag 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his dream, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> felt such profound shame upon seeing a large crowd that he immediately descended from the elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato">Thag 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon waking, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> realized his dream was a reflection of his own "sloth and restlessness" and immediately turned to intensive meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato">Thag 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> compared the color of his monastic robes to the beautiful hue of a mango flower in his verses of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato">Thag 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> successfully attained the "destruction of the taints" (āsavas) by using the emotional stir from a dream as a catalyst for his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato">Thag 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his spiritual awakening, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> admitted to being "exceedingly arrogant," but through meditation, he became "truly calmed"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato">Thag 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> was first inspired to ordain after seeing the Buddha’s wisdom and strength during a visit to the Buddha's Sakyan relatives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato">Thag 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the act of "descending from the elephant" in the dream of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> symbolized his transition from pride to spiritual humility and urgency? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato">Thag 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Usabha</b></a> achieved the goal of Arahantship by transforming his "slothful and restless" mind into one that was perfectly focused and serene? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.39/en/sujato">Thag 2.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kosiya</b></a>, also known as Sona, was born into a Brahmin family in Magadha and was inspired to go forth after hearing the Dhamma from Venerable Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato">Thag 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kosiya</b></a> describes a person of wisdom as one who understands the Teacher’s words, dwells in peace, and develops a mind of love? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato">Thag 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kosiya</b></a>, a wise person possesses a deep faith that allows them to recognize what is truly excellent within the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato">Thag 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kosiya</b></a> emphasizes that when a great disaster or danger arises, a wise person's ability to reflect remains unparalyzed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato">Thag 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kosiya</b></a> identifies perseverance and persistence as the defining characteristics of those who know the excellence of the Dhamma during times of crisis? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato">Thag 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kosiya</b></a> compares the stability of a wise person to the ocean, noting that they remain "unmoving" and free from desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato">Thag 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kosiya</b></a> describes the wisdom of an Arahant as being deep enough to clearly see meanings that are both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>subtle and exquisite</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato">Thag 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kosiya</b></a> teaches that a "much-heard" or learned person is one who not only sustains the Dhamma but also practices in accordance with it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato">Thag 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kosiya</b></a> defines a person of integrity as one who understands the true meaning of words and acts <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>according to the truth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato">Thag 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kosiya</b></a> speaks of the "inner meaning" (<i>ajjhattha</i>) that is understood by the wise who truly know the excellence of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato">Thag 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kosiya</b></a> highlights that a person who acts on what they have learned is considered to be one who truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>knows the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato">Thag 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kosiya</b></a> praised the virtue and the power of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato"><b>noble ones</b></a> after reviewing his own successful journey toward liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.12/en/sujato">Thag 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūta</b></a> was originally a commoner in Magadha who became a wandering ascetic before finding faith in the Buddha's teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato">Thag 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūta</b></a> decided to seek ordination after witnessing the immense happiness and joy of great disciples like Upatissa (Sāriputta) and Kolita (Moggallāna)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato">Thag 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūta</b></a> achieved Arahantship by entering a retreat and diligently developing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> (vipassanā) on a specific subject? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato">Thag 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūta</b></a> warns that placing oneself in an inappropriate position or state will result in a failure to achieve one's intended work? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato">Thag 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūta</b></a> teaches that the root of suffering can only be pulled up when one is willing to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>abandon the victory</b></a> of worldly attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato">Thag 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūta</b></a> compares a person who loses their spiritual path to a gambler who makes a bad throw and loses everything? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato">Thag 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūta</b></a> likens a person without wisdom to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>blind person</b></a> who is unable to distinguish between a flat road and an uneven one? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato">Thag 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūta</b></a> advises that one should only speak of what they actually do, rather than speaking of things they do not put into practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato">Thag 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūta</b></a> points out that the wise can easily recognize someone who only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>talks without acting</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato">Thag 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūta</b></a> compares well-spoken words that are actually put into practice to a beautiful flower that possesses both color and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>fragrance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato">Thag 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Subhūta</b></a> compares well-spoken words that are not backed by action to a beautiful flower that has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato"><b>vivid color</b></a> but lacks any scent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.2/en/sujato">Thag 5.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> was originally a merchant in Sāvatthī who chose to go forth after being deeply moved by an ox that spoke to him in a human voice, warning him of future karmic retribution? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> compares a person of great wisdom to a well-bred ox that, even when oppressed by a heavy burden, does not cast it off? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a>, those possessing abundant wisdom are like the ocean and do not look down upon others, as this is the nature of the Holy Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> taught that human beings suffer and grieve in this very life because they are overcome by time and dominated by the cycles of being and non-being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> observed that fools are easily harmed because they rejoice when they experience pleasure but become dejected when they encounter pain, failing to see things as they really are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> describes the wise as those who stand firm like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>door-post</b></a>, remaining unmoved by the fluctuations between pleasure and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> taught that a sage remains unattached to gain and loss, fame and disrepute, and praise and blame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>drop of water</b></a> on a lotus leaf to describe how a liberated person does not cling to anything in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> asserted that it is better to act according to the Dhamma and fail to get what one wants than to succeed through unrighteous means? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a>, a person who possesses wisdom but lacks fame is superior to a famous person who lacks wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> declared that being blamed by a wise person is far better than being praised by a fool? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> taught that the pain arising from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> is superior to the pleasure derived from sensual desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> believed it is better to die while acting righteously than to live a long life through unrighteousness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> emphasized that a person who has abandoned sensual desire and anger has a mind that is purified regarding both being and non-being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> described the ideal life as one lived without craving, and without the biases of attraction or repulsion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> taught that by developing the Factors of Enlightenment, the faculties, and the powers, one reaches the highest peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> attained the state of an Arahant and realized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>taintless Nibbāna</b></a> after contemplating the harm caused to living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> compared the "hero" who is undefeated by worldly conditions to someone who does not stick to anything, just like water on a lotus? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> taught both monastics and laypeople about the nature of the world while he himself was dwelling in the bliss of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> stated that those who do not see things as they really are remain trapped between the dualities of joy and sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>being and non-being</b></a> (bhava-abhava) was used by Venerable Godatta to describe the cyclic nature of existence that dominates the lives of the ignorant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> pointed out that even famous people can be "low in wisdom," and that such a status is inferior to being wise without any public recognition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a>, the "Holy Dhamma" is characterized by a lack of contempt for others, even when one possesses vast, ocean-like wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> taught that the end of suffering is achieved by reaching "supreme peace" through the cultivation of spiritual faculties and powers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Godatta</b></a> highlighted the importance of equanimity by listing the "eight worldly winds"—gain, loss, fame, disrepute, praise, blame, pleasure, and pain—as things the wise remain detached from? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag14.2/en/sujato">Thag 14.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Isidatta</b></a> was born in the Veḷugāma garden in the country of Avanti as the son of a caravan leader? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Isidatta</b> became close friends with the famous householder <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>Citta of Macchikasanda</b></a> through an exchange of letters before they had ever met in person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that it was the descriptions of the Buddha's excellence sent by Citta the householder that inspired Venerable Isidatta to seek <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>ordination</b></a> in the Buddhist order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Isidatta entered the monastic life under the guidance and instruction of the eminent teacher <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kaccāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his diligent practice, Venerable Isidatta successfully attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>six supernormal knowledges</b></a> (chaḷabhiññā), which include various spiritual powers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha asked about his spiritual progress, Venerable Isidatta declared that all his sorrows had ceased since he began practicing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma and Vinaya</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Isidatta described his state of liberation to the Buddha as a life lived entirely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>without fear</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Isidatta attained liberation by fully understanding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> (pañcakkhandhā) and cutting the root of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Isidatta confirmed his enlightenment by stating that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a> (āsavas) had been completely destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Isidatta traveled from his home region of Avanti to the Middle Country specifically to pay homage to the Buddha after attaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vanavaccha</b></a> was born in a forest in Kapilavatthu because his mother happened to be visiting the woods when she went into labor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato">Thag 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vanavaccha was one of the childhood friends who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato"><b>played in the sand</b></a> with the future Buddha when they were children in Kapilavatthu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato">Thag 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Vanavaccha literally means <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato"><b>"Forest-Calf"</b></a> because of his immense love for dwelling in the mountains and forests? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato">Thag 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vanavaccha attained the highest fruit of Arahantship while living in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato"><b>solitude of the forest</b></a>, a lifestyle he practiced with great energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato">Thag 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vanavaccha described the craggy mountains of his forest home as being as beautiful as the color of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato"><b>"blue clouds"</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato">Thag 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vanavaccha found great delight in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato"><b>cool and clear waters</b></a> that flowed through the rocky terrain of his forest retreat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato">Thag 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vanavaccha spoke of the "Indagopaka" insects, also known as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato"><b>Inda’s cowherds</b></a>, which covered and beautified the forest floor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato">Thag 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vanavaccha shared his poetic verses about nature in response to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato"><b>fellow monk</b></a> who asked him what he could possibly find so enjoyable about the wild mountains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato">Thag 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sight of the craggy, mountain landscapes was exactly what <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato"><b>delighted the heart</b></a> of Venerable Vanavaccha and supported his spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato">Thag 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that throughout his time in the order, Venerable Vanavaccha was renowned for his devotion to the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato"><b>forest-dwelling</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.13/en/sujato">Thag 1.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sīvaka</b></a> was the nephew of Venerable Vanavaccha and entered the monastic life specifically to care for his elderly uncle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato">Thag 1.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīvaka initially became a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato"><b>novice</b></a> (sāmaṇera) at the request of his mother, who was concerned that Venerable Vanavaccha was becoming too old to live alone in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato">Thag 1.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even when Venerable Sīvaka fell <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato"><b>seriously ill</b></a> near a village, his teacher Venerable Vanavaccha insisted they immediately return to the seclusion of the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato">Thag 1.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīvaka famously declared that even if his physical <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato"><b>body was in a village</b></a>, his mind was always directed toward the solitude of the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato">Thag 1.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīvaka was so dedicated that he expressed he would go back to the forest even if he was still <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato"><b>lying down</b></a> and recovering from illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato">Thag 1.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīvaka attained Arahantship after being encouraged and physically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato"><b>led back to the forest</b></a> by his teacher’s hand? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato">Thag 1.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīvaka used his verses to express both his deep <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato"><b>love for seclusion</b></a> and his total obedience to his teacher’s instructions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato">Thag 1.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīvaka described one who is enlightened and correctly understands the Dhamma as someone who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato"><b>"without bonds"</b></a> and cannot be tied down to village life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato">Thag 1.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīvaka considered his teacher’s command to "Go!" as the final <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato"><b>catalyst</b></a> for his spiritual breakthrough and attainment of Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.14/en/sujato">Thag 1.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Hārita</b></a> was born as the only son of a wealthy Brahmin in the city of Sāvatthī before seeking ordination under the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato">Thag 1.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while observing the beauty of his wife, <b>Venerable Hārita</b> was struck by the realization of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato"><b>impermanence</b></a> due to the merits of his past actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato">Thag 1.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that it was the sudden death of his wife from the bite of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato"><b>black snake</b></a> that drove the grieving Venerable Hārita to seek out the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato">Thag 1.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after ordaining, <b>Venerable Hārita</b> initially struggled with his practice because he was unable to keep his mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato"><b>straight and upright</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato">Thag 1.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Hārita</b> was inspired to master his own mind after watching a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato"><b>fletcher</b></a> use his tools to make an arrow straight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato">Thag 1.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha appeared in the air before <b>Venerable Hārita</b> to deliver a verse instructing him to make his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato"><b>self</b></a> straight and upward-bound? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato">Thag 1.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted <b>Venerable Hārita</b> to act like a skilled artisan and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato"><b>straighten the mind</b></a> just as an arrow-maker straightens a shaft? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato">Thag 1.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the primary goal set for <b>Venerable Hārita</b> by the Buddha was to finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato"><b>cut off ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato">Thag 1.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Hārita</b> attained the state of an Arahant shortly after receiving specific instructions on how to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato"><b>straighten</b></a> his own nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.29/en/sujato">Thag 1.29</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kaṅkhārevata</b> was born into a wealthy family in Sāvatthī and first encountered the Dhamma while standing in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato"><b>outer circle</b></a> of a crowd listening to the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato">Thag 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kaṅkhārevata</b> became so proficient in deep concentration that the Buddha declared him the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato"><b>foremost in meditation</b></a> among his disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato">Thag 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon achieving his spiritual goal, <b>Venerable Kaṅkhārevata</b> reflected on how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato"><b>doubts</b></a> that once occupied his mind had been completely destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato">Thag 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kaṅkhārevata</b> praised the wisdom of the Tathāgatas, comparing it to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato"><b>fire burning at midnight</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato">Thag 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <b>Venerable Kaṅkhārevata</b>, the wisdom of the Buddhas provides <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato"><b>light and eyes</b></a> for those who seek the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato">Thag 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kaṅkhārevata</b> stated that the Tathāgatas possess the power to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato"><b>subdue the doubts</b></a> of all who approach them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato">Thag 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of <b>Venerable Kaṅkhārevata</b> is associated with the overcoming of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato"><b>uncertainty</b></a> through the development of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato">Thag 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kaṅkhārevata</b> described his own mind as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato"><b>steadfast and concentrated</b></a> following the successful completion of his spiritual career? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato">Thag 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kaṅkhārevata</b> attained Arahantship specifically through the dedicated practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato"><b>meditation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.3/en/sujato">Thag 1.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Ittiya</b> was a Brahmin's son from Sāvatthī who originally became a wandering ascetic to search for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato"><b>Deathless</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato">Thag 1.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when <b>Venerable Ittiya</b> first ordained, he failed to reach any spiritual fruit because his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> was not yet pure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato">Thag 1.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after seeing other monks declare their enlightenment, <b>Venerable Ittiya</b> asked the Buddha for a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato"><b>brief lesson</b></a> to guide his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato">Thag 1.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed <b>Venerable Ittiya</b> that the foundation of his practice must be the purification of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato"><b>basic conduct</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato">Thag 1.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Ittiya</b> was struck by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato"><b>physical illness</b></a> while he was in the midst of developing insight meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato">Thag 1.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the obstacle of being sick, <b>Venerable Ittiya</b> used his condition as a catalyst to reach <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato">Thag 1.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Ittiya</b> declared that a time of illness is definitely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato"><b>not the time for negligence</b></a> in one's practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato">Thag 1.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Ittiya</b> found that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> arose within him specifically because of his state of illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato">Thag 1.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verse spoken by <b>Venerable Ittiya</b> was his official declaration of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato"><b>final knowledge</b></a>, made in relation to his experience with sickness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.30/en/sujato">Thag 1.30</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Ekudāniya</b> was born as the son of a wealthy councilor but chose to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato"><b>renounce the world</b></a> after being inspired by the Buddha’s majesty during the dedication of the Jeta’s Grove monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato">Thag 1.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the monk <b>Venerable Ekudāniya</b> received his nickname, which means <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato"><b>One-Verse Reciter</b></a>, because he spent his time in the forest repeating a single verse taught to him by the Buddha regarding the heightened mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato">Thag 1.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Ekudāniya</b> attained the state of Arahantship and liberation after practicing diligent meditation and insight while living in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato"><b>solitude of the forest</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato">Thag 1.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when <b>Venerable Ekudāniya</b> was invited by Venerable Sāriputta to share his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato"><b>spiritual attainment</b></a>, he responded by reciting the same verse he had mastered concerning the training of a sage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato">Thag 1.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Ekudāniya</b> taught that a sage who possesses a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato"><b>heightened mind</b></a> and stays diligent on the path of right knowledge will remain free from sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato">Thag 1.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <b>Venerable Ekudāniya</b>, a practitioner who is always tranquil and maintains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato"><b>constant mindfulness</b></a> is one who has truly conquered grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato">Thag 1.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Ekudāniya</b> initially focused entirely on a single teaching about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato"><b>sage’s mind</b></a> because he found it to be the most direct path to tranquility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato">Thag 1.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Ekudāniya</b> demonstrated that even mastering a single teaching of the Buddha, if practiced with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato"><b>non-negligence</b></a>, can lead to the highest peace of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato">Thag 1.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally gave <b>Venerable Ekudāniya</b> his specific meditation subject after observing the monk sitting in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato"><b>meditative absorption</b></a> near Venerable Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.68/en/sujato">Thag 1.68</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Channa</b> was originally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato"><b>born as a slave</b></a> in the household of King Suddhodana, the father of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato">Thag 1.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Channa</b> decided to renounce the world and ordain after the Buddha returned to visit his home city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato"><b>Kapilavatthu</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato">Thag 1.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for a long time after ordaining, <b>Venerable Channa</b> was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato"><b>hindered by pride</b></a>, constantly boasting about his personal connection to the Buddha and neglecting his monastic duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato">Thag 1.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Channa</b> was eventually disciplined with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato"><b>higher penalty</b></a> (Brahmadanda) following the Buddha's passing, which meant the other monks would not speak to or instruct him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato">Thag 1.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the shock and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato"><b>shame and motivation</b></a> resulting from the community's disciplinary action after the Buddha's death finally caused Venerable Channa to reform his behavior and attain Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato">Thag 1.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Channa</b> described the Dhamma he heard from the Buddha as possessing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato"><b>great sweetness</b></a> that leads to ultimate liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato">Thag 1.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Channa</b> praised the Buddha as a skillful guide who explains the path that effectively destroys the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato"><b>thorns of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato">Thag 1.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Channa</b> declared that by following the Buddha's instructions, he had firmly set out on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato"><b>path to the Deathless</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato">Thag 1.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Channa</b> attained the highest realization by contemplating the teachings of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato"><b>All-Knowing One</b></a> who revealed the essence of the great Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato">Thag 1.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Channa</b>'s spiritual breakthrough serves as an example that even a monk struggling with deep-seated arrogance can achieve <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato"><b>full awakening</b></a> through proper discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.69/en/sujato">Thag 1.69</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Bhalliya</b> was the son of a caravan leader from the city of Pokkharavatī and was among the first people to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato"><b>offered food</b></a> to the Buddha after his enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato">Thag 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Bhalliya</b> and his brother Tapussa were prompted to offer food to the Buddha by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato"><b>tree deity</b></a> who was a former relative of theirs and appeared to them in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato">Thag 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the first meal <b>Venerable Bhalliya</b> offered to the Buddha consisted of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato"><b>rice cakes and honey</b></a>, which the Buddha accepted while he was still sitting near the tree of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato">Thag 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after later hearing the Dhamma at Rājagaha, <b>Venerable Bhalliya</b> entered the monastic life and successfully attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato"><b>six supernormal knowledges</b></a> (chaḷabhiññā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato">Thag 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Bhalliya</b> once confronted Māra, who appeared in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato"><b>terrifying form</b></a> to frighten him, but the monk remained completely unmoved and fearless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato">Thag 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Bhalliya</b> used the metaphor of a powerful flood breaking weak reeds to describe how he successfully drove away the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato"><b>army of Death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato">Thag 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Bhalliya</b> attained a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato"><b>total tranquility</b></a> and peace by overcoming all fear and subduing his own mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato">Thag 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while <b>Venerable Bhalliya</b> chose to ordain as a monk, his brother Tapussa became the Buddha's very first <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato"><b>lay disciple</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato">Thag 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Bhalliya</b> reached the end of suffering by completely subduing the forces of Māra and dwelling in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato"><b>silence of liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato">Thag 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Bhalliya</b> declared that he had attained the status of a victor who has conquered death and now lives in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato"><b>unwavering peace</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.7/en/sujato">Thag 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kumāra-Kassapa</b></a> was born in Rājagaha to a mother who had joined the Order of nuns without realizing she was already pregnant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato">Thag 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the pregnant mother of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kumāra-Kassapa</b></a> was accused of being an unrighteous nun by Devadatta, the Buddha assigned the legal expert Venerable Upāli to investigate and resolve the matter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato">Thag 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the inquiry into the pregnancy of the mother of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kumāra-Kassapa</b></a>, the famous laywoman Visākhā and the King were present to witness the declaration that the pregnancy occurred before her ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato">Thag 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kumāra-Kassapa</b></a> was raised by a King after his mother could no longer care for him in the nunnery, and he was eventually brought to the Buddha to be ordained at a very young age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato">Thag 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even after he had grown up, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kumāra-Kassapa</b></a> continued to be known by a name meaning "Kassapa the Youth" because he had entered the Sangha as a boy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato">Thag 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kumāra-Kassapa</b></a> was meditating in the Andha Grove, a deity who was a Non-returner from the Pure Abodes appeared to him to provide guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato">Thag 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a Great Brahmā deity posed fifteen complex questions to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kumāra-Kassapa</b></a>, stating that only the Buddha himself would be able to provide the correct answers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato">Thag 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kumāra-Kassapa</b></a> achieved Arahantship after memorizing the Buddha's answers to the deity's fifteen questions and applying them to his insight meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato">Thag 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kumāra-Kassapa</b></a> to be the foremost among all the monks in the art of eloquent and witty speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato">Thag 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kumāra-Kassapa</b></a> expressed his enlightenment by praising the Buddha and the Dhamma, marveling at how a disciple can truly realize the Excellent Law? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato">Thag 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kumāra-Kassapa</b></a> reflected on his long journey through countless rebirths, noting that he had finally reached his "last body" and completed his spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato">Thag 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after attaining liberation, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kumāra-Kassapa</b></a> proclaimed that he would never again face rebirth, having successfully navigated the "path of birth and death"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato">Thag 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verses of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kumāra-Kassapa</b></a> serve as a joyful exclamation of his successful attainment of the Dhamma and his gratitude toward the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.41/en/sujato">Thag 2.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammapāla</b></a> was originally the son of a Brahmin from Avanti who traveled to Takkasilā to complete his education? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato">Thag 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammapāla</b></a> was inspired to go forth into the monkhood after hearing the Dhamma from an elderly monk he encountered sitting alone in a leaf hut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato">Thag 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon practicing the Buddha's teachings, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammapāla</b></a> successfully attained the six supernormal knowledges and the sweetness of total liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato">Thag 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammapāla</b></a> once used his psychic powers to catch and safely lower two young novices who were falling from a tree after a branch snapped? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato">Thag 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after saving the two novices from a dangerous fall, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammapāla</b></a> used the opportunity to teach them verses about the importance of diligent practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato">Thag 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammapāla</b></a> taught that even a young monk can brighten the world if he applies himself wholeheartedly to the Buddha's instructions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato">Thag 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammapāla</b></a> emphasized the importance of staying awake and mindful in a spiritual sense, even while the rest of the world is "asleep" in ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato">Thag 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammapāla</b></a>, the life of a monk who remains vigilant and focused on his practice is never lived in vain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato">Thag 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammapāla</b></a> advised that a wise person should always keep the words of the Buddha in mind to guide their spiritual journey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato">Thag 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammapāla</b></a> identified the cultivation of faith and virtue as essential components for a monk who wishes to follow the Buddha's path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato">Thag 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammapāla</b></a> spoke of the joy that comes from seeing and understanding the True Law, or Dhamma, through dedicated practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato">Thag 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verses of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammapāla</b></a> highlight the urgency of practice for young seekers, urging them to be "awake among those who sleep"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato">Thag 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Dhammapāla</b></a> considered his act of saving the novices through psychic power as a moment to impart deep wisdom regarding the benefits of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.42/en/sujato">Thag 2.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaddha attributed his attainment of supreme enlightenment to the sharp criticism and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato"><b>encouragement</b></a> he received from his mother? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato">Thag 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaddha was born in the town of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato"><b>Dhammakañcana</b></a> into a family of commoners before he entered the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato">Thag 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Vaddha became an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato"><b>Arahant nun</b></a> after leaving the household life due to her anxiety over the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato">Thag 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his final realization, Venerable Vaddha was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato"><b>famous preacher</b></a> who had mastered the teachings of the Buddha under the guidance of his teacher, Veḷudanta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato">Thag 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaddha was once scolded by his mother for visiting her at the nuns' quarters <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato"><b>alone</b></a> and without wearing his outer robe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato">Thag 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after reflecting on his mother’s rebuke, Venerable Vaddha returned to the monastery and attained Arahantship while meditating in his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato"><b>secluded cell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato">Thag 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaddha declared that he had attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā) and had seen the deathless state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato">Thag 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaddha proclaimed he had successfully conquered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato"><b>army of Māra</b></a> and now lives without any mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato">Thag 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaddha described his state of liberation by stating that all his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato"><b>internal and external taints</b></a> had been completely uprooted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato">Thag 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaddha referred to his mother as a "spiritual sister" of gentle mind, noting that for both of them, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato"><b>jungle of defilements</b></a> had been cleared? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato">Thag 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaddha confirmed that his suffering has come to an end and that this present body is his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato"><b>final body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato">Thag 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaddha stated that the path of rebirth has been cut off for him, and there is now <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato"><b>no more renewal of existence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato">Thag 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaddha considered himself a person <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato"><b>worthy of offerings</b></a> because of his complete victory over the mental defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.5/en/sujato">Thag 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nadikassapa was originally a Brahmin from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>Magadha</b></a> who lived as a matted-hair ascetic before meeting the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nadikassapa was the brother of the famous teacher Kassapa and led a group of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>three hundred followers</b></a> on the banks of the Nerañjarā River? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Nadikassapa translates to "Kassapa of the River," a title he received because he lived near the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>Nerañjarā River</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nadikassapa was converted by the Buddha alongside his brothers, a transformation that occurred after hearing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>Fire Sermon</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nadikassapa considered it a great benefit that the Buddha came to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>River Nerañjarā</b></a> to teach him the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the teachings of the Buddha, Venerable Nadikassapa was able to completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>cut off wrong views</b></a> and attain true insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his conversion, Venerable Nadikassapa spent his time performing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>sacrificial rites</b></a> and burning sacred fires in a misguided attempt at purification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nadikassapa later described his former self as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>blind worldling</b></a> who thought he was pure while remaining fundamentally impure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nadikassapa once wandered in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>jungle of views</b></a>, blinded by rules and rituals before finding the true path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nadikassapa declared that he has shattered all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>states of being</b></a> and eradicated the cycle of birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nadikassapa shifted his devotion from sacrificial fires to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>paying homage</b></a> to the Tathāgata, the Charioteer of men? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nadikassapa successfully uprooted all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> and destroyed his craving for continued existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nadikassapa stated that the path to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>rebirth</b></a> has been entirely cut off, and he will never be born again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nadikassapa realized he was finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato"><b>worthy of offerings</b></a> only after he had quenched the internal fire of defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.6/en/sujato">Thag 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> was born into a very wealthy Brahmin family in the village of Donavatthu, located near Kapilavatthu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his youth, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> mastered the three Vedas and became an expert in the science of physiognomy, which is the study of bodily marks to predict a person's future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> was the only one among eight expert Brahmins who correctly predicted that the infant Bodhisatta would definitely become a Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the Bodhisatta renounced the world at age twenty-nine, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> followed him into the homeless life along with four other Brahmins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> spent six years living in Uruvelā near the Bodhisatta while the latter was practicing extreme physical austerities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> and his four companions initially abandoned the Bodhisatta and went to Isipatana because they were dissatisfied when he stopped practicing self-mortification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha preached his first sermon, the <i>Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta</i>, at Isipatana, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> was the first human to attain the Fruit of Stream-entry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the sources, ten thousand Brahma deities also attained the Fruit of Stream-entry alongside <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> during the first sermon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> achieved final liberation as an Arahant on the fifth day after the first sermon, upon hearing the <i>Anattalakkhaṇa Sutta</i>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha formally recognized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> as being the foremost among all the senior monks (<i>rattaññūnaṃ</i>) in the monastic community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> was renowned for his ability to teach the Four Noble Truths and the three characteristics of existence with an eloquence comparable to that of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka, the ruler of the gods, was so delighted by the Dhamma taught by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> that he uttered verses of joy regarding the flavor of the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> taught that the various forms and colors found in the world can captivate the mind because of their attractive appearance and their connection to desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> used the metaphor of rain settling a cloud of dust to describe how seeing things with wisdom can calm one's intrusive thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> declared that seeing the impermanence of all conditioned things through wisdom is the direct path to attaining purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> stated that by seeing how all conditioned things are suffering, one becomes disenchanted with pain and finds the way to purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> emphasized that the path to purity requires seeing with wisdom that all phenomena are non-self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> described himself as an elder who has fully awakened to the Dhamma and has completely escaped the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> compared his state of liberation to having broken through a massive stone pillar that was previously impossible to destroy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> warned that a monk who is easily agitated and associates with bad friends will eventually drown in the great flood of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> taught that a wise person who associates with good friends and keeps their sense faculties restrained can successfully end all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his high spiritual attainment, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> described his own physical appearance as being dark, lean, thin, and covered in visible veins? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> attributed his physical condition to his strict moderation in eating and his lack of mental distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> compared his mindful endurance of biting insects in the forest to the way a war elephant endures a battle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> declared that he does not take any delight in the prospect of death nor does he delight in continuing to live? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> likened his patient waiting for the end of his life to a worker waiting for their wages after the job is finished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> stayed fully alert and mindful while waiting for the appropriate time for his final passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> affirmed that he had served the Teacher and fully carried out all of the Buddha's instructions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> stated that he had laid down the heavy burden of existence and completely uprooted the very source of birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> once questioned what further need he had for the forest, seeing as he had already achieved the ultimate goal of his renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his diligent practice, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> claimed to have escaped the bonds of Māra and crossed over the flood of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> described the objects of the world as being "attractive signs" that fundamentally lead to the arousal of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the holy life of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> was described as being completely fulfilled and perfectly realized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍañña</b></a> spent six years in the company of the Bodhisatta before the latter's eventual enlightenment under the Bodhi tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.1/en/sujato">Thag 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gahvaratīriya was born into a Brahmin family in Sāvatthī and was originally known by the name <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>Aggidatta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gahvaratīriya was inspired to go forth into the monkhood after witnessing the Buddha perform the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>Twin Miracle</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gahvaratīriya earned his name because of his long-term residence and spiritual practice in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>Ratīra forest</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after attaining Arahantship, Venerable Gahvaratīriya returned to Sāvatthī but faced opposition from his relatives who feared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>mosquitoes and flies</b></a> of the wilderness would be too difficult for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gahvaratīriya compared a monk practicing in the deep forest to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>elephant in battle</b></a>, remaining steadfast despite external irritants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gahvaratīriya emphasized that the key to living in a great mountain forest filled with biting insects is to remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>mindful</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gahvaratīriya realized the supreme goal of the spiritual life by taking up a specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>meditation subject</b></a> given to him by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his family's concerns for his comfort, Venerable Gahvaratīriya insisted that a life in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>mountain forest</b></a> was most suitable for his spiritual temperament? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gahvaratīriya used his verses to declare his attainment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>enlightenment</b></a> and his preference for the solitude of nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya was born into a family of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>cemetery keepers</b></a> in the city of Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya was converted to the Buddha’s path after hearing the Dhamma taught by his friend, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sopāka</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya attained the state of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>ageless</b></a> even while being physically afflicted by the process of aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya described his spiritual success as having reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>cool</b></a> after having been parched by the fires of worldly suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the goal Venerable Suppiya aspired to was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>supreme peace</b></a>, which is the ultimate cessation of all mental disturbances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya referred to Nibbāna as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed safety</b></a> from the bonds (yogas) that keep beings tied to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his dedicated effort, Venerable Suppiya achieved full <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>release from suffering</b></a> and the burdens of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>highest fruit</b></a> of the spiritual life, Arahantship, while undergoing a period of intense spiritual struggle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya’s life story illustrates how one can transcend their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>social background</b></a> and past karma to achieve the highest spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Sopāka was being born, his mother was mistakenly thought to be dead and was taken to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>cemetery</b></a> to be cremated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>non-human being</b></a> (amanussa) intervened to protect the infant Sopāka from the funeral fire after his mother had passed away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka was raised by a cemetery keeper and grew up as the foster brother of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Suppiya</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> to locate the seven-year-old Sopāka in the cemetery because he saw the child's potential for enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka received <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>ordination</b></a> from the Buddha at the young age of seven after his foster father gave his consent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the meditation subject the Buddha taught to Venerable Sopāka was the development of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> (metta) toward all beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka used his experiences in the cemetery to develop deep <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>meditative absorption</b></a> (jhāna) as a foundation for his enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka famously compared the heart of loving-kindness to the love a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>good mother</b></a> feels for her only son? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka taught that one’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>heart of goodness</b></a> should encompass every type of living creature in every possible place without exception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the core of Venerable Sopāka’s teaching is that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>loving care</b></a> should be universal and impartial, making no distinction between relatives and strangers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kimbila</b></a> was born into a Sakyan royal family in Kapilavatthu and lived a life of great wealth before being inspired by the Buddha to seek liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato">Thag 1.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once showed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kimbila</b></a> a vision of a beautiful young woman transforming into an old woman to help him realize the truth of impermanence and the decline of physical beauty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato">Thag 1.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kimbila</b></a> reflected on how his own identity and form had been altered by age, yet through insight, he realized that there is nothing in the self that truly changes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato">Thag 1.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Kimbila</b></a> emphasized that while physical appearance is overcome by old age, the realization of Dhamma allows one to remain unaffected by these external changes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.118/en/sujato">Thag 1.118</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vajjiputta</b></a> was the son of a Licchavi king from Vesālī and was highly skilled in the training of elephants before his spiritual awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato">Thag 1.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the Buddha’s Parinibbāna, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vajjiputta</b></a> urged Venerable Ānanda to stop being preoccupied with large crowds and instead focus on deep meditation in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato">Thag 1.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vajjiputta</b></a> achieved the six direct knowledges after being moved by the Buddha’s teaching during a visit to a monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato">Thag 1.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vajjiputta</b></a> traveled with other elders to ensure the Dhamma remained steady and did not decline after the passing of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato">Thag 1.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vajjiputta</b></a> encouraged his fellow monks to let Nibbāna sink deep into their hearts by seeking the seclusion of the forest and practicing jhána? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.119/en/sujato">Thag 1.119</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāgavaccha</b></a> was born into a Brahmin family in the village of Nālaka and decided to ordain specifically because he admired the profound wisdom of Venerable Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāgavaccha</b></a> taught that the combination of wisdom, virtue, concentration, and mindfulness is essential for achieving the fruit of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāgavaccha</b></a> lived with a state of mind that had completely detached from desire, simply waiting for the natural end of his life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahāgavaccha</b></a> highlighted that a monk should eat food only for the purpose of bringing about good results in their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.120/en/sujato">Thag 1.120</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Puṇṇa</b></a> was a merchant from the Sunāparanta region who traveled to Sāvatthī and was inspired to ordain after hearing the Buddha preach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato">Thag 1.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Puṇṇa</b></a> was so gifted in debate and rhetoric that he became a highly respected teacher among his peers in the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato">Thag 1.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha gave <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Puṇṇa</b></a> a specific teaching regarding sensory perception to prepare him for his mission to teach in the potentially dangerous region of Sunāparanta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato">Thag 1.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Puṇṇa</b></a> successfully converted over five hundred male and female lay followers to the Buddha's path in his homeland? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato">Thag 1.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Puṇṇa</b></a> declared that while virtue is supreme, it is the person who possesses wisdom who is truly unsurpassed in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato">Thag 1.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vacchapāla</b></a> was a Brahmin from Rājagaha who decided to join the Sangha after witnessing the conversion of the powerful ascetic Uruvelā Kassapa? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato">Thag 1.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vacchapāla</b></a> achieved full enlightenment and the six direct knowledges only one week after his ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato">Thag 1.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Vacchapāla</b></a> taught that Nibbāna is easily attained by one who is skillful in wisdom, humble in mind, and strictly follows the Buddha’s discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato">Thag 1.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ātuma</b></a> compared the trap of domestic life and marriage to a bamboo shoot that is unable to grow freely because it is tangled in thick branches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato">Thag 1.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ātuma</b></a> attained Arahantship while his mother was actively trying to persuade him to leave the monastery and return to a household life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato">Thag 1.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmāli was born in the country of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>Kosala</b></a> as the son of a Brahmin before he renounced the world to follow the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmāli practiced meditation in the solitude of the woods and eventually attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>six supernormal knowledges</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmāli compared a monk whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>faculties are stilled</b></a> to a horse that has been well-tamed by a skillful charioteer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Brahmāli, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>Devas</b></a> greatly admire a person whose conceit has been destroyed and who is free from all mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while dwelling in the bliss of liberation, Venerable Brahmāli spoke verses to praise the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>virtue of energy</b></a> on behalf of other forest-dwelling monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmāli successfully developed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> in the forest, leading to his attainment of spiritual perfection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmāli declared that he had personally achieved a state where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a> was completely abandoned and his senses were fully controlled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmāli reached a goal where no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a> remained in his mind, a state that makes a monk beloved by celestial beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mogharāja was declared by the Buddha to be the foremost among His disciples in the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>wearing coarse robes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before he sought out the Buddha, Venerable Mogharāja was a student under the leadership of the Brahmin <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Bāvari</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mogharāja was one of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>sixteen students</b></a> brought by Bāvari to have an audience with the Buddha after feeling a sense of fear and anxiety about the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mogharāja attained the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> immediately after questioning the Buddha and receiving His profound answers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mogharāja practiced extreme asceticism by wearing clothing made from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>discarded rags</b></a> thrown away by travelers, tailors, and dyers on the road? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that due to the ripening of past karma, Venerable Mogharāja suffered from many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>skin sores</b></a> that broke out all over his body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his physical ailments, Venerable Mogharāja spent the freezing winter nights in Magadha sleeping in a simple <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>straw hut</b></a> located in a field? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once specifically asked Venerable Mogharāja how he managed to live and what he would do while staying in a humble shelter during the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>cold winter</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even while his body was afflicted by painful skin conditions, Venerable Mogharāja was said to possess a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>good heart</b></a> and remained constant in his practice of meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mogharāja considered the peace of living in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>straw-roofed hut</b></a> to be far superior to the worldly happiness enjoyed by ordinary people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mogharāja's commitment to the spiritual life was sparked by a deep sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>fear and anxiety</b></a> regarding the nature of existence, which led him to become an ascetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mogharāja fulfilled a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>previous aspiration</b></a> when he was officially recognized by the Buddha for his ascetic habit of wearing rough, discarded clothing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that although he lived in the prosperous and wealthy country of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Magadha</b></a>, Venerable Mogharāja chose a life of extreme simplicity and renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mogharāja’s spiritual practice involved being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>always in meditation</b></a>, regardless of the harsh weather or the physical pain he endured? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mogharāja explained to the Buddha that even a humble <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>shelter of straw</b></a> was more beautiful than any worldly comfort because it supported his life of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali was originally born into a Brahmin family in Savatthi and had fully mastered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>three Vedas</b></a> before he ever met the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Vakkali first saw the Buddha, he became so captivated by the Teacher’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>physical appearance</b></a> that he could not stop looking at him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali decided to undergo <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>ordination</b></a> primarily because he felt that living as a layman would prevent him from constantly seeing the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha initially remained silent for a long time regarding Venerable Vakkali’s behavior, waiting for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>insight</b></a> to fully ripen before giving him a direct teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha famously challenged Venerable Vakkali by asking what use there was in seeing his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>foul body</b></a>, emphasizing that "he who sees the Dhamma sees me"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Venerable Vakkali that seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a> is equivalent to seeing the Buddha himself, and vice versa? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after being told by the Buddha to leave at the end of a rainy season retreat, Venerable Vakkali became so distraught that he intended to commit suicide by jumping off <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>Vulture Peak</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, knowing Venerable Vakkali was about to throw himself off a mountain, appeared before him to prevent him from destroying his potential for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>spiritual fruits</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged Venerable Vakkali by stating that a monk full of joy and faith in the Buddha's teaching would reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>peaceful state</b></a> where all conditioned things are stilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon seeing the Buddha and hearing his words, Venerable Vakkali was filled with such great joy that he was able to stand in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>the air</b></a> while reflecting on the teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali eventually attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>arahantship</b></a> along with analytical knowledge while contemplating the Buddha’s instructions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite suffering from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>wind disease</b></a> that made him weak, Venerable Vakkali remained determined to live and practice in the deep forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha asked how a sick monk with a thin body could survive in the forest, Venerable Vakkali replied that he was sustained by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>pervasive rapture</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali declared that even if his body was emaciated, he would continue to live in the forest while practicing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>four foundations of mindfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali committed himself to the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>five faculties</b></a>—faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom—even while his physical health was failing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali found the strength to remain in the forest by cultivating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>five powers</b></a> as a core part of his spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali utilized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> to maintain his practice while living in isolation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali drew inspiration from seeing his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>fellow practitioners</b></a> living in harmony and with resolute energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali practiced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Buddha</b></a> day and night, describing the Buddha as the "Supreme Tamer of men"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali vowed to live in the forest without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>laziness</b></a>, despite the fact that his almsfood was very limited? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha intervened in Venerable Vakkali’s suicide attempt because he saw that the monk was on the verge of destroying the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>conditions</b></a> necessary to attain the higher stages of the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali described himself as being filled with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>abundant happiness</b></a> and joy because of his unwavering confidence in the Buddha's teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha called out to Venerable Vakkali with the words <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>"Come, monk!"</b></a> to draw him back from the edge of the cliff? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali’s obsession with the Buddha’s body was so intense that he spent all his time, including during meals and bathing, simply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>gazing</b></a> at the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali's story is recorded in the commentaries of both the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>Anguttara Nikaya</b></a> and the Dhammapada? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his physical body being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>wasted and thin</b></a>, Venerable Vakkali claimed he would live in the forest because of the "superhuman bliss" he experienced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha waited until the end of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>rains retreat</b></a> to tell Venerable Vakkali to leave, a move designed to shock the monk into spiritual awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali believed his life had no meaning if he could not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>behold the Teacher</b></a>, which was his primary motivation before attaining enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin described the Buddha as a human being who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>self-tamed</b></a> and constantly concentrated, living in the manner of the supreme Brahmās? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Udāyin, both humans and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>devas</b></a> pay homage to the Buddha because he has reached the far shore of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin likened the Buddha's delight in renouncing sensual pleasures to the process of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>purifying gold</b></a> from raw ore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin proclaimed that the Buddha is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>supreme Nāga</b></a> (Great Being) who stands magnificent like a mountain peak amidst the Himalayas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin explained that the Buddha is called a "Nāga" specifically because he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>does no evil</b></a> whatsoever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin metaphorically described the Buddha’s qualities of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>harmlessness</b></a> and gentleness as the two front feet of the Great Elephant that represents the Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Udāyin, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> and clear comprehension serve as the other two feet supporting the Great Being known as the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin described the Buddha’s faith as his trunk and his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> as his beautiful white tusks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> as the head of the Great Elephant, which symbolizes the intellectual and spiritual power of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin compared the Buddha's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>investigation</b></a> of the Dhamma to the trunk (proboscis) of a Great Elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin identified the "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>womb of Dhamma</b></a>" (inner chamber) as the belly of the Great Elephant representing the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin compared the Buddha’s practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> to the tail of a Great Elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin stated that the Buddha, like a Great Nāga, remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>well-composed</b></a> and concentrated whether he is walking, standing, sitting, or lying down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin noted that the Buddha's achievement lies in his constant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>restraint</b></a> in all situations and circumstances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin taught that the Buddha eats only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>blameless food</b></a> and avoids that which is blameworthy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin observed that the Buddha receives requisites like robes and food but remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>without hoarding</b></a> or attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin said the Buddha has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>cut all fetters</b></a>, whether fine or coarse, and travels everywhere without any expectations or clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin compared the Buddha to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>lotus</b></a> that grows in the water but remains unsmeared and fragrant, just as the Buddha lives in the world but is not defiled by it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin used the metaphor of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>dying fire</b></a> that has run out of fuel to describe the Buddha's state of being "extinguished" and peaceful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin explained that a Great Nāga (the Buddha) eventually discards the body and attains Nibbāna through the total destruction of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin described the Buddha as an Arahant who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>transcended all bonds</b></a> and crossed from the "forest" of samsara to the safety of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin portrayed the Buddha's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>reflection on the Dhamma</b></a> as the thought processes of the Great Elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin reached the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> after going forth into homelessness upon seeing the Buddha's power during a visit to Kapilavatthu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin emphasizes that the Buddha is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>free from greed</b></a>, hatred, and delusion, and has attained final Nibbāna without any remaining taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gahvaratīriya was originally born into a Brahmin family in Sāvatthī and was given the name <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>Aggidatta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gahvaratīriya was inspired to go forth and ordain after he witnessed the Buddha perform the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>Twin Miracle</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gahvaratīriya lived in the Ratīra forest and became known by a name meaning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>inhabitant of the thicket</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after reaching enlightenment, the relatives of Venerable Gahvaratīriya tried to stop him from returning to the forest by complaining about the many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>mosquitoes and flies</b></a> there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gahvaratīriya compared his endurance of insect bites in the mountain forests to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>elephant in battle</b></a> enduring the weapons of war? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gahvaratīriya declared that even while being stung by forest insects, he remained living there <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>mindfully</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gahvaratīriya attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> shortly after entering the Ratīra forest to develop insight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.31/en/sujato">Thag 1.31</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya was born into a family of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>cemetery keepers</b></a> in the city of Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya was moved to ordain after hearing the Dhamma taught by his childhood friend, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sopāka</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya described his spiritual transition as having been aged and then reaching the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>unaging</b></a> state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya sought to reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>supreme peace</b></a> as he struggled to achieve his final spiritual goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya aspired to attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed security</b></a> from bondage, which is a synonym for Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya used the metaphor of being "burned" by worldly existence and reaching the state that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>tranquil</b></a> and cool? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya successfully reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>highest fruit</b></a> of the holy life through his dedicated practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suppiya’s realization focused on being fully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato"><b>liberated from suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.32/en/sujato">Thag 1.32</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka had a miraculous survival as an infant when his mother fell unconscious during labor and was mistakenly taken to be burned on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>funeral pyre</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when people tried to cremate the mother of Venerable Sopāka, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>non-human being</b></a> caused a storm to stop the fire and save the child? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Sopāka literally refers to the fact that he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>born in a cemetery</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka was only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>seven years old</b></a> when the Buddha used his divine eye to find him and visit him in the charnel ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally went to the cemetery to teach Venerable Sopāka because he saw the child possessed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>wholesome roots</b></a> from past lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka was given "brotherly love" or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> (mettā) as his specific subject for meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his meditation in the cemetery, Venerable Sopāka achieved <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> while still a young child? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka taught that one’s love should be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>all-encompassing</b></a>, covering all realms and all beings at all times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka famously compared the ideal heart of loving-kindness to the love a mother feels for her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>only child</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka grew up alongside the cemetery keeper’s son, and they both eventually became monks and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>attained enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka insisted that spiritual love should make <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>no distinction</b></a> between those who are close and those who are strangers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka was provided with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>suitable food</b></a> and care by a protective spirit after he was abandoned as an infant in the charnel ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka only ordained after he received the formal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato"><b>permission</b></a> of his foster father, the cemetery keeper? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.33/en/sujato">Thag 1.33</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato"><b>Punna</b></a> was originally a merchant from the country of Sunāparanta who decided to go forth into homelessness after hearing the Buddha preach at Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato">Thag 1.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Punna was highly esteemed among his teachers because of his exceptional ability and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato"><b>skill in debate</b></a> and exposition of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato">Thag 1.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Punna requested a teaching to take back to his home country, the Buddha gave him a discourse on the sense bases that was described as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato"><b>lion's roar</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato">Thag 1.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after attaining Arahantship, Venerable Punna was able to convert more than <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato"><b>five hundred</b></a> male and female lay followers in his native land of Sunāparanta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato">Thag 1.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Punna declared that while virtue is the most excellent thing in the world, the person who possesses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> is truly supreme? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato">Thag 1.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Punna, one who is complete in both virtue and wisdom is a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato"><b>victor</b></a> over the realms of both humans and celestial beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato">Thag 1.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Punna attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato"><b>three true knowledges</b></a> after retiring to a secluded place to develop insight meditation following his instruction from the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato">Thag 1.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Punna emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> is the most excellent quality, yet it is through wisdom that one reaches the highest state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.70/en/sujato">Thag 1.70</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato"><b>Vacchapāla</b></a> was born into a wealthy Brahmin family in Rājagaha and was inspired to ordain after witnessing the meeting between King Bimbisāra and the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato">Thag 1.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vacchapāla’s faith was firmly established after he saw the Buddha subdue the powerful ascetic <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato">Thag 1.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that within just one week of his ordination, Venerable Vacchapāla developed insight and attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato">Thag 1.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vacchapāla stated that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> is not difficult to attain for those who live in accordance with the training taught by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato">Thag 1.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vacchapāla taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato"><b>subtle truth</b></a> is exquisite and can only be seen by those who possess skillful wisdom and a humble mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato">Thag 1.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Vacchapāla, success in the spiritual life is achieved by those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato"><b>skillful with wisdom</b></a> and live according to the Buddha's discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato">Thag 1.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vacchapāla described the Dhamma as being reached by those who possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato"><b>humility of mind</b></a> while following the path of the Awakened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato">Thag 1.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vacchapāla celebrated his attainment of Arahantship by praising the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato"><b>viability</b></a> of the Buddha’s path for those who are diligent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.71/en/sujato">Thag 1.71</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato"><b>Atuma</b></a> was the son of a councillor in Sāvatthī who chose to go forth into the monkhood because he was weary of the household life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato">Thag 1.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his ordination, the mother of Venerable Atuma repeatedly tried to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato"><b>tempt</b></a> him to return to a secular life as a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato">Thag 1.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Atuma compared his growth in the spiritual life to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato"><b>bamboo shoot</b></a> that has grown so large it can no longer be held back by surrounding branches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato">Thag 1.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while he was in the middle of a conversation with his mother about a marriage proposal, Venerable Atuma developed insight and attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato">Thag 1.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Atuma used the imagery of a plant's growth to explain that it was impossible for him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato"><b>take a wife</b></a> now that he had matured in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato">Thag 1.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Atuma successfully abandoned all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato"><b>corruptions</b></a> and defilements at the very moment he was asserting his commitment to his monastic life to his family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato">Thag 1.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Atuma’s resolve was so firm that he requested his family to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato"><b>be content</b></a> with his decision to remain a renunciant, as he had already moved past the stage of domestic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.72/en/sujato">Thag 1.72</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>Brahmāli</b></a> was born into a Brahmin family in the kingdom of Kosala before he renounced the world to seek spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the decision of Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>Brahmāli</b></a> to go forth into the homeless life was prompted by mature spiritual conditions and the influence of good friends? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after practicing meditation in the seclusion of the forest, Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>Brahmāli</b></a> successfully developed insight and attained the six supernormal knowledges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>Brahmāli</b></a> was recognized for being highly proficient in the faculty of energy while living his life as a forest-dwelling monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>Brahmāli</b></a> once spoke verses on behalf of other forest monks to praise the virtues of persistent effort and the taming of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>Brahmāli</b></a> compared his stilled sense faculties to well-trained horses that have been perfectly disciplined by a skilled charioteer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>Brahmāli</b></a>, even the celestial devas hold in high regard those whose pride has been eliminated and who are free from the mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>Brahmāli</b></a> declared himself to have achieved a state where his conceit was entirely abandoned and his mind was no longer subject to the outflows of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before meeting the Buddha, Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Mogharāja</b></a> was a student under the guidance of the prominent Brahmin teacher Bāvarī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Mogharāja</b></a> was one of the sixteen selected disciples sent by Bāvarī to have an audience with and ask questions of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Mogharāja</b></a> attained the full enlightenment of an Arahant immediately after receiving the Buddha's answers to his spiritual questions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha designated Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Mogharāja</b></a> as the foremost among his disciples in the practice of wearing coarse robes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the robes worn by Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Mogharāja</b></a> were made from rough, discarded materials thrown away by travelers, tailors, and dyers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that due to a combination of past karma and a lack of care, Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Mogharāja</b></a> suffered from numerous painful skin sores that erupted across his body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that feeling his usual residence was contaminated by his illness, Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Mogharāja</b></a> chose to sleep on a simple bed placed in a field in the Magadhan countryside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Mogharāja</b></a> continued to live in the open fields of Magadha even during the biting cold of the winter nights? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once addressed Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Mogharāja</b></a> in verse, asking how a monk with such rough skin could endure the freezing winter in a straw hut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his physical suffering, Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Mogharāja</b></a> maintained a benevolent mind and remained constantly devoted to the practice of meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Mogharāja</b></a> informed the Buddha that even a humble straw-thatched hut in Magadha was far better than the worldly happiness experienced by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Mogharāja</b></a> viewed his simple life in a prosperous land like Magadha as the ultimate fulfillment of his spiritual aspirations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the spiritual success of Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>Brahmāli</b></a> was described as the result of his mastery over his own sense faculties through meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the physical ailments endured by Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato"><b>Mogharāja</b></a> were specifically attributed to the ripening of his previous life's karma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.44/en/sujato">Thag 2.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato"><b>Brahmāli</b></a> emphasized that a person with a tamed mind and stilled senses becomes an object of affection for the gods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.43/en/sujato">Thag 2.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin family</b></a> in the city of Sāvatthī before he encountered the Buddha and sought ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before becoming a monk, Venerable Vakkali had already mastered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>three Vedas</b></a> and was considered proficient in traditional Brahminical accomplishments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali initially joined the monastic order because he was so captivated by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>physical beauty</b></a> of the Buddha that he never wanted to stop looking at him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once famously told Venerable Vakkali that the person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>sees the Dhamma</b></a> is the one who truly sees the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha questioned Venerable Vakkali about the benefit of gazing at his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>foul body</b></a>, encouraging the monk to see the truth of the teachings instead? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha eventually told Venerable Vakkali to leave his presence to encourage his spiritual maturity, the monk became so distressed that he intended to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>commit suicide</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali went to the mountain of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>Gijjhakūṭa</b></a>, also known as Vulture Peak, with the intention of throwing himself off a precipice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha psychicly appeared to Venerable Vakkali to prevent his suicide, offering him <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>verses of encouragement</b></a> regarding the joy of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to one tradition, Venerable Vakkali attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> while suspended in the air after the Buddha called out to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali suffered from a severe <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>wind disease</b></a> while living in the forest, which caused his body to become emaciated and weak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite having limited <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> and a failing body, Venerable Vakkali remained committed to his life in the seclusion of the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali described his internal state as being pervaded by an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>abundant rapture</b></a> that sustained him through physical illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali practiced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>four foundations of mindfulness</b></a> (Satipaṭṭhāna) as a core part of his spiritual training in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>five faculties</b></a> (Indriya) was essential to the progress Venerable Vakkali made during his solitary practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali utilized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>five powers</b></a> (Bala) to strengthen his resolve and overcome the physical paralysis caused by his illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali focused on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> (Bojjhaṅga) to cultivate the wisdom necessary for his final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali drew great inspiration from seeing his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>fellow practitioners</b></a> living in harmony and with vigorous energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even when physically thin and weak, Venerable Vakkali maintained a spirit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>persistent energy</b></a> and remained dedicated to his meditation day and night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali constantly practiced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Buddha</b></a> (Buddhānussati) as a way to avoid laziness and stay focused on his goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali referred to the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>Supreme Tamer</b></a> (Anuttaro Damasārathi) when reflecting on the Buddha's ability to guide beings to peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his physical pain, Venerable Vakkali declared he would find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>spiritual joy</b></a> in the forest by following the path of the Awakened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's specific intervention was meant to prevent Venerable Vakkali from destroying his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>potential for enlightenment</b></a> through an act of despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali finally attained the goal of the holy life through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>development of insight</b></a> while reflecting on the meditation subjects given by the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the story of Venerable Vakkali serves as a powerful lesson on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>overcoming attachment</b></a> to the physical form of a teacher in favor of the truth they teach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali once spent all his time bathing and eating just to be in the proximity of the Buddha to observe his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>physical features</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali faced the challenge of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>lean body</b></a> and physical exhaustion, yet found the strength to continue his practice in the deep woods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used a sharp warning to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>awaken</b></a> Venerable Vakkali from his stagnation, eventually leading the monk to achieve the highest fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vakkali expressed that he would live in the forest because he was confident in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's Dispensation</b></a> and the peace it brings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.8/en/sujato">Thag 5.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin family</b></a> in Kapilavatthu and was inspired to go forth after witnessing the Buddha’s power during a visit to his family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin felt moved to praise the Buddha as a "Great Elephant" after hearing the king’s <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>elephant Sela</b></a> being extolled, realizing that the Buddha possessed far superior qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin described the Buddha as a human being who attained a supreme state by being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>self-controlled</b></a> and finding joy in a purified mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Udāyin, both humans and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>celestial beings</b></a> pay homage to the Buddha because he has reached the "far shore" of liberation and understood all phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin characterized the Buddha’s spiritual journey as passing beyond all fetters and moving from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>forest to Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin compared the Buddha's delight in being free from sensual lust to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>pure gold</b></a> that has been successfully extracted and separated from its ore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin used the imagery of a magnificent elephant standing on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>Himalayan peak</b></a> to illustrate the Buddha's supreme and radiant presence among all beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin explained that the Buddha is truly called a "Nāga" (Great Elephant) because he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>does no evil</b></a> in any circumstance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the symbolic description of the Buddha as an elephant, Venerable Udāyin identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>gentleness and harmlessness</b></a> as the two front feet of this Great Nāga? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a> serve as the other two supportive feet of the metaphorical "Great Elephant" Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the poetic imagery of Venerable Udāyin, the quality of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> is represented by the very body of the Great Elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> as the beautiful white tusks of the Great Elephant that represents the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin linked the faculty of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> to the elephant’s throat in his detailed spiritual anatomy of the Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in the verses of Venerable Udāyin, the quality of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> is compared to the head of the Great Elephant Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin used the elephant's trunk as a metaphor for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>investigation of Dhamma</b></a> and the capacity for deep reflection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin described the "belly" of the spiritual elephant as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>harmonious living</b></a> within the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin compared the essential practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> to the tail of the Great Elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin emphasized that the Great Elephant Buddha remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>internally composed</b></a> whether he is walking, standing, sitting, or lying down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin stated that the Buddha eats only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>blameless food</b></a> and refuses to store or hoard any offerings he receives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Udāyin, an enlightened being has severed all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>ties and bonds</b></a> and can walk anywhere without expectation or attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin used the classic analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>lotus flower</b></a>, which grows in water but remains untouched by it, to describe the Buddha's purity while living in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin compared the state of Nibbāna to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>flaming fire</b></a> that naturally goes out when it no longer has fuel to sustain it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin taught that by abandoning the physical body and reaching <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>final Nibbāna</b></a>, the Great Elephant becomes entirely free from all mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Udāyin explained that the Buddha is free from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato"><b>greed, hatred, and delusion</b></a>, which are the root causes that lead to the persistence of taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag15.2/en/sujato">Thag 15.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Posiya, who was the brother of the famous Elder Saṅgāmaji and the son of a millionaire in Sāvatthī, decided to go forth into the homeless life because he was struck by a profound sense of urgency regarding the nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato"><b>birth and death</b></a> shortly after his own son was born? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato">Thag 1.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after ordaining and living alone in the forest to practice meditation on the Four Noble Truths, Venerable Posiya returned to Sāvatthī to pay respects to the Buddha and found himself being tempted by his former wife who tried to lure him back to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato"><b>lay life</b></a> with offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato">Thag 1.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Posiya recognized the hidden intentions of his former wife when she tried to entice him during a visit to his old home, leading him to immediately depart and return to the forest without a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato"><b>backward glance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato">Thag 1.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Posiya’s fellow monks questioned why he returned to the forest so quickly after visiting his family, he explained his realization that for a wise person, it is better to maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato"><b>distance</b></a> from the distractions of the village? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato">Thag 1.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Posiya specifically practiced the meditation on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> while living in solitude in the forest, which eventually led him to the attainment of Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato">Thag 1.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Posiya utilized a verse to express his liberation, noting that a wise man knows that leaving the village for the forest, and then leaving the house again with detachment, is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato"><b>best path</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato">Thag 1.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his wealthy background as a millionaire’s son, Venerable Posiya chose to live as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato"><b>forest dweller</b></a> to focus entirely on his spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato">Thag 1.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Posiya’s swift departure from his old home and his ex-wife's advances served as a testament to his status as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> who was no longer bound by worldly attachments or familial ties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.34/en/sujato">Thag 1.34</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāmaññakāni, the son of a wanderer, was inspired to attain Arahantship after witnessing the Buddha perform the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato"><b>Twin Miracle</b></a> (Yamaka Pāṭihāriya) and subsequently devoting himself to meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato">Thag 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when an old friend named Kātiyāna, a destitute wanderer, asked for help in finding happiness, Venerable Sāmaññakāni responded that true happiness is not found in the world but is enjoyed by those who understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato"><b>path of practice</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato">Thag 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāmaññakāni taught that if a person’s conduct is upright and correct, they will not only find happiness but will also see their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato"><b>fame and respect</b></a> grow in the eyes of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato">Thag 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sāmaññakāni, the specific journey that leads one to the attainment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato"><b>Deathless state</b></a> (Nibbāna) is the cultivation of the Noble Eightfold Path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato">Thag 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāmaññakāni emphasized that the pure peace of spiritual attainment is reserved for those who have mastered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato"><b>upright way</b></a> of living and practicing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato">Thag 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāmaññakāni explained to his friend Kātiyāna that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato"><b>unalloyed bliss</b></a> is only possible for those who have gone beyond worldly concerns through the Buddhist training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato">Thag 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāmaññakāni used his own spiritual success as proof to demonstrate that following the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato"><b>Eightfold Path</b></a> is the only way to reach the ultimate goal of the religious life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato">Thag 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāmaññakāni’s encounter with Kātiyāna highlights the contrast between the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato"><b>worldly poverty</b></a> of a non-Buddhist wanderer and the spiritual wealth of an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.35/en/sujato">Thag 1.35</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that although his name was Nanda, Venerable Kummāputta was commonly known by a name meaning "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato"><b>son of Kummā</b></a>" because of the great respect or recognition given to his mother in their home city of Veḷukaṇḍaka? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato">Thag 1.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kummāputta first went forth into the homeless life after hearing a Dhamma talk by the Buddha’s chief disciple, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato">Thag 1.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Venerable Kummāputta practiced diligently on a hillside, he only reached the final stage of Arahantship after the Buddha personally intervened to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato"><b>correct his method</b></a> of meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato">Thag 1.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after becoming an Arahant, Venerable Kummāputta observed other monks becoming excessively concerned with the care of their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato"><b>physical bodies</b></a> and used his verses to redirect their focus toward the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato">Thag 1.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kummāputta praised the "homeless life" as a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato"><b>happy dwelling</b></a> where one is free from the burdens of domestic responsibilities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato">Thag 1.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kummāputta taught that a vital part of the ascetic's practice is to constantly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato"><b>inquire into the meaning</b></a> of the Dhamma while maintaining a lifestyle of respect and humility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato">Thag 1.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kummāputta defined the true duty of a seeker as the practice of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato"><b>possessionsless one</b></a>, who finds joy in hearing the truth and living in accordance with it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.36/en/sujato">Thag 1.36</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māṇava was a Brahmin from Sāvatthī who spent the first <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato"><b>seven years</b></a> of his life confined strictly within his home before ever seeing the outside world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato">Thag 1.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māṇava decided to go forth into the homeless life after witnessing the sight of an old person, a sick person, and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato"><b>dead person</b></a> for the first time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato">Thag 1.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māṇava declared that his primary motivation for leaving the household life was the profound <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato"><b>fear</b></a> he felt upon seeing the physical realities of human aging and mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato">Thag 1.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māṇava, after seeing a person whose life force was exhausted, chose to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato"><b>abandon</b></a> all sensual desires that were once attractive and pleasing to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato">Thag 1.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māṇava attained the state of an Arahant after developing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> following his ordination as a youth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato">Thag 1.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when other monks questioned why he ordained at such a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato"><b>young age</b></a>, Venerable Māṇava responded by declaring his final knowledge through a verse about the nature of aging and sickness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato">Thag 1.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māṇava explicitly stated that he left the world to escape <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato"><b>sensual desires</b></a> after realizing the transient nature of a life that ends in death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.73/en/sujato">Thag 1.73</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suyāman, though highly skilled in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.74/en/sujato"><b>three Vedas</b></a> as a Brahmin's son, found no satisfaction in the traditional household life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.74/en/sujato">Thag 1.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suyāman is said to have attained the fruit of Arahantship at the very moment his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.74/en/sujato"><b>hair was shaved</b></a> for his ordination into the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.74/en/sujato">Thag 1.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suyāman proclaimed his spiritual victory by stating he had completely and permanently abandoned the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.74/en/sujato"><b>five hindrances</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.74/en/sujato">Thag 1.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suyāman described himself as a bhikkhu who had entirely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.74/en/sujato"><b>eradicated</b></a> sensual desire, ill-will, sloth and torpor, restlessness and remorse, and doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.74/en/sujato">Thag 1.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Suyāman first established his faith in the Buddha when they met in the town of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.74/en/sujato"><b>Sāli</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.74/en/sujato">Thag 1.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his elite education in Brahmanical scriptures, Venerable Suyāman preferred the quiet path of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.74/en/sujato"><b>meditation</b></a> over worldly domestic responsibilities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.74/en/sujato">Thag 1.74</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Susārada was born in the same village as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.75/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> and was later converted and guided to enlightenment by him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.75/en/sujato">Thag 1.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Susārada was given to him because he was observed to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.75/en/sujato"><b>grow slowly</b></a> during his childhood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.75/en/sujato">Thag 1.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Susārada taught that associating with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.75/en/sujato"><b>good people</b></a> is so transformative that it can even change a fool into a wise person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.75/en/sujato">Thag 1.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Susārada praised the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.75/en/sujato"><b>vision</b></a> of those who have trained themselves, noting that such clear insight cuts through all internal doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.75/en/sujato">Thag 1.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Susārada, the cultivation of spiritual practice leads directly to the increase of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.75/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> and the removal of mental confusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.75/en/sujato">Thag 1.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Susārada attained the status of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.75/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> shortly after receiving personal instruction from Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.75/en/sujato">Thag 1.75</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piyañjaha was originally a noble from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato"><b>Licchavi</b></a> clan in Vesālī who possessed a great passion for war and combat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato">Thag 1.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Piyañjaha means "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato"><b>abandoner of the dear</b></a>" because as a fierce soldier he was famously willing to sacrifice even his most beloved possessions and relatives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato">Thag 1.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piyañjaha advised that when other people are proud and arrogant, a practitioner should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato"><b>humble</b></a> them, but when people are lowly, one should uplift their spirits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato">Thag 1.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piyañjaha lived as a dedicated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato"><b>forest dweller</b></a> after being inspired to ordain by the Buddha's visit to Vesālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato">Thag 1.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piyañjaha taught that a serious spiritual practitioner should choose to live in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato"><b>places</b></a> that ordinary people typically avoid or find undesirable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato">Thag 1.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piyañjaha cautioned monks that they should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato"><b>not delight</b></a> in preferred seats or positions of comfort and honor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato">Thag 1.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piyañjaha, after attaining enlightenment, reflected on the vast difference between the success found in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato"><b>worldly life</b></a> and the success of a Noble One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.76/en/sujato">Thag 1.76</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Visākha was not only the son of a local king in Magadha but was also commonly known by the name of his mother's family as the son of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato"><b>Pañcālī</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato">Thag 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his father passed away, Venerable Visākha initially succeeded him as king before renouncing the throne to follow the Buddha and eventually attaining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato">Thag 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Visākha taught that a true speaker of the Dhamma should never be conceited or despise others, but should remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato"><b>unshakeable</b></a> and humble? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato">Thag 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Visākha, a practitioner who has reached the "farther shore" should refrain from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato"><b>praising oneself</b></a> in the presence of assemblies? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato">Thag 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Visākha emphasized that a wise person is one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken</b></a> and well-restrained, avoiding the harm of others through their speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato">Thag 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Visākha described a person who is skilled in wisdom as someone who sees the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato"><b>meaning</b></a> and remains subtle and discreet in their conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato">Thag 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Visākha stated that for one who keeps their lifestyle well-guarded and carries out the training of the Buddhas, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> is not difficult to attain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato">Thag 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Visākha returned to his hometown out of compassion for his relatives, he was asked about the essential <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato"><b>virtues</b></a> required for someone to be a preacher of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato">Thag 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Visākha taught that one should not be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato"><b>arrogant</b></a>, but should instead practice self-control and modesty when addressing a crowd? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato">Thag 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Visākha highlighted the importance of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato"><b>skilled in wisdom</b></a> as a primary factor for reaching the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato">Thag 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Visākha defined a person who has reached the "other shore" as one who does not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato"><b>despise</b></a> or harm any living being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato">Thag 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Visākha suggested that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato"><b>discreet</b></a> and subtle is a necessary trait for those who wish to understand the deep meaning of the Buddha's teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato">Thag 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Visākha believed that the path to liberation is paved by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato"><b>well-guarded life</b></a> consistent with the precepts of the Enlightened Ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.45/en/sujato">Thag 2.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cūlaka, the son of a Brahmin from Rājagaha, decided to renounce the world after witnessing the Buddha courageously <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato"><b>taming the elephant</b></a> Dhanapāla? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato">Thag 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cūlaka practiced meditation in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato"><b>Indra-sāla cave</b></a>, where he once sat watching the storm clouds gather over the fields of Magadha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato">Thag 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cūlaka found spiritual inspiration in the sound of peacocks, noting how they sing their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato"><b>ke-la</b></a> song and dance joyfully when the thunder rolls? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato">Thag 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cūlaka described the peacocks that delighted him as having beautiful crests, wonderful tail feathers, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato"><b>green-blue necks</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato">Thag 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cūlaka observed how the rain-covered sky and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato"><b>grass-covered earth</b></a> provided a serene and pleasant atmosphere for his deep meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato">Thag 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while sitting in his cave during a storm, Venerable Cūlaka felt a sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato"><b>delight</b></a> and physical comfort that helped his mind enter a state of concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato">Thag 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cūlaka remarked on having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato"><b>healthy body</b></a> fit for exertion and a mind well-disposed toward the practice of meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato">Thag 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cūlaka encouraged himself to "touch" or experience the path leading to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato"><b>Deathless</b></a>, which he described as the supreme and pure way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato">Thag 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cūlaka described the Buddha's teaching as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato"><b>subtle</b></a> and very fine path that is truly well-seen by those who practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato">Thag 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for Venerable Cūlaka, the sight of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato"><b>storm clouds</b></a> and the sound of thunder were not distractions but rather catalysts for his final attainment of Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato">Thag 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cūlaka spoke of the path of the Buddha as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato"><b>white and healthy</b></a>, leading one away from the impurities of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato">Thag 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cūlaka used the imagery of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato"><b>well-covered sky</b></a> and the moist earth to illustrate the perfect conditions he found for his spiritual awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato">Thag 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cūlaka concluded his verses with a joyful declaration of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a> after reviewing his successful progress on the path to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.46/en/sujato">Thag 2.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before he became a monk, Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> was born into a family of expert elephant trainers in the country of Kosala? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> decided to go forth into the homeless life after witnessing the Buddha perform the Twin Miracle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> had two maternal uncles named Sena and Upasena who were both Arahants and served as his teachers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> vowed to restrain his mind by metaphorically "locking the door," just as one would prevent an elephant from escaping? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> addressed his own mind as a "sorcerer" or "illusionist" and commanded it to stop wandering into evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> compared his mental discipline to a handler using a hook to forcefully restrain an untamed elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> resolved to transform his mind with effort, even if it was unwilling, like a person using force to reform a stubborn individual? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> compared his mental mastery to an excellent horse trainer who skillfully manages a fine steed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> relied on the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Five Powers</b></a>—faith, energy, mindfulness, concentration, and wisdom—to train his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> pledged to bind his mind with firm mindfulness so that it would never again stray away from his spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> used the "weight of energy" to apply pressure to his mind, ensuring it would not wander into unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> recognized that the mind is a "net of desire" that is born from the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> warned his mind that no matter how hard it tried, he would no longer allow it to take delight in evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato"><b>Vigitasena</b></a> successfully used his professional background in elephant training as a metaphor for achieving full mental liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.9/en/sujato">Thag 5.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> was the eldest of three Brahmin brothers who were all highly proficient in the three Vedas before becoming ascetics? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> and his two brothers originally left the Brahmin life because they found their scriptures to be worldly and devoid of ultimate truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> was the leader of five hundred matted-hair ascetics before he met the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> received his name specifically because he resided in the region of Uruvelā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the conversion of Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> began after the Buddha performed several miracles, including the subjugation of a fierce serpent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite seeing the Buddha's miracles, Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> was initially so blinded by envy and pride that he refused to acknowledge the Buddha’s superiority? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> was thrown into a state of panic and "hair-raising" awe when the Buddha openly questioned him about his private thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> eventually realized that his own previous psychic powers were "insignificant" and "useless" compared to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> and his brothers were converted to the Buddha's path along with a total of one thousand disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> used to find satisfaction in animal sacrifices before he learned to uproot lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> achieved Arahantship after hearing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Fire Sermon</b></a> (the Ādittapariyāya Sutta) from the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> attained the "three knowledges," which include the memory of past lives and the purified divine eye? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> gained the divine ear, a psychic power that allowed him to hear sounds beyond the normal human range? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> also developed the psychic ability to know the thoughts and minds of other people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato"><b>Uruvela Kassapa</b></a> declared that he had attained the ultimate goal of the homeless life by destroying all of his fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.1/en/sujato">Thag 6.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya reflected on the spiritual decline of the community while sitting alone in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>meditative absorption</b></a> in the Great Wood (Mahāvana) when the forest was in full bloom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya observed a significant change in the behavior and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>deportment of monks</b></a> compared to the era when the Buddha, the Supreme Man, was still alive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Pārāpariya, the former monks used robes only to ward off the cold and to cover their nakedness, practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>strict moderation</b></a> in their use of requisites? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya noted that the elder monks of the past were satisfied with whatever food they received, whether it was refined or coarse, eating only to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>sustain life</b></a> without greed or intoxication? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya described the early disciples as being less concerned with material supplies or medicines and more focused on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>destruction of taints</b></a> (āsavas)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya praised the ancient monks for living at the roots of trees, in caves, and in mountain clefts, making <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> their primary aim? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya characterized the early practitioners as being modest, gentle, and endowed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>unwavering patience</b></a> in their spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya highlighted that the elder monks possessed elegant manners, avoided irrelevant speech, and always directed their thoughts toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>welfare of others</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya compared the graceful and mindful movements of the early monks, whether walking or eating, to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>smoothness of oil</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya lamented that the great elders who were masters of meditation and had destroyed all taints have now reached <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>final Nibbāna</b></a>, leaving behind very few like them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya warned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>good Dhamma</b></a> and the wisdom of the practitioners are wearing away, leading to the gradual collapse of the Buddha’s teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya observed that in a time of defilement and evil qualities, it becomes increasingly difficult for those seeking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> to attain realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya compared monks who are overcome by defilements and play with fools to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>demons</b></a> (Rakkhasas) who toy with those who have lost their minds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya described corrupt monks as people who run after stains and shout loudly for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>material possessions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya criticized those who abandon the true Dhamma to engage in quarrels and follow <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>wrong views</b></a>, believing their own opinions to be superior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya pointed out that some who have left behind family and wealth still perform <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>unworthy tasks</b></a> just for the sake of a spoonful of food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya spoke against monks who stuff their bellies with food, lie down to sleep, and speak words that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>Teacher censured</b></a> upon waking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya noted that some practitioners neglect the inner peace of the ascetic life to focus on learning <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>secular arts and crafts</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya criticized monks who give gifts such as oil, powder, and water to laypeople in the hope of receiving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>greater material returns</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya observed monks trading tooth-sticks, flowers, and various fruits like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>Kapittha</b></a> to secure their livelihoods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya mentioned that some monks would give away mangoes and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>Āmalaka</b></a> fruits as a means to gain more offerings from the laity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya compared monks who focus on administering medicines to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>secular physicians</b></a> rather than spiritual healers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya warned against practitioners who dress in fancy robes like courtesans and display <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>arrogant authority</b></a> like members of a royal family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya condemned the use of fraud, deception, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>false witness</b></a> by some monks to acquire wealth and resources? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya observed that many monks in his time used various <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>schemes and tricks</b></a> to amass material possessions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya noted that some monks gather followers for the sake of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>career or trade</b></a> rather than for the sincere practice of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya criticized those who preach to the masses for the sake of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>profit</b></a> rather than to convey the true spiritual meaning of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya observed monks quarreling with outsiders over the rights and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>property of the Sangha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya lamented the lack of shame and conscience in those who live off the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>labor of others</b></a> without moral integrity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya pointed out that many who wear the robe and have shaven heads are actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>lacking in faith</b></a>, seeking only fame and honor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya believed that due to current corruption, it is no longer easy to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>attain realizations</b></a> or maintain what has already been achieved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya taught that a monk entering a village should maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>constant mindfulness</b></a>, just as a barefoot person must be careful when walking on a path covered in thorns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya encouraged monks to remember the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>lifestyle of the ancients</b></a> in order to succeed in the Dhamma even in later times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya emphasized that by following the path of the early disciples, one can still touch the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>Deathless state</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya delivered these verses while his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>faculties were well-developed</b></a> and he was residing in a Sāla forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya, referred to as an "ascetic who has cut off rebirth," eventually reached <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>final Nibbāna</b></a> after sharing his reflections on the decline of the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya criticized monks who were more interested in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>aesthetic beauty</b></a> and fine clothes than in spiritual discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya lamented that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>Supreme Man's teaching</b></a>, which is the ultimate of all things, is now falling into ruin due to the negligence of practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya described the current era as one where evil qualities have become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>deeply ingrained</b></a> within the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya urged monks to remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato"><b>secluded and vigilant</b></a> despite the negative influences and defilements of the surrounding world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.10/en/sujato">Thag 16.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kumāputtasahāya (a friend of the son of Kumā) warned that traveling through many different regions without a focused mind can effectively <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato"><b>burn away concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato">Thag 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kumāputtasahāya was originally a wealthy man from Velukaṇḍa named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato"><b>Vasulokī</b></a> before he was inspired to join the Sangha by his friend's ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato">Thag 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kumāputtasahāya urged practitioners to stop mental wandering and agitation, teaching that one should practice meditation without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato"><b>aimless intent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato">Thag 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while living on a hillside, Venerable Kumāputtasahāya used the noise and disturbance caused by a large group of wandering monks as a motivation to deepen his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato"><b>spiritual striving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato">Thag 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kumāputtasahāya questioned the benefit of wandering across the country if a monk's behavior remains characterized by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato"><b>unfocused thoughts</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato">Thag 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kumāputtasahāya decided to ordain after hearing the Buddha preach, realizing that the path followed by his friend, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato"><b>son of Kumā</b></a>, was of great significance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato">Thag 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kumāputtasahāya attained his realization while living in the town of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato"><b>Velukaṇḍa</b></a>, having been born into a prosperous family there? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato">Thag 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verse of Venerable Kumāputtasahāya emphasizes that the prevention of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato"><b>mental agitation</b></a> is essential for the successful cultivation of dhyāna (meditative absorption)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.37/en/sujato">Thag 1.37</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gavampati was one of the four close friends of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato"><b>Yasa</b></a> who all ordained and attained Arahantship together after hearing of Yasa’s own renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato">Thag 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Venerable Gavampati for using his psychic powers to stop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato"><b>Sarabhū River</b></a> from rising, thereby protecting a group of young monks from a flood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato">Thag 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gavampati lived in the Añjana Forest at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato"><b>Sāketa</b></a>, where he spent his time dwelling in the bliss of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato">Thag 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his spiritual power, Venerable Gavampati was able to make a rushing river stand still like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato"><b>mountain</b></a>, a feat that led to his widespread reputation for great supernatural abilities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato">Thag 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Venerable Gavampati as a great sage who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato"><b>unshakable</b></a> and has successfully crossed over all worldly bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato">Thag 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato"><b>gods pay homage</b></a> to Venerable Gavampati because he has transcended the cycle of birth and existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato">Thag 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gavampati intervened to save fellow monks sleeping on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato"><b>sandy banks</b></a> of a river at the specific request of the Buddha during a midnight flood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato">Thag 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gavampati is characterized as a "Great Sage" (Muni) who lives without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato"><b>dependence</b></a> on the world or the senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.38/en/sujato">Thag 1.38</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa was the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato"><b>son of the Buddha’s aunt</b></a> and was born in Kapilavatthu before joining the Buddhist order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato">Thag 1.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> to see Venerable Tissa sleeping with his mouth open and immediately manifested before him to wake him up with a verse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato">Thag 1.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha exhorted Venerable Tissa to practice mindfulness as urgently as if his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato"><b>head were on fire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato">Thag 1.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after ordaining, Venerable Tissa initially struggled with being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato"><b>conceited</b></a> about his status and having an irritable, lazy temperament? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato">Thag 1.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the necessary urgency of a monk's practice to the feeling of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato"><b>struck by a sword</b></a>, urging Venerable Tissa to abandon all desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato">Thag 1.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa was living in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato"><b>forest hermitage</b></a> when the Buddha decided to personally intervene to correct his lack of spiritual enthusiasm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato">Thag 1.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically instructed Venerable Tissa to maintain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> as the primary means to eliminate sensual desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato">Thag 1.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's direct intervention helped Venerable Tissa overcome his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato"><b>unstable temper</b></a> and fulfill his duties as a dedicated monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.39/en/sujato">Thag 1.39</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Hatthārohaputta was born in Sāvatthī to a family of elephant trainers and became an expert in the craft before realizing it was more important to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.77/en/sujato"><b>subdue the self</b></a> than to subdue wild beasts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.77/en/sujato">Thag 1.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his enlightenment, Venerable Hatthārohaputta described his own mind as a wandering entity that would roam wherever it pleased and found <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.77/en/sujato"><b>delight</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.77/en/sujato">Thag 1.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Hatthārohaputta compared the practice of mental restraint to a mahout using a hook to bring a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.77/en/sujato"><b>wild elephant</b></a> from the forest under control? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.77/en/sujato">Thag 1.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Hatthārohaputta achieved the mastery of his mind by applying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.77/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> (yoniso manasikāra) to restrain his thoughts from wandering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.77/en/sujato">Thag 1.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Hatthārohaputta based his development of insight meditation on a foundation of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.77/en/sujato"><b>discipline</b></a> (vinaya) to prevent his thoughts from straying from his meditation subject? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.77/en/sujato">Thag 1.77</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Meṇḍasira literally means "Ram’s Head," a name he received because as a layman he was said to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.78/en/sujato"><b>resemble a goat</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.78/en/sujato">Thag 1.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Meṇḍasira was a citizen of the city of Sāketa who went forth into the homeless life after gaining faith in the Buddha at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.78/en/sujato"><b>Añjana Wood</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.78/en/sujato">Thag 1.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the combined practice of tranquility and insight, Venerable Meṇḍasira attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.78/en/sujato"><b>six super-knowledges</b></a> (abhiññā), which granted him profound spiritual powers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.78/en/sujato">Thag 1.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that one of the super-knowledges attained by Venerable Meṇḍasira allowed him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.78/en/sujato"><b>recollect past lives</b></a>, confirming his long journey through the round of rebirths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.78/en/sujato">Thag 1.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Meṇḍasira lamented his long history of wandering through many births, noting that he had searched in vain while trapped in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.78/en/sujato"><b>suffering of rebirth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.78/en/sujato">Thag 1.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Meṇḍasira described the nature of existence as an unstable and ever-changing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.78/en/sujato"><b>mass of suffering</b></a> (dukkha-kkhandha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.78/en/sujato">Thag 1.78</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rakkhita was born into a noble family of the Sakyan clan in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato"><b>Devadaha</b></a> before he dedicated his life to the Buddha's teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato">Thag 1.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rakkhita was part of a group of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato"><b>five hundred princes</b></a> from the Sakyan and Koliyan clans who were ordained together to serve as the Buddha’s retinue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato">Thag 1.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used the Kunāla Jātaka—a story about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato"><b>dangers of desire</b></a>—to inspire Venerable Rakkhita and his fellow Sakyan princes toward spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato">Thag 1.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rakkhita attained arahantship by linking the Buddha's lessons on the perils of sensual desire to his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato">Thag 1.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon his liberation, Venerable Rakkhita declared that every form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato"><b>sensual lust</b></a> had been completely cut off from his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato">Thag 1.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rakkhita described his state of total liberation by stating that all his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato"><b>hatred</b></a> had been entirely uprooted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato">Thag 1.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rakkhita achieved a state of mental purity where all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> had been abandoned, leading to the end of all mental fermentations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato">Thag 1.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rakkhita used the terms <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato"><b>cool and peaceful</b></a> (sītibhūto nibbuto) to characterize his final attainment of Nirvana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato">Thag 1.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rakkhita, after reflecting on his escape from all defilements, spoke a verse to express his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato"><b>attainment of final knowledge</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.79/en/sujato">Thag 1.79</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anupama was given his name, which means "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato"><b>incomparable</b></a>," because of his great physical beauty when he was born into a wealthy family in Kosala? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato">Thag 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while practicing meditation in the forest, Venerable Anupama had to rebuke his own mind for following <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato"><b>external objects</b></a> and wandering away from his meditation subjects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato">Thag 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anupama compared a mind that is obsessed with delight and pride to being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato"><b>pierced by a spear</b></a>, noting that living with such a mind is like living with weapons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato">Thag 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a moment of intense self-reflection, Venerable Anupama addressed his own mind as the "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato"><b>breaker of happiness</b></a>" and the destroyer of his life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato">Thag 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anupama warned his mind not to lead him toward harm, especially since a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato"><b>Teacher</b></a> like the Buddha is so difficult to encounter in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato">Thag 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the process of disciplining his mind and developing insight, Venerable Anupama eventually attained the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato">Thag 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anupama renounced a life of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato"><b>great wealth</b></a> in Kosala after feeling the power of spiritual causes and choosing to go forth into homelessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.47/en/sujato">Thag 2.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vajjita was born into a wealthy Kosala family after passing away from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato"><b>Brahma realm</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato">Thag 2.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as an infant, Venerable Vajjita would constantly cry while in his mother's arms because he could not endure the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato"><b>touch of a woman</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato">Thag 2.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name "Vajjita," given to the monk Venerable Vajjita, literally means "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato"><b>the one who avoids</b></a>" because of his early aversion to physical contact? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato">Thag 2.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vajjita's faith was first inspired when he witnessed the Buddha performing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato"><b>Twin Miracle</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato">Thag 2.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon attaining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a>, Venerable Vajjita reflected on his long journey through the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato">Thag 2.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vajjita described himself as having been a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato"><b>blind worldling</b></a>" during the vast amount of time he wandered through various realms without seeing the Noble Truths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato">Thag 2.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vajjita declared that he had finally broken the cycle of Saṃsāra and cut off all future destinations through a life of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato"><b>diligence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.48/en/sujato">Thag 2.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sandhita decided to go forth as a monk after hearing a teaching on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato"><b>impermanence</b></a> that filled him with a sense of spiritual urgency and fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato">Thag 2.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sandhita possessed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> and could recall his previous lives, including one during the time of the Buddha Sikhī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato">Thag 2.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a past life, Venerable Sandhita paid homage at a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato"><b>Bodhi tree</b></a> following the passing of the Buddha Sikhī, an act that planted the seeds for his future enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato">Thag 2.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sandhita achieved mindfulness by maintaining a single "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato"><b>perception of the Buddha</b></a>" while observing a lush green tree in a past life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato">Thag 2.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sandhita spent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato"><b>thirty-one eons</b></a> without fully realizing the fruit of that ancient perception of the Buddha until his current life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato">Thag 2.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sandhita attributed his final <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> to the long-term merit of a single meditative perception held many lifetimes ago? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato">Thag 2.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sandhita came from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato"><b>wealthy family</b></a> in Kosala before his "mature wisdom" led him to seek ordination under the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.49/en/sujato">Thag 2.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ajina was born into a very poor Brahmin family in Sāvatthī and was named after the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato"><b>antelope skin</b></a> he was wrapped in at birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato">Thag 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ajina's faith was sparked when he witnessed the dignified presence of the Buddha during the dedication of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato"><b>Jetavana Monastery</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato">Thag 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite becoming an Arahant and attaining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato"><b>Three Knowledges</b></a>, Venerable Ajina remained largely unknown and unrespected by others due to his past kamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato">Thag 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that some novices who were unaware of Venerable Ajina's spiritual attainments would often treat him with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato"><b>contempt</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato">Thag 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ajina observed that a person might possess the Three Knowledges and be free of taints, yet still remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato"><b>unknown</b></a> to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato">Thag 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ajina pointed out that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato"><b>foolish</b></a> may despise a person of high spiritual attainment while honoring those who have bad qualities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato">Thag 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ajina taught that an individual with wicked traits might still receive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato"><b>honor and respect</b></a> simply because they are the recipients of food and drink offerings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato">Thag 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ajina reached the state of an Arahant and achieved "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato"><b>superhuman states</b></a>" shortly after his ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.5/en/sujato">Thag 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana was only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>seven years old</b></a> when he renounced the lay life and went forth into homelessness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the father of Venerable Sumana decided to ordain his son because all his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>previous children</b></a> had died at a young age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while still a young child serving his preceptor, Venerable Sumana attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> through his spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana used his psychic powers to travel to the distant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>Lake Anotatta</b></a> to fetch water for his teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a powerful and fierce Naga king tried to block him from the water, Venerable Sumana transformed himself into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>Garuda</b></a> to subdue the serpent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, while staying at the Jetavana monastery, called upon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> to witness the young Venerable Sumana flying through the air with a water jar? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised the young Venerable Sumana for being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>well-concentrated</b></a> internally even while performing the external task of carrying water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana was the direct student and novice disciple of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Anuruddha</b></a>, one of the Buddha’s most prominent disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his youth, the Buddha described Venerable Sumana as a novice who was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>thoroughly trained</b></a> and possessed great psychic power? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana attributed his spiritual success to the excellent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>guidance</b></a> and training he received from his teacher, Venerable Anuruddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana successfully attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>supreme peace</b></a> and the unshakeable state of liberation during his time as a novice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after achieving high spiritual attainments, Venerable Sumana expressed a humble desire that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>no one</b></a> should know of his identity or greatness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana described his own conduct as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>lovely and pure</b></a> because of the way he was disciplined by his teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana subdued a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>Naga king</b></a> who had spread his hood to prevent the monk from taking water from a sacred lake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha highlighted how Venerable Sumana had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>done what had to be done</b></a>, indicating he had finished the work of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the birth of Venerable Sumana was seen as a blessing to a family of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>lay devotees</b></a> who had long supported the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana is celebrated in the Theragāthā for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>mastery</b></a> and skill in spiritual training despite his chronological age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana successfully brought back water from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato"><b>great lake</b></a> using his supernormal powers to serve his teacher’s needs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.10/en/sujato">Thag 6.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta, when captured by bandits to be killed as a sacrifice, showed no fear and remained with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>serene complexion</b></a>, unlike others who would normally tremble and cry out in such a crisis? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta explained to a bandit chief that there is no mental suffering for one who has no expectations and has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>overcome all fears</b></a> by destroying the fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta stated that he did not fear death because he had destroyed the cause of rebirth and seen all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>phenomena as they really are</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta likened the end of his life to being cured of a disease because he had successfully practiced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> and followed the well-taught path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta viewed continued existence as being as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>unpleasant as poison</b></a>, which is why he had no fear when his life was threatened? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta compared the end of his life to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>escaping a slaughterhouse</b></a>, indicating his complete lack of clinging to his current existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>highest Dhamma</b></a> and felt no sorrow at the prospect of death, comparing the experience to escaping from a house on fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta taught that one who understands the Buddha's teaching no longer <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>grasps at existence</b></a>, viewing such attachment as being like holding a red-hot iron ball? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta questioned the need for weeping when one realizes that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>conditioned formations</b></a> of the past, future, and present are not truly real or substantial? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta, while facing death at the hands of bandits, explained that he saw the world through wisdom as being like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>grass and wood</b></a>, devoid of any sense of "I" or "mine"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta expressed that he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>weary of the body</b></a> and had no desire for future existence, knowing that no new body would arise for him after death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta told his captors they could do as they wished with his body, as he would not feel any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>anger or affection</b></a> regardless of their actions toward him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the calm and wisdom of Venerable Adhimutta caused the bandit chief to drop his weapons and ask what <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>spiritual teacher</b></a> the monk followed to attain such freedom from sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta identified his teacher as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>All-Knowing Conqueror</b></a>, the Great Compassionate Teacher and the supreme physician for the whole world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the teachings of Venerable Adhimutta, a group of bandits was so moved that they abandoned their weapons and some even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>requested ordination</b></a> in the Buddha's dispensation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that those who ordained after meeting Venerable Adhimutta went on to practice the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>factors of enlightenment</b></a> and the five powers to become wise individuals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta was the nephew of Venerable Sankicca and attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> while he was still a novice monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta's fearlessness was rooted in the fact that he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>laid down the burden</b></a> of existence and achieved the complete destruction of taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta described the end of his life with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>satisfaction</b></a>, comparing his liberation to someone who has finished all their work and is free from any influx of defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta taught that for one who sees the truth, there is only the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>arising of pure phenomena</b></a> and the continuation of pure formations, leaving no room for fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the bandits who met Venerable Adhimutta eventually reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>unconditioned path to Nibbāna</b></a> through the practice he inspired in them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta's teaching highlighted that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>gathered things</b></a> and states of rebirth are subject to ending and have no escape, as declared by the Great Sage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta's encounter with the bandits occurred while he was traveling to ask his mother for permission to receive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>higher ordination</b></a> as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the bandit leader was so amazed by the lack of fear in Venerable Adhimutta that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>hair stood on end</b></a> upon hearing the monk's courageous and insightful words? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta described the Dhamma taught by the Buddha as the path that leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>attainment of the supreme</b></a> goal and a state of complete freedom from sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta remained firm and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>unsubdued</b></a> when the bandits first attacked him, which prompted the surprised leader to ask why he didn't lament in such great danger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Adhimutta practiced the holy life so well that he viewed his current body as his last, knowing that upon its breakup, there would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato"><b>no further rebirth</b></a> in any form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.1/en/sujato">Thag 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta was the son of the sister of the Buddha's first disciple, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Koṇḍañña</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta was born into a Brahmin family in a village called <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>Donavatthu</b></a>, which was located near the city of Kapilavatthu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta attained Arahantship after leaving his hometown to practice near the Buddha with the support of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>maternal uncle</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta was remarkably proficient in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>ten bases of discourse</b></a>, teaching these principles so effectively that his students also attained Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the expert guidance of Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta, all five hundred of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>clansmen disciples</b></a> successfully entered the monkhood and attained final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta as a monk who could excellently explain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>frugal life</b></a> to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta once spent time in the Andha Grove to contemplate the Dhamma, where he eventually engaged in a famous discussion with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha officially declared Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta to be the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>foremost preacher</b></a> among all the monks in the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta taught that for those seeking to escape from suffering, it is a great help to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>associate with good friends</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta emphasized that the wise realize the deep and subtle meanings of the Dhamma by being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>not heedless</b></a> and focused in their intent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the disciples of Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta traveled a distance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>sixty leagues</b></a> on foot just to pay their respects to the Buddha at the Bamboo Grove in Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta initially sent his five hundred disciples to meet the Buddha alone, preferring to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>follow later</b></a> rather than travel with such a large crowd? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta achieved the highest fruit of the spiritual life through diligent effort after completing his duties as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>novice monk</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta described the profound meaning of the Dhamma as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>subtle and difficult to see</b></a> for those who lack wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Puṇṇa Mantāṇiputta was inspired to speak his verses after reflecting with joy on his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato"><b>attained liberation</b></a> from all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.4/en/sujato">Thag 1.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaddhamāna was born into the noble family of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.40/en/sujato"><b>Licchavī king</b></a> in the city of Vesālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.40/en/sujato">Thag 1.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after joining the monkhood, Venerable Vaddhamāna initially struggled with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.40/en/sujato"><b>laziness</b></a> before receiving a stern poetic encouragement from the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.40/en/sujato">Thag 1.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged Venerable Vaddhamāna to practice with the same urgency as one whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.40/en/sujato"><b>head is on fire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.40/en/sujato">Thag 1.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaddhamāna was taught by the Buddha to abandon craving for existence with the same haste as someone avoiding a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.40/en/sujato"><b>descending sword</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.40/en/sujato">Thag 1.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaddhamāna’s spiritual breakthrough was facilitated by the Buddha’s reminder to remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.40/en/sujato"><b>mindful</b></a> in his effort to end suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.40/en/sujato">Thag 1.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, Venerable Vaddhamāna was a youth with great faith who dedicated himself to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.40/en/sujato"><b>serving the Sangha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.40/en/sujato">Thag 1.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirivaḍḍha was born in Rājagaha to a family of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato"><b>wealthy Brahmins</b></a> before deciding to renounce his worldly life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato">Thag 1.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirivaḍḍha was inspired to ordain as a monk after witnessing the significant meeting between <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato"><b>King Bimbisāra</b></a> and the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato">Thag 1.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirivaḍḍha chose to live and practice meditation on a mountain situated between the peaks of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato"><b>Vebhāra and Paṇḍava</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato">Thag 1.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Venerable Sirivaḍḍha was meditating, a great thunderstorm occurred and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato"><b>lightning struck</b></a> at the very entrance of his cave? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato">Thag 1.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wind from a major storm actually assisted Venerable Sirivaḍḍha by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato"><b>alleviating the heat</b></a> that was weighing on him, allowing him to focus on his meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato">Thag 1.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirivaḍḍha attained Arahantship during a storm, describing himself as a "son of the Peerless One" who remained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato"><b>seated in meditation</b></a> even as lightning flashed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato">Thag 1.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirivaḍḍha’s success in reaching the highest goal was attributed to his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato"><b>dedicated practice</b></a> in the mountains combined with the favorable conditions brought by a storm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.41/en/sujato">Thag 1.41</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kassapa was born in the city of Sāvatthī as the son of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato"><b>Northwestern Brahmin</b></a> family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato">Thag 1.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because Venerable Kassapa's father <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato"><b>died young</b></a>, he was raised entirely by his mother before he sought ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato">Thag 1.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kassapa attained the fruit of stream-entry (sotāpatti) immediately after hearing the Buddha preach at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato"><b>Jetavana</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato">Thag 1.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Kassapa wished to follow the Buddha on his travels, he first went to seek permission from his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato"><b>mother</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato">Thag 1.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kassapa's mother advised him to only go to places where almsfood was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato"><b>easy to obtain</b></a> and the environment was safe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato">Thag 1.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kassapa's mother urged him to stay in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato"><b>safe places</b></a> where there was no reason for fear during his journey as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato">Thag 1.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Kassapa warned him not to let his life be touched by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato"><b>affliction of sorrow</b></a> while he practiced his meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato">Thag 1.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kassapa interpreted his mother’s advice as a prompt to reach the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato"><b>freedom from defilements</b></a>, which led to his attainment of arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.82/en/sujato">Thag 1.82</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīha was born into a royal family in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato"><b>Malla country</b></a> before he became a disciple of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato">Thag 1.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Sīha first saw the Buddha, he was so deeply moved that he immediately paid homage and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato"><b>listened to the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato">Thag 1.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his early days in the forest, the mind of Venerable Sīha was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato"><b>obsessed by various objects</b></a>, which prevented him from achieving concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato">Thag 1.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally intervened to encourage Venerable Sīha to live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato"><b>without negligence</b></a> and to be persistent in his spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato">Thag 1.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Venerable Sīha to be persistent in his practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato"><b>day and night</b></a> without succumbing to laziness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato">Thag 1.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīha was exhorted by the Buddha to practice wholesome qualities and to quickly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato"><b>discard the accumulated body</b></a> (kāya-samussaya)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato">Thag 1.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after applying the Buddha's specific instructions on mindfulness, Venerable Sīha eventually achieved <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato"><b>arahantship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.83/en/sujato">Thag 1.83</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nita was a Brahmin from Sāvatthī who initially joined the Order because he thought the life of a monk was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato"><b>comfortable life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato">Thag 1.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his transformation, Venerable Nita was more interested in being well-supported with requisites like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan monastics</b></a> than in spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato">Thag 1.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his early monastic life, Venerable Nita spent his time eating his fill, engaging in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato"><b>idle chatter</b></a>, and sleeping for long periods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato">Thag 1.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha rebuked Venerable Nita for his laziness, asking how a fool who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato"><b>sleeps all night</b></a> could ever expect to end suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato">Thag 1.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha pointed out that a fool who enjoys social gatherings by day and sleeps by night would never reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato">Thag 1.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nita was so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato"><b>agitated by the Buddha's words</b></a> that he completely changed his ways and devoted himself to intensive insight meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato">Thag 1.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the Buddha's guidance, the former idle monk Venerable Nita successfully attained the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.84/en/sujato">Thag 1.84</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sunāga was born in the village of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato"><b>Nālaka</b></a> and was a close friend of Venerable Sāriputta before either of them ordained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato">Thag 1.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that it was after hearing a sermon by his friend, Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato"><b>Sāriputta</b></a>, that Sunāga decided to go forth into the homeless life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato">Thag 1.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sunāga became highly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato"><b>skilled in the signs of the mind</b></a> (cittassa nimitta), which helped him in his rapid attainment of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato">Thag 1.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sunāga emphasized that a practitioner should understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato"><b>sweetness of seclusion</b></a> (pavivekarasa) to progress on the path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato">Thag 1.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sunāga taught that one must practice meditation while remaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato"><b>mindful and cautious</b></a> (nipaka) at all times? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato">Thag 1.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a result of his dedicated practice, Venerable Sunāga experienced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato"><b>unworldly bliss</b></a> (nirāmisa sukha) that comes from total liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato">Thag 1.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sunāga shared his realization with other monks by reciting a verse that declared his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato"><b>attainment of final knowledge</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.85/en/sujato">Thag 1.85</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja was born into a wealthy and powerful Brahmin family in the city of Ukkaṭṭhā, located near the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>Himalayan mountains</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before meeting the Buddha, Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja had already mastered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>Vedic scriptures</b></a> and various traditional arts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja initially practiced a form of "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>wrong asceticism</b></a>" that involved honoring and worshipping the sacrificial fire in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja first met the Buddha while the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>Teacher</b></a> was traveling through the countryside, at which point he was inspired to abandon his previous practices? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after practicing insight meditation, Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a>, confirming his spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that out of compassion for his own family, Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja visited them to teach the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>taking refuge</b></a> and the observance of the moral precepts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja spent part of his monastic life living in a forest near the village of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>Kuṇḍiya</b></a> within the territory of the Kuru people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by other Brahmins why he abandoned his heritage, Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja explained that true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>purification</b></a> cannot be found in traditional rites but only through the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja described his transition to enlightenment by saying that he attained "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>bliss through bliss</b></a>," contrasting this with the painful and fruitless asceticism of his past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja considered himself a true Brahmin who had been "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>consecrated</b></a>" by the realization of the Three Knowledges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja stated that he had fully completed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>Teacher's instruction</b></a> and had attained a state of true security? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja realized that following his old traditions was like wandering without knowing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>path to deathlessness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja claimed that through his practice of the Buddha's teaching, he had achieved the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgaṇika Bhāradvāja used his interaction with Brahmins at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato"><b>Uggāyāman</b></a> to clarify that the fire worship he once performed was not a true path to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.1/en/sujato">Thag 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Satimattiya was born into a Brahmin family in the kingdom of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>Magadha</b></a> before he sought ordination as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Satimattiya attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> through a life of disciplined training while living with other monks in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Satimattiya was known for actively teaching many lay followers about the importance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>moral precepts</b></a> and spiritual refuge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the deity <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>Māra</b></a> attempted to discredit Venerable Satimattiya by disguising himself as the monk and approaching a young woman? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during Māra’s attempt to frame the monk, the daughter of the donor family realized the hand touching her did not belong to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>human being</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Satimattiya noticed a sudden and cold shift in the behavior of a family that previously supported him, leading him to realize he had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>slandered by Māra</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Venerable Satimattiya explained the supernatural interference of Māra, the householder <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>asked for forgiveness</b></a> and offered to continue his service? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Satimattiya observed that the faith of a worldling is often <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>unstable and wavering</b></a>, changing quickly from affection to resentment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Satimattiya told a householder that although the householder's faith had vanished, the monk himself remained free from any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>evil conduct</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Satimattiya decided to leave the support of a specific family and go for alms from house to house, noting that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>legs</b></a> were still strong enough for the task? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Satimattiya questioned why a sage should remain in a place where they must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>waste away</b></a> due to the inconsistent favor of laypeople? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Satimattiya maintained that a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>possessions</b></a> belong only to them, and his own spiritual integrity was independent of material support? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Satimattiya attained his high spiritual realizations, including the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>, after completing his studies and forest training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Satimattiya's story illustrates that even an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> can be subject to the malicious plots of Māra aimed at ruining their reputation among the laity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.10/en/sujato">Thag 3.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, Venerable Nālaka was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>brahmin family</b></a> in Rājagaha and was highly educated in Vedic literature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Nālaka refers to a specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>bathed rank</b></a> he achieved during his early education and training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that prior to meeting the Buddha, Venerable Nālaka lived as an ascetic in a forest three leagues from Rājagaha, where he subsisted on wild rice and practiced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>fire worship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally traveled to the forest hermitage of Venerable Nālaka because he perceived the potential for Arahantship shining in the monk's mind like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>light in a jar</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha first arrived at his dwelling, Venerable Nālaka welcomed him and provided him with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>cooked rice</b></a> for three days? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, noting the physical weakness of Venerable Nālaka, questioned how he was able to live in the forest while appearing so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>extremely frail</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nālaka achieved the fruit of Arahantship after hearing a discourse from the Buddha concerning the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>virtue of contentment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his spiritual liberation, Venerable Nālaka suffered from a severe physical affliction of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>paralysis</b></a> caused by a wind disease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his body being emaciated and dominated by illness, Venerable Nālaka declared that his mind was filled with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>pervasive rapture</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nālaka maintained his spiritual focus in the deep forest by diligently cultivating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while living in isolation with limited food, Venerable Nālaka relied on the development of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>five faculties and five powers</b></a> for his spiritual strength? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nālaka was able to live without any mental taints because he was fully endowed with the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>subtle meditations</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nālaka described himself as being completely released from all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>fetters</b></a> and possessing a mind that was pure and stainless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nālaka reached a state where all internal and external <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a> were completely cut off and would never arise again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because he had fully understood the nature of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a>, Venerable Nālaka declared that he had cut the root of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nālaka confirmed he had reached the end of the holy life where there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato"><b>no more rebirth</b></a>, even as he lived in a frail physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.11/en/sujato">Thag 6.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>famous Brahmin family</b></a> in Sāvatthī and was highly educated in the Brahmanical literature before meeting the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya decided to ordain after hearing the Buddha preach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>Indriyabhāvanā Sutta</b></a> at the Jetavana monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya attained Arahantship by practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> while contemplating the deep meaning of the Buddha's verses regarding the sense faculties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya reflected on his spiritual path while sitting in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>solitude and seclusion</b></a>, contemplating how a person can live without harming others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya taught that human sense faculties are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>beneficial when restrained</b></a>, but they lead to great harm when they are left unguarded? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya warned that a person who fails to restrain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>eye</b></a> when seeing forms will fail to see the inherent dangers and thus cannot escape suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya noted that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>ear</b></a>, when not blocked or controlled in relation to sounds, prevents a person from finding the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya stated that one who indulges in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>fragrances</b></a> without seeing an escape from them remains bound to the cycle of pain due to their attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya described the mind as unable to awaken if it remains enslaved by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>craving for tastes</b></a>, such as sour, sweet, or bitter flavors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya taught that dwelling on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>pleasant physical sensations</b></a> and physical contact leads a person to encounter many types of misery and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya declared that suffering inevitably follows those who cannot <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>guard the mind</b></a> regarding the five types of sense objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya compared the physical body to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>beautifully painted box</b></a> that, despite its outward appearance, is actually filled with blood and pus? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya likened the hidden danger of sensual pleasure to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>sharp blade smeared with honey</b></a>, which the greedy fail to recognize as a source of bitterness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya warned that those obsessed with the sight, scent, and touch of women encounter a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>multitude of sorrows</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya emphasized that a person with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>energetic strength</b></a> and diligence is capable of blocking the "fivefold stream" of sensual allurements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya described a wise and enlightened person as one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>skilled and insightful</b></a>, delighting in duties that are connected to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya advised spiritual seekers to abandon temporary gains and remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>heedful</b></a> to see the truth clearly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya stated that following the Dhamma with joy and acting rightly leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>supreme bliss</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya condemned the use of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>deceit and violence</b></a>, such as killing or oppressing others, to gain advantage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>strong man using a peg</b></a> to knock out another peg to explain how spiritual faculties should be used to overcome sense faculties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya taught that one should train in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>faith, energy, and concentration</b></a> to advance steadily on the path to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya emphasized that cultivating <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and wisdom</b></a> is essential for defeating unwholesome states and sense cravings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya explained that by using the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>five spiritual faculties</b></a>, a practitioner can overcome flaws and move forward without blemish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya defined a true Brahmin as one who follows the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's instructions</b></a> with their entire being and strength to enjoy peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya questioned what the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>precepts and practices</b></a> were that would allow a man to perform his duty without harming anyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya urged monks to reject unbeneficial actions and remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>insightful</b></a> so they do not engage in what should not be done? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya pointed out that those who do not see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>danger in sensual objects</b></a> are the ones who remain trapped in suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya suggested that one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>restrains the faculties</b></a> is a person who truly performs their duty and does not harm others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pārāpariya shared that he reached the end of his spiritual training by deeply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato"><b>contemplating the meaning</b></a> of the Dhamma while sitting alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.2/en/sujato">Thag 16.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Revata Khadiravaniya was born in the village of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato"><b>Nālaka</b></a> in Magadha as the son of a Brahmin woman named Rūpasārī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato">Thag 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Revata Khadiravaniya was the younger brother of the Buddha's chief disciple, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a>, whom he followed into the monkhood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato">Thag 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha officially declared Venerable Revata Khadiravaniya to be the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato"><b>foremost among forest-dwellers</b></a> in the entire Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato">Thag 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Revata Khadiravaniya attained the fruit of Arahantship while living in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato"><b>khadira forest</b></a> (acacia forest), which earned him his distinctive epithet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato">Thag 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Revata Khadiravaniya ordained three of his own nephews, who were named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato"><b>Cālā, Upacālā, and Sisūpacālā</b></a> after their mothers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato">Thag 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Revata Khadiravaniya used the image of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato"><b>hair-splitter</b></a> to describe the extreme precision and mindfulness required for his nephews' spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato">Thag 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato"><b>suffering from an illness</b></a>, Venerable Revata Khadiravaniya received a visit from his brother Sāriputta and used the occasion to exhort his nephews toward diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato">Thag 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nephews of Venerable Revata Khadiravaniya were the sons of his three sisters, also named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato"><b>Cālā, Upacālā, and Sisūpacālā</b></a>, who were well-known figures in the Buddhist community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato">Thag 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Revata Khadiravaniya's realization of the Dhamma led him to stay at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato"><b>Jetavana Monastery</b></a> in Sāvatthī after paying respects to the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato">Thag 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Revata Khadiravaniya's verse focuses on urging his disciples to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato"><b>dwell in mindfulness</b></a> as a means to achieve the highest goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.42/en/sujato">Thag 1.42</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumangala was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato"><b>poor family</b></a> near Sāvatthī and spent his early life performing exhausting manual labor in the fields? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato">Thag 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumangala joyfully celebrated his final liberation as being "well-freed" from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato"><b>three crooked things</b></a>: the sickle, the plow, and the hoe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato">Thag 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumangala was first inspired to ordain after seeing the magnificent offerings made by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato"><b>King Pasenadi</b></a> of Kosala to the Buddha and the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato">Thag 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumangala initially sought ordination not for spiritual reasons, but because he desired the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato"><b>comforts and clothing</b></a> enjoyed by the monks and nuns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato">Thag 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after struggling with boredom in the forest, Venerable Sumangala regained his spiritual urgency after observing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato"><b>misery of farm laborers</b></a> working in the dust and heat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato">Thag 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumangala attained Arahantship while practicing meditation at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato"><b>foot of a tree</b></a> after developing his insight through solitary dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato">Thag 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumangala exhorted himself to "meditate" and live <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato"><b>without negligence</b></a> to ensure he would never again have to endure the pains of worldly life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato">Thag 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, Venerable Sumangala joined other workers in bringing gifts of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato"><b>milk and butter</b></a> to the assembly of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato">Thag 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumangala used his own name in his verses as a form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato"><b>self-encouragement</b></a> to remain committed to his meditative absorption? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato">Thag 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumangala felt that his former life of physical toil was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato"><b>truly enough</b></a> for him, leading to a profound sense of disenchantment with worldly existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato">Thag 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumangala was originally given a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato"><b>meditation subject</b></a> by a compassionate elder monk who accepted him into the order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato">Thag 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumangala described his liberation specifically as being freed from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato"><b>misery of the hoe</b></a>, which represented his life of poverty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato">Thag 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the path of Venerable Sumangala highlights that even those who ordain for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato"><b>material reasons</b></a> can eventually achieve the highest spiritual fruit through proper effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.43/en/sujato">Thag 1.43</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nāgita was born in Kapilavatthu to a family of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.86/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan royal clan</b></a> before he became a disciple of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.86/en/sujato">Thag 1.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that it was after hearing the Buddha preach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.86/en/sujato"><b>Honeyball Sutta</b></a> (Madhupiṇḍika Sutta) that Venerable Nāgita gained faith and went forth into the homeless life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.86/en/sujato">Thag 1.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nāgita claimed that while many outside sects teach various paths, they do not lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.86/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> like the path taught by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.86/en/sujato">Thag 1.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nāgita praised the Buddha for showing the path to liberation as clearly as if it were resting in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.86/en/sujato"><b>palm of one's hand</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.86/en/sujato">Thag 1.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after his ordination, Venerable Nāgita attained the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.86/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> through his dedication to the Teacher's instructions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.86/en/sujato">Thag 1.86</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pavittha, who was born into a Brahmin family in Magadha, originally had a natural inclination toward the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.87/en/sujato"><b>life of a hermit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.87/en/sujato">Thag 1.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pavittha was inspired to seek out the Buddha after hearing that the famous wanderers <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.87/en/sujato"><b>Sāriputta and Moggallāna</b></a> had joined the Order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.87/en/sujato">Thag 1.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pavittha attained liberation after developing the insight to see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.87/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> (khandhā) as they truly are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.87/en/sujato">Thag 1.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon his enlightenment, Venerable Pavittha declared that he had destroyed all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.87/en/sujato"><b>states of existence</b></a> (bhava), meaning he would no longer be subject to rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.87/en/sujato">Thag 1.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pavittha described his spiritual victory as having completely cut off the cycle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.87/en/sujato"><b>birth and death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.87/en/sujato">Thag 1.87</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ajjuna was born in Sāvatthī as the son of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.88/en/sujato"><b>councillor</b></a> before he began his search for spiritual truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.88/en/sujato">Thag 1.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before finding the Buddha's path, Venerable Ajjuna had ordained as a young man in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.88/en/sujato"><b>Nigaṇṭha</b></a> sect (Jainism)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.88/en/sujato">Thag 1.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ajjuna decided to leave his first religious order because he realized he could not find the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.88/en/sujato"><b>path to liberation</b></a> within that community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.88/en/sujato">Thag 1.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after meeting the Buddha, Venerable Ajjuna achieved the state of an Arahant and expressed his breakthrough by saying he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.88/en/sujato"><b>penetrated the truth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.88/en/sujato">Thag 1.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ajjuna used the metaphor of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.88/en/sujato"><b>pulling water from dry land</b></a> to describe the incredible nature of his spiritual realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.88/en/sujato">Thag 1.88</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Devasabha was born into a royal family and actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.89/en/sujato"><b>ascended the throne</b></a> as a king at a very young age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.89/en/sujato">Thag 1.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Devasabha abandoned his royal power and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.89/en/sujato"><b>renounced the throne</b></a> after hearing the Buddha's teachings and experiencing a profound awakening to the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.89/en/sujato">Thag 1.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Devasabha compared his liberation from worldly life to returning safely from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.89/en/sujato"><b>deep abyss</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.89/en/sujato">Thag 1.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Devasabha described his conquest of defilements as having crossed over the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.89/en/sujato"><b>mire of impurity</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.89/en/sujato">Thag 1.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Devasabha claimed he had successfully escaped the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.89/en/sujato"><b>bonds of the flood</b></a> (ogha), referring to the currents of desire and ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.89/en/sujato">Thag 1.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon reaching full enlightenment, Venerable Devasabha stated that all his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.89/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a> (māna) had been completely eliminated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.89/en/sujato">Thag 1.89</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upāli was born into a family of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato"><b>barbers</b></a> before becoming one of the most prominent monks in the Buddha's dispensation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato">Thag 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upāli went forth into the homeless life following the example of several royal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan princes</b></a>, including Anuruddha, at the Anupiyā Mango Grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato">Thag 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Upāli initially asked to live in the forest to meditate, the Buddha advised him that staying near the Teacher would help him become proficient in both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato"><b>scriptural study and meditation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato">Thag 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upāli was eventually recognized by the Buddha as the monk who was foremost in the mastery of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato"><b>Vinaya Piṭaka</b></a>, the collection of monastic discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato">Thag 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha publicly commended Venerable Upāli for his wisdom in making correct legal rulings in the cases of the monks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato"><b>Ajjuka and Kurukacchaka</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato">Thag 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upāli instructed newly ordained monks to always associate with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato"><b>good friends</b></a> who maintain a pure livelihood and are never lazy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato">Thag 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upāli emphasized that a monk who has gone forth with faith should remain within the community and be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato"><b>astute</b></a> in learning the monastic rules? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato">Thag 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upāli taught that a practitioner must clearly distinguish between what is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato"><b>proper and improper</b></a> to do in their daily conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato">Thag 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upāli advised that a monk should live without seeking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato"><b>high honors</b></a> or being overly concerned with his social status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato">Thag 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upāli reached the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> after following the Buddha's guidance on balancing his practice between the community and solitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato">Thag 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while reciting the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato"><b>Pātimokkha</b></a>, the code of monastic rules, on an observance day, Venerable Upāli took the opportunity to exhort his fellow monks on proper conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato">Thag 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Upāli’s transition from a barber to a master of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato"><b>Discipline</b></a> highlights the egalitarian nature of the Buddha's monastic order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.11/en/sujato">Thag 3.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttarapāla was born into a Brahmin family in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato"><b>Sāvatthī</b></a> and was moved to ordain after witnessing the Buddha's performance of the Twin Miracle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato">Thag 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that shortly after ordaining, Venerable Uttarapāla faced a difficult internal struggle when he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato"><b>attacked by sensual desire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato">Thag 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttarapāla successfully halted his defilements and developed insight after a period of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato"><b>intense mental combat</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato">Thag 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon reaching enlightenment, Venerable Uttarapāla described himself as a sage who had abandoned the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato">Thag 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttarapāla compared the world of sensual desires to a world of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato"><b>delusion</b></a> that distracts practitioners from their true goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato">Thag 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttarapāla claimed to have escaped the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato"><b>snare of Māra</b></a> despite being born into Māra's realm and struck by his powerful arrows? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato">Thag 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttarapāla declared that he had completely destroyed all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato"><b>future existence</b></a> and that there would be no more rebirth for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato">Thag 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttarapāla roared a "lion's roar" of victory to express his joy after ending the long path of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato"><b>birth and death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.12/en/sujato">Thag 3.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Abhibhūta was a wealthy landowner in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato"><b>Veṭṭhapura</b></a> who chose to renounce his vast estate after hearing the Buddha preach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato">Thag 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Venerable Abhibhūta attained Arahantship, his relatives came to him in tears to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato"><b>lament his departure</b></a> from family life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato">Thag 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in response to his family's grief, Venerable Abhibhūta taught them that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato"><b>repeated birth</b></a> is inherently painful and filled with suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato">Thag 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Abhibhūta urged his kin to be diligent and follow the Buddha's instructions to defeat the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato"><b>army of Māra</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato">Thag 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Abhibhūta compared a practitioner defeating the forces of death to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato"><b>elephant</b></a> trampling and crushing a hut made of reeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato">Thag 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Abhibhūta emphasized that whoever lives <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato"><b>without negligence</b></a> in the Dhamma and Vinaya will eventually bring an end to all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato">Thag 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Abhibhūta described the act of abandoning the cycle of rebirth as the key to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato"><b>finishing suffering</b></a> forever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato">Thag 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Abhibhūta formally invited his relatives to listen to his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato"><b>teaching</b></a> so they might understand the deeper purpose of his renunciation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato">Thag 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Abhibhūta attained the "sweetness of liberation" and chose not to leave a successor to his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato"><b>family estate</b></a>, fully severing his worldly ties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.13/en/sujato">Thag 3.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmadatta was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>prince of Kosala</b></a> who renounced his royal life in Sāvatthī after being inspired by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmadatta attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> (chalabhiññā) after being moved by the Buddha’s presence at the Jetavana dedication ceremony? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a Brahmin verbally abused him on his almsround, Venerable Brahmadatta maintained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>noble silence</b></a>, even when the public criticized him for not responding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmadatta taught that anger cannot find a foothold in one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>fully controlled</b></a> and lives in a state of spiritual balance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Brahmadatta, a person who retaliates against someone who is angry is actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>worse than</b></a> the person who was angry first? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmadatta described the person who does not get angry in return as a winner on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>both fronts</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmadatta taught that being mindful and calming oneself when others are angry results in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>benefit of both</b></a> oneself and the other person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmadatta considered a person who calms themselves and others to be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>physician for both</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmadatta noted that people who do not understand the Dhamma often mistake the silence of the wise for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>foolishness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmadatta described the state of an Arahant as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>peaceful</b></a> and having reached a secure liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmadatta questioned how anger could even arise in a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>liberated through right knowledge</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his own conduct, Venerable Brahmadatta demonstrated that true <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>victory</b></a> comes through self-restraint rather than verbal retaliation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmadatta's conversion was triggered by witnessing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>majesty of the Buddha</b></a> during a major public offering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Brahmadatta taught that staying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato"><b>calm and mindful</b></a> in the face of another's wrath is the highest form of teacher-like behavior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.12/en/sujato">Thag 6.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda compared a concealed sin to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>heavy rain</b></a> that drenches and overwhelms the offender? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda taught that when a fault is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>revealed and acknowledged</b></a>, it no longer has the power to weigh heavily upon the person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda described the world as being pierced by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>arrow of craving</b></a> and constantly fumed by desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda warned that the world is constantly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>overwhelmed by death</b></a> and surrounded by the decay of old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda likened the onset of old age to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>robber with a club</b></a> who leaves a person with no place of safety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda emphasized that death, sickness, and old age are like an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>approaching fire</b></a> that no one has the strength to resist? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda observed that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>no speed</b></a> fast enough to run away from the inevitability of death, sickness, and old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda advised that one should never let a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>day be empty</b></a> of spiritual effort, whether that effort is small or great? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda taught that as each night passes and is wasted, a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>lifespan is shortened</b></a> accordingly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda urged practitioners to remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>without negligence</b></a> regardless of whether they are walking, standing, sitting, or lying down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda reminded us that our <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>final night</b></a> is always drawing closer with every passing moment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda highlighted how the world is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>besieged</b></a> and locked in by the inescapable forces of mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>fumes of desire</b></a> keep the world in a state of constant agitation and lack of peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimanda’s verses serve as a powerful exhortation to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato"><b>urgency</b></a> (saṃvega) before life comes to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.13/en/sujato">Thag 6.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> was born into a famous Brahmin family in Sāvatthī and became a wandering ascetic after growing disillusioned with worldly desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> traveled extensively, questioning many ascetics and Brahmins about spiritual liberation, but failed to find peace of mind until he met the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> spent a long time seeking someone who had truly reached the further shore and plunged into the state of deathless peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> compared his former spiritual bondage to a fish that has swallowed a hook while being lured by the bait of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> likened his feeling of being trapped by worldly attachments to the Asura king Vepacitti, who was bound by the snare of Sakka, the lord of gods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> described himself as having once carried heavy chains of grief and sorrow, looking desperately for someone to release him and lead him to Enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> sought a teacher who could reveal a Dhamma that would allow a person to completely abandon the cycle of birth, aging, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> admitted that before his conversion, he was bound by doubt and dominated by a passionate nature that made his mind rigid and angry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> felt his heart was being torn apart and cut by powerful greed before he learned how to restrain his desires through the Buddha's teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> described his previous state as being struck by the arrow of craving and entangled in thirty different types of wrong views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> realized his thoughts were once so unstable and agitated that they resembled leaves being blown and shaken by a strong wind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> recognized an internal fire that was rapidly burning away his sense of "mine-making" and ego-attachment after he began practicing the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> taught that the sense of a permanent self arises from the physical body in conjunction with the six sense bases? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> sought a "physician of the soul" who could extract the arrow of suffering from his heart without using a surgical knife or causing a physical wound? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> praised the Buddha as the supreme Lord of Dhamma who removes the poisonous medicine of harmful views and unwholesome states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> felt as though he had been falling into a deep abyss until the Buddha reached out a hand to pull him safely to the shore? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> described his spiritual struggle as being submerged in a lake filled with the mud of fraud, envy, stress, and mental darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> compared mental agitation to a storm where the thunder of restlessness roars and the clouds of fetters cover the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> spoke of a "waterfall of wrong thoughts" and sensual desires that threatened to sweep him away into the ocean of wrong views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> asked who could block the flowing streams of craving and cut down the creepers of attachment that sprout up everywhere? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> urged practitioners to build a dam using the power of the mind to prevent themselves from being swept away by unwholesome mental currents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> found the Buddha waiting for him with the weapon of wisdom to provide guidance and a point of refuge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> likened the Buddha's teaching to a firm ladder made of the heartwood of the Dhamma, which allowed him to climb out of the depths of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> was told by the Buddha "Do not be afraid!" as he was being rescued from the floods of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> ascended what he called the "tower of mindfulness," from which he could clearly observe people who are still obsessed with self-identity views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> confessed that he once took great pride in his own existence before he realized the truth through mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> emphasized that once he saw the supreme path and the "boat" of Dhamma, he no longer chose to dwell in the concept of a permanent self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> credited the Buddha with teaching the supreme way to extract the arrow of self-identity and end the causes of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> described the Buddha as the one who untied and cast away the long-hidden knots of defilements that had resided deep within him for a long time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> reached the goal of Arahantship shortly after hearing the Buddha's sermon and subsequently shared his journey to encourage other monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Telakāni</b></a> declared that the Buddha had successfully removed all harmful mental toxins from his life, leading to his final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.3/en/sujato">Thag 16.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sānu was born in the city of Sāvatthī and was brought to the Sangha by his mother at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>age of seven</b></a> because she believed the monastic life would bring him true happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sānu was a highly respected scholar, preacher, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>meditation master</b></a> who was beloved by both deities and humans for his great compassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a previous life, the mother of Venerable Sānu was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>Yakkhī</b></a> (a female spirit) who continued to watch over his spiritual welfare even after he was reborn as a human? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Sānu lost his mental clarity and desired to leave the monkhood to wander, his former mother who was a Yakkhī <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>alerted his current mother</b></a> to help him regain his focus? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Yakkhī mother of Venerable Sānu warned that anyone who commits evil deeds, whether <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>openly or in secret</b></a>, can never escape suffering simply by running away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sānu once questioned why his mother was weeping, arguing that people usually only cry for the dead or for those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>absent from their lives</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Sānu explained that in the discipline of the Noble Ones, returning to the lay life after being ordained is considered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>form of death</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Sānu compared his desire to return to worldly life to a person wanting to jump back into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>pit of glowing embers</b></a> after having already been rescued from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sānu was moved to a state of profound shame and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>spiritual urgency</b></a> after hearing the insightful and stern verses spoken by his mother? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the diligent development of insight, Venerable Sānu eventually attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> and reached the final goal of the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sānu attributed his spiritual success and his victory over worldly desires to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>timely verses</b></a> spoken to him by his mother? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the father of Venerable Sānu had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>abandoned his family</b></a> before Sānu was even born, which led his mother to eventually entrust her son to the care of the monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Yakkhī who protected Venerable Sānu appeared to his human mother to give a warning about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>unavoidable nature of karma</b></a> and the results of evil actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sānu visited his mother very early in the morning carrying his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>bowl and robe</b></a>, which gave her the opportunity to speak the verses that led to his enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Sānu taught him that those who abandon their lust only to return to it later are seen as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>living dead</b></a> in the eyes of the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sānu’s name was given to him by his mother, and he later became known for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>mastery of the Dhamma</b></a> after joining the Sangha as a child? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Yakkhī mother of Venerable Sānu spoke of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>suffering of the Yakkhās</b></a> (spirits) as a means to encourage him to maintain his spiritual practice and avoid unwholesome deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sānu’s struggle with the desire to leave the order shows that even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>learned practitioners</b></a> can experience moments of doubt and spiritual crisis? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Sānu relied on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>words of the Buddha</b></a> and the rules of the training to convince her son to stay on the path of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his realization, Venerable Sānu decided to keep the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato"><b>verses of his mother</b></a> as his own formal declaration of truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.44/en/sujato">Thag 1.44</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vijaya was born in Sāvatthī to a Brahmin family and mastered their teachings before choosing to live as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato"><b>forest hermit</b></a> in pursuit of deeper spiritual truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato">Thag 1.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing of the Buddha’s mission, Venerable Vijaya traveled to meet him and achieved the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> shortly after receiving the Teacher's instructions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato">Thag 1.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vijaya described the pinnacle of spiritual success as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato"><b>destroyed all taints</b></a> (āsava), leaving no traces of mental defilement in the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato">Thag 1.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vijaya explained that those who have achieved total liberation no longer <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato"><b>depend on food</b></a> or any other forms of clinging to sustain their sense of self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato">Thag 1.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vijaya referred to emptiness and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato"><b>signless</b></a> (animitta) as the meditative range or domain of those who have reached the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato">Thag 1.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vijaya used the poetic metaphor of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato"><b>track of birds</b></a> in the sky to illustrate how the destination of a liberated person cannot be traced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato">Thag 1.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vijaya emphasized that the spiritual path of one who has attained final liberation is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato"><b>hard to find</b></a>, much like footprints are impossible to see in the open air? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.92/en/sujato">Thag 1.92</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Eraka was a handsome and successful man from a high-status family in Sāvatthī who renounced the world despite possessing great <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato"><b>beauty and wealth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato">Thag 1.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Eraka was inspired to seek the Buddha's guidance after experiencing a profound sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato"><b>spiritual urgency</b></a> (saṃvega), even though he had a young and virtuous wife? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato">Thag 1.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his early days as a monk, Venerable Eraka struggled with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome thoughts</b></a> until the Buddha intervened to correct his mental state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato">Thag 1.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, knowing the heart of Venerable Eraka, personally addressed him with a verse to remind him of the inherent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato"><b>pain of sensual pleasures</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato">Thag 1.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Eraka realized through the Buddha's teaching that anyone who feels desire for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> is essentially inviting suffering into their life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato">Thag 1.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Eraka taught that to be free from suffering, one must remain entirely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato"><b>without desire</b></a> for the fleeting delights of the senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato">Thag 1.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Eraka attained full enlightenment after he became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato"><b>diligent and energetic</b></a> in his development of insight meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.93/en/sujato">Thag 1.93</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mettajī was born in Magadha as the son of a Brahmin and was led to become a hermit by his early recognition of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato"><b>suffering of sensual desire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato">Thag 1.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, Venerable Mettajī lived as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato"><b>solitary hermit</b></a> in the forest, seeking a way to transcend the pains of worldly existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato">Thag 1.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mettajī personally approached the Buddha to ask about the factors that lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato"><b>progress and decline</b></a> in a monk's spiritual training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato">Thag 1.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mettajī offered homage to the Buddha by calling him the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan son</b></a>, a title acknowledging the Buddha's noble lineage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato">Thag 1.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mettajī described the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato"><b>Auspicious One</b></a>, whose presence and teachings bring the highest blessings to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato">Thag 1.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mettajī praised the teachings of the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato"><b>supreme Dhamma</b></a>, noting that it is perfectly explained for the goal of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato">Thag 1.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mettajī celebrated his spiritual victory by declaring that he had successfully attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato"><b>foremost fruit</b></a> of the ascetic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.94/en/sujato">Thag 1.94</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbhakāma was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>noble family</b></a> in the city of Vesālī and chose to renounce his high status to seek spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbhakāma went forth into homelessness and received his training under the guidance of the Buddha's chief disciple, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Sabbhakāma returned to his hometown to visit his family, his former wife appeared before him in a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>extreme grief</b></a> and physical neglect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after seeing the distress of his former wife, Venerable Sabbhakāma felt a surge of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a> that threatened his spiritual path, which he compared to the sting of a whip on a horse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that to combat his rising lust, Venerable Sabbhakāma went to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>charnel ground</b></a> to meditate on the foulness of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbhakāma attained the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> while practicing intensive insight meditation amidst the remains in the cemetery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the father-in-law of Venerable Sabbhakāma tried to lure him back to lay life by bringing his former wife to the monastery <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>beautifully adorned</b></a> and surrounded by a large retinue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbhakāma described the human body as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>two-legged animal</b></a> that is inherently impure, foul-smelling, and filled with various corpses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sabbhakāma, worldlings are trapped by their desires just as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>deer</b></a> are caught in a hunter's snare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbhakāma likened the way people are ensnared by sensual pleasure to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>fish</b></a> being caught by a fisherman's hook? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbhakāma compared the spiritual peril of ordinary people to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>monkeys</b></a> falling into a pit trap? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbhakāma taught that the five cords of sensual pleasure find their primary <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>allure in women</b></a> for those who have not yet tamed their minds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbhakāma observed that those with infatuated minds who chase after sensual beauty only succeed in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>accumulating rebirth</b></a> and filling up cemeteries? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbhakāma advised that a wise person should avoid sensual temptation just as they would avoid stepping on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>snake's head</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbhakāma emphasized that through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a>, one can cross over the "poisoner of the world" which is sensual desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after seeing the inherent danger in lust, Venerable Sabbhakāma found total <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>safety in seclusion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbhakāma declared his final victory over all worldly attachments by reaching the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.14/en/sujato">Thag 6.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gotama, born into the Sakyan clan, was inspired to go forth and ordain after the Buddha visited his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato"><b>relatives</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato">Thag 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gotama attained spiritual liberation by achieving the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> through the practice of insight meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato">Thag 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gotama reflected on his long journey through Saṃsāra, noting that he had been repeatedly reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a> and as a hungry ghost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato">Thag 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gotama described having spent many days in the animal realm and undergoing much suffering before eventually finding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato"><b>happiness as a human</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato">Thag 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gotama reached the highest levels of meditative existence, including the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato"><b>immaterial realms</b></a> such as the base of neither-perception-nor-non-perception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato">Thag 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gotama realized that all forms of existence are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato"><b>pithless</b></a>, unstable, and conditioned by causes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato">Thag 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gotama attained a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato"><b>perfect peace</b></a> by training his mind to remain settled in mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato">Thag 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by his kin why he renounced his family, Venerable Gotama explained the futility of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato"><b>repeated birth</b></a> and the bliss of the Nibbāna he had attained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.14/en/sujato">Thag 3.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Harita was born into a Brahmin family in Sāvatthī and initially struggled with great <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato"><b>conceit</b></a> regarding his high caste? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato">Thag 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Harita overcame his pride and attained Arahantship after hearing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato"><b>teaching from the Buddha</b></a> that made him reflect on his own mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato">Thag 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Harita warned that anyone who postpones necessary spiritual work will eventually destroy their own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato"><b>ground of happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato">Thag 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Harita taught that those who intend to do things later that should have been done before will suffer the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato"><b>burn of regret</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato">Thag 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Harita emphasized that one should speak only of what they actually perform, as the wise recognize a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato"><b>speaks without acting</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato">Thag 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Harita described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> as a state that is perfectly secure, free from sorrow, and devoid of lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato">Thag 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Harita proclaimed that in Nibbāna, all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> is completely and utterly extinguished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato">Thag 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Harita proved his spiritual attainment by teaching other monks the importance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato"><b>sincerity</b></a> in their practice and speech? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.15/en/sujato">Thag 3.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala was born into a Brahmin family in Benares and ordained under the guidance of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato"><b>Elder Amitta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato">Thag 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala reached the state of an Arahant and later gave a detailed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato"><b>instruction</b></a> to a fellow monk who was his friend? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato">Thag 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala advised that a practitioner should keep away from bad friends and instead <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato"><b>associate with superior persons</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato">Thag 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala taught that following spiritual advice leads one toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato"><b>immovable happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato">Thag 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala compared associating with the lazy to someone attempting to cross the great ocean on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato"><b>small plank</b></a>, only to sink? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato">Thag 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala warned that even a person of good conduct will eventually sink if they associate with those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato"><b>indolent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato">Thag 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala encouraged practitioners to live near the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato"><b>Noble Ones</b></a> who are secluded, energetic, and meditative? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato">Thag 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala emphasized that a wise and persistent monk should always remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato"><b>steadfast in effort</b></a> to avoid spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.16/en/sujato">Thag 3.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla was born into a wealthy family in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>Thullakoṭṭhita</b></a> within the Kuru country and had to go on a hunger strike to receive his parents' permission to ordain as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla, upon returning to his family home as a monk, accepted and ate stale, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>sour rice gruel</b></a> as if it were the food of the gods, demonstrating his complete lack of attachment to fine food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla compared the physical body to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>painted puppet</b></a>, describing it as a heap of sores and a diseased mass that is full of many thoughts but lacks any permanence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla observed that while the body may be adorned with jewels and earrings, it is essentially just a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>skeleton wrapped in skin</b></a> that appears attractive only because of its clothing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla taught that things like foot-paint, face powder, and eye-salve are only capable of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>deluding the foolish</b></a>, but they hold no power over those who seek true enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla compared the decorated physical body to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>painted casket</b></a> that is beautiful on the outside but filled with inner impurities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla used the analogy of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>wise deer escaping a trap</b></a> to describe how a monk should enjoy their almsfood and depart without being caught by the "snares" of household life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla traveled through the air to visit King Koravya's deer park because his father had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>locked all seven doors</b></a> of the family house to prevent him from leaving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla explained to King Koravya that the world is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>swept away</b></a> and unstable, which is a primary reason why he chose to leave the lay life behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla taught that the world has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>no shelter</b></a> and no protector, meaning that no one can truly protect another from the inevitability of aging and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla told the king that the world is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>without ownership</b></a>, as every person must eventually pass away and leave all their belongings and wealth behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla described the world as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>insatiable</b></a> and a slave to craving, as people constantly seek more even when they are already wealthy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla pointed out that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>greedy rich people</b></a> often refuse to give because they are obsessed with accumulating more wealth and chasing after sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla used the example of a king who seeks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>conquer lands</b></a> beyond the ocean even when he already rules a vast territory to illustrate the bottomless nature of human desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla stated that at the time of death, relatives <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>weep and wail</b></a> with disheveled hair, yet they cannot prevent the deceased from being carried away to the funeral pyre? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla noted that a dying person is often just wrapped in a single cloth and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>prodded with stakes</b></a> as they are burned, leaving behind all their property and kin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla emphasized that while heirs take over a dead person's property, the deceased must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>go according to their kamma</b></a>, unable to take a single coin with them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>wealth cannot buy longevity</b></a> and that no amount of riches can help a person escape the decay brought on by old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla explained that both the rich and the poor, the wise and the foolish, must experience the same physical touches and feelings, but the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>wise person does not tremble</b></a> when facing them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla declared that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>wisdom is superior to wealth</b></a> because it leads to the ultimate fulfillment and the end of the cycle of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla likened a person caught in the cycle of bad deeds to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>thief caught in the act</b></a> of stealing, who is then harmed by their own evil crimes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla described sensual pleasures as being deceptively beautiful and sweet, yet they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>perturb the mind</b></a> and hide deep-seated dangers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla observed that youth vanishes as quickly as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>fruit falling from a tree</b></a>, which motivated him to seek the more stable path of a renunciant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla stated that he ordained with full <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> in the Buddha's teachings and that he lives without debt, having abandoned his worldly possessions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla compared sensual desires to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>blazing fire</b></a> and viewed gold and wealth as being as dangerous as a stabbing dagger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla viewed the process of being reborn in a womb as a form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> and as a great disaster comparable to falling into hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>destroyed the three taints</b></a> (sensual desire, existence, and ignorance) through his diligent practice of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla declared that he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>laid down the burden</b></a> of existence and had completely uprooted the cause of future rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla attained the goal for which people <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>leave the home life</b></a>, which is the total destruction of all fetters and attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla emphasized that life is short and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>always subject to change</b></a>, which is why the wise do not place their trust in material things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla chose to eat the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>remnants of food</b></a> from his father's house while standing in the street rather than entering the home that he had renounced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla was offered a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>huge heap of gold</b></a> by his father to return to the lay life, but he suggested it should be thrown into the Ganges River because it only causes sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla remained unmoved when his former wives dressed in their finest jewelry and asked him about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>celestial nymphs</b></a> for whose sake he was practicing the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla taught that those with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>little wisdom</b></a> are the ones who believe in and continue the cycle of rebirth by entering new wombs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla viewed the life of a wandering monk as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>truly beautiful</b></a> and far superior to the life of a householder seeking sensual gratification? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla reached a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>inner peace</b></a> and stillness after realizing the profound dangers inherent in sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla described his service to his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>Teacher</b></a>, the Buddha, as being complete because he had fulfilled everything he was instructed to do? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla considered the physical form to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>frail and diseased</b></a>, even when it is polished and presented attractively to the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla taught that a person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>deeds and kamma</b></a> are the only things that truly belong to them as they move from one life to the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Raṭṭhapāla’s summary of the Dhamma to the king—that the world is unstable and a slave to craving—is often cited as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato"><b>four summaries of the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.4/en/sujato">Thag 16.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ramaṇīyavihārin was born in Rājagaha and spent his youth living a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato"><b>dissipated life</b></a> before he was eventually inspired to join the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato">Thag 1.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ramaṇīyavihārin decided to renounce the world after witnessing soldiers arrest a man for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato"><b>committing adultery</b></a>, an event that caused him deep spiritual agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato">Thag 1.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ramaṇīyavihārin received his name, which means "one who dwells in delight," because he initially filled his monastic cell with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato"><b>luxurious furnishings</b></a> and fine food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato">Thag 1.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that at one point, Venerable Ramaṇīyavihārin felt so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato"><b>unworthy of offerings</b></a> from the faithful due to his lingering attachments that he left the monastery to wander? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato">Thag 1.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while resting under a tree, Venerable Ramaṇīyavihārin watched an exhausted bull fall down and then <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato"><b>rise again</b></a> to pull its load, which inspired him to return to his spiritual duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato">Thag 1.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ramaṇīyavihārin compared his struggle with worldly desires to falling into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato"><b>forest of sensual lust</b></a> from which he had to stand up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato">Thag 1.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ramaṇīyavihārin sought the guidance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Upāli</b></a> to confess his faults and return to the proper monastic path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato">Thag 1.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after correcting his behavior and practicing with diligence, Venerable Ramaṇīyavihārin attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> and enjoyed the bliss of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato">Thag 1.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his final verse, Venerable Ramaṇīyavihārin compared himself to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato"><b>noble animal</b></a> of good breed that remains stable after having once stumbled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato">Thag 1.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ramaṇīyavihārin described himself as a disciple of the Buddha who had become fully established in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato"><b>right view</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato">Thag 1.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ramaṇīyavihārin’s initial attachment to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasure</b></a> was so strong that he even decorated his monastic dwelling like a comfortable lay home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato">Thag 1.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the story of Venerable Ramaṇīyavihārin illustrates how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato"><b>sight of a simple animal</b></a> can provide a profound spiritual lesson on perseverance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato">Thag 1.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ramaṇīyavihārin realized that just as a bull must pull its burden, he must <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato"><b>fulfill his duty</b></a> as a practitioner of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.45/en/sujato">Thag 1.45</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi was born into a noble family in Rājagaha and was renowned for being exceptionally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato"><b>handsome and virtuous</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato">Thag 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the family of Venerable Samiddhi experienced a significant increase in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato"><b>worldly wealth</b></a> from the very moment he was born? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato">Thag 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Samiddhi actually means <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato"><b>prosperity</b></a>, reflecting the good fortune he brought to his household? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato">Thag 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi was inspired to join the Buddhist order after witnessing the powerful presence of the Buddha during a meeting with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato"><b>King Bimbisāra</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato">Thag 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Venerable Samiddhi was meditating in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato"><b>Tapoda Park</b></a>, the Evil One (Māra) attempted to disturb him by making a sound like the earth exploding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato">Thag 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally instructed Venerable Samiddhi to remain calm and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato"><b>pay no attention</b></a> to the terrifying noises created by Māra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato">Thag 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi achieved <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> shortly after receiving encouragement from the Buddha during his retreat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato">Thag 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even after Venerable Samiddhi became an Arahant, Māra tried to frighten him again with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato"><b>terrible noise</b></a>, but was unable to shake his resolve? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato">Thag 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi declared that because his mind was firmly in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato"><b>meditative absorption</b></a>, no form created by the Evil One could cause him fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato">Thag 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi stated that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and wisdom</b></a> had grown strong after he left his home out of faith to become a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato">Thag 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi’s verse emphasizes that a practitioner who has abandoned the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato"><b>household life</b></a> with faith can overcome any demonic illusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato">Thag 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samiddhi’s spiritual journey began with his birth into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato"><b>prosperous noble family</b></a> that saw their fortunes rise because of his presence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.46/en/sujato">Thag 1.46</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla was born in the city of Sāvatthī as the eldest son of a wealthy <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>landowner named Mahāsuvaṇṇa</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla was originally named Pāla, a name he shared with his younger brother, though he was known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>elder Pāla</b></a> to distinguish them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla decided to leave behind his family's wealth and inheritance, handing it all over to his brother to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>go forth into homelessness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla spent six years training as a novice monk (sāmaṇera) before he went into a forest with sixty other monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>study the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while living in a leaf hut in a remote forest, Venerable Cakkhupāla strictly practiced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>way of seclusion</b></a> to deepen his meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while practicing meditation, Venerable Cakkhupāla suffered from a severe eye affliction and refused to follow a doctor's advice because he prioritized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>ending the fetters</b></a> (saṃyojana) over saving his sight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla attained the state of an Arahant at the exact same moment he became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>completely blind</b></a> due to his intense meditative efforts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla is described as a "dry-insight" Arahant (sukkha-vipassaka) who reached <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>full liberation</b></a> through insight meditation without possessing certain psychic powers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla chose not to travel back to Sāvatthī with his fellow monks because he feared his blindness and weakness would make him a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>burden on the journey</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla requested a helper to be sent from his family after his fellow monks returned to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>Jetavana</b></a> to pay respects to the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the nephew of Venerable Cakkhupāla, named Pālika, had to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>ordain as a monk</b></a> solely for the purpose of having a safe journey to assist his blind uncle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while traveling with Venerable Cakkhupāla, his nephew was lured away by the song of a woman and engaged in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>immoral conduct</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his blindness, Venerable Cakkhupāla possessed the spiritual discernment to realize his helper had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>lost his virtue</b></a> during their journey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla refused to continue his journey with his nephew, stating that he would rather face the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>dangers of the road</b></a> alone than travel with a person of corrupt character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla dismissed his nephew and insisted on traveling by himself, even though he was blind and the path was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>covered in thorns</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the great virtue of Venerable Cakkhupāla caused the throne of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>Sakka</b></a>, the king of the gods, to grow hot, prompting divine intervention? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Sakka manifested as a human guide to lead Venerable Cakkhupāla safely to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>Jetavana Monastery</b></a> in a single evening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla emphasized in his verses that it is better to wander alone than to keep <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>company with the foolish</b></a> or the immoral? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after arriving at the monastery, Venerable Cakkhupāla was supported and cared for by his younger brother, who remained a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>faithful lay supporter</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla expressed through his poetry that he was prepared to endure his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>eyes being shattered</b></a> if it meant continuing his commitment to the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla lived in a forest near a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>border village</b></a> where the laypeople were devoted Buddhists who supported his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla achieved the fruit of Arahantship after years of dedicated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> in the wilderness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Cakkhupāla compared his resolve to travel a difficult path alone to the rejection of a helper who had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato"><b>violated his precepts</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.95/en/sujato">Thag 1.95</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paccaya was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato"><b>royal family</b></a> in the city of Rohi before deciding to renounce his inheritance and seek ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato">Thag 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his father passed away, Venerable Paccaya organized a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato"><b>massive alms-giving</b></a> for a large assembly of people to celebrate his spiritual intentions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato">Thag 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while listening to the Buddha preach from a jewel-adorned throne, Venerable Paccaya felt a deep spiritual urge to seek <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato"><b>ordination</b></a> as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato">Thag 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paccaya was motivated by the merit of a spiritual vow he had previously made during the time of the past <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato"><b>Buddha Kassapa</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato">Thag 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon entering a leaf hut for meditation, Venerable Paccaya vowed that he would not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato"><b>leave his hut</b></a> until he had reached total spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato">Thag 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paccaya made a solemn resolution that he would neither <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato"><b>eat nor drink</b></a> until he had attained the goal of his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato">Thag 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as part of his intense spiritual effort, Venerable Paccaya resolved that he would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato"><b>not lie down</b></a> or rest on his side until he was free from defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato">Thag 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paccaya defined the successful goal of his meditation as the complete <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato"><b>removal of craving</b></a>, which he likened to extracting a poisonous arrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato">Thag 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paccaya successfully attained the state of an Arahant only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato"><b>five days</b></a> after he first went forth into the homeless life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato">Thag 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his diligent and focused practice, Venerable Paccaya achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato"><b>Three Knowledges</b></a> and fully completed the Teacher's instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato">Thag 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before reaching his final goal, Venerable Paccaya described himself as a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato"><b>learner</b></a>" whose intentions had not yet been fully realized? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato">Thag 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paccaya used his own achievement to highlight the importance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato"><b>spiritual energy</b></a> and persistence in overcoming suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato">Thag 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paccaya attributed his success to the fact that his mind was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato"><b>fully focused</b></a> on seclusion and the purification of his thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.2/en/sujato">Thag 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bākula was born into a family of high officials in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato"><b>Kosambi</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato">Thag 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as an infant, Venerable Bākula was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato"><b>swallowed by a fish</b></a> in the Yamunā river while being bathed for his health? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato">Thag 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bākula was found alive and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato"><b>unharmed</b></a> inside a fish after it was caught by a fisherman and sold to a family in Benares? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato">Thag 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because he was raised by two different families, the monk Venerable Bākula was given a name that literally means "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato"><b>two families</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato">Thag 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after a long life of luxury and wealth, Venerable Bākula chose to ordain as a monk at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato"><b>age of eighty</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato">Thag 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bākula attained the state of an Arahant on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato"><b>eighth day</b></a> after his ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato">Thag 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha officially recognized Venerable Bākula as the monk who was foremost in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato"><b>good health</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato">Thag 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bākula warned that a person who delays <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato"><b>necessary work</b></a> until later will eventually destroy their own basis for happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato">Thag 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bākula taught that those who only intend to act when it is too late will eventually suffer the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato"><b>burn of regret</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato">Thag 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bākula emphasized that the wise can easily distinguish between someone who actually acts and someone who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato"><b>only speaks</b></a> without doing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato">Thag 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bākula described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> as a state of sublime bliss that is perfectly secure and free from all sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato">Thag 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bākula proclaimed that in the state of Nibbāna, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato"><b>craving and suffering</b></a> are completely and utterly extinguished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato">Thag 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that just before his final passing, Venerable Bākula shared his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato"><b>final realization</b></a> of the Dhamma with the assembly of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.3/en/sujato">Thag 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tekicchakārī received his name, which means "one saved by doctors," because he was rescued by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>surgeons</b></a> immediately after his birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before entering the monastic life, Venerable Tekicchakārī was the son of a prominent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin named Subuddha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tekicchakārī fled to a monastery and sought ordination because his father had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>imprisoned</b></a> by King Candagutta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite being born into a high-status family and educated in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>Brahminical arts</b></a>, Venerable Tekicchakārī chose a life of extreme asceticism? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tekicchakārī practiced the rigorous discipline of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>living in the open air</b></a>, ignoring the hardships of both heat and cold to focus on his meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the tempter Mara tried to disrupt the meditation of Venerable Tekicchakārī by appearing in the disguise of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>cowherd</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara attempted to distract Venerable Tekicchakārī from his spiritual goals by talking about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>finished harvest</b></a> and the lack of food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when faced with distractions, Venerable Tekicchakārī believed it was his duty to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>admonish himself</b></a> rather than trying to teach or preach to others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tekicchakārī used the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Buddha</b></a> as a primary meditation subject to gladden his mind and suffuse his body with joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that to maintain his spiritual progress, Venerable Tekicchakārī practiced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Dhamma</b></a>, finding it to be an infinite source of inspiration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tekicchakārī found that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>recollection of the Sangha</b></a> helped his body become permeated with rapture and his mind become uplifted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Mara tried to tempt Venerable Tekicchakārī to enter a monastery building by warning him about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>bitter cold</b></a> of the winter nights? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tekicchakārī viewed living in a house with locked doors and bars as a form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>spiritual bondage</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tekicchakārī considered dwelling in the open air to be a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>true liberation</b></a> and freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tekicchakārī developed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>four immeasurable states</b></a> (Brahmavihāras) as a way to dwell happily and protect himself from the elements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that by dwelling in the "four immeasurables," Venerable Tekicchakārī claimed that even the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>intense cold</b></a> of the winter could not harm or disturb him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tekicchakārī declared he lived in a state that was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>unshakable</b></a>, having successfully guarded his mind against all external conditions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the development of intensive insight meditation, Venerable Tekicchakārī eventually attained the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tekicchakārī lived during the reign of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>King Bindusāra</b></a>, the father of Emperor Ashoka? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verses of Venerable Tekicchakārī were formally recited and included in the Sutta Pitaka during the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>Third Buddhist Council</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the spiritual success of Venerable Tekicchakārī was attributed to his ability to transform a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato"><b>personal crisis</b></a> into a dedicated search for enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.2/en/sujato">Thag 6.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta, seeking a teaching in brief, was asked by the Buddha if he felt any longing or desire for things he had never seen, heard, smelled, tasted, touched, or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>cognized</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta learned from the Buddha that the end of suffering occurs when one has no greed, hatred, or delusion regarding sensory experiences, seeing them as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>pure sensation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta taught that when a person sees a form and loses mindfulness by focusing on an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>agreeable sign</b></a>, their mind becomes lustfully attached to the feeling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Māluṅkyaputta, when a person's feelings born of seeing forms increase, they accumulate suffering and remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>far from Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta warned that being enchanted by sounds and neglecting mindfulness causes the mind to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>invade and settle</b></a> on sensory objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta stated that if greed and harmful mental states grow due to attachment to sounds, the person is effectively <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>piling up misery</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta pointed out that improper attention to fragrances leads a person to become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>lustfully enchanted</b></a>, causing their defilements to grow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta taught that those who allow their minds to be overcome by the scents they smell will find themselves increasingly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>distant from liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta explained that a person who is obsessed with tastes and dwells on their sweetness will have their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>mind invaded</b></a> by craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta observed that when harmful mental states increase due to the enjoyment of flavors, one becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>entangled in suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta noted that enchantment with physical touch and a lack of mindfulness leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>growth of ill-will</b></a> and greed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta warned that even mental objects can lead to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>accumulation of pain</b></a> if the mind is not guarded with mindfulness during thought? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta emphasized that seeing a form with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>firm mindfulness</b></a> prevents the mind from becoming lustfully attached to the resulting sensation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta taught that by seeing things as they are without adding attachment, one <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>abandons the accumulation</b></a> of future suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Māluṅkyaputta, a person who maintains mindfulness while seeing forms and avoids greed is said to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>near Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta stated that hearing a sound with mindfulness allows the mind to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>remain uninvaded</b></a> by the influx of defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta taught that one who hears a sound and lets the feeling pass without greed is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>not storing up</b></a> the causes of future distress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta explained that being mindful of a fragrance prevents one from being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>infatuated</b></a> and protects the heart from harmful mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta described the practitioner who smells an odor without attachment as someone who stays <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>close to peace</b></a> and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta taught that when tasting flavors, one should practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>clear awareness</b></a> so that the mind does not settle on feelings of desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta noted that a person who tastes flavors without harboring greed is considered to be in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>presence of Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta stated that if someone experiences a physical touch while being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>mindfully restrained</b></a>, they do not create the conditions for suffering to arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta described the monk who feels touch but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>relinquishes accumulation</b></a> as one who has moved beyond the reach of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta taught that when the mind cognizes mental phenomena, it should do so with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>guarding mindfulness</b></a> to prevent any craving from taking root? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta explained that by knowing mental objects without being infatuated, one remains <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>unsettled by taints</b></a> and avoids the pile of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta was instructed by the Buddha to practice in such a way that in the seen there will be only the seen, which is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>cessation of suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after receiving brief instructions from the Buddha, Venerable Māluṅkyaputta paid homage and departed to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> in seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his diligent practice after hearing the Buddha’s sermon, Venerable Māluṅkyaputta <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>attained Arahantship</b></a> in a very short time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta used verses to teach his relatives the same <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>path of sense restraint</b></a> that he had used to achieve enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Venerable Māluṅkyaputta that a state without greed, hatred, and delusion is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato"><b>end of all pain</b></a> in this life and the next? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.5/en/sujato">Thag 16.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ujjaya was born in Rājagaha and was a master of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato"><b>three Vedas</b></a> before he realized they contained no spiritual essence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato">Thag 1.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ujjaya sought out the Buddha at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato"><b>Veḷuvana</b></a> (Bamboo Grove) to seek a deeper truth after becoming dissatisfied with his traditional Brahmin education? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato">Thag 1.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after ordaining, Venerable Ujjaya retired to the forest to meditate specifically on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato"><b>attainment of virtue</b></a> until he reached Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato">Thag 1.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ujjaya addressed the Buddha as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato"><b>hero</b></a> who is liberated in every way and whose life perfectly embodies the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato">Thag 1.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ujjaya described his own state of liberation as living without <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato"><b>effluents</b></a> and being completely free from all worldly trammels? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato">Thag 1.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ujjaya realized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato"><b>pith</b></a> of spiritual life could not be found in external rituals but only through the Buddha's guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato">Thag 1.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ujjaya’s verse serves as a declaration of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato"><b>attained Arahantship</b></a>, confirming that he reached the goal soon after his forest retreat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.47/en/sujato">Thag 1.47</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sañjaya was born into a wealthy Brahmin family in Rājagaha and was inspired to ordain after seeing other famous Brahmins like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato"><b>Pokkharasāti</b></a> find faith in the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato">Thag 1.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sañjaya achieved the state of an Arahant possessing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato"><b>six supernormal knowledges</b></a> after leaving his home to live as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato">Thag 1.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that since the moment of his ordination, Venerable Sañjaya declared he had never entertained any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato"><b>unignoble thoughts</b></a> or worldly preoccupations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato">Thag 1.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sañjaya emphasized that his mind had remained entirely free from any thoughts associated with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato"><b>ill-will</b></a> or anger since joining the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato">Thag 1.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sañjaya followed the spiritual path of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato"><b>Brahmāyu</b></a>, a renowned Brahmin who had attained the first stage of enlightenment before him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato">Thag 1.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sañjaya viewed his transition from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato"><b>home life</b></a> to homelessness as the essential foundation for his mental purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato">Thag 1.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verse of Venerable Sañjaya is considered his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato"><b>declaration of final knowledge</b></a>, proving his total mastery over his own mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.48/en/sujato">Thag 1.48</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rāmaṇeyyaka was born to a wealthy family in Sāvatthī and was spiritually stirred when he witnessed the donation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato"><b>Jetavana</b></a> monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato">Thag 1.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Rāmaṇeyyaka means "one with an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato"><b>amiable behavior</b></a>," a title given to him because of his lovable character and high spiritual attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato">Thag 1.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Venerable Rāmaṇeyyaka was practicing in the forest, Māra (the Evil One) tried to disrupt his concentration by creating a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato"><b>great noise</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato">Thag 1.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rāmaṇeyyaka declared that his mind remains completely unshaken even when faced with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato"><b>shouting of people</b></a> or loud commotions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato">Thag 1.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the distracting sounds of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato"><b>birds and beasts</b></a> in the wild, Venerable Rāmaṇeyyaka maintained a steady and peaceful mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato">Thag 1.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rāmaṇeyyaka specifically meditated on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato"><b>virtue</b></a> while living in seclusion, leading to his rapid spiritual breakthrough? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato">Thag 1.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rāmaṇeyyaka stated that his primary delight was found in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato"><b>solitude</b></a>, where he could practice without any mental agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.49/en/sujato">Thag 1.49</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dabba Mallaputta was born into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato"><b>Malla clan</b></a> at Anupiyā and was only seven years old when he first encountered the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato">Thag 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Dabba Mallaputta passed away while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato"><b>he was born</b></a>, and he was subsequently raised by his grandmother? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato">Thag 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dabba Mallaputta achieved Arahantship and the four fruits of the path exactly at the moment <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato"><b>his hair was shaved</b></a> during his ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato">Thag 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato"><b>seven-year-old child</b></a>, Venerable Dabba Mallaputta requested permission from his grandmother to leave the household life after being deeply moved by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato">Thag 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha appointed Venerable Dabba Mallaputta as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato"><b>Appointer of Lodgings</b></a> because of his extraordinary organizational skills and psychic abilities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato">Thag 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dabba Mallaputta used his psychic powers to make his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato"><b>finger glow</b></a> like a torch, using the light to guide fellow monks to their sleeping quarters at night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato">Thag 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dabba Mallaputta was once falsely slandered by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato"><b>Mettiya and Bhummajaka</b></a> monks, though the Sangha later vindicated his impeccable character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato">Thag 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dabba Mallaputta described himself as one who was difficult to tame but is now fully tamed, dwelling in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato"><b>peace</b></a> with all doubts overcome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.5/en/sujato">Thag 1.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Khandasumana was born into a royal family of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato"><b>Mallas</b></a> before he decided to follow the teachings of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato">Thag 1.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Khandasumana literally translates to "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato"><b>Jasmine Flower</b></a>," a name given to him because jasmine was in full bloom at his birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato">Thag 1.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Khandasumana entered the monastic life after hearing the Buddha preach at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato"><b>mango grove of Cunda</b></a> in the town of Pāvā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato">Thag 1.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his diligent practice, Venerable Khandasumana attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato"><b>six super-knowledges</b></a> (abhiññā), granting him profound spiritual powers and insights? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato">Thag 1.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a past life, Venerable Khandasumana earned a rebirth in the heavenly realms for eight hundred million years by offering a single <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato"><b>branch of jasmine</b></a> at the stupa of Kassapa Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato">Thag 1.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite all the flowers in his district belonging to the king, Venerable Khandasumana risked offering one to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato"><b>stupa</b></a>, which eventually led to his final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato">Thag 1.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Khandasumana spent his final years in a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato"><b>peaceful seclusion</b></a> (upasanta) after exhausting his remaining results of past wholesome deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato">Thag 1.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Khandasumana used his psychic powers to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato"><b>recollect his past life</b></a> and realize how a simple act of devotion had shaped his long journey through saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.96/en/sujato">Thag 1.96</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa was originally a king in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato"><b>Roruva</b></a> who inherited the throne after the death of his father? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato">Thag 1.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Bimbisāra of Magadha was a close <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato"><b>ally</b></a> of Venerable Tissa and sent him precious gifts of jewels, pearls, and fine garments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato">Thag 1.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa’s interest in the Dhamma was sparked when King Bimbisāra sent him a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato"><b>painting of the Buddha</b></a> along with the teachings on Dependent Origination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato">Thag 1.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa received the formula of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato"><b>Dependent Origination</b></a> (paṭicca-samuppāda) inscribed on a golden plate as a gift from a fellow monarch? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato">Thag 1.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after contemplating the teachings on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato"><b>suffering of sensual pleasures</b></a>, Venerable Tissa decided to abdicate his throne and enter the homeless life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato">Thag 1.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa left his palace with only a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato"><b>clay alms bowl</b></a>, abandoning his former hundred bowls made of gold and bronze? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato">Thag 1.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa compared his transition to the monastic life as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato"><b>second coronation</b></a>, considering his spiritual commitment far more valuable than his royal status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato">Thag 1.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa traveled to Rājagaha and met the Buddha at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato"><b>Sabhasondika Cave</b></a>, where he received the instructions that led to his arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato">Thag 1.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the cries of his subjects who followed him in sorrow, Venerable Tissa followed the example of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato"><b>Prince Pukkusāti</b></a> and renounced his kingdom for the sake of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.97/en/sujato">Thag 1.97</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Abhaya was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin family</b></a> in the city of Sāvatthī before he heard the Buddha's teachings and was ordained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato">Thag 1.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while on almsround, the mind of Venerable Abhaya became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato"><b>disturbed</b></a> after he saw a woman dressed in beautiful clothing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato">Thag 1.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Abhaya realized he had made a spiritual error because seeing an attractive form had caused <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato"><b>defilement</b></a> to arise in his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato">Thag 1.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Abhaya warned that when one sees a form and loses <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness</b></a> (sati), one becomes captivated by objects of affection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato">Thag 1.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Abhaya, dwelling on pleasurable appearances causes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato"><b>mental taints</b></a> (āsava) to increase rather than diminish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato">Thag 1.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Abhaya taught that being infatuated with sensory experiences leads directly back to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato"><b>root of existence</b></a> and the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato">Thag 1.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after returning to the monastery, Venerable Abhaya developed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> (vipassanā) to overcome the lustful thoughts he had experienced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato">Thag 1.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Abhaya achieved the status of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> by reflecting on his own mental lapses and applying the Buddha's antidote for desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.98/en/sujato">Thag 1.98</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhaniya was born into a family of potters in Rājagaha and initially continued practicing his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato"><b>ancestral craft</b></a> of making tiles even after joining the monastic order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato">Thag 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the home of Venerable Dhaniya was the location where the Buddha delivered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato"><b>Dhātuvibhaṅga Sutta</b></a> to the monk Pakkusāti? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato">Thag 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhaniya was inspired to seek ordination after realizing that the Buddha’s teaching had the power to lead a person to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a> from suffering in just a single night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato">Thag 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhaniya was once <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato"><b>rebuked by the Buddha</b></a> for constructing a grass-thatched hut using bricks and tiles rather than living more simply as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato">Thag 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhaniya eventually attained the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> while living and practicing among his fellow monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato">Thag 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhaniya taught that if a monk wishes to find happiness in the life of a recluse, he must never <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato"><b>despise the Saṅgha</b></a> or look down upon the monastic community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato">Thag 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhaniya advised recluses not to harbor any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato"><b>contempt</b></a> for the food or drink provided to the monastic community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato">Thag 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhaniya suggested that a monk seeking peace should use his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato"><b>lodgings</b></a> as discreetly and simply as a snake or a mouse uses its hole? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato">Thag 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhaniya emphasized that a practitioner should remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato"><b>content</b></a> with whatever provisions or circumstances they encounter in their spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato">Thag 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhaniya encouraged monks to practice with determination and focus on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato"><b>single state</b></a> of concentrated mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.4/en/sujato">Thag 3.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mātaṅgaputta was born in Kosala as the son of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato"><b>landowner</b></a> and spent his early life being quite lazy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato">Thag 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mātaṅgaputta initially decided to associate with monks because he observed that they seemed to live in a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato"><b>great happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato">Thag 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mātaṅgaputta was motivated to deepen his practice after seeing other monks demonstrate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato"><b>supernormal powers</b></a> through their meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato">Thag 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mātaṅgaputta eventually attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> through the dedicated practice of insight meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato">Thag 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mātaṅgaputta criticized those who avoid their spiritual work by complaining that it is "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato"><b>too cold</b></a>" or "too hot" for exertion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato">Thag 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mātaṅgaputta warned that making excuses like "it is already too late" causes the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato"><b>precious moment</b></a> for spiritual progress to slip away silently? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato">Thag 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mātaṅgaputta taught that a wise person who fulfills their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato"><b>human duties</b></a> without being deterred by the weather will not lose their inner happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato">Thag 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mātaṅgaputta used his own chest to push through rough <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato"><b>kusa and dabba grass</b></a> in order to reach the seclusion of the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato">Thag 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mātaṅgaputta endured the pain of thorny grass to live in a way that would help him grow in his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato"><b>delight for solitude</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato">Thag 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mātaṅgaputta, once a lazy youth, became a strong advocate for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato"><b>spiritual energy</b></a> and the persistent effort required to end all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.5/en/sujato">Thag 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahānāga was born into a brahmin family in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato"><b>Sāketa</b></a> as the son of Madhu Vāseṭṭha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato">Thag 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahānāga was inspired to go forth into homelessness after witnessing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato"><b>psychic powers</b></a> displayed by the Elder Gavampati? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato">Thag 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahānāga received his ordination and spiritual training while the Buddha was staying in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato"><b>Anjana Forest</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato">Thag 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahānāga directed his verses specifically to six monks who showed a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato"><b>lack of respect</b></a> toward their fellow companions in the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato">Thag 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahānāga, those who do not respect their companions in the holy life will decline in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato"><b>True Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato">Thag 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahānāga compared a monk who lacks respect for his peers to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato"><b>fish in shallow water</b></a>, destined for decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato">Thag 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahānāga likened a practitioner without respect for others to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato"><b>rotten seed</b></a> that fails to grow in a field? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato">Thag 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahānāga taught that those who lack reverence for their fellow practitioners wander far away from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato">Thag 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahānāga stated that those who maintain deep respect for their companions in the holy life are like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato"><b>fish in deep water</b></a> who never decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato">Thag 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahānāga, a practitioner who possesses true respect is like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato"><b>good seed</b></a> that flourishes in a fertile field? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato">Thag 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahānāga declared that those who are respectful and reverent toward others have truly come near to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato">Thag 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahānāga considered the practice of mutual respect to be a core element of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato"><b>teaching of the King of Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato">Thag 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahānāga used his verses as a formal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato"><b>declaration of final knowledge</b></a> (aññābyākarana) after attaining Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.3/en/sujato">Thag 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kulla was born in the city of Sāvatthī into a family of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato"><b>landowners</b></a> before he entered the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato">Thag 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his early days as a monk, Venerable Kulla struggled immensely because his mind was frequently dominated by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato"><b>sensual desire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato">Thag 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally intervened in the training of Venerable Kulla by giving him a meditation subject focused on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato"><b>impurity</b></a> (asubha)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato">Thag 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that to help him overcome lust, the Buddha instructed Venerable Kulla to perform his meditation in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato"><b>charnel ground</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato">Thag 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kulla was directed by the Buddha to observe a discarded <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato"><b>female corpse</b></a> to witness the natural process of decay and corruption? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato">Thag 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kulla described the human body as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato"><b>foul and oozing</b></a> object that only the foolish find delightful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato">Thag 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kulla attained a breakthrough in insight by using what he called the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato"><b>mirror of the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato">Thag 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after contemplating the decay of others, Venerable Kulla realized that his own body was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato"><b>empty</b></a> both inside and out? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato">Thag 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kulla achieved a state of realization where he saw that the body's nature remains the same <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato"><b>day and night</b></a> and across all time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato">Thag 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kulla taught that the nature of the body is consistent, noting that as it is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato"><b>above</b></a>, so it is below? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato">Thag 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kulla stated that a person who has attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato"><b>one-pointedness of mind</b></a> through meditation finds no delight in worldly music? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato">Thag 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while meditating on a corpse, Venerable Kulla reached a higher state of consciousness after the Buddha appeared to him by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato"><b>emitting an aura</b></a> of light? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato">Thag 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kulla successfully transformed his initial weakness for sensual pleasure into the total <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a> of an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.4/en/sujato">Thag 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sela</b></a> was a highly educated Brahmin from Aṅguttarāpa who was an expert in the three Vedas and the art of Brahmanical rituals before he met the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela served as a teacher to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>three hundred young Brahmins</b></a>, instructing them in the sacred hymns and mantras? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela, upon first seeing the Buddha, was immediately struck by the Buddha's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>perfect physical form</b></a> and radiant, golden-colored skin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela carefully examined the Buddha to see if he possessed all the thirty-two <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>marks of a Great Man</b></a>, which signify that one will become either a Wheel-turning Monarch or a Fully Enlightened Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela described the Buddha as being like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>bright sun</b></a> shining in the midst of the assembly of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela initially thought that the Buddha, with such majestic physical features, was worthy of being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>Wheel-turning Monarch</b></a> who rules the four quarters of the earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela encouraged the Buddha to act as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>king of kings</b></a> and the lord of men, urging him to rule over the country? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha responded to Venerable Sela by declaring himself to be the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>supreme King of Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Venerable Sela that he turns the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>unexcelled wheel of Dhamma</b></a>, a wheel that has never been turned in such a way before? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Sela asked who served as the general or chief disciple to the Buddha, the Buddha pointed to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described Venerable Sāriputta as the one who keeps the wheel of Dhamma turning and as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>heir of the Tathāgata</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared to Venerable Sela that he is the Enlightened One because he has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>known what should be known</b></a> and abandoned what should be abandoned? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha asserted to Venerable Sela that he had fully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>developed what should be developed</b></a>, which is why he is rightfully called a Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged Venerable Sela to dispel all doubts regarding him, noting that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>seeing a Fully Enlightened One</b></a> is an extremely rare occurrence in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha identified himself to Venerable Sela as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>supreme physician</b></a> who can cure the spiritual ailments of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha proclaimed himself to be like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>peerless Brahma</b></a>, having crushed all of Mara's armies and brought all opponents under his control? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela compared the Buddha’s powerful and clear teaching to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>roar of a lion</b></a> in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela was so moved by the Buddha's words that he invited his three hundred students to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>ordain as monks</b></a> alongside him in the Buddha's presence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela told his disciples that the Buddha is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>one of supreme wisdom</b></a>, and that anyone, even those of low birth, would find faith in him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha welcomed Venerable Sela and his followers, stating that the holy life he teaches is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>immediately effective</b></a> and timeless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized that for one who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>diligent and energetic</b></a> in training, the act of renunciation and ordination will not be in vain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela and his three hundred disciples achieved the goal of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> in just seven days after their ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela addressed the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>Seer with the Eye of Dhamma</b></a>, acknowledging that they had found refuge in him exactly seven nights prior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela praised the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>Enlightened Sage</b></a> who had conquered the forces of Mara and cut off all mental impurities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela described the Buddha as the one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>crossed the ocean of birth and death</b></a> and now helps all other living beings to cross that same flood of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela noted that the Buddha had completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>crushed the taints</b></a>, leaving him like a fearless lion king who is no longer subject to any clinging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela, along with his three hundred monks, paid homage by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>standing with hands together</b></a>, inviting the Great Hero to let them bow at his feet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela mentioned the Buddha's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>teeth were smooth and white</b></a>, which was one of the specific physical characteristics he checked to verify the Buddha's identity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela highlighted that the Buddha’s face was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>radiant and beautiful</b></a>, perfectly proportional and straight, making him stand out in any crowd? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Venerable Sela that one becomes a Buddha by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the causes of suffering</b></a> rather than through outward rituals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela initially encountered the Buddha in a forest near the village of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>Apaṇa</b></a>, where the Buddha had stopped while traveling with 1,250 monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha's presence in the region was first brought to the attention of Venerable Sela by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>ascetic Keniya</b></a>, who was preparing a massive feast for the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela felt a deep sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>spiritual urgency</b></a> as soon as he heard the word "Buddha," which prompted him to seek out the Teacher immediately? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela observed that the Buddha's skin was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>bright as gold</b></a> and wondered why such a magnificent person would want to live the life of a simple monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela identified the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>unmatched leader of men</b></a> who had reached the highest state of purity and mental calm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha’s reply to Venerable Sela emphasized that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>kingship was over the Dhamma</b></a>, not over a physical territory or a group of people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described his state as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>beyond time</b></a> (akālika), meaning that the benefits of his teaching can be realized in the present moment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sela noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta’s head</b></a> shone like a mass of gold while he sat to the right of the Buddha during their meeting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the three hundred students of Venerable Sela decided to follow him into the monkhood because they shared his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>faith in the Buddha's wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Venerable Sela that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato"><b>relinquishing all substances</b></a> and attachments, one becomes a true conqueror who is no longer afraid of anything? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.6/en/sujato">Thag 16.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala was born into a wealthy family in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato"><b>Rājagaha</b></a> before he was inspired to join the Buddhist Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato">Thag 1.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Vimala means "stainless," a title given to him because his body was said to be as pure as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato"><b>dewdrop on a lotus leaf</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato">Thag 1.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after meeting the Buddha in his hometown, Venerable Vimala decided to renounce his worldly life and live in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato"><b>mountain cave</b></a> in the region of Kosala? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato">Thag 1.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala achieved final liberation as an Arahant while contemplating during a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato"><b>thunderstorm</b></a> that cooled the air and calmed his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato">Thag 1.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala declared his mind was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato"><b>well concentrated</b></a> and his thoughts were tranquil even as lightning flashed across the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato">Thag 1.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala's spiritual breakthrough was aided by the physical sensation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato"><b>cool wind</b></a> and rain wetting the earth during his meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato">Thag 1.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala used the weather conditions of a storm as the basis for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato"><b>declaration of final knowledge</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato">Thag 1.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verse of Venerable Vimala emphasizes how the calming of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato"><b>external environment</b></a> helped him settle his internal mental state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato">Thag 1.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala's birth into a prosperous family did not prevent him from seeking the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato"><b>pith of the Dhamma</b></a> through solitary forest dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato">Thag 1.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala described his state of liberation as having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato"><b>still mind</b></a> even while nature around him was in a state of atmospheric turmoil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.50/en/sujato">Thag 1.50</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Godhika was one of four close friends, all sons of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato"><b>Malla kings</b></a> from Pāvā, who ordained together after witnessing the Buddha's miracles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato">Thag 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Godhika and his three royal friends—Subāhu, Valliya, and Uttiya—were converted by the Buddha’s performance of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato"><b>twin miracle</b></a> in Kapilavatthu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato">Thag 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Bimbisāra once invited Venerable Godhika and his companions to spend the rains retreat in Rājagaha but inadvertently forgot to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato"><b>roof their huts</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato">Thag 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Venerable Godhika and his companions lived in roofless huts, it <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato"><b>did not rain</b></a> for the entire season until the king realized his mistake and provided shelter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato">Thag 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that once their dwellings were properly covered, Venerable Godhika and his companions developed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> so powerful that a great rainstorm immediately began from the north and east? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato">Thag 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Godhika compared the sound of the falling rain to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato"><b>beautiful song</b></a> as he meditated in his newly roofed hut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato">Thag 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Godhika welcomed the storm by saying "rain, sky, if you wish," because he was already <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato"><b>securely sheltered</b></a> and his mind was perfectly tranquil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato">Thag 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Godhika and his three friends attained Arahantship with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato"><b>four analytical knowledges</b></a> shortly after their ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato">Thag 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before they became monks, Venerable Godhika and his companions were on a diplomatic mission to meet the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato"><b>King of Kapilavatthu</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato">Thag 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verse of Venerable Godhika illustrates that a monk with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato"><b>well-concentrated mind</b></a> can live in perfect bliss regardless of the intense weather conditions outside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.51/en/sujato">Thag 1.51</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vasabha was born in the city of Vesālī as a prince of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato"><b>Licchavi royal family</b></a> before he renounced his status to join the Buddha's Order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato">Thag 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vasabha was inspired to seek ordination after being deeply moved by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato"><b>majesty and virtue</b></a> of the Buddha during the Teacher's visit to Vesālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato">Thag 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that out of gratitude to those who supported him, Venerable Vasabha would not refuse any offered requisites, even though he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato"><b>criticized by worldly people</b></a> for accepting such gifts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato">Thag 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Sakka, the Lord of Gods, asked about a nearby monk who was pretending to have few desires to deceive the public, Venerable Vasabha spoke a verse to expose the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato"><b>falsehood of the wicked</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato">Thag 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vasabha compared a person who lives a life of spiritual deception to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato"><b>decoy bird</b></a>, stating that they first destroy their own character before they harm others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato">Thag 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vasabha taught that one is not a true Brahmin based on outward appearance, but rather through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato"><b>internal purity</b></a> and virtuous conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato">Thag 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vasabha used the term "dark person" to describe anyone who performs <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato"><b>evil deeds</b></a>, regardless of their social status or religious claims? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato">Thag 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while explaining the nature of spiritual integrity to Sakka, Venerable Vasabha addressed the king of gods by his title as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato"><b>husband of Sujā</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato">Thag 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his dedicated practice following his ordination, Venerable Vasabha attained the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> in a very short time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.10/en/sujato">Thag 2.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda was the younger brother of the Buddha's chief disciple, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a>, and followed him into the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato">Thag 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda was born in the village of Nālaka in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato"><b>Magadha country</b></a> to a Brahmin woman named Rūpasārī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato">Thag 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda taught that by correctly listening to the Dhamma, one's learning increases, which in turn leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato"><b>growth of wisdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato">Thag 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahācunda, once a practitioner uses wisdom to understand the deep meaning of the teachings, they experience the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato"><b>attainment of happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato">Thag 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda advised practitioners to dwell in secluded, remote places to live a life that allows them to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato"><b>escape the fetters</b></a> (saṃyojana) of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato">Thag 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda suggested that if spiritual joy is not reached in solitude, a monk should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato"><b>live among the Sangha</b></a> to support their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato">Thag 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda emphasized that even when living with others, a practitioner must remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato"><b>self-controlled</b></a> and firmly established in mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato">Thag 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after a period of diligent and energetic spiritual practice, Venerable Mahācunda successfully achieved the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato">Thag 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahācunda declared his spiritual victory and his delight in a life of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> through the recitation of inspired verses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.11/en/sujato">Thag 2.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Khujja Sobhita was born into a Brahmin family in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato"><b>Pāṭaliputta</b></a> before joining the monastic order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato">Thag 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Khujja Sobhita contains the prefix "Khujja" because he was born with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato"><b>hunchbacked</b></a> physical condition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato">Thag 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Khujja Sobhita was ordained into the Sangha by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ānanda</b></a> shortly after the Buddha’s final passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato">Thag 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Khujja Sobhita attained the state of an Arahant and possessed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> through his spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato">Thag 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the First Buddhist Council, Venerable Khujja Sobhita was assigned the important task of inviting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ānanda</b></a> to the assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato">Thag 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Venerable Khujja Sobhita was at the First Council, a deity guarded the entrance of the cave to prevent any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato"><b>hostile actions</b></a> from Māra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato">Thag 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Khujja Sobhita was highly regarded in Pāṭaliputta as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato"><b>skilled speaker</b></a> and a monk of great learning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato">Thag 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Khujja Sobhita described himself as a victor who had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato"><b>fought well</b></a> on the spiritual battlefield? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato">Thag 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the accumulation of the holy life, Venerable Khujja Sobhita declared that he had attained a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato"><b>abiding happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato">Thag 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Khujja Sobhita arrived at the assembly of monks as if <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato"><b>carried by the wind</b></a>, showcasing the power of his spiritual attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.6/en/sujato">Thag 3.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vāraṇa was born into a Brahmin family in the kingdom of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato"><b>Kosala</b></a> during the time of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato">Thag 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vāraṇa was inspired to renounce the worldly life after hearing a senior monk preach the Dhamma in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato"><b>forest grove</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato">Thag 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vāraṇa experienced a deep sense of spiritual urgency after witnessing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato"><b>violent conflict</b></a> within a family that resulted in many deaths? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato">Thag 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Venerable Vāraṇa that anyone who harms other living beings ultimately <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato"><b>harms themselves</b></a> in both the present life and the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato">Thag 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Venerable Vāraṇa that maintaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> toward all sentient beings leads to the accumulation of great merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato">Thag 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vāraṇa was encouraged by the Teacher to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato"><b>skillful speech</b></a> as a core part of his spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato">Thag 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha advised Venerable Vāraṇa to live in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> and focus his efforts on achieving mental tranquility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato">Thag 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vāraṇa was taught to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato"><b>serve the recluses</b></a> and associate with those who are advanced in the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato">Thag 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the Buddha's guidance, Venerable Vāraṇa learned that true happiness is found when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato"><b>mind is stilled</b></a> and free from agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato">Thag 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vāraṇa’s verses emphasize that a person who acts with compassion will harvest <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato"><b>abundant merit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.7/en/sujato">Thag 3.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta was born in Sāvatthī as the son of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>appraiser for King Kosala</b></a> before entering the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Māluṅkyaputta literally means the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>son of Māluṅkyā</b></a>, which was his mother's name? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before becoming a disciple of the Buddha, Venerable Māluṅkyaputta lived as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>wandering ascetic</b></a> of another sect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after receiving the Buddha's teaching and practicing diligently, Venerable Māluṅkyaputta attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> (chalabhiññā) of an enlightened being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Māluṅkyaputta visited his relatives, they tried to tempt him back to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>secular life</b></a> by suggesting he could use his wealth to perform good works while being a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta taught that craving (taṇhā) grows in a negligent person like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>māluvā creeper</b></a> that eventually chokes the tree it climbs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta likened a person driven by desire to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>monkey in the forest</b></a> that leaps from tree to tree in search of fruit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Māluṅkyaputta, when a person is overcome by wretched craving, their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>sorrows increase</b></a> just as grass grows rapidly after a rainfall? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta taught that for one who masters craving, sorrows fall away like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>drops of water</b></a> sliding off a lotus leaf? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta urged seekers to dig out the root of craving just as one would dig for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>sweet-scented usīra root</b></a> to find something valuable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta warned that those who do not conquer desire will be crushed by Mara, just as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>flood crushes reeds</b></a> along a riverbank? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta emphasized that those who let the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>opportune moment</b></a> pass will suffer greatly when they experience the consequences of their actions in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta described <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>negligence</b></a> (pamāda) as being like dust or filth that stains and hinders a practitioner's progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Māluṅkyaputta declared that through wisdom and diligence, one can <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato"><b>pull out the arrow</b></a> of suffering from oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.5/en/sujato">Thag 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sappadāsa was born in Kapilavatthu as the son of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato"><b>sacrificial chaplain</b></a> to King Suddhodana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato">Thag 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his ordination, Venerable Sappadāsa struggled for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato"><b>twenty-five years</b></a> without being able to achieve even a finger-snap's worth of mental peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato">Thag 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sappadāsa found his spiritual path repeatedly blocked by being dominated by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato"><b>sensual lust</b></a> and long-standing habits? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato">Thag 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that out of intense despair over his lack of concentration, Venerable Sappadāsa once left the monastery while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato"><b>weeping</b></a> and wringing his hands? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato">Thag 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sappadāsa reached a point of such deep frustration that he believed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato"><b>dying</b></a> was a better option than living as a monk who failed in his training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato">Thag 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sappadāsa went as far as drawing a knife while on his bed, fully intending to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato"><b>slit his own throat</b></a> in despair? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato">Thag 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that at the very threshold of suicide, Venerable Sappadāsa aroused <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato"><b>radical reflection</b></a> (yoniso manasikāra) that immediately shifted his mind toward truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato">Thag 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as Venerable Sappadāsa contemplated the inherent dangers of the world, a deep sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> arose, leading to his liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato">Thag 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sappadāsa’s mind became completely released from all taints while he was still holding the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato"><b>razor</b></a> he had intended to use on himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato">Thag 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his sudden breakthrough, Venerable Sappadāsa attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā) and finally fulfilled the Buddha's instructions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato">Thag 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sappadāsa used his verses as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato"><b>declaration of final knowledge</b></a>, showing that even the deepest despair can be transformed into enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.6/en/sujato">Thag 6.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Bhaddiya Kāligodhāyaputta</b></a> was a member of the Sakyan royal family in Kapilavatthu and was eventually declared by the Buddha to be the foremost among those of aristocratic birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato">Thag 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddiya Kāligodhāyaputta was often heard exclaiming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato"><b>“Oh, what happiness! Oh, what happiness!”</b></a> while living in the solitude of the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato">Thag 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before he became a monk, Venerable Bhaddiya Kāligodhāyaputta used to travel while sitting on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato"><b>shoulders of an elephant</b></a> and wearing the most delicate and expensive garments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato">Thag 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddiya Kāligodhāyaputta once enjoyed meals consisting of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato"><b>fragrant rice</b></a> and well-cooked meats, yet he found even greater joy in the simple food he received in his alms bowl? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato">Thag 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite being protected by high walls, watchtowers, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato"><b>sword-bearing guards</b></a> during his time as a ruler, Venerable Bhaddiya Kāligodhāyaputta lived in a state of constant fear and anxiety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato">Thag 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddiya Kāligodhāyaputta found a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato"><b>fearlessness</b></a> only after renouncing his kingdom and entering the forest to practice meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato">Thag 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddiya Kāligodhāyaputta, also known as the son of Godhā, practiced the ascetic dhutaṅga of wearing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato"><b>rag-robes</b></a> collected from refuse heaps? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.7/en/sujato">Thag 16.7</a></p></div><hr>
