<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the powerful preaching of Venerable Jotidāsa convinced a group of ascetics who formerly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.12/en/sujato"><b>believed in Brahma</b></a> to seek ordination under him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.12/en/sujato">Thag 2.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Jotidāsa warned his relatives that those who use <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.12/en/sujato"><b>robbery and violence</b></a> to harm others are actually sowing the seeds of their own future destruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.12/en/sujato">Thag 2.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Jotidāsa emphasized the law of karma by stating that everyone is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.12/en/sujato"><b>heir of their deeds</b></a>, whether those deeds are wholesome or unwholesome? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.12/en/sujato">Thag 2.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before becoming a monk, Venerable Heraññakāni was a royal servant to King Pasenadi of Kosala and a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato"><b>leader of robbers</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato">Thag 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Heraññakāni was inspired to renounce the world after witnessing the solemn ceremony of the Buddha accepting the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato"><b>Jetavana Monastery</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato">Thag 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon his ordination, Venerable Heraññakāni handed over his worldly duties and leadership position to his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato"><b>younger brother</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato">Thag 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after attaining the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a>, Venerable Heraññakāni returned to advise his brother to abandon his life of crime? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato">Thag 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Heraññakāni pointed out that while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato"><b>days and nights</b></a> pass by, a person's life is constantly being depleted and destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato">Thag 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Heraññakāni compared the rapid disappearance of a human lifespan to the flowing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato"><b>water of a small stream</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato">Thag 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Heraññakāni cautioned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato"><b>foolish</b></a> perform evil actions without realizing the consequences that will follow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato">Thag 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Heraññakāni, a person only truly experiences intense suffering when their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato"><b>evil karma</b></a> reaches maturity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato">Thag 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the persistent encouragement of Venerable Heraññakāni, his brother eventually sought the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato"><b>King's permission</b></a> to resign and ordain as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato">Thag 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the brother of Venerable Heraññakāni eventually achieved <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato"><b>total liberation</b></a> from all suffering by following the path his brother had shown him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.13/en/sujato">Thag 2.13</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nāgasamāla was born into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan royal family</b></a> before he decided to renounce his worldly life and go forth as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato">Thag 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nāgasamāla achieved spiritual liberation after encountering a woman <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato"><b>dancing to music</b></a> on a main road while he was walking for alms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato">Thag 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nāgasamāla described a beautifully dressed and perfumed dancing girl as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato"><b>snare of Death</b></a> that had been spread out to trap the unwary? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato">Thag 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a>, Venerable Nāgasamāla was able to see the inherent danger in sensual sights and develop a sense of disillusionment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato">Thag 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nāgasamāla declared that his mind was liberated and he had achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato"><b>Three Knowledges</b></a> after observing the impermanent nature of worldly beauty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato">Thag 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nāgasamāla successfully transformed a potentially distracting encounter with a woman <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato"><b>adorned with garlands</b></a> into a profound subject for insight meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato">Thag 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nāgasamāla reached the goal of the holy life by realizing that things are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato"><b>exactly as they are</b></a>, thereby completing all the Buddha’s instructions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato">Thag 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nāgasamāla was perfumed with the "scent of sandalwood" in his verses to contrast it with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato"><b>spiritual fragrance</b></a> of his own meditative discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.1/en/sujato">Thag 4.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhammika was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin family</b></a> in the kingdom of Kosala before he was inspired to join the monastic order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato">Thag 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhammika felt a deep sense of faith and decided to ordain as a monk after witnessing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato"><b>donation of the Jetavana</b></a> monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato">Thag 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhammika taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma protects</b></a> those who live according to its principles, just as a large umbrella protects one from the rain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato">Thag 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhammika explained that the results of following the Dhamma and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato"><b>following what is not Dhamma</b></a> are entirely different, leading to either a good destination or hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato">Thag 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhammika achieved liberation by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato"><b>uprooting the net of craving</b></a> and ending the long cycle of wandering in Saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato">Thag 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhammika compared a mind that has attained peace and ended rebirth to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato"><b>full moon</b></a> shining brightly in a clear night sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato">Thag 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhammika attained the state of an Arahant while he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato"><b>sitting and listening</b></a> to the Buddha recite verses about the benefits of practicing the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato">Thag 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhammika proclaimed that for one who is firmly established in the Dhamma, there is no more <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato"><b>rebirth-substrate</b></a> and all suffering has been brought to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato">Thag 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhammika emphasized that those who practice the Dhamma well will experience its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato"><b>greatest benefit</b></a> by never descending into a state of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato">Thag 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his enlightenment, Venerable Dhammika was once so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato"><b>unwelcoming to visiting monks</b></a> at his village monastery that they all left, leaving him alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato">Thag 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhammika was summoned by the Buddha to explain his behavior after a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato"><b>lay follower</b></a> reported that other monks were avoiding his residence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato">Thag 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Dhammika encouraged his fellow practitioners to feel <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato"><b>spiritual joy</b></a> and delight in the Dhamma, as it is the path that leads to the ultimate refuge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.10/en/sujato">Thag 4.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before entering the monastic life, Venerable Jenta was born as the son of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>sacrificial chaplain</b></a> to the King of Kosala? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his youth, Venerable Jenta was extremely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>intoxicated by pride</b></a> due to his noble birth, wealth, and high social status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Jenta first went to see the Buddha, he pridefully intended to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>not speak first</b></a>, waiting instead for the Buddha to address him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, knowing his thoughts, warned Venerable Jenta that dwelling in conceit is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>truly not good</b></a> and that he should focus on his own spiritual benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after being moved by the Buddha's insight, Venerable Jenta asked the Teacher which individuals <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>deserve respect</b></a> and toward whom one should not be arrogant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Venerable Jenta that it is essential to show honor and reverence toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>one’s mother and father</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Venerable Jenta that showing respect toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>one’s eldest brother</b></a> is a virtuous and good deed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha’s advice to Venerable Jenta, one must always treat <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>one's teacher</b></a> with the highest degree of reverence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emphasized to Venerable Jenta that respect should be given to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>Brahmins</b></a> and those who live according to spiritual principles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Venerable Jenta that practitioners should specifically revere <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>yellow-robed ascetics</b></a> who have renounced the world for the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Venerable Jenta that one should honor <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>Arahants</b></a>, who are peaceful, stainless, and have completely ended all forms of conceit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing the Buddha’s discourse on humility and respect, Venerable Jenta immediately attained the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>Stream-entry</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that following his ordination and a period of diligent practice, Venerable Jenta successfully reached the final goal of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his verses of realization, Venerable Jenta confessed that he used to be obsessed with his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>complexion and beauty</b></a> alongside his power and status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Jenta admitted that in his past state of delusion, he was so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>stubborn and haughty</b></a> that he refused to bow even to his own parents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Jenta described the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>supreme leader</b></a> and the peerless trainer of those to be tamed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Jenta likened the Buddha’s presence leading the community of monks to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>sun shining</b></a> brightly in the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Jenta eventually cast away all forms of pride, including <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>superiority and inferiority conceit</b></a>, through his meditative practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a sign of his complete internal transformation, Venerable Jenta declared that he now bows his head to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>Highest of Beings</b></a> with a peaceful heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Jenta proclaimed his final liberation by stating that he had completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato"><b>uprooted arrogance</b></a> and destroyed all taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.9/en/sujato">Thag 6.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha was born into the royal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan clan</b></a> as the son of Amitodana and was a cousin of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha was the brother of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan Mahānāma</b></a> and was raised in extreme luxury with separate residences for each season? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha renounced his home life at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>Anupiyā mango grove</b></a>, abandoning his parents, siblings, and the five strands of sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his first rainy season as a monk, Venerable Anuruddha successfully developed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a>, which allowed him to see beyond the range of human vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha was declared by the Buddha to be the foremost among all monks who possessed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while meditating in the Eastern Bamboo Park, Venerable Anuruddha contemplated the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>seven thoughts of a great man</b></a>, but needed the Buddha’s help to realize the eighth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> along with the three knowledges and the four analytical knowledges after receiving specific instructions from the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha described his former life of music and dance as living within the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>domain of Māra</b></a>, which he eventually transcended through meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha claimed to have crossed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>flood of existence</b></a> and found delight in the Buddha’s teachings, far surpassing his previous worldly joys? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha practiced the extreme ascetic discipline of collecting <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>rags from refuse heaps</b></a> to sew his own robes after returning from his alms rounds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha was known for being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>wise and mindful</b></a>, carefully washing and dyeing the discarded cloths he found to use as monastic garments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha warned that a person who is full of desire, discontent, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>fond of socializing</b></a> possesses corrupt and defiled states of mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha emphasized that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>fewness of desires</b></a>, contentment, and seclusion are essential qualities that belong to the factors of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha once visited Venerable Anuruddha using a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>mind-made body</b></a> through psychic power to teach him the Dhamma in person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha learned from the Buddha that the Enlightened One does not delight in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>conceptual proliferation</b></a> but teaches the Dhamma that is free from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha achieved a remarkable feat of endurance by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>never lying down</b></a> for fifty-five years as part of his monastic training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for twenty-five years of his life, Venerable Anuruddha completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>eradicated sleepiness</b></a> and drowsiness through his dedicated practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha provided a famous description of the Buddha's final passing, noting that the Buddha stayed in a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>unshakable mind</b></a> as he breathed his last? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha compared the death of the Buddha to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>flame being extinguished</b></a>, signifying the complete liberation of the Buddha's mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when a deity who was his former servant invited him to be reborn in the heaven of the Thirty-three, Venerable Anuruddha firmly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>rejected the offer</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha told a tempting deity that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>rebirth in the god-realms</b></a> was over and that the cycle of birth and death had been completely severed for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha possessed the psychic power to know a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>thousandfold world system</b></a> in a very short period of time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his divine eye, Venerable Anuruddha could clearly see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>passing away and rebirth</b></a> of various beings, including deities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a very distant past life, Venerable Anuruddha was a poor laborer named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>Annabhara</b></a> who worked hard just to survive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while he was the poor laborer Annabhara, Venerable Anuruddha made a significant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>offering of food</b></a> to a famous Paccekabuddha named Uparittha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha recalled having been reborn as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>Sakka</b></a>, the king of the gods, in the heaven of the Thirty-three during his past lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha served as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>world-ruling monarch</b></a> seven times, conquering the four quarters of the earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a king, Venerable Anuruddha ruled over the Rose-apple Island of Jambudīpa using only the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma</b></a>, without ever resorting to weapons or violence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha could remember living in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>heavenly realms</b></a> for fourteen cycles of rebirth before his final human life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha practiced a specific form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>five-factored concentration</b></a> that led his mind to become perfectly peaceful and unified? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his meditation, Venerable Anuruddha achieved a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>tranquillity</b></a> and "one-pointedness" of mind that purified his divine eye? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha declared that he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>laid down the burden</b></a> of existence and completely uprooted the causes of being reborn? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha predicted that he would attain final Nibbāna in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>village of Veluva</b></a> among the Vajjian people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha looked forward to his final passing under the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>shade of a bamboo thicket</b></a>, free from all mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha stated that all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>sensory objects</b></a>, such as forms, sounds, and tastes, had been transcended through his diligent meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha viewed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> as a path of constant effort, joyfully fulfilling all the instructions left by his Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha identified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>Buddha</b></a> as the "Peerless Master of the World" who personally came to him through psychic power to assist his progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha credited his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>faith in the Buddha</b></a> as the reason he was able to leave his life of luxury and ordain as a homeless monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Anuruddha described his final state as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>extinguished without fuel</b></a>, meaning he would never again experience the pain of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Anuruddha, a monk who lives with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato"><b>energy and resolve</b></a> is the one who truly finishes the work taught by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.9/en/sujato">Thag 16.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttiya was one of four sons of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato"><b>Malla kings</b></a> from the city of Pāvā who shared a deep and sincere friendship throughout their lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato">Thag 1.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttiya was first converted to the Buddhist path when the Buddha performed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato"><b>Twin Miracle</b></a> at the Banyan Tree Grove in Kapilavatthu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato">Thag 1.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after ordaining, Venerable Uttiya and his royal companions achieved Arahantship with a profound <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato"><b>understanding of the meaning</b></a> and essence of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato">Thag 1.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Bimbisāra once invited Venerable Uttiya to spend the rains retreat in Rājagaha but inadvertently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato"><b>forgot to roof the hut</b></a> he had built for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato">Thag 1.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because of the spiritual power of Venerable Uttiya and his companions, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato"><b>it did not rain</b></a> in their area for the entire monsoon season until the king realized his oversight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato">Thag 1.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that once King Bimbisāra provided a roof for his dwelling, Venerable Uttiya and his friends radiated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato"><b>loving-kindness</b></a> so strongly that a massive storm immediately arose from the north and east? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato">Thag 1.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttiya poetically described the sound of the falling rain as being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato"><b>beautiful song</b></a> while he sat comfortably and securely in his sheltered hut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato">Thag 1.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttiya declared that his mind was so peaceful that he lived in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato"><b>great bliss</b></a>, dwelling alone in a cell that was completely protected from the wind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato">Thag 1.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttiya invited the sky to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato"><b>rain as much as it wanted</b></a>, as external weather conditions could no longer disturb his internal concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato">Thag 1.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttiya and his royal friends were on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato"><b>diplomatic mission</b></a> to Kapilavatthu when they first encountered the Buddha and decided to join the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato">Thag 1.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the story of Venerable Uttiya illustrates how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato"><b>spiritual attainments</b></a> of monks can miraculously influence the elements of nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.54/en/sujato">Thag 1.54</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Añjanavaniya was born in the city of Vesālī as a prince in the royal family of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato"><b>Vajjan kingdom</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato">Thag 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the spiritual journey of Venerable Añjanavaniya began when Vesālī was threatened by the three great calamities of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato"><b>drought, disease, and non-humans</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato">Thag 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the historical events leading to the ordination of Venerable Añjanavaniya are documented in the traditional commentaries on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato"><b>Ratanasutta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato">Thag 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Añjanavaniya was inspired to follow the Buddha after witnessing how the Dhamma successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato"><b>relieved the fears</b></a> and suffering of the people of Vesālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato">Thag 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Añjanavaniya chose to live a solitary life in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato"><b>Añjana Forest</b></a> near the city of Sāketa to deepen his meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato">Thag 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Añjanavaniya constructed his own humble monastic dwelling by placing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato"><b>discarded bed</b></a> on four stones and covering the structure with grass? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato">Thag 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite living in a makeshift shelter, Venerable Añjanavaniya attained the fruit of Arahantship in only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato"><b>one month</b></a> of diligent practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato">Thag 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Añjanavaniya joyfully declared that he had attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā), marking his complete mastery of the spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato">Thag 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Añjanavaniya’s verse serves as a confirmation that he has successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato"><b>done the Buddha’s bidding</b></a> and reached the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato">Thag 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Añjanavaniya first had to complete his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato"><b>preliminary monastic training</b></a> after leaving his life as a prince before he retired into the deep forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato">Thag 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Añjanavaniya literally means "one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato"><b>dwells in the Añjana Forest</b></a>," reflecting his commitment to a life of forest-dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.55/en/sujato">Thag 1.55</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Somamitta was born in Benares into a Brahmin family and was an expert in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato"><b>three Vedas</b></a> before he was ordained by Elder Vimala? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato">Thag 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Somamitta decided to leave his first mentor because he realized that a person seeking virtue should not live near someone who had become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato"><b>lazy and fond of sleep</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato">Thag 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Somamitta attained the state of an Arahant after seeking out the company of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā Kassapa</b></a> to hear the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato">Thag 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Somamitta compared associating with a lazy person to a man trying to cross the ocean on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato"><b>small plank</b></a>, which causes even a virtuous person to sink? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato">Thag 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Somamitta advised spiritual seekers to abandon the company of the indolent and instead live near those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato"><b>secluded and energetic</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato">Thag 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Somamitta encouraged association with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato"><b>Noble ones</b></a> (Ariyas), who are wise, concentrated, and constantly devoted to spiritual effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato">Thag 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his verses, Venerable Somamitta was able to move <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato"><b>Elder Vimala</b></a> so deeply that the elder overcame his laziness and eventually achieved liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato">Thag 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Somamitta emphasized that a practitioner should possess clear-sighted wisdom and be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato"><b>constantly diligent</b></a> in their meditation practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato">Thag 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite fulfilling his duties to his initial teacher, Venerable Somamitta realized that spiritual progress required <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato"><b>association with the wise</b></a> rather than those who are sluggish? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato">Thag 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Somamitta used his own spiritual breakthrough to provide a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato"><b>critique of indolence</b></a> that ultimately saved his former teacher from spiritual decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato">Thag 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the attainment of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> by Venerable Somamitta was the result of developing intensive insight meditation after hearing a formal discourse on the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.14/en/sujato">Thag 2.14</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbamitta was a Brahmin from Sāvatthī who was inspired to join the Order after witnessing the majesty of the Buddha during the dedication of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato"><b>Jetavana Monastery</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato">Thag 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbamitta realized the profound suffering inherent in affection by observing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato"><b>mother deer</b></a> grieving for her fawn that had been caught in a hunter's snare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato">Thag 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbamitta taught that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato"><b>love and attachment</b></a> (pema) bring sorrow to all living beings, as even wild animals are distressed by the loss of those they love? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato">Thag 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while traveling to pay homage to the Buddha, Venerable Sabbamitta encountered a group of robbers who were preparing to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato"><b>burn a captive alive</b></a> using straw? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato">Thag 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbamitta taught that when people are bound to one another by attachment, it often leads to a cycle where they <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato"><b>harm and are harmed</b></a> by each other? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato">Thag 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbamitta reached the state of an Arahant immediately after reflecting on the cruel nature of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato"><b>bandits</b></a> and the suffering caused by worldly bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato">Thag 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the powerful words of Venerable Sabbamitta convinced the robbers to abandon their life of crime and seek ordination in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato"><b>True Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato">Thag 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbamitta warned that one should abandon those who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato"><b>harm many people</b></a>, as such associations provide no lasting benefit for one's children or oneself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato">Thag 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbamitta lived a life of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato"><b>forest seclusion</b></a>, choosing a specific subject for meditation that eventually led to his total liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato">Thag 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbamitta waited until after the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato"><b>rainy season</b></a> to travel from the forest to Sāvatthī to see the Teacher, encountering the lessons of life and death on the way? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato">Thag 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbamitta used the term "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato"><b>bound to people</b></a>" to describe the dangerous and limiting nature of worldly attachments that hinder spiritual freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.15/en/sujato">Thag 2.15</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Passika was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin family</b></a> in Kosala before he was inspired to join the monastic order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato">Thag 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Passika gained faith and sought ordination after witnessing the Buddha perform the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato"><b>Twin Miracle</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato">Thag 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Passika fell ill while training as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato"><b>recluse</b></a>, but was eventually cured through the care of his relatives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato">Thag 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after recovering from his illness, Venerable Passika practiced with even greater diligence and attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato">Thag 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Passika used his psychic powers to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato"><b>fly through the air</b></a> to visit his family and teach them the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato">Thag 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Passika established his relatives in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato"><b>Three Refuges</b></a> and the moral precepts during his visit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato">Thag 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Passika reflected that he was the only one among his kin who possessed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato"><b>faith and wisdom</b></a> before he began teaching them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato">Thag 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that several of the relatives of Venerable Passika were reborn in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato"><b>heavenly realms</b></a> after passing away because they followed his spiritual guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato">Thag 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Passika acted out of compassion for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato"><b>welfare and happiness</b></a> of his family by ensuring they understood the True Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato">Thag 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother and brothers of Venerable Passika rejoiced in their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato"><b>heavenly rebirth</b></a> after having taken refuge in the Buddha's teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato">Thag 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Passika noted his relatives were reborn as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato"><b>deities</b></a> as a direct result of their faith in the monastic community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato">Thag 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Passika was initially the only one among his many kin to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato"><b>True Dhamma</b></a> and possess virtuous conduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.8/en/sujato">Thag 3.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Yasoja was the son of the head of five hundred <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato"><b>fishing families</b></a> in Sāvatthī before becoming a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato">Thag 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while still a fisherman, Venerable Yasoja caught a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato"><b>golden-colored fish</b></a> in the Aciravatī River that was later brought before the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato">Thag 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha revealed to Venerable Yasoja that the golden fish he caught had been an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato"><b>evil monk</b></a> during the time of the Buddha Kassapa? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato">Thag 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Yasoja and his companions were initially <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato"><b>expelled</b></a> from the Jetavana monastery by the Buddha because they were making too much noise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato">Thag 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Yasoja attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> while practicing with intense resolve on the banks of the Vaggumudā River? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato">Thag 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Yasoja became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato"><b>emaciated and unattractive</b></a> because of his total commitment to austere spiritual practices in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato">Thag 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his physical frailty, Venerable Yasoja was praised by the Buddha for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato"><b>moderate eating</b></a> and his mind that was free from anxiety? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato">Thag 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Yasoja compared his ability to endure the bites of flies and mosquitoes in the forest to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato"><b>elephant in battle</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato">Thag 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Yasoja taught that living in complete solitude is like the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato"><b>Brahmā</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato">Thag 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Yasoja remarked that two monks living together experience a state similar to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato"><b>deities</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato">Thag 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Yasoja compared three people living together to the atmosphere of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato"><b>village</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato">Thag 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Yasoja likened more than three people living together to the noise and commerce of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato"><b>market</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato">Thag 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Yasoja achieved a mind that was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato"><b>free from sorrow</b></a> even while his body was weak and covered in veins from his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag3.9/en/sujato">Thag 3.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> was born in Sāvatthī into a brahmin family of the Kosiya clan, but he was known by the name of his mother's family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> decided to renounce the world and ordain after seeing his friend, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>Sāmaññakāni</b></a>, become a prominent elder in the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his spiritual training, <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> was so determined to overcome sleep that he <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>fell down</b></a> while walking on the meditation path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally intervened when he saw <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> struggling with drowsiness and exhorted him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>wake up</b></a> and sit up? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha admonished <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> not to be a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>kinsman of the negligent</b></a> by indulging in excessive sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> was warned by the Teacher that those who are passive and lazy will be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>conquered by Death</b></a> through deception? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> received a teaching comparing birth and old age to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>waves of the great ocean</b></a> that overwhelm and submerge a person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> to make himself a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>safe island</b></a> because no one else can serve as his ultimate refuge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> learned that the path established by the Teacher leads over all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>bonds and fears</b></a> related to birth and aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha urged <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> to maintain his effort without negligence during both the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>first and last watches</b></a> of the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> was encouraged to give up his former attachments and wear the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>outer robe</b></a> of a monk while seeking alms with a shaved head? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> to avoid delighting in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>playful sport</b></a> or addiction to sleep if he wished to succeed in meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> was taught that by being skillful in the path, he could attain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>Nibbāna</b></a> just as water extinguishes a fire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared a monk with weak spiritual light to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>reed in the wind</b></a>, stressing the need for firm and unshakable concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Kātiyāna</b> was instructed to expel the influence of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato"><b>Māra</b></a> and wait for his time while remaining internally "cool" and detached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.7/en/sujato">Thag 6.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Migajāla</b> was the son of the famous and wealthy female lay devotee <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato"><b>Visākhā</b></a> in the city of Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato">Thag 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after frequently visiting the monastery to hear the Buddha's teachings, <b>Venerable Migajāla</b> eventually ordained and attained the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato">Thag 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Migajāla</b> praised the Dhamma as being well-taught by the "One with Vision" who helps practitioners overcome all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato"><b>fetters</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato">Thag 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Migajāla</b> described the Buddha's teaching as a path that leads upward and effectively dries up the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato"><b>roots of evil</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato">Thag 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to <b>Venerable Migajāla</b>, the Dhamma has the power to cut the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato"><b>root of poison</b></a> and bring about total stillness and peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato">Thag 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Migajāla</b> taught that the "grasping of consciousness" is completely shattered by the application of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato"><b>diamond-like knowledge</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato">Thag 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Migajāla</b> realized that existence (bhava) is comparable to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato"><b>pit of burning coals</b></a> when viewed through the lens of wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato">Thag 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Migajāla</b> characterized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> as the great way that blocks old age and death while stilling all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato">Thag 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Migajāla</b> emphasized the importance of knowing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato"><b>kamma as kamma</b></a> and understanding that its fruits arise accordingly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato">Thag 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Migajāla</b> achieved liberation by seeing all phenomena as they really are through the principle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato">Thag 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Venerable Migajāla</b> declared that the path he followed leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato"><b>great security</b></a> and is the ultimate good? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag6.8/en/sujato">Thag 6.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla was born on a night when all the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>weapons and armor</b></a> in the city of Sāvatthī glowed with a mysterious radiance, causing the King of Kosala to lose sleep from fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla was originally born into a prestigious Brahmin family, and his father, a royal counselor, predicted at his birth that the boy would eventually become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>solitary bandit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla was initially named Ahimsaka, meaning "The Harmless One," but he later became a notorious killer after his teacher demanded <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>one thousand human fingers</b></a> as a graduation fee? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla was said to possess the physical <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>strength of seven elephants</b></a>, a power resulting from his past karma that allowed him to outrun and overpower his victims easily? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla received his famous name, which means "Finger-Garland," because he wore a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>necklace of fingers</b></a> taken from the people he had murdered in the Jālinī forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla had made the roads and villages of the Kosala kingdom so dangerous that they became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>empty and deserted</b></a>, prompting the King to send an army to hunt him down? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla was nearly driven to kill his own mother to complete his quota of a thousand fingers, but the Buddha intervened to save him from committing such a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>terrible crime</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla tried to chase the Buddha with a sword, but even though he ran with all his might, he could not catch the Buddha, who was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>walking at a normal pace</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Aṅgulimāla shouted for the Buddha to stop, the Buddha replied that He had already stopped, while Aṅgulimāla was the one who was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>still running</b></a> through his lack of self-restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla recognized the Buddha's words as a "lion's roar" of truth and immediately decided to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>abandon his thousand evils</b></a> to follow the path of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla showed his commitment to the holy life by hurling his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>swords and weapons</b></a> into a deep mountain abyss before asking the Buddha for ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla was ordained as a monk when the Buddha simply said to him, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>“Come, bhikkhu,”</b></a> instantly granting him the status of a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla taught that one who was previously negligent but later becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>heedful</b></a> illuminates the world like the moon emerging from behind the clouds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla declared that a person who covers up their past evil deeds with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>wholesome actions</b></a> illuminates the world just as the moon does when freed from clouds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even after becoming an Arahant, Venerable Aṅgulimāla suffered physical abuse on alms rounds, returning with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>broken bowl</b></a> and a bleeding head after being struck with stones and sticks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged Venerable Aṅgulimāla to endure his physical suffering, explaining that he was experiencing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>fruition of his karma</b></a> in the present life instead of suffering for ages in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla expressed a compassionate wish that even his enemies might hear the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>true Dhamma</b></a> and devote themselves to the Buddha's teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla hoped his enemies would associate with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>peaceful people</b></a> who live according to the Dhamma and maintain tranquility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla wished for his enemies to hear teachings on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>patience and gentleness</b></a>, hoping they would act in accordance with those peaceful virtues? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla stated that a person who follows the Dhamma would not harm him or anyone else, thereby achieving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>ultimate peace</b></a> for both the strong and the weak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla compared the training of the mind to how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>irrigators lead water</b></a>, fletchers shape arrows, and carpenters bend wood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla marveled at how the Buddha tamed him <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>without using a stick or a sword</b></a>, unlike worldly trainers who use violence to subdue others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla reflected on his name transformation, noting that although he was once "The Harmer," he truly became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>The Harmless One</b></a> (Ahimsaka) after he stopped harming all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla described his previous life as a bandit as being like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>sinking in a great flood</b></a> until he took refuge in the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla declared that by taking refuge in the Buddha, he had successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>uprooted the root of rebirth</b></a>, ensuring he would never be born again? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla stated that after paying off his karmic debts through his final experiences of suffering, he was now able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>eat his almsfood</b></a> without being in debt to anyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla warned that while foolish people indulge in negligence, the wise person protects <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>heedfulness</b></a> as if it were their most precious treasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla urged others not to indulge in negligence or sensual pleasures, but to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>meditation</b></a> to attain the supreme happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla felt that his decision to follow the Buddha’s advice was the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>best choice</b></a> he ever made among all the various teachings available in the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>Three Knowledges</b></a> (Te-vijja), which include the recollection of past lives, the divine eye, and the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla found deep joy and excitement in his spiritual practice while dwelling in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>forests, under trees</b></a>, and in mountain caves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla described his new life as a monk as one of pure happiness, where he could <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>lie down and wake up in peace</b></a>, free from the snares of Māra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla credited his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>compassionate Teacher</b></a>, the Buddha, for his total liberation from a life of violent crime and spiritual darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla considered himself a true "Son of the Buddha" (the Well-Farer), despite having been born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>high Brahmin lineage</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> by living with restrained sense faculties and abandoning all forms of attachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla stated that he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>laid down the burden</b></a> of existence and had fulfilled every instruction given by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla emphasized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>root of becoming</b></a> had been completely pulled out from his heart, ending his cycle of birth and death forever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla's transformation is often cited as the ultimate example of how even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>vicious murderer</b></a> can achieve full enlightenment through the Buddha's guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla viewed his spiritual attainment as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>supreme achievement</b></a>, surpassing all worldly gains or status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Aṅgulimāla taught that the Buddha is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato"><b>unmatched King of Dhamma</b></a>, whose mercy and wisdom can save even those who are deeply lost in evil? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag16.8/en/sujato">Thag 16.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kuṭivihārin declared himself to be a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato"><b>free from lust</b></a> and possesses a well-concentrated mind while seeking shelter in a field-watcher's hut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato">Thag 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the owner of the hut asked who was inside, Venerable Kuṭivihārin answered that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato"><b>monk</b></a> was dwelling there and then spoke a verse of truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato">Thag 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kuṭivihārin attained the fruit of Arahantship while seeking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato"><b>shelter from the rain</b></a> in a small, empty shelter located in a field? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato">Thag 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kuṭivihārin told the layman who built the shelter that his labor was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato"><b>not in vain</b></a> because it provided a place for a liberated practitioner to dwell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato">Thag 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after Venerable Kuṭivihārin addressed the field keeper, the Buddha used his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato"><b>divine ear</b></a> to listen to the conversation and the layman's joyful reaction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato">Thag 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha spoke a verse to the field keeper who helped Venerable Kuṭivihārin, prophesying that the layman would become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato"><b>ruler of the heavens</b></a> many times over? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato">Thag 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted the layman who supported Venerable Kuṭivihārin would eventually conquer all craving and attain the status of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato"><b>Paccekabuddha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato">Thag 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Kuṭivihārin was given to him because he attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato"><b>final liberation</b></a> inside a simple hut (kuṭi)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato">Thag 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that another monk also known as Venerable Kuṭivihārin initially intended to build a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato"><b>new hut</b></a> because his old one was falling apart and damaged? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato">Thag 1.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Venerable Kuṭivihārin was focused on constructing a new dwelling, a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato"><b>deity</b></a> appeared to urge him toward spiritual liberation instead? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato">Thag 1.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a spirit seeking his liberation warned Venerable Kuṭivihārin that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato"><b>new hut brings new suffering</b></a> and advised him to abandon such worldly desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato">Thag 1.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that hearing the deity's verse caused Venerable Kuṭivihārin to experience deep <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato"><b>anxiety</b></a> and spiritual urgency regarding his meditative practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato">Thag 1.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after being admonished by a deity, Venerable Kuṭivihārin practiced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> with such diligence that he soon attained Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato">Thag 1.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kuṭivihārin repeated the deity's warning as his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato"><b>declaration of knowledge</b></a> after he successfully reached the end of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato">Thag 1.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha described the layman who aided Venerable Kuṭivihārin as someone who would eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato"><b>conquer all craving</b></a> through his meritorious actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato">Thag 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kuṭivihārin was inspired by the lifestyle of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Añjanavaniya</b></a>, although his specific breakthrough occurred while taking refuge from a storm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato">Thag 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the field keeper who met Venerable Kuṭivihārin rejoiced at his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato"><b>good fortune</b></a> in having an enlightened monk choose to sit and meditate in his humble hut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato">Thag 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a deity cautioned Venerable Kuṭivihārin to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato"><b>abandon</b></a> the desire for a new physical structure to avoid the attachment and pain it would bring? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.57/en/sujato">Thag 1.57</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kuṭivihārin described himself as a monk with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato"><b>tranquil mind</b></a> while explaining his presence to the owner of the field hut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato">Thag 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verse of Venerable Kuṭivihārin in the first story emphasizes that his mind is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato"><b>well-concentrated</b></a> despite the temporary and simple nature of his dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.56/en/sujato">Thag 1.56</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākāla was a merchant from the city of Setavyā who traveled to Sāvatthī with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato"><b>five hundred carts</b></a> of goods before deciding to enter the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato">Thag 2.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākāla's decision to seek ordination was sparked after he saw laypeople carrying incense and flowers to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato"><b>Jetavana monastery</b></a> to hear the Buddha preach? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato">Thag 2.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon his ordination, Venerable Mahākāla made the unique choice to live in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato"><b>charnel ground</b></a> to focus his meditation on the theme of death and impermanence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato">Thag 2.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while living in the cemetery, Venerable Mahākāla encountered a woman named Kālī who processed corpses and provided him with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato"><b>subject for meditation</b></a> by dismembering a fresh body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato">Thag 2.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākāla observed the woman Kālī breaking the limbs and head of a corpse, an image he used to reflect deeply on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato"><b>nature of the body</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato">Thag 2.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākāla compared a shattered human skull to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato"><b>bowl for curds</b></a> as a way to contemplate the stark reality of physical decay and mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato">Thag 2.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākāla warned that an ignorant person who continues to create <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato"><b>acquisitions</b></a> (upadhi) will repeatedly experience suffering in the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato">Thag 2.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākāla achieved liberation by realizing that one who understands the truth does not create the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato"><b>conditions for rebirth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato">Thag 2.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahākāla described the foolish person as one who comes to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato"><b>continual suffering</b></a> because they fail to see the truth of existence and the nature of the aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato">Thag 2.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his cemetery practice, Venerable Mahākāla resolved that he would never again be subject to the suffering of having his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato"><b>head broken</b></a> in a future birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.16/en/sujato">Thag 2.16</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa was born in Rājagaha into a Brahmin family and became a renowned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato"><b>expert in the three Vedas</b></a> before he met the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato">Thag 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, Venerable Tissa was a famous teacher who instructed over <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato"><b>five hundred young Brahmins</b></a> in the recitation of mantras and charms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato">Thag 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa’s faith in the Buddha was inspired by the Teacher's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato"><b>majestic appearance</b></a> and virtuous conduct upon his arrival in the city of Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato">Thag 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after attaining Arahantship through insight meditation, Venerable Tissa found himself being envied by other monks who were primarily focused on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato"><b>worldly gain</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato">Thag 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa spoke about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato"><b>danger of fame</b></a> and honor, noting how they can lead to hostility even among those who have renounced the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato">Thag 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa observed that a monk who receives abundant food, clothing, and lodging often becomes an object of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato"><b>enmity</b></a> for those who lack spiritual depth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato">Thag 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa advised his fellow monastics to recognize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato"><b>great fear</b></a> inherent in being honored and worshipped by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato">Thag 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tissa taught that a monk should remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato"><b>mindful and detached</b></a> even when they are the recipient of many offerings and public respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato">Thag 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Tissa, the ideal monastic practice involves accepting little and staying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato"><b>without greed</b></a> for the requisites provided by laypeople? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.17/en/sujato">Thag 2.17</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kimbila was a Sakyan prince who lived in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.18/en/sujato"><b>Eastern Bamboo Park</b></a> alongside his close friends and fellow Thanas, Anuruddha and Bhaddiya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.18/en/sujato">Thag 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kimbila renounced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.18/en/sujato"><b>great wealth</b></a> and his high royal status to pursue a life of spiritual discipline under the guidance of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.18/en/sujato">Thag 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kimbila expressed that he and his fellow Sakyan monks found great joy in being content with whatever they received in their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.18/en/sujato"><b>alms bowls</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.18/en/sujato">Thag 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kimbila was known for being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.18/en/sujato"><b>energetic and resolute</b></a>, always persisting in his spiritual efforts with great enthusiasm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.18/en/sujato">Thag 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kimbila taught that a true practitioner completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.18/en/sujato"><b>abandons worldly pleasure</b></a> in favor of the superior delight found in the flavor of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.18/en/sujato">Thag 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kimbila emphasized the importance of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.18/en/sujato"><b>constantly ardent</b></a> in one's practice to achieve the ultimate goal of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.18/en/sujato">Thag 2.18</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbaka, who was born into a Brahmin family in Sāvatthī, achieved spiritual liberation while meditating at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>Lonagiri monastery</b></a> on the banks of the Ajakaraṇī River? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after attaining Arahantship, Venerable Sabbaka returned to his hometown to establish his relatives in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>Three Refuges</b></a> and the practice of moral precepts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the pleas of his relatives to stay for material offerings, Venerable Sabbaka expressed a deep preference for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>solitary life</b></a> and returned to his secluded dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbaka found great spiritual joy in observing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>white cranes</b></a> spreading their wings to find shelter as they fled from dark storm clouds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbaka described how white cranes would seek out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>hidden caves</b></a> for protection near the Ajakaraṇī River when frightened by impending rain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbaka was delighted by the sight of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>Jambu trees</b></a> that lined both banks of his favorite river and made the landscape shine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbaka resided near a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>large cave</b></a> located just behind the rows of trees that beautified the riverbank? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbaka noted the joyful cries of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>frogs</b></a> that had successfully escaped from predatory snakes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbaka warned that it is never the time to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>negligent</b></a> while living among mountain streams and forest environments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbaka praised the Ajakaraṇī River as a place that was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>safe and pleasant</b></a> for his meditative practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verses of Venerable Sabbaka regarding the beauty of the river and the cranes served as his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>declaration of final knowledge</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbaka was initially moved to go forth into the homeless life after feeling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>deep faith</b></a> upon hearing the Buddha preach in Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbaka compared his spiritual environment to a place where one can experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>constant delight</b></a> and inner peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabbaka used the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato"><b>natural scenery</b></a> of the Ajakaraṇī River to illustrate the happiness he found in seclusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.11/en/sujato">Thag 4.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mudita was born into a commoner family and only fled to the forest because of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>extreme fear</b></a> of a hostile king? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mudita first met a senior monk who was an Arahant and was told that the danger from the king would pass within <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>seven or eight months</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mudita initially sought ordination as a way to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>save his own life</b></a> from royal persecution? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after receiving higher ordination, Venerable Mudita developed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>steadfast energy</b></a> and moved beyond his initial motivations of fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mudita attained a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>total fearlessness</b></a> through the practice of insight meditation after joining the order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mudita made a solemn resolution that he would not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>leave the monastery</b></a> until he had reached the fruit of Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mudita declared he would persist in his meditation even if his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>flesh wasted away</b></a> and his body was destroyed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mudita was willing to continue his spiritual exertion even if his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>leg joints</b></a> failed him and he collapsed from exhaustion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as part of his intense effort, Venerable Mudita vowed that he would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>neither eat nor drink</b></a> until he attained total liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mudita resolved that he would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>not lie down</b></a> or rest on his side until he was completely free from all defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mudita compared sensual craving to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>poisonous arrow</b></a> that must be completely extracted to find spiritual peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his unwavering determination, Venerable Mudita successfully attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>Three Knowledges</b></a> and finished the Buddha's work? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mudita shared his verses in response to other monks who asked how he had achieved his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>spiritual goal</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mudita transitioned from a life of terror to living in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato"><b>bliss of liberation</b></a> through the diligent following of the Buddha's teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.12/en/sujato">Thag 4.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sundara-Samudda was born into the family of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>wealthy council member</b></a> in the city of Rājagaha before he entered the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sundara-Samudda received his name, which means "Beautiful Samudda," because he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>exceptionally handsome</b></a> in his youth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sundara-Samudda chose to renounce the world and ordain after being deeply moved by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>majesty of the Buddha</b></a> during a festival in Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that to focus on his spiritual development, Venerable Sundara-Samudda moved from his hometown of Rājagaha to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>Sāvatthī</b></a> to live with a friend and practice meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Sundara-Samudda was so overwhelmed with grief at his absence that she <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>hired a courtesan</b></a> to lure him back to the lay life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the woman hired to tempt Venerable Sundara-Samudda was promised <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>control of the household</b></a> and many gifts if she succeeded in bringing him home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that to seduce him, the temptress stood at the door of the house where Venerable Sundara-Samudda went for alms, wearing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>golden sandals</b></a> and fine ornaments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the temptress tried to entice Venerable Sundara-Samudda by offering him the enjoyment of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>five cords of sensual pleasure</b></a> under her guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the woman suggested to Venerable Sundara-Samudda that they should only become ascetics when they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>both old</b></a> and supported by walking sticks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the courtesan spoke to Venerable Sundara-Samudda with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>gentle voice</b></a> and placed her hands together in a respectful gesture to appear more appealing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when confronted by the temptress, Venerable Sundara-Samudda briefly experienced a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>worldly thought</b></a> before quickly regaining his spiritual composure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sundara-Samudda compared the beautifully adorned woman standing before him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>Death's bait</b></a> laid out to trap the unwary? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sundara-Samudda successfully used <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>radical reflection</b></a> (yoniso manasikāra) to see the inherent danger in the temptation he faced? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while standing right in front of the woman trying to seduce him, Venerable Sundara-Samudda attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> (chalabhiññā) of an enlightened being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sundara-Samudda achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>three true knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā) and declared that he had completed the Buddha's instructions during this encounter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite the temptress having her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>feet painted red</b></a> and being dressed in fine clothes, Venerable Sundara-Samudda remained established in revulsion toward the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sundara-Samudda described his spiritual victory as his mind being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a> after seeing the Dhamma as it truly is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Sundara-Samudda was motivated to try and bring him back because she saw the wives of other noblemen <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>decked in jewelry</b></a> during festival days? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sundara-Samudda remained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>undisturbed</b></a> and unmoving in his meditation even as the courtesan offered herself to him as his wife? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verses of Venerable Sundara-Samudda serve as a powerful testament to how <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato"><b>intensive insight meditation</b></a> can overcome even the most direct and personal temptations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.1/en/sujato">Thag 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa, who was the son of a provincial ruler, attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> after hearing a senior monk preach and deciding to renounce his royal life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the ascetic Paṇḍaracotta questioned Venerable Phussa about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>future conduct of the Sangha</b></a> after observing the disciplined and restrained behavior of the monks of that time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa predicted a future where monks would be full of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>anger and enmity</b></a>, abandoning the gentle nature required of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa warned that future practitioners would be characterized by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>hypocrisy and stubbornness</b></a>, often engaging in deceit and envy toward one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa foretold that future monks would consider themselves wise and proficient in deep Dhamma while actually being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>superficial and disrespectful</b></a> toward the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa stated that the well-expounded Dhamma would eventually be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>defiled by fools</b></a> who lack the proper training and respect for the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Phussa, future monks with poor virtue would be the ones to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>speak with confidence</b></a> in the assembly, while the truly virtuous would remain silent and timid? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa predicted a time when unlearned but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>talkative monks</b></a> would gain power and influence within the monastic community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa warned that future monks would greedily accumulate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>gold, silver, and land</b></a>, behaving more like householders than renunciants? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa prophesied that some future monks would even go as far as keeping <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>male and female slaves</b></a>, as well as livestock like goats and sheep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa compared the behavior of future undisciplined monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>wild animals</b></a> that are constantly looking for a fight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa noted that future monks would become restless and wear <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>flamboyant clothing</b></a>, showing more concern for their appearance than for their meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa described future monks as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>stubborn and deceitful talkers</b></a> who merely imitate the outward appearance of the noble ones of the past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa observed that future monks would use <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>cosmetics and eye makeup</b></a>, behaving in ways that are inappropriate for those who have renounced the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa predicted that future monks would lose their appreciation for the saffron robe and instead show a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>preference for white robes</b></a>, which are the garments of laypeople? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa described the yellow robe as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>banner of the Arahants</b></a>, which should be worn with great reverence and joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa stated that future monks would become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>greedy for gain</b></a> and lazy, losing the energy required for diligent spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa predicted that future monks would find it too difficult to live in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>remote forests</b></a> and would prefer the comfort and distractions of living near villages? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa warned that monks would eventually associate with and imitate those who follow <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>wrong livelihoods</b></a> for the sake of material benefit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa observed that in the future, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>wise and respectable monks</b></a> would be ignored or disrespected by those who lack virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa referred to an ancient story where a great elephant with six tusks showed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>respect for the saffron robe</b></a> even when the person wearing it was a hunter trying to kill him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa taught that a person who is still full of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>mental stains</b></a> and lacks self-restraint is fundamentally unworthy of wearing the monastic robe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Phussa, only those who have <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>abandoned all stains</b></a> and are well-established in virtue truly deserve to wear the saffron robe? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa emphasized that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>immoral and unlearned</b></a> has no business wearing the robe of a monk and should instead wear the white clothes of a layperson? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa warned that future monks and nuns would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>malicious</b></a> and would often oppress those who possess a heart of loving-kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa stated that future fools would refuse to listen to their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>spiritual teachers</b></a>, even when they are given proper guidance and instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa compared future unruly monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>wild horses</b></a> that refuse to be tamed by even the most skilled charioteer? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa urged current monks and nuns to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>friendly and respectful</b></a> toward each other as long as the era of the Dhamma remains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa advised his fellow practitioners to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>well-restrained in virtue</b></a> and to arouse their energy before the great dangers of the future arrive? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa encouraged monks to remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>steadfast and persistent</b></a> in their efforts to reach liberation while the opportunity is still available? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa warned that practitioners should <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>see the danger in negligence</b></a>, as heedfulness is the only path to true safety and peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa exhorted everyone to develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>Eightfold Noble Path</b></a> in order to realize the state of the deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa described a future where monks would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>unrestrained and talkative</b></a>, showing no real interest in the silence and seclusion of meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa predicted that future monks would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>harbor ill-will</b></a> and lack faith, creating a hostile environment for those who are truly sincere? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa taught that being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>mild and compassionate</b></a> toward one another is essential for the survival of the monastic community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa used the term <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>"banner of the Arahants"</b></a> to emphasize the high spiritual standard that the saffron robe represents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa warned that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>corruption of the Sangha</b></a> would be a gradual process led by those who value material gain over spiritual purity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa encouraged monks to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>practice non-negligence</b></a> as the primary way to guard the Dhamma from future decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa's verses were recorded by the collectors of the scriptures as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>solemn warning</b></a> about the inevitable challenges facing the Buddhist dispensation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Phussa concluded his teaching by urging practitioners to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato"><b>attain the deathless state</b></a> before the era of spiritual darkness fully descends? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.1/en/sujato">Thag 17.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ramaṇīyakuṭika resided in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato"><b>beautifully crafted hut</b></a> in the Vajji country that featured polished walls, fine sand, and serene ponds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato">Thag 1.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when several women tried to seduce Venerable Ramaṇīyakuṭika by boasting of their youth and beautiful clothes, he declared he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato"><b>no need</b></a> for such worldly attractions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato">Thag 1.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ramaṇīyakuṭika achieved the state of an Arahant while living in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato"><b>delightful leaf hut</b></a> that was significantly enhanced by his own pure virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato">Thag 1.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ramaṇīyakuṭika described his hut as being offered by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato"><b>faithful donors</b></a> with joyful hearts, highlighting the merit of those who support the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato">Thag 1.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ramaṇīyakuṭika explicitly told those who sought to tempt him to take their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato"><b>sensual charms</b></a> to others who still desired them, confirming his complete mental liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato">Thag 1.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the surroundings of Venerable Ramaṇīyakuṭika's hut, including the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato"><b>fine sand</b></a> and garden, were noted by visitors as contributing to the peaceful atmosphere of his dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato">Thag 1.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ramaṇīyakuṭika’s declaration of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato"><b>final knowledge</b></a> was made in response to a direct challenge to his monastic celibacy and spiritual focus? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato">Thag 1.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Ramaṇīyakuṭika literally refers to one who dwells in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato"><b>delightful hut</b></a>, a title he earned through his residence in a small Vajji village? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.58/en/sujato">Thag 1.58</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kosalavihārin was initially a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato"><b>novice monk</b></a> when he began his solitary life in the forests of the Kosala kingdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato">Thag 1.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a compassionate lay person constructed a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato"><b>leaf hut</b></a> specifically for Venerable Kosalavihārin after seeing him meditating under the open canopy of a tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato">Thag 1.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kosalavihārin described his life in the wilderness as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato"><b>without a home</b></a>, having gone forth from the household life out of pure faith in the Buddha's teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato">Thag 1.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the diligent practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and clear comprehension</b></a>, Venerable Kosalavihārin reached the goal of Arahantship within his forest retreat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato">Thag 1.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name "Kosalavihārin" was bestowed upon the Venerable because of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato"><b>extended residence</b></a> in a village forest located within the country of Kosala? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato">Thag 1.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kosalavihārin’s verse emphasizes that a monk should live with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato"><b>ardent energy</b></a> and avoid any form of mental negligence or distraction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato">Thag 1.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kosalavihārin attained the peak of the spiritual life by utilizing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato"><b>simple shelter</b></a> offered by a lay follower who was moved by his dedication? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato">Thag 1.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kosalavihārin’s declaration of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato"><b>final knowledge</b></a> highlights the joy and satisfaction found in a life dedicated to solitary meditation in the woods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.59/en/sujato">Thag 1.59</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sītavaniya (also known as Sambhūta) was the son of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato"><b>renowned Brahmin</b></a> in the city of Rājagaha before he heard the Buddha's Dhamma and ordained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato">Thag 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sītavaniya entered the monkhood alongside three close friends—<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato"><b>Bhūmija, Jeyyasena, and Abhiratana</b></a>—after they all listened to the Buddha's preaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato">Thag 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while dwelling in the Sītavana forest, Venerable Sītavaniya focused his meditative efforts on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato"><b>impurity of the body</b></a> to achieve liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato">Thag 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sītavaniya requested fellow monks traveling to see the Buddha to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato"><b>pay homage</b></a> on his behalf, indicating his commitment to his forest retreat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato">Thag 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sītavaniya described himself as a monk who stays alone and is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato"><b>content</b></a> with whatever simple requisites are available to him in the Cool Grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato">Thag 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sītavaniya achieved a state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato"><b>victorious and fearless</b></a> by conquering all internal mental defilements during his solitary stay in Sītavana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato">Thag 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the primary spiritual duty emphasized by Venerable Sītavaniya was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato"><b>guarding mindfulness of the body</b></a>, which he practiced with great persistence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato">Thag 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sītavaniya reached a level of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato"><b>mental concentration</b></a> where he remained completely undisturbed by the potential terrors of living in a remote forest grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato">Thag 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the title "Sītavaniya" means "one who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato"><b>dwells in Sītavana</b></a>," reflecting the Venerable's dedication to his chosen place of meditation in the Cool Grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.6/en/sujato">Thag 1.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nanda was born in Kapilavatthu as the son of King Suddhodana and Mahāpajāpati Gotamī, and was named because he brought <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>joy</b></a> to all his relatives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha first visited Kapilavatthu, He inspired Venerable Nanda’s father to attain the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>stream-entry</b></a> (sotāpatti) by reciting the verse starting with "Rise up!"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Nanda, Mahāpajāpati, also attained the fruit of stream-entry after the Buddha exhorted her to live according to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>Holy Life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha ordained Venerable Nanda on the very day of his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>wedding celebration</b></a> by handing him the alms bowl and leading him back to the monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his royal status, Venerable Nanda was initially <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>reluctant</b></a> to become a monk and was only ordained through the Buddha's persistent guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after much struggle and dedicated meditation, Venerable Nanda attained the state of an Arahant and declared that he had escaped the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>mire of rebirth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nanda described his ultimate spiritual success as standing safely upon the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>sands of Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his enlightenment, Venerable Nanda admitted he was obsessed with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>outer appearance</b></a> and was frequently agitated by sensual desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nanda attributed his spiritual transformation to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>skillful means</b></a> of the Buddha, whom he referred to as the "Kinsman of the Sun"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nanda realized he had previously lacked <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>careful attention</b></a> (ayoniso manasikāra), which had caused him to be distracted by vanity and lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha publicly declared Venerable Nanda to be the foremost among all His disciples in the quality of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>self-control</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nanda’s father, King Suddhodana, eventually progressed to the stage of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>Once-returner</b></a> (sakadāgāmī) through the Buddha’s continued teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nanda expressed his deep gratitude for the Buddha's help in allowing him to finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>end existence</b></a> and the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the Buddha’s visit to his hometown, He recounted the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato"><b>Vessantara Jātaka</b></a> to his family while it was raining? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.19/en/sujato">Thag 2.19</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja was the son of the royal chaplain to King Udena in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>Kosambī</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before joining the Sangha, Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja was a highly successful teacher who had mastered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>three Vedas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja became disillusioned with his life as a Brahmin teacher and went to Rājagaha, where he was inspired by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>respect and offerings</b></a> given to the Buddhist monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that by practicing the Buddha’s path and exercising <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>moderation in eating</b></a>, Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja attained the six super-knowledges (abhiññā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja was so confident in his realization that he offered to answer the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>doubts</b></a> of any monk regarding the path or its results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha praised Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja as the foremost among His disciples who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>roar the lion's roar</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when an old friend accused him of selfishness, Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja clarified that a monk's search for food is governed by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>Law</b></a> and not by personal greed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja taught that while food is necessary for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>existence of the body</b></a>, it should never be something the heart clings to? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja warned that receiving honor and offerings from families is actually a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>slough of mud</b></a> that can trap a practitioner? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja compared the public respect and devotion shown to a monk to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>subtle arrow</b></a> that is extremely difficult to remove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his verses, Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja emphasized that even though the body is sustained by food, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>wise</b></a> do not let it affect their internal mental state? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja observed that foolish people find it nearly impossible to give up their attachment to being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>honored</b></a> by others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja achieved his spiritual breakthrough by following the specific methods taught by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>Teacher</b></a> to control the senses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Piṇḍola Bhāradvāja reflects both his clan name and his habit of seeking <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato"><b>almsfood</b></a> (piṇḍa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.2/en/sujato">Thag 2.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhagu was born into the Sakyan royal family and entered the homeless life along with his kinsmen Anuruddha and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato"><b>Kimbila</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato">Thag 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while striving for enlightenment, Venerable Bhagu lived in seclusion near the village of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato"><b>Bàlakaloṇa</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato">Thag 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhagu once left his dwelling to overcome a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato"><b>drowsiness</b></a> and accidentally tripped and fell while stepping onto the walking meditation path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato">Thag 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after falling on the walking path, Venerable Bhagu composed himself, began to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato"><b>massage his limbs</b></a>, and immediately returned to his meditative practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato">Thag 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his persistence on the walking path, Venerable Bhagu achieved a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato"><b>inner concentration</b></a> and mental stillness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato">Thag 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhagu developed insight by applying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato"><b>wise attention</b></a>, which allowed him to see the dangers of existence and establish himself in detachment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato">Thag 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhagu attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato"><b>Three Knowledges</b></a> and successfully completed the Teacher’s instruction by realizing the true nature of reality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato">Thag 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the Buddha visited him in his dwelling, he praised the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato"><b>solitude</b></a> of Venerable Bhagu and questioned him about his continued diligence in the spiritual life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato">Thag 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhagu declared his final attainment by stating that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato"><b>mind was liberated</b></a> through the direct seeing of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.2/en/sujato">Thag 4.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabhiya was born into a royal clan and was raised by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato"><b>wanderer</b></a> from a young age to learn various doctrines and the ascetic way of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato">Thag 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, Venerable Sabhiya became a sharp and unmatched <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato"><b>debater</b></a> who taught the children of royalty near the city gates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato">Thag 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabhiya devised a set of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato"><b>twenty questions</b></a> to test the wisdom of recluses and Brahmins, an idea initially suggested to him by a deity from the Pure Abodes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato">Thag 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabhiya sought out the Buddha at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato"><b>Bamboo Grove</b></a> in Rājagaha, where the Teacher successfully answered all of his difficult questions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato">Thag 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after attaining Arahantship, Venerable Sabhiya used his verses to admonish monks who had mistakenly followed the schismatic <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato"><b>Devadatta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato">Thag 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabhiya taught that while the foolish do not realize we are all passing away, those who are wise understand this and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato"><b>settle their quarrels</b></a> accordingly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato">Thag 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabhiya observed that those who do not understand the Dhamma live as if they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato"><b>immortal</b></a>, whereas those who know the Dhamma remain untroubled even in illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato">Thag 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabhiya warned that a person who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato"><b>negligent</b></a> and whose conduct is corrupt will fail to achieve the great fruits of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato">Thag 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabhiya stated that a monk with suspicious conduct who lives among fellow practitioners will not receive <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato"><b>honor or respect</b></a> from the community? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato">Thag 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabhiya compared the distance between a person of bad conduct and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato"><b>True Dhamma</b></a> to the vast distance between the sky and the earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato">Thag 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabhiya entered the monastic order after his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> was deeply inspired by the Buddha's ability to resolve his spiritual doubts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato">Thag 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sabhiya taught that those who truly know the Dhamma are like those who remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato"><b>healthy among the sick</b></a> because they have attained mental tranquility? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.3/en/sujato">Thag 4.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhadda was born in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>Sāvatthī</b></a> to the family of a prominent city council member? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the parents of Venerable Bhadda were childless for a long time and only conceived him after making numerous <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>appeals to deities</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before he was even born, the parents of Venerable Bhadda made a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>vow to the Buddha</b></a> that they would offer their son to be his attendant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Bhadda was first brought to the Buddha to be offered to the Sangha, his parents dressed him in his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>finest clothes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ānanda</b></a> to provide the initial monastic training and ordination for the young Bhadda? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while he was still a young boy learning his duties, Venerable Bhadda attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> (chalabhiññā) of an enlightened being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhadda achieved complete spiritual liberation and the destruction of the taints just as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>sun was rising</b></a> on the horizon? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha granted Venerable Bhadda full ordination (upasampadā) simply by calling out the words, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>Come, Bhadda</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhadda was only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>seven years old</b></a> when he received his full ordination into the Buddhist Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhadda described his early childhood as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>delicately nurtured</b></a> and pampered by his parents before joining the monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhadda was an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>only son</b></a> who was deeply loved by both his mother and father? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhadda expressed that his parents led him to the Buddha out of a desire for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>welfare and happiness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the parents of Venerable Bhadda formally presented him to the Buddha to serve as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>attendant</b></a> (upaṭṭhāka) to the "Conqueror"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha predicted that the young Venerable Bhadda would become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>thoroughly refined</b></a> and virtuous in his character? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhadda attained his spiritual breakthrough while the Buddha was residing in his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>perfumed chamber</b></a> (gandhakuṭī)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhadda marveled at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>excellence of the Dhamma</b></a>, which allowed him to reach such high attainments so quickly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhadda reached the state of an Arahant having mastered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>three true knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā) by age seven? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his parents' long struggle to have him, they were willing to give Venerable Bhadda to the Buddha for the sake of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the story of Venerable Bhadda illustrates how a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>well-matured mind</b></a> can reach enlightenment even with very little formal training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the "Come, Bhadda" ordination of Venerable Bhadda is considered a rare and special form of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato"><b>monastic admission</b></a> presided over by the Buddha himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.3/en/sujato">Thag 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta, originally named Upatissa, and his lifelong friend Venerable Moggallāna were motivated to seek the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>Deathless</b></a> after realizing at a festival in Rājagaha that every person in the massive crowd would be dead within a hundred years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before meeting the Buddha, Venerable Sāriputta and Venerable Moggallāna studied under the teacher Sanjaya and made a pact that whoever attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>immortal state</b></a> first would immediately inform the other? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta attained Stream-entry after hearing a brief summary of the Dhamma from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Assaji</b></a>, one of the Buddha's first five disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta reached full Arahantship just fifteen days after his ordination while staying with the Buddha at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>Sūkarakhata cave</b></a> in Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha officially designated Venerable Sāriputta as the foremost among his disciples in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>wisdom and insight</b></a>, often referring to him as the Marshal of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a true monk is defined by having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>virtue and mindfulness</b></a>, acting according to right thought, and being content in solitude? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta emphasized <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>moderation in eating</b></a>, suggesting that a practitioner should stop eating when they are four or five mouthfuls away from being full and finish their meal with water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta lived with great <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>contentment</b></a>, stating that a diligent monk should be satisfied with legally obtained robes and food that merely meet his basic needs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta demonstrated incredible <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>endurance</b></a> by noting that even if his knees were soaked by rain while meditating in the cross-legged position, he remained comfortable and focused? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that one who sees <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>pleasure as suffering</b></a> and suffering as a piercing arrow can stand with equanimity between the two and escape the world's grasp? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta warned that those who possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>evil desires</b></a>, laziness, and little learning are easily caught by the world and cannot find liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta praised the quality of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>highly learned</b></a> and well-composed in virtue as the way to rise above the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta warned that those fond of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>mental proliferation</b></a>, like beasts following their instincts, fall away from Nibbāna and the supreme escape from suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>abandoning proliferation</b></a> and delighting in the path free from conceptual obsession, one reaches Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta declared that wherever <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>Arahants</b></a> dwell—whether in a village, a forest, a valley, or on a hill—that land is truly delightful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta found <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>forests and mountains</b></a> delightful because they are places where those free from lust find joy, unlike ordinary people who seek only sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta advised practitioners to seek out a teacher who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>points out faults</b></a> and offers reproof, comparing such a wise person to someone who reveals hidden treasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta believed it was a duty to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>admonish and instruct</b></a> others to restrain them from unwholesome actions, noting that such a person is loved by the good and disliked by the bad? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta described his own practice of listening to the Buddha's Dhamma with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>eager ears</b></a>, which led him to attain the destruction of the taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta stated that his liberation was achieved through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>direct knowledge</b></a> of the Dhamma, without necessarily needing to rely on the recollection of past lives or the divine eye? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta once sat in deep meditation under a tree and was struck on the head by a Yakkha, yet he remained unharmed due to his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>refined concentration</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta practiced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>Noble Silence</b></a>, a state of deep meditative stillness that he achieved as a dedicated disciple of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta compared a monk who has destroyed all delusion to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>mountain of stone</b></a>, which stands firm and unshakeable regardless of external circumstances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a person seeking purity should view even an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>evil deed as light as a hair</b></a> as being as heavy as a cloud in the sky? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta maintained perfect <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>equanimity toward death</b></a>, stating that he did not delight in dying nor in living, but awaited his time with mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta likened his approach to the end of his life to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>workman finishing his task</b></a>, simply setting down the body when the work of the holy life was complete? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta urged practitioners not to waste even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>single moment</b></a>, as those who let the opportunity for practice pass by will suffer in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta compared the need to guard one's own mind to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>frontier city</b></a> that must be protected both inside and out from any approaching danger? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta praised Venerable Mahākoṭṭhita for his ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>sweep away evil states</b></a> as easily as the wind sweeps fallen leaves from a tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta taught that a person who is calm, peaceful, and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>perfect in virtue</b></a> is the one who can successfully bring all suffering to an end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta observed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>changeable nature of people</b></a>, noting that some who were once good could become bad, while others could transform from bad to good? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta identified <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>lust, lethargy, and doubt</b></a> as part of the five hindrances that defile the mind and prevent spiritual progress? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta emphasized the importance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>unwavering meditation</b></a>, remaining steady whether one is honored by others or not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta defined a "True Person" (Sappurisa) as someone who is persistent in meditation, has refined vision, and delights in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>cessation of grasping</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta declared the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>liberation of the Buddha</b></a> to be so supreme that it cannot be compared to the greatness of the ocean, the earth, or the mountains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta possessed such deep <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a> that he was described as being like the elements of earth, water, and fire—having neither attraction nor repulsion toward anything? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta noted that the Great Sage (the Buddha) might appear "slow" to some, but he actually lives in a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>profound coolness</b></a> and supreme wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta declared at the end of his life that he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>uprooted the root of rebirth</b></a> and fulfilled every instruction given by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his final moments before entering Parinibbāna, Venerable Sāriputta's last instruction to the gathered monks was to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>diligent and non-negligent</b></a> in their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta considered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato"><b>instruction of the Buddha</b></a> to be complete in his life, having laid down the burden of existence forever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.2/en/sujato">Thag 17.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīvalī was born to a Koliyan princess named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato"><b>Suppavāsā</b></a>, who famously endured a difficult pregnancy and seven days of labor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato">Thag 1.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Venerable Sīvalī was still in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato"><b>womb</b></a>, the Buddha sent a special blessing to his mother, wishing for her happiness, health, and the birth of a healthy son? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato">Thag 1.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Sīvalī was chosen because his eventual birth brought <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato"><b>happiness</b></a> and joy to everyone in the royal household? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato">Thag 1.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that just <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato"><b>seven days</b></a> after his birth, Venerable Sīvalī was already capable of performing any task, demonstrating his extraordinary maturity and merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato">Thag 1.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīvalī was encouraged to ordain by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a>, who taught him to use the suffering he experienced during birth as his primary meditation subject? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato">Thag 1.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīvalī attained successive stages of enlightenment while his hair was being shaved, reaching <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> at the exact moment the fourth lock of hair was cut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato">Thag 1.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīvalī used the memory of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato"><b>suffering of birth</b></a> to develop deep insight, which led him to final liberation shortly after entering his leaf hut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato">Thag 1.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after achieving liberation, Venerable Sīvalī declared that he had successfully abandoned the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato"><b>underlying tendency to conceit</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato">Thag 1.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sīvalī entered his monastic hut with specific goals in mind and attained both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato"><b>clear knowledge and liberation</b></a> within that very dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato">Thag 1.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Sīvalī celebrated his safe arrival by providing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato"><b>seven days of offerings</b></a> to the Buddha and the entire community of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato">Thag 1.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the father of Venerable Sīvalī acted as a vital messenger between his wife and the Buddha, seeking a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato"><b>blessing</b></a> that ultimately ensured a safe delivery after a week of pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.60/en/sujato">Thag 1.60</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vappa was born in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato"><b>Kapilavatthu</b></a> as the son of a Brahmin named Vāseṭṭha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato">Thag 1.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vappa was inspired to renounce the household life after hearing the prediction of the sage <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato"><b>Asita</b></a>, who foretold that Prince Siddhattha would become the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato">Thag 1.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vappa was one of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato"><b>five original companions</b></a> who practiced extreme self-mortification alongside the future Buddha for six years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato">Thag 1.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vappa and his four friends initially left the Prince in disappointment and moved to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato"><b>Isipatana</b></a> when the Prince decided to stop his ascetic practices? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato">Thag 1.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vappa attained the fruit of Arahantship on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato"><b>fifth day</b></a> after the Buddha's first discourse, the Setting in Motion of the Wheel of Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato">Thag 1.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vappa reflected on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato"><b>blindness of the world</b></a> as he realized how the noble fruit of liberation brings about true spiritual vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato">Thag 1.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vappa’s verse uses a complex wordplay regarding <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato"><b>the seer and the non-seer</b></a> to illustrate the difference between an enlightened being and one who remains in ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato">Thag 1.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vappa was deeply moved by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato"><b>spiritual power</b></a> of the Buddha, which eventually led to his conversion and final realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato">Thag 1.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vappa belonged to the same group of ascetics as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Koṇḍañña</b></a>, the very first person to achieve enlightenment under the Buddha's guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato">Thag 1.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his dedicated practice, Venerable Vappa attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato"><b>right knowledge</b></a>, allowing him to see the truth that the deluded world remains unable to see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato">Thag 1.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before achieving Arahantship, Venerable Vappa spent six years participating in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato"><b>austerities</b></a> of the group of five monks in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.61/en/sujato">Thag 1.61</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara was born in the city of Rājagaha as the son of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>distinguished Brahmin</b></a> before he dedicated his life to the Buddha's teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, Venerable Uttara was an expert in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>Vedas</b></a> and was highly regarded for his intelligence, family lineage, and virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Magadhan minister Vassakāra was so impressed by Venerable Uttara that he offered to give him his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>daughter in marriage</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara declined a high-status marriage and worldly success because his heart was already inclined toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>liberation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara was inspired to go forth into the homeless life after hearing a sermon by the Buddha's chief disciple, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a novice monk, Venerable Uttara was deeply devoted to serving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> and attending to his needs? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Venerable Uttara was searching for medicine for his sick teacher, a thief escaping from soldiers threw stolen jewelry into his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>alms bowl</b></a> to frame him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because of the stolen goods found in his bowl, Venerable Uttara was arrested and brought before the minister <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>Vassakāra</b></a> for punishment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally visited Venerable Uttara while he was facing punishment to tell him that his ordeal was the result of his own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>past karma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged Venerable Uttara to accept his suffering through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>power of reflection</b></a> and diligent contemplation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after receiving a short teaching from the Buddha, Venerable Uttara attained the state of an Arahant and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>six super-knowledges</b></a> (abhiññā) while still in captivity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after achieving total liberation, Venerable Uttara rose into the air and displayed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>psychic powers</b></a> out of compassion for those witnessing his trial? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his spiritual attainment, Venerable Uttara was able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>heal his wounds</b></a> completely, much to the astonishment of the onlookers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara taught that when one truly understands the suffering of rebirth, the danger of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>present physical pain</b></a> seems insignificant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara declared that there is nothing in existence that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>permanent</b></a> or that remains forever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara emphasized that no <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>conditioned things</b></a> (saṅkhārā) are eternal or stable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Uttara, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> (khandhā) arise in one life only to perish and lead to another birth in the cycle of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because he realized the inherent danger of conditioned existence, Venerable Uttara stated that he no longer had any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>desire for rebirth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara achieved the final goal of the holy life by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>abandoning all sensual desires</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavakkhaya), meaning he had completely uprooted all mental defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara used his own experience of being falsely accused to explain the law of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato"><b>dependent origination</b></a> and how things arise according to causes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.1/en/sujato">Thag 2.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka was born into a prominent family in Sāvatthī and attained the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> after hearing the Buddha preach the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato">Thag 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha officially recognized Venerable Nandaka as the monk who was foremost in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato"><b>exhorting the nuns</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato">Thag 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his dedicated instructions, Venerable Nandaka guided <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato"><b>five hundred nuns</b></a> to achieve total spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato">Thag 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while on alms-round in Sāvatthī, Venerable Nandaka was approached by a former wife who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato"><b>laughed</b></a> at him with a mind full of lust and wrong intent? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato">Thag 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in response to his former wife's lustful behavior, Venerable Nandaka taught her that the human body is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato"><b>foul-smelling</b></a> and full of impurities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato">Thag 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka described the physical body as an object belonging to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato"><b>Māra</b></a>, characterizing it as something filled with various offensive odors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato">Thag 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka pointed out that the body has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato"><b>nine openings</b></a> from which impurities are constantly flowing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato">Thag 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka warned against disparaging the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato"><b>Tathāgata</b></a> or the ancient practitioners who have renounced the worldly life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato">Thag 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka stated that those who have renounced the world no longer have any craving for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato"><b>heavenly realms</b></a>, much less for the pleasures of the human world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato">Thag 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka taught that fools who are enveloped in delusion and wrong intent are easily bound by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato"><b>snare of Māra</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato">Thag 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Nandaka declared that those who have escaped greed, hatred, and ignorance are no longer <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato"><b>bound by ties</b></a> to future existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.4/en/sujato">Thag 4.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Jambuka spent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato"><b>fifty-five years</b></a> practicing extreme and painful naked asceticism before finally encountering the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato">Thag 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, Venerable Jambuka lived in such extreme poverty and delusion that he resorted to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato"><b>eating dung</b></a> as a form of ascetic sustenance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato">Thag 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his time as a misguided ascetic, Venerable Jambuka would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato"><b>pluck out his hair</b></a> and beard instead of using traditional shaving methods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato">Thag 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as part of his severe ascetic practices, Venerable Jambuka remained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato"><b>standing on one leg</b></a> and never allowed himself to use a bed for resting? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato">Thag 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for decades, Venerable Jambuka refused all invitations for meals and instead ate only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato"><b>one grain of a bean</b></a> at a time, taken from the tip of a blade of straw? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato">Thag 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, seeing that the spiritual faculties of Venerable Jambuka were mature, went to him and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato"><b>taught him the Dhamma</b></a> to lead him to enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato">Thag 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Jambuka was ordained into the Sangha by the Buddha through the simple and direct command, "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato"><b>Come, monk</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato">Thag 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Jambuka reflected that his long years of wrong practice would have led him to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato"><b>painful destination</b></a> in the next life if he had not taken refuge in the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato">Thag 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the Buddha's guidance, Venerable Jambuka attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato"><b>Three Knowledges</b></a>, fulfilling the ultimate goal of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato">Thag 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Jambuka described himself as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato"><b>covered in dust</b></a> during his fifty-five years of naked asceticism and bodily neglect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato">Thag 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Jambuka urged others to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato"><b>behold the Buddha</b></a> and the Dhamma as the only true sources of refuge from the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato">Thag 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Jambuka claimed that by following the Teacher's instruction, he had successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato"><b>finished the work</b></a> of the spiritual life and ended all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.5/en/sujato">Thag 4.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka was named after the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>low-caste outcaste group</b></a> into which he was born? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka lost his father when he was only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>four months old</b></a> and was subsequently raised by his uncle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the uncle of Venerable Sopāka was incited by his own jealous son to try and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>kill the child</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young Venerable Sopāka was taken to a terrifying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>charnel ground</b></a> and left there alone by his relative? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while in the cemetery, the hands of the young Venerable Sopāka were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>tied to a corpse</b></a> to ensure he could not escape? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the relative who abandoned Venerable Sopāka expected that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>jackals</b></a> and other wild beasts would devour the child during the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his divine eye to see that the young Venerable Sopāka possessed the potential for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> even while the child was crying in the cemetery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha emitted a radiant light and spoke to the terrified Venerable Sopāka, promising to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>rescue him</b></a> from his bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha compared the rescue of Venerable Sopāka to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>moon escaping</b></a> from the jaws of Rāhu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the Buddha’s power, the young Venerable Sopāka was able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>break his bonds</b></a> and immediately travel to the Buddha's monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka attained the state of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>Stream-enterer</b></a> (Sotāpanna) while standing at the door of the Buddha’s perfumed chamber? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Sopāka sought the Buddha’s help because her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>uncle remained silent</b></a> regarding the whereabouts of the missing child? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>psychic powers</b></a> to hide the young Venerable Sopāka so that his mother could not see him while she questioned the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught the mother of Venerable Sopāka that neither children nor parents can serve as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>refuge</b></a> for one who is facing death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Sopāka attained the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>Stream-entry</b></a> after hearing the Buddha’s discourse on the true nature of refuge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while his mother was listening to the Dhamma, the young Venerable Sopāka successfully attained the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Sopāka was so moved by her child's spiritual achievement that she willingly consented to his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>ordination</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha formally tested the wisdom of Venerable Sopāka by asking him a series of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>ten questions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka answered the Buddha’s first question by explaining that all sentient beings are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>sustained by food</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because of his brilliance in answering the Teacher's test, the monk became known as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>Sopāka of the Questions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka first encountered the Buddha as a monk while the Teacher was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>walking in the shadow</b></a> of his dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka showed his reverence by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>arranging his robe</b></a> over one shoulder and joining his palms in salutation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka described the Buddha as the "Immaculate One" and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>supreme being</b></a> among all creatures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his chronological age, Venerable Sopāka answered the Buddha’s questions <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>without fear</b></a> or trembling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha expressed his deep <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>satisfaction</b></a> with the insightful answers provided by the young Venerable Sopāka? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared it a great <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>benefit</b></a> for the people of Aṅga and Magadha to offer support to such a wise monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha specifically instructed the Sangha to provide Venerable Sopāka with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>medicines and lodgings</b></a> as a sign of respect for his wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka was only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>seven years old</b></a> when he was granted full ordination (upasampadā) into the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka declared he was carrying his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>final body</b></a>, confirming that he had ended the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka marveled at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>goodness of the Dhamma</b></a>, which allowed even a child to reach the highest spiritual goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally invited Venerable Sopāka to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>come and visit</b></a> him regularly, signifying the monk's successful completion of his training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the story of Venerable Sopāka demonstrates how the Buddha recognized a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>potential for enlightenment</b></a> that others could not see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sopāka proved that even a person born into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato"><b>low-caste poverty</b></a> could attain the same spiritual perfections as the highest nobles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.4/en/sujato">Thag 7.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda was given his name, which means "Joy," because his relatives said his birth brought <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>happiness to the family</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda, a cousin of the Buddha, belonged to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan royal family</b></a> and was the son of Amitodana? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda attained the first stage of enlightenment, known as Stream-entry, after hearing a sermon by the monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>Puṇṇa Mantāniputta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda only agreed to become the Buddha's permanent attendant after the Buddha accepted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>eight specific conditions</b></a>, including the refusal of special robes or food? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda refused to accept the Buddha's own robes or special invitations to meals because he did not want others to think he served the Buddha for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>material gain</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that one of the conditions Venerable Ānanda requested before becoming an attendant was that the Buddha would repeat any <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>teachings given in his absence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda was declared by the Buddha to be the foremost among monks in five categories, including being the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>most learned</b></a> and having the best memory? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his 25 years as an attendant, Venerable Ānanda would carry a lamp and a staff at night, walking around the Buddha's room to be ready to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>respond to any call</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda achieved full enlightenment (Arahantship) in a unique posture, precisely as he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>lying down on his bed</b></a>, with his head not yet touching the pillow and his feet already off the ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda warned against associating with "two-tongued" or angry people, stating that the wise should not make <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>wicked people</b></a> their friends? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda encouraged monks to seek out "true persons" as friends, describing them as people who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>faithful, pleasant, and learned</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda compared the physical body to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>painted image</b></a> and a heap of sores, emphasizing that despite its decorations, it is impermanent and diseased? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda described the body as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>skeleton covered with skin</b></a>, which only appears radiant to the foolish because of the clothing and jewelry worn over it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda claimed to have learned <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>eighty-four thousand Dhamma teachings</b></a>, receiving 82,000 directly from the Buddha and 2,000 from other senior monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda likened a person with little learning to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>ox</b></a>, whose flesh increases as they age, but whose wisdom does not? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda warned that a learned person who despises someone for having less knowledge is like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>blind man carrying a lamp</b></a>, possessing light but unable to see? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that to truly master the Dhamma, one must not only hear much but also understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>meaning and phrasing</b></a> of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda stated that a person who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>delights in the Dhamma</b></a> and constantly reflects on it will ensure that the true teaching does not decline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when he heard of the death of his friend Venerable Sāriputta, Venerable Ānanda felt as though <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>all quarters were dark</b></a> and the teachings were no longer clear to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after the death of the Buddha and Venerable Sāriputta, Venerable Ānanda described himself as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>meditating alone</b></a>, comparing his solitude to a bird seeking shelter when the rain comes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda served as the Buddha's attendant for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>twenty-five years</b></a>, during which time he claimed that a single thought of lust never arose in his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for twenty-five years, Venerable Ānanda never experienced a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>thought of ill-will</b></a> while serving the Buddha, demonstrating his exceptional mental restraint? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda described his service to the Buddha as being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>shadow that never leaves its form</b></a>, always accompanying the Teacher with loving actions of body, speech, and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda noted that whenever the Buddha preached the Dhamma, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>insight arose within him</b></a> as he stood or walked behind his Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda described the death of the Buddha as an event that was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>terrible and hair-raising</b></a>, marking the passing of the "Peerless One"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that other monks praised Venerable Ānanda as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>guardian of the treasury</b></a> of the Buddha's teachings and as the "eye for the entire world"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his final declaration, Venerable Ānanda stated that he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>laid down the burden</b></a> and completely uprooted the causes of future existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda's role included introducing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>visitors from far away</b></a> to the Buddha, ensuring they had the opportunity to hear the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda exhorted monks not to damage their learning, calling knowledge of the Dhamma the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>root of the holy life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda taught that a monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>mindful and persistent</b></a> should be approached and respected as one who holds the great treasure of the teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda once rebuked a lazy monk by asking how he could remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>fond of bodily pleasure</b></a> while his life was slipping away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even before becoming an Arahant, Venerable Ānanda's dedication was so great that he served the Buddha with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>loving body-kamma</b></a> for a quarter of a century? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda described himself as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>kin of the Sun</b></a> (the Buddha), emphasizing his deep connection to the Teacher's lineage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda succeeded in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>crossing the shore of birth and death</b></a>, carrying his final physical body before attaining complete liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda was known for his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>power in walking</b></a>, a quality of endurance that allowed him to keep pace with the Buddha's travels? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda's final Arahantship occurred right before the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>First Council</b></a> was held, allowing him to participate as an enlightened member of the assembly? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda emphasized that a learned monk who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>endowed with wisdom</b></a> is a person who should be sought out for spiritual guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda believed that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>cessation of the taints</b></a> and the release from fetters were the ultimate goals of all his efforts as an attendant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda's service included the humble task of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>washing the Buddha's feet</b></a> and providing him with tooth-sticks every morning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ānanda declared that after his long service, he had finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato"><b>finished the Buddha's bidding</b></a> and fulfilled all his duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag17.3/en/sujato">Thag 17.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vajjiputta, born into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato"><b>family of a councillor</b></a>, was inspired to ordain after seeing the Buddha's majestic demeanor in the city of Vesālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato">Thag 1.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while living in a forest near Vesālī, Venerable Vajjiputta felt mentally disturbed and agitated by the noise of singing and dancing from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato"><b>festival</b></a> being held in the city? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato">Thag 1.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vajjiputta compared his solitary life in the remote forest to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato"><b>piece of wood</b></a> that has been cast away and forgotten in the wilderness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato">Thag 1.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a compassionate <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato"><b>forest deity</b></a> intervened when they heard Venerable Vajjiputta lamenting his loneliness, urging him to reconsider the profound value of forest dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato">Thag 1.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the forest deity told Venerable Vajjiputta that even though he might look down on his life in the woods, many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato"><b>wise people</b></a> who seek purity actually speak highly of living in the mountain forests? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato">Thag 1.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vajjiputta's forest life was described by a deity as being as desirable to practitioners as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato"><b>heavenly realms</b></a> are to those suffering in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato">Thag 1.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that spurred by the deity's words like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato"><b>noble horse</b></a> being prodded by a whip, Venerable Vajjiputta deepened his insight meditation and attained Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato">Thag 1.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vajjiputta expressed his realization by repeating the very same <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato"><b>verse</b></a> the deity used to encourage him toward liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato">Thag 1.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vajjiputta initially questioned if anyone could be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato"><b>more unfortunate</b></a> than he was, living alone as the days passed one by one in the mountains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.62/en/sujato">Thag 1.62</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pakkha was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan royal family</b></a> in the city of Devadaha before he encountered the Dhamma and became a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato">Thag 1.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the original name of Venerable Pakkha was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato"><b>Sangmoda</b></a>, though he later became known by a different name due to a physical affliction he suffered as a youth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato">Thag 1.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because he suffered from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato"><b>rheumatism</b></a> as a child and walked with a limp, Sangmoda was given the nickname "Pakkha," meaning "the cripple"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato">Thag 1.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pakkha decided to join the Sangha after regaining his health and meeting the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato"><b>Buddha</b></a> during the Teacher's visit to his family? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato">Thag 1.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while resting under a tree in the forest, Venerable Pakkha observed a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato"><b>hawk</b></a> being chased and attacked by other birds after it snatched a piece of meat? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato">Thag 1.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the sight of birds fighting over meat led Venerable Pakkha to realize that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato"><b>sensual pleasures</b></a> in the world are fraught with danger and intense suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato">Thag 1.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pakkha attained Arahantship and declared his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato"><b>final knowledge</b></a> by reflecting on how greed drives beings to constant conflict and pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato">Thag 1.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that having fulfilled his spiritual duty, Venerable Pakkha proclaimed that he now dwells in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato"><b>blissful happiness</b></a>, having successfully welcomed peace into his heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato">Thag 1.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pakkha described those driven by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato"><b>greed</b></a> as swooping down and falling repeatedly, much like the birds he observed competing in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato">Thag 1.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pakkha achieved the highest goal of the spiritual life through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato"><b>development of insight</b></a> after his period of solitary forest-dwelling? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.63/en/sujato">Thag 1.63</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala Koṇḍañña was the son of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato"><b>King Bimbisāra</b></a> and a woman named Ambapālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato">Thag 1.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Vimala Koṇḍañña, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato"><b>Ambapālī</b></a>, was famously named after a tree, as her name literally refers to a "mango-grove guardian"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato">Thag 1.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala Koṇḍañña's first name, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato"><b>Vimala</b></a>, means "stainless" or "pure," a name given to him by his mother before he later added his clan name? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato">Thag 1.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala Koṇḍañña entered the monastic life because he was deeply moved by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato"><b>virtue and majesty</b></a> of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato">Thag 1.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his declaration of enlightenment, Venerable Vimala Koṇḍañña used a metaphor describing himself as being born under a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato"><b>white banner</b></a>, referencing his royal lineage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato">Thag 1.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala Koṇḍañña spoke of using a banner to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato"><b>strike down a banner</b></a>, illustrating the destruction of worldly ego through spiritual victory? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato">Thag 1.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his diligent practice, Venerable Vimala Koṇḍañña reached the end of suffering and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato"><b>attained Arahantship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato">Thag 1.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vimala Koṇḍañña referred to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato"><b>great flag</b></a> being demolished as a way to express his final triumph over the deeply rooted defilements of the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.64/en/sujato">Thag 1.64</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> was originally born into a wealthy Brahmin family as a young man named Pippali-mānava and initially refused to marry, choosing instead to serve his parents until their death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that to avoid marriage, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> had a golden statue of a beautiful woman made and told his mother he would only marry if a girl matching that beauty could be found? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the girl Bhaddā Kapilānī was found to match the beauty of his golden statue, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> and his future bride both wrote letters to each other declaring their wish to live a celibate life, though the letters were intercepted and swapped by their families? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that on their wedding night, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> and his wife Bhaddā Kapilānī agreed to sleep separately with a flower garland between them to maintain their celibacy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the earth was said to have trembled when <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> and his wife finally renounced the household life together after his parents passed away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha, knowing of his renunciation, went to wait for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> on the road between Nālandā and Rājagaha to personally accept him as a disciple? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> attained the state of Arahantship only eight days after his ordination and was later declared by the Buddha to be the foremost among monks in ascetic practices? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> warned that being honored by crowds and public acclaim can lead to mental distraction, making it difficult for a practitioner to achieve deep concentration? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> compared the honor and gifts received from lay families to a "mire" or "muddy swamp" that is extremely difficult for a bad person to escape? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> once accepted a spoonful of food from a person suffering from leprosy, even when the person’s finger fell into his alms bowl during the offering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> demonstrated his total lack of physical disgust by sitting down near a wall and calmly eating the food into which a leper's finger had fallen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> taught that a monk should be satisfied with the four basic requisites: almsfood, robes made of rags from a dust heap, living at the foot of a tree, and using fermented urine as medicine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even in his old age, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> continued to climb steep mountains to meditate, relying on his spiritual power and mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> found great joy in the solitude of the mountains, describing the rocky crags as being as beautiful as blue clouds or a magnificent palace tower? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> preferred the sound of monkeys howling and the cries of peacocks in the wilderness to the comfort of monasteries like the Bamboo Grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> stated that the joy he found in the one-pointedness of meditation was far superior to the pleasure of listening to five types of musical instruments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> advised monks to avoid having too many social tasks or engaging in disputes with the crowd, as such busyness leads to physical exhaustion and a lack of peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> cautioned against the conceit of thinking one is better than, equal to, or inferior to others, as the wise remain unshakeable by such pride? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> praised those who possess shame and fear of wrongdoing (hiri-ottappa), stating that they are the ones who can successfully end the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> compared an arrogant and restless monk wearing a rag-robe to a monkey trying to pass itself off as a lion by wearing a lion’s skin? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> described a humble, restrained monk in a rag-robe as being like a true lion dwelling within a mountain cave? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> observed thousands of deities, including Brahmas, standing with joined palms to pay homage to Venerable Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> noted that even the most skilled archers who could split a hair could not fully fathom the profound range and conduct of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> saw Venerable Kappina smile with joy when witnessing the celestial beings paying great honor to Venerable Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> declared that except for the Buddha, there was no one in the world equal to him in the excellence of ascetic (dhutaṅga) practices? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> lived as a "child of the Buddha" (Gotama's son), completely unpolluted by his surroundings, just as a lotus flower remains untouched by water? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> used a striking metaphor for his spiritual body, describing mindfulness as his neck, faith as his hands, and wisdom as his head? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> taught that for those who do not respect their companions in the holy life, the true Dhamma is as far away as the sky is from the earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> reached the end of his training having fulfilled the Teacher’s instructions and completely uprooted the causes of future existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> found delight in mountain lakes covered with "Inda's cowherd" insects, which added to the beauty of the wilderness he loved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> described the mountain forests as being adorned with Kareri flowers, creating a pleasant environment for one dedicated to meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> stated that he did not delight in death nor in living, but awaited his final hour with mindfulness, like a workman waiting for the end of his shift? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> emphasized that a person who speaks the truth and is well-composed in virtue is the one who is truly praised by the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> described his experience of meditation on a rock after returning from alms rounds as a state free from all fear and terror? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> viewed the mountain heights as a place where the air is filled with the calls of various birds, making it a perfect sanctuary for an ascetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> taught that the subtle arrow of being honored is a difficult thing to extract from a person's heart once it has taken root? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> believed that a monk who is truly diligent in yoga (spiritual practice) would find the solitude of the forest more beneficial than the company of householders? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> considered the clear water of mountain springs flowing beneath the rocks to be one of the most delightful aspects of his mountain home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> noted that even if some people die while attempting to climb the rocky mountains, he climbed them easily through the power of his spiritual attainments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> described the mountain meadows as being like a sky covered with clouds when they were filled with blossoms from thorn trees? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> warned that those who merely "mumble with their lips" without seeing the truth of themselves often walk around with stiff necks, thinking they are superior? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> compared his state after destroying the taints to a "cool" condition (nibbuta), where the fires of defilement have been completely burned away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> emphasized that a wise person who is energetic and mindful should be seen as the true heir of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> found pleasure in the sight of deer and elephants in the wild, noting that their presence made the hills even more delightful for his practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahākassapa</b></a> declared that he had successfully laid down the burden of life and achieved the ultimate freedom that comes from being an "unstainable" son of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag18.1/en/sujato">Thag 18.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimat was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato"><b>citizen family</b></a> in the city of Sāvatthī and was given his name because his family was consistently successful and fortunate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato">Thag 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimat had a younger brother named Sirivaddha, whose name signified the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato"><b>growth of fortune</b></a> within their household? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato">Thag 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimat decided to enter the monastic life after being deeply moved by the majesty of the Buddha during the dedication of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato"><b>Jetavana Monastery</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato">Thag 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite his spiritual attainments, Venerable Sirimat was initially disrespected by ordinary monks and novices because he received very little <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato"><b>public honor</b></a> compared to his popular brother? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato">Thag 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his diligent practice of tranquility and insight meditation, Venerable Sirimat achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato"><b>six super-knowledges</b></a> (abhiññā), marking his full enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato">Thag 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimat pointed out that when others praise someone whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato"><b>self is uncomposed</b></a>, that praise is actually based on a falsehood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato">Thag 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sirimat, if people criticize a person whose <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato"><b>self is well-composed</b></a> through meditation, their criticism is entirely mistaken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato">Thag 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the verses recited by Venerable Sirimat regarding praise and blame so deeply affected his brother, Sirivaddha, that he eventually attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato"><b>total liberation</b></a> as well? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato">Thag 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that those who had previously criticized Venerable Sirimat were moved to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato"><b>seek his forgiveness</b></a> after they realized the depth of his spiritual victory? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato">Thag 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimat maintained his dedication to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato"><b>tranquility and insight</b></a> even when he was being overlooked by his fellow monastics in favor of his brother? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.20/en/sujato">Thag 2.20</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara was born into a Brahmin family in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato"><b>Sāketa</b></a> before he became a disciple of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato">Thag 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the inspiration for Venerable Uttara to go forth into the homeless life came from witnessing the Buddha perform the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato"><b>Twin Miracle</b></a> at the Gandamba tree in Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato">Thag 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara was encouraged to ordain after hearing the Buddha preach a sermon at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato"><b>Kālaka Garden</b></a> in his home city of Sāketa? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato">Thag 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara achieved the state of an Arahant and mastered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato"><b>six super-knowledges</b></a> (abhiññā) after traveling to Rājagaha with the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato">Thag 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by his fellow monks about his progress, Venerable Uttara declared that he had fully understood the nature of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> (khandhā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato">Thag 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara described his spiritual success as having skillfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato"><b>uprooted all craving</b></a> (taṇhā) from his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato">Thag 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his practice, Venerable Uttara successfully developed the seven <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato"><b>factors of enlightenment</b></a> (bojjhaṅgā) to reach the final goal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato">Thag 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara characterized his liberation as having reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato"><b>destruction of taints</b></a> (āsavakkhaya), ensuring no further rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato">Thag 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Uttara described his escape from the cycle of suffering as having finally broken free from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato"><b>one who sets the net</b></a>, a reference to Māra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato">Thag 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his complete spiritual victory, Venerable Uttara declared that he would soon <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato"><b>attain Nirvana</b></a> and remain forever free from all mental fermentations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.21/en/sujato">Thag 2.21</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Senaka was born into a family as the nephew of the famous <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato"><b>Elder Kassapa of Uruvelā</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato">Thag 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, Venerable Senaka was a dedicated student of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato"><b>Vedic Brahmin tradition</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato">Thag 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Senaka first encountered the Buddha at a spring festival in the city of Gayā during the month of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato"><b>Phagguna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato">Thag 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Senaka was moved to seek ordination after hearing the Buddha preach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato"><b>Supreme Dhamma</b></a> near the riverbank at Gayā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato">Thag 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Senaka attained the state of an Arahant and later described the Buddha as a "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato"><b>Great Naga</b></a>" who was completely free from all taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato">Thag 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Senaka confessed that prior to his enlightenment, he had been defiled and bound by the chains of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato"><b>wrong views</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato">Thag 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Senaka declared his final liberation by stating he was now "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato"><b>independent and free</b></a>" from every type of spiritual bondage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato">Thag 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Senaka praised the Buddha as the leader of the assembly who had conquered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato"><b>realms of gods and humans</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato">Thag 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Senaka described the Buddha as a teacher of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato"><b>great radiance</b></a> who is difficult for anyone to measure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato">Thag 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Senaka credited the Buddha for freeing him from a long history of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato"><b>polluted</b></a> by mental defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato">Thag 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Senaka expressed immense gratitude for seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato"><b>Fully Enlightened One</b></a> during the spring festival known as the Gayā melā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.6/en/sujato">Thag 4.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sambhūta was born into a high-class family and was eventually converted and ordained by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ānanda</b></a> after the Buddha's passing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato">Thag 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sambhūta was a key figure who opposed the ten unlawful practices introduced by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato"><b>Vajjī monks</b></a> one hundred years after the Buddha’s Parinibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato">Thag 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sambhūta participated in a historic council consisting of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato"><b>seven hundred Arahants</b></a> to protect the purity of the Dhamma and Vinaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato">Thag 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sambhūta taught that an individual who acts with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato"><b>haste</b></a> when they should be patient is a fool who will inevitably encounter suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato">Thag 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sambhūta compared a person who lacks right method and falls into disgrace to the moon entering its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato"><b>dark phase</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato">Thag 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sambhūta observed that those who act without correct logic or reason often end up in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato"><b>conflict with their friends</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato">Thag 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sambhūta stated that a wise person who knows the correct timing to move slowly or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato"><b>quickly</b></a> will find lasting happiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato">Thag 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sambhūta compared the spiritual fulfillment and success of a wise practitioner to the moon in its <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato"><b>bright phase</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato">Thag 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sambhūta taught that fame and honor come to those who possess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato"><b>correct reason</b></a> and maintain harmony with their companions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato">Thag 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sambhūta reached the state of an Arahant after being instructed in the Dhamma by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ānanda</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato">Thag 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sambhūta was deeply moved to recite his verses because of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato"><b>distortion of the Teaching</b></a> and the Discipline that he witnessed during his lifetime? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.7/en/sujato">Thag 4.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga earned his name, which literally means "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>reed-breaker</b></a>," because he used to snap reeds with his own hands to build the grass hut where he lived as an ascetic? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before entering the monastic life, Venerable Sarabhaṅga was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>brahmin family</b></a> in the city of Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha used his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> to scan the world and, perceiving that the ascetic Sarabhaṅga was ready for liberation, traveled specifically to teach him the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga explained to the public that while he once manually constructed his own shelter, it was no longer appropriate for him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>break reeds</b></a> after he had dedicated himself to the training of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga declared that he had successfully seen the "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>entire disease</b></a>" of suffering in its full extent only after hearing the liberating words of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga realized that the spiritual path he followed to enlightenment was the very same one used by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>Vipassī</b></a> and other Buddhas of the distant past? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga reached his goal by following the exact same path to liberation that was walked by the ancient Buddha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>Sikhī</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga noted that his journey to the end of suffering aligned with the path followed by the former Buddha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>Vessabhū</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga stated that his spiritual progress was guided by the same path used by the Buddha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>Kakusandha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga emphasized that his spiritual attainment was made possible by following the same ancient path as the Buddha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>Konāgamana</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga taught that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>seven Buddhas</b></a> reached Nibbāna by becoming entirely detached and free from all forms of grasping and acquisition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga explained that the Buddha Gotama taught the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a>—suffering, its origin, the path, and its cessation—out of deep compassion for all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga declared that since he had attained total liberation, there would be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>no further rebirth</b></a> for him once his current physical body reached the end of its life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga described his spiritual state as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>well-released</b></a> from all acquisitions and the underlying ties that bind a person to existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga pointed out that while worldlings suffer through a continuous and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>endless cycle</b></a> of existence, he had finally reached the complete cessation of all pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga continued to live in his old grass hut even after becoming an Arahant, and when it eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>collapsed</b></a>, he used the occasion to explain to the people why he no longer needed to rebuild it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga attained the fruit of Arahantship shortly after receiving a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>personal teaching</b></a> from the Buddha at his forest hermitage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sarabhaṅga characterized his final realization as a breakthrough where he saw things as they truly are, reaching a state he called being "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato"><b>freed from ties</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag7.5/en/sujato">Thag 7.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ukkhepakatavaccha was born in the city of Sāvatthī into a Brahmin family belonging to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato"><b>Vaccha clan</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato">Thag 1.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Venerable Ukkhepakatavaccha first heard the Buddha’s teaching in a village in Kosala, he was unable to distinguish between the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato"><b>Sutta, Vinaya, and Abhidhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato">Thag 1.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that to deepen his understanding of the Dhamma, Venerable Ukkhepakatavaccha sought out the expert guidance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato">Thag 1.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ukkhepakatavaccha became so proficient in the Dhamma that he succeeded in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato"><b>memorizing the Three Baskets</b></a> even before they were formally collected? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato">Thag 1.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after achieving Arahantship, Venerable Ukkhepakatavaccha became a dedicated teacher who addressed lay followers with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato"><b>vastly joyful heart</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato">Thag 1.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ukkhepakatavaccha described his spiritual victory as having <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato"><b>discarded wealth</b></a> that had been accumulated over many months and years? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato">Thag 1.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ukkhepakatavaccha was inspired to leave the household life after hearing the Buddha preach while he was living in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato"><b>Kosala village</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato">Thag 1.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Ukkhepakatavaccha includes a term that signifies his action of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato"><b>casting away</b></a> worldly attachments to find liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.65/en/sujato">Thag 1.65</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Meghiya was born in Kapilavatthu as a member of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan royal family</b></a> before he eventually joined the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato">Thag 1.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Meghiya served as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato"><b>personal attendant</b></a> of the Buddha while they were staying in the region of Cālikā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato">Thag 1.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Meghiya became so enamored with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato"><b>mango grove</b></a> on the banks of the Kimikālā river that he requested to live there alone for meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato">Thag 1.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato"><b>refused twice</b></a> to grant Venerable Meghiya permission to leave his side for the mango grove, only yielding after a third, insistent request? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato">Thag 1.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while attempting to meditate in the mango grove, Venerable Meghiya found himself plagued by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome thoughts</b></a> and the annoyance of biting insects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato">Thag 1.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his failed meditation attempt, the Buddha taught Venerable Meghiya the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato"><b>five factors</b></a> that bring a heart to maturity for liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato">Thag 1.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that by applying the Buddha's specific instructions on mental maturity, Venerable Meghiya successfully attained Arahantship and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato">Thag 1.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Meghiya was given to him because he was said to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato"><b>like a cloud</b></a>, a name he carried from his youth in Kapilavatthu? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato">Thag 1.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his declaration of final knowledge, Venerable Meghiya joyfully stated that he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato"><b>completed the Buddha’s bidding</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.66/en/sujato">Thag 1.66</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ekadhammasavaṇīya was the son of a prominent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato"><b>councillor</b></a> in the city of Setavyā before he encountered the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato">Thag 1.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha perceived the mental readiness of Venerable Ekadhammasavaṇīya and taught him the single core truth that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato"><b>all conditioned things are impermanent</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato">Thag 1.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the unique name of Venerable Ekadhammasavaṇīya literally means "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato"><b>one who heard one teaching</b></a>," referencing his rapid path to enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato">Thag 1.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ekadhammasavaṇīya attained Arahantship by specifically investigating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato"><b>meaning of suffering and non-self</b></a> following his first encounter with the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato">Thag 1.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ekadhammasavaṇīya proclaimed his total liberation by saying he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato"><b>burned away his defilements</b></a> and uprooted all future rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato">Thag 1.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha first met Venerable Ekadhammasavaṇīya during a visit to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato"><b>Siṃsapā forest</b></a> near the city of Setavyā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato">Thag 1.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ekadhammasavaṇīya’s swift realization was made possible by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato"><b>influence of past lives</b></a>, which allowed him to grasp the truth with exceptional clarity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato">Thag 1.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ekadhammasavaṇīya confirmed in his final verse that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato"><b>path of rebirth</b></a> has been completely severed for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.67/en/sujato">Thag 1.67</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddaji was born into a family so wealthy that his father, a councillor in the city of Bhaddiya, possessed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato"><b>eight hundred million</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato">Thag 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before becoming a monk, Venerable Bhaddaji was raised in extreme luxury, a lifestyle often compared to that of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato"><b>Bodhisatta</b></a> in his final birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato">Thag 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddaji achieved the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> immediately after hearing the Buddha preach for the first time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato">Thag 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his ordination, Venerable Bhaddaji sought out <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato"><b>seclusion</b></a> on the banks of the Ganges River to enter deep meditative absorption? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato">Thag 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that to demonstrate the spiritual attainments of Venerable Bhaddaji, the Buddha invited him onto a ferry and requested him to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato"><b>display psychic powers</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato">Thag 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that using his psychic powers, Venerable Bhaddaji caused a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato"><b>sunken golden palace</b></a> to rise from the water, proving his past identity as King Mahāpanāda? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato">Thag 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddaji described a past life where he lived in a palace with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato"><b>sacrificial pillars made of gold</b></a> that was sixteen units wide? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato">Thag 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the ancient palace mentioned by Venerable Bhaddaji was described as being over a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato"><b>thousand times higher</b></a> than its width? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato">Thag 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddaji recalled a celestial residence that featured a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato"><b>thousand steps</b></a> and a hundred circular roofs decorated with banners and gems? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato">Thag 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Bhaddaji, his past royal palace was filled with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato"><b>six thousand Gandhabba</b></a> and celestial nymphs who performed in seven troupes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato">Thag 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddaji attained a state where the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato"><b>notion of self</b></a> had been so thoroughly eradicated that he spoke of his past royal life as if it belonged to someone else? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato">Thag 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhaddaji was personally sought out by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato"><b>Buddha</b></a>, who traveled to find him and invite him into the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.22/en/sujato">Thag 2.22</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sobhita was born in the city of Sāvatthī into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin family</b></a> before he went forth as a disciple of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato">Thag 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after attaining the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato"><b>six super-knowledges</b></a> (abhiññā), Venerable Sobhita became highly proficient in the recollection of past lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato">Thag 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha officially designated Venerable Sobhita as the foremost disciple in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato"><b>recollection of past lives</b></a> (pubbenivāsānussati)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato">Thag 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sobhita was able to recollect as many as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato"><b>five hundred eons</b></a> (kappa) in the span of a single night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato">Thag 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sobhita achieved his remarkable spiritual memory through the practice of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato"><b>Four Foundations of Mindfulness</b></a> (satipaṭṭhāna)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato">Thag 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sobhita emphasized the importance of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato"><b>Seven Factors of Enlightenment</b></a> (bojjhaṅgā) and the Noble Eightfold Path in his journey to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato">Thag 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sobhita described himself as a monk who stayed mindful and used <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato"><b>energetic wisdom</b></a> to traverse his past existences? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato">Thag 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the spiritual joy experienced by Venerable Sobhita upon attaining his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato"><b>breakthrough</b></a> led him to recite verses celebrating his success? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.23/en/sujato">Thag 2.23</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Valliya was originally a Brahmin from Vesālī named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato"><b>Kaṇhamitta</b></a> before he dedicated himself to the Buddha's path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato">Thag 2.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Valliya was first inspired to ordain after witnessing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato"><b>majesty and virtue</b></a> of the Buddha during a visit to Vesālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato">Thag 2.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Valliya began his monastic training under the guidance of the great Elder <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato"><b>Mahā Kassapa</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato">Thag 2.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato"><b>Valliya</b></a>" (meaning "creeper plant") was given to this monk because he initially had slow wisdom and had to rely on the support of his fellow practitioners? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato">Thag 2.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Valliya experienced a maturing of his wisdom after listening to a discourse delivered by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Venudatta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato">Thag 2.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Valliya expressed his determination by stating he would fulfill all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato"><b>monastic duties</b></a> with energy and diligence without ever backsliding? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato">Thag 2.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Valliya sought guidance from his teacher on the specific path to reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato"><b>Deathless</b></a> (amata)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato">Thag 2.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through quiet meditation, Venerable Valliya aimed to achieve the "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato"><b>Holy Silence</b></a>" (mona) that characterizes the state of a sage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato">Thag 2.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Valliya compared his ultimate spiritual destination to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato"><b>Ganges River</b></a> flowing deep into the ocean? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.24/en/sujato">Thag 2.24</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rāhula, the biological son of the Buddha and Princess <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato"><b>Yasodharā</b></a>, was raised among other Sakyan princes before entering the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato">Thag 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rāhula was known as "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato"><b>Lucky Rāhula</b></a>" by his wise companions because he possessed both the honor of being the Buddha's son and the attainment of the Dhamma eye? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato">Thag 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rāhula described the state of being trapped by sensual pleasures as being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato"><b>fish caught in a net</b></a> due to one's own negligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato">Thag 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his wisdom matured through many instructions, Venerable Rāhula attained the Three Knowledges and saw the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato"><b>deathless state</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato">Thag 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rāhula declared that he had successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato"><b>uprooted the root of craving</b></a>, attaining a state of spiritual coolness and peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato">Thag 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rāhula attained the status of an Arahant who is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato"><b>worthy of offerings</b></a> by completely destroying all his mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato">Thag 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rāhula compared the shroud of craving and the net of wrong views to being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato"><b>blinded</b></a> by the pursuit of sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato">Thag 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the story regarding the ordination of Venerable Rāhula is recorded in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato"><b>Khandhaka</b></a> section of the monastic discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato">Thag 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rāhula achieved spiritual liberation by successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato"><b>cutting the bonds of Māra</b></a> and ending the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato">Thag 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rāhula used his verses to reflect on his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato"><b>victory</b></a> over the forces of desire and the realization of final knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.8/en/sujato">Thag 4.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Candana was born into a wealthy family and achieved the stage of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato"><b>Stream-entry</b></a> immediately after hearing the Buddha preach the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato">Thag 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Candana chose to wait until his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato"><b>child was born</b></a> before he renounced his family life to become a forest-dwelling monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato">Thag 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Venerable Candana was staying in a charnel ground in Sāvatthī, his former wife attempted to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato"><b>tempt him</b></a> to disrobe by using her beauty and their child? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato">Thag 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Candana attained the six direct knowledges by developing insight specifically while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato"><b>seeing his wife</b></a> approaching him from a distance to seduce him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato">Thag 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Candana described his beautifully adorned wife as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato"><b>trap set by a demon</b></a> intended to lure him back to the worldly life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato">Thag 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the application of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato"><b>wise attention</b></a> and the clear perception of danger, Venerable Candana achieved the total liberation of his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato">Thag 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Candana taught the Dhamma to his former wife so effectively that she <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato"><b>took refuge</b></a> and committed herself to the moral precepts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato">Thag 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Candana attained the Three Knowledges and fulfilled the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's teaching</b></a> during an intense moment of meditative self-reflection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato">Thag 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the wife of Venerable Candana arrived at the charnel ground <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato"><b>covered in gold</b></a> and accompanied by many maidservants to try and win him back? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato">Thag 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by fellow monks about his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato"><b>radiant faculties</b></a>, Venerable Candana recited verses to prove his final realization of the truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato">Thag 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Candana originally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato"><b>lived in the forest</b></a> to focus on his meditation before traveling to Sāvatthī to visit the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag4.9/en/sujato">Thag 4.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana was born in the city of Ujjeni as the son of the chaplain and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>sacrificial advisor</b></a> to King Caṇḍapajjota? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana had mastered all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>three Vedas</b></a> and eventually succeeded his father in his high-ranking royal position? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana was one of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>eight people</b></a> sent by King Caṇḍapajjota on a special mission to invite the Buddha to the kingdom of Ujjeni? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana attained the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> and the four analytical knowledges immediately after hearing the Buddha’s first discourse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana and his seven companions were ordained by the Buddha through the miraculous "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>come, monks</b></a>" (ehi bhikkhu) formula? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana to return to Ujjeni <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>on his own</b></a> to fulfill the mission of converting the King, as the Buddha stayed behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana warned practitioners against taking on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>too many activities</b></a> and getting involved in the struggle of crowds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana, a person who is greedy and infatuated with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>flavors and tastes</b></a> will fail to achieve the goal of true peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana compared receiving honors and excessive respect from families to falling into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>pile of mud</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana described worldly respect as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>subtle arrow</b></a> that is extremely difficult for an individual to extract once it has pierced them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana taught King Caṇḍapajjota that a person's actions are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>their own kamma</b></a> and should not be dictated by what others say or do? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana asserted that the praise or blame of others does not define a person, as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>Devas</b></a> know a person as they truly know themselves? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana taught that those who realize that all beings are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>perishing</b></a> in this world are able to live a life of stillness and peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana, a person who lacks <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> is as if they were not living at all, regardless of the wealth they may have? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana advised the wise to be like those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>blind and deaf</b></a> to external distractions to allow for internal spiritual focus? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana suggested that one should sometimes act as if they were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>foolish or weak</b></a> to create the conditions for wholesome states to arise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana recommended lying still like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>dead person</b></a> as a meditative practice to encourage the growth of good qualities in the mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana successfully converted <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>King Caṇḍapajjota</b></a> to the Dhamma after returning from his first meeting with the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana observed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>worldly honor</b></a> is a bond that is very difficult to give up, especially for those who are not yet established in goodness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Mahā-Kaccāyana taught that even when a wise person's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato"><b>wealth is exhausted</b></a>, they continue to live a life of true strength and vigor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.1/en/sujato">Thag 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, Venerable Tālaputta was a famous actor and the leader of a stage troupe who traveled across India with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>five hundred women</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta once asked the Buddha if actors who make people laugh on stage are reborn in the heaven of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>laughing deities</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta was told by the Buddha that actors who incite lust and delusion in their audience are actually destined for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>hell</b></a> or the animal realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta wept after hearing the Buddha's teaching because he realized he had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>deceived</b></a> by generations of actors who promised a happy rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta aspired to live alone in mountain caves and forests, contemplating the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>impermanence</b></a> of all forms of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta looked forward to wearing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>yellow patchwork robes</b></a> and possessing nothing while dwelling in the happiness of the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta viewed the human body as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>abode of slaughter</b></a>, oppressed by old age, disease, and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta wished to use the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>sharp sword of wisdom</b></a> to cut through the tangled creepers of craving that lead to suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta aimed to crush Māra and his army while seated on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>lion's seat</b></a> of enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta desired to associate with the virtuous and see all things <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>as they really are</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta resolved to endure hunger, thirst, wind, and insects while meditating in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>mountain of Giribbaja</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta sought to understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> as khèo-giảng (well-taught) by the Great Sage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta viewed sense objects like sights, sounds, and tastes as being like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>blazing fire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta practiced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>equanimity</b></a>, so that his mind would not be disappointed by harsh words or satisfied by praise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta weighed internal and external phenomena as being no different from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>grass, wood, or creepers</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta described the joy of walking the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>path of the sage</b></a> even when the rain soaked his robes in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta found inspiration in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>cries of peacocks</b></a> in the mountain caves as he meditated to achieve the deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta used his psychic powers to envision crossing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>Ganges and Yamunā rivers</b></a> without fear or sinking? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta likened his spiritual progress to an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>elephant on the battlefield</b></a>, smashing through the desires for sensual pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta felt like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>debtor</b></a> who finally found buried treasure after hearing the teachings of the Great Sage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta had a long <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>dialogue with his own mind</b></a>, rebuking it for being restless after it had originally urged him to ordain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta described the human mind as being as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>fickle as a monkey</b></a>, constantly running toward what it likes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta praised the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>Great Physician</b></a> who skillfully tames those who are difficult to tame? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta observed that ordinary people are bound by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>sweet and pleasant sensual desires</b></a>, leading them straight to hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta urged his mind to develop the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>factors of enlightenment</b></a> (bojjhaṅgā) to achieve the threefold knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta followed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> to cleanse all defilements and reach the deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta taught his mind to view the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> (khandhā) as suffering and to abandon their underlying causes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta practiced seeing all conditioned things as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>not-self</b></a> (anattā) and as a source of harm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta accepted a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>shaven head and bad appearance</b></a> as he went on alms rounds, holding a bowl that resembled a skull? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta aspired for his mind to be as clear and bright as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>full moon</b></a> when it is freed from clouds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta undertook the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>ascetic practices</b></a> (dhutaṅga) of living in cemeteries and never lying down to sleep? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta compared his mind to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>magician</b></a> who had deceived him for many lifetimes through various tricks and illusions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta resolved to tame his mind using <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>wise reflection</b></a>, just as a hook is used to subdue a wild elephant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta recognized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>world is unstable</b></a> and lacks any permanent core, as taught by the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta asked his mind why it wanted to flee when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>three forms of existence</b></a> are full of disaster? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta described the human body as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>disgusting object with nine streams</b></a> flowing from it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta found the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>mountain peaks</b></a> to be a delightful home where deer and pigs roam freely? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta would lie on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>nature-soft grass</b></a> in the forest as if it were a bed of cotton? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta likened himself to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>leather worker</b></a>, skillfully taming his mind to make it flexible and obedient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta compared taming his mind to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>horse trainer</b></a> managing a well-bred horse? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta resolved to tie his mind to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>meditation object</b></a>, just as an elephant is tied firmly to a pillar? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta reflected on how his mind had caused him to be reborn as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>king, a brahmin, or a deity</b></a> in previous lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta realized his mind was the one that had sent him into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>hell, the animal realm, or the hungry ghost realm</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta warned against falling under the power of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>four distorted perceptions</b></a> (vipallāsa)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta sought to follow the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>Great Sage with Great Compassion</b></a>, the Muni who cuts all fetters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta aimed to reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>other shore</b></a>, safe from the power of the Evil One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta expressed that those without wisdom are the ones who fall into the power of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>Māra</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta used <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>rightful thought</b></a> to restrain his wandering mind and keep it from running toward pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta likened the act of crossing the world of suffering to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>crossing a great flood</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Tālaputta declared that he would no longer follow the mind's leading, but would instead follow the path of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato"><b>deathless state</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag19.1/en/sujato">Thag 19.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vīra was the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato"><b>son of a minister</b></a> to King Pasenadi in the city of Sāvatthī before he chose to ordain as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato">Thag 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before joining the Sangha, Venerable Vīra was a soldier and an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato"><b>expert in martial arts</b></a> and sports? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato">Thag 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vīra decided to renounce the world after having a son because he was deeply moved by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato"><b>suffering of rebirth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato">Thag 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vīra attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> through his diligent and energetic practice of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato">Thag 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vīra's former wife attempted to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato"><b>seduce him</b></a> back to the household life after he had already achieved the state of an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato">Thag 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vīra compared the futility of his former wife's advances to a fly trying to knock down <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato"><b>Mount Sineru</b></a> with its wing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato">Thag 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the former wife of Venerable Vīra was so inspired by his spiritual firmess that she eventually ordained as a nun and attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato">Thag 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vīra declared that he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato"><b>victorious in battle</b></a> and free from all fear because he had successfully tamed a mind that was once difficult to control? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato">Thag 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vīra achieved a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato"><b>inner peace</b></a> and satisfaction, having completely overcome all forms of spiritual doubt? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.8/en/sujato">Thag 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ugga was born into a prominent family of a councillor in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato"><b>Ugga</b></a> within the kingdom of Kosala? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato">Thag 1.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ugga was inspired to join the monastic order after hearing the Buddha preach during a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato"><b>visit</b></a> to his hometown? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato">Thag 1.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ugga attained Arahantship and declared that he had successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato"><b>severed the chain</b></a> of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato">Thag 1.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ugga stated that every <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato"><b>kammic action</b></a> he had ever performed, whether great or small, had been completely eliminated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato">Thag 1.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ugga’s verse serves as a bold <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato"><b>declaration of final knowledge</b></a>, confirming that for him there is no further existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato">Thag 1.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ugga is identified as a monk who attained the highest goal of the spiritual life through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> in the Buddha’s teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato">Thag 1.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ugga abandoned his high social status as the son of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato"><b>elite official</b></a> to follow the path of a homeless monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato">Thag 1.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Ugga achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato"><b>end of suffering</b></a> by uprooting the causes that lead to future birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.80/en/sujato">Thag 1.80</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samitigutta was a monk from Sāvatthī who suffered from a severe case of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato"><b>leprosy</b></a> that caused his limbs to fall off piece by piece? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato">Thag 1.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samitigutta resided in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato"><b>hospital</b></a> while facing the intense physical pain resulting from his past unwholesome deeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato">Thag 1.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāriputta visited Venerable Samitigutta and provided him with a meditation subject focused on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato">Thag 1.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samitigutta realized that all suffering is fundamentally a matter of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato"><b>feeling</b></a> associated with the five aggregates? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato">Thag 1.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samitigutta attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato"><b>six direct knowledges</b></a> even while his physical body was in a state of extreme decay? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato">Thag 1.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samitigutta used his spiritual power to remember his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato"><b>past lives</b></a> and the specific evil deeds that caused his current illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato">Thag 1.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samitigutta declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato"><b>root of other kamma</b></a> had been completely destroyed, and he was only experiencing the final sensations of past actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato">Thag 1.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Samitigutta was inspired to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> after hearing a brief teaching from the Buddha’s chief disciple, Venerable Sāriputta? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato">Thag 1.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the spiritual journey of Venerable Samitigutta shows that even the most <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato"><b>painful physical affliction</b></a> can be used as a basis for attaining final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.81/en/sujato">Thag 1.81</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gaṅgātiriya was born in the city of Sāvatthī as the son of a citizen and was originally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato"><b>named Datta</b></a> before his ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato">Thag 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gaṅgātiriya entered the monastic life because he felt intense remorse after committing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato"><b>moral error</b></a> while living as a layman? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato">Thag 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Gaṅgātiriya literally refers to him being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato"><b>dweller on the banks of the Ganges</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato">Thag 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gaṅgātiriya practiced extreme simplicity by living in a tiny hut constructed from only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato"><b>three palm leaves</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato">Thag 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gaṅgātiriya used an old jar originally meant for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato"><b>offering milk to the dead</b></a> as his bowl for receiving alms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato">Thag 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gaṅgātiriya wore robes that were made of various pieces of cloth <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato"><b>gathered from a rubbish heap</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato">Thag 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for two whole years, Venerable Gaṅgātiriya remained in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato"><b>complete silence</b></a> without speaking a single word to anyone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato">Thag 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his second year of practice, Venerable Gaṅgātiriya broke his silence only once to tell a woman that her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato"><b>offering of milk</b></a> was sufficient? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato">Thag 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that it was in the third year of his diligent practice that Venerable Gaṅgātiriya succeeded in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato"><b>shattering the darkness of ignorance</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato">Thag 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gaṅgātiriya reached the state of an Arahant and celebrated his achievement by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato"><b>praising his former ascetic life</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato">Thag 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gaṅgātiriya attained total liberation by following a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato"><b>solitary path</b></a> of extreme austerity and mindfulness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato">Thag 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Gaṅgātiriya chose to stay on the riverbank to maintain his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato"><b>vow of silence</b></a> and avoid all social distractions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.4/en/sujato">Thag 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kappatakura was born in Sāvatthī and was so poor that he begged for food while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato"><b>wearing rags</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato">Thag 2.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Kappatakura literally means "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato"><b>rags and rice</b></a>," reflecting the poverty he experienced as a layman? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato">Thag 2.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before joining the Sangha, Venerable Kappatakura made a living by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato"><b>selling grass</b></a> that he had cut himself in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato">Thag 2.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kappatakura was inspired to go forth after he met an Elder in the forest and was moved by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato"><b>hearing the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato">Thag 2.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even after ordaining, Venerable Kappatakura kept his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato"><b>old tattered clothes</b></a> hidden away because he was still attached to his former life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato">Thag 2.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kappatakura actually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato"><b>left the community</b></a> of monks seven times because of his lingering attachment and dissatisfaction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato">Thag 2.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha personally rebuked Venerable Kappatakura for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato"><b>nodding and swaying</b></a> his head while sitting in the assembly of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato">Thag 2.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Venerable Kappatakura that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato"><b>tattered rags</b></a> were too heavy a burden for his spiritual practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato">Thag 2.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Buddha, the mind of Venerable Kappatakura was like a bowl of immortality filled with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato"><b>True Dhamma</b></a>, even though he was failing to practice meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato">Thag 2.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha warned Venerable Kappatakura that a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato"><b>foolish person</b></a> who merely sways in the assembly will never reach the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato">Thag 2.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after being strongly admonished by the Teacher, Venerable Kappatakura developed such intense <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato"><b>meditative insight</b></a> that he achieved Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato">Thag 2.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Kappatakura eventually declared his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato"><b>attainment of final knowledge</b></a> by repeating the verses that had spurred him to awaken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.40/en/sujato">Thag 2.40</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rājadatta was born into a family of caravan travelers and was given a name meaning "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>given by the king</b></a>" because his parents prayed to the deity Vessavaṇa for a son? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before entering the monastic life, Venerable Rājadatta led a massive trade mission consisting of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>five hundred wagons</b></a> to the city of Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that prior to his ordination, Venerable Rājadatta squandered his entire fortune by spending <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>one thousand coins</b></a> on a beautiful prostitute, eventually leaving him in a state of hungry wandering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rājadatta was inspired to join the Sangha after hearing the Buddha preach a sermon at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>Veḷuvana</b></a> monastery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rājadatta chose to practice the strict dhutaṅga duties while living in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>charnel ground</b></a> to intensify his meditation practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while meditating in a cemetery, Venerable Rājadatta encountered the body of a woman that had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>cast away</b></a> after she was killed for trying to steal a valuable ring? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rājadatta initially felt a surge of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>sensual lust</b></a> when he saw the intact corpse of a beautiful woman that had not yet been scavenged by animals? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rājadatta described his temporary mental distraction upon seeing the corpse as being like a blind man bound by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>greed and desire</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that to regain his focus and overcome mental agitation, Venerable Rājadatta fled his immediate location faster than <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>boiling rice</b></a> spills over? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rājadatta attained the state of an Arahant by applying <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>wise attention</b></a> to the inherent dangers he perceived within the physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rājadatta declared he had achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>Three Knowledges</b></a> and completed all the Buddha's instructions through his meditation in the charnel ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the prostitute whose corpse Venerable Rājadatta observed had been killed by the police because her servant reported she had stolen a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>valuable ring</b></a> from another traveler? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rājadatta used the sight of a body being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>eaten by worms</b></a> in a cemetery as a primary subject to develop his insight meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after reaching spiritual success, Venerable Rājadatta stated that his mind was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>liberated</b></a> by seeing the true nature of things as they are? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rājadatta credited his progress to maintaining <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and alertness</b></a> after he realized the perils of his own mental agitation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, Venerable Rājadatta fell into extreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>misery and hunger</b></a> after squandering his caravan's wealth on fleeting pleasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rājadatta was able to establish himself in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>dispassion</b></a> after reflecting on the foul nature of the physical body he witnessed in the cemetery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rājadatta described the intact body he encountered as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>abominable corpse</b></a> that served as a catalyst for his ultimate enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his unwavering effort, Venerable Rājadatta attained a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>total mental freedom</b></a> and ended the cycle of rebirth for himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rājadatta was considered a gift from a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato"><b>sky deity</b></a> by his parents, which significantly influenced the name they chose for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.1/en/sujato">Thag 5.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimitta was born in the city of Rājagaha into the family of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>wealthy landowner</b></a> before entering the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the mother of Venerable Sirimitta was the sister of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>Sirigutta</b></a>, a well-known figure in the Buddhist history of Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimitta was inspired to find faith in the Buddha after witnessing the Teacher tame the fierce elephant <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>Dhanapāla</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his ordination and diligent practice, Venerable Sirimitta successfully attained the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimitta delivered his profound teaching while holding a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>painted fan</b></a> after standing up from the recitation of the Pātimokkha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimitta taught that a monk who is free from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>anger</b></a> and malice will not experience sorrow in the world to come? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sirimitta, a practitioner who avoids <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>slanderous speech</b></a> and deceit is one who lives without future grief? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimitta emphasized that a bhikkhu who constantly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>guards the sense faculties</b></a> ensures that his future existence is free from sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimitta stated that keeping <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>wholesome virtue</b></a> is a critical quality for an ascetic who wishes to remain free from ưu sầu (sorrow) in the next life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimitta highlighted that being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>good friend</b></a> to others is a characteristic of those who will not suffer in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimitta taught that a monk <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>endowed with wisdom</b></a> overcomes the causes of distress in the life hereafter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimitta described unshakeable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>faith in the Tathāgata</b></a> as a primary foundation for spiritual success? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimitta noted that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>virtuous conduct</b></a> is something that is praised by wise people and pleasing to the Noble Ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimitta encouraged all practitioners to develop a firm and dedicated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>faith in the Sangha</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimitta taught that possessing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>straightforward vision</b></a> or right view prevents a person's life from being empty or spiritually poor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Sirimitta, those who are established in faith and virtue are said to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>not poor</b></a> in the eyes of the wise? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sirimitta urged the wise to apply themselves to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato"><b>seeing the Dhamma</b></a> by entering into the core of the Buddha’s message? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag8.2/en/sujato">Thag 8.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> was declared by the Buddha to be the foremost among all monks in the possession of psychic powers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> attained the state of Arahantship just one week after his ordination, following a period of intense effort to overcome sloth and drowsiness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> that laziness and sloth are entirely incompatible with the "noble silence" practiced by the enlightened ones? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> encouraged forest-dwelling monks to be content with whatever scraps of almsfood are dropped into their bowls as a way to crush the forces of Māra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> likened a disciplined monk who has calmed his internal mind to a powerful elephant that easily crushes a hut made of reeds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> reprimanded a woman who tried to tempt him by describing the human body as a "bag of impurities" sewn together with sinews and flesh? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> pointed out that the physical body is a source of foul odors from which nine separate streams of waste are constantly flowing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> compared those who are infatuated with the human body to an old ox that has become helplessly stuck in the mud? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> declared that his mind was like the vast sky, which can never be stained or painted over by unwholesome thoughts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> described the decorated body as an adorned phantom and a "heap of sores" that appears attractive but lacks any lasting permanence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon hearing the news of the passing of his close friend Venerable Sāriputta, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> experienced a sense of awe and horripilation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> emphasized that all conditioned formations are impermanent, and that their total cessation through Nibbāna is the only true bliss? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> taught that those who see the five aggregates as "not-self" can penetrate the subtle nature of reality as if they were piercing a hair with an arrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> urged monks to practice with such urgency that they should feel as though their head was on fire or a sword was pressing against them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> once used his psychic power to shake the massive Mansion of Migāra’s Mother with just his big toe to startle a group of negligent monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> stated that Nibbāna and the release from all bonds cannot be achieved by someone who is weak or lacks spiritual energy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> was frequently found meditating in deep seclusion in caves located between hills such as Vebhāra and Paṇḍava? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> exhorted a Brahmin to pay homage to Venerable Mahākassapa, describing him as a peaceful and tranquil heir of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> noted that even a learned teacher of the three Vedas who honors a saint like Mahākassapa earns merit that is incomparable to worldly knowledge? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
