<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> declared that he had successfully pulled out the root of birth and fulfilled every instruction given by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> attained the goal of his renunciation by living a homeless life and destroying every remaining fetter? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> once confronted Māra, the Evil One, who had entered his belly, warning him of the terrible karmic results of such an act? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> reminded the Evil One of the intense suffering in hell that arises from attacking a disciple of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> recalled a past era where a previous Māra had suffered for many centuries after attacking an enlightened disciple named Vidhura? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> contrasted the dark fate of the Evil One with the glory of celestial palaces made of beryl and jewels that are occupied by those of merit? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> used his psychic power to shake the palace of Sakka, the king of the gods, to demonstrate the might of the Buddha's disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> visited the king of the gods in the heavenly realm to ask him if he understood the liberation that comes through the destruction of craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> interviewed the Great Brahma to challenge his wrong views about the permanence of his celestial realm? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the intervention of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a>, the Great Brahma finally admitted that his world was not eternal and that his former views were mistaken? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> possessed the ability to reach the peaks of high mountains to practice meditation far above the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> warned the Evil One that his attacks on enlightened beings were like a fool trying to touch a blazing fire and burning himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> caused a malevolent spirit to vanish in disappointment after failing to disturb his deep concentration in the Bhesakalā forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> declared that the primary purpose of his life as a monk—reaching the end of all suffering—had been successfully achieved? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> was remembered as one of the two chief disciples of the Buddha, alongside Venerable Sāriputta, both having reached the shore of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> described his liberation as being completely free from the net of fame and honors, having uprooted the causes of all future existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag20.1/en/sujato">Thag 20.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pilindavaccha was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin family</b></a> in the city of Sāvatthī before he began his spiritual search? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato">Thag 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before encountering the Buddha, Venerable Pilindavaccha was an ascetic who possessed a psychic charm known as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato"><b>Lesser Gandhāra</b></a>, which gave him the power of flight and mind-reading? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato">Thag 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the psychic powers of Venerable Pilindavaccha suddenly became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato"><b>ineffective</b></a> the moment the Buddha attained enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato">Thag 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pilindavaccha initially approached the Buddha because he mistakenly believed the Teacher possessed a more powerful <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato"><b>Greater Gandhāra</b></a> charm that had neutralized his own? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato">Thag 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pilindavaccha decided to ordain as a monk only because he believed it was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato"><b>prerequisite</b></a> for learning the Buddha's perceived psychic spells? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato">Thag 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through the Buddha's guidance in the Dhamma and meditation, Venerable Pilindavaccha eventually attained the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato">Thag 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared Venerable Pilindavaccha to be the monk who was most <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato"><b>beloved by the deities</b></a> in the entire Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato">Thag 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that a certain deity served Venerable Pilindavaccha day and night as an act of gratitude for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato"><b>spiritual guidance</b></a> he had provided in a previous life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato">Thag 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Pilindavaccha proclaimed that his arrival at the Buddha’s teaching was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato"><b>good thing</b></a> that led him to the supreme Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato">Thag 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato"><b>Vaccha</b></a> was the clan name of Venerable Pilindavaccha, who was famously known for his past-life associations with heavenly beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.9/en/sujato">Thag 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāmidatta was born into a Brahmin family in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato"><b>Rājagaha</b></a> before he was inspired to join the Buddhist order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato">Thag 1.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāmidatta was first moved to visit the monastery after hearing about the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's great power</b></a> and influence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato">Thag 1.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāmidatta initially attended the Buddha's sermons along with a group of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato"><b>lay followers</b></a> before he developed the faith to ordain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato">Thag 1.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his ordination, Venerable Sāmidatta initially struggled with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato"><b>lack of experience</b></a> and zeal in his meditative practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato">Thag 1.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāmidatta regained his spiritual energy and determination after hearing another <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato"><b>inspiring sermon</b></a> from the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato">Thag 1.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāmidatta eventually attained the fruit of Arahantship and mastered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato"><b>superhuman states</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato">Thag 1.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāmidatta explained his liberation to other monks by stating that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato"><b>five aggregates</b></a> were fully understood and known? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato">Thag 1.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his declaration of final knowledge, Venerable Sāmidatta announced that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato"><b>cycle of birth and death</b></a> had been completely terminated for him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato">Thag 1.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sāmidatta achieved a state where he could confidently proclaim that for him there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato"><b>no further rebirth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.90/en/sujato">Thag 1.90</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paripuṇṇaka was born in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato"><b>Kapilavatthu</b></a> as a member of the Sakyan royal clan? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato">Thag 1.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Paripuṇṇaka means "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato"><b>complete</b></a>" or "full," a name given to him because his wealth and social standing were perfectly fulfilled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato">Thag 1.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in his life as a layman, Venerable Paripuṇṇaka was known for his habit of always consuming the most <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato"><b>exquisite and delicious food</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato">Thag 1.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paripuṇṇaka was spiritually moved when he realized the Buddha, who had lived a life of luxury, was content eating simple <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato"><b>alms food</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato">Thag 1.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paripuṇṇaka questioned why he should pursue fine flavors when the Buddha found the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato"><b>bliss of Nibbāna</b></a> through a simple life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato">Thag 1.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his ordination, Venerable Paripuṇṇaka took the subject of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness of the body</b></a> as his primary meditation theme? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato">Thag 1.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paripuṇṇaka attained Arahantship and declared that the food he "eats" today is the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato"><b>excellent Dhamma</b></a>, which has no equal? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato">Thag 1.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paripuṇṇaka compared the taste of the Dhamma to a nourishment more refined than <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato"><b>ambrosia</b></a> with a hundred flavors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato">Thag 1.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paripuṇṇaka praised the Buddha as a teacher of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato"><b>infinite vision</b></a> who had compassionatey revealed the path to him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato">Thag 1.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Paripuṇṇaka decided to abandon his life of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato"><b>sensual indulgence</b></a> to pursue the same spiritual freedom he saw in the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag1.91/en/sujato">Thag 1.91</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Melajina was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato"><b>royal family</b></a> in Benares and was famous throughout the country for his great learning and virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato">Thag 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Melajina went to hear the Buddha preach at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato"><b>Isipatana</b></a>, an experience that inspired him to go forth into the homeless life and eventually attain Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato">Thag 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Melajina declared that by hearing the True Dhamma, he had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato"><b>overcome all doubt</b></a> regarding the Teacher and the path to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato">Thag 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Melajina praised the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato"><b>leader of the caravan</b></a> and the supreme driver among all those who guide beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato">Thag 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Melajina proclaimed that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato"><b>All-knowing One</b></a> had taught him the way to victory over the cycle of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato">Thag 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rādha was a Brahmin from Rājagaha who entered the monastic life in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato"><b>old age</b></a> when he was no longer able to perform his worldly duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato">Thag 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Venerable <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato"><b>Sāriputta</b></a> to ordain Rādha after seeing that the elderly man possessed the essential qualities for spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato">Thag 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rādha became an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato"><b>outstanding preacher</b></a> by basing his own discourses directly on the inspired words of the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato">Thag 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rādha compared an undeveloped mind to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato"><b>poorly roofed house</b></a> where rain easily leaks in, just as lust penetrates a mind lacking in meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato">Thag 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rādha taught that lust cannot enter a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato"><b>well-developed mind</b></a>, similar to how rain cannot penetrate a house that has been properly thatched? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato">Thag 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Rādha warned that unwholesome desires arise when one fails to properly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato"><b>restrain the mind</b></a> through spiritual discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato">Thag 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Surādha was the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.8/en/sujato"><b>younger brother</b></a> of Venerable Rādha and followed his brother's example by going forth into the homeless life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.8/en/sujato">Thag 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon achieving Arahantship, Venerable Surādha declared that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.8/en/sujato"><b>rebirth was exhausted</b></a> and that he had completed everything taught by the Victor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.8/en/sujato">Thag 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Surādha stated that he had successfully destroyed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.8/en/sujato"><b>net of craving</b></a> and pulled up the very root of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.8/en/sujato">Thag 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Surādha achieved the ultimate goal of the holy life by ensuring that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.8/en/sujato"><b>all fetters</b></a> (saṃyojanā) were entirely eliminated from his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.8/en/sujato">Thag 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Surādha proclaimed that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.8/en/sujato"><b>purpose of homelessness</b></a>, which he sought by leaving his home, had been fully attained through his enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.8/en/sujato">Thag 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Melajina referred to the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato"><b>supreme driver</b></a> of those who need to be tamed, as he had found no more doubt in the Teacher's route? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato">Thag 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that although Venerable Rādha was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin</b></a> by birth, he found the true path to peace only after seeking out the Buddha in Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.7/en/sujato">Thag 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Surādha expressed his spiritual victory by saying that he had done everything required by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.8/en/sujato"><b>teaching of the Victor</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.8/en/sujato">Thag 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Melajina addressed the Buddha as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato"><b>great hero</b></a> (mahāvīra) when declaring his realization that all his doubts had vanished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag2.6/en/sujato">Thag 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Girimānanda was the son of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>ritual advisor</b></a> to King Bimbisāra in the city of Rājagaha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Girimānanda was inspired to seek ordination after witnessing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>majesty and power</b></a> of the Buddha at a festival? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Bimbisāra once promised to provide all necessities for Venerable Girimānanda but <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>forgot his promise</b></a> because of his many royal duties? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because Venerable Girimānanda had to live in the open air, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>rain god</b></a> refused to rain out of fear of making the monk wet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that King Bimbisāra realized the country was suffering from a drought because he had neglected to provide a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>shelter</b></a> for the monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while living in a hut built by the king to end a drought, Venerable Girimānanda practiced meditation and attained the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Girimānanda declared his final knowledge to the world just as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>rain began to fall</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Girimānanda compared the sound of falling rain to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>melodic song</b></a> while he sat comfortably in his hut? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Girimānanda told the rain god to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>rain as much as he wished</b></a>, because his mind was already well-sheltered and at peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Girimānanda described himself as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>at-ease practitioner</b></a> who lived without any agitation while staying in his monastic cell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his spiritual practice, Venerable Girimānanda achieved a mind that was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>completely tranquil</b></a> and settled? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Girimānanda proclaimed that his heart was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>free from lust</b></a> and any remaining traces of desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Girimānanda stated that he lived in a state of happiness because his mind was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>devoid of hatred</b></a> toward any being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Girimānanda reached the ultimate goal of the holy life by becoming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato"><b>free from delusion</b></a> and ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.3/en/sujato">Thag 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana was born into a commoner family in the kingdom of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato"><b>Kosala</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato">Thag 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana was the nephew of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato"><b>Arahant monk</b></a> who lived a secluded life in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato">Thag 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon reaching adulthood, Venerable Sumana was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato"><b>ordained</b></a> into the monastic order by his own maternal uncle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato">Thag 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the first meditation subject given to Venerable Sumana by his teacher was focused on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato"><b>virtue and moral conduct</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato">Thag 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana successfully attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato"><b>four meditative absorptions</b></a> and the direct knowledges while training in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato">Thag 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana reached the state of an Arahant after his teacher instructed him in the specific <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato"><b>method of insight meditation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato">Thag 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana declared that he had attained the goal of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato"><b>deathless</b></a> and had finished everything that needed to be done? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato">Thag 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana emphasized that the Dhamma he reached was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato"><b>self-realized</b></a> and not something he had merely heard from others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato">Thag 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana reached a state where his mind was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato"><b>purified from doubt</b></a> regarding the Teacher's instruction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato">Thag 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana possessed the ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato"><b>recall his past lives</b></a> as a result of his advanced meditative practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato">Thag 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a>, which allowed him to see the passing away and rebirth of beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato">Thag 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana attributed his success to practicing <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato"><b>without negligence</b></a> and following his teacher's advice carefully? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato">Thag 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Sumana praised his teacher for providing instruction that was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato"><b>not in vain</b></a> and for acting out of compassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag5.4/en/sujato">Thag 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta was born as the only surviving child of a wealthy council member in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>Sāketa</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that all the older siblings of Venerable Bhūta were tragically devoured by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>hostile Yakkha</b></a> before he was born? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of Venerable Bhūta, meaning "Beings," was chosen after his parents prayed that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>non-human beings</b></a> would protect him with loving-kindness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during his youth, Venerable Bhūta was raised in extreme luxury, living within <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>three palaces</b></a> specifically built for different seasons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta was kept under <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>strict guard</b></a> throughout his childhood to ensure his safety from the malevolent spirit that had taken his brothers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta decided to renounce the world and ordain after hearing the Buddha preach at a monastery in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>Sāketa</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a monk, Venerable Bhūta lived in seclusion within a cave located on the banks of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>Ajakaraṇī River</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while practicing intensive meditation in the wilderness, Venerable Bhūta successfully attained the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after his enlightenment, Venerable Bhūta returned to visit his relatives in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>Anjana Forest</b></a> out of compassion for their welfare? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta taught that while a wise person sees old age and death as suffering, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>ordinary person</b></a> remains blind and continues to cling to what is painful? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to Venerable Bhūta, there is no happiness found in the world that is superior to the bliss of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>meditation and mindfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta described craving as a toxic force that brings suffering and creates the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>bondage of proliferation</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta emphasized that seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> with wisdom is the supreme way to cleanse the mind of all defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta characterized the path to liberation as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>unconditioned and peaceful</b></a>, leading to the total stilling of all mental disturbances? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that through his diligent practice, Venerable Bhūta was able to completely sever all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>spiritual fetters</b></a> that bind a person to the cycle of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta found immense spiritual joy while sitting in meditation inside a cave even as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>thunder and rain</b></a> roared outside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta enjoyed meditating on riverbanks surrounded by the vibrant colors and scents of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>blooming forest flowers</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta maintained his silent meditation on mountain slopes at midnight while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>wild beasts</b></a> with fangs and tusks roared in the empty forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta taught that by dwelling in a cave or mountain, a monk can achieve superior happiness by subduing all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>thought-constructions</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for Venerable Bhūta, the state of liberation is entirely free from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>fear and obstacles</b></a>, allowing for a comfortable and tranquil seated meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta declared that his meditation was characterized by the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>destruction of the taints</b></a> (āsavas), which is the end of all mental poisons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta described himself as being free from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>arrow of suffering</b></a>, indicating he had removed the core cause of pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even when his relatives requested that he stay with them for their benefit, Venerable Bhūta expressed his preference for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>monastic life</b></a> and bid them farewell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta realized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>path of mindfulness</b></a> provides a joy that far exceeds any worldly delight or sensual pleasure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta compared the Noble Eightfold Path to a supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>good way</b></a> that leads directly to the cessation of all sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Bhūta emphasized that a monk who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>fully understood suffering</b></a> can sit in meditation with a mind that is joyful and stainless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that even in the midst of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato"><b>stormy rain shower</b></a>, Venerable Bhūta found the cave to be a perfect place for sitting in meditative bliss? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag9.1/en/sujato">Thag 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>##DidYouKnow</b> that before his ordination, Venerable Vaṅgīsa was a Brahmin from Sāvatthī who could predict a deceased person’s rebirth by simply <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>tapping on their skull</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa once charged fees of one hundred or even one thousand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>kahāpaṇas</b></a> per skull to tell families the destination of their departed relatives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa met his match when the Buddha presented him with the skull of an Arahant, a destination that his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>mantras</b></a> could not identify? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa was eventually declared by the Buddha to be the foremost monk in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>art of eloquence</b></a> and the delivery of spontaneous verses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a young novice, Venerable Vaṅgīsa struggled with lust after seeing well-dressed women, but he successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>subdued his thoughts</b></a> by reflecting on the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa compared his spiritual resolve to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>skilled archer</b></a> who remains unshakeable even when surrounded by a thousand opponents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa described the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>Kin of the Sun</b></a>, whose teachings show the way to the ultimate peace of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa taught that a true monk is one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>abandoned both pleasure and displeasure</b></a> and no longer creates a basis for craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa realized that all realms—including the sense realm, form realm, and formless realm—are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>impermanent and unstable</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa defined a true sage as one who has <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>severed the sixty-eight wrong views</b></a> and remains unpolluted by attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa warned that those who indulge in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>conceit and pride</b></a> are effectively setting themselves on a path toward a long and sorrowful stay in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when he was burning with lust, Venerable Vaṅgīsa asked <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Ānanda</b></a> for a teaching to extinguish the fire of desire in his mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa was advised by Venerable Ānanda to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>meditation on the foul</b></a> to overcome lustful thoughts about the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa praised the Buddha for teaching that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>well-spoken speech</b></a> must be harmless to oneself and others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa described truthful words as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>immortal speech</b></a> because the virtuous are always established in truth, meaning, and the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa compared the voice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a> to the sweet call of a Sāli bird when he preached to the assembly of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa observed that the words of Venerable Sāriputta were so <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>beautiful and melodious</b></a> that listeners would find their hearts uplifted and full of joy? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>Pavāraṇā ceremony</b></a>, Venerable Vaṅgīsa witnessed five hundred Arahants who had all destroyed the fetters and ended the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa likened the Buddha to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>Wheel-turning Monarch</b></a> who is surrounded by powerful ministers and travels across the entire world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa referred to the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>Seventh Sage</b></a> among the Great Sages, describing him as a great cloud raining down Dhamma to his disciples? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when asked by the Buddha, Venerable Vaṅgīsa clarified that his verses were <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>spontaneously inspired</b></a> rather than prepared in advance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa praised the Buddha for showing the various ways to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>cross the dangerous flood</b></a> of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa declared that those who follow the Buddha’s well-taught Dhamma will remain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>steadfast and non-regressive</b></a> in their practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa honored <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Aññā Koṇḍaññā</b></a> as a disciple who had attained all spiritual goals through diligent training after the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa noted that Venerable Aññā Koṇḍaññā possessed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a> and was skilled in understanding the minds of others? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa witnessed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Mahā-Moggallāna</b></a> using his psychic power to read the minds of five hundred Arahants at the Isigili hill? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa described the Buddha as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>cleansing moon</b></a> that outshines the darkness of the world when seen at the Gaggara Lake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa, in his former life as a wandering poet, traveled from city to city until he met the Buddha and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>reached the further shore</b></a> of all phenomena? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa's faith was born after he heard the Buddha's explanation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>aggregates, sense bases, and elements</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa emphasized that the Buddha was born into the world for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>happiness of many</b></a>, including both men and women? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa found the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> to be a perfect medicine that leads to the total cessation of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after achieving Arahantship, Venerable Vaṅgīsa claimed that he had attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>supreme among analytic insights</b></a> and had done all that the Buddha instructed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa questioned the Buddha about the final destination of his teacher, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Nigrodha Kappa</b></a>, who had passed away at the Aggālavā shrine? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa described the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>best of physicians</b></a> who can cut the arrow of craving and extract all doubts? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa compared a world without the Buddha's wisdom to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>dark night</b></a> where nothing can be clearly seen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa asked the Buddha to speak with his <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>melodious swan-like voice</b></a> to clarify whether his teacher had attained Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha confirmed to Venerable Vaṅgīsa that Nigrodha Kappa had indeed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>cut off all craving</b></a> for name and form and crossed beyond death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa rejoiced that his teacher had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>cut the net of death</b></a> and escaped the power of the tempter, Māra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa honored the Buddha as the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>Best of Two-Footed Beings</b></a> and a true Great Hero of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa noted that a monk who has destroyed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a> is a true Brahmin in the eyes of the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa taught that those who are <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>careless or negligent</b></a> cannot realize the profound Dhamma that leads to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa found that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>holy life</b></a> practiced under the Buddha was a purposeful and fruitful way of living? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa described himself as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>true disciple of the Buddha</b></a> who followed his teacher's instructions with unwavering faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa achieved the mastery of psychic powers and the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>analytic insights</b></a> through his dedication to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa believed that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>instruction of the Buddha</b></a> had been completely fulfilled in his own life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa compared the presence of the Buddha to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>bright sun</b></a> that illuminates the path for all who are lost? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa praised the Buddha for being a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>leader of thousand-fold celestial beings</b></a>, much like the lord of gods? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>immortal state</b></a> (Nibbāna) is the highest destination that can be declared and reached? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Venerable Vaṅgīsa encouraged practitioners to always <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>heed the teachings</b></a> and avoid any form of spiritual negligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the spontaneous verses of Venerable Vaṅgīsa were later <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato"><b>collected by Venerable Ānanda</b></a> and others for the preservation of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thag21.1/en/sujato">Thag 21.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Dhīrā exhorted herself to be persistent and to hold fast to wholesome states while developing her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.7/en/sujato"><b>faculties</b></a> to their completion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.7/en/sujato">Thig 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Dhīrā considered her physical form to be her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.7/en/sujato"><b>final body</b></a> after having successfully conquered Mara and his forces? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.7/en/sujato">Thig 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the primary goal of Bhikkhuni Dhīrā's spiritual practice was to remain steadfast in the Dhamma until she had completely defeated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.7/en/sujato"><b>Mara and his army</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.7/en/sujato">Thig 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Dhīrā emphasized that a practitioner must be persistent and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.7/en/sujato"><b>hold fast</b></a> to all that is wholesome in order to achieve final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.7/en/sujato">Thig 1.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittā, having gone forth into the homeless life with faith, encouraged herself to always find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.8/en/sujato"><b>delight in good friends</b></a> who support one's spiritual growth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.8/en/sujato">Thig 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittā focused her training on the development of wholesome states specifically to attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.8/en/sujato"><b>supreme security from bondage</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.8/en/sujato">Thig 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittā believed that a life of renunciation based on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.8/en/sujato"><b>faith</b></a> requires constant training and joyful effort in what is good? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.8/en/sujato">Thig 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittā described the ultimate goal of her practice as reaching a state that is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.8/en/sujato"><b>secure</b></a> and completely free from all spiritual yokes or disasters? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.8/en/sujato">Thig 1.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Bhaddā sought to live a life of joy by immersing herself in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.9/en/sujato"><b>wholesome dhammas</b></a> after leaving the household life behind through faith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.9/en/sujato">Thig 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Bhaddā urged herself to train in the highest virtues so she could reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.9/en/sujato"><b>state of peace</b></a> beyond all worldly dangers? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.9/en/sujato">Thig 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for Bhikkhuni Bhaddā, the practice of renunciation meant striving for the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.9/en/sujato"><b>highest good</b></a> through the persistent cultivation of wholesome mental states? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig1.9/en/sujato">Thig 1.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīsupacālā attained Arahantship by being well-restrained in the moral precepts and by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato"><b>guarding the sense faculties</b></a> from all worldly distraction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato">Thig 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīsupacālā described the path she attained as the path of peace that leads to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato"><b>pure nectar</b></a> of the Deathless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato">Thig 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Mara tempted Bhikkhuni Sīsupacālā with the joys of various celestial realms, she rejected them because the entire world is being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato"><b>burnt</b></a> by the fires of lust, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato">Thig 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīsupacālā warned that even the highest deities are governed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato"><b>personal identity</b></a> and thus remain trapped in the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato">Thig 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīsupacālā declared that the entire world is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato"><b>trembling</b></a> and ablaze, leaving no place for a wise person to find true delight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato">Thig 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīsupacālā found joy in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato"><b>unshakable Dhamma</b></a> taught by the Buddha, which is beyond comparison and practiced by those who are not common folk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato">Thig 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīsupacālā successfully completed the Buddha’s instructions and attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a>, which signifies the end of all spiritual ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato">Thig 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīsupacālā told Mara he was defeated because she had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato"><b>uprooted delight</b></a> in all forms of existence and shattered the mass of darkness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato">Thig 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīsupacālā followed the spiritual example of her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato"><b>brother</b></a> in going forth, eventually realizing the bliss of liberation for herself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato">Thig 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīsupacālā viewed the various celestial realms, including the Thirty-three and the Tusita heavens, as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato"><b>ablaze</b></a> with the fires of greed and hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig8.1/en/sujato">Thig 8.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sāmā spent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato"><b>twenty-five years</b></a> as a renunciant without being able to gain mastery over her own mind or achieve any sense of inner peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato">Thig 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sāmā was a noblewoman from the city of Kosambi who chose to ordain after being overwhelmed by suffering following the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato"><b>death of Sāmāvatī</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato">Thig 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite her long years of spiritual struggle, Bhikkhuni Sāmā finally realized that her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato"><b>craving was dried up</b></a> on the seventh night of her final intensive effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato">Thig 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sāmā only turned toward the practice of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> in her old age, after listening to a Dhamma talk that revitalized her spiritual path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato">Thig 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sāmā once described her mind as being so agitated and lacking in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato"><b>balance</b></a> that she remained completely unable to control her thoughts for over two decades? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato">Thig 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sāmā attained the state of an Arahant by diligently following the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's teaching</b></a> to remain mindful and free from negligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato">Thig 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sāmā eventually found the strength to escape the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato"><b>state of suffering</b></a> by recollecting the instructions given by the Buddha and applying them with new-found determination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.1/en/sujato">Thig 3.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttama, the daughter of a millionaire in Sāvatthī, decided to leave the household life after hearing the renowned Bhikkhuni <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato"><b>Paṭācārā</b></a> preach the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato">Thig 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttama was initially so restless that she left her dwelling <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato"><b>four or five times</b></a> because her mind remained uncomposed and undisciplined? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato">Thig 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttama reached a spiritual breakthrough after receiving specific instructions on the nature of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato"><b>aggregates, sense bases, and elements</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato">Thig 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttama spent seven full days sitting in a cross-legged position, during which she experienced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato"><b>rapture and happiness</b></a> of successful meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato">Thig 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that on the eighth day of her retreat, Bhikkhuni Uttama declared that she had finally shattered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato"><b>mass of darkness</b></a> that represents spiritual ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato">Thig 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttama achieved liberation only after Bhikkhuni Paṭācārā recognized her mental state and offered her the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato"><b>guidance</b></a> she needed to focus her practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato">Thig 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttama symbolically marked her final release from the bonds of delusion by saying she <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato"><b>stretched her legs</b></a> on the eighth day of her meditative effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.2/en/sujato">Thig 3.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttamā was born into a distinguished <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin family</b></a> in Kosala but chose to ordain after hearing the Buddha preach while he was traveling through the countryside? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato">Thig 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttamā described herself as a true daughter of the Buddha who was spiritual <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato"><b>born from his mouth</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato">Thig 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttamā attained her final goal by cultivating all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato"><b>seven factors of enlightenment</b></a> as the direct path to Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato">Thig 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttamā reached the state she desired by contemplating the themes of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato"><b>void and the signless</b></a> during her meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato">Thig 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttamā proclaimed that her craving for all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato"><b>divine and human desires</b></a> had been completely uprooted and extinguished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato">Thig 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttamā declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato"><b>round of existence</b></a> had been cut off for her, meaning she had achieved complete liberation from the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato">Thig 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttamā lives in constant joy because she has ensured that there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato"><b>no more rebirth</b></a> for her in the future? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato">Thig 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Uttamā reached the ultimate state of liberation and now dwells in the supreme <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato"><b>peace of Nibbāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.3/en/sujato">Thig 3.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before she became Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā, she was the daughter of a wealthy <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>treasurer in Sāvatthī</b></a> who chose to elope with a servant from her household rather than marry the man her parents had selected for her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā suffered a devastating loss during a violent storm when her husband was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>bitten by a snake</b></a> and died while he was trying to gather grass and wood to build a shelter for her and her newborn child? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā witnessed a hawk swoop down and carry away her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>infant son</b></a> because she was too exhausted and weak from childbirth to reach him across a flooding river? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā was screaming in grief for her infant who had been taken by a hawk, her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>elder son</b></a> mistakenly thought she was calling for him, stepped into the swollen river, and was swept away by the current? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that upon returning to her home city, Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā learned that the same storm which claimed her family had also caused her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>parents' house</b></a> to collapse, killing her father, mother, and brother all at once? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name given to Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā literally means "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>cloak-walker</b></a>" or "rag-wearer" because she wandered in a state of madness with her clothes in tatters after the trauma of losing her entire family in a single day? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when the public saw the future Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā wandering in her grief-stricken madness, people threw <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>dirt and stones</b></a> at her, but the Buddha saw that her potential for wisdom was mature and protected her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā regained her clarity of mind at the Jetavana monastery when the Buddha spoke to her directly, causing her to feel <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>shame and modesty</b></a> for her nakedness, at which point a bystander offered her a cloak? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha explained to Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā that the amount of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>tears she had shed</b></a> throughout her many lifetimes in the cycle of rebirth (saṃsāra) is greater than the water in the four great oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā was taught that neither sons, nor fathers, nor any kinsmen can provide a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>true refuge</b></a> or protection when a person is being overcome by death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā attained the stage of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>Stream-entry</b></a> (sotāpatti-phala) immediately after hearing the Buddha’s verses regarding the inability of family to protect one from mortality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after her ordination, Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā would observe how the water she used for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>washing her feet</b></a> would sink into the ground at different distances, which became her primary subject for meditation on the nature of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā used the visual of water vanishing in three stages to realize that some people <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>die in youth</b></a>, some in middle age, and some in old age, just as the water traveled short, medium, and long distances before disappearing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha encouraged Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā by appearing in a vision from his fragrance chamber, telling her that it is better to live for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>one single day</b></a> seeing the rise and fall of the aggregates than to live a hundred years without that vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā eventually attained Arahantship by reflecting on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>impermanence of life</b></a> through the simple daily act of pouring water onto the earth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā speaks of how there is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>no shelter</b></a> among relatives for one who is seized by the End-maker (death), emphasizing the need for individual spiritual diligence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā learned that an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>astute person</b></a> who understands the reality of death and family should quickly clear the path that leads to Nibbāna through the practice of virtue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā's tragic journey began when she was forced to give birth <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>between two villages</b></a> because she could not reach her parents' home in time for her first or second delivery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā spent a night of terror lying on the ground, shielding her children with her body against the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>great rain</b></a> and wind, only to find her husband dead the following morning? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā recited verses stating that her sons, husband, father, mother, and brother were all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>consumed on one pyre</b></a>, symbolizing the total loss of her worldly support system? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha told Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā that the death of her loved ones was not a unique event, but a sorrow she had experienced for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>countless eons</b></a> throughout her journey in saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā was transformed from a woman out of her mind with grief into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>disciplined seeker</b></a> of truth the moment she understood the Dhamma of the four oceans of tears? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā emphasizes that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>virtuous person</b></a>, restrained by moral conduct (sīla), is the one who can truly find the way to the end of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā's story illustrates that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>rise and fall</b></a> of the five aggregates is the essential truth one must see to achieve liberation from the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā was once a woman so broken by grief that she did not even notice her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>lack of clothing</b></a> until the Buddha's presence restored her sense of self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā highlights that when the body is breaking up and life is ending, even <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>blood relations</b></a> are unable to provide any protection or stop the inevitable? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā to stop destroying her own life through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>excessive sorrow</b></a> and instead focus on the path to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā's realization of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>three stages of water</b></a> sinking into the ground served as a profound metaphor for the unpredictability of the time of death for all living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā was encouraged by the Teacher's teaching that seeing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>truth of reality</b></a> for even a moment is far more valuable than a century of ignorant existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā became a powerful example of how the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato"><b>Dhamma can heal</b></a> even the deepest psychological trauma and lead a person to the highest state of peace, Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.10/en/sujato">Thig 5.10</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī</b></a> was originally called "Kisā" (meaning slender or frail) because of her physical weakness and was born into a poor family in Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī was initially treated with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>contempt</b></a> by her husband's family until she gave birth to a son, which finally earned her their respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when her young son died, Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī was so overwhelmed by grief that she carried his corpse from house to house <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>asking for medicine</b></a> to bring him back to life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī to find a single <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>mustard seed</b></a> from a household where no one had ever died in order to cure her dead child? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after failing to find a house untouched by death, Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī realized that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>law of death</b></a> is a universal reality that applies to all living beings, including the devas? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī understood that <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>impermanence</b></a> is the true nature of all things after seeing that death was present in every family she visited? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha taught Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī that death carries away a person obsessed with children and possessions just as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>great flood</b></a> sweeps away a sleeping village? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī attained the first stage of enlightenment, known as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>Stream-entry</b></a>, immediately after hearing the Buddha's initial teaching on the universality of death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared to Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī that living for just <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>one day</b></a> while seeing the rise and fall of phenomena is better than living a hundred years without such wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī attained the final goal of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> after developing her insight into the rise and fall of all things? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī was officially designated by the Buddha as the foremost among all nuns who practice the ascetic virtue of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>wearing rough robes</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī taught that associating with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>good friend</b></a> (kalyāṇamitta) is so powerful that even a fool can become a wise person through their influence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī encouraged others to associate with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>true persons</b></a> because such companionship leads to the growth of wisdom and liberation from all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī emphasized the importance of directly knowing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a>, which consist of suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path to its end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> as the specific "eight-factored path" that leads one to the realization of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī spoke of the specific suffering of women, highlighting the "pain of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>shared husbands</b></a>" as a common source of misery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī reflected on the extreme physical danger of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>childbirth</b></a>, where both the mother and the infant can perish simultaneously during a difficult labor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī endured a tragic journey where her husband died on the road and she was forced to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>give birth alone</b></a> without reaching the safety of her home? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī suffered the loss of both her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>children and husband</b></a> on the very same journey before she could return to her parents? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī witnessed the devastating cremation of her parents and her brother, who were all burned together on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>one funeral pyre</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī noted that the amount of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>tears</b></a> a person sheds through thousands of births in the cycle of samsāra is truly measureless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite seeing her family "devoured" in the cemetery and losing everyone, Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī managed to attain <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>the Deathless</b></a> (Amata)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>dart of sorrow</b></a>, which causes so much mental pain, had been completely pulled out of her heart through her practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Kisā Gotamī described her state of liberation as having "laid down the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato"><b>heavy burden</b></a>" of suffering and the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig10.1/en/sujato">Thig 10.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> was said to have been miraculously born at the foot of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>mango tree</b></a> in the king's garden at Vesālī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before her ordination, <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> was a woman of such extraordinary beauty that many princes competed to possess her, leading her to eventually become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>courtesan</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that out of faith in the Buddha, <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> built a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>monastery</b></a> in her own garden and offered it to the Buddha and the community of monks? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> attained the fruit of Arahantship after listening to the Dhamma preached by her own son, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>Elder Vimalakondañña</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> achieved liberation by developing deep insight into the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>nature of impermanence</b></a> through the observation of her own aging body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> compared her youthfully black and curled hair to the color of bees, noting that in old age it became coarse like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>hemp or tree bark</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> reflected on how her head, once fragrant like a perfume box and adorned with flowers, became as foul-smelling as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>rabbit's fur</b></a> due to old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> described her youthful hair as being as dense as a well-tended forest, yet she eventually faced the reality of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>bald and bare</b></a> head in her old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> observed that her eyebrows, once beautifully painted and arched, had become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>wrinkled and drooping</b></a> as she aged? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> compared her youthful eyes to brilliant blue jewels, but noted that in her old age they became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>lusterless and damaged</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> recalled her nose being soft, slender, and youthful, only to later describe it as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>withered and faded</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> once had ears that shone like finely crafted golden rings, which eventually became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>wrinkled and sagging</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> described her teeth as once being as bright as plantain buds, but they eventually became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>broken and yellowed</b></a> like grain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> compared her youthful voice to the sweet song of a cuckoo, yet in her old age, she found her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>speech became broken</b></a> and faltering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> observed her neck, once smooth and soft like a polished shell, had become <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>wrinkled and lean</b></a> through the process of aging? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> noted her arms, which were once as shapely as round pillars, became as weak and thin as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>trumpet flowers</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Ambapālī</b> compared her youthful hands to soft flowers and golden jewelry, noting that age made them look like <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato"><b>withered tree roots</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.1/en/sujato">Thig 13.1</a></p></div><hr>
