<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Ubbirī experienced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.5/en/sujato"><b>stilling of all desires</b></a> once the "arrow" of her attachment and mourning was removed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.5/en/sujato">Thig 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Ubbirī formally went for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.5/en/sujato"><b>refuge</b></a> to the Buddha, the Dhamma, and the Sangha as a liberated Sage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.5/en/sujato">Thig 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Ubbirī used <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.5/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> to transform her personal tragedy into the realization of the ultimate truth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.5/en/sujato">Thig 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Ubbirī’s name is associated with a queen who moved from a life of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.5/en/sujato"><b>royal luxury</b></a> to the supreme peace of Nibbāna? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.5/en/sujato">Thig 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Ubbirī proclaimed her freedom from suffering by calling the Buddha the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.5/en/sujato"><b>Great Hermit</b></a> who had quelled her internal fever? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig3.5/en/sujato">Thig 3.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā taught thirty nuns that while worldly people spend their youth pounding grain to provide for their families, those following the Dhamma should prioritize the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>Buddha's teaching</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā advised thirty nuns to diligently carry out the instructions of the Buddha so that they would experience <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>no remorse</b></a> later in their spiritual lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a preliminary practice for meditation, Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā instructed thirty nuns to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>wash their feet</b></a> quickly and then sit down to one side to begin their mental training? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā emphasized that the thirty nuns should focus their minds on <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>one-pointedness</b></a> (samādhi) while practicing in accordance with the Buddha's guidance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that during the first watch of the night, thirty nuns under the direction of Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā were able to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>recollect their past lives</b></a> as a result of their intensive meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that thirty nuns practicing under Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> (dibbacakkhu) during the middle watch of the night, allowing them to see the passing away and rebirth of beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that thirty nuns guided by Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>shattered the mass of darkness</b></a>, referring to the destruction of ignorance, during the final watch of the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after attaining liberation, the thirty nuns approached Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>paid homage to her feet</b></a> in deep gratitude for the life-changing instructions she had provided? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the thirty nuns who attained enlightenment under the leadership of Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā compared their teacher to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>Sakka, King of the Gods</b></a>, who is similarly honored by the thirty-three deities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the thirty nuns who followed Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā were able to attain the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā) and become entirely free from all mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā was recognized as an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>undefeated leader</b></a> by her group of thirty disciples who successfully triumphed over the spiritual battlefield of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the thirty nuns who became Arahants under Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā came from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>diverse families</b></a> and were inspired to renounce the world after hearing her powerful Dhamma discourses? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā specifically taught the thirty nuns to practice <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a> (vipassanā) until their spiritual faculties were mature enough for final liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the thirty nuns expressed great joy in their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato"><b>spiritual achievement</b></a>, noting that they had successfully completed their training exactly as their teacher had instructed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.11/en/sujato">Thig 5.11</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before she became Bhikkhunī Candā, she was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>poor Brahmin family</b></a> and grew up in extreme deprivation after her family lost all their wealth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Candā became the sole survivor of her household after a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>devastating pestilence</b></a> killed her entire family during her youth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that for seven years before her ordination, Bhikkhunī Candā survived by wandering from house to house begging for food with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>broken bowl</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while she was a wandering beggar, Bhikkhunī Candā suffered through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>torment of heat and cold</b></a> without proper clothing, shelter, or relatives to support her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Candā first encountered her future teacher, Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā, when she arrived at a place where the community of nuns had just <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>finished their meal</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Candā was treated with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>compassion and kindness</b></a> by the other nuns, who provided her with food when they saw her miserable and pitiable condition? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after being fed by the nuns, Bhikkhunī Candā sat down to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>listen to the Dhamma</b></a> from Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā with an eager and inspired heart? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that it was the fear of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>suffering of rebirth</b></a> (saṃsāra) that prompted Bhikkhunī Candā to approach Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā and request to go forth as a nun? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Candā reflects on her past life as being truly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>wretched and unhappy</b></a> because she was without a husband, children, relatives, or even basic food and clothing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Candā credited Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>showing her mercy</b></a> by accepting her as a student and allowing her to enter the homeless life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Candā practiced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>ardent meditation</b></a> based on the specific personal advice and spiritual instructions given to her by her mentor, Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Candā eventually attained Arahantship and declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>teaching of the Elder Nun</b></a> was not in vain, as it led her to ultimate realization? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Candā attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>highest truth</b></a> through the ripening of her wisdom and the unwavering strength of her spiritual resolve? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Candā, who once owned nothing but a staff and a begging bowl, successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>uprooted all taints</b></a> (āsavas) and realized the three knowledges of an enlightened being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Candā realized that Bhikkhunī Paṭācārā's exhortation was the essential guide that led her to see the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato"><b>true nature of reality</b></a> as it is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.12/en/sujato">Thig 5.12</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā was given her name, which means "Blue Lotus," because her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>skin color</b></a> was the beautiful tint of a blue lotus? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā was born in the city of Sāvatthī as the daughter of a wealthy <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>treasurer</b></a> before she decided to enter the monastic life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā renounced the world after her father suggested she ordain to resolve a conflict among many powerful <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>suitors</b></a> who were competing to marry her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā attained the state of Arahantship while performing her duties by using the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>flame of a lamp</b></a> as an object for meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha declared Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā to be the foremost among all nuns in the exercise of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>psychic powers</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā experienced an intense sense of spiritual urgency (saṃvega) after discovering that she and her own daughter had unknowingly been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>sharing a husband</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā described sensual pleasures as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>filthy and foul</b></a> and full of thorns after witnessing the degradations of worldly life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā abandoned her home and went to the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>Rājagaha</b></a> to go forth into the homeless life after seeing the inherent danger in sensual desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā possessed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> (dibbacakkhu), which allowed her to see things far beyond the natural range of human vision? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā was able to remember her many <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>past lives</b></a> as part of her attainment of the six higher knowledges? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā attained the "knowledge of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>others' minds</b></a>," giving her the ability to know the thoughts and intentions of other people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā developed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>divine ear</b></a> element (dibbasota), which enabled her to hear sounds from both the human and celestial realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā declared she had achieved the complete <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>destruction of taints</b></a> (āsavakkhaya) and had finished everything that the Buddha had taught? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā once manifested her psychic powers by creating a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>four-horse carriage</b></a> and using it to approach and bow at the Buddha's feet? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Māra the Evil One approached Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā while she was alone under a blooming <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>Sāla tree</b></a>, she was completely unfazed and without fear? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā told Māra that even if a hundred thousand <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>seducers</b></a> came to tempt her, they would not be able to move even a single hair on her body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā demonstrated her psychic mastery by telling Māra that she could <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>vanish</b></a> instantly or even enter into his own stomach without him noticing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā claimed she could stand psychically between Māra's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>eyebrows</b></a> in such a way that he would be unable to see where she was? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā compared sensual pleasures to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>swords and stakes</b></a> that "chop to pieces" the mental and physical aggregates of living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā stated that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>delight</b></a> the world finds in sensual pleasures had no attraction for her and had been completely eliminated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā declared she had shattered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>mass of darkness</b></a>, referring to the spiritual ignorance that binds beings to the cycle of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā told Māra that he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>defeated</b></a> because she had reached a state where all forms of sensual joy and ignorance were gone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā identified having a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>well-tamed mind</b></a> and well-developed psychic powers as the reason why she was immune to Māra's attempts to frighten her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā warned Māra that his attempts to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>frighten</b></a> her while she was alone were entirely useless against an enlightened person? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>six higher knowledges</b></a> (chaḷabhiññā), confirming her completion of the Buddhist path and mastery over her own mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā viewed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>aggregates</b></a> (khandhā) as being under constant threat from the dangerous influence of sensual desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā explained that through her realization, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>delight</b></a> (nandi) had been completely destroyed in every respect and in all places? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uppalavaṇṇā reached the final goal of the spiritual life, where she could say she had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato"><b>done what was required</b></a> by the Buddha's teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig11.1/en/sujato">Thig 11.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> was born into a wealthy Brahmin family in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>Vesālī</b></a> and became a Stream-enterer after hearing the Buddha preach the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b>'s father initially criticized recluses for living off the gifts of others, accusing them of being lazy and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>fond of sweets</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> defended the recluses by explaining to her father that they are never lazy and instead perform the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>highest work</b></a> of spiritual development? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> praised recluses for having completely purged the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>three roots of evil</b></a>, which are greed, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> highlighted the fact that recluses maintain a state of total purity in their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>bodily, verbal, and mental</b></a> actions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> compared the inner and outer purity of recluses to the stainless quality of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>polished shell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> described recluses as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>deeply learned</b></a> individuals who throroughly understand the Dhamma and live their lives according to its principles? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> explained to her father that recluses possess mindfulness and use their wisdom to finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>end all suffering</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> noted that when recluses depart from a village, they do so without looking back or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>longing for anything</b></a> they may have left behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> taught her father that recluses do not store up physical wealth but instead seek the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>ultimate goal</b></a> of the holy life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> pointed out that recluses do not handle <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>gold or silver</b></a>, choosing instead to live contentedly in the present moment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> observed how recluses from diverse families and regions still maintain a deep and sincere sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>love and respect</b></a> for one another? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> successfully convinced her father that the recluses represent an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>unsurpassed field of merit</b></a> for those who offer them support and hospitality? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> urged her father to take refuge in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>Buddha, Dhamma, and Sangha</b></a> as the only way to overcome the fear of suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the father of <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b> eventually went forth as a monk and attained <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> after being moved by his daughter's profound wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b>'s father declared that through the practice of the Dhamma, he had truly become a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>real Brahmin</b></a> in the spiritual sense rather than just by birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b>'s father claimed he had attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā) and found true security through the Buddha's path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Rohiṇī</b>'s father used the metaphor of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato"><b>interior bath</b></a> to describe the spiritual purification he achieved through the practice of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig13.2/en/sujato">Thig 13.2</a></p></div><hr>
