<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīhā decided to ordain as a nun after hearing the Buddha preach a sermon directly to her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato"><b>uncle</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato">Thig 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīhā struggled with her meditation for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato"><b>seven years</b></a> because she could not stop her mind from wandering toward external objects? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato">Thig 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīhā suffered from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato"><b>unwise reflection</b></a>, which allowed sensual lust to continuously overwhelm her heart and mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato">Thig 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīhā’s spiritual frustration was so great that she became physically <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato"><b>thin and jaundiced</b></a> due to her lack of inner peace and rest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato">Thig 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīhā reached a point of such deep despair that she decided to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato"><b>hang herself</b></a> in the forest rather than continue living an uncomposed and "low" life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato">Thig 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīhā attained full liberation and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> in the very moment she placed the noose around her neck? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato">Thig 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīhā’s mind was once constantly <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato"><b>unbalanced</b></a> and dominated by the perception of pleasurable images that fueled her desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato">Thig 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīhā used her final moments of life to intensely develop <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato"><b>insight meditation</b></a>, resulting in her sudden and complete enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato">Thig 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīhā described her former state as being obsessed by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato"><b>sensual lust</b></a> and unable to find happiness during the day or the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato">Thig 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīhā successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato"><b>loosened the noose</b></a> and returned to the monastery as a fully liberated and enlightened being? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato">Thig 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīhā stated that her mind was finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato"><b>freed</b></a> from all attachments at the very second she prepared to end her life in the forest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato">Thig 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīhā felt that it was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato"><b>better to die</b></a> than to continue living a life where she could not control her own mind or stop her suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato">Thig 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sīhā’s enlightenment experience proved that even the most <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato"><b>extreme suffering</b></a> and despair can be transformed into the highest wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.3/en/sujato">Thig 5.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā was born in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>Sāvatthī</b></a> into a commoner family before she decided to renounce the world and follow the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā was inspired to ordain after hearing a Dhamma teaching from the senior nun <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>Paṭācārā</b></a>, who became her teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā compared the spiritual path to the labor of young people who use pestles for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>pounding rice</b></a> to earn wealth and support their families? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā encouraged others to practice the Buddha's teaching because it is a way of living that does not lead to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>burning regret</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā was instructed to meditate by observing all <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>conditioned formations</b></a> (saṅkhārā) as something alien and not as her self? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā made a firm determination that she would <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>not get up</b></a> from her meditation seat until her mind was liberated from all mental pollutions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā achieved her spiritual breakthrough after first making her mind <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>well-concentrated</b></a> and one-pointed through meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā attained the ability to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>remember her past lives</b></a> during the first watch of the night of her enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā purified her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>divine eye</b></a> during the middle watch of the night, allowing her to see the passing away and rebirth of beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā declared that the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>mass of ignorance</b></a> was completely shattered during the final watch of the night? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>Three Knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā), marking her transition to the state of an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā reached the highest goal of the spiritual life where she was no longer subject to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>taints</b></a> (āsava) or mental defilements? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā compared her undefeated spiritual status to that of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>Sakka</b></a>, the king of the gods, who is never defeated in battle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā carefully reviewed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>nineteen subjects</b></a> of reflection to ensure that she had correctly accomplished everything required for liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>stretched her legs</b></a> and rose from her seat at dawn, after she was certain her mind was fully released? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Uttarā celebrated her spiritual victory by repeating her verses to a fellow nun at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato"><b>break of dawn</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.1/en/sujato">Thig 7.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> was born into a prominent Brahmin family in the city of Rājagaha and was named for her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>extraordinary physical beauty</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> became a lay follower of the Buddha before eventually ordaining as a nun under the guidance of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> attained the state of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>Non-returner</b></a> (anāgāmī) through her early practice of insight meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> was intercepted by a young libertine in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>Jīvaka's Mango Grove</b></a> while she was on her way to spend the afternoon in meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> questioned her harasser by asking what offense she had committed that would justify him <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>blocking her path</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> reminded the young man that it was completely inappropriate for a man to harass a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>woman of the Order</b></a> who had renounced the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> characterized the teachings of the Buddha as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>Path of Purity</b></a> that is entirely free from mental stains? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> contrasted her own pure and passionless mind with that of her harasser, whose heart was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>filled with lust</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> was urged by the young man to cast off her yellow robes and instead enjoy the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>flowering forest</b></a> during the height of her youth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> was warned by the man that the forest was a place of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>dangerous wild beasts</b></a> and trumpeting elephants, making it no place for a woman alone? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> was compared by her suitor to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>golden puppet</b></a> or a celestial nymph as she stood dressed in her fine Kāsī silk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> was promised by the man that he would be her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>devoted slave</b></a> if she would only agree to live with him in a grand palace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> was offered a life of luxury involving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>sandalwood-carved beds</b></a>, fine rugs, and many servants to attend to her every need? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> challenged the man's obsession by asking what he found so wonderful about a body that is essentially a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>collection of decaying parts</b></a> in a cemetery? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> was told by the man that her eyes, which he compared to those of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>gazelle or a Kinnarī</b></a>, were the primary cause of his uncontrollable lust? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> was complimented on her face, which the man claimed was like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>golden lotus</b></a> and free from all blemishes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> compared the man's attempt to tempt her to someone trying to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>play with the moon</b></a> or jump over Mount Meru? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> declared that she had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>destroyed all craving</b></a> at its root and therefore had no desire for any pleasure in the human or heavenly realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> viewed sensual pleasures as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>pit of glowing coals</b></a> and a deadly poison that should be cast away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> suggested that the man should instead try to tempt women who have not <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>cultivated mindfulness</b></a> or heard the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> maintained her equanimity toward both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>praise and blame</b></a>, seeing all conditioned things as fundamentally impure? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> identified herself as a disciple of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>Sublime Conqueror</b></a> (Sugata), following the Eightfold Path to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> described her mind as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>full of joy</b></a> and completely free from the taints of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>wooden puppet</b></a> held together by strings and pins to explain the empty nature of the physical body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> explained that once the strings and pins of a puppet are removed, it becomes <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>hollow and fragmented</b></a>, just like the components of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> compared the man's infatuation with her appearance to being deluded by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>painted wall</b></a> or a magic show? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> likened the human eye to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>bubble of water</b></a> or a ball filled with secretions and tears, possessing no inherent beauty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b>, in a radical act of detachment, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>plucked out her eye</b></a> and handed it to the man to prove its lack of substance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the moment <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> handed over her eye, the man's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>lust vanished</b></a> and he was struck with terror and regret? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the young man begged <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> for forgiveness, realizing he had effectively "embraced a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>blazing fire</b></a>" by harassing her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b>'s eye was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>miraculously restored</b></a> to its original state as soon as she stood before the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> attained the highest state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> with the analytical knowledges (paṭisambhidā) after being instructed further by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> stated that her mind was "liberated in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>every way</b></a>," signifying her total freedom from the cycle of birth and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> declared that she had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>pulled out the arrow</b></a> of craving, which had previously caused her mental affliction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Subhā</b> reached a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato"><b>unshakeable peace</b></a> in Nibbāna, having completed all that was required for her spiritual journey? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig14.1/en/sujato">Thig 14.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sumangalamātā, while still a laywoman, was married to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato"><b>reed-worker</b></a> and lived a life of poverty before finding her path to liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato">Thig 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sumangalamātā joyfully proclaimed her freedom from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato"><b>pestle</b></a> and the drudgery of household chores after attaining Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato">Thig 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sumangalamātā, whose own son became an Arahant elder, declared herself free from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato"><b>shame</b></a> she once felt toward her husband and her lowly social status? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato">Thig 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sumangalamātā found ultimate peace by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato"><b>cutting off greed</b></a> and hatred, allowing her to dwell in meditation at the foot of a tree? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato">Thig 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sumangalamātā was able to find <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato"><b>bliss</b></a> in her meditation once she was released from the burdens of her kitchen pots and her miserable poverty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato">Thig 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sumangalamātā is an example of a practitioner who used her past <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato"><b>suffering</b></a> as a laywoman as a catalyst for her spiritual awakening? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato">Thig 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sumangalamātā achieved a state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato"><b>well-freed</b></a> from everything that once bound her to a life of domestic struggle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.3/en/sujato">Thig 2.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Aḍḍhakāsī was once a famous courtesan whose fee was so high it was equivalent to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato"><b>tax of Kāsī</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato">Thig 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Aḍḍhakāsī eventually grew <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato"><b>weary of her beauty</b></a> and physical form, leading her to renounce her luxurious life for the sake of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato">Thig 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Aḍḍhakāsī had to be ordained by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato"><b>messenger</b></a> because the road to the Buddha was blocked by men who sought to prevent her from leaving her profession? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato">Thig 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Aḍḍhakāsī attributed her previous life as a courtesan to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato"><b>unwholesome speech</b></a> she had committed in a past existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato">Thig 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Aḍḍhakāsī successfully attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato"><b>Three Knowledges</b></a>, thereby completing the spiritual training taught by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato">Thig 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Aḍḍhakāsī declared she would no longer run through the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato"><b>cycle of rebirth</b></a> after achieving total liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato">Thig 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Aḍḍhakāsī's name refers to the fact that her fee as a courtesan was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato"><b>half of Kāsī</b></a>'s daily revenue? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.4/en/sujato">Thig 2.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Cittā, though born into a distinguished family in Rājagaha, chose to seek ordination after hearing the Buddha preach at the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato"><b>city gate</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato">Thig 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Cittā was one of the many women who were granted ordination by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato"><b>Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī</b></a>, the founder of the order of nuns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato">Thig 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Cittā achieved Arahantship in her old age while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato"><b>climbing Vulture Peak</b></a>, showing that spiritual victory is possible at any stage of life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato">Thig 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Cittā had to rely on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato"><b>staff</b></a> to support her frail and diseased body as she ascended the mountain to meditate? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato">Thig 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Cittā experienced the ultimate liberation of her heart while leaning against a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato"><b>rock</b></a> at the summit of a mountain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato">Thig 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Cittā used her final strength to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato"><b>shatter the mass of darkness</b></a>, a metaphor for the total destruction of spiritual ignorance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato">Thig 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Cittā carried her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato"><b>outer robe</b></a> and her bowl up the mountain, demonstrating her commitment to the monastic life even in her physical weakness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.5/en/sujato">Thig 2.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mettikā was the daughter of a prominent <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin family</b></a> in Rājagaha before she renounced her social standing for the homeless life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato">Thig 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mettikā, much like Bhikkhuni Cittā, attained Arahantship while climbing a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato"><b>mountain peak</b></a> in her old age? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato">Thig 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mettikā reached the end of all suffering despite being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato"><b>painfully weak</b></a> and seeing her youth fade away? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato">Thig 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mettikā attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato"><b>Three Knowledges</b></a>, proving her successful realization of the Buddha’s path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato">Thig 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mettikā achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato"><b>liberation of the mind</b></a> while sitting upon a mountain rock, far from the distractions of the world? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato">Thig 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mettikā's spiritual journey culminated in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato"><b>fulfillment of the Teacher's word</b></a>, a testament to her dedication to the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato">Thig 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mettikā's climb up the mountain with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato"><b>staff</b></a> symbolizes the triumph of spiritual determination over physical infirmity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.6/en/sujato">Thig 2.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā was a member of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan noble family</b></a> and was the half-sister of the Buddha himself? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā was famously known as "Nandā the Beauty" or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>Janapadakalyāṇī</b></a> because of her exceptional physical appearance? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā initially entered the monastic life not out of deep faith, but out of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>love for her relatives</b></a> who had all already ordained? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā spent her early years as a nun avoiding the Buddha because she was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>intoxicated with her own beauty</b></a> and feared he would criticize her vanity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā was moved toward meditation after the Buddha showed her a vision of a woman who <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>gradually became old and ugly</b></a> right before her eyes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha instructed Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā to contemplate her own body as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>ailing, impure, and putrid</b></a> to overcome her attachment to form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā achieved a spiritual breakthrough by practicing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>meditation on the foul</b></a> and making her mind one-pointed and well-composed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā realized that the human body is essentially a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>city made of bones</b></a> that is merely plastered over with flesh and blood? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā taught that only a fool takes delight in the physical body, which she described as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>rotting and decaying</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā declared she had successfully escaped from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>pride of beauty</b></a> by seeing the body as it really is through wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā was the daughter of the Great Mother <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī</b></a>, the woman who founded the order of nuns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā described the physical body as a place where <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>old age and death</b></a>, as well as conceit and deceit, are stored? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā achieved the state of an <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>Arahant</b></a> after hearing the Buddha quote a verse that was later included in the Dhammapada? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā reached a state of inner peace by diligently contemplating the body <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>day and night</b></a> without weariness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā attained liberation by examining the true nature of her body both <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>inside and out</b></a> until she was completely disenchanted? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā characterized her final spiritual state as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>detached from passion</b></a> and freed from all mental bonds? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā found that her internal fever was quenched and she became <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>peaceful and quenched</b></a> once she saw the truth of impermanence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā decided to ordain after reflecting that her elder brother had renounced the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>inheritance of a kingdom</b></a> to become the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā’s path to enlightenment involved transforming her mind from a state of vanity to one of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>carefulness and diligence</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā was encouraged by the Buddha to see that her own body was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>exactly like</b></a> a decaying corpse, and a decaying corpse is exactly like her body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>first fruit</b></a> of the spiritual path, known as Stream-entry, before eventually progressing to full Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā described herself as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>unfettered</b></a> once she realized the true nature of reality and abandoned all attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Sundarī Nandā used her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato"><b>wisdom</b></a> as the tool to finally remove the deluded outlook that most people have toward physical form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.4/en/sujato">Thig 5.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā was born into a royal family in the city of Sāgala within the kingdom of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>Magadha</b></a> and was renowned for her exceptional beauty and skin that shone like gold? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before her ordination, Bhikkhunī Khemā was the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>chief consort</b></a> of King Bimbisāra, yet she initially avoided visiting the Buddha because she feared he would criticize her deep attachment to her own beauty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā was eventually persuaded to visit the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>Veluvana monastery</b></a> only after King Bimbisāra arranged for people to sing praises of the monastery's beautiful gardens to pique her interest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when Bhikkhunī Khemā first encountered the Buddha, he used supernormal power to create a vision of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>celestial maiden</b></a> who was far more beautiful than the queen herself, standing beside him and fanning him? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā watched in the Buddha's vision as a beautiful woman <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>aged rapidly</b></a> before her eyes, becoming toothless, grey-haired, and wrinkled until she finally collapsed on the ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after witnessing a vision of an aging woman, Bhikkhunī Khemā realized that her own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>beautiful body</b></a> was of the same nature and would inevitably face the same decay and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā was taught by the Buddha that those infatuated with sensual pleasures are like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>spider</b></a> caught in the very web it has woven for its own delight? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā attained the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> immediately after hearing the Buddha's discourse, reaching the highest stage of enlightenment even before she had formally entered the monastic order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the Buddha designated Bhikkhunī Khemā as being the foremost among the nuns in the possession of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>great wisdom</b></a> and deep insight into the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while Bhikkhunī Khemā was resting under a tree during the day, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>Māra the Evil One</b></a> appeared in the form of a handsome youth to tempt her back into the world of sensual delights? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra tried to seduce Bhikkhunī Khemā by inviting her to enjoy their youth together while listening to the music of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>five-piece ensemble</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā rejected the advances of Māra by declaring that she felt only loathing and disgust for her own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>foul body</b></a>, which she viewed as fragile and plagued by disease? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā told Māra that she had completely <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>uprooted sensual desire</b></a>, making his attempts to tempt her with lust entirely futile? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā compared sensual pleasures to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>swords and stakes</b></a>, emphasizing the inherent danger and suffering involved in pursuing worldly attachments? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā described the five aggregates of existence as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>chopping block</b></a> upon which beings are broken by the weight of their own suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā boldly told Māra that his power over her was broken and that he was the one who had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>defeated</b></a>, as she was no longer susceptible to his delusions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā criticized those who worship the stars or <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>worship fire</b></a> in the forest, calling such people foolish for not understanding the true nature of reality as it is? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that instead of external rituals, Bhikkhunī Khemā declared her devotion by paying homage to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>Enlightened One</b></a>, whom she called the supreme among men? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā asserted that she had been <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>freed from all suffering</b></a> by faithfully following the instructions provided by her teacher, the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā told Māra that her delight in all things had been severed and her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>mass of darkness</b></a>, representing ignorance, had been completely shattered? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that according to the Apadāna tradition, Bhikkhunī Khemā first attained the fruit of Stream-entry and then sought <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>King Bimbisāra's permission</b></a> to go forth and ordain as a nun? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā abandoned her life of luxury as a queen to embrace a life of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>renunciation</b></a>, seeking a peace that cannot be found in worldly status or beauty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Khemā credited her spiritual success to her diligence in carrying out the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato"><b>Teacher's word</b></a>, which led her to the ultimate state of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.3/en/sujato">Thig 6.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Cālā was born in the village of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>Nālaka</b></a> within the Magadha region before she chose to renounce her home life for the Buddhist monastic order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Cālā was the daughter of a Brahmin woman named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>Surūpasārī</b></a> and came from a family deeply connected to the Buddha’s teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Cālā was a younger sister of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>Venerable Sāriputta</b></a>, who was the Buddha's chief disciple and foremost in wisdom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Cālā decided to enter the monastic life after realizing that the Dhamma followed by her brother was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>not an ordinary teaching</b></a> but a path that led to ultimate spiritual freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Cālā often spent her afternoons meditating in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>Andhavana</b></a> (the Blind Men's Grove) after she had completed her morning almsround in the city of Sāvatthī? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra the Evil One attempted to disturb Bhikkhunī Cālā by questioning why she lived as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>shaven-headed</b></a> nun without adhering to any of the well-known religious sects of the time? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Cālā dismissed the validity of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>outside sects</b></a>, arguing that they were built upon wrong views and lacked a true understanding of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Cālā took refuge in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan Buddha</b></a>, describing him as a peerless teacher who had helped her transcend all speculative and worldly views? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Cālā received a direct teaching from the Buddha on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a> as the definitive method for bringing an end to all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Cālā practiced the contemplation of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a>, gaining full insight into suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the way to its end? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Cālā successfully attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>Three Knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā), a mark of her complete spiritual liberation and the ending of all mental taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Cālā informed the defeated Māra that her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>delight</b></a> in the world had been completely destroyed, leaving her unmoved by his attempts to tempt her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Cālā declared she had completely shattered the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>mass of darkness</b></a>, referring to the spiritual ignorance that binds beings to the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Cālā attained a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato"><b>tranquil formations</b></a> (passambhaṇa), where all conditioned mental and physical activities were stilled in perfect peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.2/en/sujato">Thig 7.2</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Upacālā was a daughter of the Brahmin woman Surūpasārī and a sister to the other famous nuns <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>Cālā and Sīsupacālā</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Upacālā reached the state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> after developing her mindfulness and spiritual faculties with intense diligence and effort? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Māra the Evil One tried to influence Bhikkhunī Upacālā by suggesting that she should desire <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>rebirth</b></a> so that she could enjoy the sensual pleasures available to living beings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Upacālā rejected Māra’s views, stating that for anyone who is born, there is an inevitable fate of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>death</b></a> and various forms of physical and mental sorrow? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Upacālā viewed the state of being born as a condition filled with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>mutilation</b></a>, bondage, and constant spiritual affliction? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Upacālā followed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>Unconquered Buddha</b></a> of the Sakyan clan, who provided her with the teaching necessary to transcend the cycle of birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Upacālā attributed her spiritual breakthrough to the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>Noble Eightfold Path</b></a>, which led her beyond the reach of Māra and suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Upacālā meditated on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>origin of suffering</b></a> to fully understand how to uproot the causes of her own pain and rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Upacālā successfully realized the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>Threefold Knowledge</b></a>, confirming her mastery over the Dhamma and her own mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Upacālā joyfully proclaimed that she had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>done what was required</b></a> by the Buddha for her enlightenment and was no longer subject to birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Upacālā warned Māra that he was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>defeated</b></a> because her mind was no longer susceptible to the illusions of delight and desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Upacālā attained a state where her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>spiritual ignorance</b></a> was completely destroyed and her mental darkness was replaced by light? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Upacālā achieved a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>well-developed mind</b></a> by training her spiritual faculties to see through the deceptions of Māra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Upacālā described her practice as becoming a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato"><b>superior person</b></a> who had successfully stilled all conditioned formations through the path of peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig7.3/en/sujato">Thig 7.3</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> was the only daughter of a wealthy and virtuous merchant in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>Ujjenī</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> recounted her life story while sitting on the sandy banks of the Ganges River in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>Pāṭaliputta</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā), including the memory of her past lives, only seven days after her ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> was first married to a merchant's son from the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>Sāketa</b></a>, serving him with great devotion for a month before he inexplicably came to hate her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> used to wash the feet of her first husband and groom him with a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>comb and mirror</b></a> like a humble handmaid, despite her own wealthy upbringing? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the first husband of <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> threatened to leave his parents' home forever if he was forced to continue living with her, despite her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>faultless behavior</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after her first marriage failed, the father of <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> married her to a second wealthy family for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>half the price</b></a> of her original bride price? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the second husband of <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> sent her back to her father after only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>one month</b></a>, even though she served him as a devoted slave? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the third husband of <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>poor mendicant</b></a> whom her father invited to give up his bowl and rags to become a householder? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the third husband of <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> abandoned her after only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>half a month</b></a>, preferring his life of begging to a marriage with her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> was so distraught by her repeated marital failures that she contemplated <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>ending her life</b></a> before deciding to seek ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> chose to go forth into the homeless life under the guidance of the learned and virtuous <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>Bhikkhunī Jinadattā</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the father of <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> initially urged her to stay home and gain merit by giving <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>food and drink</b></a> to recluses rather than ordaining? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> discovered that her current life of rejection was the ripening of an ancient karma from when she was a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>wealthy goldsmith</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a past life, <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> was a man who committed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>adultery</b></a> with the wives of others, which led to a long rebirth in hell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after emerging from hell, <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> was reborn as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>monkey</b></a> and was castrated by the leader of the troop when she was only seven days old? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> once lived as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>goat</b></a> in the Sindhava forest, where she was lame, one-eyed, and eventually castrated? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> recalled a past life as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>dark red calf</b></a> who was castrated at twelve months old and used for heavy labor pulling wagons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a result of past misconduct, <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> was once reborn as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>hermaphrodite slave</b></a> child to a household servant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> was once reborn into a poor family of carters and was eventually taken away as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>debt slave</b></a> because her father could not repay his interest? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that at the age of sixteen, <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> was forced to become the wife of a merchant's son while she was still a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>debt-slave girl</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> caused great domestic strife in a past life by making her husband's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>virtuous first wife</b></a> an object of hatred? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> explained that the "fruit" of creating enmity between a husband and wife in a past life was the reason she was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>constantly rejected</b></a> in her final birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the name of the city Pāṭaliputta, where <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> resided, is said to mean "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>Son of the Trumpet-flower</b></a>"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> and her companion, Bhikkhunī Bodhī, were both noted for being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>highly learned</b></a> and skilled in the recitation of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> shared her past-life history in response to a question about why she chose the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>life of renunciation</b></a> while she was still young? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b>'s father was so devoted to her that he allowed her to ordain only when he realized her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>deep misery</b></a> in the lay life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> described the act of serving her husband as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>supreme service</b></a>, yet it failed to win his affection due to her past karma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b>'s second husband's family returned her to her home despite her being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>faultless and virtuous</b></a> because he simply could not stand to live with her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>Erakaccha</b></a> was the location where <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> lived seven lives ago as a wealthy male goldsmith? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> mentioned that her past life as a goat was plagued by <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>worms and parasites</b></a> because of her previous sexual misconduct? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> prostrated herself before <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>Bhikkhunī Jinadattā</b></a> and offered her food before asking for permission to join the Order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b>’s father prayed for her to reach the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>highest state of peace</b></a>, Nibbāna, when he finally gave his consent for her to become a nun? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> declared that she had finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>ended all suffering</b></a> and the cycle of rebirth through her practice of the Buddha's path? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Isidāsī</b> concluded her verses by stating that she had <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato"><b>exhausted the karma</b></a> that had followed her through multiple animal and human births? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig15.1/en/sujato">Thig 15.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittā was born as a princess of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato"><b>Sakyan clan</b></a> in Kapilavatthu before she abandoned her royal life to enter the order? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato">Thig 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittā renounced her household life and sought ordination together with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato"><b>Mahāpajāpatī Gotamī</b></a>, the founder of the order of nuns? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato">Thig 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittā consistently observed the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato"><b>Uposatha</b></a> days, including the eighth, fourteenth, and fifteenth of the lunar month, as a core part of her discipline? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato">Thig 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittā maintained a high level of virtue by strictly following the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato"><b>eight precepts</b></a> throughout her monastic career? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato">Thig 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittā performed her spiritual duties with such dedication that she believed her conduct was a source of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato"><b>joy for the devas</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato">Thig 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittā practiced the austerity of eating only <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato"><b>one meal a day</b></a> after shaving her head and adopting the monastic robes? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato">Thig 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittā declared her spiritual practice was aimed at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato"><b>subduing mental suffering</b></a> rather than merely seeking a place in the celestial realms? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato">Thig 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after many years of industrious training, Bhikkhuni Mittā successfully attained the fruit of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.7/en/sujato">Thig 2.7</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā’s Mother was once a celebrated beauty in the city of Ujjenī named <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato"><b>Padumavatī</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato">Thig 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā’s Mother had a son named Abhaya with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato"><b>King Bimbisāra</b></a> of Magadha before she eventually decided to leave the world behind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato">Thig 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā’s Mother was inspired to enter the monastic life after hearing a Dhamma talk given by her own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato"><b>son</b></a>, who had already ordained as a monk? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato">Thig 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā’s Mother achieved full spiritual realization by contemplating the body from the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato"><b>head down to the feet</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato">Thig 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā’s Mother reached a state where she felt <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato"><b>cool and tranquil</b></a> once the heat of mental distress had been completely extinguished? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato">Thig 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā’s Mother successfully achieved the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato"><b>uprooting of sensual desire</b></a> through the constant observation of the body’s true nature? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato">Thig 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā’s Mother characterized the human body as being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato"><b>inherently impure</b></a> and foul-smelling to overcome her attachment to physical beauty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato">Thig 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā’s Mother attained the stage of an Arahant and later added her own verses to those taught by her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato"><b>monk son</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.8/en/sujato">Thig 2.8</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā was a noblewoman from Ujjenī who chose to ordain primarily out of love and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato"><b>affection</b></a> for her close friend, the mother of Abhaya? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato">Thig 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā visited a garden in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato"><b>Rājagaha</b></a> to specifically practice meditation on the impurity of the physical form? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato">Thig 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that while meditating, Bhikkhuni Abhayā received a direct instruction from the Buddha, who used his powers to show her a vision of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato"><b>impurity</b></a> she was contemplating? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato">Thig 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā learned from the Buddha that the human body is <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato"><b>fragile</b></a> and is often mistakenly delighted in by common people? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato">Thig 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā was exhorted by the Buddha to live with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato"><b>mindfulness and diligence</b></a> to ensure she would finally escape all spiritual suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato">Thig 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā attained the state of an Arahant while the Buddha appeared to be sitting right in front of her in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato"><b>vision of light</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato">Thig 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā proclaimed her spiritual success by declaring that she had reached the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato"><b>destruction of craving</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato">Thig 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Abhayā transformed the verses spoken to her by the Buddha into her own <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato"><b>internal declaration</b></a> of final knowledge and liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig2.9/en/sujato">Thig 2.9</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Nanduttarā was a former debating champion who traveled throughout India carrying a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato"><b>rose-apple branch</b></a> as a sign of her challenge to anyone who dared to debate her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato">Thig 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Nanduttarā was originally a follower of the Jain (Nigaṇṭha) sect before she was defeated in a debate by the eminent Elder <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato"><b>Mahā Moggallāna</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato">Thig 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that before finding the Buddha's path, Bhikkhuni Nanduttarā practiced ritual purifications by bathing at <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato"><b>river banks</b></a> and worshipping fire, the moon, and the sun? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato">Thig 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Nanduttarā once followed an ascetic vow that required her to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato"><b>shave only half of her head</b></a> and sleep on the bare ground? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato">Thig 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Nanduttarā admitted she was once deeply attached to her own appearance, taking great pleasure in <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato"><b>ornaments and jewelry</b></a> and the pampering of her body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato">Thig 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Nanduttarā reached the highest stage of enlightenment and declared her mind had attained a state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato"><b>peace</b></a> after uprooting all sensual desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato">Thig 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Nanduttarā was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin family</b></a> in the town of Kammāsadamma within the territory of the Kurus? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato">Thig 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Nanduttarā described her spiritual liberation as the total <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato"><b>releasing of all bonds</b></a> and yokes that had tied her to the cycle of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato">Thig 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Nanduttarā successfully <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato"><b>cut off all rebirth</b></a> by drying up her former cravings for both human and divine existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato">Thig 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Nanduttarā abandoned her former <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato"><b>ascetic practices</b></a>, such as not eating in the evening, once she gained faith in the Buddha's teaching? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.5/en/sujato">Thig 5.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittakāḷī decided to leave the household life and ordain after hearing a teaching on the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato"><b>Four Foundations of Mindfulness</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato">Thig 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittakāḷī struggled for <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato"><b>seven years</b></a> following her ordination because she was obsessed with receiving offerings and worldly honor? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato">Thig 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittakāḷī confessed she had been frequently <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato"><b>argumentative</b></a> and quarrelsome with others during her early years as a nun? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato">Thig 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittakāḷī realized she had initially abandoned the ultimate goal of the spiritual life to pursue <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato"><b>lowly goals</b></a> like gain and respect? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato">Thig 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittakāḷī experienced intense <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato"><b>restlessness</b></a> while sitting in her monastic cell, realizing her mind was still being dominated by craving? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato">Thig 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittakāḷī eventually overcame her negligence by reflecting on the fact that her life was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato"><b>short</b></a> and she was already being oppressed by old age and illness? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato">Thig 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittakāḷī achieved the final goal of Arahantship by carefully observing the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato"><b>arising and falling</b></a> of the five aggregates of existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato">Thig 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittakāḷī attained liberation only after she developed <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato"><b>wise reflection</b></a> and turned her mind toward intensive insight meditation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato">Thig 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittakāḷī was born into a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato"><b>Brahmin family</b></a> in the town of Kammāsadamma, located in the Kuru country? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato">Thig 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhuni Mittakāḷī proclaimed that she had finally <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato"><b>done what the Buddha taught</b></a> after achieving the complete liberation of her mind from all taints? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig5.6/en/sujato">Thig 5.6</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> was the daughter of King Koñca and his chief queen in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>Mantāvatī</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that from her childhood, <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> found no delight in sensual pleasures and instead spent her time visiting the nunnery to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>listen to the Dhamma</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> was betrothed by her parents to Anikaratta, the king of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>Vāraṇavatī</b></a>, against her own spiritual aspirations? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> declared to her parents that she had no interest in household life and preferred to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>seek ordination</b></a> as a nun? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in a bold act of renunciation, <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> used a sword to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>cut off her hair</b></a> and used it to meditate on the impurity of the body? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>First Jhāna</b></a> while locked in her palace chamber, even as her parents pleaded for her to accept the royal marriage? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> attained the state of an Arahant with the analytical knowledges while she was still a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>trainee</b></a> (sikkhamānā)? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> characterized sensual pleasures as being like the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>venom of a snake</b></a>, warning that they only lead to bitter results? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> warned that those who indulge in sensual pleasures for a long time will eventually suffer the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>torments of hell</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> criticized those who lack wisdom for failing to restrain their <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>body, speech, and mind</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> argued that even a birth in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>heavenly realms</b></a> is impermanent and not a place of lasting security? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> emphasized the difficulty of escaping the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>four planes of misery</b></a> once a being has fallen into them? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> chose to go on a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>hunger strike</b></a>, vowing to die rather than live as a laywoman? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that the parents of <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> tried to entice her with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>entire kingdom</b></a> of Vāraṇavatī and the prospect of being a chief queen? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> described the human body as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>foul-smelling bag</b></a> filled with corpses and impurities? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> challenged her suitor by asking why anyone would want a body that is essentially a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>collection of bones</b></a> and sinews, filled with filth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> noted that if the body were turned inside out, even one's <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>own mother</b></a> would be unable to bear the smell? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> understood the body to be merely a product of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>aggregates, sense bases, and elements</b></a>, with no core essence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> stated that she would rather be struck by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>hundred spears</b></a> every day for a century than continue to endure the suffering of saṃsāra? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> taught that beings suffer immeasurable slaughter in the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>animal and hungry ghost</b></a> realms throughout their many lives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> considered all worldly wealth and property to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>hollow and valueless</b></a> compared to the goal of liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> used the example of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>King Mandhātā</b></a>, a universal monarch, to show that even total worldly power cannot satisfy human desire? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> argued that even a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>rain of jewels</b></a> across the ten directions would not be enough to satisfy a person's sensual cravings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> compared sensual pleasures to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>burning torch</b></a> that will burn the hand of whoever does not let it go? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> likened sensual desire to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>gnawed bone</b></a>, which provides no real nourishment and only leads to struggle? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> described sensual pleasures as being as fleeting as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>dream or an illusion</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> compared the danger of desire to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>pit of glowing coals</b></a> or a sharp stake? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> asked who could help her when her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>head was on fire</b></a> with the urgency of old age and death? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> pointed out that the volume of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>tears, milk, and blood</b></a> shed by a single being in saṃsāra is greater than the water in the four oceans? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> stated that the bones of a single person accumulated over many lives would be as high as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>Mount Vipula</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> used the simile of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>blind turtle</b></a> putting its head through a hole in a drifting yoke to describe the extreme rarity of a human birth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> described the human body as being as fragile and essence-less as a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>water bubble</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> asked why anyone would drink the "hundred bitters" of sensual pleasure when the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>nectar of the Deathless</b></a> (Nibbāna) is available? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> identified sensual pleasures as having many "enemies," such as <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>fire, thieves, and floods</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> compared those caught in sensual pleasure to a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>fish swallowing a hook</b></a>, destined for future disaster? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> described being bound by desire as like a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>dog on a leash</b></a>, unable to find true freedom? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> praised the Path of the Buddha as the only way to reach a state free from <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>aging, death, and sorrow</b></a>? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b>'s resolve was so powerful that she convinced <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>King Anikaratta</b></a> himself to beg her father for her permission to ordain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> eventually attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>six higher powers</b></a> (abhiññā), confirming her total spiritual victory? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> recalled a past life during the time of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>Buddha Koṇāgamana</b></a>, where she and her friends built a monastery for the Sangha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that as a result of her past merit, <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> was reborn among the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>devas</b></a> hundreds of times before her final birth as a princess? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that in her past lives as a human, <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> often held the status of a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>chief queen</b></a> of a universal monarch? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> attributed her final success to her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>unwavering patience</b></a> and her devotion to the Buddha's teachings? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> compared the difficulty of attaining human status to a single person's bones becoming a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>mountain of skeletons</b></a> over the eons? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> taught that those who do not understand the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>Four Noble Truths</b></a> remain forever trapped in the cycle of rebirth? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> stated that even if a person ruled over <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>four continents</b></a>, they would still die without having fulfilled all their desires? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> described the holy life as a way to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>cut off craving</b></a> for future existence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> explained that once the body is discarded in a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>cemetery</b></a>, it is viewed with disgust even by one's closest relatives? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that <b>Bhikkhunī Sumedhā</b> attained her liberation because she saw that the world was <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato"><b>burning</b></a> with the fires of greed, hatred, and delusion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig16.1/en/sujato">Thig 16.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Sujātā was born as the daughter of a royal <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato"><b>treasurer in Sāketa</b></a> and lived a life of great luxury and happiness before encountering the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato">Thig 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Sujātā was returning from a festival of stars, adorned with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato"><b>sandalwood and garlands</b></a>, when she first saw the Buddha in the Anjana Grove? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato">Thig 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Sujātā attained the highest stage of enlightenment, <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a>, while still a laywoman listening to a sermon that the Buddha tailored specifically to her spiritual maturity? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato">Thig 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Sujātā received the full <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato"><b>consent of her husband</b></a> and her parents to enter the monastic order after she had already realized the truth of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato">Thig 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Sujātā described the Buddha as the "Light of the World" and the one with the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato"><b>Eye of Dhamma</b></a> who taught her out of deep compassion? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato">Thig 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Sujātā attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato"><b>three knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā) after going forth, proving that the Buddha’s instructions are never empty? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato">Thig 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Sujātā realized the "dust-free" state of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato"><b>immortality</b></a> (Nibbāna) during the very same session in which she first encountered the Enlightened One? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato">Thig 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Sujātā traded her life of luxury, which included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato"><b>many types of jewelry</b></a> and many servants, for the simple and homeless life of a nun? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato">Thig 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Sujātā was accompanied by a <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato"><b>group of maidservants</b></a> when she wandered into the forest monastery, an event that led to her total spiritual transformation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato">Thig 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Sujātā emphasized that her renunciation was a path toward <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato"><b>uprooting all suffering</b></a>, a goal she successfully achieved through her diligent practice? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato">Thig 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Sujātā was born into a family of high status and <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato"><b>great wealth</b></a>, yet she found true happiness only after leaving her status behind to see the Teacher? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato">Thig 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Sujātā reflected on how her life changed from attending <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato"><b>festivals and amusement gardens</b></a> to seeking the profound and liberating truth of the Dhamma? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato">Thig 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Sujātā declared her spiritual success by stating that her realization of the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato"><b>true Dhamma</b></a> allowed her to penetrate the truth at the very moment she heard it? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.4/en/sujato">Thig 6.4</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Anopamā derived her name from the Pali word for "<a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato"><b>peerless</b></a>" because her physical beauty was considered unmatched by anyone else in her region? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato">Thig 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Anopamā was so highly desired that suitors offered her father <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato"><b>eight times her weight</b></a> in gold and jewels just for the chance to marry her? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato">Thig 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that despite being the daughter of the wealthy treasurer <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato"><b>Majjha in Sāketa</b></a>, Bhikkhunī Anopamā rejected all marriage proposals to pursue a spiritual life of "nothingness"? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato">Thig 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Anopamā attained the stage of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato"><b>Non-returning</b></a> (anāgāmī-phala) immediately upon hearing the Buddha's discourse while she was still a laywoman? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato">Thig 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Anopamā attained final <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato"><b>Arahantship</b></a> on the seventh night after she had shaved her hair and entered the homeless life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato">Thig 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Anopamā's suitor list included <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato"><b>kings' sons</b></a> and wealthy merchant heirs who sent messengers to her father with immense offers of dowry? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato">Thig 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Anopamā declared that all her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato"><b>craving was goured out</b></a> (cleansed) within just one week of intensive practice after her ordination? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato">Thig 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Anopamā felt a profound sense of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato"><b>spiritual enthusiasm</b></a> when reflecting on her spiritual achievement and her decision to leave behind worldly riches? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato">Thig 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Anopamā sought out the Buddha, the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato"><b>Supreme Enlightened One</b></a>, after realizing that her physical beauty and wealth could not provide lasting peace? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato">Thig 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Anopamā attained her high spiritual realizations through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato"><b>ardent meditation</b></a> and by constantly remembering the specific instructions given to her by the Buddha? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato">Thig 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Anopamā is celebrated for her <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato"><b>unwavering resolve</b></a> to live a life with "nothing at all," despite being born into a family with vast property and treasures? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato">Thig 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Anopamā described her conversion as sitting to one side while <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato"><b>Gotama</b></a>, out of compassion, preached the Dhamma that led to her liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato">Thig 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Anopamā considered her choice to be <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato"><b>homeless</b></a> as the most precious decision, far outweighing the value of all the gold offered by her many suitors? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig6.5/en/sujato">Thig 6.5</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother was born in the city of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>Bhārukaccha</b></a> into a family of commoners before she decided to renounce her home life? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that after hearing a monk preach the Dhamma, Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother became a lay devotee and eventually <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>entrusted her son</b></a> to her relatives so she could ordain as a nun? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that when her son Vaddha visited her in the nunnery, Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>rebuked</b></a> him for his attachment to worldly desires and urged him not to be a partner in continuous suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother described the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>happiness of an ascetic</b></a> as being free from craving, having doubts cut off, and living with a cool and restrained mind? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother exhorted her son to pursue the path of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>Right View</b></a> as the definitive way to reach the end of all suffering? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother declared that not even a tiny amount of <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>sensual lust</b></a>—whether low, high, or middling—remained within her after her spiritual liberation? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother successfully attained the <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>Three Knowledges</b></a> (tevijjā) and had completely finished all that the Buddha had instructed? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother achieved the complete <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>destruction of taints</b></a> (āsavakkhaya) through her diligent meditation and commitment to being heedless? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that her son Vaddha described Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother as a compassionate guide who provided him with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>truth-filled verses</b></a> that motivated his own enlightenment? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that because of the effective encouragement from Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother, her son practiced with <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>diligent effort</b></a> day and night until he reached Arahantship? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother warned her son that falling into <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>desire</b></a> would lead to an endless participation in the cycle of continuous pain? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother lived in a state of being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>cool and restrained</b></a>, characterized by the total cessation of mental pollutions? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that her son Vaddha, upon attaining Arahantship, praised Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother for being <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>excellent</b></a> in her ability to push him toward the goal with wisdom and affection? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother urged her son to <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>go forward in training</b></a> so that he could achieve the same peace she had found? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother developed her mind through <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>meditation</b></a> and the removal of all distraction to become an Arahant? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
<div class="quote-entry"><p><b>#DidYouKnow</b> that Vaddha initially questioned whether his mother, Bhikkhunī Vaddha's Mother, had really <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato"><b>freed herself from lust</b></a> before he witnessed her profound spiritual confidence? <a href="https://suttacentral.net/thig9.1/en/sujato">Thig 9.1</a></p></div><hr>
